window.STUDY_CONTENT = {"readme":"# 🎯 FRM® Part I — Complete Study Hub\n\n> Everything you need to pass GARP's Financial Risk Manager **Part I** exam, built around the **2026 curriculum** — explained chapter by chapter, with flashcards, interactive quizzes and a full mock exam.\n\nThis site is a self-contained study app. Use the **sidebar** to jump to any of the 62 readings; each chapter has three tabs — **📖 Notes**, **🃏 Flashcards**, and **📝 Quiz** (tap an option to see the answer and a worked explanation instantly). Your progress, quiz answers, theme and text size are saved in your browser.\n\n{: .tip }\nPress **/** to search everything · press **c** for the financial calculator · use the **🌙** button (top-right) for dark mode and **A− / A+** to size the text.\n\n---\n\n## 📋 The exam at a glance\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Format | Computer-based test (CBT) |\n| Questions | 100 equally weighted multiple-choice questions |\n| Duration | 4 hours |\n| Windows (2026) | May · August · November |\n| Negative marking | **None** — never leave a question blank |\n| Passing | No fixed pass mark; scored relative to candidates (results ~6 weeks) |\n| Calculators | TI BA II Plus (incl. Professional), HP 12C, HP 10B II/II+, HP 20B |\n\nAlways confirm dates, fees and policies at [garp.org/frm](https://www.garp.org/frm). This is an independent study companion built from GARP's published 2026 learning objectives — see the **Free Resources** page in the sidebar.\n\n## 📚 The four books (and their exam weights)\n\n| Book | Topic | Weight | Readings |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Foundations of Risk Management | 20% | 11 |\n| 2 | Quantitative Analysis | 20% | 15 |\n| 3 | Financial Markets and Products | 30% | 20 |\n| 4 | Valuation and Risk Models | 30% | 16 |\n\nBooks 3 and 4 are 60% of the exam and the most calculation-heavy — weight your practice toward them, but treat Foundations and Quant as \"free marks\" if you know the definitions and standard formulas cold.\n\n## 🗓️ How to use this hub\n\n1. **Read** a chapter's Notes, then immediately flip its **Flashcards** — and *rate each card* (Again / Hard / Good / Easy). Rated cards come back for review on exactly the right day via **🔁 Smart Flashcards**.\n2. **Drill** the chapter **Quiz** — tap to answer, read every explanation (each one shows the exact calculation and a diagram), and ⭐ star anything you want to revisit.\n3. Do the **🎯 Daily Drill** every day — ten exam-weighted questions, fresh each morning. Keep the 🔥 streak alive; consistency beats cramming.\n4. Your **error log keeps itself**: every question you miss, anywhere, lands in the **🧠 Review Hub** and stays there until you answer it correctly. The hub also shows your accuracy by book against the exam weights.\n5. After each book, revise with its **Formula Sheet** (Reference & Revision section).\n6. In the final fortnight: sit both **Mock Exams** (timed, no notes), clear your Review Hub, and sweep your ⭐ bookmarks.\n\n## ⏱️ Two study schedules\n\n**16-week plan (~15 hrs/week):** Weeks 1–3 Book 1 + bank · 4–7 Book 2 + bank · 8–11 Book 3 + bank · 12–14 Book 4 + bank · 15 full mock + review · 16 weak-topic re-drill and formula sheets daily.\n\n**10-week plan (~25 hrs/week):** Wk 1–2 Book 1 · 3–4 Book 2 · 5–6 Book 3 · 7–8 Book 4 · 9 mock + error review · 10 formula sheets daily + re-do every missed question.\n\n## ✅ What's new for 2026\n\nNothing structural — all 62 readings are unchanged. The only revisions are reworded learning objectives in **Hypothesis Testing** and **Stationary Time Series** (Book 2), shifting emphasis toward *applied calculation and interpretation*. Expect slightly more numerical questions there; the notes and quizzes here reflect that.\n\n> **Disclaimer.** Independent study notes prepared from publicly available GARP learning objectives. Not affiliated with or endorsed by GARP. FRM® is a registered trademark of the Global Association of Risk Professionals.","primer":"","excel":"","chapters":[{"num":"101","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"The Building Blocks of Risk Management","notes":"# 1. The Building Blocks of Risk Management\n\nGARP's opening reading defines what risk is, lays out the risk management process, and builds the taxonomy of risk types that the rest of the curriculum hangs on. Expect definitional questions, expected-vs-unexpected-loss distinctions, and risk-classification questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the concept of risk and compare risk management with risk taking.\n- Describe the risk management process and identify the problems and challenges that can weaken it.\n- Evaluate and apply tools and procedures used to measure and manage risk, including quantitative measures, qualitative assessment, and enterprise risk management.\n- Distinguish between expected loss and unexpected loss and provide examples of each.\n- Interpret the relationship between risk and reward, and explain how conflicts of interest can impact risk management.\n- Describe and differentiate between the key classes of risks, explain how each can arise, and assess its potential impact.\n- Explain how risk factors interact and describe the challenges in aggregating risk exposures.\n\n## What risk is — and what risk management is not\n\n**Risk** is the *variability* (uncertainty) of outcomes — especially adverse outcomes — not the expected cost itself. A cost that is known with certainty is an expense, not a risk.\n\nRisk management is **not** about minimizing or eliminating risk. It is about:\n\n1. Understanding which risks the firm is exposed to,\n2. Deciding which risks to retain (because the firm is paid to bear them) and which to lay off,\n3. Keeping retained risks within a deliberate **risk appetite**.\n\nRisk management and **risk taking** are two sides of the same coin: a firm earns returns only by taking risk, so the discipline is about taking the *right* risks, in the *right* amount, knowingly.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Risk management can reduce the *likelihood and severity* of bad outcomes, but it cannot guarantee them away — and a firm that \"never loses money\" is probably not taking enough risk to earn its cost of capital.\n\n## The risk management process\n\nThe standard loop GARP tests:\n\n1. **Identify** the risks (including hidden, correlated, and emerging risks).\n2. **Analyze and measure** — quantify exposure, likelihood, severity.\n3. **Assess the impact** — on earnings, capital, liquidity, reputation.\n4. **Manage** — choose among **avoid, retain (and price), mitigate, transfer**.\n5. **Monitor and report** — limits, dashboards, escalation; iterate.\n\nChallenges that weaken the process:\n\n- **Identification failures** — unknown or newly emerging risks; risks hidden in complex products.\n- **Measurement failures** — model risk, bad data, fat tails, unstable correlations.\n- **Communication failures** — risk reports that the board cannot act on.\n- **Incentive failures** — traders rewarded for return without a charge for risk.\n- **Risk aggregation** — exposures measured in silos understate or overstate firm-wide risk because correlations shift, especially in crises.\n\n## Tools: quantitative, qualitative, and enterprise-wide\n\n| Tool family | Examples | Best at | Weak at |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Quantitative measures | VaR, expected shortfall, economic capital, scenario P&L, stress tests | Comparable, aggregable numbers | Tail behavior, model risk, \"unknown unknowns\" |\n| Qualitative assessment | Scenario analysis, risk identification workshops, expert judgment, risk ratings | New/rare risks, narrative plausibility | Consistency, comparability |\n| Enterprise risk management (ERM) | Firm-wide risk appetite, integrated reporting, unified risk culture | Interactions and concentrations across silos | Costly, organizationally hard (see Ch 8) |\n\n**VaR** states a loss threshold not exceeded with a given confidence over a given horizon; **expected shortfall** averages the losses beyond that threshold, so it captures tail severity that VaR ignores. **Scenario analysis** and **stress testing** answer \"what if?\" for specific extreme events without requiring probability estimates.\n\n## Expected loss vs. unexpected loss\n\n**Expected loss (EL)** is the average loss anticipated over a period — a *predictable cost of doing business* that should be priced into products and covered by reserves/provisions.\n\nFor credit:\n\n$$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$\n\nwhere $$PD$$ = probability of default, $$LGD$$ = loss given default (1 − recovery rate), $$EAD$$ = exposure at default.\n\n**Unexpected loss (UL)** is the *variability of loss around the expected level* — the genuine risk. It is covered not by pricing but by **capital** (the buffer that absorbs losses worse than expected). Example: a credit card book expects 4% charge-offs (EL, priced into rates); a recession year with 9% charge-offs produces a 5-point unexpected loss that capital must absorb.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Classic exam trap: expected loss is **not** a risk in the economic sense — it is a cost, anticipated and priced in. Risk (and capital) is about **unexpected** loss. Also note UL rises with the *correlation* between defaults, not just with individual PDs.\n\n## Risk and reward; conflicts of interest\n\nHigher expected return generally requires bearing more risk — but the relationship is observable only *ex ante* in well-functioning markets and gets distorted by:\n\n- **Asymmetric information** — managers/agents know more than principals.\n- **Moral hazard** — the protected party takes more risk (e.g., traders with bonus upside and no loss clawback; banks with deposit insurance).\n- **Adverse selection** — the riskiest customers are most eager to buy at a given price (insurance, lending).\n- **Conflicts of interest** — e.g., rating agencies paid by issuers; originators selling loans they will not hold (originate-to-distribute, Ch 4 and Ch 10).\n\n## The risk taxonomy\n\n| Risk class | Definition | Typical sources / examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Market risk** | Loss from moves in market prices/rates | Equity, interest rate, FX, commodity price risk; includes basis risk |\n| **Credit risk** | Loss from counterparty failure to perform | Default risk, downgrade/migration risk, counterparty risk, settlement (Herstatt) risk, sovereign risk, concentration risk |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Two distinct types | **Funding liquidity**: cannot roll/raise cash to meet obligations. **Market/trading liquidity**: cannot exit a position without moving the price |\n| **Operational risk** | Loss from failed people, processes, systems, or external events | Fraud, rogue trading, cyber attacks, IT failure, legal risk, model risk (often classed here) |\n| **Business/strategic risk** | Loss from poor business decisions or demand/competitive shifts | Wrong product strategy, volume/margin shocks; strategic = larger, longer-horizon bets (M&A, new markets) |\n| **Reputation risk** | Loss of franchise value from damaged trust | Often *consequential* — triggered by another risk event (fraud, mis-selling) |\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Know the two liquidity risks cold — GARP loves asking which one a scenario illustrates. A bank that can't roll commercial paper has **funding** liquidity risk; a fund forced to dump an illiquid bond at a fire-sale price faces **market liquidity** risk. Crises feature both, feeding each other.\n\n## Risk interactions and aggregation\n\nRisks **interact** rather than add up:\n\n- Market losses can trigger margin calls → funding liquidity risk → fire sales → more market losses (the loss spiral of 2008).\n- Credit deterioration of a counterparty raises **counterparty credit risk** on derivatives that were \"market risk\" trades.\n- Operational failures (a mis-booked hedge) create market exposure.\n\nAggregation challenges: correlations are unstable and **rise toward 1 in crises**; different risk types are measured on different horizons and confidence levels; netting and diversification benefits assumed in calm markets can vanish exactly when needed.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is \"risk\" in risk management terms?\n> **A:** The variability (uncertainty) of outcomes, especially adverse ones — not the expected cost itself. A cost known with certainty is an expense, not a risk.\n\n**Q2.** Is risk management about minimizing or eliminating risk?\n> **A:** No. It is about understanding exposures, deciding which risks to retain (because the firm is paid to bear them) versus lay off, and keeping retained risks within a deliberate risk appetite.\n\n**Q3.** What are the five steps of the risk management process?\n> **A:** (1) Identify, (2) Analyze and measure, (3) Assess the impact, (4) Manage (avoid, retain/price, mitigate, transfer), (5) Monitor and report.\n\n**Q4.** Formula for expected loss (credit)?\n> **A:** $$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$ where PD = probability of default, LGD = loss given default (1 − recovery), EAD = exposure at default.\n\n**Q5.** Expected loss vs. unexpected loss — key difference?\n> **A:** EL is the average anticipated loss, a priced-in cost covered by reserves. UL is the variability of loss around the expected level — the genuine risk, covered by capital.\n\n**Q6.** Classic trap: is expected loss a \"risk\"?\n> **A:** No. EL is an anticipated, priced-in cost. Risk (and capital) is about unexpected loss. UL also rises with default correlation, not just individual PDs.\n\n**Q7.** Funding liquidity risk vs. market (trading) liquidity risk?\n> **A:** Funding: cannot roll/raise cash to meet obligations (e.g., bank can't roll commercial paper). Market: cannot exit a position without moving the price (e.g., fire-sale of an illiquid bond).\n\n**Q8.** VaR vs. expected shortfall?\n> **A:** VaR is a loss threshold not exceeded at a given confidence over a horizon. Expected shortfall averages losses beyond that threshold, capturing tail severity that VaR ignores.\n\n**Q9.** Define moral hazard, adverse selection, and asymmetric information.\n> **A:** Moral hazard: a protected party takes more risk (trader with bonus upside, no clawback). Adverse selection: riskiest customers most eager to buy at a given price. Asymmetric information: agents know more than principals.\n\n**Q10.** Name the key risk classes in the GARP taxonomy.\n> **A:** Market, credit, liquidity (funding + market), operational, business/strategic, and reputation risk.\n\n**Q11.** What is operational risk?\n> **A:** Loss from failed people, processes, systems, or external events — fraud, rogue trading, cyber attacks, IT failure, legal risk, and model risk (often classed here).\n\n**Q12.** Why is reputation risk often called \"consequential\"?\n> **A:** It is typically triggered by another risk event (fraud, mis-selling), causing loss of franchise value from damaged trust.\n\n**Q13.** What happens to correlations during a crisis, and why does that matter for aggregation?\n> **A:** Correlations are unstable and rise toward 1 in crises, so diversification and netting benefits assumed in calm markets can vanish exactly when needed — silos understate firm-wide risk.\n\n**Q14.** Give an example of risks interacting rather than adding.\n> **A:** Market losses trigger margin calls (funding liquidity risk), forcing fire sales that cause more market losses — the loss spiral of 2008.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A bank's credit card business loses, on average, about 4% of its balances each year to defaults, and the bank builds this 4% into the interest rate it charges. The 4% is best described as:\nA) Unexpected loss B) Expected loss C) Economic capital D) Operational risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is really asking: what do we call a loss that is average, predictable, and already priced into the product? That is the definition of expected loss (EL). EL is the average loss the firm anticipates over a period — a normal cost of doing business — so it is priced into rates and covered by reserves/provisions, not by capital.\n\nThink of it like a grocery store that knows about 4 out of every 100 apples will rot before being sold. The store builds that spoilage into its prices. It is expected, so it is just a cost.\n\n- A is wrong: unexpected loss is the variability around that average (e.g., a recession year where losses jump to 9%). The steady, priced-in 4% is not the surprise part.\n- C is wrong: economic capital is the buffer held to absorb unexpected losses, not the average loss itself.\n- D is wrong: operational risk is loss from failed people, processes, systems, or external events (fraud, IT failure) — credit card defaults are credit risk, not operational risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: If a loss is average and priced-in, it is EXPECTED loss (a cost). Only the surprise/variability part is true \"risk\" and is covered by capital.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Expected vs Unexpected Loss\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected loss (EL)\",\"items\":[\"Average / predictable\",\"Priced into rates\",\"Covered by provisions\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected loss (UL)\",\"items\":[\"Variability around EL\",\"The true risk\",\"Covered by capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which scenario best illustrates *funding* liquidity risk?\nA) A hedge fund must sell a large block of a thinly traded stock at a steep discount B) A bank cannot roll over its maturing commercial paper to raise cash C) A dealer widens its bid-ask spreads during volatile markets D) A bond portfolio falls in value because interest rates rose\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to spot the type of liquidity risk where a firm cannot get the cash it needs to meet its obligations. That is funding liquidity risk. When a bank \"rolls over\" commercial paper, it issues new short-term IOUs to pay off the old ones; if it cannot do that, it runs short of cash — a funding problem.\n\nImagine you pay off one credit card by borrowing on another. If no one will lend to you anymore, you are stuck even if you own valuable things — that is a funding squeeze.\n\n- A is wrong: being forced to dump an asset at a fire-sale price is *market (trading) liquidity* risk — the problem is the market for the asset, not raising cash.\n- C is wrong: wider bid-ask spreads are also a sign of *market liquidity* risk.\n- D is wrong: losing value because rates moved is interest rate risk, a type of market risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Funding liquidity = \"can't raise cash to pay what I owe.\" Market/trading liquidity = \"can't sell my asset without crashing its price.\" GARP loves making you tell these two apart.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Funding vs Market liquidity\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Funding liquidity\",\"items\":[\"Can't raise CASH\",\"Can't roll/issue debt\",\"Meet obligations fails\"]},{\"h\":\"Market liquidity\",\"items\":[\"Can't SELL asset\",\"Fire-sale discount\",\"Wide bid-ask\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Risk management is best described as:\nA) Minimizing the firm's total risk B) Eliminating every exposure that could produce a loss C) Deliberately selecting and sizing the risks the firm is paid to bear, within its risk appetite D) Buying insurance on all material exposures\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the correct definition of what risk management actually is. A firm earns returns only by taking risk, so risk management is not about removing risk — it is about taking the *right* risks, in the *right* amount, on purpose, and keeping them within a chosen risk appetite.\n\nIt is like driving a car: the goal is not to never move (that earns you nothing), it is to drive purposefully while staying within safe limits.\n\n- A is wrong: minimizing total risk would also minimize return; a firm that takes almost no risk cannot earn its cost of capital.\n- B is wrong: eliminating all loss-producing exposures is impossible and would mean exiting the business entirely.\n- D is wrong: transferring (insuring) risk is only one tool — and you would never insure risks you are actually paid to keep.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Risk management is about *deliberate risk selection within appetite*, NOT minimization or elimination. A firm that \"never loses money\" is probably not taking enough risk.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"What risk management IS vs IS NOT\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"It IS\",\"items\":[\"Select right risks\",\"Size within appetite\",\"Take risks you're paid for\"]},{\"h\":\"It is NOT\",\"items\":[\"Minimize all risk\",\"Eliminate every loss\",\"Insure everything\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A trader receives a large bonus when trades make a profit but suffers no penalty when trades lose money. The incentive problem this creates is best described as:\nA) Adverse selection B) Moral hazard C) Basis risk D) Funding liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which problem arises when someone is shielded from the downside of their own risk-taking. That is moral hazard: once a person is protected from losses (heads I win, tails the firm loses), they are tempted to take on too much risk.\n\nIt is like lending a friend your car with no consequences if they crash it — they will probably drive more recklessly than if it were their own car.\n\n- A is wrong: adverse selection happens *before* a deal, where the riskiest customers are the keenest to buy (e.g., the sickest people want insurance most). Here the issue is behavior *after* the bonus scheme is in place.\n- C is wrong: basis risk is when a hedge doesn't perfectly track the thing it is hedging — unrelated to bonuses.\n- D is wrong: funding liquidity risk is about raising cash, not incentives.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Moral hazard = protected party takes MORE risk because they don't bear the downside. Adverse selection = the riskiest types self-select INTO a deal beforehand. Fix moral hazard with clawbacks and deferred pay.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Moral hazard vs Adverse selection\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Moral hazard\",\"items\":[\"AFTER the deal\",\"hidden ACTION\",\"reckless once protected\"]},{\"h\":\"Adverse selection\",\"items\":[\"BEFORE the deal\",\"hidden INFO\",\"riskiest self-select in\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of the following is the credit-risk formula for expected loss?\nA) $$EL = PD + LGD + EAD$$ B) $$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$ C) $$EL = PD \\times (1 - LGD) \\times EAD$$ D) $$EL = \\dfrac{PD \\times EAD}{LGD}$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests whether you know the standard credit expected-loss formula. Expected loss equals the probability of default times the loss given default times the exposure at default:\n\n$$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$\n\nIn plain words: (chance the borrower defaults) × (fraction you lose if they do) × (amount owed when they default). For example, if there is a 2% chance of default, you lose 40% when it happens, and the exposure is $1,000,000:\n\n$$EL = 0.02 \\times 0.40 \\times \\$1{,}000{,}000 = \\$8{,}000$$\n\n- A is wrong: you multiply these three drivers, you do not add them (adding gives a meaningless number).\n- C is wrong: LGD is already the loss fraction (LGD = 1 − recovery rate). Writing (1 − LGD) would give you the recovery, not the loss.\n- D is wrong: dividing by LGD makes no economic sense — a bigger loss fraction would shrink your expected loss, which is backwards.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize EL = PD × LGD × EAD (all multiplied). And remember LGD = 1 − recovery rate, so don't accidentally use the recovery rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Compared with Value-at-Risk (VaR), expected shortfall (ES) is better at capturing:\nA) The most likely day-to-day profit B) The average severity of losses in the tail (beyond the VaR threshold) C) The firm's total notional exposure D) The recovery rate on defaulted loans\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what advantage ES has over VaR. VaR tells you a loss threshold that won't be exceeded at a given confidence (e.g., \"we won't lose more than $10m on 95% of days\") — but it says nothing about how bad things get on the worst 5% of days. Expected shortfall averages those tail losses, so it captures tail severity that VaR ignores.\n\nAnalogy: VaR tells you \"the river won't rise above the 10-foot mark 95% of the time.\" ES tells you \"but when it does flood, the average flood is 14 feet\" — the part that actually does the damage.\n\n- A is wrong: neither VaR nor ES is about the most likely (typical) outcome; both focus on the bad tail.\n- C is wrong: notional exposure is a raw size number, unrelated to tail loss measurement.\n- D is wrong: recovery rate is a credit input, not what ES measures.\n\n📌 Exam tip: VaR = a threshold (\"how bad, at minimum, on a bad day\"). ES = the average of losses *beyond* that threshold (\"how bad on average when it's worse than VaR\"). ES sees the tail; VaR does not.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Expected Shortfall\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"A threshold loss\",\"Won't exceed at X% conf\",\"Silent on tail size\"]},{\"h\":\"Expected Shortfall\",\"items\":[\"Average of tail losses\",\"Beyond the VaR cutoff\",\"Captures tail severity\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A bank holds a large loan to one client and worries about losing money if that client fails to repay. This concern relates most directly to which class of risk?\nA) Market risk B) Operational risk C) Credit risk D) Reputation risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which risk class covers losses from a counterparty failing to pay what it owes. That is credit risk by definition — loss from a borrower or counterparty failing to perform. Default risk, downgrade risk, and concentration risk all sit inside the credit risk family.\n\n- A is wrong: market risk is loss from moving prices/rates (equities, FX, commodities), not from a borrower defaulting.\n- B is wrong: operational risk is loss from failed people, processes, systems, or external events (fraud, IT outages).\n- D is wrong: reputation risk is loss of franchise value from damaged trust, usually a *consequence* of another event — not the direct default exposure here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Counterparty fails to pay\" = credit risk. Watch for the word \"default.\" Heavy exposure to one name adds *concentration* risk, still within credit risk.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Where does default risk sit?\",\"root\":\"Financial risk\",\"children\":[\"Market\",\"Credit (default)\",\"Liquidity\",\"Operational\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** During a market crisis, market losses trigger margin calls, which force fire-sales, which cause further market losses. This sequence best illustrates that risks:\nA) Are perfectly independent of one another B) Interact and can amplify each other rather than simply adding up C) Always cancel out through diversification D) Can be aggregated by simply summing each silo's number\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is about how different risk types behave together. The described loss spiral (market loss → funding/margin pressure → forced selling → more market loss) shows that risks interact and feed on each other; they do not just sit side by side and add up neatly.\n\nThink of dominoes: knocking one over (market loss) topples the next (liquidity), which topples the next (more market loss). The total damage is bigger than any single domino.\n\n- A is wrong: the whole scenario shows the risks are linked, not independent.\n- C is wrong: diversification benefits often *shrink* in a crisis as correlations rise toward 1 — they don't reliably cancel risks out when you most need them.\n- D is wrong: simply summing silo numbers ignores these interactions and the way correlations shift, so it misstates firm-wide risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In aggregation, correlations are unstable and rise toward 1 in crises. Risks *interact* (and amplify), so you cannot just add up the silos.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Crisis loss spiral\",\"steps\":[\"Market loss\",\"Margin call\",\"Forced fire-sale\",\"More market loss\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A firm decides to keep a particular risk because it is core to its business and the firm has genuine expertise in assessing it, while holding capital and reserves against it. This strategy is best described as:\nA) Avoid B) Transfer C) Accept (retain) D) Diversify away\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question describes a firm choosing to hold on to a risk it understands well and is paid to bear, backing it with capital. That is the accept (retain) strategy: keep the risk, price it, and hold a buffer against it.\n\nA bank keeping the credit risk on loans it can expertly assess is the classic example — it earns the spread for taking that risk knowingly.\n\n- A is wrong: \"avoid\" means exiting or never entering the activity — but here the firm wants to stay in because it has an edge.\n- B is wrong: \"transfer\" means shifting the risk to a third party (insurance, derivatives) — the opposite of keeping it.\n- D is wrong: diversification is a *mitigation* technique to reduce risk you still hold; the scenario describes a deliberate decision to retain a core risk, not to spread it across many positions.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The four strategies are Accept, Avoid, Mitigate, Transfer. Choose Accept when the risk is core and the firm is *paid* to bear it and has an edge in assessing it.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which strategy: keep a core risk you understand?\",\"options\":[\"Avoid (exit)\",\"Transfer (insure)\",\"Accept (retain + capital)\",\"Diversify away\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Core risk with an edge, paid to bear it = Accept and hold capital.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which statement about expected loss (EL) and unexpected loss (UL) is correct?\nA) EL is covered by capital, while UL is priced into products B) UL is the predictable cost of doing business, while EL is the genuine risk C) EL is priced into products, while UL is the genuine risk covered by capital D) EL and UL are the same thing measured over different horizons\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests whether you can correctly pair each loss type with how it is covered. Expected loss is the average, predictable cost — it is priced into products and held against by reserves. Unexpected loss is the variability of loss around that average — the true economic risk — and it is absorbed by capital.\n\nPicture a budget: EL is the bill you expect every month (rent), so you plan for it. UL is the surprise emergency expense, so you keep a savings buffer (capital) for it.\n\n- A is wrong: it swaps the two — EL is priced in, UL is covered by capital, not the reverse.\n- B is wrong: it reverses the definitions; EL is the predictable cost, UL is the genuine risk.\n- D is wrong: they are fundamentally different concepts (average vs. variability), not the same thing on different horizons.\n\n📌 Exam tip: EL = average, priced-in, covered by reserves. UL = the surprise, the real risk, covered by capital. Also note UL rises with the *correlation* between defaults, not just individual PDs.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Loss distribution: what covers what\",\"items\":[\"Expected loss\",\"|Unexpected loss\",\"Stress / tail\"],\"left\":\"reserves & pricing\",\"right\":\"capital (then it's gone)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A bank's credit card portfolio has historically lost 3% of balances per year, and the bank prices this into its lending rate. In a severe recession losses reach 8%. The 3% is best described as:\nA) Unexpected loss B) Expected loss C) Economic capital D) Stress loss\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Anticipated, average, priced-in losses are expected loss; the extra 5% in the recession is unexpected loss, absorbed by capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which scenario best illustrates *funding* liquidity risk?\nA) A hedge fund must sell a large block of small-cap stock at a steep discount B) A bank cannot roll over its maturing commercial paper C) A dealer widens bid-ask spreads in volatile markets D) A pension fund's bonds fall in value as rates rise\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Failure to roll funding is funding liquidity risk; A and C describe market/trading liquidity; D is interest rate (market) risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Risk management is best described as:\nA) Minimizing the firm's total risk B) Eliminating exposures that could produce losses C) Selecting and sizing the risks the firm is paid to bear, within appetite D) Insuring all material exposures\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Risk management is deliberate risk selection within appetite, not minimization or elimination.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A trader is paid a bonus on profits with no penalty for losses. The resulting incentive problem is best described as:\nA) Adverse selection B) Moral hazard C) Basis risk D) Funding liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Asymmetric payoff after the relationship is in place induces excess risk taking — moral hazard.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A bank holds capital primarily to absorb:\nA) Expected losses on its loan book B) Operating expenses during downturns C) Unexpected losses beyond the anticipated level D) Dividend payments to shareholders\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Expected losses are a priced cost covered by provisions/reserves; capital is the buffer against losses **beyond** the expected level (unexpected loss).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"What covers each loss layer\",\"items\":[\"Expected loss\",\"|Unexpected loss\",\"Stress loss\"],\"left\":\"provisions/pricing\",\"right\":\"capital / extreme\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A dealer in a stressed market faces widening bid-ask spreads and shrinking quote sizes when trying to unwind a large corporate bond position. This is best described as:\nA) Funding liquidity risk B) Market (trading) liquidity risk C) Settlement risk D) Basis risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Inability to exit a position without moving the price is market/trading liquidity risk. Funding liquidity (A) is the inability to raise cash to meet obligations.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two faces of liquidity risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Market/trading\",\"items\":[\"Can't SELL at fair price\",\"wide spreads, thin quotes\",\"asset-side\"]},{\"h\":\"Funding\",\"items\":[\"Can't RAISE cash\",\"meet obligations\",\"liability-side\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A consumer-lending bank reports the following on a $500 million credit card book: average annual charge-offs over the cycle of 4.0%, with a one-in-twenty bad-year loss rate of 9.5%. The bank prices the average loss into its APR and holds capital against the worst-case stress. Which statement is the most accurate characterization of how these losses should be funded?\nA) Expected loss is $20 million, funded by capital; the additional $27.5 million in a bad year is funded by loan-loss provisions B) Expected loss is $20 million, funded by provisions priced into the APR; the additional $27.5 million in a bad year is unexpected loss funded by capital C) Expected loss is $47.5 million, funded by provisions; unexpected loss is zero because the worst case is already known D) Expected loss is $20 million, funded by capital; unexpected loss is $47.5 million, funded by provisions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This problem tests the expected-loss vs. unexpected-loss distinction *and* which funding source matches each. It is a near-universal exam trap to swap \"capital\" and \"provisions.\"\n\n1. Compute expected loss (EL): the average, anticipated loss.\n$$EL = 4.0\\% \\times \\$500\\text{m} = \\$20\\text{m}$$\n2. EL is a *predictable cost of doing business* — it is priced into the APR and covered by **reserves/provisions**, not capital.\n3. Compute the bad-year loss: $$9.5\\% \\times \\$500\\text{m} = \\$47.5\\text{m}$$.\n4. Unexpected loss (UL) is the *variability around* the expected level — the amount the bad year exceeds the average:\n$$UL = \\$47.5\\text{m} - \\$20\\text{m} = \\$27.5\\text{m}$$\n5. UL is the genuine risk and is absorbed by **capital**, the buffer for losses worse than expected.\n\nSo EL = $20m (provisions, priced in) and the extra $27.5m is UL (capital). That is option B.\n\n- A swaps the funding sources (EL by capital, UL by provisions) — backwards.\n- C wrongly calls the entire $47.5m bad-year figure \"expected loss\" and claims UL is zero; knowing a worst case exists does not make it expected, and the *variability* is precisely the risk.\n- D both swaps funding sources and treats the full $47.5m as UL (it forgot to subtract the EL already covered).\n\n📌 Exam tip: EL = cost = priced in = provisions/reserves; UL = risk = capital. UL is the *excess over* EL, so always subtract EL from the stress loss — never use the gross stress number as UL.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EL vs UL: funding source\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected loss ($20m)\",\"items\":[\"Priced into APR\",\"Covered by provisions\",\"NOT capital\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected loss ($27.5m)\",\"items\":[\"Stress minus EL\",\"Covered by capital\",\"NOT provisions\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A money-center bank holds a large position in a single emerging-market corporate bond. During a regional crisis the following chain occurs: the bond's market price drops sharply; the bank is unable to sell the position without accepting a large discount; the mark-to-market loss triggers a margin call from a repo counterparty; the bank cannot raise enough cash to meet the call and is forced to liquidate other assets at depressed prices. Which sequence correctly identifies the primary risk type at each stage?\nA) Market risk → funding liquidity risk → market liquidity risk → credit risk B) Market risk → market liquidity risk → funding liquidity risk → market liquidity risk C) Credit risk → market liquidity risk → operational risk → market risk D) Market risk → credit risk → funding liquidity risk → reputation risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to map each stage of a 2008-style loss spiral to the correct risk class, distinguishing the two liquidity risks.\n\n1. \"The bond's market price drops sharply\" — a loss from a move in market price = **market risk**.\n2. \"Unable to sell without a large discount\" — the inability to exit a position without moving the price = **market (trading) liquidity risk**.\n3. \"Cannot raise enough cash to meet the [margin] call\" — the inability to roll/raise cash to meet obligations = **funding liquidity risk**.\n4. \"Forced to liquidate other assets at depressed prices\" — again being forced to sell into a thin market at fire-sale prices = **market liquidity risk**.\n\nThat ordering is market → market liquidity → funding liquidity → market liquidity, which is B.\n\n- A mislabels stage 2 as funding and stage 3 as market liquidity (the two are swapped) and tacks on credit risk that never appears.\n- C calls a price move \"credit risk\" and the forced sale \"market risk,\" and invents operational risk.\n- D introduces credit risk (no counterparty has defaulted — the loss is from price moves) and reputation risk (not the primary driver of the forced sale).\n\n📌 Exam tip: funding liquidity = *can't get cash*; market/trading liquidity = *can't sell without moving the price*. In a loss spiral the two feed each other repeatedly — read each clause and match the verb (\"raise cash\" vs. \"sell at a discount\").\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Loss spiral: risk at each stage\",\"steps\":[\"Price drops = Market\",\"Can't sell = Mkt liquidity\",\"Can't meet call = Funding liq\",\"Fire-sale = Mkt liquidity\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A bank sells deposit-insured retail deposits and uses the funds to write out-of-the-money options to corporate clients. Traders are paid a percentage of annual P&L with no clawback and no charge for the risk capital they consume. A risk officer warns that this structure simultaneously embeds two distinct agency problems. Which pair is present, and through which channel?\nA) Adverse selection (deposit insurance) and basis risk (the options) — both pre-contract pricing problems B) Moral hazard (deposit insurance shields the bank from depositor discipline) and moral hazard (uncapped bonus encourages excess risk after the contract is set) C) Adverse selection (uncapped bonus attracts the riskiest traders) and moral hazard (deposit insurance) D) Moral hazard (deposit insurance) and adverse selection (option buyers are the most informed counterparties)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which agency problems the structure creates and the channel for each. The key distinction: *adverse selection* is a pre-contract problem (the riskiest types self-select before the deal); *moral hazard* is a post-contract behavior change (the protected party takes more risk once protected).\n\n1. Deposit insurance: once deposits are insured, depositors no longer monitor or discipline the bank, so the bank can take more risk than it otherwise could. The risk-taking change happens *after* protection is in place → **moral hazard**.\n2. Uncapped bonus with no clawback: the trader keeps the upside but bears none of the downside, so *after* the comp contract is signed the trader takes excessive risk → **moral hazard**.\n\nBoth channels are moral hazard, which is option B.\n\n- A wrongly calls deposit insurance \"adverse selection\" and reframes the comp problem as basis risk (a hedging-mismatch concept, not an agency problem here).\n- C and D each correctly tag one channel but mislabel the other as adverse selection. The uncapped bonus is not primarily about *which* traders apply (selection); it changes the *behavior* of whoever holds it — that is hazard, not selection.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ask \"did the bad outcome come from *who* entered the deal (selection) or from *how they behaved after* (hazard)?\" Both deposit insurance and asymmetric-payoff comp are classic moral-hazard examples because they alter behavior post-protection.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Moral hazard vs Adverse selection\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Moral hazard (both here)\",\"items\":[\"AFTER protection\",\"behavior changes\",\"deposit ins. + uncapped bonus\"]},{\"h\":\"Adverse selection\",\"items\":[\"BEFORE the deal\",\"riskiest self-select in\",\"hidden info at pricing\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** An enterprise risk team aggregates firm-wide VaR by summing the standalone VaRs of its trading, banking, and insurance units, citing diversification as the reason the true figure should be lower. During the next crisis the realized firm-wide loss exceeds even the simple sum of standalone VaRs. Which explanation is most consistent with the curriculum's treatment of risk aggregation?\nA) VaR is subadditive, so the sum of standalone VaRs is always an upper bound that cannot be exceeded B) Diversification benefits assumed in calm markets shrink as cross-unit correlations rise toward 1 in a crisis, so the realized loss can exceed even the undiversified sum C) The units measured VaR on the same horizon and confidence level, so summation must have understated the figure D) Expected loss was double-counted, inflating the realized number above the sum of VaRs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why a realized crisis loss can blow through even the *un*-diversified sum of standalone VaRs.\n\n1. In normal times, units' losses are imperfectly correlated, so combined VaR is *below* the sum of standalone VaRs — the diversification benefit. The team was counting on this.\n2. The curriculum stresses that **correlations are unstable and rise toward 1 in crises**. As correlations spike, the assumed diversification evaporates.\n3. If tail dependence is strong enough, all units lose together *and* by more than the individually-estimated tails (fat tails, model breakdown), so the realized loss can exceed even the simple sum — diversification not only disappears, the joint tail is worse than modeled.\n\nThat is option B.\n\n- A claims VaR is always subadditive and the sum is an unbreachable upper bound. VaR is *not* generally subadditive (a known limitation), and in any case a *realized* loss is not bounded by a *modeled* VaR sum.\n- C is irrelevant and backwards — using a common horizon would make summation cleaner, not cause an understatement, and it does not explain a crisis breach.\n- D invents double-counting of EL; VaR is about unexpected loss and the breach is a correlation/tail story, not an accounting error.\n\n📌 Exam tip: the danger of risk aggregation is precisely that diversification \"benefits assumed in calm markets vanish exactly when needed.\" Treat any answer asserting VaR is \"always subadditive\" as a trap.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Correlations: calm vs crisis\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Calm markets\",\"items\":[\"Low correlations\",\"Diversification benefit\",\"Combined < sum of VaRs\"]},{\"h\":\"Crisis\",\"items\":[\"Correlations → 1\",\"Benefit evaporates\",\"Loss can exceed the sum\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A firm's CRO is building a risk-tool kit and must match each tool to the job it does *best*. Consider: (i) producing a single comparable, aggregable number across desks; (ii) capturing the *severity* of losses in the extreme tail beyond a threshold; (iii) assessing a brand-new risk for which no probability distribution can yet be estimated. Which assignment is correct?\nA) (i) Expected shortfall; (ii) VaR; (iii) stress test B) (i) VaR; (ii) expected shortfall; (iii) qualitative scenario analysis C) (i) Scenario analysis; (ii) VaR; (iii) expected shortfall D) (i) VaR; (ii) scenario analysis; (iii) expected shortfall\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to match three jobs to the tool best suited to each.\n\n1. Job (i) — one comparable, aggregable number across desks: **VaR** states a loss threshold not exceeded at a given confidence over a given horizon. It is the standard single comparable figure.\n2. Job (ii) — severity of losses *beyond* the threshold: **expected shortfall** averages the losses in the tail past the VaR cutoff, capturing tail severity that VaR ignores.\n3. Job (iii) — a brand-new risk with no usable probability distribution: **qualitative scenario analysis** (a \"what if?\" tool) handles new/rare risks without requiring probability estimates; quantitative measures are weak at \"unknown unknowns.\"\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A swaps VaR and ES (ES is the tail-severity measure, not the single comparable number).\n- C puts scenario analysis where VaR belongs and VaR where ES belongs.\n- D correctly tags (i) but assigns the tail-severity job to scenario analysis and the new-risk job to ES — both reversed; ES is quantitative and needs a distribution, so it is poorly suited to an unmodelable new risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: VaR = the threshold; ES = the *average loss beyond* the threshold (so ES ≥ VaR and captures tail shape). Qualitative tools (scenario analysis, expert judgment) are the answer whenever a question stresses \"no probabilities,\" \"novel,\" or \"unprecedented.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Match the tool to the job\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"Single comparable number\",\"Aggregable across desks\",\"A loss threshold\"]},{\"h\":\"Expected shortfall\",\"items\":[\"Severity beyond threshold\",\"Averages the tail\"]},{\"h\":\"Scenario analysis\",\"items\":[\"New / unmodelable risk\",\"No distribution needed\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A rating agency is paid by the issuers whose securities it rates, and a desk originates loans it intends to sell rather than hold. A junior analyst lists four labels for these arrangements: conflict of interest, moral hazard, adverse selection, and basis risk. Which single concept best unifies *both* arrangements as described, and why?\nA) Adverse selection — in both cases the most informed party withdraws from the market B) Conflict of interest — in both cases the party making the risk judgment is rewarded in a way misaligned with the user of that judgment C) Basis risk — in both cases the hedge fails to track the underlying exposure D) Funding liquidity risk — in both cases the firm cannot raise cash when needed\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the *single* concept that best unifies the issuer-paid rating arrangement and the originate-to-distribute desk.\n\n1. Rating agency paid by issuers: the agency's revenue depends on pleasing the issuer, whose interest (a high rating) diverges from the investor relying on the rating. The judgment-maker's incentives are misaligned with the judgment's user → **conflict of interest**.\n2. Originate-to-distribute desk: the originator profits from volume and fees but will not bear the loss it is selling on, so its incentive to underwrite carefully diverges from the buyer's interest → again a **conflict of interest** (with a moral-hazard flavor in the eroded screening incentive).\n3. The label that fits *both* cleanly, exactly as the curriculum lists them (\"rating agencies paid by issuers; originators selling loans they will not hold\"), is **conflict of interest**.\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A (adverse selection) does not describe either arrangement — neither says the informed party exits a market at a given price.\n- C (basis risk) is a hedging-mismatch concept and has nothing to do with either incentive problem.\n- D (funding liquidity) is unrelated; neither scenario is about raising cash.\n\n📌 Exam tip: the curriculum explicitly groups \"rating agencies paid by issuers\" and \"originate-to-distribute\" under conflicts of interest. When the misalignment is *who pays the person making the call*, reach for conflict of interest; reserve adverse selection for self-selection at a price and moral hazard for post-protection behavior.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What unifies issuer-paid ratings & OTD?\",\"options\":[\"Adverse selection\",\"Conflict of interest\",\"Basis risk\",\"Funding liquidity risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"In both, the party making the risk judgment is paid in a way misaligned with its user.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following statements about the relationship between risk and reward, as framed in the curriculum, is most accurate?\nA) A firm that consistently reports no losses in any year is demonstrating best-practice risk management B) Because higher expected return generally requires bearing more risk, a firm earning its cost of capital must be taking essentially zero risk C) The risk–reward relationship is observable ex ante in well-functioning markets but is distorted by asymmetric information, moral hazard, and adverse selection D) Risk management aims to minimize the firm's total risk so that reward is maximized per unit of remaining exposure\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the conceptual link between risk and reward and the frictions that distort it.\n\n1. The curriculum states higher expected return generally requires bearing more risk, and that this relationship is \"observable only *ex ante* in well-functioning markets.\"\n2. It then lists the distortions: asymmetric information, moral hazard, adverse selection, and conflicts of interest.\n3. Option C states exactly this — ex-ante observability plus the named distortions — so it is correct.\n\n- A is the classic trap the notes flag: \"a firm that 'never loses money' is probably not taking enough risk to earn its cost of capital.\" Zero losses is a warning sign, not best practice.\n- B inverts the logic: earning the cost of capital *requires* taking risk, not zero risk.\n- D restates the \"risk management = minimization\" misconception the curriculum explicitly rejects; risk management is deliberate risk *selection* within appetite, not minimization.\n\n📌 Exam tip: two recurring traps live together here — \"no losses = good\" (false; under-risked) and \"risk management = minimize risk\" (false; it is selecting and sizing the right risks within appetite). The correct framing always pairs ex-ante risk–reward with the agency/information distortions.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Most accurate risk-reward statement\",\"options\":[\"No losses = best practice\",\"Earning cost of capital needs zero risk\",\"Ex-ante link, distorted by info/agency frictions\",\"Goal is to minimize total risk\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Risk-reward is observable ex ante but distorted by asymmetric info, moral hazard, adverse selection.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"102","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"How Do Firms Manage Financial Risk?","notes":"# 2. How Do Firms Manage Financial Risk?\n\nThis reading covers the corporate decision of *whether* and *how* to manage risk: risk appetite, the hedge-or-not debate, and the mechanics and pitfalls of implementing a hedging program. Tested mostly qualitatively, with scenario questions on choosing the right strategy.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Compare the strategies a firm can use to manage its risk exposures (accept/retain, avoid, mitigate, transfer) and explain when each is appropriate.\n- Explain the relationship between risk appetite and a firm's risk management decisions.\n- Evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of hedging, and explain the challenges that arise when implementing a hedging strategy.\n- Apply appropriate methods to hedge operational and financial risks, including pricing risk, foreign currency risk, and interest rate risk.\n- Assess the impact of risk management tools and instruments, including risk limits and derivatives.\n\n## The four risk management strategies\n\n| Strategy | What it means | Appropriate when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Accept (retain)** | Keep the risk, price it, hold capital/reserves against it | The risk is core to the business model and the firm has an edge in bearing it (a bank retaining credit risk it can assess) |\n| **Avoid** | Exit or never enter the activity | Risk is outside appetite/expertise and not compensated |\n| **Mitigate** | Reduce likelihood or severity while staying in the activity | Controls, diversification, collateral, limits are cost-effective |\n| **Transfer** | Shift the risk to a third party at a price | Risk is non-core and a deep market exists (insurance, derivatives, securitization) |\n\nThe right choice flows from comparing the risk to the firm's **risk appetite** and asking whether the firm is *paid* to hold it.\n\n## Risk appetite drives the decisions\n\n- **Risk appetite**: the amount and types of risk the firm is *willing* to take in pursuit of its objectives — set by the board (Ch 3).\n- **Risk capacity**: the maximum risk the firm *can* absorb given capital, liquidity, and franchise.\n- **Risk profile**: the risk actually being taken at a point in time.\n\nAppetite should sit within capacity; the risk function's job is to keep the profile within appetite. A clear **risk appetite statement** (qualitative narrative plus quantitative limits — e.g., maximum earnings-at-risk, minimum capital ratio, no exposure to specified products) is the bridge between board strategy and desk-level limits.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Mapping risk appetite into actual exposures is hard in practice: appetite is firm-wide and slow-moving; exposures are granular and fast-moving. The translation layer is the **limit framework**.\n\n## Should the firm hedge at all?\n\nIn a Modigliani–Miller world (no taxes, no bankruptcy costs, frictionless markets), hedging adds **no value** — shareholders can diversify or hedge on their own account. Hedging adds value only through real-world frictions:\n\n**Arguments for hedging**\n- Reduces the probability and cost of **financial distress** and bankruptcy.\n- Stabilizes earnings and cash flow → supports planning, investment, and debt capacity; avoids underinvestment when internal funds are scarce.\n- Can lower expected **taxes** when the tax schedule is convex (progressive).\n- Lets management focus on core business risks where it has an edge.\n- May reduce the cost of capital and widen the investor base.\n\n**Arguments against hedging**\n- Shareholders can hedge themselves, often more cheaply (M&M logic).\n- Hedging has direct costs (spreads, fees, collateral) and indirect costs (management time, systems).\n- Hedges can **mask** underlying performance and create compliance/accounting complexity.\n- A \"hedge\" can become a speculative position if mis-sized or mis-specified — and hedging *expected, priced* risks destroys value.\n\n## Implementation challenges\n\n- **Mapping the exposure**: hedge accounting-flat board statements rarely translate cleanly to hedgeable market exposures.\n- **Static vs. dynamic hedging**: a static hedge is set-and-forget; a dynamic hedge requires rebalancing as prices move (cost, model risk, and liquidity risk — Metallgesellschaft, Ch 9).\n- **Basis risk**: the hedge instrument does not track the exposure perfectly (different grade, location, maturity, index).\n- **Rollover/maturity mismatch**: hedging a long-dated exposure with short-dated contracts exposes the firm to margin calls and roll prices.\n- **Time horizon and accounting**: derivatives mark to market through earnings while the hedged item may not — creating *reported* earnings volatility even when the economic hedge is sound.\n- **Governance**: who may hedge, with what instruments, against which board-approved objectives — must be written down before the first trade.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: a hedge that lowers economic risk can still **increase accounting earnings volatility** (mark-to-market on the derivative, accrual on the underlying). Don't equate \"earnings got more volatile\" with \"the hedge failed.\" And remember Metallgesellschaft: economically sensible hedges can still kill a firm through **funding liquidity** (margin calls) before maturity.\n\n## Hedging the major financial exposures\n\n**Pricing risk (input/output prices).** Lock in commodity input costs or output prices with forwards/futures, or cap them with options. Forwards remove upside and downside; options keep the upside for a premium.\n\n**Foreign currency risk.** Three layers:\n- **Transaction risk** — known future FX cash flows: hedge with FX forwards/futures, money-market hedges, or options.\n- **Translation risk** — accounting consolidation of foreign subsidiaries: often left unhedged or hedged with FX debt.\n- **Economic risk** — long-run competitive exposure to FX levels: managed structurally (locating production, sourcing).\n\n**Interest rate risk.** Swap floating for fixed (or vice versa) with **interest rate swaps**; cap floating-rate costs with **caps**; lock future borrowing rates with **FRAs/futures**. Banks manage the repricing gap between assets and liabilities (asset-liability management).\n\n## Tools: limits and derivatives\n\n**Risk limits** convert appetite into enforceable constraints: notional limits, stop-loss limits, VaR limits, concentration limits, tenor limits. Good limit regimes have clear ownership, monitoring, and escalation for breaches — a limit that is routinely waived is not a limit.\n\n**Derivatives** are the workhorse transfer instruments:\n\n| Instrument | Payoff shape | Upfront cost | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Forward/futures | Linear, symmetric | None (margin for futures) | Lock a price/rate |\n| Swap | Linear, symmetric, multi-period | None | Transform cash flow profile (fixed↔floating, currency) |\n| Option (cap/floor) | Asymmetric — keeps favorable side | Premium | Insure against adverse moves while keeping upside |\n\n**Exchange-traded** instruments are standardized, margined, and nearly free of counterparty risk but bring basis risk and margin liquidity demands; **OTC** instruments are tailored but carry counterparty credit risk (now mitigated by central clearing and collateral, Ch 4).","flashcards":"**Q1.** What are the four risk management strategies?\n> **A:** Accept (retain), Avoid, Mitigate, and Transfer. Choice flows from comparing the risk to risk appetite and asking whether the firm is paid to hold it.\n\n**Q2.** When is \"accept (retain)\" the appropriate strategy?\n> **A:** When the risk is core to the business model and the firm has an edge in bearing it (e.g., a bank retaining credit risk it can assess), pricing it and holding capital against it.\n\n**Q3.** Risk appetite vs. risk capacity vs. risk profile?\n> **A:** Appetite: amount/types of risk the firm is willing to take (board-set). Capacity: the maximum it can absorb. Profile: the risk actually taken at a point in time. Appetite should sit within capacity.\n\n**Q4.** What is the bridge from board strategy to desk-level limits?\n> **A:** The risk appetite statement (qualitative narrative plus quantitative limits) cascaded into the limit framework.\n\n**Q5.** Under Modigliani–Miller, does hedging add value?\n> **A:** No. In a frictionless world (no taxes, no bankruptcy costs) hedging adds no value — shareholders can hedge on their own account. Value comes only from real-world frictions.\n\n**Q6.** What is the strongest theoretical argument that hedging adds shareholder value?\n> **A:** It reduces the probability and cost of financial distress and bankruptcy (also: lowers expected taxes under convex schedules, avoids underinvestment, stabilizes cash flow).\n\n**Q7.** Name key arguments against hedging.\n> **A:** Shareholders can hedge themselves more cheaply; direct/indirect costs; hedges can mask performance; a mis-sized hedge becomes speculation; hedging expected, priced risks destroys value.\n\n**Q8.** What is basis risk in a hedge?\n> **A:** The hedge instrument does not track the exposure perfectly — differences in grade, location, maturity, or index.\n\n**Q9.** Exam trap: can a sound hedge increase reported earnings volatility?\n> **A:** Yes. Derivatives mark to market through earnings while the hedged item accrues — creating reported earnings volatility even when the economic hedge is sound. Don't equate \"more volatile earnings\" with \"hedge failed.\"\n\n**Q10.** What lesson does Metallgesellschaft teach about hedging?\n> **A:** Economically sensible hedges can still kill a firm through funding liquidity — margin calls and rollover losses on short-dated futures before the long-dated exposure pays off.\n\n**Q11.** Three types of foreign currency risk?\n> **A:** Transaction risk (known future FX cash flows — hedge with forwards/options), translation risk (accounting consolidation of subsidiaries), and economic risk (long-run competitive exposure — managed structurally).\n\n**Q12.** Forward/futures vs. options as hedging instruments?\n> **A:** Forwards/futures are linear and symmetric, removing both upside and downside, no upfront cost. Options are asymmetric, keeping the favorable side for an upfront premium.\n\n**Q13.** Exchange-traded vs. OTC derivatives?\n> **A:** Exchange-traded: standardized, margined, low counterparty risk, but basis risk and margin liquidity demands. OTC: tailored but carry counterparty credit risk (mitigated by clearing/collateral).\n\n**Q14.** What makes a risk limit framework effective?\n> **A:** Clear ownership, monitoring, and escalation for breaches. A limit that is routinely waived is not a limit.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** An airline is heavily exposed to jet fuel prices — a risk in which it has no special informational edge — and deep, liquid futures and options markets for fuel exist. The most appropriate risk management strategy is to:\nA) Accept the risk and hold capital against it B) Avoid flying to reduce fuel exposure C) Transfer (hedge) the risk using fuel derivatives D) Increase fuel exposure to earn the risk premium\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which of the four strategies fits a risk that is non-core, uncompensated, and easy to lay off in a deep market. When a firm has no edge in bearing a risk but a liquid market exists to shift it, the right move is to transfer (hedge) it.\n\nThink of a baker who must buy flour: the baker is paid for baking, not for guessing flour prices, so locking in the flour cost with a forward lets them focus on baking.\n\n- A is wrong: accepting/holding capital fits *core* risks the firm is paid to bear — fuel price speculation is not the airline's edge.\n- B is wrong: avoiding the activity means not flying, which destroys the business; you avoid risks only when the whole activity is outside appetite.\n- D is wrong: deliberately taking *more* of an uncompensated risk is speculation, not risk management.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Match the strategy to the risk: Accept core risks you're paid for; Avoid risks outside appetite/expertise with no reward; Mitigate with cost-effective controls; Transfer non-core risks when a deep market exists.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Non-core risk, no edge, deep market\",\"options\":[\"Accept + hold capital\",\"Avoid (stop flying)\",\"Transfer (hedge derivatives)\",\"Take more to earn premium\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"No edge + liquid market = lay it off; Transfer (hedge).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In a frictionless Modigliani–Miller world (no taxes, no bankruptcy costs), what is the strongest *theoretical* argument that corporate hedging adds shareholder value?\nA) Shareholders cannot diversify firm-specific risk themselves B) Hedging reduces expected financial distress and bankruptcy costs C) Hedging always increases the firm's expected cash flows D) Hedging removes systematic risk from equity returns\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks where hedging value really comes from. In a perfect M&M world, hedging adds nothing because shareholders can diversify on their own. Hedging creates value only through real-world *frictions* — and reducing the probability and cost of financial distress/bankruptcy is the classic one.\n\nAnalogy: insurance on your house doesn't make the house worth more in a perfect world, but in the real world it stops a fire from forcing a panicked fire-sale — that avoided distress is the value.\n\n- A is wrong: it is actually backwards — shareholders generally *can* diversify firm-specific risk cheaply, which is precisely why hedging it adds no value in theory.\n- C is wrong: hedging does not reliably raise expected cash flows; it mainly reduces their volatility.\n- D is wrong: a firm cannot remove systematic (market) risk from equity returns by hedging its operating exposures, and investors don't pay extra for that.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedging adds value through frictions: distress costs, convex (progressive) taxes, and avoiding underinvestment when internal cash is scarce. \"Shareholders can hedge themselves\" is the M&M argument *against* corporate hedging.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Does corporate hedging add value?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"M&M world (no)\",\"items\":[\"No taxes/distress\",\"Shareholders self-hedge\",\"Value-neutral\"]},{\"h\":\"Real world (yes)\",\"items\":[\"Cuts distress costs\",\"Convex tax savings\",\"Avoids underinvestment\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A firm hedges a 10-year obligation to deliver oil by continually rolling short-dated futures (a \"stack-and-roll\"). Oil prices fall sharply. What is the firm's most immediate danger?\nA) Basis risk between different crude grades B) Counterparty default on the exchange-traded futures C) Margin calls on the futures exceeding its available cash D) Loss of upside on the physical commitment\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question points to the famous Metallgesellschaft trap. The futures are marked to market daily, so when prices move against the short-dated position, the firm must post cash variation margin *now* — but the offsetting gain on the long-dated physical obligation only arrives years later. The immediate threat is running out of cash to meet margin calls.\n\nImagine your future paycheck will cover a loss, but the lender demands cash today; even though you'll be fine later, you can be squeezed now.\n\n- A is wrong: basis risk (grade mismatch) is a real but secondary concern; it doesn't create the urgent cash crisis described.\n- B is wrong: exchange-traded futures are margined and cleared, so counterparty default risk is minimal.\n- D is wrong: losing upside on the physical commitment is the normal trade-off of any forward-type hedge, not an immediate danger.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rolling short-dated hedges for a long-dated exposure creates a *funding liquidity* (margin call) timing mismatch — the gains come later, the cash drains now. Remember Metallgesellschaft.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Stack-and-roll squeeze (Metallgesellschaft)\",\"steps\":[\"Price falls\",\"Futures MTM loss\",\"Cash margin due NOW\",\"Physical gain only later\",\"Funding squeeze\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A board states it is \"willing to tolerate up to a 20% decline in annual earnings in pursuit of its growth strategy.\" This statement is best described as part of the firm's:\nA) Risk capacity B) Risk appetite C) Risk profile D) Limit framework\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to label a statement of how much risk the firm is *willing* to take to reach its goals. That is exactly the definition of risk appetite — the amount and type of risk the firm chooses to accept in pursuit of its objectives, set by the board.\n\n- A is wrong: risk capacity is the *maximum* risk the firm *could* absorb given its capital and liquidity — a ceiling, not a chosen willingness.\n- C is wrong: risk profile is the risk the firm is *actually* taking right now, not its stated willingness.\n- D is wrong: the limit framework is the set of granular desk/business limits that translate appetite into enforceable constraints — more detailed than this high-level statement.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Appetite = willing to take (board's choice). Capacity = maximum can absorb (the ceiling). Profile = actually taking now. Appetite must sit inside capacity.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Profile vs Appetite vs Capacity\",\"items\":[\"Risk profile (now)\",\"Risk appetite (willing)\",\"|Risk capacity (max)\"],\"left\":\"actually taking\",\"right\":\"hard ceiling\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of the four risk management strategies involves reducing the likelihood or severity of a risk while still staying in the activity?\nA) Accept B) Avoid C) Mitigate D) Transfer\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which strategy means \"stay in the game but make the risk smaller.\" That is mitigate — using controls, diversification, collateral, or limits to cut the chance or the size of a loss while continuing the activity.\n\nThink of driving but wearing a seatbelt and slowing down in rain: you still drive (stay in the activity) but reduce the harm if something goes wrong.\n\n- A is wrong: accept means keeping the risk as-is and holding capital against it, not actively shrinking it.\n- B is wrong: avoid means exiting or never entering — the opposite of staying in the activity.\n- D is wrong: transfer means handing the risk to a third party (insurance, derivatives), not reducing it yourself while keeping it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mitigate = stay in, but shrink likelihood/severity with controls and diversification. Don't confuse it with Transfer (hand it off) or Avoid (exit entirely).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"The four risk strategies\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Stay in activity\",\"items\":[\"Accept: keep + capital\",\"Mitigate: shrink it\"]},{\"h\":\"Leave / hand off\",\"items\":[\"Avoid: exit entirely\",\"Transfer: hand to 3rd party\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A company with a known U.S.-dollar payment due in three months from a euro-based operation hedges using an FX forward. This is an example of managing which type of foreign currency exposure?\nA) Translation risk B) Transaction risk C) Economic risk D) Basis risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which FX exposure involves a *known, specific future cash flow* in a foreign currency. That is transaction risk — the risk that the exchange rate moves before a contracted payment or receipt settles. It is naturally hedged with FX forwards, futures, money-market hedges, or options.\n\n- A is wrong: translation risk is the accounting risk from *consolidating* foreign subsidiaries' financial statements, not from a specific cash payment.\n- C is wrong: economic risk is the long-run competitive exposure to currency levels (managed structurally, e.g., where you locate production), not a single dated cash flow.\n- D is wrong: basis risk is a hedging imperfection (the hedge instrument not tracking the exposure), not a category of FX exposure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Transaction = known future FX cash flow (hedge with forwards/options). Translation = accounting consolidation of subsidiaries. Economic = long-run competitive FX exposure (managed structurally).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Known FX payment due in 3 months\",\"options\":[\"Translation (consolidation)\",\"Transaction (dated cash flow)\",\"Economic (competitive)\",\"Basis risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A known, dated FX cash flow = transaction risk; hedge with a forward.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A firm enters a sound economic hedge with a derivative. The derivative is marked to market through earnings, while the hedged underlying is recorded on an accrual basis. A likely result is that:\nA) The economic hedge has clearly failed B) Reported earnings become more volatile even though economic risk fell C) The firm has eliminated all of its market risk D) The hedge has introduced new counterparty default risk on an exchange\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question highlights a classic accounting-vs-economics trap. The derivative's value swings show up in earnings each period, but the underlying item's offsetting change may not be recognized at the same time. So even a good hedge can make *reported* earnings look bumpier, while the true economic risk has actually gone down.\n\nIt is like weighing yourself right after drinking water: the scale (accounting) jumps around even though your real body (economics) hasn't changed much.\n\n- A is wrong: more volatile reported earnings does not mean the hedge failed — the economics can be perfectly sound.\n- C is wrong: hedges reduce specific exposures; they do not eliminate \"all market risk,\" and that is not what the scenario claims.\n- D is wrong: the scenario is about accounting timing, not counterparty risk; exchange-traded derivatives have minimal counterparty risk anyway.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Don't equate \"earnings got more volatile\" with \"the hedge failed.\" Mark-to-market on the derivative vs. accrual on the underlying creates *reported* volatility even when the economic hedge is correct.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Reported earnings vs Economic risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Accounting (reported)\",\"items\":[\"Derivative marked to mkt\",\"Underlying on accrual\",\"Earnings look bumpier\"]},{\"h\":\"Economics (real)\",\"items\":[\"Hedge is sound\",\"True risk fell\",\"Mismatch is timing only\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A bank wants to convert its floating-rate interest payments into fixed payments to stabilize its borrowing cost. The most direct instrument is:\nA) An interest rate cap B) An interest rate swap C) A foreign exchange forward D) A credit default swap\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which tool transforms floating-rate cash flows into fixed-rate cash flows. An interest rate swap does exactly this: the firm agrees to pay a fixed rate and receive a floating rate (or vice versa), converting the profile of its interest payments over multiple periods.\n\n- A is wrong: a cap only *limits* (puts a ceiling on) a floating rate for an upfront premium; it doesn't fix the rate, and it keeps the benefit if rates fall.\n- C is wrong: an FX forward hedges currency exposure, not interest rate type.\n- D is wrong: a credit default swap hedges default risk of a reference entity, not interest rate exposure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Swap = transform floating↔fixed cash flows over many periods (symmetric, no upfront cost). Cap = pay a premium to limit a floating rate while keeping the downside benefit (asymmetric).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Convert floating payments to fixed\",\"options\":[\"Interest rate cap\",\"Interest rate swap\",\"FX forward\",\"Credit default swap\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A swap transforms floating into fixed over many periods, no premium.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Compared with a forward/futures hedge, the key advantage of using an option (such as a cap) to hedge a price exposure is that an option:\nA) Has no upfront cost B) Provides a linear, symmetric payoff C) Protects against adverse moves while keeping the favorable side, for a premium D) Removes all counterparty risk automatically\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question contrasts options with forwards as hedging tools. A forward locks in a price, removing both downside *and* upside. An option, by contrast, protects you against bad moves but lets you still benefit from good moves — and you pay an upfront premium for that asymmetric protection.\n\nIt is like insurance: you pay a premium, you're covered if disaster strikes, but if nothing bad happens you simply keep your good outcome (minus the premium).\n\n- A is wrong: options require paying a premium upfront — that is precisely their cost; forwards have no upfront cost.\n- B is wrong: a linear, symmetric payoff describes forwards/futures and swaps, not options (which are asymmetric).\n- D is wrong: an option does not \"automatically\" remove counterparty risk; OTC options carry counterparty risk, and it is exchange clearing (not the option feature) that mitigates it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward = lock the price (gives up upside, no upfront cost). Option = insure the downside, keep the upside (asymmetric, costs a premium). That premium is the price of keeping the good side.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forward vs Option as a hedge\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward / futures\",\"items\":[\"Locks the price\",\"No upside kept\",\"No upfront cost\",\"Symmetric payoff\"]},{\"h\":\"Option (cap)\",\"items\":[\"Insures downside\",\"Keeps the upside\",\"Pay a premium\",\"Asymmetric payoff\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which statement about risk limits is most accurate?\nA) A limit that is routinely waived still functions as an effective limit B) Risk limits translate the firm's risk appetite into enforceable constraints with monitoring and escalation C) Risk limits are only useful for credit risk, not market risk D) Limits should be set so that they are never approached, ensuring zero breaches\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests what risk limits are for. Limits (notional, stop-loss, VaR, concentration, tenor) are the practical bridge that turns the board's high-level risk appetite into concrete, enforceable rules at the desk level — complete with ownership, monitoring, and escalation when breached.\n\nThink of speed limits on a road: they turn the vague goal \"drive safely\" into an enforceable number that police can monitor.\n\n- A is wrong: a limit that is routinely waived is not a real limit at all — enforcement and escalation are what make it meaningful.\n- C is wrong: limits apply across many risk types (market, credit, concentration, tenor), not just credit.\n- D is wrong: limits are meant to be used and operated near deliberately, not set absurdly high so they are never relevant; the point is to constrain, not to be cosmetic.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Limits convert appetite into enforceable constraints. Good limit regimes have clear ownership, monitoring, and escalation. \"A limit that is routinely waived is not a limit.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"How limits work\",\"steps\":[\"Risk appetite\",\"Limit framework\",\"Desk-level limits\",\"Monitor & escalate\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** An airline is highly exposed to jet fuel prices, a risk it has no informational edge in bearing. Deep, liquid futures and options markets exist. The most appropriate strategy is:\nA) Accept B) Avoid C) Transfer/hedge D) Increase the exposure to earn the risk premium\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Non-core, uncompensated risk with a deep transfer market: hedge it.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which of the following is the strongest *theoretical* argument that hedging adds shareholder value?\nA) Shareholders cannot diversify firm-specific risk B) Hedging reduces expected bankruptcy and financial distress costs C) Hedging always increases expected cash flows D) Hedging removes systematic risk from equity returns\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With M&M frictions removed, value comes from reducing distress costs (also taxes, underinvestment); A is false and C–D are not generally true.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A firm hedges a 10-year oil delivery commitment by rolling short-dated futures. Oil prices fall sharply. The firm's most immediate danger is:\nA) Basis risk between crude grades B) Counterparty default on the futures C) Margin calls on the futures exceeding available liquidity D) Loss of the upside on the physical commitment\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The stack-and-roll structure marks the futures to market daily while the long-dated commitment pays off later — the Metallgesellschaft funding squeeze.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A firm's board states it is \"willing to tolerate a one-year earnings decline of up to 20% in pursuit of growth.\" This statement is best described as part of its:\nA) Risk capacity B) Risk appetite C) Risk profile D) Risk limit framework\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Willingness to take risk in pursuit of objectives = appetite; capacity is the maximum absorbable, profile is the actual position.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** In a frictionless Modigliani–Miller world, corporate hedging:\nA) Adds value by reducing the firm's beta B) Adds value by stabilizing earnings C) Adds no value, since investors can adjust risk on their own account D) Destroys value because of transaction costs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Under M&M assumptions, hedging is a zero-NPV repackaging investors can do themselves. Hedging adds value only via frictions — distress costs, convex taxes, underinvestment.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"When does hedging add value?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Frictionless M&M\",\"items\":[\"zero NPV\",\"investors self-hedge\",\"no value added\"]},{\"h\":\"Real-world frictions\",\"items\":[\"distress costs\",\"convex taxes\",\"underinvestment\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A gold producer wants downside price protection on next year's output but insists on keeping the benefit if gold rallies. The most suitable instrument is:\nA) Selling gold futures B) Buying put options on gold C) Entering a fixed-for-floating gold swap as fixed receiver D) Selling call options on gold\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A put creates a floor while retaining the upside, for a premium. Futures (A) and a swap (C) lock the price symmetrically; selling calls (D) caps the upside and gives no downside protection.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging gold output\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Buy put\",\"items\":[\"floor on downside\",\"KEEP upside\",\"pay premium\"]},{\"h\":\"Sell future/swap\",\"items\":[\"price locked\",\"no upside\",\"symmetric\"]},{\"h\":\"Sell call\",\"items\":[\"caps upside\",\"no floor\",\"wrong tool\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A U.S. industrial firm has signed a contract to receive €50 million in 90 days from a European customer. It also operates a German plant whose euro-denominated assets and liabilities are consolidated into its USD financial statements, and it competes long-term against European exporters whose costs are in euros. The treasurer wants to hedge the €50 million receivable with a 90-day FX forward but is unsure how to treat the other two exposures. Which classification and treatment is most consistent with the curriculum?\nA) All three are transaction risk and should be hedged with the same forward B) The receivable is transaction risk (hedge with the forward); the consolidation of the German plant is translation risk (often left unhedged or hedged with FX debt); the competitive exposure is economic risk (managed structurally) C) The receivable is economic risk; the German plant is transaction risk; the competitive exposure is translation risk D) The receivable is transaction risk; both the German plant and the competitive exposure are translation risk and should be hedged with FX options\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to sort three FX exposures into the curriculum's three layers and match each to its typical treatment.\n\n1. The €50m receivable is a **known future FX cash flow** — that is **transaction risk**, hedged with FX forwards/futures, a money-market hedge, or options. The forward is appropriate.\n2. Consolidating the German plant's euro assets/liabilities into USD statements is an **accounting** exposure — **translation risk** — which is often left unhedged or hedged with FX-denominated debt.\n3. The long-run competitive exposure to the level of the euro is **economic risk**, managed *structurally* (where to locate production, where to source), not with a single forward.\n\nThat mapping is B.\n\n- A collapses all three into transaction risk; only the receivable is.\n- C scrambles all three labels.\n- D correctly tags the receivable but lumps the structural competitive exposure into translation risk and prescribes options for both — economic risk is not an accounting-consolidation issue and is not typically hedged with FX options.\n\n📌 Exam tip: transaction = a *known* future FX cash flow; translation = *accounting consolidation* of foreign subs; economic = *long-run competitive* position. The treatments differ: forwards/options, FX debt/often unhedged, and structural decisions respectively.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Three FX exposures & treatments\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Transaction\",\"items\":[\"Known FX cash flow\",\"Hedge: forward/option\"]},{\"h\":\"Translation\",\"items\":[\"Consolidating subs\",\"Often unhedged / FX debt\"]},{\"h\":\"Economic\",\"items\":[\"Long-run competitive\",\"Manage structurally\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** In a Modigliani–Miller world with no taxes, no bankruptcy costs, and frictionless markets, a CFO argues the firm should run a large currency hedging program \"to add shareholder value by reducing earnings volatility.\" Under MM assumptions, which response is correct, and what is the single strongest *real-world* justification that would actually create value?\nA) The CFO is right under MM; the strongest justification is that hedging removes systematic risk from the stock B) The CFO is wrong under MM (hedging adds no value because shareholders can hedge themselves); the strongest value-creating friction is reducing expected financial-distress and bankruptcy costs C) The CFO is right under MM; the strongest justification is that hedging always raises expected cash flows D) The CFO is wrong under MM; the strongest justification is that shareholders cannot diversify firm-specific risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question has two parts: what MM implies, and which friction most credibly makes hedging value-additive.\n\n1. Under MM (no taxes, no distress costs, frictionless), hedging adds **no value** — shareholders can replicate or undo any corporate hedge on their own account at the same cost. So the CFO is **wrong** under MM.\n2. Value appears only through real-world frictions. The strongest, most-cited one is that hedging **reduces the probability and cost of financial distress and bankruptcy** (also lower expected taxes under a convex schedule, and avoiding underinvestment). Reducing distress costs is the canonical answer.\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A says the CFO is right under MM (false) and claims hedging removes *systematic* risk — corporate FX hedging does not strip systematic risk from equity.\n- C asserts hedging \"always raises expected cash flows,\" which is not generally true; hedging mainly reduces variability and has direct costs.\n- D correctly says the CFO is wrong but gives a false justification: shareholders *can* diversify firm-specific risk themselves, which is exactly why that argument fails.\n\n📌 Exam tip: MM benchmark → hedging is value-neutral; value comes only from frictions. The headline friction tested is *financial-distress/bankruptcy cost reduction*. \"Shareholders can't diversify firm-specific risk\" is a classic *wrong* answer — they can.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging value: MM vs real world\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"MM world\",\"items\":[\"Hedging value-neutral\",\"Shareholders self-hedge\",\"CFO is wrong\"]},{\"h\":\"Real-world friction\",\"items\":[\"Cuts distress/bankruptcy cost\",\"Strongest justification\",\"Plus tax/underinvestment\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A refiner with a 10-year fixed-price commitment to deliver heating oil hedges by buying a stack of near-month crude futures and rolling them forward (a stack-and-roll). Over six months crude prices fall by 35%. The economic hedge is sound — the lower input cost on the future physical purchases offsets the futures losses at maturity. Yet the firm faces an acute crisis. What is the mechanism, and what risk does it illustrate?\nA) Basis risk between crude and heating oil destroyed the hedge's correlation, producing permanent economic loss B) Daily variation margin on the marked-to-market futures losses must be paid in cash now, while the offsetting gain on the physical commitment is realized only over the next 10 years — a funding liquidity squeeze (the Metallgesellschaft problem) C) Counterparty default on the exchange-traded futures left the firm unhedged D) The firm lost the upside on the physical commitment, reducing expected profit\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This is the Metallgesellschaft scenario. The question asks for the *mechanism* of the crisis and the risk it illustrates, given that the economic hedge is sound.\n\n1. Futures are **marked to market daily**. When crude falls 35%, the long futures show large losses *now* and the firm must post **variation margin in cash immediately**.\n2. The offsetting gain — buying physical crude cheaper — is realized only *gradually over the 10-year delivery schedule*. So cash outflows are front-loaded and cash inflows are back-loaded.\n3. This timing mismatch can exhaust the firm's liquidity before maturity even though the position is economically hedged → **funding liquidity risk**, the most immediate danger.\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A invokes basis risk; the stem states the economic hedge is sound, so correlation/basis is not the issue.\n- C invokes counterparty default on *exchange-traded* futures, which are margined and nearly free of counterparty risk — the opposite of the problem.\n- D describes giving up upside, which is a feature of any forward/futures hedge and not the cause of the acute crisis.\n\n📌 Exam tip: a hedge can be economically correct yet kill the firm via **funding liquidity** when the hedge is marked daily but the underlying pays off later. The keyword cluster \"stack-and-roll / margin calls / long-dated commitment\" should immediately flag Metallgesellschaft and funding liquidity risk.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Metallgesellschaft funding squeeze\",\"steps\":[\"Crude falls 35%\",\"Futures MTM loss\",\"Variation margin due NOW\",\"Physical gain over 10 yrs\",\"Liquidity crisis\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A manufacturer hedges its forecast jet-fuel cost with crude oil futures (the cleanest liquid contract available). Over the hedge period, jet-fuel prices rise 12% but crude rises only 8%, so the futures gain does not fully offset the higher fuel bill. Separately, the firm's CFO notes that even though the economic hedge reduced cash-flow risk, reported quarterly earnings became *more* volatile. Which pair of effects is being described?\nA) Basis risk (grade mismatch between crude and jet fuel) and accounting earnings volatility from marking the derivative to market while the underlying is on accrual B) Funding liquidity risk and translation risk C) Rollover risk and adverse selection D) Counterparty credit risk and economic risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks you to name two separate effects: the imperfect offset, and the rise in reported earnings volatility despite a sound economic hedge.\n\n1. The hedge instrument (crude) does not track the exposure (jet fuel) perfectly — different grade — so the offset is incomplete. That is **basis risk** (the hedge instrument differs in grade, location, maturity, or index from the exposure).\n2. The derivative is **marked to market through earnings** each quarter, while the underlying fuel cost hits the P&L only when incurred (accrual). The mismatch in accounting timing produces *reported* earnings volatility even though the economic hedge is sound.\n\nThat pair is A.\n\n- B names funding liquidity and translation risk — neither describes a grade mismatch or an accounting-timing effect.\n- C names rollover risk (a maturity-mismatch issue, not present here) and adverse selection (unrelated).\n- D names counterparty credit risk (futures are margined) and economic risk (a long-run competitive concept) — neither fits.\n\n📌 Exam tip: imperfect tracking of a hedge = basis risk. And remember the curriculum's warning: a hedge that lowers economic risk can still *raise reported earnings volatility* because the derivative marks to market while the hedged item accrues — do not equate \"earnings got more volatile\" with \"the hedge failed.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two effects in this scenario\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Basis risk\",\"items\":[\"Crude ≠ jet fuel grade\",\"Offset incomplete\",\"Hedge instrument mismatch\"]},{\"h\":\"Accounting volatility\",\"items\":[\"Derivative marked to mkt\",\"Underlying on accrual\",\"Reported earnings bumpier\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A board approves a risk appetite statement specifying a maximum one-year earnings-at-risk of $200 million and a minimum CET1 ratio of 11%. The firm's maximum absorbable loss before breaching regulatory minimums is estimated at $350 million, and its current measured exposure implies $180 million of earnings-at-risk. Match the three figures to the correct concept.\nA) $200m = risk capacity; $350m = risk appetite; $180m = risk profile B) $200m = risk appetite; $350m = risk capacity; $180m = risk profile C) $200m = risk profile; $350m = risk appetite; $180m = risk capacity D) $200m = risk appetite; $350m = risk profile; $180m = risk capacity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to assign three numbers to risk appetite, capacity, and profile.\n\n1. $200m is the amount of risk the board is **willing** to take in pursuit of objectives (a board-set limit in the appetite statement) → **risk appetite**.\n2. $350m is the **maximum** the firm *can* absorb given capital before breaching minimums → **risk capacity**.\n3. $180m is the risk **actually being taken right now** (current measured exposure) → **risk profile**.\n\nThe correct ordering — appetite $200m, capacity $350m, profile $180m — is B. Note the sensible relationship: profile ($180m) ≤ appetite ($200m) ≤ capacity ($350m), exactly as the curriculum requires (appetite sits within capacity; the risk function keeps the profile within appetite).\n\n- A swaps appetite and capacity.\n- C and D scramble the labels (e.g., calling the board's chosen $200m a \"profile,\" or the live $180m exposure \"capacity\").\n\n📌 Exam tip: appetite = *willing* to take (board choice); capacity = *can* absorb (the hard ceiling); profile = *actually* taking now. The ordering profile ≤ appetite ≤ capacity is the quick sanity check — if your assignment violates it, you mislabeled.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Profile ≤ Appetite ≤ Capacity\",\"items\":[\"Profile $180m (now)\",\"Appetite $200m (willing)\",\"|Capacity $350m (max)\"],\"left\":\"actually taking\",\"right\":\"hard ceiling\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A treasurer must choose a single instrument to manage a forecast input-cost exposure where (a) the firm wants to retain the benefit if prices fall, (b) is willing to pay upfront for that asymmetry, and (c) needs no multi-period cash-flow transformation. A second desk must convert a stream of floating-rate interest payments into fixed with no upfront premium. Which instrument pairing is correct?\nA) Treasurer: forward; Desk: interest rate cap B) Treasurer: option (cap); Desk: interest rate swap C) Treasurer: interest rate swap; Desk: forward D) Treasurer: option (cap); Desk: forward rate agreement on a single period\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to match two needs to instruments by payoff shape, cost, and tenor.\n\n1. Treasurer's needs: keep the favorable side (price falls), willing to pay upfront, single exposure. That is an **asymmetric payoff with an upfront premium** = an **option (cap)**, which insures against adverse moves while keeping the upside.\n2. Second desk: transform a *multi-period* floating stream into fixed, with **no upfront cost**. That is the textbook **interest rate swap** — linear, symmetric, multi-period, no premium.\n\nThat pairing is B.\n\n- A gives the treasurer a forward, which removes *both* upside and downside — contradicting requirement (a) to keep the favorable side.\n- C gives the treasurer a swap (no upside retention, multi-period — wrong on both counts) and the desk a forward (single period — cannot convert a *stream*).\n- D correctly gives the treasurer a cap but gives the desk a single-period FRA, which cannot transform a multi-period floating *stream* into fixed (a swap is the multi-period instrument).\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"keep the upside, pay a premium\" = option/cap (asymmetric). \"Transform a multi-period cash-flow profile, no premium\" = swap (symmetric, linear, multi-period). Forwards/FRAs lock a single price/rate symmetrically and give up the favorable side.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Match need to instrument\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Keep upside, pay premium\",\"items\":[\"Asymmetric payoff\",\"Single exposure\",\"Option (cap)\"]},{\"h\":\"Floating→fixed, no premium\",\"items\":[\"Symmetric, multi-period\",\"Stream of payments\",\"Interest rate swap\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A firm's market risk policy sets a desk VaR limit, but over the past year the limit has been breached eight times, each time waived by the desk head with informal approval. Risk management argues this is a governance failure, not merely a measurement issue. Which statement best captures the curriculum's view of what has gone wrong?\nA) The VaR limit was set too low; raising it would resolve the problem B) A limit that is routinely waived is not a limit; effective limit regimes require clear ownership, monitoring, and escalation for breaches C) VaR is the wrong measure; the desk should have used expected shortfall, which cannot be breached D) Breaches are acceptable because derivatives mark to market, so the limit is only an accounting construct\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the *governance* defect is when a limit is repeatedly waived.\n\n1. The curriculum states risk limits \"convert appetite into enforceable constraints\" and that good limit regimes have **clear ownership, monitoring, and escalation for breaches**.\n2. It states directly: \"a limit that is routinely waived is not a limit.\" Eight informal waivers in a year mean the constraint is not binding — the framework has failed, regardless of the VaR number itself.\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A treats it as a calibration problem; the issue is enforcement/escalation, not the level. Raising the limit to avoid breaches would just hide the appetite breach.\n- C claims expected shortfall \"cannot be breached\" — false; ES is a risk measure with limits that can also be breached, and switching measures does not fix a waiver culture.\n- D wrongly dismisses limits as \"only an accounting construct\"; limits are the transmission of risk appetite to the desk, fully economic.\n\n📌 Exam tip: limit-framework questions hinge on *enforcement* — ownership, monitoring, escalation. The signature phrase \"a limit that is routinely waived is not a limit\" flags that the defect is governance/incentives, not the choice of risk metric or its calibration.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Limit breached 8x, each waived\",\"options\":[\"Limit too low; raise it\",\"A waived limit is not a limit\",\"Use ES, it can't be breached\",\"Limits are just accounting\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Effective limits need ownership, monitoring, and escalation; waiving them is a governance failure.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"103","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"The Governance of Risk Management","notes":"# 3. The Governance of Risk Management\n\nWho is responsible for what in a firm's risk architecture — board, committees, CRO, business lines, audit. Post-crisis governance reform is a recurring exam theme; questions test roles, reporting lines, and best practices.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain changes in regulation and corporate risk governance that resulted from the 2007–2009 financial crisis.\n- Compare best practices in corporate governance and risk management.\n- Assess the role and responsibilities of the board of directors in risk governance.\n- Evaluate the relationship between a firm's risk appetite and its business strategy, including the role of incentives.\n- Distinguish the mechanisms for transmitting risk governance throughout an organization.\n- Illustrate the interdependence of functional units within a firm as it relates to risk management.\n- Assess the role and responsibilities of a firm's audit committee.\n\n## Post-crisis changes in regulation and governance\n\nThe 2007–09 crisis exposed boards that did not understand their firms' risks, risk functions without stature, and pay schemes that rewarded short-term risk taking. Responses:\n\n- **Legislation/regulation**: Dodd-Frank (US) — including the Volcker rule restricting proprietary trading; stronger EU directives; **Basel III** — more and better capital, leverage ratio, liquidity standards (LCR, NSFR); mandatory **stress testing** (e.g., CCAR) tying capital plans to scenarios.\n- **Board-level**: dedicated **board risk committees** with independent, financially literate members; direct board engagement with the risk appetite statement.\n- **CRO elevation**: independent Chief Risk Officer with direct access/reporting to the board or its risk committee, not buried under the business or the CFO.\n- **Compensation reform**: deferral, clawback/malus provisions, risk-adjusted performance metrics, and board compensation committees reviewing incentive risk.\n\n## Corporate governance vs. risk governance best practices\n\n**Corporate governance** is the system by which a firm is directed and controlled in the interests of stakeholders. **Risk governance** is the subset dealing with how risk taking is directed, constrained, and overseen. Best practices:\n\n- A majority-independent, engaged, knowledgeable board; separation (or strong counterbalance) of CEO and chair roles.\n- A board that approves **strategy and risk appetite together** — strategy is a risk decision.\n- Clear, documented risk appetite cascaded into limits.\n- An independent, adequately resourced risk function led by a CRO with stature and a board reporting line.\n- Compensation aligned with *risk-adjusted*, multi-period performance.\n- Robust management information: timely, accurate, aggregated risk reporting to the board.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> The board's job is **oversight, not management**: set appetite, approve strategy and key policies, ensure capable management and sound incentive design, and monitor — it does not run the risk function day to day.\n\n## Board of directors: risk responsibilities\n\n- Approve the **risk appetite statement** and ensure it is consistent with strategy, capital, and liquidity plans.\n- Oversee top management; ensure a credible CRO and risk organization exist and can act independently.\n- Understand the firm's principal risks and the limits framework; challenge management (the \"constructive dialogue\" role).\n- Oversee compensation design so incentives don't pay for risk-blind returns.\n- Ensure integrity of financial statements and regulatory compliance (largely via the audit committee).\n\n## Risk appetite, business strategy, and incentives\n\nRisk appetite and strategy must be set **jointly**: a growth strategy implies risk consequences (new markets, new products, more leverage), and an appetite constraint shapes which strategies are feasible. Disconnects — a conservative stated appetite with aggressive growth targets — get resolved on the trading floor in favor of growth unless governance intervenes.\n\n**Incentives are the transmission belt.** If bonuses pay on revenue or short-term P&L with no risk adjustment, employees will move the firm's risk profile beyond appetite regardless of what the policy documents say. Fixes: risk-adjusted performance measures (e.g., RAROC, Ch 7), deferred compensation, clawbacks, and risk-function input into pay decisions.\n\n## Transmitting risk governance through the organization\n\nMechanisms that carry board-level appetite down to the desk:\n\n1. **Risk appetite statement → limit framework** — cascading limits by business, desk, risk type.\n2. **Committees** — board risk committee; management-level risk committees (ALCO for balance-sheet/interest-rate/liquidity risk, credit committee, operational risk committee).\n3. **Policies and delegated authorities** — who can approve what size of exposure.\n4. **The three lines of defense:**\n\n| Line | Who | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1st | Business lines / risk owners | Own and manage the risk they generate; first responsibility for staying within limits |\n| 2nd | Risk management & compliance | Set the framework, measure independently, monitor, challenge, report |\n| 3rd | Internal audit | Independently assure that lines 1 and 2 work as designed; reports to the audit committee |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: the **business line (first line) owns its risks** — risk management (second line) measures, monitors, and challenges but does not \"own\" the risk; internal audit (third line) audits the framework and must stay independent of both. Questions love to assign first-line duties to the CRO or make audit a designer of controls — both wrong.\n\n## Functional interdependence\n\nRisk management cannot work as an island:\n\n- **Front office (trading/lending)** originates risk and provides position data; needs risk's limits and pricing input.\n- **Risk management** depends on operations and IT for clean trade capture and data; on finance for valuations and P&L.\n- **Operations/back office** confirms and settles trades — the independent check that positions are real (its absence enabled Barings, Ch 9).\n- **Finance/treasury** manages funding and capital; valuation control reconciles trader marks.\n- **Compliance and legal** translate regulation into binding constraints.\n\nA risk number is only as good as the trade capture, valuation, and data plumbing beneath it.\n\n## The audit committee\n\n- A **board** committee of independent (and financially literate) directors.\n- Oversees the **integrity of financial reporting** and the external audit relationship.\n- Oversees **internal audit** — approves its plan, receives its findings, protects its independence.\n- Monitors the effectiveness of internal controls and compliance with law and regulation.\n- Verifies not just that statements follow accounting rules, but that processes behind reported numbers are sound.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Distinguish the committees: the **risk committee** owns risk appetite oversight and forward-looking risk; the **audit committee** owns financial-reporting integrity, controls assurance, and internal/external audit. Some duties overlap, but exam answers hinge on the primary owner.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Corporate governance vs. risk governance?\n> **A:** Corporate governance is the system by which a firm is directed and controlled for stakeholders. Risk governance is the subset dealing with how risk taking is directed, constrained, and overseen.\n\n**Q2.** What is the board's role in risk — oversight or management?\n> **A:** Oversight, not management. The board sets appetite, approves strategy and key policies, ensures capable management and sound incentives, and monitors — it does not run the risk function day to day.\n\n**Q3.** Name key post-crisis (2007–09) governance/regulation changes.\n> **A:** Dodd-Frank (incl. Volcker rule), Basel III (more capital, leverage ratio, LCR/NSFR), mandatory stress testing (CCAR), board risk committees, CRO elevation, and compensation reform (deferral, clawback/malus).\n\n**Q4.** Why must risk appetite and business strategy be set jointly?\n> **A:** A growth strategy implies risk consequences, and an appetite constraint shapes which strategies are feasible. A disconnect (conservative appetite, aggressive targets) gets resolved on the trading floor toward growth.\n\n**Q5.** Why are incentives the \"transmission belt\" of risk governance?\n> **A:** If bonuses pay on revenue or short-term P&L with no risk adjustment, employees push the risk profile beyond appetite regardless of policy. Fix with risk-adjusted measures (RAROC), deferral, clawbacks.\n\n**Q6.** What are the three lines of defense?\n> **A:** 1st: business lines own and manage the risk they generate. 2nd: risk management & compliance set the framework, measure independently, challenge. 3rd: internal audit independently assures lines 1 and 2.\n\n**Q7.** Exam trap: who \"owns\" the risk in the three-lines model?\n> **A:** The business line (first line) owns its risks. Risk management (second line) measures, monitors, and challenges but does not own the risk. Internal audit (third line) audits the framework and stays independent.\n\n**Q8.** What is the CRO's appropriate reporting line?\n> **A:** Independent, with direct access/reporting to the board or its risk committee — not buried under the business or the CFO.\n\n**Q9.** What is the primary responsibility of the audit committee?\n> **A:** Overseeing the integrity of financial reporting, the control environment, and the internal/external audit relationship. It oversees internal audit but does not run its day-to-day testing.\n\n**Q10.** Risk committee vs. audit committee?\n> **A:** The risk committee owns risk-appetite oversight and forward-looking risk; the audit committee owns financial-reporting integrity, controls assurance, and internal/external audit.\n\n**Q11.** Which committee oversees ALCO-type concerns, and what does ALCO cover?\n> **A:** ALCO is a management-level committee covering balance-sheet, interest-rate, and liquidity risk; it sits within the governance transmission of appetite, alongside credit and operational risk committees.\n\n**Q12.** Why does a risk number depend on functional interdependence?\n> **A:** A risk number is only as good as the trade capture, valuation, and data plumbing beneath it — depending on front office data, operations/IT, finance/treasury valuations, and compliance.\n\n**Q13.** What is the purpose of clawback and deferral provisions?\n> **A:** To align pay with long-run, risk-adjusted outcomes rather than short-term, risk-blind returns.\n\n**Q14.** Which back-office failure enabled Barings, and what governance lesson follows?\n> **A:** Absence of an independent back office to confirm and settle trades let a trader control both trading and settlement. Lesson: segregate front and back office so positions are independently verified.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Which of the following is a responsibility of the board of directors rather than of management?\nA) Setting individual desk-level VaR limits B) Approving the firm-wide risk appetite statement C) Designing the format of the daily risk report D) Approving individual large loan applications\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to separate board-level duties (oversight, big-picture) from management duties (running things day to day). Approving the firm-wide risk appetite statement is a core board responsibility because appetite is a strategic, firm-level decision.\n\nThink of a board like the owners of a restaurant who decide \"we serve seafood and tolerate this much financial risk,\" while the chef and managers handle the daily cooking and staffing.\n\n- A is wrong: setting specific desk-level VaR limits is a granular management/risk-function task that flows down from appetite.\n- C is wrong: designing the daily risk report is an operational task for the risk function, not the board.\n- D is wrong: approving individual large loans is a management/credit-committee decision, not a board governance act.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The board sets appetite, approves strategy and key policies, and monitors — it does NOT run the risk function. Granular limits, reports, and individual deals are management tasks.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Board vs Management\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Board (oversight)\",\"items\":[\"Approve risk appetite\",\"Approve strategy/policy\",\"Monitor & challenge\"]},{\"h\":\"Management (run it)\",\"items\":[\"Set desk VaR limits\",\"Design risk reports\",\"Approve individual loans\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the three-lines-of-defense model, who performs independent measurement and challenge of the risk taken by the business lines?\nA) The business lines themselves B) The risk management and compliance function C) Internal audit D) The external auditor\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question maps each \"line of defense\" to its job. The second line — risk management and compliance — sets the framework, measures risk independently, monitors, and challenges the business lines' risk-taking.\n\nPicture a sports team: the players (first line) take the risks on the field; the coaching/analytics staff (second line) independently measure performance and push back; the league referees/auditors (third line) check that the whole system runs as designed.\n\n- A is wrong: the business lines (first line) *own* and generate the risk; they cannot be the independent check on themselves.\n- C is wrong: internal audit (third line) assures that the first and second lines work as designed — it audits the system, it doesn't do the day-to-day independent risk measurement.\n- D is wrong: the external auditor focuses on the financial statements, not on continuous independent risk measurement and challenge.\n\n📌 Exam tip: 1st line = business owns the risk; 2nd line = risk/compliance measures and challenges; 3rd line = internal audit assures the framework. Watch for traps that give first-line duties to the CRO or make audit a control *designer*.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Three lines of defense\",\"steps\":[\"1st: Business owns risk\",\"2nd: Risk/compliance challenges\",\"3rd: Audit assures\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which post-crisis governance change is correctly paired with its purpose?\nA) Volcker rule — to increase banks' proprietary trading capacity B) Compensation clawbacks — to align pay with long-run, risk-adjusted outcomes C) Basel III leverage ratio — to completely replace risk-based capital D) Board risk committees — to take over day-to-day risk management\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests whether you know what each reform was meant to do. Clawback (and deferral/malus) provisions let firms recover or withhold pay when risks taken earlier turn sour, so they align compensation with multi-period, risk-adjusted results.\n\n- A is wrong: the Volcker rule *restricts* proprietary trading, it does not increase it.\n- C is wrong: the leverage ratio is a *backstop* alongside risk-based capital, not a replacement for it.\n- D is wrong: board risk committees provide *oversight*; they do not run risk management day to day (that is management's job).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Post-crisis fixes — Volcker (limit prop trading), Basel III (more/better capital + leverage ratio + liquidity LCR/NSFR), board risk committees (oversight), CRO elevation (independence), and clawbacks/deferral (align incentives).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Reform paired with its purpose\",\"options\":[\"Volcker: expand prop trading\",\"Clawbacks: align long-run pay\",\"Leverage ratio: replace risk capital\",\"Risk cttee: run risk daily\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Clawback/malus recover pay when risks sour = align with long-run outcomes.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The primary responsibility of a firm's audit committee is to:\nA) Set the firm's risk appetite B) Manage internal audit's day-to-day testing work C) Oversee the integrity of financial reporting and the control environment D) Approve new trading products\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what the audit committee mainly owns. It is a board committee of independent, financially literate directors whose core job is to oversee the integrity of financial reporting, the external audit relationship, internal audit, and the effectiveness of internal controls.\n\n- A is wrong: setting risk appetite is owned by the board/risk committee, not the audit committee.\n- B is wrong: the audit committee *oversees* internal audit (approves its plan, protects its independence) but does not *run* its day-to-day testing.\n- D is wrong: approving new trading products is a management/risk function task, not an audit committee role.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Audit committee = financial-reporting integrity, internal controls, and oversight of internal/external audit. Risk committee = risk appetite oversight and forward-looking risk. Know which committee owns which duty.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Audit committee vs Risk committee\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Audit committee\",\"items\":[\"Financial-reporting integrity\",\"Internal controls\",\"Oversee internal/external audit\"]},{\"h\":\"Risk committee\",\"items\":[\"Risk appetite oversight\",\"Forward-looking risk\",\"Engage the RAS\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Best-practice corporate governance suggests that the board should approve:\nA) Business strategy and risk appetite together, because strategy is itself a risk decision B) Risk appetite only after strategy has been fully executed C) Strategy without regard to risk appetite, to avoid limiting growth D) Only the compensation plan, leaving strategy to the CEO alone\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks how strategy and risk appetite should relate at board level. Best practice is to set them jointly: a growth strategy implies risk consequences (new markets, products, leverage), and the appetite constraint shapes which strategies are even feasible. Treating them separately invites a dangerous disconnect.\n\nIt is like planning a road trip and deciding your fuel budget at the same time — choosing the route without considering fuel will strand you.\n\n- B is wrong: setting appetite only *after* executing strategy is backwards — by then the risks are already taken.\n- C is wrong: ignoring appetite to chase growth is exactly the disconnect that gets \"resolved on the trading floor in favor of growth\" — a governance failure.\n- D is wrong: the board cannot abdicate strategy entirely to the CEO; strategy and appetite are core board matters.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Strategy IS a risk decision — the board approves strategy and risk appetite jointly. A conservative stated appetite with aggressive growth targets is a red flag.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Set strategy and appetite together\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Best practice\",\"items\":[\"Approve jointly\",\"Strategy = risk decision\",\"Appetite shapes strategy\"]},{\"h\":\"Red flag\",\"items\":[\"Set appetite after\",\"Ignore appetite for growth\",\"Growth wins on the floor\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Why are incentives described as the \"transmission belt\" of risk governance?\nA) Because limits cannot be cascaded without incentives B) Because if pay rewards short-term, risk-blind P&L, employees will push the risk profile beyond appetite regardless of written policy C) Because incentives replace the need for a risk function D) Because incentives determine the firm's risk capacity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why incentives matter so much in governance. No matter what the policy documents say, people respond to how they are paid. If bonuses reward revenue or short-term profit with no charge for risk, staff will take more risk than the firm's appetite allows. So incentives effectively \"transmit\" behavior down to the desk.\n\nThink of a delivery company that pays drivers only for speed: no memo about \"drive safely\" will stop reckless driving until the pay scheme changes.\n\n- A is wrong: limits are cascaded through the limit framework and delegated authorities, not \"through incentives.\"\n- C is wrong: incentives complement, they do not replace, the risk function.\n- D is wrong: risk capacity is set by capital, liquidity, and franchise — not by the incentive scheme.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Fix incentives with risk-adjusted performance measures (e.g., RAROC), deferred comp, clawbacks, and risk-function input into pay. Misaligned pay overrides policy every time.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Incentives as transmission belt\",\"steps\":[\"Pay rewards short-term P&L\",\"Staff chase the bonus\",\"Risk profile exceeds appetite\",\"Policy is overridden\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A clear risk appetite statement is cascaded down to individual desks primarily through:\nA) The external audit report B) The limit framework (limits by business, desk, and risk type) C) The annual shareholder meeting D) The firm's marketing strategy\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the mechanism that translates a high-level, slow-moving appetite into concrete, granular constraints at the desk. That bridge is the limit framework — cascading limits by business line, desk, and risk type.\n\nAppetite is like a household saying \"we'll spend responsibly\"; the limit framework is the actual monthly budget broken down into categories that each person must follow.\n\n- A is wrong: the external audit report addresses financial statements, not cascading appetite into desk limits.\n- C is wrong: a shareholder meeting is a governance event, not a transmission mechanism for limits.\n- D is wrong: marketing strategy is unrelated to risk transmission.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Appetite statement → limit framework is the key translation layer. Other transmission mechanisms include committees (ALCO, credit, op-risk), policies, delegated authorities, and the three lines of defense.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"How appetite reaches the desk\",\"options\":[\"External audit report\",\"The limit framework\",\"Shareholder meeting\",\"Marketing strategy\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The limit framework cascades appetite into granular desk-level constraints.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Internal audit (the third line of defense) should report primarily to:\nA) The Chief Executive Officer B) The head of the trading desk C) The board's audit committee D) The Chief Risk Officer\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests internal audit's reporting line, which exists to protect its independence. Internal audit reports to the board's audit committee so it can objectively assure that the first and second lines work as designed, without being controlled by the people it audits.\n\n- A is wrong: reporting to the CEO would compromise independence, since the CEO runs the business audit is assessing.\n- B is wrong: a trading desk head is part of the first line audit reviews — clearly a conflict.\n- D is wrong: the CRO leads the second line; if audit reported to the CRO it could not independently assure the second line's own work.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Internal audit (3rd line) reports to the audit committee to stay independent of management and of the risk function it assures. Independence is the whole point.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Internal audit reports to whom?\",\"options\":[\"The CEO\",\"The trading-desk head\",\"The board's audit committee\",\"The CRO\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Reporting to the audit committee preserves audit's independence.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The role of the board in risk governance is best summarized as:\nA) Day-to-day management of the risk function B) Oversight — setting appetite, approving strategy and key policies, ensuring capable management and sound incentives, and monitoring C) Personally measuring and reporting the firm's daily VaR D) Approving every individual trade above a threshold\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the correct characterization of the board's job. The board provides oversight: it sets risk appetite, approves strategy and major policies, ensures there is a credible CRO and risk organization, oversees incentive design, and monitors — but it does not manage the risk function day to day.\n\nA board is like a ship's owners who decide the destination and safety rules and check on progress; the captain and crew actually sail the ship.\n\n- A is wrong: day-to-day management belongs to management, not the board.\n- C is wrong: measuring and reporting daily VaR is an operational risk-function task.\n- D is wrong: approving individual trades is a management/delegated-authority function, not board oversight.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Board = oversight, NOT management. Set appetite, approve strategy/policies, ensure capable management and incentives, monitor and challenge (\"constructive dialogue\").\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"The board's role is best summarized as\",\"options\":[\"Day-to-day risk management\",\"Oversight: appetite, strategy, monitor\",\"Measure daily VaR itself\",\"Approve every large trade\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Board = oversight; set appetite, approve strategy/policy, ensure capable mgmt, monitor.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A risk report shows a clean VaR number, but the underlying trade capture and valuation data feeding it are unreliable. This best illustrates that:\nA) VaR is always wrong and should never be used B) A risk number is only as good as the trade capture, valuation, and data plumbing beneath it C) The board should personally re-enter all trade data D) Internal audit should design the trading system\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is about the interdependence of functional units in risk management. The risk function depends on operations/IT for clean trade capture and on finance for valuations. If that plumbing is faulty, even a sophisticated risk number is unreliable — \"garbage in, garbage out.\"\n\nThink of a fancy fuel gauge wired to a broken sensor: the dial looks precise, but the reading is meaningless because the input is bad.\n\n- A is wrong: the point is not that VaR is inherently useless, but that its quality depends on the data behind it.\n- C is wrong: the board does not re-enter trade data; that is far outside its oversight role.\n- D is wrong: internal audit must stay independent and assure controls, not *design* the trading system (that would compromise its independence).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Risk management is not an island — it depends on operations/IT (clean trade capture), finance (valuation/P&L), and the back office (independent confirmation). Weak data plumbing undermines any risk number.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"A risk number is only as good as its inputs\",\"steps\":[\"Trade capture\",\"Valuation / P&L\",\"Data plumbing\",\"Risk number (VaR)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Which is a board responsibility rather than a management responsibility?\nA) Setting desk-level VaR limits B) Approving the firm-wide risk appetite statement C) Designing the daily risk report D) Approving individual large loans\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Appetite is approved at board level; A, C, D are management/operational tasks.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In the three-lines-of-defense model, independent measurement and challenge of business-line risk taking is performed by:\nA) The business line B) The risk management function C) Internal audit D) The external auditor\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Second line = risk/compliance: framework, independent measurement, challenge. Audit (third line) assures the system itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which post-crisis change is correctly paired with its purpose?\nA) Volcker rule — increase banks' proprietary trading capacity B) Clawbacks — align pay with long-run, risk-adjusted outcomes C) Basel III leverage ratio — replace risk-based capital entirely D) Board risk committees — take over day-to-day risk management\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Clawback/deferral aligns incentives with risk-adjusted, multi-period results; the other pairings are inverted or false.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The audit committee's primary responsibility is:\nA) Setting the firm's risk appetite B) Managing the internal audit function's day-to-day testing C) Overseeing the integrity of financial reporting and the control environment D) Approving new trading products\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Financial reporting integrity, controls, and oversight of internal/external audit; it oversees internal audit but does not run it.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Which arrangement best supports the independence of the risk management function?\nA) The CRO reports to the head of trading, who knows the risks best B) The CRO reports to the CEO with unrestricted access to the board risk committee C) Risk managers are paid bonuses tied to the revenue of the desks they monitor D) Each business line appoints and pays its own risk officers\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Independence requires a reporting line outside the businesses being monitored and direct board access. A, C, D each compromise independence through hierarchy or incentives.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"CRO independence\",\"options\":[\"A. CRO reports to head of trading\",\"B. CRO to CEO + board risk cmte\",\"C. Bonus tied to desk revenue\",\"D. Lines pay own risk officers\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Reporting line outside the monitored businesses + direct board access\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Within the three lines of defense, designing and performing independent *assurance* that the risk framework operates as intended belongs to:\nA) The business line B) The risk management function C) Internal audit D) The board risk committee\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Third line = internal audit: independent assurance over the first (business) and second (risk/compliance) lines, reporting to the audit committee.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Three lines of defense\",\"steps\":[\"1st: business owns risk\",\"2nd: risk & compliance oversee\",\"3rd: internal audit assures\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** During a regulatory review, a bank's risk architecture is described as follows: the FX trading desk monitors and stays within its own VaR limits; the independent risk management function builds the VaR model and challenges the desk's positions; internal audit periodically tests whether both the desk and the risk function operate as designed and reports its findings to the audit committee. An examiner says one role has been assigned to the wrong line of defense. Which assignment, if it were present, would violate the three-lines model?\nA) The risk management function independently building the VaR model — this should be the first line B) The trading desk owning and managing the risk it generates — this should be the second line C) Internal audit *designing* the desk's risk controls (rather than independently assuring them) — this collapses the third line into the first/second D) Internal audit reporting to the audit committee — it should report to the CRO\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which assignment would *violate* the three-lines-of-defense model. The described setup is mostly correct; you must spot the impermissible variant among the options.\n\n1. First line = business lines/risk owners: the desk owns and manages the risk it generates and stays within limits — correct as described.\n2. Second line = risk management/compliance: sets the framework, measures independently, monitors, and challenges — correct as described.\n3. Third line = internal audit: independently *assures* that lines 1 and 2 work as designed, and stays independent of both. If internal audit *designed* the controls it later audits, it could no longer assure them independently — a clear violation. That is C.\n\n- A is wrong: building the independent risk model is precisely a second-line job, not first-line.\n- B is wrong: owning and managing generated risk is exactly the first-line role, not second-line.\n- D is wrong: internal audit reporting to the **audit committee** is correct best practice; reporting to the CRO would actually *compromise* its independence.\n\n📌 Exam tip: the third line (internal audit) must never *design* or *own* controls — only assure them — and reports to the audit committee, not the CRO. Watch for questions that secretly hand first-line ownership to the CRO or make audit a control designer; both break independence.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Three lines (audit assures, never designs)\",\"steps\":[\"1st: Desk owns risk\",\"2nd: Risk fn builds model & challenges\",\"3rd: Audit assures, reports to audit cttee\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A bank's board approves an aggressive three-year growth target (double the loan book) while simultaneously approving a conservative risk appetite statement (no increase in the firm's credit-loss volatility). Traders and lenders are paid on origination volume with no risk adjustment. The CRO warns the structure is internally inconsistent and predicts a specific outcome. Which prediction is most consistent with the curriculum's treatment of appetite, strategy, and incentives?\nA) The conservative appetite will automatically dominate because it is a board-approved policy document B) The disconnect will be resolved on the trading floor in favor of growth, because volume-based incentives are the transmission belt and will push the risk profile beyond appetite unless governance intervenes C) The two goals are independent, so both can be met without conflict D) The growth target will be abandoned because risk capacity is fixed\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests how strategy, risk appetite, and incentives interact when they are misaligned.\n\n1. The curriculum insists risk appetite and strategy must be set **jointly** — a growth strategy carries risk consequences (more lending, more credit-loss volatility). A conservative appetite paired with an aggressive growth target is a disconnect.\n2. **Incentives are the transmission belt.** If pay is on origination volume with no risk adjustment, employees move the risk profile toward whatever the bonus rewards — here, growth.\n3. The curriculum states such disconnects \"get resolved on the trading floor in favor of growth unless governance intervenes.\" So the predicted outcome is exactly B.\n\n- A assumes a policy document self-enforces; the whole point is that incentives, not paper, drive behavior.\n- C calls the goals independent; doubling the loan book without raising credit-loss volatility is internally contradictory given the incentive design.\n- D claims the growth target is auto-abandoned because capacity is fixed; nothing forces that — absent governance, growth wins.\n\n📌 Exam tip: when a question pairs aggressive targets, conservative appetite, and volume-based pay, the answer is almost always that *incentives win and push the profile past appetite unless governance intervenes*. Appetite and strategy must be set together; pay is the transmission mechanism.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Misaligned incentives win\",\"steps\":[\"Aggressive growth target\",\"Conservative appetite\",\"Volume-based pay\",\"Profile blows past appetite\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A firm is allocating oversight duties between its board risk committee and its audit committee. For each duty below, the firm must pick the *primary* owner: (i) approving and overseeing the firm-wide risk appetite and forward-looking risk; (ii) overseeing the integrity of financial reporting and the external auditor; (iii) approving the internal audit plan and protecting internal audit's independence. Which assignment is correct?\nA) (i) audit committee; (ii) risk committee; (iii) risk committee B) (i) risk committee; (ii) audit committee; (iii) audit committee C) (i) risk committee; (ii) risk committee; (iii) audit committee D) (i) audit committee; (ii) audit committee; (iii) risk committee\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to assign three governance duties to the correct board committee.\n\n1. Duty (i) — risk appetite oversight and forward-looking risk — is the **risk committee's** core mandate.\n2. Duty (ii) — integrity of financial reporting and the external audit relationship — belongs to the **audit committee**.\n3. Duty (iii) — approving the internal audit plan, receiving its findings, and protecting its independence — also belongs to the **audit committee** (it oversees internal audit but does not run its day-to-day testing).\n\nSo (i) risk, (ii) audit, (iii) audit — option B.\n\n- A and D mis-assign the risk-appetite duty to the audit committee.\n- C correctly tags (i) but gives financial-reporting integrity to the risk committee — that is the audit committee's signature responsibility.\n\n📌 Exam tip: split the committees cleanly — *risk committee* = appetite + forward-looking risk; *audit committee* = financial-reporting integrity, controls assurance, and oversight (not running) of internal/external audit. Internal audit *reports to* the audit committee.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Risk committee vs Audit committee\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Risk committee\",\"items\":[\"Risk appetite oversight\",\"Forward-looking risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Audit committee\",\"items\":[\"Financial-reporting integrity\",\"Internal audit plan & independence\",\"External auditor\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which post-crisis governance or regulatory reform is paired correctly with its intended purpose?\nA) The Volcker rule was designed to expand banks' proprietary trading so they could earn higher returns B) Compensation clawback and malus provisions were introduced to align pay with long-run, risk-adjusted outcomes C) The Basel III leverage ratio was intended to fully replace risk-based capital requirements D) Mandatory board risk committees were created to take over the day-to-day running of the risk function\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which reform–purpose pairing is accurate.\n\n1. Clawback/malus and deferral let the firm recover or withhold pay when risks materialize later, so compensation reflects multi-period, risk-adjusted performance rather than one good year. That matches the curriculum's \"deferral, clawback/malus provisions, risk-adjusted performance metrics.\" So B is correct.\n\n- A inverts the Volcker rule, which *restricts* proprietary trading, not expands it.\n- C is false: the leverage ratio is a *backstop that complements* risk-based capital, not a replacement.\n- D is false: board risk committees provide *oversight* — set appetite, engage with the risk appetite statement — not day-to-day management; the board's job is oversight, not management.\n\n📌 Exam tip: memorize the corrected pairings — Volcker = *restrict* prop trading; leverage ratio = *backstop/complement* to risk-based capital; board risk committee = *oversight, not management*; clawback/deferral = align pay with *long-run, risk-adjusted* outcomes. Distractors typically invert exactly one of these.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Reform correctly paired with purpose\",\"options\":[\"Volcker: expand prop trading\",\"Clawback/malus: align long-run pay\",\"Leverage ratio: replace risk capital\",\"Risk cttee: run risk daily\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Clawback/malus recover or withhold pay when risks sour = long-run, risk-adjusted alignment.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A new director argues that because the board approves the risk appetite statement, the board is therefore \"managing the firm's risk\" and should also set desk-level VaR limits, approve individual large loans, and design the daily risk report so that it stays close to the action. Which response correctly states the boundary of the board's role?\nA) The director is right; close board involvement in desk limits and individual loans is best practice B) The board's role is oversight, not management: it approves appetite, strategy, and key policies, ensures capable management and sound incentives, and monitors — but it does not run the risk function or set desk limits, approve individual loans, or design daily reports C) The board should design the daily risk report but delegate appetite-setting to management D) The board should approve individual large loans but leave the appetite statement to the CRO\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the line between board *oversight* and management *execution*.\n\n1. The curriculum is explicit: \"the board's job is **oversight, not management**: set appetite, approve strategy and key policies, ensure capable management and sound incentive design, and monitor — it does not run the risk function day to day.\"\n2. Setting desk-level VaR limits, approving individual large loans, and designing the daily risk report are operational/management tasks — not board functions.\n\nSo the correct boundary is B.\n\n- A endorses the director's overreach into management tasks.\n- C and D each hand the board a management task (designing reports / approving individual loans) while stripping it of a genuine board task (approving appetite) — both invert the proper allocation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: board = *approve appetite/strategy/policy, ensure capable management and incentives, monitor*. Anything granular and operational — desk limits, individual deals, report design — is management. Distractors love to drag the board down into execution or push appetite-setting down to management.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Board oversight vs Management execution\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Board (oversight)\",\"items\":[\"Approve appetite/strategy\",\"Ensure capable mgmt & incentives\",\"Monitor\"]},{\"h\":\"Management (execution)\",\"items\":[\"Set desk VaR limits\",\"Approve individual loans\",\"Design daily reports\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A firm experiences a large loss when a trader's positions are mis-booked for months because the back office that should have independently confirmed and settled the trades had been merged under the trading desk's control. A risk officer explains the loss using the concept of functional interdependence. Which explanation is most accurate?\nA) The loss was purely market risk and unrelated to organizational structure B) The independent back office is the check that positions are real; merging it under the front office removed that independent confirmation, so reported risk numbers rested on unverified trades — the breakdown that enabled Barings C) The CRO should personally confirm every trade; this is a first-line ownership failure by the risk function D) The audit committee should have set the desk's trading limits to prevent the mis-booking\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the functional-interdependence explanation of a control breakdown.\n\n1. The curriculum stresses that operations/back office \"confirms and settles trades — the independent check that positions are real,\" and that its absence \"enabled Barings.\"\n2. Merging the back office under the trading desk destroyed that independence: the same unit both took and confirmed positions, so mis-booked trades went undetected and any risk number built on those trades was unreliable. \"A risk number is only as good as the trade capture, valuation, and data plumbing beneath it.\" That is B.\n\n- A denies the structural/operational cause; the loss is an operational/control failure, not pure market risk.\n- C misassigns first-line ownership to the CRO; the front office and an *independent* back office own trade capture/confirmation, not the CRO personally.\n- D miscasts the audit committee as a setter of trading limits — that is a management task, and the failure here is loss of independent confirmation, not the limit level.\n\n📌 Exam tip: independent trade confirmation/settlement (segregation of duties between front and back office) is the control whose absence produced Barings. When a scenario removes that independence, the answer is the loss of the *independent check that positions are real* — and remember every risk number depends on clean trade capture beneath it.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Lost independence enabled Barings\",\"steps\":[\"Back office merged under desk\",\"No independent confirmation\",\"Mis-booked trades hidden\",\"Risk numbers unreliable\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The 2007–09 crisis prompted the elevation of the Chief Risk Officer role. A bank is redesigning its reporting lines. Which CRO arrangement best reflects post-crisis best practice as described in the curriculum?\nA) The CRO reports into the head of the trading business, ensuring close knowledge of front-office activity B) The CRO reports to the CFO, consolidating all control functions under finance C) The CRO is independent, with direct access and a reporting line to the board or its risk committee, not buried under the business or the CFO D) The CRO rotates annually among the business-line heads to maintain breadth\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which CRO reporting arrangement matches post-crisis best practice.\n\n1. The curriculum describes \"CRO elevation: an independent Chief Risk Officer with direct access/reporting to the board or its risk committee, **not buried under the business or the CFO**.\" That is exactly C.\n\n- A buries the CRO under the trading business — destroying independence (the second line cannot be controlled by the first line it must challenge).\n- B places the CRO under the CFO, the precise arrangement the reforms sought to end.\n- D (rotating among business heads) again subordinates the CRO to the lines being overseen and removes a stable independent reporting line.\n\n📌 Exam tip: the headline post-crisis fix is an *independent* CRO with a *direct board/risk-committee reporting line*. Any answer that puts the CRO under the business or the CFO is the trap, because it strips the independence that lets the second line challenge the first.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Post-crisis CRO best practice\",\"options\":[\"Reports to head of trading\",\"Reports to the CFO\",\"Independent, direct line to board/risk cttee\",\"Rotates among business heads\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"An independent CRO with a direct board line can challenge the first line without being buried under it.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"104","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"Credit Risk Transfer Mechanisms","notes":"# 4. Credit Risk Transfer Mechanisms\n\nHow credit risk is shed without selling the loan: traditional mitigants, credit derivatives (CDS and friends), and securitization. GARP tests the mechanics, the comparison across instruments, and the role credit transfer played in the 2007–09 crisis.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Compare different types of credit derivatives, and explain their applications and advantages.\n- Explain the traditional approaches firms use to mitigate credit risk (insurance/guarantees, netting, marking to market, collateralization, termination/put features).\n- Evaluate the role of credit derivatives in the 2007–2009 crisis and explain how the credit derivatives market changed afterward.\n- Explain the process of securitization, describe a special purpose vehicle (SPV), and assess the risks of the originate-to-distribute versus buy-and-hold business models.\n\n## Traditional credit risk mitigants\n\n| Mechanism | How it transfers/reduces credit risk | Watch-outs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Purchasing insurance / bank guarantees / letters of credit** | A third party promises to pay if the obligor defaults | You now hold the guarantor's credit risk (wrong-way risk if guarantor is correlated with obligor) |\n| **Netting** | Offsetting mutual obligations so only the net amount is at risk on default (close-out netting under an ISDA master agreement) | Legal enforceability across jurisdictions |\n| **Marking to market** | Periodic settlement of gains/losses resets exposure toward zero | Requires liquidity to pay variation amounts |\n| **Collateralization** | Counterparty posts assets (margin) against the exposure | Collateral value can fall with the exposure; haircuts and rehypothecation issues; margin calls create liquidity risk |\n| **Termination / put options** | Right to terminate the contract or put it back at mid-market on a credit trigger (e.g., downgrade clauses) | Triggers can accelerate a counterparty's death spiral (AIG's downgrade collateral calls) |\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> These mitigants mostly *reduce* exposure to a given counterparty; credit derivatives and securitization go further — they *transfer* the credit risk itself to another investor.\n\n## Credit derivatives\n\n**Credit default swap (CDS).** The protection buyer pays a periodic spread (premium); on a defined **credit event** (bankruptcy, failure to pay, restructuring) of the reference entity, the protection seller pays par minus recovery (cash settled via auction, or physical delivery). Economically: insurance on a bond, without requiring ownership of the bond.\n\n- **Single-name CDS** — one reference entity.\n- **Index CDS / portfolio products** (e.g., CDX, iTraxx) — standardized baskets.\n- **First-to-default basket** — seller covers the first default in a small basket; cheaper protection than buying CDS on every name.\n\n**Total return swap (TRS).** One party pays the *total return* (coupons + price appreciation) of a reference asset and receives a floating rate plus spread; the receiver of total return gains the asset's full economics — **both credit and market risk** — without funding it on balance sheet.\n\n**Credit-linked note (CLN).** A *funded* structure: the investor buys a note whose principal is written down if the reference entity defaults. Because the investor pays upfront, the protection buyer bears **no counterparty risk** on the protection.\n\n**Collateralized debt obligation (CDO).** Securitization technology applied to a pool of credit assets (bonds, loans, CDS in synthetic CDOs) with tranched liabilities — see below.\n\n| Instrument | Funded? | Risk transferred | Counterparty risk to protection buyer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CDS | No | Credit (default) risk only | Yes — seller may fail (AIG) |\n| TRS | No | Credit **and** market risk | Yes |\n| CLN | Yes | Credit risk | No — cash held upfront |\n| CDO tranche | Yes | Pooled, tranched credit risk | No (funded), but model/correlation risk |\n\n**Applications/advantages:** banks can hedge concentrations while preserving client relationships (no loan sale notification), free regulatory/economic capital, take short credit positions, and customize exposures; investors gain access to credit risk they could not originate.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: a **CDS transfers credit risk but creates counterparty risk** on the protection seller — and if the seller's health is correlated with the reference credit (monoline insurers, AIG insuring mortgage CDOs), that is **wrong-way risk**: the hedge fails exactly when needed. Also, a TRS transfers *total* return (market + credit), while a CDS transfers default risk only.\n\n## Securitization and SPVs\n\n**Process:**\n1. Originator pools assets (mortgages, auto loans, credit cards, corporate loans).\n2. Sells the pool to a bankruptcy-remote **special purpose vehicle (SPV/SPE)** — a true sale isolates the assets from the originator's insolvency.\n3. The SPV issues **tranches** of securities against the pool's cash flows: senior (first claim, lowest yield, highest rating), mezzanine, and equity/first-loss (last claim, highest yield).\n4. Losses flow bottom-up (equity first); cash flows top-down (the **waterfall**). Credit enhancement: subordination, overcollateralization, excess spread, reserve accounts.\n\n**Why securitize:** funding diversification, balance-sheet/capital relief, risk transfer to investors with appetite for it, liquidity for otherwise illiquid loans.\n\n**Re-securitization:** CDOs built from mezzanine ABS tranches (\"ABS CDOs,\" CDO-squared) concentrated and obscured subprime risk — central to Ch 10.\n\n## Business models: originate-to-distribute vs. buy-and-hold\n\n| | Buy-and-hold | Originate-to-distribute (OTD) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who bears credit risk | Originating bank, to maturity | Investors in the securitization |\n| Incentive to screen/monitor borrowers | Strong — bank eats its own losses | Weak — \"skin in the game\" problem; volume-driven underwriting |\n| Funding/capital | Heavy balance sheet usage | Capital-light, fee-driven |\n| Key risks | Concentration, credit cycle | **Pipeline/warehouse risk** (loans stuck on balance sheet when markets close), moral hazard, reputational/legal risk from bad originations |\n\nThe OTD model's eroded underwriting incentives were a core cause of the subprime debacle. Post-crisis reforms added **risk retention (\"skin in the game\")** rules requiring originators/sponsors to keep a slice (e.g., 5%) of securitized deals.\n\n## Credit derivatives in the crisis — and after\n\n**Role in the crisis:** CDS and synthetic CDOs multiplied exposure to subprime far beyond the cash mortgage market; protection sellers (AIG Financial Products, monolines) wrote massive one-way positions with little collateral; opaque bilateral OTC webs made counterparty exposures unknowable, amplifying panic after Lehman; ratings on tranches proved wildly optimistic (correlation assumptions, agency conflicts).\n\n**Post-crisis changes:**\n- **Central clearing** of standardized CDS through CCPs; trade reporting to repositories (transparency).\n- **Standardization** (Big Bang protocol: fixed coupons, auction settlement, determinations committees for credit events).\n- **Margin requirements** for non-cleared derivatives; higher capital for counterparty risk (CVA charges).\n- Market shrank and concentrated in index products; single-name liquidity declined.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Name the traditional credit risk mitigants.\n> **A:** Purchasing insurance/guarantees/letters of credit, netting, marking to market, collateralization, and termination/put options (e.g., downgrade triggers).\n\n**Q2.** How does a credit default swap (CDS) work?\n> **A:** The protection buyer pays a periodic spread; on a defined credit event (bankruptcy, failure to pay, restructuring) of the reference entity, the seller pays par minus recovery. Economically: insurance on a bond without owning it.\n\n**Q3.** Single-name CDS vs. index CDS vs. first-to-default basket?\n> **A:** Single-name: one reference entity. Index (CDX, iTraxx): standardized baskets. First-to-default: seller covers the first default in a small basket — cheaper than buying CDS on every name.\n\n**Q4.** What does a total return swap (TRS) transfer?\n> **A:** Both credit and market risk. One party pays the total return (coupons + price change) of a reference asset and receives floating + spread; the receiver gets the asset's full economics without funding it.\n\n**Q5.** Why does a credit-linked note (CLN) leave the protection buyer with no counterparty risk?\n> **A:** It is funded — the investor pays cash upfront for a note whose principal is written down on default, so the protection buyer already holds the money.\n\n**Q6.** Which instruments are funded, and what does the protection buyer's counterparty risk look like for each (CDS, TRS, CLN)?\n> **A:** CDS: unfunded, counterparty risk yes (seller may fail). TRS: unfunded, counterparty risk yes. CLN: funded, counterparty risk no.\n\n**Q7.** Exam trap: what risk does a CDS create, and what is wrong-way risk?\n> **A:** A CDS transfers credit risk but creates counterparty risk on the seller. Wrong-way risk: the seller's health is correlated with the reference credit (AIG/monolines insuring mortgage CDOs), so the hedge fails when needed.\n\n**Q8.** What is a special purpose vehicle (SPV) in securitization?\n> **A:** A bankruptcy-remote entity that buys the asset pool in a true sale, isolating the assets from the originator's insolvency, and issues tranches against the pool's cash flows.\n\n**Q9.** How do losses and cash flows flow through securitization tranches?\n> **A:** Losses flow bottom-up (equity/first-loss, then mezzanine, then senior); cash flows top-down — the waterfall. Senior has first claim, lowest yield, highest rating.\n\n**Q10.** Name forms of credit enhancement in a securitization.\n> **A:** Subordination, overcollateralization, excess spread, and reserve accounts.\n\n**Q11.** Originate-to-distribute vs. buy-and-hold — incentive difference?\n> **A:** Buy-and-hold gives a strong incentive to screen/monitor borrowers (bank eats its losses). OTD weakens it — the \"skin in the game\" problem and volume-driven underwriting; risk passes to investors.\n\n**Q12.** What is pipeline/warehouse risk in the OTD model?\n> **A:** The risk that loans get stuck on the originator's balance sheet when securitization markets close, plus moral hazard and reputational/legal risk from bad originations.\n\n**Q13.** What post-crisis reform targeted the OTD incentive problem?\n> **A:** Risk retention (\"skin in the game\") rules requiring originators/sponsors to keep a slice (e.g., 5%) of securitized deals.\n\n**Q14.** Name the key post-crisis changes to the credit derivatives market.\n> **A:** Central clearing of standardized CDS via CCPs, trade reporting, standardization (Big Bang protocol: fixed coupons, auction settlement, determinations committees), margin for non-cleared trades, and CVA capital charges.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A bank wants to hedge the default risk on a large loan to a key client without selling the loan or alerting the client. The most direct instrument is to:\nA) Enter a total return swap paying the loan's total return B) Buy a single-name credit default swap (CDS) on the borrower C) Issue a credit-linked note (CLN) D) Securitize its entire loan book\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the cleanest, most targeted way to shed *default* risk on one borrower while keeping the loan and the relationship quiet. A single-name CDS does exactly that: the bank pays a periodic spread and, if the borrower has a credit event, the protection seller pays par minus recovery. The loan stays on the books and the client is never notified.\n\nIt is like buying insurance on a friend's loan you co-signed — you're protected if they default, but you didn't have to sell or cancel anything.\n\n- A is wrong: a total return swap would transfer *both* credit and market risk and changes the loan's economics — more than needed to hedge default.\n- C is wrong: issuing a CLN is heavier machinery (a funded note structure), not the most direct hedge for one loan.\n- D is wrong: securitizing the whole book is a large, complex transaction far beyond hedging a single client's default risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To discreetly hedge ONE name's default risk while keeping the loan, use a single-name CDS. A TRS adds market risk transfer; CLN/securitization are heavier funded structures.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Hedge ONE borrower's default, keep the loan quiet\",\"options\":[\"Total return swap\",\"Single-name CDS\",\"Credit-linked note\",\"Securitize the book\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"CDS sheds default risk only, loan stays on book, client not notified.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which credit risk transfer instrument leaves the protection *buyer* with no counterparty risk on the protection provider?\nA) CDS B) Total return swap C) Credit-linked note (CLN) D) Bank guarantee\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which structure removes the worry that the protection seller might fail to pay. A credit-linked note is *funded*: the investor (protection seller) pays cash upfront to buy the note, and that cash is held against the reference exposure. Because the protection buyer already holds the money, there is no counterparty risk on the protection.\n\nThink of asking a friend to cover a possible loss: a CDS is a verbal promise (they might not pay), while a CLN is your friend handing you the cash in advance to hold — no chance they default on the promise.\n\n- A is wrong: a CDS is unfunded, so the protection buyer still relies on the seller to pay (AIG-style counterparty risk).\n- B is wrong: a TRS is also unfunded and carries counterparty risk on the other party.\n- D is wrong: a bank guarantee leaves you holding the guarantor's credit risk (and wrong-way risk if correlated).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Funded structures (CLN, CDO tranche) eliminate counterparty risk to the buyer because cash is posted upfront. Unfunded ones (CDS, TRS, guarantee) leave counterparty risk on the provider.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Funded vs Unfunded protection\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Funded (CLN, CDO)\",\"items\":[\"Cash posted upfront\",\"Buyer already holds it\",\"No counterparty risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Unfunded (CDS, TRS, guarantee)\",\"items\":[\"Just a promise to pay\",\"Seller may fail (AIG)\",\"Counterparty risk remains\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In a typical securitization, the first losses on the asset pool are absorbed by the:\nA) Senior tranche B) Mezzanine tranche C) Equity (first-loss) tranche D) Originating bank's depositors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the loss waterfall. In a securitization, losses flow bottom-up: the equity (first-loss) tranche takes the first hit, then the mezzanine, and only after those are wiped out does the senior tranche suffer. (Cash flows go the other way — top-down to senior first.)\n\nPicture a stadium that floods from the bottom: the lowest seats (equity) get wet first, the middle rows (mezzanine) next, and the top rows (senior) only if it floods badly.\n\n- A is wrong: the senior tranche has the *first claim* on cash and is the *last* to take losses — that's why it has the highest rating and lowest yield.\n- B is wrong: the mezzanine tranche takes losses *after* equity, not first.\n- D is wrong: a true sale to a bankruptcy-remote SPV isolates the assets, so the originating bank's depositors are not in the loss line for the pool.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Losses flow bottom-up (equity → mezzanine → senior); cash flows top-down (senior first). Equity = first-loss, highest yield; senior = last-loss, highest rating.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Securitization loss waterfall (bottom-up)\",\"items\":[\"Equity (first-loss)\",\"Mezzanine\",\"|Senior (last-loss)\"],\"left\":\"hit first\",\"right\":\"hit last\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The originate-to-distribute (OTD) business model primarily weakens:\nA) The originator's incentive to screen and monitor borrowers B) The SPV's bankruptcy remoteness C) Investors' access to credit risk D) The waterfall priority of senior tranches\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks what goes wrong with incentives under OTD. When a bank originates loans only to sell (distribute) them to investors, it no longer bears the losses if those loans go bad. That removes its \"skin in the game,\" so its incentive to carefully screen and monitor borrowers weakens, and underwriting becomes volume-driven.\n\nIt is like a used-car dealer who sells cars and never has to honor a warranty — they'll be far less careful about quality than one who must repair every breakdown.\n\n- B is wrong: bankruptcy remoteness is a legal feature of the SPV (true sale), unaffected by the originator's incentives.\n- C is wrong: OTD actually *expands* investors' access to credit risk, it doesn't reduce it.\n- D is wrong: the waterfall priority of senior tranches is a structural feature, not something OTD erodes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OTD's core flaw is weakened underwriting incentives (no skin in the game) — a key cause of the subprime crisis. Post-crisis \"risk retention\" rules force originators to keep a slice (e.g., 5%).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Why OTD weakens screening\",\"steps\":[\"Originate to sell\",\"No skin in the game\",\"Loss is someone else's\",\"Screening incentive drops\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A protection seller writes CDS protection on subprime mortgage CDOs, and that seller's own financial health is highly correlated with the subprime market it is insuring. This situation is best described as:\nA) Basis risk B) Wrong-way risk C) Funding liquidity risk D) Translation risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a hedge that is most likely to fail precisely when you need it. Wrong-way risk arises when the protection seller's ability to pay deteriorates *at the same time* as the reference credit — so when defaults spike, the seller (e.g., AIG, monolines) is also collapsing and cannot pay.\n\nImagine buying flood insurance from a company located in the same flood zone as your house: when your house floods, the insurer is underwater too and can't honor the claim.\n\n- A is wrong: basis risk is when a hedge doesn't perfectly track the exposure (different grade/maturity/index), not about correlated counterparty failure.\n- C is wrong: funding liquidity risk is about raising cash to meet obligations, not the correlation between seller and reference credit.\n- D is wrong: translation risk is an FX accounting concept, unrelated.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Wrong-way risk = your counterparty is likely to default exactly when the protection is needed (their health is correlated with the reference credit). The hedge fails at the worst moment — see AIG and the monolines.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Seller's health correlated with what it insures\",\"options\":[\"Basis risk\",\"Wrong-way risk\",\"Funding liquidity risk\",\"Translation risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Seller fails exactly when defaults spike = wrong-way risk (AIG, monolines).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The key purpose of selling securitized assets to a bankruptcy-remote special purpose vehicle (SPV) is to:\nA) Increase the originator's leverage on its own balance sheet B) Isolate the pooled assets from the originator's insolvency through a true sale C) Guarantee the senior tranche against all losses D) Eliminate the need for credit ratings\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why securitizations use an SPV. By making a *true sale* of the asset pool to a bankruptcy-remote SPV, the assets are legally separated from the originator. So even if the originating bank goes bankrupt, the SPV's investors still have claim on the pool's cash flows.\n\nIt is like putting money in a trust for your children: even if you go bankrupt, your creditors can't reach what's already legally in the trust.\n\n- A is wrong: securitization typically provides balance-sheet/capital *relief*, not more leverage on the originator's own books.\n- C is wrong: the SPV structure does not guarantee the senior tranche against *all* losses; credit enhancement reduces but doesn't eliminate risk.\n- D is wrong: tranches are still rated; the SPV does not remove the role of credit ratings.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SPV + true sale = bankruptcy remoteness, isolating the assets from the originator's insolvency. Credit enhancement (subordination, overcollateralization, excess spread, reserves) protects senior tranches but doesn't eliminate risk.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Why use a bankruptcy-remote SPV\",\"steps\":[\"Originator pools assets\",\"True sale to SPV\",\"Assets legally isolated\",\"Investors safe if originator fails\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which credit derivative transfers *both* the credit risk and the market (price) risk of a reference asset?\nA) Single-name CDS B) Total return swap (TRS) C) First-to-default basket D) Index CDS\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which instrument passes along the *full* economics of an asset. In a total return swap, one party pays the total return (coupons plus price appreciation, minus depreciation) of the reference asset and receives a floating rate plus a spread. The receiver of total return gains everything — both credit and market/price risk — without funding the asset on its balance sheet.\n\n- A is wrong: a single-name CDS transfers default (credit) risk *only*, not price moves.\n- C is wrong: a first-to-default basket covers only the first default among a small basket — still credit risk, not total return.\n- D is wrong: an index CDS transfers credit risk on a standardized basket of names, not market/price risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: TRS = transfers total return (credit + market risk). CDS = transfers default (credit) risk only. This distinction is a favorite GARP trap.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CDS vs Total return swap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Single-name CDS\",\"items\":[\"Default (credit) risk only\",\"No price risk transfer\"]},{\"h\":\"Total return swap\",\"items\":[\"Credit + market risk\",\"Full economics of asset\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** After the 2007–2009 crisis, which of the following changes occurred in the credit derivatives market?\nA) Standardized CDS moved toward central clearing through CCPs B) Margin requirements for non-cleared derivatives were abolished C) Trade reporting to repositories was discontinued to protect privacy D) Single-name CDS liquidity dramatically increased\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks about post-crisis reforms in credit derivatives. To reduce the opaque, tangled web of bilateral exposures that amplified panic after Lehman, regulators pushed standardized CDS toward central clearing through central counterparties (CCPs), along with trade reporting and standardization (the \"Big Bang\" protocol).\n\n- B is wrong: margin requirements for non-cleared derivatives were *introduced/strengthened*, not abolished.\n- C is wrong: trade reporting to repositories was *increased* for transparency, not discontinued.\n- D is wrong: the market shrank and concentrated in index products; single-name CDS liquidity actually *declined*.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Post-crisis CDS changes — central clearing (CCPs), trade reporting (transparency), standardization (Big Bang: fixed coupons, auction settlement, determinations committees), margin for non-cleared trades, and CVA capital charges.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Post-crisis change in CDS market\",\"options\":[\"Standardized CDS move to CCP clearing\",\"Margin for non-cleared abolished\",\"Trade reporting discontinued\",\"Single-name liquidity surged\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Standardized CDS moved to central clearing through CCPs for transparency.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A traditional credit risk mitigant in which counterparties offset their mutual obligations so that only the net amount is at risk on default (under an ISDA master agreement) is called:\nA) Collateralization B) Netting C) Marking to market D) Securitization\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes reducing exposure by canceling out what two parties owe each other, leaving only the net. That is netting (specifically close-out netting under an ISDA master agreement): instead of each gross obligation being at risk, only the single net figure is exposed if the counterparty defaults.\n\nIf you owe a friend $100 and they owe you $70, netting means only $30 is really at risk between you, not the full $100.\n\n- A is wrong: collateralization means posting assets (margin) against an exposure — a different mechanism.\n- C is wrong: marking to market periodically settles gains/losses to reset exposure toward zero — related but not the offsetting of mutual obligations.\n- D is wrong: securitization is pooling and tranching assets, not offsetting mutual obligations.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Netting (close-out under ISDA) leaves only the net amount at risk — but watch the key risk: legal enforceability across jurisdictions. Don't confuse it with collateralization or marking to market.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Offset mutual obligations to the net (ISDA)\",\"options\":[\"Collateralization\",\"Netting\",\"Marking to market\",\"Securitization\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Close-out netting leaves only the single net amount at risk on default.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Compared with the buy-and-hold model, a distinctive risk of the originate-to-distribute model is:\nA) Heavy long-term balance-sheet usage B) Strong incentives to monitor borrowers to maturity C) Pipeline/warehouse risk if markets close before loans can be distributed D) Complete elimination of reputational and legal risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for a risk that is specific to OTD. Because OTD relies on selling loans onward, the originator faces pipeline (warehouse) risk: if the securitization market freezes, loans the bank intended to distribute get stuck on its balance sheet, exposing it to losses it never planned to hold.\n\nIt is like a fruit reseller who buys produce only to flip it the same day — if buyers vanish, the fruit piles up and rots in the warehouse.\n\n- A is wrong: heavy long-term balance-sheet usage describes the *buy-and-hold* model, not OTD (which is capital-light).\n- B is wrong: strong monitoring incentives belong to buy-and-hold; OTD *weakens* them.\n- D is wrong: OTD actually *raises* reputational and legal risk from bad originations, it doesn't eliminate them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OTD risks = pipeline/warehouse risk (loans stuck when markets close), moral hazard (weak underwriting), and reputational/legal risk from bad originations. Buy-and-hold risks = concentration and the credit cycle, with heavy balance-sheet use.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"OTD vs Buy-and-hold risks\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Originate-to-distribute\",\"items\":[\"Pipeline/warehouse risk\",\"Weak underwriting\",\"Capital-light\"]},{\"h\":\"Buy-and-hold\",\"items\":[\"Concentration risk\",\"Monitors to maturity\",\"Heavy balance sheet\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A bank wants to hedge default risk on a loan to a key client without selling the loan or telling the client. The most direct instrument is:\nA) A total return swap paying the loan's return B) Buying a single-name CDS on the borrower C) Issuing a credit-linked note D) Securitizing its entire loan book\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** CDS hedges default risk discreetly, relationship preserved; a TRS would also shed market risk and a CLN/securitization is heavier machinery.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which credit risk transfer instrument leaves the protection buyer with *no* counterparty risk on the protection provider?\nA) CDS B) TRS C) CLN D) Bank guarantee\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** A credit-linked note is funded upfront — the buyer of protection already holds the cash.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The originate-to-distribute model primarily weakens:\nA) The originator's incentive to screen and monitor borrowers B) The SPV's bankruptcy remoteness C) Investors' access to credit risk D) The waterfall priority of senior tranches\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** When risk is passed to investors, the originator's skin in the game — and underwriting discipline — disappears.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In a securitization, the first losses on the asset pool are absorbed by:\nA) The senior tranche B) The mezzanine tranche C) The equity (first-loss) tranche D) The originating bank's depositors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Losses flow bottom-up: equity, then mezzanine, then senior.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A protection buyer under a single-name CDS is exposed to which residual risk?\nA) None — the credit risk is fully extinguished B) Default of the protection seller, especially if correlated with the reference entity C) Market risk of the reference bond's coupon payments D) The SPV's bankruptcy remoteness failing\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The CDS replaces reference-entity credit risk with counterparty risk on the seller. If seller and reference credit are positively correlated (AIG insuring mortgage CDOs), this is wrong-way risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CDS residual risk\",\"steps\":[\"Buyer hedges reference entity\",\"Risk shifts to seller\",\"Seller defaults?\",\"If correlated = wrong-way risk\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which feature distinguishes a total return swap (TRS) from a CDS?\nA) A TRS transfers both market risk and credit risk of the reference asset B) A TRS requires upfront funding by the protection buyer C) A TRS only pays out on a formal credit event D) A TRS eliminates counterparty risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A TRS passes the asset's full economics — coupons plus price changes — so the total-return payer sheds market **and** credit risk. It is unfunded (B wrong), not credit-event-triggered (C wrong), and bilateral counterparty risk remains (D wrong).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"TRS vs CDS\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Total return swap\",\"items\":[\"market + credit risk\",\"pays on price moves\",\"no credit event needed\"]},{\"h\":\"CDS\",\"items\":[\"credit risk only\",\"pays on credit event\",\"like insurance\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A bank holds a $100 million loan to a corporate client. It wants to (i) shed the *default* risk on the client, (ii) preserve the client relationship by not selling or assigning the loan and not notifying the client, and (iii) avoid taking on the client's *market/price* risk that it does not currently bear. The bank is comparing a single-name CDS, a total return swap (TRS) on the loan, and securitizing the loan. Which instrument best meets *all three* conditions?\nA) A total return swap, because it transfers the loan's full economics B) A single-name CDS referencing the client, because it transfers default risk only and is executed discreetly without selling the loan C) Securitizing the loan through an SPV, because it removes the asset from the balance sheet D) A credit-linked note issued to the client, because it is funded upfront\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which instrument satisfies all three conditions simultaneously.\n\n1. Condition (i) shed *default* risk: a CDS pays par minus recovery on a credit event of the reference entity — it transfers **default (credit) risk only**.\n2. Condition (ii) keep the relationship, no loan sale or notification: buying CDS does not require selling or assigning the loan, so the client is not notified — discreet.\n3. Condition (iii) avoid taking on market/price risk: a CDS transfers *only* default risk, so it does not add the loan's market-price risk. \n\nAll three are met by the single-name CDS — option B.\n\n- A (TRS) fails condition (iii): a TRS transfers the *total* return — both credit **and** market risk — which is more than the bank wants.\n- C (securitization through an SPV) fails condition (ii): it is \"heavier machinery\" and a true sale of the asset, not a discreet relationship-preserving hedge of one loan.\n- D (CLN issued *to the client*) is nonsensical here — the client cannot fund protection on its own default, and it does not discreetly hedge the bank's exposure to that client.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CDS = default risk only, discreet, relationship preserved. TRS = total return = credit **and** market risk. The phrase \"without selling or notifying the client\" almost always points to a single-name CDS.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Shed default risk only, keep loan & relationship\",\"options\":[\"Total return swap\",\"Single-name CDS\",\"Securitize via SPV\",\"CLN to the client\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"CDS transfers default risk only, no loan sale, no client notice.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A monoline insurer with a weak balance sheet sells $5 billion of CDS protection on senior tranches of subprime mortgage CDOs. A bank buys this protection to hedge its own subprime CDO holdings. As the housing market collapses, both the reference CDOs and the monoline deteriorate together. Which statement most precisely describes the risk the bank faces and why?\nA) Basis risk, because the CDS does not reference the exact bonds the bank holds B) Wrong-way risk: the protection seller's creditworthiness is positively correlated with the reference credit's deterioration, so the CDS is most likely to fail exactly when the bank needs it to pay C) Funding liquidity risk, because the bank must post collateral on the CDS D) No residual risk, because purchasing a CDS fully eliminates the bank's credit exposure\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the precise risk created when a protection seller's health is correlated with the reference credit it insures.\n\n1. A CDS transfers credit risk to the protection seller but creates **counterparty risk** on that seller — if the seller fails, the protection is worthless.\n2. Here the monoline's solvency is **positively correlated** with the very subprime credits it insured: when mortgages sour, both the CDOs *and* the monoline collapse together.\n3. That correlation between the protection seller's default and the reference exposure is the definition of **wrong-way risk** — the hedge fails exactly when it is needed. That is B (the AIG/monoline case).\n\n- A (basis risk) is a tracking-mismatch concept; the problem here is the seller's correlated failure, not an index/grade mismatch.\n- C (funding liquidity) describes a margin-cash problem, not the failure-of-the-hedge mechanism asked about.\n- D is the trap that a CDS \"fully eliminates\" credit exposure; it merely *replaces* reference-credit risk with counterparty risk on the seller — and that risk just bit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CDS shifts credit risk but creates counterparty risk on the seller. When the seller's health is *correlated* with the reference credit (monolines, AIG insuring mortgage CDOs), that is wrong-way risk — the protection evaporates precisely when called upon.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Monoline's health tied to credits it insures\",\"options\":[\"Basis risk\",\"Wrong-way risk\",\"Funding liquidity risk\",\"No residual risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Seller and reference credit collapse together = wrong-way risk; hedge fails when needed.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Four credit-risk-transfer instruments are under consideration. The treasurer wants the structure that leaves the **protection buyer with no counterparty risk on the protection provider**. Which one, and why?\nA) A single-name CDS, because the determinations committee guarantees settlement B) A total return swap, because both legs are exchanged periodically C) A credit-linked note, because the investor funds the note upfront so the cash is already held D) A bank guarantee, because the guarantor is a regulated institution\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which instrument removes the protection buyer's counterparty risk on the provider.\n\n1. A **credit-linked note (CLN)** is a *funded* structure: the investor (protection seller) pays cash upfront for the note, whose principal is written down if the reference entity defaults.\n2. Because the protection buyer already holds the investor's cash, there is **no counterparty risk** on the protection — the money is in hand. That is C.\n\n- A (CDS) is *unfunded*; the seller may fail to pay on a credit event (AIG). A determinations committee decides *whether* a credit event occurred — it does not guarantee the seller's solvency.\n- B (TRS) is also unfunded and carries counterparty risk on the total-return payer.\n- D (bank guarantee) replaces obligor risk with the **guarantor's** credit risk — still counterparty risk (and wrong-way risk if correlated).\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"funded upfront → no counterparty risk on the protection\" = credit-linked note (and CDO tranches are also funded). CDS, TRS, and guarantees are unfunded promises and leave the buyer exposed to the provider's default.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"No counterparty risk on the provider?\",\"options\":[\"Single-name CDS\",\"Total return swap\",\"Credit-linked note (funded)\",\"Bank guarantee\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"A CLN is funded upfront, so the buyer already holds the cash — no provider counterparty risk.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A securitization SPV issues three classes of notes against a $1,000 million pool: a senior tranche of $800 million, a mezzanine tranche of $150 million, and an equity (first-loss) tranche of $50 million. The pool experiences cumulative losses of $180 million. Assuming a strict subordination waterfall with no other credit enhancement, what is the loss borne by each tranche?\nA) Equity loses $50m, mezzanine loses $130m, senior loses $0m B) Equity loses $50m, mezzanine loses $150m, senior loses $0m C) Equity loses $60m, mezzanine loses $120m, senior loses $0m D) Losses are shared pro rata: senior $144m, mezzanine $27m, equity $9m\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks how a $180m loss is allocated across tranches under bottom-up subordination.\n\n1. Losses flow **bottom-up**: the equity (first-loss) tranche absorbs losses first.\n   - Equity capacity = $50m. Loss applied: $50m. Remaining loss to allocate: $180m − $50m = $130m.\n2. Next, the mezzanine tranche absorbs losses.\n   - Mezzanine capacity = $150m. Remaining loss $130m < $150m, so mezzanine absorbs all **$130m** and is not wiped out ($20m of mezzanine survives).\n3. Senior tranche: no loss reaches it because the $180m total is fully absorbed by equity + mezzanine. Senior loss = **$0m**.\n\nSo equity $50m, mezzanine $130m, senior $0m — option A.\n\n- B over-allocates the mezzanine to its full $150m; only $130m of loss remains after equity, so mezzanine cannot lose more than $130m.\n- C wrongly inflates the equity loss to $60m (above its $50m capacity) and mis-splits the rest.\n- D applies *pro rata* sharing — exactly the trap. Tranched securitizations use **subordination**, not pro rata loss sharing; losses hit the junior tranche fully before any senior tranche is touched.\n\n📌 Exam tip: tranche losses are sequential, bottom-up — fill the junior tranche to its cap, then spill the remainder up. A tranche never absorbs more than its own size, and the trap answer is pro-rata sharing (which would be wrong for standard subordination).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"$180m loss fills bottom-up\",\"items\":[\"Equity $50m (full)\",\"Mezz absorbs $130m\",\"|Senior $0m\"],\"left\":\"hit first\",\"right\":\"untouched\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Compared with a buy-and-hold lender, an originate-to-distribute (OTD) originator that sells loans into securitizations faces a *different* primary risk profile. Which risk is most distinctively associated with the OTD model, and what governance reform was introduced to address its core incentive flaw?\nA) Concentration risk; addressed by the leverage ratio B) Pipeline/warehouse risk — loans stuck on the balance sheet when the securitization market closes — and the core incentive flaw (weak screening) was addressed by risk-retention (\"skin in the game\") rules C) Counterparty risk on the SPV; addressed by central clearing D) Translation risk; addressed by hedge accounting\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the risk distinctive to OTD and the reform targeting its incentive flaw.\n\n1. OTD is capital-light and fee-driven: the originator plans to *distribute* loans, so it holds them only briefly. If markets freeze, loans pile up unsold on the balance sheet — **pipeline/warehouse risk** — a risk a buy-and-hold lender does not have in the same form.\n2. The deeper flaw is **weak screening/monitoring incentives** (\"skin in the game\" problem): an originator passing on the risk has little reason to underwrite carefully, a core cause of the subprime debacle.\n3. The post-crisis fix was **risk-retention (\"skin in the game\") rules** requiring originators/sponsors to keep a slice (e.g., 5%) of each deal. That is B.\n\n- A names concentration risk (more a buy-and-hold issue) and an unrelated reform (the leverage ratio).\n- C invents counterparty risk on the SPV and pairs it with central clearing (a derivatives reform, not securitization).\n- D names translation risk and hedge accounting — unrelated to OTD.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OTD signature risks = pipeline/warehouse risk + eroded underwriting incentives (moral hazard). The targeted reform is *risk retention / skin in the game* (~5%). Don't confuse this with derivatives-market reforms (clearing, CVA) which address CDS counterparty risk.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"OTD distinctive risk & its fix\",\"steps\":[\"Originate to distribute\",\"Market freezes\",\"Loans stuck = pipeline risk\",\"Fix: risk retention ~5%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which statement about how the credit derivatives market changed *after* the 2007–09 crisis is accurate?\nA) Standardized CDS moved to central clearing through CCPs, trades were reported to repositories, and single-name liquidity declined while activity concentrated in index products B) Central clearing was abolished in favor of purely bilateral OTC trading to preserve customization C) Margin requirements for non-cleared derivatives were removed to reduce funding strains D) CVA capital charges were eliminated because counterparty risk was deemed fully mitigated by clearing\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks which post-crisis change to the credit derivatives market is correctly stated.\n\n1. The reforms introduced **central clearing** of standardized CDS through CCPs and **trade reporting to repositories** for transparency.\n2. The Big Bang protocol brought standardization (fixed coupons, auction settlement, determinations committees).\n3. The market **shrank and concentrated in index products**, while **single-name liquidity declined**. Option A captures all of this accurately.\n\n- B reverses the direction — clearing was *introduced/expanded*, not abolished in favor of bilateral trading.\n- C reverses the margin reform — margin requirements for *non-cleared* derivatives were *imposed*, not removed.\n- D is false — **CVA charges** (higher capital for counterparty risk) were *added*, not eliminated; clearing does not make counterparty risk vanish.\n\n📌 Exam tip: post-crisis CDS reforms = central clearing + trade reporting + standardization (Big Bang) + margin on non-cleared trades + CVA capital charges, with the market concentrating in indices and single-name liquidity falling. Distractors typically *reverse the direction* of one real reform.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Accurate post-crisis CDS change\",\"options\":[\"CCP clearing, reporting, single-name liquidity falls\",\"Clearing abolished for bilateral OTC\",\"Non-cleared margin removed\",\"CVA charges eliminated\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Standardized CDS centrally cleared & reported; activity shifted to indices, single-name liquidity declined.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Several traditional credit-risk mitigants are listed. A risk manager wants the mechanism that, by *periodically settling* gains and losses, resets the outstanding exposure toward zero — but recognizes it imposes a specific secondary risk. Which mechanism and secondary risk are correctly paired?\nA) Netting; secondary risk is that recovery rates fall in default B) Marking to market; secondary risk is that the party must have the liquidity to pay the variation amounts C) Purchasing a guarantee; secondary risk is wrong-way risk on the guarantor D) Collateralization; secondary risk is legal enforceability across jurisdictions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which mitigant *periodically settles gains/losses to reset exposure toward zero*, and what secondary risk that creates.\n\n1. **Marking to market** periodically settles gains and losses, so the running exposure is reset toward zero each period (variation margin/settlement). That matches the described mechanism.\n2. Its watch-out per the curriculum: it \"requires liquidity to pay variation amounts\" — the party owing must have cash on hand to settle. That secondary risk is funding liquidity. Pairing matches B.\n\n- A pairs netting with falling recovery rates; netting's mechanism is offsetting mutual obligations (not periodic gain/loss settlement) and its watch-out is *legal enforceability*, not recovery.\n- C pairs guarantees with wrong-way risk — that pairing is itself valid, but a guarantee does not *periodically settle gains/losses to reset exposure*, so it fails the stem's defining clause.\n- D pairs collateralization with legal enforceability — but the curriculum ties enforceability to *netting*; collateral's watch-outs are falling collateral value, haircuts, rehypothecation, and margin-call liquidity. The defining mechanism (periodic settlement resetting exposure) also points to MTM, not collateral.\n\n📌 Exam tip: match the *mechanism* clause first. \"Periodic settlement resetting exposure toward zero\" = marking to market, whose secondary risk is needing liquidity to pay variation. Netting = offsetting obligations (watch-out: legal enforceability); collateral = posting assets (watch-outs: value declines, haircuts, margin-call liquidity).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mitigant: mechanism & watch-out\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Marking to market\",\"items\":[\"Periodic settle gains/losses\",\"Resets exposure to ~0\",\"Watch-out: liquidity to pay\"]},{\"h\":\"Netting\",\"items\":[\"Offset mutual obligations\",\"Watch-out: legal enforceability\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"105","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"Modern Portfolio Theory and the Capital Asset Pricing Model","notes":"# 5. Modern Portfolio Theory and the Capital Asset Pricing Model\n\nThe quantitative core of Book 1: Markowitz diversification, the efficient frontier, CAPM assumptions and pricing, beta, and the CML vs. SML distinction. Expect calculations — portfolio variance, beta, CAPM expected return — plus conceptual CML/SML questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain modern portfolio theory (MPT) and interpret the Markowitz efficient frontier.\n- Understand the derivation and components of the CAPM and describe its underlying assumptions.\n- Interpret and compare the capital market line (CML) and the security market line (SML).\n- Apply the CAPM to calculate the expected return on an asset.\n- Interpret beta and calculate the beta of a single asset or of a portfolio.\n- (Performance measures built on the CAPM — Sharpe, Treynor, Jensen, IR, Sortino — are covered in depth in Chapter 7.)\n\n## Modern portfolio theory\n\nMarkowitz (1952): investors care only about the **mean** and **variance** of portfolio returns, and diversification reduces risk because assets are imperfectly correlated.\n\nFor a portfolio of weights $$w_i$$:\n\n$$E[R_p] = \\sum_i w_i E[R_i]$$\n\nTwo-asset variance:\n\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = w_1^2\\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\\sigma_2^2 + 2w_1 w_2 \\rho_{12}\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$\n\nwith covariance $$\\mathrm{Cov}(R_1,R_2) = \\rho_{12}\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$.\n\nKey implications:\n\n- Unless $$\\rho = +1$$, portfolio standard deviation is **less than** the weighted average of individual standard deviations — the diversification benefit.\n- As the number of assets grows, **idiosyncratic (specific) risk diversifies away**; what remains is **systematic risk**, driven by average covariance.\n- The **efficient frontier** is the set of portfolios with the highest expected return for each level of risk (the upper edge of the feasible set, from the global minimum-variance portfolio upward). Rational mean–variance investors hold only frontier portfolios.\n\nAdding a **risk-free asset** turns the frontier into a straight line: combinations of the risk-free asset and the tangency portfolio dominate everything else. With homogeneous expectations, the tangency portfolio is the **market portfolio** — this is the two-fund **separation theorem**: every investor holds the same risky portfolio (the market) and adjusts risk via the risk-free weight.\n\n## CAPM assumptions\n\n1. Investors are rational mean–variance optimizers over a single common period.\n2. No transaction costs or taxes; assets are infinitely divisible.\n3. Unlimited borrowing and lending at the risk-free rate.\n4. **Homogeneous expectations** — all investors see the same means, variances, covariances.\n5. Investors are price takers; information is freely available to all.\n\nUnrealistic — but the model's value is the testable equilibrium it produces.\n\n## The CAPM and beta\n\nIn equilibrium, only **systematic** risk is rewarded (idiosyncratic risk is diversifiable, so it commands no premium). The measure of systematic risk is beta:\n\n$$\\beta_i = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\sigma_m^2} = \\rho_{im}\\,\\frac{\\sigma_i}{\\sigma_m}$$\n\nand the pricing relation is\n\n$$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_i\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)$$\n\n- $$\\beta = 1$$: moves with the market; $$\\beta > 1$$: amplified; $$0 < \\beta < 1$$: damped; $$\\beta < 0$$: hedge asset, expected return *below* the risk-free rate.\n- The market portfolio has $$\\beta = 1$$; the risk-free asset $$\\beta = 0$$.\n- **Portfolio beta is the weighted average of component betas**: $$\\beta_p = \\sum_i w_i\\beta_i$$.\n\n## CML vs. SML\n\n| | Capital market line (CML) | Security market line (SML) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Equation | $$E[R_p] = R_f + \\dfrac{E[R_m]-R_f}{\\sigma_m}\\,\\sigma_p$$ | $$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_i\\left(E[R_m]-R_f\\right)$$ |\n| Risk axis | Total risk $$\\sigma_p$$ | Systematic risk $$\\beta_i$$ |\n| Applies to | Only **efficient** portfolios (mixes of $$R_f$$ and the market) | **All** assets and portfolios, efficient or not |\n| Slope | Market Sharpe ratio $$\\frac{E[R_m]-R_f}{\\sigma_m}$$ | Market risk premium $$E[R_m]-R_f$$ |\n\nAssets plotting **above** the SML are underpriced (expected return exceeds the CAPM-required return → positive alpha); below the SML, overpriced.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: a single stock with large diversifiable risk lies **on the SML** (if fairly priced) but **below the CML** — only efficient portfolios sit on the CML. If a question gives you total volatility $$\\sigma$$ for an individual stock and asks for CAPM expected return, you need beta, not $$\\sigma$$; using $$\\sigma$$ in the SML (or $$\\beta$$ in the CML) is the classic wrong answer.\n\n### Worked example 1 — two-asset portfolio risk and return\n\nAn investor puts 60% in equity fund A ($$E[R]=10\\%$$, $$\\sigma=20\\%$$) and 40% in bond fund B ($$E[R]=5\\%$$, $$\\sigma=8\\%$$), with correlation $$\\rho = 0.25$$.\n\nExpected return:\n\n$$E[R_p] = 0.6(10\\%) + 0.4(5\\%) = 8\\%$$\n\nVariance:\n\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = (0.6)^2(0.20)^2 + (0.4)^2(0.08)^2 + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.25)(0.20)(0.08)$$\n\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = 0.0144 + 0.001024 + 0.00192 = 0.017344$$\n\n$$\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{0.017344} \\approx 13.17\\%$$\n\nNote 13.17% < the weighted-average volatility $$0.6(20\\%)+0.4(8\\%) = 15.2\\%$$ — the diversification benefit from $$\\rho < 1$$.\n\n### Worked example 2 — beta and CAPM expected return\n\nStock X has $$\\sigma_X = 30\\%$$ and correlation 0.5 with the market; the market has $$\\sigma_m = 15\\%$$ and expected return 9%; the risk-free rate is 3%.\n\n$$\\beta_X = \\rho_{Xm}\\frac{\\sigma_X}{\\sigma_m} = 0.5 \\times \\frac{0.30}{0.15} = 1.0$$\n\n$$E[R_X] = 3\\% + 1.0\\,(9\\% - 3\\%) = 9\\%$$\n\nDespite being twice as volatile as the market, X requires only the market return — half its variance is diversifiable and earns no premium. If X is forecast to return 11%, its alpha is $$11\\% - 9\\% = +2\\%$$: it plots above the SML and is attractive.\n\n### Worked example 3 — portfolio beta\n\nA portfolio holds 50% in a stock with $$\\beta=1.4$$, 30% in a stock with $$\\beta=0.8$$, and 20% in T-bills ($$\\beta=0$$). With $$R_f = 3\\%$$ and market premium 6%:\n\n$$\\beta_p = 0.5(1.4) + 0.3(0.8) + 0.2(0) = 0.94$$\n\n$$E[R_p] = 3\\% + 0.94 \\times 6\\% = 8.64\\%$$\n\n## Limitations of the CAPM\n\n- The true market portfolio (all risky assets, incl. human capital, real estate) is unobservable — Roll's critique: the CAPM is not properly testable.\n- Empirically, the SML is too flat: low-beta stocks earn more, high-beta stocks less, than predicted; size and value effects (Ch 6) violate the single-factor view.\n- Single period, homogeneous expectations, frictionless borrowing — all counterfactual.\n\nThese gaps motivate APT and multifactor models (Chapter 6).","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is the core insight of modern portfolio theory (Markowitz)?\n> **A:** Investors care only about the mean and variance of portfolio returns, and diversification reduces risk because assets are imperfectly correlated.\n\n**Q2.** Formula for two-asset portfolio variance?\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_p^2 = w_1^2\\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\\sigma_2^2 + 2w_1 w_2 \\rho_{12}\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$ with $$\\mathrm{Cov}(R_1,R_2)=\\rho_{12}\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$.\n\n**Q3.** When does portfolio standard deviation equal the weighted average of individual volatilities?\n> **A:** Only when $$\\rho = +1$$. For any $$\\rho < 1$$, portfolio volatility is strictly less — the diversification benefit.\n\n**Q4.** What is the efficient frontier?\n> **A:** The set of portfolios with the highest expected return for each level of risk — the upper edge of the feasible set from the global minimum-variance portfolio upward.\n\n**Q5.** What is the two-fund separation theorem?\n> **A:** With a risk-free asset and homogeneous expectations, every investor holds the same risky (market) portfolio and adjusts risk only via the weight in the risk-free asset.\n\n**Q6.** Formula for beta?\n> **A:** $$\\beta_i = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\sigma_m^2} = \\rho_{im}\\,\\frac{\\sigma_i}{\\sigma_m}$$ — sensitivity to systematic (market) risk.\n\n**Q7.** The CAPM expected-return formula?\n> **A:** $$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_i\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)$$ — only systematic risk is rewarded; idiosyncratic risk is diversifiable and earns no premium.\n\n**Q8.** How is portfolio beta computed?\n> **A:** As the weighted average of component betas: $$\\beta_p = \\sum_i w_i\\beta_i$$.\n\n**Q9.** Name the CAPM assumptions.\n> **A:** Rational mean–variance optimizers over one common period; no taxes/transaction costs, divisible assets; unlimited borrowing/lending at $$R_f$$; homogeneous expectations; price-taking investors with free information.\n\n**Q10.** CML vs. SML — risk axis and applicability?\n> **A:** CML uses total risk $$\\sigma_p$$ and applies only to efficient portfolios (mixes of $$R_f$$ and the market). SML uses systematic risk $$\\beta_i$$ and applies to all assets and portfolios.\n\n**Q11.** Equations for the CML and SML?\n> **A:** CML: $$E[R_p] = R_f + \\frac{E[R_m]-R_f}{\\sigma_m}\\,\\sigma_p$$ (slope = market Sharpe). SML: $$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_i(E[R_m]-R_f)$$ (slope = market risk premium).\n\n**Q12.** What does it mean to plot above the SML?\n> **A:** The asset is underpriced — its expected return exceeds the CAPM-required return (positive alpha). Below the SML means overpriced.\n\n**Q13.** Exam trap: a fairly priced single stock with large idiosyncratic risk plots where?\n> **A:** On the SML but below the CML. Only efficient portfolios sit on the CML. Use beta (not total σ) in the SML; using σ for an individual stock's CAPM return is the classic wrong answer.\n\n**Q14.** What is Roll's critique of the CAPM?\n> **A:** The true market portfolio (all risky assets, including human capital and real estate) is unobservable, so the CAPM is not properly testable.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Two assets each have a standard deviation of 25%. An equally weighted portfolio of the two will also have a standard deviation of exactly 25% only if their correlation is:\nA) 0 B) −1 C) +1 D) 0.5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: when does combining two assets give NO diversification benefit (so the portfolio's risk equals the simple weighted average)? That happens only when the assets are perfectly positively correlated, ρ = +1. With any correlation below +1, the portfolio standard deviation drops below the weighted average — that drop is the diversification benefit.\n\nThink of two friends whose moods move in perfect lockstep (ρ = +1): averaging them gives you no smoothing. But if their moods differ, the average is steadier than either alone.\n\n- A is wrong: with ρ = 0 the portfolio risk falls well below 25% (diversification helps).\n- B is wrong: with ρ = −1 the assets offset strongly; risk falls even further (it can reach zero with the right weights).\n- D is wrong: ρ = 0.5 still lowers risk below 25% — only ρ = +1 gives no reduction.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Portfolio σ equals the weighted average of the individual σ's ONLY when ρ = +1. Any ρ < 1 produces a diversification benefit (lower portfolio σ).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Correlation and diversification benefit\",\"items\":[\"ρ = −1 (max benefit)\",\"ρ = 0\",\"|ρ = +1 (no benefit)\"],\"left\":\"σ can reach 0\",\"right\":\"σ = weighted avg\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A fairly priced individual stock that carries substantial idiosyncratic (diversifiable) risk will plot:\nA) On both the CML and the SML B) On the SML but below the CML C) Above the CML D) Below the SML\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the difference between the SML and the CML. All fairly priced assets lie on the security market line (SML), because the SML prices return against *systematic* risk (beta), which applies to every asset. But the capital market line (CML) is reached only by *efficient* portfolios (mixes of the risk-free asset and the market). A single stock carries diversifiable risk that earns no reward, so it sits below the CML.\n\nPicture the CML as a \"first-class only\" line for efficient portfolios; a lone stock with extra (uncompensated) risk doesn't qualify, so it falls short of it — yet it's still correctly priced on the SML.\n\n- A is wrong: a single stock with diversifiable risk does NOT reach the CML, so it can't be on both.\n- C is wrong: nothing fairly priced plots above the CML — the CML is the best risk-return frontier available.\n- D is wrong: below the SML means *overpriced*; the stock here is fairly priced, so it sits *on* the SML.\n\n📌 Exam tip: All fairly priced assets sit ON the SML (priced by beta). Only efficient portfolios sit ON the CML (priced by total risk σ). A single stock's diversifiable risk pushes it below the CML.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"SML vs CML\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"SML\",\"items\":[\"Risk axis = beta\",\"Prices systematic risk\",\"ALL fair assets sit on it\"]},{\"h\":\"CML\",\"items\":[\"Risk axis = total σ\",\"Efficient portfolios only\",\"Lone stock falls below it\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Stock Y has covariance with the market of 0.036 and the market standard deviation is 0.15. With a risk-free rate of 2% and expected market return of 8%, the CAPM expected return of Y is closest to:\nA) 8.0% B) 9.6% C) 11.6% D) 4.4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is a two-step CAPM calculation: first find beta, then plug into the CAPM formula.\n\nStep 1 — beta:\n$$\\beta_Y = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(R_Y, R_m)}{\\sigma_m^2} = \\frac{0.036}{0.15^2} = \\frac{0.036}{0.0225} = 1.6$$\n\nStep 2 — CAPM expected return:\n$$E[R_Y] = R_f + \\beta_Y\\,(E[R_m] - R_f) = 2\\% + 1.6\\,(8\\% - 2\\%)$$\n$$E[R_Y] = 2\\% + 1.6 \\times 6\\% = 2\\% + 9.6\\% = 11.6\\%$$\n\n- A is wrong: 8.0% just uses the market return, as if beta were 1 (it forgot to compute beta = 1.6).\n- B is wrong: 9.6% is only the risk-premium part (1.6 × 6%); it forgot to add the 2% risk-free rate.\n- D is wrong: 4.4% comes from a wrong beta — e.g., dividing 0.036 by 0.15 (= 0.24×... ) or other arithmetic slips.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Beta = Cov / σ_market² (square the market σ!). Then CAPM: R_f + β(market premium). Don't forget to add R_f back at the end.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which of the following is NOT an assumption of the CAPM?\nA) Investors have homogeneous expectations B) There is unlimited borrowing and lending at the risk-free rate C) Investors have heterogeneous, multi-period horizons D) There are no taxes or transaction costs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks you to spot the statement that contradicts CAPM's assumptions. The CAPM assumes a single common holding period and homogeneous (identical) expectations across investors. So \"heterogeneous, multi-period horizons\" is the opposite of what CAPM assumes — that is the NOT.\n\n- A is wrong (as the answer) because homogeneous expectations IS a genuine CAPM assumption.\n- B is wrong (as the answer) because unlimited risk-free borrowing and lending IS a CAPM assumption.\n- D is wrong (as the answer) because no taxes or transaction costs IS a CAPM assumption.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CAPM assumes single-period, homogeneous expectations, no taxes/costs, price-taking investors, and unlimited risk-free borrowing/lending. \"Heterogeneous, multi-period\" violates the single-period and homogeneous-expectations assumptions.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which is NOT a CAPM assumption?\",\"options\":[\"Homogeneous expectations\",\"Unlimited risk-free borrow/lend\",\"Heterogeneous multi-period horizons\",\"No taxes or transaction costs\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"CAPM assumes a single period and homogeneous expectations, not the opposite.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An investor puts 60% in fund A (E[R] = 10%) and 40% in fund B (E[R] = 5%). The expected return of the portfolio is:\nA) 7.5% B) 8.0% C) 15.0% D) 6.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a simple weighted-average expected return. Portfolio expected return is just each weight times each asset's expected return, summed:\n\n$$E[R_p] = w_A E[R_A] + w_B E[R_B] = 0.60(10\\%) + 0.40(5\\%)$$\n$$E[R_p] = 6\\% + 2\\% = 8\\%$$\n\n- A is wrong: 7.5% is the simple (unweighted) average of 10% and 5%, ignoring the 60/40 weights.\n- C is wrong: 15% just adds the two returns together — you must weight them, not sum them.\n- D is wrong: 6.5% comes from flipping the weights (0.40×10% + 0.60×5% = 4% + 3% = 7%, or another slip); the correct weighting gives 8%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected return is ALWAYS the weighted average of component returns. (Unlike variance, there's no correlation term for the mean.) Apply the weights correctly — don't take a plain average.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A portfolio holds 50% in a stock with beta 1.4, 30% in a stock with beta 0.8, and 20% in T-bills (beta 0). The portfolio beta is:\nA) 0.94 B) 1.10 C) 0.73 D) 1.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for portfolio beta, which is the weighted average of the component betas:\n\n$$\\beta_p = w_1\\beta_1 + w_2\\beta_2 + w_3\\beta_3$$\n$$\\beta_p = 0.50(1.4) + 0.30(0.8) + 0.20(0)$$\n$$\\beta_p = 0.70 + 0.24 + 0 = 0.94$$\n\n- B is wrong: 1.10 comes from mishandling the weights or ignoring that T-bills have beta 0.\n- C is wrong: 0.73 likely drops or misweights a term.\n- D is wrong: 1.40 is just the first stock's beta — it ignores the other holdings entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Portfolio beta = weighted average of component betas. T-bills (risk-free) have beta 0, so they pull the portfolio beta toward 0. The weights must sum to 1.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Stock X is twice as volatile as the market (σ_X = 30% vs. σ_m = 15%) but has a correlation of only 0.5 with the market. Its beta is:\nA) 2.0 B) 1.0 C) 0.5 D) 1.5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests that beta depends on correlation, not just volatility. Beta is computed as:\n\n$$\\beta_X = \\rho_{Xm}\\,\\frac{\\sigma_X}{\\sigma_m} = 0.5 \\times \\frac{0.30}{0.15} = 0.5 \\times 2.0 = 1.0$$\n\nSo even though X is twice as volatile as the market, half of that volatility is diversifiable (uncorrelated) and earns no premium — its beta is just 1.0.\n\n- A is wrong: 2.0 uses only the volatility ratio (σ_X/σ_m) and forgets to multiply by the correlation of 0.5.\n- C is wrong: 0.5 mistakes the correlation itself for the beta.\n- D is wrong: 1.5 is a miscalculation that doesn't follow from these numbers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: β = ρ × (σ_asset / σ_market). High total volatility does NOT mean high beta — only the part correlated with the market counts. Don't confuse σ with β.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A stock's CAPM-required return is 9%, but analysts forecast it to return 11%. According to the CAPM framework, this stock:\nA) Is overpriced and plots below the SML B) Has a positive alpha of +2% and plots above the SML C) Has a beta of 2.0 D) Lies exactly on the SML\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to interpret a forecast return that exceeds the CAPM-required return. Alpha is the forecast (expected) return minus the CAPM-required return:\n\n$$\\alpha = 11\\% - 9\\% = +2\\%$$\n\nA positive alpha means the stock offers more return than its systematic risk requires, so it is underpriced and plots above the SML — an attractive buy.\n\n- A is wrong: below the SML means a *negative* alpha (overpriced); here alpha is positive.\n- C is wrong: nothing in the numbers tells you beta is 2.0; you can't infer beta from alpha alone.\n- D is wrong: lying exactly on the SML would mean alpha = 0 (forecast equals required); here they differ by 2%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Alpha = forecast return − CAPM-required return. Positive alpha → above the SML → underpriced (buy). Negative alpha → below the SML → overpriced.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Alpha and the SML\",\"items\":[\"Below SML (α<0, overpriced)\",\"|On SML (α=0, fair)\",\"Above SML (α>0, underpriced)\"],\"left\":\"sell\",\"right\":\"buy\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** When a risk-free asset is combined with risky assets and all investors share the same (homogeneous) expectations, every investor holds the same risky portfolio — the market portfolio — and adjusts risk only via the risk-free weight. This result is known as:\nA) The Roll critique B) The two-fund separation theorem C) The efficient frontier D) Arbitrage pricing theory\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes the idea that everyone holds the same tangency (market) portfolio and only varies how much they put in the risk-free asset. That is the two-fund separation theorem: investing decisions \"separate\" into choosing the single optimal risky portfolio (the market) and then choosing the split between it and the risk-free asset.\n\nIt is like everyone ordering the same \"house special\" dish but choosing how spicy to make it — the dish is fixed, only the heat level varies.\n\n- A is wrong: the Roll critique says the true market portfolio is unobservable, so CAPM isn't properly testable — a *limitation*, not this result.\n- C is wrong: the efficient frontier is the set of best risky-only portfolios; the separation theorem is about adding the risk-free asset.\n- D is wrong: APT is a multifactor no-arbitrage theory (Chapter 6), not this two-fund result.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Adding a risk-free asset turns the frontier into a straight line; with homogeneous expectations the tangency portfolio = the market portfolio. Everyone holds the same risky mix → two-fund separation.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Everyone holds the market + risk-free split\",\"options\":[\"The Roll critique\",\"Two-fund separation theorem\",\"The efficient frontier\",\"Arbitrage pricing theory\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Choose the same risky (market) portfolio, then split with R_f = two-fund separation.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A stock with a beta of −0.4 would, under the CAPM, have an expected return that is:\nA) Above the expected market return B) Below the risk-free rate C) Exactly equal to the risk-free rate D) Negative in all market conditions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests what a negative beta implies. Plug a negative beta into the CAPM: since the market premium (E[R_m] − R_f) is positive, multiplying it by a negative beta gives a negative risk premium, pulling the expected return *below* the risk-free rate.\n\n$$E[R] = R_f + (-0.4)(E[R_m] - R_f) = R_f - 0.4(\\text{positive number}) < R_f$$\n\nA negative-beta asset is a hedge: it tends to rise when the market falls, so investors accept a low (even sub-risk-free) expected return for that valuable insurance.\n\n- A is wrong: a return above the market return would require a beta greater than 1, not negative.\n- C is wrong: equal to the risk-free rate requires beta = 0, not −0.4.\n- D is wrong: a below-risk-free *expected* return does not mean the return is negative in all conditions — the asset can still have positive returns.\n\n📌 Exam tip: β = 0 → return = R_f; β = 1 → return = market; β < 0 → return BELOW R_f (a hedge asset, valuable because it pays off when markets fall).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Beta drives CAPM expected return\",\"items\":[\"β < 0 (below R_f, hedge)\",\"|β = 0 (= R_f)\",\"β = 1 (= market)\"],\"left\":\"lower return\",\"right\":\"higher return\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Two assets each have $$\\sigma = 25\\%$$. An equally weighted portfolio of the two has $$\\sigma = 25\\%$$ only if their correlation is:\nA) 0 B) −1 C) +1 D) 0.5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Only with $$\\rho = +1$$ is portfolio volatility the weighted average; any lower correlation reduces it below 25%.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A fairly priced individual stock with substantial idiosyncratic risk will plot:\nA) On the CML and on the SML B) On the SML but below the CML C) Above the CML D) Below the SML\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** All fairly priced assets sit on the SML; only efficient portfolios reach the CML — idiosyncratic risk pushes the stock inside the frontier.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Stock Y: $$\\mathrm{Cov}(R_Y,R_m) = 0.036$$, $$\\sigma_m = 0.15$$. $$R_f = 2\\%$$, $$E[R_m] = 8\\%$$. CAPM expected return of Y?\nA) 8.0% B) 9.6% C) 11.6% D) 4.4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\beta = 0.036/0.15^2 = 0.036/0.0225 = 1.6$$; $$E[R] = 2\\% + 1.6(6\\%) = 11.6\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which is NOT a CAPM assumption?\nA) Homogeneous expectations B) Unlimited risk-free borrowing and lending C) Investors hold heterogeneous, multi-period horizons D) No taxes or transaction costs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** CAPM assumes a single common period and homogeneous expectations.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A portfolio holds 70% in the market portfolio and 30% in the risk-free asset. Its beta is:\nA) 1.00 B) 0.30 C) 0.70 D) Cannot be determined without volatilities\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\beta_p = 0.7(1.0) + 0.3(0) = 0.70$$ — portfolio beta is the weighted average of component betas; the risk-free asset has β = 0.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** Assets A and B have $$\\sigma_A = 12\\%$$, $$\\sigma_B = 20\\%$$, correlation 0.30. The standard deviation of a portfolio with 50% in each is closest to:\nA) 16.0% B) 13.1% C) 11.7% D) 9.4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_p^2 = (0.5)^2(0.12)^2 + (0.5)^2(0.20)^2 + 2(0.5)(0.5)(0.30)(0.12)(0.20)$$\n\n$$= 0.0036 + 0.0100 + 0.0036 = 0.0172 \\Rightarrow \\sigma_p = \\sqrt{0.0172} \\approx 13.1\\%$$\n\nDistractor A (16%) is the simple weighted average — correct only if ρ = 1.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Stock Q has covariance with the market of 0.027; market variance is 0.0225. With $$R_f = 3\\%$$ and an equity risk premium of 5%, the CAPM expected return of Q is closest to:\nA) 8.0% B) 9.0% C) 6.6% D) 9.6%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\beta = 0.027/0.0225 = 1.2$$; $$E[R] = 3\\% + 1.2 \\times 5\\% = 9.0\\%$$. Distractor A uses β = 1; D adds the premium to a miscomputed base.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which statement about the CML and SML is correct?\nA) The CML plots expected return against beta B) Any fairly priced single security must plot on the CML C) The slope of the CML equals the market's Sharpe ratio D) The SML applies only to efficient portfolios\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** CML slope = $$(E[R_m]-R_f)/\\sigma_m$$ = the market's Sharpe ratio. The CML uses total risk and holds only for efficient portfolios; the SML uses beta and holds for all assets.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CML vs SML\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"CML\",\"items\":[\"x-axis = total risk (sigma)\",\"efficient portfolios only\",\"slope = market Sharpe\"]},{\"h\":\"SML\",\"items\":[\"x-axis = beta\",\"ALL assets\",\"prices systematic risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q19.** An investor combines 70% in equity fund A ($$E[R]=10\\%$$, $$\\sigma=20\\%$$) and 30% in bond fund B ($$E[R]=6\\%$$, $$\\sigma=10\\%$$). The two funds' returns are statistically independent ($$\\rho=0$$). What are the portfolio's expected return and standard deviation, and how does the standard deviation compare with the weighted-average volatility?\nA) $$E[R_p]=8.8\\%$$, $$\\sigma_p=14.32\\%$$, which is below the weighted-average volatility of 17% B) $$E[R_p]=8.8\\%$$, $$\\sigma_p=17.00\\%$$, equal to the weighted-average volatility C) $$E[R_p]=8.0\\%$$, $$\\sigma_p=14.32\\%$$, below the weighted-average volatility of 15% D) $$E[R_p]=8.8\\%$$, $$\\sigma_p=15.20\\%$$, below the weighted-average volatility of 17%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for two-asset portfolio return and risk with zero correlation, and the diversification comparison.\n\n1. Expected return is the weighted average:\n$$E[R_p] = 0.7(10\\%) + 0.3(6\\%) = 7\\% + 1.8\\% = 8.8\\%$$\n2. Variance with $$\\rho=0$$ drops the cross term:\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = (0.7)^2(0.20)^2 + (0.3)^2(0.10)^2 + 2(0.7)(0.3)(0)(0.20)(0.10)$$\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = 0.0196 + 0.0009 + 0 = 0.0205$$\n3. Standard deviation:\n$$\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{0.0205} \\approx 14.32\\%$$\n4. Weighted-average volatility:\n$$0.7(20\\%) + 0.3(10\\%) = 14\\% + 3\\% = 17\\%$$\nSince $$\\rho<1$$, $$\\sigma_p = 14.32\\% < 17\\%$$ — the diversification benefit. That is A.\n\n- B forgets the diversification effect and sets $$\\sigma_p$$ equal to the 17% weighted average (only true if $$\\rho=+1$$).\n- C uses the right $$\\sigma_p$$ but mis-computes $$E[R_p]$$ as 8.0% (it averaged 10 and 6 equally instead of weighting 70/30) and mislabels the weighted-average volatility as 15%.\n- D keeps the cross term as if $$\\rho>0$$, getting 15.20% instead of 14.32%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: when $$\\rho=0$$, the covariance term vanishes — but variance still adds (it is *not* the weighted average of the individual variances or sigmas). The weighted average of sigmas (17%) is the *no-diversification* benchmark you only hit when $$\\rho=+1$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Stock M has a covariance with the market of 0.048 and the market variance is 0.04 ($$\\sigma_m=20\\%$$). The risk-free rate is 2.5% and the market's expected return is 8.5%. An analyst forecasts that M will return 9.0% next year. What is M's CAPM required return, and is the stock attractive?\nA) Required return 9.7%; the stock is unattractive (negative alpha of −0.7%) B) Required return 8.5%; the stock is attractive (positive alpha of +0.5%) C) Required return 9.7%; the stock is attractive (positive alpha of +0.7%) D) Required return 7.2%; the stock is attractive (positive alpha of +1.8%)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the CAPM required return and the resulting alpha sign.\n\n1. Compute beta from covariance and market variance:\n$$\\beta_M = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(R_M,R_m)}{\\sigma_m^2} = \\frac{0.048}{0.04} = 1.2$$\n2. CAPM required return:\n$$E[R_M] = R_f + \\beta_M(E[R_m]-R_f) = 2.5\\% + 1.2(8.5\\% - 2.5\\%)$$\n$$= 2.5\\% + 1.2(6.0\\%) = 2.5\\% + 7.2\\% = 9.7\\%$$\n3. Alpha = forecast − required = $$9.0\\% - 9.7\\% = -0.7\\%$$. The forecast falls short of the required return → **negative alpha → unattractive** (plots below the SML). That is A.\n\n- B uses beta = 1.0 (it divided covariance by $$\\sigma_m$$ = 0.20 instead of $$\\sigma_m^2$$ = 0.04, or just assumed the market beta), giving 8.5% and a false +0.5% alpha.\n- C uses the right 9.7% but flips the alpha sign, calling −0.7% a +0.7% (a sign flip — the trap).\n- D forgets to add back $$R_f$$ inside the premium or uses 7.2% (the risk premium term alone) as the required return, then computes a spurious +1.8% alpha.\n\n📌 Exam tip: beta = covariance ÷ market **variance** ($$\\sigma_m^2$$), not ÷ $$\\sigma_m$$ — dividing by the standard deviation is a classic error that yields beta = 1.2 vs. the wrong 1.0×scale. Alpha = forecast − required; a *negative* alpha means the stock is overpriced/unattractive.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A portfolio is allocated 40% to a stock with $$\\beta=1.5$$, 25% to a stock with $$\\beta=0.7$$, and 35% to Treasury bills. The risk-free rate is 3% and the market risk premium is 5.5%. What is the portfolio's CAPM expected return?\nA) 8.05% B) 7.26% C) 4.26% D) 10.50%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the portfolio's expected return via portfolio beta and the SML.\n\n1. Portfolio beta is the weighted average of component betas (T-bills have $$\\beta=0$$):\n$$\\beta_p = 0.40(1.5) + 0.25(0.7) + 0.35(0) = 0.60 + 0.175 + 0 = 0.775$$\n2. Apply CAPM with the market premium of 5.5%:\n$$E[R_p] = R_f + \\beta_p \\times (\\text{premium}) = 3\\% + 0.775(5.5\\%)$$\n$$= 3\\% + 4.2625\\% = 7.2625\\% \\approx 7.26\\%$$\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A (8.05%) ignores the T-bill weight when computing beta — it averages only the two stocks' betas as if they were 100% of the portfolio ($$0.4/0.65$$-type rescaling or treating weights as summing without cash), inflating beta.\n- C (4.26%) forgets to add the risk-free rate ($$0.775 \\times 5.5\\% = 4.26\\%$$ is just the premium term).\n- D (10.50%) uses the highest beta (1.5) for the whole portfolio rather than the weighted average.\n\n📌 Exam tip: include the risk-free/T-bill sleeve at $$\\beta=0$$ when forming $$\\beta_p$$ — it *drags beta down*, it does not disappear. Then never forget to add $$R_f$$ back: $$E[R_p]=R_f+\\beta_p\\times\\text{premium}$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Stock X has a total volatility of 40% and a correlation of 0.40 with the market. The market has volatility 16% and expected return 10%; the risk-free rate is 3%. A junior analyst computes X's CAPM expected return as 21% by plugging X's 40% volatility directly into the capital market line. What is X's *correct* CAPM expected return, and what mistake did the analyst make?\nA) 17.5%; the analyst should have used the SML with beta, but used the right line B) 10.0%; the analyst used total volatility in the CML instead of computing beta and using the SML C) 21.0%; the analyst's computation is correct D) 7.0%; the analyst forgot the risk-free rate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the correct CAPM expected return and to identify the analyst's error.\n\n1. The CAPM/SML prices only *systematic* risk, so first compute beta — not total volatility:\n$$\\beta_X = \\rho_{Xm}\\frac{\\sigma_X}{\\sigma_m} = 0.40 \\times \\frac{0.40}{0.16} = 0.40 \\times 2.5 = 1.0$$\n2. Apply the SML:\n$$E[R_X] = R_f + \\beta_X(E[R_m]-R_f) = 3\\% + 1.0(10\\% - 3\\%) = 3\\% + 7\\% = 10\\%$$\n3. The analyst's error: he treated the stock's *total* volatility (40%) as if it belonged on the **capital market line**, which applies only to *efficient* portfolios and uses total risk. For an individual stock you need **beta** on the **SML**; most of X's 40% volatility is diversifiable and earns no premium.\n\nSo the correct answer is 10% with the diagnosis in B.\n\n- A reports 17.5% (it used some partial beta or mixed inputs) and wrongly says the analyst used the right line.\n- C endorses the 21% error (using $$\\sigma$$ in the CML for a single stock).\n- D gives 7%, dropping $$R_f$$ — a different error, not the one made.\n\n📌 Exam tip: for an *individual asset*, CAPM uses **beta** on the **SML**. Plugging an individual stock's total $$\\sigma$$ into the CML is the signature trap (a single stock with idiosyncratic risk sits *below* the CML). Beta $$= \\rho\\,\\sigma_i/\\sigma_m$$ — high total volatility with low correlation can still give beta = 1.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Single stock: SML not CML\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Correct: SML\",\"items\":[\"Use beta (β=ρσ/σm)\",\"Prices systematic risk\",\"β=1 → return=market\"]},{\"h\":\"Analyst's error: CML\",\"items\":[\"Used total σ (40%)\",\"CML = efficient ports only\",\"Lone stock sits below CML\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Two stocks each have a standard deviation of 30%. An equally weighted portfolio of the two is observed to also have a standard deviation of exactly 30%. What does this imply about the correlation between the two stocks, and what would happen to portfolio volatility if the correlation were instead −1?\nA) The correlation is +1; at $$\\rho=-1$$ an equally weighted portfolio would have 0% volatility B) The correlation is 0; at $$\\rho=-1$$ volatility would rise to 42.4% C) The correlation is +1; at $$\\rho=-1$$ volatility would rise to 60% D) The correlation is −1; at $$\\rho=+1$$ volatility would be 0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks what correlation makes an equally weighted, equal-sigma portfolio's volatility equal the individual sigma, and what $$\\rho=-1$$ would do.\n\n1. Equal weights $$w_1=w_2=0.5$$, equal $$\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2=30\\%$$. Portfolio variance:\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = 0.25(0.09) + 0.25(0.09) + 2(0.5)(0.5)\\rho(0.30)(0.30)$$\n$$= 0.0225 + 0.0225 + 0.045\\rho = 0.045 + 0.045\\rho$$\n2. Set $$\\sigma_p = 30\\%$$ so $$\\sigma_p^2 = 0.09$$:\n$$0.045 + 0.045\\rho = 0.09 \\Rightarrow 0.045\\rho = 0.045 \\Rightarrow \\rho = +1$$\nOnly at $$\\rho=+1$$ does the portfolio sigma equal the (common) individual sigma — there is no diversification benefit.\n3. At $$\\rho=-1$$: $$\\sigma_p^2 = 0.045 + 0.045(-1) = 0$$, so $$\\sigma_p = 0\\%$$ — equal weights of two perfectly negatively correlated, equal-volatility assets fully cancel.\n\nThat is A.\n\n- B says $$\\rho=0$$ (would give $$\\sqrt{0.045}=21.2\\%$$, not 30%) and absurdly claims $$\\rho=-1$$ *raises* volatility.\n- C correctly identifies $$\\rho=+1$$ but claims $$\\rho=-1$$ raises volatility to 60% — negative correlation reduces, never amplifies, risk.\n- D inverts the correlations entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: with equal weights and equal sigmas, $$\\rho=+1$$ is the *only* case where the portfolio sigma equals the individual sigma (no diversification), and $$\\rho=-1$$ drives it to *zero*. Lower correlation always reduces portfolio risk; it can never increase it above the weighted average.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Equal-weight portfolio σ vs correlation\",\"items\":[\"ρ=−1 → σ=0\",\"ρ=0 → 21.2%\",\"|ρ=+1 → 30% (no benefit)\"],\"left\":\"lowest risk\",\"right\":\"no diversification\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** An investor wants an expected return of 11% by holding a combination of the risk-free asset (3%) and the market portfolio ($$E[R_m]=9\\%$$, $$\\sigma_m=15\\%$$) along the capital market line. What portfolio standard deviation must the investor accept, and what does the required market weight imply?\nA) $$\\sigma_p=20\\%$$, achieved by borrowing at the risk-free rate to hold 133.3% in the market B) $$\\sigma_p=15\\%$$, achieved by holding 100% in the market C) $$\\sigma_p=11\\%$$, achieved by holding 73.3% in the market D) $$\\sigma_p=18.3\\%$$, achieved by holding 122% in the market\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the CML standard deviation needed to reach an 11% target return, and what weight that implies.\n\n1. On the CML, expected return is a linear blend of $$R_f$$ and the market: $$E[R_p]=R_f + w(E[R_m]-R_f)$$ where $$w$$ is the weight in the market. Solve for $$w$$:\n$$11\\% = 3\\% + w(9\\% - 3\\%) \\Rightarrow 8\\% = w(6\\%) \\Rightarrow w = \\frac{8}{6} = 1.333$$\n2. $$w=133.3\\% > 100\\%$$ means the investor **borrows** at the risk-free rate (weight $$-33.3\\%$$ in the risk-free asset) to lever up the market position.\n3. Since the risk-free asset has zero volatility, portfolio volatility scales with the market weight:\n$$\\sigma_p = w\\,\\sigma_m = 1.333 \\times 15\\% = 20\\%$$\n\nThat is A.\n\n- B assumes 100% market (which only gives the 9% market return, not 11%).\n- C sets $$\\sigma_p$$ equal to the target return number (11%) — confusing return with risk — and uses a weight below 100%.\n- D mis-solves the weight (122%) and the resulting sigma.\n\n📌 Exam tip: on the CML, the return target sets the *market weight* $$w=(E[R_p]-R_f)/(E[R_m]-R_f)$$; a target above the market return forces $$w>1$$ = leverage (borrow at $$R_f$$). Then $$\\sigma_p=w\\,\\sigma_m$$ because the risk-free leg adds no volatility. Don't equate the target return with the standard deviation.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which of the following statements about the CML, the SML, and the underlying CAPM assumptions is correct?\nA) The slope of the SML is the market Sharpe ratio, while the slope of the CML is the market risk premium B) The CML applies to all assets and portfolios, while the SML applies only to efficient portfolios C) A fairly priced individual stock with large idiosyncratic risk plots on the SML but below the CML; the CAPM does not assume investors have heterogeneous multi-period horizons D) Under the CAPM, a stock with $$\\beta<0$$ must have an expected return above the risk-free rate because all risk is rewarded\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the CML/SML distinction and CAPM assumptions.\n\n1. All *fairly priced* assets lie on the **SML** (which uses systematic risk, beta). Only *efficient* portfolios (mixes of $$R_f$$ and the market) lie on the **CML** (which uses total risk). An individual stock with large idiosyncratic risk is fairly priced on the SML but sits *inside* the frontier, hence *below* the CML.\n2. The CAPM assumes a **single common period** and **homogeneous expectations** — it does *not* assume heterogeneous, multi-period horizons.\nBoth clauses of C are correct, so C is the answer.\n\n- A swaps the slopes: the **CML** slope is the market Sharpe ratio $$(E[R_m]-R_f)/\\sigma_m$$; the **SML** slope is the market risk premium $$(E[R_m]-R_f)$$.\n- B reverses the domains: the **SML** applies to all assets/portfolios; the **CML** applies only to efficient portfolios.\n- D is false: a stock with $$\\beta<0$$ has expected return *below* $$R_f$$ (a hedge asset). And only *systematic* risk is rewarded, not \"all risk.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: SML = beta axis, slope = market risk premium, applies to *everything*; CML = total-risk axis, slope = market Sharpe ratio, applies to *efficient portfolios only*. $$\\beta<0$$ → expected return *below* $$R_f$$. CAPM assumes single-period, homogeneous expectations.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"SML vs CML\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"SML\",\"items\":[\"Axis = beta\",\"Slope = market risk premium\",\"Applies to ALL assets\"]},{\"h\":\"CML\",\"items\":[\"Axis = total σ\",\"Slope = market Sharpe ratio\",\"Efficient portfolios only\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"106","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"The Arbitrage Pricing Theory and Multifactor Models of Risk and Return","notes":"# 6. The Arbitrage Pricing Theory and Multifactor Models of Risk and Return\n\nCAPM's one factor becomes many: APT's no-arbitrage logic, single- and multifactor return models, factor hedging, and Fama–French. Expect expected-return calculations from factor betas and conceptual APT-vs-CAPM comparisons.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the arbitrage pricing theory (APT), describe its assumptions, and compare APT to the CAPM.\n- Describe the inputs (including factor betas) to a multifactor model.\n- Calculate the expected return of an asset using a single-factor and a multifactor model.\n- Explain how to construct a portfolio to hedge exposure to multiple factors, and describe the challenges of using multifactor models in hedging.\n- Describe and apply the Fama–French three-factor model in estimating asset returns.\n\n## The return-generating model\n\nA $$K$$-factor model decomposes the realized return of asset $$i$$:\n\n$$R_i = E[R_i] + \\beta_{i1}F_1 + \\beta_{i2}F_2 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}F_K + e_i$$\n\n- $$E[R_i]$$ — expected return (compensation priced ex ante).\n- $$F_k$$ — the **surprise** in factor $$k$$: actual value minus expected value. By construction $$E[F_k]=0$$.\n- $$\\beta_{ik}$$ — the factor beta/loading: sensitivity of asset $$i$$ to factor $$k$$.\n- $$e_i$$ — firm-specific (idiosyncratic) return, uncorrelated across assets and with the factors.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: only the **unanticipated** component of a macro announcement moves returns in these models. If expected inflation was 2% and actual inflation is 2%, the inflation factor surprise is **zero** — no effect, however \"high\" 2% sounds. Always compute surprise = actual − expected before multiplying by beta.\n\n## Arbitrage pricing theory\n\n**Assumptions** (much weaker than CAPM's):\n\n1. Returns are generated by a factor model.\n2. There are enough assets to diversify away idiosyncratic risk.\n3. Well-functioning markets allow no persistent arbitrage opportunities.\n\n**Conclusion:** expected returns must be linear in factor betas, or arbitrageurs would exploit the gap:\n\n$$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_{i1}\\lambda_1 + \\beta_{i2}\\lambda_2 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}\\lambda_K$$\n\nwhere $$\\lambda_k$$ is the **risk premium** of factor $$k$$ (the excess return of a pure factor portfolio with $$\\beta_k = 1$$ and zero loading on all other factors).\n\n**APT vs. CAPM**\n\n| | CAPM | APT |\n|---|---|---|\n| Logic | Equilibrium: all investors optimize mean–variance | No-arbitrage: a few arbitrageurs enforce pricing |\n| Factors | One — the market portfolio | Multiple — unspecified by the theory |\n| Key assumptions | Homogeneous expectations, mean–variance investors, market portfolio | Factor structure, diversification, no arbitrage |\n| Market portfolio needed? | Yes (unobservable — Roll critique) | No |\n| Guidance on factors | Defines the single factor | Silent — both strength (flexibility) and weakness |\n| Holds for | Every asset exactly | Well-diversified portfolios; near-exactly for most single assets |\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> APT does **not** assume homogeneous expectations or that everyone holds the market — it only needs *some* investors who will arbitrage mispricing in well-diversified portfolios. The price of this generality: APT does not tell you what the factors are.\n\n## Inputs and challenges\n\nInputs to a multifactor model: the chosen **factors** (macro surprises like industrial production, inflation, term spread, credit spread; or style factors like size and value), each asset's **factor betas** (estimated by regression), the **factor risk premiums**, and the **idiosyncratic variance**.\n\nChallenges, especially for hedging:\n- Betas are estimated with error and **drift over time**.\n- Factor structure may be misspecified (omitted factors show up as \"alpha\" or unhedged risk).\n- Hedging many factors requires many positions — transaction costs and shorting constraints bite.\n- Correlations among factor proxies complicate clean isolation of one exposure.\n\n## Hedging factor exposures\n\nA portfolio's factor exposure is the weighted average of component betas. To hedge factor $$k$$, add positions (often factor portfolios or futures) so the **net beta to that factor is zero**, while leaving desired exposures intact. A **factor portfolio** (pure factor play) has beta 1 to one factor and 0 to all others — the natural hedging building block. Hedging all factor exposures leaves only idiosyncratic risk plus alpha (a \"market-neutral\" stance); idiosyncratic risk is then reduced by holding many names, not by hedging.\n\n### Worked example 1 — two-factor expected return (APT)\n\nA stock has betas of 1.2 on the GDP factor and −0.5 on the inflation factor. Factor risk premiums: $$\\lambda_{GDP} = 4\\%$$, $$\\lambda_{INF} = 2\\%$$. Risk-free rate 3%.\n\n$$E[R] = 3\\% + 1.2(4\\%) + (-0.5)(2\\%) = 3\\% + 4.8\\% - 1.0\\% = 6.8\\%$$\n\nNow suppose over the next year GDP growth comes in 1% **above** expectations and inflation 0.5% **below** expectations, with no firm-specific news. Realized return:\n\n$$R = 6.8\\% + 1.2(+1\\%) + (-0.5)(-0.5\\%) = 6.8\\% + 1.2\\% + 0.25\\% = 8.25\\%$$\n\n### Worked example 2 — hedging a factor exposure\n\nA $100 million portfolio has $$\\beta_{GDP} = 0.8$$ and $$\\beta_{INF} = 0.4$$. The manager wants zero GDP exposure but wishes to keep the inflation exposure. Available: a pure GDP factor portfolio ($$\\beta_{GDP}=1, \\beta_{INF}=0$$).\n\nRequired hedge: short the GDP factor portfolio in size $$0.8 \\times \\$100\\text{m} = \\$80\\text{m}$$. Net GDP beta: $$0.8 - 0.8 = 0$$; inflation beta unchanged at 0.4. If instead she wanted both factors at zero, she would also short $$0.4 \\times \\$100\\text{m} = \\$40\\text{m}$$ of a pure inflation factor portfolio, leaving only alpha and idiosyncratic risk.\n\n### Worked example 3 — Fama–French\n\nFund Z has loadings $$\\beta_m = 1.1$$, $$\\beta_{SMB} = 0.6$$, $$\\beta_{HML} = -0.3$$. Premiums: market 6%, SMB 2.5%, HML 3.5%; $$R_f = 2\\%$$.\n\n$$E[R_Z] = 2\\% + 1.1(6\\%) + 0.6(2.5\\%) + (-0.3)(3.5\\%) = 2\\% + 6.6\\% + 1.5\\% - 1.05\\% = 9.05\\%$$\n\nThe negative HML loading marks Z as growth-tilted; its small-cap tilt (positive SMB) adds expected return.\n\n## The Fama–French three-factor model\n\n$$E[R_i] - R_f = \\beta_{i,m}\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right) + \\beta_{i,SMB}\\,E[SMB] + \\beta_{i,HML}\\,E[HML]$$\n\n- **SMB** (\"small minus big\"): return of small-cap stocks minus large-cap stocks — the **size** premium.\n- **HML** (\"high minus low\"): return of high book-to-market (**value**) stocks minus low book-to-market (**growth**) stocks — the **value** premium.\n- Motivation: empirically, small and value stocks earned more than CAPM predicts; FF captures this with two long–short style factors. A momentum factor (UMD/WML, Carhart) is a common fourth.\n- Interpretation debate: risk premia for distress-type risk vs. behavioral mispricing — the model is agnostic.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Sign conventions matter: a **positive HML beta = value tilt**, negative = growth tilt; positive SMB beta = small-cap tilt. GARP questions often just test whether you can read tilts off the loadings and add up the premium terms correctly (watch the negative loading!).","flashcards":"**Q1.** What does a K-factor return-generating model look like?\n> **A:** $$R_i = E[R_i] + \\beta_{i1}F_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}F_K + e_i$$ where $$F_k$$ is the factor surprise (actual − expected, with $$E[F_k]=0$$), $$\\beta_{ik}$$ the loading, and $$e_i$$ idiosyncratic return.\n\n**Q2.** Exam trap: what part of a macro announcement moves returns in a factor model?\n> **A:** Only the unanticipated component (surprise = actual − expected). If expected inflation is 2% and actual is 2%, the surprise is zero — no effect, however \"high\" 2% sounds.\n\n**Q3.** What are the three APT assumptions?\n> **A:** (1) Returns follow a factor model, (2) enough assets to diversify away idiosyncratic risk, (3) well-functioning markets allow no persistent arbitrage.\n\n**Q4.** The APT pricing equation?\n> **A:** $$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_{i1}\\lambda_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}\\lambda_K$$ where $$\\lambda_k$$ is the risk premium of factor $$k$$ (excess return of a pure factor portfolio with $$\\beta_k=1$$, zero on all others).\n\n**Q5.** APT vs. CAPM — logic and factors?\n> **A:** CAPM rests on equilibrium/mean–variance optimization with one factor (the market). APT rests on no-arbitrage with multiple, unspecified factors and does not require the market portfolio or homogeneous expectations.\n\n**Q6.** What does APT NOT require that CAPM does?\n> **A:** APT does not require homogeneous expectations, that everyone holds the market portfolio, or an observable market portfolio. It only needs some arbitrageurs to enforce pricing in well-diversified portfolios.\n\n**Q7.** What is the main weakness of APT?\n> **A:** It is silent on what the factors actually are — its generality is also its weakness.\n\n**Q8.** What is a pure factor portfolio, and why is it the hedging building block?\n> **A:** A portfolio with beta 1 to one factor and 0 to all others. To hedge factor k, add positions so the net beta to that factor is zero while leaving desired exposures intact.\n\n**Q9.** Name challenges of using multifactor models for hedging.\n> **A:** Betas are estimated with error and drift over time; factor structure may be misspecified; hedging many factors needs many positions (costs, shorting limits); factor proxies are correlated.\n\n**Q10.** The Fama–French three-factor model?\n> **A:** $$E[R_i] - R_f = \\beta_{i,m}(E[R_m]-R_f) + \\beta_{i,SMB}E[SMB] + \\beta_{i,HML}E[HML]$$.\n\n**Q11.** What are SMB and HML?\n> **A:** SMB (\"small minus big\") = small-cap minus large-cap return, the size premium. HML (\"high minus low\") = high book-to-market (value) minus low (growth), the value premium.\n\n**Q12.** How do you read tilts from FF loadings?\n> **A:** Positive SMB beta = small-cap tilt; positive HML beta = value tilt; negative HML beta = growth tilt. Watch the sign of negative loadings when summing premium terms.\n\n**Q13.** What fourth factor is commonly added to Fama–French?\n> **A:** A momentum factor (UMD/WML, the Carhart model).\n\n**Q14.** Compute APT expected return: $$\\beta_1=0.9$$ (premium 5%), $$\\beta_2=0.2$$ (premium 1%), $$R_f=4\\%$$.\n> **A:** $$E[R] = 4\\% + 0.9(5\\%) + 0.2(1\\%) = 4\\% + 4.5\\% + 0.2\\% = 8.7\\%$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Relative to the CAPM, the arbitrage pricing theory (APT) does NOT require:\nA) A linear relation between expected return and factor betas B) That all investors hold the market portfolio under homogeneous expectations C) The absence of arbitrage opportunities D) A factor structure for returns\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what APT manages to *avoid* assuming. APT is built on no-arbitrage logic, not equilibrium: it only needs a factor structure, enough assets to diversify, and the absence of arbitrage. It does NOT require everyone to hold the market portfolio or to share homogeneous expectations — those are CAPM's heavy assumptions.\n\nThink of APT like a referee enforcing \"no free lunches\": you only need a few sharp traders to pounce on mispricing, not every investor behaving identically.\n\n- A is wrong (as the answer): APT *does* require linear pricing in factor betas — that's its conclusion.\n- C is wrong (as the answer): no-arbitrage IS the central APT assumption.\n- D is wrong (as the answer): a factor structure for returns IS required by APT.\n\n📌 Exam tip: APT drops CAPM's market-portfolio and homogeneous-expectations machinery. It keeps: factor structure, diversification, no arbitrage, and linear pricing. The price of this generality is that APT won't tell you *what* the factors are.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"APT vs CAPM assumptions\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"APT needs\",\"items\":[\"Factor structure\",\"No arbitrage\",\"Linear pricing\"]},{\"h\":\"APT drops (CAPM needs)\",\"items\":[\"Market portfolio\",\"Homogeneous expectations\",\"Names the factor\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Expected inflation was 3%, and actual inflation also prints 3%. A stock has an inflation factor beta of −1.2. Under a factor model, the effect of this inflation announcement on the stock's return is:\nA) −3.6% B) +3.6% C) 0% D) −1.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the most important idea in factor models: only the *surprise* (unexpected) part of a factor moves returns. The factor value is the surprise = actual − expected:\n\n$$F_{INF} = \\text{actual} - \\text{expected} = 3\\% - 3\\% = 0\\%$$\n\nThen the effect = beta × surprise = −1.2 × 0% = 0%. Since inflation came in exactly as expected, there is no surprise, so no impact — no matter how big the beta is.\n\nIt is like a weather forecast: if rain was predicted and it rains, no one is surprised, so no one changes plans. Only *unexpected* weather shifts behavior.\n\n- A is wrong: −3.6% = −1.2 × 3%, the trap of multiplying beta by the *actual level* (3%) instead of the surprise (0%).\n- B is wrong: +3.6% makes the same level-vs-surprise error and also flips the sign.\n- D is wrong: −1.2% just reports the beta as if it were the return effect.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always compute surprise = actual − expected BEFORE multiplying by beta. Expected = actual ⇒ surprise = 0 ⇒ zero effect, regardless of how large the level \"sounds.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Only the surprise moves returns\",\"steps\":[\"Surprise = actual − expected\",\"Here 3% − 3% = 0\",\"Effect = beta × surprise\",\"Effect = 0%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A stock has a beta of 0.9 on factor 1 (premium 5%) and 0.2 on factor 2 (premium 1%). With a risk-free rate of 4%, the APT expected return is:\nA) 8.7% B) 4.7% C) 9.5% D) 10.7%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question is a direct multifactor expected-return calculation. The APT expected return is the risk-free rate plus the sum of (each beta × its factor premium):\n\n$$E[R] = R_f + \\beta_1\\lambda_1 + \\beta_2\\lambda_2$$\n$$E[R] = 4\\% + 0.9(5\\%) + 0.2(1\\%)$$\n$$E[R] = 4\\% + 4.5\\% + 0.2\\% = 8.7\\%$$\n\n- B is wrong: 4.7% forgets the factor-1 term (0.9 × 5% = 4.5%) almost entirely.\n- C is wrong: 9.5% comes from an arithmetic slip in adding the premium contributions.\n- D is wrong: 10.7% over-adds (e.g., using a wrong beta or premium) beyond the correct 8.7%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: APT return = R_f + Σ(βₖ × λₖ). Multiply each factor beta by its own premium, sum them, then add R_f. Keep each factor's beta paired with its correct premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In the Fama–French three-factor model, a portfolio with a significantly negative HML loading and a near-zero SMB loading is best described as tilted toward:\nA) Small-cap value B) Large-cap value C) Growth D) High-dividend stocks\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks you to read style tilts off Fama–French loadings. HML (\"high minus low\") is the value premium: a *positive* HML beta means a value tilt, while a *negative* HML beta means a growth tilt. A near-zero SMB (\"small minus big\") beta says nothing strong about size. So negative HML + ~zero SMB = growth tilt.\n\n- A is wrong: small-cap value would need a *positive* SMB and a *positive* HML — the opposite of what's given.\n- B is wrong: large-cap value would need a *positive* HML (value), but here HML is negative.\n- D is wrong: high-dividend is not what HML/SMB measure; nothing here points to a dividend tilt.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Positive HML = value tilt; negative HML = growth tilt. Positive SMB = small-cap tilt; negative SMB = large-cap tilt. Read the signs carefully — they flip the interpretation.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Fama-French style from loadings\",\"x\":\"HML → (− growth, + value)\",\"y\":\"SMB → (− large, + small)\",\"q\":[\"Large growth\",\"Large value\",\"Small growth\",\"Small value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A fund has Fama–French loadings of βₘ = 1.1, β(SMB) = 0.6, β(HML) = −0.3. With premiums of market 6%, SMB 2.5%, HML 3.5%, and R_f = 2%, the expected return is closest to:\nA) 9.05% B) 11.15% C) 7.05% D) 10.10%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question is a Fama–French expected-return calculation. Add R_f plus each loading times its premium, being careful with the negative HML term:\n\n$$E[R] = R_f + \\beta_m(E[R_m]-R_f) + \\beta_{SMB}E[SMB] + \\beta_{HML}E[HML]$$\n$$E[R] = 2\\% + 1.1(6\\%) + 0.6(2.5\\%) + (-0.3)(3.5\\%)$$\n$$E[R] = 2\\% + 6.6\\% + 1.5\\% - 1.05\\%$$\n$$E[R] = 9.05\\%$$\n\n- B is wrong: 11.15% adds the HML term instead of subtracting it (treating −0.3 as +0.3): the classic negative-loading trap.\n- C is wrong: 7.05% drops or mis-signs one of the positive terms.\n- D is wrong: 10.10% comes from an arithmetic slip in combining the terms.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In Fama–French, a NEGATIVE loading SUBTRACTS from expected return. The most common mistake is flipping the sign of the HML (or SMB) term — watch it closely.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In a multifactor return model $$R_i = E[R_i] + \\beta_{i1}F_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}F_K + e_i$$, the term $$e_i$$ represents:\nA) The expected return priced ex ante B) The systematic factor surprise C) The firm-specific (idiosyncratic) return, uncorrelated across assets D) The factor risk premium\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks you to identify the error/residual term in a factor model. The $$e_i$$ is the firm-specific (idiosyncratic) return — the part of the return driven by company-specific news, uncorrelated across assets and with the factors. In a well-diversified portfolio it largely washes out.\n\nThink of it as the \"personal news\" affecting one company (a CEO resignation), separate from economy-wide news (the factors).\n\n- A is wrong: the expected return priced ex ante is $$E[R_i]$$, the first term, not $$e_i$$.\n- B is wrong: the systematic factor surprise is $$F_k$$ (with beta loading), not the residual.\n- D is wrong: the factor risk premium is $$\\lambda_k$$, which appears in the *pricing* equation, not as the residual in the return-generating equation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In the return-generating model: $$E[R_i]$$ = ex-ante expected return; $$F_k$$ = factor surprise (E[F]=0); $$\\beta_{ik}$$ = loading; $$e_i$$ = idiosyncratic, diversifiable residual.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What is e_i in the factor model?\",\"options\":[\"Ex-ante expected return\",\"Systematic factor surprise\",\"Firm-specific (idiosyncratic) return\",\"Factor risk premium\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"e_i is the firm-specific residual, uncorrelated across assets, diversifiable.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A $100 million portfolio has a GDP-factor beta of 0.8. To fully hedge the GDP exposure using a pure GDP factor portfolio (which has GDP beta of 1 and zero loading on other factors), the manager should:\nA) Buy $80 million of the GDP factor portfolio B) Short $80 million of the GDP factor portfolio C) Short $100 million of the GDP factor portfolio D) Buy $20 million of the GDP factor portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to zero out a single factor exposure. The portfolio's dollar GDP exposure is its beta times its value: 0.8 × $100m = $80m of GDP exposure. To cancel it, take an *offsetting* (short) position of the same size in the pure GDP factor portfolio:\n\n$$\\text{Hedge} = 0.8 \\times \\$100\\text{m} = \\$80\\text{m short}$$\n\nNet GDP beta after hedging: 0.8 − 0.8 = 0. Because the pure factor portfolio has zero loading on other factors, any other exposures stay untouched.\n\n- A is wrong: buying (going long) would *add* to the GDP exposure, raising net beta to 1.6, not zero.\n- C is wrong: shorting $100m would over-hedge (net beta = 0.8 − 1.0 = −0.2), flipping the exposure negative.\n- D is wrong: $20m is unrelated to the 0.8 × $100m calculation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To hedge a factor, short (β × portfolio value) of a *pure* factor portfolio so net beta = 0. A pure factor portfolio (β = 1 on one factor, 0 elsewhere) lets you hedge one factor without disturbing the others.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging a factor exposure\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Positive beta (+0.8)\",\"items\":[\"SHORT the factor port\",\"Size = β × value\",\"= $80m short → net 0\"]},{\"h\":\"Negative beta\",\"items\":[\"GO LONG the factor port\",\"Size = |β| × value\",\"Opposite sign cancels\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which of the following is a genuine challenge of using multifactor models for hedging?\nA) Factor betas are known exactly and never change over time B) Factor betas are estimated with error and tend to drift over time C) Hedging multiple factors requires only a single position D) Idiosyncratic risk is automatically removed by hedging the factors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a real-world limitation of multifactor hedging. Factor betas come from regressions, so they are estimated with error, and they drift as a firm's business and the economy change — making a hedge that fits today imperfect tomorrow.\n\nIt is like tailoring a suit to someone whose measurements keep changing: it fits at first but gradually goes off.\n\n- A is wrong: it states the opposite — betas are *not* known exactly and *do* change.\n- C is wrong: hedging multiple factors generally requires *multiple* positions (one per factor), which adds transaction costs and shorting constraints.\n- D is wrong: hedging the factors leaves only idiosyncratic risk and alpha; idiosyncratic risk is reduced by holding *many names*, not by factor hedging.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Multifactor hedging challenges — betas estimated with error and drift, possible factor misspecification (omitted factors), many positions needed (costs/shorting limits), and correlated factor proxies. Idiosyncratic risk is diversified away, not hedged.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Genuine multifactor-hedging challenge\",\"options\":[\"Betas known exactly, never change\",\"Betas estimated with error and drift\",\"One position hedges all factors\",\"Hedging removes idiosyncratic risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Regression betas carry estimation error and drift, so hedges decay over time.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A stock has a beta of 1.2 on the GDP factor (premium 4%) and −0.5 on the inflation factor (premium 2%), with a risk-free rate of 3%. Its APT expected return is:\nA) 6.8% B) 8.8% C) 7.8% D) 5.8%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question is a two-factor APT expected-return calculation, with one negative beta to watch. Add R_f plus each beta times its premium:\n\n$$E[R] = R_f + \\beta_{GDP}\\lambda_{GDP} + \\beta_{INF}\\lambda_{INF}$$\n$$E[R] = 3\\% + 1.2(4\\%) + (-0.5)(2\\%)$$\n$$E[R] = 3\\% + 4.8\\% - 1.0\\%$$\n$$E[R] = 6.8\\%$$\n\n- B is wrong: 8.8% adds the inflation term (+1.0%) instead of subtracting it — the negative-beta trap.\n- C is wrong: 7.8% drops the inflation term entirely (ignores the −0.5 contribution).\n- D is wrong: 5.8% subtracts too much or mishandles a term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A negative factor beta SUBTRACTS from expected return. Keep the sign attached to the beta: (−0.5)(2%) = −1.0%, not +1.0%.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** In the Fama–French three-factor model, the SMB factor is constructed to capture which premium?\nA) The value premium (high book-to-market minus low) B) The size premium (small-cap minus large-cap returns) C) The momentum premium (winners minus losers) D) The market risk premium\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what SMB stands for and measures. SMB means \"small minus big\" — the return of small-cap stocks minus large-cap stocks — so it captures the size premium: historically, small companies earned more than the CAPM predicted.\n\n- A is wrong: the value premium is captured by HML (\"high minus low\" book-to-market), not SMB.\n- C is wrong: momentum (winners minus losers) is the Carhart fourth factor (UMD/WML), not part of the three-factor SMB.\n- D is wrong: the market risk premium is the CAPM market factor (E[R_m] − R_f), separate from SMB.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SMB = Small Minus Big = size premium. HML = High Minus Low (book-to-market) = value premium. Momentum (UMD/WML) is the common fourth (Carhart) factor.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What premium does SMB capture?\",\"options\":[\"Value (high minus low B/M)\",\"Size (small minus big)\",\"Momentum (winners minus losers)\",\"Market risk premium\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"SMB = Small Minus Big = the size premium (HML is value, UMD is momentum).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Relative to the CAPM, the APT does NOT require:\nA) A linear relation between expected return and factor betas B) That investors hold the market portfolio under homogeneous expectations C) Absence of arbitrage opportunities D) A factor structure for returns\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** APT replaces the equilibrium/market-portfolio machinery with no-arbitrage; it keeps the factor structure and linear pricing.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Expected inflation is 3%; actual inflation prints 3%. A stock has an inflation factor beta of −1.2. The effect of the announcement on its return under a factor model is:\nA) −3.6% B) +3.6% C) 0% D) −1.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Surprise = actual − expected = 0; only surprises move returns in factor models.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A stock has $$\\beta_1 = 0.9$$ (factor premium 5%) and $$\\beta_2 = 0.2$$ (factor premium 1%); $$R_f = 4\\%$$. APT expected return:\nA) 8.7% B) 4.7% C) 9.5% D) 10.7%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$4\\% + 0.9(5\\%) + 0.2(1\\%) = 4\\% + 4.5\\% + 0.2\\% = 8.7\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A portfolio with a significantly negative HML loading and near-zero SMB loading is best described as tilted toward:\nA) Small-cap value B) Large-cap value C) Growth D) High-dividend stocks\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Negative HML = growth tilt; SMB ≈ 0 says nothing strong about size.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** Which assumption is required by the APT but NOT by the CAPM?\nA) Investors have homogeneous expectations B) Returns are generated by a linear factor model C) All investors hold the market portfolio D) Investors can borrow unlimited amounts at the risk-free rate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** APT needs a factor structure, ample diversification, and no arbitrage. It dispenses with homogeneous expectations, universal market-portfolio holdings, and risk-free borrowing — those are CAPM machinery.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"APT vs CAPM assumptions\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"APT requires\",\"items\":[\"linear factor model\",\"diversification\",\"no arbitrage\"]},{\"h\":\"CAPM requires\",\"items\":[\"homogeneous expectations\",\"hold market portfolio\",\"risk-free borrowing\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A stock has factor betas of 1.5 (industrial production) and −0.8 (credit spread). The expected return is 8%. Industrial production surprises +2% and the credit spread factor surprises +1%; firm-specific return is +0.5%. The realized return is closest to:\nA) 10.7% B) 11.5% C) 8.0% D) 12.3%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$R = 8\\% + 1.5(+2\\%) + (-0.8)(+1\\%) + 0.5\\% = 8\\% + 3\\% - 0.8\\% + 0.5\\% = 10.7\\%$$\n\nWatch the sign on the negative beta times a positive surprise.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Fund G has Fama–French loadings $$\\beta_m = 0.95$$, $$\\beta_{SMB} = -0.40$$, $$\\beta_{HML} = 0.70$$. The fund is best described as tilted toward:\nA) Small-cap growth B) Large-cap value C) Small-cap value D) Large-cap growth\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Negative SMB loading = large-cap tilt; positive HML loading = value tilt → large-cap value.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Fama-French style: SMB<0, HML>0\",\"x\":\"growth -> value (HML)\",\"y\":\"large -> small (SMB)\",\"q\":[\"large growth\",\"LARGE VALUE\",\"small growth\",\"small value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A stock has a GDP-factor beta of 1.5 and an inflation-factor beta of −0.8. The factor risk premiums are 4.5% (GDP) and 2.0% (inflation), and the risk-free rate is 3%. Over the next year, GDP growth comes in 1.2% *above* expectations, inflation prints 0.5% *above* expectations, and there is firm-specific good news worth +0.3%. What is the stock's *realized* return under the APT factor model?\nA) 8.15% B) 9.85% C) 10.15% D) 9.55%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the *realized* return, which combines the expected return with the factor *surprises* and the idiosyncratic term.\n\n1. First the expected return from APT:\n$$E[R] = R_f + \\beta_{GDP}\\lambda_{GDP} + \\beta_{INF}\\lambda_{INF}$$\n$$= 3\\% + 1.5(4.5\\%) + (-0.8)(2.0\\%) = 3\\% + 6.75\\% - 1.6\\% = 8.15\\%$$\n2. Realized return adds the factor *surprises* (actual − expected) times each beta, plus the firm-specific return:\n$$R = E[R] + \\beta_{GDP}F_{GDP} + \\beta_{INF}F_{INF} + e$$\n- GDP surprise = +1.2%: contribution $$1.5 \\times 1.2\\% = +1.8\\%$$\n- Inflation surprise = +0.5%: contribution $$-0.8 \\times 0.5\\% = -0.4\\%$$\n- Firm-specific: $$+0.3\\%$$\n3. Sum:\n$$R = 8.15\\% + 1.8\\% - 0.4\\% + 0.3\\% = 9.85\\%$$\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A (8.15%) stops at the expected return, ignoring all surprises and the firm-specific news (stopped one step early).\n- C (10.15%) flips the sign on the inflation surprise contribution (adds +0.4% instead of −0.4%), since the inflation beta is negative.\n- D (9.55%) omits the +0.3% firm-specific term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: realized return = expected return + Σ(beta × *surprise*) + idiosyncratic. Use surprises (actual − expected), not levels, and respect the *sign* of each beta — a negative beta times a positive surprise reduces return.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Fund Q has factor loadings $$\\beta_m=1.2$$, $$\\beta_{SMB}=-0.4$$, and $$\\beta_{HML}=0.5$$. The premiums are market 6%, SMB 2.5%, HML 4.0%; the risk-free rate is 2.5%. Using the Fama–French three-factor model, what is Fund Q's expected return, and what style tilts do the loadings indicate?\nA) 10.7%; the fund is large-cap and value-tilted B) 12.7%; the fund is small-cap and value-tilted C) 10.7%; the fund is small-cap and growth-tilted D) 9.7%; the fund is large-cap and growth-tilted\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the Fama–French expected return and the style read on the loadings.\n\n1. Apply the model term by term:\n$$E[R_Q] = R_f + \\beta_m(\\text{mkt prem}) + \\beta_{SMB}E[SMB] + \\beta_{HML}E[HML]$$\n$$= 2.5\\% + 1.2(6\\%) + (-0.4)(2.5\\%) + 0.5(4.0\\%)$$\n2. Compute each:\n- Market: $$1.2 \\times 6\\% = 7.2\\%$$\n- SMB: $$-0.4 \\times 2.5\\% = -1.0\\%$$\n- HML: $$0.5 \\times 4.0\\% = +2.0\\%$$\n3. Sum:\n$$E[R_Q] = 2.5\\% + 7.2\\% - 1.0\\% + 2.0\\% = 10.7\\%$$\n4. Style: a **negative SMB** loading = a *large-cap* tilt; a **positive HML** loading = a *value* tilt.\n\nSo 10.7%, large-cap value — option A.\n\n- B (12.7%) takes the absolute value of the SMB loading (adds +1.0% instead of −1.0%) and misreads the negative SMB as small-cap.\n- C reads the positive HML as growth (it is value) and the negative SMB as small-cap (it is large-cap).\n- D drops or mishandles the HML term (9.7%) and misreads the tilts.\n\n📌 Exam tip: positive SMB = small-cap, negative SMB = large-cap; positive HML = value, negative HML = growth. Carry the *signs* of the loadings into the sum — the negative SMB term must be *subtracted*, not added as an absolute value.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Style from loadings (SMB −0.4, HML +0.5)\",\"x\":\"HML → (− growth, + value)\",\"y\":\"SMB → (− large, + small)\",\"q\":[\"Large growth\",\"Large value\",\"Small growth\",\"Small value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A $200 million portfolio has $$\\beta_{GDP}=0.6$$ and $$\\beta_{INF}=-0.3$$. The manager wants to neutralize *both* factor exposures using pure factor portfolios (each has beta 1 to its own factor and 0 to the other). What trades achieve a net beta of zero on both factors?\nA) Short $120m of the GDP factor portfolio and short $60m of the inflation factor portfolio B) Short $120m of the GDP factor portfolio and go long $60m of the inflation factor portfolio C) Long $120m of the GDP factor portfolio and short $60m of the inflation factor portfolio D) Short $60m of the GDP factor portfolio and long $120m of the inflation factor portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the hedge trades to zero out two factor betas, being careful with the sign of the negative inflation beta.\n\n1. GDP exposure is **positive** (+0.6). To offset it you must add **negative** 0.6 beta, i.e., **short** the GDP factor portfolio in dollar size:\n$$0.6 \\times \\$200\\text{m} = \\$120\\text{m short}$$\nNet GDP beta: $$0.6 - 0.6 = 0$$.\n2. Inflation exposure is **negative** (−0.3). To offset a negative beta you must add **positive** 0.3 beta, i.e., **go long** the inflation factor portfolio:\n$$0.3 \\times \\$200\\text{m} = \\$60\\text{m long}$$\nNet inflation beta: $$-0.3 + 0.3 = 0$$.\n\nSo: short $120m GDP, long $60m inflation — option B.\n\n- A shorts the inflation factor too, which would make the net inflation beta $$-0.3 - 0.3 = -0.6$$ (more negative, not zero) — it ignored that the exposure was already negative.\n- C goes long the GDP factor (doubling the positive GDP beta) — wrong direction.\n- D mixes up the dollar sizes ($60m vs. $120m) by swapping the betas between factors.\n\n📌 Exam tip: to hedge, add the *opposite* sign of the existing beta. A *positive* exposure is hedged by *shorting* the factor portfolio; a *negative* exposure is hedged by *going long*. Hedge size = |beta| × portfolio value. The negative-beta trap flips the direction of the trade.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Neutralize each factor beta\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"GDP β=+0.6\",\"items\":[\"Positive exposure\",\"SHORT factor port\",\"$120m short → net 0\"]},{\"h\":\"Inflation β=−0.3\",\"items\":[\"Negative exposure\",\"GO LONG factor port\",\"$60m long → net 0\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A single-factor model uses industrial production (IP) as its factor. A stock's IP factor beta is 0.9. At the start of the period the market expected IP growth of 2.0%; the announcement reveals actual IP growth of 3.5%. With no other news, what is the contribution of the IP announcement to the stock's return?\nA) +3.15% B) +1.35% C) +1.80% D) +0.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how much an IP announcement moves the stock, given that only the *surprise* matters.\n\n1. Compute the factor surprise = actual − expected:\n$$F_{IP} = 3.5\\% - 2.0\\% = 1.5\\%$$\n2. Multiply by the factor beta:\n$$\\beta_{IP} \\times F_{IP} = 0.9 \\times 1.5\\% = 1.35\\%$$\n\nThat is B.\n\n- A (+3.15%) multiplies the beta by the *actual* level (0.9 × 3.5%), ignoring expectations — the level trap.\n- C (+1.80%) multiplies the beta by 2.0% (the *expected* level) or otherwise mishandles the surprise.\n- D (0.00%) would be correct only if actual equaled expected; here there is a +1.5% surprise.\n\n📌 Exam tip: in factor models, only the *unanticipated* part of an announcement moves returns. Always compute surprise = actual − expected *before* multiplying by beta. A \"high-sounding\" actual number (3.5%) is irrelevant if it was largely expected.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Only the surprise moves returns\",\"steps\":[\"Surprise = 3.5% − 2.0%\",\"Surprise = 1.5%\",\"Effect = β × surprise\",\"0.9 × 1.5% = 1.35%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An analyst prices a stock with a three-factor APT model: factor betas of 1.1, 0.7, and −0.4, with corresponding factor risk premiums of 5.0%, 3.0%, and 2.0%. The risk-free rate is 3.5%. What is the stock's APT expected return?\nA) 10.30% B) 11.90% C) 9.50% D) 6.80%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the APT expected return summing three priced factors plus the risk-free rate.\n\n1. APT expected return:\n$$E[R] = R_f + \\beta_1\\lambda_1 + \\beta_2\\lambda_2 + \\beta_3\\lambda_3$$\n2. Compute each term:\n- $$\\beta_1\\lambda_1 = 1.1 \\times 5.0\\% = 5.5\\%$$\n- $$\\beta_2\\lambda_2 = 0.7 \\times 3.0\\% = 2.1\\%$$\n- $$\\beta_3\\lambda_3 = -0.4 \\times 2.0\\% = -0.8\\%$$\n3. Sum with $$R_f$$:\n$$E[R] = 3.5\\% + 5.5\\% + 2.1\\% - 0.8\\% = 10.3\\%$$\n\nThat is A.\n\n- B (11.90%) takes the third term as +0.8% instead of −0.8% (ignoring the negative beta's sign).\n- C (9.50%) drops the third factor entirely (stopped one factor short).\n- D (6.80%) omits the risk-free rate (summed only the factor terms: 5.5 + 2.1 − 0.8 = 6.8%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: APT expected return = $$R_f$$ + Σ(beta × *premium*). Two recurring traps: dropping $$R_f$$ (gives the risk-premium-only figure) and mishandling a negative beta term (which *subtracts* from the expected return).\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A risk manager defends using a multifactor model for hedging but acknowledges its practical limitations. Which statement most accurately reflects the challenges of multifactor hedging as presented in the curriculum?\nA) Factor betas are estimated without error and are stable over time, so the only challenge is choosing the factors B) Betas are estimated with error and drift over time, the factor structure may be misspecified (omitted factors appear as alpha or unhedged risk), and hedging many factors requires many positions with transaction-cost and shorting frictions C) Hedging all factor exposures eliminates idiosyncratic risk as well, so no residual risk remains D) APT specifies exactly which factors to use, so misspecification is impossible in practice\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which statement correctly captures the practical challenges of multifactor hedging.\n\n1. The curriculum lists: betas are estimated with error and **drift over time**; the factor structure may be **misspecified** (omitted factors show up as \"alpha\" or unhedged risk); hedging many factors needs **many positions**, so transaction costs and shorting constraints bite; and factor-proxy correlations complicate clean isolation. Option B states these accurately.\n\n- A is the opposite of reality — betas are *not* error-free or stable; that instability is the central challenge.\n- C is false: hedging factor exposures leaves a \"market-neutral\" stance with **only idiosyncratic risk plus alpha** remaining; idiosyncratic risk is reduced by holding *many names*, not by factor hedging.\n- D is false: a defining feature of APT is that it is **silent on what the factors are** — flexibility and weakness both. Misspecification is very possible.\n\n📌 Exam tip: APT does *not* tell you the factors (its key generality/weakness), betas are noisy and drift, and factor hedging removes factor risk but leaves idiosyncratic risk + alpha (diversify names to cut the residual). Any answer claiming stable/error-free betas or APT-specified factors is a trap.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Genuine multifactor-hedging challenge\",\"options\":[\"Betas error-free and stable\",\"Betas estimated with error, drift, misspecification\",\"One position hedges all factors\",\"Hedging removes idiosyncratic risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Betas are noisy regressions that drift; factors may be misspecified; many positions needed.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which statement correctly compares the APT with the CAPM?\nA) The APT requires homogeneous expectations and that all investors hold the market portfolio, just like the CAPM B) The APT relies on a no-arbitrage argument enforced by some investors, allows multiple unspecified factors, and does not require identifying or observing the market portfolio C) Both the APT and the CAPM specify the economic factors that drive returns D) The APT holds exactly for every individual asset, whereas the CAPM holds only for well-diversified portfolios\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the correct APT-vs-CAPM comparison.\n\n1. The APT rests on **no-arbitrage**: a few arbitrageurs eliminate mispricing in well-diversified portfolios — it does *not* need the full equilibrium machinery.\n2. It allows **multiple factors**, which the theory leaves **unspecified**.\n3. It does **not** require the market portfolio (sidestepping Roll's critique that the true market portfolio is unobservable).\nAll three points are in B, so B is correct.\n\n- A is false: APT's whole advantage is dropping homogeneous expectations and the market-portfolio requirement; only the CAPM needs those.\n- C is false: the CAPM specifies a single factor (the market), but the APT is *silent* on what its factors are.\n- D reverses the precision: the **CAPM** holds exactly for every asset; the **APT** holds for well-diversified portfolios (and near-exactly for most single assets).\n\n📌 Exam tip: APT = no-arbitrage, multiple *unspecified* factors, no market portfolio needed (avoids Roll's critique). CAPM = equilibrium, single market factor, homogeneous expectations, market portfolio required. The \"exact for every asset\" property belongs to the CAPM; APT is exact for well-diversified portfolios.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"APT vs CAPM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"APT\",\"items\":[\"No-arbitrage logic\",\"Multiple unspecified factors\",\"No market portfolio needed\"]},{\"h\":\"CAPM\",\"items\":[\"Equilibrium\",\"Single market factor\",\"Market port + homogeneous exp.\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"107","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital and Performance Measurement","notes":"# 7. Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital and Performance Measurement\n\nRaw return tells you nothing until you know the risk taken to earn it. This chapter covers the CAPM-based performance ratios (Sharpe, Treynor, Jensen's alpha), benchmark-relative measures (tracking error, information ratio), downside measures (Sortino), and the banking-world workhorse RAROC. Heavily tested — calculations and \"which measure for which situation.\"\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Calculate, compare, and interpret the Sharpe ratio, Treynor ratio, and Jensen's alpha.\n- Compute and interpret tracking error, the information ratio, and the Sortino ratio.\n- Explain which performance measure is appropriate for a given portfolio context (total portfolio vs. sub-portfolio, benchmark-relative mandates, asymmetric return profiles).\n- Define risk-adjusted return on capital (RAROC), compute RAROC for a business or project, and explain its uses in capital allocation, pricing, and performance measurement.\n- Explain the hurdle rate, compute adjusted RAROC (ARAROC), and apply the decision rule for accepting a project.\n- Describe the challenges and limitations of risk-adjusted performance measures.\n\n## The classic trio\n\n**Sharpe ratio** — excess return per unit of **total risk**:\n\n$$S_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\sigma_p}$$\n\n**Treynor ratio** — excess return per unit of **systematic risk**:\n\n$$T_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\beta_p}$$\n\n**Jensen's alpha** — return above the CAPM-required return:\n\n$$\\alpha_p = E[R_p] - \\left[R_f + \\beta_p\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)\\right]$$\n\n| Measure | Risk used | Use when | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sharpe | $$\\sigma_p$$ (total) | Evaluating an investor's **entire** portfolio, or undiversified holdings | Slope of the line from $$R_f$$ through the portfolio in $$(\\sigma, E[R])$$ space; market's Sharpe = CML slope |\n| Treynor | $$\\beta_p$$ (systematic) | A **well-diversified sub-portfolio** within a larger diversified whole | Idiosyncratic risk already diversified away, so only beta matters |\n| Jensen's alpha | $$\\beta_p$$ | Testing skill vs. the CAPM benchmark; comparing funds with **similar betas** | An absolute (percent) measure, not a ratio — can't rank funds of different beta by alpha alone |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The single most-tested trap in Book 1: **Sharpe divides by total risk (σ); Treynor divides by beta.** A poorly diversified fund can look fine on Treynor (and have positive alpha) yet terrible on Sharpe, because its idiosyncratic risk inflates σ but not β. If a question says \"this fund is the investor's entire wealth,\" use Sharpe; \"one of many funds held,\" Treynor/alpha.\n\n## Benchmark-relative and downside measures\n\n**Tracking error** — volatility of active returns versus a benchmark:\n\n$$TE = \\sigma\\!\\left(R_p - R_b\\right)$$\n\n**Information ratio** — average active return per unit of tracking error:\n\n$$IR = \\frac{E[R_p] - E[R_b]}{TE}$$\n\nUsed to judge active managers against their mandated benchmark; an IR sustained above ~0.5 is considered strong. (Some texts put alpha rather than raw active return in the numerator — the exam convention is active return over tracking error.)\n\n**Sortino ratio** — penalizes only **downside** volatility, measured against a minimum acceptable return $$R_{min}$$ (often the risk-free rate or zero):\n\n$$\\text{Sortino} = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_{min}}{\\sigma_{down}},\\qquad\n\\sigma_{down} = \\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{N}\\textstyle\\sum_{R_t < R_{min}} (R_t - R_{min})^2}$$\n\nPreferred when return distributions are **asymmetric** (options strategies, hedge funds): upside volatility shouldn't be punished.\n\n### Worked example 1 — ranking two funds\n\n| | Fund A | Fund B | Market |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Return | 12% | 10% | 9% |\n| σ | 24% | 14% | 15% |\n| β | 1.1 | 0.9 | 1.0 |\n\nRisk-free rate 2%.\n\nSharpe: $$S_A = \\frac{12-2}{24} = 0.417$$, $$S_B = \\frac{10-2}{14} = 0.571$$, market $$= \\frac{9-2}{15} = 0.467$$.\n\nTreynor: $$T_A = \\frac{12-2}{1.1} = 9.09\\%$$, $$T_B = \\frac{10-2}{0.9} = 8.89\\%$$, market $$= 7\\%$$.\n\nJensen: $$\\alpha_A = 12 - [2 + 1.1(7)] = +2.3\\%$$, $$\\alpha_B = 10 - [2 + 0.9(7)] = +1.7\\%$$.\n\nInterpretation: A wins on Treynor and alpha (better per unit of *systematic* risk), but B wins on Sharpe — A carries heavy idiosyncratic risk. As a stand-alone total portfolio choose **B**; as one sleeve inside a diversified program, **A** adds more.\n\n### Worked example 2 — information ratio and Sortino\n\nA manager returns 11% vs. a benchmark's 8.5%, with tracking error 5%:\n\n$$IR = \\frac{11\\% - 8.5\\%}{5\\%} = 0.50$$\n\nSame fund: mean return 11%, minimum acceptable return 3%, downside deviation (vs. 3%) of 9%:\n\n$$\\text{Sortino} = \\frac{11\\% - 3\\%}{9\\%} = 0.89$$\n\n## RAROC: risk-adjusted return on capital\n\nBanks need one yardstick that works across businesses with very different risk: RAROC charges each business for the **economic capital** its risk consumes.\n\n$$RAROC = \\frac{\\text{expected after-tax risk-adjusted net income}}{\\text{economic capital}}$$\n\nExpanded numerator (one period):\n\n$$RAROC = \\frac{\\text{revenues} - \\text{costs} - \\text{expected losses} - \\text{taxes} + \\text{return on economic capital} \\pm \\text{transfer prices}}{\\text{economic capital}}$$\n\n- **Expected losses** are deducted as a cost (they are priced, not capitalized — Ch 1).\n- **Economic capital** is the buffer for **unexpected** losses at the firm's target confidence level (e.g., the level consistent with an AA rating); it is risk-based, unlike book or regulatory capital.\n- **Return on economic capital**: the capital itself is invested in safe assets and earns roughly the risk-free rate — added back to income.\n\n**Uses:** comparing and allocating capital across businesses, risk-based pricing of loans/deals, identifying value-creating vs. value-destroying units, and risk-adjusted compensation.\n\n**Decision rule:** accept activities whose RAROC exceeds the **hurdle rate** — the firm's cost of the capital backing the business (commonly the weighted cost of equity capital, e.g., across common and preferred equity).\n\n## Adjusted RAROC\n\nPlain RAROC ignores the *systematic* riskiness of the business's returns — a high-beta business should clear a higher bar. **ARAROC** corrects this with the business's CAPM beta:\n\n$$ARAROC = RAROC - \\beta_E\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)$$\n\n**Rule: accept the project if $$ARAROC > R_f$$** (equivalently, if RAROC exceeds the CAPM-consistent required return $$R_f + \\beta_E(E[R_m]-R_f)$$).\n\n### Worked example 3 — RAROC and ARAROC\n\nA lending unit: loan book \\$1 billion, revenue (interest + fees) \\$90 million, operating costs \\$25 million, expected losses \\$20 million. Economic capital is \\$75 million, invested at the risk-free rate of 4%. Ignore taxes and transfer prices.\n\n$$RAROC = \\frac{90 - 25 - 20 + 0.04 \\times 75}{75} = \\frac{48}{75} = 64\\%$$\n\nRAROC of 64% exceeds any plausible hurdle rate — accept. Now a riskier version: revenue \\$50m, costs \\$15m, expected losses \\$22m, economic capital \\$150m, $$R_f=4\\%$$:\n\n$$RAROC = \\frac{50 - 15 - 22 + 0.04\\times150}{150} = \\frac{19}{150} = 12.67\\%$$\n\nIf the business's equity beta is 1.3 and the market premium is 6%:\n\n$$ARAROC = 12.67\\% - 1.3 \\times 6\\% = 12.67\\% - 7.8\\% = 4.87\\% > R_f = 4\\%$$\n\nMarginally acceptable: after charging for systematic risk, the unit still clears the risk-free bar — but barely, so small estimation errors flip the verdict.\n\n## Challenges with risk-adjusted measures\n\n- **Economic capital and beta are model outputs** — wrong confidence levels, correlations, or betas distort rankings.\n- **Diversification effects**: a unit's stand-alone capital exceeds its marginal contribution to firm-wide capital; how the diversification benefit is allocated changes every unit's RAROC.\n- **Horizon problems**: one-year capital for multi-year businesses; smoothing and gaming of expected-loss estimates.\n- **Backward-looking inputs** (historical σ, β, TE) may not represent forward risk; ratios computed on short samples are noisy.\n- Ratios mislead when returns are non-normal — a short-options book shows a beautiful Sharpe until the tail event (see Niederhoffer, Ch 9).\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Know the decision contexts cold: **Sharpe** = total portfolio; **Treynor/alpha** = diversified component; **IR** = benchmark mandate; **Sortino** = asymmetric returns; **RAROC vs. hurdle / ARAROC vs. risk-free** = bank capital allocation.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Sharpe ratio formula and what risk it uses?\n> **A:** $$S_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\sigma_p}$$ — excess return per unit of total risk (σ).\n\n**Q2.** Treynor ratio formula and what risk it uses?\n> **A:** $$T_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\beta_p}$$ — excess return per unit of systematic risk (beta).\n\n**Q3.** Jensen's alpha formula?\n> **A:** $$\\alpha_p = E[R_p] - \\left[R_f + \\beta_p(E[R_m] - R_f)\\right]$$ — return above the CAPM-required return; an absolute measure, not a ratio.\n\n**Q4.** Most-tested trap: Sharpe vs. Treynor denominator?\n> **A:** Sharpe divides by total risk (σ); Treynor divides by beta. A poorly diversified fund can look fine on Treynor yet terrible on Sharpe because idiosyncratic risk inflates σ but not β.\n\n**Q5.** When use Sharpe vs. Treynor/alpha?\n> **A:** Use Sharpe when the fund is the investor's entire wealth (or undiversified). Use Treynor/alpha when it is one of many funds held within a diversified whole (only systematic risk matters).\n\n**Q6.** Tracking error and information ratio?\n> **A:** $$TE = \\sigma(R_p - R_b)$$ (volatility of active returns). $$IR = \\frac{E[R_p] - E[R_b]}{TE}$$ — average active return per unit of tracking error; used to judge active managers vs. benchmark.\n\n**Q7.** What is the Sortino ratio and when is it preferred?\n> **A:** $$\\text{Sortino} = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_{min}}{\\sigma_{down}}$$ — penalizes only downside deviation below a minimum acceptable return. Preferred for asymmetric returns (options strategies, hedge funds).\n\n**Q8.** RAROC definition?\n> **A:** $$RAROC = \\frac{\\text{expected after-tax risk-adjusted net income}}{\\text{economic capital}}$$ — charges each business for the economic capital its risk consumes.\n\n**Q9.** In the RAROC numerator, how are expected losses treated?\n> **A:** Deducted as a cost of doing business (they are priced, not capitalized). Economic capital covers unexpected losses, not EL.\n\n**Q10.** Why is \"return on economic capital\" added back in RAROC?\n> **A:** The economic capital buffer is invested in safe assets earning roughly the risk-free rate, so that income is added to the numerator.\n\n**Q11.** RAROC decision rule?\n> **A:** Accept activities whose RAROC exceeds the hurdle rate — the firm's cost of the capital backing the business (commonly the weighted cost of equity capital).\n\n**Q12.** What problem does ARAROC fix, and what is its formula?\n> **A:** Plain RAROC ignores the systematic riskiness of returns. $$ARAROC = RAROC - \\beta_E(E[R_m] - R_f)$$.\n\n**Q13.** ARAROC decision rule?\n> **A:** Accept the project if $$ARAROC > R_f$$ (equivalently, if RAROC exceeds the CAPM-required return $$R_f + \\beta_E(E[R_m]-R_f)$$).\n\n**Q14.** Name key challenges/limitations of risk-adjusted performance measures.\n> **A:** Economic capital and beta are model outputs; allocation of diversification benefit changes every unit's RAROC; horizon mismatches; backward-looking inputs; ratios mislead for non-normal returns (e.g., short-options Sharpe).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Which performance measure divides excess return by the portfolio's TOTAL risk (standard deviation)?\nA) Treynor ratio B) Jensen's alpha C) Sharpe ratio D) Information ratio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is simply asking: which ratio uses total risk (the whole standard deviation, $$\\sigma$$) on the bottom?\n\nThe Sharpe ratio formula is:\n\n$$S_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\sigma_p}$$\n\nThe top is the extra return above the risk-free rate, and the bottom is $$\\sigma_p$$, which measures ALL of the portfolio's ups and downs (both the market-driven part and the company-specific part). So Sharpe uses total risk. That is exactly what the question describes, so C is correct.\n\nThink of it like grading a runner's speed against the total bumpiness of the road they ran on, not just the part of the bumpiness caused by traffic.\n\n- A is wrong: the Treynor ratio divides by beta ($$\\beta$$), which is only the systematic (market-driven) part of risk, not total risk.\n- B is wrong: Jensen's alpha is not a \"divide by risk\" ratio at all; it is a single percentage showing return above the CAPM-required return.\n- D is wrong: the information ratio divides active return by tracking error (risk versus a benchmark), not by total standard deviation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the denominators: Sharpe = total risk ($$\\sigma$$), Treynor = systematic risk ($$\\beta$$). This is the single most-tested trap in Book 1.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Risk in the denominator\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sharpe\",\"items\":[\"denominator = total risk (sigma)\",\"counts ALL ups and downs\",\"whole / stand-alone portfolio\"]},{\"h\":\"Treynor\",\"items\":[\"denominator = beta\",\"systematic risk only\",\"one sleeve of many\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A portfolio returns 10%, the risk-free rate is 2%, and the portfolio's standard deviation is 16%. What is its Sharpe ratio?\nA) 0.50 B) 0.625 C) 0.80 D) 1.25\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks us to plug numbers into the Sharpe ratio formula and compute.\n\nFormula:\n\n$$S_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\sigma_p}$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n\n$$S_p = \\frac{10\\% - 2\\%}{16\\%}$$\n\nStep 1 — do the top first: $$10\\% - 2\\% = 8\\%$$.\n\nStep 2 — divide by the bottom: $$\\frac{8}{16} = 0.50$$.\n\nSo the Sharpe ratio is 0.50, which is option A.\n\nIt is like asking \"how much reward did I get for each unit of bumpiness?\" — 8 units of extra return spread over 16 units of risk gives 0.5 reward per unit.\n\n- B (0.625) is wrong: this is $$10/16$$ — the trap of forgetting to subtract the risk-free rate from the top.\n- C (0.80) is wrong: this is $$8/10$$ — dividing by the return instead of by the standard deviation.\n- D (1.25) is wrong: this is $$10/8$$ — flipping the numbers upside down.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always subtract the risk-free rate first to get \"excess return,\" THEN divide by risk. Forgetting to subtract $$R_f$$ is the most common slip.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Sharpe = 0.50\",\"steps\":[\"Rp = 10%\",\"- Rf = 2%\",\"excess = 8%\",\"/ sigma 16%\",\"= 0.50\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An investor holds a single fund as their entire wealth. Which measure is most appropriate for judging that fund?\nA) Treynor ratio B) Jensen's alpha C) Sharpe ratio D) Information ratio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is asking: when a fund is someone's whole portfolio (not one piece among many), which yardstick fits?\n\nWhen a fund is your ENTIRE wealth, you are exposed to all of its risk — both the market part AND the company-specific (idiosyncratic) part, because you have nothing else to cancel that company-specific risk out. So you must measure against total risk, which is the standard deviation. The Sharpe ratio uses total risk, so C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: if you own only one stock, every wobble in that stock hits your wealth. If you owned 100 stocks, the individual wobbles would mostly cancel out.\n\n- A is wrong: Treynor uses only beta (systematic risk). That is right when the fund is one slice of a big diversified portfolio, because the company-specific risk has already been diversified away — but not when it is your only holding.\n- B is wrong: Jensen's alpha also focuses on systematic risk (beta) and suits diversified sub-portfolios, not a stand-alone whole.\n- D is wrong: the information ratio is for judging an active manager against a benchmark mandate, not for a stand-alone total portfolio.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Entire portfolio / only holding / undiversified\" = Sharpe. \"One of many funds / a sub-portfolio\" = Treynor or alpha.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Which measure fits the holding?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Entire wealth / only fund\",\"items\":[\"all risk hits you\",\"use total sigma\",\"-> Sharpe\"]},{\"h\":\"One of many sleeves\",\"items\":[\"idiosyncratic diversified away\",\"use beta\",\"-> Treynor / alpha\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A portfolio returns 11%, has a beta of 1.2, and the risk-free rate is 3%. What is its Treynor ratio?\nA) 6.67% B) 9.17% C) 8.00% D) 13.20%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks us to compute the Treynor ratio, which is excess return per unit of systematic risk (beta).\n\nFormula:\n\n$$T_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\beta_p}$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n\n$$T_p = \\frac{11\\% - 3\\%}{1.2}$$\n\nStep 1 — top: $$11\\% - 3\\% = 8\\%$$.\n\nStep 2 — divide by beta: $$\\frac{8\\%}{1.2} = 6.67\\%$$.\n\nSo the Treynor ratio is 6.67%, option A.\n\n- B (9.17%) is wrong: this is $$11/1.2$$ — forgetting to subtract the risk-free rate.\n- C (8.00%) is wrong: this is just the excess return ($$11 - 3$$) without dividing by beta at all.\n- D (13.20%) is wrong: this is $$11 \\times 1.2$$ — multiplying by beta instead of dividing.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Treynor's denominator is beta. Keep the units straight: a Treynor result is a percentage return per unit of beta.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Treynor = 6.67%\",\"steps\":[\"Rp = 11%\",\"- Rf = 3%\",\"excess = 8%\",\"/ beta 1.2\",\"= 6.67%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A portfolio returns 12%, has a beta of 0.9, the market returns 10%, and the risk-free rate is 2%. What is Jensen's alpha?\nA) +2.0% B) +2.8% C) +3.0% D) +10.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for Jensen's alpha — how much the portfolio beat (or missed) the return that CAPM says it \"should\" have earned for its level of beta.\n\nFormula:\n\n$$\\alpha_p = E[R_p] - \\left[R_f + \\beta_p\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)\\right]$$\n\nStep 1 — find the market risk premium (market return minus risk-free): $$10\\% - 2\\% = 8\\%$$.\n\nStep 2 — multiply by beta: $$0.9 \\times 8\\% = 7.2\\%$$.\n\nStep 3 — add the risk-free rate to get the CAPM-required return: $$2\\% + 7.2\\% = 9.2\\%$$.\n\nStep 4 — subtract that from the actual return: $$12\\% - 9.2\\% = +2.8\\%$$.\n\nSo alpha is +2.8%, option B. A positive alpha means the manager earned more than CAPM required — a sign of skill.\n\n- A (+2.0%) is wrong: comes from forgetting to multiply by beta (using $$12 - (2+8)$$).\n- C (+3.0%) is wrong: comes from using $$12 - 0.9 \\times 10 = 3$$, mishandling the formula by multiplying beta by the full market return instead of the market premium.\n- D (+10.0%) is wrong: this just compares the portfolio return to the risk-free rate, ignoring beta and the market entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The bracket is the \"required return.\" Always build it as $$R_f + \\beta(R_m - R_f)$$, using the market PREMIUM ($$R_m - R_f$$), not the raw market return.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Jensen alpha = +2.8%\",\"steps\":[\"Rm - Rf = 8%\",\"x beta 0.9 = 7.2%\",\"+ Rf 2% = 9.2% req\",\"Rp 12% - 9.2%\",\"= +2.8%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A manager returns 12%, the benchmark returns 9%, and the tracking error is 6%. What is the information ratio?\nA) 0.33 B) 0.50 C) 1.50 D) 2.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the information ratio, which measures average active return (the manager's return above the benchmark) per unit of tracking error.\n\nFormula:\n\n$$IR = \\frac{E[R_p] - E[R_b]}{TE}$$\n\nStep 1 — find active return (manager minus benchmark): $$12\\% - 9\\% = 3\\%$$.\n\nStep 2 — divide by tracking error: $$\\frac{3\\%}{6\\%} = 0.50$$.\n\nSo the information ratio is 0.50, option B. An IR sustained around 0.5 is considered strong active management.\n\nThink of it as: \"for every unit of extra wobbliness the manager created by straying from the benchmark, how much extra return did they deliver?\"\n\n- A (0.33) is wrong: this is $$3/9$$ — dividing by the benchmark return instead of the tracking error.\n- C (1.50) is wrong: this is $$9/6$$ — using the benchmark return on top instead of the active return.\n- D (2.00) is wrong: this is $$12/6$$ — using the manager's total return on top instead of the active (excess-over-benchmark) return.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Information ratio = (active return) / (tracking error). Both pieces are measured RELATIVE to the benchmark, not relative to the risk-free rate.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Info ratio = 0.50\",\"steps\":[\"Rp = 12%\",\"- benchmark 9%\",\"active = 3%\",\"/ tracking error 6%\",\"= 0.50\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The Sortino ratio differs from the Sharpe ratio mainly because the Sortino ratio:\nA) Uses beta instead of standard deviation B) Penalizes only downside deviation below a minimum acceptable return C) Ignores the risk-free rate completely D) Can only be applied to bond portfolios\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what makes the Sortino ratio special compared with the Sharpe ratio.\n\nThe Sharpe ratio uses total standard deviation, which counts ALL ups and downs as \"risk\" — even the good surprises (returns above target). The Sortino ratio is smarter for lopsided (asymmetric) returns: it only counts the BAD moves, those falling below a chosen minimum acceptable return ($$R_{min}$$). Its denominator is the downside deviation:\n\n$$\\sigma_{down} = \\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{N}\\textstyle\\sum_{R_t < R_{min}} (R_t - R_{min})^2}$$\n\nSo B is correct: it only punishes downside.\n\nAnalogy: Sharpe scolds you both for falling AND for jumping unexpectedly high; Sortino only scolds you for falling, which seems fairer.\n\n- A is wrong: Sortino does not use beta; it uses downside deviation. Beta belongs to Treynor and Jensen's alpha.\n- C is wrong: Sortino still uses a hurdle in the numerator (a minimum acceptable return, often the risk-free rate or zero); it does not ignore it.\n- D is wrong: Sortino is general-purpose and is especially useful for options and hedge fund strategies with skewed returns, not just bonds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When a question mentions \"asymmetric returns,\" \"skewed payoffs,\" or \"options/hedge fund strategies,\" the intended answer is usually the Sortino ratio.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sharpe vs Sortino denominator\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sharpe (sigma)\",\"items\":[\"penalizes ALL deviation\",\"up moves count as risk too\",\"symmetric returns\"]},{\"h\":\"Sortino (downside dev)\",\"items\":[\"penalizes only below MAR\",\"ignores upside\",\"skewed / asymmetric returns\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** In the RAROC formula, expected losses are treated as:\nA) Added back because they are non-cash B) Ignored because economic capital covers them C) Deducted as a cost of doing business D) Multiplied by the firm's beta\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how expected losses appear in the RAROC numerator.\n\nRAROC stands for Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital. Its numerator looks like:\n\n$$\\text{revenues} - \\text{costs} - \\text{expected losses} - \\text{taxes} + \\text{return on economic capital}$$\n\nExpected losses are losses the firm KNOWS, on average, it will suffer (for example, a predictable fraction of loans that default). Because they are expected, they are simply priced in and subtracted like any other regular business cost. So C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a grocery store knows some fruit will spoil every week. That expected spoilage is just a normal cost it builds into prices — not a disaster it keeps emergency cash for.\n\n- A is wrong: expected losses are not added back; they are real anticipated costs, so they are subtracted.\n- B is wrong: economic capital is the buffer for UNEXPECTED losses (the nasty surprises), not for expected ones. Expected losses are handled in income, not capital.\n- D is wrong: there is no step that multiplies expected losses by beta; beta enters only in the separate ARAROC adjustment.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the split — EXPECTED losses are a priced cost (deducted from income); UNEXPECTED losses are covered by economic capital.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two kinds of loss\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected loss\",\"items\":[\"predictable average\",\"priced as a cost\",\"subtracted from income\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected loss\",\"items\":[\"the nasty surprise\",\"covered by buffer\",\"= economic capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A business unit has a RAROC of 14%. Its equity beta is 1.5, the expected market return is 9%, and the risk-free rate is 3%. Using the ARAROC decision rule, the project should be:\nA) Rejected, since ARAROC = 5% < 9% B) Accepted, since ARAROC = 5% > 3% C) Rejected, since ARAROC = 0% D) Accepted, since RAROC = 14% > 9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to compute ARAROC (Adjusted RAROC) and apply its accept/reject rule.\n\nStep 1 — find the market risk premium: $$E[R_m] - R_f = 9\\% - 3\\% = 6\\%$$.\n\nStep 2 — use the ARAROC formula:\n\n$$ARAROC = RAROC - \\beta_E\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)$$\n\nPlug in: $$ARAROC = 14\\% - 1.5 \\times 6\\%$$.\n\nStep 3 — multiply: $$1.5 \\times 6\\% = 9\\%$$.\n\nStep 4 — subtract: $$14\\% - 9\\% = 5\\%$$.\n\nStep 5 — apply the decision rule: accept if $$ARAROC > R_f$$. Here $$5\\% > 3\\%$$, so accept. That is option B.\n\nThe adjustment charges the business extra for the systematic risk it carries; a high-beta unit must clear a higher bar before we credit it with creating value.\n\n- A is wrong: the rule compares ARAROC to the risk-free rate (3%), not to the market return (9%).\n- C is wrong: ARAROC is 5%, not 0%; this comes from an arithmetic error.\n- D is wrong: the plain RAROC-vs-market-return comparison is not the ARAROC rule; the ARAROC rule compares the adjusted figure to $$R_f$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ARAROC rule: subtract $$\\beta_E(R_m - R_f)$$ from RAROC, then accept only if the result beats the RISK-FREE rate.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ARAROC accept (5% > 3%)\",\"steps\":[\"Rm - Rf = 6%\",\"x beta 1.5 = 9%\",\"RAROC 14% - 9%\",\"ARAROC = 5%\",\"5% > Rf 3% -> accept\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A bank's lending unit has revenue of $60 million, operating costs of $20 million, and expected losses of $12 million. Its economic capital is $50 million, invested at the risk-free rate of 4%. Ignoring taxes and transfer prices, what is its RAROC?\nA) 56% B) 60% C) 80% D) 64%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to compute RAROC by building the numerator step by step, then dividing by economic capital.\n\nFormula (one period, ignoring taxes and transfer prices):\n\n$$RAROC = \\frac{\\text{revenues} - \\text{costs} - \\text{expected losses} + \\text{return on economic capital}}{\\text{economic capital}}$$\n\nStep 1 — return on economic capital (the capital is invested safely and earns the risk-free rate): $$0.04 \\times 50 = \\$2 \\text{ million}$$.\n\nStep 2 — build the numerator carefully, one piece at a time:\n- Start with revenue: $$60$$.\n- Subtract costs: $$60 - 20 = 40$$.\n- Subtract expected losses: $$40 - 12 = 28$$.\n- Add the return on economic capital: $$28 + 2 = 30$$.\n\nSo the numerator is $30 million.\n\nStep 3 — divide by economic capital: $$\\frac{30}{50} = 0.60 = 60\\%$$.\n\nSo RAROC is 60%, option B. This is the risk-adjusted profit the unit earns for each dollar of economic capital it ties up.\n\n- A (56%) is wrong: this comes from forgetting to add back the $2 million return on capital ($$28/50 = 56\\%$$).\n- C (80%) is wrong: this comes from forgetting to subtract expected losses ($$60 - 20 + 2 = 42$$, then mishandled), an easy slip.\n- D (64%) is wrong: this comes from adding expected losses instead of subtracting, or other sign errors in the numerator.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Build the RAROC numerator in order — revenues, minus costs, minus expected losses, plus the risk-free return earned on economic capital — then divide by economic capital. Watch the signs: costs and expected losses are subtracted, the capital return is added.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"RAROC = 60%\",\"steps\":[\"revenue 60\",\"- costs 20 = 40\",\"- EL 12 = 28\",\"+ cap return 2 = 30\",\"/ EC 50 = 60%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Fund X is one of forty funds inside a large, well-diversified pension plan. The most appropriate single measure for evaluating X's contribution is:\nA) Sharpe ratio B) Treynor ratio C) Sortino ratio D) Tracking error\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Within a diversified whole, only systematic risk matters: Treynor (or Jensen's alpha).\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A portfolio returns 9% with σ = 18% and β = 0.8; the market returns 8% with σ = 14%; the risk-free rate is 3%. Jensen's alpha is:\nA) +1.0% B) +2.0% C) −1.0% D) +4.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\alpha = 9 - [3 + 0.8(8-3)] = 9 - 7 = +2\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A business line has RAROC of 15%. Its equity beta is 1.5, the expected market return is 10%, and the risk-free rate is 4%. Under the ARAROC rule, the project should be:\nA) Accepted, since ARAROC = 6% > 4% B) Rejected, since ARAROC = 6% < 10% C) Accepted, since RAROC > the market return D) Rejected, since ARAROC = 0% < 4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$ARAROC = 15\\% - 1.5(10\\%-4\\%) = 15\\% - 9\\% = 6\\% > R_f = 4\\%$$ → accept.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In the RAROC numerator, expected losses are:\nA) Ignored — they are covered by economic capital B) Deducted — they are a cost of doing business C) Added back — they are non-cash D) Multiplied by beta\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** EL is an anticipated cost, deducted from revenue; capital covers **unexpected** losses.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** An investor holds a single mutual fund as her entire investment portfolio. The most appropriate measure for comparing candidate funds is the:\nA) Treynor ratio B) Jensen's alpha C) Sharpe ratio D) Information ratio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** When the fund is the whole portfolio, idiosyncratic risk is borne in full — use total risk: Sharpe. Treynor/alpha suit components of a diversified whole; IR suits benchmark mandates.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Which performance measure?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Whole portfolio\",\"items\":[\"total risk borne\",\"use SHARPE\",\"denominator = sigma\"]},{\"h\":\"Component of portfolio\",\"items\":[\"only systematic risk\",\"use Treynor/alpha\",\"denominator = beta\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** An active manager's annual returns exceed the benchmark by 1.8% on average, with tracking error of 4.5%. Her information ratio is closest to:\nA) 0.40 B) 2.50 C) 0.18 D) 0.045\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$IR = 1.8\\%/4.5\\% = 0.40$$. Distractor B inverts the ratio.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q17.** Fund M returned 14% with σ = 22% and β = 1.2. The market returned 10%, σ = 16%, and the risk-free rate is 2%. Which is correct?\nA) Sharpe = 0.545; Treynor = 10.0%; α = +2.4% B) Sharpe = 0.545; Treynor = 11.7%; α = +2.4% C) Sharpe = 0.636; Treynor = 10.0%; α = +4.0% D) Sharpe = 0.545; Treynor = 10.0%; α = −2.4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Sharpe: $$(14-2)/22 = 0.545$$. Treynor: $$(14-2)/1.2 = 10.0\\%$$. Alpha: $$14 - [2 + 1.2(10-2)] = 14 - 11.6 = +2.4\\%$$.\n\nB's Treynor wrongly divides by σ-scaled beta; C's Sharpe uses the market σ; D flips the alpha sign.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A business unit generates revenues of \\$60m, costs of \\$18m, and expected losses of \\$12m. Economic capital is \\$120m, invested at the risk-free rate of 3%. Ignoring taxes, the unit's RAROC is closest to:\nA) 25.0% B) 28.0% C) 32.4% D) 22.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$RAROC = \\frac{60 - 18 - 12 + 0.03 \\times 120}{120} = \\frac{30 + 3.6}{120} = \\frac{33.6}{120} = 28.0\\%$$\n\nDistractor A (25%) omits the return on economic capital; D divides by gross revenue components incorrectly.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"RAROC\",\"steps\":[\"Revenue\",\"- costs\",\"- expected loss\",\"+ return on econ. capital\",\"/ economic capital\",\"= RAROC\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q19.** Using Q19's unit: its equity beta is 1.4 and the expected market return is 9% (risk-free 3%). Under the adjusted RAROC rule, the unit:\nA) Destroys value, because ARAROC is below the risk-free rate B) Creates value, because ARAROC exceeds the risk-free rate C) Creates value, because RAROC exceeds the market return D) Cannot be evaluated without the hurdle rate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$ARAROC = 28.0\\% - 1.4 \\times (9\\% - 3\\%) = 28.0\\% - 8.4\\% = 19.6\\% > R_f = 3\\%$$\n\nARAROC exceeds the risk-free rate, so the unit clears its systematic-risk-adjusted hurdle. C states the wrong comparison; the ARAROC rule benchmarks against $$R_f$$, not $$E[R_m]$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Adjusted RAROC decision\",\"steps\":[\"RAROC 28%\",\"- beta x (Rm - Rf) = 8.4%\",\"ARAROC = 19.6%\",\"compare to Rf = 3%\",\"19.6% > 3% -> create value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q20.** A multi-strategy fund is held as one of fifty sleeves inside a fully diversified institutional portfolio. Over the year the sleeve returned 14% with a standard deviation of 30% and a beta of 0.8. A second candidate sleeve returned 11% with a standard deviation of 16% and a beta of 1.1. The market returned 9% and the risk-free rate is 3%. Which sleeve adds more value to the existing diversified portfolio, and on which measure should the decision be based?\nA) The first sleeve, based on its higher Sharpe ratio B) The first sleeve, based on its higher Treynor ratio C) The second sleeve, based on its higher Treynor ratio D) The second sleeve, based on its higher Sharpe ratio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: choose the better *sleeve within an already diversified portfolio* and name the correct measure.\n\n1. Because each candidate is just one of many holdings in a diversified whole, idiosyncratic risk is already diversified away — only **systematic risk (beta)** matters. The correct yardstick is the **Treynor ratio**, not Sharpe.\n2. Treynor formula: $$T_p = \\dfrac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\beta_p}$$\n3. Sleeve 1: $$T_1 = \\dfrac{14 - 3}{0.8} = \\dfrac{11}{0.8} = 13.75\\%$$\n4. Sleeve 2: $$T_2 = \\dfrac{11 - 3}{1.1} = \\dfrac{8}{1.1} = 7.27\\%$$\n5. Sleeve 1 has the higher Treynor ratio (13.75% vs. 7.27%), so it adds more per unit of systematic risk.\n\nWhy B is correct: the context (one sleeve of many) dictates Treynor, and sleeve 1 wins on it.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A names the right sleeve but the wrong measure. Sleeve 1's Sharpe is $$\\frac{14-3}{30}=0.367$$, *lower* than sleeve 2's $$\\frac{11-3}{16}=0.50$$ — its huge 30% σ (mostly idiosyncratic) makes it look bad on Sharpe. Using Sharpe here is the classic \"wrong-context\" error.\n- C uses the right measure (Treynor) but mis-ranks: it claims sleeve 2 has the higher Treynor, which is false (7.27% < 13.75%).\n- D picks the higher Sharpe sleeve — correct math for Sharpe, but Sharpe is the wrong measure for a diversified component.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Match measure to context first, *then* compute. \"One of many funds\" → Treynor/alpha (beta risk); \"entire wealth / stand-alone\" → Sharpe (total σ). A fund with big idiosyncratic risk looks great on Treynor and awful on Sharpe — that gap is the test.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sleeve of many -> Treynor\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sleeve 1\",\"items\":[\"Treynor 11/0.8 = 13.75%\",\"Sharpe 11/30 = 0.37\",\"wins on Treynor\"]},{\"h\":\"Sleeve 2\",\"items\":[\"Treynor 8/1.1 = 7.27%\",\"Sharpe 8/16 = 0.50\",\"wins on Sharpe (wrong context)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** An analyst computes the following for an actively managed equity fund and its benchmark over five years: fund mean return 13.2%, benchmark mean return 10.0%, fund σ = 19%, benchmark σ = 15%, correlation between fund and benchmark = 0.90. The annualized standard deviation of active (fund minus benchmark) returns is to be used as tracking error. The fund's information ratio is closest to:\nA) 0.36 B) 0.40 C) 0.64 D) 0.21\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the information ratio, where tracking error must be derived from the variance of active returns.\n\n1. Active return = $$R_p - R_b$$. Its variance: $$\\sigma^2_{p-b} = \\sigma_p^2 + \\sigma_b^2 - 2\\rho\\,\\sigma_p\\sigma_b$$\n2. Plug in: $$\\sigma^2_{p-b} = 0.19^2 + 0.15^2 - 2(0.90)(0.19)(0.15)$$\n3. Compute pieces: $$0.0361 + 0.0225 - 2(0.90)(0.0285) = 0.0586 - 0.0513 = 0.0073$$\n4. Tracking error: $$TE = \\sqrt{0.0073} = 0.08544 \\approx 8.54\\%$$\n5. Information ratio: $$IR = \\dfrac{E[R_p]-E[R_b]}{TE} = \\dfrac{13.2\\% - 10.0\\%}{8.54\\%} = \\dfrac{3.2\\%}{8.54\\%} \\approx 0.375 \\approx 0.40$$\n\nWhy B is correct: 0.375 rounds to roughly 0.40 and is the only option consistent with the proper TE derivation.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A (0.36) results from using $$TE = \\sqrt{0.0073}$$ but a slightly different rounding chain; more commonly it comes from a small arithmetic slip in the cross term. It is close enough to be a deliberate \"almost-right\" lure — recompute carefully.\n- C (0.64) comes from wrongly using $$TE = \\sigma_p - \\sigma_b = 19\\% - 15\\% = 4\\%$$ → $$3.2/4 = 0.80$$... or from forgetting correlation entirely and using $$\\sqrt{0.0361+0.0225}=0.242$$. Either way it mishandles the variance-of-difference formula.\n- D (0.21) comes from forgetting that variance subtracts the covariance term, e.g., adding instead: $$\\sqrt{0.0586+0.0513}$$ gives ~33% → 0.097. Adding the covariance instead of subtracting it overstates TE and shrinks IR.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Tracking error is the σ of the *difference* of two correlated series — you must use $$\\sigma_p^2+\\sigma_b^2-2\\rho\\sigma_p\\sigma_b$$, never $$\\sigma_p-\\sigma_b$$. High correlation (here 0.90) shrinks TE sharply, which is why active managers track closely to a benchmark.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Info ratio ~ 0.40\",\"steps\":[\"var = .19^2+.15^2-2(.9)(.19)(.15)\",\"= 0.0073\",\"TE = sqrt = 8.54%\",\"active = 13.2-10 = 3.2%\",\"3.2/8.54 = 0.375\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A lending unit reports for the year: revenues (interest + fees) $120 million, operating costs $40 million, expected losses $30 million, and taxes $8 million. The economic capital allocated is $200 million, invested in risk-free assets earning 5%. Transfer-price credits add $2 million to income. What is the unit's RAROC?\nA) 27.0% B) 24.0% C) 22.0% D) 21.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute RAROC using the full expanded numerator.\n\n1. RAROC formula: $$RAROC = \\dfrac{\\text{revenues} - \\text{costs} - EL - \\text{taxes} + (R_f \\times EC) \\pm \\text{transfer prices}}{EC}$$\n2. Return on economic capital: $$R_f \\times EC = 0.05 \\times 200 = 10$$ million.\n3. Numerator, step by step: $$120 - 40 = 80;\\; 80 - 30 = 50;\\; 50 - 8 = 42;\\; 42 + 10 = 52;\\; 52 + 2 = 54$$ million.\n4. RAROC: $$\\dfrac{54}{200} = 0.27 = 27\\%$$.\n\nWhy A is correct: every income item is netted (revenues less costs, expected losses, and taxes), then the return earned on economic capital and the transfer-price credit are added back, all divided by economic capital.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- B (24.0%) drops the transfer-price credit *and* mishandles one item: e.g., $$(120-40-30-8+10)/200 = 52/200 = 26\\%$$ is close; 24% arises from also netting transfer prices as a cost: $$(120-40-30-8+10-2)/200 = 50/200 = 25\\%$$ — these \"sign-of-transfer-price\" slips cluster near the right answer to bait you.\n- C (22.0%) omits the $10m return-on-capital add-back entirely: $$(120-40-30-8+0+2)/200 = 44/200 = 22\\%$$. Forgetting to add $$R_f \\times EC$$ is the single most common RAROC error.\n- D (21.0%) omits *both* the return on capital and treats transfer prices as a deduction: $$(120-40-30-8-2)/200 = 40/200 = 20\\%$$, near 21%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In the RAROC numerator, expected losses are *subtracted* (a priced cost), while the *return earned on the economic capital* (≈ $$R_f \\times EC$$) and transfer-price credits are *added back*. Missing the capital-return add-back is the most common slip.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"RAROC = 27%\",\"steps\":[\"120 - 40 = 80\",\"- EL 30 = 50\",\"- tax 8 = 42\",\"+ cap ret 10 + xfer 2 = 54\",\"/ EC 200 = 27%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A bank's structured-credit business has a RAROC of 16%. Its equity beta is 1.4, the expected market return is 11%, and the risk-free rate is 3%. The bank's overall hurdle rate (weighted cost of equity capital) is 13%. Using adjusted RAROC (ARAROC), the project should be:\nA) Accepted, because RAROC (16%) exceeds the hurdle rate (13%) B) Rejected, because ARAROC (4.8%) exceeds the risk-free rate (3%) C) Accepted, because ARAROC (4.8%) exceeds the risk-free rate (3%) D) Rejected, because ARAROC (4.8%) is below the hurdle rate (13%)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: apply the *ARAROC* decision rule, not the plain RAROC-vs-hurdle rule.\n\n1. ARAROC formula: $$ARAROC = RAROC - \\beta_E\\big(E[R_m] - R_f\\big)$$\n2. Market risk premium: $$E[R_m] - R_f = 11\\% - 3\\% = 8\\%$$.\n3. Systematic risk charge: $$\\beta_E \\times 8\\% = 1.4 \\times 8\\% = 11.2\\%$$.\n4. ARAROC: $$16\\% - 11.2\\% = 4.8\\%$$.\n5. Decision rule: accept if $$ARAROC > R_f$$. Here $$4.8\\% > 3\\%$$ → **accept**.\n\nWhy C is correct: the ARAROC rule benchmarks against the *risk-free rate* (the systematic risk has already been charged in the adjustment), and 4.8% clears 3%.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A applies the *plain* RAROC-vs-hurdle test. That gives \"accept\" too, but the question demands the ARAROC method; A also fails to charge for the business's high beta, which is the whole point of ARAROC.\n- B does the math right (ARAROC = 4.8% > 3%) but mislabels the verdict as \"Rejected\" — a careless reading where the comparison supports acceptance.\n- D compares ARAROC to the *hurdle rate* (13%). That is the wrong benchmark for ARAROC — once beta is netted out, the correct comparison is to $$R_f$$, not the hurdle.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two distinct rules. Plain **RAROC > hurdle rate** (cost of equity capital). **ARAROC > risk-free rate** — because ARAROC has already subtracted the CAPM systematic-risk charge $$\\beta_E(E[R_m]-R_f)$$. Don't compare ARAROC to the hurdle rate.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"RAROC vs ARAROC rule\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Plain RAROC\",\"items\":[\"compare to hurdle rate\",\"no beta charge\",\"16% vs 13%\"]},{\"h\":\"ARAROC\",\"items\":[\"subtract beta(Rm-Rf) first\",\"compare to risk-free rate\",\"4.8% vs 3% -> accept\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A long-volatility hedge fund and a short-volatility hedge fund both report a mean annual return of 12% and identical standard deviations of 10%, giving identical Sharpe ratios. The short-vol fund, however, has frequent small gains and rare large losses, while the long-vol fund has frequent small losses and rare large gains. The minimum acceptable return is 4%. The short-vol fund's downside deviation is 12% and the long-vol fund's is 5%. Which statement is most accurate?\nA) The Sortino ratios are equal because mean returns and MARs are equal B) The long-vol fund has the higher Sortino ratio (1.60 vs. 0.67), better reflecting the asymmetry of returns C) Sharpe is the superior measure here because it already captures the asymmetry D) The short-vol fund has the higher Sortino ratio because its volatility is mostly upside\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compare the two funds using the Sortino ratio and explain why it is the right tool for asymmetric returns.\n\n1. Sortino formula: $$\\text{Sortino} = \\dfrac{E[R_p] - R_{min}}{\\sigma_{down}}$$\n2. Long-vol fund: $$\\dfrac{12\\% - 4\\%}{5\\%} = \\dfrac{8}{5} = 1.60$$\n3. Short-vol fund: $$\\dfrac{12\\% - 4\\%}{12\\%} = \\dfrac{8}{12} = 0.67$$\n4. The long-vol fund has the higher Sortino ratio. Sortino penalizes only *downside* deviation (returns below the MAR), so it correctly rewards the long-vol fund whose bad outcomes are mild and punishes the short-vol fund whose bad outcomes are severe — even though Sharpe rates them identically.\n\nWhy B is correct: identical Sharpe ratios hide the asymmetry; downside deviation differs (5% vs. 12%), so Sortino separates the funds correctly.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A confuses total σ with downside deviation. Equal means and MARs do *not* make Sortino equal — the denominators (downside deviations) differ.\n- C is backward: Sharpe uses *total* σ and treats upside and downside volatility identically, so it *cannot* capture asymmetry — that is precisely why Sortino exists.\n- D mis-describes the short-vol fund. Short-vol strategies have *rare large losses* (fat left tail), so their downside deviation is high (12%), giving a *lower* Sortino. The claim that its volatility is \"mostly upside\" is false.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When returns are asymmetric (options strategies, hedge funds), Sharpe is misleading because it punishes upside volatility equally. Sortino divides excess return by *downside* deviation only — favoring positively skewed payoffs and exposing hidden tail risk in negatively skewed ones (the Niederhoffer short-vol trap).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Same Sharpe, different Sortino\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Long-vol fund\",\"items\":[\"downside dev 5%\",\"Sortino 8/5 = 1.60\",\"rare large gains\"]},{\"h\":\"Short-vol fund\",\"items\":[\"downside dev 12%\",\"Sortino 8/12 = 0.67\",\"rare large losses\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A risk committee is told that Business Unit X's stand-alone economic capital is $400 million, but its *marginal* contribution to firm-wide economic capital — after diversification benefits with other units — is only $260 million. Unit X generates $52 million of risk-adjusted net income (including return on capital). Which statement best describes the implication for RAROC-based capital allocation?\nA) RAROC must always be computed on stand-alone capital to avoid double counting B) Using marginal capital ($260m) gives RAROC = 20%, versus 13% on stand-alone capital — the choice of capital base materially changes the unit's apparent profitability C) The diversification benefit should be ignored because it cannot be measured D) RAROC is unaffected by the choice of capital base since net income is fixed\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: show how the diversification/capital-allocation choice changes RAROC and identify the correct implication.\n\n1. RAROC on **stand-alone** capital: $$\\dfrac{52}{400} = 0.13 = 13\\%$$\n2. RAROC on **marginal (diversified)** capital: $$\\dfrac{52}{260} = 0.20 = 20\\%$$\n3. The numerator (net income) is fixed, but the denominator (economic capital) is not — using the smaller marginal capital base lifts RAROC from 13% to 20%. A unit that fails a 15% hurdle on stand-alone capital *passes* on marginal capital.\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly computes both figures and identifies the central limitation — how the diversification benefit is allocated changes every unit's RAROC and thus capital-allocation and pricing decisions.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A overstates a rule. There is no universal mandate to use stand-alone capital; in fact marginal/diversified capital often better reflects the unit's true contribution. The \"always\" is the giveaway.\n- C is wrong because diversification benefits *can* be estimated (correlations, marginal capital) and ignoring them distorts incentives — it is a challenge to *allocate*, not a reason to discard.\n- D is mathematically false: changing the denominator changes the ratio even with fixed income (13% vs. 20%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: A unit's stand-alone economic capital always exceeds its marginal contribution to firm-wide capital (because of diversification). Whichever base the firm picks shifts every unit's RAROC — a recognized limitation of RAROC for capital allocation and a favorite \"challenges of risk-adjusted measures\" question.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Capital base changes RAROC\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Stand-alone EC 400\",\"items\":[\"52 / 400\",\"= 13%\",\"fails 15% hurdle\"]},{\"h\":\"Marginal EC 260\",\"items\":[\"52 / 260\",\"= 20%\",\"passes 15% hurdle\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Two equity funds are evaluated against the CAPM. Fund P returned 10% with β = 0.6; Fund Q returned 14% with β = 1.6. The market returned 9% and the risk-free rate is 2%. An analyst ranks the funds by Jensen's alpha and concludes Fund Q is the superior manager. What is the most accurate critique?\nA) The analyst is correct; Q's alpha of +1.4% exceeds P's +3.8%, so Q is better B) Jensen's alpha is an absolute measure and should not be used to rank funds with very different betas; on a per-unit-of-beta basis P is superior despite a smaller alpha C) Alpha cannot be computed without the funds' standard deviations D) Both funds have negative alpha, so neither manager added value\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: critique ranking dissimilar-beta funds by raw Jensen's alpha.\n\n1. Jensen's alpha: $$\\alpha_p = E[R_p] - \\big[R_f + \\beta_p(E[R_m]-R_f)\\big]$$, with market premium $$9\\%-2\\%=7\\%$$.\n2. Fund P: $$\\alpha_P = 10 - [2 + 0.6(7)] = 10 - [2 + 4.2] = 10 - 6.2 = +3.8\\%$$\n3. Fund Q: $$\\alpha_Q = 14 - [2 + 1.6(7)] = 14 - [2 + 11.2] = 14 - 13.2 = +0.8\\%$$\n4. P's alpha (+3.8%) actually *exceeds* Q's (+0.8%). Moreover, alpha is an absolute percentage, not scaled by risk taken; to compare managers with very different betas you should scale alpha by beta (or use the appraisal/Treynor framework). P delivers far more alpha while taking on much less systematic risk.\n\nWhy B is correct: alpha is absolute, so it does not rank funds of different beta cleanly; once you account for that, P is the superior manager.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A miscomputes both alphas (it swaps the values and claims Q = +1.4%, P = +3.8% then calls Q better — internally contradictory) and accepts naive alpha ranking. Q's true alpha is only +0.8%.\n- C confuses alpha (needs beta) with Sharpe/Sortino (need σ). Jensen's alpha uses beta, not standard deviation — σ is not required.\n- D is factually wrong: both alphas are positive (+3.8% and +0.8%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Jensen's alpha is a *level* (percentage), not a ratio, so it cannot fairly rank funds with different betas — a low-beta fund earning a smaller-looking return can still post a higher alpha and a far higher alpha-per-unit-of-beta. To rank, scale by risk (Treynor, or the appraisal ratio alpha/σ_ε).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Alpha is a level, not a ranking\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Fund P (beta 0.6)\",\"items\":[\"req 2+0.6(7)=6.2%\",\"alpha 10-6.2 = +3.8%\",\"more alpha, less risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Fund Q (beta 1.6)\",\"items\":[\"req 2+1.6(7)=13.2%\",\"alpha 14-13.2 = +0.8%\",\"high beta, small alpha\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"108","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"Enterprise Risk Management and Future Trends","notes":"# 8. Enterprise Risk Management and Future Trends\n\nERM versus silo risk management, why firms adopt it, how to implement it, risk culture, and the role of scenario analysis. Qualitative, definition-heavy, and a reliable source of one or two exam questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe enterprise risk management (ERM) and compare an ERM program with a traditional silo-based approach.\n- Describe the motivations for a firm to adopt an ERM initiative.\n- Explain best practices for the governance and implementation of an ERM program.\n- Describe risk culture, the characteristics of a strong risk culture, and the challenges to establishing one.\n- Explain the role of scenario analysis in ERM and describe its advantages and disadvantages.\n- Explain the use of scenario analysis in stress testing programs and capital planning.\n\n## ERM vs. silo risk management\n\n**ERM** is a comprehensive, integrated, firm-wide approach: all material risks — market, credit, operational, liquidity, strategic, reputational — are identified, measured where possible, and managed **together**, against a single board-approved risk appetite, with risk explicitly tied to strategy and capital.\n\n| | Silo approach | ERM |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope | Each risk type managed separately by its own unit | All risks viewed as one portfolio |\n| Interactions | Missed — correlations and concentrations across silos invisible | Explicitly captured (e.g., same counterparty in trading, lending, ops) |\n| Appetite | Implicit, fragmented limits | Single firm-wide appetite cascaded down |\n| Hedging | Each silo hedges its own risk — may over-hedge | Natural offsets netted first; only net exposure hedged (cheaper) |\n| Link to strategy/capital | Weak | Central — risk informs strategic choices and capital allocation |\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> ERM is not \"more risk measurement\" — it is an organizational and governance change: one risk appetite, one aggregated view, one accountable executive (typically the CRO), and risk embedded in strategic decisions.\n\n## Why adopt ERM\n\n- **Capture risk interactions/concentrations** that silos miss (the 2008 lesson: correlated exposures everywhere).\n- **Hedging efficiency** — netting natural offsets before buying protection lowers cost.\n- **Better capital allocation** — one consistent economic-capital framework across businesses (links to RAROC, Ch 7).\n- **Earnings stability and lower distress probability** — integrated view reduces nasty surprises.\n- **Regulatory and rating-agency pressure** — supervisors and S&P/Moody's assess ERM quality.\n- **Strategic advantage** — firms that understand their aggregate risk can take the *right* risks faster.\n\nCosts/obstacles: expensive data and systems integration, turf resistance, hard-to-quantify risks forced into one framework, and false comfort if the aggregate model is wrong.\n\n## Implementing ERM: best practices\n\n1. **Board ownership** — the board approves the risk appetite and holds management to it (Ch 3).\n2. **A CRO with stature** — firm-wide mandate, independent reporting line.\n3. **Risk appetite cascaded** into consistent limits and policies across all risk types.\n4. **Common risk language and taxonomy** so businesses report comparably.\n5. **Integrated data and aggregation capability** — timely, accurate, firm-wide (the thrust of the Basel risk-data aggregation principles).\n6. **Risk in decision-making** — pricing, product approval, M&A, compensation all use risk-adjusted metrics.\n7. **Continuous improvement** — back-test, learn from near-misses, refresh scenarios.\n\n## Risk culture\n\n**Risk culture** is the shared attitudes, values, and behaviors that determine how people actually identify, discuss, and act on risk — what employees do when no one is watching.\n\nMarkers of a **strong** risk culture (per FSB guidance):\n\n- **Tone from the top** — leaders model the behavior, not just memo it.\n- **Accountability** — individuals own the risks they create; breaches have consequences.\n- **Effective communication and challenge** — bad news travels up fast; dissent is safe (\"psychological safety\"); no shooting the messenger.\n- **Incentives** — compensation and promotion reward risk-adjusted performance and good escalation.\n\nChallenges: short-term profit pressure, star-trader cultures, growth-at-all-costs strategy, merger integration of incompatible cultures, measuring culture at all, and \"tick-box\" compliance masquerading as culture.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: a firm can have a state-of-the-art ERM *framework* and still fail through weak risk *culture* — policies don't stop a desk that hides positions or a management that ignores escalations (Barings, SocGen, the London Whale all had limit frameworks). Culture questions are answered by behavior, not documents.\n\n## Scenario analysis, stress testing, and capital planning\n\n**Scenario analysis** evaluates the firm-wide impact of specified adverse states — historical (1987 crash, 2008) or hypothetical/forward-looking (cyber attack + funding freeze) — moving **many risk factors coherently at once**.\n\nAdvantages:\n- Captures tail events and risk interactions that probabilistic models (VaR) miss.\n- No probability estimates required; intuitive narrative for boards.\n- Forward-looking — can include never-observed events.\n- Reveals concentrations and channels of contagion across silos — the natural ERM tool.\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Scenario choice is subjective; \"failure of imagination\" risk.\n- No likelihoods attached — hard to convert results into capital mechanically.\n- Hypothetical scenarios may be internally inconsistent; historical ones may never recur.\n- Resource-intensive; results sensitive to modeling assumptions in translating scenario to P&L.\n\n**In stress testing and capital planning:** regulators (CCAR/DFAST, EBA stress tests) prescribe macro scenarios that banks must translate into projected losses, revenues, and capital ratios over a multi-year horizon; banks must show they stay above minimum capital **under stress**, and dividend/buyback plans depend on passing. Internally, reverse stress testing asks \"what scenario breaks us?\" Scenario results feed the capital buffer over and above model-based economic capital.\n\n## Future trends\n\nGARP flags the moving frontier: cyber risk and data security, machine learning/AI in risk models (and the model risk it creates), climate and ESG risk integration, fintech/crypto disruption, and ever-greater demands on risk data aggregation and real-time reporting.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is enterprise risk management (ERM)?\n> **A:** A comprehensive, integrated, firm-wide approach in which all material risks are managed together as one portfolio against a single board-approved risk appetite, with risk tied to strategy and capital.\n\n**Q2.** ERM vs. silo approach — the key distinction?\n> **A:** Silos manage each risk type separately and miss cross-silo correlations/concentrations. ERM views all risks as one portfolio under a single firm-wide appetite, capturing interactions.\n\n**Q3.** Exam trap: is ERM just \"more risk measurement\"?\n> **A:** No. It is an organizational and governance change — one appetite, one aggregated view, one accountable executive (CRO), and risk embedded in strategic decisions.\n\n**Q4.** What is the hedging-cost argument for ERM?\n> **A:** Silos each hedge separately and ignore natural offsets, causing over-hedging. ERM nets natural offsets first and hedges only the net exposure — cheaper.\n\n**Q5.** Name motivations for adopting ERM.\n> **A:** Capture risk interactions/concentrations, hedging efficiency, better capital allocation (one economic-capital framework), earnings stability/lower distress, regulatory and rating-agency pressure, and strategic advantage.\n\n**Q6.** Name the main costs/obstacles to ERM.\n> **A:** Expensive data and systems integration, turf resistance, hard-to-quantify risks forced into one framework, and false comfort if the aggregate model is wrong.\n\n**Q7.** Name ERM implementation best practices.\n> **A:** Board ownership of appetite, a CRO with stature and independent reporting, appetite cascaded into limits, common risk taxonomy, integrated data/aggregation, risk in decision-making, and continuous improvement.\n\n**Q8.** What is risk culture?\n> **A:** The shared attitudes, values, and behaviors that determine how people actually identify, discuss, and act on risk — what employees do when no one is watching.\n\n**Q9.** Markers of a strong risk culture (per FSB)?\n> **A:** Tone from the top, accountability (breaches have consequences), effective communication and challenge (bad news travels up; dissent is safe), and incentives that reward risk-adjusted performance and escalation.\n\n**Q10.** Exam trap: can a strong ERM framework coexist with failure?\n> **A:** Yes. A firm can have a state-of-the-art ERM framework and still fail through weak risk culture (Barings, SocGen, London Whale all had limit frameworks). Culture questions are answered by behavior, not documents.\n\n**Q11.** What is scenario analysis in ERM?\n> **A:** Evaluating the firm-wide impact of specified adverse states (historical or hypothetical/forward-looking) by moving many risk factors coherently at once.\n\n**Q12.** Advantages of scenario analysis over VaR?\n> **A:** Captures tail events and interactions VaR misses, needs no probability estimates, is forward-looking (can include never-observed events), and reveals concentrations and contagion channels across silos.\n\n**Q13.** Disadvantages of scenario analysis?\n> **A:** Scenario choice is subjective (\"failure of imagination\"), no likelihoods attached, hypothetical scenarios may be inconsistent, and results are resource-intensive and sensitive to modeling assumptions.\n\n**Q14.** What is reverse stress testing?\n> **A:** Asking \"what scenario breaks us?\" — starting from firm failure and working back to the conditions that cause it, feeding the capital buffer above model-based economic capital.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Which statement best describes how enterprise risk management (ERM) differs from a traditional silo approach?\nA) ERM produces more separate risk reports, one per risk type B) ERM manages all material risks together as one portfolio against a single firm-wide risk appetite C) ERM removes the need for any business-line risk owners D) ERM only measures market risk, leaving other risks to the silos\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is asking for the core idea of ERM versus the old silo style.\n\nIn a silo approach, each kind of risk (market, credit, operational, and so on) is handled by its own separate team, and no one looks at how the risks add up across the whole firm. ERM instead pulls everything together: all material risks are viewed as a single portfolio and judged against ONE board-approved risk appetite, with risk tied to strategy and capital. That is exactly what B says, so B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: silos are like each player on a soccer team watching only their own patch of grass; ERM is like a coach watching the whole field at once.\n\n- A is wrong: ERM is not about producing MORE reports; it is about one integrated, aggregated view. More paperwork is not the point.\n- C is wrong: ERM still relies on business-line risk owners; it adds firm-wide coordination on top, it does not abolish local ownership.\n- D is wrong: ERM covers ALL material risks (market, credit, operational, liquidity, strategic, reputational), not just market risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The one-line definition to memorize: ERM = all risks as one portfolio, against one firm-wide risk appetite, linked to strategy and capital.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Silo vs ERM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Silo\",\"items\":[\"each risk its own team\",\"no aggregate view\",\"separate reports\"]},{\"h\":\"ERM\",\"items\":[\"all risks as one portfolio\",\"one firm-wide appetite\",\"linked to strategy/capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which of the following is a HEDGING-COST argument in favor of adopting ERM?\nA) ERM removes the need to hedge at all B) By netting natural offsets across the firm before hedging, ERM avoids over-hedging and lowers hedge cost C) Silo hedging is illegal under Basel III D) ERM guarantees lower derivative bid-ask spreads from dealers\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks specifically for the argument about saving money on hedging.\n\nWhen each silo hedges its own risk separately, two desks might be hedging exposures that already cancel each other out. For example, one desk is long an exposure and another is short the same thing — together the firm is roughly flat, but each silo buys protection anyway. ERM looks across the firm first, NETS those natural offsets, and only hedges the leftover net exposure. Less hedging bought means lower cost. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: if one family member turns the heater up while another opens the window, coordinating first saves the energy bill.\n\n- A is wrong: ERM does not eliminate hedging; it makes hedging more efficient.\n- C is wrong: silo hedging is not illegal under Basel III; that is a made-up claim.\n- D is wrong: ERM does not guarantee tighter dealer spreads; the saving comes from hedging less, not from cheaper quotes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The hedging-cost benefit of ERM is \"net first, then hedge only the leftover.\" It reduces over-hedging, not the existence of hedging.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ERM hedging saving\",\"steps\":[\"aggregate firm-wide\",\"net natural offsets\",\"hedge only the residual\",\"lower hedge cost\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Under best-practice ERM governance, who has ultimate ownership of the firm's risk appetite?\nA) The head of the trading desk B) The external auditor C) The board of directors D) Individual line managers acting independently\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks who is ultimately responsible for approving and owning the firm's risk appetite.\n\nIn sound ERM, the board of directors approves the risk appetite and holds management accountable to it. The appetite then cascades down into limits and policies across all risk types, and a CRO (Chief Risk Officer) with firm-wide stature oversees the framework day to day. But the buck stops at the board. So C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: the board sets the speed limit for the whole company; the CRO is the traffic officer who makes sure everyone obeys it.\n\n- A is wrong: a trading desk head runs one part of the business; they do not own the firm-wide appetite.\n- B is wrong: the external auditor checks the books from outside; they do not set or own risk appetite.\n- D is wrong: line managers operating independently is the silo problem ERM is meant to fix; appetite must be set centrally, not by each manager alone.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Board approves and owns the risk appetite; the CRO implements and oversees it with an independent reporting line.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Risk appetite ownership\",\"root\":\"Board owns risk appetite\",\"children\":[\"CRO implements & oversees\",\"Limits & policies cascade\",\"Business lines operate within\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Risk culture is best described as:\nA) The set of formal limit documents a firm files with regulators B) The shared attitudes, values, and behaviors that drive how people actually act on risk, especially when no one is watching C) The amount of economic capital a firm holds D) The number of risk committees a firm operates\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the meaning of \"risk culture.\"\n\nRisk culture is about behavior, not paperwork. It is the shared attitudes, values, and habits that decide what employees really do when faced with risk — including what they do when no supervisor is looking. A firm can have perfect written policies but still behave badly. So B captures the idea.\n\nAnalogy: a household can have a \"no junk food\" rule on the fridge door, but the culture is whether everyone actually follows it when home alone.\n\n- A is wrong: formal limit documents are part of the framework, not the culture. Culture is how people behave around those documents.\n- C is wrong: economic capital is a number measuring risk capacity, not a description of culture.\n- D is wrong: counting committees measures structure, not the lived behaviors that define culture.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Culture questions are answered by behavior, not documents. A great framework with a weak culture still fails (Barings, SocGen, the London Whale all had limit frameworks).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Framework vs Culture\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Framework (paper)\",\"items\":[\"limit documents\",\"committees\",\"policies on file\"]},{\"h\":\"Culture (behavior)\",\"items\":[\"shared values & habits\",\"what people do unwatched\",\"drives real conduct\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which observation most strongly signals a WEAK risk culture?\nA) The CRO reports directly to the board's risk committee B) Junior staff are afraid to escalate concerns about a highly profitable desk C) Compensation includes deferred, risk-adjusted components D) The board receives a quarterly aggregated risk report\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which sign points to a poor risk culture.\n\nA hallmark of a strong culture is that bad news travels up quickly and people feel safe to challenge or escalate (\"psychological safety,\" no shooting the messenger). If junior staff are scared to raise concerns about a profitable desk, that is exactly the opposite — an untouchable profit center where problems get buried. That is a classic red flag, so B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: in a healthy kitchen, the new cook can tell the head chef \"this chicken smells off.\" In a toxic one, they stay silent and someone gets sick.\n\n- A is wrong: a CRO reporting directly to the board's risk committee is a sign of GOOD governance (independence), not weakness.\n- C is wrong: deferred, risk-adjusted pay is a good practice — it rewards sustainable, prudent performance.\n- D is wrong: the board receiving an aggregated risk report is healthy ERM behavior, not a warning sign.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Suppressed escalation and \"untouchable\" profitable desks are the textbook culture red flags. Look for behavior that silences bad news.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Weak-culture red flag\",\"options\":[\"A: CRO reports to board\",\"B: staff fear to escalate on a profitable desk\",\"C: deferred risk-adjusted pay\",\"D: board gets aggregated report\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Suppressed escalation around an untouchable profit center is the classic weak-culture sign.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A key advantage of scenario analysis over a standard VaR model is that scenario analysis:\nA) Produces a precise probability for each loss outcome B) Requires no assumptions or management judgment C) Can capture coherent, multi-factor extreme events that may never have appeared in historical data D) Always gives a smaller estimated loss than VaR\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for a genuine strength of scenario analysis relative to VaR.\n\nScenario analysis describes a specific adverse state of the world (say, a cyber attack combined with a funding freeze) and moves MANY risk factors together in a coherent story. It is forward-looking, so it can include events that have never been seen in the data — which a history-based model like VaR can miss. So C is the real advantage.\n\nAnalogy: VaR is like predicting tomorrow's weather from past records; scenario analysis is like asking \"what if a hurricane AND a heatwave hit at once?\" even if that exact combo never happened before.\n\n- A is wrong: scenario analysis does NOT attach precise probabilities to outcomes; that is actually one of its disadvantages.\n- B is wrong: scenarios rely heavily on judgment to choose and build them; \"no assumptions\" is false.\n- D is wrong: there is no rule that scenario losses are always smaller than VaR; often they reveal LARGER tail losses.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Scenario analysis strengths = forward-looking, multi-factor, captures tail interactions. Weaknesses = subjective choice, no probabilities attached, resource-intensive.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Scenario analysis\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"history-calibrated\",\"attaches probabilities\",\"can miss new tails\"]},{\"h\":\"Scenario\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking\",\"multi-factor coherent events\",\"no probability attached\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which is a recognized DISADVANTAGE of scenario analysis?\nA) It produces objective probabilities that cannot be questioned B) The choice of scenarios is subjective, risking a \"failure of imagination\" C) It is mathematically guaranteed to capture every possible crisis D) It is free of any modeling assumptions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a true weakness of scenario analysis.\n\nBecause humans pick which scenarios to test, they may simply fail to imagine the crisis that actually happens. This subjectivity — the \"failure of imagination\" — is a well-known drawback. Scenarios also lack attached probabilities and can be resource-intensive. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a fire drill only prepares you for the fires you thought of; if you never imagined a flood, the flood still catches you off guard.\n\n- A is wrong: scenario analysis does NOT produce objective, unquestionable probabilities; it attaches no probabilities at all.\n- C is wrong: nothing guarantees every crisis is captured; that is the very flaw being described.\n- D is wrong: translating a scenario into profit-and-loss requires many modeling assumptions, so it is not assumption-free.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The big knock against scenario analysis is subjectivity (you only test what you think of) plus no probabilities. Pair these with its forward-looking strength.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Scenario analysis weakness\",\"options\":[\"A: objective unquestionable probabilities\",\"B: subjective choice, failure of imagination\",\"C: guaranteed to capture every crisis\",\"D: free of all assumptions\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Humans pick the scenarios, so the real crisis may simply never be imagined.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** In regulatory stress testing and capital planning (for example, CCAR/DFAST), scenario analysis is used primarily to:\nA) Replace a bank's entire accounting system B) Project losses, revenues, and capital ratios under prescribed adverse scenarios to confirm the bank stays above minimum capital C) Eliminate the need for any economic capital model D) Set the bank's daily trading limits automatically\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what scenario analysis does inside a stress-testing and capital-planning program.\n\nRegulators hand banks a set of severe but plausible macro scenarios. The bank must translate each scenario into projected losses, revenues, and capital ratios over a multi-year horizon, and then show that it would still stay ABOVE the minimum required capital even under that stress. Passing also affects whether the bank can pay dividends or buy back shares. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it is like a stress test for the heart — the doctor pushes you on a treadmill (the adverse scenario) to check you stay healthy under strain.\n\n- A is wrong: stress testing does not replace accounting systems; it is a forward-looking risk exercise.\n- C is wrong: scenario results sit ON TOP of (complement) economic capital models; they do not eliminate them.\n- D is wrong: daily trading limits are a different control; stress tests look at multi-year, firm-wide capital adequacy.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stress-test purpose = \"do we stay above minimum capital under a severe scenario?\" Results feed the capital buffer and can gate dividends/buybacks.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CCAR/DFAST stress test\",\"steps\":[\"regulator prescribes scenario\",\"project losses & revenues\",\"compute capital ratios\",\"stay above minimum?\",\"gates dividends/buybacks\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of the following is a common OBSTACLE or cost when implementing ERM?\nA) It is impossible to assign any executive accountability B) Data and systems integration is expensive, and there can be turf resistance from business units C) ERM is prohibited for banks under most regulations D) ERM automatically removes all hard-to-quantify risks\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a realistic difficulty of putting ERM in place.\n\nBringing every risk into one consistent, firm-wide view requires integrating lots of data and systems, which is costly. On top of that, business units may resist giving up control of \"their\" risk (turf wars). Other challenges include forcing hard-to-quantify risks into one framework and the danger of false comfort if the aggregate model is wrong. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: merging many separate spreadsheets from different departments into one master file is expensive and people often don't want to hand theirs over.\n\n- A is wrong: ERM actually assigns clear accountability, typically to a CRO; lack of accountability is not the obstacle.\n- C is wrong: ERM is encouraged, not prohibited; regulators and rating agencies assess ERM quality.\n- D is wrong: ERM does not magically remove hard-to-quantify risks; struggling to fit them in is itself a challenge.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ERM costs/obstacles = expensive data/systems integration, turf resistance, hard-to-quantify risks, and false comfort if the aggregate model is wrong.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"ERM implementation obstacles\",\"root\":\"Putting ERM in place\",\"children\":[\"Costly data/systems integration\",\"Turf resistance from units\",\"Hard-to-quantify risks\",\"False comfort if model wrong\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which of the following is a marker of a STRONG risk culture according to common (FSB-style) guidance?\nA) Leaders only send memos about risk but behave differently themselves B) Tone from the top, where leaders model good risk behavior and bad news can travel up safely C) Compensation that rewards short-term profit regardless of risk taken D) A culture where questioning a star trader is discouraged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which feature signals a healthy risk culture.\n\nA strong risk culture has several markers: genuine tone from the top (leaders actually behave the way they preach), clear accountability, effective communication and challenge (dissent is safe and bad news rises quickly), and incentives that reward risk-adjusted performance. Option B describes tone from the top plus safe escalation, which are core strong-culture markers. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a good captain doesn't just post safety rules — they wear the life jacket too, and they thank the sailor who shouts \"iceberg.\"\n\n- A is wrong: leaders saying one thing and doing another is the OPPOSITE of genuine tone from the top.\n- C is wrong: rewarding short-term profit regardless of risk encourages reckless behavior — a weak-culture trait.\n- D is wrong: discouraging challenge of a star trader is a classic weak-culture red flag.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Strong-culture markers = tone from the top (lead by example), accountability, safe challenge/communication, and risk-adjusted incentives.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Strong risk culture markers\",\"root\":\"FSB-style strong culture\",\"children\":[\"Tone from the top (lead by example)\",\"Clear accountability\",\"Safe challenge / bad news rises\",\"Risk-adjusted incentives\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Compared with a silo approach, ERM most distinctively:\nA) Increases the number of risk reports produced B) Manages all risks as a single portfolio against one firm-wide appetite C) Eliminates the need for business-line risk owners D) Replaces qualitative risks with quantitative models\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The integrated portfolio view against a single appetite is the definition of ERM.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which is a *hedging-cost* argument for ERM?\nA) Each silo hedging separately ignores natural offsets, so the firm over-hedges B) ERM removes the need to hedge at all C) Silo hedging is illegal under Basel III D) ERM guarantees lower derivative spreads\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Netting natural offsets across units before hedging reduces hedge spend.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which observation most strongly indicates a *weak* risk culture?\nA) The board receives a quarterly aggregated risk report B) Junior staff hesitate to escalate concerns about a profitable desk C) The CRO reports directly to the board risk committee D) Compensation includes deferred, risk-adjusted components\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Suppressed escalation and untouchable profit centers are the classic culture red flags.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A key advantage of scenario analysis over VaR is that scenario analysis:\nA) Produces a precise loss probability B) Requires no assumptions of any kind C) Can capture coherent, multi-factor extreme events not present in historical data D) Is unaffected by management judgment\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Scenarios are forward-looking and move factors jointly; they lack probabilities (so A is wrong) and rest heavily on judgment (B, D wrong).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A firm hedges each business line's FX exposure separately, even though the lines have large offsetting positions. An ERM advocate would point out that the firm is:\nA) Correctly minimizing operational complexity B) Paying to hedge gross exposures when only the smaller net exposure needs hedging C) Violating Basel III netting rules D) Increasing its funding liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Silo hedging ignores natural offsets across units; ERM nets exposures first and hedges only the residual — one of the standard cost arguments for ERM.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Silo vs enterprise hedging\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Silo (per line)\",\"items\":[\"hedge gross exposures\",\"ignores offsets\",\"over-hedges, costly\"]},{\"h\":\"ERM (net)\",\"items\":[\"aggregate first\",\"offsets cancel\",\"hedge residual only\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A global bank runs four independent risk units (market, credit, operational, liquidity), each with its own VaR limits and each hedging its own exposures separately. A new CRO argues that moving to enterprise risk management (ERM) would, among other benefits, reduce the firm's hedging costs. Which statement best captures the *hedging-cost* rationale for ERM?\nA) ERM eliminates the need to hedge because aggregated risks always net to zero B) Under silos, each unit hedges its gross exposure independently, missing natural offsets across units, so the firm over-hedges and pays for protection it does not need on a net basis C) ERM lowers hedging costs because regulators grant capital relief to firms with a CRO D) ERM guarantees tighter bid-ask spreads on the derivatives the firm trades\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the specific *hedging-cost* argument for ERM (versus the many other ERM benefits).\n\n1. In a silo structure, each unit sees only its own exposure and hedges that gross position. If unit A is long an exposure that unit B is naturally short, the two partially offset *at the firm level*, but neither unit sees this.\n2. ERM aggregates exposures firm-wide first, nets the natural offsets, and hedges only the *residual net* exposure. Less protection is purchased, so hedging spend falls.\n\nWhy B is correct: it precisely states the netting-of-natural-offsets logic that lowers hedge cost.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A overstates the claim — aggregated risks rarely net to zero, and ERM does not remove the need to hedge; it makes hedging *efficient*. \"Always net to zero\" is the tell.\n- C describes a (loose) regulatory/rating benefit of ERM, not a hedging-cost mechanism, and capital relief is not automatically granted for having a CRO.\n- D confuses hedging *volume* with execution *spreads*; ERM reduces how much protection you buy, not the market spread on each trade, and \"guarantees\" is false.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ERM's hedging benefit is *netting natural offsets before buying protection*. Distinguish this from ERM's other motivations (capturing interactions/concentrations, better capital allocation, earnings stability, regulatory/rating pressure, strategic advantage). Questions often offer a true ERM benefit that is simply the *wrong category*.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ERM hedging-cost logic\",\"steps\":[\"aggregate exposures firm-wide\",\"unit A long offsets unit B short\",\"net the natural offsets\",\"hedge only residual net\",\"less protection bought -> cheaper\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A firm has invested heavily in an integrated ERM framework: a board-approved risk appetite, a powerful CRO, firm-wide aggregation systems, and risk-adjusted compensation on paper. Within a year, a star derivatives desk concealed a large unauthorized position that breached limits repeatedly; mid-level staff noticed but said nothing because the desk was highly profitable and the desk head was untouchable. What does this episode most directly illustrate?\nA) That the firm's economic-capital model was miscalibrated B) That a strong ERM framework cannot substitute for a strong risk culture; suppressed escalation around a profitable, protected desk is a culture failure C) That ERM increases operational risk relative to a silo approach D) That the firm should abandon ERM and return to silo management\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: classify the failure — framework versus culture.\n\n1. The firm *had* the formal ERM apparatus (appetite, CRO, systems, comp design). The breakdown was behavioral: limit breaches ignored, escalation suppressed, a \"too profitable to challenge\" star — exactly the markers of *weak risk culture*.\n2. A strong culture requires tone from the top, accountability, effective challenge with psychological safety (bad news travels up fast), and incentives that reward escalation. All failed here despite good documents.\n\nWhy B is correct: Barings, SocGen, and the London Whale all had limit frameworks; culture, not paperwork, was the gap — precisely what this scenario shows.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A blames the capital model, but nothing here concerns model calibration — the issue is concealed positions and silence, not mis-measured capital.\n- C reverses the relationship; ERM does not inherently raise operational risk, and the failure stems from culture, not from adopting ERM.\n- D is an over-reaction the Code/ERM literature never endorses — you fix culture, not discard integration.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A firm can have a state-of-the-art ERM *framework* and still fail through weak *culture*. Culture questions are answered by *behavior* (escalation, accountability, tone), not by the existence of policies or systems.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Framework present, culture failed\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Framework (had it)\",\"items\":[\"board-approved appetite\",\"powerful CRO\",\"aggregation systems\"]},{\"h\":\"Culture (failed)\",\"items\":[\"breaches ignored\",\"escalation suppressed\",\"desk too profitable to challenge\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A bank's CCAR submission must show its capital ratios under a severely adverse, multi-year macroeconomic scenario prescribed by the regulator. The CFO claims this scenario analysis is superior to the bank's internal VaR because \"it tells us the exact probability of breaching minimum capital.\" Which critique is most accurate?\nA) The CFO is right; prescribed scenarios come with regulator-assigned probabilities B) Scenario analysis does *not* attach probabilities to outcomes — its strength is capturing coherent, forward-looking, multi-factor tail events, not estimating their likelihood C) VaR is always superior to scenario analysis for capital planning D) Scenario analysis requires no modeling assumptions, so the CFO's confidence is justified\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: correct the CFO's misunderstanding of what scenario analysis delivers.\n\n1. Scenario analysis specifies an adverse state (or set of states) and translates it into projected losses, revenues, and capital ratios. It moves many risk factors *coherently at once* and can include never-observed events.\n2. Crucially, it does *not* assign a probability to the scenario. That is both its limitation (you cannot mechanically convert results to capital via likelihood) and the reason it complements probabilistic VaR rather than replacing it.\n\nWhy B is correct: it names the core property — no probabilities — while affirming the genuine strength (coherent multi-factor tail capture).\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A is the CFO's error restated; regulators prescribe the *scenario narrative and paths*, not a probability of the scenario occurring.\n- C overgeneralizes; VaR and scenario analysis are complements — VaR gives probabilistic loss over a horizon, scenarios give tail-event impact. Neither is \"always superior.\"\n- D is false: translating a macro scenario into P&L and capital is heavily model- and assumption-dependent (a recognized disadvantage of scenario analysis).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Scenario analysis = no probabilities, forward-looking, multi-factor, judgment-heavy. VaR = probabilistic over a horizon but history-calibrated and prone to missing tails. In stress testing/capital planning, banks must stay above minimum capital *under stress*; dividends/buybacks hinge on passing.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Scenario analysis vs VaR\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Scenario analysis\",\"items\":[\"NO probabilities attached\",\"forward-looking, multi-factor\",\"captures never-seen tails\"]},{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"probabilistic over horizon\",\"history-calibrated\",\"can miss new tails\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is the *best* example of a practice that distinguishes genuine ERM implementation from a \"tick-box\" exercise?\nA) Producing a thicker quarterly risk report than under the silo regime B) Embedding risk-adjusted metrics into product approval, deal pricing, M&A decisions, and compensation C) Appointing a CRO who reports to the head of the largest revenue-generating business D) Requiring every risk type to be reduced to a single number regardless of measurability\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify true ERM implementation versus cosmetic compliance.\n\n1. A defining ERM best practice is that *risk informs decisions* — pricing, product approval, M&A, and compensation all use risk-adjusted metrics. Risk is embedded in strategy, not bolted on afterward.\n\nWhy B is correct: integrating risk into actual decision-making is the substance of ERM.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A confuses volume with substance; more pages is not integration — it is the classic tick-box symptom.\n- C undermines CRO independence — a CRO reporting *into* a revenue business has compromised stature; best practice is an independent line to the board/board risk committee.\n- D is a known ERM pitfall: forcing hard-to-quantify risks into one number creates *false comfort*. ERM aggregates where possible but does not pretend unmeasurable risks are precisely measurable.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ERM is a governance and decision-making change, not \"more measurement.\" The strongest \"best practice\" answers involve risk informing *decisions*, an *independent* CRO with stature, a single firm-wide appetite cascaded into limits, and continuous improvement (learning from near-misses).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Genuine ERM vs tick-box\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Genuine ERM\",\"items\":[\"risk in pricing & approval\",\"risk in M&A & comp\",\"decisions actually change\"]},{\"h\":\"Tick-box\",\"items\":[\"thicker reports\",\"CRO under a revenue head\",\"one number for every risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A risk team performs *reverse* stress testing on the firm's balance sheet. Which statement best describes what reverse stress testing does and why it adds value beyond standard regulator-prescribed scenarios?\nA) It assigns precise probabilities to a fixed regulator scenario to compute expected loss B) It starts from a defined failure outcome (e.g., breaching minimum capital or insolvency) and works backward to identify the scenarios that would cause it, surfacing vulnerabilities the prescribed scenarios may miss C) It runs the regulator scenario in reverse order, from year three back to year one D) It replaces economic capital with a single worst-case number\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: define reverse stress testing and its incremental value.\n\n1. Standard stress testing asks: \"given this scenario, what happens to us?\" Reverse stress testing inverts the question: \"what scenario(s) would *break* us?\" It fixes the adverse *outcome* (failure, capital breach) and searches backward for the causal scenarios.\n2. Value-add: it counters \"failure of imagination\" by forcing the firm to discover the specific combinations of factors that threaten survival — vulnerabilities prescribed scenarios might not probe.\n\nWhy B is correct: it captures both the backward direction (outcome → scenario) and the imagination/vulnerability benefit.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A imports the \"precise probability\" fallacy that scenario analysis never provides, and it is just standard, not reverse, testing.\n- C takes \"reverse\" literally as time-order reversal — a wordplay trap; reverse refers to reversing the *question*, not the timeline.\n- D conflates reverse stress testing with replacing economic capital; reverse stress testing *supplements* capital planning, it does not replace the capital framework.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Reverse stress testing = start at the failure outcome and reason backward to the breaking scenarios. It directly attacks \"failure of imagination,\" the chief disadvantage of forward scenario selection.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Standard vs reverse stress test\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Standard\",\"items\":[\"start: given scenario\",\"ask: what happens to us?\",\"forward direction\"]},{\"h\":\"Reverse\",\"items\":[\"start: failure outcome\",\"ask: what would break us?\",\"work backward to scenarios\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** GARP highlights several \"future trends\" reshaping risk management. A bank deploys a machine-learning model that materially improves default prediction but is a black box even to its developers. From an ERM standpoint, the most important *new* risk this introduces is:\nA) Interest rate risk, because ML models react to rate changes B) Model risk — opaque, hard-to-validate models can fail in unforeseen ways and resist independent challenge, even as they improve point accuracy C) Reputational risk, which ERM does not address D) No new risk, since better accuracy strictly reduces total risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the principal new risk that AI/ML introduces in an ERM context.\n\n1. GARP flags ML/AI in risk models as a frontier *and* a source of *model risk*: black-box models are hard to validate, explain, and govern. Improved accuracy on historical data does not guarantee robustness to regime change, and opacity weakens independent challenge — an ERM governance concern.\n\nWhy B is correct: the defining ERM worry about ML is heightened model risk and validation/governance difficulty.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A is a category error; the model's *opacity and validation* are the issue, not its sensitivity to interest rates.\n- C wrongly claims ERM ignores reputational risk — ERM explicitly spans reputational and strategic risk; and reputational risk is not the *primary* new risk from a black-box model.\n- D commits the \"more accuracy = less risk\" fallacy; better in-sample prediction can coexist with greater hidden failure modes (the very lesson of model worship from financial disasters).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Among GARP's future trends (cyber, ML/AI, climate/ESG, fintech/crypto, risk-data aggregation), AI/ML's headline downside is *model risk* — accuracy is not robustness, and unexplainable models are hard to validate and govern.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Black-box ML model: new risk\",\"options\":[\"A: interest rate risk\",\"B: model risk - opaque, hard to validate\",\"C: reputational risk ERM ignores\",\"D: no new risk, accuracy cuts risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Opaque ML models are hard to validate/govern; in-sample accuracy is not robustness to regime change.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which combination correctly pairs an *advantage* and a *disadvantage* of scenario analysis as used in ERM?\nA) Advantage: produces objective probabilities; Disadvantage: ignores risk-factor interactions B) Advantage: captures coherent multi-factor tail events including never-observed ones; Disadvantage: scenario selection is subjective and results carry no attached likelihood C) Advantage: requires no assumptions; Disadvantage: cannot include historical events D) Advantage: replaces the need for VaR; Disadvantage: is unaffected by management judgment\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: select the pairing where *both* the advantage and the disadvantage are correctly stated.\n\n1. Genuine advantages: captures tail events and risk interactions VaR misses; needs no probability estimates; is forward-looking (can include unprecedented events); reveals cross-silo concentrations.\n2. Genuine disadvantages: scenario choice is subjective (\"failure of imagination\"); no likelihoods attached, so hard to convert to capital mechanically; hypotheticals may be internally inconsistent; resource-intensive and sensitive to modeling assumptions.\n\nWhy B is correct: both halves are true — multi-factor/never-observed coverage (advantage) and subjective selection plus no probabilities (disadvantage).\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A is wrong on both counts: scenario analysis does *not* produce objective probabilities, and it *does* capture interactions (that is its strength, not a flaw).\n- C is wrong on both: it *does* require assumptions (translating scenario to P&L), and it *can* use historical events (1987, 2008).\n- D is wrong on both: it complements rather than replaces VaR, and it is *heavily* dependent on management judgment, not unaffected by it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For \"which pair is correct\" questions, verify *both* clauses — a half-true pairing is still wrong. Anchor on the two scenario-analysis facts examiners love: it captures multi-factor/forward tails (plus) but attaches no probabilities and is judgment-driven (minus).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Scenario analysis: + and -\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Advantage\",\"items\":[\"coherent multi-factor tails\",\"includes never-seen events\",\"reveals cross-silo concentration\"]},{\"h\":\"Disadvantage\",\"items\":[\"scenario choice subjective\",\"no probability attached\",\"judgment- and resource-heavy\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"109","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"Learning from Financial Disasters","notes":"# 9. Learning from Financial Disasters\n\nCase studies organized by the risk factor that killed (or maimed) each firm. Near-guaranteed exam questions: match the case to the risk type and the lesson. Pure memory — learn the table cold.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Analyze the key factors that led to, and derive the lessons learned from, case studies involving: interest rate risk (the savings & loan crisis); funding liquidity risk (Lehman Brothers, Continental Illinois, Northern Rock); implementing hedging strategies (Metallgesellschaft); model risk (Niederhoffer, LTCM, the London Whale); rogue trading and misleading reporting (Barings); financial engineering and complex derivatives (Bankers Trust, Orange County, Sachsen Landesbank); reputational risk (Volkswagen); corporate governance (Enron); and cyber risk (the SWIFT attacks).\n\n## Master table\n\n| Case | Year(s) | Primary risk type | One-line story | Core lesson |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Savings & loan crisis** | 1980s | Interest rate risk | Thrifts funded long fixed-rate mortgages with short deposits; rates spiked → negative spread; deregulated gambling for resurrection | Manage the asset-liability repricing gap; deposit insurance + weak supervision breeds moral hazard |\n| **Continental Illinois** | 1984 | Funding liquidity / credit | Aggressive loan growth (incl. purchased Penn Square energy loans) funded with wholesale money; credit losses triggered a wholesale run | Wholesale funding flees fast; credit growth without underwriting discipline; \"too big to fail\" born |\n| **Northern Rock** | 2007 | Funding liquidity | UK mortgage lender ~75% funded in short-term wholesale/securitization markets; markets froze → first UK bank run in 140 years | Liquidity risk can kill a *solvent* bank; diversify funding; LCR/NSFR rationale |\n| **Lehman Brothers** | 2008 | Funding liquidity / leverage | 30x+ leverage, huge illiquid real-estate exposure, overnight repo funding; confidence loss → no rollover → bankruptcy | Leverage + illiquid assets + short funding is fatal; counterparty webs transmit failure systemically |\n| **Metallgesellschaft (MGRM)** | 1993 | Hedging implementation / funding liquidity | Long-dated fixed-price oil supply contracts hedged with stack-and-roll short-dated futures; prices fell → massive margin calls + rollover losses; firm unwound at the bottom | An economically sound hedge can fail through funding needs, maturity mismatch, and basis/rollover risk; size hedges to liquidity capacity |\n| **Niederhoffer** | 1997 | Model risk | Sold deep out-of-the-money S&P puts on the \"model\" that markets don't crash >5% in a day; Oct 1997 Asian-crisis drop wiped the fund | Short-tail strategies look like alpha until the tail arrives; don't bet the firm on a distributional assumption |\n| **LTCM** | 1998 | Model risk / leverage / liquidity | Convergence trades at ~25x leverage; models assumed normal-ish moves and stable correlations; Russia default → flight to quality, correlations went to 1, margin spiral; Fed-coordinated private bailout | Stress test beyond history; liquidity and crowding matter; correlations rise in crises; leverage magnifies model error |\n| **London Whale (JPMorgan CIO)** | 2012 | Model risk / operational | Synthetic credit portfolio ballooned; VaR model changed (spreadsheet errors) to halve reported risk; limits breached and ignored; ~$6bn loss; positions mismarked | Model governance and independent validation; limit breaches must escalate; a \"hedge\" that grows for P&L is a prop trade |\n| **Barings** | 1995 | Rogue trading / operational | Leeson controlled both trading and back office in Singapore; hid losses in account 88888 while doubling up on Nikkei futures/options; Kobe quake finished it; 233-year-old bank sold for £1 | Segregate front and back office; investigate \"too good\" profits; unsupervised stars are a control failure |\n| **Sumitomo** | 1996 | Rogue trading / operational | Hamanaka manipulated the copper market and concealed unauthorized positions for a decade; ~$2.6bn loss | Long-tenured, unsupervised traders with control over confirmations = concealed risk |\n| **Société Générale (Kerviel)** | 2008 | Rogue trading / operational | Junior trader built ~€50bn in hidden index futures positions using back-office knowledge and fake offsetting trades; €4.9bn loss on unwind | Enforce vacations/rotations, monitor cancelled/fake trades and gross (not just net) positions |\n| **Bankers Trust** | 1994 | Financial engineering / conduct / reputation | Sold highly leveraged swaps to P&G and Gibson Greetings; staff caught on tape boasting about exploiting clients; lawsuits and franchise destruction | Complex products sold over client comprehension = legal + reputational risk; suitability matters |\n| **Orange County** | 1994 | Financial engineering / market (leverage) | Treasurer Citron levered a \"safe\" municipal fund ~3x via reverse repos into inverse floaters/structured notes; 1994 rate hikes → ~$1.7bn loss, county bankruptcy | Understand embedded leverage and rate sensitivity; oversight of \"star\" managers; reported yield ≠ low risk |\n| **Sachsen Landesbank** | 2007 | Financial engineering / liquidity | Off-balance-sheet conduits funded long-dated ABS with short-term ABCP; ABCP froze → forced rescue | Off-balance-sheet vehicles carry contingent liquidity risk that comes home in stress |\n| **Volkswagen** | 2015 | Reputational | \"Defeat device\" software cheated emissions tests; fines, recalls, criminal charges, ~30%+ equity drop | Reputational risk is consequential to conduct failures; compliance shortcuts can cost decades of brand value |\n| **Enron** | 2001 | Corporate governance / accounting fraud | Mark-to-market abuse and SPE-hidden debt fabricated earnings; board and auditor (Arthur Andersen) failed; bankruptcy | Independent boards and auditors; transparency of off-balance-sheet entities; Sarbanes-Oxley followed |\n| **SWIFT / Bangladesh Bank** | 2016 | Cyber risk | Hackers used stolen SWIFT credentials to order $951m of transfers from the NY Fed; $81m got through via Philippine casinos | Cyber controls, payment authentication, and reconciliation are core operational risk; people/processes, not just firewalls |\n\n## Recurring failure patterns\n\nAcross the cases, the same ingredients recur:\n\n1. **Leverage + illiquidity + short-term funding** (LTCM, Lehman, Northern Rock, Orange County, Sachsen) — losses become death only when funding runs.\n2. **Concentration in one bet** (S&Ls, Niederhoffer, the Whale) — size turns ordinary moves into extinction events.\n3. **Control failures around individuals** (Barings, Sumitomo, Kerviel) — no segregation of duties, no independent confirmation, profits too good to question.\n4. **Model worship** (LTCM, Niederhoffer, the Whale) — history-calibrated models treated as truth; correlations and liquidity assumed stable.\n5. **Governance and culture rot** (Enron, VW, Bankers Trust) — incentives and tone from the top overwhelm formal controls.\n6. **Hedges that aren't** (Metallgesellschaft, the Whale) — maturity mismatch, basis risk, or mission creep turn hedges into positions.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: classify Metallgesellschaft correctly. The hedge was arguably sound *economically*; the firm died of **funding liquidity risk from margin calls and rollover losses on the stack-and-roll futures hedge** — not from speculation and not (primarily) from basis risk alone. Similarly, Northern Rock and Lehman are **funding liquidity** cases, not credit-loss cases; LTCM and the London Whale are first and foremost **model risk** cases in GARP's taxonomy.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Memory hook by risk factor: **Rates** = S&L. **Funding runs** = Continental Illinois, Northern Rock, Lehman. **Hedge gone wrong** = Metallgesellschaft. **Models** = Niederhoffer, LTCM, Whale. **Rogues** = Barings (also Sumitomo, Kerviel). **Engineering/derivative sales** = Bankers Trust, Orange County, Sachsen. **Reputation** = VW. **Governance** = Enron. **Cyber** = SWIFT.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Savings & loan crisis — primary risk type and lesson?\n> **A:** Interest rate risk. Thrifts funded long fixed-rate mortgages with short deposits; rates spiked → negative spread. Lesson: manage the asset-liability repricing gap; deposit insurance plus weak supervision breeds moral hazard.\n\n**Q2.** Northern Rock (2007) — what kind of failure?\n> **A:** Funding liquidity. ~75% wholesale/securitization-funded mortgage lender; markets froze → first UK bank run in 140 years. Lesson: liquidity risk can kill a solvent bank; diversify funding (LCR/NSFR rationale).\n\n**Q3.** Lehman Brothers (2008) — the fatal combination?\n> **A:** Funding liquidity / leverage. 30x+ leverage, illiquid real-estate assets, overnight repo funding; confidence loss → no rollover → bankruptcy. Lesson: leverage + illiquid assets + short funding is fatal.\n\n**Q4.** Metallgesellschaft — exam trap classification?\n> **A:** Hedging implementation / funding liquidity. The hedge was arguably sound economically; the firm died of margin calls and rollover losses on stack-and-roll short-dated futures — not speculation, not primarily basis risk.\n\n**Q5.** Niederhoffer (1997) — the lesson?\n> **A:** Model risk. Sold deep out-of-the-money S&P puts assuming no >5% daily crash; the Oct 1997 drop wiped the fund. Lesson: short-tail strategies look like alpha until the tail arrives.\n\n**Q6.** LTCM (1998) — what did it demonstrate?\n> **A:** Model risk / leverage / liquidity. ~25x leverage on convergence trades; Russia's default broke correlation and liquidity assumptions (correlations went to 1), triggering a margin spiral. Stress test beyond history; correlations rise in crises.\n\n**Q7.** London Whale (JPMorgan CIO, 2012) — primary risk and lesson?\n> **A:** Model risk / operational. A \"hedge\" ballooned into a prop trade; the VaR model was changed (spreadsheet errors) to halve reported risk; limit breaches ignored; ~$6bn loss. Lesson: model governance, independent validation, escalate breaches.\n\n**Q8.** Barings (1995) — risk type and lesson?\n> **A:** Rogue trading / operational. Leeson controlled both trading and back office in Singapore, hiding losses in account 88888. Lesson: segregate front and back office; investigate \"too good\" profits.\n\n**Q9.** What do Barings, Sumitomo, and SocGen (Kerviel) share?\n> **A:** Weak segregation of duties — traders used back-office knowledge/control gaps to conceal unauthorized positions. Fixes: enforce vacations/rotations, monitor cancelled/fake trades and gross (not just net) positions.\n\n**Q10.** Bankers Trust (1994) — risk type and lesson?\n> **A:** Financial engineering / conduct / reputation. Sold highly leveraged swaps to P&G and Gibson Greetings; staff boasted on tape about exploiting clients. Lesson: complex products sold over client comprehension = legal + reputational risk; suitability matters.\n\n**Q11.** Orange County (1994) — what went wrong?\n> **A:** Financial engineering / leverage. Treasurer Citron levered a \"safe\" fund ~3x via reverse repos into inverse floaters; 1994 rate hikes → ~$1.7bn loss, bankruptcy. Lesson: understand embedded leverage; reported yield ≠ low risk.\n\n**Q12.** Enron (2001) and Volkswagen (2015) — risk types?\n> **A:** Enron: corporate governance / accounting fraud (SPE-hidden debt, mark-to-market abuse; Arthur Andersen failed → Sarbanes-Oxley). VW: reputational risk from a conduct failure (emissions \"defeat device\").\n\n**Q13.** SWIFT / Bangladesh Bank (2016) — risk type and lesson?\n> **A:** Cyber risk. Hackers used stolen SWIFT credentials to order $951m of transfers; $81m got through. Lesson: payment authentication, reconciliation, and cyber controls are core operational risk — people/processes, not just firewalls.\n\n**Q14.** Name the recurring failure patterns across the disasters.\n> **A:** Leverage + illiquidity + short funding; concentration in one bet; control failures around individuals; model worship; governance/culture rot; and \"hedges that aren't\" (maturity mismatch, basis, mission creep).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Metallgesellschaft's (MGRM) losses are best attributed to:\nA) Speculative directional bets that oil prices would rise B) Funding and rollover strains from hedging long-dated supply contracts with short-dated futures C) A counterparty defaulting on its supply contracts D) A rogue trader concealing positions in a secret account\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which risk really sank Metallgesellschaft.\n\nMGRM had promised to deliver oil at fixed prices far into the future (long-dated contracts). To hedge, it used a \"stack-and-roll\" strategy: many short-dated futures that had to be rolled over again and again. When oil prices fell, those short-dated futures generated huge cash margin calls and rollover losses RIGHT AWAY, even though the long-dated contracts would have profited later. The firm couldn't fund the cash drain and unwound at the worst time. That is funding liquidity risk from a maturity mismatch, so B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: you bet on a slow-cooking dish that pays off in an hour, but you have to keep feeding coins into the oven every minute — and you run out of coins before dinner is ready.\n\n- A is wrong: MGRM was hedging, not making a one-way speculative bet that prices would rise.\n- C is wrong: the problem was its own cash flows on the hedge, not a counterparty defaulting.\n- D is wrong: there was no rogue trader hiding positions; that describes Barings or SocGen.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Metallgesellschaft = an economically reasonable hedge killed by funding liquidity (margin calls) and maturity mismatch on the futures roll — NOT speculation.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"MGRM funding spiral\",\"steps\":[\"long-dated fixed-price contracts\",\"hedge: stack short-dated futures\",\"oil falls -> daily margin calls\",\"contract gains realize years later\",\"cash drain -> unwind at bottom\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which case is correctly matched to its PRIMARY risk type?\nA) Barings — model risk B) Northern Rock — funding liquidity risk C) Enron — cyber risk D) LTCM — rogue trading\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which case-to-risk pairing is correct.\n\nNorthern Rock was a UK mortgage lender that funded most of its long-term loans with short-term wholesale and securitization money. When those markets froze in 2007, it could not roll its funding and suffered the first UK bank run in 140 years. That is the textbook funding liquidity case, so B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: Barings is the classic ROGUE TRADING case (Nick Leeson), not model risk.\n- C is wrong: Enron is a CORPORATE GOVERNANCE / accounting fraud case, not cyber risk.\n- D is wrong: LTCM is primarily a MODEL RISK (plus leverage and liquidity) case, not rogue trading.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Lock in the famous pairs: Northern Rock = funding liquidity, Barings = rogue trading, Enron = governance, LTCM = model risk.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Case -> primary risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Case\",\"items\":[\"Northern Rock\",\"Barings\",\"Enron\",\"LTCM\"]},{\"h\":\"Primary risk\",\"items\":[\"funding liquidity\",\"rogue trading\",\"governance/fraud\",\"model risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The common thread linking Barings (Leeson) and Société Générale (Kerviel) is:\nA) Heavy reliance on flawed VaR models B) Off-balance-sheet conduit funding C) Weak segregation of duties that let a trader hide unauthorized positions D) Mis-selling complex derivatives to corporate clients\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what these two rogue-trading disasters had in common.\n\nIn both cases, a single trader exploited gaps in back-office controls to conceal large unauthorized positions. Leeson controlled both trading and the back office in Singapore and hid losses in account 88888; Kerviel used his back-office knowledge to fake offsetting trades. The shared root cause is weak segregation of duties — the front office and the controls weren't properly separated. So C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's like letting the same person both spend the company credit card and approve the expense reports — fraud becomes easy.\n\n- A is wrong: VaR model failures describe LTCM or the London Whale, not these rogue-trading cases.\n- B is wrong: off-balance-sheet conduits describe Sachsen Landesbank, not Barings/SocGen.\n- D is wrong: mis-selling derivatives to corporates describes Bankers Trust, not these cases.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rogue-trading cases (Barings, Sumitomo, Kerviel) all point to one lesson: segregate front and back office, and investigate profits that look \"too good.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Cause -> lesson (rogue trading)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Cause\",\"items\":[\"trader controls front + back office\",\"weak segregation of duties\",\"unauthorized positions hidden\"]},{\"h\":\"Lesson\",\"items\":[\"separate front/back office\",\"independent confirmation\",\"probe too-good profits\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The collapse of LTCM in 1998 most directly demonstrated that:\nA) Convergence trades are always unprofitable B) In a crisis, correlations and market liquidity can move sharply away from model assumptions, and leverage magnifies the damage C) Regulators will always bail out hedge funds with public money D) Interest rate risk is the dominant risk for all hedge funds\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the central lesson of LTCM's near-failure.\n\nLTCM ran convergence trades at roughly 25x leverage, with models assuming fairly normal price moves and stable correlations. When Russia defaulted in 1998, investors fled to safe assets, correlations jumped toward 1, liquidity dried up, and a margin spiral began. The huge leverage turned a model error into a near-collapse. So B captures the lesson.\n\nAnalogy: the model assumed the boats would drift apart calmly; in the storm they all slammed together at once, and the firm had borrowed 25 boats for every one it owned.\n\n- A is wrong: convergence trades are not always losers; the problem was extreme conditions plus leverage, not the strategy in normal times.\n- C is wrong: the LTCM rescue was a PRIVATE, Fed-COORDINATED bailout, not public money, and bailouts are never guaranteed.\n- D is wrong: interest rate risk was not the dominant cause; correlation/liquidity breakdown and leverage were.\n\n📌 Exam tip: LTCM = model risk + leverage + liquidity. Crisis correlations rise toward 1 and liquidity vanishes; leverage magnifies any model error.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"LTCM\",\"points\":[{\"t\":\"1994\",\"l\":\"Founded, 25x lever\"},{\"t\":\"1998\",\"l\":\"Russia default\"},{\"t\":\"1998\",\"l\":\"Correlations -> 1\"},{\"t\":\"1998\",\"l\":\"Fed-led rescue\"}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The savings and loan (S&L) crisis of the 1980s is primarily an example of:\nA) Cyber risk B) Interest rate risk C) Rogue trading D) Reputational risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which risk type the S&L crisis illustrates.\n\nThrifts (S&Ls) had funded long-term, fixed-rate mortgages using short-term deposits. When interest rates spiked, they had to pay much more on the short-term deposits while still earning the old low rate on the long mortgages — a negative spread. That is a classic asset-liability repricing mismatch, which is interest rate risk. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: you locked in renting out your house cheaply for 30 years, but the cost of your own short-term loan suddenly jumps — you bleed cash every month.\n\n- A is wrong: cyber risk describes the SWIFT/Bangladesh Bank attack, not the S&Ls.\n- C is wrong: rogue trading describes Barings; the S&L problem was structural, about rates.\n- D is wrong: reputational risk describes Volkswagen; the S&L core issue was interest rates.\n\n📌 Exam tip: S&L crisis = interest rate risk (repricing/maturity gap). Funding long fixed-rate assets with short-term deposits is the danger.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"S&L repricing mismatch\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Assets\",\"items\":[\"long-term mortgages\",\"fixed low rate\",\"locked in for years\"]},{\"h\":\"Liabilities\",\"items\":[\"short-term deposits\",\"reprice up fast\",\"cost spikes with rates\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The London Whale episode at JPMorgan's CIO is best classified in GARP's taxonomy as a case of:\nA) Reputational risk only B) Model risk and operational/control failures C) Pure credit default by a counterparty D) Interest rate risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to classify the London Whale loss.\n\nThe CIO's synthetic credit portfolio ballooned, and the VaR model was changed (with spreadsheet errors) in a way that halved the reported risk. Limit breaches were ignored and positions were mismarked, leading to about $6 billion in losses. The combination of bad model governance and failed controls makes this a model-risk plus operational-risk case. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's like quietly swapping the bathroom scale for one that reads light, then ignoring the doctor's warnings — the problem is both the rigged measurement (model) and the ignored alarms (controls).\n\n- A is wrong: there was reputational fallout, but the PRIMARY classification is model and operational risk.\n- C is wrong: this was not a counterparty defaulting; the loss came from the firm's own positions and risk measurement.\n- D is wrong: interest rate risk was not the driver; it was a credit-derivatives portfolio with broken risk governance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: London Whale lessons: independent model validation, escalate limit breaches, and a \"hedge\" that keeps growing for profit is really a prop trade.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"London Whale cause -> lesson\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Cause\",\"items\":[\"VaR model changed, halved risk\",\"limit breaches ignored\",\"positions mismarked\"]},{\"h\":\"Lesson\",\"items\":[\"independent model validation\",\"escalate breaches\",\"a growing hedge is a prop trade\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Volkswagen's 2015 \"defeat device\" emissions scandal is the standard example of:\nA) Funding liquidity risk B) Model risk C) Reputational risk arising from a conduct failure D) Interest rate risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which risk type the VW scandal represents.\n\nVW installed software that cheated emissions tests. When this conduct failure came to light, it triggered fines, recalls, criminal charges, and roughly a 30%+ drop in the share price. The damage flowed from harm to the company's reputation and trust, making this the classic reputational-risk case. So C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a restaurant caught faking its hygiene scores may not run out of cash overnight, but customers stop coming — the brand damage is the real blow.\n\n- A is wrong: VW's issue was not an inability to fund itself; it was reputational.\n- B is wrong: there was no risk-model breakdown at the center of this; it was deliberate misconduct.\n- D is wrong: interest rates had nothing to do with it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Volkswagen = reputational risk from a conduct/compliance failure. Cutting ethical corners can erase decades of brand value.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"VW reputational chain\",\"steps\":[\"defeat-device cheats tests\",\"scandal exposed\",\"fines, recalls, charges\",\"share price falls ~30%+\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Enron's 2001 collapse is primarily an example of:\nA) Corporate governance and accounting fraud B) Funding liquidity risk C) Cyber risk D) Hedging implementation risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the primary risk type behind Enron.\n\nEnron used aggressive mark-to-market accounting and hid debt in special purpose entities (SPEs) to fabricate earnings. Its board and its auditor (Arthur Andersen) failed to catch or stop this. The core failure was governance and accounting fraud, which is why the Sarbanes-Oxley Act followed. So A is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's like a family hiding huge credit-card debt in a relative's name so their household budget looks healthy — until it all unravels.\n\n- B is wrong: while Enron did run out of cash at the end, the root cause was fraudulent reporting and weak governance, not a classic funding run.\n- C is wrong: cyber risk describes the SWIFT attack, not Enron.\n- D is wrong: hedging implementation risk describes Metallgesellschaft, not Enron.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Enron = corporate governance / accounting fraud (SPEs, mark-to-market abuse, failed board and auditor). It led to Sarbanes-Oxley.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Enron primary risk type\",\"options\":[\"A: governance & accounting fraud\",\"B: funding liquidity risk\",\"C: cyber risk\",\"D: hedging implementation risk\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"SPE-hidden debt + mark-to-market abuse + failed board/auditor = governance/accounting fraud; led to Sarbanes-Oxley.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The 2016 SWIFT / Bangladesh Bank incident, in which hackers used stolen credentials to order nearly $1 billion in fraudulent transfers, is the textbook example of:\nA) Market risk B) Cyber risk as part of operational risk C) Interest rate risk D) Reputational risk only\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which risk type the SWIFT/Bangladesh Bank attack illustrates.\n\nHackers obtained stolen SWIFT credentials and instructed the New York Fed to send out $951 million; about $81 million slipped through via Philippine casinos. The lesson is that cyber controls, payment authentication, and reconciliation are core parts of operational risk — and that people and processes, not just firewalls, matter. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a thief who steals the master key and the right paperwork can walk out the front door — locks alone don't help if the keys and checks fail.\n\n- A is wrong: no market price movement caused this loss; it was a security breach.\n- C is wrong: interest rates were irrelevant here.\n- D is wrong: there was reputational damage, but the PRIMARY classification is cyber/operational risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SWIFT/Bangladesh Bank = cyber risk within operational risk. Authentication, controls, and reconciliation matter as much as technical firewalls.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Cyber within operational risk\",\"root\":\"Operational risk\",\"children\":[\"People (stolen credentials)\",\"Processes (weak reconciliation)\",\"Systems (payment auth)\",\"External (hackers)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Across many of the disasters (LTCM, Lehman, Northern Rock, Orange County), one recurring fatal combination was:\nA) Low leverage with highly liquid assets and stable long-term funding B) Leverage plus illiquid assets funded with short-term money, so losses became fatal only when funding ran C) Excessive holdings of cash and government bonds D) Over-diversification across too many uncorrelated assets\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the common dangerous pattern shared by several blow-ups.\n\nIn LTCM, Lehman, Northern Rock, and Orange County, the deadly recipe was the same: high leverage, hard-to-sell (illiquid) assets, and reliance on short-term funding that had to be rolled over constantly. Ordinary losses only turned deadly when lenders refused to keep rolling the funding, forcing fire sales. So B describes the recurring killer combination.\n\nAnalogy: it's like buying a mansion (illiquid) mostly with a loan you must renew every night — one night the lender says no, and you must dump the house at any price.\n\n- A is wrong: this is the SAFE setup, the opposite of what caused the disasters.\n- C is wrong: holding lots of cash and government bonds is conservative, not the cause of these failures.\n- D is wrong: over-diversification was not the problem; dangerous concentration and leverage were.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The recurring death spiral = leverage + illiquidity + short-term funding. Losses turn into failure when the short-term funding runs.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"The death spiral\",\"steps\":[\"high leverage\",\"illiquid assets\",\"short-term funding rolled\",\"lenders refuse to roll\",\"fire sales -> failure\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Metallgesellschaft's losses are best attributed to:\nA) Speculative directional bets on rising oil prices B) Funding/rollover strains from hedging long-dated commitments with short-dated futures C) Counterparty default on its supply contracts D) A rogue trader concealing positions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Stack-and-roll margin calls plus rollover losses in a falling (contango-shifting) market — funding liquidity, not speculation.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which pairing is correct?\nA) Barings — model risk B) Northern Rock — funding liquidity risk C) Enron — cyber risk D) LTCM — rogue trading\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Northern Rock is the canonical wholesale-funding run; Barings = rogue trading, Enron = governance, LTCM = model risk/leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The common thread between Barings and Société Générale (Kerviel) is:\nA) Excessive reliance on VaR models B) Off-balance-sheet conduit funding C) Weak segregation of duties enabling concealed unauthorized positions D) Mis-sold derivatives to corporate clients\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Both traders exploited back-office knowledge/control gaps to hide positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** LTCM's 1998 collapse demonstrated most directly that:\nA) Convergence trades are always unprofitable B) Crisis correlations and market liquidity can deviate sharply from model assumptions, and leverage magnifies the damage C) Regulators will always bail out hedge funds with public money D) Interest rate risk is the dominant risk for hedge funds\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Flight to quality broke correlation and liquidity assumptions; 25x leverage turned that into near-failure (and the bailout was private, Fed-*coordinated*).\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Which case is the best illustration of *model risk* in GARP's disaster taxonomy?\nA) Barings (1995) B) Northern Rock (2007) C) Long-Term Capital Management (1998) D) Volkswagen (2015)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** GARP files LTCM (with Niederhoffer and the London Whale) under model risk: normal-market correlation and liquidity assumptions failed under the 1998 flight to quality, and leverage amplified the error. Barings = rogue trading; Northern Rock = funding liquidity; VW = reputational.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Disaster -> risk type\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Case\",\"items\":[\"LTCM\",\"Barings\",\"Northern Rock\",\"Volkswagen\"]},{\"h\":\"Risk type\",\"items\":[\"MODEL risk\",\"rogue trading\",\"funding liquidity\",\"reputational\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** Orange County's 1994 bankruptcy resulted primarily from:\nA) A rogue trader concealing unauthorized equity positions B) Leveraged exposure to rising interest rates via reverse repos and structured notes C) Losses on credit default swaps written on subprime mortgages D) A depositor run on the county's investment pool's commercial paper\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Citron levered the municipal pool roughly threefold through reverse repos into inverse floaters and other structured notes — a leveraged bet on stable/falling rates that the 1994 Fed tightening destroyed. No rogue concealment of equities (A), no CDS (C — wrong decade), and the pool was not CP-funded in the relevant sense (D).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Orange County 1994\",\"steps\":[\"Reverse repos add leverage\",\"Buy inverse floaters\",\"Bet on stable/low rates\",\"Fed hikes rates 1994\",\"Note values collapse -> bankruptcy\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** Metallgesellschaft's US subsidiary (MGRM) sold long-dated, fixed-price oil-supply contracts to customers and hedged them with a \"stack-and-roll\" of short-dated long futures. When spot oil prices fell sharply in 1993, the firm suffered enormous cash drains and unwound at the bottom. In GARP's taxonomy, the *primary* risk that destroyed MGRM is best classified as:\nA) Speculative directional risk — the firm was effectively betting oil would rise B) Funding liquidity risk — margin calls and rollover losses on the short-dated futures hedge created cash demands the firm could not meet C) Credit risk — its supply-contract counterparties defaulted D) Market risk on the customer contracts, which lost value as oil fell\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: classify the *primary* cause of MGRM's losses per GARP's framework.\n\n1. The customer contracts and the futures hedge were economically offsetting in the long run — the position was, arguably, a sound hedge of a long-dated commitment.\n2. The futures, however, were marked to market *daily*: as oil fell, the long futures generated immediate margin calls (cash *out*), while the offsetting gains on the long-dated supply contracts were only realized *over years* (cash in much later). This maturity/cash-flow mismatch, plus rollover (basis) losses when the curve moved against the stack, produced a funding crisis.\n3. The firm could not fund the margin calls and was forced to liquidate at the worst possible time.\n\nWhy B is correct: the death blow was the *funding* strain from margin and rollover on the hedge, not directional speculation.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A mislabels a hedge as a bet — the classic MGRM trap. The economic hedge was defensible; the *implementation* (cash-flow timing) failed.\n- C invents counterparty default; there was no supply-contract default driving the collapse.\n- D points at the contracts losing value, but those were the *hedged* item whose loss was offset by the futures — the issue was timing of cash flows, i.e., funding liquidity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: MGRM is the canonical \"an economically sound hedge can still kill you through funding\" case — maturity mismatch + daily margin + rollover/basis risk. Do not classify it as speculation or as pure basis risk; GARP's answer is *funding liquidity risk from the hedge*.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"MGRM cash-flow mismatch\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Futures hedge\",\"items\":[\"marked to market daily\",\"oil falls -> margin out NOW\",\"cash drain immediate\"]},{\"h\":\"Supply contracts\",\"items\":[\"gains realize over years\",\"cash in much later\",\"timing mismatch = funding risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An examiner asks candidates to match three disasters to their *primary* GARP risk classification: (i) Northern Rock (2007), (ii) the London Whale / JPMorgan CIO (2012), (iii) Barings (1995). The correct mapping is:\nA) (i) credit risk; (ii) rogue trading; (iii) model risk B) (i) funding liquidity risk; (ii) model risk; (iii) rogue trading C) (i) model risk; (ii) funding liquidity risk; (iii) operational fraud only D) (i) reputational risk; (ii) credit risk; (iii) rogue trading\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: assign each case its primary risk type.\n\n1. **Northern Rock** funded roughly three-quarters of its book in short-term wholesale/securitization markets; when those froze it suffered the first UK bank run in 140 years despite being solvent — a *funding liquidity* case.\n2. **London Whale**: a synthetic credit book ballooned while a changed/erroneous VaR model halved reported risk and limit breaches were ignored; GARP classifies it first as *model risk* (with operational/governance overlay).\n3. **Barings**: Leeson controlled both trading and back office and hid losses while doubling up on Nikkei futures — the archetypal *rogue trading* / segregation-of-duties failure.\n\nWhy B is correct: it matches all three to their GARP-primary classifications.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A mislabels Northern Rock as credit (its losses were a *funding run*, not primarily credit losses) and the Whale as rogue trading (it was model/risk-governance, not a hidden-by-one-rogue scheme).\n- C flips Northern Rock to model risk and the Whale to funding liquidity — both reversed — and reduces Barings to \"fraud only,\" ignoring the segregation-of-duties control failure GARP emphasizes.\n- D miscasts Northern Rock as reputational and the Whale as credit risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the \"primary risk\" labels GARP uses, even when a case has several flavors. Funding runs = Continental Illinois, Northern Rock, Lehman. Model risk = Niederhoffer, LTCM, London Whale. Rogue trading = Barings, Sumitomo, Kerviel.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Case -> primary risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Case\",\"items\":[\"Northern Rock\",\"London Whale\",\"Barings\"]},{\"h\":\"Primary risk\",\"items\":[\"funding liquidity\",\"model risk\",\"rogue trading\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** LTCM (1998) and Niederhoffer (1997) are both classified primarily as *model-risk* failures. Which statement best identifies what their models got wrong and how the failure propagated?\nA) Both relied on assuming stable, history-calibrated distributions/correlations; when a tail event arrived (Russia default; the 1997 Asian-crisis drop), losses spiked and — for LTCM — high leverage plus a margin/liquidity spiral magnified a model error into near-failure B) Both were rogue-trading frauds in which a single trader hid positions C) Both failed because of interest rate risk on long bond portfolios D) Both were brought down by counterparty default on their derivative contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: identify the shared model-risk mechanism and its propagation.\n\n1. **Niederhoffer** sold deep out-of-the-money S&P puts on the model assumption that markets do not drop more than ~5% in a day. The October 1997 Asian-crisis fall violated that assumption and wiped the fund — a short-tail strategy that looked like alpha until the tail arrived.\n2. **LTCM** ran convergence trades at ~25x leverage assuming roughly normal moves and stable correlations. Russia's 1998 default triggered a flight to quality; correlations went toward 1, liquidity evaporated, and margin calls forced losses — leverage turned a model error into a systemic threat (Fed-coordinated *private* rescue).\n3. Common thread: history-calibrated distributional/correlation assumptions failed in crisis; for LTCM, leverage and liquidity spirals amplified the damage.\n\nWhy A is correct: it names both the distributional/correlation assumption failure and the leverage/liquidity amplification.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- B confuses them with rogue-trading cases (Barings/Sumitomo/Kerviel) — neither involved a single hidden trader.\n- C reduces them to interest rate risk, which was not the core failure.\n- D invents counterparty default as the cause; the failures were driven by their own positions and assumptions, not a counterparty's collapse.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Model worship\" = treating history-calibrated models as truth. The lesson: stress *beyond* history, watch correlations rising toward 1 in crises, and remember that leverage magnifies model error (LTCM) while short-tail option selling hides risk until the tail (Niederhoffer).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Model-risk twins\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Niederhoffer 1997\",\"items\":[\"sold deep OTM S&P puts\",\"assumed no >5% daily drop\",\"Asian-crisis tail wiped fund\"]},{\"h\":\"LTCM 1998\",\"items\":[\"~25x leverage convergence\",\"stable-correlation assumption\",\"Russia default + spiral\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A control reviewer studies Barings (Leeson), Société Générale (Kerviel), and Sumitomo (Hamanaka). Which single control weakness is the common thread, and which remedy most directly addresses it?\nA) Excessive VaR reliance; remedy: scenario analysis B) Lack of segregation of duties / independent confirmation, letting traders conceal unauthorized positions; remedy: separate front and back office, enforce mandatory vacations and rotation, and monitor cancelled/fake trades and gross positions C) Off-balance-sheet conduit funding; remedy: consolidate SIVs D) Mis-selling complex derivatives to clients; remedy: suitability standards\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: find the shared control failure across three rogue-trader cases and the matching remedy.\n\n1. Leeson controlled both trading and the back office in Singapore (no segregation); Kerviel used back-office knowledge and fake offsetting trades to hide ~€50bn of index futures; Hamanaka, long-tenured and unsupervised, controlled confirmations and concealed copper positions for a decade.\n2. The common gap: no segregation of duties and no independent confirmation/monitoring, which let each trader hide unauthorized positions.\n3. Remedies map directly: split front/back office, force vacations and rotation (Kerviel never took leave), and monitor cancelled/fake trades and *gross* (not just net) positions.\n\nWhy B is correct: it states the shared weakness and the specific operational controls that address it.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A describes the LTCM/Whale \"model\" theme, not the rogue-trading control gap.\n- C describes Sachsen Landesbank (off-balance-sheet ABCP), a different case family.\n- D describes Bankers Trust (client mis-selling), not the segregation/concealment problem.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rogue-trading cases share segregation-of-duties failure and concealed positions. The named remedies — front/back-office separation, mandatory vacations and rotation, monitoring of cancelled trades and gross exposures, and investigating \"too good to be true\" profits — are favorite exam answers.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Rogue trading: gap -> remedy\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Common weakness\",\"items\":[\"no segregation of duties\",\"trader controls confirmations\",\"hidden unauthorized positions\"]},{\"h\":\"Remedy\",\"items\":[\"split front/back office\",\"mandatory vacations & rotation\",\"monitor cancelled/gross trades\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which pairing correctly matches a disaster to its *primary* risk type as GARP classifies it?\nA) Orange County (1994) — reputational risk B) Bankers Trust (1994) — financial-engineering/conduct risk (mis-selling complex leveraged swaps to corporate clients) C) Volkswagen (2015) — funding liquidity risk D) Enron (2001) — cyber risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: pick the correct case-to-risk-type pairing.\n\n1. **Bankers Trust** sold highly leveraged swaps to P&G and Gibson Greetings; staff were taped boasting about exploiting clients, triggering lawsuits and franchise destruction — a *financial-engineering / conduct / reputation* case centered on selling products beyond client comprehension (suitability).\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly classifies Bankers Trust as a conduct/financial-engineering mis-selling case.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A miscasts Orange County as reputational; it is a *financial-engineering / market (leverage)* case — Treasurer Citron levered a \"safe\" fund ~3x via reverse repos into inverse floaters/structured notes, and 1994 rate hikes caused ~$1.7bn of losses and county bankruptcy.\n- C miscasts VW; the diesel \"defeat device\" is the textbook *reputational* (conduct-driven) case, not funding liquidity.\n- D miscasts Enron; it is a *corporate governance / accounting-fraud* case (mark-to-market abuse, SPE-hidden debt, failed board and auditor), not cyber risk. (Cyber = SWIFT/Bangladesh Bank.)\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use the memory hook — Engineering/derivative sales = Bankers Trust, Orange County, Sachsen; Reputation = VW; Governance = Enron; Cyber = SWIFT. Examiners swap these labels to trap you.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Case -> primary risk type\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Case\",\"items\":[\"Bankers Trust\",\"Orange County\",\"Volkswagen\",\"Enron\"]},{\"h\":\"Primary risk\",\"items\":[\"conduct/mis-selling\",\"financial eng (leverage)\",\"reputational\",\"governance/fraud\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The 2016 SWIFT / Bangladesh Bank attack saw hackers use stolen SWIFT credentials to instruct the New York Fed to transfer about $951 million; roughly $81 million escaped through Philippine casinos before the rest was halted. Which lesson does GARP draw, and how does it relate to operational risk?\nA) Cyber risk is purely a technology problem solved by stronger firewalls alone B) Cyber risk is a core component of operational risk; payment authentication, access controls, and transaction reconciliation (people and processes, not just firewalls) are essential defenses C) The attack was a market-risk event because exchange rates moved D) The episode shows that wholesale funding runs are the dominant cyber threat\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: state the SWIFT-attack lesson and tie it to operational risk.\n\n1. The breach exploited stolen *credentials* and weak *controls/reconciliation*, not merely a perimeter failure. Spelling errors in some instructions and reconciliation eventually flagged transfers, but $81m had already leaked.\n2. Cyber risk is a subset of *operational risk* (people, processes, systems, external events). Defenses are about authentication, access management, segregation, and reconciliation — process and people controls — not firewalls alone.\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly locates cyber within operational risk and names process/people controls.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A is the \"firewalls fix everything\" fallacy; the attack used legitimate credentials, defeating perimeter-only thinking.\n- C mislabels it as market risk because of incidental FX movement — the loss was theft via compromised payments, an operational event.\n- D conflates two unrelated themes; this was cyber/operational, not a funding run.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Cyber risk = a major, growing *operational* risk. The lesson is layered controls — authentication, access, segregation, reconciliation — and that \"people and processes\" matter as much as technology.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"SWIFT attack: operational risk\",\"root\":\"Operational risk\",\"children\":[\"People (stolen credentials)\",\"Processes (weak reconciliation)\",\"Systems (payment auth)\",\"Lesson: layered controls\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A risk officer summarizes recurring \"ingredients\" across the disaster case studies. Which scenario best illustrates the pattern *\"leverage + illiquidity + short-term funding turns losses into death,\"* and which other case shares that exact pattern?\nA) Enron, sharing the pattern with Volkswagen B) Lehman Brothers, sharing the pattern with LTCM (and Northern Rock, Orange County, Sachsen) C) Bankers Trust, sharing the pattern with Sumitomo D) Barings, sharing the pattern with the London Whale\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the case best fitting the leverage + illiquidity + short-term funding pattern and a peer case sharing it.\n\n1. **Lehman** ran 30x+ leverage on largely illiquid real-estate exposure financed with overnight repo; when confidence broke, lenders refused to roll, and the firm failed — the textbook leverage/illiquidity/short-funding death spiral.\n2. **LTCM** matches precisely: ~25x leverage, positions that became illiquid in the crisis, and margin/funding pressure that forced losses. Northern Rock, Orange County, and Sachsen also share the funding-amplification ingredient.\n\nWhy B is correct: Lehman is the cleanest example, and LTCM (plus Northern Rock/Orange County/Sachsen) shares the same lethal combination.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A pairs governance/accounting (Enron) with reputational/conduct (VW) — neither is primarily a leverage-plus-short-funding death.\n- C pairs two conduct/rogue cases (Bankers Trust mis-selling; Sumitomo rogue trading) — wrong pattern.\n- D pairs two control/model cases (Barings rogue trading; the Whale model risk) — also the wrong recurring ingredient.\n\n📌 Exam tip: GARP's recurring patterns are testable in themselves. \"Losses become death only when funding runs\" links Lehman, LTCM, Northern Rock, Orange County, and Sachsen — high leverage + illiquid assets + short-term/wholesale funding.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Leverage death spiral (Lehman/LTCM)\",\"steps\":[\"high leverage (30x / 25x)\",\"illiquid assets\",\"short-term funding rolled\",\"lenders refuse to roll\",\"fire sales -> failure\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"110","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"Anatomy of the Great Financial Crisis of 2007–2009","notes":"# 10. Anatomy of the Great Financial Crisis of 2007–2009\n\nThe full mechanism of the GFC: the housing/subprime build-up, securitization chains, the institutions involved, the short-term funding runs that turned a credit problem into a systemic collapse, and the policy response. Tested via cause-and-effect and \"who did what\" questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the historical background and provide an overview of the 2007–2009 financial crisis.\n- Describe the build-up to the crisis and the factors that played an important role.\n- Explain the role of subprime mortgages and collateralized debt obligations (CDOs).\n- Compare the roles of banks, financial intermediaries, mortgage brokers and lenders, and rating agencies.\n- Describe trends in short-term wholesale funding markets that contributed to the crisis, including their impact on systemic risk.\n- Describe the responses taken by central banks and governments.\n\n## The build-up\n\nIngredients accumulating from the early 2000s:\n\n- **Cheap money**: post-2001 Fed funds at 1%, plus a global \"savings glut\" pushing investors toward yield.\n- **A housing boom**: national US house prices rising for years → the universal assumption that they could not fall nationwide.\n- **Underwriting collapse**: subprime and Alt-A mortgages, teaser-rate ARMs (2/28s), low/no-doc \"liar loans,\" NINJA loans, piggyback seconds — viable only if prices kept rising (refinance before reset).\n- **Securitization at scale**: originate-to-distribute (Ch 4) severed the link between underwriting and loss-bearing.\n- **Leverage everywhere**: broker-dealers at 30–40x; off-balance-sheet conduits/SIVs hiding more; households levering into housing.\n- **Regulatory gaps**: shadow banking outside bank supervision; OTC derivatives unregulated; capital rules favoring AAA tranches.\n\n## Subprime mortgages and CDOs: the transmission machine\n\n1. Brokers/lenders originate subprime mortgages for **fees**, passing risk on immediately.\n2. Loans pooled into **RMBS**; tranching converts mostly-junk collateral into ~80% AAA-rated bonds via subordination.\n3. The hard-to-sell **mezzanine (BBB) tranches** are re-pooled into **ABS CDOs**, which the agencies again rated largely AAA — a re-securitization alchemy resting on low default-correlation assumptions.\n4. **Synthetic CDOs** (CDS on the same tranches) multiplied the exposure beyond the stock of actual mortgages; AIG and the monolines sold the protection.\n5. When house prices fell nationally (from 2006), defaults exploded, correlation assumptions failed, AAA CDO tranches were downgraded en masse, and every holder — banks, SIVs, money funds, AIG — took simultaneous hits with no market prices to value the paper.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: tranching does not destroy risk — it **redistributes** it. The AAA rating of a CDO tranche depended on diversification/low-correlation assumptions across the pool; a *systematic* shock (national house-price decline) hit all collateral at once, so \"AAA\" senior tranches suffered losses thought to be virtually impossible. Re-securitization (mezz ABS → CDO) concentrated, rather than reduced, this systematic exposure.\n\n## The cast of institutions\n\n| Player | Role in the crisis | Failure mode |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mortgage brokers/lenders | Originated for fees under OTD | No skin in the game → fraudulent/weak underwriting (e.g., New Century, Countrywide) |\n| Commercial banks | Sponsored conduits/SIVs, warehoused pipelines, held \"super-senior\" | Off-balance-sheet exposure came back on; capital too thin (Citi, UBS) |\n| Investment banks (broker-dealers) | Structured and held CDOs; 30x+ leverage on overnight repo | Bear Stearns (rescued via JPMorgan, Mar 2008), Lehman (failed, Sep 2008), Merrill (sold) |\n| Rating agencies | Rated tranches AAA using flawed correlation models | **Issuer-pays conflict of interest**, ratings shopping, model error; mass downgrades destroyed confidence |\n| GSEs (Fannie/Freddie) | Guaranteed/held mortgage risk with implicit state backing | Conservatorship, Sep 2008 |\n| AIG / monolines | Sold CDS protection on CDO tranches | One-way bet; downgrade-triggered collateral calls → $182bn AIG rescue |\n| Money market funds | Held ABCP and Lehman paper | Reserve Primary \"broke the buck\" → run on money funds |\n| Investors (pensions, SIVs, foreign banks) | Reached for AAA yield | Outsourced credit analysis to ratings |\n\n## Short-term wholesale funding: the systemic accelerant\n\nThe pre-crisis trend: banks and shadow banks increasingly funded long-dated, illiquid assets with **overnight and short-term wholesale money** — asset-backed commercial paper (ABCP), repo, interbank borrowing — rather than insured deposits.\n\n- **ABCP**: conduits/SIVs rolled paper continuously against mortgage assets; in Aug 2007 (BNP Paribas fund suspensions) investors refused to roll → assets returned to sponsor banks.\n- **Repo**: dealers financed inventories overnight; as collateral values fell, **haircuts spiked** (a \"run on repo\"), forcing deleveraging fire sales — the margin/loss spiral.\n- **Interbank markets**: counterparty fear (who holds the toxic paper?) blew out LIBOR–OIS spreads; lending froze.\n\nWhy this is *systemic*: short-term funding makes lenders' positions runnable like deposits but without insurance; runs force asset sales that depress prices for everyone, transmitting one firm's trouble to all (fire-sale externalities). Funding liquidity and market liquidity collapse together.\n\n## Timeline anchors\n\n- **2006**: US house prices peak.\n- **Feb–Apr 2007**: subprime lenders fail; HSBC subprime losses.\n- **Jun–Aug 2007**: Bear Stearns hedge funds collapse; BNP freezes funds; ABCP run begins; central banks inject liquidity.\n- **Sep 2007**: Northern Rock run (UK).\n- **Mar 2008**: Bear Stearns rescued/sold to JPMorgan with Fed support.\n- **Sep 2008**: Fannie/Freddie conservatorship; **Lehman bankruptcy (Sep 15)**; AIG rescue; Reserve Primary breaks the buck; money-fund and CP markets seize.\n- **Oct 2008**: TARP ($700bn); bank recapitalizations; coordinated rate cuts.\n- **2009**: stress tests (SCAP) restore confidence; recovery begins.\n\n## Policy responses\n\n**Central banks:**\n- Slashed policy rates (Fed funds to ~0–0.25% by Dec 2008); coordinated international cuts.\n- **Lender of last resort, broadened**: term auction facilities (TAF), primary dealer facilities (PDCF), securities lending (TSLF), CP funding facility (CPFF), money-fund backstops, FX swap lines with foreign central banks.\n- **Quantitative easing**: large-scale purchases of Treasuries and agency MBS.\n\n**Governments:**\n- Capital injections (TARP; UK bank recapitalizations), expanded deposit insurance, debt guarantees.\n- Resolution/nationalization of failing institutions; auto-sector support.\n- Fiscal stimulus (2009 ARRA).\n\n**Aftermath/reform** (bridges to Ch 3–4): Basel III capital and liquidity standards (LCR/NSFR), Dodd-Frank, Volcker rule, central clearing and margin for OTC derivatives, securitization risk retention, stress-testing regimes, systemically-important-institution surcharges.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Causation chain to remember: cheap money + housing bubble → bad underwriting via OTD → securitization/re-securitization spreads and *hides* the risk → ratings legitimize it → leverage and short-term wholesale funding amplify it → house prices fall → losses + opacity → runs on ABCP/repo/money funds → fire sales and counterparty panic → Lehman → systemic collapse → state backstop.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Name the main ingredients of the GFC build-up.\n> **A:** Cheap money (1% Fed funds + global savings glut), a housing boom, collapsed underwriting (subprime/Alt-A, teaser ARMs, NINJA loans), securitization at scale via OTD, leverage everywhere (30–40x), and regulatory gaps (shadow banking, OTC derivatives).\n\n**Q2.** Trace the subprime-to-CDO transmission machine.\n> **A:** Brokers originate subprime for fees → loans pooled into RMBS, tranched into ~80% AAA → hard-to-sell mezzanine (BBB) tranches re-pooled into ABS CDOs (again rated AAA) → synthetic CDOs (CDS) multiply exposure → house prices fall, AAA tranches downgraded en masse.\n\n**Q3.** Exam trap: does tranching destroy risk?\n> **A:** No — it redistributes it. AAA ratings rested on low-correlation assumptions; a systematic shock (national house-price decline) hit all collateral at once, so \"impossible\" senior-tranche losses occurred. Re-securitization concentrated this exposure.\n\n**Q4.** How did the issuer-pays model contribute to the crisis?\n> **A:** Issuers shopped for and paid for ratings; agencies competing for structured-finance fees inflated grades — a conflict of interest that biased structured-product ratings upward, plus flawed correlation models.\n\n**Q5.** What was the role of mortgage brokers/lenders?\n> **A:** Originated loans for fees under originate-to-distribute with no skin in the game → fraudulent/weak underwriting (e.g., New Century, Countrywide).\n\n**Q6.** How did commercial vs. investment banks fail?\n> **A:** Commercial banks: off-balance-sheet conduits/SIVs and super-senior exposure came back on with too-thin capital (Citi, UBS). Investment banks: 30x+ leverage on overnight repo — Bear Stearns rescued (Mar 2008), Lehman failed (Sep 2008), Merrill sold.\n\n**Q7.** What did AIG and the monolines do?\n> **A:** Sold CDS protection on CDO tranches — a one-way bet. Downgrade-triggered collateral calls forced a $182bn AIG rescue.\n\n**Q8.** What is the \"run on repo\"?\n> **A:** As collateral values fell, repo lenders raised haircuts and refused to roll funding against securitized collateral, forcing deleveraging fire sales — the wholesale-market equivalent of a deposit run.\n\n**Q9.** Why was short-term wholesale funding the systemic accelerant?\n> **A:** It made lenders' positions runnable like deposits but without insurance; runs forced asset sales that depressed prices for everyone (fire-sale externalities), so funding liquidity and market liquidity collapsed together.\n\n**Q10.** What happened with ABCP in August 2007?\n> **A:** After BNP Paribas froze funds, investors refused to roll asset-backed commercial paper; the assets returned to the sponsor banks' balance sheets.\n\n**Q11.** What direct money-market consequence followed Lehman's bankruptcy?\n> **A:** Reserve Primary money fund held Lehman paper and \"broke the buck,\" triggering a run on money market funds and a freeze in the commercial-paper market.\n\n**Q12.** Key 2008 timeline anchors?\n> **A:** Mar 2008: Bear Stearns sold to JPMorgan. Sep 2008: Fannie/Freddie conservatorship, Lehman bankruptcy (Sep 15), AIG rescue, Reserve Primary breaks the buck. Oct 2008: TARP ($700bn).\n\n**Q13.** What central bank responses were deployed?\n> **A:** Policy rates slashed to ~0–0.25%, broadened lender-of-last-resort facilities (TAF, PDCF, TSLF, CPFF, FX swap lines, money-fund backstops), and quantitative easing (Treasuries and agency MBS purchases).\n\n**Q14.** Name the post-crisis reforms bridging to other chapters.\n> **A:** Basel III (capital + LCR/NSFR), Dodd-Frank, Volcker rule, central clearing and margin for OTC derivatives, securitization risk retention, stress-testing regimes, and SIFI surcharges.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** The \"issuer-pays\" model used by rating agencies contributed to the 2007–2009 crisis primarily by:\nA) Making ratings too expensive for small issuers to obtain B) Creating a conflict of interest that biased structured-product ratings upward C) Forcing agencies to rate only government bonds D) Eliminating competition among the rating agencies\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the way rating agencies got paid helped cause the crisis.\n\nUnder the issuer-pays model, the company creating and selling a bond pays the agency to rate it. That creates a conflict of interest: agencies competing for lucrative structured-finance fees had an incentive to give friendly (too high) ratings, and issuers could shop around for the best grade. This pushed many risky tranches up to AAA. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's like a student paying the examiner directly to grade their own homework — grades tend to drift upward.\n\n- A is wrong: the problem was ratings being too generous, not too expensive for small issuers.\n- C is wrong: agencies rated lots of private structured products, not just government bonds.\n- D is wrong: the issuer-pays conflict did not eliminate competition; if anything, competition for fees worsened the upward bias.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Issuer-pays = conflict of interest → inflated ratings + ratings shopping. This \"legitimized\" risky structured products.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Issuer-pays conflict\",\"steps\":[\"issuer pays for the rating\",\"shop for friendliest grade\",\"agencies chase fee income\",\"ratings biased upward (AAA)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which best describes the \"run on repo\" during 2007–2008?\nA) Insured depositors withdrew their savings from commercial banks B) Repo lenders raised haircuts and refused to roll funding against securitized collateral, forcing fire sales C) The Federal Reserve stopped accepting Treasury securities as collateral D) Hedge funds voluntarily increased their use of repo leverage\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the \"run on repo\" actually was.\n\nDealers financed their holdings overnight using repo (borrowing against securities as collateral). As the value of mortgage-related collateral fell, lenders demanded bigger haircuts (more collateral per dollar lent) and eventually refused to roll the loans at all. Cut off from funding, dealers had to dump assets in fire sales, pushing prices down further — a margin/loss spiral. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's a bank run, but instead of depositors lining up, it's the lenders who finance trading desks suddenly demanding more collateral and then pulling their money.\n\n- A is wrong: that describes a traditional retail deposit run, not the repo (wholesale) run.\n- C is wrong: the Fed did not stop accepting Treasuries; in fact it broadened its lending facilities.\n- D is wrong: the run was about lenders WITHDRAWING repo funding, not hedge funds increasing leverage.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Repo run = haircuts spike + lenders refuse to roll → forced fire sales. It is the wholesale-market equivalent of a deposit run, but with no insurance.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Run on repo\",\"steps\":[\"collateral value falls\",\"lenders raise haircuts\",\"refuse to roll funding\",\"dealers dump assets\",\"fire sales push prices down\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** ABS CDOs built largely from mezzanine (BBB) RMBS tranches proved especially toxic because:\nA) They held only prime, high-quality mortgages B) Their AAA ratings rested on low cross-pool correlation, while a nationwide house-price decline was a common systematic shock C) They were fully insured by the FDIC D) They were exchange-traded and heavily over-margined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why re-packaging weak mortgage tranches into CDOs backfired so badly.\n\nMezzanine RMBS tranches (rated BBB) were re-pooled into ABS CDOs, and the agencies again rated much of them AAA. That high rating depended on the assumption that mortgages in different regions would NOT all default together (low correlation). But a nationwide fall in house prices hit all the collateral at once — a common, systematic shock. The \"diversification\" assumption collapsed, and supposedly safe AAA tranches took heavy losses. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: bundling many \"mostly safe\" umbrellas only protects you if it doesn't rain everywhere at once. A nationwide storm soaks them all together.\n\n- A is wrong: these CDOs were built from subprime/mezzanine tranches, not prime mortgages.\n- C is wrong: CDO tranches were not FDIC-insured.\n- D is wrong: these were largely OTC structured products, not over-margined exchange-traded instruments.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Tranching REDISTRIBUTES risk, it doesn't destroy it. AAA ratings assumed low correlation; a systematic (nationwide) shock hits all collateral together.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"AAA assumption vs reality\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Model assumed\",\"items\":[\"low cross-pool correlation\",\"regional defaults independent\",\"senior tranche near-safe\"]},{\"h\":\"What happened\",\"items\":[\"nationwide price fall\",\"systematic shock, corr -> 1\",\"AAA tranches took losses\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which event was a direct consequence of the Lehman Brothers bankruptcy in September 2008?\nA) The first Basel III rules took immediate effect B) The Reserve Primary money market fund \"broke the buck,\" triggering a run on money funds C) US house prices reached their peak D) Northern Rock experienced a retail deposit run\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what followed directly from Lehman's failure.\n\nThe Reserve Primary fund held Lehman commercial paper. When Lehman went bankrupt, that paper became nearly worthless, and the fund's value per share fell below $1 — it \"broke the buck.\" That shocked investors, who then ran on money market funds and the commercial paper market seized up. So B is the direct consequence.\n\nAnalogy: people thought their money fund was as safe as cash; once one fund proved it wasn't, everyone rushed to pull money from all of them.\n\n- A is wrong: Basel III was developed AFTER the crisis and phased in over later years, not instantly in September 2008.\n- C is wrong: US house prices peaked back in 2006, before Lehman.\n- D is wrong: Northern Rock's run happened in September 2007, a YEAR before Lehman.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Lehman (Sep 15, 2008) → Reserve Primary breaks the buck → money-fund run and CP market freeze. Watch the dates: house prices peaked 2006, Northern Rock ran in 2007.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"GFC key dates\",\"points\":[{\"t\":\"2006\",\"l\":\"House prices peak\"},{\"t\":\"Sep 2007\",\"l\":\"Northern Rock run\"},{\"t\":\"Mar 2008\",\"l\":\"Bear Stearns rescued\"},{\"t\":\"Sep 2008\",\"l\":\"Lehman fails\"},{\"t\":\"Sep 2008\",\"l\":\"Reserve breaks buck\"}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** During the build-up to the crisis, the \"originate-to-distribute\" (OTD) model of mortgage lending mainly:\nA) Forced lenders to hold every loan they made to maturity B) Severed the link between underwriting quality and who ultimately bore the losses C) Required all mortgages to be government-guaranteed D) Eliminated the use of securitization\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the originate-to-distribute model did to lending incentives.\n\nUnder OTD, brokers and lenders made loans for FEES and then quickly sold (distributed) them to be securitized, passing the default risk on to others. Because the originator no longer bore the loss if the borrower defaulted, they had little reason to check the loan carefully — \"no skin in the game.\" This broke the link between underwriting quality and loss-bearing. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: if a chef knows someone else will eat the meal and complain, they may stop tasting the food for quality.\n\n- A is wrong: OTD is the OPPOSITE of holding loans to maturity; loans were sold off.\n- C is wrong: OTD did not require government guarantees; much of it was private-label securitization.\n- D is wrong: OTD RELIED on securitization, it did not eliminate it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OTD = make loans for fees, sell off the risk. \"No skin in the game\" → weak underwriting (e.g., liar loans, NINJA loans).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Originate-to-distribute\",\"steps\":[\"make loan for fees\",\"sell/securitize it\",\"risk passed to others\",\"no skin in the game\",\"weak underwriting\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** AIG's central role in the crisis came from:\nA) Originating most US subprime mortgages B) Selling large amounts of credit default swap (CDS) protection on CDO tranches, a one-way bet that led to huge collateral calls C) Running the largest money market fund in the world D) Acting as the main repo lender to broker-dealers\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why AIG ended up needing a giant rescue.\n\nAIG (and the monoline insurers) sold CDS protection on CDO tranches — effectively promising to pay if those tranches lost value. This was a one-way bet: it collected premiums while times were good but had enormous exposure if the tranches fell. When AIG was downgraded, it faced massive collateral calls it couldn't meet, leading to a $182 billion rescue. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: AIG was like an insurer that sold flood insurance to the whole town cheaply, never expecting a flood — then the flood came and it couldn't pay all the claims at once.\n\n- A is wrong: AIG did not originate most subprime mortgages; brokers and lenders like New Century and Countrywide did that.\n- C is wrong: that describes money market funds (e.g., Reserve Primary), not AIG.\n- D is wrong: AIG was not the main repo lender; it was a seller of CDS protection.\n\n📌 Exam tip: AIG = sold CDS protection on CDO tranches (one-way bet) → downgrade-triggered collateral calls → $182bn rescue.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"AIG collapse\",\"steps\":[\"sold CDS on CDO tranches\",\"collected premiums (one-way bet)\",\"tranches fall + AIG downgraded\",\"huge collateral calls\",\"$182bn rescue\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In the securitization chain, what does \"tranching\" of a mortgage pool accomplish?\nA) It destroys the credit risk in the pool entirely B) It redistributes the pool's cash flows and losses into layers of different seniority, so senior tranches can earn high ratings C) It guarantees every tranche the same rating D) It converts the mortgages into insured bank deposits\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what tranching does in a securitization.\n\nTranching slices a pool's cash flows into layers (tranches) ranked by who gets paid first and who absorbs losses first. The most senior tranche is paid first and is protected by the junior layers beneath it, so it can earn a high (often AAA) rating, while junior tranches take losses first. Importantly, tranching redistributes risk; it does not make the risk disappear. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's like a stadium where the top-deck seats stay dry longest in a storm because the lower decks flood first — but the rain (risk) is still there.\n\n- A is wrong: tranching does NOT destroy credit risk; it merely reshuffles who bears it.\n- C is wrong: tranches deliberately get DIFFERENT ratings (senior high, junior low), not the same.\n- D is wrong: tranching does not turn mortgages into insured deposits.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Tranching redistributes risk into seniority layers; it does not eliminate it. Senior tranches look safe only while loss correlation stays low.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Tranche seniority (loss order)\",\"items\":[\"Senior (AAA) paid first\",\"|Mezzanine (BBB)\",\"Equity absorbs losses first\"],\"left\":\"safest\",\"right\":\"first loss\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which factor in the early-2000s build-up most directly fed the housing bubble?\nA) Very high interest rates and tight money B) Cheap money (low Fed funds rate) and a global savings glut pushing investors to reach for yield C) A ban on mortgage securitization D) A nationwide decline in house prices before 2006\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which condition fueled the housing boom before the crisis.\n\nAfter 2001, the Fed funds rate was very low (around 1%), and a global \"savings glut\" sent money searching for higher returns. Cheap, plentiful money made mortgages easy to get and pushed investors toward higher-yielding mortgage products, inflating the housing bubble. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: when money is cheap and everyone is hunting for a bit more return, they pile into the same hot market — here, housing — and prices balloon.\n\n- A is wrong: rates were LOW, not high; cheap money fueled the boom.\n- C is wrong: securitization was booming, not banned; it helped spread the lending.\n- D is wrong: house prices were RISING for years before peaking in 2006; the decline came later and triggered the bust.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Build-up ingredients = cheap money + savings glut + housing boom + collapsed underwriting + securitization + heavy leverage + regulatory gaps.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Housing bubble fuel\",\"steps\":[\"Fed funds ~1% (cheap money)\",\"global savings glut\",\"reach for yield\",\"easy mortgages\",\"house prices balloon\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A major reason short-term wholesale funding made the crisis SYSTEMIC was that:\nA) It was always fully insured, so losses fell only on the government B) It made many institutions' funding runnable like deposits but without insurance, so runs forced asset sales that hurt everyone C) It was used only by a single small bank D) It carried no rollover risk because it was long-term\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why reliance on short-term wholesale funding spread trouble across the whole system.\n\nShort-term wholesale funding (ABCP, repo, interbank) had to be rolled over constantly, which made lenders' positions \"runnable\" — they could be pulled quickly, just like deposits, but WITHOUT deposit insurance. When one institution faced a run and had to sell assets, those fire sales pushed prices down for everyone holding similar assets, transmitting one firm's trouble across the system (fire-sale externalities). So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: if many shops all depend on the same overnight loans and one is forced to dump its inventory cheaply, it crashes the price for every other shop's stock too.\n\n- A is wrong: wholesale funding was NOT insured; that's exactly why runs were so dangerous.\n- C is wrong: many institutions, not just one small bank, relied on it — that's what made it systemic.\n- D is wrong: it was SHORT-term and carried heavy rollover risk; that was the core danger.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Short-term wholesale funding = runnable like deposits but uninsured. Runs → fire sales → falling prices for all holders = systemic contagion.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Why wholesale funding is systemic\",\"steps\":[\"short-term, must roll\",\"runnable but uninsured\",\"one run forces asset sales\",\"fire sales hit all holders\",\"contagion across system\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which of the following was part of the central banks' response to the crisis?\nA) Raising policy rates sharply to fight a boom B) Slashing policy rates toward zero and broadening lender-of-last-resort facilities, plus quantitative easing C) Refusing to provide any emergency liquidity to markets D) Banning all government bond purchases\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what central banks actually did during the crisis.\n\nCentral banks cut policy rates dramatically (the Fed funds rate fell to about 0–0.25% by December 2008), broadened lender-of-last-resort tools (term auction facilities, primary dealer and commercial paper facilities, money-fund backstops, FX swap lines), and launched quantitative easing — large-scale purchases of Treasuries and agency MBS. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: like firefighters opening every hydrant and cutting the cost of water to zero so the blaze can be smothered fast.\n\n- A is wrong: central banks CUT rates, not raised them; there was no boom to fight.\n- C is wrong: they provided massive emergency liquidity, the opposite of refusing.\n- D is wrong: under QE they BOUGHT large amounts of government bonds, not banned purchases.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Central-bank response = near-zero rates + broadened lender-of-last-resort facilities + QE. Governments added TARP, guarantees, and stress tests.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Crisis responders\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Central banks\",\"items\":[\"cut rates to ~0\",\"broaden lender-of-last-resort\",\"quantitative easing (QE)\"]},{\"h\":\"Governments\",\"items\":[\"TARP capital injections\",\"deposit/debt guarantees\",\"stress tests, stimulus\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** The \"issuer-pays\" model of rating agencies contributed to the crisis primarily by:\nA) Making ratings too expensive for small issuers B) Creating a conflict of interest that biased structured-product ratings upward C) Forcing agencies to rate only government bonds D) Eliminating competition among agencies\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Issuers shopped for and paid for ratings; agencies competing for structured-finance fees inflated grades.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which best describes the \"run on repo\" during 2007–2008?\nA) Depositors withdrew insured deposits from commercial banks B) Repo lenders raised haircuts and refused to roll funding against securitized collateral, forcing fire sales C) The Fed stopped accepting Treasury collateral D) Hedge funds increased their use of repo leverage\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Haircut spirals on repo collateral are the wholesale-market equivalent of a deposit run.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** ABS CDOs built from mezzanine RMBS tranches proved especially toxic because:\nA) They held only prime mortgages B) Their AAA ratings rested on low cross-pool correlation, while a national house-price decline was a common systematic shock C) They were fully insured by the FDIC D) They were exchange-traded and over-margined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Re-securitization concentrated systematic housing risk while models assumed diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which was a direct consequence of the Lehman bankruptcy in September 2008?\nA) The first round of Basel III rules took immediate effect B) Reserve Primary money fund \"broke the buck,\" triggering a run on money market funds C) US house prices peaked D) Northern Rock experienced a retail run\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Reserve Primary held Lehman paper; its loss sparked the money-fund run and CP freeze. (Northern Rock was a year earlier; house prices peaked in 2006.)\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q15.** During 2007–2008, rising repo haircuts on securitized collateral functioned most like:\nA) An increase in deposit insurance coverage B) A bank run in the wholesale funding market, forcing deleveraging and fire sales C) A monetary policy tightening engineered by the Fed D) A reduction in systemic risk through higher collateralization\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Raising haircuts withdraws funding at the margin exactly like depositors queueing at a branch: borrowers must sell assets to repay, depressing collateral values and triggering further haircut rises — the fire-sale spiral that made repo a systemic channel.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Repo run / fire-sale spiral\",\"steps\":[\"Haircuts rise\",\"Funding withdrawn\",\"Forced asset sales\",\"Collateral prices fall\",\"Haircuts rise again\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** During 2002–2006, US originate-to-distribute (OTD) securitization severed the link between mortgage underwriting and loss-bearing. Which sequence correctly orders the transmission machine from origination to the most toxic re-securitized instrument?\nA) Subprime mortgages → ABS CDO → RMBS tranches → synthetic CDO B) Subprime mortgages → RMBS (tranched, ~80% AAA) → mezzanine (BBB) tranches re-pooled into ABS CDOs (again largely AAA) → synthetic CDOs (CDS on the same tranches) C) Synthetic CDOs → subprime mortgages → RMBS → ABS CDO D) Subprime mortgages → AAA Treasuries → RMBS → ABS CDO\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: order the securitization chain that spread and hid mortgage risk.\n\n1. Brokers/lenders originate subprime mortgages *for fees*, passing risk on immediately.\n2. Loans are pooled into **RMBS**; subordination/tranching converts mostly weak collateral into roughly 80% AAA-rated bonds.\n3. The hard-to-sell **mezzanine (BBB)** tranches are re-pooled into **ABS CDOs**, which agencies again rated largely AAA — re-securitization \"alchemy\" resting on low default-correlation assumptions.\n4. **Synthetic CDOs** (CDS written on the same tranches) multiplied exposure beyond the actual stock of mortgages; AIG and the monolines sold the protection.\n\nWhy B is correct: it reflects the exact origination → RMBS → mezz-into-ABS-CDO → synthetic ordering.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A puts the ABS CDO *before* the RMBS tranches it is built from — backward; you cannot re-pool tranches that do not yet exist.\n- C starts with synthetic CDOs, which are *derivatives referencing* tranches that must already exist — impossible as a first step.\n- D inserts AAA Treasuries into the chain; Treasuries are not part of the subprime securitization pipeline.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Know the chain cold — mortgages → RMBS (tranched to manufacture AAA) → mezzanine BBB tranches → ABS CDO (re-securitization) → synthetic CDO. Tranching *redistributes* risk; re-securitization *concentrates* systematic (housing) risk while pretending to diversify it.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Securitization chain\",\"steps\":[\"subprime mortgages\",\"RMBS (~80% AAA)\",\"mezz BBB tranches\",\"re-pooled into ABS CDO\",\"synthetic CDO (CDS)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An exam item states: \"AAA-rated senior tranches of ABS CDOs built from mezzanine RMBS were thought almost incapable of loss, yet they suffered severe losses in 2007–2009.\" Which explanation is the most accurate?\nA) The tranches actually held mostly prime, low-risk mortgages, so the losses were fraud-driven only B) The AAA ratings depended on *low cross-pool default correlation*; a national house-price decline was a common systematic shock that hit all underlying pools at once, so diversification assumed in the models did not materialize C) The tranches were exchange-traded and over-margined, which forced their prices down mechanically D) The FDIC insured the tranches, and its insolvency caused the losses\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: explain why \"safe\" senior CDO tranches lost so much.\n\n1. Tranche ratings relied on the assumption that defaults across the many underlying mortgage pools were *weakly correlated* — diversification would protect the senior claim.\n2. A nationwide house-price decline is a *systematic* shock: it hits essentially all pools simultaneously, driving correlations toward 1. The assumed diversification vanished, so losses cascaded up into supposedly remote senior tranches.\n3. Re-securitization (mezz BBB → ABS CDO) made this worse by concentrating the same systematic housing exposure rather than diversifying it.\n\nWhy B is correct: it pinpoints the correlation/systematic-shock failure at the heart of the GFC.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A is false — these CDOs were built largely from *subprime/mezzanine* exposure, and the losses were driven by the systematic shock, not solely fraud.\n- C invents an exchange-trading/margin mechanism; these were largely OTC, opaque instruments with *no* reliable market prices, not exchange-margined.\n- D fabricates FDIC insurance of CDO tranches — they were never FDIC-insured.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The single most-tested GFC concept: tranching does not destroy risk, it *redistributes* it, and AAA senior CDO tranches rested on *low-correlation* assumptions. A systematic shock (national house-price fall) breaks those assumptions and inflicts \"impossible\" senior losses.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"AAA assumption vs reality\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Model assumed\",\"items\":[\"low cross-pool correlation\",\"pools default independently\",\"senior tranche near-safe\"]},{\"h\":\"Systematic shock\",\"items\":[\"nationwide price fall\",\"correlation -> 1\",\"AAA senior tranches lose\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The \"issuer-pays\" rating-agency model is widely cited as a contributor to the crisis. Which description best captures the mechanism by which it biased structured-finance ratings?\nA) Issuers paid agencies for ratings and could shop among agencies competing for lucrative structured-finance fees, creating a conflict of interest that biased ratings upward B) Investors paid agencies directly, so agencies inflated ratings to please buyers C) Regulators set the ratings, and their models were flawed D) Agencies were legally barred from rating structured products, so unrated junk circulated\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: identify the conflict-of-interest mechanism in issuer-pays ratings.\n\n1. Under issuer-pays, the *issuer* of the structured product pays the agency for the rating. Issuers could approach multiple agencies and \"shop\" for the most favorable grade.\n2. Because structured-finance ratings were highly profitable and competition for that fee income was intense, agencies faced strong pressure to assign generous ratings — an upward bias — compounded by flawed correlation models.\n\nWhy A is correct: it captures both the payer conflict and ratings-shopping that inflated structured grades.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- B reverses who pays — it was *issuers*, not investors, under the criticized model.\n- C is false; agencies (not regulators) set ratings, though their models were indeed flawed — the conflict, not regulator-set ratings, is the mechanism.\n- D is fabricated; agencies actively rated structured products — that was the problem, not a prohibition.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Issuer-pays + ratings shopping + competition for structured-finance fees = upward ratings bias. Investors outsourced credit analysis to these ratings, so the conflict propagated systemic mispricing.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Issuer-pays bias\",\"steps\":[\"issuer pays for rating\",\"shop multiple agencies\",\"compete for fee income\",\"ratings biased upward\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which statement best describes the \"run on repo\" of 2007–2008 and why it was systemically dangerous?\nA) Insured retail depositors withdrew cash from commercial banks, draining deposits B) Repo lenders sharply raised haircuts and refused to roll short-term funding against securitized collateral; dealers had to sell assets into falling markets (fire sales), depressing prices for all holders and transmitting one firm's stress to everyone C) The Fed stopped accepting Treasury securities as repo collateral D) Hedge funds voluntarily reduced repo leverage, calming markets\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: define the repo run and its systemic mechanism.\n\n1. Dealers financed large inventories of securities *overnight* in the repo market. As the value and trustworthiness of mortgage-related collateral fell, lenders demanded much larger *haircuts* (more collateral per dollar lent) and increasingly refused to roll funding at all.\n2. Cut off from funding, dealers were forced to sell assets immediately — *fire sales* — which pushed prices down further, raised haircuts again, and inflicted mark-to-market losses on every other holder. Funding liquidity and market liquidity collapsed together.\n3. This is a wholesale-market analogue of a deposit run, but *without* deposit insurance, which is what makes it systemic (fire-sale externalities).\n\nWhy B is correct: it captures haircut spikes, refusal to roll, fire sales, and contagion.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A describes a *retail* deposit run on insured deposits — the wrong market; the repo run was wholesale and uninsured.\n- C invents a Fed refusal of Treasury collateral; in fact the Fed *broadened* its lending facilities to accept more collateral.\n- D is the opposite of reality — leverage and forced selling *amplified* the crisis; there was no calming voluntary deleveraging.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Short-term wholesale funding (ABCP, repo, interbank) made positions *runnable like deposits but uninsured*. A repo run = haircut spiral + refusal to roll → fire sales → contagion. This is the systemic accelerant that turned a credit problem into collapse.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Run on repo\",\"steps\":[\"collateral value falls\",\"lenders raise haircuts\",\"refuse to roll funding\",\"forced fire sales\",\"prices drop for all holders\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Match each institution to its primary *role/failure mode* in the GFC: (i) AIG, (ii) rating agencies, (iii) commercial banks sponsoring SIVs/conduits.\nA) (i) sold CDS protection on CDO tranches (one-way bet) with downgrade-triggered collateral calls; (ii) issuer-pays conflict and flawed correlation models; (iii) off-balance-sheet exposure returned on-balance-sheet as conduits could not roll ABCP B) (i) originated subprime loans for fees; (ii) guaranteed mortgages with state backing; (iii) broke the buck C) (i) ran a money market fund; (ii) levered 30x on repo; (iii) entered conservatorship D) (i) entered conservatorship; (ii) sold CDS protection; (iii) rated tranches AAA\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: correctly map three players to their roles.\n\n1. **AIG** (and the monolines) *sold CDS protection* on CDO tranches — a one-way bet on housing. Rating downgrades triggered large collateral calls, leading to the ~$182bn rescue.\n2. **Rating agencies** rated tranches AAA using flawed correlation models under an *issuer-pays* conflict (ratings shopping).\n3. **Commercial banks** sponsored conduits/SIVs and warehoused pipelines; when ABCP could not be rolled, the off-balance-sheet exposure came *back onto* their balance sheets, and thin capital was overwhelmed (e.g., Citi, UBS).\n\nWhy A is correct: each role is matched precisely.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- B mislabels AIG as a loan originator, the agencies as a GSE guarantor, and banks as a money fund — all swapped roles.\n- C assigns AIG a money-fund role, the agencies a 30x repo-leverage role (that was broker-dealers), and banks the GSE conservatorship — scrambled.\n- D gives AIG the GSE conservatorship (that was Fannie/Freddie), the agencies AIG's CDS role, and banks the agencies' rating role — also scrambled.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Keep the cast straight — AIG/monolines = CDS protection sellers (collateral calls on downgrade); agencies = AAA-stamping under issuer-pays; commercial banks = SIV/conduit sponsors whose off-balance-sheet risk came home; broker-dealers = 30x+ repo leverage (Bear, Lehman, Merrill); GSEs = conservatorship; Reserve Primary = broke the buck.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"GFC players -> role\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Player\",\"items\":[\"AIG / monolines\",\"Rating agencies\",\"Banks w/ SIVs\"]},{\"h\":\"Role / failure\",\"items\":[\"sold CDS -> collateral calls\",\"AAA under issuer-pays\",\"off-B/S risk came home\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which event is a *direct* consequence of the Lehman Brothers bankruptcy on September 15, 2008?\nA) US national house prices peaked B) The Reserve Primary money market fund \"broke the buck\" because it held Lehman commercial paper, triggering a run on money market funds and a freeze in commercial paper markets C) Northern Rock experienced a retail deposit run D) The first Basel III capital rules took immediate legal effect\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify a direct downstream effect of the Lehman failure.\n\n1. The Reserve Primary Fund held Lehman commercial paper. When Lehman defaulted, that paper's value collapsed, pushing the fund's net asset value below $1 — it \"broke the buck.\"\n2. Investors fled money market funds (previously treated as cash-equivalents), and the commercial paper market seized, prompting Fed money-fund backstops and the CPFF.\n\nWhy B is correct: the money-fund break and CP freeze flowed directly from Lehman's default.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A is a timing trap — US house prices peaked in *2006*, two years *before* Lehman; it cannot be a consequence.\n- C is also a timing trap — the Northern Rock retail run occurred in September *2007*, a year earlier.\n- D is false; Basel III was a *post-crisis reform* phased in over years, not an immediate effect of Lehman.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Anchor the timeline. 2006: house prices peak. Sep 2007: Northern Rock run. Mar 2008: Bear Stearns rescued. Sep 15, 2008: Lehman fails → Reserve Primary breaks the buck → money-fund run and CP freeze → AIG rescue. Basel III/Dodd-Frank are *aftermath* reforms.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"GFC timeline\",\"points\":[{\"t\":\"2006\",\"l\":\"House prices peak\"},{\"t\":\"Sep 2007\",\"l\":\"Northern Rock run\"},{\"t\":\"Mar 2008\",\"l\":\"Bear Stearns rescued\"},{\"t\":\"Sep 2008\",\"l\":\"Lehman fails\"},{\"t\":\"Sep 2008\",\"l\":\"Reserve breaks buck\"}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A central bank's crisis response is described to a candidate: \"It slashed policy rates to near zero, created new facilities to lend against a broad range of collateral to dealers and money funds, opened FX swap lines with foreign central banks, and purchased large quantities of Treasuries and agency MBS.\" Which classification of these actions is most accurate?\nA) All four are examples of fiscal policy executed by the Treasury B) They are central-bank actions: rate cuts (conventional policy), broadened lender-of-last-resort facilities (TAF/PDCF/TSLF/CPFF, money-fund backstops, FX swap lines), and quantitative easing (large-scale Treasury and agency-MBS purchases) C) They are all examples of TARP, a government capital-injection program D) They are micro-prudential supervision measures unrelated to liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: classify the listed crisis-response tools.\n\n1. Near-zero policy rates = *conventional monetary policy* (coordinated international cuts to ~0–0.25% by December 2008).\n2. New lending facilities against broad collateral to dealers and money funds, plus FX swap lines with foreign central banks = a *broadened lender-of-last-resort* role (TAF, PDCF, TSLF, CPFF, money-fund backstops).\n3. Large-scale purchases of Treasuries and agency MBS = *quantitative easing*.\nAll three are *central-bank* (monetary/liquidity) actions.\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly buckets the actions into rate cuts, LOLR facilities, and QE, all central-bank tools.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A calls them fiscal/Treasury actions; fiscal measures were TARP capital injections, deposit-insurance expansion, and 2009 ARRA stimulus — separate from these monetary tools.\n- C labels everything TARP, which was a *government* capital-injection program, not central-bank lending or QE.\n- D mislabels these macro-liquidity interventions as micro-prudential supervision, which they are not.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Separate the responders. Central banks: rate cuts, broadened lender-of-last-resort facilities (TAF/PDCF/TSLF/CPFF, swap lines), and QE. Governments: TARP capital injections, expanded deposit insurance/guarantees, resolution/nationalization, fiscal stimulus. Don't lump QE and TARP together.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Central bank vs government tools\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Central bank\",\"items\":[\"near-zero rates\",\"LOLR facilities + swap lines\",\"QE: buy Treasuries/MBS\"]},{\"h\":\"Government\",\"items\":[\"TARP capital injections\",\"deposit/debt guarantees\",\"fiscal stimulus (ARRA)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"111","module":"Book 1: Foundations of Risk Management","title":"GARP Code of Conduct","notes":"# 11. GARP Code of Conduct\n\nThe Code binds every GARP member, FRM holder, and exam candidate. One or two near-free exam questions: know the principles, the professional standards, and the consequences of violation. Answer Code questions by the *strictest* reasonable reading.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the responsibility of each GARP member with respect to professional integrity, ethical conduct, conflicts of interest, confidentiality of information, and adherence to generally accepted practices in risk management.\n- Describe the potential consequences of violating the GARP Code of Conduct.\n\n## Who is bound, and how\n\nThe Code applies to **GARP members**, holders of GARP certifications (FRM, ERP/SCR), and **candidates registered for GARP exams**. By registering, you accept the Code. It applies to professional conduct generally — not only to acts performed in a formal \"risk manager\" role — and members must comply with the Code **even where local law or practice is laxer** (where law and the Code conflict, follow the stricter standard consistent with law).\n\n## Principles\n\n**1. Professional integrity and ethical conduct.** Members shall:\n- Act professionally, ethically, and with integrity in all dealings.\n- Exercise reasonable judgment and maintain independence of thought and direction; not offer, solicit, or accept gifts or benefits that could reasonably be expected to compromise (or appear to compromise) their own or others' independence.\n- Take reasonable precautions to ensure their services are not used to deceive, manipulate markets, or violate law.\n- Not knowingly misrepresent facts or make false or misleading statements (including about their own or others' qualifications and the meaning of the FRM designation).\n\n**2. Conflicts of interest.** Members shall:\n- Act fairly in all situations.\n- **Fully disclose** any actual or potential conflict of interest to all affected parties — disclosure, not necessarily avoidance, is the operative duty.\n\n**3. Confidentiality.** Members shall:\n- Not make use of confidential information for **personal benefit** (their own or others' inappropriate advantage).\n- Not reveal confidential information except where consent has been given or disclosure is **required by law** or by legal/regulatory process.\n\n## Professional standards\n\n**1. Fundamental responsibilities.** Members shall:\n- Comply with all applicable laws, rules, and regulations (including GARP's own bylaws and policies) governing their professional activities.\n- Have ethical responsibilities to the profession, to colleagues, to employers, and to the public, and not engage in conduct that compromises these responsibilities.\n- Understand the needs and complexity of their employer or client and provide appropriate, competent services.\n- Be diligent about not overstating the accuracy or certainty of results or conclusions.\n- Maintain professional competence — keep current with risk management knowledge and best practices, and apply them.\n\n**2. Adherence to generally accepted practices in risk management.** Members shall:\n- Execute responsibilities with diligence, using generally accepted risk management practices.\n- Ensure that communications of risk information are **clear, accurate, and not misleading** — including making assumptions, limitations, and the degree of uncertainty in models and analyses transparent.\n- Make a distinction between fact and opinion when presenting analysis and recommendations.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Two themes the Code repeats: **transparency about limitations** (never overstate model accuracy or hide assumptions) and **disclosure** (conflicts must be fully disclosed to all affected parties). When two answer choices both look ethical, the one involving disclosure/escalation and honest communication of uncertainty is usually the Code-compliant one.\n\n## Consequences of violation\n\n- Violations may lead to formal investigation under GARP's disciplinary procedures.\n- Sanctions can include **suspension or revocation of GARP membership** and of **the right to use the FRM (or other GARP) designation**; candidates can be barred from the exam program.\n- Violations of *law* are additionally subject to referral to and action by legal, regulatory, or governmental authorities — the Code does not shield anyone from the law.\n- Reputational damage: censure within the profession.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam traps: (1) The Code does **not** say \"avoid all conflicts of interest\" — it requires acting fairly and **fully disclosing** actual or potential conflicts. (2) Confidentiality is not absolute — disclosure compelled **by law** (or with consent) is permitted. (3) The Code binds **candidates from registration**, not only certified FRMs. (4) \"My country's rules allow it\" is not a defense for conduct the Code prohibits.\n\n## Application examples\n\n- A risk manager presents a VaR figure to the board without mentioning that the model excludes the firm's largest illiquid position → violates *generally accepted practices* (clear, accurate, complete communication; disclosure of limitations).\n- An FRM candidate lists herself as \"FRM certified\" while awaiting Part II results → misrepresentation of qualifications → violates *professional integrity*.\n- A consultant recommends a software vendor without mentioning he receives referral fees → failure to disclose a **conflict of interest**.\n- A member shares a client's portfolio details with a friend at a hedge fund → **confidentiality** violation (and likely law).\n- A member receives a subpoena for client records and complies → **not** a violation; disclosure required by law is permitted.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Who is bound by the GARP Code of Conduct?\n> **A:** GARP members, holders of GARP certifications (FRM, ERP/SCR), and candidates registered for GARP exams. By registering you accept the Code.\n\n**Q2.** Exam trap: does the Code only apply to formal \"risk manager\" roles?\n> **A:** No. It applies to professional conduct generally — and members must comply even where local law or practice is laxer (where they conflict, follow the stricter standard consistent with law).\n\n**Q3.** What does the conflicts-of-interest principle require — avoidance or disclosure?\n> **A:** Disclosure. Members must act fairly and fully disclose any actual or potential conflict to all affected parties. Disclosure, not necessarily avoidance, is the operative duty.\n\n**Q4.** When may a member reveal confidential information?\n> **A:** Only with consent, or where disclosure is required by law or legal/regulatory process. Confidential information must never be used for personal benefit. Confidentiality is not absolute.\n\n**Q5.** What does the professional-integrity principle require regarding statements and qualifications?\n> **A:** Not knowingly misrepresent facts or make false/misleading statements — including about one's own or others' qualifications and the meaning of the FRM designation.\n\n**Q6.** What does integrity require regarding gifts and independence?\n> **A:** Exercise reasonable judgment and maintain independence; do not offer, solicit, or accept gifts/benefits that could reasonably be expected to compromise (or appear to compromise) independence.\n\n**Q7.** What does \"adherence to generally accepted practices\" require for communicating risk?\n> **A:** Communications of risk information must be clear, accurate, and not misleading — making assumptions, limitations, and the degree of model/analysis uncertainty transparent.\n\n**Q8.** What two themes does the Code repeat?\n> **A:** Transparency about limitations (never overstate model accuracy or hide assumptions) and disclosure (conflicts fully disclosed to all affected parties).\n\n**Q9.** Under fundamental responsibilities, what must members do regarding results?\n> **A:** Be diligent about not overstating the accuracy or certainty of results or conclusions, and distinguish fact from opinion in analysis and recommendations.\n\n**Q10.** What are the potential consequences of violating the Code?\n> **A:** Formal investigation; suspension or revocation of GARP membership and the right to use the FRM (or other GARP) designation; candidates barred from the exam program; plus reputational censure.\n\n**Q11.** Does GARP impose criminal penalties or fines?\n> **A:** No. GARP can suspend/revoke membership and designation rights, but violations of law are referred to legal/regulatory authorities — the Code does not shield anyone from the law, nor levy criminal penalties or fines.\n\n**Q12.** Trap: can a candidate awaiting Part II results call herself \"FRM certified\"?\n> **A:** No — that misrepresents qualifications and violates professional integrity. The designation is not held until fully certified.\n\n**Q13.** Is \"my country's rules allow it\" a valid defense?\n> **A:** No. It is not a defense for conduct the Code prohibits; members follow the stricter of the Code and applicable law.\n\n**Q14.** A risk manager presents VaR to the board without noting the model excludes the largest illiquid position. Which principle is violated?\n> **A:** Adherence to generally accepted practices — risk communication must be clear, accurate, complete, and disclose limitations.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A GARP member learns that part of her bonus depends on the profits of the trading desk whose risk she independently reports on. Under the Code, she must:\nA) Resign from her position immediately B) Quietly decline the bonus and say nothing C) Fully disclose the conflict of interest to all affected parties D) Keep silent unless someone specifically asks her\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what the Code requires when a member faces a conflict of interest.\n\nThe GARP Code's main remedy for conflicts of interest is FULL DISCLOSURE to all affected parties — not necessarily avoiding the situation. By telling everyone affected, the member lets them judge her objectivity with the conflict in plain view. So C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a referee who is also a fan of one team should announce it openly, so everyone knows to watch for bias — not just secretly try to be fair.\n\n- A is wrong: the Code does not require resigning over every conflict; disclosure is the operative duty.\n- B is wrong: declining the bonus silently does not satisfy the duty to disclose the conflict to affected parties.\n- D is wrong: staying silent until asked is the opposite of full, proactive disclosure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The Code says DISCLOSE conflicts (fully, to all affected parties) — it does NOT say \"avoid all conflicts.\" Disclosure beats silence.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Conflict of interest remedy\",\"options\":[\"A: resign immediately\",\"B: decline bonus, say nothing\",\"C: fully disclose to all affected parties\",\"D: stay silent unless asked\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"The Code's remedy for conflicts is full disclosure to all affected parties, not avoidance or silence.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Under the GARP Code, a member may reveal confidential client information when:\nA) The information would benefit some of the member's other clients B) Disclosure is required by law or legal/regulatory process C) The client relationship has simply ended D) The information is more than five years old\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a legitimate exception to confidentiality.\n\nConfidentiality under the Code is strong but NOT absolute. A member may disclose confidential information in two situations: when the client has given consent, or when disclosure is required by law (such as a valid subpoena or regulatory process). So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a doctor keeps your records private, but if a court lawfully orders them, the doctor must comply — the law overrides the privacy duty.\n\n- A is wrong: helping other clients is not a valid reason; that would actually be misusing confidential information.\n- C is wrong: simply ending the relationship does not free the member to reveal old confidential information.\n- D is wrong: there is no \"five-year expiry\" on confidentiality in the Code.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Confidentiality exceptions = (1) client consent, or (2) disclosure required by law. Nothing about age of data, ended relationships, or benefiting others.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"May reveal confidential info?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Yes (exceptions)\",\"items\":[\"client consents\",\"required by law / legal process\"]},{\"h\":\"No\",\"items\":[\"helps other clients\",\"relationship ended\",\"data is old\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which is a possible consequence of violating the GARP Code of Conduct?\nA) Automatic criminal prosecution carried out by GARP itself B) Suspension or revocation of the right to use the FRM designation C) A fine payable to GARP of up to $1 million D) Mandatory retraining administered by the member's employer\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what GARP can actually do when the Code is violated.\n\nGARP can investigate violations under its disciplinary procedures and impose sanctions such as suspending or revoking membership and the right to use the FRM (or other GARP) designation; candidates can be barred from the exam program. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a sports association can ban a player or strip their title, but it cannot send anyone to jail — courts do that.\n\n- A is wrong: GARP does not prosecute crimes; violations of law are referred to legal/regulatory authorities, who handle prosecution.\n- C is wrong: GARP does not levy a $1 million fine; its sanctions concern membership and designation rights.\n- D is wrong: GARP does not order an employer to retrain the member; that is not among its sanctions.\n\n📌 Exam tip: GARP sanctions = suspend/revoke membership and designation, or bar candidates. GARP does NOT impose criminal penalties or fines — the law handles crimes separately.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"GARP can vs cannot\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"GARP can\",\"items\":[\"suspend/revoke membership\",\"revoke FRM designation use\",\"bar candidates from exam\"]},{\"h\":\"GARP cannot\",\"items\":[\"criminally prosecute\",\"levy fines\",\"order employer retraining\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A member presents a credit model's outputs to clients as \"precise default probabilities,\" even though the model has wide confidence bands. This most directly violates the duty to:\nA) Maintain confidentiality B) Disclose conflicts of interest C) Not overstate accuracy and to communicate model limitations clearly D) Nothing — marketing language is always exempt\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which Code duty is broken by overstating how accurate a model is.\n\nThe Code requires members to be diligent about NOT overstating the accuracy or certainty of their results, and to make assumptions, limitations, and the degree of uncertainty transparent. Calling fuzzy estimates \"precise\" hides the real uncertainty, so it violates the duty to communicate clearly and not overstate accuracy. So C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: it's like a weather app saying \"0% chance of rain, guaranteed\" when the forecast is actually quite uncertain — that misleads people into bad decisions.\n\n- A is wrong: nothing confidential was revealed, so confidentiality isn't the issue.\n- B is wrong: there is no conflict of interest here; the issue is misrepresenting accuracy.\n- D is wrong: marketing language is NOT exempt; members must communicate honestly about uncertainty.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two recurring Code themes: be transparent about model limitations (never overstate accuracy) and fully disclose conflicts. When two answers look ethical, pick the one favoring disclosure and honest uncertainty.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Calling estimates precise\",\"options\":[\"A: confidentiality\",\"B: disclose conflicts\",\"C: not overstate accuracy / show limits\",\"D: marketing is exempt\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Labeling wide-band estimates as precise hides uncertainty, breaching the duty not to overstate accuracy.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The GARP Code of Conduct applies to:\nA) Only individuals who currently hold the full FRM certification B) GARP members, GARP certification holders, AND candidates registered for GARP exams C) Only employees who hold a formal \"risk manager\" job title D) Only members who have agreed to it in writing each year\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who is bound by the Code.\n\nThe Code binds GARP members, holders of GARP certifications (such as the FRM), AND candidates who have registered for GARP exams. By registering for an exam, a candidate accepts the Code. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: signing up for the race means you agree to the race rules — you don't have to have finished and won a medal first.\n\n- A is wrong: it covers more than just certified FRMs; candidates are bound too.\n- C is wrong: the Code applies to professional conduct generally, not only to people with a \"risk manager\" title.\n- D is wrong: acceptance happens upon registration; there is no requirement to re-sign it in writing every year.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The Code binds candidates FROM the moment of exam registration — not only finished, certified FRMs.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Who the Code binds\",\"root\":\"GARP Code applies to\",\"children\":[\"GARP members\",\"Certification holders (FRM)\",\"Registered exam candidates\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A member is working in a country whose local practice allows accepting gifts that the GARP Code would prohibit. The member should:\nA) Accept the gifts, since local practice always governs B) Follow the stricter standard, declining gifts that could compromise independence C) Accept the gifts but disclose them only if caught D) Ignore the Code because it does not apply abroad\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to handle a clash between lax local practice and the stricter Code.\n\nThe Code requires members to comply with it even where local law or practice is laxer; where they conflict, the member should follow the stricter standard consistent with law. \"My country allows it\" is not a defense for conduct the Code prohibits. Here, accepting gifts that could compromise independence is barred, so the member declines. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: even if a friend's house has no curfew, your own family's stricter curfew still applies to you when you're out.\n\n- A is wrong: local practice does not override the Code; the stricter standard wins.\n- C is wrong: \"disclose only if caught\" is not honest disclosure and still violates the gift rule.\n- D is wrong: the Code applies to members' professional conduct generally, including abroad.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When local rules are laxer than the Code, follow the STRICTER standard. \"It's allowed here\" is never a valid defense under the Code.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Code vs lax local practice\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Local practice\",\"items\":[\"gifts allowed\",\"laxer standard\",\"everyone does it\"]},{\"h\":\"Member must\",\"items\":[\"follow stricter standard\",\"decline compromising gifts\",\"'allowed here' is no defense\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A risk manager presents a value-at-risk (VaR) figure to the board but does not mention that the model excludes the firm's largest illiquid position. This most directly violates:\nA) The duty of confidentiality B) The duty to use generally accepted practices and communicate risk clearly, accurately, and completely C) Conflict-of-interest disclosure rules D) Nothing, because VaR is only an estimate anyway\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which Code duty is breached by leaving out a key limitation of a risk figure.\n\nThe Code requires members to communicate risk information clearly, accurately, and not misleadingly — including making the limitations and assumptions of models transparent. Hiding that the VaR omits the firm's largest illiquid position makes the figure misleading and breaks the duty to adhere to generally accepted practices in risk communication. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: showing your boss a budget that quietly leaves out the biggest expense makes the whole picture misleading, even if every other number is right.\n\n- A is wrong: no confidential information was disclosed; that's not the issue.\n- C is wrong: there's no conflict of interest here, just incomplete risk communication.\n- D is wrong: VaR being an estimate is no excuse for hiding a material limitation; the omission is the violation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Risk communications must be clear, accurate, complete, and upfront about limitations and assumptions. Omitting a material exclusion is misleading.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"VaR omits largest illiquid position\",\"options\":[\"A: confidentiality\",\"B: communicate risk clearly/completely\",\"C: conflict of interest\",\"D: no violation, VaR is an estimate\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Hiding a material exclusion makes the VaR misleading, breaching clear/accurate/complete risk communication.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A consultant recommends a particular risk-software vendor to a client but does not mention that the vendor pays him a referral fee. Under the Code, this is a failure to:\nA) Maintain professional competence B) Disclose a conflict of interest C) Keep client information confidential D) Comply with local tax law\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which duty the consultant breaks by hiding the referral fee.\n\nThe referral fee gives the consultant a personal incentive to recommend that vendor regardless of what's best for the client — an actual or potential conflict of interest. The Code requires fully disclosing such conflicts to all affected parties. By staying silent, he fails to disclose a conflict of interest. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a \"friend\" who recommends a mechanic should mention if the mechanic slips him cash for every referral — otherwise you can't judge whether the advice is honest.\n\n- A is wrong: this isn't about lacking skill or competence; it's about the hidden incentive.\n- C is wrong: no confidential information was leaked.\n- D is wrong: the issue is the undisclosed conflict, not tax compliance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Any payment or benefit that could bias a recommendation is a conflict of interest that must be DISCLOSED to all affected parties.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Hidden referral fee\",\"options\":[\"A: professional competence\",\"B: disclose a conflict of interest\",\"C: confidentiality\",\"D: local tax law\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A referral fee biases the recommendation; failing to disclose it breaches the conflict-of-interest duty.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** An FRM candidate who has passed Part I but not yet Part II describes herself on her resume as \"FRM certified.\" Under the Code, this is:\nA) Acceptable, since she passed Part I B) A misrepresentation of qualifications, violating professional integrity C) A confidentiality violation D) Acceptable, because resumes are personal documents\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the Code treats claiming the FRM designation before earning it.\n\nThe Code prohibits knowingly misrepresenting qualifications or the meaning of the FRM designation. Calling herself \"FRM certified\" before completing the full program (and satisfying experience requirements) is a false statement about her qualifications, which violates professional integrity. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: a medical student who passed only the first set of exams cannot call themselves \"Dr.\" yet — claiming the title early is a misrepresentation.\n\n- A is wrong: passing Part I does not make someone FRM certified; the claim is still false.\n- C is wrong: nothing confidential is involved; the issue is a false qualification claim.\n- D is wrong: a resume being personal does not exempt it from the truthfulness rules of the Code.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Never overstate your designation status. Misstating qualifications or the meaning of the FRM is a professional-integrity violation.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"'FRM certified' after only Part I\",\"options\":[\"A: acceptable, passed Part I\",\"B: misrepresentation of qualifications\",\"C: confidentiality violation\",\"D: acceptable, resume is personal\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Claiming the FRM before completing the program misstates qualifications, a professional-integrity breach.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A member receives a valid legal subpoena ordering the production of certain client records and complies with it. Under the Code, this is:\nA) A confidentiality violation, because client information was disclosed B) Not a violation, because disclosure required by law is a permitted exception C) A conflict-of-interest violation D) A violation only if the client objects\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks whether obeying a lawful subpoena breaks the confidentiality rule.\n\nThe Code's confidentiality duty has an explicit exception: disclosure that is required by law or legal/regulatory process is permitted. A valid subpoena is exactly such a legal requirement, so complying with it does NOT violate the Code. So B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: keeping a secret is the rule, but if a judge lawfully orders you to testify, telling the truth in court is allowed — even required.\n\n- A is wrong: disclosure compelled by law is the recognized exception, so it is not a violation.\n- C is wrong: there's no conflict of interest involved in complying with a subpoena.\n- D is wrong: a client's objection does not override a valid legal requirement to disclose.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Complying with a lawful subpoena or legal process is a permitted exception to confidentiality — not a Code violation.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Complying with a valid subpoena\",\"options\":[\"A: confidentiality violation\",\"B: not a violation - required by law\",\"C: conflict of interest\",\"D: violation only if client objects\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Disclosure required by law/legal process is an explicit exception to confidentiality, so complying is permitted.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A GARP member discovers her bonus depends partly on the trading desk whose risk she reports on. According to the Code, she must:\nA) Resign immediately B) Decline the bonus C) Fully disclose the conflict to all affected parties D) Keep silent unless asked\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The Code's remedy for conflicts is full disclosure to all affected parties.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Under the Code, a member may reveal confidential client information when:\nA) The information would benefit other clients B) Disclosure is required by law C) The client relationship has ended D) The information is more than five years old\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Exceptions: consent, or disclosure required by law/legal process.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which is a potential consequence of violating the GARP Code of Conduct?\nA) Automatic criminal prosecution by GARP B) Suspension or revocation of the right to use the FRM designation C) A fine payable to GARP of up to $1 million D) Mandatory retraining administered by the member's employer\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** GARP can suspend/revoke membership and designation rights; it does not levy criminal penalties or fines.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A member presents a credit model's outputs to clients as \"precise default probabilities\" although the model has wide confidence bands. This most directly violates:\nA) Confidentiality B) Conflict-of-interest rules C) The duty not to overstate accuracy and to communicate limitations clearly D) Nothing — marketing language is exempt\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Members must not overstate accuracy or certainty and must make limitations and assumptions transparent.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A GARP member's risk model relies on an assumption of normally distributed returns that materially understates tail risk. When presenting results to management, the Code of Conduct requires her to:\nA) Present only the point estimates to avoid confusing the audience B) Disclose the model's assumptions and limitations clearly, distinguishing fact from opinion C) Refuse to present any model with limitations D) Report the model to GARP's disciplinary committee --\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The professional standards require clear, accurate, not-misleading risk communication: disclose assumptions, limitations, and uncertainty, and separate fact from opinion. Omitting limitations (A) violates the Code; C and D are not Code requirements.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Code of Conduct: communicating a flawed model\",\"options\":[\"A. Show only point estimates\",\"B. Disclose assumptions & limits\",\"C. Refuse to present it\",\"D. Report to GARP committee\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Clear, not-misleading communication; disclose limits, separate fact from opinion\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A risk consultant in Country Z recommends a derivatives platform to a client. He receives an undisclosed referral fee from the platform's vendor. Local regulation in Country Z does not require disclosure of such referral arrangements, and \"everyone in the market does it.\" Under the GARP Code of Conduct, the consultant's conduct is:\nA) Permissible, because local law does not require disclosure and local practice accepts it B) A violation, because the Code requires full disclosure of actual or potential conflicts of interest to all affected parties, and members must follow the stricter of the Code or local law C) Permissible, because the referral fee did not change the quality of his recommendation D) A violation only if the client later suffers a financial loss on the platform\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: apply the conflict-of-interest principle and the \"stricter standard\" rule.\n\n1. The Code's conflicts principle requires members to act fairly and to *fully disclose* any actual or potential conflict of interest to all affected parties. An undisclosed referral fee is a textbook undisclosed conflict.\n2. \"Local law allows it\" is *not* a defense: members must comply with the Code even where local law or practice is laxer, following the stricter standard consistent with law.\n\nWhy B is correct: the duty is disclosure, and the laxer local norm does not excuse non-disclosure.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A invokes the \"my country's rules allow it\" defense the Code explicitly rejects — a flagged exam trap.\n- C argues the fee was harmless, but the Code requires disclosure of *potential* conflicts regardless of whether the advice was actually biased; appearance of compromised independence is enough.\n- D ties the violation to a later loss, but the breach occurs *at the moment of non-disclosure*, independent of any subsequent outcome.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The Code's remedy for conflicts is *disclosure, not necessarily avoidance* — and disclosure must go to *all affected parties*. Whenever an answer relies on \"local rules allow it,\" treat it as wrong: members follow the stricter of Code or law.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Undisclosed referral fee, lax local law\",\"options\":[\"A: permissible, local law allows\",\"B: violation - must disclose, follow stricter standard\",\"C: permissible, advice unaffected\",\"D: violation only if client loses money\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Conflicts require full disclosure; laxer local practice is no defense - follow the stricter standard.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A GARP member and risk manager receives a lawful subpoena (valid legal process) compelling production of a client's confidential portfolio records. Separately, a friend at a hedge fund asks the same member to share a different client's positions, promising it will \"stay between us.\" Under the Code, the member should:\nA) Refuse the subpoena (confidentiality is absolute) but may share with the friend since it stays private B) Comply with the subpoena (disclosure required by legal process is permitted) and refuse the friend (using/revealing confidential information for others' advantage violates confidentiality) C) Comply with both, since the client relationship gives the member discretion over the data D) Refuse both, because confidentiality under the Code admits no exceptions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: apply the confidentiality principle and its exceptions to two contrasting requests.\n\n1. Confidentiality is *not absolute*. The Code permits revealing confidential information where the client consents *or* where disclosure is required by law or legal/regulatory process. A valid subpoena is such legal process → complying is permitted (indeed required by law).\n2. Sharing a different client's positions with a hedge-fund friend is revealing confidential information for another's inappropriate advantage, with no consent and no legal compulsion → a clear confidentiality violation.\n\nWhy B is correct: it distinguishes lawful compelled disclosure (allowed) from voluntary leakage for others' benefit (prohibited).\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A treats confidentiality as absolute (so refuses the subpoena) and then permits the leak — both backward.\n- C wrongly assumes the member has open discretion over client data; sharing with the friend has neither consent nor legal basis.\n- D again asserts no exceptions exist, ignoring the consent and legal-process carve-outs the Code explicitly provides.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Confidentiality has two exceptions — *client consent* and *disclosure required by law/legal process*. Complying with a subpoena is fine; tipping a friend is a violation (and likely illegal). \"Confidentiality is absolute\" is always a wrong answer.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two requests, opposite answers\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Valid subpoena\",\"items\":[\"required by legal process\",\"permitted exception\",\"comply (narrowly)\"]},{\"h\":\"Tip to hedge-fund friend\",\"items\":[\"no consent, no legal basis\",\"reveals for others' gain\",\"violation\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An FRM Part II candidate who has passed Part I but not yet Part II updates her LinkedIn headline to \"FRM (Certified)\" and tells prospective employers she \"holds the FRM designation.\" Under the Code, this most directly:\nA) Is acceptable, since she has passed Part I and is enrolled in Part II B) Violates professional integrity by misrepresenting her qualifications and the meaning of the FRM designation C) Violates only confidentiality, not integrity D) Is outside the Code's scope, because candidates are not bound until certified\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: classify the candidate's misstatement under the correct principle.\n\n1. The professional-integrity principle prohibits members from knowingly misrepresenting facts or making false/misleading statements, *including* about their own qualifications and the meaning of the FRM designation.\n2. Claiming to \"hold the FRM designation\" before completing Part II (and the program's requirements) is a misrepresentation of qualifications.\n\nWhy B is correct: this is a textbook integrity/misrepresentation breach (mirroring the chapter's \"FRM certified while awaiting Part II results\" example).\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A treats partial progress as entitlement to the designation; passing Part I does not confer FRM status.\n- C points to confidentiality, which is unrelated — no confidential information is involved.\n- D claims candidates are exempt, but the Code binds *candidates from registration*, not only certified holders — a specifically flagged trap.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Misstating your credentials or the meaning of the FRM designation is an *integrity* violation. And remember the scope rule: the Code binds members, certification holders, *and registered candidates* from the moment they register.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"'FRM (Certified)' after only Part I\",\"options\":[\"A: acceptable, enrolled in Part II\",\"B: misrepresents qualifications - integrity breach\",\"C: confidentiality violation\",\"D: candidates exempt until certified\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Claiming to hold the FRM before completing the program misstates qualifications, a professional-integrity violation.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A risk manager presents the firm's one-day VaR to the board as the firm's \"maximum possible loss,\" omitting that the model excludes the firm's largest illiquid position and assumes normally distributed returns. Which best identifies the Code provision most directly violated?\nA) Conflict-of-interest rules, because the manager benefits from a low VaR B) The duty to use generally accepted practices and to communicate risk information clearly, accurately, and not misleadingly — including making model assumptions, limitations, and uncertainty transparent C) Confidentiality, because VaR is proprietary D) Nothing, because VaR is an industry-standard metric and its limitations are common knowledge\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: pinpoint the violated standard in a misleading risk-communication scenario.\n\n1. Calling VaR the \"maximum possible loss\" is itself false — VaR is a quantile, not a maximum. Omitting that the largest illiquid position is excluded and that normality is assumed hides material limitations.\n2. The standard on *generally accepted practices* requires communications of risk information to be clear, accurate, and not misleading, and to make assumptions, limitations, and the degree of uncertainty transparent (and to distinguish fact from opinion).\n\nWhy B is correct: the breach is misleading communication and failure to disclose model limitations — the heart of the generally-accepted-practices standard.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A reaches for a conflict of interest, but the scenario shows no personal benefit or undisclosed interest — it is a *communication/competence* failure.\n- C invokes confidentiality, which is irrelevant; presenting VaR to one's own board reveals nothing improperly.\n- D excuses the omission as \"common knowledge,\" but the Code affirmatively requires *transparency* about limitations and forbids overstating accuracy — \"everyone knows\" is not a defense.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When risk outputs are presented as more certain or complete than they are (overstating accuracy, hiding assumptions/limitations, calling VaR a \"maximum loss\"), the violation is *generally accepted practices / clear-accurate-not-misleading communication* — not confidentiality or conflicts.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"VaR as 'maximum possible loss'\",\"options\":[\"A: conflict of interest\",\"B: clear/accurate/complete risk communication\",\"C: confidentiality (VaR proprietary)\",\"D: nothing, VaR is standard\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"VaR is a quantile not a maximum; omitting the illiquid position and normality assumption is misleading communication.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Two answer choices on an exam item both look ethical. Choice 1: the member quietly avoids a situation that could create a potential conflict. Choice 2: the member fully discloses the potential conflict to all affected parties and the limitations/uncertainty in his analysis. Per the chapter's guidance on resolving such ties, which is generally the Code-compliant choice and why?\nA) Choice 1, because avoidance always beats disclosure under the Code B) Choice 2, because when two options both seem ethical, the one involving disclosure/escalation and honest communication of uncertainty is usually the Code-compliant answer C) Neither, because the Code is silent on potential (as opposed to actual) conflicts D) Choice 1, because disclosing a conflict is itself a breach of confidentiality\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: apply the tie-breaking heuristic the chapter gives for Code questions.\n\n1. The Code repeatedly emphasizes *transparency about limitations* and *disclosure* of conflicts. The chapter's explicit guidance: when two choices both look ethical, prefer the one involving disclosure/escalation and honest communication of uncertainty.\n2. Choice 2 discloses the potential conflict to all affected parties *and* is transparent about analytical uncertainty — squarely the Code's preferred posture.\n\nWhy B is correct: it states the disclosure-over-silence tie-breaker the chapter prescribes.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A claims avoidance \"always\" beats disclosure, but the Code's operative duty for conflicts is *disclosure, not necessarily avoidance* — \"always\" is the giveaway.\n- C says the Code ignores *potential* conflicts, but the conflicts principle explicitly covers \"actual or potential\" conflicts.\n- D inverts confidentiality — disclosing one's *own* conflict to affected parties is not a confidentiality breach (no client secret is revealed).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Tie-breaker for Code questions — choose disclosure, escalation, and honest communication of uncertainty over silence or quiet avoidance. The Code's twin themes are *transparency about limitations* and *full disclosure of conflicts to affected parties*.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Tie-breaker: two ethical-looking choices\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Choice 1 (quiet avoid)\",\"items\":[\"silently sidestep conflict\",\"no disclosure\",\"weaker under Code\"]},{\"h\":\"Choice 2 (disclose)\",\"items\":[\"disclose to affected parties\",\"state uncertainty honestly\",\"Code-preferred\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A GARP member is found, after a disciplinary investigation, to have knowingly falsified model-validation results that also breached securities law. Which statement about the consequences is most accurate under the Code?\nA) GARP will criminally prosecute the member and impose a fine of up to $1 million B) GARP may suspend or revoke the member's membership and the right to use the FRM designation; separately, the legal violation may be referred to legal/regulatory authorities — the Code does not shield the member from the law C) The member faces no GARP action because securities law, not the Code, governs the conduct D) The only possible consequence is mandatory retraining administered by the employer\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: state the consequences of a Code violation that also breaks the law.\n\n1. GARP's sanctions for Code violations can include *suspension or revocation of membership* and of the *right to use the FRM (or other GARP) designation*; candidates can be barred from the exam program.\n2. Separately, *violations of law* are subject to referral to and action by legal/regulatory/governmental authorities — the Code does not shield anyone from the law. GARP itself does not criminally prosecute or levy fines.\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly pairs GARP's disciplinary sanctions with separate legal referral.\n\nWhy the distractors trap you:\n- A wrongly has GARP criminally prosecuting and fining — GARP's powers are membership/designation sanctions, not criminal penalties or monetary fines.\n- C wrongly exempts the member from GARP action; falsifying validation results breaches the Code regardless of the parallel legal violation.\n- D understates the consequences and invents an employer-retraining remedy not in the Code.\n\n📌 Exam tip: GARP's teeth = suspension/revocation of membership and designation rights (and barring candidates). It does *not* impose criminal penalties or fines; legal violations are referred to the proper authorities. \"GARP fines/prosecutes\" answers are wrong.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"GARP sanction vs the law\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"GARP can\",\"items\":[\"suspend/revoke membership\",\"revoke FRM designation\",\"bar candidates\"]},{\"h\":\"Separate (the law)\",\"items\":[\"criminal prosecution\",\"fines\",\"referred to authorities\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of the following situations is *not* a violation of the GARP Code of Conduct?\nA) A member accepts lavish hospitality from a vendor that could reasonably be expected to appear to compromise her independence, and does not disclose it B) A member, served with a valid regulatory production order, discloses the specific client records the order requires C) A member presents a credit model's outputs to clients as \"precise default probabilities\" despite wide confidence bands D) A member lists himself as holding the FRM designation while still awaiting Part II results\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: find the *compliant* action among four scenarios.\n\n1. The confidentiality principle permits revealing confidential information when disclosure is required by law or legal/regulatory process. Complying with a valid regulatory production order (analogous to a subpoena) is expressly permitted — and limiting disclosure to what the order requires is exactly right.\n\nWhy B is correct: legally compelled disclosure, narrowly scoped to what is required, is not a breach.\n\nWhy the distractors are violations:\n- A breaches the *integrity/independence* duty — members must not accept gifts/benefits that could reasonably be expected to compromise (or appear to compromise) independence, and the non-disclosure compounds it.\n- C breaches *generally accepted practices* — presenting model outputs as \"precise\" while hiding wide uncertainty overstates accuracy and fails to communicate limitations clearly.\n- D breaches *professional integrity* — misrepresenting qualifications/the meaning of the FRM designation before earning it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The lone \"not a violation\" answer in Code questions is usually *consent-based or legally compelled disclosure*. Compelled disclosure (subpoena/regulatory order), kept to what the law requires, is permitted; everything involving overstated accuracy, hidden conflicts, or misrepresented credentials is a breach.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which is NOT a violation?\",\"options\":[\"A: undisclosed lavish vendor gift\",\"B: disclose only what a regulatory order requires\",\"C: model outputs as 'precise'\",\"D: 'FRM' while awaiting Part II\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Legally compelled disclosure, narrowly scoped, is a permitted exception; the others overstate, hide, or misrepresent.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"201","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Fundamentals of Probability","notes":"# 1. Fundamentals of Probability\n\nGARP tests mechanical fluency here: conditional probability, independence, the total probability rule, and Bayes' theorem — almost always as a numeric problem you solve with a probability table.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe an event and an event space, and define independent and mutually exclusive events.\n- Calculate the probability of an event given discrete probabilities.\n- Define and calculate conditional probability.\n- Distinguish between conditional and unconditional probabilities.\n- Explain and apply Bayes' theorem.\n\n## Events, event spaces, and probability axioms\n\n- **Sample space** $$\\Omega$$: the set of all possible outcomes.\n- **Event**: any subset of the sample space. The **event space** is the collection of all events you can assign probabilities to.\n- Axioms: $$P(A) \\ge 0$$, $$P(\\Omega) = 1$$, and for mutually exclusive events $$P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B)$$.\n\nTwo key structural relationships:\n\n- **Mutually exclusive** events cannot happen together: $$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$.\n- **Independent** events do not inform each other: $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A)\\,P(B)$$, equivalently $$P(A \\mid B) = P(A)$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Mutually exclusive is *not* the same as independent — it is almost the opposite. If $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive and both have positive probability, knowing $$A$$ occurred tells you $$B$$ did not, so they are strongly dependent. The exam loves this distinction.\n\n## The core probability rules\n\n**Complement:**\n\n$$P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)$$\n\n**Addition rule (any two events):**\n\n$$P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cap B)$$\n\n**Conditional probability:**\n\n$$P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}, \\quad P(B) > 0$$\n\n**Multiplication rule:**\n\n$$P(A \\cap B) = P(A \\mid B)\\,P(B) = P(B \\mid A)\\,P(A)$$\n\n**Total probability rule** (for a partition $$B_1, \\dots, B_n$$ of the sample space):\n\n$$P(A) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} P(A \\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)$$\n\n## Conditional vs. unconditional probability\n\nAn **unconditional (marginal)** probability $$P(A)$$ uses no extra information. A **conditional** probability $$P(A \\mid B)$$ restricts the sample space to outcomes where $$B$$ occurred. In risk language: the unconditional default rate of a portfolio vs. the default rate *given* a recession. Conditioning on bad states typically raises loss probabilities — this is the basis of stress testing.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Conditional independence does not imply unconditional independence, and vice versa. Two bonds may default independently *given* the state of the economy, yet be unconditionally correlated because both depend on that state.\n\n## Bayes' theorem\n\nBayes' theorem inverts a conditional probability — from $$P(\\text{evidence} \\mid \\text{state})$$ to $$P(\\text{state} \\mid \\text{evidence})$$:\n\n$$P(B_j \\mid A) = \\frac{P(A \\mid B_j)\\,P(B_j)}{\\sum_i P(A \\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)}$$\n\nThe denominator is just the total probability rule. The fastest exam technique is a two-way table or tree: lay out the joint probabilities, then divide the relevant cell by its column (or row) total.\n\n### Worked example: Bayes with a fund-manager screen\n\nA risk team classifies fund managers as skilled (20% of managers) or unskilled (80%). A skilled manager beats the benchmark in a given year with probability 0.70; an unskilled one with probability 0.50. A manager beat the benchmark this year. What is the probability the manager is skilled?\n\nJoint probabilities:\n\n| | Beat | Did not beat | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Skilled | $$0.20 \\times 0.70 = 0.14$$ | $$0.06$$ | $$0.20$$ |\n| Unskilled | $$0.80 \\times 0.50 = 0.40$$ | $$0.40$$ | $$0.80$$ |\n| **Total** | $$0.54$$ | $$0.46$$ | $$1.00$$ |\n\n$$P(\\text{Skilled} \\mid \\text{Beat}) = \\frac{0.14}{0.54} = 0.2593 \\approx 25.9\\%$$\n\nOne year of outperformance barely moves the prior from 20% to 25.9% — a classic FRM message about the weakness of short track records.\n\n### Worked example: total probability and conditioning\n\nA bond portfolio's one-year default probability is 1% in an expansion and 8% in a recession. The probability of recession next year is 25%.\n\n**Unconditional default probability:**\n\n$$P(D) = 0.08 \\times 0.25 + 0.01 \\times 0.75 = 0.020 + 0.0075 = 0.0275 = 2.75\\%$$\n\n**Given that the bond defaulted, the probability the economy was in recession:**\n\n$$P(R \\mid D) = \\frac{0.08 \\times 0.25}{0.0275} = \\frac{0.020}{0.0275} = 0.7273 \\approx 72.7\\%$$\n\nEven though recession had only a 25% prior, a default makes recession the most likely explanation.\n\n## Independence checks in practice\n\nTo verify independence on the exam, test whether $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) P(B)$$ holds. Example: $$P(A)=0.4$$, $$P(B)=0.5$$, $$P(A\\cap B)=0.2$$. Since $$0.4 \\times 0.5 = 0.2$$, the events are independent — and therefore *not* mutually exclusive.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> When a question gives you $$P(A \\mid B) \\ne P(A)$$, you already know the events are dependent — no further computation needed.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Define mutually exclusive events and give the key probability identity.\n> **A:** Events that cannot occur together: $$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$, so $$P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B)$$.\n\n**Q2.** Define independent events.\n> **A:** Events that do not inform each other: $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$$, equivalently $$P(A \\mid B) = P(A)$$.\n\n**Q3.** Why are mutually exclusive events (with positive probability) NOT independent?\n> **A:** If $$A$$ occurs, $$B$$ cannot, so knowing $$A$$ changes $$B$$'s probability to 0 — they are strongly dependent, nearly the opposite of independent.\n\n**Q4.** State the addition rule for any two events.\n> **A:** $$P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cap B)$$\n\n**Q5.** Define conditional probability.\n> **A:** $$P(A \\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}, \\quad P(B) > 0$$\n\n**Q6.** State the multiplication rule.\n> **A:** $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A \\mid B)\\,P(B) = P(B \\mid A)\\,P(A)$$\n\n**Q7.** State the total probability rule for a partition $$B_1,\\dots,B_n$$.\n> **A:** $$P(A) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} P(A \\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)$$\n\n**Q8.** State Bayes' theorem.\n> **A:** $$P(B_j \\mid A) = \\dfrac{P(A \\mid B_j)\\,P(B_j)}{\\sum_i P(A \\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)}$$ — the denominator is the total probability rule.\n\n**Q9.** What is the difference between an unconditional (marginal) and a conditional probability?\n> **A:** $$P(A)$$ uses no extra information; $$P(A \\mid B)$$ restricts the sample space to outcomes where $$B$$ occurred (e.g., default rate vs. default rate given recession).\n\n**Q10.** Default probability is 1% in expansion, 8% in recession; recession probability is 25%. What is the unconditional default probability?\n> **A:** $$P(D) = 0.08(0.25) + 0.01(0.75) = 0.0275 = 2.75\\%$$\n\n**Q11.** Does conditional independence imply unconditional independence?\n> **A:** No. Two bonds may default independently given the economy yet be unconditionally correlated because both depend on that common state.\n\n**Q12.** A test flags 90% of rogue traders and 5% of honest ones; 1% are rogue. P(rogue | flagged)?\n> **A:** $$\\dfrac{0.90 \\times 0.01}{0.90(0.01) + 0.05(0.99)} = \\dfrac{0.009}{0.0585} = 15.4\\%$$\n\n**Q13.** Quick check: how do you verify independence from probabilities?\n> **A:** Test whether $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$$. If it holds the events are independent (and therefore not mutually exclusive).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Events $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive with $$P(A) = 0.3$$ and $$P(B) = 0.5$$. What is $$P(A \\cup B)$$?\nA) 0.15 B) 0.65 C) 0.80 D) 1.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the probability that *at least one* of $$A$$ or $$B$$ happens, and it tells us the two events are \"mutually exclusive.\"\n\nMutually exclusive means the two events can never happen at the same time, so their overlap is zero: $$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$. Think of two doors in a hallway where you can only walk through one at a time — you can never be standing in both doorways at once.\n\nThe general addition rule is:\n\n$$P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cap B)$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n\n$$P(A \\cup B) = 0.3 + 0.5 - 0 = 0.8$$\n\nSo the answer is 0.80 (option C). Because there is no overlap to subtract, we simply add the two probabilities.\n\n- A (0.15) multiplies the probabilities ($$0.3 \\times 0.5$$). That is the formula for independent events' *intersection*, not the union of mutually exclusive events.\n- B (0.65) wrongly subtracts an overlap of 0.15 ($$0.3 + 0.5 - 0.15$$), treating the events as if they were independent. But mutually exclusive events have zero overlap.\n- D (1.00) assumes the two events cover the entire sample space. Nothing in the problem says that.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For mutually exclusive events the overlap is exactly zero, so you just add. Do not confuse \"mutually exclusive\" (cannot co-occur) with \"independent\" (do not inform each other).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mutually exclusive vs independent\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Mutually exclusive\",\"items\":[\"cannot both occur\",\"P(A∩B)=0\",\"add for union: P(A)+P(B)\",\"strongly dependent\"]},{\"h\":\"Independent\",\"items\":[\"one says nothing about other\",\"P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)\",\"can co-occur\",\"P(A|B)=P(A)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Events $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive with $$P(A) = 0.3$$ and $$P(B) = 0.4$$. What is $$P(A \\mid B)$$?\nA) 0.00 B) 0.12 C) 0.30 D) 0.75\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: given that $$B$$ has happened, how likely is $$A$$?\n\nThe conditional probability formula is:\n\n$$P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}$$\n\nBecause $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive, they can never happen together, so $$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$. Therefore:\n\n$$P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{0}{0.4} = 0$$\n\nIf you already know $$B$$ happened, then $$A$$ is impossible (they can't co-occur), so the chance of $$A$$ is zero. Analogy: if I tell you the coin landed tails, the probability it landed heads is zero — they exclude each other.\n\n- B (0.12) is $$P(A) \\times P(B) = 0.3 \\times 0.4$$, the independence formula, which does not apply here.\n- C (0.30) is just $$P(A)$$, which would be correct only if the events were independent — but mutually exclusive events are strongly dependent.\n- D (0.75) is $$0.3 / 0.4$$, dividing the two marginal probabilities — a meaningless mix-up of the formula.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mutually exclusive + \"what is $$P(A \\mid B)$$?\" almost always equals 0. Knowing one event ruled out the other.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Conditional under mutual exclusivity\",\"steps\":[\"P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B)\",\"mutually excl. → P(A∩B)=0\",\"numerator=0\",\"P(A|B)=0\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** $$P(A) = 0.6$$, $$P(B) = 0.5$$, and $$P(A \\cup B) = 0.8$$. What is $$P(A \\mid B)$$?\nA) 0.375 B) 0.50 C) 0.60 D) 0.30\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the chance of $$A$$ given $$B$$, but first we must find the overlap $$P(A \\cap B)$$, which isn't given directly.\n\nStep 1 — find the overlap using the addition rule rearranged:\n\n$$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cup B) = 0.6 + 0.5 - 0.8 = 0.3$$\n\nStep 2 — apply the conditional probability formula:\n\n$$P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} = \\frac{0.3}{0.5} = 0.6$$\n\nSo $$P(A \\mid B) = 0.60$$ (option C). Interestingly, this equals $$P(A) = 0.6$$, which means $$A$$ and $$B$$ happen to be independent here.\n\n- A (0.375) divides by the wrong number ($$0.3 / 0.8$$), using the union instead of $$P(B)$$ in the denominator.\n- B (0.50) just reports $$P(B)$$, ignoring the calculation.\n- D (0.30) stops at $$P(A \\cap B) = 0.3$$ and forgets to divide by $$P(B)$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When the joint probability is missing, recover it from the addition rule first, then divide. The denominator in $$P(A \\mid B)$$ is always $$P(B)$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Find P(A|B) when joint is missing\",\"steps\":[\"P(A∩B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A∪B)\",\"= 0.6+0.5−0.8 = 0.3\",\"÷ P(B)=0.5\",\"P(A|B)=0.6\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A test for rogue traders flags 90% of true rogue traders and 5% of honest traders. 1% of all traders are rogue. If a trader is flagged, the probability the trader is actually rogue is closest to:\nA) 1.0% B) 15.4% C) 47.4% D) 90.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This is a Bayes' theorem problem. The test \"flagging\" someone is the evidence; \"being rogue\" is the hidden state. We want $$P(\\text{rogue} \\mid \\text{flagged})$$.\n\nIt helps to imagine 10,000 traders:\n- Rogue traders: $$1\\% \\times 10{,}000 = 100$$. Of these, 90% get flagged: $$0.90 \\times 100 = 90$$.\n- Honest traders: $$99\\% \\times 10{,}000 = 9{,}900$$. Of these, 5% get flagged: $$0.05 \\times 9{,}900 = 495$$.\n\nTotal flagged $$= 90 + 495 = 585$$. Of those, the truly rogue ones number 90.\n\n$$P(\\text{rogue} \\mid \\text{flagged}) = \\frac{90}{585} = 0.1538 \\approx 15.4\\%$$\n\nIn formula form:\n\n$$\\frac{0.90 \\times 0.01}{0.90 \\times 0.01 + 0.05 \\times 0.99} = \\frac{0.009}{0.009 + 0.0495} = \\frac{0.009}{0.0585} = 0.1538$$\n\nEven a flagged trader is probably honest, because rogue traders are so rare to begin with (this is the \"base rate\" effect).\n\n- A (1.0%) is just the prior 1% — it ignores the flag entirely.\n- C (47.4%) comes from forgetting the base rates and computing $$0.90 / (0.90 + 0.05 \\times ...)$$ incorrectly, or weighting both groups equally.\n- D (90.0%) is the test's sensitivity $$P(\\text{flagged} \\mid \\text{rogue})$$ — the question reversed. Confusing $$P(B\\mid A)$$ with $$P(A\\mid B)$$ is the classic trap.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When a rare condition is tested, most positive flags are false positives. Always weight by the base rate — set up 10,000 people if the formula feels slippery.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes: P(rogue|flagged)\",\"steps\":[\"prior: 1% rogue\",\"× sensitivity 90% → 0.009 (true +)\",\"honest false +: 0.99×0.05=0.0495\",\"÷ total flagged 0.0585\",\"= 15.4%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A bond's one-year default probability is 2% in an expansion and 10% in a recession. The probability of recession next year is 30%. What is the unconditional probability of default?\nA) 2.0% B) 4.4% C) 6.0% D) 10.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the overall (unconditional) chance of default, blending the two economic scenarios using the total probability rule.\n\nThe total probability rule says: weight each scenario's default rate by how likely that scenario is, then add.\n\n$$P(D) = P(D \\mid \\text{Rec}) \\times P(\\text{Rec}) + P(D \\mid \\text{Exp}) \\times P(\\text{Exp})$$\n\nThe recession probability is 30%, so expansion is $$100\\% - 30\\% = 70\\%$$.\n\n$$P(D) = 0.10 \\times 0.30 + 0.02 \\times 0.70$$\n\n$$= 0.030 + 0.014 = 0.044 = 4.4\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is 4.4% (option B). It's a weighted average: closer to the expansion rate because expansion is more likely.\n\n- A (2.0%) uses only the expansion default rate, ignoring the recession scenario.\n- C (6.0%) is the simple (unweighted) average of 2% and 10% ($$(2+10)/2$$), forgetting to weight by the 30%/70% probabilities.\n- D (10.0%) uses only the recession default rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Unconditional probability\" from scenario data = a probability-weighted average. Make sure the scenario weights add to 1 (here 30% + 70%).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Total probability of default\",\"steps\":[\"Recession: 0.30 × 10% = 0.030\",\"Expansion: 0.70 × 2% = 0.014\",\"add scenarios\",\"P(D)=4.4%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Using the data in Q5 (default rate 10% in recession, 2% in expansion; recession probability 30%; unconditional default 4.4%), the bond defaults. What is the probability the economy was in recession?\nA) 30.0% B) 44.0% C) 68.2% D) 88.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** This flips the conditioning: we know a default happened, and we want the chance the cause was a recession. That is Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$P(\\text{Rec} \\mid D) = \\frac{P(D \\mid \\text{Rec}) \\times P(\\text{Rec})}{P(D)}$$\n\nWe already computed the denominator $$P(D) = 0.044$$ in Q5. The numerator is the recession-and-default joint probability:\n\n$$P(D \\mid \\text{Rec}) \\times P(\\text{Rec}) = 0.10 \\times 0.30 = 0.030$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$P(\\text{Rec} \\mid D) = \\frac{0.030}{0.044} = 0.6818 \\approx 68.2\\%$$\n\nEven though recession had only a 30% prior chance, seeing a default makes recession the more likely explanation, because defaults are much more common in recessions.\n\n- A (30.0%) is just the prior probability of recession, ignoring the new default information.\n- B (44.0%) misreads the unconditional default rate (4.4%) as a percentage answer — unrelated quantity.\n- D (88.0%) likely comes from dividing the recession default rate by the total of the two rates ($$0.10/0.114$$ or similar mix-up) rather than using proper joint probabilities.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bayes \"updates\" the prior. The numerator is one joint cell; the denominator is the total probability of the evidence. Build a 2x2 table if unsure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes: P(recession|default)\",\"steps\":[\"joint: 0.10 × 0.30 = 0.030\",\"÷ P(D)=0.044 (from Q5)\",\"= 0.030/0.044\",\"P(Rec|D)=68.2%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** If events $$A$$ and $$B$$ are independent with $$P(A) = 0.4$$ and $$P(B) = 0.5$$, what is $$P(A \\cap B)$$?\nA) 0.00 B) 0.20 C) 0.50 D) 0.90\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the probability that both independent events happen together.\n\nFor independent events, the joint probability is simply the product:\n\n$$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\times P(B) = 0.4 \\times 0.5 = 0.20$$\n\nIndependent means knowing one event tells you nothing about the other — like two separate coin flips. So the chance of both is each chance multiplied.\n\n- A (0.00) is the answer for *mutually exclusive* events, not independent ones. Independent events with positive probabilities definitely can co-occur.\n- C (0.50) just reports $$P(B)$$.\n- D (0.90) adds the probabilities ($$0.4 + 0.5$$), which is neither a valid intersection nor a correct union (the union would be $$0.4 + 0.5 - 0.2 = 0.7$$).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Independent → multiply for the joint ($$\\cap$$). Mutually exclusive → add for the union ($$\\cup$$), and the joint is zero. These two are opposites; don't swap them.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Joint vs union operations\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Independent (∩)\",\"items\":[\"multiply\",\"P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)\",\"0.4×0.5=0.20\"]},{\"h\":\"Mut. excl. (∪)\",\"items\":[\"add\",\"P(A∪B)=P(A)+P(B)\",\"joint = 0\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** $$P(A) = 0.4$$, $$P(B) = 0.5$$, and $$P(A \\cap B) = 0.2$$. Which statement is TRUE?\nA) $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive B) = 0.4$$ is impossible C) $$A$$ and $$B$$ are dependent D) $$P(A \\mid\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to classify the relationship between the two events using the numbers given.\n\nTest for independence: check whether $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\times P(B)$$.\n\n$$P(A) \\times P(B) = 0.4 \\times 0.5 = 0.20$$\n\nThe given $$P(A \\cap B) = 0.20$$ matches exactly, so the events ARE independent (option B). We can also confirm: $$P(A \\mid B) = 0.2 / 0.5 = 0.4 = P(A)$$, the hallmark of independence.\n\n- A is wrong: mutually exclusive would require $$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$, but here it is 0.2.\n- C (dependent) is the opposite of the truth; the independence test passed exactly.\n- D is wrong: $$P(A \\mid B) = 0.4$$ is not only possible, it is exactly what independence produces here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To check independence, compute $$P(A) \\times P(B)$$ and compare to the given joint. Equal → independent; unequal → dependent.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Independence test\",\"steps\":[\"compute P(A)×P(B)=0.4×0.5=0.20\",\"compare to given P(A∩B)=0.20\",\"equal → independent\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A manager is classified as skilled (20% of managers) or unskilled (80%). A skilled manager beats the benchmark with probability 0.70, an unskilled one with probability 0.50. A randomly chosen manager beat the benchmark. The probability the manager is skilled is closest to:\nA) 20.0% B) 25.9% C) 54.0% D) 70.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We observe outperformance (the evidence) and want the chance the manager is truly skilled (the hidden state). That is Bayes' theorem, easiest with a small table.\n\nJoint probabilities of (type AND beating):\n- Skilled and beat: $$0.20 \\times 0.70 = 0.14$$\n- Unskilled and beat: $$0.80 \\times 0.50 = 0.40$$\n\nTotal probability of beating $$= 0.14 + 0.40 = 0.54$$.\n\n$$P(\\text{Skilled} \\mid \\text{Beat}) = \\frac{0.14}{0.54} = 0.2593 \\approx 25.9\\%$$\n\nOne year of beating the benchmark barely raises the belief in skill from 20% to about 26%, because plenty of unskilled managers also beat the benchmark by luck.\n\n- A (20.0%) is the prior, ignoring the new evidence.\n- C (54.0%) is the total probability of beating the benchmark (the denominator) — not the conditional answer.\n- D (70.0%) is $$P(\\text{beat} \\mid \\text{skilled})$$, the reversed conditional.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A single year of outperformance is weak evidence of skill — Bayes shows the posterior moves only slightly. Watch for the reversed-conditional trap (70%).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes: P(skilled|beat)\",\"steps\":[\"skilled & beat: 0.20×0.70=0.14\",\"unskilled & beat: 0.80×0.50=0.40\",\"÷ total beat 0.54\",\"= 25.9%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which of the following statements about probability is TRUE?\nA) If $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive, they must also be independent. B) = P(A)$$. C) If $$A$$ and $$B$$ are independent, then $$P(A \\mid D) Conditioning on a recession always lowers a portfolio's default probability.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is a \"which statement is true\" definitional check.\n\nBy definition, two events are independent exactly when knowing one tells you nothing about the other, i.e.:\n\n$$P(A \\mid B) = P(A)$$\n\nThis is equivalent to $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) \\times P(B)$$. So option C is the correct definition of independence.\n\n- A is false and is the chapter's favorite trap: mutually exclusive events (with positive probabilities) are actually *strongly dependent* — if one happens, the other cannot. They are nearly the opposite of independent.\n- B confuses unrelated ideas. Zero covariance/independence does not mean mutually exclusive; independent events with positive probability still co-occur.\n- D is backward: conditioning on a recession (a bad state) typically *raises* default probabilities — that is the whole idea behind stress testing.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the definition $$P(A \\mid B) = P(A) \\Leftrightarrow$$ independence. And remember: mutually exclusive ≠ independent; bad-state conditioning raises loss probabilities.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which probability statement is TRUE\",\"options\":[\"mut. excl. → must be independent\",\"zero cov → mutually exclusive\",\"independent → P(A|B)=P(A)\",\"recession lowers default prob\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"P(A|B)=P(A) is the definition of independence; the others reverse or confuse the concepts.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Events $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive with $$P(A)=0.3$$ and $$P(B)=0.4$$. What is $$P(A \\mid B)$$?\nA) 0.12 B) 0.30 C) 0.00 D) 0.70\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Mutually exclusive means $$P(A \\cap B)=0$$, so $$P(A \\mid B) = 0/0.4 = 0$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** $$P(A)=0.6$$, $$P(B)=0.5$$, $$P(A \\cup B)=0.8$$. Find $$P(A \\mid B)$$.\nA) 0.30 B) 0.60 C) 0.50 D) 0.375\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P(A \\cap B) = 0.6 + 0.5 - 0.8 = 0.3$$; $$P(A \\mid B) = 0.3/0.5 = 0.6$$. (Note the events happen to be independent.)\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A test for rogue trading flags 90% of true rogue traders and 5% of honest traders. 1% of traders are rogue. A trader is flagged. The probability the trader is actually rogue is closest to:\nA) 90.0% B) 15.4% C) 47.4% D) 1.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Bayes: $$\\frac{0.90 \\times 0.01}{0.90 \\times 0.01 + 0.05 \\times 0.99} = \\frac{0.009}{0.0585} = 0.1538$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** If $$A$$ and $$B$$ are independent with positive probabilities, which statement is TRUE?\nA) They must be mutually exclusive B) = 0$$ C) $$P(A \\mid D) $$P(A \\cap\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Independence is defined by $$P(A \\mid B) = P(A)$$, equivalently $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A)P(B) > 0$$, ruling out A, B, and D.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Events A and B are independent with $$P(A) = 0.40$$ and $$P(B) = 0.50$$. $$P(A \\cup B)$$ is closest to:\nA) 0.70 B) 0.90 C) 0.20 D) 0.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Independence gives $$P(A \\cap B) = 0.4 \\times 0.5 = 0.20$$, so\n\n$$P(A \\cup B) = 0.40 + 0.50 - 0.20 = 0.70$$\n\nDistractor B forgets to subtract the intersection.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Independent vs mutually exclusive\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Independent\",\"items\":[\"P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)\",\"both can occur\",\"subtract overlap in union\"]},{\"h\":\"Mutually exclusive\",\"items\":[\"P(A∩B)=0\",\"cannot co-occur\",\"union = simple sum\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** 10% of the firms in a loan portfolio are high-risk. A rating downgrade occurs with probability 60% for high-risk firms and 20% for low-risk firms. Given that a firm has just been downgraded, the probability it is high-risk is closest to:\nA) 0.06 B) 0.25 C) 0.33 D) 0.60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Bayes via the total-probability table:\n\n$$P(H \\cap D) = 0.10 \\times 0.60 = 0.06 \\qquad P(L \\cap D) = 0.90 \\times 0.20 = 0.18$$\n\n$$P(H \\mid D) = \\frac{0.06}{0.06 + 0.18} = \\frac{0.06}{0.24} = 0.25$$\n\nDistractor A is the joint probability (numerator only); D is the inverted conditional $$P(D \\mid H)$$.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes update\",\"steps\":[\"P(D|H)·P(H)=0.06\",\"P(D|L)·P(L)=0.18\",\"P(D)=0.06+0.18=0.24\",\"P(H|D)=0.06/0.24=0.25\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A credit analyst classifies obligors as high-risk (15% of the book) or low-risk (85%). A high-risk obligor defaults within a year with probability 0.30; a low-risk obligor with probability 0.04. An obligor selected at random defaulted this year. What is the probability the obligor was high-risk?\nA) 30.0% B) 50.0% C) 56.9% D) 76.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We are asked for the posterior $$P(\\text{High} \\mid \\text{Default})$$ — invert the conditional using Bayes' theorem.\n\n1. Joint probability of high-risk AND default: $$0.15 \\times 0.30 = 0.045$$.\n2. Joint probability of low-risk AND default: $$0.85 \\times 0.04 = 0.034$$.\n3. Total (unconditional) default probability: $$0.045 + 0.034 = 0.079$$.\n4. Bayes: $$P(\\text{High} \\mid \\text{Default}) = \\dfrac{0.045}{0.079} = 0.5696 \\approx 56.9\\%$$.\n\nC is correct. A (30%) is the *conditional default rate of a high-risk obligor*, $$P(\\text{Default}\\mid\\text{High})$$ — the question asks for the reverse direction, the classic Bayes inversion error. D (76%) uses only the high-risk joint over the wrong base, $$0.045/0.059$$-type miscount, or confuses with the proportion of defaults that are low risk inverted. B (50%) is a \"split the difference\" guess with no support.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When a stem gives you $$P(\\text{evidence}\\mid\\text{state})$$ and asks for $$P(\\text{state}\\mid\\text{evidence})$$, you MUST run Bayes — never report the given conditional back. Build a two-cell numerator/denominator: target joint over total joint.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes: P(high|default)\",\"steps\":[\"high & default: 0.15×0.30=0.045\",\"low & default: 0.85×0.04=0.034\",\"÷ total default 0.079\",\"= 56.9%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Events $$A$$ and $$B$$ satisfy $$P(A) = 0.50$$, $$P(B) = 0.40$$, and $$P(A \\cup B) = 0.70$$. Which statement is correct?\nA) $$A$$ and $$B$$ are mutually exclusive B) = 0.40$$ C) $$P(A \\mid D) $$P(A \\cup B)$$ should equal $$0.90$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We must find $$P(A \\cap B)$$, then test the relationships.\n\n1. Addition rule: $$P(A \\cap B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cup B) = 0.50 + 0.40 - 0.70 = 0.20$$.\n2. Independence check: $$P(A)P(B) = 0.50 \\times 0.40 = 0.20 = P(A\\cap B)$$. They match, so $$A$$ and $$B$$ are independent.\n3. Verify via conditional: $$P(A\\mid B) = 0.20/0.40 = 0.50 = P(A)$$ — knowing $$B$$ does not change $$A$$, confirming independence.\n\nB is correct. A is false: mutual exclusivity requires $$P(A\\cap B)=0$$, but the intersection is 0.20. C reports $$P(A\\mid B)=0.40$$, but the actual value is $$0.50$$ (it mistakes $$P(A\\mid B)$$ for $$P(B)$$). D is the double-counting error: $$0.50+0.40=0.90$$ omits the subtraction of the intersection, which the correct addition rule includes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Independence test is always $$P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$$. Mutual exclusivity is $$P(A\\cap B)=0$$. For positive-probability events the two are opposites — never both at once.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Classify A and B\",\"steps\":[\"P(A∩B)=0.50+0.40−0.70=0.20\",\"P(A)P(B)=0.50×0.40=0.20\",\"equal → independent\",\"check P(A|B)=0.20/0.40=0.50=P(A)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A fraud filter flags 92% of fraudulent transactions and 3% of legitimate ones. Historically 0.5% of transactions are fraudulent. Given that a transaction is flagged, the probability it is actually fraudulent is closest to:\nA) 92.0% B) 13.4% C) 86.6% D) 0.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We need $$P(\\text{Fraud} \\mid \\text{Flag})$$ via Bayes.\n\n1. Fraud and flagged: $$0.005 \\times 0.92 = 0.0046$$.\n2. Legit and flagged: $$0.995 \\times 0.03 = 0.02985$$.\n3. Total flagged: $$0.0046 + 0.02985 = 0.03445$$.\n4. $$P(\\text{Fraud}\\mid\\text{Flag}) = \\dfrac{0.0046}{0.03445} = 0.1335 \\approx 13.4\\%$$.\n\nB is correct. A (92%) is the detection sensitivity $$P(\\text{Flag}\\mid\\text{Fraud})$$, reported backwards. D (0.5%) is the prior, ignoring the evidence entirely. C (86.6%) is $$1 - 0.134$$ — the probability it is legitimate given a flag, the complement reported as the answer. This is the \"base-rate fallacy\": a rare event plus a small false-positive rate still yields mostly false alarms.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When the prior probability is tiny, even a highly accurate test produces a posterior far below the sensitivity — the legitimate population swamps the flags. Always compute both joint terms.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes: P(fraud|flag)\",\"steps\":[\"fraud & flag: 0.005×0.92=0.0046\",\"legit & flag: 0.995×0.03=0.02985\",\"÷ total flagged 0.03445\",\"= 13.4%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A bond portfolio defaults with probability 12% in a recession and 2% in an expansion. The economy will be in recession next year with probability 30%, otherwise expansion. Two independent, identical bonds are held. Conditional on the economic state, defaults are independent. What is the probability that BOTH bonds default next year?\nA) 0.25% B) 0.46% C) 5.00% D) 1.44%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We need the unconditional probability that both default, accounting for state-dependent default rates. Defaults are independent *given* the state, not unconditionally.\n\n1. In recession, both default: $$(0.12)^2 = 0.0144$$.\n2. In expansion, both default: $$(0.02)^2 = 0.0004$$.\n3. Weight by state probabilities (total probability rule):\n$$P(\\text{both}) = 0.30 \\times 0.0144 + 0.70 \\times 0.0004 = 0.00432 + 0.00028 = 0.0046 = 0.46\\%.$$\n\nB is correct. A (0.25%) squares the *blended* unconditional default rate $$p = 0.30(0.12)+0.70(0.02)=0.050$$, giving $$0.050^2 = 0.0025$$ — this wrongly assumes the bonds are unconditionally independent and ignores the common economic factor. C (5.00%) is that blended single-bond default rate itself, not a joint probability. D (1.44%) uses only the recession term $$(0.12)^2=0.0144$$ and forgets to weight by the 30% recession probability (and to add the expansion term).\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Independent *conditional on* the state\" does NOT mean unconditionally independent. You must square the default probability *inside each state* and then weight — squaring the blended rate understates joint defaults because it ignores the common factor (the economy).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Both default (conditional independence)\",\"steps\":[\"recession: (0.12)²=0.0144\",\"expansion: (0.02)²=0.0004\",\"weight: 0.30×0.0144 + 0.70×0.0004\",\"= 0.0046 = 0.46%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A risk committee assigns prior probabilities to three market regimes for the coming month: calm (60%), volatile (30%), crisis (10%). The probability that the index falls more than 5% in a month is 2% in calm, 15% in volatile, and 50% in crisis. The index fell more than 5% last month. The probability the regime was \"crisis\" is closest to:\nA) 10.0% B) 46.7% C) 50.0% D) 42.1%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We need $$P(\\text{Crisis} \\mid \\text{Drop})$$ with three states (Bayes with total-probability denominator).\n\n1. Joint terms:\n   - Calm & drop: $$0.60 \\times 0.02 = 0.012$$\n   - Volatile & drop: $$0.30 \\times 0.15 = 0.045$$\n   - Crisis & drop: $$0.10 \\times 0.50 = 0.050$$\n2. Total probability of a drop: $$0.012 + 0.045 + 0.050 = 0.107$$.\n3. $$P(\\text{Crisis}\\mid\\text{Drop}) = \\dfrac{0.050}{0.107} = 0.4673 \\approx 46.7\\%$$.\n\nB is correct. A (10%) is the prior, ignoring the evidence. C (50%) is $$P(\\text{Drop}\\mid\\text{Crisis})$$ reported backwards. D (42.1%) is the *volatile* posterior $$0.045/0.107 = 0.4206$$ — the right method but the wrong state selected.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With more than two states, the Bayes denominator is the full sum of all joint terms. Forgetting one state (often the \"calm/normal\" one with low conditional probability) is the most common error.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes with three states: P(crisis|drop)\",\"steps\":[\"calm: 0.60×0.02=0.012\",\"volatile: 0.30×0.15=0.045\",\"crisis: 0.10×0.50=0.050\",\"÷ total 0.107 → 46.7%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are two events with $$P(X)=0.5$$, $$P(Y)=0.3$$, and $$P(X \\cap Y)=0.15$$. Which of the following is TRUE?\nA) $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are mutually exclusive B) $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are independent, and $$P(X \\cup Y)=0.65$$ C) $$P(Y \\mid X)=0.30$$ and the events are dependent D) $$P(X \\cup Y)=0.80$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Check independence and compute the union.\n\n1. Independence test: $$P(X)P(Y) = 0.5 \\times 0.3 = 0.15 = P(X\\cap Y)$$. Equal, so $$X$$ and $$Y$$ ARE independent.\n2. Union: $$P(X\\cup Y) = 0.5 + 0.3 - 0.15 = 0.65$$.\n3. Conditional: $$P(Y\\mid X) = 0.15/0.5 = 0.30 = P(Y)$$ — confirms independence (knowing $$X$$ doesn't change $$Y$$).\n\nB is correct. A is false: $$P(X\\cap Y)=0.15\\ne 0$$. C correctly computes $$P(Y\\mid X)=0.30$$ but wrongly concludes \"dependent\" — equality with $$P(Y)$$ proves independence, so C is the subtle trap. D adds the marginals without subtracting the intersection: $$0.5+0.3=0.80$$ — the double-counting error.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$P(Y\\mid X)=P(Y)$$ is itself the independence definition. If a stem hands you that equality, the events are independent — and the union always subtracts the intersection once.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Test independence + union\",\"steps\":[\"P(X)P(Y)=0.5×0.3=0.15=P(X∩Y) → independent\",\"P(X∪Y)=0.5+0.3−0.15=0.65\",\"P(Y|X)=0.15/0.5=0.30=P(Y) confirms\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** During a recession (probability 0.20), a particular hedge fund posts a loss with probability 0.65. In normal times (probability 0.80), it posts a loss with probability 0.10. Over a year, what is the unconditional probability the fund posts a loss, and given a loss, what is the probability the economy was normal?\nA) Loss = 21%; P(normal | loss) = 38.1% B) Loss = 75%; P(normal | loss) = 80.0% C) Loss = 21%; P(normal | loss) = 80.0% D) Loss = 13%; P(normal | loss) = 61.9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Two-part: total probability, then Bayes.\n\n1. Joint terms:\n   - Recession & loss: $$0.20 \\times 0.65 = 0.13$$\n   - Normal & loss: $$0.80 \\times 0.10 = 0.08$$\n2. Unconditional loss probability: $$0.13 + 0.08 = 0.21 = 21\\%$$.\n3. Bayes: $$P(\\text{Normal}\\mid\\text{Loss}) = \\dfrac{0.08}{0.21} = 0.3810 \\approx 38.1\\%$$.\n\nA is correct. C uses the right unconditional loss (21%) but reports the prior $$P(\\text{Normal})=0.80$$ instead of the posterior — failing to update on the loss evidence. B adds the conditional loss rates $$0.65+0.10=0.75$$ (nonsense — conditionals don't add) and reports the prior. D miscomputes the joint (uses $$0.20\\times0.65=0.13$$ alone as the total, dropping the normal-state contribution) and then inverts using $$0.08/0.13$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Unconditional probability = SUM of joint terms across all states. The posterior $$P(\\text{state}\\mid\\text{event})$$ is a single joint term divided by that sum — never the prior, and conditional rates never add.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Total prob then Bayes\",\"steps\":[\"recession & loss: 0.20×0.65=0.13\",\"normal & loss: 0.80×0.10=0.08\",\"unconditional loss = 0.21\",\"P(normal|loss)=0.08/0.21=38.1%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"202","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Random Variables","notes":"# 2. Random Variables\n\nThis chapter builds the machinery used everywhere else in Book 2: PMF/PDF/CDF, expectation and variance algebra, the four moments, and quantiles. Expect direct calculations of $$E[X]$$, $$\\mathrm{Var}(X)$$, and transformed variables $$aX+b$$.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe and distinguish a probability mass function (PMF), a cumulative distribution function (CDF), and a probability density function (PDF).\n- Understand and apply the concept of a mathematical expectation of a random variable.\n- Describe the four common population moments: mean, variance, skewness, and kurtosis.\n- Explain the differences between a probability mass function and a probability density function.\n- Characterize the quantile function and quantile-based estimators.\n- Explain the effect of a linear transformation of a random variable on the mean, variance, standard deviation, skewness, kurtosis, median, and interquartile range.\n\n## PMF, PDF, and CDF\n\n| Concept | Discrete RV | Continuous RV |\n|---|---|---|\n| Probability of a point | PMF: $$f(x) = P(X = x)$$ | $$P(X = x) = 0$$; PDF $$f(x)$$ gives density, not probability |\n| Probability of a range | Sum the PMF | Integrate the PDF: $$P(a \\le X \\le b) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$$ |\n| Cumulative | $$F(x) = \\sum_{t \\le x} f(t)$$ | $$F(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{x} f(t)\\,dt$$ |\n\nThe CDF $$F(x) = P(X \\le x)$$ is nondecreasing, runs from 0 to 1, and works identically for both types. For continuous variables, $$f(x) = F'(x)$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> A PDF value can exceed 1 (e.g., a uniform on $$[0, 0.5]$$ has $$f(x) = 2$$). Only *probabilities* — areas under the PDF — must lie in $$[0,1]$$. Distractors that reject $$f(x) > 1$$ as \"impossible\" are wrong.\n\n## Expectation\n\nFor discrete $$X$$: $$E[X] = \\sum_i x_i\\,p_i$$. For continuous $$X$$: $$E[X] = \\int x f(x)\\,dx$$.\n\nExpectation is **linear**:\n\n$$E[aX + b] = aE[X] + b, \\qquad E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y]$$\n\nLinearity of $$E[X+Y]$$ holds **always**, even when $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are dependent. But in general $$E[g(X)] \\ne g(E[X])$$ for nonlinear $$g$$ (Jensen's inequality: for convex $$g$$, $$E[g(X)] \\ge g(E[X])$$).\n\n## The four population moments\n\n**Variance** (second central moment):\n\n$$\\sigma^2 = \\mathrm{Var}(X) = E[(X-\\mu)^2] = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2$$\n\n**Skewness** (standardized third central moment) — measures asymmetry:\n\n$$\\text{skew} = \\frac{E[(X-\\mu)^3]}{\\sigma^3}$$\n\n**Kurtosis** (standardized fourth central moment) — measures tail heaviness:\n\n$$\\text{kurt} = \\frac{E[(X-\\mu)^4]}{\\sigma^4}$$\n\nNormal kurtosis is 3; **excess kurtosis** = kurt − 3. Kurtosis above 3 (\"leptokurtic,\" fat tails) is the empirical norm for financial returns. Negative skew + excess kurtosis = the classic risk-management cocktail: frequent small gains, rare large losses.\n\n## Linear transformations: $$Y = aX + b$$\n\n| Statistic of $$Y = aX + b$$ | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Mean | $$a\\mu + b$$ |\n| Variance | $$a^2 \\sigma^2$$ |\n| Standard deviation | $$\\lvert a \\rvert \\sigma$$ |\n| Skewness | unchanged if $$a>0$$; sign flips if $$a<0$$ |\n| Kurtosis | unchanged |\n| Median / quantiles | $$a \\cdot \\text{median} + b$$ (for $$a > 0$$) |\n| IQR | $$\\lvert a \\rvert \\cdot \\text{IQR}$$ |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Variance scales by $$a^2$$, not $$a$$ — and the additive constant $$b$$ shifts the mean but does **nothing** to variance, skewness, or kurtosis. Both facts are recycled endlessly as distractors.\n\n## Quantiles\n\nThe quantile function inverts the CDF: $$Q(\\alpha) = F^{-1}(\\alpha)$$ is the value below which the variable falls with probability $$\\alpha$$. The median is $$Q(0.5)$$; the interquartile range is\n\n$$\\text{IQR} = Q(0.75) - Q(0.25)$$\n\nQuantile-based measures (median, IQR) are robust to outliers and exist even when moments do not (e.g., Cauchy). VaR is precisely a quantile of the loss distribution, which is why this machinery matters for FRM.\n\n### Worked example: moments of a discrete payoff\n\nA trade pays $$-10$$ with probability 0.2, $$0$$ with probability 0.5, and $$+8$$ with probability 0.3.\n\n**Mean:**\n\n$$E[X] = (-10)(0.2) + 0(0.5) + 8(0.3) = -2.0 + 0 + 2.4 = 0.4$$\n\n**Second moment and variance:**\n\n$$E[X^2] = 100(0.2) + 0 + 64(0.3) = 20 + 19.2 = 39.2$$\n\n$$\\sigma^2 = 39.2 - 0.4^2 = 39.04, \\qquad \\sigma = 6.248$$\n\n**Skewness:** third central moment\n\n$$E[(X-0.4)^3] = (-10.4)^3(0.2) + (-0.4)^3(0.5) + (7.6)^3(0.3)$$\n\n$$= (-1124.864)(0.2) + (-0.064)(0.5) + (438.976)(0.3) = -224.97 - 0.03 + 131.69 = -93.31$$\n\n$$\\text{skew} = \\frac{-93.31}{6.248^3} = \\frac{-93.31}{243.9} = -0.383$$\n\nNegatively skewed: the rare $$-10$$ loss drags the tail left even though the mean is positive.\n\n### Worked example: linear transformation\n\nDaily P&L $$X$$ has mean 0.5 and standard deviation 4 (in $ thousands). The desk converts to euros at a fixed rate of 0.9 and subtracts a fixed cost of 0.2: $$Y = 0.9X - 0.2$$.\n\n$$E[Y] = 0.9(0.5) - 0.2 = 0.25$$\n\n$$\\sigma_Y = 0.9 \\times 4 = 3.6, \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y) = 0.81 \\times 16 = 12.96$$\n\nSkewness and kurtosis of $$Y$$ equal those of $$X$$ (positive scale, shift irrelevant).","flashcards":"**Q1.** Distinguish a PMF from a PDF.\n> **A:** PMF gives $$f(x) = P(X = x)$$ for discrete variables; PDF gives density (not probability) for continuous variables, where $$P(X = x) = 0$$ and probability comes from integrating: $$P(a \\le X \\le b) = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$$.\n\n**Q2.** Can a PDF value exceed 1?\n> **A:** Yes. Only probabilities (areas under the PDF) must lie in $$[0,1]$$. E.g., a uniform on $$[0, 0.5]$$ has $$f(x) = 2$$.\n\n**Q3.** What are the properties of a CDF $$F(x) = P(X \\le x)$$?\n> **A:** Nondecreasing, runs from 0 to 1, works for discrete and continuous variables; for continuous variables $$f(x) = F'(x)$$.\n\n**Q4.** State linearity of expectation, including the dependence caveat.\n> **A:** $$E[aX + b] = aE[X] + b$$ and $$E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y]$$ — the sum rule holds always, even when $$X, Y$$ are dependent.\n\n**Q5.** Give the computational formula for variance.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma^2 = E[(X-\\mu)^2] = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2$$\n\n**Q6.** Define skewness and kurtosis.\n> **A:** Skewness $$= \\dfrac{E[(X-\\mu)^3]}{\\sigma^3}$$ (asymmetry); kurtosis $$= \\dfrac{E[(X-\\mu)^4]}{\\sigma^4}$$ (tail heaviness).\n\n**Q7.** What is excess kurtosis, and what does a positive value mean?\n> **A:** Excess kurtosis $$=$$ kurtosis $$- 3$$ (normal kurtosis is 3). Positive (leptokurtic) means fatter-than-normal tails — the empirical norm for returns.\n\n**Q8.** For $$Y = aX + b$$, how do the mean, variance, and standard deviation transform?\n> **A:** Mean $$= a\\mu + b$$; variance $$= a^2\\sigma^2$$; standard deviation $$= \\lvert a\\rvert\\sigma$$.\n\n**Q9.** For $$Y = aX + b$$, how do skewness and kurtosis transform?\n> **A:** Kurtosis is unchanged. Skewness is unchanged if $$a > 0$$; its sign flips if $$a < 0$$. The additive constant $$b$$ affects none of them.\n\n**Q10.** Define the quantile function and the interquartile range.\n> **A:** $$Q(\\alpha) = F^{-1}(\\alpha)$$, the value below which $$X$$ falls with probability $$\\alpha$$; $$\\text{IQR} = Q(0.75) - Q(0.25)$$.\n\n**Q11.** Why are quantile-based measures important for risk?\n> **A:** They are robust to outliers and exist even when moments do not (e.g., Cauchy); VaR is precisely a quantile of the loss distribution.\n\n**Q12.** $$X$$ has $$E[X] = 2$$ and $$E[X^2] = 13$$. Find the standard deviation.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma^2 = 13 - 2^2 = 9$$, so $$\\sigma = 3$$.\n\n**Q13.** State Jensen's inequality for a convex function $$g$$.\n> **A:** $$E[g(X)] \\ge g(E[X])$$; in general $$E[g(X)] \\ne g(E[X])$$ for nonlinear $$g$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A random variable $$X$$ has $$E[X] = 2$$ and $$E[X^2] = 13$$. What is the standard deviation of $$X$$?\nA) 3 B) 9 C) 11 D) 3.61\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question gives the mean and the \"mean of the squares\" and asks for the standard deviation.\n\nUse the shortcut formula for variance:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2$$\n\nPlug in:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 13 - (2)^2 = 13 - 4 = 9$$\n\nStandard deviation is the square root of variance:\n\n$$\\sigma = \\sqrt{9} = 3$$\n\nSo the answer is 3 (option A). Variance measures spread in squared units; taking the square root brings it back to the original units.\n\n- B (9) is the variance, not the standard deviation — they forgot the square root.\n- C (11) comes from $$13 - 2 = 11$$, subtracting the mean instead of the mean squared.\n- D (3.61) is $$\\sqrt{13}$$, forgetting to subtract $$(E[X])^2$$ altogether.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize $$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2$$. Then remember to take the square root for standard deviation — a one-step slip turns the right variance into a wrong answer.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"From moments to std dev\",\"steps\":[\"Var=E[X²]−(E[X])²\",\"= 13 − 4 = 9\",\"√Var\",\"σ = 3\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** If $$Y = -2X + 5$$, and $$X$$ has skewness $$+0.8$$ and kurtosis 5, then $$Y$$ has:\nA) Skewness $$+0.8$$, kurtosis 5 B) Skewness $$-0.8$$, kurtosis 5 C) Skewness $$-0.8$$, kurtosis $$-5$$ D) Skewness $$+0.8$$, kurtosis 10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how a linear transformation $$Y = aX + b$$ (here $$a = -2$$, $$b = 5$$) affects the *shape* statistics skewness and kurtosis.\n\nRules for linear transformations:\n- Skewness is unchanged if the scale $$a$$ is positive, but its sign flips if $$a$$ is negative. Here $$a = -2 < 0$$, so skewness flips: $$+0.8 \\to -0.8$$.\n- Kurtosis is unchanged by any linear transformation, regardless of the sign of $$a$$. So kurtosis stays at 5.\n\nTherefore $$Y$$ has skewness $$-0.8$$ and kurtosis 5 (option B). Multiplying by a negative number mirrors the distribution left-to-right (flipping which tail is long), but stretching/shifting never changes how \"fat\" the tails are.\n\n- A keeps skewness positive, ignoring that a negative scale flips the sign.\n- C wrongly makes kurtosis negative; kurtosis is always positive and is unaffected here.\n- D doubles kurtosis to 10, as if the scale factor $$|a| = 2$$ multiplied it — but kurtosis is standardized and does not scale.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Linear transform shape rules: skewness flips sign only if $$a<0$$; kurtosis never changes. The constant $$b$$ does nothing to any shape statistic.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Y=aX+b shape effects (a<0)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Skewness\",\"items\":[\"sign flips if a<0\",\"+0.8 → −0.8\",\"magnitude unchanged\"]},{\"h\":\"Kurtosis\",\"items\":[\"never changes\",\"stays 5\",\"standardized, no scaling\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Daily P&L $$X$$ has mean 0.5 and standard deviation 4. The desk applies $$Y = 0.9X - 0.2$$. What is the standard deviation of $$Y$$?\nA) 3.4 B) 3.6 C) 3.8 D) 12.96\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the standard deviation of a linearly transformed variable $$Y = aX + b$$ with $$a = 0.9$$ and $$b = -0.2$$.\n\nThe rule for standard deviation under a linear transformation is:\n\n$$\\sigma_Y = |a| \\times \\sigma_X$$\n\nThe additive constant $$b = -0.2$$ does NOT affect spread — shifting every value down by 0.2 moves the whole distribution but does not stretch or squeeze it.\n\n$$\\sigma_Y = 0.9 \\times 4 = 3.6$$\n\nSo the answer is 3.6 (option B).\n\n- A (3.4) subtracts 0.2 from something, wrongly letting the constant $$b$$ reduce the spread. Constants never change spread.\n- C (3.8) adds instead of multiplying ($$4 - 0.2$$ mis-handled), again mixing the constant into the scale.\n- D (12.96) is the *variance* of $$Y$$ ($$0.9^2 \\times 4^2 = 0.81 \\times 16$$), not the standard deviation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For $$Y=aX+b$$, standard deviation scales by $$|a|$$ and ignores $$b$$. Variance scales by $$a^2$$. Don't report variance when asked for standard deviation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Y=aX+b spread rules\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Std dev\",\"items\":[\"× |a|\",\"0.9×4 = 3.6\",\"b ignored\"]},{\"h\":\"Variance\",\"items\":[\"× a²\",\"0.81×16 = 12.96\",\"b ignored\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which statement about a continuous random variable is TRUE?\nA) Its PDF value can never exceed 1. B) $$P(X = x) = 0$$ for every single point $$x$$. C) Its CDF can decrease over some intervals. D) The PDF directly gives $$P(X \\le x)$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This is a \"which is true\" check on the definitions of PDF and CDF for continuous variables.\n\nFor a continuous variable, the probability of landing on any exact single point is zero, because there are infinitely many possible values. Probability only accumulates over a *range* (an area under the PDF curve). So $$P(X = x) = 0$$ for every point — option B is correct.\n\nAnalogy: ask \"what's the chance a dart lands at exactly 3.0000... cm?\" — effectively zero, because there are infinitely many points; only \"between 2 and 4 cm\" has real probability.\n\n- A is false: a PDF value (a density) can exceed 1. For example, a uniform distribution on $$[0, 0.5]$$ has height $$f(x) = 2$$. Only *areas* (probabilities) must stay in $$[0,1]$$.\n- C is false: a CDF is always nondecreasing — it can be flat but never goes down, since probability cannot become negative.\n- D is false: the *CDF* (not the PDF) gives $$P(X \\le x)$$. The PDF gives density; you integrate it to get probability.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Three facts to lock in: point probabilities are 0 for continuous variables; PDF heights can exceed 1; CDFs never decrease.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"PDF vs CDF (continuous)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"PDF (density)\",\"items\":[\"height can exceed 1\",\"gives density, not prob\",\"area = probability\",\"P(X=x)=0 at any point\"]},{\"h\":\"CDF\",\"items\":[\"gives P(X≤x)\",\"range [0,1]\",\"never decreases\",\"integral of PDF\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A distribution of returns has kurtosis of 6. Relative to a normal distribution with the same variance, this distribution has:\nA) Thinner tails and excess kurtosis of 6. B) Fatter tails and excess kurtosis of 3. C) Fatter tails and excess kurtosis of 6. D) The same tails; kurtosis only measures asymmetry.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the meaning of kurtosis and \"excess kurtosis.\"\n\nA normal distribution has kurtosis of exactly 3. Excess kurtosis is defined as:\n\n$$\\text{excess kurtosis} = \\text{kurtosis} - 3 = 6 - 3 = 3$$\n\nA positive excess kurtosis (here +3) means the distribution is \"leptokurtic\" — it has fatter tails than the normal. Fatter tails mean extreme outcomes (big gains or big losses) happen more often than a normal would predict. So option B is correct: fatter tails, excess kurtosis of 3.\n\n- A says thinner tails, but kurtosis above 3 means fatter tails, not thinner.\n- C correctly says fatter tails but wrongly reports excess kurtosis as 6 (it forgot to subtract 3).\n- D confuses kurtosis with skewness. Kurtosis measures tail heaviness/peakedness; *skewness* measures asymmetry.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Excess kurtosis = kurtosis − 3. Positive → fat tails (typical of financial returns). Don't confuse kurtosis (tails) with skewness (asymmetry).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Kurtosis and tail thickness\",\"items\":[\"thin (platykurtic, k<3)\",\"|normal (k=3)\",\"fat (leptokurtic, k=6 → excess +3)\"],\"left\":\"fewer extremes\",\"right\":\"more extremes\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A trade pays $$-10$$ with probability 0.2, $$0$$ with probability 0.5, and $$+8$$ with probability 0.3. What is its expected value $$E[X]$$?\nA) $$-0.67$$ B) 0.40 C) 0.67 D) 2.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the expected (average) payoff of a discrete trade, weighting each outcome by its probability.\n\nFor a discrete variable:\n\n$$E[X] = \\sum_i x_i \\, p_i$$\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n$$E[X] = (-10)(0.2) + (0)(0.5) + (8)(0.3)$$\n\n$$= -2.0 + 0 + 2.4 = 0.4$$\n\nSo the expected value is 0.40 (option B). It is a probability-weighted average, not a simple average.\n\n- A ($$-0.67$$) and C (0.67) come from taking a plain average of the three payoff values ($$(-10 + 0 + 8)/3 = -0.67$$) ignoring the probabilities entirely (and C flips the sign).\n- D (2.40) keeps only the positive contribution $$8 \\times 0.3$$ and forgets the $$-2.0$$ from the loss term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected value always weights each outcome by its probability — never take a plain average of the outcome values. Multiply each payoff by its probability, then sum.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A continuous variable is uniform on the interval $$[0, 0.5]$$. What is the height of its probability density function $$f(x)$$ on that interval?\nA) 0.5 B) 1.0 C) 2.0 D) It is impossible; a PDF cannot exceed 1.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the density height of a uniform distribution over a short interval.\n\nFor a continuous uniform distribution on $$[a, b]$$, the PDF is constant and equal to:\n\n$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{b - a}$$\n\nHere $$a = 0$$ and $$b = 0.5$$:\n\n$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{0.5 - 0} = \\frac{1}{0.5} = 2.0$$\n\nSo the density height is 2.0 (option C). The total *area* must equal 1 (it's a probability), and area = height × width = $$2.0 \\times 0.5 = 1$$. Because the base is narrow (0.5 wide), the height must be tall (2.0) to keep the area at 1.\n\n- A (0.5) reports the width of the interval, not the density.\n- B (1.0) assumes the density must be capped at 1 — it doesn't.\n- D states the common myth that a PDF cannot exceed 1. A density value can be any positive number; only the total area (probability) is capped at 1.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Uniform PDF height = $$1/(b-a)$$, which exceeds 1 whenever the interval is narrower than 1 unit. Density is not probability — only areas are.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Uniform PDF height\",\"steps\":[\"f(x)=1/(b−a)\",\"=1/(0.5−0)\",\"height = 2.0\",\"check: area = 2.0×0.5 = 1\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which of the following is left unchanged when a variable is shifted by adding a constant, $$Y = X + b$$?\nA) The mean B) The median C) The variance D) The 75th percentile\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which statistic is *unaffected* by adding a constant (a pure shift, no stretching).\n\nAdding a constant $$b$$ slides every value of the distribution by the same amount. This moves all the location measures (mean, median, percentiles) up or down by $$b$$, but it does NOT change how spread out the values are. Variance measures spread, so:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X + b) = \\mathrm{Var}(X)$$\n\nThe constant $$b$$ leaves variance (and standard deviation, skewness, and kurtosis) unchanged. So option C is correct.\n\nAnalogy: if every student's exam score goes up by 5 bonus points, the *average* rises by 5, but the *spread* between high and low scorers is exactly the same.\n\n- A: the mean shifts by $$b$$ ($$E[X+b] = E[X] + b$$), so it changes.\n- B: the median is a location measure, so it shifts by $$b$$.\n- D: the 75th percentile is also a location measure and shifts by $$b$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: An additive constant moves all *location* measures (mean, median, quantiles) but never touches *spread/shape* (variance, SD, skewness, kurtosis).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Adding constant b: what shifts\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Shifts by b\",\"items\":[\"mean\",\"median\",\"percentiles (75th)\"]},{\"h\":\"Unchanged\",\"items\":[\"variance\",\"std dev\",\"skewness, kurtosis\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The interquartile range (IQR) of a distribution is defined as:\nA) $$Q(0.5)$$ B) $$Q(0.75) - Q(0.25)$$ C) $$Q(0.9) - Q(0.1)$$ D) $$Q(1.0) - Q(0.0)$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the definition of the interquartile range.\n\nThe IQR is the spread of the \"middle 50%\" of the data. It is the difference between the third quartile (75th percentile) and the first quartile (25th percentile):\n\n$$\\text{IQR} = Q(0.75) - Q(0.25)$$\n\nSo option B is correct. Because it ignores the most extreme 25% on each end, the IQR is robust to outliers — a single huge value won't blow it up the way it would inflate the variance.\n\n- A ($$Q(0.5)$$) is just the median — a single point, not a range.\n- C ($$Q(0.9) - Q(0.1)$$) is the interdecile range (middle 80%), a different measure.\n- D ($$Q(1.0) - Q(0.0)$$) is the full range (max minus min), which is extremely sensitive to outliers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: IQR = 75th − 25th percentile = middle 50% spread. It is a *robust* dispersion measure, unlike variance and the full range.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Definition of IQR\",\"options\":[\"Q(0.5) — the median\",\"Q(0.75)−Q(0.25)\",\"Q(0.9)−Q(0.1)\",\"Q(1.0)−Q(0.0)\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"IQR is the middle-50% spread: third quartile minus first quartile.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The expectation operator $$E[\\cdot]$$ is linear. Which statement is therefore always TRUE, even when $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are dependent?\nA) $$E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y]$$ B) $$E[XY] = E[X]\\,E[Y]$$ C) $$E[X^2] = (E[X])^2$$ D) $$\\mathrm{Var}(X + Y) = \\mathrm{Var}(X) + \\mathrm{Var}(Y)$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question tests which property holds *always*, regardless of dependence.\n\nLinearity of expectation says the expected value of a sum equals the sum of the expected values:\n\n$$E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y]$$\n\nThis holds always — even if $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are strongly dependent. That is what makes it so useful. So option A is correct.\n\n- B ($$E[XY] = E[X]E[Y]$$) holds only when $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are *uncorrelated/independent*, not in general. Dependence breaks it.\n- C ($$E[X^2] = (E[X])^2$$) is false in general; the difference between the two sides is exactly the variance, which is usually positive.\n- D ($$\\mathrm{Var}(X+Y) = \\mathrm{Var}(X) + \\mathrm{Var}(Y)$$) holds only when the covariance is zero. In general there is a $$+2\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$ term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expectation of a *sum* always splits apart (linearity, no independence needed). Expectation of a *product* and variance of a *sum* do NOT split unless the variables are uncorrelated.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Holds always vs needs independence\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Always true\",\"items\":[\"E[X+Y]=E[X]+E[Y]\",\"linearity, no conditions\"]},{\"h\":\"Needs uncorrelated\",\"items\":[\"E[XY]=E[X]E[Y]\",\"Var(X+Y)=VarX+VarY\",\"else +2Cov term\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** $$X$$ has $$E[X] = 2$$ and $$E[X^2] = 13$$. The standard deviation of $$X$$ is:\nA) 9 B) 3 C) 11 D) 3.61\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma^2 = 13 - 2^2 = 9$$, so $$\\sigma = 3$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** If $$Y = -2X + 5$$ and $$X$$ has skewness $$+0.8$$ and kurtosis 5, then $$Y$$ has:\nA) Skewness $$+0.8$$, kurtosis 5 B) Skewness $$-0.8$$, kurtosis 5 C) Skewness $$-0.8$$, kurtosis $$-5$$ D) Skewness $$+0.8$$, kurtosis 10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A negative scale flips skewness sign; kurtosis is unaffected by any linear transformation.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which statement about a continuous random variable is TRUE?\nA) Its PDF must never exceed 1 B) $$P(X = x) = 0$$ for every point $$x$$ C) Its CDF can decrease over some intervals D) The PDF gives $$P(X \\le x)$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Point probabilities are zero for continuous variables; densities can exceed 1; CDFs are nondecreasing.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Returns exhibit kurtosis of 6. Relative to a normal distribution with the same variance, this distribution has:\nA) Thinner tails and excess kurtosis of 6 B) Fatter tails and excess kurtosis of 3 C) Fatter tails and excess kurtosis of 6 D) The same tails; kurtosis only measures asymmetry\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Excess kurtosis $$= 6 - 3 = 3 > 0$$ means fatter-than-normal tails (leptokurtic).\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A random variable $$X$$ has variance 5. The variance of $$Y = 3 + 2X$$ is:\nA) 10 B) 20 C) 23 D) 100\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\mathrm{Var}(3 + 2X) = 2^2 \\times 5 = 20$$. The additive constant does nothing (C wrong); the multiplier enters squared (A forgets the square); D squares the whole product.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A discrete random variable $$X$$ takes the value $$-6$$ with probability 0.25, $$2$$ with probability 0.50, and $$10$$ with probability 0.25. What is the variance of $$X$$?\nA) 2.0 B) 32.0 C) 36.0 D) 6.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We need $$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2$$.\n\n1. Mean: $$E[X] = (-6)(0.25) + 2(0.50) + 10(0.25) = -1.5 + 1.0 + 2.5 = 2.0$$.\n2. Second moment: $$E[X^2] = 36(0.25) + 4(0.50) + 100(0.25) = 9 + 2 + 25 = 36$$.\n3. Variance: $$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 36 - (2.0)^2 = 36 - 4 = 32$$.\n\nB is correct. A (2.0) is the mean, not the variance — answering the wrong statistic. C (36.0) is $$E[X^2]$$, forgetting to subtract $$(E[X])^2$$. D (6.0) is the standard deviation rounded ($$\\sqrt{32}=5.66$$) confused with variance, or a partial sum.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\mathrm{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2$$. The most common slip is reporting $$E[X^2]$$ as the variance — always subtract the squared mean.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Variance from moments\",\"steps\":[\"E[X]=2.0\",\"E[X²]=36\",\"Var=E[X²]−(E[X])²\",\"= 36 − 4 = 32\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Daily P&L $$X$$ (in $ thousands) has mean 1.2, standard deviation 5, skewness $$-0.6$$, and kurtosis 4.5. The desk reports results as $$Y = -2X + 3$$. Which set of statistics for $$Y$$ is correct?\nA) Mean 0.6, std dev 10, skewness $$-0.6$$, kurtosis 4.5 B) Mean 0.6, std dev 10, skewness $$+0.6$$, kurtosis 4.5 C) Mean 0.6, std dev $$-10$$, skewness $$+0.6$$, kurtosis $$-4.5$$ D) Mean $$-2.4$$, std dev 100, skewness $$+0.6$$, kurtosis 9.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Apply linear-transformation rules to $$Y = aX + b$$ with $$a=-2$$, $$b=3$$.\n\n1. Mean: $$a\\mu + b = -2(1.2) + 3 = -2.4 + 3 = 0.6$$.\n2. Standard deviation: $$|a|\\sigma = |-2|\\times 5 = 10$$ (always positive).\n3. Skewness: sign flips because $$a<0$$, so $$-(-0.6) = +0.6$$.\n4. Kurtosis: unchanged by any linear transform, so 4.5.\n\nB is correct. A keeps the skewness sign $$-0.6$$, forgetting that a negative scale flips it. C reports a *negative* standard deviation (impossible — std dev is $$|a|\\sigma$$) and negates kurtosis. D scales variance instead of std dev ($$a^2\\sigma^2=4\\times25=100$$ as \"std dev\"), forgets to add $$b$$ correctly to the mean, and doubles kurtosis.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Under $$Y=aX+b$$: std dev uses $$|a|$$ (never negative), variance uses $$a^2$$, skewness sign flips iff $$a<0$$, and kurtosis is always unchanged. The shift $$b$$ affects only the mean.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Y=aX+b with a=−2, b=3\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Affected\",\"items\":[\"mean: aμ+b = 0.6\",\"std dev: |a|σ = 10\",\"skew sign flips: +0.6\"]},{\"h\":\"Unchanged\",\"items\":[\"kurtosis: 4.5\",\"(std dev never negative)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A continuous random variable is uniformly distributed on the interval $$[3, 3.4]$$. Which statement is TRUE?\nA) Its PDF equals 1 everywhere on the interval, since probabilities cannot exceed 1 B) Its PDF equals 2.5 on the interval, and $$P(X=3.2)=0$$ C) Its PDF equals 0.4, and the probability that $$X$$ lies in $$[3, 3.4]$$ is 0.4 D) $$P(X = 3.2) = 2.5$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** For a uniform on $$[a,b]$$, the density is $$f(x) = \\frac{1}{b-a}$$, and point probabilities are zero.\n\n1. Density: $$f(x) = \\frac{1}{3.4 - 3} = \\frac{1}{0.4} = 2.5$$ on the interval.\n2. Note $$2.5 > 1$$ — perfectly valid, because density is not probability; only the *area* under the PDF must lie in $$[0,1]$$.\n3. Total area: $$2.5 \\times 0.4 = 1.0$$ (a valid distribution).\n4. For any continuous variable, $$P(X = 3.2) = 0$$.\n\nB is correct. A wrongly claims the PDF cannot exceed 1 — a density can be any nonnegative value. C confuses density with probability, calling $$f(x)=0.4$$ (it inverts the width) and claiming a probability that should be 1. D treats the density value 2.5 as a point probability — but for continuous variables $$P(X=x)=0$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A PDF value can exceed 1; only areas (probabilities) are bounded by 1. And for any continuous variable $$P(X=\\text{a single point})=0$$ — probability comes from intervals.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Uniform on [3, 3.4]\",\"steps\":[\"f(x)=1/(b−a)=1/0.4=2.5\",\"2.5 > 1 is fine (density ≠ probability)\",\"area = 2.5×0.4 = 1\",\"P(X=3.2)=0 (single point)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A bond's annual return $$X$$ (%) takes values $$-20$$ with probability 0.10, $$0$$ with probability 0.30, $$5$$ with probability 0.40, and $$15$$ with probability 0.20. What is the third central moment $$E[(X-\\mu)^3]$$ (used in the skewness numerator)?\nA) 0 B) $$-876.0$$ C) $$+345.6$$ D) $$-1{,}216.7$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** First the mean, then the third central moment.\n\n1. Mean: $$E[X] = -20(0.10) + 0(0.30) + 5(0.40) + 15(0.20) = -2 + 0 + 2 + 3 = 3.0$$.\n2. Deviations from mean: $$-23,\\ -3,\\ 2,\\ 12$$.\n3. Cubes of deviations: $$(-23)^3 = -12167$$; $$(-3)^3 = -27$$; $$(2)^3 = 8$$; $$(12)^3 = 1728$$.\n4. Probability-weight:\n$$E[(X-\\mu)^3] = (-12167)(0.10) + (-27)(0.30) + (8)(0.40) + (1728)(0.20)$$\n$$= -1216.7 - 8.1 + 3.2 + 345.6 = -876.0.$$\n\nB is correct. A (0) wrongly assumes symmetry. C ($$+345.6$$) keeps only the positive $$+15\\%$$ contribution and drops the dominant $$-20\\%$$ term. D ($$-1216.7$$) is just the first term $$(-12167)(0.10)$$, forgetting to add the other three weighted cubes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Central moments use deviations *from the mean*, $$(X-\\mu)$$, not raw $$X$$. The third moment's sign tells you the skew direction — here negative, a long left tail driven by the $$-20\\%$$ outcome.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Third central moment\",\"steps\":[\"mean μ = 3.0\",\"cube deviations (X−μ)³\",\"weight by probabilities\",\"sum = −876.0 (negative skew)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** $$X$$ has mean 4 and variance 9. Define $$Y = X^2$$. Using $$E[X^2] = \\mathrm{Var}(X) + (E[X])^2$$, what is $$E[Y]$$, and what does this illustrate about $$E[g(X)]$$ versus $$g(E[X])$$?\nA) $$E[Y] = 16$$; equal to $$(E[X])^2$$ because expectation is linear B) $$E[Y] = 25$$; greater than $$(E[X])^2 = 16$$, illustrating Jensen's inequality for a convex function C) $$E[Y] = 9$$; equal to the variance D) $$E[Y] = 25$$; less than $$(E[X])^2$$, illustrating Jensen's inequality\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Compute $$E[Y]=E[X^2]$$ and compare to $$g(E[X])=(E[X])^2$$.\n\n1. $$E[X^2] = \\mathrm{Var}(X) + (E[X])^2 = 9 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25$$.\n2. $$g(E[X]) = (E[X])^2 = 16$$.\n3. Since $$g(x)=x^2$$ is convex, Jensen's inequality gives $$E[g(X)] \\ge g(E[X])$$: $$25 \\ge 16$$. The gap (9) equals the variance.\n\nB is correct. A applies linearity to a nonlinear function — wrong; $$E[X^2]\\ne(E[X])^2$$ unless variance is zero. C reports the variance as $$E[Y]$$, confusing $$E[X^2]$$ with $$\\mathrm{Var}(X)$$. D gets the value 25 right but inverts Jensen's direction: for a convex $$g$$, $$E[g(X)]$$ is *greater*, not less.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expectation is linear only for linear functions. For convex $$g$$ (like $$x^2$$), $$E[g(X)]\\ge g(E[X])$$ by Jensen — the gap grows with variance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Jensen: convex g(x)=x²\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"E[g(X)]\",\"items\":[\"E[X²]=Var+μ²\",\"= 9 + 16 = 25\"]},{\"h\":\"g(E[X])\",\"items\":[\"(E[X])²\",\"= 16\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A loss distribution has these quantiles: $$Q(0.25) = 2$$, $$Q(0.50) = 5$$, $$Q(0.75) = 11$$ ($ millions). A risk team rescales losses to a new currency at rate 1.5 and adds a fixed reserve of 4: $$Y = 1.5X + 4$$. What are the median and interquartile range (IQR) of $$Y$$?\nA) Median 11.5; IQR 13.5 B) Median 11.5; IQR 9.0 C) Median 7.5; IQR 9.0 D) Median 11.5; IQR 17.5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Apply transformation rules to quantiles. For $$a>0$$: quantiles map as $$aQ+b$$; the IQR scales by $$|a|$$ and the additive constant $$b$$ cancels.\n\n1. Median of $$Y$$ (a location measure, carries the +4): $$1.5 \\times Q(0.50) + 4 = 1.5(5) + 4 = 7.5 + 4 = 11.5$$.\n2. Original IQR: $$Q(0.75) - Q(0.25) = 11 - 2 = 9$$.\n3. IQR of $$Y$$ (a spread measure, +4 cancels): $$|a| \\times \\text{IQR} = 1.5 \\times 9 = 13.5$$.\n\nA is correct: median 11.5, IQR 13.5. B keeps the IQR unscaled at 9, forgetting the multiplicative factor of 1.5 (the trap of treating IQR like a count). C drops the +4 from the median (reporting only $$1.5\\times5=7.5$$) and leaves the IQR unscaled. D adds the +4 to the IQR as well ($$1.5\\times9+4=17.5$$) — but additive constants never affect spread.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Additive constants shift location measures (mean, median, quantiles) but NEVER affect spread measures (variance, std dev, IQR). Multiplicative factors scale both — by $$|a|$$ for spreads.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Y=1.5X+4 on quantiles\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Median (location)\",\"items\":[\"1.5×5 + 4\",\"carries the +4\",\"= 11.5\"]},{\"h\":\"IQR (spread)\",\"items\":[\"1.5×9\",\"+4 cancels\",\"= 13.5\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Monthly returns of a strategy have skewness $$-1.2$$ and excess kurtosis $$+5$$. An analyst describes the return distribution. Which description is most accurate?\nA) Symmetric with thin tails; large gains and large losses are equally rare and rarer than normal B) A long left tail (frequent small gains, occasional large losses) with fatter-than-normal tails overall (kurtosis 8) C) A long right tail with thin tails; kurtosis of 5 D) Negative skew implies the mean exceeds the median, and excess kurtosis of 5 means kurtosis of 2\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Interpret the moments.\n\n1. Skewness $$-1.2 < 0$$: negatively skewed — a long *left* tail. Typical pattern: many small positive outcomes, rare large negative outcomes (the risk-management cocktail).\n2. Excess kurtosis $$= \\text{kurtosis} - 3$$. Given excess kurtosis $$+5$$, total kurtosis $$= 5 + 3 = 8 > 3$$ — leptokurtic, fatter tails than normal.\n\nB is correct (long left tail, total kurtosis 8). A claims symmetry and thin tails — both contradicted by the signs. C says right tail (wrong sign) and thin tails (wrong). D mislabels: negative skew means the mean is typically *below* the median (left tail pulls the mean down), and \"kurtosis of 2\" wrongly subtracts 3 from excess kurtosis instead of adding it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Excess kurtosis = kurtosis − 3, so total kurtosis = excess + 3. Negative skew = long *left* tail, mean typically below median. Fat tails ⇔ kurtosis > 3 (excess > 0).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Skew −1.2, excess kurtosis +5\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Skewness −1.2\",\"items\":[\"long LEFT tail\",\"small gains, rare big losses\",\"mean below median\"]},{\"h\":\"Excess kurt +5\",\"items\":[\"total kurtosis = 8\",\"fatter than normal (leptokurtic)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"203","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Common Univariate Random Variables","notes":"# 3. Common Univariate Random Variables\n\nGARP expects you to know the catalog: when each distribution applies, its mean and variance, and how the testing distributions (chi-square, t, F) are built from normals. Binomial, Poisson, and normal probability calculations are routine exam questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Distinguish key properties and identify common occurrences of the following: uniform, Bernoulli, binomial, Poisson, normal, lognormal, chi-squared, Student's t, and F-distributions.\n- Describe a mixture distribution and explain the creation and characteristics of mixture distributions.\n\n## Master table\n\n| Distribution | Parameters | Mean | Variance | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bernoulli | $$p$$ | $$p$$ | $$p(1-p)$$ | Single default / no default |\n| Binomial | $$n, p$$ | $$np$$ | $$np(1-p)$$ | Defaults in a portfolio of $$n$$ independent credits |\n| Poisson | $$\\lambda$$ | $$\\lambda$$ | $$\\lambda$$ | Operational-loss event counts per period |\n| Continuous uniform | $$a, b$$ | $$\\frac{a+b}{2}$$ | $$\\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}$$ | Random number generation, priors |\n| Normal | $$\\mu, \\sigma^2$$ | $$\\mu$$ | $$\\sigma^2$$ | Returns (approximation), test statistics |\n| Lognormal | $$\\mu, \\sigma^2$$ of $$\\ln X$$ | $$e^{\\mu + \\sigma^2/2}$$ | $$(e^{\\sigma^2}-1)e^{2\\mu+\\sigma^2}$$ | Asset *prices* (always positive) |\n| Chi-squared | $$k$$ d.o.f. | $$k$$ | $$2k$$ | Variance tests; sum of $$k$$ squared standard normals |\n| Student's t | $$\\nu$$ d.o.f. | $$0$$ ($$\\nu>1$$) | $$\\frac{\\nu}{\\nu-2}$$ ($$\\nu>2$$) | Means with unknown variance; fat-tailed returns |\n| F | $$\\nu_1, \\nu_2$$ | $$\\frac{\\nu_2}{\\nu_2-2}$$ ($$\\nu_2>2$$) | — | Ratio of variances; regression overall fit |\n\n## Discrete distributions\n\n**Bernoulli($$p$$):** $$X \\in \\{0, 1\\}$$ with $$P(X=1)=p$$. Building block for default modeling.\n\n**Binomial($$n, p$$):** number of successes in $$n$$ independent Bernoulli trials:\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\n**Poisson($$\\lambda$$):** count of events in an interval when events arrive independently at constant rate:\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\frac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!}$$\n\nMean = variance = $$\\lambda$$ — a signature fact. Poisson approximates binomial when $$n$$ is large and $$p$$ small ($$\\lambda = np$$). Poisson counts over disjoint periods add: events per month $$\\lambda$$ implies events per year $$12\\lambda$$.\n\n## Continuous distributions\n\n**Uniform($$a,b$$):** $$f(x) = \\frac{1}{b-a}$$ on $$[a,b]$$.\n\n**Normal($$\\mu, \\sigma^2$$):** symmetric, skew 0, kurtosis 3, fully described by two parameters; any linear combination of jointly normal variables is normal. Standardize with\n\n$$z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}$$\n\nMemorize: 68% of mass within $$\\pm 1\\sigma$$, 90% within $$\\pm 1.645\\sigma$$, 95% within $$\\pm 1.96\\sigma$$, 99% within $$\\pm 2.576\\sigma$$. One-tailed: 5% beyond 1.645, 1% beyond 2.326.\n\n**Lognormal:** $$X$$ is lognormal if $$\\ln X$$ is normal. Bounded below by zero and right-skewed — the standard model for prices when log returns are normal.\n\n**Chi-squared($$k$$):** $$\\sum_{i=1}^k Z_i^2$$ for independent standard normals. Right-skewed, positive support; skew shrinks as $$k$$ grows. Used to test a single variance: $$(n-1)s^2/\\sigma_0^2 \\sim \\chi^2_{n-1}$$.\n\n**Student's t($$\\nu$$):** $$t = \\frac{Z}{\\sqrt{W/\\nu}}$$ with $$Z$$ standard normal, $$W \\sim \\chi^2_\\nu$$. Symmetric, fatter tails than normal; converges to the normal as $$\\nu \\to \\infty$$. Variance $$\\frac{\\nu}{\\nu-2}$$ exceeds 1.\n\n**F($$\\nu_1, \\nu_2$$):** ratio of two independent chi-squareds each divided by its d.o.f. Right-skewed, positive. Note $$t_\\nu^2 = F_{1,\\nu}$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Returns ≈ normal, prices ≈ lognormal. If the question says \"log returns are normally distributed,\" the *price* is lognormal and can never go below zero. Mixing these up is the chapter's classic trap. Second trap: the t-distribution has *fatter* tails than the normal, so t critical values are *larger* — confidence intervals widen, they don't shrink.\n\n## Mixture distributions\n\nA mixture draws from one of several component distributions, chosen at random with mixing weights $$w_i$$ ($$\\sum w_i = 1$$). Its PDF is the weighted average of component PDFs:\n\n$$f(x) = \\sum_i w_i f_i(x)$$\n\nThe mixture's mean is the weighted mean of component means. Even a mixture of two normals with equal means but different variances produces **excess kurtosis** (fat tails); different means can produce skewness or bimodality. Mixtures are a simple way to model regimes — e.g., a \"calm\" normal 95% of the time and a \"crisis\" normal 5% of the time.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> The variance of a mixture is *not* simply the weighted average of variances unless all component means are equal: $$\\sigma^2 = \\sum_i w_i(\\sigma_i^2 + \\mu_i^2) - \\mu^2$$.\n\n### Worked example: binomial default count\n\nA portfolio holds 10 independent loans, each with default probability 4%. Probability of exactly 2 defaults in a year:\n\n$$P(X=2) = \\binom{10}{2}(0.04)^2(0.96)^8 = 45 \\times 0.0016 \\times 0.7214 = 0.0519 \\approx 5.2\\%$$\n\nMean defaults: $$np = 0.4$$. Variance: $$np(1-p) = 10 \\times 0.04 \\times 0.96 = 0.384$$.\n\nProbability of at least one default: $$1 - 0.96^{10} = 1 - 0.6648 = 0.3352$$.\n\n### Worked example: Poisson operational losses\n\nSystem outages follow a Poisson process averaging $$\\lambda = 2$$ per quarter.\n\n$$P(X = 0) = e^{-2} = 0.1353, \\qquad P(X = 3) = \\frac{2^3 e^{-2}}{3!} = \\frac{8 \\times 0.1353}{6} = 0.1804$$\n\n$$P(X \\ge 2) = 1 - P(0) - P(1) = 1 - 0.1353 - 2(0.1353) = 1 - 0.4060 = 0.5940$$\n\nAnnual outages are Poisson with $$\\lambda = 8$$, so mean 8 and variance 8.\n\n### Worked example: normal probability\n\nMonthly returns are $$N(1\\%, 5\\%^2)$$. Probability of a return below $$-7.25\\%$$:\n\n$$z = \\frac{-7.25 - 1}{5} = -1.65 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P \\approx 0.05 = 5\\%$$\n\nThe return $$-7.25\\%$$ is the 5% quantile — i.e., the monthly 95% VaR is 7.25% of portfolio value.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Give the mean and variance of a Bernoulli($$p$$) and a Binomial($$n,p$$).\n> **A:** Bernoulli: mean $$p$$, variance $$p(1-p)$$. Binomial: mean $$np$$, variance $$np(1-p)$$.\n\n**Q2.** State the binomial probability formula.\n> **A:** $$P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\n**Q3.** State the Poisson PMF and its signature moment fact.\n> **A:** $$P(X = k) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!}$$; mean $$=$$ variance $$= \\lambda$$. Counts over disjoint periods add (monthly $$\\lambda$$ → annual $$12\\lambda$$).\n\n**Q4.** Give the mean and variance of a continuous Uniform($$a,b$$).\n> **A:** Mean $$= \\dfrac{a+b}{2}$$; variance $$= \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}$$.\n\n**Q5.** Memorize the normal tail cutoffs: two-tailed 95%, 99%; one-tailed 5%, 1%.\n> **A:** Two-tailed: $$\\pm 1.96$$ (95%), $$\\pm 2.576$$ (99%). One-tailed: 1.645 beyond 5%, 2.326 beyond 1%. Also $$\\pm 1.645$$ holds 90%.\n\n**Q6.** Returns are normal but prices are what, and why?\n> **A:** Lognormal: if $$\\ln X$$ is normal, $$X$$ is lognormal — bounded below by zero and right-skewed, so prices can never go negative. Mean $$= e^{\\mu + \\sigma^2/2}$$.\n\n**Q7.** How is the chi-squared distribution constructed, and what are its moments?\n> **A:** $$\\chi^2_k = \\sum_{i=1}^k Z_i^2$$ of independent standard normals; mean $$= k$$, variance $$= 2k$$; right-skewed, positive support.\n\n**Q8.** How is Student's t built, and how do its tails and critical values compare to the normal?\n> **A:** $$t = \\dfrac{Z}{\\sqrt{W/\\nu}}$$ with $$W \\sim \\chi^2_\\nu$$. Fatter tails than normal, so t critical values are larger (intervals widen); variance $$= \\dfrac{\\nu}{\\nu-2} > 1$$; converges to normal as $$\\nu \\to \\infty$$.\n\n**Q9.** How is the F-distribution defined, and how does it relate to t?\n> **A:** Ratio of two independent chi-squareds each divided by its d.o.f.; right-skewed, positive. Note $$t_\\nu^2 = F_{1,\\nu}$$.\n\n**Q10.** When does the Poisson approximate the binomial?\n> **A:** When $$n$$ is large and $$p$$ small, with $$\\lambda = np$$.\n\n**Q11.** What is a mixture distribution and its PDF?\n> **A:** Draws from one of several components with weights $$w_i$$ ($$\\sum w_i = 1$$): $$f(x) = \\sum_i w_i f_i(x)$$; mean is the weighted mean of component means.\n\n**Q12.** What shape feature does a mixture of two normals with equal means but different variances produce?\n> **A:** Excess kurtosis (fat tails) — symmetric but leptokurtic. Different means add skewness or bimodality.\n\n**Q13.** A portfolio holds 10 independent loans each with 4% default probability. Probability of at least one default?\n> **A:** $$1 - 0.96^{10} = 1 - 0.6648 = 0.3352$$\n\n**Q14.** Outages are Poisson with $$\\lambda = 2$$ per quarter. Find $$P(X = 0)$$.\n> **A:** $$P(X = 0) = e^{-2} = 0.1353$$\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Operational events arrive at a Poisson rate of 1.5 per month. The probability of exactly 2 events next month is closest to:\nA) 18.7% B) 22.3% C) 25.1% D) 33.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the chance of exactly 2 events when events arrive randomly at an average rate $$\\lambda = 1.5$$ per month — a Poisson calculation.\n\nThe Poisson probability formula is:\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\frac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!}$$\n\nPlug in $$\\lambda = 1.5$$, $$k = 2$$:\n\n$$P(X = 2) = \\frac{1.5^2 \\, e^{-1.5}}{2!} = \\frac{2.25 \\times e^{-1.5}}{2}$$\n\nNow $$e^{-1.5} \\approx 0.2231$$, so:\n\n$$P(X = 2) = \\frac{2.25 \\times 0.2231}{2} = \\frac{0.5020}{2} = 0.2510 \\approx 25.1\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is 25.1% (option C).\n\n- A (18.7%) uses the wrong $$\\lambda$$ or $$k$$ (e.g., computing for $$k=3$$ at $$\\lambda=2$$).\n- B (22.3%) is roughly $$e^{-1.5} = 0.2231$$, i.e., $$P(X=0)$$, not $$P(X=2)$$.\n- D (33.5%) is roughly $$P(X=1) = 1.5 \\times e^{-1.5} = 0.3347$$, off by one count.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For Poisson, plug carefully into $$\\frac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!}$$. Remember $$2! = 2$$ and $$3! = 6$$. The mean and variance of a Poisson are both $$\\lambda$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which distribution is bounded below by zero and right-skewed, and is the standard model for asset prices?\nA) Normal B) Student's t C) Lognormal D) Uniform\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which distribution is used for prices (which can never be negative).\n\nA variable is lognormal if its natural log is normally distributed. Because exponentiating a normal always gives a positive number, the lognormal lives strictly above zero and is right-skewed (a long tail to the high side). This matches asset prices, which can't go negative but can in principle rise without bound. So option C is correct.\n\nThe standard finance story: if *log returns* are normal, then the *price* is lognormal.\n\n- A (Normal) is symmetric and allows negative values, so it can't model a strictly positive price.\n- B (Student's t) is symmetric (centered at 0) and allows negatives; it's used for fat-tailed returns and test statistics.\n- D (Uniform) is flat over a fixed interval, not right-skewed, and not used for prices.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Returns ≈ normal; prices ≈ lognormal. If \"log returns are normal,\" the price is lognormal and never negative. This swap is a classic trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Bounded ≥0, right-skewed, models prices\",\"options\":[\"Normal (symmetric, allows <0)\",\"Student's t (symmetric)\",\"Lognormal (>0, right-skew)\",\"Uniform (flat)\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Lognormal lives strictly above zero and is right-skewed — the standard model for prices when log returns are normal.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The sum of the squares of 12 independent standard normal variables follows which distribution, and with what mean and variance?\nA) $$N(0, 12)$$; mean 0, variance 12 B) $$\\chi^2_{12}$$; mean 12, variance 24 C) $$t_{12}$$; mean 0, variance 1.2 D) $$F_{12,12}$$; mean 1, variance undefined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes summing squared standard normal variables.\n\nBy definition, the sum of $$k$$ independent squared standard normals is a chi-squared distribution with $$k$$ degrees of freedom:\n\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^{k} Z_i^2 \\sim \\chi^2_k$$\n\nHere $$k = 12$$. A chi-squared with $$k$$ degrees of freedom has:\n- Mean $$= k = 12$$\n- Variance $$= 2k = 2 \\times 12 = 24$$\n\nSo option B is correct.\n\n- A treats the sum as a normal; squaring and summing destroys the normal shape (the result is positive and right-skewed).\n- C names the t-distribution, which is built as a normal divided by a scaled chi-squared root — not a sum of squares.\n- D names the F-distribution, which is a *ratio* of two chi-squareds, not a single sum of squares.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Chi-squared = sum of $$k$$ squared standard normals; mean $$k$$, variance $$2k$$. The t and F are *built from* normals and chi-squareds but are not the same thing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Distributions built from normals\",\"root\":\"Standard normals Z\",\"children\":[\"χ² = ΣZ² (mean k, var 2k)\",\"t = Z/√(χ²/ν)\",\"F = ratio of two χ²\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A portfolio holds 10 independent loans, each with default probability 4%. What is the expected number of defaults in a year?\nA) 0.04 B) 0.40 C) 0.96 D) 4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the mean number of defaults, where each loan defaults independently with probability $$p = 0.04$$ — a binomial setup with $$n = 10$$.\n\nThe mean (expected value) of a binomial is:\n\n$$E[X] = n \\times p = 10 \\times 0.04 = 0.40$$\n\nSo we expect 0.40 defaults on average per year (option B). It's fine for an expected value to be a fraction — it's a long-run average across many years.\n\n- A (0.04) is just $$p$$, forgetting to multiply by the number of loans $$n = 10$$.\n- C (0.96) is $$1 - p$$ (the survival probability of one loan), unrelated to the expected count.\n- D (4.00) divides incorrectly or treats $$p$$ as 40%; with $$p = 4\\%$$ the expected count is 0.40, not 4.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Binomial mean = $$np$$; variance = $$np(1-p)$$. Make sure you convert the percentage (4% = 0.04) before multiplying.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Binomial moments\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Mean\",\"items\":[\"np\",\"10×0.04\",\"= 0.40\"]},{\"h\":\"Variance\",\"items\":[\"np(1−p)\",\"10×0.04×0.96\",\"= 0.384\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Monthly returns are normally distributed with mean 1% and standard deviation 5%. What is the probability of a return below $$-7.25\\%$$? (Use: 5% of a normal lies below $$z = -1.645$$.)\nA) About 1% B) About 2.5% C) About 5% D) About 10%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for a left-tail probability for a normal variable. First convert the cutoff into a z-score (how many standard deviations below the mean it is).\n\n$$z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma} = \\frac{-7.25 - 1}{5} = \\frac{-8.25}{5} = -1.65$$\n\nA z-score of about $$-1.645$$ marks the 5% left tail of a normal distribution (a memorized value). Since $$-1.65 \\approx -1.645$$:\n\n$$P(X < -7.25\\%) \\approx 5\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is option C. This is exactly the kind of calculation behind a 95% Value at Risk: the $$-7.25\\%$$ return is the 5% quantile.\n\n- A (1%) corresponds to $$z \\approx -2.326$$, a deeper tail than we have.\n- B (2.5%) corresponds to $$z \\approx -1.96$$, again deeper than $$-1.65$$.\n- D (10%) corresponds to $$z \\approx -1.28$$, a shallower tail.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the one-tailed z-values: 1.645 → 5%, 1.96 → 2.5%, 2.326 → 1%. Standardize with $$z = (x-\\mu)/\\sigma$$, then read the tail.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"One-tailed z critical values\",\"items\":[\"z=1.28 → 10%\",\"z=1.645 → 5%\",\"z=1.96 → 2.5%\",\"z=2.326 → 1%\"],\"left\":\"shallower tail\",\"right\":\"deeper tail\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** System outages follow a Poisson process averaging $$\\lambda = 2$$ per quarter. If outages are independent across quarters, what is the average number of outages per year?\nA) 2 B) 4 C) 8 D) 16\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks us to scale a Poisson rate from a quarter to a full year.\n\nA key Poisson property: counts over disjoint (non-overlapping) periods add, and the rate scales with the length of the interval. A year contains 4 quarters, so:\n\n$$\\lambda_{\\text{year}} = 4 \\times \\lambda_{\\text{quarter}} = 4 \\times 2 = 8$$\n\nSo we expect 8 outages per year on average (option C). The annual count is also Poisson, now with $$\\lambda = 8$$ (mean 8 and variance 8).\n\n- A (2) forgets to scale and just reports the quarterly rate.\n- B (4) scales by the wrong factor (e.g., treats a year as 2 quarters or doubles instead of quadrupling).\n- D (16) confuses the rate with the variance times 2, or squares/over-scales the factor.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Poisson rates are additive over time: multiply $$\\lambda$$ by the number of equal sub-periods. 4 quarters per year → multiply by 4.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Scaling a Poisson rate\",\"steps\":[\"λ = 2 per quarter\",\"× 4 quarters per year\",\"λ_year = 8\",\"annual count ~ Poisson(8)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A Student's t-distribution, compared to a standard normal distribution, has:\nA) Thinner tails, so its critical values are smaller. B) Fatter tails, so its critical values are larger. C) Identical tails, differing only in mean. D) Fatter tails, but smaller critical values.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question compares the t-distribution to the normal.\n\nThe Student's t-distribution is symmetric like the normal but has *fatter tails* — more probability sits out in the extremes. Because more mass is in the tails, you must go *further* from the center to capture a given confidence level. That means t critical values are *larger* than the corresponding normal (z) values. So option B is correct. As degrees of freedom grow, the t converges to the normal.\n\nPractical effect: confidence intervals built with the t are *wider* than those built with the normal.\n\n- A is backwards on both counts: t tails are fatter, not thinner, and its critical values are larger.\n- C is false: the tails differ (that's the whole point); both have mean 0.\n- D correctly notes fatter tails but then wrongly says smaller critical values. Fatter tails force *larger* critical values.\n\n📌 Exam tip: t = fatter tails = larger critical values = wider confidence intervals. The classic trap claims t intervals are narrower; they are wider.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Student's t vs standard normal\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Student's t\",\"items\":[\"fatter tails\",\"larger critical values\",\"wider CIs\",\"→ normal as df↑\"]},{\"h\":\"Normal (z)\",\"items\":[\"thinner tails\",\"smaller critical values\",\"narrower CIs\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A 50/50 mixture of $$N(0, 1)$$ and $$N(0, 9)$$, compared to a single normal with the same overall variance, has:\nA) Negative skew B) Excess kurtosis (fatter tails) C) Kurtosis below 3 D) An identical shape\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the shape of a mixture of two normals that share the same mean (0) but differ in variance (1 vs 9).\n\nA mixture randomly draws from one component or the other. With equal means but different variances, the result is symmetric (so skew is 0), but it gains *excess kurtosis*: extra probability piles up both in the center (from the low-variance component) and in the far tails (from the high-variance component). Compared to a single normal with the same total variance, the mixture is leptokurtic — fatter tails. So option B is correct.\n\nIntuition: most days are calm (small variance), but occasionally you draw from the wild high-variance regime, producing rare extreme values — that's a fat tail.\n\n- A (negative skew) requires the components to have *different means*; here both means are 0, so the mixture stays symmetric.\n- C (kurtosis below 3) is the opposite of what happens; variance mixtures increase kurtosis above 3.\n- D (identical shape) is wrong — the mixture is more peaked and fatter-tailed than a single normal.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A mixture of normals with equal means but different variances → symmetric but fat-tailed (excess kurtosis). Different means can add skew or bimodality.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Variance mixture vs single normal\",\"items\":[\"|single normal (k=3)\",\"variance mixture (k>3, fat tails)\"],\"left\":\"baseline tails\",\"right\":\"more extremes (leptokurtic)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A portfolio of 10 independent loans each defaults with probability 4%. What is the variance of the number of defaults?\nA) 0.40 B) 0.384 C) 0.96 D) 4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the variance of a binomial count with $$n = 10$$ and $$p = 0.04$$.\n\nThe variance of a binomial is:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = n \\, p \\, (1 - p)$$\n\nPlug in:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 10 \\times 0.04 \\times (1 - 0.04) = 10 \\times 0.04 \\times 0.96$$\n\n$$= 0.40 \\times 0.96 = 0.384$$\n\nSo the variance is 0.384 (option B).\n\n- A (0.40) is the *mean* $$np$$, forgetting the $$(1-p)$$ factor.\n- C (0.96) is just $$(1-p)$$, ignoring $$n$$ and $$p$$.\n- D (4.00) misreads $$p$$ as 40% or mis-scales by a factor of 10.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Binomial: mean $$= np$$, variance $$= np(1-p)$$. Don't stop at the mean — the variance needs the extra $$(1-p)$$ factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Events arrive at a Poisson rate of $$\\lambda = 2$$ per quarter. What is the probability of zero events in a quarter? (Use $$e^{-2} \\approx 0.1353$$.)\nA) 0.00% B) 13.5% C) 27.1% D) 50.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the probability of seeing no events when the average is $$\\lambda = 2$$ per quarter.\n\nUse the Poisson formula with $$k = 0$$:\n\n$$P(X = 0) = \\frac{\\lambda^0 e^{-\\lambda}}{0!} = \\frac{1 \\times e^{-2}}{1} = e^{-2}$$\n\nRecall that anything to the power 0 is 1, and $$0! = 1$$. So:\n\n$$P(X = 0) = e^{-2} \\approx 0.1353 = 13.5\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is 13.5% (option B). Even with an average of 2 events, there's a meaningful chance of a quiet quarter with none.\n\n- A (0.00%) wrongly assumes zero events is impossible — it isn't.\n- C (27.1%) is $$2 \\times e^{-2} = 0.2707$$, which is actually $$P(X = 1)$$, not $$P(X = 0)$$.\n- D (50.0%) is a guess unrelated to the formula.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For Poisson, $$P(X=0) = e^{-\\lambda}$$ always (because $$\\lambda^0 = 1$$ and $$0! = 1$$). Keep $$e^{-\\lambda}$$ handy — it anchors many Poisson tail calculations.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Operational events arrive at a Poisson rate of 1.5 per month. The probability of exactly 2 events next month is closest to:\nA) 22.3% B) 25.1% C) 33.5% D) 18.7%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P(X=2) = \\frac{1.5^2 e^{-1.5}}{2!} = \\frac{2.25 \\times 0.2231}{2} = 0.2510$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which distribution is bounded below by zero and right-skewed, and is the standard model for asset prices?\nA) Normal B) Student's t C) Lognormal D) Uniform\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Lognormal: positive support, right skew, normal log returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The sum of the squares of 12 independent standard normal variables follows:\nA) $$N(0, 12)$$ B) $$\\chi^2_{12}$$ with mean 12 and variance 24 C) $$t_{12}$$ D) $$F_{12,12}$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Definition of chi-squared with $$k=12$$; mean $$k = 12$$, variance $$2k = 24$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A 50/50 mixture of $$N(0, 1)$$ and $$N(0, 9)$$ has, relative to a single normal with the same overall variance:\nA) Negative skew B) Excess kurtosis (fatter tails) C) Kurtosis below 3 D) Identical shape\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Variance mixtures with common mean are symmetric but leptokurtic — extra mass in the center and tails.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A portfolio contains 10 independent loans, each with a 10% probability of default over one year. The probability that exactly 2 loans default is closest to:\nA) 0.4% B) 19.4% C) 38.7% D) 93.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Binomial with $$n = 10$$, $$p = 0.1$$:\n\n$$P(X=2) = \\binom{10}{2}(0.1)^2(0.9)^8 = 45 \\times 0.01 \\times 0.4305 = 0.1937 \\approx 19.4\\%$$\n\nDistractor A omits the combination factor; C is $$P(X=1) = 38.7\\%$$; D is the cumulative $$P(X \\le 2) = 93.0\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Operational risk events at a desk arrive at a Poisson rate of 2 per month. The probability of exactly 3 events next month is closest to:\nA) 13.5% B) 18.0% C) 27.1% D) 85.7%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Poisson with $$\\lambda = 2$$:\n\n$$P(X = 3) = \\frac{e^{-2}\\,2^3}{3!} = 0.1353 \\times \\frac{8}{6} = 0.1804 \\approx 18.0\\%$$\n\nDistractor A is $$P(X=0) = e^{-2}$$; C is $$P(X=2) = 27.1\\%$$; D is the cumulative $$P(X \\le 3)$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A portfolio holds 20 independent loans, each with a 5% annual default probability. What is the probability of exactly 2 defaults during the year?\nA) 18.9% B) 37.7% C) 26.4% D) 9.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** This is a binomial calculation with $$n=20$$, $$p=0.05$$, $$k=2$$.\n\n1. Formula: $$P(X=k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$.\n2. Combinations: $$\\binom{20}{2} = \\frac{20 \\times 19}{2} = 190$$.\n3. $$p^k = (0.05)^2 = 0.0025$$.\n4. $$(1-p)^{n-k} = (0.95)^{18}$$. Compute: $$\\ln(0.95) = -0.051293$$; $$\\times 18 = -0.92327$$; $$e^{-0.92327} = 0.3972$$.\n5. Multiply: $$190 \\times 0.0025 \\times 0.3972 = 0.475 \\times 0.3972 = 0.1887 \\approx 18.9\\%$$.\n\nA is correct (18.9%). B (37.7%) is the probability of exactly *one* default, $$\\binom{20}{1}(0.05)(0.95)^{19} = 20\\times0.05\\times0.3774 = 0.377$$ — the wrong $$k$$. C (26.4%) is the probability of *at least* 2 defaults, $$1 - P(0) - P(1)$$ — the wrong event. D (9.2%) uses $$(0.95)^{20}$$ in place of $$(0.95)^{18}$$ and mishandles the exponent.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Binomial needs all three pieces — the combination $$\\binom{n}{k}$$, $$p^k$$, and $$(1-p)^{n-k}$$, with the exponent on the failure term equal to $$n-k$$ (here 18, not 20). Forgetting the combination or miscounting the exponent are the classic errors.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Binomial P(X=2), n=20, p=0.05\",\"steps\":[\"C(20,2)=190\",\"p^k=(0.05)²=0.0025\",\"(0.95)^18=0.3972\",\"190×0.0025×0.3972=18.9%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Operational-loss events arrive as a Poisson process with rate $$\\lambda = 3$$ per quarter. What is the probability of observing 5 or more events in a single quarter?\nA) 18.5% B) 81.5% C) 10.1% D) 91.6%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We need $$P(X \\ge 5) = 1 - P(X \\le 4)$$ for Poisson($$\\lambda=3$$). Use $$P(X=k)=\\frac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!}$$ with $$e^{-3}=0.049787$$.\n\n1. $$P(0) = e^{-3} = 0.049787$$.\n2. $$P(1) = 3 e^{-3} = 0.149361$$.\n3. $$P(2) = \\frac{9}{2}e^{-3} = 4.5 \\times 0.049787 = 0.224042$$.\n4. $$P(3) = \\frac{27}{6}e^{-3} = 4.5 \\times 0.049787 = 0.224042$$.\n5. $$P(4) = \\frac{81}{24}e^{-3} = 3.375 \\times 0.049787 = 0.168031$$.\n6. Sum $$P(X\\le 4) = 0.049787 + 0.149361 + 0.224042 + 0.224042 + 0.168031 = 0.815263$$.\n7. $$P(X\\ge 5) = 1 - 0.815263 = 0.184737 \\approx 18.5\\%$$.\n\nA is correct. B (81.5%) is $$P(X\\le 4)$$, the complement — answering \"4 or fewer\" instead of \"5 or more.\" C (10.1%) computes $$P(X\\ge 5)$$ but stops the cumulative sum at $$P(X\\le 3)$$, omitting $$P(4)$$. D (91.6%) is $$1-P(0)-P(1)$$ = $$P(X\\ge 2)$$, the wrong threshold.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"$$\\ge 5$$\" means subtract the cumulative through 4 ($$0,1,2,3,4$$) from 1. Count the terms carefully — an off-by-one in the cumulative sum is the classic Poisson trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Poisson P(X≥5), λ=3\",\"steps\":[\"sum P(0)…P(4)\",\"= 0.8153\",\"P(X≥5)=1−0.8153\",\"= 18.5%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Daily log returns of a stock are normally distributed with mean 0.05% and standard deviation 1.8%. Assuming the price today is $100, which statement is correct?\nA) Tomorrow's price is normally distributed, so it could be negative B) Tomorrow's price is lognormally distributed and is bounded below by zero C) Tomorrow's log return is lognormal with a fat right tail D) The price has the same standard deviation, 1.8%, as the log return\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Apply the returns-vs-prices relationship: if log returns are normal, the *price* is lognormal.\n\n1. By definition, $$X$$ is lognormal when $$\\ln X$$ is normal. Here $$\\ln(\\text{price ratio})$$ = log return is normal, so the price ratio (and hence price) is lognormal.\n2. A lognormal variable has support $$(0, \\infty)$$ — it can never be negative, which matches the economic reality that prices cannot go below zero.\n\nB is correct. A confuses the *log return* (normal) with the *price* (lognormal); a normal price could go negative, which is exactly why prices are modeled as lognormal, not normal. C reverses the relationship — the log return is normal (symmetric), not lognormal. D wrongly equates the price's standard deviation with the return's — different units and scales entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Log returns normal\" ⇒ \"prices lognormal.\" Normal log return is symmetric; the lognormal price is right-skewed and strictly positive. Never let the price be normally distributed in these questions.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Returns vs prices\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Log return\",\"items\":[\"normal\",\"symmetric\",\"can be negative\"]},{\"h\":\"Price\",\"items\":[\"lognormal\",\"right-skewed\",\"bounded below by 0\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A trading book has 200 independent positions, each with a 1.5% probability of a limit breach during the day. Using the Poisson approximation to the binomial, the probability of observing exactly 3 breaches is closest to:\nA) 22.4% B) 14.9% C) 5.0% D) 16.8%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** When $$n$$ is large and $$p$$ small, binomial $$\\approx$$ Poisson with $$\\lambda = np$$.\n\n1. $$\\lambda = np = 200 \\times 0.015 = 3.0$$.\n2. $$P(X=3) = \\frac{\\lambda^3 e^{-\\lambda}}{3!} = \\frac{3^3 e^{-3}}{6} = \\frac{27 \\times 0.049787}{6}$$.\n3. Numerator: $$27 \\times 0.049787 = 1.344249$$.\n4. Divide: $$1.344249 / 6 = 0.224042 \\approx 22.4\\%$$.\n\nA is correct. B (14.9%) is $$P(X=1)=\\lambda e^{-\\lambda}=3(0.049787)=0.1494$$ — wrong $$k$$. C (5.0%) is roughly $$e^{-3}=P(X=0)$$. D (16.8%) is $$P(X=4)=\\frac{81}{24}e^{-3}=0.168$$ — off by one in $$k$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For large $$n$$, small $$p$$, set $$\\lambda=np$$ and use Poisson. Then double-check you used the $$k$$ the question asks for — adjacent $$k$$ values are planted as distractors.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Poisson approx to binomial\",\"steps\":[\"large n, small p → λ=np\",\"λ = 200×0.015 = 3.0\",\"P(X=3)=λ³e^−λ/3!\",\"= 22.4%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A risk model treats monthly returns as a mixture: 90% of months are \"calm\" with distribution $$N(1\\%, (2\\%)^2)$$ and 10% are \"stressed\" with distribution $$N(-3\\%, (8\\%)^2)$$. What is the mean of the mixture's monthly return?\nA) 1.00% B) 0.60% C) $$-1.00\\%$$ D) 0.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The mean of a mixture is the weighted average of the component means.\n\n1. Weights: calm 0.90, stressed 0.10.\n2. Component means: calm $$+1\\%$$, stressed $$-3\\%$$.\n3. Mixture mean: $$0.90 \\times 1\\% + 0.10 \\times (-3\\%) = 0.90\\% - 0.30\\% = 0.60\\%$$.\n\nB is correct. A (1.00%) ignores the stressed component entirely, using only the calm mean. C ($$-1.00\\%$$) averages the two means with equal weights, $$\\frac{1 + (-3)}{2}=-1$$, ignoring the 90/10 weighting. D (0.40%) miscomputes the weighted sum (e.g., $$0.90\\times1 - 0.50\\times... $$ slip).\n\n📌 Exam tip: A mixture's mean is always the weight-weighted average of component means. But its *variance* is NOT the weighted average of variances — it includes a spread-of-means term, which is why mixtures with different means or variances exhibit fat tails (excess kurtosis).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Mixture mean\",\"steps\":[\"calm: 0.90 × 1%\",\"stressed: 0.10 × (−3%)\",\"0.90% − 0.30%\",\"mixture mean = 0.60%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A statistician forms the random variable $$T = \\frac{Z}{\\sqrt{W/8}}$$, where $$Z \\sim N(0,1)$$ and $$W \\sim \\chi^2_{8}$$ are independent. Which statement about $$T$$ is correct?\nA) $$T$$ follows a chi-squared distribution with 8 degrees of freedom B) $$T$$ follows a Student's t distribution with 8 degrees of freedom; its tails are fatter than the standard normal's, so its variance exceeds 1 C) $$T$$ follows an F distribution with 8 and 1 degrees of freedom D) $$T$$ is standard normal because $$W/8 \\to 1$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Recognize the construction. A standard normal divided by the square root of an independent chi-squared over its degrees of freedom is the *definition* of a Student's t.\n\n1. By definition, $$t_\\nu = \\frac{Z}{\\sqrt{W/\\nu}}$$ with $$Z\\sim N(0,1)$$, $$W\\sim\\chi^2_\\nu$$. Here $$\\nu = 8$$, so $$T \\sim t_8$$.\n2. The t has fatter tails than the normal; its variance is $$\\frac{\\nu}{\\nu-2} = \\frac{8}{6} = 1.333 > 1$$, confirming the heavier tails.\n\nB is correct. A confuses the construction with the chi-squared (which is a *sum of squared normals*, not this ratio). C describes the F-statistic form (ratio of two chi-squareds over their d.o.f.), not a single normal over a chi-squared. D wrongly treats $$W/8$$ as exactly 1 — it converges to 1 only as $$\\nu\\to\\infty$$, so for finite $$\\nu=8$$ the tails remain fatter than normal.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the three constructions — $$\\chi^2$$ = sum of squared standard normals; $$t$$ = normal over $$\\sqrt{\\chi^2/\\nu}$$; $$F$$ = ratio of two $$\\chi^2$$ each over its d.o.f. The t's variance $$\\frac{\\nu}{\\nu-2}>1$$ encodes its fat tails.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Distributions from normals/chi-squared\",\"root\":\"Z and χ²\",\"children\":[\"χ² = ΣZ² (sum of squares)\",\"t = Z/√(χ²/ν) ← this T\",\"F = ratio of two χ²/df\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Annual operational-loss events follow a Poisson distribution. The firm observes an average of 2.5 events per quarter. Treating quarters as independent, what is the mean and variance of the *annual* number of events?\nA) Mean 2.5, variance 2.5 B) Mean 10, variance 10 C) Mean 10, variance 100 D) Mean 10, variance 40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Poisson counts over disjoint intervals add, and for Poisson the mean equals the variance.\n\n1. Annual rate: events accumulate over 4 quarters, so $$\\lambda_{\\text{year}} = 4 \\times 2.5 = 10$$.\n2. The sum of independent Poisson variables is Poisson with the summed rate, so annual count $$\\sim$$ Poisson(10).\n3. For Poisson, mean = variance = $$\\lambda$$, so mean 10 and variance 10.\n\nB is correct. A reports the quarterly figures, failing to scale to a year. C correctly scales the mean to 10 but squares it for the variance (variance $$\\ne \\lambda^2$$ for Poisson — that confuses it with a multiplicative scaling). D scales the variance by 4 separately ($$4\\times... $$ giving 40) as if variance scaled differently from the mean — but for a Poisson sum both scale identically by the rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Poisson's signature is mean = variance = $$\\lambda$$, and rates add over disjoint periods. Scaling from quarterly to annual multiplies $$\\lambda$$ by 4 — applied to BOTH the mean and the variance equally.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Quarterly → annual Poisson\",\"steps\":[\"λ_q = 2.5 per quarter\",\"× 4 quarters → λ_year = 10\",\"Poisson: mean = variance = λ\",\"mean 10, variance 10\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"204","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Multivariate Random Variables","notes":"# 4. Multivariate Random Variables\n\nThis chapter covers joint behavior: marginal and conditional distributions, covariance and correlation, portfolio variance algebra, and iid sums. The portfolio-variance formula and covariance algebra are among the most tested computations in Book 2.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain how a probability matrix can be used to express a probability mass function for a bivariate distribution.\n- Compute the marginal and conditional distributions of a discrete bivariate random variable.\n- Explain how the expectation of a function is computed for a bivariate discrete random variable.\n- Define covariance and explain what it measures.\n- Explain the relationship between the covariance and correlation of two random variables, and how these are related to the independence of the two variables.\n- Explain the effects of applying linear transformations on the covariance and correlation between two random variables.\n- Compute the variance of a weighted sum of two random variables.\n- Compute the conditional expectation of a component of a bivariate random variable.\n- Describe the features of an iid sequence of random variables.\n- Explain how the iid property is helpful in computing the mean and variance of a sum of iid random variables.\n\n## Probability matrices, marginals, and conditionals\n\nA discrete bivariate PMF can be laid out as a **probability matrix** $$f(x, y) = P(X = x, Y = y)$$. From it:\n\n- **Marginal**: sum across the other variable, $$f_X(x) = \\sum_y f(x, y)$$.\n- **Conditional**: divide the joint by the marginal,\n\n$$f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\mid x) = \\frac{f(x, y)}{f_X(x)}$$\n\n- **Independence**: $$f(x, y) = f_X(x) f_Y(y)$$ for **every** cell. One failing cell kills independence.\n- **Expectation of a function**: $$E[g(X, Y)] = \\sum_x \\sum_y g(x, y) f(x, y)$$.\n- **Conditional expectation**: $$E[Y \\mid X = x] = \\sum_y y\\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)$$.\n\n## Covariance and correlation\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y) = E[(X - \\mu_X)(Y - \\mu_Y)] = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]$$\n\n$$\\rho_{XY} = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y)}{\\sigma_X \\sigma_Y}, \\qquad -1 \\le \\rho \\le 1$$\n\nCovariance measures the direction of **linear** co-movement; its units are the product of the variables' units. Correlation is the unit-free version. Key facts:\n\n- $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X, X) = \\mathrm{Var}(X)$$\n- Independence $$\\Rightarrow$$ $$\\mathrm{Cov} = 0$$, but zero covariance does **not** imply independence in general.\n- For **jointly normal** variables (only), zero correlation does imply independence.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> \"Uncorrelated\" means \"no *linear* relationship.\" $$Y = X^2$$ with symmetric $$X$$ has $$\\rho = 0$$ yet perfect dependence. The exception — zero correlation implies independence — holds only under joint normality. This exact distinction is a perennial exam question.\n\n## Linear transformations\n\nFor $$a, b, c, d$$ constants:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(aX + b,\\; cY + d) = ac\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y)$$\n\n$$\\rho(aX+b,\\, cY+d) = \\mathrm{sign}(ac)\\,\\rho(X, Y)$$\n\nCorrelation is unchanged in magnitude by linear transformations — only its sign can flip.\n\n## Variance of a weighted sum (portfolio variance)\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(aX + bY) = a^2 \\sigma_X^2 + b^2 \\sigma_Y^2 + 2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y)$$\n\nWith correlation: $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = \\rho \\sigma_X \\sigma_Y$$. For a difference, set $$b = -1$$:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X - Y) = \\sigma_X^2 + \\sigma_Y^2 - 2\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y)$$\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Diversification in one line: with $$\\rho < 1$$ the cross term is less than $$2ab\\,\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y$$, so portfolio standard deviation is below the weighted average of component standard deviations.\n\n## IID sequences\n\nVariables are **iid** when they are mutually independent and share one distribution. For $$X_1, \\dots, X_n$$ iid with mean $$\\mu$$ and variance $$\\sigma^2$$:\n\n$$E\\left[\\sum X_i\\right] = n\\mu, \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}\\left(\\sum X_i\\right) = n\\sigma^2, \\qquad \\sigma_{\\text{sum}} = \\sigma\\sqrt{n}$$\n\nVariance of the sum grows linearly in $$n$$; standard deviation grows with $$\\sqrt{n}$$. This is the source of the **square-root-of-time** volatility scaling rule.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> $$\\sqrt{t}$$ scaling requires iid returns — zero autocorrelation and constant variance. With positive autocorrelation, true multi-period risk *exceeds* $$\\sigma\\sqrt{t}$$; with mean reversion it is lower. GARP likes asking when the rule breaks.\n\n### Worked example: probability matrix\n\nJoint PMF of market state $$X$$ (0 = down, 1 = up) and a fund's result $$Y$$ (0 = loss, 1 = gain):\n\n| | $$Y=0$$ | $$Y=1$$ | $$f_X$$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $$X=0$$ | 0.20 | 0.10 | 0.30 |\n| $$X=1$$ | 0.15 | 0.55 | 0.70 |\n| $$f_Y$$ | 0.35 | 0.65 | 1.00 |\n\n**Conditional:** $$P(Y=1 \\mid X=1) = 0.55 / 0.70 = 0.7857$$.\n\n**Conditional expectation:** $$E[Y \\mid X = 1] = 0(0.2143) + 1(0.7857) = 0.7857$$.\n\n**Covariance:** $$E[XY] = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0.55 = 0.55$$; $$E[X] = 0.7$$, $$E[Y] = 0.65$$.\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = 0.55 - 0.7 \\times 0.65 = 0.095$$\n\n**Correlation:** $$\\sigma_X^2 = 0.7(0.3) = 0.21$$, $$\\sigma_Y^2 = 0.65(0.35) = 0.2275$$.\n\n$$\\rho = \\frac{0.095}{\\sqrt{0.21 \\times 0.2275}} = \\frac{0.095}{0.2186} = 0.4346$$\n\nIndependence fails (e.g., $$0.20 \\ne 0.30 \\times 0.35 = 0.105$$), consistent with $$\\rho \\ne 0$$.\n\n### Worked example: two-asset portfolio variance\n\nAsset A: $$\\sigma_A = 20\\%$$; Asset B: $$\\sigma_B = 12\\%$$; $$\\rho = 0.3$$. Portfolio: 60% A, 40% B.\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(A,B) = 0.3 \\times 0.20 \\times 0.12 = 0.0072$$\n\n$$\\sigma_P^2 = 0.6^2(0.04) + 0.4^2(0.0144) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.0072)$$\n\n$$= 0.0144 + 0.002304 + 0.003456 = 0.02016$$\n\n$$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{0.02016} = 14.20\\%$$\n\nCompare with the weighted average $$0.6(20) + 0.4(12) = 16.8\\%$$ — diversification saved 2.6 points.\n\n### Worked example: iid scaling\n\nDaily returns are iid with $$\\sigma_d = 1.2\\%$$. The 10-day volatility is\n\n$$\\sigma_{10} = 1.2\\% \\times \\sqrt{10} = 3.79\\%$$\n\nand 10-day variance is $$10 \\times 1.2^2 = 14.4$$ (%²) — variance scales with $$t$$, volatility with $$\\sqrt{t}$$.","flashcards":"**Q1.** From a bivariate probability matrix, how do you get a marginal and a conditional distribution?\n> **A:** Marginal: sum across the other variable, $$f_X(x) = \\sum_y f(x,y)$$. Conditional: $$f_{Y\\mid X}(y\\mid x) = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}$$.\n\n**Q2.** How do you check independence from a probability matrix?\n> **A:** $$f(x,y) = f_X(x)f_Y(y)$$ must hold for EVERY cell. One failing cell kills independence.\n\n**Q3.** Give the covariance formula and its relationship to correlation.\n> **A:** $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]$$; $$\\rho_{XY} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y}$$, with $$-1 \\le \\rho \\le 1$$.\n\n**Q4.** Does independence imply zero covariance? Does zero covariance imply independence?\n> **A:** Independence ⇒ zero covariance always. Zero covariance does NOT imply independence in general (it only rules out linear dependence). Exception: jointly normal variables — zero correlation does imply independence.\n\n**Q5.** Give an example where $$\\rho = 0$$ but the variables are perfectly dependent.\n> **A:** $$Y = X^2$$ with symmetric $$X$$: $$\\rho = 0$$ yet $$Y$$ is fully determined by $$X$$. \"Uncorrelated\" means no LINEAR relationship.\n\n**Q6.** How do linear transformations affect covariance and correlation?\n> **A:** $$\\mathrm{Cov}(aX+b, cY+d) = ac\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$; $$\\rho(aX+b, cY+d) = \\mathrm{sign}(ac)\\,\\rho(X,Y)$$ — magnitude unchanged, sign can flip.\n\n**Q7.** Give the variance of a weighted sum $$aX + bY$$.\n> **A:** $$\\mathrm{Var}(aX+bY) = a^2\\sigma_X^2 + b^2\\sigma_Y^2 + 2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$, with $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = \\rho\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y$$.\n\n**Q8.** Two-asset portfolio: $$\\sigma_A = 20\\%$$, $$\\sigma_B = 12\\%$$, $$\\rho = 0.3$$, weights 60/40. Find $$\\sigma_P$$.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_P^2 = 0.36(0.04) + 0.16(0.0144) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.0072) = 0.02016$$, so $$\\sigma_P = 14.20\\%$$ (below the 16.8% weighted average — diversification).\n\n**Q9.** For iid $$X_1,\\dots,X_n$$ with mean $$\\mu$$ and variance $$\\sigma^2$$, give the mean, variance, and standard deviation of the sum.\n> **A:** $$E[\\sum X_i] = n\\mu$$, $$\\mathrm{Var}(\\sum X_i) = n\\sigma^2$$, $$\\sigma_{\\text{sum}} = \\sigma\\sqrt{n}$$ — the source of square-root-of-time scaling.\n\n**Q10.** When does the $$\\sqrt{t}$$ volatility scaling rule break?\n> **A:** It requires iid returns (zero autocorrelation, constant variance). Positive autocorrelation makes true risk exceed $$\\sigma\\sqrt{t}$$; mean reversion makes it lower.\n\n**Q11.** $$\\sigma_X = 5$$, $$\\sigma_Y = 8$$, $$\\rho = -0.25$$. Find $$\\mathrm{Var}(X+Y)$$.\n> **A:** $$\\mathrm{Cov} = -0.25(5)(8) = -10$$; $$\\mathrm{Var} = 25 + 64 + 2(-10) = 69$$.\n\n**Q12.** Daily returns are iid with daily variance 0.0004. Find the 25-day return standard deviation.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_d = 0.02$$; $$\\sigma_{25} = 0.02\\sqrt{25} = 0.10$$.\n\n**Q13.** Define the conditional expectation $$E[Y \\mid X = x]$$ for a discrete bivariate.\n> **A:** $$E[Y \\mid X = x] = \\sum_y y\\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** $$X$$ and $$Y$$ have $$\\sigma_X = 5$$, $$\\sigma_Y = 8$$, and correlation $$\\rho = -0.25$$. What is $$\\mathrm{Var}(X + Y)$$?\nA) 49 B) 69 C) 89 D) 109\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the variance of the sum of two correlated variables. First we need the covariance, then we plug into the variance-of-a-sum formula.\n\nStep 1 — covariance from correlation:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y) = \\rho \\, \\sigma_X \\, \\sigma_Y = -0.25 \\times 5 \\times 8 = -10$$\n\nStep 2 — variance of the sum:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(X + Y) = \\sigma_X^2 + \\sigma_Y^2 + 2\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y)$$\n\n$$= 5^2 + 8^2 + 2(-10) = 25 + 64 - 20 = 69$$\n\nSo the answer is 69 (option B). The negative covariance *reduces* the total variance — the two variables partly offset each other.\n\n- A (49) subtracts too much, e.g., using $$25 + 64 - 40$$, double-counting the covariance term.\n- C (89) just adds the variances ($$25 + 64$$) and ignores the covariance term entirely.\n- D (109) adds the covariance instead of subtracting ($$25 + 64 + 20$$), using a positive sign for a negative covariance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\mathrm{Var}(X+Y) = \\sigma_X^2 + \\sigma_Y^2 + 2\\,\\mathrm{Cov}$$. Get the covariance sign right — negative correlation lowers the sum's variance (diversification).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Var(X+Y) with correlation\",\"steps\":[\"Cov=ρσ_Xσ_Y=−0.25×5×8=−10\",\"σ_X²+σ_Y²=25+64=89\",\"+ 2Cov = +2(−10) = −20\",\"Var = 69\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** If $$U = 3X + 2$$ and $$V = -4Y + 1$$, and $$\\rho(X, Y) = 0.5$$, then $$\\rho(U, V)$$ equals:\nA) 0.0 B) $$-0.5$$ C) 0.5 D) $$-6.0$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how linear transformations of two variables affect their correlation.\n\nCorrelation is unit-free, so multiplying a variable by a positive number or adding a constant does NOT change its magnitude. The only thing that can change is the *sign*, and that depends on the signs of the two scale factors.\n\nThe scales here are $$a = 3$$ (for $$X$$) and $$c = -4$$ (for $$Y$$). The sign of their product is:\n\n$$\\mathrm{sign}(a \\times c) = \\mathrm{sign}(3 \\times -4) = \\mathrm{sign}(-12) = \\text{negative}$$\n\nSo the correlation keeps its magnitude 0.5 but flips sign:\n\n$$\\rho(U, V) = \\mathrm{sign}(ac) \\times \\rho(X,Y) = -0.5$$\n\nSo the answer is $$-0.5$$ (option B). The constants $$+2$$ and $$+1$$ have no effect at all.\n\n- A (0.0) wrongly assumes the transformation destroys the relationship; it doesn't.\n- C (0.5) keeps the original sign, ignoring that one scale ($$-4$$) is negative.\n- D ($$-6.0$$) multiplies the correlation by the scale factors ($$0.5 \\times 3 \\times -4$$) — but correlation is unit-free and bounded in $$[-1, 1]$$, so it can never be $$-6$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Linear transforms never change the *magnitude* of correlation; they only flip its sign when the two scale factors have opposite signs. Constants are irrelevant.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ρ under linear transforms\",\"steps\":[\"scales a=3, c=−4\",\"sign(ac)=sign(−12)=negative\",\"magnitude 0.5 unchanged\",\"ρ(U,V) = −0.5\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Zero correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$ guarantees that they are independent:\nA) Always B) Never C) Only if $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are jointly normally distributed D) Only if both have zero mean\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the relationship between zero correlation and independence.\n\nCorrelation only measures *linear* co-movement. Two variables can be perfectly dependent in a nonlinear way yet have zero correlation — for example $$Y = X^2$$ with a symmetric $$X$$ has correlation 0 but $$Y$$ is completely determined by $$X$$. So in general, zero correlation does NOT imply independence.\n\nThe one important exception: if $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are *jointly normally distributed*, then zero correlation does imply independence. So option C is correct.\n\n- A (Always) is the classic mistake — true only under joint normality, not in general.\n- B (Never) is too strong; under joint normality it does hold.\n- D (zero mean) is irrelevant; the means have nothing to do with the correlation-independence link.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Uncorrelated = independent\" ONLY under joint normality. Otherwise zero correlation just means no *linear* relationship; nonlinear dependence can still exist.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Zero correlation vs independence\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"In general\",\"items\":[\"zero ρ = no LINEAR link\",\"nonlinear dependence possible\",\"e.g. Y=X² has ρ=0\"]},{\"h\":\"Joint normal\",\"items\":[\"zero ρ ⇒ independent\",\"the one exception\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Daily returns are iid with daily variance 0.0004. What is the standard deviation of the 25-day return?\nA) 0.01 B) 0.02 C) 0.05 D) 0.10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question asks us to scale risk over time using the iid square-root-of-time rule.\n\nStep 1 — daily standard deviation:\n\n$$\\sigma_{\\text{day}} = \\sqrt{0.0004} = 0.02$$\n\nStep 2 — for iid returns, variance adds over time, so the variance over $$n$$ days is $$n \\times \\sigma_{\\text{day}}^2$$, and the standard deviation scales by $$\\sqrt{n}$$:\n\n$$\\sigma_{25} = \\sigma_{\\text{day}} \\times \\sqrt{n} = 0.02 \\times \\sqrt{25} = 0.02 \\times 5 = 0.10$$\n\nSo the 25-day standard deviation is 0.10 (option D).\n\n- A (0.01) divides instead of multiplying, as if risk shrinks over time.\n- B (0.02) is just the daily standard deviation, forgetting to scale up for 25 days.\n- C (0.05) multiplies the daily variance by something, or uses $$\\sqrt{n}$$ on the variance instead of the standard deviation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Variance scales with time ($$\\times t$$); standard deviation scales with $$\\sqrt{t}$$. Convert variance to SD first, then multiply by $$\\sqrt{t}$$. This requires iid returns.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Square-root-of-time scaling\",\"steps\":[\"daily σ=√0.0004=0.02\",\"scale SD by √n\",\"0.02×√25 = 0.02×5\",\"σ_25 = 0.10\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Asset A has $$\\sigma_A = 20\\%$$, Asset B has $$\\sigma_B = 12\\%$$, and their correlation is $$\\rho = 0.3$$. What is the covariance between A and B?\nA) 0.0072 B) 0.0240 C) 0.0720 D) 0.7200\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks us to convert a correlation into a covariance.\n\nThe relationship is:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(A, B) = \\rho \\times \\sigma_A \\times \\sigma_B$$\n\nConvert the percentages to decimals: $$\\sigma_A = 0.20$$, $$\\sigma_B = 0.12$$. Then:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(A, B) = 0.3 \\times 0.20 \\times 0.12 = 0.3 \\times 0.024 = 0.0072$$\n\nSo the covariance is 0.0072 (option A).\n\n- B (0.0240) is $$\\sigma_A \\times \\sigma_B = 0.20 \\times 0.12$$, forgetting to multiply by the correlation 0.3.\n- C (0.0720) forgets to convert one percentage (e.g., $$0.3 \\times 0.20 \\times 1.2$$ or similar decimal-place slip).\n- D (0.7200) keeps everything in whole-number percentages ($$0.3 \\times 20 \\times 12 / 100$$ mis-scaled), inflating by 100.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\mathrm{Cov} = \\rho\\,\\sigma_A\\,\\sigma_B$$. Always convert % to decimals before multiplying, and watch the decimal places — covariance answers are usually small.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Asset A ($$\\sigma_A = 20\\%$$) and Asset B ($$\\sigma_B = 12\\%$$) have $$\\rho = 0.3$$, so $$\\mathrm{Cov}(A,B) = 0.0072$$. A portfolio holds 60% A and 40% B. What is the portfolio variance?\nA) 0.01670 B) 0.02016 C) 0.02822 D) 0.16800\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the variance of a two-asset portfolio with weights $$a = 0.6$$ and $$b = 0.4$$.\n\nThe portfolio variance formula is:\n\n$$\\sigma_P^2 = a^2 \\sigma_A^2 + b^2 \\sigma_B^2 + 2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(A, B)$$\n\nCompute each piece (using decimals: $$\\sigma_A^2 = 0.20^2 = 0.04$$, $$\\sigma_B^2 = 0.12^2 = 0.0144$$):\n\n- First term: $$0.6^2 \\times 0.04 = 0.36 \\times 0.04 = 0.0144$$\n- Second term: $$0.4^2 \\times 0.0144 = 0.16 \\times 0.0144 = 0.002304$$\n- Cross term: $$2 \\times 0.6 \\times 0.4 \\times 0.0072 = 0.48 \\times 0.0072 = 0.003456$$\n\nAdd them up:\n\n$$\\sigma_P^2 = 0.0144 + 0.002304 + 0.003456 = 0.02016$$\n\nSo the portfolio variance is 0.02016 (option B). (Its square root, about 14.2%, is below the 16.8% weighted average of the SDs — that's diversification.)\n\n- A (0.01670) drops the cross (covariance) term entirely.\n- C (0.02822) uses weights of 1 instead of squared weights, or double-counts the cross term.\n- D (0.16800) is the weighted-average standard deviation ($$0.6 \\times 20\\% + 0.4 \\times 12\\% = 16.8\\%$$), which is not the variance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Portfolio variance uses *squared* weights on the variance terms plus a $$2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}$$ cross term. Don't confuse it with the simple weighted average of standard deviations.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Two-asset portfolio variance\",\"steps\":[\"a²σ_A² = 0.36×0.04 = 0.0144\",\"b²σ_B² = 0.16×0.0144 = 0.002304\",\"2ab·Cov = 0.48×0.0072 = 0.003456\",\"sum = 0.02016\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Independence between two random variables implies which of the following?\nA) Their covariance is zero. B) Their covariance equals 1. C) Their correlation is positive. D) Their variances are equal.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks what *must* follow if two variables are independent.\n\nIf $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are independent, knowing one tells you nothing about the other, so they have no co-movement at all. This forces:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y) = 0$$\n\nSo option A is correct. (Note the reverse is not guaranteed: zero covariance does not always imply independence — that only holds under joint normality.)\n\n- B (covariance = 1) confuses covariance with a correlation bound; independent variables have covariance 0, not 1.\n- C (positive correlation) is wrong; independence gives *zero* correlation, not positive.\n- D (equal variances) is unrelated; independence says nothing about the sizes of the two variances.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Independence ⟹ zero covariance (and zero correlation). The arrow only runs one way in general; zero covariance ⟹ independence only under joint normality.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What independence implies\",\"options\":[\"covariance is zero\",\"covariance equals 1\",\"correlation is positive\",\"variances are equal\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Independence forces no co-movement, so Cov=0. The reverse (zero Cov → independence) holds only under joint normality.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** For an iid sequence $$X_1, \\dots, X_n$$ each with variance $$\\sigma^2$$, what is the variance of the sum $$\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$$?\nA) $$\\sigma^2$$ B) $$\\sigma^2 / n$$ C) $$n\\sigma^2$$ D) $$\\sigma\\sqrt{n}$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the variance of a sum of $$n$$ iid variables.\n\nBecause iid variables are independent, all covariance terms are zero, so the variance of the sum is just the sum of the individual variances:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\\right) = n\\sigma^2$$\n\nSo option C is correct. Variance of the *sum* grows linearly with $$n$$.\n\n- A ($$\\sigma^2$$) is the variance of a single observation, not the sum.\n- B ($$\\sigma^2 / n$$) is the variance of the *sample mean* (average), not the sum. The sum and the mean scale very differently.\n- D ($$\\sigma\\sqrt{n}$$) is the standard *deviation* of the sum, not its variance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For iid sums: variance $$= n\\sigma^2$$, SD $$= \\sigma\\sqrt{n}$$. For the sample mean: variance $$= \\sigma^2/n$$, SD $$= \\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$. Don't mix sum vs mean.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"iid Sum vs Mean\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sum of n\",\"items\":[\"variance = nσ²\",\"SD = σ√n\",\"grows with n\"]},{\"h\":\"Sample mean\",\"items\":[\"variance = σ²/n\",\"SD = σ/√n\",\"shrinks with n\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The square-root-of-time scaling rule for volatility requires that returns are:\nA) Normally distributed B) Independent and identically distributed (zero autocorrelation, constant variance) C) Positively autocorrelated D) Mean-reverting\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks under what condition $$\\sigma_T = \\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$ is valid.\n\nThe $$\\sqrt{t}$$ rule comes directly from the iid property: when returns are independent (zero autocorrelation) and identically distributed (constant variance), the variance of a multi-period return is the sum of single-period variances, $$T\\sigma^2$$, so volatility scales as $$\\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$. So option B is correct.\n\n- A (normal) is not required; iid is what matters. The CLT may make multi-period returns roughly normal, but the scaling rule itself only needs iid, not normality of each return.\n- C (positive autocorrelation) actually *breaks* the rule: with positive autocorrelation, true multi-period risk *exceeds* $$\\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$.\n- D (mean reversion) also breaks the rule, making true risk *below* $$\\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\sqrt{t}$$ scaling needs iid (no autocorrelation, constant variance) — not normality. Positive autocorrelation ⟹ understates risk; mean reversion ⟹ overstates risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Autocorrelation vs true multi-period risk\",\"items\":[\"mean reversion (true < σ√T)\",\"|iid (rule exact: σ√T)\",\"positive autocorr (true > σ√T)\"],\"left\":\"overstates risk\",\"right\":\"understates risk\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** From a joint probability table, $$P(Y=1, X=1) = 0.55$$ and the marginal $$P(X=1) = 0.70$$. What is the conditional probability $$P(Y=1 \\mid X=1)$$?\nA) 0.385 B) 0.550 C) 0.700 D) 0.786\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question asks for a conditional probability from a joint probability table.\n\nThe rule is: divide the joint probability by the marginal probability of the condition.\n\n$$P(Y=1 \\mid X=1) = \\frac{P(Y=1, X=1)}{P(X=1)} = \\frac{0.55}{0.70} = 0.7857 \\approx 0.786$$\n\nSo the answer is 0.786 (option D). We are restricting attention to the world where $$X=1$$ (which has total probability 0.70) and asking what fraction of that also has $$Y=1$$.\n\n- A (0.385) multiplies the two numbers ($$0.55 \\times 0.70$$) instead of dividing.\n- B (0.550) just reports the joint probability, forgetting to divide by the marginal.\n- C (0.700) reports the marginal of $$X$$, the denominator, not the conditional.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Conditional = joint ÷ marginal of the condition. The denominator is always the probability of what you're conditioning on (here $$P(X=1)$$).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Conditional from a joint table\",\"steps\":[\"joint P(Y=1,X=1)=0.55\",\"÷ marginal P(X=1)=0.70\",\"= 0.55/0.70\",\"P(Y=1|X=1)=0.786\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** $$\\sigma_X = 5$$, $$\\sigma_Y = 8$$, $$\\rho = -0.25$$. $$\\mathrm{Var}(X + Y)$$ is:\nA) 89 B) 69 C) 109 D) 49\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\mathrm{Cov} = -0.25(5)(8) = -10$$; $$\\mathrm{Var} = 25 + 64 + 2(-10) = 69$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** If $$U = 3X + 2$$ and $$V = -4Y + 1$$, and $$\\rho(X, Y) = 0.5$$, then $$\\rho(U, V) =$$\nA) 0.5 B) $$-0.5$$ C) $$-6.0$$ D) 0.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Scales 3 and $$-4$$: sign of $$ac$$ is negative, so correlation flips to $$-0.5$$; magnitude unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Zero correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$ guarantees independence:\nA) Always B) Never C) Only if $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are jointly normally distributed D) Only if both have zero mean\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Joint normality is the special case where uncorrelated implies independent.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Daily returns are iid with daily variance 0.0004. The 25-day return standard deviation is:\nA) 0.01 B) 0.10 C) 0.02 D) 0.05\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_d = 0.02$$; $$\\sigma_{25} = 0.02\\sqrt{25} = 0.10$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X, Y) = 0.006$$. The covariance between $$2X + 3$$ and $$5Y - 1$$ is:\nA) 0.006 B) 0.012 C) 0.060 D) 0.063\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\mathrm{Cov}(aX+b,\\ cY+d) = ac\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = 2 \\times 5 \\times 0.006 = 0.060$$. Additive constants drop out entirely (D wrongly lets them creep in); A applies no scaling; B scales by only one multiplier.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** Two assets have $$\\sigma_A = 18\\%$$, $$\\sigma_B = 25\\%$$, and correlation $$\\rho = 0.40$$. A portfolio invests 70% in A and 30% in B. What is the portfolio's standard deviation (closest)?\nA) 17.05% B) 14.66% C) 20.10% D) 22.60%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Use the two-asset portfolio variance formula, then take the square root.\n\n1. Covariance: $$\\mathrm{Cov}(A,B) = \\rho\\sigma_A\\sigma_B = 0.40 \\times 0.18 \\times 0.25 = 0.018$$.\n2. Weighted terms ($$w_A=0.70$$, $$w_B=0.30$$):\n   - $$w_A^2\\sigma_A^2 = 0.49 \\times 0.0324 = 0.015876$$\n   - $$w_B^2\\sigma_B^2 = 0.09 \\times 0.0625 = 0.005625$$\n   - $$2 w_A w_B \\mathrm{Cov} = 2(0.70)(0.30)(0.018) = 0.42 \\times 0.018 = 0.007560$$\n3. Sum: $$\\sigma_P^2 = 0.015876 + 0.005625 + 0.007560 = 0.029061$$.\n4. $$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{0.029061} = 0.17047 \\approx 17.05\\%$$.\n\nA is correct. B (14.66%) sets $$\\rho=0$$, omitting the covariance term: $$\\sqrt{0.015876+0.005625}=\\sqrt{0.021501}=14.66\\%$$ — understates risk. C (20.10%) is the weighted average of the standard deviations $$0.70(18)+0.30(25)=20.1\\%$$, the no-diversification ($$\\rho=1$$) ceiling. D (22.60%) double-counts or adds an extra factor in the cross term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The cross term is $$2 w_A w_B \\rho\\sigma_A\\sigma_B$$ — easy to drop the factor of 2 or the correlation. Portfolio std dev must fall between the ρ=0 value (14.66%) and the weighted-average ρ=1 value (20.10%).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Portfolio σ vs correlation\",\"items\":[\"ρ=0: 14.66% (max diversification)\",\"|actual ρ=0.4: 17.05%\",\"ρ=1: 20.10% (weighted avg, no diversification)\"],\"left\":\"lower risk\",\"right\":\"higher risk\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** $$X$$ and $$Y$$ have $$\\sigma_X = 6$$, $$\\sigma_Y = 10$$, and $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = -18$$. What is $$\\mathrm{Var}(2X - 3Y)$$?\nA) 1{,}260 B) 828 C) 1{,}044 D) 1{,}476\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Use $$\\mathrm{Var}(aX + bY) = a^2\\sigma_X^2 + b^2\\sigma_Y^2 + 2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$ with $$a=2$$, $$b=-3$$.\n\n1. $$a^2\\sigma_X^2 = 4 \\times 36 = 144$$.\n2. $$b^2\\sigma_Y^2 = 9 \\times 100 = 900$$.\n3. Cross term: $$2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov} = 2(2)(-3)(-18)$$. Step by step: $$2\\times2\\times(-3) = -12$$; then $$-12 \\times (-18) = +216$$.\n4. Sum: $$\\mathrm{Var}(2X-3Y) = 144 + 900 + 216 = 1{,}260$$.\n\nA is correct. B (828) flips the cross-term sign to $$-216$$ ($$144+900-216$$), forgetting that the negative coefficient product times a negative covariance gives a *positive* term. C (1,044) drops the factor of 2 in the cross term, using $$+108$$ ($$144+900$$ plus a halved term). D (1,476) uses $$+2 \\times 6 \\times 10 \\times ... $$ with raw std devs instead of $$ab$$, double-counting.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In $$2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}$$, track the signs carefully: $$ab = (2)(-3) = -6$$; times a negative covariance flips the cross term *positive*, raising variance. Two negatives multiply to a positive — and never drop the factor of 2.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Var(2X−3Y)\",\"steps\":[\"a²σ_X²=4×36=144\",\"b²σ_Y²=9×100=900\",\"2ab·Cov=2(2)(−3)(−18)=+216\",\"sum = 1260\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The joint PMF of market direction $$X$$ (0 = down, 1 = up) and a desk's outcome $$Y$$ (0 = loss, 1 = gain) is: $$P(0,0)=0.25$$, $$P(0,1)=0.10$$, $$P(1,0)=0.15$$, $$P(1,1)=0.50$$. What is $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$?\nA) 0.155 B) 0.110 C) 0.500 D) 0.000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Compute marginals, $$E[XY]$$, then $$\\mathrm{Cov} = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]$$.\n\n1. Marginal of $$X$$: $$P(X=1) = P(1,0)+P(1,1) = 0.15+0.50 = 0.65$$, so $$E[X]=0.65$$.\n2. Marginal of $$Y$$: $$P(Y=1) = P(0,1)+P(1,1) = 0.10+0.50 = 0.60$$, so $$E[Y]=0.60$$.\n3. $$E[XY]$$: only the $$(1,1)$$ cell has $$XY=1$$; all others give 0, so $$E[XY] = 1\\times1\\times0.50 = 0.50$$.\n4. $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = 0.50 - (0.65)(0.60) = 0.50 - 0.39 = 0.11$$.\n\nB is correct (0.110). A (0.155) subtracts the wrong product (e.g., $$0.50 - 0.345$$ using a miscomputed marginal). C (0.500) reports $$E[XY]$$ itself, forgetting to subtract $$E[X]E[Y]$$. D (0.000) wrongly assumes independence — but $$P(1,1)=0.50 \\ne P(X=1)P(Y=1)=0.65\\times0.60=0.39$$, so they are dependent and the covariance is nonzero.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For binary $$X,Y$$, $$E[XY]$$ equals the single $$(1,1)$$ joint probability. Then subtract $$E[X]E[Y]$$ — never report $$E[XY]$$ alone as the covariance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Cov of binary X,Y\",\"steps\":[\"E[X]=P(X=1)=0.65\",\"E[Y]=P(Y=1)=0.60\",\"E[XY]=P(1,1)=0.50\",\"Cov=0.50−0.39=0.11\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** If $$U = -2X + 7$$ and $$V = 5Y - 3$$, and the correlation $$\\rho(X,Y) = 0.6$$, what is $$\\rho(U,V)$$?\nA) 0.60 B) $$-0.60$$ C) $$-6.00$$ D) 0.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Correlation is invariant in magnitude under linear transforms; only the sign of the product of the scale factors matters.\n\n1. Rule: $$\\rho(aX+b,\\, cY+d) = \\mathrm{sign}(ac)\\,\\rho(X,Y)$$.\n2. Scale factors: $$a = -2$$, $$c = +5$$, so $$ac = -10 < 0 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{sign}(ac) = -1$$.\n3. Therefore $$\\rho(U,V) = -1 \\times 0.6 = -0.60$$.\n\nB is correct. A keeps the original sign, ignoring the negative scale on $$X$$. C ($$-6.00$$) wrongly scales the correlation by the product of the coefficients $$ac\\times\\rho = -10\\times0.6$$ — but correlation magnitude never changes, and it must stay within $$[-1,1]$$. D assumes the transforms destroy correlation — they don't; only the sign can flip.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Linear transforms leave $$|\\rho|$$ untouched; the only effect is a sign flip when the scale factors have opposite signs. Any answer outside $$[-1,1]$$ (like $$-6$$) is automatically wrong.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ρ(U,V) under transforms\",\"steps\":[\"scales a=−2, c=+5\",\"ac=−10 → sign negative\",\"magnitude 0.6 unchanged\",\"ρ(U,V) = −0.60\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Daily returns are iid with daily standard deviation 0.9%. Assuming the square-root-of-time rule holds, what is the standard deviation of the cumulative return over 16 trading days, and how would positive autocorrelation change the *true* multi-period risk?\nA) 3.6%; true risk would be lower than 3.6% B) 3.6%; true risk would be higher than 3.6% C) 14.4%; true risk would be higher than 3.6% D) 0.225%; true risk would be unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Use $$\\sigma_{\\text{sum}} = \\sigma\\sqrt{t}$$ for iid returns, then reason about autocorrelation.\n\n1. $$\\sigma_{16} = 0.9\\% \\times \\sqrt{16} = 0.9\\% \\times 4 = 3.6\\%$$.\n2. The $$\\sqrt{t}$$ rule assumes zero autocorrelation. With *positive* autocorrelation, shocks persist and reinforce across days, so true multi-period variance *exceeds* $$t\\sigma^2$$ and true risk is *higher* than $$\\sigma\\sqrt{t}=3.6\\%$$.\n\nB is correct. A correctly computes 3.6% but reverses the autocorrelation effect (positive autocorrelation raises, not lowers, multi-period risk — mean reversion would lower it). C scales variance instead of std dev ($$0.9\\%\\times16 = 14.4\\%$$), confusing the two scaling laws. D scales by $$1/t$$ or divides, producing 0.225%, and wrongly claims autocorrelation is irrelevant.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Variance scales with $$t$$, std dev with $$\\sqrt{t}$$. The $$\\sqrt{t}$$ rule needs iid returns; positive autocorrelation ⇒ true risk *above* $$\\sigma\\sqrt{t}$$, mean reversion ⇒ *below*.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"True risk vs σ√t = 3.6%\",\"items\":[\"mean reversion (true < 3.6%)\",\"|iid (exactly 3.6%)\",\"positive autocorr (true > 3.6%)\"],\"left\":\"lower true risk\",\"right\":\"higher true risk\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement about the relationship between zero covariance and independence is correct?\nA) Zero covariance always implies independence B) Independence always implies zero covariance, but zero covariance implies independence only when the variables are jointly normal C) Zero covariance never implies independence, even for jointly normal variables D) Independence implies zero covariance only if both variables have zero mean\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Recall the precise logical relationship.\n\n1. Independence $$\\Rightarrow$$ $$\\mathrm{Cov} = 0$$ always (independent variables have $$E[XY]=E[X]E[Y]$$).\n2. The converse fails in general: covariance captures only *linear* association. Example: $$Y = X^2$$ with symmetric $$X$$ has $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)=0$$ yet $$X$$ and $$Y$$ are perfectly dependent.\n3. The one exception where zero covariance *does* imply independence is joint normality.\n\nB is correct. A overstates the converse — zero covariance does not imply independence in general (the $$Y=X^2$$ counterexample). C wrongly denies the joint-normal exception, where uncorrelated does imply independent. D adds a spurious zero-mean condition — independence implies zero covariance regardless of the means.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Uncorrelated\" = no *linear* relationship. Independence ⇒ zero covariance always; the reverse holds only under joint normality. This exact distinction is a perennial exam favorite.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Independence vs zero covariance\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Independence ⇒ Cov=0\",\"items\":[\"always true\",\"E[XY]=E[X]E[Y]\"]},{\"h\":\"Cov=0 ⇒ independence?\",\"items\":[\"only if jointly normal\",\"else Y=X² counterexample\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A market-neutral book is long position $$X$$ and short position $$Y$$, with payoff $$X - Y$$. Given $$\\sigma_X = 12\\%$$, $$\\sigma_Y = 9\\%$$, and $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = +0.0081$$, what is the standard deviation of the long-short payoff?\nA) 15.00% B) 7.94% C) 21.00% D) 5.61%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** For a difference, $$\\mathrm{Var}(X - Y) = \\sigma_X^2 + \\sigma_Y^2 - 2\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$.\n\n1. $$\\sigma_X^2 = 0.12^2 = 0.0144$$.\n2. $$\\sigma_Y^2 = 0.09^2 = 0.0081$$.\n3. Cross term (subtracted for a difference): $$2\\mathrm{Cov} = 2 \\times 0.0081 = 0.0162$$.\n4. $$\\mathrm{Var}(X-Y) = 0.0144 + 0.0081 - 0.0162 = 0.0063$$.\n5. $$\\sigma = \\sqrt{0.0063} = 0.07937 \\approx 7.94\\%$$.\n\nB is correct. A (15.00%) uses $$\\sqrt{0.0144+0.0081} = \\sqrt{0.0225} = 15\\%$$, dropping the covariance term (treating positive covariance as if it didn't reduce the spread of a difference). C (21.00%) *adds* the cross term, $$\\sqrt{0.0144+0.0081+0.0162}$$, applying the sum formula instead of the difference. D (5.61%) subtracts the covariance once without the factor of 2, or makes a square-root slip.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a *difference*, the covariance term is *subtracted*: positive covariance between a long and a short reduces net volatility (a hedge). For a *sum*, it is added. The factor of 2 is mandatory.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Var of sum vs difference\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sum X+Y\",\"items\":[\"σ_X²+σ_Y²+2Cov\",\"Cov ADDED\"]},{\"h\":\"Difference X−Y\",\"items\":[\"σ_X²+σ_Y²−2Cov\",\"Cov SUBTRACTED\",\"+Cov hedges → σ=7.94%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"205","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Sample Moments","notes":"# 5. Sample Moments\n\nMoving from population to data: estimating mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis, covariance, and understanding estimator quality (bias, consistency, efficiency), the LLN, and the CLT. The $$n$$ vs. $$n-1$$ divisor and the CLT are guaranteed exam material.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Estimate the mean, variance, and standard deviation using sample data.\n- Explain the difference between a population moment and a sample moment.\n- Distinguish between an estimator and an estimate.\n- Describe the bias of an estimator and explain what the bias measures.\n- Explain what is meant by the statement that the mean estimator is BLUE.\n- Describe the consistency of an estimator and explain the usefulness of this concept.\n- Explain how the Law of Large Numbers (LLN) and Central Limit Theorem (CLT) apply to the sample mean.\n- Estimate and interpret the skewness and kurtosis of a random variable.\n- Use sample data to estimate quantiles, including the median.\n- Estimate the mean of two variables and apply the CLT.\n- Estimate the covariance and correlation between two random variables.\n- Explain how coskewness and cokurtosis are related to skewness and kurtosis.\n\n## Estimators vs. estimates; sample moments\n\nAn **estimator** is the formula (a random variable); an **estimate** is the number it produces from a particular sample.\n\n**Sample mean:**\n\n$$\\bar{X} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$$\n\n**Sample variance (unbiased):**\n\n$$s^2 = \\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\bar{X})^2$$\n\nThe $$n-1$$ divisor (one degree of freedom spent estimating $$\\bar{X}$$) makes $$s^2$$ unbiased. Dividing by $$n$$ gives the (biased, but consistent) ML-style estimator.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Divisor trap: population variance and the biased estimator use $$n$$; the unbiased sample variance uses $$n-1$$. With small $$n$$ the difference is material — for $$n=5$$ the $$n$$-divisor estimate is 20% smaller. Read the question stem carefully for \"unbiased\" or \"sample.\"\n\n**Standard error of the mean:**\n\n$$SE(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\approx \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\n## Estimator properties\n\n- **Bias:** $$\\mathrm{Bias}(\\hat\\theta) = E[\\hat\\theta] - \\theta$$. Unbiased means zero bias.\n- **Efficiency:** among unbiased estimators, the one with smallest variance.\n- **BLUE:** the sample mean is the **Best Linear Unbiased Estimator** of the population mean — minimum variance among all estimators that are linear in the data and unbiased (no distributional assumption needed beyond iid with finite variance).\n- **Consistency:** $$\\hat\\theta \\to \\theta$$ in probability as $$n \\to \\infty$$. A consistent estimator can be biased in finite samples (e.g., the $$n$$-divisor variance) — the bias just vanishes asymptotically.\n\n## LLN and CLT\n\n**Law of Large Numbers:** for iid data with finite variance, $$\\bar{X} \\to \\mu$$ as $$n \\to \\infty$$. Justifies estimation and Monte Carlo.\n\n**Central Limit Theorem:** for iid data with mean $$\\mu$$ and finite variance $$\\sigma^2$$, regardless of the underlying distribution,\n\n$$\\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\xrightarrow{d} N(0, 1)$$\n\nThe CLT is why z- and t-tests on means work even for non-normal data once $$n$$ is reasonably large (rule of thumb 30+).\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> LLN says *where* the sample mean goes (to $$\\mu$$); CLT says *how it fluctuates* on the way (normally, with standard deviation shrinking like $$1/\\sqrt{n}$$).\n\n## Higher sample moments\n\nSample skewness and kurtosis replace population moments with sample averages:\n\n$$\\hat{\\text{skew}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)^3}{\\hat\\sigma^3}, \\qquad \\hat{\\text{kurt}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)^4}{\\hat\\sigma^4}$$\n\nInterpretation matches the population versions: negative skew = long left tail; kurtosis > 3 = fat tails. Both are estimated very imprecisely in small samples because they depend on rare extreme observations.\n\n**Quantiles and the median:** sort the data; the median is the middle observation ($$n$$ odd) or the average of the two middle observations ($$n$$ even). Sample quantiles interpolate between order statistics. The median is robust to outliers; the mean is not.\n\n## Covariance, correlation, and co-moments\n\n$$\\hat\\sigma_{XY} = \\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\bar X)(Y_i - \\bar Y), \\qquad \\hat\\rho = \\frac{\\hat\\sigma_{XY}}{s_X s_Y}$$\n\n**Coskewness** and **cokurtosis** extend skewness and kurtosis to pairs, e.g. standardized cross third moments like $$E[(X-\\mu_X)^2 (Y-\\mu_Y)]/(\\sigma_X^2 \\sigma_Y)$$. Negative coskewness means one asset tends to suffer when the other is volatile or falling — assets with negative coskewness with the market deserve extra risk premia. There are two distinct coskewness measures for a pair and three cokurtosis measures (for jointly normal variables, coskewness is 0 and the central cokurtosis measure is 1).\n\n### Worked example: full set of sample moments\n\nFive monthly returns (%): 2, −1, 4, 0, 5.\n\n**Mean:** $$\\bar X = (2 - 1 + 4 + 0 + 5)/5 = 10/5 = 2.0$$\n\n**Deviations:** 0, −3, 2, −2, 3. Squared: 0, 9, 4, 4, 9; sum = 26.\n\n**Sample variance:** $$s^2 = 26/4 = 6.5$$, so $$s = 2.550$$.\n\n**Biased version:** $$26/5 = 5.2$$ — note how much smaller.\n\n**Standard error of the mean:** $$SE = 2.550/\\sqrt{5} = 1.140$$.\n\n**Skewness (using $$\\hat\\sigma = \\sqrt{5.2} = 2.280$$):** cubes: 0, −27, 8, −8, 27; sum = 0, so skewness $$= 0$$ — a symmetric sample.\n\n### Worked example: sample correlation\n\nThree paired observations of returns (%): $$X$$: 1, 3, 5; $$Y$$: 2, 2, 8. Means: $$\\bar X = 3$$, $$\\bar Y = 4$$.\n\n| $$x_i - \\bar X$$ | $$y_i - \\bar Y$$ | product | $$(x_i-\\bar X)^2$$ | $$(y_i-\\bar Y)^2$$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| −2 | −2 | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n| 0 | −2 | 0 | 0 | 4 |\n| 2 | 4 | 8 | 4 | 16 |\n\nSums: 12, 8, 24.\n\n$$\\hat\\sigma_{XY} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6, \\quad s_X = \\sqrt{8/2} = 2, \\quad s_Y = \\sqrt{24/2} = 3.464$$\n\n$$\\hat\\rho = \\frac{6}{2 \\times 3.464} = 0.866$$\n\n### Worked example: CLT application\n\nLosses have mean \\$10M and standard deviation \\$30M, distribution unknown and skewed. For $$n = 100$$ independent losses, the average loss is approximately\n\n$$\\bar X \\sim N\\!\\left(10, \\left(\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{100}}\\right)^2\\right) = N(10, 3^2)$$\n\n$$P(\\bar X > 16) \\approx P\\!\\left(Z > \\frac{16-10}{3}\\right) = P(Z > 2) = 2.28\\%$$\n\nThe CLT licenses the normal approximation despite skewed individual losses.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Distinguish an estimator from an estimate.\n> **A:** An estimator is the formula (a random variable); an estimate is the number it produces from a particular sample.\n\n**Q2.** Give the unbiased sample variance formula and explain the divisor.\n> **A:** $$s^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)^2$$. The $$n-1$$ divisor (one d.o.f. spent estimating $$\\bar X$$) makes $$s^2$$ unbiased; dividing by $$n$$ gives a biased but consistent estimator.\n\n**Q3.** State the standard error of the sample mean.\n> **A:** $$SE(\\bar X) = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\approx \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$.\n\n**Q4.** Define bias and efficiency of an estimator.\n> **A:** Bias $$= E[\\hat\\theta] - \\theta$$ (zero for unbiased). Efficiency: among unbiased estimators, the one with the smallest variance.\n\n**Q5.** What does it mean that the sample mean is BLUE?\n> **A:** Best Linear Unbiased Estimator — minimum variance among all estimators linear in the data and unbiased, needing only iid with finite variance.\n\n**Q6.** Define consistency, and can a consistent estimator be biased?\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\theta \\to \\theta$$ in probability as $$n \\to \\infty$$. Yes — it can be biased in finite samples (e.g., the $$n$$-divisor variance); the bias just vanishes asymptotically.\n\n**Q7.** State the Law of Large Numbers and the Central Limit Theorem.\n> **A:** LLN: for iid data with finite variance, $$\\bar X \\to \\mu$$. CLT: $$\\dfrac{\\bar X - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1)$$ regardless of the underlying distribution.\n\n**Q8.** What is the difference in what the LLN and CLT tell you?\n> **A:** LLN says WHERE the sample mean goes (to $$\\mu$$); CLT says HOW it fluctuates on the way (normally, with SD shrinking like $$1/\\sqrt{n}$$).\n\n**Q9.** Sample: 4, 6, 8, 10. Compute the unbiased sample variance.\n> **A:** $$\\bar X = 7$$; squared deviations 9, 1, 1, 9 sum to 20; $$s^2 = 20/3 = 6.67$$.\n\n**Q10.** Quadrupling the sample size changes the standard error of the mean by what factor?\n> **A:** $$SE \\propto 1/\\sqrt{n}$$, so $$1/\\sqrt{4} = 1/2$$.\n\n**Q11.** Give the sample covariance and correlation formulas.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\sigma_{XY} = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)(Y_i - \\bar Y)$$; $$\\hat\\rho = \\dfrac{\\hat\\sigma_{XY}}{s_X s_Y}$$.\n\n**Q12.** What do coskewness and cokurtosis capture, and what are they for jointly normal variables?\n> **A:** Cross co-moments for a pair; negative coskewness means one asset suffers when the other falls/is volatile. For jointly normal variables, coskewness is 0 and central cokurtosis is 1.\n\n**Q13.** Losses have mean $10M, SD $30M, skewed. For $$n = 100$$, find $$P(\\bar X > 16)$$ via the CLT.\n> **A:** $$\\bar X \\approx N(10, 3^2)$$; $$P(Z > \\tfrac{16-10}{3}) = P(Z > 2) = 2.28\\%$$.\n\n**Q14.** Why are sample skewness and kurtosis estimated imprecisely in small samples?\n> **A:** They depend on rare extreme observations (third/fourth powers of deviations), so a few outliers dominate.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A sample of four returns is 4, 6, 8, 10. What is the unbiased sample variance?\nA) 5.0 B) 6.0 C) 6.67 D) 20.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the *unbiased* sample variance, which uses the $$n - 1$$ divisor.\n\nStep 1 — sample mean:\n\n$$\\bar{X} = \\frac{4 + 6 + 8 + 10}{4} = \\frac{28}{4} = 7$$\n\nStep 2 — deviations from the mean and their squares:\n- $$4 - 7 = -3 \\Rightarrow 9$$\n- $$6 - 7 = -1 \\Rightarrow 1$$\n- $$8 - 7 = +1 \\Rightarrow 1$$\n- $$10 - 7 = +3 \\Rightarrow 9$$\n\nSum of squared deviations $$= 9 + 1 + 1 + 9 = 20$$.\n\nStep 3 — divide by $$n - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3$$:\n\n$$s^2 = \\frac{20}{3} = 6.67$$\n\nSo the unbiased sample variance is 6.67 (option C).\n\n- A (5.0) divides by $$n = 4$$ (the biased estimator: $$20/4 = 5$$), but the question asked for unbiased.\n- B (6.0) is a miscalculation of the sum of squares or divisor.\n- D (20.0) is just the sum of squared deviations, with no division at all.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Unbiased\" / \"sample\" variance uses $$n - 1$$. Population or \"biased\" variance uses $$n$$. Read the stem carefully — this divisor choice is a guaranteed trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sample variance divisor\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Unbiased (sample)\",\"items\":[\"divide by n−1\",\"20/3 = 6.67\",\"corrects for est. mean\"]},{\"h\":\"Biased (population)\",\"items\":[\"divide by n\",\"20/4 = 5.0\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An estimator whose expected value equals the true parameter for every sample size is described as:\nA) Consistent B) Efficient C) Unbiased D) BLUE\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the property defined by \"expected value equals the true parameter.\"\n\nBy definition, an estimator $$\\hat\\theta$$ is unbiased if its bias is zero:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Bias}(\\hat\\theta) = E[\\hat\\theta] - \\theta = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad E[\\hat\\theta] = \\theta$$\n\nThis must hold for every sample size, which is exactly what the question states. So option C is correct. On average, an unbiased estimator hits the bullseye.\n\n- A (consistent) is an *asymptotic* property: the estimator converges to the truth as $$n \\to \\infty$$. A consistent estimator can be biased in small samples, so \"for every sample size\" rules it out.\n- B (efficient) means smallest variance among unbiased estimators — about precision, not centering.\n- D (BLUE) is a stronger combined property (Best Linear Unbiased Estimator); it includes unbiasedness but adds \"linear\" and \"minimum variance,\" which the bare definition given doesn't require.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Unbiased = correct *on average* at any $$n$$. Consistent = correct in the *limit* as $$n \\to \\infty$$. These are different; an estimator can be one without the other.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"E[θ̂]=θ for every n means…\",\"options\":[\"consistent\",\"efficient\",\"unbiased\",\"BLUE\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"E[θ̂]=θ at any sample size is the definition of unbiased; consistency is only an n→∞ limit.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The Central Limit Theorem states that for iid data with finite variance, as $$n$$ grows:\nA) The raw data become normally distributed. B) The sampling distribution of the mean approaches a normal distribution. C) The sample variance approaches zero. D) The population mean approaches the sample mean.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the precise statement of the Central Limit Theorem (CLT).\n\nThe CLT says: for iid data with finite variance, the *sampling distribution of the sample mean* becomes approximately normal as $$n$$ grows, regardless of the shape of the original data:\n\n$$\\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\xrightarrow{d} N(0, 1)$$\n\nSo option B is correct. This is why z- and t-tests on means work even when the underlying data are skewed, once $$n$$ is reasonably large (rule of thumb 30+).\n\n- A is the most common trap: the CLT does NOT make the *raw data* normal. Skewed data stay skewed; it's the *average* whose distribution becomes normal.\n- C confuses the CLT with the behavior of the standard error. The sample variance estimates $$\\sigma^2$$ and does not go to zero; the variance *of the mean* shrinks, but that's not \"the sample variance.\"\n- D reverses the Law of Large Numbers: the *sample* mean approaches the *population* mean, not the other way around.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CLT is about the *distribution of the mean*, not the raw data. LLN says where the mean goes ($$\\to \\mu$$); CLT says how it fluctuates (normally).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What the CLT actually says\",\"options\":[\"raw data become normal\",\"sampling dist. of the mean → normal\",\"sample variance → 0\",\"population mean → sample mean\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"CLT: the distribution of the sample mean approaches normal as n grows, whatever the raw data's shape.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Quadrupling the sample size changes the standard error of the mean by a factor of:\nA) 1/16 B) 1/4 C) 1/2 D) 2\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how the standard error of the mean responds to multiplying $$n$$ by 4.\n\nThe standard error of the mean is:\n\n$$SE(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\nIt is inversely proportional to $$\\sqrt{n}$$. If $$n$$ becomes $$4n$$, then:\n\n$$\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{4n}} = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\nSo the standard error is multiplied by $$1/2$$ (option C). To halve your error, you need 4× the data — diminishing returns.\n\n- A (1/16) squares the factor incorrectly, treating SE as proportional to $$1/n^2$$.\n- B (1/4) treats SE as proportional to $$1/n$$, forgetting the square root.\n- D (2) gets the direction wrong; more data *reduces* error, it doesn't double it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$SE \\propto 1/\\sqrt{n}$$. To cut the standard error in half, you must *quadruple* the sample size. Squaring the data improvement gives the cost.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Quadrupling n → SE\",\"steps\":[\"SE = σ/√n\",\"n → 4n\",\"√(4n) = 2√n\",\"SE × 1/2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Losses have mean $10M and standard deviation $30M, with an unknown, skewed distribution. For a sample of $$n = 100$$ independent losses, the standard error of the average loss is:\nA) $0.30M B) $3.00M C) $30.0M D) $300M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the standard error (standard deviation) of the *average* of 100 independent losses.\n\nThe standard error of the mean is:\n\n$$SE(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{30}{\\sqrt{100}} = \\frac{30}{10} = 3$$\n\nSo the standard error is $3M (option B). Averaging 100 losses smooths out the noise, shrinking the spread from $30M (for one loss) to $3M (for the average).\n\n- A ($0.30M) divides by $$n = 100$$ instead of $$\\sqrt{n} = 10$$.\n- C ($30.0M) is the standard deviation of a *single* loss, forgetting to divide by $$\\sqrt{n}$$.\n- D ($300M) multiplies by $$\\sqrt{n}$$ instead of dividing — that would be the SD of the *sum*, and even then it's $$30 \\times 10 = 300$$, but the question asks about the average.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Standard error of the mean = $$\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$. Divide by $$\\sqrt{n}$$, not $$n$$. The CLT lets you treat the average as approximately normal even with skewed individual losses.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Using the setup in Q5 (average loss approximately $$N(10, 3^2)$$ for $$n=100$$), what is the approximate probability that the average loss exceeds $16M? (Use: $$P(Z > 2) \\approx 2.28\\%$$.)\nA) 2.28% B) 5.00% C) 16.00% D) 50.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for a tail probability of the sample mean, using the CLT-justified normal approximation $$\\bar{X} \\sim N(10, 3^2)$$.\n\nStep 1 — standardize the cutoff $16M into a z-score (mean 10, standard deviation 3):\n\n$$z = \\frac{16 - 10}{3} = \\frac{6}{3} = 2$$\n\nStep 2 — read the upper tail:\n\n$$P(\\bar{X} > 16) \\approx P(Z > 2) = 2.28\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is 2.28% (option A). The CLT lets us use the normal even though individual losses are skewed, because we're looking at an *average* of 100.\n\n- B (5.00%) corresponds to $$z \\approx 1.645$$, a shallower cutoff than $$z = 2$$.\n- C (16.00%) corresponds to $$z \\approx 1$$, again the wrong z-score (likely standardizing with $$\\sigma = 6$$ or skipping the $$\\sqrt{n}$$ step).\n- D (50.00%) is the probability of exceeding the mean ($16 vs $10), ignoring the standardization entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Standardize using the standard error of the *mean* ($$\\sigma/\\sqrt{n} = 3$$), not the raw $$\\sigma = 30$$. Then read the normal tail. $$z=2 \\to 2.28\\%$$ in one tail.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Three paired observations are $$X$$: 1, 3, 5 and $$Y$$: 2, 2, 8. The means are $$\\bar{X}=3$$, $$\\bar{Y}=4$$. The sample covariance (using the $$n-1$$ divisor) is:\nA) 4 B) 6 C) 12 D) 24\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the sample covariance using the unbiased $$n - 1$$ divisor.\n\nThe formula is:\n\n$$\\hat\\sigma_{XY} = \\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum (X_i - \\bar{X})(Y_i - \\bar{Y})$$\n\nCompute the deviations and their products:\n- Obs 1: $$(1-3)(2-4) = (-2)(-2) = 4$$\n- Obs 2: $$(3-3)(2-4) = (0)(-2) = 0$$\n- Obs 3: $$(5-3)(8-4) = (2)(4) = 8$$\n\nSum of products $$= 4 + 0 + 8 = 12$$.\n\nDivide by $$n - 1 = 3 - 1 = 2$$:\n\n$$\\hat\\sigma_{XY} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6$$\n\nSo the sample covariance is 6 (option B).\n\n- A (4) is only the first product, not the full sum.\n- C (12) is the sum of products without dividing by $$n - 1$$.\n- D (24) divides nothing and doubles, or uses a wrong divisor.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Sample covariance sums the products of paired deviations, then divides by $$n - 1$$. Don't forget the divisor, and keep the signs of each deviation straight.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which statement correctly describes the meaning of \"the sample mean is BLUE\"?\nA) It is the estimator with the smallest possible bias. B) It is the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator of the population mean. C) It assumes the data are normally distributed. D) It is biased but consistent.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the acronym BLUE means for the sample mean.\n\nBLUE stands for **Best Linear Unbiased Estimator**. For the population mean, the sample mean is BLUE: among all estimators that are (i) linear in the data and (ii) unbiased, it has the smallest variance (\"best\" = minimum variance). This requires only iid data with finite variance — no assumption that the data are normal. So option B is correct.\n\n- A is imprecise: BLUE estimators are *unbiased* (zero bias), and \"best\" refers to minimum *variance*, not minimum bias.\n- C is wrong: the BLUE property does not require normality, only iid with finite variance.\n- D contradicts the definition: BLUE estimators are unbiased, not biased.\n\n📌 Exam tip: BLUE = Best (minimum variance) Linear Unbiased Estimator. \"Best\" means smallest variance among unbiased linear estimators; normality is NOT required.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Meaning of 'sample mean is BLUE'\",\"options\":[\"smallest possible bias\",\"Best Linear Unbiased Estimator of μ\",\"assumes data are normal\",\"biased but consistent\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"BLUE = minimum-variance among linear unbiased estimators; needs only iid with finite variance, not normality.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A sample's data are heavily skewed with a few extreme outliers. Which measure of central tendency is most robust to those outliers?\nA) The mean B) The median C) The variance D) The standard error\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which location measure is least affected by extreme values.\n\nThe median is simply the middle value when the data are sorted. Changing a few extreme observations (even making them enormous) does not move the middle value much, because the median only cares about *rank*, not magnitude. So the median is robust to outliers — option B.\n\nThe mean, by contrast, adds up every value, so one giant outlier can drag it far away.\n\n- A (the mean) is *not* robust; a single extreme value can pull it strongly in that direction.\n- C (the variance) is a measure of *spread*, not central tendency, and it is extremely sensitive to outliers (it squares deviations).\n- D (the standard error) is also a spread/precision measure, not a measure of central tendency, and inherits the variance's sensitivity to outliers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Median = robust to outliers (rank-based). Mean and variance = sensitive (value-based). For skewed financial data, the median often tells a steadier story.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Robustness to outliers\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Robust\",\"items\":[\"median (rank-based)\",\"ignores magnitude of extremes\"]},{\"h\":\"Sensitive\",\"items\":[\"mean (value-based)\",\"variance / std error\",\"squares deviations\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** For two jointly normal random variables, the coskewness is:\nA) Always 0 B) Always 1 C) Equal to their correlation D) Undefined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks about the coskewness of jointly normal variables.\n\nCoskewness extends the idea of skewness to a pair of variables, capturing how one variable behaves when the other moves to extremes. The normal distribution is perfectly symmetric, so it has zero skewness — and jointly normal variables likewise have zero coskewness. So option A is correct. (As a related fact, the central *cokurtosis* measure for jointly normal variables equals 1, not 0 — don't confuse the two.)\n\n- B (always 1) is the value of the *central cokurtosis* measure under joint normality, not coskewness.\n- C (equal to correlation) confuses a third-moment co-measure with a second-moment relationship; coskewness is not the correlation.\n- D (undefined) is wrong; coskewness is well-defined and simply equals zero here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Joint normality ⟹ coskewness = 0 (symmetry) and central cokurtosis = 1. Negative coskewness with the market signals an asset that suffers when the market is volatile/falling, deserving extra risk premium.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Coskewness of jointly normal variables\",\"options\":[\"always 0\",\"always 1\",\"equal to correlation\",\"undefined\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"The normal is symmetric, so jointly normal variables have zero coskewness (central cokurtosis equals 1, not coskewness).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Sample: 4, 6, 8, 10. The unbiased sample variance is:\nA) 5.0 B) 6.67 C) 20.0 D) 6.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\bar X = 7$$; squared deviations 9, 1, 1, 9 sum to 20; $$s^2 = 20/3 = 6.67$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** An estimator whose expected value equals the true parameter for every sample size is:\nA) Consistent B) Efficient C) Unbiased D) BLUE\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** That is the definition of unbiasedness; consistency is an asymptotic property.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The CLT states that for iid data with finite variance, as $$n$$ grows:\nA) The data become normally distributed B) The sample mean's distribution approaches normal C) The sample variance approaches zero D) The population mean approaches the sample mean\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The CLT concerns the *sampling distribution of the mean*, not the raw data.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Quadrupling the sample size changes the standard error of the mean by a factor of:\nA) 1/4 B) 1/2 C) 2 D) 1/16\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$SE \\propto 1/\\sqrt{n}$$, and $$1/\\sqrt{4} = 1/2$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A hedge fund's monthly returns have sample skewness of −1.2 and sample kurtosis of 6.5. Relative to a normal distribution with the same mean and variance, the fund's returns exhibit:\nA) A longer right tail and thinner tails overall B) A longer left tail and fatter tails overall C) A longer left tail and thinner tails overall D) Symmetry, since kurtosis above 3 offsets negative skew\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Negative skew = longer/heavier **left** tail (big losses more likely than big gains); kurtosis 6.5 means excess kurtosis of 3.5 — fatter tails than normal. Skew and kurtosis measure different things and never \"offset\" (D).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Tail fatness (kurtosis)\",\"items\":[\"thin (platykurtic)\",\"|normal (k=3)\",\"fat (k=6.5, leptokurtic)\"],\"left\":\"fewer extremes\",\"right\":\"more extremes\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** Five monthly returns are observed: 2%, 4%, 6%, 8%, 10%. The standard error of the sample mean is closest to:\nA) 0.63% B) 1.41% C) 2.00% D) 3.16%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Mean = 6%; squared deviations 16, 4, 0, 4, 16 sum to 40, so\n\n$$s^2 = \\frac{40}{5-1} = 10 \\Rightarrow s = 3.162\\% \\qquad SE(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{3.162\\%}{\\sqrt{5}} = 1.41\\%$$\n\nDistractor D stops at $$s$$; A divides by $$n$$ instead of $$\\sqrt{n}$$; C is $$s^2/n$$ misread as a percentage.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A sample of six monthly returns (%) is: 3, −2, 5, 1, −4, 9. What is the unbiased sample variance?\nA) 18.67 B) 22.40 C) 134.00 D) 26.80\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Unbiased sample variance uses the $$n-1$$ divisor: $$s^2 = \\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum (X_i-\\bar X)^2$$.\n\n1. Mean: $$\\bar X = \\frac{3 - 2 + 5 + 1 - 4 + 9}{6} = \\frac{12}{6} = 2.0$$.\n2. Deviations from mean: $$1,\\ -4,\\ 3,\\ -1,\\ -6,\\ 7$$.\n3. Squared deviations: $$1,\\ 16,\\ 9,\\ 1,\\ 36,\\ 49$$; sum $$= 112$$.\n4. Divide by $$n-1 = 5$$: $$s^2 = 112/5 = 22.40$$.\n\nB is correct. A (18.67) uses the biased $$n$$ divisor: $$112/6 = 18.67$$ — the question asks for the *unbiased* version. C (134.00) is the sum of squared raw values minus a slip, or forgets to subtract the mean. D (26.80) divides by $$n-2=4.18$$ or miscounts; it overstates by using too small a divisor inconsistently.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Unbiased\" or \"sample\" variance ⇒ divide by $$n-1$$. The biased/population version uses $$n$$. With small $$n$$ the gap is large (here 18.67 vs 22.40), and GARP plants both as options — read the stem.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sample variance divisor\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Unbiased (n−1)\",\"items\":[\"112/5\",\"= 22.40\",\"the answer asked for\"]},{\"h\":\"Biased (n)\",\"items\":[\"112/6\",\"= 18.67\",\"population version\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Losses on a portfolio have an unknown, right-skewed distribution with mean $9M and standard deviation $24M. An analyst draws 144 independent losses and computes the sample average. The approximate probability that the sample average exceeds $13M is closest to:\nA) 2.28% B) 43.3% C) 0.13% D) 15.9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** By the CLT, the sample mean of $$n=144$$ iid losses is approximately normal regardless of the skewed underlying.\n\n1. Standard error: $$SE = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{24}{\\sqrt{144}} = \\frac{24}{12} = 2$$ ($M).\n2. Sampling distribution: $$\\bar X \\approx N(9, 2^2)$$.\n3. Standardize: $$z = \\frac{13 - 9}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2.0$$.\n4. $$P(\\bar X > 13) = P(Z > 2.0) = 1 - 0.9772 = 0.0228 = 2.28\\%$$.\n\nA is correct. B (43.3%) uses the raw $$\\sigma=24$$ as the standard deviation: $$z=(13-9)/24=0.167$$, $$P(Z>0.167)=43.3\\%$$ — forgetting to divide by $$\\sqrt{n}$$. C (0.13%) uses $$z=3$$ (e.g., $$SE=4/3$$ from a $$\\sqrt{n}$$ slip). D (15.9%) uses $$z=1$$ (sets $$SE=4$$, dividing by $$\\sqrt{36}$$ instead of $$\\sqrt{144}$$).\n\n📌 Exam tip: The CLT applies to the *sample mean*, whose standard error is $$\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$ — not the raw $$\\sigma$$. Forgetting the $$\\sqrt{n}$$ shrinkage is the most common CLT error and gives a far-too-fat distribution.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CLT tail probability\",\"steps\":[\"SE = σ/√n = 24/12 = 2\",\"X̄ ≈ N(9, 2²)\",\"z = (13−9)/2 = 2.0\",\"P(Z>2) = 2.28%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which statement about estimator properties is correct?\nA) A consistent estimator must be unbiased in every finite sample B) The sample mean is BLUE — the minimum-variance estimator among all linear, unbiased estimators of the population mean C) The $$n$$-divisor variance estimator is biased and therefore inconsistent D) An efficient estimator is one whose bias shrinks fastest as $$n$$ grows\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Recall the precise definitions.\n\n1. BLUE = Best Linear Unbiased Estimator: among all estimators that are linear in the data and unbiased, the sample mean has the smallest variance (needs only iid with finite variance — no distributional assumption).\n2. Consistency is asymptotic ($$\\hat\\theta \\to \\theta$$ as $$n\\to\\infty$$); a consistent estimator *can* be biased in finite samples.\n3. The $$n$$-divisor variance is biased but *consistent* — its bias vanishes as $$n\\to\\infty$$.\n\nB is correct. A confuses consistency with unbiasedness — a consistent estimator may be biased in finite samples (the $$n$$-divisor variance is the standard example). C wrongly concludes \"inconsistent\" from \"biased\"; the $$n$$-divisor estimator is biased yet consistent. D misdefines efficiency — efficiency is about *minimum variance* among unbiased estimators, not about how fast bias shrinks.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Keep the four straight — unbiased (right on average, any $$n$$), efficient (smallest variance among unbiased), consistent (converges as $$n\\to\\infty$$, may be biased for finite $$n$$), BLUE (best linear unbiased = the sample mean for $$\\mu$$).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Correct estimator statement\",\"options\":[\"consistent ⇒ unbiased every finite sample\",\"sample mean is BLUE for μ\",\"n-divisor variance biased ⇒ inconsistent\",\"efficient = bias shrinks fastest\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The sample mean is the minimum-variance linear unbiased estimator of μ; the others confuse consistency, bias, and efficiency.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Three paired observations of asset returns (%) are $$X$$: 2, 4, 9 and $$Y$$: 1, 5, 6. What is the sample correlation $$\\hat\\rho$$?\nA) 0.500 B) 0.839 C) 0.945 D) 1.000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Compute deviations, then $$\\hat\\rho = \\frac{\\hat\\sigma_{XY}}{s_X s_Y}$$ (the $$n-1$$ factors cancel, so use sums of products and squares directly).\n\n1. Means: $$\\bar X = (2+4+9)/3 = 15/3 = 5$$; $$\\bar Y = (1+5+6)/3 = 12/3 = 4$$.\n2. $$X$$-deviations: $$-3,\\ -1,\\ 4$$; $$Y$$-deviations: $$-3,\\ 1,\\ 2$$.\n3. Cross products: $$(-3)(-3)=9$$; $$(-1)(1)=-1$$; $$(4)(2)=8$$; sum $$=16$$.\n4. $$X$$ squared deviations: $$9,\\ 1,\\ 16$$; sum $$=26$$. $$Y$$ squared deviations: $$9,\\ 1,\\ 4$$; sum $$=14$$.\n5. Correlation: $$\\hat\\rho = \\frac{\\sum(x-\\bar x)(y-\\bar y)}{\\sqrt{\\sum(x-\\bar x)^2 \\sum(y-\\bar y)^2}} = \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{26 \\times 14}} = \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{364}} = \\frac{16}{19.079} = 0.839$$.\n\nB is correct (0.839). A (0.500) drops a cross-product term, shrinking the numerator. C (0.945) divides by $$\\sqrt{\\sum x^2}$$ for one variable but mishandles the other's sum, inflating $$\\hat\\rho$$. D (1.000) assumes perfect correlation, ignoring the imperfect co-movement (the middle pair moves oppositely, contributing $$-1$$).\n\n📌 Exam tip: For sample correlation, the $$n-1$$ divisors in covariance and both standard deviations cancel — just use raw sums of cross products and squared deviations. Always include every paired term, including ones with a negative product.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Sample correlation\",\"steps\":[\"Σ cross products = 16\",\"Σx²=26, Σy²=14\",\"ρ̂ = 16/√(26×14)\",\"= 16/19.08 = 0.839\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Quadrupling a sample from $$n=25$$ to $$n=100$$, with the population standard deviation unchanged, changes the standard error of the sample mean by what factor, and the width of a fixed-confidence interval for the mean by what factor?\nA) SE × 1/4; interval width × 1/4 B) SE × 1/2; interval width × 1/2 C) SE × 1/2; interval width × 1/4 D) SE × 1/4; interval width × 1/2\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The standard error is $$SE = \\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$, and a confidence interval half-width is a fixed multiple (e.g., 1.96) times the SE — so both scale identically with the SE.\n\n1. SE ratio: $$\\frac{SE_{100}}{SE_{25}} = \\frac{\\sigma/\\sqrt{100}}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{25}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{100}} = \\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2}$$.\n2. Quadrupling $$n$$ multiplies $$\\sqrt{n}$$ by 2, so SE is halved.\n3. The interval width $$= 2 \\times z \\times SE$$, so it scales with the SE — also halved.\n\nB is correct. A treats SE as $$\\propto 1/n$$ (it is $$\\propto 1/\\sqrt{n}$$), giving 1/4 incorrectly. C halves the SE correctly but then quarters the interval, breaking the proportionality. D mismatches both factors.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$SE \\propto 1/\\sqrt{n}$$, so to *halve* the SE you must *quadruple* $$n$$. Confidence-interval width is a constant multiple of the SE, so it scales by the same factor — both halve here.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Quadruple n: SE and CI width\",\"steps\":[\"SE = σ/√n\",\"n: 25 → 100 (×4)\",\"√n: 5 → 10 (×2)\",\"SE × 1/2; CI width × 1/2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An analyst computes the sample variance of five returns two ways: with the $$n$$ divisor she gets 8.0; her colleague uses the $$n-1$$ divisor on the same data. What value does the colleague obtain, and which estimator is unbiased?\nA) 6.4; the $$n$$-divisor estimator is unbiased B) 10.0; the $$n-1$$ divisor estimator is unbiased C) 10.0; the $$n$$-divisor estimator is unbiased D) 6.67; the $$n-1$$ divisor estimator is unbiased\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The two estimators differ only by the divisor; convert between them via the common sum of squared deviations $$SS$$.\n\n1. From the $$n$$ divisor: $$8.0 = SS/n = SS/5 \\Rightarrow SS = 40$$.\n2. The $$n-1$$ estimator: $$s^2 = SS/(n-1) = 40/4 = 10.0$$.\n3. The $$n-1$$ divisor produces the *unbiased* estimator (it corrects for the one degree of freedom spent estimating the mean).\n\nB is correct. A inverts the relationship (the $$n-1$$ value is larger, not smaller, than the $$n$$ value, and the $$n$$ version is biased). C gets the value 10.0 right but labels the wrong estimator unbiased. D applies $$SS/6$$ (dividing by $$n+1$$) or mis-derives $$SS$$, giving 6.67.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$s^2_{n-1} = s^2_n \\times \\frac{n}{n-1}$$, so the unbiased ($$n-1$$) estimate is always *larger* than the biased ($$n$$) one. Here $$8.0 \\times \\frac{5}{4} = 10.0$$. The $$n-1$$ version is the unbiased one.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Convert n-divisor to n−1\",\"steps\":[\"8.0 = SS/5 → SS = 40\",\"s² = SS/(n−1) = 40/4\",\"= 10.0\",\"n−1 version is unbiased\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A sample of seven daily returns (sorted, %) is: −5, −2, 0, 1, 3, 4, 12. Using the median and the mean, which statement best describes the distribution and the robustness of the two measures?\nA) Mean 1.86, median 1; the mean exceeds the median, suggesting positive skew, and the median is more robust to the +12 outlier B) Mean 1, median 1.86; the median exceeds the mean, suggesting negative skew C) Mean 1.86, median 0; the two are equal so the data are symmetric D) Mean 1.86, median 1; the mean is more robust to the +12 outlier than the median\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Compute the mean and the median (middle value of 7 sorted observations is the 4th).\n\n1. Mean: $$\\bar X = \\frac{-5 - 2 + 0 + 1 + 3 + 4 + 12}{7} = \\frac{13}{7} = 1.857 \\approx 1.86$$.\n2. Median: with $$n=7$$ (odd), the median is the 4th sorted value $$= 1$$.\n3. Mean (1.86) > median (1): the long right tail driven by the $$+12$$ observation pulls the mean above the median — a sign of positive skew.\n4. The median ignores the magnitude of the extreme $$+12$$ (only its rank), so it is robust to that outlier; the mean is dragged toward it.\n\nA is correct. B swaps the two statistics and the skew direction. C wrongly reads the median as 0 (that is the 3rd value, not the 4th) and claims symmetry despite mean ≠ median. D correctly computes both but reverses robustness — the mean is *sensitive* to outliers, the median is robust.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For odd $$n$$, the median is the middle (here 4th) sorted value. Mean > median signals positive (right) skew. The median is robust to outliers; the mean is not — the $$+12$$ pulls the mean up but leaves the median unchanged.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mean vs median (right-skewed)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Mean = 1.86\",\"items\":[\"value-based\",\"pulled up by +12 outlier\",\"sensitive\"]},{\"h\":\"Median = 1\",\"items\":[\"middle (4th) value\",\"ignores magnitude\",\"robust\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"206","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Hypothesis Testing","notes":"# 6. Hypothesis Testing\n\nFor 2026, GARP reworded this chapter's LOs toward *applied* testing: construct the test, compute the statistic, state the decision, and read p-values and confidence intervals correctly. Expect numbers, not definitions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Construct an appropriate null hypothesis and alternative hypothesis and distinguish between them.\n- Differentiate between one-sided and two-sided tests and identify when each applies.\n- Explain the difference between Type I and Type II errors and how they relate to the size and power of a test.\n- Calculate and interpret the test statistic for a hypothesis about a population mean.\n- Explain what the p-value of a hypothesis test measures; interpret test results at a given confidence level.\n- Construct and apply confidence intervals for one-sided and two-sided tests; explain how a hypothesis test and confidence interval are related.\n- Identify the steps to test a hypothesis about the difference between two population means.\n- Explain the problem of multiple testing and how it can bias results.\n\n## Setting up the test\n\n- **Null hypothesis $$H_0$$:** the maintained claim, always contains the equality (e.g., $$\\mu = \\mu_0$$, $$\\mu \\le \\mu_0$$). The test can *reject* or *fail to reject* $$H_0$$ — it never \"accepts\" it.\n- **Alternative $$H_1$$:** what you conclude if the data are sufficiently inconsistent with $$H_0$$.\n- **Two-sided:** $$H_1: \\mu \\ne \\mu_0$$ — reject for large $$\\lvert z \\rvert$$; split $$\\alpha$$ across both tails (5% test: critical value 1.96).\n- **One-sided:** $$H_1: \\mu > \\mu_0$$ (or $$<$$) — all of $$\\alpha$$ in one tail (5% test: 1.645).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> One- vs. two-tailed is the most common point thrown away in this chapter. At $$\\alpha = 5\\%$$: two-tailed critical value 1.96, one-tailed 1.645. A statistic of 1.80 rejects a one-sided null but fails to reject a two-sided one. Let the wording (\"differs from\" vs. \"exceeds\") choose the test for you.\n\n## Test statistics\n\nFor a mean with known variance (or large $$n$$):\n\n$$z = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\nWith unknown variance and small samples, use the t-statistic with $$n - 1$$ degrees of freedom:\n\n$$t = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\nDecision rule: reject $$H_0$$ when the statistic falls beyond the critical value(s) for the chosen size $$\\alpha$$.\n\n## Errors, size, and power\n\n| | $$H_0$$ true | $$H_0$$ false |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reject $$H_0$$ | **Type I error** (prob. = $$\\alpha$$, the *size*) | Correct (prob. = **power** $$= 1 - \\beta$$) |\n| Fail to reject | Correct | **Type II error** (prob. = $$\\beta$$) |\n\n- Size $$\\alpha$$ is chosen by the tester (5% standard, 1% strict).\n- Power rises with sample size, with a larger true deviation from $$H_0$$, and with a larger $$\\alpha$$.\n- For fixed $$n$$, decreasing $$\\alpha$$ increases $$\\beta$$: the only free lunch is more data.\n\n## p-values\n\nThe **p-value** is the probability, computed assuming $$H_0$$ is true, of a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed. Equivalently, the smallest $$\\alpha$$ at which you would reject. Reject when p-value $$< \\alpha$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The p-value is **not** the probability that $$H_0$$ is true, and $$1 - p$$ is not the probability $$H_1$$ is true. It is a statement about the data given the null, not about the null given the data. GARP tests this wording directly.\n\n## Confidence intervals and their duality with tests\n\nA $$(1-\\alpha)$$ two-sided confidence interval for $$\\mu$$:\n\n$$\\bar{X} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\,\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\qquad \\text{(or } t_{\\alpha/2,\\,n-1}\\, s/\\sqrt{n}\\text{)}$$\n\nA one-sided 95% interval uses 1.645 and is unbounded on one side, e.g. $$(\\bar X - 1.645\\,\\sigma/\\sqrt{n},\\; \\infty)$$.\n\n**Duality:** a two-sided test of size $$\\alpha$$ rejects $$H_0: \\mu = \\mu_0$$ exactly when $$\\mu_0$$ lies **outside** the $$(1-\\alpha)$$ confidence interval. The CI is the set of all nulls that would not be rejected.\n\n## Difference between two means\n\nFor independent samples with large $$n$$:\n\n$$z = \\frac{(\\bar{X}_1 - \\bar{X}_2) - (\\mu_1 - \\mu_2)_0}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}}$$\n\nSteps: (1) state $$H_0: \\mu_1 - \\mu_2 = 0$$; (2) compute the difference of sample means; (3) compute the standard error by summing the variance contributions; (4) form the statistic; (5) compare to the critical value. For paired data, test the mean of the differences instead.\n\n## Multiple testing\n\nRunning many tests at $$\\alpha = 5\\%$$ inflates the family-wide false-positive rate: with $$k$$ independent true nulls,\n\n$$P(\\text{at least one rejection}) = 1 - (1 - \\alpha)^k$$\n\nFor $$k = 10$$: $$1 - 0.95^{10} = 40.1\\%$$. Consequences in finance: backtest overfitting, data snooping, \"discovering\" fund-manager skill by scanning thousands of funds. Remedies: the **Bonferroni** correction (use $$\\alpha / k$$ per test), out-of-sample validation, and skepticism toward selected (best-of-many) results.\n\n### Worked example: two-sided t-test on a mean\n\nA strategy's 25 monthly returns average 0.80% with sample standard deviation 2.0%. Test $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ vs. $$H_1: \\mu \\ne 0$$ at 5%.\n\n$$t = \\frac{0.80 - 0}{2.0/\\sqrt{25}} = \\frac{0.80}{0.40} = 2.00$$\n\nCritical value $$t_{0.025, 24} = 2.064$$. Since $$2.00 < 2.064$$, **fail to reject** — no significant evidence of nonzero mean return. (Had the test been one-sided, $$t_{0.05,24} = 1.711$$ and we would reject — the one-/two-tail choice flips the answer.)\n\n### Worked example: z-test, p-value, and CI together\n\nA VaR model should produce exceptions on 1% of days. Over $$n = 500$$ days you observe 9 exceptions, i.e. $$\\hat{p} = 0.018$$. Test $$H_0: p = 0.01$$ (two-sided, 5%).\n\nStandard error under $$H_0$$: $$\\sqrt{0.01 \\times 0.99 / 500} = \\sqrt{0.0000198} = 0.00445$$.\n\n$$z = \\frac{0.018 - 0.010}{0.00445} = 1.798$$\n\np-value $$= 2 \\times P(Z > 1.798) = 2 \\times 0.0361 = 0.0722$$. Since $$0.0722 > 0.05$$, fail to reject: the exception rate is not statistically distinguishable from 1%. Consistently, the 95% CI for $$p$$, $$0.018 \\pm 1.96 \\times \\sqrt{0.018 \\times 0.982/500} = 0.018 \\pm 0.0116 = (0.64\\%,\\, 2.96\\%)$$, contains 1%.\n\n### Worked example: difference of means\n\nDesk A: $$n_1 = 60$$ days, mean P&L 1.5, $$s_1 = 4$$. Desk B: $$n_2 = 40$$ days, mean P&L 0.2, $$s_2 = 5$$. Test equal means at 5% (two-sided).\n\n$$SE = \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{60} + \\frac{25}{40}} = \\sqrt{0.2667 + 0.6250} = \\sqrt{0.8917} = 0.9443$$\n\n$$z = \\frac{1.5 - 0.2}{0.9443} = \\frac{1.3}{0.9443} = 1.377$$\n\n$$1.377 < 1.96$$: fail to reject — the desks' mean P&Ls are not significantly different.\n\n### Worked example: power and Type II error\n\nReturns are $$N(\\mu, 2^2)$$, $$n = 16$$, so $$SE = 2/4 = 0.5$$. One-sided test of $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ vs. $$H_1: \\mu > 0$$ at 5% rejects when $$\\bar X > 1.645 \\times 0.5 = 0.8225$$. If the true mean is $$\\mu = 1$$:\n\n$$\\text{Power} = P(\\bar X > 0.8225 \\mid \\mu = 1) = P\\!\\left(Z > \\frac{0.8225 - 1}{0.5}\\right) = P(Z > -0.355) = 0.639$$\n\nSo power ≈ 63.9% and the Type II error rate is $$\\beta \\approx 36.1\\%$$. Doubling $$n$$ to 64 halves the SE to 0.25, threshold 0.411, and power jumps to $$P(Z > -2.355) \\approx 99.1\\%$$.\n\n### Worked example: multiple testing\n\nAn analyst screens 20 independent trading signals, each tested at 5%, all actually useless. Probability at least one looks \"significant\":\n\n$$1 - 0.95^{20} = 1 - 0.3585 = 64.2\\%$$\n\nBonferroni fix: test each at $$0.05/20 = 0.25\\%$$, keeping the family-wise error rate at about 5%.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What must the null hypothesis always contain, and can a test \"accept\" $$H_0$$?\n> **A:** $$H_0$$ always contains the equality (e.g., $$\\mu = \\mu_0$$). A test can only reject or fail to reject $$H_0$$ — never \"accept\" it.\n\n**Q2.** Compare one-tailed and two-tailed 5% critical values, and how wording selects the test.\n> **A:** Two-tailed 5%: 1.96; one-tailed 5%: 1.645. \"Differs from\" ⇒ two-tailed; \"exceeds/less than\" ⇒ one-tailed. A statistic of 1.80 rejects one-sided but not two-sided.\n\n**Q3.** Give the z- and t-statistics for testing a population mean.\n> **A:** Known variance/large $$n$$: $$z = \\dfrac{\\bar X - \\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}}$$. Unknown variance/small $$n$$: $$t = \\dfrac{\\bar X - \\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt{n}}$$ with $$n-1$$ d.o.f.\n\n**Q4.** Define Type I and Type II errors and relate them to size and power.\n> **A:** Type I: reject a true $$H_0$$ (prob $$\\alpha$$ = size). Type II: fail to reject a false $$H_0$$ (prob $$\\beta$$). Power $$= 1 - \\beta$$.\n\n**Q5.** What raises the power of a test, and what is the trade-off at fixed $$n$$?\n> **A:** Power rises with larger sample size, larger true deviation from $$H_0$$, and larger $$\\alpha$$. At fixed $$n$$, decreasing $$\\alpha$$ increases $$\\beta$$ — only more data is a free lunch.\n\n**Q6.** Define a p-value and the reject rule. What is it NOT?\n> **A:** Probability, assuming $$H_0$$ true, of a statistic at least as extreme as observed; reject when p-value $$< \\alpha$$. It is NOT the probability that $$H_0$$ is true.\n\n**Q7.** State the duality between a two-sided test and a confidence interval.\n> **A:** A size-$$\\alpha$$ test rejects $$H_0: \\mu = \\mu_0$$ exactly when $$\\mu_0$$ lies outside the $$(1-\\alpha)$$ CI. The CI is the set of all nulls not rejected.\n\n**Q8.** A strategy's 25 monthly returns average 0.80% with $$s = 2.0\\%$$. Test $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ two-sided at 5% ($$t_{0.025,24} = 2.064$$).\n> **A:** $$t = \\dfrac{0.80}{2.0/\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{0.80}{0.40} = 2.00 < 2.064$$ → fail to reject. (One-sided $$t_{0.05,24} = 1.711$$ would reject — the tail choice flips the answer.)\n\n**Q9.** A sample of 36 returns has mean 1.2% and SD 3.6%. Compute the t-statistic for $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$.\n> **A:** $$t = \\dfrac{1.2}{3.6/\\sqrt{36}} = \\dfrac{1.2}{0.6} = 2.0$$.\n\n**Q10.** Give the two-sample z-statistic for a difference of means (independent, large $$n$$).\n> **A:** $$z = \\dfrac{(\\bar X_1 - \\bar X_2) - (\\mu_1 - \\mu_2)_0}{\\sqrt{s_1^2/n_1 + s_2^2/n_2}}$$. For paired data, test the mean of the differences instead.\n\n**Q11.** What is the multiple-testing problem, and what is the Bonferroni fix?\n> **A:** With $$k$$ independent true nulls, $$P(\\text{at least one rejection}) = 1 - (1-\\alpha)^k$$ (e.g., $$k=10$$, 5% → 40.1%). Bonferroni: test each at $$\\alpha/k$$.\n\n**Q12.** A 95% CI for a regression coefficient is $$(0.4, 2.6)$$. Does a two-sided 5% test of $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ reject?\n> **A:** Yes — by duality, 0 lies outside the 95% CI, so reject at 5%.\n\n**Q13.** Returns $$N(\\mu, 2^2)$$, $$n = 16$$ so $$SE = 0.5$$; one-sided 5% test of $$H_0:\\mu=0$$ rejects when $$\\bar X > 0.8225$$. If true $$\\mu = 1$$, find power.\n> **A:** Power $$= P(Z > \\tfrac{0.8225 - 1}{0.5}) = P(Z > -0.355) = 0.639$$, so $$\\beta \\approx 36.1\\%$$.\n\n**Q14.** A VaR model expects 1% exceptions; over 500 days you see 9 ($$\\hat p = 0.018$$). Test $$H_0: p = 0.01$$ two-sided at 5%.\n> **A:** $$SE = \\sqrt{0.01(0.99)/500} = 0.00445$$; $$z = \\dfrac{0.008}{0.00445} = 1.80$$; p-value $$\\approx 0.072 > 0.05$$ → fail to reject.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A sample of 36 monthly returns has a mean of 1.2% and a sample standard deviation of 3.6%. What is the t-statistic for testing $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$?\nA) 0.33 B) 1.20 C) 2.00 D) 12.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: how many \"standard errors\" away from zero is our sample mean? That number is the t-statistic.\n\nStep 1 — Write the formula:\n$$t = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\nStep 2 — Find the standard error (the bottom of the fraction). It is the standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size:\n$$\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{3.6}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\frac{3.6}{6} = 0.60$$\n\nStep 3 — Plug into the formula. The mean is 1.2%, the value we are testing ($$\\mu_0$$) is 0:\n$$t = \\frac{1.2 - 0}{0.60} = \\frac{1.2}{0.60} = 2.00$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.33): this divides 1.2 by 3.6 and forgets to use the standard error — it ignores the sample size entirely.\n- B (1.20): this just uses the mean by itself with no division — forgetting the whole point of dividing by the standard error.\n- D (12.0): this divides 1.2 by 0.1, which would happen if you used $$3.6/36 = 0.1$$ instead of $$3.6/\\sqrt{36} = 0.6$$. The trap is forgetting the square root.\n\nAnalogy: the standard error is like the \"wobble\" in a measurement. The t-statistic asks \"how many wobbles away from zero are we?\" Two wobbles away is a meaningful distance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always take the square root of $$n$$ in the denominator. $$s/\\sqrt{n}$$, never $$s/n$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A test produces a p-value of 0.03. Which statement is TRUE?\nA) There is a 3% probability that $$H_0$$ is true. B) Reject $$H_0$$ at the 5% level but not at the 1% level. C) Reject $$H_0$$ at the 1% level but not at the 5% level. D) The power of the test is 97%.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is really asking: at which significance levels does a p-value of 0.03 lead us to reject the null?\n\nThe decision rule is simple: reject $$H_0$$ whenever the p-value is smaller than $$\\alpha$$ (the significance level).\n\n- At $$\\alpha = 5\\%$$ (i.e., 0.05): is $$0.03 < 0.05$$? Yes → reject.\n- At $$\\alpha = 1\\%$$ (i.e., 0.01): is $$0.03 < 0.01$$? No → fail to reject.\n\nSo we reject at 5% but not at 1%. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: this is the single most common misreading. The p-value is the probability of seeing data this extreme *if the null were true* — it is NOT the probability that the null is true. GARP loves to test this exact trap.\n- C: this reverses the logic. A smaller $$\\alpha$$ (1%) is a stricter bar, so it is harder to reject, not easier.\n- D: power has nothing to do with $$1 - p$$. Power is $$1 - \\beta$$ and depends on the true effect size and sample size, not on the p-value.\n\nAnalogy: the p-value is like the \"surprise level\" of your evidence assuming nothing special is going on. A surprise of 0.03 clears the everyday 5% bar but not the strict 1% bar.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Reject when p < α. And remember: the p-value is about the data, not about the probability the null is true.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"p = 0.03 decision\",\"items\":[\"reject at 5%\",\"|p = 0.03\",\"|α = 0.01 (1%)\",\"fail to reject at 1%\"],\"left\":\"smaller p = stronger evidence\",\"right\":\"larger p\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Lowering the significance level of a test from 5% to 1% while keeping the sample size fixed will:\nA) Decrease both Type I and Type II error probabilities. B) Decrease the Type I error probability but increase the Type II error probability. C) Increase the power of the test. D) Increase the size of the test.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what happens to the two kinds of errors when we make the test stricter (smaller $$\\alpha$$) but do not collect more data?\n\nKey definitions:\n- Type I error = rejecting a true null. Its probability equals the size $$\\alpha$$.\n- Type II error = failing to reject a false null. Its probability is $$\\beta$$.\n- Power = $$1 - \\beta$$.\n\nIf we move $$\\alpha$$ from 5% down to 1%, we are demanding stronger evidence before rejecting. This directly lowers the Type I error rate (it IS $$\\alpha$$). But because we now reject less often, we will also more often miss real effects — so the Type II error rate $$\\beta$$ rises. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: you cannot reduce both at once with a fixed sample — there is a trade-off. The only way to shrink both is to collect more data.\n- C: power = $$1 - \\beta$$; since $$\\beta$$ went up, power went down, not up.\n- D: the size of the test IS $$\\alpha$$. We just lowered it to 1%, so the size decreased, not increased.\n\nAnalogy: imagine a strict smoke alarm. Make it less sensitive (smaller $$\\alpha$$) and it cries \"fire!\" less often by mistake (fewer Type I errors), but it will also miss more real fires (more Type II errors).\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"The only free lunch is more data.\" For fixed n, lowering α raises β.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Lower α (5%→1%), fixed n\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Type I error (α)\",\"items\":[\"reject true H0\",\"equals the size\",\"goes DOWN to 1%\"]},{\"h\":\"Type II error (β)\",\"items\":[\"miss false H0\",\"power = 1−β\",\"goes UP (β rises)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A 95% confidence interval for a regression coefficient is $$(0.4, 2.6)$$. A two-sided 5% test of $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ would:\nA) Fail to reject, since the interval contains only positive values. B) Reject, since 0 lies outside the interval. C) Be inconclusive without the actual t-statistic. D) Reject only at the 1% level.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the duality between confidence intervals and hypothesis tests.\n\nThe rule: a two-sided test of size $$\\alpha$$ rejects $$H_0: \\beta = \\beta_0$$ exactly when $$\\beta_0$$ lies OUTSIDE the $$(1-\\alpha)$$ confidence interval. The interval is the complete set of null values that would NOT be rejected.\n\nHere the 95% interval is $$(0.4, 2.6)$$. Is the tested value 0 inside this interval? No — 0 is below 0.4, so it lies outside. Therefore we reject $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ at the 5% level. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: the fact that the interval contains positive values is exactly why 0 is excluded — that means reject, not fail to reject. The conclusion is backwards.\n- C: we do not need the t-statistic. The confidence interval already contains all the information; that is the whole point of the duality.\n- D: the interval is a 95% interval, which corresponds to the 5% test. It tells us nothing directly about the 1% level (that would need a 99% interval).\n\nAnalogy: the confidence interval is a \"fence\" around the believable values. If 0 is outside the fence, the data says 0 is not a believable value — so reject it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: 95% CI excludes the null value ⇔ reject at 5%. No extra calculation needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A strategy's 25 monthly returns average 0.80% with a sample standard deviation of 2.0%. Testing $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ against $$H_1: \\mu \\ne 0$$ at the 5% level (critical value $$t_{0.025,24} = 2.064$$), the correct decision is:\nA) Reject $$H_0$$; the mean return is significantly positive. B) Fail to reject $$H_0$$; no significant evidence of a nonzero mean. C) Reject $$H_0$$; the mean return is significantly negative. D) The test cannot be performed with only 25 observations.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: is an average return of 0.80% far enough from zero, given the noise, to be statistically convincing in a two-sided test?\n\nStep 1 — Standard error:\n$$\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{2.0}{\\sqrt{25}} = \\frac{2.0}{5} = 0.40$$\n\nStep 2 — Compute the t-statistic:\n$$t = \\frac{0.80 - 0}{0.40} = \\frac{0.80}{0.40} = 2.00$$\n\nStep 3 — Compare to the critical value. For a two-sided 5% test with $$24$$ degrees of freedom, the critical value is 2.064.\n$$2.00 < 2.064$$\n\nSince our statistic (2.00) does not exceed the critical value (2.064), we fail to reject. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: it is a close call (2.00 is near 2.064), but 2.00 does not clear the bar, so we cannot claim significance. The trap is rounding \"almost there\" up to \"significant.\"\n- C: the mean is positive (0.80%), so a \"significantly negative\" conclusion makes no sense even before the math.\n- D: 25 observations is perfectly fine — we just use the t-distribution with $$n - 1 = 24$$ degrees of freedom.\n\nNote the contrast: had this been a one-sided test, the critical value would be $$t_{0.05,24} = 1.711$$, and $$2.00 > 1.711$$ would reject. The one- vs. two-tail choice flips the answer here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Compare the t-statistic to the correct critical value before declaring significance — \"almost\" does not count.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A risk manager wants to test whether a fund's mean monthly return \"differs from\" zero. The appropriate setup is:\nA) A one-sided test with $$H_1: \\mu > 0$$. B) A one-sided test with $$H_1: \\mu < 0$$. C) A two-sided test with $$H_1: \\mu \\ne 0$$. D) No hypothesis test is needed; just report the mean.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is about reading the wording to choose between a one-sided and a two-sided test.\n\nThe phrase \"differs from zero\" does not specify a direction — the mean could be above OR below zero and still \"differ.\" Whenever the question cares about deviation in either direction, the alternative is $$H_1: \\mu \\ne 0$$, which is a two-sided test. The correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: $$H_1: \\mu > 0$$ is one-sided and would only be correct if the question asked whether the mean \"exceeds\" or is \"greater than\" zero.\n- B: $$H_1: \\mu < 0$$ is one-sided in the opposite direction — appropriate only for wording like \"is less than\" or \"is below\" zero.\n- D: reporting the mean alone does not tell you whether the difference from zero is statistically meaningful or just random noise. That is exactly what a test decides.\n\nAnalogy: \"differs from zero\" is like asking \"is this thermometer reading wrong?\" — it could read too high or too low, so you check both directions (two-sided). \"Exceeds zero\" is like asking \"is it hotter than freezing?\" — only one direction matters (one-sided).\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Differs / not equal to\" → two-sided (1.96 at 5%). \"Exceeds / greater / less\" → one-sided (1.645 at 5%).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Reading the wording\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Two-sided H1: μ≠0\",\"items\":[\"\\\"differs from\\\"\",\"both directions\",\"crit 1.96 at 5%\"]},{\"h\":\"One-sided H1: μ>0 or <0\",\"items\":[\"\\\"exceeds / less than\\\"\",\"one direction\",\"crit 1.645 at 5%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Desk A has 60 days of P&L with mean 1.5 and standard deviation 4. Desk B has 40 days with mean 0.2 and standard deviation 5. Testing whether the two desks have equal mean P&L (two-sided, 5%, critical value 1.96), the z-statistic and decision are closest to:\nA) z = 1.38; fail to reject equal means. B) z = 2.10; reject equal means. C) z = 0.26; fail to reject equal means. D) z = 1.96; reject equal means.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: is the gap between the two desks' average P&L (1.5 vs 0.2) big enough, given the noise, to be statistically real?\n\nStep 1 — The difference of the sample means:\n$$\\bar{X}_1 - \\bar{X}_2 = 1.5 - 0.2 = 1.3$$\n\nStep 2 — The standard error. We add up each desk's variance contribution (variance = standard deviation squared, divided by its sample size):\n$$SE = \\sqrt{\\frac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\frac{s_2^2}{n_2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4^2}{60} + \\frac{5^2}{40}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{60} + \\frac{25}{40}}$$\n\nCompute each piece:\n$$\\frac{16}{60} = 0.2667, \\qquad \\frac{25}{40} = 0.6250$$\n$$SE = \\sqrt{0.2667 + 0.6250} = \\sqrt{0.8917} = 0.9443$$\n\nStep 3 — The z-statistic (under $$H_0$$ the true difference is 0):\n$$z = \\frac{1.3 - 0}{0.9443} = 1.377 \\approx 1.38$$\n\nStep 4 — Compare to 1.96. Since $$1.38 < 1.96$$, fail to reject. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B (2.10): you would get this if you forgot to square the standard deviations or mis-added the variances, making the SE too small.\n- C (0.26): this comes from dividing 1.3 by 5+4 = 9 or some similar error that hugely overstates the SE.\n- D (1.96): this just copies the critical value rather than computing the statistic.\n\nAnalogy: the two desks' averages differ by 1.3, but the \"wobble\" in that difference is about 0.94. Since 1.3 is less than two wobbles away from zero, the gap could easily be luck.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For two means, the SE adds variance contributions: $$\\sqrt{s_1^2/n_1 + s_2^2/n_2}$$ — square the SDs, divide by each n, sum, then square-root.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Returns are normally distributed with known $$\\sigma = 2$$ and $$n = 16$$, so the standard error is 0.5. A one-sided 5% test of $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ against $$H_1: \\mu > 0$$ rejects when $$\\bar{X} > 0.8225$$. If the TRUE mean is $$\\mu = 1$$, the power of the test is closest to:\nA) 5% B) 36% C) 64% D) 95%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: if the true mean is really 1, what is the chance the test correctly detects it (i.e., rejects $$H_0$$)? That chance is the power.\n\nPower = probability that $$\\bar{X}$$ lands in the rejection region (above 0.8225) GIVEN the true mean is 1.\n\nStep 1 — Standardize the rejection threshold using the true mean (1) and the standard error (0.5):\n$$Z = \\frac{0.8225 - 1}{0.5} = \\frac{-0.1775}{0.5} = -0.355$$\n\nStep 2 — Power is the probability that $$Z$$ exceeds $$-0.355$$:\n$$\\text{Power} = P(Z > -0.355)$$\n\nBecause the normal curve is symmetric, $$P(Z > -0.355) = P(Z < 0.355) \\approx 0.639$$, i.e., about 64%. The correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (5%): that is $$\\alpha$$, the Type I error rate — the chance of rejecting when the null is actually true, not the power.\n- B (36%): that is $$\\beta$$, the Type II error rate ($$1 - 0.64 = 0.36$$) — the chance of MISSING the effect, the opposite of power.\n- D (95%): this would be the power only with a much larger sample. With $$n = 16$$ the test is not that powerful.\n\nAnalogy: power is how good a metal detector is at finding a coin that is really there. Here it finds the coin about 64% of the time and misses it about 36% of the time.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Power and β are flip sides: power + β = 1. To raise power, increase n, increase the true effect, or accept a larger α.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Decision vs truth\",\"x\":\"H0 true →\",\"y\":\"reject H0 →\",\"q\":[\"correct (1−α)\",\"Type II error (β=36%)\",\"Type I error (α=5%)\",\"power (1−β=64%)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** An analyst screens 20 independent trading signals, all genuinely useless, each tested at the 5% level. The probability that at least one signal appears \"significant\" by chance is closest to:\nA) 5% B) 36% C) 64% D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is about the multiple-testing problem: running many tests inflates the chance of at least one false positive.\n\nStep 1 — For a single test of a true null, the chance of NOT getting a false positive is $$1 - 0.05 = 0.95$$.\n\nStep 2 — For 20 independent tests, the chance that ALL of them correctly stay quiet is:\n$$0.95^{20}$$\n\nCompute: $$0.95^{20} = 0.3585$$ (about 36%).\n\nStep 3 — The chance of at least one false positive is the complement:\n$$P(\\text{at least one rejection}) = 1 - 0.95^{20} = 1 - 0.3585 = 0.6415 \\approx 64\\%$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (5%): this is the false-positive rate for a SINGLE test. With 20 tests the family-wide rate is much higher — that is the whole point.\n- B (36%): this is $$0.95^{20}$$, the probability of ZERO false positives, not at least one. It is the complement of the right answer.\n- D (100%): false positives are likely but not certain; the probability is 64%, not a guarantee.\n\nAnalogy: buying one lottery ticket gives you a small chance to win. Buy 20 tickets and your chance of winning *at least one* prize climbs a lot — even if each ticket is a long shot. Here \"winning\" is a false discovery.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Family-wide false-positive rate = $$1 - (1-\\alpha)^k$$. Bonferroni fix: test each at $$\\alpha/k$$ (here $$0.05/20 = 0.25\\%$$).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Family-wide false positive (k=20)\",\"steps\":[\"1 test stays quiet: 0.95\",\"All 20 quiet: 0.95^20 = 0.36\",\"At least one false +: 1−0.36 = 0.64\",\"Bonferroni fix: test each at α/20\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A VaR model should produce exceptions on 1% of days. Over 500 days a bank observes 9 exceptions ($$\\hat{p} = 0.018$$). The two-sided test of $$H_0: p = 0.01$$ gives a p-value of 0.0722. At the 5% level, the correct conclusion is:\nA) Reject $$H_0$$; the model is clearly broken. B) Fail to reject $$H_0$$; the exception rate is not statistically distinguishable from 1%. C) Reject $$H_0$$; there are too few exceptions. D) The p-value is meaningless because only 9 exceptions occurred.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: given a p-value of 0.0722, what do we decide at the 5% significance level?\n\nThe decision rule: reject $$H_0$$ only if the p-value is less than $$\\alpha$$.\n$$\\text{Is } 0.0722 < 0.05? \\quad \\text{No.}$$\n\nSince the p-value (0.0722) is larger than 0.05, we fail to reject. The observed exception rate of 1.8% is higher than the target 1%, but not by a statistically convincing margin. The correct answer is B.\n\nA consistency check via the confidence interval: the 95% CI for $$p$$ is\n$$0.018 \\pm 1.96\\sqrt{\\frac{0.018 \\times 0.982}{500}} = 0.018 \\pm 0.0116 = (0.64\\%,\\ 2.96\\%),$$\nwhich contains 1% — so the test and the CI agree (the duality holds).\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: 1.8% looks worse than 1%, but \"looks worse\" is not the same as \"statistically significant.\" The p-value clears 0.05, so we cannot reject.\n- C: there were MORE exceptions (1.8%) than expected (1%), not fewer — the direction is wrong.\n- D: the p-value is perfectly valid here; with 500 days and 9 exceptions the test is legitimate.\n\nAnalogy: the model misbehaved a little (9 exceptions instead of about 5), but in 500 noisy days that could easily be bad luck rather than a broken model. The evidence is not strong enough to convict.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Compare p-value to α. p = 0.0722 > 0.05 → fail to reject, even when the point estimate \"looks\" off-target.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A sample of 36 returns has mean 1.2% and standard deviation 3.6%. The t-statistic for $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ is:\nA) 0.33 B) 1.20 C) 2.00 D) 12.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$t = 1.2 / (3.6/\\sqrt{36}) = 1.2 / 0.6 = 2.0$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A test statistic has a p-value of 0.03. Which is TRUE?\nA) There is a 3% probability that $$H_0$$ is true B) Reject $$H_0$$ at 5% but not at 1% C) Reject $$H_0$$ at 1% but not at 5% D) The power of the test is 97%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Reject whenever p-value $$ 0.01$$. (A) is the classic misreading of a p-value.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Reducing the significance level from 5% to 1% with a fixed sample:\nA) Decreases both Type I and Type II error probabilities B) Decreases Type I but increases Type II error probability C) Increases power D) Increases the size of the test\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Smaller $$\\alpha$$ means fewer rejections: fewer false positives (Type I) but more missed detections (Type II); power falls.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A 95% confidence interval for a regression coefficient is $$(0.4, 2.6)$$. A two-sided 5% test of $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ would:\nA) Fail to reject, since the interval contains positive values B) Reject, since 0 lies outside the interval C) Be inconclusive without the t-statistic D) Reject only at the 1% level\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** By test–CI duality, any value outside the 95% CI is rejected at 5%; 0 is outside $$(0.4, 2.6)$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A strategy's 36 monthly excess returns average 1.2% with a sample standard deviation of 3.6%. Testing $$H_0\\!: \\mu = 0$$ against $$H_1\\!: \\mu \\neq 0$$ (two-tailed critical values: $$t_{35,2.5\\%} = 2.03$$, $$t_{35,5\\%} = 1.69$$), the best conclusion is:\nA) t = 2.00; reject $$H_0$$ at 5% because 2.00 > 1.96 B) t = 2.00; fail to reject $$H_0$$ at 5%, but reject at 10% C) t = 0.33; fail to reject $$H_0$$ at any conventional level D) t = 7.20; reject $$H_0$$ at both 5% and 10%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$t = \\frac{1.2 - 0}{3.6/\\sqrt{36}} = \\frac{1.2}{0.6} = 2.00$$\n\nWith 35 df, the 5% two-tailed critical value is 2.03, so 2.00 falls just short — fail to reject at 5%. At 10% the critical value is 1.69, so reject. Distractor A applies the normal value 1.96 despite the small-sample t-test — exactly the trap the question sets; C forgets $$\\sqrt{n}$$; D forgets to divide $$s$$ by $$\\sqrt{n}$$ at all.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"t = 2.00 vs critical values\",\"items\":[\"1.69 (10%)\",\"|2.00 (stat)\",\"2.03 (5%)\"],\"left\":\"fail to reject\",\"right\":\"reject\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A test of $$H_0\\!: \\mu = 0$$ is run at a 5% significance level and has power of 0.80. Which statement is correct?\nA) The probability of a Type II error is 0.20, and increasing the sample size would increase power B) The probability of a Type I error is 0.20, and increasing the sample size would reduce it C) Lowering the significance level to 1% while holding the sample fixed would increase power D) Power is the probability of rejecting $$H_0$$ when $$H_0$$ is true\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Power $$= 1 - \\beta$$, so $$\\beta = P(\\text{Type II}) = 0.20$$; more observations sharpen the test and raise power. B confuses the two error types ($$P(\\text{Type I}) = \\alpha = 0.05$$). C is backwards — a stricter significance level makes rejection harder and *lowers* power for a fixed sample. D defines a Type I error, not power: power conditions on $$H_0$$ being **false**.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Test outcomes\",\"x\":\"H0 true →\",\"y\":\"reject H0 →\",\"q\":[\"Type II error (β=0.20)\",\"correct (1−α)\",\"power (1−β=0.80)\",\"Type I error (α=0.05)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q17.** A risk manager tests whether daily return volatility exceeds 5%. From $$n = 20$$ observations, the sample standard deviation is 6%. Testing $$H_0\\!: \\sigma^2 = 0.0025$$ against $$H_1\\!: \\sigma^2 > 0.0025$$ at 5% (critical value $$\\chi^2_{19,\\,0.05} = 30.14$$), the best conclusion is:\nA) Test statistic 27.36; fail to reject $$H_0$$ — the evidence does not establish that volatility exceeds 5% B) Test statistic 27.36; reject $$H_0$$ — volatility significantly exceeds 5% C) Test statistic 28.80; fail to reject $$H_0$$ D) Test statistic 1.44; reject $$H_0$$ because 1.44 is less than 30.14\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Chi-square test of a variance:\n\n$$\\chi^2 = \\frac{(n-1)s^2}{\\sigma_0^2} = \\frac{19 \\times 0.0036}{0.0025} = \\frac{0.0684}{0.0025} = 27.36$$\n\nSince 27.36 < 30.14, fail to reject — a sample standard deviation of 6% from only 20 observations is not statistically distinguishable from 5%. Distractor C uses $$n = 20$$ instead of $$n - 1 = 19$$ (and even then 28.80 < 30.14 would still fail to reject); D is the variance ratio $$s^2/\\sigma_0^2$$ without the df scaling.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"χ² = 27.36 vs critical 30.14\",\"items\":[\"27.36 (stat)\",\"|30.14 (5% crit)\",\"reject region\"],\"left\":\"fail to reject\",\"right\":\"reject\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A portfolio manager's strategy produced 36 monthly excess returns with a sample mean of 0.45% and a sample standard deviation of 1.80%. She claims the strategy delivers a positive mean excess return. Using a one-sided test of $$H_0: \\mu \\le 0$$ versus $$H_1: \\mu > 0$$ at the 5% significance level (critical t value 1.690), what is the test statistic and the correct decision?\nA) t = 1.50; fail to reject — no significant positive return B) t = 1.50; reject — significant positive return C) t = 0.25; fail to reject — no significant positive return D) t = 9.00; reject — significant positive return\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the t-statistic and decision for a one-sided test that the mean excess return exceeds 0.\n\n1. Compute the standard error of the mean:\n$$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{1.80}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\frac{1.80}{6} = 0.30\\%$$\n2. Compute the test statistic:\n$$t = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{SE} = \\frac{0.45 - 0}{0.30} = 1.50$$\n3. Compare to the one-sided critical value 1.690. Since $$1.50 < 1.690$$, we **fail to reject** $$H_0$$ — the data do not provide significant evidence of a positive mean.\n\nWhy A is correct: the statistic is 1.50 and it does not clear even the (lenient) one-sided hurdle, so there is no significant evidence.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes the statistic correctly but flips the decision — 1.50 is below 1.690, so you cannot reject.\n- **C** divides by the raw standard deviation instead of the standard error ($$0.45/1.80 = 0.25$$) — it forgets the $$\\sqrt{n}$$ scaling.\n- **D** divides by $$s/n$$ instead of $$s/\\sqrt{n}$$ ($$0.45 / (1.80/36) = 0.45/0.05 = 9.0$$) — using $$n$$ instead of $$\\sqrt{n}$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always build the denominator as $$s/\\sqrt{n}$$. Forgetting the square root (or dropping it entirely) is the single most common arithmetic slip in mean tests.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A risk analyst tests 100 firm-level signals, all of which are in truth worthless, each at the 5% significance level using independent tests. The team is alarmed when several signals appear \"significant.\" Using the Bonferroni correction to control the family-wise error rate at 5%, what per-test significance level should be used, and approximately what is the probability of at least one false positive under the uncorrected procedure?\nA) 0.05% per test; ≈ 99.4% chance of at least one false positive uncorrected B) 0.5% per test; ≈ 99.4% chance of at least one false positive uncorrected C) 0.05% per test; ≈ 5% chance of at least one false positive uncorrected D) 5% per test; ≈ 40% chance of at least one false positive uncorrected\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for (i) the Bonferroni per-test level for $$k = 100$$ tests and (ii) the uncorrected family-wise false-positive probability.\n\n1. Bonferroni splits the target family-wise level evenly:\n$$\\alpha_{\\text{per test}} = \\frac{\\alpha}{k} = \\frac{0.05}{100} = 0.0005 = 0.05\\%$$\n2. Uncorrected family-wise probability of at least one false rejection, with $$k = 100$$ independent true nulls each at 5%:\n$$P(\\text{at least one}) = 1 - (1 - 0.05)^{100} = 1 - 0.95^{100}$$\n3. Compute $$0.95^{100}$$: $$\\ln(0.95) = -0.051293$$, so $$0.95^{100} = e^{-5.1293} = 0.00592$$.\n$$P = 1 - 0.00592 = 0.9941 \\approx 99.4\\%$$\n\nWhy A is correct: $$0.05/100 = 0.05\\%$$ and the inflated false-positive rate is about 99.4%.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** uses $$0.05/10$$ logic (0.5%) — wrong $$k$$.\n- **C** correctly states the per-test level but reports 5% for the family-wise rate — that is the per-test rate, not the inflated family-wide one.\n- **D** fails to correct at all (5% per test) and quotes the $$k=10$$ figure (40.1%) instead of $$k=100$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bonferroni divides ($$\\alpha/k$$); the inflation formula is $$1-(1-\\alpha)^k$$, which climbs fast — at $$k=100$$ a false positive is virtually guaranteed.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Multiple testing (k=100, α=5%)\",\"steps\":[\"Bonferroni per-test: α/k = 0.05/100 = 0.05%\",\"Uncorrected: P(≥1 false +) = 1−0.95^100\",\"0.95^100 ≈ 0.006\",\"≈ 99.4% chance of a false positive\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A trader observes a sample of $$n = 64$$ daily P&L figures with mean $1{,}200 and known population standard deviation $4{,}800. He wants to test $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ against $$H_1: \\mu \\ne 0$$. Which statement about the 95% two-sided confidence interval and the corresponding test is correct?\nA) The 95% CI is $(\\$24,\\,\\$2{,}376)$; the test rejects $$H_0$$ at 5% B) The 95% CI is $(-\\$176,\\,\\$2{,}576)$; the test fails to reject $$H_0$$ at 5% C) The 95% CI is $(-\\$2{,}500,\\,\\$4{,}900)$; the test fails to reject $$H_0$$ at 5% D) The 95% CI is $(-\\$1{,}176,\\,\\$3{,}576)$; the test fails to reject $$H_0$$ at 5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the 95% two-sided CI for the mean and what the linked test concludes.\n\n1. Standard error (variance known, so use z):\n$$SE = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{4800}{\\sqrt{64}} = \\frac{4800}{8} = 600$$\n2. Two-sided 95% CI uses $$z_{0.025} = 1.96$$:\n$$\\bar{X} \\pm 1.96 \\times SE = 1200 \\pm 1.96 \\times 600 = 1200 \\pm 1176$$\n$$= (\\$24,\\; \\$2{,}376)$$\n3. By test–CI duality, a two-sided 5% test rejects $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ exactly when 0 lies outside the 95% CI. Since 0 < 24, it lies outside the interval, so **reject** $$H_0$$.\n\nCheck via the statistic: $$z = 1200/600 = 2.00 > 1.96$$ ⇒ reject. Consistent.\n\nWhy A is correct: margin $$1.96 \\times 600 = 1176$$ gives $(24, 2376)$, which excludes 0, so reject.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** overstates the margin (interval $$1200 \\pm 1376$$) so the lower bound goes negative and 0 falls inside, leading to a wrong \"fail to reject.\" The correct margin is $$1.96 \\times 600 = 1176$$, not larger.\n- **C** uses the wrong SE by dividing by $$n$$ implicitly or using $$\\sigma = 4800$$ directly, then a $$1.96 \\times \\sigma$$ margin ($$1200 \\pm 3700$$-ish), producing a huge interval that swallows 0 and wrongly fails to reject — the SE must be $$\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$.\n- **D** uses a margin near $$1.96 \\times 1200$$ (interval $$1200 \\pm 2376$$) — applying the multiplier to the mean instead of the SE, so 0 falls inside and the test wrongly fails to reject.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CI–test duality is a free check: build the two-sided CI with $$z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96$$ and ask whether the null value is inside. Outside ⇒ reject. Inside ⇒ fail to reject.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Two trading desks are compared on independent samples. Desk A: $$n_1 = 50$$ days, mean daily P&L $80$ (thousands), $$s_1 = 30$$. Desk B: $$n_2 = 75$$ days, mean daily P&L $68$, $$s_2 = 24$$. Test $$H_0: \\mu_1 - \\mu_2 = 0$$ against a two-sided alternative at 5%. What is the test statistic and decision?\nA) z = 2.34; reject — the desks' mean P&Ls differ significantly B) z = 2.34; fail to reject — no significant difference C) z = 1.71; fail to reject — no significant difference D) z = 0.44; fail to reject — no significant difference\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the difference-of-means statistic and the two-sided 5% decision.\n\n1. Difference of sample means: $$80 - 68 = 12$$.\n2. Standard error sums the variance contributions:\n$$SE = \\sqrt{\\frac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\frac{s_2^2}{n_2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{30^2}{50} + \\frac{24^2}{75}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{900}{50} + \\frac{576}{75}}$$\n$$= \\sqrt{18 + 7.68} = \\sqrt{25.68} = 5.068$$\n3. Statistic:\n$$z = \\frac{12 - 0}{5.068} = 2.368 \\approx 2.34$$\n4. Two-sided 5% critical value is 1.96. Since $$2.37 > 1.96$$, **reject** $$H_0$$ — the means differ significantly.\n\nWhy A is correct: SE ≈ 5.07 and the statistic ≈ 2.34 clears 1.96.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes the statistic correctly but flips the decision (2.34 > 1.96 ⇒ reject).\n- **C** pools the standard deviations incorrectly or uses $$\\sqrt{(s_1^2+s_2^2)/(n_1+n_2)}$$, deflating the statistic.\n- **D** divides by the *sum of standard errors of each mean* improperly or by $$s$$ rather than building SE from variances — undershoots dramatically.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For independent samples the SE is the square root of the **sum of variance-over-n terms**; never add standard deviations or pool unless told the variances are equal.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A VaR model is designed so exceptions occur on 2% of days. Over $$n = 250$$ trading days, 9 exceptions are observed ($$\\hat{p} = 0.036$$). A two-sided test of $$H_0: p = 0.02$$ produces a test statistic of $$z = 1.808$$, with $$P(Z > 1.808) = 0.0353$$. Which statement is correct at the 5% level?\nA) The p-value is 0.0706; fail to reject — the exception rate is not significantly different from 2% B) The p-value is 0.0353; reject — the exception rate is significantly different from 2% C) The p-value is 0.0706; reject — there is a 7.06% chance the null is true D) The p-value is 0.9647; fail to reject — the model is validated\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the correct p-value and decision for a two-sided test given $$z = 1.808$$.\n\n1. For a two-sided test, the p-value doubles the one-tail probability:\n$$\\text{p-value} = 2 \\times P(Z > 1.808) = 2 \\times 0.0353 = 0.0706$$\n2. Decision rule: reject when p-value $$< \\alpha$$. Here $$0.0706 > 0.05$$, so **fail to reject** $$H_0$$.\n\nWhy A is correct: two-sided doubling gives 0.0706, which exceeds 0.05 ⇒ fail to reject.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** uses the one-tailed p-value (0.0353) for a two-sided test, then wrongly rejects.\n- **C** has the right p-value but misinterprets it as \"the probability the null is true\" — the p-value is computed *assuming* $$H_0$$ is true; it is not the probability of $$H_0$$.\n- **D** reports $$P(Z < 1.808) = 0.9647$$ — the wrong tail entirely — and adds the loaded word \"validated.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two-tailed ⇒ double the single-tail probability. And memorize the wording: the p-value is \"probability of data this extreme *given* $$H_0$$,\" never \"probability $$H_0$$ is true.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Two-sided p-value decision\",\"steps\":[\"One-tail prob = 0.0353\",\"Two-sided: double it → 0.0706\",\"Compare to α = 0.05\",\"0.0706 > 0.05 → fail to reject\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A one-sided test of $$H_0: \\mu = 0$$ versus $$H_1: \\mu > 0$$ uses returns that are $$N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$$ with known $$\\sigma = 4$$. The sample size is $$n = 25$$, and the test runs at the 5% level (critical z = 1.645). If the true mean is $$\\mu = 1.5$$, what is the power of the test? (Use $$P(Z > -0.27) = 0.6064$$.)\nA) ≈ 39.4% B) ≈ 60.6% C) ≈ 50.0% D) ≈ 95.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We are asked for the power of a one-sided test under a specific true mean.\n\n1. Standard error of the sample mean:\n$$SE = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}} = \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$$\n2. The test rejects when the sample mean exceeds the critical threshold:\n$$\\bar{X}_{\\text{crit}} = 0 + 1.645 \\times 0.8 = 1.316$$\n3. Power is the probability of rejecting (i.e., $$\\bar{X} > 1.316$$) when the true mean is 1.5:\n$$\\text{Power} = P\\!\\left(Z > \\frac{1.316 - 1.5}{0.8}\\right) = P\\!\\left(Z > \\frac{-0.184}{0.8}\\right) = P(Z > -0.23) \\approx P(Z > -0.27)$$\n$$\\approx 0.6064 \\approx 60.6\\%$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the rejection threshold (1.316) sits below the true mean (1.5), so more than half the sampling distribution falls in the rejection region — power ≈ 60.6%.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **A** is $$\\beta$$ (Type II error) $$= 1 - 0.606 = 0.394$$ — confusing power with its complement.\n- **C** assumes power = 50% (the threshold equal to the true mean) — but the threshold is below the true mean.\n- **D** confuses power with the confidence level / $$1-\\alpha$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Power = $$P(\\text{reject} \\mid H_1 \\text{ true})$$. Find the critical value of $$\\bar X$$ under $$H_0$$, then standardize it using the *true* mean. Power rises with $$n$$, with a larger true deviation, and with a larger $$\\alpha$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Decision vs truth (this test)\",\"x\":\"H0 true →\",\"y\":\"reject H0 →\",\"q\":[\"correct (1−α)\",\"Type II error β = 39.4%\",\"Type I error α = 5%\",\"power 1−β = 60.6%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** An analyst keeps the significance level fixed at 5% but increases the sample size from 25 to 100, with everything else unchanged. Which combination of effects on the test correctly describes the consequences?\nA) The size stays at 5%, power increases, and the probability of a Type II error decreases B) The size decreases below 5%, power increases, and the Type II error decreases C) The size stays at 5%, power decreases, and the Type II error increases D) The size increases above 5%, power and Type II error both increase\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked how a larger sample (with $$\\alpha$$ fixed) affects size, power, and Type II error.\n\n1. The **size** $$\\alpha$$ is chosen by the tester. Holding it at 5% means the Type I error rate stays exactly 5% regardless of $$n$$.\n2. Larger $$n$$ shrinks the standard error $$\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$, so the sampling distribution tightens. This makes it easier to detect a true deviation from $$H_0$$ ⇒ **power increases**.\n3. Power $$= 1 - \\beta$$, so if power rises, the **Type II error $$\\beta$$ falls**.\n\nWhy A is correct: only the quantities affected by precision (power, $$\\beta$$) change; the chosen size is invariant to $$n$$.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** wrongly claims the size falls — size is set by the tester, not by $$n$$.\n- **C** reverses the power effect; more data raises, not lowers, power.\n- **D** wrongly inflates the size and contradictorily raises both power and $$\\beta$$ (they are complements and cannot both rise).\n\n📌 Exam tip: More data is the \"free lunch\": it boosts power and cuts $$\\beta$$ while leaving the chosen size unchanged. By contrast, *lowering* $$\\alpha$$ for fixed $$n$$ trades less Type I error for more Type II error.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Larger n (25→100), α fixed at 5%\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Unchanged\",\"items\":[\"size α stays 5%\",\"Type I error = 5%\",\"tester chooses α, not n\"]},{\"h\":\"Improves\",\"items\":[\"SE = σ/√n shrinks\",\"power INCREASES\",\"Type II error β DECREASES\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A two-sided 99% confidence interval for the mean monthly alpha of a fund, built from a large sample, is $(-0.10\\%,\\; 0.50\\%)$. Based only on this interval, which conclusion is justified?\nA) A two-sided test of $$H_0: \\alpha = 0$$ fails to reject at the 1% level, since 0 lies inside the interval B) A two-sided test of $$H_0: \\alpha = 0$$ rejects at the 1% level, since the interval is mostly positive C) There is a 99% probability that the true alpha lies between $$-0.10\\%$$ and $$0.50\\%$$ for this particular sample D) A two-sided test of $$H_0: \\alpha = 0.50\\%$$ fails to reject at the 1% level\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked what a single confidence interval lets us conclude about a hypothesis test.\n\n1. Test–CI duality: a two-sided test of size $$\\alpha$$ rejects $$H_0: \\mu = \\mu_0$$ exactly when $$\\mu_0$$ lies **outside** the $$(1-\\alpha)$$ CI. The 99% CI corresponds to a 1% two-sided test.\n2. The null value 0 lies **inside** $(-0.10\\%, 0.50\\%)$, so the test **fails to reject** $$H_0: \\alpha = 0$$ at the 1% level.\n\nWhy A is correct: 0 is inside the interval, so by duality the 1% test cannot reject.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** rejects because the interval \"leans positive\" — irrelevant; what matters is whether 0 is inside (it is).\n- **C** is the classic CI misinterpretation. The frequentist 99% refers to the long-run coverage of the *procedure*; for one realized interval the true alpha either is or is not inside — there is no 99% probability attached to this specific interval.\n- **D** addresses $$H_0: \\alpha = 0.50\\%$$. The value 0.50% is exactly the boundary of the interval, a borderline case — and 0.50% is not safely \"inside,\" so this is at best ambiguous and not a clean \"fail to reject.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Reject $$H_0: \\mu = \\mu_0$$ ⇔ $$\\mu_0$$ is outside the matching CI. And never say \"99% probability the parameter is in *this* interval\" — the probability statement is about the method across repeated samples.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"99% CI (−0.10%, 0.50%) vs H0: α=0\",\"items\":[\"−0.10% lower bound\",\"|0 (null value) — INSIDE\",\"0.50% upper bound\"],\"left\":\"inside ⇒ fail to reject at 1%\",\"right\":\"outside ⇒ reject\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"207","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Linear Regression","notes":"# 7. Linear Regression\n\nSingle-variable OLS: the slope/intercept formulas, the OLS assumptions, interpreting coefficients, and $$R^2$$. You should be able to compute $$\\hat\\beta$$ from covariances and test a slope coefficient cold.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the models that can be estimated using linear regression and differentiate them from those that cannot.\n- Interpret the results of an ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with a single explanatory variable.\n- Describe the key assumptions of OLS parameter estimation.\n- Characterize the properties of OLS estimators and their sampling distributions.\n- Construct, apply, and interpret hypothesis tests and confidence intervals for a single regression coefficient.\n\n## The model\n\n$$Y_i = \\alpha + \\beta X_i + \\epsilon_i$$\n\n- $$Y$$: dependent (explained) variable; $$X$$: independent (explanatory) variable; $$\\epsilon$$: error/shock capturing everything else.\n- \"Linear\" means **linear in the parameters**. $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X^2 + \\epsilon$$ and $$\\ln Y = \\alpha + \\beta \\ln X + \\epsilon$$ are fine (transform the data); $$Y = \\alpha + X^\\beta + \\epsilon$$ is not estimable by OLS.\n\n## OLS estimators\n\nOLS minimizes the sum of squared residuals $$\\sum \\hat\\epsilon_i^2$$. The solutions:\n\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)(Y_i - \\bar Y)}{\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)^2} = \\frac{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(X, Y)}{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(X)} = \\rho_{XY}\\frac{s_Y}{s_X}$$\n\n$$\\hat\\alpha = \\bar Y - \\hat\\beta \\bar X$$\n\nThe fitted line always passes through $$(\\bar X, \\bar Y)$$; residuals sum to zero and are uncorrelated with $$X$$ in-sample. The CAPM beta is exactly this estimator with market excess returns as $$X$$.\n\n**Interpretation:** $$\\hat\\beta$$ = expected change in $$Y$$ per one-unit change in $$X$$; $$\\hat\\alpha$$ = expected $$Y$$ when $$X = 0$$.\n\n## Key OLS assumptions\n\n1. The mean of the error is zero given $$X$$: $$E[\\epsilon \\mid X] = 0$$ (exogeneity — $$X$$ uncorrelated with the error).\n2. $$(X_i, Y_i)$$ pairs are iid draws (random sampling).\n3. The variance of $$X$$ is positive and large outliers are unlikely (finite fourth moments).\n4. Constant error variance, $$\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon \\mid X) = \\sigma^2$$ (homoskedasticity), and no correlation across errors — needed for the textbook standard errors and efficiency (Gauss–Markov: OLS is BLUE).\n\nUnder 1–3, OLS is unbiased and consistent, and by the CLT $$\\hat\\beta$$ is approximately normal in large samples.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Violating homoskedasticity (assumption 4) does **not** bias $$\\hat\\beta$$ — it invalidates the *standard errors* and thus the t-tests. Violating exogeneity (assumption 1) is the deadly one: it biases the coefficient itself. Know which assumption breaks what.\n\n## Goodness of fit\n\nTotal variation splits into explained and residual parts:\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\sum (Y_i - \\bar Y)^2}_{TSS} = \\underbrace{\\sum (\\hat Y_i - \\bar Y)^2}_{ESS} + \\underbrace{\\sum \\hat\\epsilon_i^2}_{RSS}$$\n\n$$R^2 = \\frac{ESS}{TSS} = 1 - \\frac{RSS}{TSS}$$\n\nIn single-variable regression, $$R^2 = \\rho_{XY}^2$$. The **standard error of the regression** is $$s_\\epsilon = \\sqrt{RSS/(n-2)}$$ (two parameters estimated).\n\n## Inference on coefficients\n\n$$t = \\frac{\\hat\\beta - \\beta_0}{SE(\\hat\\beta)} \\sim t_{n-2}$$\n\nMost often $$\\beta_0 = 0$$ (is $$X$$ relevant at all?). Confidence interval:\n\n$$\\hat\\beta \\pm t_{\\alpha/2,\\,n-2}\\,SE(\\hat\\beta)$$\n\nReject $$H_0: \\beta = \\beta_0$$ at level $$\\alpha$$ exactly when $$\\beta_0$$ falls outside the interval.\n\n### Worked example: estimating beta from moments\n\nStock and market excess returns give: $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(R_s, R_m) = 0.0024$$, $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(R_m) = 0.0016$$, $$\\bar R_s = 1.0\\%$$, $$\\bar R_m = 0.5\\%$$.\n\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{0.0024}{0.0016} = 1.50$$\n\n$$\\hat\\alpha = 1.0 - 1.50 \\times 0.5 = 0.25\\%$$\n\nFitted model: $$\\hat R_s = 0.25 + 1.50\\,R_m$$. If $$\\rho = 0.6$$ between stock and market, $$R^2 = 0.36$$: the market explains 36% of the stock's variance.\n\n### Worked example: testing a slope\n\nA regression on $$n = 62$$ observations yields $$\\hat\\beta = 0.85$$ with $$SE(\\hat\\beta) = 0.30$$.\n\n**Test $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ (5%, two-sided):**\n\n$$t = \\frac{0.85}{0.30} = 2.833$$\n\nWith 60 d.o.f. the critical value is 2.000; $$2.833 > 2.000$$, so reject — $$X$$ is significant.\n\n**Test $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$ (e.g., is the hedge ratio one?):**\n\n$$t = \\frac{0.85 - 1}{0.30} = -0.50$$\n\n$$\\lvert -0.50 \\rvert < 2.000$$: fail to reject — statistically indistinguishable from a one-for-one hedge.\n\n**95% CI:** $$0.85 \\pm 2.000 \\times 0.30 = (0.25,\\, 1.45)$$ — contains 1, excludes 0, matching both tests.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> \"Significant\" answers come straight off the t-table: with large $$n$$, $$\\lvert t \\rvert > 1.96$$ at 5% and $$> 2.58$$ at 1%. If GARP gives a coefficient and its SE, they almost certainly want $$t = \\hat\\beta / SE$$.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What does \"linear\" mean in linear regression, and give an estimable vs. non-estimable example.\n> **A:** Linear in the PARAMETERS. $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X^2 + \\epsilon$$ and $$\\ln Y = \\alpha + \\beta\\ln X + \\epsilon$$ are estimable (transform the data); $$Y = \\alpha + X^\\beta + \\epsilon$$ is not.\n\n**Q2.** Give the OLS slope estimator in its three equivalent forms.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\beta = \\dfrac{\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)(Y_i - \\bar Y)}{\\sum (X_i - \\bar X)^2} = \\dfrac{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(X,Y)}{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(X)} = \\rho_{XY}\\dfrac{s_Y}{s_X}$$.\n\n**Q3.** Give the OLS intercept and two in-sample properties of the fitted line.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\alpha = \\bar Y - \\hat\\beta\\bar X$$. The line passes through $$(\\bar X, \\bar Y)$$; residuals sum to zero and are uncorrelated with $$X$$.\n\n**Q4.** List the key OLS assumptions.\n> **A:** (1) $$E[\\epsilon \\mid X] = 0$$ (exogeneity); (2) $$(X_i, Y_i)$$ iid; (3) positive variance of $$X$$, finite fourth moments; (4) homoskedasticity $$\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon \\mid X) = \\sigma^2$$ and no error correlation.\n\n**Q5.** Which assumption violation biases the coefficient, and which only corrupts the standard errors?\n> **A:** Violating exogeneity (assumption 1) biases $$\\hat\\beta$$ — the deadly one. Violating homoskedasticity does NOT bias $$\\hat\\beta$$; it invalidates the standard errors and t-tests.\n\n**Q6.** Give the total-variation decomposition and the formula for $$R^2$$.\n> **A:** $$TSS = ESS + RSS$$; $$R^2 = \\dfrac{ESS}{TSS} = 1 - \\dfrac{RSS}{TSS}$$. In single-variable regression $$R^2 = \\rho_{XY}^2$$.\n\n**Q7.** Give the standard error of the regression.\n> **A:** $$s_\\epsilon = \\sqrt{RSS/(n-2)}$$ — two parameters estimated.\n\n**Q8.** Give the t-statistic and CI for a single regression coefficient.\n> **A:** $$t = \\dfrac{\\hat\\beta - \\beta_0}{SE(\\hat\\beta)} \\sim t_{n-2}$$; CI $$= \\hat\\beta \\pm t_{\\alpha/2, n-2}\\,SE(\\hat\\beta)$$.\n\n**Q9.** $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(R_s, R_m) = 0.0024$$, $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(R_m) = 0.0016$$. Find beta.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\beta = 0.0024/0.0016 = 1.50$$ (this is exactly the CAPM beta estimator).\n\n**Q10.** $$s_X = 4\\%$$, $$s_Y = 6\\%$$, $$\\rho_{XY} = 0.5$$. Find the OLS slope of $$Y$$ on $$X$$.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\beta = \\rho\\dfrac{s_Y}{s_X} = 0.5 \\times 6/4 = 0.75$$.\n\n**Q11.** $$\\hat\\beta = 0.85$$, $$SE = 0.30$$, $$n = 62$$ (≈60 d.o.f., crit 2.0). Test $$H_0:\\beta = 0$$ and $$H_0:\\beta = 1$$.\n> **A:** $$\\beta=0$$: $$t = 0.85/0.30 = 2.83 > 2.0$$ → reject (significant). $$\\beta=1$$: $$t = (0.85-1)/0.30 = -0.50$$ → fail to reject (indistinguishable from a one-for-one hedge).\n\n**Q12.** In single-variable OLS, $$R^2 = 0.49$$. What is the correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$?\n> **A:** $$\\rho = \\pm\\sqrt{0.49} = \\pm 0.70$$; the sign matches the slope's sign.\n\n**Q13.** Interpret $$\\hat\\beta$$ and $$\\hat\\alpha$$.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\beta$$ = expected change in $$Y$$ per one-unit change in $$X$$; $$\\hat\\alpha$$ = expected $$Y$$ when $$X = 0$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** In a single-variable OLS regression, the standard deviation of $$X$$ is 4%, the standard deviation of $$Y$$ is 6%, and the correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$ is 0.5. The OLS slope of $$Y$$ on $$X$$ is:\nA) 0.33 B) 0.50 C) 0.75 D) 1.33\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: how much does $$Y$$ change for a one-unit change in $$X$$, given the correlation and the two standard deviations?\n\nStep 1 — Use the slope formula written in terms of correlation:\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\rho_{XY}\\,\\frac{s_Y}{s_X}$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in the numbers ($$\\rho = 0.5$$, $$s_Y = 6$$, $$s_X = 4$$):\n$$\\hat\\beta = 0.5 \\times \\frac{6}{4} = 0.5 \\times 1.5 = 0.75$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.33): this divides $$s_X/s_Y$$ the wrong way around or computes $$0.5 \\times 4/6$$. Remember $$s_Y$$ goes on top.\n- B (0.50): this is just the correlation $$\\rho$$, forgetting to scale by the ratio of standard deviations.\n- D (1.33): this is $$s_Y/s_X = 6/4$$ without multiplying by the correlation 0.5.\n\nAnalogy: the slope is the correlation \"stretched\" by how spread out $$Y$$ is compared to $$X$$. Half the correlation, stretched by 1.5, gives 0.75.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\hat\\beta = \\rho\\,\\frac{s_Y}{s_X}$$. The dependent variable's SD ($$s_Y$$) is always on top.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Stock and market excess returns give $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(R_s, R_m) = 0.0024$$ and $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(R_m) = 0.0016$$. The CAPM beta (the OLS slope of stock on market) is:\nA) 0.67 B) 1.00 C) 1.50 D) 0.0040\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the regression slope (the stock's beta), given the covariance with the market and the market's variance.\n\nStep 1 — The slope formula in covariance/variance form:\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(R_s, R_m)}{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(R_m)}$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in:\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{0.0024}{0.0016} = 1.50$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.67): this flips the fraction ($$0.0016/0.0024$$) — variance on top instead of covariance.\n- B (1.00): a \"default\" beta of 1, ignoring the actual numbers.\n- D (0.0040): this adds the two figures ($$0.0024 + 0.0016$$) instead of dividing.\n\nAnalogy: beta measures how much the stock \"swings\" with the market. Covariance is the shared movement; dividing by the market's own variance scales it into a per-unit sensitivity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Beta = Cov(stock, market) ÷ Var(market). Covariance on top, the X-variable's variance on the bottom.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In a single-variable OLS regression, the $$R^2$$ is 0.49. The correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$ is:\nA) 0.49 B) $$\\pm 0.70$$ C) 0.24 D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to back out the correlation from $$R^2$$ in a single-variable regression.\n\nKey fact: in a regression with ONE explanatory variable, $$R^2$$ equals the squared correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$:\n$$R^2 = \\rho_{XY}^2$$\n\nStep 1 — Solve for the correlation by taking the square root:\n$$\\rho_{XY} = \\pm\\sqrt{R^2} = \\pm\\sqrt{0.49} = \\pm 0.70$$\n\nThe square root has two possible signs, so the correlation is $$\\pm 0.70$$. The sign matches the sign of the slope, which we are not told. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.49): this confuses $$R^2$$ with $$\\rho$$; $$R^2$$ is the SQUARE of the correlation, not the correlation itself.\n- C (0.24): this squares 0.49 again ($$0.49^2 \\approx 0.24$$), going the wrong way.\n- D: the correlation's MAGNITUDE is perfectly determined ($$0.70$$); only its sign is unknown, so \"cannot be determined\" overstates the uncertainty.\n\nAnalogy: $$R^2$$ is like the area of a square. To get back the side length (the correlation) you take a square root — and a square's side could correspond to either a positive or negative original number.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Single-variable regression: $$\\rho = \\pm\\sqrt{R^2}$$. The magnitude is fixed; the sign follows the slope.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Back out ρ from R²\",\"steps\":[\"Single-variable: R² = ρ²\",\"ρ = ±√R² = ±√0.49\",\"Magnitude = 0.70 (fixed)\",\"Sign = sign of slope (unknown) → ±0.70\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Errors in an OLS regression are heteroskedastic (their variance depends on $$X$$). The consequence is:\nA) The slope estimates are biased. B) The slope estimates are unbiased, but the usual standard errors are invalid. C) The slope estimates become inconsistent. D) The $$R^2$$ is biased downward.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what damage heteroskedasticity does to an OLS regression.\n\nHeteroskedasticity means the spread of the errors changes with $$X$$ (e.g., bigger firms have noisier returns). The key facts:\n- OLS coefficients stay UNBIASED and consistent — the point estimates are still fine.\n- But the textbook formula for the standard errors assumes constant variance, so those standard errors become wrong (usually too small). That makes the t-tests and F-tests misleading.\n\nSo the coefficients survive but the inference does not. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: heteroskedasticity does NOT bias the slope. The deadly assumption for bias is exogeneity ($$E[\\epsilon|X]=0$$), not constant variance.\n- C: OLS remains consistent under heteroskedasticity; it just is not efficient (no longer BLUE).\n- D: $$R^2$$ is not biased by heteroskedasticity; the problem is with the standard errors, not the goodness-of-fit measure.\n\nAnalogy: heteroskedasticity is like a bathroom scale that is correct on average but jumps around more for heavy people. Your average reading is still right (unbiased), but your stated \"± margin of error\" is wrong.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Heteroskedasticity → unbiased coefficients, invalid SEs. Fix with White (robust) standard errors. Omitted-variable bias is the one that biases coefficients.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Heteroskedasticity effects\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Coefficients (β̂)\",\"items\":[\"still UNBIASED\",\"still consistent\",\"point estimates fine\"]},{\"h\":\"Standard errors\",\"items\":[\"INVALID (usually too small)\",\"t/F tests misleading\",\"fix: White robust SEs\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** \"Linear regression\" requires the model to be linear in the parameters. Which of the following CANNOT be estimated by OLS?\nA) $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X^2 + \\epsilon$$ B) $$\\ln Y = \\alpha + \\beta \\ln X + \\epsilon$$ C) $$Y = \\alpha + X^\\beta + \\epsilon$$ D) $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X + \\epsilon$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests what \"linear\" really means in linear regression. It means linear in the PARAMETERS ($$\\alpha, \\beta$$), not necessarily in the variables.\n\nCheck each model — can we write it as (parameter) × (some transformed variable) + (parameter)?\n- A: $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X^2$$. Treat $$X^2$$ as a single variable; then $$\\beta$$ multiplies it linearly. Estimable. ✓\n- B: $$\\ln Y = \\alpha + \\beta \\ln X$$. Transform both sides into logs; then it is a straight line in those logged variables. Estimable. ✓\n- C: $$Y = \\alpha + X^\\beta$$. Here $$\\beta$$ sits in the EXPONENT, not as a multiplier. There is no way to transform this into something linear in $$\\beta$$. NOT estimable by OLS. ✗\n- D: $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X$$. The plain straight line. Estimable. ✓\n\nThe one that cannot be done is C. The correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong (i.e., why they ARE estimable):\n- A and B are nonlinear in the VARIABLES but linear in the parameters once you transform the data — perfectly fine for OLS.\n- D is the textbook linear model — obviously estimable.\n\nAnalogy: OLS cares about how the dials ($$\\alpha, \\beta$$) enter the machine. If a dial multiplies an input, fine. If a dial becomes a power (an exponent), the machine cannot be solved by ordinary least squares.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Linear\" = linear in the parameters. A parameter in the exponent ($$X^\\beta$$) breaks OLS; powers/logs of the variables do not.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which CANNOT be estimated by OLS?\",\"options\":[\"Y = α + βX² + ε\",\"ln Y = α + β ln X + ε\",\"Y = α + X^β + ε\",\"Y = α + βX + ε\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"β sits in the exponent → not linear in the parameter; others are linear in α,β after transforming variables\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A regression on 62 observations gives $$\\hat\\beta = 0.85$$ with $$SE(\\hat\\beta) = 0.30$$. Testing $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ (two-sided, 5%, critical value 2.000), the t-statistic and decision are:\nA) t = 2.83; reject $$H_0$$ — $$X$$ is significant. B) t = 2.83; fail to reject $$H_0$$. C) t = 0.85; fail to reject $$H_0$$. D) t = 0.30; reject $$H_0$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks whether the slope is statistically different from zero.\n\nStep 1 — The t-statistic for testing $$\\beta = 0$$:\n$$t = \\frac{\\hat\\beta - 0}{SE(\\hat\\beta)} = \\frac{0.85}{0.30} = 2.833$$\n\nStep 2 — Compare to the critical value. With $$n - 2 = 60$$ degrees of freedom the two-sided 5% critical value is 2.000.\n$$2.833 > 2.000$$\n\nSince the statistic exceeds the critical value, reject $$H_0$$ — $$X$$ is a significant predictor. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B: the t-statistic of 2.83 is correct, but exceeding 2.000 means REJECT, not fail to reject. The decision is backwards.\n- C (0.85): this just reports the coefficient as if it were the t-statistic, skipping the division by the SE.\n- D (0.30): this reports the SE instead of computing the ratio.\n\nAnalogy: the t-statistic asks \"how many standard errors is the slope away from zero?\" Here it is 2.83 — clearly more than the 2.0 cutoff, so the slope is convincingly nonzero.\n\n📌 Exam tip: t = coefficient ÷ its SE. Degrees of freedom in single-variable regression are $$n - 2$$ (two parameters estimated).\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Using the same regression ($$\\hat\\beta = 0.85$$, $$SE = 0.30$$, critical value 2.000), a risk manager tests whether the hedge ratio equals one: $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$. The conclusion is:\nA) Reject; the hedge ratio is significantly different from one. B) Fail to reject; the estimate is statistically indistinguishable from one. C) The test cannot be done unless $$\\beta_0 = 0$$. D) Reject; the hedge ratio is significantly greater than one.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks whether the estimated slope of 0.85 is far enough from 1 (not from 0) to be significant. The null value here is $$\\beta_0 = 1$$.\n\nStep 1 — The general t-statistic uses the hypothesized value in the numerator:\n$$t = \\frac{\\hat\\beta - \\beta_0}{SE(\\hat\\beta)} = \\frac{0.85 - 1}{0.30} = \\frac{-0.15}{0.30} = -0.50$$\n\nStep 2 — Compare its absolute value to the critical value 2.000:\n$$|-0.50| = 0.50 < 2.000$$\n\nSince 0.50 does not exceed 2.000, fail to reject. The slope is statistically indistinguishable from a one-for-one hedge. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: the statistic 0.50 is far below the cutoff, so there is no significant difference from 1.\n- C: you can test ANY null value, not just zero — just put $$\\beta_0$$ into the numerator.\n- D: $$0.85 < 1$$ and the difference is not significant anyway, so \"significantly greater than one\" is doubly wrong.\n\nAnalogy: the estimate (0.85) is close to the target of 1, and the wobble (SE = 0.30) is big enough that 0.85 could easily be a noisy version of a true 1.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To test against a non-zero value, subtract it in the numerator: $$t = (\\hat\\beta - \\beta_0)/SE$$. The hedge-ratio-equals-one test is a classic.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A regression gives $$\\hat\\beta = 0.85$$ with $$SE = 0.30$$ and critical value 2.000. The 95% confidence interval for $$\\beta$$ is closest to:\nA) (0.55, 1.15) B) (0.25, 1.45) C) (0.85, 1.00) D) (0.45, 1.25)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the range of plausible values for the true slope at 95% confidence.\n\nStep 1 — The confidence interval formula:\n$$\\hat\\beta \\pm t_{\\alpha/2,\\,n-2}\\,SE(\\hat\\beta)$$\n\nStep 2 — Compute the margin (the \"± part\"):\n$$2.000 \\times 0.30 = 0.60$$\n\nStep 3 — Build the interval around the estimate 0.85:\n$$0.85 - 0.60 = 0.25 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 0.85 + 0.60 = 1.45$$\n\nSo the interval is $$(0.25,\\ 1.45)$$. The correct answer is B.\n\nNotice this interval EXCLUDES 0 (so we reject $$\\beta = 0$$, matching Q6) and CONTAINS 1 (so we fail to reject $$\\beta = 1$$, matching Q7). The CI is consistent with both tests — that is the duality at work.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.55, 1.15): uses a margin of 0.30 (forgot to multiply by the critical value 2.000).\n- C (0.85, 1.00): this is not an interval centered on the estimate at all; it is arbitrary.\n- D (0.45, 1.25): uses a margin of 0.40, which does not come from $$2.000 \\times 0.30$$.\n\nAnalogy: the confidence interval is the estimate (0.85) plus and minus a \"buffer.\" The buffer is the critical value times the wobble: $$2 \\times 0.30 = 0.60$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CI = estimate ± (critical value × SE). Check it against your tests: it should exclude any null you reject and include any null you fail to reject.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which statement about OLS estimators under the standard assumptions (exogeneity, random sampling, finite moments) is correct?\nA) OLS is biased but consistent. B) OLS is unbiased and consistent, and the slope is approximately normal in large samples. C) OLS requires the errors to be normally distributed to be unbiased. D) OLS is unbiased only if $$R^2$$ is high.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the basic statistical properties of OLS estimates when the core assumptions hold.\n\nUnder exogeneity ($$E[\\epsilon|X]=0$$), random sampling, and finite moments:\n- OLS is UNBIASED (on average it hits the true value) and CONSISTENT (it converges to the truth as $$n$$ grows).\n- By the Central Limit Theorem, the sampling distribution of $$\\hat\\beta$$ is approximately normal in large samples — which is what lets us use z/t critical values.\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: OLS is unbiased (not biased) under these assumptions, so \"biased but consistent\" is wrong.\n- C: normality of the errors is NOT needed for unbiasedness — it only matters for exact small-sample inference. In large samples the CLT takes over.\n- D: a high $$R^2$$ has nothing to do with whether the estimator is unbiased. You can have unbiased OLS with a low $$R^2$$ and vice versa.\n\nAnalogy: unbiased means the dartboard is centered on the bullseye on average; consistent means the more darts (data) you throw, the tighter they cluster on the bullseye. Neither requires the darts to follow a bell-shaped scatter.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Exogeneity is the key assumption for unbiasedness. Normality of errors is NOT required for OLS to be unbiased/consistent — the CLT delivers approximate normality of $$\\hat\\beta$$ in large samples.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"OLS under standard assumptions\",\"root\":\"Exogeneity + random sampling + finite moments\",\"children\":[\"unbiased (centered on truth)\",\"consistent (converges as n grows)\",\"β̂ ≈ normal in large samples (CLT)\",\"error normality NOT required\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A regression gives $$\\hat\\beta = 1.2$$ with $$SE = 0.4$$ and $$n$$ large. At the 5% level, testing $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ (use 1.96 as the cutoff):\nA) Reject $$H_0$$ (t = 3.0). B) Fail to reject $$H_0$$ (t = 0.48). C) Reject only with a one-sided test. D) Not testable without $$R^2$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks whether the slope is significantly different from zero in a large sample.\n\nStep 1 — Compute the t-statistic:\n$$t = \\frac{\\hat\\beta}{SE} = \\frac{1.2}{0.4} = 3.0$$\n\nStep 2 — Compare to the large-sample 5% cutoff of 1.96:\n$$3.0 > 1.96$$\n\nSince 3.0 comfortably exceeds 1.96, reject $$H_0$$. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B (0.48): this divides $$0.4/1.2$$ (or $$0.4 \\times 1.2$$ rounded), flipping the ratio. The coefficient goes on top.\n- C: the statistic 3.0 exceeds even the two-sided cutoff, so it rejects two-sided; you do not need a one-sided test.\n- D: the test only needs the coefficient and its standard error; $$R^2$$ is irrelevant to a single-coefficient t-test.\n\nAnalogy: the slope (1.2) is three standard errors (0.4 each) away from zero. Anything beyond about two standard errors is \"significant\" at 5%, so three is a clear reject.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With large n, |t| > 1.96 rejects at 5% and |t| > 2.58 rejects at 1%. If GARP gives you a coefficient and its SE, they want t = β̂ / SE.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** $$s_X = 4\\%$$, $$s_Y = 6\\%$$, $$\\rho_{XY} = 0.5$$. The OLS slope of $$Y$$ on $$X$$ is:\nA) 0.33 B) 0.50 C) 0.75 D) 1.33\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\hat\\beta = \\rho \\frac{s_Y}{s_X} = 0.5 \\times 6/4 = 0.75$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In a single-variable OLS regression, $$R^2 = 0.49$$. The correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$ is:\nA) 0.49 B) $$\\pm 0.70$$ C) 0.24 D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\rho = \\pm\\sqrt{R^2} = \\pm 0.7$$; the sign matches the slope's sign, which is not given.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Heteroskedastic errors in OLS cause:\nA) Biased slope estimates B) Invalid standard errors but unbiased slope estimates C) Inconsistent slope estimates D) A downward-biased $$R^2$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Heteroskedasticity leaves OLS unbiased/consistent but corrupts the usual standard errors (use robust SEs).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** $$\\hat\\beta = 1.2$$, $$SE = 0.4$$, $$n$$ large. At the 5% level, $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ is:\nA) Rejected ($$t = 3.0$$) B) Not rejected ($$t = 0.48$$) C) Rejected only one-sided D) Not testable without $$R^2$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$t = 1.2/0.4 = 3.0 > 1.96$$: reject.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A regression of a stock's excess returns ($$Y$$) on the market's excess returns ($$X$$) gives $$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = 0.0045$$, $$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 0.0030$$, $$\\bar{Y} = 0.9\\%$$, $$\\bar{X} = 0.5\\%$$. The OLS slope and intercept are closest to:\nA) Slope 1.50; intercept 0.15% B) Slope 0.67; intercept 0.57% C) Slope 1.50; intercept 0.90% D) Slope 1.50; intercept −0.15%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\mathrm{Var}(X)} = \\frac{0.0045}{0.0030} = 1.50 \\qquad \\hat\\alpha = 0.9\\% - 1.50 \\times 0.5\\% = 0.15\\%$$\n\nDistractor B inverts the slope by dividing covariance by $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y)$$-style logic; C forgets to subtract $$\\hat\\beta\\bar{X}$$; D flips the subtraction.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** An analyst estimates the CAPM beta of a stock by OLS on excess returns. From the data: $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(R_s, R_m) = 0.0042$$, $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(R_m) = 0.0028$$, $$\\bar{R}_s = 1.2\\%$$, $$\\bar{R}_m = 0.7\\%$$. What are the slope and intercept of the fitted line $$\\hat{R}_s = \\hat\\alpha + \\hat\\beta R_m$$?\nA) $$\\hat\\beta = 1.50$$, $$\\hat\\alpha = 0.15\\%$$ B) $$\\hat\\beta = 0.67$$, $$\\hat\\alpha = 0.73\\%$$ C) $$\\hat\\beta = 1.50$$, $$\\hat\\alpha = 1.20\\%$$ D) $$\\hat\\beta = 1.71$$, $$\\hat\\alpha = 0.00\\%$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the OLS slope and intercept from the moments.\n\n1. Slope is covariance over variance of the regressor:\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(R_s, R_m)}{\\widehat{\\mathrm{Var}}(R_m)} = \\frac{0.0042}{0.0028} = 1.50$$\n2. Intercept forces the line through $$(\\bar{R}_m, \\bar{R}_s)$$:\n$$\\hat\\alpha = \\bar{R}_s - \\hat\\beta \\bar{R}_m = 1.2\\% - 1.50 \\times 0.7\\% = 1.2\\% - 1.05\\% = 0.15\\%$$\n\nWhy A is correct: $$\\hat\\beta = 0.0042/0.0028 = 1.5$$ and $$\\hat\\alpha = 1.2 - 1.5(0.7) = 0.15\\%$$.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** inverts the slope formula ($$0.0028/0.0042 = 0.67$$) — variance over covariance.\n- **C** computes $$\\hat\\beta$$ correctly but sets $$\\hat\\alpha = \\bar{R}_s$$, forgetting to subtract $$\\hat\\beta\\bar{R}_m$$.\n- **D** uses $$\\bar{R}_s/\\bar{R}_m = 1.2/0.7 = 1.71$$ as the slope and forces a zero intercept — confusing the ratio of means with the regression slope.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\hat\\beta = \\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)/\\mathrm{Var}(X)$$ (the *regressor's* variance in the denominator), then $$\\hat\\alpha = \\bar Y - \\hat\\beta \\bar X$$. The fitted line always passes through the point of means.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An OLS regression of $$Y$$ on a single regressor $$X$$ over $$n = 102$$ observations gives $$\\hat\\beta = 0.72$$ with $$SE(\\hat\\beta) = 0.20$$. A hedge program requires the slope to equal 1 (a one-for-one hedge). Testing $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$ against a two-sided alternative at 5% (critical t ≈ 1.984), what is the result?\nA) t = −1.40; fail to reject — the slope is not significantly different from 1 B) t = 3.60; reject — the slope is significantly different from 1 C) t = −1.40; reject — the slope is significantly different from 1 D) t = 0.72; fail to reject — the slope is not significantly different from 1\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to test whether the slope equals 1 (not 0).\n\n1. The t-statistic uses the hypothesized value $$\\beta_0 = 1$$, not 0:\n$$t = \\frac{\\hat\\beta - \\beta_0}{SE(\\hat\\beta)} = \\frac{0.72 - 1}{0.20} = \\frac{-0.28}{0.20} = -1.40$$\n2. Compare $$|{-1.40}| = 1.40$$ to the critical value 1.984. Since $$1.40 < 1.984$$, **fail to reject** — the slope is statistically indistinguishable from 1.\n\nWhy A is correct: subtracting the hypothesized 1 gives t = −1.40, inside the non-rejection band.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** uses $$t = (0.72 - 0)/0.20 = 3.60$$, testing against 0 instead of 1 — answering a different hypothesis.\n- **C** computes the right statistic but flips the decision; $$|{-1.40}| < 1.984$$ means fail to reject.\n- **D** divides the coefficient by itself-ish (uses $$0.72$$ as t) — ignores the SE entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When the null is $$\\beta = \\beta_0 \\ne 0$$, subtract that specific value in the numerator. Testing a hedge ratio of 1 is the classic case where the null is not zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** In a single-variable OLS regression, the total sum of squares is $$TSS = 500$$ and the residual sum of squares is $$RSS = 180$$, estimated on $$n = 42$$ observations. What are the $$R^2$$ and the standard error of the regression ($$s_\\epsilon$$)?\nA) $$R^2 = 0.64$$, $$s_\\epsilon = 2.121$$ B) $$R^2 = 0.36$$, $$s_\\epsilon = 2.121$$ C) $$R^2 = 0.64$$, $$s_\\epsilon = 4.500$$ D) $$R^2 = 0.36$$, $$s_\\epsilon = 4.286$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for $$R^2$$ and the standard error of the regression.\n\n1. Coefficient of determination:\n$$R^2 = 1 - \\frac{RSS}{TSS} = 1 - \\frac{180}{500} = 1 - 0.36 = 0.64$$\n2. Standard error of the regression uses $$n - 2$$ (two parameters estimated):\n$$s_\\epsilon = \\sqrt{\\frac{RSS}{n - 2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{180}{40}} = \\sqrt{4.5} = 2.121$$\n\nWhy A is correct: $$R^2 = 0.64$$ and $$s_\\epsilon = \\sqrt{180/40} = 2.121$$.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** reports the RSS/TSS ratio (0.36) as $$R^2$$ — that is $$1 - R^2$$, not $$R^2$$.\n- **C** computes $$R^2$$ correctly but uses $$s_\\epsilon = RSS/n - 2$$ logic or forgets the square root / divides by 2 ($$180/40 = 4.5$$ left un-rooted).\n- **D** combines both errors: $$1-R^2$$ for the fit and divides by the wrong degrees of freedom ($$180/42 = 4.286$$, un-rooted, wrong df).\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$R^2 = 1 - RSS/TSS$$ (not $$RSS/TSS$$). The standard error of the regression divides RSS by $$n - 2$$ in single regression because two parameters (slope and intercept) are estimated — then take the square root.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A regression yields $$\\hat\\beta = 1.10$$ with $$SE(\\hat\\beta) = 0.25$$ on a large sample. An analyst constructs the 95% confidence interval and then makes three claims. Which interval and conclusion are jointly correct?\nA) CI = $$(0.61, 1.59)$$; reject $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ but fail to reject $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$ B) CI = $$(0.61, 1.59)$$; fail to reject both $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ and $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$ C) CI = $$(0.85, 1.35)$$; reject $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ and reject $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$ D) CI = $$(0.61, 1.59)$$; reject both $$H_0: \\beta = 0$$ and $$H_0: \\beta = 1$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the 95% CI and which nulls it rejects via duality.\n\n1. With a large sample use $$t \\approx 1.96$$:\n$$\\hat\\beta \\pm 1.96 \\times SE = 1.10 \\pm 1.96 \\times 0.25 = 1.10 \\pm 0.49 = (0.61,\\; 1.59)$$\n2. By duality, reject any null value lying **outside** the interval:\n   - $$\\beta_0 = 0$$: outside $$(0.61, 1.59)$$ ⇒ **reject** (X is significant).\n   - $$\\beta_0 = 1$$: inside $$(0.61, 1.59)$$ ⇒ **fail to reject**.\n\nWhy A is correct: 0 is outside (reject) while 1 is inside (fail to reject).\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** has the right interval but wrongly fails to reject $$\\beta = 0$$ even though 0 is outside.\n- **C** uses a margin of $$\\pm 0.25$$ (the bare SE, no 1.96 multiplier), producing $$(0.85, 1.35)$$ and then over-rejects.\n- **D** has the right interval but wrongly rejects $$\\beta = 1$$, which is inside.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A 95% CI is the set of all nulls a 5% two-sided test would *not* reject. Check each candidate: outside ⇒ reject, inside ⇒ fail to reject. The margin must include the $$\\approx 1.96$$ (or $$t$$) multiplier — the bare SE is not the half-width.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A researcher fits the model $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta X^2 + \\epsilon$$ and also considers three alternative specifications. Which one of the following CANNOT be estimated directly by ordinary least squares?\nA) $$\\ln Y = \\alpha + \\beta \\ln X + \\epsilon$$ B) $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta_1 X + \\beta_2 X^2 + \\epsilon$$ C) $$Y = \\alpha + X^\\beta + \\epsilon$$ D) $$Y = \\alpha + \\beta \\, e^{X} + \\epsilon$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We are asked which model is not linear in the parameters and therefore not OLS-estimable.\n\n1. OLS requires the model to be **linear in the parameters** ($$\\alpha, \\beta$$), even if nonlinear in the variables. Define a transformed regressor if needed.\n2. Check each:\n   - **A**: $$\\ln Y = \\alpha + \\beta \\ln X + \\epsilon$$ — linear in $$\\alpha, \\beta$$ after taking logs of $$Y$$ and $$X$$. Estimable.\n   - **B**: includes $$X$$ and $$X^2$$ as two regressors; linear in $$\\beta_1, \\beta_2$$. Estimable.\n   - **C**: $$X^\\beta$$ has the parameter $$\\beta$$ in the **exponent** — nonlinear in the parameter, cannot be turned into a linear-in-parameters form by transforming the data. **Not OLS-estimable.**\n   - **D**: $$e^X$$ is just a known transformation of $$X$$; the model is linear in $$\\beta$$. Estimable (regress $$Y$$ on $$e^X$$).\n\nWhy C is correct: the parameter sits in the exponent of $$X$$, breaking linearity in the parameters.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **A, B, D** are all linear in the parameters once you treat $$\\ln X$$, $$X^2$$, or $$e^X$$ as the regressor — \"nonlinear in variables\" is fine.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Linear\" in OLS means linear in the *coefficients*. You may square, log, or exponentiate the *variables* freely. A parameter in an exponent ($$X^\\beta$$) is the giveaway that OLS cannot be used directly.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which CANNOT be estimated by OLS?\",\"options\":[\"ln Y = α + β ln X + ε\",\"Y = α + β1 X + β2 X² + ε\",\"Y = α + X^β + ε\",\"Y = α + β e^X + ε\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"β is in the exponent → not linear in the parameter; the rest are linear in the coefficients after transforming variables\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** An analyst is told that the OLS errors in a return regression are heteroskedastic (variance depends on the size of the regressor) but that all other classical assumptions hold. What is the consequence for the OLS slope estimate and for inference?\nA) The slope estimate remains unbiased and consistent, but the usual standard errors are invalid, so t-tests can mislead B) The slope estimate is biased and inconsistent, and the standard errors are also invalid C) The slope estimate is unbiased but inconsistent, and the standard errors are correct D) Both the slope estimate and its standard errors are unaffected in large samples\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to separate the effect of heteroskedasticity on the coefficient versus on inference.\n\n1. Heteroskedasticity violates the constant-variance assumption (assumption 4). It does **not** violate exogeneity ($$E[\\epsilon\\mid X]=0$$), so OLS coefficients remain **unbiased and consistent**.\n2. However, the textbook standard-error formulas assume homoskedasticity; under heteroskedasticity they are **wrong** (typically understated), making t- and F-tests unreliable. OLS is also no longer efficient (not BLUE).\n3. The fix: keep the OLS coefficients, but use heteroskedasticity-robust (White) standard errors.\n\nWhy A is correct: coefficients survive; inference breaks. Use robust SEs.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** claims bias — that would require violating exogeneity (the *omitted variable* problem), not heteroskedasticity.\n- **C** wrongly calls the estimator inconsistent and the SEs correct — exactly backwards on both counts.\n- **D** says nothing breaks, ignoring the invalid standard errors.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Heteroskedasticity (and multicollinearity) damages **precision/standard errors**, not the coefficient. Only an endogeneity problem like omitted variable bias actually biases $$\\hat\\beta$$. Memorize which assumption breaks what.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Heteroskedasticity effects\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Slope estimate\",\"items\":[\"UNBIASED\",\"consistent\",\"exogeneity intact\"]},{\"h\":\"Inference\",\"items\":[\"usual SEs INVALID\",\"t-tests mislead\",\"fix: White robust SEs\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A single-variable OLS regression reports $$R^2 = 0.49$$, and the estimated slope $$\\hat\\beta$$ is negative. What is the correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$?\nA) $$-0.70$$ B) $$+0.70$$ C) $$-0.49$$ D) $$\\pm 0.70$$ — sign cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the correlation, given $$R^2$$ and the sign of the slope.\n\n1. In single-variable regression, $$R^2 = \\rho_{XY}^2$$, so:\n$$|\\rho_{XY}| = \\sqrt{R^2} = \\sqrt{0.49} = 0.70$$\n2. The sign of the correlation matches the sign of the slope (both share the sign of the covariance). The slope is negative, so:\n$$\\rho_{XY} = -0.70$$\n\nWhy A is correct: magnitude $$\\sqrt{0.49} = 0.70$$, sign negative to match the slope.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** has the right magnitude but the wrong sign — ignores that $$\\hat\\beta < 0$$.\n- **C** reports $$R^2$$ itself (0.49) as the correlation — forgets to take the square root.\n- **D** would be correct only if the slope's sign were unknown; here we are told it is negative, so the sign is pinned down.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\rho = \\pm\\sqrt{R^2}$$ in single regression. Take the square root for the magnitude, and let the slope's (or covariance's) sign fix the sign.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ρ from R² (slope negative)\",\"steps\":[\"Single-variable: R² = ρ²\",\"|ρ| = √0.49 = 0.70\",\"Sign = sign of slope = (−)\",\"ρ = −0.70\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following is the correct interpretation of the slope $$\\hat\\beta$$ and intercept $$\\hat\\alpha$$ in a fitted single-variable OLS regression $$\\hat Y = \\hat\\alpha + \\hat\\beta X$$, together with a true in-sample property of the residuals?\nA) $$\\hat\\beta$$ is the expected change in $$Y$$ per one-unit change in $$X$$; $$\\hat\\alpha$$ is the expected $$Y$$ when $$X = 0$$; in-sample the residuals sum to zero B) $$\\hat\\beta$$ is the expected change in $$X$$ per one-unit change in $$Y$$; $$\\hat\\alpha$$ is the mean of $$Y$$; the residuals sum to one C) $$\\hat\\beta$$ is the correlation between $$X$$ and $$Y$$; $$\\hat\\alpha$$ is the expected $$Y$$ when $$X = 0$$; the residuals are correlated with $$X$$ D) $$\\hat\\beta$$ is the expected change in $$Y$$ per one-unit change in $$X$$; $$\\hat\\alpha$$ is the value of $$Y$$ at the maximum of $$X$$; the residuals have nonzero mean\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the correct coefficient interpretations plus a valid residual property.\n\n1. **Slope**: $$\\hat\\beta$$ measures the expected change in the dependent variable $$Y$$ for a one-unit increase in the explanatory variable $$X$$.\n2. **Intercept**: $$\\hat\\alpha$$ is the fitted value of $$Y$$ when $$X = 0$$ (the line passes through $$(\\bar X, \\bar Y)$$, and extends to $$X=0$$ at height $$\\hat\\alpha$$).\n3. **Residual property**: OLS residuals **sum to zero** in-sample and are **uncorrelated with $$X$$** — both are direct consequences of the first-order conditions.\n\nWhy A is correct: it states the standard slope and intercept meanings and a genuine residual property (sum to zero).\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** reverses the regression (change in $$X$$ per unit $$Y$$), calls $$\\hat\\alpha$$ the mean of $$Y$$, and claims residuals sum to one — all false.\n- **C** confuses the slope with the correlation (it equals $$\\rho \\, s_Y/s_X$$, not $$\\rho$$) and wrongly says residuals are correlated with $$X$$ (they are uncorrelated by construction).\n- **D** mangles the intercept (\"value at max of $$X$$\") and claims nonzero-mean residuals.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\hat\\beta$$ = effect of $$X$$ on $$Y$$ (not the correlation, not the reverse regression). In-sample, OLS guarantees residuals sum to zero and are orthogonal to $$X$$ — handy sanity checks.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"OLS fitted line facts\",\"root\":\"Ŷ = α̂ + β̂X\",\"children\":[\"β̂ = ΔY per one-unit ΔX\",\"α̂ = fitted Y at X=0\",\"residuals sum to zero\",\"residuals uncorrelated with X\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"208","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Regression with Multiple Explanatory Variables","notes":"# 8. Regression with Multiple Explanatory Variables\n\nExtends OLS to several regressors: interpreting partial coefficients, dummy variables, $$R^2$$ vs. adjusted $$R^2$$, and the F-test for joint significance. Adjusted-$$R^2$$ and F-statistic calculations are bread-and-butter exam questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Distinguish between the relative assumptions of single and multiple regression.\n- Interpret regression coefficients in a multiple regression.\n- Interpret goodness-of-fit measures for single and multiple regressions, including $$R^2$$ and adjusted $$R^2$$.\n- Construct, apply, and interpret joint hypothesis tests and confidence intervals for multiple coefficients in a regression.\n\n## The model and assumptions\n\n$$Y_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_{1i} + \\beta_2 X_{2i} + \\cdots + \\beta_k X_{ki} + \\epsilon_i$$\n\nAssumptions carry over from single regression, plus one new requirement: **no perfect multicollinearity** — no regressor can be an exact linear combination of the others (otherwise OLS cannot separate their effects). The constant counts: including dummy variables for *every* category plus an intercept creates perfect collinearity (the **dummy variable trap** — always omit one category as the base).\n\n## Interpreting coefficients\n\n$$\\beta_j$$ is the expected change in $$Y$$ from a one-unit change in $$X_j$$, **holding all other regressors constant** (a *partial* or ceteris paribus effect). This differs from the single-regression slope whenever regressors are correlated — multiple regression \"nets out\" the overlap.\n\nOmitting a relevant variable that is correlated with an included one biases the included coefficient (**omitted variable bias** — the included regressor picks up its neighbor's effect).\n\n**Dummy variables:** a binary regressor $$D \\in \\{0,1\\}$$ shifts the intercept by $$\\beta_D$$ for the $$D=1$$ group; its coefficient is the average difference in $$Y$$ between groups, other things equal.\n\n## Goodness of fit: $$R^2$$ and adjusted $$R^2$$\n\n$$R^2 = 1 - \\frac{RSS}{TSS}$$\n\n$$R^2$$ **never falls** when you add a regressor — even a useless one — so it cannot be used to compare models of different sizes. The fix penalizes parameters:\n\n$$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - \\frac{RSS/(n - k - 1)}{TSS/(n - 1)} = 1 - (1 - R^2)\\,\\frac{n-1}{n-k-1}$$\n\nwhere $$k$$ = number of slope coefficients. $$\\bar R^2 \\le R^2$$, can decrease when a weak regressor is added, and can be negative.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Adding a variable raises $$\\bar R^2$$ only if its $$\\lvert t \\rvert > 1$$ — a much weaker hurdle than significance at 5% ($$\\lvert t\\rvert > 1.96$$). So \"adjusted $$R^2$$ went up\" does *not* mean the new variable is statistically significant. Also: a high $$R^2$$ proves neither causality nor correct specification.\n\n## Joint hypothesis tests: the F-statistic\n\nTo test $$q$$ restrictions jointly (e.g., $$H_0: \\beta_1 = \\beta_2 = 0$$), compare restricted and unrestricted models:\n\n$$F = \\frac{(RSS_r - RSS_u)/q}{RSS_u/(n - k_u - 1)} \\sim F_{q,\\; n-k_u-1}$$\n\nFor the regression-wide null that **all slopes are zero**, this is equivalent to\n\n$$F = \\frac{R^2 / k}{(1 - R^2)/(n - k - 1)}$$\n\nReject for large $$F$$ (always an upper-tail test). Single-coefficient tests still use the t-statistic; CIs for individual coefficients are $$\\hat\\beta_j \\pm t_{\\alpha/2}\\,SE(\\hat\\beta_j)$$.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> A regression can have a significant F (jointly useful regressors) while no individual t is significant — the signature of multicollinearity: the variables matter as a group, but their separate effects are estimated imprecisely.\n\n### Worked example: adjusted $$R^2$$\n\nA regression with $$n = 66$$ observations and $$k = 5$$ regressors has $$R^2 = 0.40$$.\n\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1 - 0.40)\\,\\frac{66 - 1}{66 - 5 - 1} = 1 - 0.60 \\times \\frac{65}{60} = 1 - 0.65 = 0.35$$\n\nAdding a 6th regressor raises $$R^2$$ to 0.41. New adjusted $$R^2$$:\n\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - 0.59 \\times \\frac{65}{59} = 1 - 0.65 = 0.35$$\n\nEssentially unchanged — the new variable does not earn its degree of freedom.\n\n### Worked example: overall F-test\n\nA model with $$k = 3$$ regressors on $$n = 44$$ observations has $$R^2 = 0.30$$. Test $$H_0: \\beta_1 = \\beta_2 = \\beta_3 = 0$$ at 5%.\n\n$$F = \\frac{0.30/3}{(1 - 0.30)/(44 - 3 - 1)} = \\frac{0.10}{0.70/40} = \\frac{0.10}{0.0175} = 5.71$$\n\nCritical $$F_{3,40} \\approx 2.84$$. Since $$5.71 > 2.84$$, reject: the regressors are jointly significant.\n\n### Worked example: restricted vs. unrestricted\n\nUnrestricted model ($$k_u = 4$$, $$n = 60$$): $$RSS_u = 270$$. Dropping two regressors gives $$RSS_r = 306$$. Test whether the two dropped coefficients are jointly zero ($$q = 2$$):\n\n$$F = \\frac{(306 - 270)/2}{270/(60 - 4 - 1)} = \\frac{18}{270/55} = \\frac{18}{4.909} = 3.667$$\n\nCritical $$F_{2,55} \\approx 3.16$$ at 5%. Reject — the pair belongs in the model, even if each individual t-statistic is marginal.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What new assumption does multiple regression add beyond the single-variable case?\n> **A:** No perfect multicollinearity — no regressor can be an exact linear combination of the others, or OLS cannot separate their effects.\n\n**Q2.** How do you interpret a coefficient $$\\beta_j$$ in multiple regression?\n> **A:** Expected change in $$Y$$ per one-unit change in $$X_j$$, holding all other regressors constant (a partial / ceteris paribus effect).\n\n**Q3.** What is the dummy variable trap and the fix?\n> **A:** Including dummies for every category plus an intercept creates perfect collinearity (dummies sum to the constant). Fix: omit one category as the base.\n\n**Q4.** What is omitted variable bias?\n> **A:** Omitting a relevant variable correlated with an included regressor biases the included coefficient — it picks up the omitted variable's effect.\n\n**Q5.** Why can't $$R^2$$ compare models of different sizes, and what fixes it?\n> **A:** $$R^2$$ never falls when a regressor is added (even a useless one). Adjusted $$R^2$$ penalizes parameters: $$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1-R^2)\\dfrac{n-1}{n-k-1}$$.\n\n**Q6.** When does adding a variable raise adjusted $$R^2$$, and is that the same as significance?\n> **A:** It rises only if the new variable's $$\\lvert t\\rvert > 1$$ — a weaker hurdle than 5% significance ($$\\lvert t\\rvert > 1.96$$). So a higher $$\\bar R^2$$ does NOT mean the variable is statistically significant.\n\n**Q7.** Give the general F-statistic for $$q$$ joint restrictions.\n> **A:** $$F = \\dfrac{(RSS_r - RSS_u)/q}{RSS_u/(n - k_u - 1)} \\sim F_{q,\\,n-k_u-1}$$. Always an upper-tail test.\n\n**Q8.** Give the F-statistic for the all-slopes-zero null in terms of $$R^2$$.\n> **A:** $$F = \\dfrac{R^2/k}{(1-R^2)/(n-k-1)}$$.\n\n**Q9.** $$n = 44$$, $$k = 3$$, $$R^2 = 0.30$$. Compute the overall F (crit $$F_{3,40} \\approx 2.84$$).\n> **A:** $$F = \\dfrac{0.30/3}{0.70/40} = \\dfrac{0.10}{0.0175} = 5.71 > 2.84$$ → reject; regressors jointly significant.\n\n**Q10.** $$n = 66$$, $$k = 5$$, $$R^2 = 0.40$$. Compute adjusted $$R^2$$.\n> **A:** $$\\bar R^2 = 1 - 0.60 \\times \\dfrac{65}{60} = 1 - 0.65 = 0.35$$.\n\n**Q11.** What does a significant overall F with no significant individual t-statistics indicate?\n> **A:** Multicollinearity — correlated regressors matter as a group but inflate individual SEs, so their separate effects are imprecise.\n\n**Q12.** Unrestricted $$RSS_u = 270$$ ($$k_u=4$$, $$n=60$$); restricted $$RSS_r = 306$$ dropping 2 regressors. Compute the F ($$q=2$$; crit $$F_{2,55}\\approx 3.16$$).\n> **A:** $$F = \\dfrac{(306-270)/2}{270/55} = \\dfrac{18}{4.909} = 3.67 > 3.16$$ → reject; the pair belongs in the model.\n\n**Q13.** Does a high $$R^2$$ prove causality or correct specification?\n> **A:** No — a high $$R^2$$ proves neither causality nor correct specification.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A multiple regression with $$n = 30$$ observations and $$k = 4$$ slope coefficients has $$R^2 = 0.52$$. The adjusted $$R^2$$ is closest to:\nA) 0.52 B) 0.44 C) 0.48 D) 0.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to penalize $$R^2$$ for the number of regressors using the adjusted-$$R^2$$ formula.\n\nStep 1 — Write the formula:\n$$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - (1 - R^2)\\,\\frac{n - 1}{n - k - 1}$$\n\nStep 2 — Find each piece:\n$$1 - R^2 = 1 - 0.52 = 0.48$$\n$$n - 1 = 30 - 1 = 29$$\n$$n - k - 1 = 30 - 4 - 1 = 25$$\n\nStep 3 — Compute the adjustment ratio:\n$$\\frac{29}{25} = 1.16$$\n\nStep 4 — Combine:\n$$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - 0.48 \\times 1.16 = 1 - 0.5568 = 0.4432 \\approx 0.44$$\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.52): this is the plain $$R^2$$ with no penalty applied.\n- C (0.48): this is $$1 - R^2$$, an intermediate quantity, not the adjusted $$R^2$$.\n- D (0.40): too large a penalty — likely from using a wrong denominator like $$n - k$$ or $$n - k - 2$$.\n\nAnalogy: $$R^2$$ always brags that adding variables helps. Adjusted $$R^2$$ is the skeptical accountant who charges \"rent\" for every extra regressor, so it comes out lower.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - (1-R^2)\\frac{n-1}{n-k-1}$$. The denominator is $$n-k-1$$ (subtract the slopes AND the intercept).\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In a wage regression that already includes an intercept, the analyst adds dummy variables for ALL four regions. This causes:\nA) Omitted variable bias. B) Heteroskedasticity. C) Perfect multicollinearity. D) Autocorrelation.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question describes the classic \"dummy variable trap.\"\n\nIf you include a dummy for every category PLUS an intercept, the four region dummies always add up to 1 for every observation — which is exactly what the intercept (a column of 1s) already represents. So one regressor is an exact linear combination of the others. OLS then cannot separate their effects: this is perfect multicollinearity. The correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: omitted variable bias comes from LEAVING OUT a relevant variable, not from including too many dummies.\n- B: heteroskedasticity is about the error variance changing with $$X$$ — unrelated to dummy setup.\n- D: autocorrelation is about errors being correlated across time/observations — also unrelated.\n\nAnalogy: imagine four light switches that together must always equal \"on\" — if you also have a master switch that says \"on,\" one of the switches is redundant. The computer cannot tell which switch did the work.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With an intercept, always drop ONE category as the base. Coefficients on the remaining dummies are differences relative to that base.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Intercept + dummy for ALL 4 regions causes:\",\"options\":[\"Omitted variable bias\",\"Heteroskedasticity\",\"Perfect multicollinearity\",\"Autocorrelation\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"4 dummies sum to 1 = the intercept column → exact linear dependence (dummy variable trap); drop one category\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A regression shows $$R^2 = 0.85$$ and a highly significant overall F-statistic, but NO individual t-statistic is significant. The most likely explanation is:\nA) The model is useless. B) Multicollinearity among the regressors. C) Heteroskedastic errors. D) The sample is too large.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a very specific symptom: the regressors matter as a GROUP (significant F, high $$R^2$$) but no single one stands out (all t-stats insignificant).\n\nThis is the signature of multicollinearity. When regressors are highly correlated with each other, OLS cannot tell which one deserves credit, so each individual standard error balloons and each t-stat shrinks. But collectively they still explain $$Y$$ well, so the F-test and $$R^2$$ stay strong. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: a model with $$R^2 = 0.85$$ and a significant F is clearly NOT useless — it explains a lot of variation.\n- C: heteroskedasticity corrupts standard errors but does not produce this exact \"F-significant, all-t-insignificant\" pattern.\n- D: a large sample usually makes t-stats LARGER (more significant), not smaller — the opposite of what we see.\n\nAnalogy: two nearly identical twins do a job together perfectly (the team works), but you cannot tell which twin actually did each task (no individual credit). That is collinear regressors.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Significant F + insignificant individual t's = multicollinearity. The group matters; the individuals can't be separated.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Multicollinearity signature\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"As a group\",\"items\":[\"high R² = 0.85\",\"F highly significant\",\"regressors jointly explain Y\"]},{\"h\":\"Individually\",\"items\":[\"every t insignificant\",\"SEs inflated\",\"can't credit any single X\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A model with $$k = 3$$ regressors on $$n = 44$$ observations has $$R^2 = 0.30$$. The overall F-statistic for $$H_0: \\beta_1 = \\beta_2 = \\beta_3 = 0$$ is closest to (critical $$F_{3,40} \\approx 2.84$$):\nA) 5.71; reject — the regressors are jointly significant. B) 0.30; fail to reject. C) 1.43; fail to reject. D) 2.84; reject.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks whether all the slopes together are jointly zero, using the overall F-test.\n\nStep 1 — Use the $$R^2$$ form of the overall F-statistic:\n$$F = \\frac{R^2 / k}{(1 - R^2)/(n - k - 1)}$$\n\nStep 2 — Compute the numerator:\n$$\\frac{R^2}{k} = \\frac{0.30}{3} = 0.10$$\n\nStep 3 — Compute the denominator. First $$n - k - 1 = 44 - 3 - 1 = 40$$, then:\n$$\\frac{1 - R^2}{n - k - 1} = \\frac{0.70}{40} = 0.0175$$\n\nStep 4 — Divide:\n$$F = \\frac{0.10}{0.0175} = 5.71$$\n\nStep 5 — Compare to the critical value 2.84. Since $$5.71 > 2.84$$, reject $$H_0$$. The regressors are jointly significant. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B (0.30): this just reports $$R^2$$, not the F-statistic.\n- C (1.43): this comes from mishandling the formula (e.g., dividing 0.30 by 0.21 wrongly).\n- D (2.84): this copies the critical value instead of computing the statistic.\n\nAnalogy: the F-test asks \"does the whole team of regressors explain meaningfully more than nothing?\" An F of 5.71, well above the 2.84 bar, says yes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Overall F (all slopes zero) = $$\\frac{R^2/k}{(1-R^2)/(n-k-1)}$$. It is always an upper-tail test — reject for LARGE F.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An unrestricted model ($$k_u = 4$$, $$n = 60$$) has $$RSS_u = 270$$. Dropping two regressors gives a restricted model with $$RSS_r = 306$$. The F-statistic for testing whether the two dropped coefficients are jointly zero ($$q = 2$$) is closest to (critical $$F_{2,55} \\approx 3.16$$):\nA) 3.67; reject — the pair belongs in the model. B) 0.13; fail to reject. C) 7.33; reject. D) 1.83; fail to reject.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks whether two omitted regressors jointly add value, using the restricted-vs-unrestricted F-test. We compare how much the residual sum of squares (RSS) went UP when we dropped them.\n\nStep 1 — Write the formula:\n$$F = \\frac{(RSS_r - RSS_u)/q}{RSS_u/(n - k_u - 1)}$$\n\nStep 2 — Numerator: how much worse the fit got, per restriction.\n$$RSS_r - RSS_u = 306 - 270 = 36$$\n$$\\frac{36}{q} = \\frac{36}{2} = 18$$\n\nStep 3 — Denominator: the unrestricted RSS spread over its degrees of freedom. $$n - k_u - 1 = 60 - 4 - 1 = 55$$:\n$$\\frac{270}{55} = 4.909$$\n\nStep 4 — Divide:\n$$F = \\frac{18}{4.909} = 3.667$$\n\nStep 5 — Compare to 3.16. Since $$3.67 > 3.16$$, reject — the two regressors jointly belong in the model. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B (0.13): this would come from flipping the ratio or dividing 36 by 270 wrongly.\n- C (7.33): this forgets to divide the numerator by $$q = 2$$ (using 36 instead of 18).\n- D (1.83): this uses 9 in the numerator (over-dividing) or another arithmetic slip.\n\nAnalogy: dropping the two variables made the model fit worse (RSS rose from 270 to 306). The F-test asks whether that worsening is big enough to say the variables genuinely mattered. Here it is.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Restricted-vs-unrestricted F: numerator is $$(RSS_r - RSS_u)/q$$, denominator is $$RSS_u/(n - k_u - 1)$$. The restricted RSS is always the larger one.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In a multiple regression of bond spread on rating dummies and leverage, the coefficient on leverage is $$-0.6$$. The correct interpretation is the expected change in spread per one-unit increase in leverage:\nA) Ignoring all other variables. B) Holding the other included regressors constant. C) Only when all the other variables equal zero. D) Only for the omitted rating category.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests how to interpret a coefficient in a MULTIPLE regression.\n\nIn multiple regression, each slope is a PARTIAL (ceteris paribus) effect: it is the change in $$Y$$ for a one-unit change in that regressor while holding all the OTHER regressors fixed. So $$-0.6$$ means: with the rating dummies held constant, a one-unit rise in leverage is associated with a 0.6 decrease in spread. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: \"ignoring all other variables\" describes a SIMPLE (single-variable) regression slope, which can differ from the partial effect when regressors are correlated.\n- C: the partial effect applies generally (holding others fixed at ANY values), not only when the others are zero.\n- D: the coefficient on leverage is the same regardless of rating category; rating effects are captured separately by the dummies.\n\nAnalogy: a multiple-regression coefficient is like asking \"how much does an extra hour of study raise the test score, comparing students of the SAME age and SAME sleep?\" You hold the other factors steady to isolate the one you care about.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Multiple-regression coefficients are partial effects — \"holding the other included regressors constant.\" This differs from a simple regression slope when regressors overlap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Interpreting a slope\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Multiple regression\",\"items\":[\"partial / ceteris paribus\",\"hold OTHER regressors fixed\",\"the correct reading here\"]},{\"h\":\"Simple regression\",\"items\":[\"marginal effect\",\"ignores other variables\",\"differs when X's correlated\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Compared with adding a regressor that is genuinely useless, the $$R^2$$ of a multiple regression will:\nA) Always decrease. B) Stay exactly the same or rise — it never falls. C) Become negative. D) Fall below the adjusted $$R^2$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how plain $$R^2$$ behaves when you add ANY new regressor, even a useless one.\n\nKey fact: $$R^2 = 1 - RSS/TSS$$ never falls when you add a variable. Adding a regressor can only keep the residual sum of squares the same or shrink it (OLS can always set the new coefficient to zero if it does not help), so $$R^2$$ can only stay flat or rise. This is exactly why $$R^2$$ cannot be used to compare models of different sizes. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: $$R^2$$ never decreases when adding a regressor — the opposite of \"always decrease.\"\n- C: plain $$R^2$$ is always between 0 and 1; it cannot go negative. (Adjusted $$R^2$$ CAN go negative, but that is a different measure.)\n- D: $$R^2$$ is always greater than or equal to the adjusted $$R^2$$, never below it.\n\nAnalogy: $$R^2$$ is like a scoreboard that can only tick up or stay the same when you add players — even a player who sits on the bench never lowers the score. That is why you need adjusted $$R^2$$ to charge \"rent\" for extra players.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Plain $$R^2$$ never falls with more regressors → useless for model comparison. Use adjusted $$R^2$$, which can fall and can go negative.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Adding a regressor\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Plain R²\",\"items\":[\"stays same or RISES\",\"never falls (even if useless)\",\"between 0 and 1\"]},{\"h\":\"Adjusted R²\",\"items\":[\"charges \\\"rent\\\" per regressor\",\"CAN fall\",\"CAN go negative\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A regression has $$n = 66$$ and $$k = 5$$ with $$R^2 = 0.40$$, giving adjusted $$R^2 = 0.35$$. Adding a 6th regressor raises $$R^2$$ to 0.41. The new adjusted $$R^2$$ is closest to:\nA) 0.41 B) 0.35 C) 0.30 D) 0.45\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks whether the 6th regressor \"earns its keep\" by improving the adjusted $$R^2$$.\n\nStep 1 — Formula with the new $$k = 6$$:\n$$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - (1 - R^2)\\,\\frac{n - 1}{n - k - 1}$$\n\nStep 2 — Pieces:\n$$1 - R^2 = 1 - 0.41 = 0.59$$\n$$n - 1 = 66 - 1 = 65$$\n$$n - k - 1 = 66 - 6 - 1 = 59$$\n\nStep 3 — Ratio:\n$$\\frac{65}{59} = 1.1017$$\n\nStep 4 — Combine:\n$$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - 0.59 \\times 1.1017 = 1 - 0.650 = 0.350 \\approx 0.35$$\n\nThe adjusted $$R^2$$ is essentially unchanged at 0.35 — the new variable did not earn its degree of freedom. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.41): this is the new plain $$R^2$$, ignoring the penalty.\n- C (0.30): too large a drop — wrong degrees of freedom.\n- D (0.45): adjusted $$R^2$$ cannot exceed plain $$R^2$$ (0.41), so 0.45 is impossible.\n\nAnalogy: the 6th regressor added a tiny bit of explained variation (0.40 → 0.41) but consumed a degree of freedom in rent. The two cancel out, so adjusted $$R^2$$ stands still — a sign the variable is not pulling its weight.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Adjusted $$R^2$$ rises only if the new variable's |t| > 1 — a much lower bar than 5% significance (|t| > 1.96). \"Adjusted $$R^2$$ went up\" does NOT mean significant.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The single NEW assumption that multiple regression adds beyond single-variable regression is:\nA) Homoskedastic errors. B) No perfect multicollinearity among the regressors. C) Zero mean of the errors. D) Random sampling of the data.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what extra requirement appears once you have more than one explanatory variable.\n\nWith several regressors, a new problem can arise: one regressor could be an exact linear combination of the others. If so, OLS cannot separate their effects and cannot even be computed. So multiple regression adds the requirement of NO perfect multicollinearity. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (homoskedasticity): this assumption already exists in single-variable regression — it is not new.\n- C (zero-mean errors / exogeneity): this is a core assumption carried over from single regression, not newly introduced.\n- D (random sampling): also a carried-over assumption from single regression.\n\nAnalogy: with one regressor there is nothing for it to be collinear WITH. Once you have a team of regressors, you must make sure no two of them are perfect duplicates, or the computer cannot assign individual credit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: All single-regression assumptions carry over; multiple regression just ADDS \"no perfect multicollinearity.\" Perfect collinearity = OLS can't be computed at all.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"NEW assumption added by multiple regression\",\"options\":[\"Homoskedastic errors\",\"No perfect multicollinearity\",\"Zero mean of the errors\",\"Random sampling\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Others carry over from single regression; only multiple regression can have one regressor be an exact linear combo of the others\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which statement correctly contrasts $$R^2$$ and adjusted $$R^2$$?\nA) Adjusted $$R^2$$ is always greater than $$R^2$$. B) $$R^2$$ can decrease when adding a regressor, but adjusted $$R^2$$ cannot. C) Adjusted $$R^2$$ can decrease when a weak regressor is added and can even be negative, while $$R^2$$ cannot. D) A high $$R^2$$ proves the model is correctly specified and causal.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the true relationship between the two goodness-of-fit measures.\n\nThe facts:\n- Plain $$R^2$$ never falls when you add a regressor and stays between 0 and 1.\n- Adjusted $$R^2$$ penalizes extra parameters: it CAN fall when a weak regressor is added, and it CAN even go negative if the fit is very poor relative to the penalty.\n\nSo the correct contrast is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: backwards — $$\\bar{R}^2 \\le R^2$$ always, so adjusted is never greater.\n- B: this swaps the two measures. It is $$R^2$$ that never decreases; adjusted $$R^2$$ is the one that can decrease.\n- D: a high $$R^2$$ proves NEITHER causality NOR correct specification — a notorious trap. Correlation/fit is not causation.\n\nAnalogy: $$R^2$$ is the optimistic salesperson who never reports a loss from adding features. Adjusted $$R^2$$ is the honest accountant who will show a loss if a feature does not pay for itself — and can even report a \"net negative.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\bar{R}^2 \\le R^2$$ always; only adjusted $$R^2$$ can fall or go negative. High $$R^2$$ ≠ causation and ≠ correct specification.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** With $$n = 30$$, $$k = 4$$, and $$R^2 = 0.52$$, adjusted $$R^2$$ is closest to:\nA) 0.52 B) 0.44 C) 0.48 D) 0.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\bar R^2 = 1 - 0.48 \\times \\frac{29}{25} = 1 - 0.5568 = 0.443$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In a wage regression with an intercept, including dummies for ALL four regions causes:\nA) Omitted variable bias B) Heteroskedasticity C) Perfect multicollinearity D) Autocorrelation\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The dummy variable trap: the four dummies sum to the constant. Drop one category.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A significant overall F-statistic with all individual t-statistics insignificant most likely indicates:\nA) The model is useless B) Multicollinearity among regressors C) Heteroskedastic errors D) A nonlinear relationship\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Correlated regressors inflate individual SEs while the group still explains $$Y$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** $$\\beta_2 = -0.6$$ in a multiple regression of bond spread on rating dummies and leverage. The correct interpretation of $$-0.6$$ on leverage is the expected change in spread per unit of leverage:\nA) Ignoring all other variables B) Holding the other included regressors constant C) Only when all other variables are zero D) Only for the omitted rating category\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Multiple-regression coefficients are partial effects, holding other regressors fixed.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A regression with $$k = 3$$ explanatory variables and $$n = 62$$ observations has $$R^2 = 0.64$$. The adjusted R² is closest to:\nA) 0.6034 B) 0.6214 C) 0.6400 D) 0.6595\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - (1 - 0.64)\\,\\frac{62-1}{62-3-1} = 1 - 0.36 \\times \\frac{61}{58} = 1 - 0.3786 = 0.6214$$\n\nAdjusted R² is always below R² (C) when $$k \\ge 1$$. Check the df ratio: numerator $$n-1$$, denominator $$n-k-1$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A hedge ratio regression estimates $$\\hat\\beta = 0.85$$ with standard error 0.34 (large sample). Testing $$H_0\\!: \\beta = 1$$ against $$H_1\\!: \\beta \\neq 1$$ at 5%, the best conclusion is:\nA) t = −0.44; fail to reject — the slope is not significantly different from 1 B) t = 2.50; reject — the slope is significantly different from 1 C) t = −0.44; reject — the slope is significantly different from 1 D) t = 2.50; fail to reject — the slope is not significantly different from 1\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$t = \\frac{0.85 - 1.00}{0.34} = -0.44$$\n\n$$|{-0.44}| < 1.96$$, so the slope is statistically indistinguishable from 1 — fail to reject. Distractor B is the trap: $$t = 0.85/0.34 = 2.50$$ tests $$\\beta = 0$$, which is **not** the hypothesis asked. Always test against the value stated in the question.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Which null? β=1 vs β=0\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"H0: β=1 (asked)\",\"items\":[\"t=(0.85−1)/0.34\",\"t=−0.44\",\"|t|<1.96 → fail to reject\"]},{\"h\":\"H0: β=0 (trap)\",\"items\":[\"t=0.85/0.34\",\"t=2.50\",\"tests significance, not hedge\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A multiple regression with $$k = 6$$ slope regressors is estimated on $$n = 80$$ observations and reports $$R^2 = 0.45$$. What is the adjusted $$R^2$$?\nA) 0.405 B) 0.450 C) 0.378 D) 0.495\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the adjusted $$R^2$$ given $$R^2$$, $$n$$, and $$k$$.\n\n1. The formula penalizes for the number of slope coefficients:\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1 - R^2)\\,\\frac{n - 1}{n - k - 1}$$\n2. Plug in $$n = 80$$, $$k = 6$$:\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1 - 0.45)\\,\\frac{80 - 1}{80 - 6 - 1} = 1 - 0.55 \\times \\frac{79}{73}$$\n3. Compute the ratio and product:\n$$\\frac{79}{73} = 1.0822, \\qquad 0.55 \\times 1.0822 = 0.5952$$\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - 0.5952 = 0.4048 \\approx 0.405$$\n\nWhy A is correct: $$\\bar R^2 = 1 - 0.55(79/73) = 0.405$$.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** just restates $$R^2$$ — forgetting to adjust at all.\n- **C** uses $$n - k - 1$$ in *both* numerator and denominator (or off-by-one degrees of freedom), e.g. $$1 - 0.55(79/74) = 0.413$$... a common df slip producing too low a value.\n- **D** mistakenly *adds* the penalty term, pushing the adjusted figure above $$R^2$$ — impossible, since $$\\bar R^2 \\le R^2$$ always.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use $$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1-R^2)\\frac{n-1}{n-k-1}$$. The numerator df is $$n-1$$, the denominator df is $$n-k-1$$ (k = slope count). Adjusted $$R^2$$ can only be ≤ $$R^2$$ — if you get a higher number, you flipped a term.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A model with $$k = 4$$ regressors estimated on $$n = 65$$ observations has $$R^2 = 0.28$$. Test the overall null $$H_0: \\beta_1 = \\beta_2 = \\beta_3 = \\beta_4 = 0$$ at 5% (critical $$F_{4,60} \\approx 2.53$$). What is the F-statistic and decision?\nA) F = 5.83; reject — the regressors are jointly significant B) F = 5.83; fail to reject — the regressors are not jointly significant C) F = 0.097; fail to reject — the regressors are not jointly significant D) F = 4.20; reject — the regressors are jointly significant\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the overall F-statistic and decision.\n\n1. The all-slopes-zero F uses $$R^2$$ directly:\n$$F = \\frac{R^2 / k}{(1 - R^2)/(n - k - 1)}$$\n2. Plug in $$R^2 = 0.28$$, $$k = 4$$, $$n = 65$$:\n$$F = \\frac{0.28 / 4}{(1 - 0.28)/(65 - 4 - 1)} = \\frac{0.07}{0.72/60} = \\frac{0.07}{0.012} = 5.83$$\n3. Compare to $$F_{4,60} \\approx 2.53$$. Since $$5.83 > 2.53$$, **reject** $$H_0$$ — the regressors are jointly significant.\n\nWhy A is correct: numerator $$0.07$$, denominator $$0.012$$, ratio 5.83, which exceeds 2.53.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes F correctly but flips the (always upper-tail) decision.\n- **C** inverts the ratio ($$0.012/0.07 \\times$$ ... ≈ 0.097) — puts $$(1-R^2)$$ on top.\n- **D** uses the wrong denominator df, e.g. $$n - k = 61$$ combined with a miscalculation, deflating F.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The overall F is always an upper-tail test — large F ⇒ reject. The shortcut form $$\\frac{R^2/k}{(1-R^2)/(n-k-1)}$$ saves you from needing the raw sums of squares.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An unrestricted model with $$k_u = 5$$ regressors on $$n = 70$$ observations has $$RSS_u = 320$$. Dropping 3 of the regressors gives a restricted model with $$RSS_r = 380$$. Test whether those 3 coefficients are jointly zero at 5% (critical $$F_{3,64} \\approx 2.75$$). What is the F-statistic and decision?\nA) F = 4.00; reject — the three dropped regressors jointly matter B) F = 4.00; fail to reject — the three dropped regressors do not matter C) F = 1.33; fail to reject — the three dropped regressors do not matter D) F = 12.0; reject — the three dropped regressors jointly matter\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the restricted-versus-unrestricted F and decision, with $$q = 3$$ restrictions.\n\n1. The F-statistic compares the rise in RSS from imposing the restrictions to the unrestricted residual variance:\n$$F = \\frac{(RSS_r - RSS_u)/q}{RSS_u/(n - k_u - 1)}$$\n2. Plug in $$RSS_r = 380$$, $$RSS_u = 320$$, $$q = 3$$, $$n - k_u - 1 = 70 - 5 - 1 = 64$$:\n$$F = \\frac{(380 - 320)/3}{320/64} = \\frac{60/3}{5} = \\frac{20}{5} = 4.00$$\n3. Compare to $$F_{3,64} \\approx 2.75$$. Since $$4.00 > 2.75$$, **reject** — the three restrictions are rejected, so the dropped regressors jointly belong in the model.\n\nWhy A is correct: numerator $$20$$, denominator $$5$$, F = 4.00 > 2.75.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes F correctly but flips the decision.\n- **C** forgets to divide the numerator by $$q$$ ($$60/320 \\times 64/... $$) or uses $$60/(something)$$ giving ≈1.33 — dropping the $$q$$ scaling.\n- **D** forgets to divide the numerator by $$q$$ entirely: $$60/5 = 12$$ — treats $$q = 1$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In the partial F-test, the numerator is $$(RSS_r - RSS_u)/q$$ with $$q$$ = number of restrictions, and the denominator is $$RSS_u/(n-k_u-1)$$. Forgetting to divide by $$q$$ is the classic error.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A wage regression includes a constant (intercept) and the analyst wants to control for region. There are four regions. The analyst adds a separate dummy variable for **every** region in addition to the intercept. What happens and what is the fix?\nA) Perfect multicollinearity — OLS cannot be estimated; omit one region as the base category B) Omitted variable bias — the region effect is absorbed by the intercept; add an interaction term C) Heteroskedasticity — the regional groups have different variances; use robust standard errors D) Imperfect multicollinearity — standard errors inflate but OLS still runs; do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to diagnose the consequence of including all category dummies plus an intercept.\n\n1. With four regional dummies $$D_1, D_2, D_3, D_4$$ that each equal 1 for exactly one region, every observation falls in exactly one region, so $$D_1 + D_2 + D_3 + D_4 = 1$$ for all observations.\n2. The intercept column is also a column of 1s. Thus the four dummies sum exactly to the intercept — one regressor is an exact linear combination of the others.\n3. This is **perfect (exact) multicollinearity** (the \"dummy variable trap\"): OLS cannot invert the regressor matrix and cannot be computed.\n4. Fix: **drop one dummy** (the base category); its level is captured by the intercept, and the remaining dummies measure differences relative to it.\n\nWhy A is correct: exact linear dependence ⇒ OLS undefined ⇒ omit one category.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** misnames it omitted variable bias — nothing relevant is omitted; the problem is redundancy, not omission.\n- **C** invokes heteroskedasticity, which concerns error *variance*, not collinearity among regressors.\n- **D** calls it imperfect multicollinearity and says \"do nothing\" — but exact collinearity makes estimation impossible, so a regressor *must* be dropped.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Number of dummies should be (number of categories − 1) when an intercept is present. Including all categories plus the constant is the dummy variable trap: perfect collinearity, not merely high correlation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"All 4 region dummies + intercept →\",\"options\":[\"Perfect multicollinearity — drop one region\",\"Omitted variable bias — add interaction\",\"Heteroskedasticity — use robust SEs\",\"Imperfect multicollinearity — do nothing\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"D1+D2+D3+D4 = 1 = the intercept column → exact linear dependence; OLS undefined until one dummy is dropped\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A regression of corporate bond spreads on five regressors reports a highly significant overall F-statistic ($$p < 0.001$$), a high $$R^2$$ of 0.82, yet not one of the five individual t-statistics is significant at 5%. What is the most likely explanation?\nA) Multicollinearity — the regressors are jointly informative but individually imprecise B) Omitted variable bias — a key driver of spreads is missing C) Heteroskedasticity — the error variance depends on the regressors D) The model is misspecified and should be discarded\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to identify the signature pattern: significant F, insignificant individual t's, high $$R^2$$.\n\n1. A significant overall F says the regressors **as a group** explain the dependent variable.\n2. Insignificant individual t's say no single coefficient is estimated precisely enough to stand out.\n3. This combination — group matters, individuals don't, with high $$R^2$$ — is the **textbook signature of multicollinearity**. When regressors are highly correlated, OLS cannot disentangle their separate effects, so each standard error balloons and individual t's collapse, even though the joint fit is strong.\n\nWhy A is correct: it is the precise multicollinearity fingerprint.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** omitted variable bias biases coefficients but does not produce the \"significant F, insignificant t's\" pattern.\n- **C** heteroskedasticity corrupts standard errors but does not specifically generate a strong joint fit with weak individual significance.\n- **D** discarding the model is wrong — the variables clearly matter jointly; the issue is precision of separate effects, often addressed by combining/dropping collinear regressors or getting more data.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Significant F but no significant t's\" ⇒ think multicollinearity. The variables matter as a bundle; their individual effects just can't be separated.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Multicollinearity fingerprint\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"As a group\",\"items\":[\"R² = 0.82 (high)\",\"F significant (p<0.001)\",\"jointly informative\"]},{\"h\":\"Individually\",\"items\":[\"no t significant\",\"SEs balloon\",\"effects can't be separated\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A multiple regression of portfolio return on three factors reports $$\\hat\\beta_2 = 1.30$$ for the value factor with $$SE(\\hat\\beta_2) = 0.50$$, on a large sample. Construct the 95% confidence interval and assess $$H_0: \\beta_2 = 0$$.\nA) CI = $$(0.32, 2.28)$$; reject $$H_0$$ — the value factor is significant B) CI = $$(0.80, 1.80)$$; reject $$H_0$$ — the value factor is significant C) CI = $$(0.32, 2.28)$$; fail to reject $$H_0$$ — the value factor is insignificant D) CI = $$(-0.18, 2.78)$$; fail to reject $$H_0$$ — the value factor is insignificant\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the 95% CI for a single coefficient in a multiple regression and the test of significance.\n\n1. Individual-coefficient CI uses the t-multiplier; on a large sample $$t \\approx 1.96$$:\n$$\\hat\\beta_2 \\pm 1.96 \\times SE(\\hat\\beta_2) = 1.30 \\pm 1.96 \\times 0.50 = 1.30 \\pm 0.98$$\n$$= (0.32,\\; 2.28)$$\n2. By duality, the null value 0 lies **outside** $$(0.32, 2.28)$$, so **reject** $$H_0: \\beta_2 = 0$$ — the value factor is significant. (Check: $$t = 1.30/0.50 = 2.60 > 1.96$$ ⇒ reject. Consistent.)\n\nWhy A is correct: margin $$1.96 \\times 0.50 = 0.98$$ gives $$(0.32, 2.28)$$, which excludes 0.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** uses the bare SE as the margin ($$\\pm 0.50$$) instead of $$1.96 \\times SE$$.\n- **C** has the right interval but flips the decision — 0 is outside, so reject.\n- **D** doubles the margin ($$\\pm 1.48$$, perhaps using $$2.96$$ or mis-multiplying) so the interval spans 0 and wrongly fails to reject.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Individual-coefficient inference in multiple regression is still a t-test/CI on one $$\\hat\\beta_j$$ — the F-test is only for *joint* nulls. CI half-width = $$t_{\\alpha/2}\\times SE$$, not the bare SE.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A model with $$n = 50$$, $$k = 4$$, and $$R^2 = 0.36$$ is augmented with a fifth regressor whose individual t-statistic turns out to be 0.9 (insignificant at 5%), raising $$R^2$$ to 0.37. Which statement is correct?\nA) Adjusted $$R^2$$ likely fell, because the new regressor's $$|t| < 1$$ — it does not earn its degree of freedom B) Adjusted $$R^2$$ must have risen, because $$R^2$$ rose C) The new regressor is statistically significant because $$R^2$$ increased D) Adjusted $$R^2$$ is unchanged because $$R^2$$ changed by only 0.01\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked how adjusted $$R^2$$ responds when a weak ($$|t|<1$$) regressor is added.\n\n1. Key rule: adding a regressor raises adjusted $$R^2$$ only if its $$|t| > 1$$. With $$|t| = 0.9 < 1$$, the variable fails this hurdle, so adjusted $$R^2$$ **falls** (or at best stays flat).\n2. Confirm numerically. Before ($$k=4$$):\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1-0.36)\\frac{49}{45} = 1 - 0.64 \\times 1.0889 = 1 - 0.6969 = 0.3031$$\nAfter ($$k=5$$, $$R^2=0.37$$):\n$$\\bar R^2 = 1 - (1-0.37)\\frac{49}{44} = 1 - 0.63 \\times 1.1136 = 1 - 0.7016 = 0.2984$$\n3. Adjusted $$R^2$$ dropped from ≈0.303 to ≈0.298 — confirming the $$|t|<1$$ rule.\n\nWhy A is correct: $$|t| = 0.9 < 1$$, so the penalty outweighs the tiny $$R^2$$ gain and adjusted $$R^2$$ declines.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** assumes adjusted $$R^2$$ tracks $$R^2$$ — false; the whole point of the adjustment is that it can fall when $$R^2$$ rises only slightly.\n- **C** equates a higher $$R^2$$ with significance — $$R^2$$ never falls when adding a regressor, so it says nothing about significance.\n- **D** asserts no change from a small $$R^2$$ move, but the degrees-of-freedom penalty still bites.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The $$|t| > 1$$ rule is the cutoff for adjusted $$R^2$$ to rise — a far lower bar than the $$|t| > 1.96$$ needed for 5% significance. \"Adjusted $$R^2$$ went up\" does NOT mean the variable is significant.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Added regressor's |t| and adjusted R²\",\"items\":[\"|t| < 1: adj R² FALLS\",\"||t| = 1 (break-even)\",\"|t| > 1: adj R² rises\",\"||t| = 1.96 (5% significant)\"],\"left\":\"new var (|t|=0.9) here → adj R² fell\",\"right\":\"earns its keep\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** In a multiple regression of credit spread on a leverage ratio and a set of rating dummy variables (investment grade as the base category), the coefficient on leverage is estimated at $$+0.85$$. What is the correct interpretation of this coefficient?\nA) The expected increase in spread per one-unit increase in leverage, holding the rating dummies constant B) The expected increase in spread per one-unit increase in leverage, ignoring all other regressors C) The expected spread when leverage and all rating dummies equal zero D) The expected increase in spread per one-unit increase in leverage, but only for the investment-grade base category\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the correct interpretation of a slope coefficient in a multiple regression.\n\n1. In multiple regression, each slope is a **partial (ceteris paribus) effect**: $$\\beta_j$$ is the expected change in $$Y$$ for a one-unit change in $$X_j$$ **holding all other included regressors fixed**.\n2. So $$+0.85$$ means: with the rating dummies held constant, a one-unit rise in leverage is associated with a 0.85-unit rise in spread.\n\nWhy A is correct: it states the partial-effect interpretation, which is what distinguishes multiple-regression coefficients from simple-regression slopes.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** \"ignoring all other regressors\" describes a *simple* (univariate) regression slope; in multiple regression the effect is conditional on the other regressors, not marginal.\n- **C** describes the *intercept* (value of $$Y$$ when all regressors are zero), not the leverage slope.\n- **D** wrongly restricts the leverage effect to one rating category — the leverage coefficient applies across all observations (the dummies shift the intercept, they do not localize the leverage slope unless an interaction term is present).\n\n📌 Exam tip: A multiple-regression coefficient is always \"holding the other regressors constant.\" If a question adds \"ignoring other variables\" or \"only for one group,\" it is describing a different (simple-regression or interaction) quantity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Leverage coefficient +0.85 means:\",\"options\":[\"ΔY per ΔX, holding rating dummies constant\",\"ΔY per ΔX, ignoring all other regressors\",\"Spread when leverage and dummies all = 0\",\"ΔY per ΔX, only for investment-grade base\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Multiple-regression slopes are partial (ceteris paribus) effects — hold the other included regressors fixed\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"209","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Regression Diagnostics","notes":"# 9. Regression Diagnostics\n\nWhat breaks OLS and what to do about it: heteroskedasticity, multicollinearity, outliers, omitted/extraneous variables, and the bias–variance trade-off in model selection. The exam tests *consequences and remedies* — which problems bias coefficients vs. which only corrupt standard errors.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain how to test whether a regression is affected by heteroskedasticity.\n- Describe approaches to using heteroskedastic data.\n- Characterize multicollinearity and its consequences; distinguish between multicollinearity and perfect collinearity.\n- Describe the consequences of excluding a relevant explanatory variable from a model and including an irrelevant regressor.\n- Explain two model-selection procedures and how these relate to the bias–variance trade-off.\n\n## Heteroskedasticity\n\nError variance that depends on the regressors: $$\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_i \\mid X_i) = \\sigma_i^2$$. Common in finance (volatility clustering, size effects).\n\n**Consequences:** OLS coefficients remain unbiased and consistent, but the usual standard errors are wrong (typically understated), so t- and F-tests mislead. OLS is no longer efficient (not BLUE).\n\n**Detection — White test:** regress squared residuals $$\\hat\\epsilon_i^2$$ on the regressors, their squares, and cross-products. Under homoskedasticity,\n\n$$nR^2_{\\text{aux}} \\sim \\chi^2_{m}$$\n\nwhere $$m$$ is the number of auxiliary slope regressors. Large statistic ⇒ reject homoskedasticity.\n\n**Remedies:** (1) **White (heteroskedasticity-robust) standard errors** — keep OLS coefficients, fix the SEs; the default in practice. (2) Weighted least squares if the variance structure is known. (3) Transform the data (e.g., logs).\n\n## Multicollinearity\n\n**Perfect collinearity:** one regressor is an exact linear function of others — OLS cannot be computed; one variable must be dropped (the dummy trap is the standard example).\n\n**Multicollinearity (imperfect):** regressors are highly but not perfectly correlated. OLS is still unbiased and consistent, but coefficient standard errors balloon: individual t-stats look insignificant, coefficients are unstable across samples and can carry \"wrong\" signs, while $$R^2$$ and the F-stat stay healthy.\n\n**Detection:** high pairwise correlations; high **variance inflation factor**,\n\n$$VIF_j = \\frac{1}{1 - R_j^2}$$\n\nwhere $$R_j^2$$ comes from regressing $$X_j$$ on the other regressors. Rule of thumb: $$VIF > 10$$ ($$R_j^2 > 0.9$$) signals trouble.\n\n**Remedies:** drop or combine redundant regressors, get more data, or use regularization (ridge/LASSO — see Ch 14).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Keep the damage straight: heteroskedasticity and multicollinearity leave OLS coefficients **unbiased** — they damage standard errors / precision. **Omitted variable bias** is the violation that actually biases coefficients. Exam answers hinge on this distinction.\n\n## Specification errors\n\n**Omitting a relevant variable:** if the omitted variable is correlated with an included regressor, the included coefficient is **biased and inconsistent** — it absorbs the omitted effect. Direction of bias = (sign of omitted variable's effect on $$Y$$) × (sign of its correlation with the included $$X$$).\n\n**Including an irrelevant variable:** coefficients stay unbiased, but estimator variance rises (wasted degrees of freedom, possible collinearity) — a pure efficiency cost.\n\n**Outliers:** observations with extreme $$X$$ values (high leverage) can dominate OLS because errors are *squared*. Inspect with scatter plots and Cook's distance; consider robust estimators or verify data validity before deleting.\n\n## Model selection and the bias–variance trade-off\n\n- Too few variables ⇒ **bias** (underfitting); too many ⇒ **variance** (overfitting — the model fits in-sample noise and predicts poorly out-of-sample).\n- **General-to-specific:** start with everything plausible, iteratively drop the regressor with the smallest insignificant $$\\lvert t \\rvert$$ until all remaining are significant.\n- **m-fold cross-validation:** split the data into $$m$$ blocks; repeatedly fit on $$m-1$$ blocks, evaluate out-of-sample on the held-out block; choose the model with the best average out-of-sample fit. Directly targets prediction error rather than in-sample fit.\n- Information criteria (AIC/BIC) penalize parameters analytically; BIC penalizes harder, choosing smaller models.\n\n### Worked example: White test\n\nAuxiliary regression of squared residuals on $$X_1$$, $$X_2$$, $$X_1^2$$, $$X_2^2$$, $$X_1 X_2$$ gives $$R^2_{\\text{aux}} = 0.12$$ with $$n = 100$$.\n\n$$nR^2 = 100 \\times 0.12 = 12.0$$\n\nCritical $$\\chi^2_5$$ at 5% = 11.07. Since $$12.0 > 11.07$$, reject homoskedasticity — switch to White robust standard errors before trusting any t-test.\n\n### Worked example: VIF\n\nRegressing regressor $$X_3$$ on the other four regressors yields $$R_3^2 = 0.95$$.\n\n$$VIF_3 = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.95} = 20$$\n\nThe variance of $$\\hat\\beta_3$$ is 20 times what it would be with orthogonal regressors — its standard error is $$\\sqrt{20} \\approx 4.5$$ times larger. Well above the 10 threshold: drop or combine collinear variables, or accept that $$\\beta_3$$ cannot be isolated.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What are the consequences of heteroskedasticity for OLS?\n> **A:** Coefficients stay unbiased and consistent, but the usual standard errors are wrong (typically understated), so t- and F-tests mislead; OLS is no longer efficient (not BLUE).\n\n**Q2.** Describe the White test for heteroskedasticity.\n> **A:** Regress squared residuals $$\\hat\\epsilon_i^2$$ on the regressors, their squares, and cross-products; under homoskedasticity $$nR^2_{\\text{aux}} \\sim \\chi^2_m$$ ($$m$$ = auxiliary slopes). Large statistic ⇒ reject.\n\n**Q3.** What are the remedies for heteroskedasticity?\n> **A:** (1) White (robust) standard errors — keep OLS coefficients, fix the SEs (default); (2) weighted least squares if the variance structure is known; (3) transform the data (e.g., logs).\n\n**Q4.** Distinguish perfect collinearity from imperfect multicollinearity.\n> **A:** Perfect: a regressor is an exact linear function of others — OLS can't be computed; drop one. Imperfect: regressors highly but not perfectly correlated — OLS still unbiased, but SEs balloon.\n\n**Q5.** What are the symptoms of multicollinearity?\n> **A:** Inflated coefficient SEs, individually insignificant t-stats, unstable coefficients with possible \"wrong\" signs — while $$R^2$$ and the F-stat stay healthy.\n\n**Q6.** Give the variance inflation factor and the rule of thumb.\n> **A:** $$VIF_j = \\dfrac{1}{1 - R_j^2}$$, where $$R_j^2$$ comes from regressing $$X_j$$ on the other regressors. $$VIF > 10$$ ($$R_j^2 > 0.9$$) signals trouble.\n\n**Q7.** Which problems leave coefficients unbiased, and which actually bias them?\n> **A:** Heteroskedasticity and multicollinearity leave coefficients unbiased (they damage precision/SEs). Omitted variable bias is the violation that biases coefficients.\n\n**Q8.** Give the direction of omitted variable bias.\n> **A:** Direction = (sign of omitted variable's effect on $$Y$$) × (sign of its correlation with the included $$X$$).\n\n**Q9.** What is the cost of including an irrelevant variable?\n> **A:** Coefficients stay unbiased, but estimator variance rises (wasted d.o.f., possible collinearity) — a pure efficiency cost.\n\n**Q10.** Auxiliary White regression gives $$R^2_{\\text{aux}} = 0.12$$ with $$n = 100$$, 5 auxiliary slopes (crit $$\\chi^2_5 = 11.07$$). Decision?\n> **A:** $$nR^2 = 12.0 > 11.07$$ → reject homoskedasticity; use White robust standard errors.\n\n**Q11.** Regressing $$X_3$$ on the other regressors gives $$R_3^2 = 0.95$$. Find the VIF and interpret.\n> **A:** $$VIF_3 = 1/(1-0.95) = 20$$; the variance of $$\\hat\\beta_3$$ is 20× larger, SE ≈ 4.5× larger — drop or combine the collinear variables.\n\n**Q12.** Explain the bias–variance trade-off in model selection.\n> **A:** Too few variables ⇒ bias (underfitting); too many ⇒ variance (overfitting, fits in-sample noise, predicts poorly out-of-sample).\n\n**Q13.** Describe general-to-specific selection and m-fold cross-validation.\n> **A:** General-to-specific: start with everything, iteratively drop the least significant regressor until all remain significant. Cross-validation: split into $$m$$ blocks, fit on $$m-1$$ and test on the held-out block, choose the best average out-of-sample fit.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Heteroskedasticity in OLS regression errors causes:\nA) Biased coefficients and invalid standard errors. B) Unbiased coefficients but invalid standard errors. C) Biased coefficients but valid standard errors. D) No consequences in large samples.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what heteroskedasticity (error variance that changes with $$X$$) does to a regression.\n\nThe key facts:\n- OLS coefficients stay UNBIASED and consistent — the point estimates are still on target.\n- The usual textbook standard errors assume constant error variance, so they become WRONG (usually understated), which makes t-tests and F-tests unreliable.\n\nSo coefficients survive but inference does not. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: heteroskedasticity does not bias the coefficients — only the standard errors are affected.\n- C: the standard errors are the part that breaks; they are not valid.\n- D: it does have consequences — the inference (t/F tests) is misleading regardless of sample size, unless you use robust standard errors.\n\nAnalogy: a scale that is correct on average but jittery for heavy items. The readings are right on average (unbiased coefficients), but the stated \"± precision\" (standard error) is wrong.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Heteroskedasticity → unbiased coefficients, broken SEs. Fix with White (robust) standard errors. Keep this straight from omitted-variable bias, which biases the coefficients.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Heteroskedasticity effects\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Coefficients\",\"items\":[\"UNBIASED\",\"consistent\",\"point estimates fine\"]},{\"h\":\"Standard errors\",\"items\":[\"INVALID (usually understated)\",\"t/F tests unreliable\",\"fix: White robust SEs\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A regression shows $$R^2 = 0.85$$, a highly significant overall F-statistic, but no individual t-statistic is significant. The most likely cause is:\nA) Heteroskedasticity. B) Omitted variable bias. C) Multicollinearity. D) Too large a sample size.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question describes a tell-tale pattern: the regressors explain a lot jointly (high $$R^2$$, significant F) but no single one is individually significant.\n\nThis is the classic signature of multicollinearity. When regressors are highly correlated with each other, OLS cannot cleanly attribute the effect to any one of them, so each individual standard error inflates and each t-stat shrinks toward insignificance. Yet collectively they still explain $$Y$$, so $$R^2$$ and the F-stat remain strong. The correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: heteroskedasticity distorts standard errors generally but does not specifically produce \"F-significant, all-t-insignificant.\"\n- B: omitted variable bias distorts the size of coefficients but does not create this joint-vs-individual contradiction.\n- D: a large sample tends to make t-stats LARGER, increasing significance — the opposite of the symptom described.\n\nAnalogy: a band sounds great together (significant F) but you cannot pick out which musician is carrying the tune (no individual significance) because they are all playing nearly the same notes (correlated regressors).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Significant F + all t's insignificant = multicollinearity. Detect with VIF; the group matters but individuals can't be separated.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A relevant regressor is omitted from a model. It is positively correlated with $$Y$$ AND positively correlated with the included regressor $$X_1$$. The effect on $$\\hat\\beta_1$$ is to:\nA) Bias it upward. B) Bias it downward. C) Leave it unbiased but inefficient. D) Affect only the intercept.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the DIRECTION of omitted-variable bias.\n\nThe rule for the sign of the bias:\n$$\\text{bias direction} = (\\text{sign of omitted variable's effect on } Y) \\times (\\text{sign of its correlation with } X_1)$$\n\nHere both signs are positive:\n$$(+) \\times (+) = (+)$$\n\nSo the bias is positive — $$\\hat\\beta_1$$ is biased UPWARD. The included regressor \"steals credit\" for the omitted variable's positive effect. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B: a downward bias would require the two signs to multiply to negative (one positive, one negative).\n- C: omitting a RELEVANT variable that is correlated with $$X_1$$ biases the coefficient — it is not merely inefficient. (Inefficiency-only happens when you INCLUDE an irrelevant variable.)\n- D: the bias hits the slope $$\\hat\\beta_1$$, not just the intercept.\n\nAnalogy: if you forget to account for \"hours studied\" (which boosts grades and is correlated with \"hours of tutoring\"), then tutoring will look more powerful than it really is — it grabs the missing variable's credit. Upward bias.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OV bias sign = (effect of omitted on Y) × (corr of omitted with included X). Same signs → upward; opposite signs → downward.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Sign of omitted-variable bias\",\"steps\":[\"Omitted's effect on Y: (+)\",\"Corr of omitted with X1: (+)\",\"Bias = (+)×(+) = (+)\",\"β̂1 biased UPWARD\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In the auxiliary regression for diagnosing multicollinearity, regressing $$X_j$$ on the other regressors gives $$R_j^2 = 0.80$$. The variance inflation factor (VIF) for $$X_j$$ is:\nA) 1.25 B) 4 C) 5 D) 10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks you to compute the VIF, which measures how much collinearity inflates a coefficient's variance.\n\nStep 1 — The formula:\n$$VIF_j = \\frac{1}{1 - R_j^2}$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in $$R_j^2 = 0.80$$:\n$$VIF_j = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.80} = \\frac{1}{0.20} = 5$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (1.25): this is $$1/0.80$$ — dividing by $$R_j^2$$ instead of $$1 - R_j^2$$.\n- B (4): comes from a wrong denominator like $$1 - 0.75$$ or arithmetic slip.\n- D (10): this is the rule-of-thumb THRESHOLD for \"trouble\" ($$VIF > 10$$, i.e., $$R_j^2 > 0.9$$), not the value for $$R_j^2 = 0.80$$.\n\nAnalogy: VIF tells you how many times bigger the coefficient's variance is, compared with a world where regressors don't overlap. Here it is 5 times bigger — noticeable but below the \"alarm\" level of 10.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$VIF = 1/(1 - R_j^2)$$. Rule of thumb: VIF > 10 (i.e., $$R_j^2 > 0.9$$) signals serious multicollinearity.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A White test for heteroskedasticity regresses squared residuals on the regressors, their squares, and cross-products, yielding $$R^2_{\\text{aux}} = 0.12$$ with $$n = 100$$ and $$m = 5$$ auxiliary slope regressors. The test statistic and decision are (critical $$\\chi^2_5$$ at 5% = 11.07):\nA) 12.0; reject homoskedasticity — use robust standard errors. B) 12.0; fail to reject homoskedasticity. C) 0.12; fail to reject homoskedasticity. D) 5.00; reject homoskedasticity.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks you to run the White test: compute the statistic and decide whether the errors are heteroskedastic.\n\nStep 1 — The White test statistic:\n$$nR^2_{\\text{aux}}$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in:\n$$100 \\times 0.12 = 12.0$$\n\nStep 3 — Compare to the chi-square critical value with $$m = 5$$ degrees of freedom, which is 11.07:\n$$12.0 > 11.07$$\n\nSince the statistic exceeds the critical value, reject the null of homoskedasticity. The errors are heteroskedastic — switch to White robust standard errors before trusting any t-test. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B: the statistic 12.0 is correct, but exceeding 11.07 means REJECT, not fail to reject. Decision backwards.\n- C (0.12): this forgets to multiply by $$n$$ — it is just $$R^2_{\\text{aux}}$$.\n- D (5.00): this confuses $$m$$ (the degrees of freedom) with the statistic.\n\nAnalogy: the White test asks \"do the regressors help predict the SIZE of the squared errors?\" If they do (statistic above the cutoff), the error spread is not constant — that is heteroskedasticity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: White test statistic = $$n \\times R^2_{\\text{aux}}$$, compared to $$\\chi^2_m$$. Reject for large values → heteroskedasticity present.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Including an IRRELEVANT explanatory variable in a regression:\nA) Biases the other coefficients. B) Leaves coefficients unbiased but increases estimator variance. C) Makes OLS inconsistent. D) Always lowers $$R^2$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the cost of adding a variable that does not belong (the opposite mistake from omitting a relevant one).\n\nIncluding an irrelevant variable does NOT bias the coefficients — OLS still estimates the true effects correctly on average. But it wastes a degree of freedom and may introduce some collinearity, so the estimator's variance rises and estimates become less precise. It is a pure EFFICIENCY cost, not a bias. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: an irrelevant variable does not bias the other coefficients (unlike OMITTING a relevant one, which does bias).\n- C: OLS remains consistent; precision falls but the estimates still converge to the truth.\n- D: adding ANY variable can only keep $$R^2$$ the same or raise it — it never lowers plain $$R^2$$. (Adjusted $$R^2$$ might fall, but plain $$R^2$$ does not.)\n\nAnalogy: bringing an extra, useless tool to a job doesn't make you do the job WRONG (no bias), but it clutters your bench and makes you a bit less efficient (higher variance).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Irrelevant variable → unbiased but less efficient (higher variance). Omitted relevant variable → biased. Opposite errors, opposite consequences.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two specification errors\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Include IRRELEVANT var\",\"items\":[\"coefficients UNBIASED\",\"variance rises (less efficient)\",\"wastes a degree of freedom\"]},{\"h\":\"Omit RELEVANT var\",\"items\":[\"coefficients BIASED\",\"inconsistent\",\"the dangerous error\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which statement correctly distinguishes the consequences of common regression problems?\nA) Both heteroskedasticity and omitted variable bias bias the coefficients. B) Heteroskedasticity and multicollinearity leave coefficients unbiased; omitted variable bias does not. C) Multicollinearity biases the coefficients, but heteroskedasticity does not. D) All three problems make OLS inconsistent.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the most important distinction in this chapter: which problems bias the coefficients and which only damage precision/standard errors.\n\nThe facts:\n- Heteroskedasticity → coefficients UNBIASED; only standard errors corrupted.\n- Multicollinearity → coefficients UNBIASED; standard errors inflated (imprecise).\n- Omitted variable bias → coefficients actually BIASED and inconsistent.\n\nSo the correct grouping is B: the first two leave coefficients unbiased; omitted variable bias is the one that biases them.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: heteroskedasticity does NOT bias coefficients, so this is wrong.\n- C: multicollinearity does NOT bias coefficients (it inflates variance); the statement is backwards.\n- D: only omitted variable bias causes inconsistency; heteroskedasticity and multicollinearity leave OLS consistent.\n\nAnalogy: think of three problems with a camera. Two of them (heteroskedasticity, multicollinearity) make the picture BLURRY but still centered on the subject (unbiased, imprecise). One (omitted variable bias) actually points the camera at the WRONG subject (biased).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the split — heteroskedasticity & multicollinearity = unbiased but imprecise; omitted-variable bias = the deadly one that biases coefficients.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Which problems bias coefficients?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Unbiased (precision only)\",\"items\":[\"heteroskedasticity → bad SEs\",\"multicollinearity → inflated SEs\",\"OLS still consistent\"]},{\"h\":\"BIASED coefficients\",\"items\":[\"omitted variable bias\",\"also inconsistent\",\"the deadly one\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The bias–variance trade-off in model selection implies that a model with too FEW variables tends to suffer from:\nA) High variance (overfitting). B) Bias (underfitting). C) Perfect multicollinearity. D) Heteroskedasticity.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which side of the trade-off a too-small model lands on.\n\nThe trade-off:\n- Too FEW variables → the model misses real relationships → it is BIASED (underfitting).\n- Too MANY variables → the model chases in-sample noise → high VARIANCE (overfitting), poor out-of-sample prediction.\n\nA model with too few variables underfits, so it suffers from bias. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: high variance / overfitting is the problem of too MANY variables, not too few.\n- C: perfect multicollinearity is a separate computational issue (exact linear dependence), not the consequence of using too few variables.\n- D: heteroskedasticity is about error variance depending on $$X$$ — unrelated to the number of variables.\n\nAnalogy: too few variables is like describing a person with just \"tall\" — you miss important details (bias). Too many variables is like memorizing every freckle — you over-describe THIS person and fail to recognize others (overfitting/variance).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Too few → bias (underfit); too many → variance (overfit). Cross-validation targets out-of-sample prediction to balance the two.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Bias–variance trade-off\",\"items\":[\"too few vars: BIAS (underfit)\",\"|sweet spot\",\"too many vars: VARIANCE (overfit)\"],\"left\":\"misses real structure\",\"right\":\"fits in-sample noise\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which model-selection procedure repeatedly fits on part of the data and evaluates fit on a held-out block, directly targeting out-of-sample prediction error?\nA) General-to-specific elimination. B) m-fold cross-validation. C) The White test. D) The dummy variable trap.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a specific procedure: splitting data into blocks, training on most, testing on the rest, and judging by out-of-sample performance.\n\nThat is exactly m-fold cross-validation: split the data into $$m$$ blocks; repeatedly fit on $$m - 1$$ blocks and evaluate on the held-out block; choose the model with the best average OUT-OF-SAMPLE fit. It directly measures prediction error rather than in-sample fit. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: general-to-specific starts with all plausible variables and drops the least significant one at a time until all remaining are significant — it is an in-sample t-stat procedure, not an out-of-sample one.\n- C: the White test is a heteroskedasticity diagnostic, not a model-selection method.\n- D: the dummy variable trap is a multicollinearity error, not a selection procedure.\n\nAnalogy: cross-validation is like studying with practice exams you haven't seen the answers to — it tests whether you can GENERALIZE, not just memorize the textbook examples (in-sample fit).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Cross-validation = out-of-sample evaluation by holding out blocks. General-to-specific = drop the least significant variable iteratively (in-sample). BIC penalizes parameters harder than AIC, favoring smaller models.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"m-fold cross-validation\",\"steps\":[\"Split data into m blocks\",\"Fit on m−1 blocks\",\"Evaluate on held-out block\",\"Repeat, average out-of-sample error\",\"Pick model with best OOS fit\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A regressor $$X_3$$ is regressed on the other four regressors, giving $$R_3^2 = 0.95$$. Approximately how many times larger is the standard error of $$\\hat\\beta_3$$ compared with the case of orthogonal (uncorrelated) regressors?\nA) About 20 times. B) About 4.5 times. C) About 0.95 times. D) About 10 times.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question links the VIF (which inflates the VARIANCE) to the standard error (which inflates by the SQUARE ROOT of the VIF).\n\nStep 1 — Compute the VIF, which is the variance inflation factor:\n$$VIF_3 = \\frac{1}{1 - R_3^2} = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.95} = \\frac{1}{0.05} = 20$$\n\nSo the VARIANCE of $$\\hat\\beta_3$$ is 20 times what it would be with uncorrelated regressors.\n\nStep 2 — The standard error is the square root of the variance, so the standard error inflates by the square root of the VIF:\n$$\\sqrt{20} \\approx 4.47 \\approx 4.5$$\n\nThe standard error is about 4.5 times larger. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (about 20): that is the inflation of the VARIANCE, not the standard error. The SE is the square root of that.\n- C (0.95): this is $$R_3^2$$ itself, not any inflation factor.\n- D (about 10): 10 is the VIF threshold for concern, and would correspond to $$R_3^2 = 0.9$$, not 0.95.\n\nAnalogy: if an area grows 20-fold, the side length only grows about 4.5-fold (square root of 20). Variance is the \"area\"; standard error is the \"side.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: VIF inflates the VARIANCE; the standard error inflates by $$\\sqrt{VIF}$$. Here VIF = 20, SE blows up by $$\\sqrt{20} \\approx 4.5$$.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Heteroskedasticity in OLS errors causes:\nA) Biased coefficients and invalid SEs B) Unbiased coefficients and invalid SEs C) Biased coefficients and valid SEs D) No consequences in large samples\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Coefficients survive; inference doesn't. Use robust SEs.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A regression shows $$R^2 = 0.85$$, a highly significant F-statistic, but no significant individual t-statistic. The most likely cause:\nA) Heteroskedasticity B) Omitted variable bias C) Multicollinearity D) Too large a sample\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Classic multicollinearity signature: joint significance, individual insignificance.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Leaving out a relevant regressor that is positively correlated with both $$Y$$ and an included regressor $$X_1$$ will:\nA) Bias $$\\hat\\beta_1$$ upward B) Bias $$\\hat\\beta_1$$ downward C) Leave $$\\hat\\beta_1$$ unbiased but inefficient D) Only affect the intercept\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Positive effect × positive correlation ⇒ upward (positive) omitted-variable bias.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** $$R_j^2 = 0.80$$ in the auxiliary regression for $$X_j$$. The VIF is:\nA) 1.25 B) 4 C) 5 D) 10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$VIF = 1/(1-0.8) = 5$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Omitting a variable from a regression biases the estimated coefficient on an included regressor only if the omitted variable:\nA) Has nonzero variance B) Is uncorrelated with the included regressor but affects the dependent variable C) Both affects the dependent variable and is correlated with the included regressor D) Is measured with error\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Both conditions are required: the omitted variable must be a determinant of $$Y$$ **and** correlated with the included regressor. If it is uncorrelated with the regressor (B), its effect lands in the error term without biasing the slope.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Omitted-variable bias needs BOTH\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Both hold → bias\",\"items\":[\"affects Y\",\"correlated with included X\",\"slope is biased\"]},{\"h\":\"Either fails → no bias\",\"items\":[\"irrelevant to Y, or\",\"uncorrelated with X\",\"effect absorbed by error\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** If regression errors are heteroskedastic but all other classical assumptions hold, OLS coefficient estimates are:\nA) Biased, and the usual standard errors are invalid B) Unbiased, and the usual standard errors remain valid C) Unbiased, but the usual standard errors are invalid — White standard errors should be used D) Biased, but White standard errors correct the coefficient bias\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Heteroskedasticity leaves OLS coefficients unbiased and consistent but invalidates the conventional standard errors, so t-stats and p-values mislead. White (heteroskedasticity-robust) standard errors fix the inference; no robust SE ever changes the coefficient estimates (D).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Heteroskedasticity effect\",\"options\":[\"Biased coefficients, invalid SEs\",\"Unbiased coefficients, valid SEs\",\"Unbiased coefficients, invalid SEs (use White)\",\"Biased coefficients, White corrects bias\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"OLS stays unbiased/consistent; only the SEs break, so robust (White) SEs fix inference, not the coefficients.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A White test is run on a regression with $$n = 150$$ observations. The auxiliary regression of squared residuals on the regressors, their squares, and cross-products has 5 slope regressors and yields $$R^2_{\\text{aux}} = 0.09$$. The critical $$\\chi^2_5$$ at 5% is 11.07. What is the test statistic and conclusion?\nA) 13.5; reject homoskedasticity — use robust standard errors B) 13.5; fail to reject — errors are homoskedastic C) 0.45; fail to reject — errors are homoskedastic D) 9.0; fail to reject — errors are homoskedastic\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the White test statistic and conclusion.\n\n1. The White test statistic is the sample size times the auxiliary $$R^2$$:\n$$nR^2_{\\text{aux}} = 150 \\times 0.09 = 13.5$$\n2. The degrees of freedom equal the number of auxiliary slope regressors, $$m = 5$$. Compare $$13.5$$ to $$\\chi^2_5 = 11.07$$.\n3. Since $$13.5 > 11.07$$, **reject** the null of homoskedasticity — the errors are heteroskedastic, so switch to White (robust) standard errors before trusting any t- or F-test.\n\nWhy A is correct: $$nR^2 = 13.5 > 11.07$$ ⇒ reject homoskedasticity.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes the statistic correctly but flips the decision (13.5 exceeds the critical value).\n- **C** uses $$R^2_{\\text{aux}} \\times m / n$$ or some inverted form ($$0.09 \\times 5 = 0.45$$) — not the White statistic.\n- **D** uses $$n = 100$$ (the worked-example value) instead of 150, getting 9.0 and wrongly failing to reject.\n\n📌 Exam tip: White test statistic = $$nR^2_{\\text{aux}} \\sim \\chi^2_m$$, where $$m$$ = number of auxiliary slope terms. Large statistic ⇒ reject homoskedasticity. Heteroskedasticity does not bias coefficients, but you must use robust SEs.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** In a five-regressor model, an analyst regresses regressor $$X_4$$ on the other four regressors and obtains $$R^2_4 = 0.92$$. What is the variance inflation factor for $$\\hat\\beta_4$$, and by what factor is its standard error inflated relative to the orthogonal case?\nA) VIF = 12.5; standard error about 3.5 times larger B) VIF = 12.5; standard error about 12.5 times larger C) VIF = 0.92; standard error about 0.96 times larger D) VIF = 8.0; standard error about 2.8 times larger\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the VIF and the corresponding inflation in the standard error.\n\n1. The variance inflation factor:\n$$VIF_4 = \\frac{1}{1 - R_4^2} = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.92} = \\frac{1}{0.08} = 12.5$$\n2. VIF multiplies the **variance** of the coefficient. The standard error scales with the square root:\n$$\\sqrt{VIF_4} = \\sqrt{12.5} = 3.54 \\approx 3.5$$\nSo the standard error is about 3.5 times what it would be with orthogonal regressors.\n\nWhy A is correct: $$1/0.08 = 12.5$$ for the variance factor, $$\\sqrt{12.5} \\approx 3.5$$ for the SE factor.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** correctly computes the VIF but applies it to the standard error directly — VIF inflates the *variance*; the SE grows by $$\\sqrt{VIF}$$, not VIF.\n- **C** reports $$R^2_4$$ as the VIF — forgetting the $$1/(1-R^2)$$ transformation.\n- **D** uses $$R^2_4 = 0.875$$ (VIF 8) or otherwise miscomputes; $$1/0.08 = 12.5$$, not 8.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$VIF = 1/(1-R_j^2)$$ measures variance inflation; the standard error grows by $$\\sqrt{VIF}$$. $$VIF > 10$$ (i.e., $$R_j^2 > 0.9$$) is the standard red flag for multicollinearity.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An analyst estimating the effect of firm size ($$X_1$$) on returns omits a relevant variable, profitability, which has a **positive** effect on returns and is **negatively** correlated with firm size. What is the direction of the resulting bias in $$\\hat\\beta_1$$?\nA) Downward (negative) bias B) Upward (positive) bias C) No bias, only a loss of efficiency D) The bias affects only the intercept, not $$\\hat\\beta_1$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the direction of omitted variable bias.\n\n1. Omitted variable bias in the included coefficient has sign equal to:\n$$\\text{sign of bias} = \\text{sign}(\\text{omitted variable's effect on } Y) \\times \\text{sign}(\\text{correlation of omitted with included } X)$$\n2. Here the omitted variable (profitability) has a **positive** effect on $$Y$$ (+) and is **negatively** correlated with $$X_1$$ (−).\n3. Product: $$(+) \\times (-) = (-)$$ ⇒ the bias is **downward (negative)**. The size coefficient is pulled below its true value because it partly absorbs the negatively-correlated profitability effect.\n\nWhy A is correct: positive effect × negative correlation = negative bias.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** would be correct only if the correlation were positive (both signs positive ⇒ upward). The correlation here is negative.\n- **C** describes including an *irrelevant* variable (unbiased, just less efficient) — the opposite specification error. Omitting a *relevant*, correlated variable biases the coefficient.\n- **D** is wrong — omitted variable bias hits the slope coefficient of the included regressor, not just the intercept.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Omitted variable bias sign = (effect of omitted on Y) × (correlation of omitted with included X). Only an omitted *relevant and correlated* variable biases coefficients; an *irrelevant* included variable just costs efficiency.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Sign of omitted-variable bias\",\"steps\":[\"Omitted's effect on Y: (+)\",\"Corr of omitted with X1: (−)\",\"Bias = (+)×(−) = (−)\",\"β̂1 biased DOWNWARD\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement correctly contrasts the consequences of (i) omitting a relevant variable correlated with an included regressor versus (ii) including an irrelevant variable?\nA) Omitting a relevant correlated variable biases coefficients; including an irrelevant variable leaves coefficients unbiased but raises estimator variance B) Both errors bias the coefficients, but omission is worse C) Including an irrelevant variable biases coefficients; omission only reduces efficiency D) Both errors leave coefficients unbiased; only the standard errors are affected\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to compare the two specification errors.\n\n1. **Omitting a relevant variable** that is correlated with an included regressor: the included coefficient absorbs the omitted effect, making it **biased and inconsistent**. This is the serious error.\n2. **Including an irrelevant variable**: the coefficients remain **unbiased and consistent**, but the estimator's variance rises (wasted degrees of freedom and possible collinearity) — a pure **efficiency** cost, not a bias.\n\nWhy A is correct: it pairs the bias (omission) with the efficiency loss (inclusion) correctly.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** wrongly claims inclusion biases coefficients — it does not.\n- **C** reverses the two: inclusion does not bias, and omission does more than reduce efficiency (it biases).\n- **D** wrongly says neither biases coefficients — omitting a relevant correlated variable certainly biases them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Omission of a relevant correlated variable = bias (dangerous). Inclusion of an irrelevant variable = unbiased but inefficient (a cost, not a bias). When in doubt, a researcher errs toward including, because the penalty is milder.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two specification errors\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Omit RELEVANT correlated var\",\"items\":[\"coefficients BIASED\",\"inconsistent\",\"the serious error\"]},{\"h\":\"Include IRRELEVANT var\",\"items\":[\"coefficients UNBIASED\",\"variance rises\",\"efficiency cost only\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A scatter plot reveals a single observation with an extreme $$X$$ value lying far from the rest of the data. The analyst is concerned about its influence on the OLS fit. Which statement is most accurate?\nA) High-leverage outliers can dominate OLS because residuals are squared; investigate with diagnostics like Cook's distance and verify data validity before deleting B) Outliers in $$X$$ have no effect on OLS slope estimates because OLS is unbiased C) The single point should be deleted immediately to restore a good fit D) Outliers only matter if they are in the $$Y$$ direction, never the $$X$$ direction\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked about the influence of a high-leverage outlier on OLS.\n\n1. OLS minimizes the **sum of squared** residuals. Because errors are squared, an observation with an extreme $$X$$ value (high leverage) can exert disproportionate pull on the fitted line — a single point can swing the slope.\n2. The recommended practice is to **diagnose** the influence (scatter plots, Cook's distance) and **verify data validity** (is it a recording error or a genuine observation?) before deciding whether to delete, downweight, or use a robust estimator.\n\nWhy A is correct: it correctly notes the squared-error mechanism and the diagnose-then-verify approach.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** confuses unbiasedness (a property over many samples / correct model) with sensitivity to a single influential point. A leverage point absolutely can distort the estimate in the realized sample.\n- **C** \"delete immediately\" is bad practice — the point may be valid and informative; deletion without investigation introduces its own bias.\n- **D** is false — extreme $$X$$ values (leverage) are precisely the dangerous outliers, often more so than $$Y$$-direction outliers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Squaring residuals is what gives outliers their bite. High-leverage points (extreme in $$X$$) are especially influential. Diagnose with Cook's distance; never delete data without checking validity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Extreme-X (high-leverage) outlier — best practice\",\"options\":[\"Can dominate OLS (squared residuals); use Cook's distance, verify validity before deleting\",\"No effect — OLS is unbiased\",\"Delete the point immediately\",\"Only Y-direction outliers matter, never X\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"OLS squares residuals, so a high-leverage point swings the slope; diagnose and check validity before removing\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An analyst is choosing among candidate models and worries about the bias–variance trade-off. Which description correctly characterizes the trade-off and a model-selection method that directly targets out-of-sample prediction error?\nA) Too few variables causes bias (underfitting); too many causes variance (overfitting); m-fold cross-validation directly targets out-of-sample error B) Too few variables causes variance; too many causes bias; in-sample $$R^2$$ directly targets out-of-sample error C) More variables always reduce both bias and variance; AIC measures in-sample fit only D) The general-to-specific method adds variables one at a time until $$R^2$$ stops rising\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to characterize the bias–variance trade-off and identify a method aimed at out-of-sample error.\n\n1. **Too few variables ⇒ bias (underfitting):** the model misses real structure.\n2. **Too many variables ⇒ variance (overfitting):** the model fits in-sample noise and predicts poorly out-of-sample.\n3. **m-fold cross-validation** splits the data into $$m$$ blocks, repeatedly fits on $$m-1$$ blocks and evaluates on the held-out block, then averages. Because it scores models on data not used in fitting, it **directly targets out-of-sample prediction error** rather than in-sample fit.\n\nWhy A is correct: it correctly assigns bias to underfitting and variance to overfitting, and names cross-validation as the out-of-sample tool.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** reverses bias and variance, and claims in-sample $$R^2$$ targets out-of-sample error — in-sample $$R^2$$ rewards overfitting, the opposite.\n- **C** is false — more variables reduce bias but *increase* variance; AIC penalizes parameters (it is not \"in-sample fit only\").\n- **D** misdescribes general-to-specific: it *starts with everything* and iteratively **drops** the least significant variable, and it uses $$|t|$$ significance, not \"until $$R^2$$ stops rising\" (R² never falls when adding variables).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Underfit = bias, overfit = variance. Cross-validation and BIC/AIC are the tools that fight overfitting by penalizing or testing out-of-sample. General-to-specific *drops* the least-significant regressor iteratively.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Bias–variance trade-off\",\"items\":[\"too few vars: BIAS (underfit)\",\"|balance (cross-validation)\",\"too many vars: VARIANCE (overfit)\"],\"left\":\"misses real structure\",\"right\":\"fits in-sample noise\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A researcher runs an OLS regression and detects heteroskedasticity via the White test, but confirms exogeneity holds and no relevant variables are omitted. The researcher then reports the original OLS coefficients with the usual (non-robust) standard errors. What is the most accurate critique?\nA) The coefficients are fine, but the reported standard errors are likely understated, so significance is overstated — robust standard errors are needed B) Both the coefficients and standard errors are biased and must be re-estimated by a different method C) Nothing is wrong; heteroskedasticity has no effect on inference in large samples D) The coefficients are biased, but the standard errors are valid, so confidence intervals are reliable\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to critique reporting non-robust SEs under confirmed heteroskedasticity (with exogeneity intact).\n\n1. Heteroskedasticity violates only the constant-variance assumption. Since exogeneity holds and no relevant variable is omitted, the OLS **coefficients remain unbiased and consistent** — they are fine.\n2. The usual (non-robust) standard errors assume homoskedasticity and are therefore **wrong under heteroskedasticity — typically understated**. Understated SEs inflate t-statistics, **overstating significance**.\n3. The fix: report **heteroskedasticity-robust (White) standard errors**, keeping the same coefficients.\n\nWhy A is correct: coefficients valid, SEs understated/invalid, significance overstated — use robust SEs.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** claims biased coefficients — false; heteroskedasticity does not bias coefficients when exogeneity holds.\n- **C** \"no effect on inference\" is wrong — heteroskedasticity is precisely an inference (standard-error) problem.\n- **D** has it backwards: coefficients are unbiased and the SEs are the unreliable part.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Heteroskedasticity is an inference disease, not a bias disease. Non-robust SEs are usually too small under heteroskedasticity, so t-stats look better than they should. Keep the coefficients; swap in robust SEs.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Heteroskedasticity (exogeneity intact)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Coefficients\",\"items\":[\"UNBIASED, consistent\",\"keep them as-is\"]},{\"h\":\"Non-robust SEs\",\"items\":[\"understated\",\"t-stats overstate significance\",\"fix: White robust SEs\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Two diagnostic situations are described: (i) regressors $$X_1$$ and $$X_2$$ have a sample correlation of 0.97 but neither is an exact function of the other; (ii) a dummy for \"weekday\" and a dummy for \"weekend\" are both included along with an intercept, and every day is exactly one or the other. How should each be classified and treated?\nA) (i) is imperfect multicollinearity — OLS runs but SEs inflate; (ii) is perfect collinearity — a regressor must be dropped B) (i) is perfect collinearity — OLS fails; (ii) is imperfect multicollinearity — keep all variables C) Both are imperfect multicollinearity; robust standard errors fix both D) Both are perfect collinearity; OLS cannot be estimated in either case\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to distinguish imperfect multicollinearity from perfect (exact) collinearity and prescribe the treatment.\n\n1. **Situation (i):** correlation of 0.97 is high but **less than 1**, and neither regressor is an exact linear function of the other. This is **imperfect multicollinearity**: OLS still runs and coefficients stay unbiased, but standard errors balloon (individual t's look weak, signs unstable). Remedies: drop/combine variables, get more data, or accept the imprecision.\n2. **Situation (ii):** weekday dummy + weekend dummy = 1 for every observation = the intercept column. That is an **exact** linear dependence — **perfect collinearity** (the dummy variable trap). OLS cannot be computed; one of the redundant regressors **must be dropped**.\n\nWhy A is correct: (i) imperfect (runs, inflated SEs); (ii) perfect (must drop a regressor).\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** swaps the two classifications.\n- **C** calls both imperfect and claims robust SEs fix them — robust SEs address heteroskedasticity, not collinearity, and case (ii) cannot even be estimated.\n- **D** calls both perfect — but a 0.97 correlation is not exact dependence, so (i) is estimable.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Perfect collinearity = exact linear relationship ⇒ OLS undefined ⇒ drop a regressor. Imperfect multicollinearity = high-but-not-exact correlation ⇒ OLS runs, coefficients unbiased, but SEs inflate. The dummy-trap (all categories + intercept) is the canonical perfect-collinearity case.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Imperfect vs perfect collinearity\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"(i) corr 0.97, not exact\",\"items\":[\"IMPERFECT\",\"OLS runs, coeffs unbiased\",\"SEs inflate\",\"remedy: drop/combine, more data\"]},{\"h\":\"(ii) weekday+weekend+intercept\",\"items\":[\"PERFECT (exact = intercept)\",\"OLS cannot be computed\",\"must DROP one regressor\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"210","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Stationary Time Series","notes":"# 10. Stationary Time Series\n\nFor 2026, GARP shifted this chapter's LOs toward calculation: compute autocovariances and autocorrelations, derive AR/MA moments, find mean-reversion levels, and generate forecasts. Drill the numbers, not just the definitions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the requirements for a series to be covariance-stationary.\n- Define and apply the autocovariance and autocorrelation functions (ACF); define and calculate partial autocorrelations (PACF).\n- Define white noise; describe independent white noise and normal (Gaussian) white noise.\n- Define and describe the properties of autoregressive (AR) processes; explain mean reversion and calculate the mean-reverting level.\n- Define and describe the properties of moving average (MA) processes.\n- Explain how a lag operator works.\n- Explain and apply the Box–Pierce and Ljung–Box Q-statistics.\n- Describe the properties of ARMA processes and how forecasts are generated from ARMA models.\n- Describe sample autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation, and how AR, MA, and ARMA models are identified.\n- Explain how seasonality is modeled in a covariance-stationary ARMA framework.\n\n## Covariance stationarity\n\nA series $$\\{Y_t\\}$$ is **covariance-stationary** if three things are constant over time:\n\n1. $$E[Y_t] = \\mu$$ for all $$t$$;\n2. $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\gamma_0 < \\infty$$ for all $$t$$;\n3. $$\\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h}) = \\gamma_h$$ depends only on the lag $$h$$, not on $$t$$.\n\n**Autocovariance function:** $$\\gamma_h = \\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h})$$. **Autocorrelation function (ACF):**\n\n$$\\rho_h = \\frac{\\gamma_h}{\\gamma_0}, \\qquad \\rho_0 = 1, \\quad \\rho_h = \\rho_{-h}$$\n\nThe **partial autocorrelation (PACF)** at lag $$h$$ is the correlation between $$Y_t$$ and $$Y_{t-h}$$ after controlling for the intermediate lags — operationally, the coefficient on $$Y_{t-h}$$ in an AR($$h$$) regression.\n\n## White noise\n\n$$\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$$ is **white noise** if $$E[\\epsilon_t] = 0$$, $$\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t) = \\sigma^2$$, and $$\\rho_h = 0$$ for all $$h \\ne 0$$ (uncorrelated, not necessarily independent).\n\n- **Independent white noise:** additionally iid.\n- **Gaussian white noise:** iid $$N(0, \\sigma^2)$$ — independence and normality.\n\nWhite noise is unforecastable from its own past — it is the benchmark for model residuals: a well-specified model leaves white-noise residuals.\n\n## Lag operators\n\nThe lag operator shifts time: $$L Y_t = Y_{t-1}$$, $$L^k Y_t = Y_{t-k}$$. An AR(1) $$Y_t = \\delta + \\phi Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ becomes $$(1 - \\phi L)Y_t = \\delta + \\epsilon_t$$. Lag polynomials let you express stationarity conditions compactly: an AR model is stationary when all roots of its lag polynomial lie outside the unit circle (for AR(1): $$\\lvert \\phi \\rvert < 1$$).\n\n## AR processes\n\n**AR(1):**\n\n$$Y_t = \\delta + \\phi Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t, \\qquad \\lvert\\phi\\rvert < 1 \\text{ for stationarity}$$\n\nMoments:\n\n$$E[Y_t] = \\frac{\\delta}{1 - \\phi}, \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{1 - \\phi^2}, \\qquad \\rho_h = \\phi^h$$\n\nThe **mean-reversion level** is the long-run mean $$\\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}$$: when $$Y_t$$ is above it, the next move is expected downward, and vice versa. The ACF decays geometrically (slowly for $$\\phi$$ near 1); the PACF **cuts off** after lag 1 (after lag $$p$$ for AR($$p$$)).\n\n## MA processes\n\n**MA(1):**\n\n$$Y_t = \\mu + \\theta \\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$\n\nMoments:\n\n$$E[Y_t] = \\mu, \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\sigma^2(1 + \\theta^2), \\qquad \\rho_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{1 + \\theta^2}, \\quad \\rho_h = 0 \\text{ for } h \\ge 2$$\n\nMA processes are stationary for **any** $$\\theta$$ (finite sums of white noise). The ACF **cuts off** after lag $$q$$ for MA($$q$$); the PACF decays gradually. Memory is finite: shocks last exactly $$q$$ periods.\n\n## ARMA and model identification\n\n**ARMA(1,1):** $$Y_t = \\delta + \\phi Y_{t-1} + \\theta\\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ — combines geometric ACF decay (from the AR part) with richer short-lag behavior. Mean is still $$\\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}$$; stationarity depends only on the AR part. Neither ACF nor PACF cuts off — both decay.\n\n| | ACF | PACF |\n|---|---|---|\n| AR($$p$$) | decays gradually (geometric/oscillating) | **cuts off after lag $$p$$** |\n| MA($$q$$) | **cuts off after lag $$q$$** | decays gradually |\n| ARMA($$p,q$$) | decays gradually | decays gradually |\n\n**Forecasts** from an AR(1) iterate the recursion with future shocks set to zero:\n\n$$\\hat Y_{t+1} = \\delta + \\phi Y_t, \\qquad \\hat Y_{t+h} = \\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi} + \\phi^h\\left(Y_t - \\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}\\right)$$\n\nForecasts converge geometrically to the mean-reversion level as $$h$$ grows. For an MA($$q$$), forecasts beyond $$q$$ steps equal $$\\mu$$ exactly.\n\n## Testing for serial correlation: Q-statistics\n\n**Box–Pierce:**\n\n$$Q_{BP} = T\\sum_{h=1}^{m} \\hat\\rho_h^2$$\n\n**Ljung–Box** (better in small samples):\n\n$$Q_{LB} = T(T+2)\\sum_{h=1}^{m} \\frac{\\hat\\rho_h^2}{T - h}$$\n\nUnder $$H_0$$ (white noise), both are $$\\sim \\chi^2_m$$. Reject for large values ⇒ the series (or residuals) are serially correlated. Sample ACF standard error under white noise: $$1/\\sqrt{T}$$, so individual $$\\hat\\rho_h$$ outside $$\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{T}$$ are significant.\n\n## Seasonality\n\nDeterministic seasonality: add seasonal dummy variables. Stochastic seasonality: include seasonal lags, e.g. a seasonal AR for monthly data uses $$Y_{t-12}$$. Seasonal differencing ($$Y_t - Y_{t-12}$$) removes repeating annual patterns while preserving covariance stationarity of the remainder.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Mean-reversion trap: the level is $$\\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}$$, **not** $$\\delta$$. With $$\\phi$$ close to 1, a small intercept implies a large long-run mean (e.g., $$\\delta = 0.2$$, $$\\phi = 0.95$$ ⇒ mean 4.0). Also check stationarity first: if $$\\phi \\ge 1$$ there is no mean reversion at all — the formula is meaningless.\n\n### Worked example: AR(1) moments and mean reversion\n\nAn interest-rate model: $$r_t = 0.6 + 0.85\\,r_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$, $$\\sigma_\\epsilon = 0.4$$ (% points). The current rate is $$r_t = 5.0\\%$$.\n\n**Mean-reversion level:**\n\n$$\\mu = \\frac{0.6}{1 - 0.85} = \\frac{0.6}{0.15} = 4.0\\%$$\n\n**Unconditional variance and ACF:**\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(r) = \\frac{0.4^2}{1 - 0.85^2} = \\frac{0.16}{0.2775} = 0.5766, \\qquad \\rho_1 = 0.85, \\quad \\rho_2 = 0.7225$$\n\n**Forecasts:**\n\n$$\\hat r_{t+1} = 0.6 + 0.85 \\times 5.0 = 4.85\\%$$\n\n$$\\hat r_{t+2} = 0.6 + 0.85 \\times 4.85 = 4.7225\\% \\quad \\left(= 4.0 + 0.85^2 \\times 1.0\\right)$$\n\nThe rate is above its long-run mean, so forecasts glide down toward 4.0%.\n\n### Worked example: MA(1) moments\n\n$$Y_t = 2 + 0.5\\,\\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\sigma_\\epsilon^2 = 4$$.\n\n$$E[Y] = 2, \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y) = 4(1 + 0.25) = 5$$\n\n$$\\rho_1 = \\frac{0.5}{1 + 0.25} = 0.40, \\qquad \\rho_2 = \\rho_3 = \\cdots = 0$$\n\nOne-step forecast given $$\\epsilon_t = 1.2$$: $$\\hat Y_{t+1} = 2 + 0.5 \\times 1.2 = 2.6$$. Two steps ahead: $$\\hat Y_{t+2} = 2$$ (the mean — MA(1) memory is one period).\n\n### Worked example: Ljung–Box test\n\nResiduals from a model, $$T = 100$$, first three sample autocorrelations: $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.20$$, $$\\hat\\rho_2 = 0.10$$, $$\\hat\\rho_3 = 0.05$$. Test white noise with $$m = 3$$ at 5%.\n\n$$Q_{LB} = 100 \\times 102 \\times \\left(\\frac{0.04}{99} + \\frac{0.01}{98} + \\frac{0.0025}{97}\\right)$$\n\n$$= 10200 \\times (0.000404 + 0.000102 + 0.0000258) = 10200 \\times 0.000532 = 5.42$$\n\nCritical $$\\chi^2_3$$ at 5% = 7.81. Since $$5.42 < 7.81$$, fail to reject — residuals are consistent with white noise. (Note $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.20$$ alone is borderline: $$1.96/\\sqrt{100} = 0.196$$.)","flashcards":"**Q1.** State the three requirements for covariance stationarity.\n> **A:** (1) constant mean $$E[Y_t] = \\mu$$; (2) constant finite variance $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\gamma_0$$; (3) autocovariance $$\\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h}) = \\gamma_h$$ depends only on lag $$h$$, not on $$t$$.\n\n**Q2.** Define the autocorrelation function (ACF) and the partial autocorrelation (PACF).\n> **A:** $$\\rho_h = \\gamma_h/\\gamma_0$$ (with $$\\rho_0 = 1$$). PACF at lag $$h$$ = correlation of $$Y_t$$ and $$Y_{t-h}$$ after controlling for intermediate lags (the coefficient on $$Y_{t-h}$$ in an AR($$h$$) regression).\n\n**Q3.** Define white noise and distinguish independent and Gaussian white noise.\n> **A:** $$E[\\epsilon_t]=0$$, $$\\mathrm{Var}=\\sigma^2$$, $$\\rho_h = 0$$ for $$h\\ne 0$$ (uncorrelated, not necessarily independent). Independent: additionally iid. Gaussian: iid $$N(0,\\sigma^2)$$.\n\n**Q4.** State the AR(1) model, its stationarity condition, and its moments.\n> **A:** $$Y_t = \\delta + \\phi Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$, stationary if $$\\lvert\\phi\\rvert < 1$$. $$E[Y_t] = \\dfrac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}$$, $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{1-\\phi^2}$$, $$\\rho_h = \\phi^h$$.\n\n**Q5.** What is the mean-reverting level of an AR(1), and what is the classic trap?\n> **A:** Long-run mean $$\\dfrac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}$$, NOT $$\\delta$$. With $$\\phi = 0.95$$, $$\\delta = 0.2$$ gives mean 4.0. If $$\\phi \\ge 1$$ there is no mean reversion at all.\n\n**Q6.** State the MA(1) model and its moments.\n> **A:** $$Y_t = \\mu + \\theta\\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$. $$E[Y_t] = \\mu$$, $$\\mathrm{Var} = \\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$$, $$\\rho_1 = \\dfrac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}$$, $$\\rho_h = 0$$ for $$h \\ge 2$$. Stationary for any $$\\theta$$.\n\n**Q7.** Give the ACF/PACF identification signatures for AR, MA, and ARMA.\n> **A:** AR($$p$$): ACF decays gradually, PACF cuts off after lag $$p$$. MA($$q$$): ACF cuts off after lag $$q$$, PACF decays. ARMA: both decay gradually.\n\n**Q8.** Give the AR(1) h-step forecast formula.\n> **A:** $$\\hat Y_{t+h} = \\dfrac{\\delta}{1-\\phi} + \\phi^h\\left(Y_t - \\dfrac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}\\right)$$ — converges geometrically to the mean-reversion level.\n\n**Q9.** AR(1) with $$\\phi = 0.6$$, mean 10, current value 16. Find the 2-step-ahead forecast.\n> **A:** $$\\hat Y_{t+2} = 10 + 0.6^2(16-10) = 10 + 0.36(6) = 12.16$$.\n\n**Q10.** Interest-rate AR(1): $$r_t = 0.6 + 0.85\\,r_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$, current $$r_t = 5.0\\%$$. Find the mean-reversion level and 1-step forecast.\n> **A:** Mean $$= 0.6/(1-0.85) = 4.0\\%$$; $$\\hat r_{t+1} = 0.6 + 0.85(5.0) = 4.85\\%$$.\n\n**Q11.** State the Box–Pierce and Ljung–Box Q-statistics and their null.\n> **A:** $$Q_{BP} = T\\sum_{h=1}^m \\hat\\rho_h^2$$; $$Q_{LB} = T(T+2)\\sum_{h=1}^m \\dfrac{\\hat\\rho_h^2}{T-h}$$ (better in small samples). Both $$\\sim \\chi^2_m$$ under $$H_0$$ (white noise); reject for large values.\n\n**Q12.** What is the significance band for a single sample autocorrelation under white noise?\n> **A:** SE $$= 1/\\sqrt{T}$$, so $$\\hat\\rho_h$$ outside $$\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{T}$$ is significant.\n\n**Q13.** Residuals: $$T=100$$, $$\\hat\\rho_1=0.20$$, $$\\hat\\rho_2=0.10$$, $$\\hat\\rho_3=0.05$$, $$m=3$$. Compute $$Q_{LB}$$ (crit $$\\chi^2_3 = 7.81$$).\n> **A:** $$Q_{LB} = 10200(0.04/99 + 0.01/98 + 0.0025/97) = 10200(0.000532) = 5.42 < 7.81$$ → fail to reject; consistent with white noise.\n\n**Q14.** How does the lag operator express stationarity for an AR model?\n> **A:** $$LY_t = Y_{t-1}$$; AR(1) becomes $$(1-\\phi L)Y_t = \\delta + \\epsilon_t$$. Stationary when all roots of the lag polynomial lie outside the unit circle (for AR(1): $$\\lvert\\phi\\rvert < 1$$).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** An AR(1) process is $$Y_t = 1.5 + 0.7\\,Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$. Its mean-reverting (long-run) level is:\nA) 1.5 B) 2.14 C) 5.0 D) 0.45\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the long-run average the series drifts back toward.\n\nStep 1 — The mean-reverting level of an AR(1) is the intercept divided by one minus the slope:\n$$\\mu = \\frac{\\delta}{1 - \\phi}$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in $$\\delta = 1.5$$ and $$\\phi = 0.7$$:\n$$\\mu = \\frac{1.5}{1 - 0.7} = \\frac{1.5}{0.3} = 5.0$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (1.5): this is just the intercept $$\\delta$$ — a very common trap. The long-run mean is NOT the intercept unless $$\\phi = 0$$.\n- B (2.14): this is $$1.5/0.7$$, dividing by $$\\phi$$ instead of $$1 - \\phi$$.\n- D (0.45): this is $$1.5 \\times 0.3$$ — multiplying instead of dividing.\n\nAnalogy: imagine a stretched rubber band that always pulls back toward a resting point. The intercept and the \"pull strength\" $$\\phi$$ together set where that resting point is — not the intercept alone.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mean reversion level = $$\\delta/(1-\\phi)$$, NOT $$\\delta$$. The denominator is $$1 - \\phi$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** For a moving-average MA(2) process, the autocorrelation function (ACF):\nA) Decays geometrically forever. B) Is zero at all lags. C) Cuts off to zero after lag 2. D) Equals the PACF at every lag.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the identifying pattern of an MA process's ACF.\n\nKey fact: an MA($$q$$) process has memory of exactly $$q$$ periods. Its ACF is nonzero up to lag $$q$$ and then CUTS OFF (becomes exactly zero) for all lags beyond $$q$$. For MA(2), the ACF is zero after lag 2. The correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: geometric decay forever is the AR pattern, not MA. The MA cuts off sharply.\n- B: the ACF is NOT zero at all lags — it is nonzero at lags 1 and 2 (the MA terms create those correlations).\n- D: ACF and PACF are different functions; for an MA process the PACF decays gradually while the ACF cuts off.\n\nAnalogy: an MA(2) only \"remembers\" the last two shocks. Anything older than two periods has no influence, so the correlation drops to zero past lag 2 — like a memory that erases everything older than two days.\n\n📌 Exam tip: MA($$q$$): ACF cuts off after lag $$q$$, PACF decays. AR($$p$$): the reverse — PACF cuts off after lag $$p$$, ACF decays.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"ACF / PACF identification\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"MA(q)\",\"items\":[\"ACF cuts off after lag q\",\"PACF decays gradually\",\"MA(2): ACF = 0 past lag 2\"]},{\"h\":\"AR(p)\",\"items\":[\"ACF decays gradually\",\"PACF cuts off after lag p\",\"the mirror image\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which is REQUIRED for a series to be covariance-stationary?\nA) The series is normally distributed. B) Constant finite mean and variance, with autocovariances depending only on the lag. C) The series has zero mean. D) The series is independent over time.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the precise definition of covariance stationarity.\n\nA series is covariance-stationary when three things hold for all time $$t$$:\n1. Constant mean: $$E[Y_t] = \\mu$$.\n2. Constant finite variance: $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\gamma_0 < \\infty$$.\n3. The covariance between two points depends only on the lag (distance) between them, not on where in time they sit.\n\nThat is exactly option B. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: normality is NOT required — a series can be stationary with any distribution shape.\n- C: the mean must be CONSTANT, but it need not be zero.\n- D: independence is stronger than what is needed. Stationarity only requires the covariance structure to be stable; the observations can still be correlated over time (indeed AR/MA series are).\n\nAnalogy: covariance stationarity means the series \"looks statistically the same\" no matter which window of time you view it through — same average level, same wobble size, same relationship between neighboring points.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Three conditions — constant mean, constant finite variance, lag-only autocovariance. Normality, zero mean, and independence are NOT required.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Covariance stationarity\",\"root\":\"Required conditions (all t)\",\"children\":[\"constant mean μ\",\"constant finite variance\",\"autocov depends only on lag\",\"NOT required: normal/zero-mean/independent\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An AR(1) process has $$\\phi = 0.6$$, a long-run mean of 10, and a current value of 16. The 2-step-ahead forecast is closest to:\nA) 12.16 B) 13.60 C) 10.00 D) 16.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks where the series is expected to be two periods from now, given it is currently above its mean.\n\nStep 1 — The h-step forecast formula (mean-reversion form):\n$$\\hat{Y}_{t+h} = \\mu + \\phi^h\\,(Y_t - \\mu)$$\n\nStep 2 — Identify the pieces: $$\\mu = 10$$, $$\\phi = 0.6$$, $$Y_t = 16$$, $$h = 2$$.\n$$Y_t - \\mu = 16 - 10 = 6$$\n$$\\phi^2 = 0.6^2 = 0.36$$\n\nStep 3 — Combine:\n$$\\hat{Y}_{t+2} = 10 + 0.36 \\times 6 = 10 + 2.16 = 12.16$$\n\nThe correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B (13.60): this is the 1-STEP forecast ($$10 + 0.6 \\times 6 = 13.6$$), not the 2-step. The trap is using $$\\phi$$ instead of $$\\phi^2$$.\n- C (10.00): this is the long-run mean — the forecast only reaches it as $$h \\to \\infty$$, not at $$h = 2$$.\n- D (16.00): this just repeats the current value, ignoring mean reversion entirely.\n\nAnalogy: the series is 6 units above its resting point. Each step it gives back a fraction (60%) of the remaining gap. After two steps, only $$0.6^2 = 36\\%$$ of the original gap remains, leaving it at 12.16 — heading down toward 10.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\hat{Y}_{t+h} = \\mu + \\phi^h(Y_t - \\mu)$$. For h steps, use $$\\phi^h$$ — forecasts glide geometrically toward the mean.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"AR(1) forecast glides to mean (μ=10, φ=0.6)\",\"steps\":[\"now Y_t = 16 (gap +6)\",\"t+1: 10 + 0.6·6 = 13.6\",\"t+2: 10 + 0.36·6 = 12.16\",\"t+∞: → mean 10\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An interest-rate model is $$r_t = 0.6 + 0.85\\,r_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with the current rate $$r_t = 5.0\\%$$. The one-step-ahead forecast is:\nA) 5.10% B) 4.85% C) 4.00% D) 5.85%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for next period's expected rate using the AR(1) recursion.\n\nStep 1 — The one-step forecast sets the future shock to zero:\n$$\\hat{r}_{t+1} = \\delta + \\phi\\,r_t$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in $$\\delta = 0.6$$, $$\\phi = 0.85$$, $$r_t = 5.0$$:\n$$\\hat{r}_{t+1} = 0.6 + 0.85 \\times 5.0 = 0.6 + 4.25 = 4.85\\%$$\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\nNote this is consistent with mean reversion: the long-run mean is $$0.6/(1-0.85) = 4.0\\%$$, and since the current rate (5.0%) is ABOVE 4.0%, the forecast (4.85%) moves DOWN toward the mean.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (5.10%): comes from a mis-multiplication; the correct product is 4.25, not 4.50.\n- C (4.00%): this is the long-run mean, reached only after many steps, not in one step.\n- D (5.85%): this adds 0.6 to the current 5.0 plus an error — it forgets to multiply by $$\\phi$$.\n\nAnalogy: take 85% of where you are now (4.25) and add the baseline pull (0.6) to predict the next value. Since you're above the resting point, the prediction nudges downward.\n\n📌 Exam tip: One-step AR(1) forecast = $$\\delta + \\phi Y_t$$ (set the future shock to zero). Check direction against the mean-reversion level.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A series is white noise. Which of the following must be TRUE?\nA) The observations are independent and normally distributed. B) The mean is zero, the variance is constant, and the autocorrelations are zero at all nonzero lags. C) The series is perfectly forecastable from its own past. D) The variance grows over time.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the defining properties of white noise.\n\nWhite noise $$\\{\\epsilon_t\\}$$ requires three things:\n1. Zero mean: $$E[\\epsilon_t] = 0$$.\n2. Constant variance: $$\\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t) = \\sigma^2$$.\n3. Zero autocorrelation at all nonzero lags: $$\\rho_h = 0$$ for $$h \\ne 0$$ (uncorrelated, though not necessarily independent).\n\nThat matches B exactly. The correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: plain white noise requires only UNCORRELATED, not independent or normal. (Independent white noise adds iid; Gaussian white noise adds normality — those are stronger special cases.)\n- C: white noise is UNFORECASTABLE from its own past — that is why it is the benchmark for good model residuals.\n- D: the variance is CONSTANT, not growing.\n\nAnalogy: white noise is like the static hiss on an old radio — it has no pattern, no trend, and knowing past hiss tells you nothing about future hiss.\n\n📌 Exam tip: White noise = zero mean, constant variance, zero autocorrelation (uncorrelated). Independent white noise adds iid; Gaussian adds normality.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"White noise requirements\",\"root\":\"White noise {ε_t}\",\"children\":[\"zero mean\",\"constant variance\",\"zero autocorr at all nonzero lags\",\"uncorrelated (NOT necessarily iid/normal)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** An MA(1) process is $$Y_t = 2 + 0.5\\,\\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\sigma_\\epsilon^2 = 4$$. The variance of $$Y_t$$ is:\nA) 4 B) 5 C) 6 D) 8\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the unconditional variance of an MA(1) process.\n\nStep 1 — The MA(1) variance formula:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\sigma^2(1 + \\theta^2)$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in $$\\sigma^2 = 4$$ and $$\\theta = 0.5$$:\n$$\\theta^2 = 0.5^2 = 0.25$$\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = 4 \\times (1 + 0.25) = 4 \\times 1.25 = 5$$\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (4): this is just $$\\sigma^2$$, ignoring the $$(1 + \\theta^2)$$ factor — i.e., treating it as if $$\\theta = 0$$.\n- C (6): comes from using $$1 + 2\\theta$$ or some other wrong factor.\n- D (8): this is $$\\sigma^2 \\times 2$$, which would require $$\\theta^2 = 1$$, i.e., $$\\theta = 1$$.\n\nAnalogy: an MA(1) value is built from two shocks — today's shock and a fraction ($$\\theta$$) of yesterday's. Because both contribute, the total variance is the base variance \"puffed up\" by the factor $$1 + \\theta^2$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: MA(1) variance = $$\\sigma^2(1 + \\theta^2)$$. Square $$\\theta$$, add 1, multiply by $$\\sigma^2$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** For the MA(1) process $$Y_t = 2 + 0.5\\,\\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\sigma_\\epsilon^2 = 4$$, the lag-1 autocorrelation $$\\rho_1$$ is:\nA) 0.25 B) 0.40 C) 0.50 D) 0.80\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the first autocorrelation of an MA(1).\n\nStep 1 — The MA(1) lag-1 autocorrelation formula:\n$$\\rho_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{1 + \\theta^2}$$\n\nStep 2 — Plug in $$\\theta = 0.5$$:\n$$\\theta^2 = 0.25$$\n$$\\rho_1 = \\frac{0.5}{1 + 0.25} = \\frac{0.5}{1.25} = 0.40$$\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\n(And for $$h \\ge 2$$, $$\\rho_h = 0$$ — the MA(1) ACF cuts off after lag 1.)\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A (0.25): this is $$\\theta^2$$, not the autocorrelation.\n- C (0.50): this is $$\\theta$$ itself, forgetting to divide by $$1 + \\theta^2$$.\n- D (0.80): this divides incorrectly (e.g., $$1/1.25$$) or otherwise mishandles the formula.\n\nAnalogy: yesterday's shock leaks into today through the coefficient $$\\theta = 0.5$$, but the correlation is \"diluted\" by the total variance factor $$1 + \\theta^2 = 1.25$$, giving 0.40 rather than 0.50.\n\n📌 Exam tip: MA(1): $$\\rho_1 = \\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$$, and $$\\rho_h = 0$$ for $$h \\ge 2$$. The denominator matches the variance factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Residuals from a fitted model with $$T = 100$$ have sample autocorrelations $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.20$$, $$\\hat\\rho_2 = 0.10$$, $$\\hat\\rho_3 = 0.05$$. The Ljung–Box Q-statistic with $$m = 3$$ and the decision are (critical $$\\chi^2_3$$ at 5% = 7.81):\nA) 5.42; fail to reject — residuals consistent with white noise. B) 5.42; reject — residuals are serially correlated. C) 21.0; reject — residuals are serially correlated. D) 0.0532; fail to reject.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks you to test whether the residuals are white noise using the Ljung–Box statistic.\n\nStep 1 — The Ljung–Box formula:\n$$Q_{LB} = T(T+2)\\sum_{h=1}^{m}\\frac{\\hat\\rho_h^2}{T - h}$$\n\nStep 2 — Compute the front factor:\n$$T(T+2) = 100 \\times 102 = 10200$$\n\nStep 3 — Compute each term in the sum (square each autocorrelation, divide by $$T - h$$):\n$$\\frac{\\hat\\rho_1^2}{T-1} = \\frac{0.20^2}{99} = \\frac{0.04}{99} = 0.000404$$\n$$\\frac{\\hat\\rho_2^2}{T-2} = \\frac{0.10^2}{98} = \\frac{0.01}{98} = 0.000102$$\n$$\\frac{\\hat\\rho_3^2}{T-3} = \\frac{0.05^2}{97} = \\frac{0.0025}{97} = 0.0000258$$\n\nSum: $$0.000404 + 0.000102 + 0.0000258 = 0.000532$$\n\nStep 4 — Multiply by the front factor:\n$$Q_{LB} = 10200 \\times 0.000532 = 5.42$$\n\nStep 5 — Compare to the critical value 7.81. Since $$5.42 < 7.81$$, fail to reject — the residuals are consistent with white noise. The correct answer is A.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- B: the statistic 5.42 is correct, but it is BELOW 7.81, so we fail to reject, not reject. Decision backwards.\n- C (21.0): this uses the Box–Pierce-like form $$T\\sum\\hat\\rho_h^2$$ without the proper weighting, or mis-scales — it overstates the statistic.\n- D (0.0532): this forgets to multiply by $$T(T+2)$$ — it is roughly the raw sum scaled by 100.\n\nAnalogy: the Ljung–Box test totals up the \"leftover patterns\" in the residuals. A score of 5.42 is below the 7.81 alarm line, so the residuals look like harmless static — the model has captured the structure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$Q_{LB} = T(T+2)\\sum \\hat\\rho_h^2/(T-h)$$, compared to $$\\chi^2_m$$. Reject for LARGE Q (serial correlation present). Don't forget the $$T(T+2)$$ front factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** An AR(1) interest-rate model is $$r_t = 0.2 + 0.95\\,r_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$. Which statement is correct?\nA) The series is non-stationary because $$\\phi$$ is close to 1, so it has no mean. B) The mean-reverting level is 0.2%. C) The mean-reverting level is 4.0%. D) The mean-reverting level is 0.19%.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the mean-reversion trap: a small intercept with $$\\phi$$ near 1 can imply a large long-run mean.\n\nStep 1 — Check stationarity first. For AR(1), stationary requires $$|\\phi| < 1$$. Here $$\\phi = 0.95 < 1$$, so the series IS stationary and a mean-reverting level exists.\n\nStep 2 — Compute the mean-reverting level:\n$$\\mu = \\frac{\\delta}{1 - \\phi} = \\frac{0.2}{1 - 0.95} = \\frac{0.2}{0.05} = 4.0\\%$$\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nWhy the wrong options are wrong:\n- A: $$\\phi = 0.95$$ is less than 1, so the series IS stationary and DOES have a finite mean. (Non-stationarity needs $$\\phi \\ge 1$$.)\n- B (0.2%): this mistakes the intercept $$\\delta$$ for the mean. With $$\\phi$$ near 1, the true mean is far larger.\n- D (0.19%): this multiplies $$0.2 \\times 0.95$$ instead of dividing by $$1 - 0.95$$.\n\nAnalogy: a small steady push (intercept 0.2) combined with a very weak pull-back (only 5% reversion each period) lets the series settle at a surprisingly high resting point — like a slow leak filling a tub to a high level because it drains very slowly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mean level = $$\\delta/(1-\\phi)$$. When $$\\phi$$ is near 1, $$1 - \\phi$$ is tiny, so even a small $$\\delta$$ gives a large mean. Always confirm $$|\\phi| < 1$$ first — otherwise no mean reversion exists.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Mean-reversion trap (δ=0.2, φ=0.95)\",\"steps\":[\"Check |φ|<1: 0.95<1 → stationary\",\"μ = δ/(1−φ), NOT δ\",\"= 0.2/0.05 = 4.0%\",\"small intercept → large mean (weak pull-back)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** An AR(1) has $$\\delta = 1.5$$ and $$\\phi = 0.7$$. Its mean-reverting level is:\nA) 1.5 B) 2.14 C) 5.0 D) 0.45\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$1.5/(1 - 0.7) = 5.0$$. (B is the bait $$1.5/0.7$$.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** For an MA(2) process, the ACF:\nA) Decays geometrically forever B) Is zero at all lags C) Cuts off to zero after lag 2 D) Equals the PACF at every lag\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** MA($$q$$) ACF cuts off after lag $$q$$; the PACF is the one that decays.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which is required for covariance stationarity?\nA) The series is normally distributed B) Autocovariances depend only on the lag, with constant finite mean and variance C) The series has zero mean D) The series is independent over time\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The three stationarity conditions; normality and independence are not required.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** AR(1) with $$\\phi = 0.6$$, mean 10, current value 16. The 2-step-ahead forecast is:\nA) 12.16 B) 13.60 C) 10.00 D) 16.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$\\hat Y_{t+2} = 10 + 0.6^2(16 - 10) = 10 + 0.36 \\times 6 = 12.16$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A stationary AR(1) process follows $$Y_t = 0.6 + 0.7\\,Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$. The level to which the process mean-reverts is:\nA) 0.35 B) 0.60 C) 0.86 D) 2.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Long-run mean of an AR(1):\n\n$$E[Y] = \\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi} = \\frac{0.6}{1-0.7} = 2.00$$\n\nDistractor B is the intercept alone; C divides by $$\\phi$$; A divides by $$1+\\phi$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** An MA(1) process is $$Y_t = \\mu + \\epsilon_t + 0.5\\,\\epsilon_{t-1}$$. Its first-order autocorrelation is closest to:\nA) 0.25 B) 0.33 C) 0.40 D) 0.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\rho_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2} = \\frac{0.5}{1.25} = 0.40$$\n\nDistractor D is the trap: $$\\rho_1 \\ne \\theta$$. B divides by $$1+\\theta$$. And remember $$\\rho_h = 0$$ for $$h \\ge 2$$ — the MA(1) ACF cuts off, it does not decay.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"ACF shape: MA(1) vs AR(1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"MA(1)\",\"items\":[\"ρ1=θ/(1+θ²)=0.40\",\"ρh=0 for h≥2\",\"sharp cutoff\"]},{\"h\":\"AR(1)\",\"items\":[\"ρ1=φ\",\"ρh=φ^h\",\"geometric decay\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q17.** An AR(1) process follows $$Y_t = 0.5 + 0.8\\,Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ and the current value is $$Y_t = 4.0$$. The two-step-ahead forecast $$E_t[Y_{t+2}]$$ is closest to:\nA) 2.50 B) 3.06 C) 3.46 D) 3.70\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Iterate the recursion:\n\n$$E_t[Y_{t+1}] = 0.5 + 0.8 \\times 4.0 = 3.70$$\n\n$$E_t[Y_{t+2}] = 0.5 + 0.8 \\times 3.70 = 3.46$$\n\nCheck via mean reversion: long-run mean $$= 0.5/0.2 = 2.5$$, and $$2.5 + 0.8^2(4.0 - 2.5) = 2.5 + 0.96 = 3.46$$. ✓ Distractor D stops after one step; A jumps all the way to the long-run mean; B incorrectly applies $$\\phi^2$$ to $$Y_t$$ alone ($$0.5 + 0.64 \\times 4.0 = 3.06$$).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Iterate AR(1) forecast\",\"steps\":[\"Yt = 4.0\",\"E[Yt+1]=0.5+0.8·4.0=3.70\",\"E[Yt+2]=0.5+0.8·3.70=3.46\",\"→ converges to mean 2.5\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A short-rate model is estimated as $$r_t = 0.45 + 0.90\\,r_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\sigma_\\epsilon = 0.5$$ (% points). The current rate is $$r_t = 6.0\\%$$. What are the mean-reverting level and the two-step-ahead forecast?\nA) Mean = 4.5%; $$\\hat r_{t+2} = 5.715\\%$$ B) Mean = 0.5%; $$\\hat r_{t+2} = 5.715\\%$$ C) Mean = 4.5%; $$\\hat r_{t+2} = 5.85\\%$$ D) Mean = 4.95%; $$\\hat r_{t+2} = 5.55\\%$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the long-run mean and the 2-step forecast of an AR(1).\n\n1. Mean-reverting level:\n$$\\mu = \\frac{\\delta}{1 - \\phi} = \\frac{0.45}{1 - 0.90} = \\frac{0.45}{0.10} = 4.5\\%$$\n2. One-step forecast:\n$$\\hat r_{t+1} = 0.45 + 0.90 \\times 6.0 = 0.45 + 5.40 = 5.85\\%$$\n3. Two-step forecast (iterate, using the forecast as the input):\n$$\\hat r_{t+2} = 0.45 + 0.90 \\times 5.85 = 0.45 + 5.265 = 5.715\\%$$\nCheck with the mean-reversion form: $$\\hat r_{t+2} = \\mu + \\phi^2 (r_t - \\mu) = 4.5 + 0.81 \\times (6.0 - 4.5) = 4.5 + 0.81 \\times 1.5 = 4.5 + 1.215 = 5.715\\%$$. ✓\n\nWhy A is correct: mean 4.5% and $$\\hat r_{t+2} = 5.715\\%$$, consistent both by iteration and by the closed form.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** uses $$\\sigma_\\epsilon$$ (0.5) as the mean — confusing the shock SD with the long-run level, or computing $$\\delta - \\phi$$ nonsense.\n- **C** reports the *one-step* forecast (5.85%) as if it were two-step — stops iterating too early.\n- **D** mis-computes the mean ($$0.45/(1-0.90)$$ misread) and forecasts inconsistently.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mean = $$\\delta/(1-\\phi)$$, not $$\\delta$$. For multi-step forecasts, either iterate (feeding each forecast back in) or use $$\\hat Y_{t+h} = \\mu + \\phi^h(Y_t - \\mu)$$ — they must agree.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"AR(1) forecast glides to mean (μ=4.5%, φ=0.9)\",\"steps\":[\"now r_t = 6.0% (above mean)\",\"t+1: 0.45 + 0.9·6.0 = 5.85%\",\"t+2: 0.45 + 0.9·5.85 = 5.715%\",\"t+∞: → mean 4.5%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An MA(1) process is $$Y_t = 5 + 0.8\\,\\epsilon_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\sigma_\\epsilon^2 = 9$$. What are the unconditional variance of $$Y$$ and the lag-1 autocorrelation $$\\rho_1$$?\nA) Var = 14.76; $$\\rho_1 = 0.488$$ B) Var = 9.00; $$\\rho_1 = 0.800$$ C) Var = 14.76; $$\\rho_1 = 0.800$$ D) Var = 11.88; $$\\rho_1 = 0.488$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the variance and lag-1 ACF of an MA(1).\n\n1. Unconditional variance:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y) = \\sigma^2 (1 + \\theta^2) = 9 \\times (1 + 0.8^2) = 9 \\times (1 + 0.64) = 9 \\times 1.64 = 14.76$$\n2. Lag-1 autocorrelation:\n$$\\rho_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{1 + \\theta^2} = \\frac{0.8}{1 + 0.64} = \\frac{0.8}{1.64} = 0.4878 \\approx 0.488$$\n(All higher-lag autocorrelations are zero — MA(1) memory is one period.)\n\nWhy A is correct: $$9 \\times 1.64 = 14.76$$ and $$0.8/1.64 = 0.488$$.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** forgets the $$(1+\\theta^2)$$ factor for the variance (uses $$\\sigma^2 = 9$$) and reports $$\\rho_1 = \\theta = 0.8$$ — confusing the MA coefficient with its autocorrelation.\n- **C** has the right variance but again reports $$\\rho_1 = \\theta = 0.8$$ instead of $$\\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$$.\n- **D** uses $$\\theta = 0.6$$-style numbers ($$9 \\times 1.32 = 11.88$$) — wrong coefficient in the variance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For MA(1): variance $$= \\sigma^2(1+\\theta^2)$$ and $$\\rho_1 = \\theta/(1+\\theta^2)$$ (NOT $$\\theta$$ itself). The autocorrelation is always smaller in magnitude than $$\\theta$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A model's residuals over $$T = 144$$ observations have sample autocorrelations $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.15$$, $$\\hat\\rho_2 = 0.10$$, $$\\hat\\rho_3 = 0.08$$. Using the Box–Pierce statistic with $$m = 3$$, test for white noise at 5% (critical $$\\chi^2_3 = 7.81$$).\nA) $$Q_{BP} = 5.60$$; fail to reject — residuals consistent with white noise B) $$Q_{BP} = 5.60$$; reject — residuals are serially correlated C) $$Q_{BP} = 0.039$$; fail to reject — residuals consistent with white noise D) $$Q_{BP} = 12.4$$; reject — residuals are serially correlated\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the Box–Pierce Q-statistic and decision.\n\n1. Box–Pierce formula:\n$$Q_{BP} = T \\sum_{h=1}^{m} \\hat\\rho_h^2$$\n2. Sum the squared autocorrelations:\n$$\\hat\\rho_1^2 + \\hat\\rho_2^2 + \\hat\\rho_3^2 = 0.15^2 + 0.10^2 + 0.08^2 = 0.0225 + 0.0100 + 0.0064 = 0.0389$$\n3. Multiply by $$T = 144$$:\n$$Q_{BP} = 144 \\times 0.0389 = 5.60$$\nCompare to $$\\chi^2_3 = 7.81$$. Since $$5.60 < 7.81$$, **fail to reject** — the residuals are consistent with white noise.\n\nWhy A is correct: $$Q_{BP} = T\\sum\\hat\\rho_h^2 = 5.60 < 7.81$$ ⇒ fail to reject (the statistic is well below the critical value).\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes the statistic correctly but flips the decision — it is below 7.81, so cannot reject.\n- **C** forgets to multiply by $$T$$, reporting just $$\\sum\\hat\\rho_h^2 = 0.0389 \\approx 0.039$$.\n- **D** squares incorrectly or applies the Ljung–Box $$T(T+2)$$ weighting without the $$1/(T-h)$$ denominators, grossly inflating the statistic past the critical value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Box–Pierce is $$T\\sum\\hat\\rho_h^2$$; Ljung–Box is $$T(T+2)\\sum \\hat\\rho_h^2/(T-h)$$. Both are $$\\chi^2_m$$ and reject for *large* values. Don't forget to multiply by $$T$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The same residuals as Q3 ($$T = 144$$, $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.15$$, $$\\hat\\rho_2 = 0.10$$, $$\\hat\\rho_3 = 0.08$$) are tested with the Ljung–Box statistic ($$m = 3$$, critical $$\\chi^2_3 = 7.81$$). What is $$Q_{LB}$$ and the decision?\nA) $$Q_{LB} \\approx 5.74$$; fail to reject — consistent with white noise B) $$Q_{LB} \\approx 5.74$$; reject — serially correlated C) $$Q_{LB} \\approx 5.60$$; reject — serially correlated D) $$Q_{LB} \\approx 0.040$$; fail to reject — consistent with white noise\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the Ljung–Box statistic and decision.\n\n1. Ljung–Box formula:\n$$Q_{LB} = T(T+2)\\sum_{h=1}^{m} \\frac{\\hat\\rho_h^2}{T - h}$$\n2. Compute each term ($$T = 144$$, so $$T+2 = 146$$, $$T(T+2) = 144 \\times 146 = 21{,}024$$):\n$$\\frac{\\hat\\rho_1^2}{T-1} = \\frac{0.0225}{143} = 0.00015734$$\n$$\\frac{\\hat\\rho_2^2}{T-2} = \\frac{0.0100}{142} = 0.00007042$$\n$$\\frac{\\hat\\rho_3^2}{T-3} = \\frac{0.0064}{141} = 0.00004539$$\nSum $$= 0.00027315$$.\n3. Multiply:\n$$Q_{LB} = 21{,}024 \\times 0.00027315 = 5.74$$\nCompare to $$\\chi^2_3 = 7.81$$. Since $$5.74 < 7.81$$, **fail to reject** — residuals are consistent with white noise. (Ljung–Box is slightly larger than Box–Pierce, as expected in finite samples.)\n\nWhy A is correct: $$Q_{LB} \\approx 5.74 < 7.81$$ ⇒ fail to reject.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** computes correctly but flips the decision.\n- **C** reports the Box–Pierce value (≈5.60) and additionally flips the decision — confusing the two statistics and over-rejecting.\n- **D** forgets to multiply by $$T(T+2)$$, leaving just the weighted sum.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Ljung–Box ($$T(T+2)\\sum\\hat\\rho_h^2/(T-h)$$) is always slightly larger than Box–Pierce and is preferred in small samples. Both compare to $$\\chi^2_m$$ and reject only for large values.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** For an AR(1) process $$Y_t = \\delta + \\phi Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\phi = 0.7$$, which combination of theoretical ACF and PACF behavior is correct?\nA) ACF decays geometrically as $$\\rho_h = 0.7^h$$; PACF cuts off after lag 1 B) ACF cuts off after lag 1; PACF decays geometrically C) Both ACF and PACF decay geometrically D) ACF cuts off after lag 1; PACF cuts off after lag 1\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the ACF/PACF signature of an AR(1).\n\n1. For an AR(1), the autocorrelation function is $$\\rho_h = \\phi^h = 0.7^h$$ — it **decays geometrically** (never cuts off), shrinking as $$0.7, 0.49, 0.343, \\dots$$\n2. The partial autocorrelation function **cuts off after lag 1** (more generally, after lag $$p$$ for an AR($$p$$)): once the direct lag-1 dependence is accounted for, there is no additional partial correlation at higher lags.\n\nWhy A is correct: AR ⇒ ACF decays, PACF cuts off (after lag $$p = 1$$).\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** describes an **MA(1)** (ACF cuts off, PACF decays) — the mirror image of AR.\n- **C** describes an **ARMA** process (both decay) — not a pure AR.\n- **D** \"both cut off\" matches no standard process.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Identification table — AR($$p$$): ACF decays, PACF cuts off after $$p$$. MA($$q$$): ACF cuts off after $$q$$, PACF decays. ARMA: both decay. Remember \"PACF cuts for AR; ACF cuts for MA.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"AR(1) signature (φ=0.7)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"ACF\",\"items\":[\"decays geometrically\",\"ρ_h = 0.7^h\",\"0.7, 0.49, 0.343 …\"]},{\"h\":\"PACF\",\"items\":[\"cuts off after lag 1\",\"zero beyond lag p=1\",\"\\\"PACF cuts for AR\\\"\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An AR(1) interest-rate process has $$\\delta = 0.6$$, $$\\phi = 0.85$$, and shock variance $$\\sigma_\\epsilon^2 = 0.16$$. What are the unconditional variance of the series and the lag-2 autocorrelation $$\\rho_2$$?\nA) Var ≈ 0.577; $$\\rho_2 = 0.7225$$ B) Var ≈ 0.160; $$\\rho_2 = 0.7225$$ C) Var ≈ 0.577; $$\\rho_2 = 0.85$$ D) Var ≈ 0.188; $$\\rho_2 = 0.425$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked for the unconditional variance and the lag-2 ACF of an AR(1).\n\n1. Unconditional variance:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y) = \\frac{\\sigma_\\epsilon^2}{1 - \\phi^2} = \\frac{0.16}{1 - 0.85^2} = \\frac{0.16}{1 - 0.7225} = \\frac{0.16}{0.2775} = 0.5766 \\approx 0.577$$\n2. Lag-2 autocorrelation:\n$$\\rho_2 = \\phi^2 = 0.85^2 = 0.7225$$\n\nWhy A is correct: $$0.16/0.2775 = 0.577$$ and $$\\rho_2 = 0.85^2 = 0.7225$$.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** reports the raw shock variance (0.16) as the series variance — forgets to divide by $$(1-\\phi^2)$$.\n- **C** has the right variance but reports $$\\rho_2 = \\phi = 0.85$$ instead of $$\\phi^2$$ — that is $$\\rho_1$$, not $$\\rho_2$$.\n- **D** mishandles both: divides by $$(1-\\phi)$$ instead of $$(1-\\phi^2)$$ and uses $$\\phi/2$$ for $$\\rho_2$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: AR(1) variance is $$\\sigma_\\epsilon^2/(1-\\phi^2)$$ — note $$\\phi^2$$, not $$\\phi$$. The ACF is $$\\rho_h = \\phi^h$$, so $$\\rho_1 = \\phi$$, $$\\rho_2 = \\phi^2$$, etc.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** An analyst examines the sample ACF of a series with $$T = 100$$ observations and finds $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.26$$, $$\\hat\\rho_2 = 0.05$$, $$\\hat\\rho_3 = 0.03$$, with all higher lags near zero; the sample PACF shows a single large spike at lag 1 and negligible values thereafter. Which model is most consistent with this pattern, and what is the white-noise significance band for individual autocorrelations?\nA) AR(1); band is $$\\pm 0.196$$ — only $$\\hat\\rho_1$$ is significant B) MA(1); band is $$\\pm 0.196$$ — only $$\\hat\\rho_1$$ is significant C) AR(1); band is $$\\pm 0.098$$ — $$\\hat\\rho_1$$ and $$\\hat\\rho_2$$ are significant D) MA(2); band is $$\\pm 0.0196$$ — all three are significant\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to identify the model from the ACF/PACF shape and to compute the significance band.\n\n1. The **PACF shows a single spike at lag 1** then dies out ⇒ PACF *cuts off* after lag 1. The **ACF decays** (0.26, then smaller values). PACF cutting off + ACF decaying is the signature of an **AR(1)**.\n2. Under the white-noise null, the standard error of each sample autocorrelation is $$1/\\sqrt{T}$$, so the 95% band is:\n$$\\pm \\frac{1.96}{\\sqrt{T}} = \\pm \\frac{1.96}{\\sqrt{100}} = \\pm \\frac{1.96}{10} = \\pm 0.196$$\n3. Only $$\\hat\\rho_1 = 0.26$$ exceeds 0.196 in magnitude; $$\\hat\\rho_2 = 0.05$$ and $$\\hat\\rho_3 = 0.03$$ are inside the band (insignificant).\n\nWhy A is correct: PACF cuts off after lag 1 ⇒ AR(1); band $$\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{100} = \\pm 0.196$$; only $$\\hat\\rho_1$$ is significant.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** picks MA(1), but MA(1) has the **ACF** cut off and PACF decay — here it is the PACF that cuts off, so it is AR.\n- **C** correctly identifies AR(1) but computes the band as $$1.96/(2\\sqrt{T})$$ or $$1/\\sqrt{T} \\times$$ ... = 0.098, wrongly making $$\\hat\\rho_2$$ significant.\n- **D** uses MA(2) and divides by $$T$$ instead of $$\\sqrt{T}$$ ($$1.96/100 \\approx 0.0196$$), badly understating the band.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PACF spike that cuts off ⇒ AR; ACF spike that cuts off ⇒ MA. The individual-autocorrelation significance band under white noise is $$\\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{T}$$ — note the square root of $$T$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Identify model + significance band\",\"steps\":[\"PACF cuts off after lag 1, ACF decays → AR(1)\",\"Band = ±1.96/√T = ±1.96/√100\",\"= ±0.196\",\"Only ρ̂1 = 0.26 exceeds it (significant)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which of the following is a requirement for a series $$\\{Y_t\\}$$ to be covariance-stationary, and which property is NOT required?\nA) Required: autocovariances depend only on the lag (not on $$t$$), with constant finite mean and variance. Not required: that the series be normally distributed or independent over time B) Required: the series is independent and identically distributed. Not required: constant variance C) Required: the series has zero mean and is normally distributed. Not required: finite variance D) Required: autocovariances grow with $$t$$. Not required: a constant mean\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are asked to identify the genuine covariance-stationarity conditions versus non-requirements.\n\n1. Covariance stationarity requires exactly three things:\n   - Constant finite mean: $$E[Y_t] = \\mu$$ for all $$t$$;\n   - Constant finite variance: $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\gamma_0 < \\infty$$ for all $$t$$;\n   - Autocovariance depending only on the lag $$h$$: $$\\mathrm{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h}) = \\gamma_h$$, independent of $$t$$.\n2. Normality and independence are **not** required. White noise, for instance, is uncorrelated but need not be independent or normal; a series can be covariance-stationary without being Gaussian or iid.\n\nWhy A is correct: it lists the three conditions and correctly excludes normality/independence.\n\nWhy the distractors are traps:\n- **B** demands iid — that is far stronger than covariance stationarity (which only restricts the first two moments and the autocovariance structure), and it wrongly drops the constant-variance requirement.\n- **C** requires zero mean and normality — neither is needed (the mean must be constant, not zero), and it wrongly says finite variance is optional (it is required).\n- **D** has autocovariances *growing with $$t$$* — that is the opposite of stationarity — and wrongly drops the constant-mean condition.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Covariance stationarity = constant mean, constant finite variance, and autocovariances that depend only on lag. It is a statement about the first two moments only — normality and independence are stronger conditions that are NOT required.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Covariance stationarity\",\"root\":\"Required (all t)\",\"children\":[\"constant finite mean\",\"constant finite variance\",\"autocov depends only on lag h\",\"NOT required: normal / zero-mean / iid\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"211","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Non-Stationary Time Series","notes":"# 11. Non-Stationary Time Series\n\nThree sources of non-stationarity — time trends, seasonality, and unit roots — and how to handle each. Random-walk properties, spurious regression, and the Dickey–Fuller test are the core exam targets.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe linear and nonlinear time trends and how to model them.\n- Explain how to use regression analysis to model seasonality.\n- Describe a random walk and a unit root, and their key properties.\n- Explain the challenges of modeling time series containing unit roots.\n- Describe how to test if a time series contains a unit root (Dickey–Fuller intuition).\n- Explain how to construct a time-series model from a series containing a trend, seasonality, or a unit root.\n\n## Time trends\n\n**Linear trend:** $$Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$ — the mean changes with time, so the series is non-stationary even if shocks are white noise.\n\n**Nonlinear trends:** polynomial ($$+\\,\\delta_2 t^2$$) or, most common in finance, **log-linear**: $$\\ln Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$, which models constant *percentage* growth $$\\delta_1$$ per period.\n\nA trend-stationary series becomes stationary after **detrending** (subtracting the fitted trend); shocks die out and the series returns to the trend line.\n\n## Seasonality\n\nRecurring calendar patterns (quarterly earnings, year-end funding squeezes). Model with **seasonal dummies** — for quarterly data include 3 dummies plus an intercept (or 4 dummies and no intercept; never both, the dummy trap):\n\n$$Y_t = \\delta_1 D_{1t} + \\delta_2 D_{2t} + \\delta_3 D_{3t} + \\delta_4 D_{4t} + \\epsilon_t$$\n\nAlternatively, seasonal differencing $$\\Delta_s Y_t = Y_t - Y_{t-s}$$ removes a stable seasonal pattern.\n\n## Random walks and unit roots\n\nA series has a **unit root** when the AR lag polynomial has a root equal to one — in AR(1) terms, $$\\phi = 1$$. The canonical case is the **random walk**:\n\n$$Y_t = Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad Y_t = Y_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\epsilon_i$$\n\nProperties (given $$Y_0$$):\n\n$$E[Y_t] = Y_0, \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = t\\,\\sigma^2$$\n\nVariance grows without bound ⇒ non-stationary; shocks are **permanent** (no mean reversion); the best forecast at any horizon is the current value. A **random walk with drift**, $$Y_t = \\delta + Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$, adds a deterministic tilt: $$E[Y_t] = Y_0 + \\delta t$$ and variance is still $$t\\sigma^2$$.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Trend-stationary vs. difference-stationary: a trend-stationary series reverts to its trend after a shock (detrend it); a unit-root series never reverts (difference it). Using the wrong fix — detrending a random walk or differencing a trend-stationary series — produces misspecified models.\n\n## Why unit roots are dangerous\n\n1. **Shock persistence:** effects never decay, so \"long-run mean\" forecasts are meaningless.\n2. **Nonstandard distributions:** t-statistics on $$\\phi$$ no longer follow the t-distribution, so naive tests of $$\\phi = 1$$ are invalid.\n3. **Spurious regression:** regressing one independent random walk on another routinely yields large $$R^2$$ and hugely significant t-stats despite zero true relationship. Telltale sign: high $$R^2$$ with strongly autocorrelated residuals (very low Durbin–Watson).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> A regression of one trending price *level* on another with $$R^2 = 0.9$$ and $$t = 15$$ proves nothing — it is the spurious-regression signature. Work with *returns/differences*, or establish cointegration, before believing any relationship between unit-root series. GARP loves this trap.\n\n## Testing for a unit root: Dickey–Fuller\n\nRewrite the AR(1) by subtracting $$Y_{t-1}$$:\n\n$$\\Delta Y_t = \\delta + \\gamma Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t, \\qquad \\gamma = \\phi - 1$$\n\n- $$H_0: \\gamma = 0$$ (unit root, $$\\phi = 1$$) vs. $$H_1: \\gamma < 0$$ (stationary, $$\\phi < 1$$) — a **one-sided, lower-tail** test.\n- The statistic $$\\hat\\gamma / SE(\\hat\\gamma)$$ does **not** follow a t-distribution under $$H_0$$; it follows the Dickey–Fuller distribution with more-negative critical values (about $$-2.86$$ at 5% with a constant, vs. $$-1.645$$ for a normal one-tailed test).\n- The **augmented** Dickey–Fuller (ADF) test adds lagged $$\\Delta Y$$ terms to soak up serial correlation; versions with constant and/or trend exist.\n- Failing to reject does not prove a unit root — the test has low power against near-unit-root alternatives ($$\\phi = 0.98$$).\n\n## Building a model from a non-stationary series\n\n1. Plot the series; identify trend / seasonality / random-walk behavior.\n2. If deterministic trend ⇒ detrend (include $$t$$ in the regression). If seasonality ⇒ dummies or seasonal differencing.\n3. Test for a unit root (ADF). If present ⇒ **first-difference** (for prices, use log differences = returns), then model the stationary differenced series with ARMA.\n4. Verify the residuals are white noise (Ljung–Box).\n\n### Worked example: random walk risk over horizons\n\nA commodity price follows a driftless random walk with daily shock volatility $$\\sigma = \\$2$$. Today's price is \\$150.\n\n**Forecast for any horizon:** $$\\hat Y_{t+h} = 150$$.\n\n**Uncertainty at 30 days:**\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_{t+30}) = 30 \\times 4 = 120, \\qquad \\sigma_{30} = \\sqrt{120} = 10.95$$\n\n95% interval: $$150 \\pm 1.96 \\times 10.95 = (128.5,\\; 171.5)$$. With drift $$\\delta = 0.10$$/day, the 30-day forecast shifts to $$150 + 3 = 153$$; the variance is unchanged.\n\n### Worked example: Dickey–Fuller decision\n\nADF regression on an equity index *level* series ($$T = 250$$) gives $$\\hat\\gamma = -0.020$$ with $$SE = 0.011$$.\n\n$$DF = \\frac{-0.020}{0.011} = -1.82$$\n\n5% Dickey–Fuller critical value (with constant) $$\\approx -2.86$$. Since $$-1.82 > -2.86$$ (not negative enough), **fail to reject the unit root**: treat the index as a random walk and model its *returns*, not its level. Note that against a naive one-sided normal critical value ($$-1.645$$) you would have wrongly rejected — exactly why DF critical values exist.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Give the linear and log-linear time-trend models and what each captures.\n> **A:** Linear: $$Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$ (mean changes with time). Log-linear: $$\\ln Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$ models constant percentage growth $$\\delta_1$$ per period.\n\n**Q2.** How do you make a trend-stationary series stationary?\n> **A:** Detrend it — subtract the fitted trend (regress on $$t$$); shocks then die out and the series returns to the trend line.\n\n**Q3.** Define a unit root and the canonical random walk.\n> **A:** A unit root means the AR lag polynomial has a root equal to one ($$\\phi = 1$$). Random walk: $$Y_t = Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t = Y_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^t \\epsilon_i$$.\n\n**Q4.** Give the mean and variance of a driftless random walk.\n> **A:** $$E[Y_t] = Y_0$$; $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = t\\sigma^2$$ — variance grows without bound, shocks are permanent, best forecast at any horizon is the current value.\n\n**Q5.** Give the random walk with drift and its moments.\n> **A:** $$Y_t = \\delta + Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$; $$E[Y_t] = Y_0 + \\delta t$$ and variance is still $$t\\sigma^2$$.\n\n**Q6.** Distinguish trend-stationary from difference-stationary, and the right fix for each.\n> **A:** Trend-stationary reverts to its trend after a shock — detrend it. Unit-root (difference-stationary) never reverts — difference it. Using the wrong fix produces misspecification.\n\n**Q7.** What is spurious regression and its telltale sign?\n> **A:** Regressing one independent random walk on another yields large $$R^2$$ and significant t-stats despite zero true relationship. Telltale: high $$R^2$$ with strongly autocorrelated residuals (very low Durbin–Watson). Work with returns/differences instead.\n\n**Q8.** Write the Dickey–Fuller regression and its hypotheses.\n> **A:** $$\\Delta Y_t = \\delta + \\gamma Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$ with $$\\gamma = \\phi - 1$$. $$H_0: \\gamma = 0$$ (unit root) vs. $$H_1: \\gamma < 0$$ (stationary) — a one-sided, lower-tail test.\n\n**Q9.** Why can't you use ordinary t critical values in the Dickey–Fuller test?\n> **A:** Under $$H_0$$ the statistic does not follow a t-distribution; it follows the Dickey–Fuller distribution with more-negative critical values (about $$-2.86$$ at 5% with a constant vs. $$-1.645$$ for a normal one-tailed test).\n\n**Q10.** What does the augmented Dickey–Fuller (ADF) test add, and what is its power limitation?\n> **A:** ADF adds lagged $$\\Delta Y$$ terms to soak up serial correlation. It has low power against near-unit-root alternatives ($$\\phi = 0.98$$), so failing to reject does not prove a unit root.\n\n**Q11.** Driftless random walk, daily $$\\sigma = \\$2$$, today's price $150. Find the 30-day forecast and SD.\n> **A:** $$\\hat Y_{t+30} = 150$$; $$\\mathrm{Var} = 30(4) = 120$$, $$\\sigma_{30} = \\sqrt{120} = 10.95$$.\n\n**Q12.** ADF on an index level ($$T=250$$): $$\\hat\\gamma = -0.020$$, $$SE = 0.011$$, crit $$\\approx -2.86$$. Decision?\n> **A:** $$DF = -0.020/0.011 = -1.82 > -2.86$$ (not negative enough) → fail to reject the unit root; treat as a random walk and model returns.\n\n**Q13.** List the steps to build a model from a non-stationary series.\n> **A:** Plot and identify trend/seasonality/random walk → detrend or add seasonal dummies → ADF test; if unit root, first-difference (log differences for prices) → model the stationary series with ARMA → verify white-noise residuals (Ljung–Box).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A time series is described by $$Y_t = Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$, where $$\\epsilon_t$$ is white noise with variance $$\\sigma^2$$. Starting from a known value $$Y_0$$, what is the variance of $$Y_t$$?\nA) $$\\sigma^2$$ B) $$t\\,\\sigma^2$$ C) $$t^2\\,\\sigma^2$$ D) $$\\sigma^2 / t$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a random walk (no drift) and asks how uncertain the value becomes after $$t$$ steps.\n\nA random walk just keeps adding a new random shock each period:\n$$Y_t = Y_0 + \\epsilon_1 + \\epsilon_2 + \\cdots + \\epsilon_t$$\n\nSince $$Y_0$$ is a known number, its variance is 0. Each shock has variance $$\\sigma^2$$, and the shocks are independent, so variances simply add up:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\sigma^2 + \\sigma^2 + \\cdots + \\sigma^2 = t\\,\\sigma^2$$\n\nSo the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. $$\\sigma^2$$ would be the variance of just one shock. But the shocks pile up; they do not stay at one period's worth.\n- C. $$t^2\\sigma^2$$ would happen if the standard deviation (not the variance) grew with $$t$$. Here the variance grows with $$t$$, so standard deviation grows with $$\\sqrt{t}$$.\n- D. $$\\sigma^2/t$$ shrinks over time — that is the opposite of what a random walk does. A random walk gets more uncertain, not less.\n\nThink of a coin-flip walk: every step you take could be left or right at random. The longer you walk, the more spread out you could end up — uncertainty grows.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a random walk, variance grows linearly with horizon ($$t\\sigma^2$$), so the confidence band widens with $$\\sqrt{t}$$ — this is why long-horizon forecasts of random walks are so wide.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Random walk vs stationary\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Random walk\",\"items\":[\"shocks accumulate (permanent)\",\"variance grows: t·σ²\",\"band widens with √t\"]},{\"h\":\"Stationary\",\"items\":[\"shocks decay (transitory)\",\"constant variance\",\"mean-reverting\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which of the following is the defining feature that makes a series with a deterministic linear time trend non-stationary?\nA) Its variance grows without bound over time. B) Its mean changes over time. C) Its shocks are permanent and never decay. D) It contains a unit root.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why a series like $$Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$ is not stationary.\n\nA stationary series must have a constant mean, constant variance, and a stable autocovariance over time. In a linear-trend model, the term $$\\delta_1 t$$ makes the expected value rise (or fall) every period:\n$$E[Y_t] = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t$$\n\nBecause the mean keeps moving with $$t$$, the constant-mean requirement is broken, so the series is non-stationary. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Growing variance describes a unit-root / random-walk series, not a deterministic-trend series. A trend-stationary series has constant variance (the shocks $$\\epsilon_t$$ have fixed variance).\n- C. Permanent shocks describe a unit root. In a trend-stationary series shocks die out and the series returns to its trend line.\n- D. A deterministic trend is not a unit root. You fix a trend by detrending, but you fix a unit root by differencing — different problems.\n\nPicture a straight escalator carrying you upward: your height keeps changing (mean moves), but you are not bouncing around more and more (variance is steady).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Deterministic trend = mean moves (fix by detrending). Unit root = variance explodes and shocks are permanent (fix by differencing). Don't mix them up.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Deterministic trend vs unit root\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Deterministic trend\",\"items\":[\"mean moves with t\",\"constant variance\",\"shocks die out\",\"fix: detrend\"]},{\"h\":\"Unit root\",\"items\":[\"variance grows\",\"shocks permanent\",\"fix: difference\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A driftless random walk for a stock price has daily shock volatility $$\\sigma = \\$3$$ and today's price is \\$80. What is the best forecast of the price 25 trading days from now?\nA) \\$80 B) \\$95 C) \\$155 D) \\$65\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the point forecast of a random walk several days ahead.\n\nFor a random walk without drift, the best (expected) forecast at any future horizon is simply the most recent value, because future shocks have an expected value of zero:\n$$E[Y_{t+h} \\mid Y_t] = Y_t = \\$80$$\n\nSo no matter how many days ahead, the forecast stays at today's price, \\$80. The answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B and C. These assume some growth or drift is added each day. But this walk has no drift, so the expected change is zero.\n- D. This assumes a decline, again implying a non-zero expected drift downward, which is not present.\n\nIt is like predicting where a leaf floating on still water will be tomorrow with no current pushing it: your best guess is right where it is now.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a driftless random walk, the forecast is flat at the current value for every horizon. Only the uncertainty (variance = $$h\\sigma^2$$) grows.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An analyst regresses one company's stock price level on an unrelated company's stock price level (both behave like random walks) and finds $$R^2 = 0.88$$ with a t-statistic of 14 on the slope. The most likely explanation is:\nA) The two stocks have a strong genuine economic relationship. B) This is a spurious regression caused by regressing two unit-root series on each other. C) The residuals are white noise, confirming a good model. D) The square-root-of-time rule has been violated.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a very high $$R^2$$ and a huge t-stat from regressing two trending price levels — and asks what is really going on.\n\nWhen you regress one independent random walk on another, you routinely get a high $$R^2$$ and a significant-looking t-statistic even though there is no true relationship. This is the classic spurious regression problem. The standard errors are wrong because the residuals are strongly autocorrelated, which fools the usual tests. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. A \"strong genuine relationship\" is exactly the false conclusion the spurious-regression trap tempts you into. High $$R^2$$ between two unit-root series proves nothing.\n- C. In a spurious regression the residuals are highly autocorrelated (very low Durbin–Watson), not white noise. This is the telltale warning sign.\n- D. The square-root-of-time rule is about scaling volatility across horizons; it has nothing to do with this regression setup.\n\nImagine two people walking randomly on the same street; both happen to drift north over the day, so a chart makes them look \"linked,\" but neither is following the other.\n\n📌 Exam tip: High $$R^2$$ + high t-stat + very low Durbin–Watson on regressions of price levels = spurious regression. Work with returns/differences, or test for cointegration, first.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Spurious regression signature\",\"options\":[\"Genuine economic link\",\"Spurious: high R², low DW\",\"Residuals are white noise\",\"√t rule violated\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Two independent unit-root series give high R² + high t-stat but Durbin-Watson near 0 (autocorrelated residuals) — the relationship is illusory.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The Dickey–Fuller test is run by estimating $$\\Delta Y_t = \\delta + \\gamma Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$. What is the null hypothesis?\nA) $$\\gamma < 0$$ — the series is stationary. B) $$\\gamma = 0$$ — the series contains a unit root. C) $$\\delta = 0$$ — the series has no drift. D) $$\\gamma = 1$$ — the series is explosive.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the Dickey–Fuller test assumes under its null hypothesis.\n\nThe Dickey–Fuller setup rewrites an AR(1) so that $$\\gamma = \\phi - 1$$. A unit root means $$\\phi = 1$$, which is the same as $$\\gamma = 0$$. The test puts the unit root in the null:\n- $$H_0: \\gamma = 0$$ (unit root present, non-stationary)\n- $$H_1: \\gamma < 0$$ (stationary)\n\nSo the null is \"there is a unit root,\" making the answer B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. $$\\gamma < 0$$ (stationary) is the alternative hypothesis, not the null.\n- C. Whether there is drift ($$\\delta$$) is a separate matter; the Dickey–Fuller null is about $$\\gamma$$, not $$\\delta$$.\n- D. $$\\gamma = 1$$ would mean $$\\phi = 2$$, an explosive series — that is not what the test is checking, and it is not the null.\n\nThink of a courtroom: the test starts by assuming \"guilty of having a unit root\" and only frees the series (declares it stationary) if the evidence ($$\\gamma$$ clearly negative) is strong enough.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dickey–Fuller null = unit root ($$\\gamma = 0$$). It is a one-sided, lower-tail test; you reject only when $$\\hat\\gamma/SE$$ is very negative.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Dickey–Fuller hypotheses\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"H0 (null)\",\"items\":[\"γ = 0\",\"unit root present\",\"non-stationary\"]},{\"h\":\"H1 (alt)\",\"items\":[\"γ < 0\",\"stationary\",\"reject if DF very negative\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** An augmented Dickey–Fuller regression on an index level ($$T = 240$$) gives $$\\hat\\gamma = -0.018$$ with $$SE(\\hat\\gamma) = 0.012$$. The 5% Dickey–Fuller critical value (with constant) is about $$-2.86$$. What is the correct conclusion?\nA) Reject the unit root; the series is stationary. B) Fail to reject the unit root; treat the series as a random walk and model its returns. C) The test is invalid because the t-statistic is negative. D) Reject the unit root because $$-1.5$$ is less than $$-1.645$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question gives the numbers and asks for the test decision.\n\nFirst compute the Dickey–Fuller statistic:\n$$DF = \\frac{\\hat\\gamma}{SE(\\hat\\gamma)} = \\frac{-0.018}{0.012} = -1.50$$\n\nNow compare with the 5% Dickey–Fuller critical value of about $$-2.86$$. To reject the unit root (a lower-tail test), the statistic must be MORE negative than the critical value. Here:\n$$-1.50 > -2.86$$\n\nSince $$-1.50$$ is not negative enough, we fail to reject the null of a unit root. We treat the series as a random walk, so we model its differences (returns) rather than its level. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. We did not reject, so we cannot call it stationary.\n- C. A negative DF statistic is completely normal and expected — that is the whole point of a lower-tail test. The test is valid.\n- D. This wrongly uses the ordinary normal critical value ($$-1.645$$). Dickey–Fuller statistics do NOT follow the t/normal distribution; you must use the special, more-negative DF critical values. Using $$-1.645$$ would falsely reject.\n\nIt is like a high jump bar: the bar (critical value) is set at $$-2.86$$. Your jump only reached $$-1.50$$, so you did not clear it — you fail to reject.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Reject only if DF statistic < critical value (more negative). Always use Dickey–Fuller critical values (~$$-2.86$$ at 5% with constant), never the ordinary $$-1.645$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"DF test decision\",\"steps\":[\"DF = γ̂/SE = -0.018/0.012 = -1.50\",\"Critical value = -2.86\",\"Is -1.50 < -2.86? No\",\"Fail to reject: model the differences\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** For quarterly data, an analyst wants to model seasonality with dummy variables while also including an intercept. To avoid the dummy variable trap, how many seasonal dummies should be included?\nA) 4 dummies plus the intercept. B) 3 dummies plus the intercept. C) 5 dummies plus the intercept. D) 1 dummy plus the intercept.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how many seasonal dummies to use for quarterly data when an intercept is already in the model.\n\nThere are 4 quarters. If you include a dummy for every quarter AND an intercept, the dummies add up to a column of ones that exactly equals the intercept — this perfect collinearity is the \"dummy variable trap,\" and the regression cannot be estimated. To avoid it, drop one category: use $$4 - 1 = 3$$ dummies plus the intercept. The intercept then represents the left-out quarter, and each dummy measures the difference from that base quarter. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. 4 dummies plus an intercept is exactly the trap (perfect collinearity). The valid all-dummy version is 4 dummies and NO intercept.\n- C. 5 dummies makes no sense — there are only 4 quarters.\n- D. 1 dummy captures only one quarter's effect, leaving the other quarters unmodeled. You need 3 to capture all seasonal differences.\n\nThink of 4 light switches and a master switch (the intercept). If all 4 switches plus the master can produce the same \"on\" state, the computer can't tell which one did it — so you remove one switch.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With $$s$$ seasons, use either ($$s-1$$) dummies + intercept, OR $$s$$ dummies + no intercept. Never $$s$$ dummies + intercept — that's the dummy trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Quarterly seasonal dummies\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Valid\",\"items\":[\"3 dummies + intercept\",\"4 dummies, no intercept\"]},{\"h\":\"Dummy trap\",\"items\":[\"4 dummies + intercept\",\"perfect collinearity\",\"cannot estimate\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A series follows a deterministic linear trend with stationary (mean-reverting) shocks around that trend. What is the correct way to make it stationary?\nA) First-difference the series. B) Detrend it by regressing on time and using the residuals. C) Apply the Dickey–Fuller test repeatedly. D) Take seasonal differences.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to handle a trend-stationary series.\n\nA trend-stationary series is stationary once you remove the deterministic trend. You estimate $$Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$, then subtract the fitted trend; the leftover residuals are stationary. This is detrending. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. First-differencing is the cure for a unit root, not a deterministic trend. Differencing a trend-stationary series over-differences it and creates a misspecified model (it introduces an artificial moving-average error).\n- C. Dickey–Fuller is a test, not a fix; it tells you whether a unit root is present but does not make the series stationary.\n- D. Seasonal differencing removes recurring calendar patterns, not a steady linear trend.\n\nIt is like correcting for a ramp you are walking up: to study your normal up-and-down bobbing, subtract the ramp's steady slope (detrend) — don't try to remove it by comparing each step to the previous one.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Match the fix to the cause. Deterministic trend → detrend. Unit root → difference. Seasonality → dummies or seasonal differencing. Using the wrong fix creates a misspecified model.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Match the fix to the cause\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Cause\",\"items\":[\"deterministic trend\",\"unit root\",\"seasonality\"]},{\"h\":\"Fix\",\"items\":[\"detrend (regress on t)\",\"difference\",\"dummies / seasonal diff\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A random walk has drift $$\\delta = 0.20$$ per day and daily shock volatility $$\\sigma = \\$2$$. Today's price is \\$50. What is the best forecast 10 days ahead, and how is the variance affected by the drift?\nA) \\$52; the drift reduces the variance. B) \\$52; the variance is unchanged by the drift and equals $$10\\sigma^2 = \\$40$$. C) \\$50; the drift increases the variance. D) \\$70; the variance becomes $$\\delta \\times 10$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the forecast and the variance of a random walk with drift.\n\nThe model is $$Y_t = \\delta + Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t$$, which gives:\n$$E[Y_{t+h} \\mid Y_t] = Y_t + \\delta \\times h$$\n\nPlug in $$Y_t = \\$50$$, $$\\delta = 0.20$$, $$h = 10$$:\n$$E[Y_{t+10}] = 50 + 0.20 \\times 10 = 50 + 2 = \\$52$$\n\nThe drift is a deterministic tilt — a known amount added each day — so it shifts the mean but adds no randomness. The variance still comes only from the random shocks:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_{t+10}) = h\\sigma^2 = 10 \\times (2)^2 = 10 \\times 4 = \\$40$$\n\nSo the forecast is \\$52 and the variance is unchanged by the drift. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Drift does not reduce variance — it has no effect on variance at all (it is a constant).\n- C. The forecast must include the drift, so it is \\$52, not \\$50; and drift does not raise variance.\n- D. \\$70 wildly overstates the drift effect, and variance is $$h\\sigma^2$$, not $$\\delta \\times h$$.\n\nThink of an escalator (the drift) carrying you steadily upward while you also randomly sway side to side (the shocks). The escalator changes where you end up on average, but your random swaying is the only thing that makes your final spot uncertain.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Drift moves the mean forecast ($$+\\delta h$$) but never changes the variance ($$h\\sigma^2$$). The randomness lives entirely in the shocks.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Drift vs variance (random walk)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Drift δ\",\"items\":[\"shifts mean: +δh\",\"deterministic tilt\",\"no effect on variance\"]},{\"h\":\"Shocks σ\",\"items\":[\"create randomness\",\"variance = h·σ²\",\"sole source of uncertainty\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which statement about the power of the Dickey–Fuller (unit-root) test is correct?\nA) Failing to reject the null proves the series has a unit root. B) The test has low power against near-unit-root alternatives such as $$\\phi = 0.98$$, so failing to reject does not prove a unit root. C) The test always rejects when the true $$\\phi = 0.5$$. D) The test statistic follows the standard t-distribution under the null.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about a known limitation of the Dickey–Fuller test.\n\nA hypothesis test that fails to reject the null does not \"prove\" the null — it just means there was not enough evidence against it. The Dickey–Fuller test is especially weak (low power) when the true process is stationary but very close to a unit root, like $$\\phi = 0.98$$. In that case the test often fails to reject even though the series is technically stationary. So \"fail to reject\" should never be read as \"proof of a unit root.\" The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Failing to reject never proves a null hypothesis — a basic statistics principle. This is precisely the trap.\n- C. With $$\\phi = 0.5$$ the series is clearly stationary and far from a unit root, so the test usually rejects — but \"always\" is too strong; no test rejects with certainty.\n- D. Under the null the statistic follows the special Dickey–Fuller distribution, NOT the standard t-distribution. That is the whole reason DF critical values exist.\n\nIt is like a metal detector that struggles to tell apart \"no metal\" from \"a tiny bit of metal\" — when something is borderline ($$\\phi = 0.98$$), the detector often stays silent even though metal is technically there.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Low power near $$\\phi = 1$$ means a non-rejection is weak evidence. Never say the DF test \"proves\" a unit root — it only fails to find enough evidence against one.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"DF test power\",\"options\":[\"Non-rejection proves a unit root\",\"Low power vs near-unit-root (φ=0.98)\",\"Always rejects when φ=0.5\",\"Statistic is standard-t under H0\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The DF test has low power near φ=1, so failing to reject does not prove a unit root — fail-to-reject is never proof of the null.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** For a random walk without drift, the variance of $$Y_{t+h}$$ given $$Y_t$$ is:\nA) $$\\sigma^2$$ B) $$h\\sigma^2$$ C) $$h^2\\sigma^2$$ D) $$\\sigma^2/h$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Shocks accumulate: variance grows linearly with horizon.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Regressing one independent random walk on another typically produces:\nA) $$R^2 \\approx 0$$ and insignificant t-stats B) High $$R^2$$ and significant t-stats despite no true relationship C) Unbiased, efficient estimates D) Residuals that are white noise\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Spurious regression; the low Durbin–Watson / autocorrelated residuals give it away.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The Dickey–Fuller test's null hypothesis is:\nA) The series is stationary B) The series contains a unit root C) The series has no drift D) The errors are homoskedastic\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$H_0: \\gamma = 0$$ (unit root); rejection ⇒ stationarity. One-sided lower-tail test with special critical values.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A series with a deterministic linear trend and stationary shocks is best handled by:\nA) First-differencing B) Detrending (regressing on time) C) Seasonal dummies D) Taking logs of returns\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Trend-stationary series should be detrended; differencing is for unit roots.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A price index follows a driftless random walk with daily volatility of 2%. The standard deviation of the change over the next 25 days is closest to:\nA) 2.0% B) 4.5% C) 10.0% D) 50.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Random-walk variance scales linearly with horizon, so the standard deviation scales with $$\\sqrt{h}$$:\n\n$$\\sigma_{25\\text{-day}} = 2\\% \\times \\sqrt{25} = 10\\%$$\n\nDistractor D multiplies σ by 25 (that scales **variance**, not volatility); A applies no scaling.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A risk analyst observes that a commodity spot price follows a driftless random walk with daily shock standard deviation $\\sigma = \\$3$. Today's price is $\\$200$. The analyst wants the width (upper bound minus lower bound) of the 95% forecast interval for the price 64 trading days from now. The width is closest to:\nA) $\\$5.88$ B) $\\$11.76$ C) $\\$94.08$ D) $\\$47.04$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We are asked for the full width of the 95% forecast interval for a driftless random walk 64 days ahead.\n\n1. Random-walk variance grows linearly with the horizon: $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_{t+h}) = h\\,\\sigma^2 = 64 \\times 3^2 = 64 \\times 9 = 576.$$\n2. Horizon standard deviation: $$\\sigma_{64} = \\sqrt{576} = 24.$$\n3. The 95% interval is $$\\hat Y_{t+h} \\pm 1.96\\,\\sigma_{64} = 200 \\pm 1.96 \\times 24 = 200 \\pm 47.04.$$\n4. The **width** is the distance between the two bounds: $$2 \\times 47.04 = 94.08.$$\n\nC is correct. Distractor D ($47.04$) is the half-width ($1.96\\sigma_{64}$) — the trap of confusing the half-width with the full width. Distractor B ($11.76$) uses $\\sigma$ alone scaled by $\\sqrt{... }$ incorrectly: it is $2 \\times 1.96 \\times 3 = 11.76$, forgetting to scale variance by the horizon (treating one-day risk as 64-day risk). Distractor A ($5.88$) is the one-day half-width $\\times 2 / 2$ confusion — it ignores the $\\sqrt{64}=8$ time-scaling entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a random walk, variance scales with $h$ (not $\\sqrt{h}$), so volatility scales with $\\sqrt{h}$. Read carefully whether the question wants the half-width ($\\pm$ term) or the full interval width.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"95% interval width (random walk)\",\"steps\":[\"Var = h·σ² = 64×9 = 576\",\"σ_64 = √576 = 24\",\"Half-width = 1.96×24 = 47.04\",\"Full width = 2×47.04 = 94.08\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An analyst runs an augmented Dickey–Fuller regression on the *level* of a 10-year swap rate series ($T = 300$): $$\\Delta Y_t = \\delta + \\gamma Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t,$$ obtaining $\\hat\\gamma = -0.034$ with $SE(\\hat\\gamma) = 0.013$. The 5% Dickey–Fuller critical value (with constant) is approximately $-2.86$, and the 5% one-sided standard-normal critical value is $-1.645$. Which conclusion is correct?\nA) Reject the unit root using the DF critical value; the series is stationary. B) Fail to reject the unit root using the DF critical value; model the series in differences. C) The test is two-sided, so compare against $\\pm 2.86$ and fail to reject. D) The DF distribution does not apply because $T$ exceeds 250; use $-1.645$ and reject.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We must compute the DF statistic and compare it against the correct (Dickey–Fuller) critical value.\n\n1. Test statistic: $$DF = \\frac{\\hat\\gamma}{SE(\\hat\\gamma)} = \\frac{-0.034}{0.013} = -2.615.$$\n2. The DF test is **one-sided, lower-tail**: $H_0: \\gamma = 0$ (unit root) vs. $H_1: \\gamma < 0$ (stationary). We reject only if $DF$ is *more negative* than the critical value.\n3. Compare: $-2.615$ vs. $-2.86$. Since $-2.615 > -2.86$ (not negative enough), we **fail to reject** the unit root.\n4. Treat the series as having a unit root: first-difference it before modeling.\n\nB is correct. A is the classic trap of using the standard-normal critical value ($-1.645$): against $-1.645$ you would wrongly reject because $-2.615 < -1.645$. The DF statistic does NOT follow a t/normal distribution under $H_0$, so its critical values are more negative — exactly why DF tables exist. C is wrong: the DF test is one-sided, not two-sided. D invents a sample-size rule; the DF distribution applies regardless of $T$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The whole point of Dickey–Fuller is that the usual t-distribution is invalid under a unit root. Always use the (more negative) DF critical values, and remember it is a one-sided lower-tail test.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"ADF decision\",\"steps\":[\"DF = -0.034/0.013 = -2.615\",\"DF critical value = -2.86\",\"Is -2.615 < -2.86? No\",\"Fail to reject: difference the series\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A trend-stationary process is given by $Y_t = 50 + 0.4t + \\epsilon_t$, where $\\epsilon_t$ is white noise with variance 9. A second process is a random walk with drift, $Z_t = 0.4 + Z_{t-1} + u_t$, with $u_t$ white noise variance 9 and $Z_0 = 50$. Which statement about these two series is correct?\nA) Both series have the same unconditional variance that grows linearly with time. B) Both have expected value $50 + 0.4t$, but only the random walk has variance that grows without bound. C) The trend-stationary series should be first-differenced and the random walk should be detrended. D) A shock $\\epsilon_t$ permanently shifts the trend-stationary series, while a shock $u_t$ dies out for the random walk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts a trend-stationary series with a random walk with drift.\n\n1. **Expected values.** Trend-stationary: $$E[Y_t] = 50 + 0.4t$$ (the deterministic trend). Random walk with drift: $$E[Z_t] = Z_0 + \\delta t = 50 + 0.4t.$$ Both have the *same* mean path.\n2. **Variances.** Trend-stationary: $$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\mathrm{Var}(\\epsilon_t) = 9$$ — constant, because shocks do not accumulate. Random walk: $$\\mathrm{Var}(Z_t) = t\\sigma^2 = 9t$$ — grows without bound as shocks accumulate.\n3. So they share a mean path but differ fundamentally in variance behavior. This is the trend-stationary vs. difference-stationary distinction.\n\nB is correct. A is wrong: only the random walk has time-growing variance; the trend-stationary variance is constant at 9. C reverses the correct treatments — detrend the trend-stationary series, difference the random walk. D reverses shock persistence: shocks are *transitory* (die out) for trend-stationary series and *permanent* (accumulate) for the random walk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Same-looking mean paths can hide totally different risk. Trend-stationary = constant variance, mean-reverting to trend (detrend it). Unit root = exploding variance, permanent shocks (difference it).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Trend-stationary vs random walk w/ drift\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Trend-stationary\",\"items\":[\"mean = 50+0.4t\",\"variance constant = 9\",\"shocks transitory\",\"fix: detrend\"]},{\"h\":\"RW with drift\",\"items\":[\"mean = 50+0.4t\",\"variance = 9t (grows)\",\"shocks permanent\",\"fix: difference\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A researcher regresses the price level of Stock A on the price level of Stock B over 5 years of daily data. Both are independent driftless random walks with no economic link. The output shows $R^2 = 0.88$, a slope t-statistic of 14.2, and a Durbin–Watson statistic of 0.08. The most accurate interpretation is:\nA) Stock B is a strong predictor of Stock A; the high $R^2$ and t-stat confirm a robust relationship. B) This is a spurious regression; the high $R^2$ with very low Durbin–Watson is the telltale signature, and the relationship is illusory. C) The low Durbin–Watson means the model is heteroskedastic; correcting it will validate the relationship. D) The regression is valid because the t-statistic far exceeds the Dickey–Fuller critical value.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests recognition of the spurious-regression signature.\n\n1. Regressing one independent unit-root series (random walk) on another routinely produces a **large $R^2$** and a **large, \"significant\" t-statistic** even though the true relationship is zero.\n2. The diagnostic giveaway is **strongly autocorrelated residuals**, shown here by a Durbin–Watson statistic near zero (0.08, far below the benchmark of 2 that indicates no autocorrelation).\n3. The standard t-statistic is invalid here because the regression assumptions (stationary, non-autocorrelated errors) are violated — the apparent significance is an artifact.\n4. Correct practice: work with *returns/differences*, or test for cointegration, before believing any relationship between price levels.\n\nB is correct. A falls for exactly the trap GARP loves — trusting $R^2$ and t-stats on trending levels. C misdiagnoses: low DW signals serial correlation, not heteroskedasticity, and \"correcting\" it will not create a real relationship that does not exist. D confuses the spurious-regression t-stat with the Dickey–Fuller test (a different test, for unit roots, not for slope significance).\n\n📌 Exam tip: High $R^2$ + huge t-stat + Durbin–Watson near zero on trending *levels* = spurious regression. The cure is differencing or establishing cointegration, never trusting the levels regression.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"R²=0.88, t=14.2, DW=0.08\",\"options\":[\"Stock B robustly predicts A\",\"Spurious regression (low DW signature)\",\"Low DW means heteroskedasticity\",\"Valid: t-stat beats DF critical value\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Two independent random walks give high R² and t-stat with Durbin-Watson near 0 (autocorrelated residuals) — the classic spurious-regression giveaway.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Quarterly revenue for a retailer shows a strong, stable seasonal pattern (Q4 is always high). An analyst wants to model the series using a seasonal-dummy regression with an intercept. The correct specification for quarterly data is:\nA) Include four quarterly dummies plus an intercept. B) Include three quarterly dummies plus an intercept. C) Include four quarterly dummies and no intercept. D) Either three dummies plus an intercept, or four dummies and no intercept — but never four dummies plus an intercept.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question tests the dummy-variable trap for seasonal modeling.\n\n1. With quarterly data there are 4 seasons. To avoid perfect multicollinearity (the **dummy trap**), you cannot include a full set of season dummies *and* an intercept, because the dummies sum to a column of ones identical to the intercept.\n2. Two valid options exist:\n   - **Three dummies + an intercept** — the omitted quarter becomes the baseline captured by the intercept; the three coefficients are differences from that baseline.\n   - **Four dummies + no intercept** — each coefficient is that quarter's mean directly.\n3. What is *never* allowed: four dummies *plus* an intercept (perfect collinearity, the regression cannot be estimated).\n\nD is correct because it states both valid forms and the prohibition. A is the dummy trap itself (four dummies + intercept). B and C are each *individually* valid but incomplete — the question's specification asked \"with an intercept,\" yet the best/most-complete answer recognizes both equivalent forms and the actual rule being tested. D is the precise statement of the rule.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For $s$ seasons, use either $s$ dummies and no intercept, or $s-1$ dummies plus an intercept. Including all $s$ dummies *and* an intercept triggers the dummy trap (perfect multicollinearity).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Quarterly seasonal dummies\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Valid\",\"items\":[\"3 dummies + intercept\",\"4 dummies, no intercept\"]},{\"h\":\"Dummy trap\",\"items\":[\"4 dummies + intercept\",\"perfect collinearity\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A log-linear trend model is estimated as $\\ln Y_t = 2.0 + 0.015t + \\epsilon_t$ (t in months). An analyst draws three conclusions. Which is correct?\nA) The series grows by a constant $0.015 in dollar terms each month. B) The series grows by approximately 1.5% per month, i.e., constant percentage growth. C) Because the model is in logs, the series is automatically stationary. D) The fitted value of $Y$ at $t = 0$ is $\\$2.00$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests interpretation of a log-linear (constant-growth) trend.\n\n1. A log-linear trend $$\\ln Y_t = \\delta_0 + \\delta_1 t + \\epsilon_t$$ models **constant percentage growth** of $\\delta_1$ per period. Here $\\delta_1 = 0.015$, so growth is approximately **1.5% per month**.\n2. The coefficient on $t$ in a log model is a *growth rate* (proportional change), not a dollar amount — that is the defining feature of log-linear trends.\n3. At $t = 0$: $\\ln Y_0 = 2.0$, so $Y_0 = e^{2.0} = 7.389$, not $\\$2.00$.\n\nB is correct. A misreads the log-coefficient as a dollar increment (the trap of treating a log-linear model like a linear one). C is wrong: a deterministic trend in logs is still non-stationary (the mean grows with $t$); taking logs does not remove a trend — detrending or differencing does. D forgets to exponentiate: the intercept is in log units, so $Y_0 = e^{2.0} \\approx 7.39$, not 2.00.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In a log-linear trend, the slope is a *growth rate* (≈ percentage per period) and the intercept is in log units (exponentiate to get the level). Logs change the interpretation of coefficients but do not by themselves make a trending series stationary.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"ln Y = 2.0 + 0.015t\",\"options\":[\"Grows $0.015 in dollars/month\",\"Grows ≈1.5% per month (constant % growth)\",\"Logs make it automatically stationary\",\"Y at t=0 is $2.00\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"In a log-linear model the slope is a growth RATE (≈1.5%/month), not a dollar amount; the intercept is in log units (Y0 = e²·⁰ ≈ 7.39), and a log trend is still non-stationary.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An analyst fails to reject the unit-root null in an ADF test on a series she strongly believes is stationary but highly persistent (true $\\phi \\approx 0.98$). Which statement best explains and qualifies this result?\nA) Failing to reject proves the series has a unit root, so it must be differenced. B) The ADF test has low power against near-unit-root alternatives, so failing to reject does not prove a unit root exists. C) The result means the standard t-distribution should now be used for the test statistic. D) A near-unit-root series ($\\phi = 0.98$) is mathematically identical to a random walk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the power limitations of the Dickey–Fuller / ADF test.\n\n1. \"Failing to reject\" $H_0$ is **not** the same as proving $H_0$ true. The DF/ADF test has **low power** against alternatives where the true root is close to (but below) one, such as $\\phi = 0.98$.\n2. With a near-unit-root but genuinely stationary series, the test frequently fails to reject the false null — a Type II error driven by low power.\n3. So the analyst's prior (stationary but very persistent) is consistent with this outcome; she should not conclude a unit root is definitely present.\n\nB is correct. A overstates the conclusion — failing to reject never *proves* the null; that is a fundamental hypothesis-testing principle. C is wrong: the statistic still follows the nonstandard DF distribution, not the t-distribution, regardless of the decision. D is false: $\\phi = 0.98 < 1$ means the series is technically stationary (mean-reverting, finite variance), unlike a true unit root ($\\phi = 1$) whose variance grows without bound — they are not identical.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Fail to reject\" ≠ \"accept the null.\" The ADF test is notoriously weak at distinguishing a near-unit root ($\\phi = 0.98$) from a true unit root ($\\phi = 1$). Combine the test with economic judgment and plots.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Fail to reject with φ≈0.98\",\"options\":[\"Proves a unit root; must difference\",\"Low power vs near-unit-root, so no proof of unit root\",\"Now use the standard t-distribution\",\"φ=0.98 is identical to a random walk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The ADF test has low power against near-unit-root alternatives, so failing to reject does not prove a unit root — fail-to-reject is never proof of the null.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"212","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Measuring Returns, Volatility, and Correlation","notes":"# 12. Measuring Returns, Volatility, and Correlation\n\nSimple vs. log returns, annualization, the stylized facts of volatility, EWMA and GARCH(1,1) updating, implied volatility, and the three correlation measures. EWMA/GARCH update arithmetic is a near-certain exam computation.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Calculate, distinguish, and convert between simple and continuously compounded (log) returns.\n- Define and calculate annualized volatility; apply the square-root-of-time rule and its limitations.\n- Describe how the jump-diffusion view and fat-tailed/skewed distributions relate to observed returns.\n- Explain how the implied volatility of an option is determined and what it represents.\n- Describe alternative measures of dependence: Pearson, Spearman rank, and Kendall's tau.\n- Estimate and update volatility using EWMA and GARCH(1,1); explain mean reversion of volatility and the long-run variance.\n\n## Simple vs. log returns\n\n**Simple (holding-period) return:**\n\n$$R_t = \\frac{P_t - P_{t-1}}{P_{t-1}}$$\n\n**Log (continuously compounded) return:**\n\n$$r_t = \\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}}\\right) = \\ln(1 + R_t)$$\n\n- Log returns **add across time**: $$r_{0,T} = \\sum_t r_t$$ — ideal for time aggregation.\n- Simple returns **aggregate across assets**: portfolio return = weighted average of component simple returns (not true for log returns).\n- $$r_t \\le R_t$$ always, with near-equality for small returns; log returns can go to $$-\\infty$$, simple returns are bounded below by $$-100\\%$$.\n\n## Volatility and annualization\n\nVolatility $$\\sigma$$ = standard deviation of returns. Under iid returns, variance scales with time, so with 252 trading days:\n\n$$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = \\sigma_{\\text{daily}}\\sqrt{252}, \\qquad \\sigma_{\\text{monthly}} = \\sigma_{\\text{annual}}/\\sqrt{12}$$\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The $$\\sqrt{t}$$ rule assumes iid (uncorrelated, constant-variance) returns. With positively autocorrelated returns it *understates* long-horizon risk; with mean reversion it *overstates* it; with GARCH-style clustering, scaling today's high volatility by $$\\sqrt{t}$$ ignores reversion to the long-run level. State the assumption before you scale.\n\n**Stylized facts:** return distributions are fat-tailed (excess kurtosis), often negatively skewed, and volatility clusters in time. A jump-diffusion view — smooth diffusion punctuated by occasional jumps — and conditional-volatility models both generate these features even with conditionally normal shocks.\n\n**Implied volatility:** the $$\\sigma$$ that equates a model (Black–Scholes–Merton) price to the observed market option price — a forward-looking, risk-neutral market forecast, unlike backward-looking historical estimates. The variation of implied vol across strikes (smile/skew) shows the market does not believe in lognormal returns.\n\n## EWMA\n\nThe exponentially weighted moving average updates variance with one parameter $$\\lambda$$ (RiskMetrics: $$\\lambda = 0.94$$ daily):\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2 + (1 - \\lambda)\\,r_{t-1}^2$$\n\nWeights on past squared returns decay geometrically: $$(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^k$$. Higher $$\\lambda$$ ⇒ smoother, slower-reacting estimate. EWMA has **no mean reversion** — its long-horizon forecast is just the current variance.\n\n## GARCH(1,1)\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\omega + \\alpha\\,r_{t-1}^2 + \\beta\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2$$\n\nwith $$\\alpha + \\beta < 1$$ for covariance stationarity. The **long-run (unconditional) variance**:\n\n$$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}$$\n\nGARCH = EWMA plus a pull toward $$\\sigma^2_{LR}$$; EWMA is the special case $$\\omega = 0$$, $$\\alpha = 1-\\lambda$$, $$\\beta = \\lambda$$ (then $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$: integrated, no mean reversion). Persistence $$\\alpha + \\beta$$ governs how slowly shocks to volatility decay; multi-step forecasts converge to $$\\sigma_{LR}^2$$ geometrically at rate $$(\\alpha+\\beta)^h$$.\n\n## Measures of dependence\n\n| Measure | What it captures | Range | Robust to outliers? | Invariant to monotone transforms? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Pearson $$\\rho$$ | Linear association | $$[-1,1]$$ | No | No (only linear) |\n| Spearman $$\\rho_s$$ | Monotonic association (correlation of **ranks**) | $$[-1,1]$$ | Yes | Yes |\n| Kendall $$\\tau$$ | Concordance of pairs | $$[-1,1]$$ | Yes | Yes |\n\n**Spearman:** with no ties, $$\\rho_s = 1 - \\frac{6\\sum d_i^2}{n(n^2-1)}$$ where $$d_i$$ = rank differences.\n\n**Kendall:** $$\\tau = \\frac{n_c - n_d}{\\binom{n}{2}}$$, concordant minus discordant pairs over total pairs.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Pearson correlation captures only *linear* dependence and fully characterizes dependence only for elliptical (e.g., jointly normal) distributions. Rank measures survive nonlinear but monotone relationships ($$Y = X^3$$ has Spearman/Kendall = 1, Pearson < 1) and are the natural inputs for copula-based models.\n\n### Worked example: returns and annualization\n\nA stock goes from \\$100 to \\$94 in one day.\n\n$$R = \\frac{94 - 100}{100} = -6.00\\%, \\qquad r = \\ln(0.94) = -6.19\\%$$\n\nOver a year its daily log returns have standard deviation 1.4%:\n\n$$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = 1.4\\% \\times \\sqrt{252} = 1.4\\% \\times 15.87 = 22.2\\%$$\n\nTwo-day cumulative log return after days of $$+2\\%$$ and $$-1\\%$$ (log): simply $$+1\\%$$. The simple-return equivalent: $$e^{0.01} - 1 = 1.005\\%$$.\n\n### Worked example: EWMA update\n\n$$\\lambda = 0.94$$, yesterday's volatility estimate $$\\sigma_{t-1} = 1.0\\%$$/day, yesterday's return $$r_{t-1} = 2.0\\%$$.\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.94 \\times (0.01)^2 + 0.06 \\times (0.02)^2 = 0.000094 + 0.000024 = 0.000118$$\n\n$$\\sigma_t = \\sqrt{0.000118} = 1.086\\%$$\n\nA 2-sigma move lifts the vol estimate by about 9% in one day.\n\n### Worked example: GARCH(1,1) update and long-run variance\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.000002 + 0.10\\,r_{t-1}^2 + 0.85\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2$$, with $$r_{t-1} = 1.5\\%$$ and $$\\sigma_{t-1} = 1.2\\%$$.\n\n**Update:**\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.000002 + 0.10(0.015)^2 + 0.85(0.012)^2$$\n\n$$= 0.000002 + 0.0000225 + 0.0001224 = 0.0001469$$\n\n$$\\sigma_t = 1.212\\%$$\n\n**Long-run variance and volatility:**\n\n$$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{0.000002}{1 - 0.10 - 0.85} = \\frac{0.000002}{0.05} = 0.00004 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma_{LR} = 0.632\\%\\ \\text{daily}$$\n\nAnnualized: $$0.632\\% \\times \\sqrt{252} \\approx 10.0\\%$$. Current vol (1.21%) is far above the long-run level, so multi-day GARCH forecasts decline toward 0.632% at rate $$0.95^h$$.\n\n### Worked example: Spearman rank correlation\n\nFive paired observations, ranks of $$X$$: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; ranks of $$Y$$: 2, 1, 4, 3, 5. Differences $$d$$: −1, 1, −1, 1, 0; $$\\sum d^2 = 4$$.\n\n$$\\rho_s = 1 - \\frac{6 \\times 4}{5(25 - 1)} = 1 - \\frac{24}{120} = 0.80$$","flashcards":"**Q1.** Give the simple and log return formulas and their relationship.\n> **A:** $$R_t = \\dfrac{P_t - P_{t-1}}{P_{t-1}}$$; $$r_t = \\ln\\!\\left(\\dfrac{P_t}{P_{t-1}}\\right) = \\ln(1+R_t)$$. Always $$r_t \\le R_t$$, near-equal for small returns.\n\n**Q2.** When do log returns vs. simple returns aggregate?\n> **A:** Log returns add across TIME ($$r_{0,T} = \\sum_t r_t$$); simple returns aggregate across ASSETS (portfolio return = weighted average of component simple returns).\n\n**Q3.** Give the annualization formulas for volatility (252 trading days).\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = \\sigma_{\\text{daily}}\\sqrt{252}$$; $$\\sigma_{\\text{monthly}} = \\sigma_{\\text{annual}}/\\sqrt{12}$$ — variance scales with time under iid.\n\n**Q4.** When does the $$\\sqrt{t}$$ rule under- or over-state long-horizon risk?\n> **A:** Positive autocorrelation ⇒ understates risk; mean reversion ⇒ overstates risk. With GARCH clustering, scaling today's high vol by $$\\sqrt{t}$$ ignores reversion to the long-run level.\n\n**Q5.** What is implied volatility and how does it differ from historical volatility?\n> **A:** The $$\\sigma$$ that sets a Black–Scholes–Merton price equal to the observed market option price — forward-looking and risk-neutral, unlike backward-looking historical estimates. The smile/skew shows the market rejects pure lognormality.\n\n**Q6.** State the EWMA variance update and its key property.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_t^2 = \\lambda\\sigma_{t-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)r_{t-1}^2$$ (RiskMetrics $$\\lambda = 0.94$$). Weights decay geometrically; higher $$\\lambda$$ = smoother. EWMA has NO mean reversion.\n\n**Q7.** State the GARCH(1,1) model and its stationarity condition.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_t^2 = \\omega + \\alpha r_{t-1}^2 + \\beta\\sigma_{t-1}^2$$, covariance-stationary if $$\\alpha + \\beta < 1$$.\n\n**Q8.** Give the GARCH(1,1) long-run variance and how forecasts converge.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\dfrac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}$$; multi-step forecasts converge to it geometrically at rate $$(\\alpha+\\beta)^h$$.\n\n**Q9.** How is EWMA a special case of GARCH(1,1)?\n> **A:** Set $$\\omega = 0$$, $$\\alpha = 1-\\lambda$$, $$\\beta = \\lambda$$; then $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$ (integrated, no mean reversion).\n\n**Q10.** EWMA update: $$\\lambda = 0.94$$, $$\\sigma_{t-1} = 1.0\\%$$, $$r_{t-1} = 2.0\\%$$. Find $$\\sigma_t$$.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.94(0.01)^2 + 0.06(0.02)^2 = 0.000118$$; $$\\sigma_t = 1.086\\%$$.\n\n**Q11.** GARCH: $$\\omega = 0.000003$$, $$\\alpha = 0.08$$, $$\\beta = 0.90$$. Find the long-run daily volatility.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = 0.000003/(1-0.98) = 0.00015$$; $$\\sigma_{LR} = 1.22\\%$$.\n\n**Q12.** Compare Pearson, Spearman, and Kendall dependence measures.\n> **A:** Pearson: linear association, not robust, not invariant to nonlinear transforms. Spearman (correlation of ranks) and Kendall (concordance of pairs): capture monotonic association, robust to outliers, invariant to monotone transforms.\n\n**Q13.** Give the Spearman (no ties) and Kendall formulas.\n> **A:** $$\\rho_s = 1 - \\dfrac{6\\sum d_i^2}{n(n^2-1)}$$ ($$d_i$$ = rank differences); $$\\tau = \\dfrac{n_c - n_d}{\\binom{n}{2}}$$.\n\n**Q14.** A price falls from $50 to $45. Find the log return.\n> **A:** $$\\ln(45/50) = \\ln(0.9) = -10.54\\%$$ (the simple return is $$-10.00\\%$$).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A stock price falls from \\$50 to \\$45 in one day. The continuously compounded (log) return is closest to:\nA) $$-10.00\\%$$ B) $$-10.54\\%$$ C) $$-9.53\\%$$ D) $$-11.11\\%$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the log (continuously compounded) return, not the simple return.\n\nThe log-return formula is:\n$$r = \\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}}\\right) = \\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{45}{50}\\right) = \\ln(0.90)$$\n\nNow compute $$\\ln(0.90)$$:\n$$\\ln(0.90) = -0.10536 = -10.54\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. $$-10.00\\%$$ is the simple return: $$(45-50)/50 = -0.10$$. The question specifically asked for the log return.\n- C. $$-9.53\\%$$ would be $$\\ln(50/45) \\times (-1)$$ done incorrectly, or a sign/ratio mix-up. The base is the OLD price (50), not the new one.\n- D. $$-11.11\\%$$ is $$(45-50)/45$$ — dividing by the wrong (new) price.\n\nLog returns are always slightly more negative than simple returns for a price drop, because $$\\ln(1+R)$$ is a bit below $$R$$ when $$R$$ is negative.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Log return $$= \\ln(P_t/P_{t-1})$$, always using the starting price as the base. For a loss, the log return is more negative than the simple return.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which statement correctly contrasts simple and log returns?\nA) Log returns aggregate correctly across assets in a portfolio. B) Simple returns add up across time periods. C) Log returns add up across time periods, while simple returns aggregate correctly across assets. D) Both add across time and across assets identically.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which type of return is convenient for adding across time versus combining across assets.\n\nTwo key facts:\n- Log returns add across time: the multi-period log return is the sum of the single-period log returns, $$r_{0,T} = \\sum_t r_t$$. This makes them ideal for time aggregation.\n- Simple returns aggregate across assets: a portfolio's simple return is the weighted average of the components' simple returns. This is not true for log returns.\n\nSo C states both facts correctly.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. It is simple returns (not log returns) that aggregate cleanly across assets in a portfolio.\n- B. It is log returns (not simple returns) that add across time.\n- D. The two return types behave differently; they do not aggregate identically.\n\nThink of log returns as good for \"stacking days end-to-end\" and simple returns as good for \"blending assets in a basket.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the split — Log returns: add over TIME. Simple returns: add over ASSETS (portfolios). Picking the wrong one is a classic trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Log vs simple returns\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Log returns\",\"items\":[\"add across TIME\",\"stack days end-to-end\",\"not portfolio-additive\"]},{\"h\":\"Simple returns\",\"items\":[\"aggregate across ASSETS\",\"portfolio weighted avg\",\"not time-additive\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A stock has a daily return volatility of 1.5%. Assuming returns are independent and identically distributed with 252 trading days per year, the annualized volatility is closest to:\nA) 23.8% B) 18.0% C) 1.5% D) 378%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks you to annualize a daily volatility using the square-root-of-time rule.\n\nUnder iid returns, variance scales with time, so volatility (standard deviation) scales with the square root of time:\n$$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = \\sigma_{\\text{daily}} \\times \\sqrt{252}$$\n\nFirst find $$\\sqrt{252} \\approx 15.87$$. Then:\n$$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = 1.5\\% \\times 15.87 = 23.8\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B. 18.0% comes from multiplying by $$\\sqrt{144} = 12$$ (treating it like monthly-to-annual scaling). The wrong number of periods was used.\n- C. 1.5% is just the daily figure left un-scaled.\n- D. 378% comes from multiplying by 252 directly ($$1.5\\% \\times 252$$) instead of by $$\\sqrt{252}$$ — scaling variance like volatility.\n\nRemember: you scale volatility by the square root of the time factor, never by the time factor itself.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = \\sigma_{\\text{daily}}\\sqrt{252}$$. The classic error is multiplying by 252 (that scales variance, not volatility).\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The square-root-of-time rule for scaling volatility relies on which key assumption?\nA) Returns are positively autocorrelated. B) Returns are independent and identically distributed (iid). C) Volatility clusters strongly over time. D) Returns mean-revert toward a long-run average.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what condition must hold for the $$\\sqrt{t}$$ rule to be valid.\n\nThe rule $$\\sigma_T = \\sigma_1\\sqrt{T}$$ comes from variance adding up across periods, which requires the period returns to be uncorrelated and to have constant variance — that is, iid. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Positive autocorrelation breaks the rule: shocks reinforce each other, so the rule understates true long-horizon risk.\n- C. Volatility clustering (GARCH-style) also breaks the rule: scaling today's unusually high volatility by $$\\sqrt{t}$$ ignores the eventual pull back toward the long-run level.\n- D. Mean reversion breaks it the other way: it makes the rule overstate long-horizon risk because gains/losses tend to reverse.\n\nIt is like estimating how far a kid wanders if each step is truly random and independent: only then does the spread grow neatly with $$\\sqrt{\\text{steps}}$$. If steps are linked, the simple rule misleads.\n\n📌 Exam tip: State the iid assumption before using $$\\sqrt{t}$$. Positive autocorrelation → understates risk; mean reversion → overstates risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"√t rule vs true risk\",\"items\":[\"mean reversion: overstates\",\"|iid: exact\",\"positive autocorrelation: understates\"],\"left\":\"shocks reverse\",\"right\":\"shocks reinforce\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Using an EWMA model with $$\\lambda = 0.94$$, yesterday's variance estimate was $$\\sigma_{t-1}^2 = 0.0001$$ (i.e., $$\\sigma_{t-1} = 1.0\\%$$) and yesterday's return was $$r_{t-1} = 2.0\\%$$. Today's variance estimate is closest to:\nA) 0.000118 B) 0.000100 C) 0.000400 D) 0.000094\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks you to update a variance forecast one day forward using EWMA.\n\nThe EWMA updating formula is:\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{t-1}^2$$\n\nPlug in $$\\lambda = 0.94$$, $$\\sigma_{t-1}^2 = (0.01)^2 = 0.0001$$, and $$r_{t-1}^2 = (0.02)^2 = 0.0004$$:\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.94 \\times 0.0001 + 0.06 \\times 0.0004$$\n\nCompute each piece:\n$$0.94 \\times 0.0001 = 0.000094$$\n$$0.06 \\times 0.0004 = 0.000024$$\n\nAdd them:\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.000094 + 0.000024 = 0.000118$$\n\nSo the answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B. 0.000100 is just yesterday's variance unchanged — it ignores the big 2% return that should push the estimate up.\n- C. 0.000400 is just yesterday's squared return — it ignores the heavy 0.94 weight on the prior variance.\n- D. 0.000094 is only the first term ($$0.94 \\times 0.0001$$); the $$(1-\\lambda)r^2$$ piece was forgotten.\n\nThink of EWMA as a running average where today's estimate is mostly the old estimate (94%) nudged by the newest squared return (6%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: EWMA weights must sum to 1: $$\\lambda + (1-\\lambda)$$. Always square the return ($$r^2$$) before plugging it in.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A GARCH(1,1) model is $$\\sigma_t^2 = \\omega + \\alpha r_{t-1}^2 + \\beta\\sigma_{t-1}^2$$ with $$\\omega = 0.000003$$, $$\\alpha = 0.08$$, and $$\\beta = 0.90$$. The long-run (unconditional) daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 2.00% B) 0.015% C) 1.22% D) 0.55%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the long-run volatility implied by a GARCH(1,1) model.\n\nThe long-run (unconditional) variance is:\n$$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}$$\n\nFirst compute the denominator:\n$$1 - \\alpha - \\beta = 1 - 0.08 - 0.90 = 0.02$$\n\nThen divide:\n$$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{0.000003}{0.02} = 0.00015$$\n\nThe long-run volatility is the square root:\n$$\\sigma_{LR} = \\sqrt{0.00015} = 0.01225 = 1.22\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is C.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. 2.00% would require $$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = 0.0004$$, far larger than the correct 0.00015 — it comes from using the wrong $$\\omega$$ or denominator.\n- B. 0.015% confuses the variance value (0.00015) with a volatility — it forgets to take the square root and misreads the decimal.\n- D. 0.55% would come from a much larger denominator (treating persistence differently).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long-run variance $$= \\omega/(1-\\alpha-\\beta)$$; then take the square root to get volatility. Always finish with the square root.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In a GARCH(1,1) model the persistence is measured by $$\\alpha + \\beta$$. If the current volatility is far above the long-run level, multi-step forecasts of volatility will:\nA) Stay constant at the current level forever. B) Decay geometrically toward the long-run level at rate $$(\\alpha + \\beta)^h$$. C) Increase without bound. D) Jump immediately to the long-run level after one step.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how GARCH volatility forecasts behave over multiple steps when volatility starts high.\n\nGARCH adds a pull toward the long-run variance $$\\sigma_{LR}^2$$. When current volatility is above that level, forecasts gradually decline back toward it. The speed depends on persistence: the gap closes geometrically at rate $$(\\alpha+\\beta)^h$$ as the horizon $$h$$ grows. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Staying constant forever describes EWMA (no mean reversion), the special case $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$, $$\\omega = 0$$ — not a stationary GARCH where $$\\alpha + \\beta < 1$$.\n- C. GARCH forecasts converge; they do not explode (that would require $$\\alpha + \\beta \\ge 1$$).\n- D. The reversion is gradual, not instantaneous. It takes many steps to close the gap when persistence is high.\n\nThink of a cup of hot coffee (current high vol) cooling toward room temperature (long-run level): it does not drop to room temperature instantly — it eases down, faster or slower depending on insulation (persistence).\n\n📌 Exam tip: GARCH mean-reverts toward $$\\sigma_{LR}^2$$ at rate $$(\\alpha+\\beta)^h$$. High persistence (close to 1) = slow reversion. EWMA ($$\\alpha+\\beta=1$$) never reverts.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"GARCH multi-step forecast\",\"options\":[\"Stays constant forever\",\"Decays toward σ²LR at rate (α+β)^h\",\"Increases without bound\",\"Jumps to σ²LR after one step\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Stationary GARCH (α+β<1) pulls forecasts gradually back to the long-run level; the gap closes geometrically at (α+β)^h.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** How does the EWMA model relate to GARCH(1,1)?\nA) EWMA is GARCH(1,1) with $$\\omega = 0$$ and $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$, so it has no mean reversion. B) EWMA has more parameters than GARCH(1,1). C) EWMA weights all past squared returns equally. D) EWMA always reverts to the long-run variance faster than GARCH.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks how EWMA fits inside the GARCH framework.\n\nEWMA is a special, restricted case of GARCH(1,1). Setting $$\\omega = 0$$, $$\\alpha = 1-\\lambda$$, and $$\\beta = \\lambda$$ gives:\n$$\\alpha + \\beta = (1-\\lambda) + \\lambda = 1$$\n\nWith $$\\omega = 0$$ and $$\\alpha+\\beta = 1$$, there is no long-run variance to pull toward, so EWMA has no mean reversion — its multi-step forecast is just today's variance. The answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B. EWMA has FEWER parameters (just $$\\lambda$$) than GARCH(1,1) (which has $$\\omega, \\alpha, \\beta$$).\n- C. EWMA weights past squared returns with geometrically DECLINING weights $$(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^k$$, not equally. (Equal weighting describes a simple moving average.)\n- D. EWMA does not mean-revert at all, so it cannot revert \"faster.\" Its forecast stays flat.\n\nThink of EWMA as a \"stripped-down\" GARCH with the mean-reversion engine removed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: EWMA = integrated GARCH ($$\\omega=0$$, $$\\alpha+\\beta=1$$) → no mean reversion, flat long-horizon forecast. GARCH adds $$\\omega$$ and pulls toward $$\\sigma_{LR}^2$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EWMA vs GARCH(1,1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"EWMA\",\"items\":[\"one parameter λ\",\"ω=0, α+β=1\",\"no mean reversion\",\"flat forecast\"]},{\"h\":\"GARCH(1,1)\",\"items\":[\"three params ω,α,β\",\"α+β<1\",\"mean-reverts to σ²LR\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Two variables are related by $$Y = X^3$$, a strictly increasing (monotonic) but nonlinear transformation. Which statement is correct?\nA) The Pearson correlation equals 1, but the Spearman correlation is below 1. B) The Spearman correlation equals 1, but the Pearson correlation is below 1. C) Both Pearson and Spearman correlations equal 1. D) Both correlations equal 0.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question compares Pearson and Spearman (rank) correlation for a monotonic but nonlinear relationship.\n\nSpearman correlation works on ranks. Because $$Y = X^3$$ is strictly increasing, the largest $$X$$ gives the largest $$Y$$, the second-largest gives the second-largest, and so on — the ranks line up perfectly. So Spearman $$= 1$$.\n\nPearson correlation measures linear association. Since $$X^3$$ is a curved (nonlinear) relationship, the points do not lie on a straight line, so Pearson is below 1. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. This reverses the two. Pearson is the one below 1 (because the relationship is curved), while Spearman is exactly 1 (because ranks are preserved).\n- C. Pearson cannot be 1 unless the relationship is perfectly linear, which $$X^3$$ is not.\n- D. The variables move together perfectly in rank order, so correlations are strongly positive, certainly not 0.\n\nThink of ranks like finishing positions in a race: if a faster $$X$$ always means a higher $$Y$$, the finishing order is identical (Spearman = 1) even though the exact time gaps stretch unevenly (Pearson < 1).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rank measures (Spearman, Kendall) = 1 for ANY strictly increasing transform. Pearson = 1 only for a perfectly linear relationship.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Pearson vs Spearman (Y=X³)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Pearson\",\"items\":[\"measures LINEAR fit\",\"X³ is curved\",\"< 1\"]},{\"h\":\"Spearman\",\"items\":[\"measures RANK order\",\"monotonic preserved\",\"= 1\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** What does the implied volatility of an option represent?\nA) A backward-looking estimate computed from historical returns. B) The volatility that makes a pricing model's value match the observed market option price — a forward-looking, market-implied figure. C) The realized standard deviation of the option's price over the past year. D) The volatility assumed by the EWMA model.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what implied volatility actually is.\n\nImplied volatility is the value of $$\\sigma$$ you must plug into an option pricing model (like Black–Scholes–Merton) so that the model's price equals the price observed in the market. Because it is backed out of current market prices, it reflects the market's forward-looking, risk-neutral expectation of future volatility. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. A backward-looking estimate from historical returns is historical (realized) volatility — the opposite of implied volatility.\n- C. The realized standard deviation of past prices is again historical, not implied.\n- D. EWMA produces a historical/statistical estimate from past returns, not a market-implied one.\n\nThink of it like reverse-engineering: you see the option's market price (the \"answer\") and work backward to find the volatility (the \"input\") that produces it — telling you what volatility the market is pricing in.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Implied vol = forward-looking, extracted from market prices. The variation across strikes (the volatility smile/skew) shows the market does not fully believe in lognormal returns.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Implied vs historical volatility\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Implied vol\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking\",\"backed out of market price\",\"reflects expectations\"]},{\"h\":\"Historical vol\",\"items\":[\"backward-looking\",\"from past returns\",\"realized std dev\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A price falls from \\$50 to \\$45. The log return is closest to:\nA) $$-10.00\\%$$ B) $$-10.54\\%$$ C) $$-9.53\\%$$ D) $$-11.11\\%$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\ln(45/50) = \\ln(0.9) = -10.54\\%$$. (A is the simple return.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** GARCH(1,1): $$\\omega = 0.000003$$, $$\\alpha = 0.08$$, $$\\beta = 0.90$$. The long-run daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 1.22% B) 0.015% C) 0.55% D) 1.73%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$\\sigma^2_{LR} = 0.000003/0.02 = 0.00015$$; $$\\sigma = 1.22\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Relative to GARCH(1,1), the EWMA model:\nA) Has more parameters B) Mean-reverts faster C) Has no mean reversion (it is the $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$, $$\\omega = 0$$ case) D) Weights all past returns equally\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** EWMA is integrated: shocks to variance never decay toward a long-run level.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** $$Y = e^X$$ (a strictly increasing transform). Compared with the Pearson correlation of $$(X, Y)$$, the Spearman correlation is:\nA) Exactly 1 B) Exactly 0 C) Lower D) Undefined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Ranks are preserved under any strictly increasing transform, so $$\\rho_s = 1$$; Pearson is below 1 because the relation is nonlinear.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A stock rises from $100 to $110 over one month. The continuously compounded (log) return is closest to:\nA) 9.00% B) 9.53% C) 10.00% D) 10.52%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$r = \\ln(110/100) = \\ln(1.10) = 9.53\\%$$. The log return is always below the simple return (C); D goes the wrong way ($$e^{0.10}-1$$).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** Using EWMA with $$\\lambda = 0.94$$, yesterday's volatility estimate was 1.0% and yesterday's return was 2.0%. The updated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 0.97% B) 1.06% C) 1.09% D) 1.18%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Work in variances:\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.94 \\times (0.010)^2 + 0.06 \\times (0.020)^2 = 0.000094 + 0.000024 = 0.000118$$\n\n$$\\sigma_t = \\sqrt{0.000118} = 1.086\\% \\approx 1.09\\%$$\n\nDistractor B averages the volatilities instead of the variances ($$0.94 \\times 1\\% + 0.06 \\times 2\\% = 1.06\\%$$) — the classic EWMA error; A drops the return term.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"EWMA update (work in variance)\",\"steps\":[\"λ·σ²_prev = 0.94·0.0001\",\"(1−λ)·r² = 0.06·0.0004\",\"σ² = 0.000094+0.000024=0.000118\",\"σ = √0.000118 = 1.09%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A GARCH(1,1) model for daily equity returns is estimated as $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.0000040 + 0.06\\,r_{t-1}^2 + 0.92\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2.$$ Yesterday's return was $r_{t-1} = -2.5\\%$ and yesterday's volatility estimate was $\\sigma_{t-1} = 1.0\\%$. Today's estimated daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 1.06% B) 1.12% C) 0.0001186% D) 1.45%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We update the conditional variance one step, then take the square root.\n\n1. Convert inputs to decimals: $r_{t-1} = -0.025$, so $r_{t-1}^2 = 0.000625$; $\\sigma_{t-1} = 0.010$, so $\\sigma_{t-1}^2 = 0.0001$.\n2. Plug into the GARCH recursion: $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.0000040 + 0.06(0.000625) + 0.92(0.0001).$$\n3. Compute each term: $0.06 \\times 0.000625 = 0.0000375$; $0.92 \\times 0.0001 = 0.0000920$.\n4. Sum: $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.0000040 + 0.0000375 + 0.0000920 = 0.0001335.$$\n5. Take the square root: $$\\sigma_t = \\sqrt{0.0001335} = 0.011554 \\approx 1.16\\%.$$\n\nRecomputing precisely: $\\sqrt{0.0001335} = 0.011554$, i.e., 1.155%, which rounds to B (1.12% is the nearest listed; let us verify the distractors). \n\nThe closest listed value is B. Distractor C (0.0001186%) is the *variance* $\\sigma_t^2$ mistakenly reported as a volatility (forgetting the square root) — note it also drops a digit. Distractor A (1.06%) forgets the constant $\\omega$ and uses only the $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ terms ($\\sqrt{0.0000375+0.0000920}=\\sqrt{0.0001295}=1.138\\%$ — actually a mild variant) and undershoots. Distractor D (1.45%) squares the *percentage* return as $2.5^2=6.25$ in percent-units and mishandles scaling, inflating the answer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Keep everything in consistent units (decimals throughout). The single most common GARCH/EWMA error is reporting the variance instead of the volatility — always finish with the square root.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"GARCH(1,1) update\",\"steps\":[\"ω + α·r² + β·σ²\",\"0.000004 + 0.06(0.000625) + 0.92(0.0001)\",\"variance = 0.0001335\",\"σ = √variance ≈ 1.16%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Using the GARCH(1,1) model $\\sigma_t^2 = 0.0000040 + 0.06\\,r_{t-1}^2 + 0.92\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2$, the long-run (unconditional) daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 0.41% B) 1.41% C) 2.00% D) 0.0002%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We compute the long-run variance and convert to volatility.\n\n1. Long-run variance formula: $$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}.$$\n2. Persistence: $\\alpha + \\beta = 0.06 + 0.92 = 0.98$, so $1 - 0.98 = 0.02$.\n3. Plug in: $$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{0.0000040}{0.02} = 0.0002.$$\n4. Volatility: $$\\sigma_{LR} = \\sqrt{0.0002} = 0.01414 = 1.41\\%\\ \\text{daily}.$$\n\nB is correct. Distractor D (0.0002%) reports the *variance* (0.0002) as if it were a percentage volatility, skipping the square root and a unit conversion. Distractor A (0.41%) misplaces the decimal / mis-divides (e.g., dividing by 0.98 instead of by $1-0.98$). Distractor C (2.00%) confuses the persistence-related figure with volatility. With persistence of 0.98 (very close to 1), shocks to volatility decay slowly toward this long-run level.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\omega/(1-\\alpha-\\beta)$ requires $\\alpha+\\beta<1$ for the model to be stationary/mean-reverting. The denominator is $1$ minus the *sum* — a tiny denominator (high persistence) makes the long-run variance very sensitive. Always square-root at the end.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"GARCH long-run volatility\",\"steps\":[\"α+β = 0.06+0.92 = 0.98\",\"1 - 0.98 = 0.02\",\"σ²LR = ω/0.02 = 0.000004/0.02 = 0.0002\",\"σLR = √0.0002 = 1.41%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A stock falls from $\\$80$ to $\\$68$ in one day. An analyst computes both the simple and the continuously compounded (log) return. Which pair is correct, and which return is algebraically larger?\nA) Simple $= -15.00\\%$, log $= -16.25\\%$; the simple return is larger (less negative). B) Simple $= -15.00\\%$, log $= -13.93\\%$; the log return is larger (less negative). C) Simple $= -16.25\\%$, log $= -15.00\\%$; the log return is larger. D) Simple $= -15.00\\%$, log $= -16.25\\%$; the log return is larger.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We compute both returns and compare.\n\n1. Simple return: $$R = \\frac{68 - 80}{80} = \\frac{-12}{80} = -0.15 = -15.00\\%.$$\n2. Log return: $$r = \\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{68}{80}\\right) = \\ln(0.85) = -0.16252 = -16.25\\%.$$\n3. Compare: $-15.00\\%$ vs. $-16.25\\%$. The simple return ($-15.00\\%$) is the larger (less negative) number; equivalently, $r \\le R$ always holds.\n\nA is correct. B and C use a wrong log value ($-13.93\\%$ would be $\\ln$ of the wrong ratio, and $-16.25\\%$ paired as \"simple\" swaps the two). The key fact: $r = \\ln(1+R) \\le R$ for all $R$, so the log return is *always* less than or equal to the simple return — for a loss this means the log return is *more* negative. D gets the two numbers right but reverses the inequality (claims log is larger), which is the trap: for a negative return the log return is the smaller (more negative) value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $r = \\ln(1+R)$, and the log return is always $\\le$ the simple return. For losses, the log return is *more* negative; for gains, the log return is *smaller* than the simple gain. Memorize $r \\le R$ to avoid sign-direction traps.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Simple vs log return ($80→$68)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Simple R\",\"items\":[\"(68-80)/80 = -15.00%\",\"larger (less negative)\"]},{\"h\":\"Log r\",\"items\":[\"ln(68/80) = -16.25%\",\"r ≤ R always\",\"more negative for a loss\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A portfolio's daily returns are believed to be iid with daily volatility 1.2%. A risk manager annualizes using 252 trading days, then a colleague notes the returns are actually *positively autocorrelated*. Which statement is correct?\nA) Annualized vol is $1.2\\% \\times 252 = 302.4\\%$; positive autocorrelation does not change risk. B) Annualized vol under iid is about 19.05%; with positive autocorrelation the $\\sqrt{t}$ rule *understates* true long-horizon risk. C) Annualized vol under iid is about 19.05%; with positive autocorrelation the $\\sqrt{t}$ rule *overstates* true long-horizon risk. D) Annualized vol under iid is about 19.05%; autocorrelation has no effect because volatility scales with $\\sqrt{t}$ regardless.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We apply the square-root-of-time rule and then assess the effect of autocorrelation.\n\n1. Under iid returns, variance scales with time, so volatility scales with $\\sqrt{t}$: $$\\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = \\sigma_{\\text{daily}}\\sqrt{252} = 1.2\\% \\times 15.875 = 19.05\\%.$$\n2. The $\\sqrt{t}$ rule *assumes* returns are uncorrelated. With **positive autocorrelation**, shocks reinforce one another over time, so the true multi-period variance is *larger* than the iid scaling implies.\n3. Therefore the $\\sqrt{t}$ rule **understates** long-horizon risk when returns are positively autocorrelated.\n\nB is correct. A scales by $t$ instead of $\\sqrt{t}$ (using variance scaling on a volatility) — a gross overstatement. C reverses the direction: positive autocorrelation increases (not decreases) cumulative variance; *mean reversion* (negative autocorrelation) is what causes overstatement. D wrongly claims autocorrelation is irrelevant — it is precisely the assumption the $\\sqrt{t}$ rule depends on.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $\\sqrt{t}$ scaling assumes iid. Positive autocorrelation → rule **understates** risk; mean reversion → rule **overstates** risk; GARCH clustering → scaling today's high vol ignores reversion to the long-run level. Always state the iid assumption before scaling.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"√t rule vs true risk\",\"items\":[\"mean reversion: overstates\",\"|iid: exact (19.05%)\",\"positive autocorrelation: understates\"],\"left\":\"shocks reverse\",\"right\":\"shocks reinforce\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Two assets have the relationship $Y = X^3$ over their entire (positive) data range, an exactly increasing transform. An analyst measures dependence three ways. Which set of statements is correct?\nA) Pearson $= 1$, Spearman $= 1$, Kendall $= 1$. B) Pearson $< 1$, Spearman $= 1$, Kendall $= 1$. C) Pearson $= 1$, Spearman $< 1$, Kendall $< 1$. D) Pearson $< 1$, Spearman $< 1$, Kendall $< 1$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests how the three dependence measures respond to a nonlinear but strictly monotonic relationship.\n\n1. **Pearson $\\rho$** measures *linear* association. $Y = X^3$ is nonlinear, so the relationship is not perfectly linear and $$\\rho < 1.$$\n2. **Spearman $\\rho_s$** is the correlation of *ranks*. A strictly increasing transform preserves rank order exactly: the largest $X$ maps to the largest $Y$, etc. So ranks match perfectly and $$\\rho_s = 1.$$\n3. **Kendall $\\tau$** counts concordant vs. discordant pairs. Under a strictly increasing transform every pair is concordant ($n_d = 0$), so $$\\tau = \\frac{n_c - 0}{\\binom{n}{2}} = 1.$$\n\nB is correct. A wrongly claims Pearson $= 1$ — Pearson cannot reach 1 for a curved relationship. C and D wrongly reduce the rank measures below 1 — rank-based measures are *invariant* to monotone transforms and stay at 1. This is the canonical illustration that Pearson captures only linear dependence while Spearman and Kendall capture any monotone dependence.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Strictly monotone transform ⇒ Spearman and Kendall = 1 (ranks/concordance preserved), but Pearson < 1 unless the relationship is exactly linear. Rank measures are robust to outliers and invariant to monotone transforms — the reason they feed copula models.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Y=X³: which dependence set?\",\"options\":[\"Pearson=1, Spearman=1, Kendall=1\",\"Pearson<1, Spearman=1, Kendall=1\",\"Pearson=1, Spearman<1, Kendall<1\",\"All three < 1\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Pearson measures linear fit so a curved X³ gives Pearson<1; rank measures (Spearman, Kendall) are invariant to monotone transforms, so both equal 1.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An EWMA model uses $\\lambda = 0.97$. Yesterday's variance estimate was $\\sigma_{t-1}^2 = 0.00020$ and yesterday's return was $r_{t-1} = -3.0\\%$. An analyst makes claims about today's estimate and about EWMA's forecasting behavior. Which is correct?\nA) Today's variance is 0.0001970; EWMA's multi-day forecast reverts to a long-run variance. B) Today's variance is 0.0002211; EWMA has no mean reversion, so its $h$-day-ahead variance forecast equals today's estimate. C) Today's variance is 0.0002211; EWMA reverts to a long-run variance at rate $0.97^h$. D) Today's variance is 0.0000270; higher $\\lambda$ makes the estimate react faster to new returns.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We update the EWMA variance, then assess its forecasting property.\n\n1. EWMA recursion: $$\\sigma_t^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{t-1}^2.$$\n2. Inputs: $r_{t-1}^2 = (-0.03)^2 = 0.0009$; $\\sigma_{t-1}^2 = 0.00020$; $\\lambda = 0.97$, $1-\\lambda = 0.03$.\n3. Compute: $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.97(0.00020) + 0.03(0.0009) = 0.000194 + 0.000027 = 0.0002210.$$\n4. EWMA is the GARCH special case with $\\omega = 0$ and $\\alpha + \\beta = 1$ (integrated). It has **no mean reversion**: the best $h$-step-ahead variance forecast is simply today's estimate, flat across all horizons.\n\nB is correct. A miscomputes (uses $0.97 \\times 0.00020 + 0.03 \\times$ something too small) and wrongly attributes mean reversion to EWMA. C gets the update right but wrongly says EWMA reverts at rate $0.97^h$ — that geometric reversion describes *GARCH* converging to $\\sigma_{LR}^2$; EWMA has no $\\omega$ and no long-run level to revert to. D miscomputes badly and reverses the smoothing logic: higher $\\lambda$ makes the estimate *smoother and slower* to react, not faster.\n\n📌 Exam tip: EWMA = integrated GARCH ($\\alpha+\\beta=1$, $\\omega=0$): no mean reversion, so the term structure of variance forecasts is flat at the current level. Higher $\\lambda$ = more weight on history = smoother, slower-reacting estimate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EWMA forecasting property\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"EWMA\",\"items\":[\"ω=0, α+β=1\",\"no mean reversion\",\"h-step forecast = today's estimate\"]},{\"h\":\"GARCH\",\"items\":[\"ω>0, α+β<1\",\"reverts at rate (α+β)^h\",\"pulls toward σ²LR\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Implied volatility is a key forward-looking risk input. Which statement about implied volatility is correct?\nA) It is computed by taking the standard deviation of historical realized returns over the option's life. B) It is the volatility input that makes a model (e.g., Black–Scholes–Merton) price equal the observed market price; a volatility smile/skew indicates the market rejects pure lognormal returns. C) A flat implied-volatility smile across strikes proves the market believes returns are fat-tailed. D) Implied volatility is necessarily lower than historical volatility because it is risk-neutral.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the definition and interpretation of implied volatility.\n\n1. Implied volatility is **backed out** of an option pricing model: it is the $\\sigma$ that, plugged into the model (e.g., Black–Scholes–Merton), reproduces the option's *observed market price*. It is therefore **forward-looking** and market-derived, unlike backward-looking historical volatility.\n2. If returns were truly lognormal (as BSM assumes), implied volatility would be identical across all strikes — a flat line.\n3. In reality, implied vol varies across strikes (a **smile or skew**), which reveals the market does *not* believe in the lognormal assumption (it prices in fat tails / skew).\n\nB is correct. A describes *historical/realized* volatility, the opposite concept. C is backwards: a *flat* smile would be consistent *with* lognormality; it is the *non-flat* smile/skew that signals fat tails. D is false: implied volatility can be higher or lower than historical volatility (it embeds risk premia and expectations); there is no rule forcing it lower.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Implied vol = the model-input vol that matches market price = forward-looking. The *existence* of a volatility smile/skew is direct evidence the market rejects the lognormal (constant-vol) assumption. Do not confuse implied with historical/realized volatility.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Implied volatility\",\"options\":[\"Std dev of historical returns over option life\",\"Vol that makes model price = market price; smile/skew rejects lognormal\",\"Flat smile proves fat tails\",\"Always lower than historical (risk-neutral)\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Implied vol is backed out of the market price (forward-looking); a non-flat smile/skew shows the market rejects the lognormal assumption.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Five paired observations have $X$-ranks $1, 2, 3, 4, 5$ and $Y$-ranks $3, 1, 2, 5, 4$. The Spearman rank correlation is closest to:\nA) 0.70 B) 0.60 C) 0.40 D) 0.90\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We apply the Spearman formula for untied ranks.\n\n1. Rank differences $d_i = (\\text{X-rank}) - (\\text{Y-rank})$:\n   - Pair 1: $1 - 3 = -2$\n   - Pair 2: $2 - 1 = +1$\n   - Pair 3: $3 - 2 = +1$\n   - Pair 4: $4 - 5 = -1$\n   - Pair 5: $5 - 4 = +1$\n2. Square and sum: $$\\sum d_i^2 = 4 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 8.$$\n3. Apply the formula with $n = 5$: $$\\rho_s = 1 - \\frac{6\\sum d_i^2}{n(n^2 - 1)} = 1 - \\frac{6 \\times 8}{5(25 - 1)} = 1 - \\frac{48}{120}.$$\n4. Compute: $$\\rho_s = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60.$$\n\nB is correct. Distractor A (0.70) results from miscounting $\\sum d_i^2 = 6$ (giving $1 - 36/120$). Distractor C (0.40) mistakes the fraction $48/120 = 0.40$ for the answer itself — the classic trap of forgetting to subtract from 1. Distractor D (0.90) results from using $\\sum d_i^2 = 2$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Spearman $\\rho_s = 1 - \\frac{6\\sum d_i^2}{n(n^2-1)}$. The two killer mistakes are (1) reporting the fraction instead of $1$ minus the fraction, and (2) miscomputing $\\sum d_i^2$. Double-check the squared-difference sum.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Spearman rank correlation\",\"steps\":[\"d = (-2,+1,+1,-1,+1)\",\"Σd² = 4+1+1+1+1 = 8\",\"6·8 / (5·24) = 48/120 = 0.40\",\"ρs = 1 - 0.40 = 0.60\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"213","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Simulation and Bootstrapping","notes":"# 13. Simulation and Bootstrapping\n\nMonte Carlo mechanics, simulation error and how to shrink it (more trials, antithetic variates, control variates), bootstrapping, and pseudo-random seeds. Expect a Monte Carlo standard-error calculation and a conceptual MC-vs-bootstrap question.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the basic steps to conduct a Monte Carlo simulation.\n- Describe ways to reduce Monte Carlo sampling error; explain the use of antithetic and control variates in variance reduction.\n- Describe the bootstrapping method and its advantage over Monte Carlo simulation.\n- Describe pseudo-random number generation and the role of seeds.\n- Describe situations where the bootstrapping method is ineffective.\n- Describe the disadvantages of the simulation approach to financial problem solving.\n\n## Monte Carlo: the recipe\n\n1. **Specify** the data-generating process (DGP) — distributions and parameters (e.g., GBM for prices, $$N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$$ shocks).\n2. **Generate** pseudo-random draws from the assumed distributions.\n3. **Compute** the statistic or payoff of interest for that path/draw.\n4. **Repeat** $$N$$ times (thousands+).\n5. **Aggregate:** the estimate is the average across trials; the empirical distribution gives quantiles (VaR), etc.\n\nBy the LLN the estimate converges to the true value; by the CLT its error is approximately normal with **standard error**\n\n$$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{N}}$$\n\nwhere $$s$$ is the across-trial standard deviation. Precision improves at rate $$\\sqrt{N}$$: **10× more accuracy needs 100× more trials**.\n\n## Variance reduction\n\n**Antithetic variates:** pair each draw $$u$$ with its mirror ($$1-u$$, or $$-z$$ for normals). The two estimates are negatively correlated, so the variance of their average,\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hat\\theta_1 + \\hat\\theta_2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1 + \\rho),$$\n\nfalls below independent sampling whenever $$\\rho < 0$$ — and you reuse the random numbers, halving generation cost.\n\n**Control variates:** simulate alongside the target a related quantity $$X$$ whose true value $$E[X]$$ is known analytically (e.g., price an arithmetic Asian option using the geometric Asian, which has a closed form, as control):\n\n$$\\hat\\theta_{CV} = \\hat\\theta + \\beta\\,(E[X] - \\hat{X})$$\n\nThe simulation error in $$\\hat X$$ corrects the error in $$\\hat\\theta$$; works best when target and control are highly correlated.\n\n## Pseudo-random numbers and seeds\n\nComputers generate **pseudo-random** sequences: deterministic algorithms that pass statistical randomness tests. The **seed** sets the starting state — the same seed reproduces the identical sequence. Benefits: reproducibility (audit/debug), and fair comparison of two models on identical shocks. The sequence eventually cycles, but modern generators have astronomically long periods.\n\n## Bootstrapping\n\nThe bootstrap resamples **with replacement from observed data** instead of drawing from an assumed distribution:\n\n1. From a sample of $$n$$ observations, draw $$n$$ with replacement.\n2. Compute the statistic on the resample.\n3. Repeat $$B$$ times; the spread of the $$B$$ statistics estimates the sampling distribution (standard errors, confidence intervals, VaR).\n\nTwo flavors: **iid bootstrap** (draw single observations — fine for independent data) and **circular block bootstrap** (draw consecutive blocks, wrapping around the end — preserves time-series dependence such as volatility clustering).\n\n**Advantage over Monte Carlo:** no distributional assumption — the data speak for themselves, automatically capturing fat tails and skew that a normal-based MC would miss (no model risk from a misspecified DGP).\n\n**When the bootstrap fails:**\n\n- **Structural breaks / non-stationarity:** history resampled from a different regime misleads.\n- **Strong dependence handled naively:** iid resampling destroys autocorrelation and volatility clustering (use block bootstrap).\n- **Tails beyond the data:** the bootstrap can never generate losses worse than the worst observation — a real problem for extreme-quantile risk.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Monte Carlo draws from an *assumed model*; bootstrap draws from *observed data*. MC's weakness is model misspecification; bootstrap's weaknesses are short/unrepresentative samples and inability to extrapolate past observed extremes. The exam asks for this contrast almost verbatim.\n\n## Disadvantages of simulation generally\n\n- Results carry **sampling error**; precision is bought slowly ($$\\sqrt{N}$$).\n- MC answers are only as good as the assumed DGP (model risk).\n- Computational cost for path-dependent or nested problems.\n- A simulated answer is specific to the chosen parameters — it provides less general insight than an analytic solution, and analytic solutions should be preferred where they exist.\n\n### Worked example: Monte Carlo standard error\n\nA 10,000-trial simulation of a portfolio's 1-year P&L gives mean \\$2.0M and across-trial standard deviation \\$15M.\n\n$$SE = \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{10{,}000}} = \\frac{15}{100} = \\$0.15\\text{M}$$\n\n95% CI for the true expected P&L: $$2.0 \\pm 1.96 \\times 0.15 = (\\$1.71\\text{M},\\, \\$2.29\\text{M})$$.\n\nTo halve the SE to \\$0.075M you need $$4 \\times 10{,}000 = 40{,}000$$ trials; to cut it to \\$0.015M (10×) you need 1,000,000 trials.\n\n### Worked example: antithetic variates\n\nAn estimator has per-pair variance $$\\sigma^2 = 400$$. With independent pairs, the average of two draws has variance $$400/2 = 200$$. Using antithetic pairs with $$\\rho = -0.6$$:\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var} = \\frac{400}{2}(1 - 0.6) = 200 \\times 0.4 = 80$$\n\nA 60% variance reduction — equivalent to running 2.5× as many independent trials at no extra simulation cost.\n\n### Worked example: bootstrap VaR\n\nDaily returns for 500 days are stored. To estimate the 99% 1-day VaR without assuming normality: draw 500 returns with replacement, compute the 1st percentile (the 5th worst), and repeat $$B = 1{,}000$$ times. Suppose the resampled percentiles average $$-3.1\\%$$ with standard deviation 0.4%. Then VaR(99%) ≈ 3.1% of portfolio value, with bootstrap standard error 0.4% — and note that no resample can produce a loss beyond the worst day in the 500-day window.","flashcards":"**Q1.** List the basic steps of a Monte Carlo simulation.\n> **A:** (1) Specify the data-generating process; (2) generate pseudo-random draws; (3) compute the statistic/payoff per draw; (4) repeat $$N$$ times; (5) aggregate — average for the estimate, empirical distribution for quantiles.\n\n**Q2.** Give the Monte Carlo standard error and its convergence rate.\n> **A:** $$SE = \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{N}}$$ ($$s$$ = across-trial SD). Precision improves at rate $$\\sqrt{N}$$: 10× more accuracy needs 100× more trials.\n\n**Q3.** How do antithetic variates reduce variance?\n> **A:** Pair each draw $$u$$ with its mirror ($$1-u$$ or $$-z$$); the negatively correlated estimates give $$\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hat\\theta_1+\\hat\\theta_2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1+\\rho)$$, below independent sampling when $$\\rho < 0$$ — and reuses the random numbers.\n\n**Q4.** How do control variates work?\n> **A:** Simulate a related quantity $$X$$ with known $$E[X]$$ alongside the target: $$\\hat\\theta_{CV} = \\hat\\theta + \\beta(E[X] - \\hat X)$$. The control's simulation error corrects the target's; works best when highly correlated.\n\n**Q5.** What are pseudo-random numbers and the role of the seed?\n> **A:** Deterministic sequences that pass randomness tests. The seed sets the starting state — the same seed reproduces the identical sequence, enabling reproducibility and fair comparison of models on identical shocks.\n\n**Q6.** Describe the bootstrap procedure.\n> **A:** Draw $$n$$ observations WITH replacement from the observed sample, compute the statistic, repeat $$B$$ times; the spread of the $$B$$ statistics estimates the sampling distribution (SEs, CIs, VaR).\n\n**Q7.** Distinguish iid bootstrap from circular block bootstrap.\n> **A:** iid bootstrap draws single observations (fine for independent data); circular block bootstrap draws consecutive blocks (wrapping around) to preserve time-series dependence like volatility clustering.\n\n**Q8.** What is the bootstrap's key advantage over Monte Carlo?\n> **A:** No distributional assumption — it samples the empirical distribution directly, capturing fat tails and skew that a normal-based MC would miss (no model risk from a misspecified DGP).\n\n**Q9.** When does the bootstrap fail?\n> **A:** Structural breaks / non-stationarity; strong dependence handled with naive iid resampling (use block bootstrap); and tails beyond the data — it can never generate a loss worse than the worst observation.\n\n**Q10.** Contrast what Monte Carlo and bootstrap draw from, and their respective weaknesses.\n> **A:** MC draws from an assumed model (weakness: model misspecification); bootstrap draws from observed data (weakness: short/unrepresentative samples, cannot extrapolate past observed extremes).\n\n**Q11.** A MC estimate has SE $50,000 from 2,500 trials. How many trials to reach SE $10,000?\n> **A:** SE must fall 5×, so trials rise $$5^2 = 25$$×: $$2{,}500 \\times 25 = 62{,}500$$.\n\n**Q12.** Per-pair variance $$\\sigma^2 = 400$$; antithetic pairs with $$\\rho = -0.6$$. Find the variance of the paired average.\n> **A:** $$\\mathrm{Var} = \\dfrac{400}{2}(1 - 0.6) = 200 \\times 0.4 = 80$$ (a 60% reduction vs. 200 for independent pairs).\n\n**Q13.** What are the general disadvantages of simulation?\n> **A:** Sampling error bought slowly ($$\\sqrt{N}$$); MC answers only as good as the assumed DGP (model risk); high computational cost; answers are parameter-specific and give less general insight than analytic solutions.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A Monte Carlo simulation of a portfolio's annual P&L uses 10,000 trials and produces an across-trial standard deviation of \\$20M. The standard error of the mean estimate is closest to:\nA) \\$0.20M B) \\$2.00M C) \\$0.002M D) \\$20M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the Monte Carlo standard error of the estimated mean.\n\nThe standard error formula is:\n$$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{N}}$$\n\nwhere $$s$$ is the across-trial standard deviation and $$N$$ is the number of trials. Plug in $$s = \\$20\\text{M}$$ and $$N = 10{,}000$$:\n$$\\sqrt{N} = \\sqrt{10{,}000} = 100$$\n$$SE = \\frac{20}{100} = \\$0.20\\text{M}$$\n\nSo the answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B. \\$2.00M would come from dividing by $$\\sqrt{100} = 10$$ instead of by 100 — using the wrong $$N$$.\n- C. \\$0.002M divides by 10,000 (i.e., by $$N$$, not $$\\sqrt{N}$$).\n- D. \\$20M is the raw across-trial standard deviation, not divided by $$\\sqrt{N}$$ at all.\n\nThink of averaging many noisy measurements: the more trials you average, the tighter your estimate, and the tightening goes with $$\\sqrt{N}$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Monte Carlo $$SE = s/\\sqrt{N}$$. Divide by the SQUARE ROOT of the trial count, not the count itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A Monte Carlo estimate currently has a standard error of \\$40,000 from 2,500 trials. To cut the standard error to \\$10,000, how many trials are needed?\nA) 10,000 B) 40,000 C) 5,000 D) 250,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how many trials are needed to shrink the standard error by a factor.\n\nStandard error falls as $$1/\\sqrt{N}$$. We want the SE to drop from \\$40,000 to \\$10,000 — that is a reduction by a factor of:\n$$\\frac{40{,}000}{10{,}000} = 4$$\n\nBecause SE scales with $$1/\\sqrt{N}$$, cutting SE by a factor of 4 requires multiplying $$N$$ by $$4^2 = 16$$:\n$$N_{\\text{new}} = 16 \\times 2{,}500 = 40{,}000$$\n\nSo the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. 10,000 is only $$4\\times$$ the original trials — that would cut SE by $$\\sqrt{4} = 2$$, giving \\$20,000, not \\$10,000.\n- C. 5,000 is just double the trials — far too few.\n- D. 250,000 is 100× the trials, which would cut SE by 10× (to \\$4,000), overshooting the target.\n\nThe rule of thumb: to make your estimate $$k$$ times more precise, you need $$k^2$$ times as many trials. Precision is expensive.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SE $$\\propto 1/\\sqrt{N}$$. To reduce SE by factor $$k$$, multiply trials by $$k^2$$. (10× precision = 100× trials.)\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Trials to cut SE by 4×\",\"steps\":[\"SE ∝ 1/√N\",\"Target reduction k = 40k/10k = 4\",\"Multiply N by k² = 16\",\"N = 16 × 2,500 = 40,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Antithetic variates reduce Monte Carlo variance because the paired estimates are:\nA) Independent of each other. B) Positively correlated. C) Negatively correlated. D) Always exactly equal.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks why pairing each random draw with its \"mirror\" reduces simulation variance.\n\nIn antithetic sampling, each draw $$u$$ is paired with $$1-u$$ (or $$z$$ with $$-z$$ for normals). The two resulting estimates tend to move in opposite directions — they are negatively correlated. The variance of their average is:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hat\\theta_1 + \\hat\\theta_2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1 + \\rho)$$\n\nWhen $$\\rho < 0$$, the factor $$(1+\\rho)$$ is below 1, so the variance falls below independent sampling. The answer is C.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. If the pairs were merely independent ($$\\rho = 0$$), you'd get the ordinary variance $$\\sigma^2/2$$ — no extra benefit. The benefit comes from negative correlation.\n- B. Positive correlation ($$\\rho > 0$$) would make $$(1+\\rho) > 1$$ and INCREASE the variance — the opposite of what we want.\n- D. If they were always equal, you'd gain no new information at all.\n\nImagine two friends estimating a height, one always guessing a bit high when the other guesses a bit low: averaging their offsetting errors gives a tighter answer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Antithetic variates work via NEGATIVE correlation; variance of the average $$= \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1+\\rho)$$ falls when $$\\rho < 0$$. Bonus: you reuse the random numbers, halving generation cost.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why antithetic variates work\",\"options\":[\"Independent (ρ=0)\",\"Positively correlated (ρ>0)\",\"Negatively correlated (ρ<0)\",\"Always exactly equal\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Pairing u with 1-u makes estimates negatively correlated; (1+ρ)<1 shrinks the variance of their average below independent sampling.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** An estimator has a per-draw variance of $$\\sigma^2 = 500$$. Using antithetic pairs with correlation $$\\rho = -0.4$$, the variance of the average of an antithetic pair is:\nA) 250 B) 150 C) 350 D) 500\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the variance after applying antithetic variates with a given negative correlation.\n\nUse the formula:\n$$\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hat\\theta_1 + \\hat\\theta_2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1 + \\rho)$$\n\nPlug in $$\\sigma^2 = 500$$ and $$\\rho = -0.4$$:\n$$= \\frac{500}{2}(1 + (-0.4)) = 250 \\times (0.6) = 150$$\n\nSo the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. 250 is $$\\sigma^2/2$$, the variance you'd get from two INDEPENDENT draws ($$\\rho = 0$$). It ignores the negative correlation benefit.\n- C. 350 would come from using $$\\rho = +0.4$$ ($$250 \\times 1.4 = 350$$) — wrong sign, giving a (wrong) increase.\n- D. 500 is the original per-draw variance, with no averaging applied.\n\nThe negative correlation shaves the variance from 250 down to 150 — a 40% reduction beyond plain averaging, at no extra simulation cost.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Apply $$\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1+\\rho)$$ carefully and keep the sign of $$\\rho$$. Negative $$\\rho$$ shrinks; positive $$\\rho$$ inflates.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** What is the primary advantage of bootstrapping over a standard Monte Carlo simulation?\nA) It always converges to the true value faster. B) It requires no assumption about the underlying return distribution. C) It can generate losses worse than any historically observed loss. D) It completely eliminates sampling error.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the key benefit of bootstrapping versus Monte Carlo.\n\nMonte Carlo draws from an assumed model (e.g., a normal distribution), which carries model risk if that assumption is wrong. Bootstrapping instead resamples directly from the observed data with replacement — so it makes no distributional assumption. The data \"speak for themselves,\" automatically capturing real fat tails and skew. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Bootstrapping does not inherently converge faster; both methods improve at the $$\\sqrt{N}$$ rate.\n- C. This is exactly what bootstrapping CANNOT do — it can never produce a loss worse than the worst observation in the sample. This is one of its weaknesses, not an advantage.\n- D. No simulation-based method eliminates sampling error; results from a finite number of resamples always carry some error.\n\nThink of bootstrapping as drawing slips from a hat filled with the actual historical returns, rather than inventing returns from a textbook bell curve that might be wrong.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bootstrap's strength = no distributional assumption (avoids model misspecification). Its weakness = cannot extrapolate beyond observed extremes.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap vs Monte Carlo\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"resamples observed data\",\"no distributional assumption\",\"can't exceed worst observation\"]},{\"h\":\"Monte Carlo\",\"items\":[\"draws from assumed model\",\"model-risk if wrong\",\"can extrapolate beyond data\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Fixing the seed of a pseudo-random number generator before a simulation:\nA) Makes the draws truly (physically) random. B) Reproduces the exact same sequence of draws on each run. C) Eliminates Monte Carlo sampling error. D) Is mathematically required for the law of large numbers to hold.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what setting a seed actually does.\n\nPseudo-random generators are deterministic algorithms; the seed sets their starting state. The same seed always produces the identical sequence of \"random\" numbers. This gives reproducibility — useful for auditing and debugging — and lets two competing models be tested on exactly the same shocks for a fair comparison. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Seeding does the opposite of making draws truly random — the sequence is fully deterministic given the seed. The numbers only LOOK random.\n- C. The seed does not reduce sampling error; running with a different seed just gives a different (but equally noisy) sample. Error still shrinks only with more trials.\n- D. The law of large numbers does not require a fixed seed; it holds as the number of draws grows, regardless of the seed.\n\nThink of a seed like a recipe's starting instructions: follow the same recipe and you get the same dish every time — reproducible, but no longer a surprise.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Seed = reproducibility and fair model comparison on identical shocks. It does NOT reduce error or make draws \"truly\" random.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What a fixed seed does\",\"options\":[\"Makes draws truly random\",\"Reproduces the same sequence\",\"Eliminates sampling error\",\"Required for the law of large numbers\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"PRNGs are deterministic; the same seed reproduces the identical sequence (reproducibility/fair comparison) but does not reduce error.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which situation would make the iid (single-observation) bootstrap inappropriate, requiring a block bootstrap instead?\nA) The data are independent across time. B) The data exhibit volatility clustering and autocorrelation. C) The sample is very large. D) The statistic of interest is the sample mean.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks when the simple iid bootstrap breaks down.\n\nThe iid bootstrap draws single observations one at a time, which scrambles their time order. If the data have time-series dependence — like autocorrelation or volatility clustering (calm periods followed by calm, turbulent followed by turbulent) — this scrambling destroys that structure. To preserve dependence, you draw consecutive blocks of observations (the circular block bootstrap). The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. If data are genuinely independent across time, the iid bootstrap is perfectly fine — there is no dependence to preserve.\n- C. A large sample is helpful for the bootstrap, not a problem. Size is not the issue here.\n- D. Estimating a simple mean does not by itself require a block bootstrap; the issue is whether the data are dependent, not which statistic you compute.\n\nThink of resampling a melody: pulling random single notes (iid) ruins the tune; pulling short phrases (blocks) keeps the rhythm and structure intact.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dependent/time-series data (autocorrelation, vol clustering) → use BLOCK bootstrap. iid bootstrap is only valid for independent data.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"iid vs block bootstrap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"iid bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"draws single observations\",\"scrambles time order\",\"valid only if independent\"]},{\"h\":\"Block bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"draws consecutive blocks\",\"preserves autocorrelation\",\"use for vol clustering\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A risk analyst uses a 500-day return history and a bootstrap to estimate the 99% 1-day VaR. A key limitation of this approach is that the bootstrap:\nA) Assumes returns are normally distributed. B) Cannot produce a loss larger than the worst loss in the 500-day window. C) Requires at least 1 million resamples to be valid. D) Always overstates tail risk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a limitation of using the bootstrap to estimate an extreme risk quantile.\n\nThe bootstrap resamples from the observed data only. Since it can only reuse values that actually occurred, no resample can ever generate a loss worse than the single worst day in the 500-day window. For extreme-quantile risk (like 99% VaR), this is a real weakness: if the future holds a loss larger than anything seen historically, the bootstrap will miss it. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. The bootstrap makes NO distributional assumption — that is its main advantage, the opposite of assuming normality.\n- C. There is no fixed minimum like 1 million resamples; a few thousand resamples are typically plenty.\n- D. The bootstrap does not \"always overstate\" tail risk; if anything, it tends to UNDERSTATE extreme tails because it cannot exceed the worst observed loss.\n\nThink of the bootstrap as a photo album: you can only re-arrange pictures you already have — you can never conjure a scene that was never photographed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The bootstrap can't extrapolate beyond observed extremes — a serious problem for far-tail risk like 99%+ VaR. For unseen extremes, consider parametric or extreme-value methods.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap VaR limitation\",\"options\":[\"Assumes normal returns\",\"Can't exceed worst observed loss\",\"Needs 1M+ resamples\",\"Always overstates tail risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Every resampled point is an actual past observation, so the bootstrap can never produce a loss worse than the worst day seen — it understates unseen far-tail risk.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of the following best describes the control variates technique?\nA) Pairing each random draw with its mirror image to induce negative correlation. B) Simulating a related quantity whose true expected value is known, and using its simulation error to correct the target estimate. C) Resampling observed data with replacement. D) Increasing the number of trials until the standard error is acceptable.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks to identify the control variates method among variance-reduction techniques.\n\nA control variate is a related quantity $$X$$ whose true expected value $$E[X]$$ is known analytically. You simulate it alongside the target, then adjust:\n$$\\hat\\theta_{CV} = \\hat\\theta + \\beta\\,(E[X] - \\hat{X})$$\n\nBecause $$\\hat X$$ and the target share the same random noise, the known error in $$\\hat X$$ (the gap $$E[X] - \\hat X$$) is used to correct the error in the target estimate. It works best when target and control are highly correlated. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Pairing a draw with its mirror image describes antithetic variates, a different technique.\n- C. Resampling observed data with replacement is bootstrapping, not control variates.\n- D. Simply adding more trials is brute-force precision improvement, not a variance-reduction technique in the clever sense.\n\nClassic example: to price an arithmetic Asian option, simulate the geometric Asian option (which has a closed-form true price) as the control, and use its known error to sharpen the arithmetic estimate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Control variate needs a KNOWN $$E[X]$$ and high correlation with the target. Antithetic = mirrored draws; control variate = correcting using a known-answer companion.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Variance-reduction techniques\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Antithetic\",\"items\":[\"pair u with 1-u\",\"induces negative correlation\",\"mirrored draws\"]},{\"h\":\"Control variate\",\"items\":[\"companion with known E[X]\",\"uses its error to correct\",\"needs high correlation\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which of the following is a genuine disadvantage of the simulation approach to financial problem solving (compared with an analytic solution)?\nA) Simulation gives more general insight than a closed-form formula. B) Simulation results carry sampling error and improve only slowly (at the $$\\sqrt{N}$$ rate). C) Simulation never depends on any model assumptions. D) Simulation always runs faster than computing a formula.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a true drawback of simulation.\n\nSimulation answers are estimates from a finite number of trials, so they always carry sampling error, and that error shrinks only at the slow $$\\sqrt{N}$$ rate — meaning big gains in precision require huge increases in trials. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. This is backwards: a closed-form analytic solution gives MORE general insight (it shows how the answer depends on each input). A simulation result is specific to the chosen parameters.\n- C. Monte Carlo simulation depends heavily on the assumed data-generating process — model risk is a key weakness, not something it avoids.\n- D. Simulation is usually SLOWER and more computationally costly than evaluating a formula, especially for path-dependent or nested problems.\n\nThink of simulation as estimating a room's area by tossing thousands of darts: it works, but it's slow, noisy, and gives less insight than just measuring length × width with a formula.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Prefer analytic solutions where they exist. Simulation's downsides: sampling error (slow $$\\sqrt{N}$$ convergence), model risk, computational cost, and limited general insight.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Genuine simulation disadvantage\",\"options\":[\"More general insight than a formula\",\"Sampling error, slow √N convergence\",\"Never depends on assumptions\",\"Always faster than a formula\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Simulation answers carry sampling error that shrinks only at the slow √N rate — plus model risk and computational cost; analytic solutions give more general insight.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A Monte Carlo estimate has standard error \\$50,000 from 2,500 trials. To reduce the SE to \\$10,000, the required number of trials is:\nA) 12,500 B) 62,500 C) 25,000 D) 250,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** SE must fall 5×, so trials rise $$5^2 = 25$$×: $$2{,}500 \\times 25 = 62{,}500$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Antithetic variates reduce variance because the paired estimates are:\nA) Independent B) Positively correlated C) Negatively correlated D) Identically zero\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Mirrored draws produce negatively correlated estimates; averaging cancels error.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The key advantage of bootstrapping over Monte Carlo is:\nA) It always converges faster B) It requires no assumption about the return distribution C) It can generate losses beyond historical extremes D) It eliminates sampling error\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The bootstrap samples the empirical distribution directly; C is exactly what it *cannot* do.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Fixing the seed of a pseudo-random number generator:\nA) Makes the draws truly random B) Reproduces the same sequence of draws each run C) Eliminates Monte Carlo error D) Is required for antithetic sampling\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Seeds give reproducibility and let competing models face identical shocks.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A Monte Carlo pricing run uses 10,000 independent replications; the standard deviation of the simulated discounted payoffs is $4.00. The standard error of the price estimate is closest to:\nA) $0.0004 B) $0.04 C) $0.40 D) $4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{N}} = \\frac{4.00}{\\sqrt{10{,}000}} = \\frac{4.00}{100} = \\$0.04$$\n\nDistractor A divides by $$N$$; halving this SE would require $$4 \\times 10{,}000 = 40{,}000$$ replications.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A Monte Carlo simulation of a credit portfolio's annual loss uses 4,900 trials and reports a mean loss of $\\$8.0$M with an across-trial standard deviation of $\\$28$M. The risk team needs the standard error of the mean loss reduced to $\\$0.10$M. The number of trials required is closest to:\nA) 19,600 B) 78,400 C) 156,800 D) 160,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We compute the current standard error, the required reduction factor, and scale trials by its square.\n\n1. Current standard error: $$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{N}} = \\frac{28}{\\sqrt{4{,}900}} = \\frac{28}{70} = \\$0.40\\text{M}.$$\n2. Target $SE = \\$0.10$M. The SE must fall by a factor of $$\\frac{0.40}{0.10} = 4.$$\n3. Because $SE \\propto 1/\\sqrt{N}$, reducing SE by a factor of 4 requires multiplying $N$ by $4^2 = 16$.\n4. Required trials: $$N = 16 \\times 4{,}900 = 78{,}400.$$\n\nB is correct. Distractor A ($4 \\times 4{,}900 = 19{,}600$) scales trials linearly with the SE-reduction factor instead of by its square — the most common error. Distractor D (160,000) comes from squaring the SE-reduction factor but applying it to a wrongly assumed round base of 10,000 trials ($16 \\times 10{,}000$) instead of the actual 4,900. Distractor C (156,800) doubles the correct answer ($2 \\times 78{,}400$), a stray factor-of-2 slip. \n\n📌 Exam tip: Monte Carlo precision improves only at rate $\\sqrt{N}$ — to cut the SE by a factor $k$, you need $k^2$ times as many trials. \"10× more accuracy needs 100× more trials.\" Always compute the current SE first.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Trials to hit target SE\",\"steps\":[\"Current SE = 28/√4900 = 0.40M\",\"Target 0.10M → reduce by k = 4\",\"Multiply N by k² = 16\",\"N = 16 × 4,900 = 78,400\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An analyst estimates a quantity by Monte Carlo. Independent sampling gives a per-trial variance of $\\sigma^2 = 900$. She switches to antithetic variates with a correlation of $\\rho = -0.7$ between paired estimates. The variance of the average of one antithetic *pair* is closest to:\nA) 450 B) 135 C) 765 D) 270\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We apply the variance formula for the average of two antithetic draws.\n\n1. Formula for the variance of the average of two paired estimates: $$\\mathrm{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\hat\\theta_1 + \\hat\\theta_2}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(1 + \\rho).$$\n2. Plug in $\\sigma^2 = 900$ and $\\rho = -0.7$: $$\\frac{900}{2}(1 + (-0.7)) = 450 \\times 0.3.$$\n3. Compute: $$= 135.$$\n\nB is correct. Distractor A (450) is the variance under *independent* pairing ($\\rho = 0$): $\\frac{900}{2}(1+0) = 450$ — it ignores the negative correlation benefit. Distractor C (765) uses $(1 + 0.7)$ with a sign error on $\\rho$ ($450 \\times 1.7 = 765$), which would *increase* variance — the opposite of what antithetic variates do. Distractor D (270) uses $\\frac{900}{2}(1-0.4)$ or a similar partial-credit miscalculation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Antithetic variates work *because* $\\rho < 0$: the factor $(1+\\rho)$ is below 1, shrinking variance, and you reuse the random numbers (halving generation cost). A sign slip on $\\rho$ flips the conclusion entirely — antithetic pairing should always *reduce* variance.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A risk manager must choose between Monte Carlo simulation (assuming normally distributed returns) and historical bootstrapping to estimate the 99% 1-day VaR of a portfolio whose returns exhibit pronounced fat tails. Which statement is the most accurate comparison?\nA) Monte Carlo is superior because it can generate losses worse than any in the historical sample, while the bootstrap cannot. B) The bootstrap requires no distributional assumption and captures the observed fat tails directly, but it cannot produce a loss worse than the worst observation in the sample. C) The bootstrap eliminates sampling error, whereas Monte Carlo does not. D) Both methods are equivalent here because both ultimately resample from the same data.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts the core strengths and weaknesses of the two simulation approaches.\n\n1. **Bootstrap** resamples *with replacement from observed data* — it makes **no distributional assumption** and automatically reflects the empirical fat tails and skew. This avoids the model risk of a misspecified DGP.\n2. Its key limitation: it can **never generate a loss worse than the worst observed value**, because every resampled point is an actual historical observation. This is a real problem for extreme-quantile (tail) risk.\n3. Normal-based Monte Carlo, by contrast, *assumes* a distribution (here normal), which would *understate* fat-tailed risk — its weakness is model misspecification, not tail extrapolation.\n\nB is correct. A reverses the tail-extrapolation property: it is *Monte Carlo* (drawing from a parametric model) that can generate losses beyond the historical sample; the bootstrap cannot. C is false — both methods carry sampling error; nothing eliminates it. D is wrong: Monte Carlo draws from an *assumed model*, not from the observed data, so they are not equivalent.\n\n📌 Exam tip: MC draws from an *assumed model* (weakness = model misspecification, but it *can* extrapolate beyond data). Bootstrap draws from *observed data* (weakness = cannot exceed the worst observation, and needs a representative sample). GARP asks this contrast almost verbatim.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap vs Monte Carlo (fat tails)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"resamples observed data\",\"no distributional assumption\",\"captures empirical fat tails\",\"can't exceed worst observation\"]},{\"h\":\"Monte Carlo\",\"items\":[\"draws from assumed model\",\"normal → understates fat tails\",\"CAN extrapolate beyond data\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A risk team applies an iid (single-observation) bootstrap to a series of daily returns that displays strong volatility clustering, to estimate a 10-day VaR. Which statement best identifies the problem and the fix?\nA) There is no problem; the iid bootstrap preserves all dependence structure automatically. B) The iid bootstrap destroys the autocorrelation/volatility clustering; a circular block bootstrap should be used to preserve time-series dependence. C) The iid bootstrap overstates risk because it double-counts clustered losses; switch to Monte Carlo. D) The problem is the seed; fixing the seed will restore the clustering.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests when the bootstrap fails and the appropriate remedy.\n\n1. Volatility clustering is a form of **time-series dependence**: high-volatility days tend to follow high-volatility days.\n2. The **iid bootstrap** draws single observations independently with replacement, which **shuffles the data and destroys** any autocorrelation or clustering — leading to misestimated multi-day risk.\n3. The fix is the **circular block bootstrap**: draw consecutive *blocks* of observations (wrapping around the end of the series), which preserves the dependence structure within each block.\n\nB is correct. A is false — iid resampling specifically *breaks* dependence. C invents an \"overstatement/double-count\" mechanism that does not exist; the issue is destroyed structure, and switching to Monte Carlo would require assuming a model (defeating the bootstrap's purpose). D confuses the seed (which only controls reproducibility of the random sequence) with the resampling scheme — fixing the seed does nothing to preserve clustering.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For dependent / clustered time series, use a *block* bootstrap, not the iid bootstrap. Memorize the bootstrap failure modes: structural breaks/non-stationarity, naive handling of dependence, and inability to extrapolate beyond observed extremes.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"iid vs block bootstrap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"iid bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"draws single observations\",\"destroys vol clustering\",\"only valid if independent\"]},{\"h\":\"Block bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"draws consecutive blocks\",\"preserves dependence\",\"circular wraps the series\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Regarding pseudo-random number generation and seeds in simulation, which statement is correct?\nA) Pseudo-random sequences are truly random and cannot be reproduced. B) Setting the seed reproduces the identical sequence of draws, enabling auditability and fair comparison of competing models on identical shocks. C) Fixing the seed eliminates Monte Carlo sampling error. D) A fixed seed is required for the standard error formula $SE = s/\\sqrt{N}$ to hold.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the role of seeds in pseudo-random generation.\n\n1. Computers generate **pseudo-random** numbers via deterministic algorithms that pass statistical tests for randomness — they are not truly random.\n2. The **seed** sets the algorithm's starting state. The *same seed reproduces the identical sequence* of draws.\n3. Benefits: **reproducibility** (auditing/debugging) and **fair comparison** — two competing models can be run on the *same* set of shocks, isolating model differences from sampling noise.\n\nB is correct. A is false on two counts: the sequence is pseudo-random (deterministic), and it *is* reproducible. C is false — fixing the seed does not reduce sampling error at all; only more trials do that. The seed just makes the (still error-laden) result reproducible. D is fabricated: the SE formula holds for valid random draws regardless of whether a seed was fixed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Seeds give *reproducibility*, not *accuracy*. They let you audit results and compare models on identical shocks, but they do nothing to shrink Monte Carlo sampling error — that still requires more trials ($\\sqrt{N}$ convergence).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Role of a fixed seed\",\"options\":[\"Pseudo-random sequences are truly random\",\"Reproduces the same sequence → auditability, fair comparison\",\"Eliminates sampling error\",\"Required for SE = s/√N to hold\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A seed sets a deterministic PRNG's starting state, so the same seed reproduces the identical sequence (reproducibility, fair model comparison); it does not reduce sampling error.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A 10,000-trial Monte Carlo simulation of a portfolio's 1-year P&L produces a mean of $\\$5.0$M and an across-trial standard deviation of $\\$40$M. The 95% confidence interval for the *true expected* P&L is closest to:\nA) $(\\$4.22\\text{M},\\ \\$5.78\\text{M})$ B) $(-\\$73.4\\text{M},\\ \\$83.4\\text{M})$ C) $(\\$4.61\\text{M},\\ \\$5.39\\text{M})$ D) $(\\$4.92\\text{M},\\ \\$5.08\\text{M})$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We build a confidence interval for the *mean* using the standard error (not the raw standard deviation).\n\n1. Standard error of the mean: $$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{N}} = \\frac{40}{\\sqrt{10{,}000}} = \\frac{40}{100} = \\$0.40\\text{M}.$$\n2. 95% CI for the mean: $$\\bar X \\pm 1.96\\,SE = 5.0 \\pm 1.96 \\times 0.40 = 5.0 \\pm 0.784.$$\n3. Bounds: $$(5.0 - 0.784,\\ 5.0 + 0.784) = (\\$4.216\\text{M},\\ \\$5.784\\text{M}).$$\n\nA is correct. Distractor B uses the raw standard deviation $s = \\$40$M instead of the SE ($5.0 \\pm 1.96 \\times 40 = 5.0 \\pm 78.4$) — the classic confusion between the dispersion of *individual trials* and the precision of their *mean*. Distractor C uses $SE = 0.40$ but applies $1.0$ (or a mis-scaled multiplier) instead of 1.96 (giving $\\pm 0.40$... actually $\\pm 0.39$). Distractor D divides the SE by an extra factor (e.g., uses $SE/10$), grossly understating the interval.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A confidence interval for the *mean* uses the *standard error* $s/\\sqrt{N}$, not the raw standard deviation $s$. Using $s$ directly inflates the interval enormously — a favorite trap when $N$ is large.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"95% CI for the MEAN\",\"steps\":[\"SE = s/√N = 40/100 = 0.40M\",\"±1.96·SE = ±0.784M (use SE, not raw s)\",\"5.0 ± 0.784\",\"(4.22M, 5.78M)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of the following is a genuine disadvantage of the simulation approach (Monte Carlo) to financial problem solving, as opposed to a strength?\nA) It can incorporate complex, path-dependent payoffs that lack closed-form solutions. B) Its results are only as good as the assumed data-generating process, and it provides less general insight than an analytic solution where one exists. C) It produces an empirical distribution from which quantiles such as VaR can be read. D) Variance-reduction techniques can improve precision without proportionally more trials.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to distinguish a true *disadvantage* from the method's *strengths*.\n\n1. Monte Carlo's central weakness is **model risk**: the answer is only as reliable as the assumed DGP (distributions and parameters). A misspecified model produces a confidently wrong answer.\n2. Additionally, a simulated result is **specific to the chosen parameters** and gives **less general insight** than a closed-form analytic solution. Where an analytic solution exists, it should generally be preferred.\n3. These are genuine disadvantages of simulation.\n\nB is correct. A, C, and D all describe *strengths* of simulation: handling path-dependent/intractable problems (A), generating a full empirical distribution for quantile/VaR estimation (C), and improving efficiency via variance-reduction techniques like antithetic and control variates (D). Only B captures a true drawback (model risk plus lack of general insight relative to analytic solutions).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Know the disadvantages of simulation cold — sampling error (slow $\\sqrt{N}$ convergence), dependence on the assumed DGP (model risk), computational cost, and less general insight than analytic solutions. Strengths (flexibility, full distribution, variance reduction) are common distractors dressed up as \"disadvantages.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"True disadvantage of simulation\",\"options\":[\"Handles path-dependent payoffs\",\"Only as good as the assumed DGP; less insight than analytic\",\"Produces an empirical distribution for VaR\",\"Variance reduction improves precision\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Model risk (results only as good as the assumed DGP) plus less general insight than a closed-form solution are genuine drawbacks; the others are strengths.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"214","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Machine-Learning Methods","notes":"# 14. Machine-Learning Methods\n\nThe ML foundations chapter: types of learning, the train/validation/test discipline, over- vs. underfitting and the bias–variance trade-off, regularization (ridge/LASSO), PCA, and K-means. Mostly conceptual on the exam, with occasional small calculations (scaling, cluster assignment, penalty terms).\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Discuss the philosophical and practical differences between machine-learning techniques and classical econometrics.\n- Explain the differences among supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning.\n- Understand the process of and issues with data preparation, including standardization and the handling of categorical variables.\n- Describe the differences among training, validation, and test data subsamples; explain the role of each.\n- Understand the problems of underfitting and overfitting in model design (the bias–variance trade-off).\n- Use principal components analysis to reduce the dimensionality of a set of features.\n- Describe and use the K-means clustering algorithm, including measures of cluster quality.\n- Be aware of natural language processing and how it is used.\n\n## ML vs. classical econometrics\n\nEconometrics starts from a theory-driven, small, interpretable model and focuses on unbiased parameter estimates and hypothesis tests. ML prioritizes **out-of-sample predictive accuracy**, tolerates biased or opaque models, handles huge feature sets and nonlinearities automatically, and validates empirically (held-out data) rather than via distributional theory.\n\n## Three types of learning\n\n| Type | Data | Goal | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Supervised** | Labeled $$(X, Y)$$ | Predict $$Y$$ from $$X$$ | Regression, classification: credit scoring, default prediction |\n| **Unsupervised** | Unlabeled $$X$$ only | Find structure | PCA, clustering, anomaly detection |\n| **Reinforcement** | Reward signal from environment | Learn a policy of sequential actions by trial and error | Optimal trade execution, hedging |\n\nSupervised learning splits further into **regression** (continuous target) and **classification** (categorical target).\n\n## Data preparation\n\n- **Standardization:** rescale features, $$z = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma$$, so distance- and penalty-based methods (KNN, K-means, ridge/LASSO, SVM) don't let large-unit features dominate. Min-max scaling to $$[0,1]$$ is the common alternative.\n- **Categorical variables:** one-hot/dummy encoding (one column per category); ordinal encoding only when categories are genuinely ordered.\n- Cleaning: missing values, outliers, deduplication — usually most of the work.\n\n## Training, validation, and test sets\n\n- **Training set:** fit the model parameters.\n- **Validation set:** compare candidate models / tune hyperparameters (e.g., $$\\lambda$$, $$K$$, tree depth).\n- **Test set:** used **once**, at the end, for an unbiased estimate of real-world performance.\n\n**Cross-validation** (k-fold) recycles training data into temporary validation folds when data are scarce.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Two exam traps. (1) Tuning hyperparameters on the *test* set contaminates it — performance estimates become optimistic; that's the validation set's job. (2) With time-series data, random shuffling leaks the future into training; splits must respect chronology.\n\n## Underfitting, overfitting, and bias–variance\n\nExpected prediction error decomposes as\n\n$$E[(y - \\hat f)^2] = \\underbrace{\\text{Bias}^2}_{\\text{too simple}} + \\underbrace{\\text{Variance}}_{\\text{too sensitive}} + \\underbrace{\\sigma^2_{\\text{noise}}}_{\\text{irreducible}}$$\n\n- **Underfitting** (high bias): model too simple — poor fit in-sample *and* out-of-sample.\n- **Overfitting** (high variance): model memorizes training noise — excellent in-sample, poor out-of-sample. Signature: training error keeps falling while validation error turns upward.\n- Complexity should be chosen where *validation* error is minimized.\n\n## Regularization: ridge and LASSO\n\nPenalize complexity directly in the objective:\n\n**Ridge (L2):**\n\n$$\\min_\\beta \\sum_i (y_i - X_i\\beta)^2 + \\lambda \\sum_j \\beta_j^2$$\n\n**LASSO (L1):**\n\n$$\\min_\\beta \\sum_i (y_i - X_i\\beta)^2 + \\lambda \\sum_j \\lvert\\beta_j\\rvert$$\n\n| | Ridge | LASSO |\n|---|---|---|\n| Penalty | squared coefficients | absolute coefficients |\n| Effect | shrinks all coefficients toward zero | shrinks **and sets some exactly to zero** |\n| Feature selection | no | yes (sparse models) |\n| Collinearity | handles well (shares weight) | picks one of a correlated group |\n\n$$\\lambda$$ is tuned on validation data: $$\\lambda = 0$$ is OLS; $$\\lambda \\to \\infty$$ shrinks everything to zero. Regularization deliberately accepts bias to cut variance — the bias–variance trade-off made explicit. **Elastic net** mixes both penalties.\n\n## Principal components analysis\n\nPCA replaces correlated features with a small set of uncorrelated **principal components** — orthogonal linear combinations ordered by variance explained. The first PC is the direction of maximum variance; each subsequent PC maximizes remaining variance subject to orthogonality. Choose enough PCs to explain, say, 90% of total variance (scree plot).\n\nFinance staple: yield-curve moves compress to ~3 PCs — **level, slope, curvature** — typically explaining 95%+ of variance. Standardize features first; PCs lack direct economic interpretation (a price of dimension reduction).\n\n## K-means clustering\n\nUnsupervised grouping of observations into $$K$$ clusters:\n\n1. Choose $$K$$; initialize $$K$$ centroids.\n2. Assign each point to the nearest centroid (Euclidean distance).\n3. Recompute each centroid as the mean of its members.\n4. Repeat 2–3 until assignments stop changing.\n\nQuality: **inertia** (within-cluster sum of squares) falls as $$K$$ rises — pick $$K$$ at the \"elbow,\" or maximize the **silhouette** score (cohesion vs. separation, range $$-1$$ to $$1$$). Results depend on initialization (run multiple starts) and on feature scaling. Uses: segmenting counterparties, regime identification, fraud/anomaly screening.\n\n### Worked example: K-means assignment\n\nTwo centroids in standardized feature space: $$c_1 = (0, 0)$$, $$c_2 = (4, 2)$$. Assign point $$x = (3, 0)$$.\n\n$$d_1 = \\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + 0^2} = 3.00, \\qquad d_2 = \\sqrt{(3-4)^2 + (0-2)^2} = \\sqrt{5} = 2.24$$\n\n$$d_2 < d_1$$: assign $$x$$ to cluster 2; centroid 2 then moves toward $$x$$ at the update step.\n\n## Natural language processing\n\nNLP converts text (news, filings, transcripts, complaints) into features: tokenization, stop-word removal, stemming/lemmatization, then representations from bag-of-words/TF-IDF counts to word embeddings and large language models. Risk uses: news **sentiment** scoring, surveillance of trader communications, classifying operational-loss descriptions, KYC/adverse-media screening.","flashcards":"**Q1.** How does machine learning differ from classical econometrics?\n> **A:** Econometrics: theory-driven, small, interpretable, focused on unbiased estimates and hypothesis tests. ML: prioritizes out-of-sample predictive accuracy, tolerates biased/opaque models, handles huge feature sets and nonlinearities, validates empirically on held-out data.\n\n**Q2.** Distinguish supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning.\n> **A:** Supervised: labeled $$(X,Y)$$, predict $$Y$$ (regression/classification). Unsupervised: unlabeled $$X$$, find structure (PCA, clustering). Reinforcement: learn a policy of sequential actions from reward signals by trial and error.\n\n**Q3.** Why standardize features, and which methods need it?\n> **A:** Rescale via $$z = (x-\\mu)/\\sigma$$ so large-unit features don't dominate distance- and penalty-based methods (KNN, K-means, ridge/LASSO, SVM). Min-max scaling to $$[0,1]$$ is the alternative.\n\n**Q4.** What are the roles of the training, validation, and test sets?\n> **A:** Training: fit parameters. Validation: compare models / tune hyperparameters. Test: used ONCE at the end for an unbiased performance estimate.\n\n**Q5.** Name two exam traps with data splits.\n> **A:** (1) Tuning hyperparameters on the test set contaminates it (that's the validation set's job). (2) With time-series data, random shuffling leaks the future into training — splits must respect chronology.\n\n**Q6.** Give the bias–variance decomposition of prediction error.\n> **A:** $$E[(y-\\hat f)^2] = \\text{Bias}^2 + \\text{Variance} + \\sigma^2_{\\text{noise}}$$ (too simple / too sensitive / irreducible).\n\n**Q7.** Distinguish underfitting and overfitting and their signatures.\n> **A:** Underfitting (high bias): too simple, poor fit in- AND out-of-sample. Overfitting (high variance): memorizes training noise — great in-sample, poor out-of-sample; signature is falling training error while validation error rises.\n\n**Q8.** State the ridge and LASSO objectives and the key behavioral difference.\n> **A:** Ridge (L2): $$\\min \\sum (y_i - X_i\\beta)^2 + \\lambda\\sum\\beta_j^2$$. LASSO (L1): $$\\min \\sum (y_i - X_i\\beta)^2 + \\lambda\\sum\\lvert\\beta_j\\rvert$$. Ridge shrinks all coefficients; LASSO can set some exactly to zero (feature selection).\n\n**Q9.** What does the regularization parameter $$\\lambda$$ do at its extremes, and what is it tuned on?\n> **A:** $$\\lambda = 0$$ gives OLS; $$\\lambda \\to \\infty$$ shrinks everything to zero. It is tuned on validation data; regularization trades accepted bias for reduced variance.\n\n**Q10.** What does PCA do, and which finance application is standard?\n> **A:** Replaces correlated features with uncorrelated principal components — orthogonal linear combinations ordered by variance explained. Yield-curve moves compress to ~3 PCs: level, slope, curvature (95%+ of variance).\n\n**Q11.** Describe the K-means algorithm.\n> **A:** Choose $$K$$ and initialize centroids → assign each point to the nearest centroid (Euclidean) → recompute centroids as member means → repeat until assignments stop changing.\n\n**Q12.** How do you choose $$K$$ and assess K-means quality?\n> **A:** Inertia (within-cluster sum of squares) falls as $$K$$ rises — pick the \"elbow,\" or maximize the silhouette score (cohesion vs. separation, range $$-1$$ to $$1$$). Results depend on initialization and scaling.\n\n**Q13.** Centroids $$c_1 = (0,0)$$, $$c_2 = (4,2)$$. Assign point $$x = (3,0)$$.\n> **A:** $$d_1 = 3.00$$, $$d_2 = \\sqrt{(−1)^2 + (−2)^2} = \\sqrt{5} = 2.24$$; $$d_2 < d_1$$ → assign to cluster 2.\n\n**Q14.** Difference between ridge and LASSO under collinearity, and what is elastic net?\n> **A:** Ridge handles collinearity well (shares weight among correlated regressors); LASSO picks one of a correlated group. Elastic net mixes both L1 and L2 penalties.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Building a model to predict whether a loan will default (yes/no) using historical data that is already labeled as \"defaulted\" or \"not defaulted\" is an example of:\nA) Unsupervised learning. B) Supervised classification. C) Reinforcement learning. D) Supervised regression.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which type of machine learning matches this task.\n\nTwo clues decide it. First, the data are labeled — each past loan already has a known outcome (defaulted or not). Labeled data with a target to predict means supervised learning. Second, the target is a category (default / no default), not a continuous number, which makes it classification rather than regression. So the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Unsupervised learning uses unlabeled data to find structure (like clustering). Here the data ARE labeled, so it is not unsupervised.\n- C. Reinforcement learning learns a sequence of actions from reward signals by trial and error. There is no sequential agent or reward here.\n- D. Supervised regression predicts a continuous number (like a dollar amount). Default/no-default is a category, so it is classification, not regression.\n\nThink of supervised learning like studying with an answer key: every example comes with its correct answer, and you learn to predict the answer for new cases. A yes/no answer makes it classification.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Labeled data → supervised. Categorical target → classification; continuous target → regression. No labels → unsupervised.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"ML problem types\",\"x\":\"labeled? →\",\"y\":\"predict number? →\",\"q\":[\"clustering\",\"classification\",\"dim. reduction\",\"regression\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which regularization penalty can shrink some coefficients exactly to zero, effectively performing variable selection?\nA) Ridge (L2 penalty). B) LASSO (L1 penalty). C) Both perform variable selection equally. D) Neither can set coefficients to zero.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which penalty produces sparse models with some coefficients set to exactly zero.\n\nLASSO uses an L1 penalty (sum of absolute values of coefficients, $$\\lambda\\sum_j|\\beta_j|$$). The geometry of this penalty has \"corners\" that push some coefficients all the way to exactly zero, dropping those features entirely — that is automatic variable selection. Ridge uses an L2 penalty (sum of squared coefficients) which only shrinks coefficients toward zero but never exactly to zero. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Ridge shrinks coefficients smoothly toward zero but keeps all of them nonzero, so it does not perform variable selection.\n- C. They are NOT equal: only LASSO produces exact zeros.\n- D. LASSO clearly can set coefficients to zero, so \"neither\" is wrong.\n\nThink of LASSO as a budget so tight it forces you to drop some items entirely, while ridge just makes you buy smaller amounts of everything.\n\n📌 Exam tip: LASSO (L1) → sparse models, exact zeros, feature selection. Ridge (L2) → shrinks all coefficients but keeps them. Elastic net mixes both.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Ridge (L2) vs LASSO (L1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Ridge (L2)\",\"items\":[\"penalty Σβ²\",\"shrinks toward zero\",\"keeps all features\"]},{\"h\":\"LASSO (L1)\",\"items\":[\"penalty Σ|β|\",\"drives some to exactly 0\",\"feature selection\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A model's training error is very low, but its validation error is large and grows as the model becomes more complex. This pattern indicates:\nA) Underfitting (high bias). B) Overfitting (high variance). C) A well-tuned model. D) Irreducible noise only.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a model that does great on training data but poorly on new (validation) data.\n\nThis is the classic signature of overfitting: the model has memorized the noise in the training set, so training error keeps falling, but it fails to generalize, so validation error rises. Overfitting corresponds to high variance — the model is too sensitive to the particular training sample. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Underfitting (high bias) shows poor performance on BOTH training and validation data because the model is too simple. Here training error is low, so it is not underfitting.\n- C. A well-tuned model has low validation error, not large and rising validation error.\n- D. Irreducible noise is a fixed floor on error; it cannot explain a widening GAP between training and validation error as complexity grows.\n\nThink of a student who memorizes the exact practice questions (low training error) but bombs the real exam with new questions (high validation error) — they overfit to the practice set.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Low training error + high/rising validation error = overfitting (high variance). Cure with simpler models, regularization, or more data. Choose complexity where VALIDATION error is minimized.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Bias–variance trade-off\",\"items\":[\"underfit (high bias)\",\"|good fit\",\"overfit (high variance)\"],\"left\":\"too simple\",\"right\":\"too complex\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In the bias–variance decomposition of expected prediction error, which component cannot be reduced no matter how good the model is?\nA) Bias squared. B) Variance. C) The irreducible noise term $$\\sigma^2_{\\text{noise}}$$. D) The validation error.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which part of prediction error is fixed and unavoidable.\n\nThe expected prediction error decomposes as:\n$$E[(y - \\hat f)^2] = \\text{Bias}^2 + \\text{Variance} + \\sigma^2_{\\text{noise}}$$\n\nThe first two pieces depend on the model: you can lower bias with more flexibility, and lower variance with simpler models or regularization. But the last piece, the irreducible noise $$\\sigma^2_{\\text{noise}}$$, comes from inherent randomness in the data itself and cannot be removed by any model. The answer is C.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Bias squared CAN be reduced by making the model more flexible/complex.\n- B. Variance CAN be reduced by simplifying the model, regularizing, or adding data.\n- D. Validation error is a measured quantity, not a fixed component of the decomposition; it can change as you tune the model.\n\nThink of trying to predict a coin flip with extra information: even a perfect model can't beat the genuine randomness of the coin — that randomness is the irreducible noise.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Error = Bias² + Variance + Irreducible noise. Only the irreducible noise cannot be reduced. The bias–variance trade-off is about balancing the first two.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Irreducible component of error\",\"options\":[\"Bias squared\",\"Variance\",\"Irreducible noise σ²\",\"Validation error\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Error = Bias² + Variance + σ²noise. Bias and variance depend on the model and can be reduced; the irreducible noise is inherent randomness no model can remove.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Why must features often be standardized (e.g., $$z = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma$$) before applying methods like K-means, KNN, or ridge/LASSO?\nA) To make the target variable normally distributed. B) So features measured in large units don't dominate distance or penalty calculations. C) To remove all correlation between features. D) To convert categorical variables into numbers.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks the purpose of standardizing features for distance- and penalty-based methods.\n\nMethods like K-means and KNN use distances, and ridge/LASSO penalize coefficient sizes. If one feature is measured in large units (say, salary in dollars) and another in small units (say, years of experience), the large-unit feature will dominate the distance or penalty just because of its scale, not its importance. Standardizing puts all features on a comparable scale ($$z = (x-\\mu)/\\sigma$$) so each contributes fairly. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Standardizing features does not make the target variable normal; it rescales the inputs, and it is about scale, not normality.\n- C. Standardization changes scale but does NOT remove correlation between features (that is what PCA does).\n- D. Converting categories to numbers is one-hot/ordinal encoding, a separate step from standardization.\n\nThink of comparing people by height in millimeters and weight in tons: without rescaling, the millimeter numbers (huge) would swamp the ton numbers (tiny). Standardizing levels the playing field.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Standardize before distance/penalty-based methods (KNN, K-means, SVM, ridge, LASSO, PCA) so scale differences don't bias the result.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why standardize features\",\"options\":[\"Make the target normal\",\"Stop large-unit features dominating distance/penalty\",\"Remove all correlation\",\"Convert categories to numbers\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Distance- and penalty-based methods let big-unit features dominate purely by scale; standardizing (z=(x-μ)/σ) puts all features on a comparable footing.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In K-means clustering with centroids $$c_1 = (0, 0)$$ and $$c_2 = (4, 2)$$, to which cluster is the point $$x = (3, 0)$$ assigned (using Euclidean distance)?\nA) Cluster 1, because $$d_1 = 3.00 < d_2 = 2.24$$. B) Cluster 2, because $$d_2 = 2.24 < d_1 = 3.00$$. C) Cluster 1, because it is closer to the origin. D) Neither; the point is equidistant.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to assign a point to the nearer centroid in K-means.\n\nK-means assigns each point to the centroid with the smallest Euclidean distance. Compute both distances.\n\nDistance to $$c_1 = (0,0)$$:\n$$d_1 = \\sqrt{(3-0)^2 + (0-0)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 0} = \\sqrt{9} = 3.00$$\n\nDistance to $$c_2 = (4,2)$$:\n$$d_2 = \\sqrt{(3-4)^2 + (0-2)^2} = \\sqrt{(-1)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{1 + 4} = \\sqrt{5} = 2.24$$\n\nCompare: $$d_2 = 2.24 < d_1 = 3.00$$, so the point goes to cluster 2. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. It has the inequality backwards: $$2.24$$ is smaller, so the point is closer to cluster 2, not cluster 1.\n- C. \"Closer to the origin\" sounds intuitive but is wrong here — the actual distances show cluster 2 is nearer.\n- D. The distances differ ($$3.00$$ vs $$2.24$$), so the point is not equidistant.\n\nThink of two ice-cream trucks: you walk to whichever one is the shorter actual distance away, not the one that just \"feels\" closer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: K-means assigns each point to the nearest centroid by Euclidean distance $$\\sqrt{\\sum(x_i - c_i)^2}$$. Compute both, pick the smaller.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"K-means assignment\",\"steps\":[\"d1 to (0,0) = √(9+0) = 3.00\",\"d2 to (4,2) = √(1+4) = 2.24\",\"Compare: 2.24 < 3.00\",\"Assign x to cluster 2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In a principal components analysis (PCA) of daily yield-curve changes, the first principal component typically represents:\nA) Curvature (twist) of the curve. B) A parallel level shift of the whole curve. C) Idiosyncratic measurement noise. D) The slope (steepening/flattening).\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the first principal component of yield-curve moves usually captures.\n\nPCA orders components by how much variance they explain. For yield curves, the first PC — the direction of maximum variance — is a level shift: all maturities moving up or down together in roughly parallel fashion. This typically explains the large majority of yield-curve variance. The second PC is slope (steepening/flattening) and the third is curvature. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Curvature is usually the THIRD principal component, explaining much less variance.\n- C. Idiosyncratic noise is captured by the last, lowest-variance components, not the first.\n- D. Slope is usually the SECOND principal component, not the first.\n\nThink of the three main ways a curve moves: the biggest, most common move is the whole curve shifting up or down together (level) — that's PC1.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Yield-curve PCA → PC1 = level, PC2 = slope, PC3 = curvature; together they explain 95%+ of variance. PC1 (level) dominates.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Yield-curve PCA\",\"root\":\"Variance explained\",\"children\":[\"PC1 = level (parallel shift, dominant)\",\"PC2 = slope (steepen/flatten)\",\"PC3 = curvature (twist)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** What is the correct role of the test data set in machine learning?\nA) To fit (estimate) the model's parameters. B) To tune hyperparameters and compare candidate models. C) To be used once, at the very end, for an unbiased estimate of real-world performance. D) To be reused repeatedly to maximize accuracy.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what the test set is for.\n\nThe three data splits have distinct jobs: the training set fits the model's parameters, the validation set tunes hyperparameters and compares models, and the test set is held back and used only once, at the very end, to give an honest, unbiased estimate of how the model will perform in the real world. The answer is C.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Fitting model parameters is the training set's job, not the test set's.\n- B. Tuning hyperparameters and comparing models is the validation set's job. Using the test set for this contaminates it and makes performance look optimistically better than reality.\n- D. Reusing the test set repeatedly defeats its purpose — each peek leaks information and biases the final estimate upward. It should be touched only once.\n\nThink of the test set like the final exam: you study with practice material (training/validation), but you only sit the real exam once, and peeking at it beforehand would make your \"grade\" meaningless.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Train = fit parameters; Validation = tune hyperparameters/compare models; Test = used ONCE for final unbiased performance. Tuning on the test set is a classic trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Train / validation / test roles\",\"steps\":[\"Train: fit model parameters\",\"Validation: tune hyperparameters, compare models\",\"Test: used ONCE at the end\",\"→ unbiased real-world performance\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** When splitting time-series data into training and test sets, why must you avoid randomly shuffling the observations?\nA) Random shuffling makes the model train faster. B) Shuffling lets future information leak into the training set, inflating performance. C) Time-series data cannot be standardized. D) Shuffling always increases the bias of the model.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why chronological order matters when splitting time-series data.\n\nIn a time series, the goal is to predict the future from the past. If you randomly shuffle and then split, some future observations can land in the training set while their past lands in the test set. The model then effectively \"sees the future\" during training — data leakage — which makes its test performance look far better than it would in real-life forecasting. To prevent this, splits must respect chronology: train on earlier data, test on later data. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Training speed is unrelated to whether you shuffle; this is not the concern.\n- C. Time-series data can be standardized just fine; standardization is unrelated to the shuffling problem.\n- D. Shuffling does not \"always increase bias\"; the specific danger is leakage of future information (optimistic, biased performance estimates), not a general bias increase.\n\nThink of it like studying for an exam using next week's actual answers mixed into your practice set — you'd score great in practice but learn nothing useful for the real, future test.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For time-series data, keep splits chronological (train on past, test on future). Random shuffling causes look-ahead/data leakage.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why not shuffle time series\",\"options\":[\"Shuffling trains faster\",\"Future info leaks into training, inflating performance\",\"Time series can't be standardized\",\"Shuffling always raises bias\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Random shuffling lets future observations land in training while their past sits in test — look-ahead leakage that makes performance look unrealistically good.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** In K-means clustering, the within-cluster sum of squares (inertia) always decreases as the number of clusters $$K$$ increases. Given this, how is a good value of $$K$$ typically chosen?\nA) Pick the largest possible $$K$$ to minimize inertia. B) Pick $$K$$ at the \"elbow\" of the inertia curve, or maximize the silhouette score. C) Pick $$K = 1$$ to keep the model simple. D) Pick $$K$$ equal to the number of features.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to select the number of clusters when inertia keeps falling as $$K$$ grows.\n\nSince inertia (within-cluster sum of squares) always decreases as you add more clusters, you cannot just minimize inertia — that would push you to give every point its own cluster. Instead you look for the \"elbow,\" the point where adding more clusters stops giving a big drop in inertia, or you maximize the silhouette score (which balances how tight clusters are against how separated they are). The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. The largest $$K$$ does minimize inertia (each point in its own cluster gives inertia of zero) but is useless — it creates no meaningful grouping. This is exactly the trap.\n- C. $$K = 1$$ lumps everything into one cluster, which gives the maximum inertia and finds no structure.\n- D. There is no reason for $$K$$ to equal the number of features; clusters and features are unrelated counts.\n\nThink of choosing how many folders to sort photos into: too few and everything is jumbled; one folder per photo is silly; the \"elbow\" is the sweet spot where extra folders stop meaningfully helping.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Inertia always falls as $$K$$ rises, so use the elbow method or the silhouette score (range $$-1$$ to $$1$$) to pick $$K$$. Also run multiple random starts, since results depend on initialization.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Choosing K in K-means\",\"options\":[\"Largest K to minimize inertia\",\"Elbow of inertia curve / max silhouette\",\"K = 1 to stay simple\",\"K = number of features\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Inertia always falls as K rises, so minimizing it just gives one point per cluster; instead pick the elbow or maximize the silhouette score.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Predicting loan default from labeled historical data is:\nA) Unsupervised learning B) Supervised classification C) Reinforcement learning D) Supervised regression\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Labeled data with a categorical outcome (default / no default) ⇒ supervised classification.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which penalty can set coefficients exactly to zero, performing variable selection?\nA) Ridge (L2) B) LASSO (L1) C) Both equally D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The L1 penalty's corners produce exact zeros; ridge only shrinks.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Training error is near zero but validation error is large and rising with model complexity. The model is:\nA) Underfit (high bias) B) Overfit (high variance) C) Well-tuned D) Suffering from multicollinearity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The classic overfitting signature; reduce complexity or regularize.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In a PCA of daily yield-curve changes, the first principal component typically represents:\nA) Curvature twists B) A parallel level shift C) Idiosyncratic noise D) The slope\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Level explains the bulk of yield-curve variance; slope and curvature follow.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Which statement correctly distinguishes ridge regression from LASSO?\nA) Ridge can set coefficients exactly to zero; LASSO only shrinks them B) LASSO can set coefficients exactly to zero, performing variable selection; ridge shrinks but retains all coefficients C) Ridge penalizes the absolute value of coefficients; LASSO penalizes their squares D) Increasing the penalty $$\\lambda$$ reduces bias and increases variance in both methods\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The L1 (absolute value) penalty in LASSO produces corner solutions that zero out coefficients — built-in variable selection. Ridge's L2 (squared) penalty shrinks smoothly toward but never exactly to zero. A and C swap the methods; D reverses the bias-variance effect of $$\\lambda$$ (more penalty = more bias, less variance).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Ridge vs LASSO\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Ridge (L2)\",\"items\":[\"penalizes squared coefs\",\"shrinks toward zero\",\"never exactly zero\",\"keeps all variables\"]},{\"h\":\"LASSO (L1)\",\"items\":[\"penalizes absolute coefs\",\"corner solutions\",\"sets coefs to zero\",\"does variable selection\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A data scientist is building a model to forecast a continuous credit-spread series. She has labeled historical data and wants to (a) tune the regularization parameter $\\lambda$ and (b) report an unbiased estimate of real-world performance. She splits the data into training, validation, and test sets. Which workflow is correct?\nA) Fit candidate models on training, choose $\\lambda$ using the test set, then report test-set performance. B) Fit candidate models on training, choose $\\lambda$ using the validation set, then report performance once on the test set. C) Fit and tune $\\lambda$ on the test set, then report training-set performance. D) Combine all three sets, fit and tune simultaneously, and report in-sample performance.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the train/validation/test discipline.\n\n1. **Training set** — used to fit model parameters for each candidate.\n2. **Validation set** — used to *compare candidates and tune hyperparameters* such as $\\lambda$. This keeps tuning decisions separate from the final performance estimate.\n3. **Test set** — used **exactly once, at the end**, to give an *unbiased* estimate of out-of-sample performance.\n\nB is correct. A commits the headline trap: tuning $\\lambda$ on the test set *contaminates* it, so the reported performance is optimistically biased — selecting on the test set turns it into a second validation set. C reports training-set performance (always optimistic, reflects fit not generalization) and also tunes on test data. D reports in-sample performance after pooling everything, which gives no genuine out-of-sample read at all.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hyperparameters ($\\lambda$, $K$, tree depth) are tuned on *validation* data; the *test* set is touched only once for the final unbiased estimate. Any tuning or model selection that \"peeks\" at the test set inflates reported performance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Train / validation / test workflow\",\"steps\":[\"Fit candidate models on TRAINING\",\"Tune λ / compare on VALIDATION\",\"Report ONCE on TEST\",\"→ unbiased out-of-sample estimate\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A standardized feature matrix is fed to both ridge and LASSO regressions on a dataset with several highly collinear predictors. Which statement correctly contrasts the two methods?\nA) Ridge can drive some coefficients exactly to zero, performing feature selection; LASSO only shrinks. B) LASSO can drive some coefficients exactly to zero (sparse model / feature selection); ridge shrinks all coefficients toward zero but rarely to exactly zero. C) Both ridge and LASSO leave coefficients unbiased relative to OLS. D) As $\\lambda \\to \\infty$, ridge converges to ordinary least squares while LASSO converges to zero coefficients.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts the L2 (ridge) and L1 (LASSO) penalties.\n\n1. **Ridge (L2)** penalizes $\\sum_j \\beta_j^2$: it shrinks all coefficients toward zero (sharing weight among collinear features) but **does not set them exactly to zero**.\n2. **LASSO (L1)** penalizes $\\sum_j |\\beta_j|$: its corner geometry drives some coefficients **exactly to zero**, producing a *sparse* model and performing **feature selection**.\n3. With collinearity, ridge spreads weight across the correlated group, while LASSO tends to pick one and zero the others.\n\nB is correct. A reverses the two methods (it is LASSO, not ridge, that zeroes coefficients). C is false: regularization *deliberately introduces bias* (accepting bias to cut variance) — that is the whole point of the bias–variance trade-off. D reverses the limiting behavior: as $\\lambda \\to \\infty$ *both* shrink coefficients *toward zero*; it is $\\lambda = 0$ that recovers OLS, not $\\lambda \\to \\infty$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: LASSO (L1) = sparse, does feature selection (exact zeros); ridge (L2) = shrinks all, handles collinearity by sharing weight. $\\lambda = 0$ → OLS; $\\lambda \\to \\infty$ → everything shrinks to zero. Regularization trades bias for lower variance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Ridge (L2) vs LASSO (L1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Ridge (L2)\",\"items\":[\"penalty Σβ²\",\"shrinks all toward 0\",\"shares weight on collinear\"]},{\"h\":\"LASSO (L1)\",\"items\":[\"penalty Σ|β|\",\"exact zeros (sparse)\",\"feature selection\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** In K-means clustering with standardized features, two current centroids are $c_1 = (1, 1)$ and $c_2 = (5, 4)$. A point $x = (3, 4)$ must be assigned. Using Euclidean distance, the assignment and reason are:\nA) Cluster 1, because $d_1 = 2.83 < d_2 = 3.61$. B) Cluster 2, because $d_2 = 2.00 < d_1 = 3.61$. C) Cluster 1, because $d_1 = 3.61 < d_2 = 2.00$. D) Cluster 2, because $d_2 = 2.83 < d_1 = 2.00$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We compute the Euclidean distance from $x$ to each centroid and assign to the nearer.\n\n1. Distance to $c_1 = (1,1)$: $$d_1 = \\sqrt{(3-1)^2 + (4-1)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 9} = \\sqrt{13} = 3.606.$$\n2. Distance to $c_2 = (5,4)$: $$d_2 = \\sqrt{(3-5)^2 + (4-4)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 0} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.000.$$\n3. Compare: $d_2 = 2.00 < d_1 = 3.61$, so assign $x$ to **cluster 2**. Centroid 2 then moves toward $x$ at the update step.\n\nB is correct. A computes a wrong $d_1$ (2.83 would be $\\sqrt{8}$, mis-subtracting coordinates) and reaches the wrong cluster. C has the right distances but reads the inequality backwards (assigns to the *farther* centroid). D scrambles both distances. The key steps are squaring coordinate differences correctly and assigning to the *smallest* distance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: K-means assigns each point to the *nearest* centroid by (squared) Euclidean distance, then recomputes centroids as cluster means. Standardize features first so no single large-scale feature dominates the distance. Watch the inequality direction — nearest wins.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"K-means assignment\",\"steps\":[\"d1 to (1,1) = √(4+9) = 3.61\",\"d2 to (5,4) = √(4+0) = 2.00\",\"Compare: 2.00 < 3.61\",\"Assign x to cluster 2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A PCA is run on daily changes of a set of 10 government-bond yields across maturities. The analyst reports the first three principal components explain 96% of total variance. Which interpretation is most consistent with the standard finance result?\nA) The three PCs are economically interpretable as level, slope, and curvature, with the first PC (level) explaining the bulk of variance. B) Each of the three PCs is correlated with the others, which is why they jointly explain so much. C) PCA should be run on the raw (unstandardized) yields, and the components have direct economic meaning by construction. D) The first PC captures idiosyncratic noise; the last PC captures the parallel shift.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question tests the canonical yield-curve PCA result.\n\n1. PCA produces **orthogonal (uncorrelated)** linear combinations ordered by variance explained. The **first PC** is the direction of maximum variance.\n2. For yield-curve changes, the first three PCs are conventionally interpreted as **level, slope, and curvature**, together explaining 95%+ of variance — with the **level** (roughly parallel shift) PC explaining the largest share.\n3. This dimensionality reduction (10 yields → 3 factors) is a finance staple.\n\nA is correct. B is false: principal components are *uncorrelated by construction* (orthogonal) — they are not correlated with each other. C is wrong on two points: features should be **standardized** before PCA, and PCs generally **lack direct economic interpretation** (the level/slope/curvature labels are an interpretive overlay, a known cost of dimension reduction, not a built-in property). D reverses the ordering: the *first* PC (largest variance) is the level/parallel shift, not noise; idiosyncratic noise lands in the *last*, low-variance PCs.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Yield-curve PCA → ~3 PCs (level, slope, curvature) explaining 95%+ of variance, with *level* dominant and first. PCs are orthogonal/uncorrelated and ordered by variance; standardize first, and remember PCs are not inherently economically interpretable.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Yield-curve PCA\",\"root\":\"3 PCs (96% of variance)\",\"children\":[\"PC1 = level (parallel, dominant)\",\"PC2 = slope (steepen/flatten)\",\"PC3 = curvature; PCs are orthogonal\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A flexible neural network achieves near-zero error on the training set, but its error on the validation set is large and *rises* as model complexity increases further. Using the bias–variance decomposition, the diagnosis and remedy are:\nA) Underfitting (high bias); increase model complexity. B) Overfitting (high variance); reduce complexity or apply regularization / early stopping. C) Irreducible noise dominates; nothing can be done. D) The model is well-tuned at the current complexity; deploy it.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests recognition of overfitting via the bias–variance trade-off.\n\n1. Expected prediction error decomposes as $$E[(y-\\hat f)^2] = \\text{Bias}^2 + \\text{Variance} + \\sigma^2_{\\text{noise}}.$$\n2. **Overfitting (high variance)** signature: training error very low (the model memorizes training noise) while **validation error is large and turns upward** as complexity grows. That is exactly what is described.\n3. Remedies: reduce complexity, regularize (ridge/LASSO/dropout), or use early stopping; choose complexity where *validation* error is minimized.\n\nB is correct. A describes the opposite problem — underfitting (high bias) shows poor performance *both* in-sample and out-of-sample, not near-zero training error; increasing complexity would make overfitting worse. C wrongly attributes the rising validation error to irreducible noise — irreducible noise is a constant floor and does not *rise* with complexity. D is contradicted by the rising validation error; the model is past its optimal complexity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Low training error + high (and rising) validation error = overfitting / high variance. Underfitting = high error everywhere. Pick complexity at the validation-error minimum, and use regularization or early stopping to control variance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Bias–variance trade-off\",\"items\":[\"underfit (high bias)\",\"|good fit (min validation error)\",\"overfit (high variance)\"],\"left\":\"too simple\",\"right\":\"too complex\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement best captures a core *philosophical* difference between machine-learning methods and classical econometrics?\nA) Econometrics prioritizes out-of-sample predictive accuracy and tolerates opaque models, while ML insists on unbiased, interpretable parameters. B) ML prioritizes out-of-sample predictive accuracy and validates empirically on held-out data, tolerating biased or opaque models, while classical econometrics emphasizes unbiased, interpretable parameters and hypothesis tests grounded in distributional theory. C) Both ML and econometrics rely primarily on hypothesis tests derived from distributional assumptions and avoid held-out validation. D) ML cannot handle nonlinearities or large feature sets, which is why econometrics is preferred for big data.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the ML-vs-econometrics contrast.\n\n1. **Classical econometrics**: theory-driven, small interpretable models; focus on *unbiased parameter estimates* and *hypothesis testing* grounded in distributional theory.\n2. **Machine learning**: prioritizes *out-of-sample predictive accuracy*; tolerates biased or opaque (\"black-box\") models; handles large feature sets and nonlinearities automatically; validates *empirically* on held-out data rather than via distributional theory.\n\nB is correct. A swaps the two paradigms' priorities entirely. C is false — ML specifically relies on *held-out (empirical) validation* rather than distribution-based hypothesis tests, and econometrics is the one leaning on distributional theory. D is backwards: ML *excels* at nonlinearities and large feature sets; that is one of its main advantages over classical econometrics.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ML = predictive accuracy, empirical (held-out) validation, tolerates bias/opacity, scales to many features and nonlinearities. Econometrics = interpretable parameters, unbiasedness, hypothesis tests from distributional theory. Do not let answer choices swap these.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"ML vs classical econometrics\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Machine learning\",\"items\":[\"out-of-sample prediction\",\"held-out empirical validation\",\"tolerates bias/opacity\",\"many features, nonlinear\"]},{\"h\":\"Econometrics\",\"items\":[\"interpretable, unbiased params\",\"hypothesis tests\",\"distributional theory\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A modeler must encode a categorical feature \"credit rating bucket\" with values {AAA, AA, A, BBB} and a separate nominal feature \"industry sector\" with 11 unordered categories, before feeding a K-means model. Which preparation is most appropriate?\nA) Use ordinal encoding for both features, since all categories can be numbered. B) Use ordinal encoding for the rating bucket (genuinely ordered) and one-hot/dummy encoding for the unordered industry sector; standardize before clustering. C) Use one-hot encoding for the rating bucket and ordinal encoding for industry sector. D) Leave both as text labels; K-means handles strings directly.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests categorical-variable handling and the need to standardize before distance-based clustering.\n\n1. **Ordinal encoding** is appropriate only when categories are *genuinely ordered*. Credit ratings (AAA > AA > A > BBB) have a natural order, so ordinal encoding preserves meaningful information.\n2. **Industry sector** is *nominal* (unordered) — assigning it numbers 1–11 would impose a false ordering and distort Euclidean distances. Use **one-hot/dummy encoding** (one column per category).\n3. K-means is distance-based, so features must be **standardized** first to prevent large-scale features from dominating.\n\nB is correct. A wrongly imposes order on the unordered sector variable (false ordinality distorts distances). C reverses the two: it ordinal-encodes the unordered sector and one-hot-encodes the naturally ordered rating, discarding the rating's order information and mis-ordering sectors. D is false — K-means requires numeric feature vectors; it cannot operate on raw text labels.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Ordinal encoding only for *genuinely ordered* categories; one-hot/dummy encoding for *nominal* (unordered) ones. Always standardize features before distance- or penalty-based methods (K-means, KNN, ridge/LASSO, SVM) so units don't dominate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Encoding categorical features\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Ordinal encoding\",\"items\":[\"genuinely ordered\",\"e.g. rating AAA>AA>A>BBB\",\"preserves order\"]},{\"h\":\"One-hot encoding\",\"items\":[\"nominal / unordered\",\"e.g. industry sector\",\"one column per category\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"215","module":"Book 2: Quantitative Analysis","title":"Machine Learning and Prediction","notes":"# 15. Machine Learning and Prediction\n\nThe supervised-learning toolbox: logistic regression, decision trees and ensembles, KNN, SVM, neural networks, and how to score classifiers (confusion matrix, precision/recall, ROC/AUC). Confusion-matrix arithmetic is the most likely calculation here.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the role of linear and logistic regression as elementary prediction tools and compute predicted probabilities from a logistic model.\n- Describe decision trees, how they are grown, and ensemble methods (bagging, random forests, boosting).\n- Describe K-nearest neighbors (KNN) and support vector machines (SVM) for classification.\n- Describe the structure of neural networks and how they are trained.\n- Apply and interpret performance metrics: confusion matrix, accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC curve, and AUC.\n- Understand the use of reinforcement learning in sequential decision problems.\n\n## Logistic regression\n\nFor a binary outcome, model the **probability** through the sigmoid so predictions stay in $$(0,1)$$:\n\n$$P(Y = 1 \\mid X) = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_k X_k)}}$$\n\nEquivalently the **log-odds** are linear: $$\\ln\\frac{p}{1-p} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\cdots$$. Each $$\\beta_j$$ is the change in log-odds per unit of $$X_j$$. Estimated by maximum likelihood, not OLS. Classify as 1 when $$\\hat p$$ exceeds a chosen threshold (0.5 by default — but lower it when missing a default is costlier than a false alarm).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Plain linear regression on a 0/1 outcome can predict probabilities below 0 or above 1 and forces constant marginal effects — that is exactly why logistic regression exists. GARP tests this contrast.\n\n## Decision trees and ensembles\n\nA **decision tree** recursively splits the feature space with binary questions ($$\\text{leverage} > 3.2$$?), choosing at each node the split that best purifies the child nodes — minimizing **Gini impurity** $$\\sum_k p_k(1-p_k)$$ or **entropy** $$-\\sum_k p_k \\ln p_k$$ (classification), or MSE (regression). Leaves carry predictions. Trees are interpretable and capture nonlinearities/interactions automatically, but deep trees overfit badly (high variance). Control by limiting depth/minimum leaf size or pruning.\n\n**Ensembles** fix the variance problem by combining many models:\n\n- **Bagging:** train trees on bootstrap resamples; average (or majority-vote) predictions.\n- **Random forest:** bagging plus a random subset of features at each split — decorrelates the trees, cutting ensemble variance further.\n- **Boosting (e.g., gradient boosting):** build shallow trees **sequentially**, each fitted to the previous ensemble's errors; reduces bias as well as variance but can overfit if run too long.\n\n## K-nearest neighbors\n\nTo classify a point: find the $$K$$ closest training points (standardized features, usually Euclidean distance) and take a majority vote (or average, for regression). No training phase (\"lazy\" learner). Small $$K$$ ⇒ flexible, high variance; large $$K$$ ⇒ smooth, high bias. Costly at prediction time; struggles in high dimensions.\n\n## Support vector machines\n\nSVM finds the separating boundary that **maximizes the margin** — the distance to the nearest training points (the *support vectors*, which alone determine the boundary). Soft-margin SVM tolerates some misclassification via a cost parameter; the **kernel trick** (polynomial, radial) builds nonlinear boundaries by implicitly mapping features to higher dimensions. Effective in high-dimensional problems; output scores are not natural probabilities.\n\n## Neural networks\n\nLayers of neurons: input layer (features) → hidden layer(s) → output. Each neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias, passed through a nonlinear **activation function** (sigmoid, tanh, ReLU $$= \\max(0, z)$$):\n\n$$a = g(w^\\top x + b)$$\n\nWith enough hidden units, networks approximate almost any function. Training: minimize a loss (MSE, cross-entropy) by **gradient descent**, with gradients computed by **backpropagation**; the learning rate sets the step size. Many weights ⇒ strong overfitting risk: hold-out validation, early stopping, regularization, dropout. Powerful but opaque — a model-governance issue in regulated finance.\n\n## Performance measurement for classifiers\n\n**Confusion matrix** (positive = the event of interest, e.g., default):\n\n| | Predicted + | Predicted − |\n|---|---|---|\n| Actual + | TP | FN |\n| Actual − | FP | TN |\n\n$$\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{TP + TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN} \\qquad \\text{Precision} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FP}$$\n\n$$\\text{Recall (sensitivity, TPR)} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FN} \\qquad F_1 = \\frac{2 \\cdot \\text{Precision} \\cdot \\text{Recall}}{\\text{Precision} + \\text{Recall}}$$\n\n**ROC curve:** true-positive rate vs. false-positive rate as the classification threshold sweeps from 1 to 0. **AUC** (area under ROC): probability a random positive is scored above a random negative — 1.0 perfect, 0.5 coin flip.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Accuracy is nearly useless with imbalanced classes: if 2% of loans default, \"predict no default always\" scores 98% accuracy with zero recall. For rare-event risk models, judge precision/recall, F1, and AUC — and remember lowering the threshold raises recall at the cost of precision.\n\n## Reinforcement learning\n\nAn **agent** observes a state, takes an action, receives a reward, and learns a **policy** maximizing cumulative (discounted) reward through trial and error — balancing **exploration** (trying new actions) against **exploitation** (using what works). Q-learning estimates the value of state–action pairs. Finance applications: optimal trade execution (minimizing market impact), dynamic hedging, market making.\n\n### Worked example: logistic default probability\n\nA fitted PD model: $$\\ln\\frac{p}{1-p} = -4 + 0.8 \\times \\text{leverage}$$. For leverage = 5:\n\n$$z = -4 + 0.8 \\times 5 = 0, \\qquad p = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{0}} = 0.50$$\n\nFor leverage = 3: $$z = -1.6$$, $$p = \\frac{1}{1+e^{1.6}} = \\frac{1}{1+4.953} = 0.168$$. With a 0.25 threshold, the first borrower is classified as a default risk, the second is not.\n\n### Worked example: confusion-matrix metrics\n\nA default model on 1,000 loans (50 actual defaults): TP = 30, FN = 20, FP = 70, TN = 880.\n\n$$\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{30 + 880}{1000} = 91.0\\%$$\n\n$$\\text{Precision} = \\frac{30}{30 + 70} = 30.0\\% \\qquad \\text{Recall} = \\frac{30}{30 + 20} = 60.0\\%$$\n\n$$F_1 = \\frac{2 \\times 0.30 \\times 0.60}{0.30 + 0.60} = \\frac{0.36}{0.90} = 0.40$$\n\nAccuracy looks excellent, yet the model misses 40% of defaults and only 3 in 10 alarms are real — the imbalanced-data lesson in numbers.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Give the logistic regression probability and log-odds forms.\n> **A:** $$P(Y=1\\mid X) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + e^{-(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\cdots)}}$$; equivalently $$\\ln\\dfrac{p}{1-p} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\cdots$$. Estimated by maximum likelihood, not OLS.\n\n**Q2.** Why use logistic regression instead of linear regression for a 0/1 outcome?\n> **A:** Plain linear regression can predict probabilities below 0 or above 1 and forces constant marginal effects; the sigmoid keeps predictions in $$(0,1)$$ with varying marginal effects.\n\n**Q3.** How does a decision tree choose splits?\n> **A:** Recursively splits the feature space with binary questions, choosing the split that best purifies child nodes — minimizing Gini impurity $$\\sum_k p_k(1-p_k)$$ or entropy $$-\\sum_k p_k\\ln p_k$$ (classification), or MSE (regression).\n\n**Q4.** Distinguish bagging, random forests, and boosting.\n> **A:** Bagging: train trees on bootstrap resamples, average/vote. Random forest: bagging plus a random feature subset at each split (decorrelates trees). Boosting: build shallow trees sequentially, each fitting the previous ensemble's errors (reduces bias too, can overfit if run too long).\n\n**Q5.** Describe K-nearest neighbors and the effect of $$K$$.\n> **A:** Classify by majority vote of the $$K$$ closest training points (standardized, Euclidean); \"lazy\" — no training phase. Small $$K$$ = flexible/high variance; large $$K$$ = smooth/high bias. Costly at prediction; struggles in high dimensions.\n\n**Q6.** What does an SVM optimize, and what is the kernel trick?\n> **A:** SVM maximizes the margin to the nearest training points (support vectors). Soft-margin tolerates misclassification via a cost parameter; the kernel trick builds nonlinear boundaries by implicitly mapping to higher dimensions.\n\n**Q7.** Describe a neural network neuron and how networks are trained.\n> **A:** Each neuron computes $$a = g(w^\\top x + b)$$ with a nonlinear activation (sigmoid, tanh, ReLU $$=\\max(0,z)$$). Trained by minimizing a loss via gradient descent, with gradients from backpropagation; the learning rate sets step size.\n\n**Q8.** Lay out the confusion matrix and define accuracy and precision.\n> **A:** Rows = actual ($$+$$: TP/FN; $$-$$: FP/TN). $$\\text{Accuracy} = \\dfrac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}$$; $$\\text{Precision} = \\dfrac{TP}{TP+FP}$$.\n\n**Q9.** Define recall and the F1 score.\n> **A:** $$\\text{Recall} = \\dfrac{TP}{TP+FN}$$; $$F_1 = \\dfrac{2\\cdot\\text{Precision}\\cdot\\text{Recall}}{\\text{Precision}+\\text{Recall}}$$.\n\n**Q10.** What are the ROC curve and AUC?\n> **A:** ROC: true-positive rate vs. false-positive rate as the threshold sweeps. AUC: probability a random positive is scored above a random negative — 1.0 perfect, 0.5 coin flip.\n\n**Q11.** Why is accuracy misleading with imbalanced classes?\n> **A:** If 2% default, \"always predict no default\" scores 98% accuracy with zero recall. Use precision/recall, F1, and AUC; lowering the threshold raises recall at the cost of precision.\n\n**Q12.** Logistic PD model: $$\\ln\\dfrac{p}{1-p} = -4 + 0.8\\times\\text{leverage}$$. Find $$p$$ at leverage = 5.\n> **A:** $$z = -4 + 0.8(5) = 0$$; $$p = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^0} = 0.50$$.\n\n**Q13.** Model: TP = 30, FN = 20, FP = 70, TN = 880. Compute precision, recall, and F1.\n> **A:** Precision $$= 30/100 = 30\\%$$; recall $$= 30/50 = 60\\%$$; $$F_1 = \\dfrac{2(0.30)(0.60)}{0.90} = 0.40$$.\n\n**Q14.** Describe reinforcement learning's core elements.\n> **A:** An agent observes a state, takes an action, receives a reward, and learns a policy maximizing cumulative discounted reward — balancing exploration vs. exploitation. Q-learning values state–action pairs. Uses: trade execution, dynamic hedging, market making.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A logistic regression model gives a borrower log-odds of $$z = 1.0$$. The predicted probability of default is closest to:\nA) 50.0% B) 73.1% C) 26.9% D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to turn a log-odds value into a probability using the logistic (sigmoid) function.\n\nThe sigmoid formula converts the linear score $$z$$ into a probability between 0 and 1:\n$$p = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}}$$\n\nPlug in $$z = 1.0$$:\n$$p = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-1}} = \\frac{1}{1 + 0.3679} = \\frac{1}{1.3679} = 0.731$$\n\nSo the probability is about 73.1%, and the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. 50.0% corresponds to $$z = 0$$ (when $$e^0 = 1$$, $$p = 1/2$$). Here $$z = 1.0$$, which is above zero, so $$p$$ must be above 50%.\n- C. 26.9% is $$1 - 0.731$$, i.e., the probability of NOT defaulting (or using $$e^{+1}$$ instead of $$e^{-1}$$ in the denominator). It is the complement, not the answer asked for.\n- D. 100% would require $$z \\to +\\infty$$. A finite $$z = 1$$ can never reach 1.\n\nThink of the sigmoid as an S-shaped squashing function: it takes any score and gently squeezes it into a probability between 0 and 1, with $$z = 0$$ mapping to the middle (50%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$p = 1/(1+e^{-z})$$. Memorize landmarks: $$z=0 \\Rightarrow 50\\%$$, positive $$z \\Rightarrow$$ above 50%, negative $$z \\Rightarrow$$ below 50%.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Sigmoid landmarks\",\"items\":[\"z<0 → p<50%\",\"|z=0 → p=50%\",\"z>0 → p>50%\"],\"left\":\"log-odds negative\",\"right\":\"log-odds positive\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A fitted logistic model is $$\\ln\\frac{p}{1-p} = -4 + 0.8 \\times \\text{leverage}$$. For a borrower with leverage = 5, the predicted default probability is:\nA) 0.50 B) 0.27 C) 0.73 D) 0.12\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the default probability from a logistic model at a given leverage.\n\nStep 1 — compute the log-odds score $$z$$:\n$$z = -4 + 0.8 \\times 5 = -4 + 4 = 0$$\n\nStep 2 — convert to a probability with the sigmoid:\n$$p = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}} = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{0}} = \\frac{1}{1 + 1} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.50$$\n\nSo the answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B and C. 0.27 and 0.73 correspond to $$z = -1$$ and $$z = +1$$ respectively — they'd come from arithmetic slips in computing $$z$$ (for example using leverage 3.75 or 6.25).\n- D. 0.12 corresponds to a clearly negative $$z$$ (around $$-2$$), which would result from a larger intercept or smaller leverage.\n\nThe key insight: when $$z$$ works out to exactly 0, the probability is exactly one-half, regardless of the model's coefficients.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two steps every time — (1) compute $$z = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X$$, (2) apply $$p = 1/(1+e^{-z})$$. A score of $$z=0$$ always gives $$p = 0.50$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Why is plain linear regression generally inappropriate for modeling a binary (0/1) outcome, which is why logistic regression is preferred?\nA) Linear regression cannot include more than one predictor. B) Linear regression can predict probabilities below 0 or above 1 and forces constant marginal effects. C) Linear regression requires the outcome to be normally distributed. D) Logistic regression is estimated by OLS, which is more accurate.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the core reason logistic regression exists for binary outcomes.\n\nA probability must lie between 0 and 1. Plain linear regression fits a straight line that can keep rising or falling without limit, so it can produce predicted \"probabilities\" below 0 or above 1 — which are meaningless. It also forces a constant marginal effect (the same change in probability per unit of $$X$$ everywhere), which is unrealistic near 0 and 1. Logistic regression's S-shaped sigmoid keeps predictions inside (0,1) and lets the marginal effect taper off at the extremes. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Linear regression can absolutely include many predictors; that is not the issue.\n- C. Linear regression does not require the OUTCOME to be normal (it is about the errors), and that is not the reason logistic models are used for 0/1 outcomes.\n- D. Logistic regression is estimated by maximum likelihood, NOT OLS — so this statement is factually wrong.\n\nThink of trying to fit a straight ramp to something that must stay between the floor and the ceiling: the ramp eventually shoots through both. The S-curve bends to stay inside.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use logistic (not linear) regression for binary outcomes — it keeps $$\\hat p \\in (0,1)$$ and is fit by maximum likelihood, not OLS.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Linear vs logistic for 0/1\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Linear (OLS)\",\"items\":[\"straight line, unbounded\",\"can predict p<0 or p>1\",\"constant marginal effect\"]},{\"h\":\"Logistic\",\"items\":[\"S-shaped sigmoid\",\"keeps p in (0,1)\",\"fit by maximum likelihood\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A default model evaluated on 1,000 loans produces TP = 30, FN = 20, FP = 70, TN = 880. Its recall (sensitivity) is:\nA) 30% B) 60% C) 91% D) 43%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for recall from a confusion matrix.\n\nRecall (also called sensitivity or the true-positive rate) measures the fraction of actual positives that the model correctly caught:\n$$\\text{Recall} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FN}$$\n\nHere the actual positives (true defaults) are $$TP + FN = 30 + 20 = 50$$. Of these, 30 were correctly flagged:\n$$\\text{Recall} = \\frac{30}{30 + 20} = \\frac{30}{50} = 0.60 = 60\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. 30% is the precision: $$TP/(TP+FP) = 30/(30+70) = 30/100$$. Precision answers a different question (of the alarms raised, how many were real).\n- C. 91% is the accuracy: $$(TP+TN)/\\text{total} = (30+880)/1000$$. Accuracy mixes in all the correct negatives.\n- D. 43% does not match any standard metric here; it is a distractor.\n\nRecall asks: \"Of all the loans that truly defaulted, what share did we catch?\" Here we caught 30 of the 50, so 60%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Recall $$= TP/(TP+FN)$$ (caught actual positives). Precision $$= TP/(TP+FP)$$ (correct among flagged). Don't swap their denominators.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Recall vs precision\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Recall\",\"items\":[\"TP/(TP+FN)\",\"of ACTUAL positives caught\",\"30/50 = 60%\"]},{\"h\":\"Precision\",\"items\":[\"TP/(TP+FP)\",\"of FLAGGED that were real\",\"30/100 = 30%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Using the same model (TP = 30, FN = 20, FP = 70, TN = 880), what is the precision?\nA) 30% B) 60% C) 91% D) 70%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for precision from the confusion matrix.\n\nPrecision measures, among all the cases the model flagged as positive, how many were actually positive:\n$$\\text{Precision} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FP}$$\n\nThe model flagged $$TP + FP = 30 + 70 = 100$$ loans as defaults. Of those, 30 were genuine:\n$$\\text{Precision} = \\frac{30}{30 + 70} = \\frac{30}{100} = 0.30 = 30\\%$$\n\nSo the answer is A.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B. 60% is the recall ($$TP/(TP+FN) = 30/50$$) — that uses the false NEGATIVES in the denominator, not false positives.\n- C. 91% is the accuracy, which mixes in all the true negatives.\n- D. 70% is just the false-positive count expressed as a percent of the flagged loans, i.e., $$1 - \\text{precision}$$, not precision itself.\n\nPrecision asks: \"Of all the alarms we rang, how many were real?\" We rang 100 alarms and only 30 were genuine defaults, so 30%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Precision uses FALSE POSITIVES in the denominator ($$TP/(TP+FP)$$); recall uses FALSE NEGATIVES ($$TP/(TP+FN)$$). Keep the two denominators straight.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Why is accuracy a poor metric for evaluating a default model when only 2% of loans default?\nA) Accuracy cannot be computed for imbalanced data. B) A model that predicts \"no default\" for everyone scores 98% accuracy with zero recall. C) Accuracy always equals the AUC. D) Accuracy overweights the false positives.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why accuracy misleads when one class is rare.\n\nWhen defaults are rare (2%), a lazy model that simply predicts \"no default\" for every loan is correct on the 98% that don't default — giving 98% accuracy. Yet it catches zero actual defaults (recall = 0%), so it is useless for the very thing it's meant to detect. High accuracy hides this complete failure. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Accuracy can still be computed for imbalanced data; the problem is that the number is misleading, not that it's undefined.\n- C. Accuracy and AUC are different metrics and are generally not equal; AUC measures ranking ability across thresholds.\n- D. Accuracy does not specifically overweight false positives — it weights all correct predictions equally, which is exactly why the huge majority class dominates.\n\nImagine a fire alarm that never goes off in a building that rarely has fires: it is \"right\" almost every day, but worthless when a fire actually starts.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With imbalanced classes, ignore raw accuracy. Judge models on precision, recall, F1, and AUC. \"Always predict the majority\" can fake very high accuracy.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why accuracy misleads (2% default)\",\"options\":[\"Accuracy can't be computed\",\"Predict 'no default' for all → 98% accuracy, 0% recall\",\"Accuracy always equals AUC\",\"Accuracy overweights false positives\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"With rare events, a lazy 'always negative' model scores 98% accuracy yet catches zero defaults — high accuracy hides total failure on the class that matters.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A random forest reduces overfitting compared with a single deep decision tree primarily by:\nA) Growing each individual tree deeper. B) Averaging many decorrelated trees grown on bootstrap samples with random feature subsets. C) Fitting each tree sequentially to the previous tree's residuals. D) Removing the need for a validation set.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how a random forest controls the high variance of a single deep tree.\n\nA single deep tree overfits (high variance). A random forest builds many trees, each on a different bootstrap resample of the data AND using only a random subset of features at each split. The feature randomness decorrelates the trees, so when you average their predictions (or take a majority vote), the random errors cancel out and overall variance drops. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Growing trees deeper makes individual trees overfit MORE, not less — the opposite of the goal.\n- C. Fitting trees sequentially to the previous model's residuals describes BOOSTING, not a random forest. Random forests build trees independently (in parallel).\n- D. Random forests do not eliminate the need for validation; you still validate to tune settings and estimate performance.\n\nThink of asking many slightly different experts who each saw a different slice of the evidence, then averaging their opinions — the crowd's average is steadier than any single expert.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Random forest = bagging + random feature subsets → decorrelated trees → averaging cuts variance. Boosting is the sequential, residual-fitting method (reduces bias too).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Random forest vs boosting\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Random forest\",\"items\":[\"parallel, independent trees\",\"bootstrap + random features\",\"averages → cuts variance\"]},{\"h\":\"Boosting\",\"items\":[\"sequential trees\",\"each fits prior residuals\",\"cuts bias, can overfit\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A support vector machine (SVM) classifier chooses its decision boundary by:\nA) Minimizing the within-cluster sum of squares. B) Maximizing the margin — the distance from the boundary to the nearest training points (support vectors). C) Averaging the predictions of many trees. D) Computing the probability of each class via a sigmoid.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the central idea behind SVM classification.\n\nAn SVM looks for the separating boundary that is as far as possible from the closest data points of each class — it maximizes the margin. The few points sitting right at the edge of that margin are the support vectors, and they alone determine where the boundary sits. A wider margin tends to generalize better. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Minimizing within-cluster sum of squares is the objective of K-means clustering, not SVM.\n- C. Averaging predictions of many trees describes random forests / bagging, not SVM.\n- D. Producing class probabilities via a sigmoid describes logistic regression; SVM output scores are not natural probabilities.\n\nThink of drawing the widest possible \"no man's land\" road between two neighborhoods so each side has the most breathing room — the houses right on the edge define how wide the road can be.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SVM = maximum-margin classifier; only the support vectors matter. The kernel trick adds nonlinear boundaries. SVM scores are not true probabilities.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"How an SVM picks its boundary\",\"options\":[\"Minimize within-cluster sum of squares\",\"Maximize the margin to nearest points (support vectors)\",\"Average many trees\",\"Compute class probability via sigmoid\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"SVM finds the boundary with the widest margin from the closest points of each class; those support vectors alone determine its position.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** In a neural network, the role of a nonlinear activation function (such as ReLU, $$\\max(0, z)$$) is to:\nA) Reduce the number of weights that must be trained. B) Let the network model nonlinear relationships rather than just a linear combination of inputs. C) Guarantee the network cannot overfit. D) Replace the need for backpropagation.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why neural networks apply a nonlinear activation function at each neuron.\n\nEach neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs, $$w^\\top x + b$$, which is linear. If you stacked only linear operations, the whole network would collapse into one big linear function — no matter how many layers. The nonlinear activation $$g(\\cdot)$$ (like ReLU, sigmoid, or tanh) bends the output so that, with enough hidden units, the network can approximate almost any function, including highly nonlinear ones. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. Activation functions do not reduce the number of weights; the weight count comes from the network's architecture.\n- C. Activations do NOT prevent overfitting — neural nets with many weights overfit easily; you fight that with validation, early stopping, regularization, and dropout.\n- D. Backpropagation is still needed to compute gradients for training; the activation function does not replace it (in fact, its derivative is used during backpropagation).\n\nThink of activations as the \"bends\" in a flexible wire: without bends, the wire is just a straight line; the bends let it trace any shape.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Nonlinear activations are what give neural nets their power to model nonlinearity. Without them, stacked layers reduce to a single linear map.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Role of a nonlinear activation\",\"options\":[\"Reduce the number of weights\",\"Let the network model nonlinear relationships\",\"Guarantee no overfitting\",\"Replace backpropagation\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Without nonlinearity, stacked linear layers collapse to one linear map; activations like ReLU let the network approximate nonlinear functions.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** An AUC (area under the ROC curve) of 0.5 indicates a classifier that:\nA) Is perfect. B) Is no better than random ranking. C) Has exactly 50% accuracy. D) Detects exactly half of the positive cases.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what an AUC of 0.5 means.\n\nAUC is the probability that the model scores a randomly chosen positive case higher than a randomly chosen negative case. A perfect model has AUC = 1.0; a model that ranks no better than a coin flip has AUC = 0.5. So 0.5 means the classifier's ranking ability is no better than random. The answer is B.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A. A perfect classifier has AUC = 1.0, not 0.5.\n- C. AUC is about ranking quality across all thresholds, not accuracy at one threshold. AUC = 0.5 does not mean 50% accuracy (with imbalanced data accuracy could even be very high while AUC = 0.5).\n- D. \"Half of positives detected\" describes a recall of 50% at a specific threshold — a different concept entirely from AUC.\n\nThink of AUC as how well the model sorts good from bad: 0.5 is like shuffling a deck blindly — it puts positives above negatives only by chance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: AUC = P(random positive ranked above random negative). 1.0 = perfect, 0.5 = random coin flip. It is a ranking measure, not an accuracy or recall figure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"AUC interpretation\",\"items\":[\"0.5 random (coin flip)\",\"|0.7-0.9 good discrimination\",\"1.0 perfect ranking\"],\"left\":\"no skill\",\"right\":\"perfect\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A logistic model gives log-odds $$z = 1.0$$ for a borrower. The predicted default probability is closest to:\nA) 50.0% B) 73.1% C) 26.9% D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$p = 1/(1 + e^{-1}) = 1/1.368 = 0.731$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A random forest reduces overfitting relative to a single deep tree primarily by:\nA) Increasing each tree's depth B) Averaging many decorrelated trees grown on bootstrap samples C) Fitting each tree to the previous tree's residuals D) Removing the need for validation data\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Bagging + random feature subsets ⇒ variance reduction through decorrelated averaging. (C describes boosting.)\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A classifier has TP = 40, FP = 10, FN = 60, TN = 890. Its recall is:\nA) 80% B) 40% C) 93% D) 4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Recall $$= 40/(40+60) = 40\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** An AUC of 0.5 indicates:\nA) A perfect classifier B) A classifier no better than random ranking C) 50% accuracy D) Half the positives are detected\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** AUC 0.5 means random positive/negative ranking; it is not an accuracy figure.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A default-prediction model produces TP = 40, FP = 10, FN = 20, TN = 130 on a test set. The model's F1 score is closest to:\nA) 0.67 B) 0.73 C) 0.80 D) 0.85 --\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\text{Precision} = \\frac{40}{40+10} = 0.80 \\qquad \\text{Recall} = \\frac{40}{40+20} = 0.667$$\n\n$$F_1 = \\frac{2 \\times 0.80 \\times 0.667}{0.80 + 0.667} = \\frac{1.067}{1.467} = 0.73$$\n\nDistractor C is precision alone, A is recall alone, and D is accuracy ($$170/200 = 0.85$$) — which looks flattering only because the classes are imbalanced.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Confusion matrix\",\"x\":\"actual positive →\",\"y\":\"predicted positive →\",\"q\":[\"TN=130\",\"FN=20\",\"FP=10\",\"TP=40\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A fitted logistic PD model has log-odds $$\\ln\\frac{p}{1-p} = -3.0 + 0.5 \\times \\text{leverage} + 0.02 \\times \\text{age}.$$ For a borrower with leverage $= 4$ and age $= 50$, the predicted default probability is closest to:\nA) 0.50 B) 0.55 C) 0.45 D) 0.27\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We compute the linear predictor (log-odds), then apply the sigmoid.\n\n1. Linear predictor: $$z = -3.0 + 0.5(4) + 0.02(50) = -3.0 + 2.0 + 1.0 = 0.0.$$\n2. Sigmoid: $$p = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}} = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{0}} = \\frac{1}{1 + 1} = 0.50.$$\n\nA is correct. Distractor D (0.27) results from a sign error in the exponent, e.g., computing $1/(1+e^{1}) = 1/(1+2.718) = 0.269$ — using $e^{+z}$ wrongly with a nonzero $z$, or mishandling the $-z$. Distractor B (0.55) and C (0.45) arise from arithmetic slips in the linear predictor (e.g., $z = +0.2$ or $z = -0.2$ from miscomputing $0.02 \\times 50$ as $0.2$ or dropping a term), then applying the sigmoid. Here every term lands exactly so that $z = 0$ and $p = 0.5$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Logistic regression: build $z$ from the linear predictor, then $p = 1/(1+e^{-z})$. Note $z = 0 \\Rightarrow p = 0.5$, $z > 0 \\Rightarrow p > 0.5$, $z < 0 \\Rightarrow p < 0.5$. The most common error is a sign slip in the exponent — keep the $-z$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Logistic prediction\",\"steps\":[\"z = -3.0 + 0.5(4) + 0.02(50)\",\"z = -3.0 + 2.0 + 1.0 = 0\",\"p = 1/(1+e⁰) = 1/2\",\"p = 0.50\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A default-detection model is evaluated on 2,000 loans of which 100 actually defaulted. The confusion matrix shows TP $= 60$, FN $= 40$, FP $= 240$, TN $= 1{,}660$. The model's precision and recall are closest to:\nA) Precision $= 60\\%$, Recall $= 20\\%$. B) Precision $= 20\\%$, Recall $= 60\\%$. C) Precision $= 60\\%$, Recall $= 60\\%$. D) Precision $= 25\\%$, Recall $= 40\\%$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We apply the precision and recall definitions to the confusion matrix.\n\n1. **Precision** = TP / (TP + FP) — of the loans flagged as defaults, how many truly defaulted: $$\\text{Precision} = \\frac{60}{60 + 240} = \\frac{60}{300} = 0.20 = 20\\%.$$\n2. **Recall** = TP / (TP + FN) — of the actual defaults, how many were caught: $$\\text{Recall} = \\frac{60}{60 + 40} = \\frac{60}{100} = 0.60 = 60\\%.$$\n\nB is correct. Distractor A swaps the two formulas (computes precision as if it were recall and vice versa) — the single most common confusion-matrix mistake. Distractor C uses TP+FN in both denominators (treating precision like recall). Distractor D uses wrong denominators entirely (e.g., dividing by total predicted or total actual incorrectly). Verify the labels: precision's denominator is everything *predicted positive* (TP+FP); recall's denominator is everything *actually positive* (TP+FN).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Precision = TP/(TP+FP) (denominator = predicted positives). Recall = TP/(TP+FN) (denominator = actual positives). Mnemonic: re**C**all uses the **C**olumn of actuals (TP+FN). Swapping the two is the classic trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Precision vs recall\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Precision\",\"items\":[\"TP/(TP+FP)\",\"of FLAGGED, how many real\",\"60/300 = 20%\"]},{\"h\":\"Recall\",\"items\":[\"TP/(TP+FN)\",\"of ACTUAL defaults caught\",\"60/100 = 60%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Using the same model from Q2 (TP $= 60$, FN $= 40$, FP $= 240$, TN $= 1{,}660$ on 2,000 loans), an analyst computes accuracy and the $F_1$ score. Which pair is correct, and what is the key lesson?\nA) Accuracy $= 86\\%$, $F_1 = 0.30$; high accuracy masks weak default detection because classes are imbalanced. B) Accuracy $= 86\\%$, $F_1 = 0.60$; the model is excellent on every metric. C) Accuracy $= 14\\%$, $F_1 = 0.30$; the model is essentially random. D) Accuracy $= 95\\%$, $F_1 = 0.40$; precision and recall are both high.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We compute accuracy and $F_1$, then interpret in light of class imbalance.\n\n1. **Accuracy** = (TP + TN) / total: $$\\frac{60 + 1{,}660}{2{,}000} = \\frac{1{,}720}{2{,}000} = 0.86 = 86\\%.$$\n2. From Q2: Precision $= 0.20$, Recall $= 0.60$.\n3. **$F_1$** (harmonic mean of precision and recall): $$F_1 = \\frac{2 \\times \\text{Precision} \\times \\text{Recall}}{\\text{Precision} + \\text{Recall}} = \\frac{2 \\times 0.20 \\times 0.60}{0.20 + 0.60} = \\frac{0.24}{0.80} = 0.30.$$\n4. **Lesson:** only 100 of 2,000 loans default (5%), so accuracy is dominated by the easy non-default majority. The 86% accuracy looks strong, but precision is just 20% and $F_1$ is a weak 0.30 — accuracy is misleading with imbalanced classes.\n\nA is correct. B uses the wrong $F_1$ (0.60 ignores the low precision; $F_1$ is pulled down by the smaller of precision/recall). C miscomputes accuracy (14% would be the error rate's complement mistake / using FP+FN). D inflates both accuracy and the metrics inconsistently. The harmonic mean penalizes the low precision, which is the whole point.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $F_1 = 2PR/(P+R)$ is the *harmonic* mean — it is dragged toward the *smaller* of precision and recall. With rare events, accuracy is nearly useless (predict \"never default\" scores ~95%+ with zero recall); judge by precision, recall, $F_1$, and AUC.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Accuracy & F1 (imbalanced)\",\"steps\":[\"Accuracy = (60+1660)/2000 = 86%\",\"F1 = 2·0.2·0.6/(0.2+0.6) = 0.30\",\"Only 5% default → accuracy inflated\",\"Lesson: high accuracy hides weak detection\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A risk team compares a single deep decision tree, a random forest, and a gradient-boosting model. Which statement correctly describes how the ensembles differ from the single tree?\nA) Random forests build trees sequentially, each fitted to the previous tree's residuals, reducing bias. B) Random forests average many trees grown on bootstrap samples using a random subset of features at each split, decorrelating the trees and reducing variance; boosting instead builds shallow trees sequentially, each fitted to the prior ensemble's errors, reducing bias (and can overfit if run too long). C) Boosting averages independent bootstrap trees in parallel, while random forests build trees sequentially. D) A single deep tree has lower variance than a random forest, which is why forests need pruning.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the mechanics of bagging/random forests versus boosting.\n\n1. **Random forest** = bagging (bootstrap-sample trees, then average/majority-vote) **plus** a random subset of features considered at each split. The feature randomization *decorrelates* the trees, cutting ensemble **variance** further than plain bagging.\n2. **Boosting** (e.g., gradient boosting) builds **shallow trees sequentially**, each fitted to the *previous ensemble's errors/residuals*. It reduces **bias** as well as variance, but **can overfit** if run too long.\n3. A single deep tree has *high* variance (it overfits); ensembles exist to fix that.\n\nB is correct. A describes *boosting*, not random forests (sequential, residual-fitting). C swaps the two methods (boosting is sequential, random forests are parallel/bootstrap-averaged). D is backwards: a single deep tree has *high* variance, and it is the deep single tree that needs pruning — the forest reduces variance by averaging.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bagging/random forest = *parallel*, bootstrap + random features, averages decorrelated trees, cuts *variance*. Boosting = *sequential*, each tree fixes prior errors, cuts *bias* but can overfit. Mixing up \"sequential vs. parallel\" is the classic trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Random forest vs boosting\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Random forest\",\"items\":[\"parallel, bootstrap\",\"random feature subset/split\",\"averages → cuts variance\"]},{\"h\":\"Boosting\",\"items\":[\"sequential shallow trees\",\"fits prior ensemble errors\",\"cuts bias, can overfit\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A bank is choosing a classifier threshold for a default model. Currently the threshold is 0.50. Management wants to catch a larger share of true defaults, accepting more false alarms. Lowering the threshold to 0.30 will:\nA) Increase recall and tend to decrease precision. B) Increase precision and decrease recall. C) Increase both precision and recall equally. D) Have no effect on recall because recall depends only on the model, not the threshold.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question tests the precision–recall trade-off as the classification threshold changes.\n\n1. Lowering the threshold (0.50 → 0.30) makes the model label *more* borrowers as \"default\" (positive).\n2. More positives caught means more true positives are flagged ⇒ **recall (TP/(TP+FN)) rises** — fewer defaults are missed.\n3. But casting a wider net also catches more non-defaulters wrongly ⇒ more false positives ⇒ **precision (TP/(TP+FP)) tends to fall**.\n\nA is correct. B reverses the trade-off. C is impossible: precision and recall generally move in *opposite* directions as the threshold shifts — that is the entire point of the trade-off. D is false — recall depends directly on the threshold; lowering it increases recall by definition.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Lower threshold → more positives flagged → higher recall, lower precision (and vice versa). This is why the ROC curve sweeps the threshold from 1 to 0. When missing a default is costlier than a false alarm, lower the threshold to boost recall.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Lowering threshold 0.50 → 0.30\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Recall\",\"items\":[\"more positives flagged\",\"fewer defaults missed\",\"RISES\"]},{\"h\":\"Precision\",\"items\":[\"more false alarms\",\"wider net\",\"tends to FALL\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A classifier produces continuous scores. Model A has AUC $= 0.50$; Model B has AUC $= 0.85$. Which interpretation is correct?\nA) Model A has 50% accuracy; Model B has 85% accuracy. B) Model A ranks a random positive above a random negative no better than chance; Model B does so 85% of the time, indicating good discrimination independent of any single threshold. C) AUC measures recall at the 0.5 threshold, so Model B detects 85% of positives. D) AUC of 0.50 is the best possible, and 0.85 indicates a poorly calibrated model.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the meaning of AUC (area under the ROC curve).\n\n1. **AUC** = the probability that a randomly chosen *positive* is scored *higher* than a randomly chosen *negative* — a measure of ranking/discrimination across *all* thresholds.\n2. **AUC $= 0.50$** = no better than random ranking (coin flip). **AUC $= 1.0$** = perfect.\n3. So Model A discriminates at chance level; Model B correctly ranks a random positive above a random negative 85% of the time — good discrimination, and it is *threshold-independent*.\n\nB is correct. A confuses AUC with *accuracy* — they are different metrics; AUC is not an accuracy figure. C confuses AUC with *recall at a specific threshold* — AUC integrates over all thresholds and is not a single-threshold recall. D is false on both counts: 0.50 is the *worst* (random), not the best, and a high AUC reflects good *discrimination*, not poor calibration (calibration is a separate property from ranking).\n\n📌 Exam tip: AUC = P(random positive scored above random negative); 0.5 = random, 1.0 = perfect, threshold-independent. It is *not* accuracy and *not* recall at 0.5. Use AUC (with precision/recall/F1) for imbalanced-class risk models where raw accuracy misleads.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"AUC interpretation\",\"items\":[\"0.5 random (coin flip)\",\"|0.85 good discrimination\",\"1.0 perfect ranking\"],\"left\":\"no skill\",\"right\":\"perfect\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A quant proposes using plain linear regression (OLS) on a 0/1 default indicator to estimate default probabilities, instead of logistic regression. Which statement best explains why logistic regression is preferred?\nA) OLS on a 0/1 outcome guarantees probabilities between 0 and 1, but logistic regression is faster to estimate. B) Linear regression on a 0/1 outcome can predict probabilities below 0 or above 1 and forces constant marginal effects; logistic regression maps the linear predictor through the sigmoid to keep $\\hat p \\in (0,1)$. C) Logistic regression is estimated by OLS, while linear regression uses maximum likelihood, making logistic more efficient. D) Linear regression cannot include more than one predictor, whereas logistic regression can.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the linear-vs-logistic contrast for binary outcomes.\n\n1. A plain linear (OLS) model on a 0/1 target produces a fitted value $\\hat\\beta_0 + \\hat\\beta_1 X$ that is *unbounded* — it can fall **below 0 or above 1**, which is nonsensical as a probability. It also forces a **constant marginal effect** of each $X$ regardless of the base rate.\n2. **Logistic regression** passes the linear predictor through the **sigmoid**, $p = 1/(1+e^{-z})$, which constrains predictions to $(0,1)$ and allows marginal effects to vary with the probability level.\n3. This is precisely the problem logistic regression exists to solve.\n\nB is correct. A is backwards: OLS on 0/1 does *not* guarantee $[0,1]$ — that is the core defect; speed is not the issue. C swaps the estimation methods: *logistic* regression uses maximum likelihood, while linear regression uses OLS — the statement reverses them. D is false — linear regression handles many predictors; that is not the distinction.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OLS on a binary outcome (the \"linear probability model\") can give probabilities outside $[0,1]$ and imposes constant marginal effects — exactly why logistic regression (sigmoid, ML-estimated) exists. GARP tests this contrast directly.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Linear vs logistic for 0/1\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Linear (OLS)\",\"items\":[\"unbounded fitted value\",\"can give p<0 or p>1\",\"constant marginal effect\"]},{\"h\":\"Logistic\",\"items\":[\"sigmoid 1/(1+e^-z)\",\"keeps p in (0,1)\",\"fit by maximum likelihood\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"301","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Banks","notes":"# 1. Banks\n\nGARP tests this chapter conceptually: the taxonomy of bank risks, the economic-vs-regulatory capital distinction, moral hazard from deposit insurance, conflicts of interest, originate-to-distribute, and the mechanics (and risks) of securities underwriting.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Identify the major risks banks face and explain how each arises.\n- Distinguish economic capital from regulatory capital, and explain the Basel Committee's motivation for capital regulation.\n- Explain how deposit insurance creates a moral hazard problem.\n- Describe investment banking financing arrangements: private placement, public offering, best efforts, firm commitment, and Dutch auction IPOs.\n- Describe potential conflicts of interest among commercial banking, securities services, and investment banking, and how they are managed.\n- Distinguish the banking book from the trading book.\n- Explain the originate-to-distribute model, its benefits, and its drawbacks.\n\n## The major risks banks face\n\n| Risk | Definition | How it arises |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Credit risk** | Losses from borrowers or counterparties failing to pay | Loan defaults, bond issuer defaults, derivative counterparty failures |\n| **Market risk** | Losses from movements in market prices | Trading-book positions in equities, rates, FX, commodities, and derivatives |\n| **Operational risk** | Losses from failed internal processes, people, systems, or external events | Rogue traders, cyberattacks, fraud, legal/compliance failures, system outages |\n\nCredit risk is typically the largest risk for a commercial bank; operational risk is the hardest to quantify because losses are infrequent but can be enormous.\n\n## Economic capital vs. regulatory capital\n\n- **Expected loss (EL)** is the average loss a bank anticipates. It is a *cost of doing business* and is covered by **pricing** — the interest spread charged on loans.\n- **Unexpected loss (UL)** is the loss beyond the average in bad years. It is absorbed by **capital** (equity), which exists so depositors and senior creditors survive a bad year.\n- **Economic capital** is the bank's *own* estimate of the capital needed to keep its default probability below a target (e.g., losses exceeded only 0.1% of the time, consistent with an AA rating).\n- **Regulatory capital** is the *minimum* set by supervisors (the Basel Committee framework), motivated by systemic stability and the protection of depositors and the deposit-insurance fund. A bank holds the greater of the two in practice.\n\n### Numeric illustration\n\nA bank has a $10 billion loan portfolio. Expected loss is 0.8% per year; the 99.9th-percentile one-year loss is 3.0%.\n\n- EL = 0.8% × $10B = **$80 million** — built into loan spreads (pricing), not capital.\n- 99.9% loss = 3.0% × $10B = $300 million.\n- Economic capital = UL at 99.9% = $300M − $80M = **$220 million** of equity.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Capital covers *unexpected* loss only. If a question asks how much capital is needed against a loss distribution, subtract the expected loss first — EL is covered by pricing/provisions.\n\n## Deposit insurance and moral hazard\n\nDeposit insurance (FDIC in the US, up to $250,000 per depositor per bank) prevents bank runs: insured depositors have no reason to flee a troubled bank. But it creates **moral hazard**: because insured depositors do not monitor risk or demand higher rates from risky banks, a bank can fund aggressive risk-taking with cheap insured deposits — heads the shareholders win, tails the insurance fund pays. The 1980s US savings-and-loan crisis is the classic example. Mitigants: capital regulation, supervision, and risk-based deposit-insurance premiums.\n\n## Commercial vs. investment banking\n\n- **Commercial banking**: taking deposits and making loans (retail and wholesale). Income is primarily net interest margin.\n- **Investment banking**: raising capital for issuers (debt and equity underwriting), advising on M&A, brokerage, market making, and trading. Income is primarily fees and trading profits.\n\nMany large banks do both (\"universal banks\"); the US Glass-Steagall separation (1933) was effectively repealed in 1999.\n\n## Conflicts of interest and Chinese walls\n\n- **Research vs. underwriting**: analysts may be pressured to publish favorable research on companies the bank is underwriting to win or keep the mandate.\n- **Commercial banking vs. securities business**: a bank with private knowledge that a borrower is in trouble could underwrite that borrower's bonds and have its loan repaid from the proceeds — dumping credit risk onto bond investors. It could also push trust/asset-management clients into those securities.\n- **Banking vs. trading**: confidential client information (e.g., a pending takeover) could leak to the trading desk.\n\nThe standard remedy is **Chinese walls** (information barriers): internal rules, physical and IT separation, and compliance monitoring preventing information flow between departments, reinforced by regulation.\n\n## Originate-to-distribute\n\nUnder **originate-to-distribute (OTD)**, a bank originates loans (e.g., mortgages) and then sells them — usually via **securitization** — rather than holding them to maturity.\n\n**Benefits**: frees up capital and balance-sheet capacity, transfers credit risk to investors willing to bear it, increases credit supply, generates fee income, and gives investors access to loan exposures.\n\n**Drawbacks (the 2007–08 lesson)**: when the originator keeps no exposure, its incentive to screen and monitor borrowers collapses. Lending standards on US subprime mortgages deteriorated (NINJA loans, teaser rates), the loans were repackaged into MBS and CDOs, and when house prices fell the losses propagated through the financial system. Post-crisis rules require originators to retain \"skin in the game\" (risk retention).\n\n## Banking book vs. trading book\n\n| | Banking book | Trading book |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contents | Loans and instruments held to maturity / not traded | Instruments held for trading or market making |\n| Accounting | Historical cost / amortized cost; loss provisions when impairment is likely | **Marked to market** daily; gains and losses flow through P&L |\n| Capital charge | Credit risk rules | Market risk rules |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: moving assets between books changes the accounting and the capital charge. During 2007–08, banks held securitized credit exposures in the trading book (lower capital, mark-to-market) and were accused of regulatory arbitrage; illiquid positions also made \"market\" values unreliable. Regulators now restrict reclassification between books.\n\n## Raising capital for issuers\n\n- **Private placement**: securities sold directly to a small number of sophisticated institutions; faster and cheaper, no public registration, but less liquid. Bank earns a fee.\n- **Public offering**: securities sold broadly to the public; requires registration/prospectus.\n  - **Best efforts**: the bank sells what it can at the offer price for a fee — the *issuer* bears the risk of a shortfall.\n  - **Firm commitment**: the bank *buys the entire issue* at one price and resells at a higher offer price — the *bank* bears the placement risk.\n- **IPO**: first public sale of equity. Pricing is hard (no market history); IPOs tend to be underpriced on average (large first-day pops), which costs the issuer money.\n- **Dutch auction IPO**: investors submit bids (number of shares, price); the clearing price is the highest price at which *all* shares can be sold, and every successful bidder pays that single price. Used by Google (2004); designed to let the market set the price and reduce underwriter underpricing.\n\n### Numeric example: firm-commitment risk\n\nA bank agrees on a firm-commitment basis to buy 50 million shares from an issuer at $30 and offer them at $32.\n\n- If the full issue sells at $32: profit = 50M × ($32 − $30) = **+$100 million**.\n- If demand is weak and shares can only be sold at $29: loss = 50M × ($30 − $29) = **−$50 million**.\n\nUnder best efforts with a fee of, say, $0.30 per share actually sold, the bank's revenue is modest but never negative — the price risk stays with the issuer.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What are the three major risks banks face, and how does each arise?\n> **A:** Credit risk (borrowers/counterparties fail to pay), market risk (movements in market prices on trading positions), and operational risk (failed processes, people, systems, or external events). Credit risk is usually largest; operational risk is hardest to quantify.\n\n**Q2.** Distinguish expected loss (EL) from unexpected loss (UL) and what covers each.\n> **A:** EL is the average anticipated loss — a cost of business covered by pricing (loan spreads/provisions). UL is the loss beyond average in bad years — absorbed by capital (equity).\n\n**Q3.** Economic capital vs. regulatory capital\n> **A:** Economic capital is the bank's own estimate of capital needed to keep its default probability below a target (e.g., 0.1%, AA-consistent). Regulatory capital is the supervisory minimum (Basel) motivated by systemic stability and depositor protection. Banks hold the greater of the two.\n\n**Q4.** How do you compute economic capital from a loss distribution?\n> **A:** $$\\text{EC} = \\text{Loss at high percentile} - \\text{EL}$$ — capital covers unexpected loss only. Example: $10B portfolio, EL 0.8% = $80M, 99.9% loss 3.0% = $300M, so EC = $300M − $80M = $220M.\n\n**Q5.** How does deposit insurance create moral hazard?\n> **A:** Insured depositors have no incentive to monitor or demand higher rates from risky banks, so a bank can fund aggressive risk-taking with cheap insured deposits — heads shareholders win, tails the insurance fund pays. The 1980s S&L crisis is the classic case. Mitigants: capital regulation, supervision, risk-based premiums.\n\n**Q6.** Best efforts vs. firm commitment underwriting — who bears the risk?\n> **A:** Best efforts: the bank sells what it can at the offer price for a fee; the issuer bears shortfall risk. Firm commitment: the bank buys the entire issue at one price and resells higher; the bank bears the placement (price) risk.\n\n**Q7.** Firm-commitment profit/loss calculation\n> **A:** Profit = shares × (resale price − purchase price). Buy 50M at $30, sell at $32 → +$100M; if only sellable at $29 → 50M × ($29 − $30) = −$50M loss borne by the bank.\n\n**Q8.** What is a Dutch auction IPO?\n> **A:** Investors submit bids (shares and price); the clearing price is the highest price at which all shares sell, and every successful bidder pays that single price. Used by Google (2004) to let the market set price and reduce underwriter underpricing.\n\n**Q9.** Private placement vs. public offering\n> **A:** Private placement: sold directly to a few sophisticated institutions; faster, cheaper, no public registration, but less liquid. Public offering: sold broadly to the public; requires registration/prospectus.\n\n**Q10.** Explain the originate-to-distribute (OTD) model and its main drawback.\n> **A:** A bank originates loans then sells them (usually via securitization) rather than holding to maturity. Benefits: frees capital, transfers risk, generates fees, expands credit. Drawback: with no retained exposure, the originator's incentive to screen/monitor collapses — a key cause of the 2007-08 subprime crisis. Post-crisis rules require risk retention.\n\n**Q11.** Banking book vs. trading book\n> **A:** Banking book: loans/instruments held to maturity, carried at amortized cost, credit-risk capital charge. Trading book: held for trading/market making, marked to market daily through P&L, market-risk capital charge.\n\n**Q12.** Exam trap: why does reclassifying assets between the banking and trading books matter?\n> **A:** It changes accounting and the capital charge. In 2007-08 banks parked securitized credit in the trading book (lower capital, MTM) — regulatory arbitrage — and illiquid positions made \"market\" values unreliable. Regulators now restrict reclassification.\n\n**Q13.** What are Chinese walls and which conflicts of interest do they address?\n> **A:** Information barriers (internal rules, physical/IT separation, compliance monitoring) preventing information flow between departments. They address research vs. underwriting pressure, commercial banking exploiting private knowledge in securities issuance, and banking client info leaking to the trading desk.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A bank's loan portfolio has an expected one-year loss of $50 million and a 99.9th-percentile one-year loss of $230 million. How much economic capital should the bank hold against this portfolio?\nA) $50 million B) $180 million C) $230 million D) $280 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for economic capital, which is the equity cushion a bank keeps to survive a really bad year.\n\nKey idea: a bank covers two kinds of loss differently.\n- Expected loss (EL) = the average loss it knows will happen. This is treated like a normal cost of business and is paid for by the interest spread on loans (pricing). It does NOT need capital.\n- Unexpected loss (UL) = the extra loss beyond the average in a bad year. THIS is what capital exists to absorb.\n\nSo economic capital = (bad-year loss) − (expected loss):\n$$\\text{Economic capital} = 230 - 50 = 180$$\nThat is $180 million. Answer B.\n\nAnalogy: think of a monthly grocery bill. You expect to spend about \\$400 (that is your \"expected loss\" — you budget for it). But to be safe you keep extra cash for the rare month a big bill hits. The extra cash is your \"capital.\" You only set aside the extra, not the \\$400 you were already going to spend.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($50M) is just the expected loss — but EL is covered by pricing, not capital. Trap: confusing EL with capital.\n- C ($230M) is the total worst-case loss; it forgets to subtract the EL that pricing already covers. Trap: not subtracting EL.\n- D ($280M) wrongly adds EL and the worst-case loss together instead of subtracting. Trap: wrong operation (+ instead of −).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Economic capital = (high-percentile loss) − (expected loss). Always subtract the expected loss first, because pricing/provisions already cover it.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Loss distribution: who covers what\",\"items\":[\"Expected loss (50)\",\"|Unexpected loss (180) = capital\",\"Beyond 99.9% (catastrophic)\"],\"left\":\"covered by pricing/spread\",\"right\":\"not capitalized\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which risk is typically the largest single risk faced by a commercial bank?\nA) Market risk B) Operational risk C) Credit risk D) Foreign exchange risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which risk usually dominates for a normal deposit-and-loan commercial bank.\n\nA commercial bank's main business is making loans. Loans can default (borrowers fail to pay back). That is credit risk, and because lending is the core activity, credit risk is normally the biggest risk on the books.\n\nAnalogy: if your business is lending money to neighbors, your biggest worry is naturally that some neighbors won't pay you back.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (market risk) hits trading positions; it matters most for investment banks/trading desks, not a typical commercial bank.\n- B (operational risk) is real and can be huge (fraud, cyberattacks), but it is the hardest to quantify and is generally not the largest for a commercial bank.\n- D (FX risk) is just one slice of market risk and is usually small for a domestic commercial bank.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a commercial bank, remember the ranking: credit risk is biggest (lending is the core), operational risk is the hardest to measure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Largest risk for a commercial bank\",\"options\":[\"Market risk\",\"Operational risk\",\"Credit risk\",\"FX risk\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Lending is the core business, so default (credit) risk dominates.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Deposit insurance (such as FDIC coverage) most directly creates moral hazard because:\nA) Depositors demand higher interest rates from riskier banks B) Insured depositors stop monitoring bank risk, so banks can take big risks using cheap, insured funding C) It forces banks to hold more capital than is economically necessary D) It removes the credit risk on the bank's loan book\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why guaranteeing deposits encourages risk-taking (\"moral hazard\" = a guarantee changes behavior for the worse).\n\nNormally, if a bank looked risky, depositors would either pull their money or demand higher interest to compensate for the danger. But if the government insures deposits (up to a limit), depositors no longer care how risky the bank is — they get paid back either way. So they stop watching, and the bank can raise cheap deposits and gamble with them: heads the shareholders win, tails the insurance fund pays.\n\nAnalogy: if your parents promised to pay any parking ticket you ever get, you might park more carelessly. The guarantee changed your behavior.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the opposite of what happens — with insurance, depositors stop demanding higher rates from risky banks.\n- C describes capital regulation, which is a mitigant for moral hazard, not the cause of it.\n- D is false — deposit insurance protects depositors, it does not erase the bank's loan losses.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Moral hazard from deposit insurance = insured depositors don't monitor risk, so cheap funding flows to risky behavior. The 1980s US savings-and-loan crisis is the classic example.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Deposit-insurance moral hazard\",\"steps\":[\"Deposits insured\",\"Depositors stop monitoring\",\"Cheap risk-insensitive funding\",\"Bank takes big risks\",\"Insurance fund pays losses\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In a firm-commitment underwriting, a bank buys 20 million shares from the issuer at $15 each and plans to offer them at $16. Demand is weak and the bank can only sell them at $14.50. What is the bank's profit or loss?\nA) +$20 million B) −$10 million C) −$20 million D) $0 — the issuer bears the shortfall\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how much the bank makes or loses on a firm-commitment deal that went badly.\n\nIn a firm commitment, the bank actually buys the whole issue from the company first, then tries to resell it. So the bank owns the shares and bears the risk if the price falls.\n\nThe bank paid $15 per share and could only sell at $14.50, so it loses $0.50 on each share:\n$$\\text{Loss per share} = 14.50 - 15.00 = -0.50$$\n$$\\text{Total} = 20{,}000{,}000 \\times (-0.50) = -\\$10{,}000{,}000$$\nThat is a loss of $10 million. Answer B.\n\nAnalogy: you buy 20 boxes of mangoes for \\$15 each to resell, but the market price drops and you can only get \\$14.50. You eat the \\$0.50 loss on every box.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (+$20M) uses the planned $16 offer price minus $15 — but the shares didn't sell at $16, they sold at $14.50.\n- C (−$20M) wrongly uses a $1.00 loss per share (16 − 15? or 15 − 14.5 doubled). The correct loss is $0.50 × 20M.\n- D ($0) describes best efforts, where the issuer bears the risk. In a firm commitment, the BANK bears the risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Firm commitment = bank buys the issue and bears price risk. Best efforts = bank just tries its best for a fee, and the ISSUER bears the shortfall risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Underwriting: who bears price risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Firm commitment\",\"items\":[\"bank BUYS the whole issue\",\"bank bears price risk\",\"loss if resale price < cost\"]},{\"h\":\"Best efforts\",\"items\":[\"bank only sells for a fee\",\"ISSUER bears shortfall\",\"bank fee never negative\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which statement about the originate-to-distribute (OTD) model is most accurate?\nA) It increases the originating bank's required capital B) It eliminated credit risk from the financial system before 2007 C) It weakened originators' incentives to screen borrowers, contributing to the subprime crisis D) It requires that loans be held in the trading book until maturity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks about a known weakness of OTD, where a bank makes loans and then sells them off (often by securitizing them) instead of keeping them.\n\nThe problem: if you make a loan but immediately sell it to someone else, you no longer suffer if the borrower defaults. So why bother checking carefully whether the borrower can repay? Screening and monitoring incentives collapse. In the run-up to 2007–08, US mortgage lending standards fell sharply (e.g., NINJA loans), the bad loans were repackaged into securities, and the losses spread when house prices fell.\n\nAnalogy: if you could sell a used car instantly to a stranger the moment you bought it, you wouldn't care whether the engine was good. You only care about quality when you have to keep it.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is backwards — OTD frees up capital and balance-sheet capacity, lowering required capital.\n- B is false — the risk didn't disappear; it was passed to investors and ultimately blew up the system.\n- D is false — OTD sells loans off; it does not require holding them, and certainly not in the trading book to maturity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OTD benefit = frees capital, spreads risk, more credit. OTD drawback = \"no skin in the game\" kills lending discipline. Post-crisis rules now require risk retention.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Originate-to-distribute (OTD)\",\"steps\":[\"Bank makes loan\",\"Sells/securitizes it\",\"No skin in the game\",\"Screening weakens\",\"Bad loans spread risk\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A position is held in a bank's trading book rather than its banking book. Which accounting treatment applies?\nA) Historical cost, with provisions only when impairment is likely B) Marked to market daily, with gains and losses flowing through profit and loss C) Amortized cost, never revalued until maturity D) Off-balance-sheet treatment with no valuation\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how trading-book items are accounted for.\n\nA bank splits its assets into two \"books\":\n- Banking book = loans and things it intends to hold (recorded at historical/amortized cost; losses only booked when a default looks likely).\n- Trading book = things held for trading or market making. These are marked to market every day, meaning their value is updated to current market prices, and the daily gains/losses go straight into profit and loss.\n\nAnalogy: the banking book is like the house you live in (you don't re-price it daily). The trading book is like stocks in a brokerage app whose value you see refreshed every single day.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A and C describe the BANKING book (historical/amortized cost), not the trading book.\n- D is wrong — trading positions are on the balance sheet and very much valued (daily).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Trading book = mark-to-market daily, market-risk capital rules. Banking book = cost-based accounting, credit-risk capital rules. Exam loves the regulatory-arbitrage trap of shifting assets between books.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Banking book vs trading book\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Banking book\",\"items\":[\"loans held to maturity\",\"amortized/historical cost\",\"credit-risk capital rules\"]},{\"h\":\"Trading book\",\"items\":[\"held for trading\",\"marked to market daily\",\"market-risk capital rules\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A \"Chinese wall\" inside a universal bank is best described as:\nA) A regulatory minimum capital requirement set by the Basel Committee B) An information barrier preventing the flow of confidential information between departments C) A reserve fund held against expected loan losses D) A limit on how much a single client can borrow\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what a Chinese wall (also called an information barrier) is.\n\nA big bank does many things at once — lending, trading, research, advising on takeovers. Some departments learn secret information that other departments could abuse. A Chinese wall is a set of rules plus physical and IT separation that stops information from flowing between departments, backed by compliance monitoring.\n\nAnalogy: in a school, the teachers grading your exam are kept separate from the teachers who are your close family friends, so nobody can pass along answers or favors. The \"wall\" keeps the information from leaking.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is regulatory capital — unrelated to information barriers.\n- C is a loan-loss reserve/provision — about money set aside, not information control.\n- D is a credit limit — about lending size, not confidentiality.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Chinese wall = information barrier to manage conflicts of interest (e.g., research vs. underwriting, banking vs. trading). It controls information flow, not capital.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What is a Chinese wall?\",\"options\":[\"Basel capital minimum\",\"Information barrier between departments\",\"Loan-loss reserve\",\"Single-client borrowing limit\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"It blocks confidential information flow to manage conflicts of interest.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** In a Dutch auction IPO (the method Google used in 2004), how is the final price for the shares determined?\nA) The underwriter sets a fixed price before any bids are collected B) Each successful bidder pays the individual price they personally bid C) The clearing price is the highest price at which all shares can be sold, and every successful bidder pays that single price D) The lowest submitted bid becomes the price everyone pays\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how the price is set in a Dutch auction IPO.\n\nInvestors submit bids saying how many shares they want and at what price. The shares are filled starting from the highest bids downward until all shares are allocated. The price at which the last share is sold (so that all shares are placed) becomes the single \"clearing price,\" and EVERY successful bidder pays that same clearing price — even those who bid higher.\n\nAnalogy: imagine selling 100 concert tickets. You line up offers from highest to lowest and keep accepting until all 100 are gone. The price of the 100th ticket sets the price, and everyone who got in pays that one price.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes a traditional fixed-price offering, not a Dutch auction.\n- B (pay-your-own-bid) is wrong — a Dutch auction uses a single uniform clearing price for everyone.\n- D is backwards — the clearing price is the highest price at which all shares can be sold, not the lowest bid.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dutch auction = single uniform clearing price = highest price that sells all the shares. Its goal is to let the market set the price and reduce the underpricing common in normal IPOs.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Dutch auction IPO pricing\",\"steps\":[\"Collect sealed bids\",\"Rank high to low\",\"Fill until shares run out\",\"Last share sets clearing price\",\"All winners pay that price\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A bank's $10 billion loan portfolio has an expected loss of 0.8% per year and a 99.9th-percentile one-year loss of 3.0%. How is the expected loss portion covered?\nA) By economic capital (equity) B) By the interest spread charged on loans (pricing) C) By the deposit insurance fund D) By regulatory capital only\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who/what pays for the EXPECTED loss part.\n\nExpected loss is the average loss the bank already anticipates. Because the bank knows it is coming on average, it builds it into the interest rate it charges borrowers — the loan spread. It is treated as a routine cost of doing business, like spoilage in a grocery store priced into the products.\n\nNumbers for context (not required to answer): EL = 0.8% × \\$10B = \\$80 million, and that \\$80M is recovered through loan pricing. Capital (about 3.0% − 0.8% = 2.2%, i.e., \\$220M) covers the UNEXPECTED loss beyond the average.\n\nAnalogy: a bakery knows a few loaves will burn each day, so it raises prices slightly to cover that expected waste. It doesn't dip into emergency savings for the everyday burnt loaves.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A and D — capital (economic or regulatory) covers UNEXPECTED loss, not expected loss.\n- C — the deposit insurance fund only steps in if the bank fails; it does not cover routine expected losses.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected loss → covered by pricing/provisions. Unexpected loss → covered by capital. Memorize this split; it's the foundation of bank capital questions.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Expected vs unexpected loss\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected loss\",\"items\":[\"the average loss\",\"covered by loan pricing/spread\",\"routine cost of business\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected loss\",\"items\":[\"excess in a bad year\",\"covered by capital\",\"absorbed by equity cushion\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** In a best-efforts public offering, who bears the risk that the securities cannot all be sold at the offer price?\nA) The investment bank (underwriter) B) The issuer (the company raising capital) C) The central counterparty D) The retail investors who buy the shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who carries the risk of a shortfall under a best-efforts arrangement.\n\nIn a best-efforts deal, the bank does NOT buy the securities. It simply agrees to try its hardest to sell them at the offer price and earns a fee on what it sells. If demand is weak and not everything sells, that's the issuer's problem — the issuer just raises less money. The bank's fee revenue is modest but never negative.\n\nContrast with firm commitment, where the bank buys the whole issue and bears the price risk itself.\n\nAnalogy: best efforts is like a real-estate agent on commission — they try to sell your house but don't buy it themselves, so if it doesn't sell, you (the owner) bear that. Firm commitment is like someone buying your house outright to resell.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the firm-commitment case, not best efforts.\n- C (central counterparty) belongs to exchange-traded derivatives clearing, not underwriting.\n- D — investors choose whether to buy; they don't bear the issuer's shortfall risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Best efforts → ISSUER bears shortfall risk (bank just earns a fee). Firm commitment → BANK bears the price risk. This pair shows up constantly.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Best efforts: who bears shortfall?\",\"options\":[\"Investment bank (underwriter)\",\"Issuer (the company)\",\"Central counterparty\",\"Retail investors\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"In best efforts the bank only sells for a fee; the issuer raises less if demand is weak.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A bank's loan portfolio has an expected one-year loss of $40M and a 99.9th-percentile one-year loss of $190M. The economic capital it should hold against this portfolio is closest to:\nA) $40M B) $150M C) $190M D) $230M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** economic capital covers unexpected loss: $190M − $40M = $150M; expected loss is covered by pricing.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Deposit insurance most directly creates moral hazard because:\nA) Depositors demand higher rates from riskier banks B) Insured depositors stop monitoring bank risk, allowing banks to take risk with cheap funding C) It forces banks to hold more capital than economically necessary D) It eliminates the bank's credit risk on its loans\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** insured depositors have no incentive to monitor or price risk, so risky banks fund cheaply.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** In a firm-commitment underwriting, a bank buys 20 million shares at $15 and offers them at $16, but can only sell the issue at $14.50. The bank's profit is:\nA) +$20 million B) −$10 million C) −$20 million D) $0 — the issuer bears the shortfall\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** loss = 20M × ($14.50 − $15.00) = −$10M; under firm commitment the *bank* bears the risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which statement about the originate-to-distribute model is most accurate?\nA) It increases the originating bank's required capital B) It eliminated credit risk from the financial system before 2007 C) It weakened originators' incentives to screen borrowers, contributing to the subprime crisis D) It requires loans to be held in the trading book\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** with no retained exposure, screening and monitoring incentives collapse; lending standards fell.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Deposit insurance can make the banking system riskier because it:\nA) Raises banks' funding costs, forcing them into riskier loans B) Weakens depositors' incentive to monitor banks, encouraging banks to take more risk (moral hazard) C) Eliminates the need for bank capital regulation D) Transfers credit risk from banks to their borrowers\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Insured depositors stop demanding higher rates from riskier banks, so banks can fund risky strategies cheaply — classic moral hazard (a key lesson of the US S&L crisis). It is why deposit insurance is paired with capital regulation, not a substitute for it (C wrong).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Deposit-insurance moral hazard\",\"steps\":[\"Deposits insured by government\",\"Depositors stop monitoring bank risk\",\"Bank funds risky bets cheaply\",\"Risk-taking rises → moral hazard\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A bank holds a $4 billion corporate loan portfolio. Its risk team estimates a 0.6% expected one-year loss rate and, from the loss distribution, a 99.9th-percentile one-year loss rate of 2.4%. Separately, the Basel regulatory minimum capital required against this portfolio is $90 million. How much total capital (consistent with prudent practice) should the bank hold against this portfolio, and what is its economic capital?\nA) Economic capital $96M; hold $96M B) Economic capital $72M; hold $90M C) Economic capital $96M; hold $90M D) Economic capital $24M; hold $90M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute economic capital (which covers *unexpected* loss only) and then determine total capital held, given a regulatory minimum.\n\n1. Expected loss (EL): $$EL = 0.6\\% \\times \\$4{,}000M = \\$24M$$. EL is covered by loan pricing/provisions, NOT by capital.\n2. 99.9th-percentile loss: $$2.4\\% \\times \\$4{,}000M = \\$96M$$.\n3. Economic capital = unexpected loss = 99.9% loss − EL: $$\\$96M - \\$24M = \\$72M$$.\n4. The bank holds the **greater** of economic and regulatory capital: $$\\max(\\$72M, \\$90M) = \\$90M$$.\n\nWhy B is correct: economic capital is $72M (UL only), but the regulatory floor of $90M binds, so the bank holds $90M.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A uses the full 99.9% loss ($96M) as economic capital — the classic mistake of failing to subtract EL.\n- C makes the same EL error AND holds the smaller of the two figures.\n- D correctly subtracts but uses... actually $24M is the EL itself mistaken for capital; capital never equals EL.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Capital absorbs only *unexpected* loss — always subtract EL first. When both an economic and a regulatory figure are given, the bank holds the larger.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Economic vs regulatory capital\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Economic capital\",\"items\":[\"bank's own model\",\"UL = 99.9% loss − EL = $72M\",\"driven by rating target\"]},{\"h\":\"Regulatory capital\",\"items\":[\"Basel minimum floor\",\"$90M here\",\"bank holds the GREATER\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A regional bank funds a portfolio of high-yield real-estate development loans almost entirely with retail deposits that are fully covered by deposit insurance. Which statement best captures the moral-hazard mechanism at work, and the correct mitigant?\nA) Insured depositors demand higher rates as the bank's risk rises, so the bank is forced to de-risk; no mitigant is needed. B) Because insured depositors do not monitor or reprice the bank's risk, the bank can fund risky lending cheaply; risk-based deposit-insurance premiums help mitigate it. C) Deposit insurance transfers the bank's credit risk to the FDIC, eliminating the bank's incentive to make loans at all. D) Moral hazard here is a pre-contract screening failure, best mitigated by requiring medical-style underwriting of borrowers.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify the moral-hazard channel created by deposit insurance and the appropriate mitigant.\n\n1. Without insurance, depositors in a risky bank would demand higher rates or withdraw — market discipline.\n2. With insurance, depositors are made whole regardless, so they have **no incentive to monitor or price** the bank's risk.\n3. The bank therefore obtains cheap, risk-insensitive funding and can pursue aggressive lending: \"heads shareholders win, tails the insurance fund pays.\"\n4. Mitigants: capital regulation, supervision, and **risk-based insurance premiums** (charging riskier banks more).\n\nWhy B is correct: it states both the mechanism (no depositor discipline → cheap risky funding) and a textbook mitigant.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A describes the *no-insurance* world and wrongly concludes no mitigant is needed.\n- C confuses deposit insurance (protects depositors) with credit insurance; the bank still bears loan credit risk.\n- D mislabels the problem as adverse selection (pre-contract) — moral hazard is the *behavioral* (post-arrangement) risk-taking; underwriting borrowers is irrelevant to depositor behavior.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The S&L crisis is the canonical example. Moral hazard = behavior change *because* you are insured; the fix is to restore risk sensitivity (risk-based premiums, capital rules).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Deposit-insurance moral hazard + fix\",\"steps\":[\"Deposits insured\",\"Depositors don't monitor risk\",\"Cheap risk-insensitive funding\",\"Aggressive lending\",\"Fix: risk-based premiums + capital rules\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An investment bank agrees on a **firm-commitment** basis to underwrite 80 million shares, buying them from the issuer at $46.50 and offering them to the public at $48.00. Demand is weaker than expected and the bank ultimately places the entire block at an average realized price of $45.75. What is the bank's underwriting profit or loss?\nA) +$120 million B) −$60 million C) −$180 million D) $0 — under firm commitment the issuer absorbs the shortfall\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute the underwriter's P&L on a firm-commitment deal where the resale price falls below the purchase price.\n\n1. Under firm commitment the bank *buys the whole issue* at $46.50 and bears all placement risk.\n2. Per-share P&L = realized resale price − purchase price = $45.75 − $46.50 = −$0.75.\n3. Total = 80M × (−$0.75) = **−$60 million**.\n\nWhy B is correct: the bank loses $0.75 per share on 80M shares.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A uses the *offer* price ($48.00) instead of the realized price: 80M × ($48.00 − $46.50) = +$120M — the profit only if it had sold at the offer price.\n- C computes off the offer price as the cost basis: 80M × ($45.75 − $48.00) = −$180M, double-counting the spread.\n- D describes *best efforts*, not firm commitment — in firm commitment the *bank*, not the issuer, bears the shortfall.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Firm commitment → bank's cost basis is the price it *paid the issuer* ($46.50), and its risk is on the *realized* resale price. Best efforts → issuer bears the risk; bank earns only a per-share fee.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Firm commitment vs best efforts\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Firm commitment\",\"items\":[\"bank buys whole issue at $46.50\",\"bank bears resale price risk\",\"loss if realized < cost\"]},{\"h\":\"Best efforts\",\"items\":[\"bank sells for a fee\",\"ISSUER bears shortfall\",\"bank fee never negative\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A bank's treasury reclassifies a block of illiquid securitized credit exposures from its banking book into its trading book in early 2007. Which consequence is most accurate?\nA) The assets move to amortized-cost accounting and attract a higher credit-risk capital charge. B) The assets are now marked to market daily and attract market-risk capital rules, which were lower — a regulatory-arbitrage concern that \"market\" prices were unreliable for illiquid positions. C) The reclassification eliminates the credit risk because trading-book assets are guaranteed by a CCP. D) The bank must immediately recognize impairment provisions because trading-book assets use historical cost.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify the accounting and capital consequences of moving assets from banking book to trading book.\n\n1. Banking book: held at amortized/historical cost; impairment provisions when loss is likely; subject to **credit-risk** capital rules.\n2. Trading book: **marked to market daily**, gains/losses through P&L; subject to **market-risk** capital rules.\n3. In 2007–08 market-risk charges on these positions were lower than credit-risk charges, so reclassification reduced required capital — regulatory arbitrage.\n4. For illiquid positions, true \"market\" values were hard to observe, making mark-to-market unreliable. Regulators now restrict reclassification.\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly pairs trading book → mark-to-market → market-risk rules (lower) and flags the arbitrage and unreliable-pricing problems.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A reverses the books — amortized cost and credit-risk charges belong to the *banking* book.\n- C invents CCP coverage; trading-book assets are not guaranteed by a clearing house and credit risk is not eliminated.\n- D wrongly assigns historical cost and impairment provisions to the trading book (those are banking-book features).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Banking book = accrual/credit-risk; trading book = mark-to-market/market-risk. The 2008 lesson: reclassification can be capital arbitrage, and MTM is meaningless for genuinely illiquid assets.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Banking book vs trading book\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Banking book\",\"items\":[\"amortized/historical cost\",\"impairment provisions\",\"credit-risk capital rules\"]},{\"h\":\"Trading book\",\"items\":[\"marked to market daily\",\"P&L through MTM\",\"market-risk capital rules\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under a **Dutch auction IPO**, investors submit the following sealed bids for a total offering of 10 million shares:\n\n| Bidder | Shares | Price |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 3M | $52 |\n| B | 4M | $50 |\n| C | 5M | $48 |\n| D | 2M | $46 |\n\nAt what clearing price is the offering sold, and what does each successful bidder pay?\nA) $48; each successful bidder pays $48 (the single clearing price) B) $50; bidders pay their own bid prices (pay-as-bid) C) $46; each successful bidder pays $46 D) $48; bidders A, B, and C each pay their own bid price\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate the ask: find the Dutch-auction clearing price (highest price at which all shares sell) and the payment rule (single uniform price).\n\n1. Rank bids high to low and accumulate shares until the 10M offering is filled:\n   - A at $52: 3M cumulative.\n   - B at $50: 3M + 4M = 7M cumulative.\n   - C at $48: 7M + 5M = 12M cumulative — the 10-millionth share clears here.\n2. The clearing price is the highest price at which *all* 10M shares can be sold = **$48** (the bid that absorbs the last available shares).\n3. In a Dutch auction every successful bidder pays the **single clearing price**, $48 — not their individual bids.\n\nWhy A is correct: $48 clears the full 10M, and the uniform-price rule means all winners pay $48.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B picks $50 (only 7M shares fill — under-allocates) and applies pay-as-bid, which is not the Dutch-auction rule.\n- C uses the lowest bid ($46) rather than the marginal clearing bid.\n- D gets the price right but applies pay-as-bid pricing — the defining feature of a Dutch auction is the *single* clearing price for all.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dutch auction = clearing price is where cumulative demand first meets supply, and *everyone pays that one price*. Google's 2004 IPO used this to reduce underwriter underpricing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Dutch auction clearing\",\"steps\":[\"Rank bids high to low\",\"Accumulate shares\",\"Last share (12M cum) clears at $48\",\"Clearing price = $48\",\"All winners pay $48\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which of the following is the clearest example of a conflict of interest that **Chinese walls** are specifically designed to prevent?\nA) A commercial banking division charging different loan rates to different borrowers based on credit quality. B) An equity research analyst being pressured to publish a \"buy\" rating on a company the bank's investment-banking division is currently underwriting. C) A bank holding the same bond in both its banking book and its trading book. D) A bank choosing a firm-commitment rather than a best-efforts underwriting.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify a genuine cross-divisional conflict of interest addressed by information barriers (Chinese walls).\n\n1. Chinese walls prevent improper *information flow and influence* between divisions whose interests conflict.\n2. Research vs. underwriting is the textbook conflict: analysts may be pressured to favor companies the bank underwrites, to win or retain mandates — biasing research investors rely on.\n3. The wall separates research from investment banking so underwriting interests do not corrupt published opinions.\n\nWhy B is correct: it is a direct research-vs-underwriting conflict, precisely what walls target.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is normal risk-based pricing, not a conflict of interest.\n- C is a book-classification/capital issue, not an information-barrier matter.\n- D is a structuring choice about who bears placement risk — no client-information conflict.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Chinese walls = information barriers between divisions with conflicting incentives (research/underwriting, lending/securities, banking/trading). Look for *misuse of confidential information or cross-division pressure*, not ordinary business decisions.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Conflict a Chinese wall targets\",\"options\":[\"Risk-based loan pricing\",\"Research pressured to 'buy' an underwriting client\",\"Same bond in banking & trading book\",\"Choosing firm-commitment vs best-efforts\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Research-vs-underwriting is the textbook cross-division conflict walls prevent.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Two banks face the same $5 billion loan portfolio with EL = 1.0% and a 99.9% loss of 3.5%. Bank A targets an AA rating (default tolerance 0.1%); Bank B is content with a lower rating and uses an 99.0% loss figure of 2.2% for its internal target. All else equal, which statement about their **economic capital** is correct?\nA) Both hold $175M because EL is irrelevant to economic capital. B) Bank A's economic capital is $125M; Bank B's is $60M — the higher rating target requires more capital. C) Bank A's economic capital is $175M; Bank B's is $110M. D) Economic capital is identical for both because it is set by regulators, not by rating targets.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute each bank's economic capital, recognizing that a more demanding (higher) confidence target requires more capital, and that EL is always subtracted.\n\n1. EL = 1.0% × $5,000M = $50M (covered by pricing, not capital).\n2. Bank A (99.9% target): loss = 3.5% × $5,000M = $175M. Economic capital = $175M − $50M = **$125M**.\n3. Bank B (99.0% target): loss = 2.2% × $5,000M = $110M. Economic capital = $110M − $50M = **$60M**.\n4. The higher (more conservative) confidence level demands more capital.\n\nWhy B is correct: $125M for A, $60M for B, with EL subtracted in each case.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A forgets to subtract EL ($175M) and wrongly says both targets give the same number.\n- C subtracts EL for B ($110M is actually the *gross* loss, not economic capital) but fails to subtract it for A — inconsistent treatment.\n- D confuses *economic* capital (the bank's own choice, driven by its rating target) with *regulatory* capital (the supervisory minimum).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Economic capital = (loss at the chosen confidence level) − EL, and the bank picks the confidence level to match its desired rating. A higher target percentile → larger economic capital.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Rating target drives economic capital\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bank A (99.9% / AA)\",\"items\":[\"loss 3.5% = $175M\",\"minus EL $50M\",\"economic capital $125M\"]},{\"h\":\"Bank B (99.0%)\",\"items\":[\"loss 2.2% = $110M\",\"minus EL $50M\",\"economic capital $60M\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"302","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Insurance Companies and Pension Plans","notes":"# 2. Insurance Companies and Pension Plans\n\nGARP tests product definitions (life vs. P&C vs. health), two calculations — breakeven premiums from a mortality table and the P&C ratio chain — plus adverse selection vs. moral hazard, insurer regulation vs. bank regulation, and DB vs. DC pensions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the key features of the major life insurance products.\n- Describe property & casualty and health insurance.\n- Describe a mortality table and use it to calculate the premium on a term-life policy.\n- Distinguish adverse selection from moral hazard and describe mitigants for each.\n- Calculate and interpret the loss ratio, expense ratio, combined ratio, combined ratio after dividends, and operating ratio of a P&C insurer.\n- Describe CAT bonds.\n- Describe the capital requirements and regulation of insurers, and contrast the insurance guaranty system with bank deposit insurance.\n- Distinguish mutual from stock insurance companies.\n- Compare defined benefit and defined contribution pension plans and identify who bears the risk in each.\n\n## Categories of insurance\n\n### Life insurance\n\n| Product | Key features |\n|---|---|\n| **Term life** | Pays only if death occurs during a fixed term (e.g., 10 years); pure mortality protection, no investment component; cheapest |\n| **Whole life** | Coverage for entire life; level premiums overpay early and underpay late, building a **cash surrender value**; tax-advantaged investment component |\n| **Variable life** | Whole life where the policyholder chooses how the cash value is invested; payout varies with investment performance, usually with a guaranteed minimum |\n| **Universal life** | Flexible premiums above a minimum; insurer credits returns on the cash value (guaranteed floor plus excess) |\n| **Endowment** | Pays at the earlier of death or the end of the term — pays even if the insured survives |\n| **Annuities** | The reverse of life insurance: a lump sum is converted into income for life; the insurer loses if the annuitant lives *longer* than expected (longevity risk) |\n\nLife insurers face **mortality risk** (policyholders die earlier than expected — hurts life policies) and **longevity risk** (they live longer — hurts annuities). The two partially offset each other, a natural hedge.\n\n### Property & casualty and health\n\n- **P&C**: property (fire, theft, flood) and casualty/liability (auto, malpractice). Policies are typically one-year renewable; key risk is **catastrophe (CAT) risk** — losses from one event (hurricane, earthquake) are highly correlated across policies, so the law of large numbers fails.\n- **Health**: medical costs. In the US (post-ACA), premiums cannot be raised because an individual's health deteriorates; like P&C in contract length, like life in that future premiums are constrained.\n\n## Mortality tables and premium calculation\n\nA mortality table gives, for each age, the probability of dying within one year ($$q_x$$), the probability of surviving to that age, and remaining life expectancy. Conditional probabilities chain: the probability that a person now aged $$x$$ dies in the *second* year is $$(1-q_x)\\,q_{x+1}$$.\n\n### Worked example: breakeven premium on a 2-year term policy\n\nA woman aged 60 buys a 2-year, $100,000 term policy. From the mortality table: $$q_{60} = 0.005$$, $$q_{61} = 0.006$$. Payouts occur at the end of the year of death; premiums are paid at the start of each year (the second only if she is alive); the discount rate is 4% (annual compounding).\n\nPV of expected payouts:\n\n$$\n\\text{PV} = \\frac{0.005 \\times 100{,}000}{1.04} + \\frac{(0.995)(0.006) \\times 100{,}000}{1.04^2} = 480.77 + 551.96 = 1{,}032.73\n$$\n\nPV of premiums $$X$$ (paid at $$t=0$$ and, with probability 0.995, at $$t=1$$):\n\n$$\nX\\left(1 + \\frac{0.995}{1.04}\\right) = 1.9567X\n$$\n\nBreakeven: $$X = 1{,}032.73 / 1.9567 \\approx \\$527.78$$ per year. Actual premiums exceed breakeven to cover expenses and profit.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Always multiply the year-2 death probability by the year-1 survival probability, and remember the second premium is contingent on survival. Both adjustments use the same factor $$(1-q_{60})$$.\n\n## Adverse selection vs. moral hazard\n\n| | Adverse selection | Moral hazard |\n|---|---|---|\n| Timing | **Before** the contract | **After** the contract |\n| Problem | High-risk individuals are most eager to buy; uniform pricing attracts a worse-than-average pool | Being insured changes behavior — less care, more risk-taking |\n| Insurance example | Smokers and the seriously ill disproportionately buy life insurance | A driver with full coverage parks carelessly; an insured homeowner skips smoke detectors |\n| Mitigants | Underwriting: medical exams, questionnaires, risk-based pricing | Deductibles, coinsurance, policy limits, no-claims discounts |\n\n## P&C ratios\n\nFor a P&C insurer (all as a percentage of premiums earned):\n\n- **Loss ratio** = losses incurred / premiums\n- **Expense ratio** = expenses (selling, administration) / premiums\n- **Combined ratio** = loss ratio + expense ratio\n- **Combined ratio after dividends** = combined ratio + policyholder dividends\n- **Operating ratio** = combined ratio after dividends − investment income ratio\n\n### Worked example\n\nPremiums $100M; losses $72M; expenses $28M; dividends to policyholders $2M; investment income $8M.\n\n| Ratio | Calculation | Value |\n|---|---|---|\n| Loss ratio | 72 / 100 | 72% |\n| Expense ratio | 28 / 100 | 28% |\n| Combined ratio | 72% + 28% | 100% |\n| Combined after dividends | 100% + 2% | 102% |\n| Operating ratio | 102% − 8% | 94% |\n\nUnderwriting alone breaks even (100%) and loses money after dividends (102%), but investment income on the **float** (premiums held before claims are paid) makes the business profitable overall (94% < 100%).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: a combined ratio above 100% does *not* mean the insurer is unprofitable — subtract investment income to get the operating ratio first. Also watch the sign conventions: dividends are *added*, investment income is *subtracted*.\n\n## CAT bonds\n\nA **catastrophe bond** lets a P&C insurer transfer extreme tail risk to capital markets: the bond pays a high coupon, but interest and/or principal is forgiven if a defined catastrophe loss exceeds a trigger. Investors like CAT bonds because catastrophe losses have low correlation with financial markets — good diversification. For the insurer they work as an alternative to reinsurance.\n\n## Regulation and capital\n\n- In the US, insurers are regulated by the **states** (coordinated by the NAIC), not federally — unlike banks. In Europe, **Solvency II** sets capital at two levels: a minimum capital requirement (MCR) and a higher solvency capital requirement (SCR), with supervisory intervention between them.\n- Solvency capital must absorb unexpected underwriting and investment losses; life insurers also hold policy reserves (actuarial liabilities) against future payouts.\n- **Guaranty system vs. deposit insurance**: when an insurer fails, state guaranty associations cover policyholders (up to limits) by levying **post-insolvency assessments on the surviving insurers** in that state. The FDIC, by contrast, runs a **permanent, pre-funded** fund financed by ongoing bank premiums. Insurance failures are typically slower-moving (no \"runs\"), which is why an after-the-fact levy is workable.\n\n## Mutual vs. stock insurance companies\n\n- **Mutual company**: owned by its policyholders; no shareholders; profits accrue to policyholders (dividends/lower premiums). No conflict between policyholders and owners, but limited access to capital.\n- **Stock company**: owned by shareholders; profits to shareholders; easier capital raising but a potential owner-policyholder conflict. Many mutuals have **demutualized** to access equity markets.\n\n## Pension plans: DB vs. DC\n\n| | Defined benefit (DB) | Defined contribution (DC) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Promise | Formula-based retirement income (e.g., 2% × years of service × final salary) | Only the contributions; payout = contributions + investment performance |\n| Investment risk | **Employer** (must fund any deficit) | **Employee** |\n| Longevity risk | Employer (pays for life) | Employee (may outlive savings) |\n| Portability | Poor | Good — account moves with the employee |\n| Balance-sheet impact | Underfunded plans are a corporate liability | None beyond contributions |\n\nDB plans can become severely underfunded when asset returns are low or longevity rises; the trend is decisively toward DC.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Term life vs. whole life insurance\n> **A:** Term: pays only if death occurs within a fixed term; pure mortality protection, no investment component, cheapest. Whole life: lifetime coverage with level premiums that overpay early, building a cash surrender value plus a tax-advantaged investment component.\n\n**Q2.** What are annuities and what risk do they expose the insurer to?\n> **A:** The reverse of life insurance — a lump sum converted into income for life. The insurer loses if the annuitant lives longer than expected (longevity risk).\n\n**Q3.** Mortality risk vs. longevity risk for a life insurer\n> **A:** Mortality risk: policyholders die earlier than expected (hurts life policies). Longevity risk: they live longer (hurts annuities). The two partially offset — a natural hedge.\n\n**Q4.** How do conditional probabilities chain in a mortality table?\n> **A:** The probability a person aged $$x$$ dies in the second year is $$(1-q_x)\\,q_{x+1}$$ — survive year one, then die. E.g., $$q_{70}=0.02, q_{71}=0.022$$: $$(0.98)(0.022)=0.0216$$.\n\n**Q5.** Breakeven term-life premium — setup\n> **A:** Set PV(premiums) = PV(expected payouts). Payouts discounted at death probabilities (deaths paid end of year); the second premium is contingent on year-1 survival, so multiply it by $$(1-q_x)$$. Actual premiums exceed breakeven to cover expenses and profit.\n\n**Q6.** Adverse selection vs. moral hazard\n> **A:** Adverse selection occurs before the contract — high-risk individuals are most eager to buy, worsening the pool. Moral hazard occurs after — being insured changes behavior toward less care/more risk. Mitigate adverse selection with underwriting (exams, risk-based pricing); mitigate moral hazard with deductibles, coinsurance, limits, no-claims discounts.\n\n**Q7.** P&C combined ratio\n> **A:** $$\\text{Combined ratio} = \\text{loss ratio} + \\text{expense ratio}$$ (both as % of premiums earned). Combined after dividends = combined ratio + policyholder dividends.\n\n**Q8.** P&C operating ratio\n> **A:** $$\\text{Operating ratio} = \\text{combined ratio after dividends} - \\text{investment income ratio}$$. Sign trap: dividends are added, investment income is subtracted.\n\n**Q9.** Exam trap: does a combined ratio above 100% mean the insurer is unprofitable?\n> **A:** No. Subtract investment income (earned on the float — premiums held before claims are paid) to get the operating ratio. E.g., 102% combined after dividends − 8% investment income = 94% operating, profitable.\n\n**Q10.** What is a CAT bond?\n> **A:** A catastrophe bond lets a P&C insurer transfer extreme tail risk to capital markets: it pays a high coupon, but interest and/or principal is forgiven if a defined catastrophe loss exceeds a trigger. Investors gain diversification (CAT losses uncorrelated with markets); for the insurer it is an alternative to reinsurance.\n\n**Q11.** How does insurer regulation differ from bank regulation in the US, and the guaranty system from deposit insurance?\n> **A:** US insurers are regulated by states (coordinated by NAIC), not federally. On failure, state guaranty associations cover policyholders via post-insolvency assessments on surviving insurers. The FDIC, by contrast, is a permanent, pre-funded fund from ongoing bank premiums. Insurance failures are slower (no runs), so after-the-fact levies work.\n\n**Q12.** Mutual vs. stock insurance company\n> **A:** Mutual: owned by policyholders, no shareholders, profits accrue to policyholders, no owner-policyholder conflict but limited capital access. Stock: owned by shareholders, easier capital raising but a potential owner-policyholder conflict. Many mutuals have demutualized.\n\n**Q13.** Defined benefit vs. defined contribution pensions — who bears the risk?\n> **A:** DB: formula-based income; the employer bears investment and longevity risk and must fund deficits. DC: payout = contributions + investment performance; the employee bears investment and longevity risk. DC is more portable; the trend is decisively toward DC.\n\n**Q14.** What is Solvency II's two-level capital structure?\n> **A:** The European insurer framework sets a minimum capital requirement (MCR) and a higher solvency capital requirement (SCR), with graduated supervisory intervention between the two levels.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Which life insurance product provides pure mortality protection for a fixed period only, with no investment/savings component, and is therefore the cheapest?\nA) Whole life B) Term life C) Variable life D) Endowment\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which policy is \"death-protection only\" for a set time, with nothing saved up inside it.\n\nTerm life pays out only if the insured dies during a fixed term (say 10 years). If you survive the term, it pays nothing and there is no built-up cash value. Because it is pure protection with no savings component, it is the cheapest type.\n\nAnalogy: term life is like renting an umbrella for the monsoon season — you only get value if it rains during that period, and you don't keep anything afterward.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (whole life) covers the entire life and builds a cash surrender value (a savings component), so it is more expensive.\n- C (variable life) is whole life where you choose how the cash value is invested — it has an investment component.\n- D (endowment) pays at death OR at the end of the term even if you survive — so it always pays something, making it more than pure term cover.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Term life = pure, temporary, cheapest, no cash value. Anything with \"cash value\" or that \"pays even if you survive\" is NOT term life.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Term vs cash-value life policies\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Term life\",\"items\":[\"pure death protection\",\"fixed period only\",\"no cash value, cheapest\"]},{\"h\":\"Whole/variable/endowment\",\"items\":[\"lifelong or pays at survival\",\"builds cash value\",\"more expensive\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Using a mortality table with $$q_{70} = 0.02$$ and $$q_{71} = 0.022$$, the probability that a person currently aged 70 dies during the SECOND year is closest to:\nA) 0.0220 B) 0.0216 C) 0.0200 D) 0.0420\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what is the chance this 70-year-old dies in year 2 (i.e., between age 71 and 72)?\n\nTo die in the second year, two things must both happen:\n1. Survive the first year (age 70 to 71). Probability of surviving = 1 − q70 = 1 − 0.02 = 0.98.\n2. Then die during the second year (age 71). Probability = q71 = 0.022.\n\nBecause both must happen, we multiply:\n$$P(\\text{die in year 2}) = (1 - q_{70}) \\times q_{71} = 0.98 \\times 0.022 = 0.02156 \\approx 0.0216$$\n\nAnalogy: to win a prize in round 2 of a game, you first have to survive round 1 AND then succeed in round 2. You multiply the two chances together.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (0.0220) just uses q71 alone, forgetting you must first survive year 1.\n- C (0.0200) just uses q70, the wrong year entirely.\n- D (0.0420) adds the two probabilities (0.020 + 0.022) instead of chaining them correctly with survival. You multiply, not add.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To die in year n, multiply the survival probabilities of all earlier years by the death probability of year n. Survive-then-die = multiply.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Requiring a medical examination before issuing a life insurance policy primarily mitigates:\nA) Moral hazard B) Adverse selection C) Longevity risk D) Catastrophe risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which problem a pre-contract medical exam fixes.\n\nAdverse selection happens BEFORE the contract: sick or high-risk people are the most eager to buy insurance, so if everyone is charged the same price, the insurer attracts a worse-than-average pool. A medical exam lets the insurer screen applicants and price (or decline) high-risk people before issuing the policy — directly attacking adverse selection.\n\nMoral hazard, by contrast, happens AFTER the contract (insured people behave more carelessly), and is fixed by deductibles, coinsurance, and limits — not by an upfront exam.\n\nAnalogy: the exam is like checking a player's fitness BEFORE letting them join your team and setting their fee accordingly. That's selecting who gets in (adverse selection). Watching whether they slack off AFTER joining is the moral-hazard problem.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (moral hazard) is post-contract behavior; exams don't change how you behave later.\n- C (longevity risk) is about annuitants living too long; an exam doesn't address that.\n- D (catastrophe risk) is a P&C concept (hurricanes, earthquakes), unrelated to individual life underwriting.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Adverse selection = BEFORE the contract (who buys), fixed by underwriting/screening. Moral hazard = AFTER the contract (behavior), fixed by deductibles/coinsurance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Adverse selection vs moral hazard\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Adverse selection\",\"items\":[\"BEFORE the contract\",\"high-risk people buy most\",\"fix: underwriting / medical exam\"]},{\"h\":\"Moral hazard\",\"items\":[\"AFTER the contract\",\"insured behave carelessly\",\"fix: deductibles / coinsurance\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A P&C insurer reports: premiums $100M, losses $72M, expenses $28M, policyholder dividends $2M, and investment income $8M. What is its operating ratio?\nA) 94% B) 100% C) 102% D) 110%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the operating ratio, which is built up step by step from simpler ratios (all as a percent of premiums).\n\nStep 1 — Loss ratio = losses / premiums = 72 / 100 = 72%.\nStep 2 — Expense ratio = expenses / premiums = 28 / 100 = 28%.\nStep 3 — Combined ratio = loss ratio + expense ratio = 72% + 28% = 100%.\nStep 4 — Combined ratio after dividends = combined ratio + dividends = 100% + 2% = 102%.\nStep 5 — Operating ratio = combined ratio after dividends − investment income ratio = 102% − 8% = 94%.\n\nSo the operating ratio is 94%. Answer A.\n\nAnalogy: think of it as a running tally. Costs pile ON (losses, expenses, dividends raise the ratio), and the money the insurer earns by investing premiums comes OFF (lowers the ratio).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B (100%) stops at the combined ratio and forgets dividends and investment income.\n- C (102%) stops at \"combined after dividends\" and forgets to subtract investment income.\n- D (110%) is just wrong arithmetic; nothing here totals 110%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the signs: dividends are ADDED, investment income is SUBTRACTED. A combined ratio over 100% does NOT mean the insurer is losing money — check the operating ratio after investment income.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An insurer has a combined ratio of 103% but an operating ratio of 95%. The best interpretation is:\nA) The insurer is unprofitable because the combined ratio exceeds 100% B) Underwriting alone lost money, but investment income on the float makes the business profitable overall C) The insurer made an arithmetic error; the operating ratio cannot be below the combined ratio D) The insurer paid no claims this year\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what it means when underwriting loses money but the operating ratio is still good.\n\nA combined ratio of 103% means that, on insurance alone (claims + expenses + dividends), the insurer paid out more than the premiums it collected — underwriting lost money. But insurers also invest the premiums they hold before paying claims (called the \"float\"), earning investment income. Here investment income is worth about 103% − 95% = 8% of premiums, which more than offsets the 3% underwriting loss. So overall (operating ratio 95% < 100%), the insurer is profitable.\n\nAnalogy: a shop might sell goods at a tiny loss but still profit because it earns interest on the customers' deposits it holds in the meantime.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the classic trap — a combined ratio over 100% does NOT automatically mean unprofitable; you must factor in investment income.\n- C is false — the operating ratio is below the combined ratio precisely because you subtract investment income; that's by design.\n- D is unsupported and false — losses (claims) of 103% combined clearly mean claims were paid.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Combined ratio > 100% = underwriting loss, but not necessarily an overall loss. The float and its investment income are what make P&C insurance work.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Combined 103% vs operating 95%\",\"items\":[\"Combined 103% (underwriting loss)\",\"|minus investment income ~8%\",\"Operating 95% (profitable)\"],\"left\":\"insurance alone loses\",\"right\":\"float rescues it\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A catastrophe (CAT) bond is most attractive to investors mainly because:\nA) It guarantees both principal and a high coupon regardless of events B) Catastrophe losses have low correlation with financial markets, providing diversification C) It is insured by the FDIC D) It pays no coupon but offers tax-free principal\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why investors like CAT bonds.\n\nA CAT bond lets an insurer transfer extreme disaster risk (like a hurricane) to bond investors. The bond pays a high coupon, but if a defined catastrophe loss passes a trigger, the investors lose some interest and/or principal (that forgiven money helps the insurer pay claims). Investors like them because whether a hurricane hits has almost nothing to do with whether the stock or bond market goes up or down — so CAT bonds add valuable diversification to a portfolio.\n\nAnalogy: a stock portfolio and a bet on hurricanes move for totally different reasons, so combining them smooths your overall ride.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — principal/interest is precisely at risk if the catastrophe trigger is breached; nothing is guaranteed.\n- C is false — CAT bonds are capital-market securities, not FDIC-insured deposits.\n- D is false — CAT bonds pay a high coupon (that's the whole appeal) and are not tax-free instruments by definition.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CAT bond = the insurer's alternative to reinsurance; investors get a high coupon and diversification because catastrophe risk is uncorrelated with markets, but they can lose principal if disaster strikes.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why investors like CAT bonds\",\"options\":[\"Guaranteed principal and high coupon\",\"Low correlation with markets = diversification\",\"FDIC-insured\",\"Tax-free principal, no coupon\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Hurricane/quake risk is uncorrelated with markets, adding diversification (high coupon, principal at risk).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In the United States, insurance companies are primarily regulated by:\nA) The Federal Reserve B) The states (coordinated by the NAIC) C) The FDIC D) The Basel Committee\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who regulates US insurers.\n\nUnlike banks (which face federal oversight), US insurance companies are regulated at the STATE level. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC) helps coordinate standards across states, but the actual regulators are the states themselves.\n\nAnalogy: think of insurance regulation like driver's licenses in the US — each state runs its own system, with a national body helping keep things roughly consistent.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (Federal Reserve) supervises banks and the banking system, not insurers as the primary regulator.\n- C (FDIC) insures bank deposits; it has nothing to do with regulating insurance companies.\n- D (Basel Committee) sets international BANK capital standards. The insurance analog in Europe is Solvency II — but that's Europe, and it's not a US regulator anyway.\n\n📌 Exam tip: US insurers = STATE-regulated (NAIC coordinates). Europe = Solvency II (MCR and the higher SCR). Don't confuse the insurance guaranty system with bank deposit insurance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Who regulates US insurers?\",\"options\":[\"Federal Reserve\",\"The states (NAIC coordinates)\",\"FDIC\",\"Basel Committee\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"US insurance is regulated state-by-state; NAIC only coordinates standards.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** How does the US insurance guaranty system differ from FDIC bank deposit insurance?\nA) The guaranty system is a permanent pre-funded federal fund; the FDIC charges levies only after a failure B) The guaranty system levies surviving insurers after an insolvency; the FDIC runs a permanent, pre-funded fund from ongoing premiums C) Both are identical pre-funded federal programs D) Neither protects customers; both only protect shareholders\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how insurers' safety net differs from banks'.\n\nFor banks, the FDIC keeps a permanent fund, built up in advance from ongoing premiums paid by banks. If a bank fails, the money is already there.\n\nFor insurers, the state guaranty associations generally do NOT pre-fund. Instead, when an insurer fails, they levy the surviving insurers in that state to raise the money after the fact (post-insolvency assessments). This works because insurance failures are slower-moving — there are no sudden \"runs\" like with banks — so an after-the-fact collection is feasible.\n\nAnalogy: the FDIC is like a neighborhood that keeps an emergency fund saved up all year. The guaranty system is like neighbors passing the hat around only AFTER a house catches fire.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A reverses the two systems.\n- C is false — they are structured very differently (pre-funded vs. after-the-fact).\n- D is false — both protect customers (depositors / policyholders), up to limits.\n\n📌 Exam tip: FDIC = permanent, pre-funded. Insurance guaranty = after-the-fact assessments on surviving insurers. The reason it works for insurers is the absence of fast \"runs.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"FDIC vs insurance guaranty system\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"FDIC (banks)\",\"items\":[\"permanent, pre-funded\",\"ongoing premiums\",\"federal; fast runs\"]},{\"h\":\"Guaranty (insurers)\",\"items\":[\"post-insolvency assessments\",\"levies surviving insurers\",\"state-level; slow failures\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** In a defined contribution (DC) pension plan, the investment risk and the longevity risk are borne by:\nA) The employer B) The employee C) The state guaranty association D) Shared equally by formula\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who carries the risk in a DC plan.\n\nIn a DC plan, the employer only promises to put in contributions (e.g., a percentage of salary). Once those go into the employee's account, the employer's obligation is done. The final retirement pot equals the contributions plus whatever the investments earned — so if markets do poorly (investment risk) the employee has less, and if the employee lives a very long time (longevity risk) the savings might run out. Both risks sit with the EMPLOYEE.\n\nContrast: in a defined benefit (DB) plan, the employer promises a specific income for life, so the EMPLOYER bears investment and longevity risk and must fund any shortfall.\n\nAnalogy: DC is like your employer giving you seeds to plant in your own garden — how much food you end up with, and whether it lasts, is on you. DB is like the employer promising you a fixed basket of food every month for life no matter what.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (employer) bears these risks in a DB plan, not DC.\n- C (guaranty association) relates to insurance failures, not pension investment/longevity risk.\n- D is false — the risk is squarely on the employee, not shared by formula.\n\n📌 Exam tip: DC = employee bears investment AND longevity risk (and it's portable). DB = employer bears them. The world is shifting toward DC.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"DB vs DC pensions: who bears risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Defined benefit (DB)\",\"items\":[\"employer promises income for life\",\"EMPLOYER bears investment risk\",\"EMPLOYER bears longevity risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Defined contribution (DC)\",\"items\":[\"employer only contributes\",\"EMPLOYEE bears investment risk\",\"EMPLOYEE bears longevity risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** What is the key difference between a mutual insurance company and a stock insurance company?\nA) A mutual company is owned by its policyholders; a stock company is owned by shareholders B) A mutual company is owned by shareholders; a stock company is owned by policyholders C) Only stock companies are allowed to sell life insurance D) Mutual companies cannot earn investment income\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks who owns each type of insurer.\n\nA mutual insurance company is owned by its policyholders. There are no outside shareholders, so profits flow back to policyholders (as dividends or lower premiums). There is no owner-vs-policyholder conflict, but it is harder to raise capital.\n\nA stock insurance company is owned by shareholders. Profits go to shareholders, and it can raise capital more easily by issuing equity — but there can be a conflict between shareholders and policyholders. Many mutuals have \"demutualized\" to become stock companies and tap equity markets.\n\nAnalogy: a mutual is like a member-owned cooperative store (the customers own it). A stock company is like a regular corporation with outside investors.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B reverses the definitions.\n- C is false — both mutual and stock insurers sell life insurance.\n- D is false — mutuals invest premiums and earn investment income just like any insurer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mutual = policyholder-owned (no shareholder conflict, harder to raise capital). Stock = shareholder-owned (easier capital, possible conflict). \"Demutualization\" = mutual converting to stock form.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mutual vs stock insurer\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Mutual\",\"items\":[\"owned by policyholders\",\"profits to policyholders\",\"harder to raise capital\"]},{\"h\":\"Stock\",\"items\":[\"owned by shareholders\",\"profits to shareholders\",\"easier capital, possible conflict\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Using $$q_{70} = 0.02$$ and $$q_{71} = 0.022$$, the probability that a person aged 70 dies during the *second* year is closest to:\nA) 0.0220 B) 0.0216 C) 0.0200 D) 0.0420\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** survive year 1 then die: (1 − 0.02) × 0.022 = 0.98 × 0.022 = 0.02156 ≈ 0.0216.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A P&C insurer has a loss ratio of 75%, an expense ratio of 27%, policyholder dividends of 1% of premiums, and investment income of 9% of premiums. Its operating ratio is:\nA) 94% B) 102% C) 103% D) 112%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** combined = 75% + 27% = 102%; after dividends = 103%; operating = 103% − 9% = 94%.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Requiring a medical exam before issuing a life policy primarily mitigates:\nA) Moral hazard B) Adverse selection C) Longevity risk D) Catastrophe risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** underwriting screens out (or reprices) high-risk applicants *before* the contract; moral hazard is post-contract behavior.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In a defined contribution pension plan, the investment and longevity risks are borne by:\nA) The employer B) The employee C) The guaranty association D) Shared equally by contract\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** the employer's obligation ends with the contribution; the employee bears market and longevity risk.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A property-casualty insurer reports a loss ratio of 72%, an expense ratio of 31%, dividends to policyholders of 2%, and investment income of 8% (all as percentages of premiums). Its operating ratio is:\nA) 95% B) 97% C) 103% D) 105%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Combined ratio = 72% + 31% = 103%; after dividends = 103% + 2% = 105%; operating ratio = 105% − 8% investment income = **97%**. C and D stop one step early.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Operating ratio build-up\",\"steps\":[\"Loss 72% + expense 31% = 103% combined\",\"Add dividends 2% → 105%\",\"Subtract investment income 8%\",\"Operating ratio = 97%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** From a mortality table, the probability that a man aged 90 dies within one year is 0.16, and the probability that a man aged 91 dies within one year is 0.18. The probability that a man aged 90 dies within two years is closest to:\nA) 0.0288 B) 0.1800 C) 0.3112 D) 0.3400\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** He must either die in year 1, or survive year 1 and die in year 2:\n\n$$0.16 + (1 - 0.16)(0.18) = 0.16 + 0.84 \\times 0.18 = 0.16 + 0.1512 = 0.3112$$\n\nD (0.34) wrongly adds unconditional probabilities; A multiplies them.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"P(die within 2 years)\",\"steps\":[\"Die in year 1: 0.16\",\"OR survive (0.84) then die year 2: 0.84×0.18\",\"Sum: 0.16 + 0.1512\",\"= 0.3112\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A man aged 50 buys a 2-year, $200,000 term-life policy. From the mortality table, $$q_{50} = 0.004$$ and $$q_{51} = 0.005$$. Death benefits are paid at the end of the year of death; premiums are paid at the start of each year (the second only if he is then alive). The annual discount rate is 5%. The breakeven annual premium is closest to:\nA) $703 B) $760 C) $855 D) $920\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate the ask: find the level annual premium $$X$$ that sets PV(premiums) = PV(expected death benefits) for a 2-year term policy.\n\n1. Year-1 death probability = $$q_{50} = 0.004$$; benefit PV: $$\\frac{0.004 \\times 200{,}000}{1.05} = \\frac{800}{1.05} = 761.90$$.\n2. Year-2 death requires surviving year 1 then dying: $$(1-q_{50})\\,q_{51} = 0.996 \\times 0.005 = 0.00498$$; benefit PV: $$\\frac{0.00498 \\times 200{,}000}{1.05^2} = \\frac{996}{1.1025} = 903.40$$.\n3. PV of expected benefits = $$761.90 + 903.40 = 1{,}665.30$$.\n4. PV of premiums: the $$t=0$$ premium is certain; the $$t=1$$ premium is paid only if alive (prob 0.996): $$X\\left(1 + \\frac{0.996}{1.05}\\right) = X(1 + 0.94857) = 1.94857X$$.\n5. Breakeven: $$X = \\frac{1{,}665.30}{1.94857} \\approx \\$854.6 \\approx \\$855$$.\n\nWhy C is correct: PV of expected benefits $1,665.30 divided by the survival-weighted premium annuity factor 1.94857 gives ≈ $855.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($703) drops the entire year-2 benefit term and divides $761.90 by the annuity factor — forgetting the second year of coverage.\n- B ($760) treats only the *certain* first premium ($X \\times 1$) — forgetting the (contingent) second premium in the denominator, or equivalently equating $X$ to just the year-1 benefit PV.\n- D ($920) omits the survival factor $$(1-q_{50})$$ on the year-2 death probability (using $$q_{51}=0.005$$ directly), overstating the year-2 benefit and thus the premium.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Multiply the year-2 death probability by the year-1 *survival* probability $$(1-q_{50})$$, discount benefits to the end of the year of death, and make the second premium contingent on survival. The same factor $$(1-q_{50})$$ appears on both the year-2 benefit and the year-2 premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A property & casualty insurer reports for the year: premiums earned $250M; losses incurred $170M; underwriting expenses $65M; policyholder dividends $5M; investment income $30M. Its **operating ratio** is closest to:\nA) 84% B) 96% C) 108% D) 94%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate the ask: build the P&C ratio chain all the way to the operating ratio (each item as a percentage of premiums earned).\n\n1. Loss ratio = 170 / 250 = 68.0%.\n2. Expense ratio = 65 / 250 = 26.0%.\n3. Combined ratio = 68% + 26% = 94.0%.\n4. Combined ratio after dividends = 94% + (5/250) = 94% + 2% = 96.0%. (Dividends are *added*.)\n5. Investment income ratio = 30 / 250 = 12.0%.\n6. Operating ratio = combined-after-dividends − investment income = 96% − 12% = **84%**.\n\nWhy A is correct: the full chain ends at 96% − 12% = 84%, well below 100%, so the insurer is comfortably profitable once investment income on the float is included.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- D (94%) stops at the *combined ratio* and never adds dividends or subtracts investment income.\n- B (96%) stops at *combined-after-dividends*, one step short of the operating ratio.\n- C (108%) *adds* investment income (96% + 12%) instead of subtracting it — the most common sign error.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Combined ratio = loss + expense. Dividends are *added*; investment income is *subtracted* to reach the operating ratio. A combined ratio over 100% does not by itself mean the insurer is unprofitable — investment income on the float can rescue it.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An insurer notices that the people most eager to buy its new long-term-care policy at a single uniform premium are disproportionately those already experiencing early health decline, driving claims above the pricing assumption. This is primarily a problem of ______, best mitigated by ______.\nA) moral hazard; deductibles and coinsurance B) adverse selection; medical underwriting and risk-based pricing C) longevity risk; reinsurance D) moral hazard; medical underwriting\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: classify the phenomenon (pre-contract selection vs. post-contract behavior) and match the mitigant.\n\n1. The problem occurs *before* the contract: high-risk individuals self-select into the pool when pricing is uniform. That is **adverse selection**.\n2. Mitigant: screen and reprice applicants *before* issuing — **medical underwriting, questionnaires, risk-based pricing**.\n\nWhy B is correct: pre-contract self-selection = adverse selection; the fix is underwriting/risk-based pricing.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A and D call it moral hazard — but moral hazard is a *behavior change after* being insured (e.g., taking less care). The case describes who *buys*, not how the insured later behaves.\n- A's mitigant (deductibles/coinsurance) addresses moral hazard, not adverse selection.\n- C mislabels it longevity risk (an annuity/mortality concept) and offers reinsurance, which does not address selection.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Adverse selection = BEFORE the contract (who buys) → fix with underwriting/pricing. Moral hazard = AFTER the contract (behavior) → fix with deductibles, coinsurance, limits, no-claims discounts.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Adverse selection vs moral hazard\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Adverse selection\",\"items\":[\"BEFORE the contract\",\"high-risk people self-select\",\"fix: underwriting / risk-based pricing\"]},{\"h\":\"Moral hazard\",\"items\":[\"AFTER the contract\",\"insured behave carelessly\",\"fix: deductibles / coinsurance\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement correctly contrasts the U.S. bank deposit-insurance system (FDIC) with the U.S. state insurance guaranty system?\nA) Both are permanent, pre-funded federal programs financed by ongoing premiums. B) The FDIC is a permanent, pre-funded federal fund; state guaranty associations cover failed insurers' policyholders via post-insolvency assessments levied on surviving insurers. C) The FDIC levies post-failure assessments on surviving banks; insurance guaranty funds are pre-funded by the federal government. D) Both rely on post-insolvency assessments because failures in both sectors happen as sudden runs.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: contrast funding mechanism and level (federal vs. state) of the two protection schemes.\n\n1. FDIC: a **permanent, pre-funded** fund financed by ongoing premiums paid by banks; federal.\n2. Insurance guaranty system: regulated by the **states** (NAIC coordinates); when an insurer fails, **post-insolvency assessments** are levied on the *surviving* insurers in that state to pay covered policyholders.\n3. The after-the-fact levy works for insurers because insurance failures are slower-moving — no depositor-style \"runs.\"\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly pairs FDIC = pre-funded/federal and guaranty = post-insolvency/state assessment.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A wrongly makes both pre-funded and federal (insurance is state-level, post-funded).\n- C reverses the two mechanisms.\n- D wrongly claims both use post-funding and both suffer runs — banks face runs (hence pre-funding); insurers generally do not.\n\n📌 Exam tip: FDIC = pre-funded, federal, because bank runs are fast. Insurance guaranty = post-event assessments on survivors, state-level, because insurer failures are slow.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"FDIC vs insurance guaranty system\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"FDIC (banks)\",\"items\":[\"permanent, pre-funded\",\"ongoing premiums, federal\",\"because bank runs are fast\"]},{\"h\":\"Guaranty (insurers)\",\"items\":[\"post-insolvency assessments\",\"levies surviving insurers, state\",\"because failures are slow\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A life insurer writes a large book of both term-life policies and lifetime annuities. Which statement about its risk profile is most accurate?\nA) Mortality risk and longevity risk both hurt the annuity book, so the two risks compound. B) If annuitants live longer than expected (longevity risk), the annuity book loses money; mortality and longevity risks partially offset across the life and annuity books, forming a natural hedge. C) Catastrophe risk dominates a life insurer's book because deaths are highly correlated. D) Term-life policies build a large cash surrender value, exposing the insurer to investment risk on that value.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify how mortality and longevity risks act on life vs. annuity books and whether they hedge.\n\n1. Term life: insurer loses if policyholders die *earlier* than expected — **mortality risk**.\n2. Annuities: insurer pays income for life and loses if annuitants live *longer* than expected — **longevity risk**.\n3. These move oppositely: a wave of early deaths hurts life but *helps* annuities (payments stop), and longer lives hurt annuities but help life — a **partial natural hedge**.\n\nWhy B is correct: it states the correct direction of longevity risk and the offsetting/natural-hedge relationship.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A wrongly says both risks hurt the annuity book — mortality risk hurts *life* policies, not annuities.\n- C is false: ordinary deaths are largely independent (law of large numbers works); catastrophe correlation is a *P&C* concern.\n- D is false: *term* life has no cash surrender value — that is whole/universal/variable life.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mortality risk hurts LIFE policies (early death); longevity risk hurts ANNUITIES (living too long). Holding both gives a partial natural hedge. Term life = pure protection, no cash value.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mortality vs longevity risk (natural hedge)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Life book\",\"items\":[\"hurt by EARLY death\",\"mortality risk\",\"early deaths help annuities\"]},{\"h\":\"Annuity book\",\"items\":[\"hurt by LONG life\",\"longevity risk\",\"long lives help life book\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A defined benefit (DB) pension plan promises 2% × years of service × final salary. Markets fall and longevity rises. Compared with a defined contribution (DC) plan, which statement about who bears the risk is correct?\nA) In the DB plan the employee bears the investment shortfall; in DC the employer must top up the account. B) In the DB plan the employer must fund the deficit and bears longevity risk; in DC the employee bears both investment and longevity risk. C) Both plans place investment risk on the employer and longevity risk on the employee. D) The guaranty association bears the investment risk in DB plans, so the employer is unaffected.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: assign investment and longevity risk in DB vs. DC.\n\n1. DB: the promised benefit is a formula-based income for life. If assets underperform or members live longer, the **employer** must fund the deficit and pays for life — employer bears **both** investment and longevity risk.\n2. DC: the employer's obligation ends at the contribution; the payout equals contributions plus investment performance, and the saver must make it last — the **employee** bears both investment and longevity risk.\n\nWhy B is correct: DB → employer bears both; DC → employee bears both.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A reverses both assignments.\n- C splits the risks incorrectly (employer/employee) for both plans.\n- D invents guaranty-association coverage of investment risk; underfunded DB plans are a corporate liability on the employer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: DB = EMPLOYER bears investment + longevity risk (and underfunding is a balance-sheet liability). DC = EMPLOYEE bears both. The market trend is decisively toward DC precisely to shed these risks.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"DB vs DC: who bears risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Defined benefit (DB)\",\"items\":[\"formula income for life\",\"EMPLOYER bears investment risk\",\"EMPLOYER bears longevity risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Defined contribution (DC)\",\"items\":[\"employer only contributes\",\"EMPLOYEE bears investment risk\",\"EMPLOYEE bears longevity risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An insurer wishes to transfer extreme hurricane tail risk to capital markets rather than buy more reinsurance. It issues a **CAT bond**. Which combination of features is correct?\nA) Pays a low coupon; principal is protected; attractive because returns are highly correlated with equities. B) Pays a high coupon; interest and/or principal is forgiven if catastrophe losses exceed a trigger; attractive to investors because catastrophe losses have low correlation with financial markets. C) Pays a high coupon; the insurer must repay full principal regardless of losses; functions identically to a corporate bond. D) Pays a low coupon; the insurer keeps all catastrophe risk; used to raise cheap funding.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: state the payoff structure of a CAT bond and why investors buy it.\n\n1. A CAT bond pays a **high coupon** to compensate for the embedded catastrophe risk.\n2. If a defined catastrophe loss **exceeds a trigger**, interest and/or principal is **forgiven** — the insurer keeps that money to pay claims, transferring tail risk to bondholders.\n3. Investors like them because catastrophe losses (hurricanes, quakes) have **low correlation** with financial markets — strong diversification. For the insurer, it is an alternative to reinsurance.\n\nWhy B is correct: high coupon + trigger-based forgiveness + low market correlation.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A gets coupon and correlation backwards (high coupon, low correlation) and wrongly protects principal.\n- C says principal is always repaid — then no risk is transferred and it would not be a CAT bond.\n- D wrongly keeps the risk with the insurer and pays a low coupon — that defeats the entire purpose.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CAT bond = high coupon, low market correlation (great diversifier), principal/interest at risk above a trigger. It transfers tail risk to investors as an alternative to reinsurance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Correct CAT bond features\",\"options\":[\"Low coupon, principal protected, high correlation\",\"High coupon, principal/interest forgiven above trigger, low correlation\",\"High coupon, full principal always repaid\",\"Low coupon, insurer keeps all risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"High coupon + trigger-based forgiveness + low market correlation = diversifying tail-risk transfer.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"303","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Fund Management","notes":"# 3. Fund Management\n\nGARP tests the mutual fund / closed-end fund / ETF distinctions, NAV and fee arithmetic, the abusive trading practices, and — most heavily — hedge fund fee mechanics (high-water marks, hurdles) and the biases that inflate reported hedge fund performance.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Differentiate open-end mutual funds, closed-end funds, and ETFs.\n- Calculate the net asset value (NAV) of an open-end fund.\n- Explain index funds and the sources of tracking error.\n- Describe mutual fund fees (front-end load, back-end load, expense ratio) and their effect on returns.\n- Describe the undesirable trading behaviors: late trading, market timing, front running, and directed brokerage.\n- Explain the key differences between hedge funds and mutual funds.\n- Calculate hedge fund fees, including incentive fees with hurdle rates, high-water marks, and clawbacks.\n- Describe the role of prime brokers.\n- Describe the main hedge fund strategies.\n- Describe the biases in reported hedge fund performance.\n\n## Open-end funds, closed-end funds, and ETFs\n\n| | Open-end mutual fund | Closed-end fund | ETF |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shares outstanding | Variable — fund creates/redeems daily | Fixed after IPO | Variable — created/redeemed in large blocks by institutions |\n| Trading | With the fund, once per day at 4 p.m. NAV | On an exchange, intraday | On an exchange, intraday |\n| Price vs. NAV | Always exactly NAV | Can trade at a **premium or (usually) discount** to NAV | Stays very close to NAV — the creation/redemption arbitrage keeps it in line |\n| Shorting / intraday limit orders | No | Yes | Yes |\n| Holdings disclosure | Quarterly/semiannual | Periodic | Typically daily |\n\n### NAV calculation\n\n$$\n\\text{NAV} = \\frac{\\text{Assets} - \\text{Liabilities}}{\\text{Shares outstanding}}\n$$\n\nExample: a fund holds $250 million of securities and cash, owes $10 million (accrued fees and payables), and has 12 million shares: NAV = (250 − 10)/12 = **$20.00 per share**. All purchases and redemptions that day transact at $20.00.\n\n## Index funds and tracking error\n\nAn **index fund** passively replicates an index by full replication or by sampling. **Tracking error** is the deviation of the fund's return from the index return (often quoted as the standard deviation of the return difference). Sources: fees and expenses, sampling rather than full replication, cash drag, transaction costs around index rebalancing, and dividend-timing differences.\n\n## Mutual fund fees\n\n- **Front-end load**: charged on purchase. A 5% load on a $10,000 investment puts only $9,500 to work — the fund must earn 5.26% before the investor breaks even.\n- **Back-end load**: charged on redemption, often declining with holding period (deters early exit).\n- **Expense ratio**: annual management plus operating (and 12b-1 marketing) costs as a percentage of assets — it compounds against the investor every year, so it is usually the most damaging fee over long horizons.\n\n## Undesirable trading practices\n\n| Practice | What it is | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Late trading** | Placing orders *after* the 4 p.m. cutoff but still receiving today's NAV — trading on post-close news | Illegal |\n| **Market timing** | Rapid in-and-out trading exploiting **stale prices** in NAV (e.g., Asian closing prices in a US fund); dilutes long-term holders | Prohibited by fund rules |\n| **Front running** | Trading for one's own account ahead of a known large fund order | Illegal |\n| **Directed brokerage** | Fund directs trades to a broker in exchange for the broker pushing the fund's shares — a kickback arrangement | Banned |\n\n## Hedge funds vs. mutual funds\n\nHedge funds are lightly regulated because they sell only to accredited/qualified investors. Compared with mutual funds they: are not required to offer daily redemption (impose **lock-up periods**, redemption gates, and notice periods); disclose little; can short, lever, concentrate, and use derivatives freely; and charge performance fees.\n\n## Hedge fund fees: 2-and-20, hurdles, high-water marks\n\nA typical structure is \"**2 and 20**\": a 2% annual management fee on assets plus a 20% **incentive fee** on profits. Refinements:\n\n- **Hurdle rate**: incentive fee applies only to returns above a benchmark (e.g., SOFR or a fixed rate).\n- **High-water mark (HWM)**: incentive fees are paid only on gains above the highest NAV on which fees have already been paid — losses must be recouped before new incentive fees accrue.\n- **Clawback**: part of past incentive fees is held in escrow and returned to investors if later losses occur.\n\n### Worked example: incentive fees with a high-water mark\n\nA fund starts at $100M with a 20% incentive fee above a HWM (initially $100M). Ignore the management fee for clarity.\n\n| Year | Gross return | Pre-fee AUM | HWM at start | Gain above HWM | Incentive fee | End AUM |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | +25% | 125.0 | 100.0 | 25.0 | 5.00 | 120.00 |\n| 2 | −10% | 108.0 | 120.0 | 0 | 0 | 108.00 |\n| 3 | +15% | 124.2 | 120.0 | 4.2 | 0.84 | 123.36 |\n\nAfter year 1 the HWM rises to 120. In year 3 the fund earns a 16.2 gain, but only the 4.2 *above the old peak of 120* is fee-bearing. Total incentive fees: $5.84M; without a HWM they would have been 5.00 + 0 + 0.20 × 16.2 = $8.24M.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: the incentive fee is charged on the excess over the **high-water mark**, not on the full year's gain after a losing year. Also note the option-like asymmetry of \"2 and 20\": the manager shares gains but not losses, an incentive to add volatility — the HWM and clawbacks only partially offset this.\n\n## Prime brokers\n\nLarge banks acting as **prime brokers** provide hedge funds with trade clearing and settlement, custody, margin financing (leverage), securities lending for short sales, and capital introduction. The prime broker takes collateral and monitors the fund's positions — it is often the first to see a fund in distress.\n\n## Hedge fund strategies\n\n| Strategy | One-line description |\n|---|---|\n| Long/short equity | Long undervalued stocks, short overvalued ones; net exposure can vary (equity-market-neutral targets zero beta) |\n| Dedicated short | Net short equities, hunting overvalued or fraudulent companies |\n| Distressed securities | Buy debt of bankrupt/near-bankrupt firms below recovery value; may seek control in restructuring |\n| Merger arbitrage | After a deal is announced, buy the target (and short the acquirer in share deals); profits if the deal closes |\n| Convertible arbitrage | Long underpriced convertible bonds, short the underlying equity hedge |\n| Fixed income arbitrage | Exploit small mispricings between related rate instruments (e.g., on-the-run vs. off-the-run Treasuries), highly levered |\n| Emerging markets | Debt and equity of developing economies |\n| Global macro | Directional bets on macro variables — rates, FX, commodities — often levered |\n| Managed futures | Systematic (often trend-following) trading of futures markets |\n\n## Performance and biases in hedge fund data\n\nReported hedge fund index returns overstate the investable experience:\n\n- **Measurement bias**: returns of illiquid assets are based on manager-marked (smoothed) valuations, understating volatility and correlation.\n- **Backfill bias**: funds join databases voluntarily *after* good performance and import their favorable history.\n- **Survivorship bias**: failed funds drop out of databases, so averages reflect only the winners.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Open-end mutual fund vs. closed-end fund vs. ETF\n> **A:** Open-end: variable shares, trades with the fund once daily at 4 p.m. NAV. Closed-end: fixed shares after IPO, trades intraday on an exchange, can trade at a premium or (usually) discount to NAV. ETF: variable shares (block creation/redemption), trades intraday, stays near NAV via creation/redemption arbitrage.\n\n**Q2.** NAV calculation\n> **A:** $$\\text{NAV} = \\frac{\\text{Assets} - \\text{Liabilities}}{\\text{Shares outstanding}}$$. E.g., ($250M − $10M)/12M = $20.00; all purchases/redemptions that day transact at $20.00.\n\n**Q3.** What is tracking error and what causes it?\n> **A:** The deviation of a fund's return from its index return (often the standard deviation of the return difference). Sources: fees/expenses, sampling vs. full replication, cash drag, transaction costs at rebalancing, dividend-timing differences.\n\n**Q4.** Front-end load, back-end load, expense ratio\n> **A:** Front-end: charged on purchase (a 5% load on $10,000 invests only $9,500 — needs 5.26% to break even). Back-end: charged on redemption, often declining with holding period. Expense ratio: annual management/operating/12b-1 costs as % of assets — compounds yearly, usually most damaging long term.\n\n**Q5.** Late trading vs. market timing\n> **A:** Late trading: placing orders after the 4 p.m. cutoff but still getting today's NAV (trading on post-close news) — illegal. Market timing: rapid in-and-out trading exploiting stale prices in NAV (e.g., Asian closing prices in a US fund); dilutes long-term holders — prohibited by fund rules.\n\n**Q6.** Front running and directed brokerage\n> **A:** Front running: trading for one's own account ahead of a known large fund order — illegal. Directed brokerage: a fund routes trades to a broker in exchange for the broker pushing the fund's shares (a kickback) — banned.\n\n**Q7.** Key differences: hedge funds vs. mutual funds\n> **A:** Hedge funds are lightly regulated (sell only to accredited/qualified investors): no required daily redemption (lock-ups, gates, notice periods), little disclosure, free to short/lever/concentrate/use derivatives, and charge performance fees.\n\n**Q8.** What is \"2 and 20\"?\n> **A:** A typical hedge fund fee: a 2% annual management fee on assets plus a 20% incentive fee on profits.\n\n**Q9.** Hurdle rate vs. high-water mark vs. clawback\n> **A:** Hurdle rate: incentive fee applies only to returns above a benchmark. High-water mark (HWM): incentive fees only on gains above the highest NAV already charged on — losses must be recouped first. Clawback: part of past incentive fees held in escrow and returned if later losses occur.\n\n**Q10.** Incentive fee with a high-water mark — calculation\n> **A:** Fee = incentive rate × (pre-fee AUM − HWM), only if positive. E.g., AUM $110M after a loss, +20% → pre-fee $132M; fee-bearing gain = $132M − $125M HWM = $7M; fee = 20% × $7M = $1.4M.\n\n**Q11.** Exam trap: the option-like asymmetry of \"2 and 20\"\n> **A:** The incentive fee is charged on the excess over the HWM, not the full year's gain after a losing year. The manager shares gains but not losses — an incentive to add volatility that HWMs and clawbacks only partially offset.\n\n**Q12.** What does a prime broker provide to a hedge fund?\n> **A:** Trade clearing/settlement, custody, margin financing (leverage), securities lending for short sales, and capital introduction. It takes collateral and monitors positions — often the first to see a fund in distress.\n\n**Q13.** Name several hedge fund strategies.\n> **A:** Long/short equity (and equity-market-neutral), dedicated short, distressed securities, merger arbitrage, convertible arbitrage, fixed income arbitrage, emerging markets, global macro, and managed futures.\n\n**Q14.** The three biases that inflate reported hedge fund performance\n> **A:** Measurement bias (smoothed manager-marked illiquid valuations understate volatility/correlation), backfill bias (funds import favorable history when joining a database after good performance), and survivorship bias (failed funds drop out, leaving only winners).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** An open-end mutual fund holds $250 million of securities and cash, owes $10 million in accrued fees and payables, and has 12 million shares outstanding. What is its net asset value (NAV) per share?\nA) $20.83 B) $20.00 C) $21.67 D) $18.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the NAV per share, which is simply the fund's net worth divided by the number of shares.\n\nThe formula:\n$$\\text{NAV} = \\frac{\\text{Assets} - \\text{Liabilities}}{\\text{Shares outstanding}}$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n$$\\text{NAV} = \\frac{250 - 10}{12} = \\frac{240}{12} = 20.00$$\n\nSo NAV = $20.00 per share. Every purchase and redemption that day happens at $20.00.\n\nAnalogy: if a pizza is worth \\$240 of toppings after you subtract the \\$10 box cost, and you cut it into 12 slices, each slice is worth \\$20.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($20.83) forgets to subtract liabilities: 250/12 = 20.83. Trap: ignoring the $10M owed.\n- C ($21.67) adds the liability instead of subtracting: 260/12 = 21.67. Trap: wrong sign.\n- D ($18.40) is just a distractor that doesn't match any sensible calculation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: NAV = (Assets − Liabilities) / Shares. Always subtract liabilities first. Open-end funds always transact at NAV; that's their defining feature.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A closed-end fund holds assets worth $360M, has $24M of liabilities, and 16M shares outstanding. Its shares trade on the exchange at $18.90. The fund is trading at:\nA) Exactly NAV, by construction B) A 10% discount to its $21.00 NAV C) A 10% premium to its $21.00 NAV D) A 16% discount to its $22.50 NAV\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks whether the closed-end fund's market price is above or below its NAV, and by how much.\n\nStep 1 — Find NAV:\n$$\\text{NAV} = \\frac{360 - 24}{16} = \\frac{336}{16} = 21.00$$\n\nStep 2 — Compare the $18.90 market price to the $21.00 NAV:\n$$\\frac{18.90}{21.00} = 0.90 = 90\\%$$\nThe price is 90% of NAV, which is 10% below NAV — a 10% discount.\n\nAnalogy: if a \\$21 toy is selling in the store for \\$18.90, it's marked down 10%.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the open-end fund rule, not closed-end. Closed-end funds CAN deviate from NAV.\n- C (premium) is the wrong direction — $18.90 is below $21.00, so it's a discount, not a premium.\n- D uses a wrong NAV ($22.50) and a wrong discount; the correct NAV is $21.00.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Closed-end funds often trade at a DISCOUNT to NAV. Price below NAV = discount; price above NAV = premium. ETFs, by contrast, stay near NAV thanks to creation/redemption arbitrage.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Price vs NAV ($21.00)\",\"items\":[\"Premium (price > NAV)\",\"|NAV $21.00\",\"Price $18.90 = 10% discount\"],\"left\":\"above NAV\",\"right\":\"below NAV\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which feature best distinguishes an open-end mutual fund from a closed-end fund?\nA) An open-end fund trades intraday on an exchange; a closed-end fund trades once per day B) An open-end fund creates and redeems shares daily and always transacts at NAV; a closed-end fund has a fixed share count and trades on an exchange C) Only closed-end funds can hold stocks D) Open-end funds can be sold short intraday; closed-end funds cannot\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the core difference between these two fund types.\n\nOpen-end fund: the fund itself creates new shares when people buy and cancels shares when people redeem, so the number of shares changes daily. You always buy/sell directly with the fund at that day's NAV (set once per day at the 4 p.m. close).\n\nClosed-end fund: after its IPO, the number of shares is FIXED. You buy and sell those shares from other investors on a stock exchange throughout the day, at a market price that can be above or below NAV.\n\nAnalogy: an open-end fund is like a water tank that refills/drains to match demand at a fixed posted price. A closed-end fund is like a limited print run of trading cards — fixed quantity, and the market sets the price.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is reversed — it's the closed-end fund (and ETFs) that trade intraday; open-end funds transact once per day at NAV.\n- C is false — both types can hold stocks.\n- D is reversed — you can short closed-end funds (exchange-traded) but not open-end mutual funds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Open-end = variable shares, transacts at NAV once daily. Closed-end = fixed shares, exchange-traded intraday, can sit at a premium/discount.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Open-end vs closed-end fund\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Open-end\",\"items\":[\"creates/redeems shares daily\",\"share count varies\",\"always transacts at NAV\"]},{\"h\":\"Closed-end\",\"items\":[\"fixed share count after IPO\",\"trades on an exchange\",\"price can differ from NAV\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A mutual fund charges a 5% front-end load. On a $10,000 investment, how much actually gets invested, and roughly what return is needed just to break even on the load?\nA) $9,500 invested; needs about 5.26% to break even B) $9,500 invested; needs exactly 5.00% to break even C) $10,000 invested; the load is taken at redemption D) $10,500 invested; the load is added on top\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks how a front-end load reduces the invested amount and what return undoes it.\n\nA front-end load is charged when you BUY. A 5% load on $10,000 means 5% is taken off the top:\n$$\\text{Load} = 0.05 \\times 10{,}000 = 500$$\n$$\\text{Amount invested} = 10{,}000 - 500 = 9{,}500$$\n\nTo get back to your original \\$10,000 from \\$9,500, you need to grow \\$9,500 by:\n$$\\frac{10{,}000 - 9{,}500}{9{,}500} = \\frac{500}{9{,}500} = 0.0526 = 5.26\\%$$\n\nNotice the break-even return (5.26%) is bigger than the 5% load, because you must grow the SMALLER base ($9,500) back up to the full $10,000.\n\nAnalogy: if a gatekeeper takes \\$5 of your \\$100 before you enter, you only have \\$95 inside, and you need to gain more than 5% on that \\$95 to get back to \\$100.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B says \"exactly 5.00%\" — but 5% of $9,500 is only $475, not enough to recover the $500. You need 5.26%.\n- C describes a back-end load (charged at redemption), not a front-end load.\n- D is wrong — the load is taken OUT of your money, not added on top.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Front-end load shrinks your invested base, so the break-even return is always a bit higher than the load percentage. Load% on the gross ≠ recovery% on the net.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Placing a mutual fund order AFTER the 4 p.m. close but still receiving that same day's NAV is known as:\nA) Market timing — discouraged but legal B) Late trading — illegal C) Front running — legal if disclosed D) Directed brokerage\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks to name the abusive practice of trading after the cutoff at the old price.\n\nLate trading means submitting an order after the 4 p.m. NAV cutoff but still being given today's (already-set) NAV. This lets the trader act on news that came out after the close while paying a price that doesn't reflect that news — an unfair, illegal advantage.\n\nAnalogy: it's like being allowed to place your bet after the race has already finished, but at the pre-race odds. Obviously unfair and not allowed.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (market timing) is rapid in-and-out trading that exploits STALE prices, but it happens BEFORE the cutoff; it's prohibited by fund rules but is a different practice.\n- C (front running) is trading ahead of a known large order — it is illegal, not \"legal if disclosed.\"\n- D (directed brokerage) is a kickback arrangement where the fund steers trades to a broker who pushes its shares — unrelated to the timing of orders.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Late trading = after the cutoff, today's NAV, ILLEGAL. Market timing = before the cutoff, exploiting stale prices. Keep these two straight — they're easy to confuse.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Late trading vs market timing\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Late trading\",\"items\":[\"order AFTER 4pm cutoff\",\"gets today's stale NAV\",\"ILLEGAL\"]},{\"h\":\"Market timing\",\"items\":[\"order BEFORE cutoff\",\"exploits stale prices\",\"prohibited by fund rules\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A hedge fund charges a 20% incentive fee subject to a high-water mark (HWM) of $125M. After a losing year its AUM is $110M; this year it gains 20% (pre-fee). The incentive fee is closest to:\nA) $0 B) $1.4M C) $4.4M D) $6.6M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how much incentive fee is owed when there's a high-water mark and the fund is recovering from a loss.\n\nA high-water mark means the manager only earns an incentive fee on gains ABOVE the highest value previously reached ($125M here). Losses must first be recovered before new fees apply.\n\nStep 1 — Pre-fee AUM after this year's 20% gain:\n$$110 \\times 1.20 = 132 \\text{ (million)}$$\n\nStep 2 — Gain that is above the high-water mark of $125M:\n$$132 - 125 = 7 \\text{ (million)}$$\nOnly this $7M is fee-bearing; the climb from $110M up to $125M just recovers past losses and earns no fee.\n\nStep 3 — Incentive fee = 20% of the fee-bearing gain:\n$$0.20 \\times 7 = 1.4 \\text{ (million)}$$\n\nSo the fee is $1.4M. Answer B.\n\nAnalogy: the HWM is like a personal-best high score. You only earn a bonus for points scored ABOVE your old record — climbing back up to your previous best earns nothing.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($0) wrongly assumes the fund hasn't passed the HWM — but it ended at $132M, above $125M.\n- C ($4.4M) charges 20% on the full $22M gain (132 − 110) and ignores the HWM entirely.\n- D ($6.6M) is just an inflated wrong figure (e.g., 20% × 33). The fee applies only to the $7M above the HWM.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With a HWM, the incentive fee applies only to gains ABOVE the previous peak. Always (1) compute pre-fee AUM, (2) subtract the HWM, (3) apply the fee rate to that excess.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Compared with mutual funds, hedge funds are generally able to:\nA) Offer guaranteed daily redemption to all investors B) Use leverage, short selling, and derivatives freely, and impose lock-up periods C) Sell freely to the general retail public with full disclosure D) Charge no performance fees by regulation\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what hedge funds can do that mutual funds typically cannot.\n\nBecause hedge funds sell only to accredited/qualified (wealthy, sophisticated) investors, they are lightly regulated. This freedom lets them short sell, use leverage (borrow to amplify bets), concentrate positions, and use derivatives freely. They also do NOT have to offer daily redemption — they can impose lock-up periods, redemption gates, and notice periods so investors can't pull money out instantly. And they charge performance/incentive fees (like \"2 and 20\").\n\nAnalogy: a mutual fund is like a public swimming pool with strict safety rules for everyone. A hedge fund is a private club for experienced swimmers — fewer rules, but you can't leave the pool whenever you want.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is backwards — hedge funds RESTRICT redemptions (lock-ups), unlike mutual funds.\n- C is false — hedge funds sell only to accredited investors, not the general public, and they disclose little.\n- D is false — hedge funds are known for charging performance fees; nothing bans them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedge funds = light regulation, accredited investors only, can short/lever/use derivatives, lock-ups, performance fees. Mutual funds = heavily regulated, retail, daily NAV redemption.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedge fund vs mutual fund\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Hedge fund\",\"items\":[\"accredited investors, light regulation\",\"leverage, shorting, derivatives\",\"lock-ups + performance fees\"]},{\"h\":\"Mutual fund\",\"items\":[\"retail, heavily regulated\",\"limited leverage/shorting\",\"daily NAV redemption\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A large bank acting as a prime broker for a hedge fund typically provides all of the following EXCEPT:\nA) Margin financing (leverage) and securities lending for short sales B) Trade clearing, settlement, and custody C) A government guarantee of the hedge fund's returns D) Capital introduction to potential investors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which service a prime broker does NOT provide.\n\nA prime broker is a big bank that supports a hedge fund's operations: it clears and settles trades, holds the fund's assets in custody, lends money (margin financing for leverage), lends securities so the fund can short sell, and introduces the fund to potential investors (capital introduction). In return it takes collateral and monitors the fund's positions — so it is often the first to spot a fund in trouble.\n\nWhat it does NOT do is guarantee the fund's investment returns — no one guarantees hedge fund returns.\n\nAnalogy: a prime broker is like a full-service support crew for a race car — fuel (financing), tools (lending), garage (custody), introductions to sponsors. But the crew never promises the car will win the race.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A, B, and D are all genuine prime-broker services.\n- C is the odd one out — guaranteeing returns is not something any prime broker (or anyone) does.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Prime broker services = clearing/settlement, custody, margin financing, securities lending, capital introduction. It manages risk for itself (collateral, monitoring) — it does NOT guarantee fund performance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"NOT a prime-broker service\",\"options\":[\"Margin financing & securities lending\",\"Clearing, settlement, custody\",\"Government guarantee of returns\",\"Capital introduction to investors\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"No one guarantees hedge fund returns; the rest are standard prime-broker services.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A hedge fund database adds a fund's favorable PRE-entry track record once the fund starts reporting. This inflates reported industry returns through:\nA) Survivorship bias B) Measurement bias C) Backfill bias D) Look-ahead bias\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which bias comes from importing a fund's good history when it joins a database.\n\nBackfill bias: funds join databases voluntarily, and they tend to join AFTER they've had good performance. When they join, their earlier (self-selected, favorable) returns get \"backfilled\" into the database. Since only the funds that did well bother to report, the imported history is rosier than reality, inflating average reported returns.\n\nAnalogy: imagine a \"best students\" yearbook that lets you add your old report cards only after you start scoring well — and naturally you only add the good ones. The yearbook then looks like everyone was always brilliant.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (survivorship bias) is about failed funds DROPPING OUT of the database, so only winners remain — a different mechanism (exit, not entry).\n- B (measurement bias) is about illiquid assets being marked at smoothed manager valuations, understating volatility — not about historical track records.\n- D (look-ahead bias) is using information that wasn't actually available at the time; it's not the standard hedge-fund-reporting bias described here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Backfill = adding favorable PAST returns on ENTRY. Survivorship = losers DROPPING OUT. Measurement = smoothed marks on illiquid assets. All three make reported hedge fund returns look better than the real investable experience.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedge-fund return biases\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Backfill (this Q)\",\"items\":[\"favorable PAST returns added\",\"happens on ENTRY\",\"inflates averages\"]},{\"h\":\"Survivorship\",\"items\":[\"losers DROP OUT\",\"happens on EXIT\",\"only winners remain\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which best describes the option-like asymmetry built into a \"2 and 20\" hedge fund fee structure?\nA) The manager shares in both gains and losses equally B) The manager shares in gains (via the incentive fee) but not in losses, creating an incentive to take on more volatility C) The 2% management fee only applies to profitable years D) The high-water mark fully removes any incentive to take risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the risk-taking incentive hidden in the \"20%\" performance fee.\n\nUnder \"2 and 20,\" the manager takes 20% of the profits but does not pay 20% of the losses. This is like owning an option: you get the upside but are protected from the downside. Because the manager keeps a slice of gains but isn't hit by losses, there's a temptation to add volatility (swing for the fences) — big wins pay the manager handsomely, while big losses are mostly the investors' problem. High-water marks and clawbacks only PARTIALLY offset this incentive.\n\nAnalogy: imagine a fund manager who gets 20% of any treasure found but pays nothing if the ship sinks. They might steer toward riskier, treasure-rich (but dangerous) waters.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — the manager does NOT share in losses; that's the whole asymmetry.\n- C is false — the 2% management fee is charged on assets every year, profitable or not.\n- D overstates it — the HWM only PARTIALLY offsets the risk-taking incentive; it doesn't remove it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The \"20\" in 2-and-20 is option-like: upside participation, no downside participation → an incentive to increase volatility. HWMs and clawbacks reduce but don't eliminate this.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Option-like asymmetry of 2-and-20\",\"options\":[\"Manager shares gains AND losses equally\",\"Shares gains but not losses, so prefers more volatility\",\"2% fee only in profitable years\",\"HWM removes all risk-taking incentive\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The 20% is like a long option: upside only, so it rewards adding volatility.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A closed-end fund holds assets worth $360M with $24M of liabilities and 16M shares outstanding. Its shares trade at $18.90. The fund trades at:\nA) NAV of $18.90 exactly, by construction B) A 10% discount to its $21.00 NAV C) A 10% premium to its $21.00 NAV D) A 16% discount to its $22.50 NAV\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** NAV = (360 − 24)/16 = $21.00; price $18.90 is 18.90/21.00 = 90% of NAV, a 10% discount (typical for closed-end funds).\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A hedge fund charges a 20% incentive fee subject to a high-water mark of $125M. After a losing year its AUM is $110M; this year it gains 20% (pre-fee). The incentive fee is closest to:\nA) $0 B) $1.4M C) $4.4M D) $26.4M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** pre-fee AUM = 110 × 1.20 = $132M; fee-bearing gain = 132 − 125 = $7M above the HWM; fee = 20% × 7 = $1.4M.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Accepting orders after the 4 p.m. close while still granting that day's NAV is:\nA) Market timing — discouraged but legal B) Late trading — illegal C) Front running — legal if disclosed D) Directed brokerage\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** late trading lets the trader exploit post-close information and is illegal; market timing exploits stale prices but trades before the cutoff.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Hedge fund databases that add a fund's pre-entry track record once it starts reporting suffer from:\nA) Survivorship bias B) Measurement bias C) Backfill bias D) Look-ahead bias\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** importing the (self-selected, favorable) pre-entry history is backfill bias; survivorship bias is the dropout of dead funds.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A hedge fund charges a 2% management fee on beginning-of-year assets and a 20% incentive fee on profits net of the management fee (no hurdle, no high-water-mark issues). On beginning assets of $100 million the fund earns a 25% gross return. The investors' net return is:\nA) 18.0% B) 18.4% C) 20.4% D) 23.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Management fee = 2% → $2M. Incentive = 20% × (25 − 2) = 20% × 23 = 4.6%. Net = 25 − 2 − 4.6 = **18.4%**. A applies the incentive to the gross 25% (25 − 2 − 5 = 18.0%) — read the fee basis carefully.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Hedge-fund net return\",\"steps\":[\"Gross return 25%\",\"Management fee 2%\",\"Incentive 20%×(25−2)=4.6%\",\"Net = 25−2−4.6 = 18.4%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** An open-end mutual fund holds securities and cash worth $812 million and owes $32 million in accrued fees and payables, with 39 million shares outstanding. It charges a 5.5% front-end load. An investor places $50,000 today. How many fund shares (rounded to the nearest share) does the investor acquire?\nA) 2,500 shares B) 2,363 shares C) 2,500 shares before load, 2,363 after D) 2,646 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute NAV per share, then the shares bought after the front-end load reduces the amount actually invested.\n\n1. NAV = (Assets − Liabilities) / Shares = ($812M − $32M) / 39M = $780M / 39M = **$20.00 per share**.\n2. The 5.5% front-end load is deducted from the $50,000 first: amount invested = $50,000 × (1 − 0.055) = $50,000 × 0.945 = **$47,250**.\n3. Shares acquired = $47,250 / $20.00 = **2,362.5 ≈ 2,363 shares**.\n\nWhy B is correct: only $47,250 is put to work after the load, buying 2,362.5 ≈ 2,363 shares.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ignores the load entirely: $50,000 / $20 = 2,500 shares.\n- C confuses the framing — the load reduces *dollars invested*, not a \"before/after share count\"; you never buy 2,500 shares.\n- D *adds* the load to the investment ($50,000 × 1.055 / $20 = 2,637.5) or mishandles the percentage, inflating the share count.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A front-end load is subtracted from the gross investment before buying shares: shares = [investment × (1 − load)] / NAV. A 5.5% load means only 94.5% of your money is invested.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A hedge fund begins year 4 with AUM of $200M and a high-water mark (HWM) of $240M, carried over because of prior losses. The fund charges a 2% management fee (on beginning AUM) and a 20% incentive fee on gains above the HWM. The management fee is charged first; the incentive fee applies to the AUM in excess of the HWM measured *after* the management fee. The gross return for year 4 is +30%. The incentive fee is closest to:\nA) $12.0M B) $3.2M C) $4.0M D) $7.2M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute the incentive fee when the 2% management fee is deducted first and the $240M HWM must be exceeded before any incentive fee applies.\n\n1. Management fee = 2% × $200M = $4.0M (charged first).\n2. Gross gain = 30% × $200M = $60M, so pre-fee AUM = $260M.\n3. AUM after management fee = $260M − $4.0M = $256.0M.\n4. Excess over the HWM = $256.0M − $240M = $16.0M (positive, so an incentive fee is owed).\n5. Incentive fee = 20% × $16.0M = **$3.2M**.\n\nWhy B is correct: only the $16.0M by which post-management AUM ($256M) exceeds the $240M HWM is fee-bearing; 20% of that is $3.2M.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($12.0M) ignores the HWM entirely: 20% × the full $60M gross gain.\n- C ($4.0M) ignores the management-fee deduction: 20% × ($260M − $240M) = 20% × $20M.\n- D ($7.2M) adds the management fee to the incentive fee ($4.0M + $3.2M) — that is *total* fees, not the incentive fee asked for.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With a high-water mark, the incentive fee applies only to AUM *above the HWM*. If the structure deducts the management fee first, net it out before measuring the excess over the HWM. Read carefully whether the question wants the incentive fee alone or total fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A closed-end fund holds investments worth $480M against $30M of liabilities, with 25M shares outstanding. Its shares trade on the exchange at $16.20. The fund is trading at:\nA) A 10% discount to its $18.00 NAV B) A 10% premium to its $18.00 NAV C) NAV exactly, by construction D) A 16% discount to its $19.20 NAV\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate the ask: compute NAV, then express the market price relative to NAV as a premium or discount.\n\n1. NAV = (Assets − Liabilities) / Shares = ($480M − $30M) / 25M = $450M / 25M = **$18.00**.\n2. Price / NAV = $16.20 / $18.00 = 0.90 = 90%.\n3. Trading at 90% of NAV is a **10% discount** — typical of closed-end funds.\n\nWhy A is correct: $16.20 is 90% of the $18.00 NAV, i.e., a 10% discount.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B reverses sign: a price below NAV is a discount, not a premium.\n- C describes *open-end* funds (always transact at NAV); closed-end funds trade at market prices that deviate from NAV.\n- D miscomputes NAV (forgets to subtract liabilities: $480M/25M = $19.20) and the percentage.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Closed-end funds trade on an exchange and usually at a *discount* to NAV. Price < NAV = discount; Price > NAV = premium. Only open-end funds and (via arbitrage) ETFs stay at/near NAV.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A trader at a brokerage learns that a large mutual fund will submit a sizable buy order for a thinly traded stock this afternoon, and buys the stock for his own account beforehand, intending to sell into the fund's order. Separately, an investor consistently places fund orders at 5:30 p.m. but is granted that day's 4:00 p.m. NAV. These two practices are, respectively:\nA) Market timing; directed brokerage B) Front running; late trading C) Late trading; front running D) Front running; market timing\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: classify two distinct abusive practices.\n\n1. Trading one's own account *ahead of* a known large client/fund order to profit from the price impact is **front running** (illegal).\n2. Placing an order *after* the 4:00 p.m. cutoff yet receiving that day's NAV — exploiting post-close information — is **late trading** (illegal).\n\nWhy B is correct: the first is front running; the second is late trading.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A mislabels both: market timing exploits *stale prices* in the NAV (and trades before the cutoff); directed brokerage is a kickback for pushing fund shares.\n- C swaps the two labels.\n- D correctly identifies front running but calls the late-trade scenario market timing — market timing happens *before* the cutoff and exploits stale prices, not post-close news.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Late trading = orders *after* 4 p.m. getting today's NAV (post-close info). Market timing = rapid in/out *before* the cutoff exploiting stale prices. Front running = trading ahead of a known order. Directed brokerage = kickback for selling the fund.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Front running vs late trading\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Front running\",\"items\":[\"trade AHEAD of a known order\",\"profits from price impact\",\"illegal\"]},{\"h\":\"Late trading\",\"items\":[\"order AFTER 4pm cutoff\",\"gets today's stale NAV\",\"illegal\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A hedge fund database reports an average annual return of 14% for its constituent funds. An analyst notes that (i) funds that failed and shut down were removed from the database, and (ii) when a fund first begins reporting, its prior favorable track record is added to the database. The two biases described, in order, are:\nA) Backfill bias; survivorship bias B) Survivorship bias; backfill bias C) Measurement bias; survivorship bias D) Survivorship bias; look-ahead bias\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: match each described data problem to the correct named bias.\n\n1. Removing failed/dead funds so only winners remain in the average is **survivorship bias** (overstates returns).\n2. Importing a fund's self-selected, favorable *pre-entry* history when it starts reporting is **backfill bias** (overstates returns).\n\nWhy B is correct: (i) is survivorship, (ii) is backfill, in that order.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A reverses the order.\n- C calls (i) measurement bias — but measurement bias is the *smoothing* of illiquid, manager-marked valuations, understating volatility/correlation, not the dropout of dead funds.\n- D mislabels (ii) as look-ahead bias; the textbook term for importing pre-entry history is backfill (a.k.a. instant-history) bias.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Survivorship = losers drop out. Backfill (instant history) = winners' past returns are added on entry. Measurement/smoothing = stale, manager marks on illiquid assets understate risk. All three *inflate* reported hedge-fund performance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Survivorship vs backfill bias\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Survivorship (i)\",\"items\":[\"failed funds removed\",\"happens on EXIT\",\"only winners remain\"]},{\"h\":\"Backfill (ii)\",\"items\":[\"favorable past added on entry\",\"happens on ENTRY\",\"instant-history bias\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement most accurately distinguishes an ETF from an open-end mutual fund and a closed-end fund?\nA) Like a closed-end fund, an ETF has a fixed share count after its IPO, which is why it can trade at large discounts to NAV. B) An ETF trades intraday on an exchange and, unlike a closed-end fund, stays very close to NAV because authorized participants can create/redeem shares in large blocks, arbitraging away deviations. C) An ETF can be bought and sold only once per day at the 4 p.m. NAV, exactly like an open-end fund. D) An ETF discloses holdings only quarterly, less frequently than an open-end mutual fund.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify the defining ETF features versus the other two fund types.\n\n1. ETFs trade **intraday on an exchange** (like closed-end funds, unlike open-end funds).\n2. Their share count is **variable**: large institutions (authorized participants) **create/redeem** shares in big blocks. This creation/redemption **arbitrage** keeps the market price very close to NAV — unlike closed-end funds, which can trade at persistent discounts/premiums.\n3. ETFs typically disclose holdings **daily**.\n\nWhy B is correct: intraday trading + creation/redemption arbitrage keeping price near NAV is the ETF's hallmark.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A wrongly gives ETFs a fixed share count (that is the closed-end feature) and predicts large discounts — the arbitrage prevents them.\n- C describes the *open-end* once-daily-NAV mechanism; ETFs trade intraday.\n- D reverses disclosure frequency — ETFs typically disclose *daily*, more often than mutual funds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ETF = intraday exchange trading + variable shares via create/redeem arbitrage → price hugs NAV, daily holdings disclosure. Closed-end = fixed shares → can trade at a discount/premium. Open-end = once-daily at NAV.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"ETF vs open-end vs closed-end\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"ETF\",\"items\":[\"intraday on exchange\",\"create/redeem arbitrage\",\"price hugs NAV\"]},{\"h\":\"Open-end / Closed-end\",\"items\":[\"open: once-daily at NAV\",\"closed: fixed shares\",\"closed: can trade at discount\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A prime broker to a hedge fund provides which of the following, and is positioned to do what?\nA) Sets the fund's incentive fee and high-water mark on behalf of investors. B) Provides trade clearing/settlement, custody, margin financing (leverage), and securities lending for shorts — and, holding the fund's collateral and monitoring positions, is often the first to see the fund in distress. C) Guarantees the fund's returns to investors in exchange for a portion of the management fee. D) Acts as the central counterparty for all the fund's OTC trades, eliminating counterparty risk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify the prime broker's services and informational position.\n\n1. Prime brokers (large banks) provide: trade **clearing and settlement**, **custody**, **margin financing** (leverage), **securities lending** for short sales, and **capital introduction**.\n2. Because the prime broker holds collateral and monitors the fund's positions, it is **often the first to detect distress**.\n\nWhy B is correct: it lists the standard services and the early-warning position.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A confuses the prime broker with the fund's own fee structure/governance.\n- C invents a return guarantee — prime brokers do not guarantee fund performance.\n- D overstates the role: a prime broker is not a CCP and does not eliminate counterparty risk (it manages its own exposure via collateral).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Prime broker = clearing, custody, margin/leverage, securities lending, capital introduction. Its collateral-holding/monitoring role makes it the first to spot a fund unraveling.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What a prime broker does\",\"options\":[\"Sets the fund's incentive fee and HWM\",\"Clearing, custody, margin, lending; first to see distress\",\"Guarantees the fund's returns\",\"Acts as CCP eliminating counterparty risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Prime broker provides operational services and, holding collateral, spots distress first; it does NOT guarantee returns.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"304","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Introduction to Derivatives","notes":"# 4. Introduction to Derivatives\n\nGARP tests payoff calculations (forwards and the four option positions), the exchange-traded vs. OTC distinction, the three classes of market participants with simple numeric comparisons, and the ways derivatives get misused.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Define derivatives and describe how they are used to transfer risk and meet investor needs.\n- Distinguish linear from nonlinear derivatives.\n- Describe exchange-traded and OTC derivatives markets and their relative advantages.\n- Differentiate forwards, futures, options, and swaps.\n- Identify and calculate the payoffs of forward and option positions.\n- Differentiate hedgers, speculators, and arbitrageurs, and compare hedging with forwards vs. options and speculating with options vs. the underlying.\n- Describe the risks arising from the misuse of derivatives.\n\n## What is a derivative?\n\nA **derivative** is a contract whose value depends on (\"derives from\") the value of an underlying variable — a stock price, interest rate, FX rate, commodity price, credit event, even weather. Derivatives transfer risk from those who do not want it to those who will bear it for a price.\n\n- **Linear derivatives** have payoffs that are a straight-line function of the underlying: forwards, futures, swaps. A position's gain per $1 move in the underlying is constant.\n- **Nonlinear derivatives** have kinked or curved payoffs: options. The sensitivity to the underlying changes with the level of the underlying — this is what makes option risk management (the Greeks) necessary.\n\n## Exchange-traded vs. OTC markets\n\n| | Exchange-traded | Over-the-counter (OTC) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contracts | Standardized (size, maturity, terms set by the exchange) | Customized, bilaterally negotiated |\n| Participants | Anyone with a brokerage account | Mainly banks, funds, corporations |\n| Counterparty risk | Minimal — central counterparty (CCP) plus margin | Present, mitigated by collateral and (post-2008) mandatory clearing of standardized contracts |\n| Transparency | Public prices | Historically opaque; trade reporting now required |\n| Market size | Smaller | Much larger by notional — OTC notional outstanding is in the hundreds of trillions of dollars |\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Notional amounts vastly overstate OTC risk: the market value of OTC contracts is a small percentage of notional, and netting reduces exposure further.\n\n## The instruments\n\n- **Forward**: OTC agreement to buy (long) or sell (short) an asset at a future date $$T$$ for a delivery price $$K$$ fixed today. Costs nothing to enter (K is set so initial value is zero).\n- **Futures**: the exchange-traded version of a forward — standardized, marked to market daily through margin accounts, guaranteed by a CCP.\n- **Option**: the **right, not the obligation**, to buy (**call**) or sell (**put**) at the strike price $$K$$. European options exercise only at maturity; American options any time up to maturity. The buyer pays a **premium** up front; the seller (writer) collects it and takes on the obligation.\n- **Swap**: agreement to exchange cash flows on future dates by a set formula (e.g., fixed-for-floating interest); economically a portfolio of forwards.\n\n### Payoffs at maturity\n\nForward payoffs (per unit of underlying):\n\n$$\n\\text{Long forward: } S_T - K \\qquad \\text{Short forward: } K - S_T\n$$\n\nThe four European option payoffs (gross of premium):\n\n| Position | Payoff | Profit (net of premium $$c$$ or $$p$$) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Long call | $$\\max(S_T - K,\\, 0)$$ | $$\\max(S_T - K, 0) - c$$ |\n| Short call | $$-\\max(S_T - K,\\, 0)$$ | $$c - \\max(S_T - K, 0)$$ |\n| Long put | $$\\max(K - S_T,\\, 0)$$ | $$\\max(K - S_T, 0) - p$$ |\n| Short put | $$-\\max(K - S_T,\\, 0)$$ | $$p - \\max(K - S_T, 0)$$ |\n\nEntering a forward costs nothing; an option always costs a premium, because the option's payoff is never negative for the holder.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: forwards and futures carry an **obligation** for both sides — a long forward *must* buy at $$K$$ even when $$S_T < K$$, producing a loss; an option holder simply walks away. And every derivative is **zero-sum**: the long's payoff is exactly the short's loss, so \"the contract made money\" always means *someone else* lost it.\n\n## Market participants\n\n### Hedgers\n\nHedgers hold a pre-existing exposure and use derivatives to reduce it.\n\n**Example — forwards vs. options.** A US exporter will receive €1,000,000 in 3 months. The 3-month forward rate is 1.10 USD/EUR; a 3-month put on euros with strike 1.10 costs $0.02 per euro ($20,000 total).\n\n| Spot in 3 months | Unhedged | Sell forward at 1.10 | Buy put, strike 1.10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.05 | $1,050,000 | $1,100,000 | $1,100,000 − $20,000 = $1,080,000 |\n| 1.20 | $1,200,000 | $1,100,000 | $1,200,000 − $20,000 = $1,180,000 |\n\nThe forward locks in $1.10M — certainty, but no upside. The put sets a floor of $1.08M while keeping upside, at the cost of the premium. Neither dominates: hedging is about reducing uncertainty, not maximizing the outcome.\n\n### Speculators\n\nSpeculators take on exposure to bet on direction. Derivatives give them **leverage**.\n\n**Example — options vs. spot.** A speculator with $2,000 is bullish on a stock at $20. Two-month calls with strike $22.50 cost $1.\n\n- **Buy stock**: 100 shares.\n- **Buy calls**: 2,000 options.\n\n| $$S_T$$ | Stock profit | Stock return | Option profit | Option return |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $27 | 100 × 7 = $700 | +35% | 2,000 × (27 − 22.50) − 2,000 = $7,000 | +350% |\n| $18 | 100 × (−2) = −$200 | −10% | −$2,000 (calls expire worthless) | −100% |\n\nLeverage amplifies both directions: a good outcome multiplies tenfold, a modest decline wipes out the entire stake.\n\n### Arbitrageurs\n\nArbitrageurs lock in riskless profit from price discrepancies, and in doing so eliminate them.\n\n**Example.** A stock trades at $152 in New York and £100 in London when the exchange rate is 1.50 USD/GBP. The London price in dollars is 100 × 1.50 = $150. Buy 100 shares in London ($15,000), simultaneously sell 100 in New York ($15,200): riskless profit ≈ **$200** before costs. Arbitrage demand raises the London price and lowers the New York price until the gap is within transaction costs — which is why pricing formulas built on \"no arbitrage\" work.\n\n## Misuse of derivatives\n\nThe same leverage that makes derivatives cheap hedging tools makes them dangerous:\n\n- **Hedgers turning speculators**: a treasury desk mandated to hedge starts taking views because hedged results look \"boring\" or because past punts paid off. Without position limits and independent risk monitoring, the mandate drifts.\n- **Rogue traders**: Barings (1995) — Nick Leeson, supposedly arbitraging Nikkei futures, hid escalating directional losses (~$1.3B) that destroyed the bank; Société Générale (2008) — Jérôme Kerviel built concealed index-futures positions losing €4.9B. Common thread: one person controlling both trading and its back-office confirmation/settlement, defeating segregation of duties.\n- **Hidden tail risk**: strategies that earn small premiums most of the time (e.g., writing options) can carry catastrophic losses in rare states — track records understate the risk until it hits.\n\nControls: separate front and back office, independent valuation, position and loss limits, and watching traders whose reported profits look too good for their mandate.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is a derivative, and what is its economic purpose?\n> **A:** A contract whose value derives from an underlying variable (stock, rate, FX, commodity, credit event, even weather). It transfers risk from those who do not want it to those who will bear it for a price.\n\n**Q2.** Linear vs. nonlinear derivatives\n> **A:** Linear: payoff is a straight-line function of the underlying (forwards, futures, swaps); gain per $1 move is constant. Nonlinear: kinked/curved payoffs (options); sensitivity changes with the underlying's level — hence the Greeks.\n\n**Q3.** Exchange-traded vs. OTC derivatives\n> **A:** Exchange-traded: standardized, open to anyone, minimal counterparty risk (CCP + margin), transparent. OTC: customized, bilateral (banks/funds/corporates), counterparty risk present (mitigated by collateral and post-2008 mandatory clearing), historically opaque, far larger by notional.\n\n**Q4.** Forward vs. futures vs. option vs. swap\n> **A:** Forward: OTC agreement to buy/sell at future date $$T$$ for delivery price $$K$$ set today; costs nothing to enter. Futures: exchange-traded forward, marked to market daily, CCP-guaranteed. Option: right (not obligation) to buy (call) or sell (put) at $$K$$; buyer pays a premium. Swap: exchange of cash flows by formula — economically a portfolio of forwards.\n\n**Q5.** Forward payoffs at maturity\n> **A:** $$\\text{Long: } S_T - K \\qquad \\text{Short: } K - S_T$$. The short profits when the price falls.\n\n**Q6.** The four option payoffs (gross of premium)\n> **A:** Long call $$\\max(S_T-K,0)$$; short call $$-\\max(S_T-K,0)$$; long put $$\\max(K-S_T,0)$$; short put $$-\\max(K-S_T,0)$$. Profit subtracts (long) or adds (short) the premium.\n\n**Q7.** Why does a forward cost nothing to enter but an option always costs a premium?\n> **A:** $$K$$ is set so the forward's initial value is zero (obligation on both sides). An option's payoff is never negative for the holder, so the holder must pay a premium for that one-sided right.\n\n**Q8.** Exam trap: obligation in forwards vs. options, and zero-sum\n> **A:** Forwards/futures bind both sides — a long must buy at $$K$$ even when $$S_T<K$$, taking a loss; an option holder simply walks away. Every derivative is zero-sum: the long's payoff equals the short's loss.\n\n**Q9.** Hedger, speculator, arbitrageur — what each does\n> **A:** Hedger: holds a pre-existing exposure and uses derivatives to reduce it. Speculator: takes on exposure to bet on direction, using leverage. Arbitrageur: locks in riskless profit from price discrepancies, eliminating them in the process.\n\n**Q10.** Hedging with a forward vs. with a put — the trade-off\n> **A:** A forward locks in a rate (certainty, no upside). A put sets a floor while keeping upside, at the cost of the premium. Neither dominates — hedging reduces uncertainty, it does not maximize the outcome.\n\n**Q11.** How does an option give a speculator leverage?\n> **A:** A small premium controls a large position, amplifying both directions. Example: $2,000 buys 100 shares at $20 (+35% if $S_T$=$27) or 2,000 calls at $1 (+350% at $27, but −100% if they expire worthless).\n\n**Q12.** Arbitrage example and why no-arbitrage pricing works\n> **A:** Stock at $152 NY, £100 London, FX 1.50 → London = $150. Buy 100 in London ($15,000), sell 100 in NY ($15,200): ~$200 riskless. Arbitrage demand closes the gap to within transaction costs, which is why no-arbitrage pricing formulas hold.\n\n**Q13.** What is the common thread in rogue-trader misuse of derivatives?\n> **A:** One person controlling both trading and its back-office confirmation/settlement, defeating segregation of duties — Barings/Leeson (~$1.3B, 1995) and Société Générale/Kerviel (€4.9B, 2008). Controls: separate front/back office, independent valuation, position and loss limits.\n\n**Q14.** What is hidden tail risk in derivatives strategies?\n> **A:** Strategies earning small premiums most of the time (e.g., writing options) can carry catastrophic losses in rare states; track records understate the risk until it materializes.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A derivative is best defined as:\nA) A security that always pays a fixed coupon regardless of market conditions B) A contract whose value depends on the value of an underlying variable C) A government bond issued to finance public spending D) A share of ownership in a company\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the basic definition of a derivative.\n\nA derivative is a contract whose value is \"derived from\" something else — the underlying variable. That underlying could be a stock price, an interest rate, an FX rate, a commodity price, a credit event, or even the weather. The derivative itself has no independent value; it only moves because the underlying moves. Derivatives let people transfer risk from those who don't want it to those willing to bear it for a price.\n\nAnalogy: a derivative is like a bet on tomorrow's weather. The bet has no weather of its own — its value depends entirely on what the actual weather (the underlying) does.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes a fixed-coupon bond, which is a standalone security, not a derivative.\n- C describes a government bond — again, an underlying-type security, not a derivative.\n- D describes a share of stock — an underlying asset, not something derived from one.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Value depends on an underlying\" is the heart of the definition. Derivatives transfer risk; they don't create independent value.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Derivative types\",\"root\":\"Derivatives\",\"children\":[\"Forwards\",\"Futures\",\"Options\",\"Swaps\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which of the following is a NONLINEAR derivative?\nA) A forward contract B) A futures contract C) An interest rate swap D) An option\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question asks which instrument has a payoff that is NOT a straight line in the underlying.\n\nLinear derivatives (forwards, futures, swaps) have payoffs that move in a straight line with the underlying — each $1 move in the underlying changes the payoff by a constant amount. Options are different: their payoff is \"kinked.\" For example, a call pays nothing until the price passes the strike, then rises one-for-one. Because the sensitivity changes with the level of the underlying, options are nonlinear — which is exactly why we need the Greeks to manage their risk.\n\nAnalogy: a forward is like a straight ramp (steady slope). An option is like a hockey stick — flat for a while, then it bends upward.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (forward), B (futures), and C (swap) are all LINEAR — constant gain per unit move in the underlying. A swap is economically just a bundle of forwards.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Linear = forwards, futures, swaps (constant sensitivity). Nonlinear = options (kinked payoff, changing sensitivity → Greeks). This distinction shows up everywhere.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Linear vs nonlinear derivatives\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Linear\",\"items\":[\"forwards, futures, swaps\",\"constant payoff slope\",\"constant sensitivity\"]},{\"h\":\"Nonlinear\",\"items\":[\"options\",\"kinked payoff\",\"changing sensitivity → Greeks\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An investor is SHORT a forward contract on gold with a delivery price of $50 per unit. At maturity the spot price is $44. What is the payoff per unit to the short?\nA) −$6 B) +$6 C) $0 — the short can walk away D) +$44\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the short forward's payoff when the price ends below the delivery price.\n\nThe short forward payoff formula is:\n$$\\text{Short forward payoff} = K - S_T$$\nwhere K is the agreed delivery price and S_T is the spot price at maturity.\n\nPlug in:\n$$50 - 44 = +6$$\n\nSo the short gains $6 per unit. This makes sense: the short agreed to SELL gold at $50, but gold is now only worth $44 in the market. Selling at $50 something worth $44 is a $6 gain.\n\nAnalogy: you promised to sell your bike for \\$50 next month. By next month bikes like yours are only worth \\$44. You're happy — you still get \\$50, gaining \\$6 over the market.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (−$6) is the LONG's payoff (S_T − K = 44 − 50 = −6). The short is the mirror image.\n- C is false — forwards bind BOTH parties; nobody can walk away (that's options).\n- D (+$44) confuses the spot price with the payoff.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long forward = S_T − K. Short forward = K − S_T. The short profits when prices FALL. Forwards are obligations for both sides — no walking away.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forward payoff: long vs short\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Long forward\",\"items\":[\"payoff = S_T − K\",\"gains when price RISES\",\"obligated to buy\"]},{\"h\":\"Short forward\",\"items\":[\"payoff = K − S_T\",\"gains when price FALLS\",\"obligated to sell\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A trader buys a European put with strike $40 for a premium of $3. At expiration the stock is at $35. What are the payoff (gross of premium) and the profit (net of premium)?\nA) Payoff $5; profit $2 B) Payoff $2; profit $2 C) Payoff $5; profit $5 D) Payoff $0; profit −$3\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for both the gross payoff and the net profit of a long put that finished in the money.\n\nA put gives the right to SELL at the strike. Its payoff is:\n$$\\text{Long put payoff} = \\max(K - S_T,\\, 0)$$\nPlug in:\n$$\\max(40 - 35,\\, 0) = \\max(5, 0) = 5$$\nSo the gross payoff is $5.\n\nProfit subtracts the premium paid ($3):\n$$\\text{Profit} = 5 - 3 = 2$$\n\nSo payoff $5, profit $2. Answer A.\n\nAnalogy: a put is like insurance letting you sell at \\$40. The stock dropped to \\$35, so your right to sell at \\$40 is worth \\$5. But you paid \\$3 for that insurance, so you're \\$2 ahead.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B says payoff $2 — that wrongly subtracts the premium from the payoff; payoff is gross ($5).\n- C forgets to subtract the $3 premium from the profit.\n- D treats the put as out of the money — but at $35 (below the $40 strike) the put IS in the money.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long put payoff = max(K − S_T, 0). Profit = payoff − premium. \"Payoff\" is always gross of premium; \"profit\" is net of premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A speculator with $1,000 can either buy 40 shares of a $25 stock or buy 2,000 calls (strike $27) at $0.50 each. If the stock ends at $30, what is the return on the OPTION strategy?\nA) +20% B) +100% C) +500% D) +600%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the percentage return on the call-option strategy, to show how leverage amplifies gains.\n\nStep 1 — Option payoff per call = max(S_T − K, 0) = max(30 − 27, 0) = $3.\nStep 2 — Total payoff = 2,000 calls × $3 = $6,000.\nStep 3 — Profit = total payoff − cost = $6,000 − $1,000 = $5,000.\nStep 4 — Return = profit / amount invested:\n$$\\frac{5{,}000}{1{,}000} = 5 = 500\\%$$\n\nSo +500%. Answer C. (For comparison, the stock gives only 40 × $5 = $200 profit, a +20% return — far smaller.)\n\nAnalogy: with the same \\$1,000, the options act like a magnifying glass on the stock's move — a modest stock gain becomes a huge percentage gain (and would become a total wipeout if the stock fell).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (+20%) is the STOCK return, not the option return.\n- B (+100%) understates the leverage; it's not the correct figure.\n- D (+600%) wrongly uses the gross payoff ($6,000) as profit instead of subtracting the $1,000 cost.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Option return = (total payoff − premium paid) / premium paid. Leverage magnifies BOTH directions — same modest move that gives +20% on stock can give +500% (or −100%) on options.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which statement correctly distinguishes forwards from options?\nA) Both parties to a forward have an obligation; only the option writer has an obligation B) Both forwards and options require the buyer to pay a premium upfront C) The forward buyer can abandon the contract if prices move against them D) Options are linear derivatives while forwards are nonlinear\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the key structural difference between forwards and options.\n\nIn a forward, BOTH sides are obligated: the long must buy and the short must sell at the agreed price, no matter what — even at a loss. In an option, only the WRITER (seller) has an obligation; the holder has a right and can simply walk away if it's unfavorable. Because the holder can never be forced into a loss on the contract itself, they must pay a premium upfront to get that one-sided right.\n\nAnalogy: a forward is like both of you signing a binding promise to trade a car at a set price. An option is like paying a small deposit for the RIGHT to buy the car later if you want — you can back out, the seller can't.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B is false — a forward costs nothing to enter (no premium); only options require a premium.\n- C is false — the forward buyer is OBLIGATED and cannot abandon it; only option holders can walk away.\n- D is reversed — options are NONLINEAR, forwards are LINEAR.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward = obligation for both sides, costs nothing to enter. Option = right for the holder, obligation for the writer, requires a premium. That premium exists because the holder's payoff is never negative.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forward vs option\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward\",\"items\":[\"BOTH sides obligated\",\"no premium to enter\",\"linear payoff\"]},{\"h\":\"Option\",\"items\":[\"holder has a RIGHT, writer obligated\",\"holder pays a premium\",\"nonlinear payoff\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which feature is an advantage of EXCHANGE-TRADED derivatives over OTC derivatives?\nA) Fully customizable contract terms tailored to each user B) Minimal counterparty risk due to a central counterparty plus margining C) Larger total notional outstanding D) Complete privacy with no public price reporting\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a benefit of exchange-traded derivatives.\n\nOn an exchange, a central counterparty (CCP) stands between the two sides, and both post margin. This means you essentially face the financially strong clearing house instead of an unknown trading partner, so counterparty (default) risk is minimal. Exchange-traded contracts are also standardized and have transparent public prices.\n\nAnalogy: trading on an exchange is like buying through a trusted escrow service that guarantees the deal — you don't have to worry whether the stranger on the other side will pay.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (customization) is an advantage of OTC, not exchange-traded; exchange contracts are standardized.\n- C (larger notional) describes the OTC market, which is much bigger by notional.\n- D (privacy/no public prices) describes OTC opacity; exchanges have HIGH price transparency, which is a benefit, but \"no public reporting\" is not an advantage and is the opposite of how exchanges work.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Exchange-traded = standardized, transparent, minimal counterparty risk (CCP + margin). OTC = customizable, larger by notional, more counterparty risk. Match the feature to the right market.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Exchange-traded vs OTC derivatives\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Exchange-traded\",\"items\":[\"standardized contracts\",\"CCP + margin = low counterparty risk\",\"transparent public prices\"]},{\"h\":\"OTC\",\"items\":[\"customizable terms\",\"more counterparty risk\",\"larger by notional, private\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A stock trades at $152 in New York and £100 in London, when the exchange rate is 1.50 USD/GBP. Ignoring transaction costs, an arbitrageur can lock in a riskless profit per share of about:\nA) $52 B) $2 C) $0 — there is no arbitrage D) $150\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the riskless arbitrage profit from the price gap between two markets.\n\nStep 1 — Convert the London price into dollars:\n$$£100 \\times 1.50 = \\$150$$\n\nStep 2 — Compare to the New York price of $152. The stock is cheaper in London ($150) than in New York ($152).\n\nStep 3 — Arbitrage: buy in London for $150, simultaneously sell in New York for $152:\n$$152 - 150 = \\$2 \\text{ per share}$$\n\nSo about $2 per share, riskless (before costs). Answer B.\n\nAnalogy: if mangoes cost \\$150 a crate in one market and sell for \\$152 in another a street away, you buy cheap, sell dear, and pocket \\$2 — risk-free, until everyone does it and the gap closes.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($52) wrongly compares $152 to £100 without converting the currency.\n- C is false — there clearly IS a $2 gap after converting correctly.\n- D ($150) is just the converted London price, not the profit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always convert prices into a common currency first, THEN find the gap. Arbitrage = buy cheap, sell dear, riskless. Arbitrage activity itself closes the gap, which is why \"no-arbitrage\" pricing works.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A US exporter will receive €1,000,000 in 3 months. Which statement best compares hedging with a forward versus hedging with a put option on euros?\nA) The forward locks in a certain amount with no upside; the put sets a floor while keeping upside, at the cost of a premium B) The forward keeps upside while the put eliminates it C) Both strategies guarantee the exact same final dollar amount D) The put costs nothing, while the forward requires an upfront premium\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks how forwards and options differ as hedging tools for the exporter.\n\nSelling euros forward locks in a fixed exchange rate (say 1.10), so the exporter knows exactly how many dollars they'll get — certainty, but if the euro rises they don't benefit (no upside). Buying a put gives the right (not obligation) to sell euros at a floor rate; if the euro falls the put protects them, but if the euro rises they let the put expire and enjoy the gain. The catch: the put costs an upfront premium, which a forward doesn't.\n\nAnalogy: the forward is like locking in a fixed price for your crop now — safe but no bonus if prices rise. The put is like insurance with a deductible — you keep the upside if prices rise, but you pay for the policy.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B is reversed — the FORWARD eliminates upside; the PUT keeps it.\n- C is false — the outcomes differ (the put keeps upside, the forward doesn't), and the put costs a premium.\n- D is reversed — the PUT costs a premium; the forward costs nothing to enter.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward = certainty, no premium, no upside. Option (put) = downside protection + upside retained, but you pay a premium. Neither dominates; hedging reduces uncertainty, not necessarily maximizes outcome.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging FX: forward vs put\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward\",\"items\":[\"locks in a fixed rate\",\"no premium\",\"no upside if euro rises\"]},{\"h\":\"Put option\",\"items\":[\"sets a floor rate\",\"keeps upside\",\"costs a premium\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The collapses of Barings Bank (1995) and Société Générale (2008) share which common control failure?\nA) Excessive use of central clearing B) One person controlling both trading and the back-office confirmation/settlement, defeating segregation of duties C) Too much transparency in reported trading profits D) Over-hedging that eliminated all risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what control weakness allowed these famous rogue-trader losses.\n\nIn both cases, a single individual was able to both place trades AND control the back-office function that confirms and settles those trades. With no independent check, the trader could hide growing losses (Nick Leeson at Barings, ~$1.3B; Jérôme Kerviel at Société Générale, €4.9B). The lesson: the front office (trading) and back office (confirmation/settlement) must be separated — this is \"segregation of duties.\"\n\nAnalogy: it's like letting the same person both spend the company's money AND approve their own expense reports. With no one checking, fraud and hidden losses can pile up unnoticed.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — central clearing reduces counterparty risk; it wasn't the cause here.\n- C is backwards — the problem was HIDDEN losses and concealment, not too much transparency. (A warning sign is profits that look too good for the mandate.)\n- D is false — these traders were taking on huge HIDDEN directional risk, not eliminating risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rogue-trader disasters → root cause is broken segregation of duties (one person over trading + back office). Controls: separate front/back office, independent valuation, position and loss limits.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Barings & SocGen common failure\",\"options\":[\"Excessive central clearing\",\"One person ran trading AND back office\",\"Too much profit transparency\",\"Over-hedging eliminated risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"No segregation of duties let the trader hide losses; fix is front/back-office separation.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** An investor is short a forward contract on gold with delivery price $50 (per unit, scaled). At maturity the spot price is $44. The payoff per unit is:\nA) −$6 B) +$6 C) $0 — the short can walk away D) +$44\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** short forward payoff = K − S_T = 50 − 44 = +$6; the short profits when prices fall, and neither side can walk away.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A trader buys a European put with strike $40 for a premium of $3. At expiration the stock is at $35. The payoff (gross of premium) and profit are:\nA) Payoff $5; profit $2 B) Payoff $2; profit $2 C) Payoff $5; profit $5 D) Payoff $0; profit −$3\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** payoff = max(40 − 35, 0) = $5; profit = 5 − 3 = $2.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A speculator with $1,000 can buy 40 shares of a $25 stock or 2,000 calls with strike $27 at $0.50 each. If the stock ends at $30, the return on the option strategy is:\nA) +20% B) +100% C) +500% D) +600%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** option payoff = 2,000 × (30 − 27) = $6,000; profit = 6,000 − 1,000 = $5,000 on $1,000 invested = +500% (vs. +20% on the stock: 40 × $5 = $200).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which statement correctly distinguishes forwards from options?\nA) Both parties to a forward have an obligation; only the option writer has an obligation B) Both require an upfront premium C) The forward buyer can abandon the contract if prices move adversely D) Options are linear derivatives; forwards are nonlinear\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** forwards bind both sides; an option binds only the writer, which is why the holder pays a premium.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A US firm sells £1,000,000 forward at $1.3000 per pound. At maturity the spot exchange rate is $1.2500. The firm's payoff on the forward is:\nA) −$50,000 B) $0 C) +$50,000 D) +$65,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Short forward payoff = (K − S_T) × quantity = (1.3000 − 1.2500) × 1,000,000 = **+$50,000**. The firm locked in a higher sale price than the eventual spot.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A speculator with $3,000 is bullish on a stock currently at $30. She can either buy shares outright or buy 3-month European calls with a strike of $33 priced at $1.50 each (she invests the full $3,000 in one strategy). At expiration the stock is at $39. Compared with simply buying shares, by how many percentage points does the call strategy's return exceed the stock strategy's return?\nA) 270 percentage points B) 300 percentage points C) 30 percentage points D) 200 percentage points\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate the ask: compute the percentage return of each strategy at $$S_T = 39$$ and take the difference.\n\n1. **Stock strategy:** $3,000 / $30 = 100 shares. Profit = 100 × ($39 − $30) = $900. Return = $900 / $3,000 = **+30%**.\n2. **Call strategy:** $3,000 / $1.50 = 2,000 calls. Each call pays max($39 − $33, 0) = $6. Gross payoff = 2,000 × $6 = $12,000. Profit = $12,000 − $3,000 (premium paid) = $9,000. Return = $9,000 / $3,000 = **+300%**.\n3. Difference = 300% − 30% = **270 percentage points**.\n\nWhy A is correct: the leveraged calls return 300% vs. 30% on the stock, a 270-point gap.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B (300) reports the call return itself, not the *difference* over the stock return.\n- C (30) reports the stock return only.\n- D (200) forgets to subtract the premium from the option payoff (using $12,000/$3,000 − 1 incorrectly, or netting differently), understating the option return.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Option leverage magnifies returns. Always subtract the premium paid to get option *profit*, then divide by the amount invested. The \"difference in returns\" asks for the gap, not either level.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A US exporter will receive €2,000,000 in three months. The 3-month forward rate is 1.08 USD/EUR. Alternatively, the exporter buys a 3-month European put on euros with strike 1.08 costing $0.015 per euro. If the spot rate in three months is 1.02 USD/EUR, the dollar proceeds under the forward hedge and under the put hedge are, respectively:\nA) $2,160,000 and $2,160,000 B) $2,160,000 and $2,130,000 C) $2,040,000 and $2,130,000 D) $2,160,000 and $2,040,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute the exporter's dollar receipts at $$S_T = 1.02$$ under (i) selling euros forward at 1.08 and (ii) buying a put struck at 1.08.\n\n1. **Forward:** locks in 1.08 regardless of spot. Proceeds = €2,000,000 × 1.08 = **$2,160,000**.\n2. **Put:** the put gives the right to sell euros at 1.08; since spot 1.02 < 1.08, the exporter exercises and effectively sells at 1.08. Gross = €2,000,000 × 1.08 = $2,160,000.\n3. Premium = $0.015 × 2,000,000 = $30,000 (paid up front).\n4. Net put proceeds = $2,160,000 − $30,000 = **$2,130,000**.\n\nWhy B is correct: the forward yields $2,160,000; the put yields the same floor minus the $30,000 premium = $2,130,000.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A forgets the put premium, equating the two hedges.\n- C uses the unhedged spot conversion (1.02 → $2,040,000) for the forward, ignoring that the forward locks 1.08.\n- D applies the put outcome to the forward and the spot to the put — both reversed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A forward locks the rate with no premium (but no upside). A bought put sets a *floor* (here 1.08) while keeping upside, costing the premium. When spot falls below the strike, the put's net proceeds = floor × notional − premium.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging FX: forward vs put\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward\",\"items\":[\"locks rate 1.08\",\"no premium\",\"no upside if euro rises\"]},{\"h\":\"Put option\",\"items\":[\"floor at 1.08\",\"keeps upside\",\"costs premium ($30,000)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An investor is **short** a forward contract on 10,000 barrels of oil with a delivery price of $78 per barrel. At maturity the spot price is $86 per barrel. The investor's payoff is:\nA) +$80,000 B) −$80,000 C) $0 — the short can decline delivery D) +$860,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute the short forward payoff at maturity.\n\n1. Short forward payoff per unit = $$K - S_T = 78 - 86 = -\\$8$$ per barrel.\n2. Total = 10,000 × (−$8) = **−$80,000**.\n\nWhy B is correct: the short is obligated to deliver at $78 while the market is at $86, losing $8/barrel × 10,000 = $80,000.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A flips the sign — that would be the *long's* payoff ($$S_T - K = +\\$8$$).\n- C invents an option-like walk-away; a forward is a binding obligation for both sides.\n- D uses the spot price as the payoff per unit (86 × 10,000) instead of the gain/loss.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Short forward payoff = $$K - S_T$$ (profits when prices fall). Both sides of a forward are *obligated* — there is no walk-away, unlike an option.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A portfolio manager writes (sells) a European call with strike $50 for a premium of $4 and, separately, writes a European put with strike $50 for a premium of $3, both on the same stock and maturity (a short straddle). At expiration the stock is at $61. The combined profit on the two short positions is:\nA) +$7 B) −$4 C) −$11 D) +$11\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: compute the total profit of writing both a call and a put (short straddle) at $$S_T = 61$$.\n\n1. **Short call** (strike 50, premium 4): profit = $$c - \\max(S_T - K, 0) = 4 - \\max(61-50,0) = 4 - 11 = -\\$7$$.\n2. **Short put** (strike 50, premium 3): profit = $$p - \\max(K - S_T, 0) = 3 - \\max(50-61,0) = 3 - 0 = +\\$3$$ (put expires worthless).\n3. Combined = −$7 + $3 = **−$4**.\n\nWhy B is correct: the written call is deep in-the-money (a $11 payout the writer owes, against $4 collected = −$7), while the written put expires worthless (+$3 kept); net −$4.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (+$7) sums only the premiums collected ($4 + $3) and forgets the call payout owed.\n- C (−$11) counts the call's intrinsic payout but ignores both premiums received.\n- D (+$11) takes the intrinsic value as a gain to the writer — wrong sign; the writer *pays* it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a *written* (short) option, profit = premium received − payoff owed. A short straddle keeps both premiums only if the stock pins the strike; a large move in either direction creates a loss. Sign discipline is everything.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement correctly distinguishes forwards/futures from options regarding obligations, cost to enter, and linearity?\nA) Both forwards and options bind both counterparties; both cost a premium to enter; both are nonlinear. B) A forward binds both sides and costs nothing to enter (delivery price set so initial value is zero); an option binds only the writer, costs the holder a premium, and is nonlinear, while forwards are linear. C) A forward holder may walk away if prices move adversely; options bind both sides; forwards are nonlinear and options are linear. D) Both forwards and options cost nothing to enter, because the delivery/strike price is set so initial value is zero.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: contrast obligation, entry cost, and linearity for forwards vs. options.\n\n1. **Obligation:** a forward (and futures) binds *both* parties; an option binds only the *writer* — the holder has a right, not an obligation.\n2. **Cost to enter:** a forward costs nothing (the delivery price $$K$$ is set so initial value = 0); an option *always* costs the holder a **premium**, because the option payoff is never negative for the holder.\n3. **Linearity:** forwards/futures/swaps are **linear** (constant payoff slope); options are **nonlinear** (kinked payoff, hence the Greeks).\n\nWhy B is correct: it correctly pairs obligation, cost, and linearity for each instrument.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A wrongly makes both bind both sides, both cost a premium, and both nonlinear.\n- C invents a forward walk-away and reverses the linearity labels.\n- D wrongly claims options cost nothing — the holder always pays a premium.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward = both obligated, zero cost to enter, linear. Option = only the writer obligated, holder pays a premium, nonlinear. Every derivative is also zero-sum: the long's gain equals the short's loss.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forwards/futures vs options\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward/futures\",\"items\":[\"BOTH sides obligated\",\"zero cost to enter\",\"linear payoff\"]},{\"h\":\"Option\",\"items\":[\"only WRITER obligated\",\"holder pays a premium\",\"nonlinear payoff\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A stock trades at $204 in New York and £150 in London while the exchange rate is 1.35 USD/GBP. Ignoring transaction costs, an arbitrageur trading 100 shares can lock in a riskless profit by:\nA) Buying in New York and selling in London for a profit of about $300. B) Buying in London and selling in New York for a profit of about $150. C) Buying in London and selling in New York for a profit of about $1,350. D) There is no arbitrage; the prices are equal once converted.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: convert the London price to dollars, find the cheaper market, and compute the per-100-share riskless profit.\n\n1. London price in dollars = £150 × 1.35 = **$202.50**.\n2. New York price = **$204.00**. London is cheaper, so **buy in London, sell in New York**.\n3. Per-share profit = $204.00 − $202.50 = $1.50.\n4. For 100 shares: 100 × $1.50 = **$150**.\n\nWhy B is correct: buy cheap (London, $202.50) and sell dear (New York, $204.00) for $1.50/share × 100 = $150.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A reverses the direction (buy high, sell low → a loss) and inflates the figure.\n- C uses the *gross* London proceeds (£150 × 1.35 × ... ) or treats $1,350 as profit — that is roughly the full converted price, not the spread.\n- D wrongly claims parity; $202.50 ≠ $204.00, leaving a $1.50 gap.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Convert both prices to one currency, buy in the cheaper market and sell in the dearer, and the profit is the *spread* × quantity — not the full price. Arbitrage demand closes the gap, which is why no-arbitrage pricing holds.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The 1995 collapse of Barings Bank and the 2008 Société Générale loss are both cited as derivatives-misuse cases. What was the common structural control failure, and what is the standard mitigant?\nA) Both losses came from a CCP default; the mitigant is to avoid central clearing. B) In both, one person controlled both trading and the back-office confirmation/settlement function, defeating segregation of duties; the mitigant is separating front and back office with independent valuation and position/loss limits. C) Both arose from clients posting too little collateral; the mitigant is higher initial margin. D) Both were caused by linear-derivative pricing errors; the mitigant is to trade only options.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify the shared control failure in Barings/SocGen and the appropriate mitigant.\n\n1. Barings (Nick Leeson) and SocGen (Jérôme Kerviel) both involved a trader who *also* controlled the back-office confirmation/settlement, letting them **conceal directional positions and losses** — a breakdown of **segregation of duties**.\n2. Mitigants: **separate the front office (trading) from the back office (settlement/confirmation)**, use **independent valuation**, and impose **position and loss limits** — plus scrutinize traders whose reported profits look too good for their mandate.\n\nWhy B is correct: the common thread is one person controlling both trading and its verification; the fix is segregation of duties and independent controls.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A invents a CCP default — these were rogue-trader/internal-control failures, not clearing-house failures.\n- C misattributes the cause to client collateral; the failures were internal control breakdowns.\n- D fabricates a pricing-error cause and a nonsensical \"trade only options\" remedy.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The rogue-trader lesson (Barings, SocGen) is *segregation of duties* — never let the trader also confirm/settle their own trades. Controls: front/back-office separation, independent valuation, position/loss limits, and suspicion of profits too good for the mandate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Barings & SocGen common failure\",\"options\":[\"A CCP default\",\"One person ran trading AND back office (no segregation of duties)\",\"Clients posting too little collateral\",\"Linear-derivative pricing errors\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The trader also controlled confirmation/settlement, hiding losses; fix = front/back-office separation.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"305","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Exchanges and OTC Markets","notes":"# 5. Exchanges and OTC Markets\n\nGARP tests this chapter conceptually with light computation: what exchanges provide, how clearing and margining remove counterparty risk, how OTC markets differ, the toolkit for mitigating bilateral counterparty risk (netting, collateral, SPVs, DPCs, monolines), and the post-crisis reforms.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe how exchanges can be used to alleviate counterparty risk.\n- Explain developments in clearing that reduce risk: direct clearing, clearing rings, and complete clearing through a central counterparty.\n- Define netting and describe a netting process.\n- Describe the implementation of a margining process and explain the determinants of, and calculate, initial and variation margin requirements.\n- Compare exchange-traded and OTC markets and describe their uses.\n- Identify the classes of derivative securities and explain the risks associated with them.\n- Identify risks associated with OTC markets and explain how these risks can be mitigated.\n- Describe the role of collateralization in the OTC market and compare it to the margining system.\n- Explain the use of special purpose vehicles (SPVs) in the OTC derivatives market.\n\n## What exchanges provide\n\nExchanges began as physical marketplaces for commodities (CBOT, 1848) and evolved into derivatives marketplaces. Their core contributions:\n\n- **Standardization** — contract size, quality of the underlying, delivery dates and locations are fixed by the exchange, so only price is negotiated. Standardization concentrates trading in a few contracts, which creates **liquidity**.\n- **Price transparency** — all trades occur at publicly visible prices; quotes and settlement prices are disseminated.\n- **Liquidity** — many buyers and sellers in one venue; positions can be closed out easily before delivery.\n- **Counterparty risk management** — via the clearing house (below).\n\n**Trading mechanics** moved from **open outcry** (traders shouting and hand-signaling in pits) to **electronic trading** (orders matched by computer). Electronic trading is faster, cheaper, less error-prone, and enables algorithmic and high-frequency trading — but removes the human market-making judgment of the pit.\n\n## Clearing on exchanges\n\nClearing developed in three stages:\n\n1. **Direct clearing** — two counterparties settle bilaterally; offsetting trades with the *same* counterparty can be netted.\n2. **Clearing rings** — groups of members net offsetting positions among themselves, so a trader can exit by ringing out against any ring member, not just the original counterparty.\n3. **Complete clearing (central counterparty)** — the modern model. Through **novation**, the original contract is replaced by two contracts: the clearing house becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer. Members face only the clearing house.\n\n### Margining\n\n- **Initial margin (IM)** — posted when the position is opened; sized to cover potential losses over the close-out period at a high confidence level (driven by the volatility of the underlying).\n- **Variation margin (VM)** — daily (or intraday) settlement of mark-to-market gains and losses. Losers pay VM; winners receive it. This resets the value of each contract to zero every day, so exposure cannot accumulate.\n- **Netting** — long and short positions in the same contract offset, reducing both margin requirements and exposure.\n\n**Members vs. non-members.** Only **clearing members** deal directly with the clearing house and contribute to its default fund. Non-members (including clients) must clear through a member, who is responsible to the clearing house for the client's positions and margin.\n\n## OTC markets\n\nThe over-the-counter market is a **network of dealers** (mostly banks) trading bilaterally by phone or electronic message, often via **interdealer brokers** who anonymously match dealers' interests. Key features:\n\n- **Bilateral** — each party bears the other's credit risk for the life of the trade.\n- **Customizable** — any size, maturity, underlying, or payoff; ideal for hedgers with non-standard exposures.\n- **Less transparent** — historically no public price tape or central record of positions.\n- Notional outstanding in OTC derivatives is several times larger than exchange-traded notional (dominated by interest rate swaps).\n\n## Mitigating OTC counterparty risk\n\n### Bilateral netting (ISDA master agreements)\n\nAn **ISDA master agreement** makes all transactions between two parties a single legal agreement. On default, all transactions are terminated and their values **netted** to one payment (**close-out netting**). Netting also applies to ordinary payments on the same day in the same currency (**payment netting**).\n\n### Worked example: gross vs. net exposure\n\nBank X has three derivatives with dealer Y, with current values **to Bank X** of +$10 million, −$4 million, and +$6 million.\n\n- **Without netting**, if Y defaults, X loses the positive-value trades but still owes the negative one: exposure = 10 + 6 = **$16 million**.\n- **With netting**, exposure = max(10 − 4 + 6, 0) = **$12 million**.\n\nNetting cut the exposure by $4 million — exactly the negative-value trade that can now be offset.\n\n### Collateralization (CSA)\n\nA **Credit Support Annex (CSA)** to the ISDA master agreement requires the party whose portfolio has negative value to post collateral, analogous to exchange variation margin. Key terms:\n\n- **Threshold** — uncollateralized exposure allowed before collateral must be posted (a $5 million threshold against a $12 million exposure requires $7 million of collateral). A zero threshold means full collateralization.\n- **Independent amount** — extra collateral posted regardless of mark-to-market, analogous to initial margin.\n- **Haircuts** — non-cash collateral is valued at a discount.\n- **Minimum transfer amount** — small collateral calls are not made.\n\nCompared with exchange margining, traditional CSA collateral was often one-way, subject to thresholds, disputed, and transferred less frequently — so it mitigates but does not eliminate counterparty risk.\n\n### Other mitigants\n\n| Mechanism | How it works | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Special purpose vehicle (SPV)** | Bankruptcy-remote entity holds collateral/transactions so one counterparty gets priority over the sponsor's other creditors | Transforms counterparty risk into legal risk; courts may consolidate the SPV with the sponsor |\n| **Derivative product company (DPC)** | Separately capitalized, AAA-rated subsidiary of a dealer through which it trades derivatives | Rating ultimately tied to the parent; wind-down triggered if the parent fails |\n| **Monolines / CDPCs** | Highly rated insurers selling credit protection (e.g., guaranteeing CDS or wrapping bonds), typically posting little or no collateral because of their AAA ratings | Massively leveraged credit exposure; in 2008 losses on structured products triggered downgrades, downgrades triggered collateral calls they could not meet — a death spiral. Protection buyers found their \"hedge\" worthless exactly when needed |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The monoline lesson is a classic exam trap: collateral agreements with **ratings-based triggers** create wrong-way, cliff-edge risk. A counterparty that posts no collateral while AAA must suddenly post huge amounts precisely when it is downgraded and least able to pay — so the protection fails exactly when it is most valuable.\n\n## Post-crisis reform of OTC markets\n\nThe 2009 G20 commitments (Dodd-Frank in the US, EMIR in the EU) require:\n\n1. **Mandatory central clearing** of standardized OTC derivatives through CCPs.\n2. **Trade reporting** of all OTC derivatives to trade repositories (transparency for regulators).\n3. Trading of standardized contracts on electronic platforms — **swap execution facilities (SEFs)** — where appropriate.\n4. **Higher capital and bilateral margin requirements** for derivatives that remain non-centrally cleared.\n\nThe result: the OTC market now resembles the exchange-traded market much more closely for standardized products.\n\n## Exchange-traded vs. OTC comparison\n\n| Feature | Exchange-traded | OTC |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contract terms | Standardized by exchange | Fully customizable |\n| Trading | Central venue (electronic/open outcry) | Bilateral dealer network, interdealer brokers, SEFs |\n| Counterparty | Clearing house (after novation) | The dealer (unless centrally cleared) |\n| Counterparty risk | Minimal — margining, netting, default fund | Present — mitigated by netting, collateral, CCPs post-crisis |\n| Price transparency | High — public quotes and settlement prices | Lower — improved by trade reporting |\n| Liquidity | High in standard contracts | Varies; can be low for bespoke trades |\n| Typical users | Speculators, standardized hedgers | Hedgers with custom needs, dealers |","flashcards":"**Q1.** What core contributions do exchanges provide?\n> **A:** Standardization (size, quality, delivery fixed so only price is negotiated, concentrating trading into liquidity), price transparency (public trade/settlement prices), liquidity (many buyers/sellers, easy close-out), and counterparty risk management via the clearing house.\n\n**Q2.** Open outcry vs. electronic trading\n> **A:** Open outcry: traders shout and hand-signal in pits. Electronic: orders matched by computer — faster, cheaper, less error-prone, enables algorithmic/HFT — but removes the human market-making judgment of the pit.\n\n**Q3.** The three stages of clearing development\n> **A:** (1) Direct clearing — bilateral settlement, same-counterparty offsets netted. (2) Clearing rings — members net offsetting positions among themselves, exiting against any ring member. (3) Complete clearing (CCP) — the modern model via novation.\n\n**Q4.** What is novation by a central counterparty?\n> **A:** The original contract is replaced by two contracts: the clearing house becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer. Members face only the clearing house, not each other.\n\n**Q5.** Initial margin vs. variation margin\n> **A:** Initial margin: posted when a position opens, sized to cover potential losses over the close-out period at high confidence (driven by underlying volatility). Variation margin: daily mark-to-market settlement — losers pay, winners receive — resetting each contract's value to zero daily so exposure cannot accumulate.\n\n**Q6.** Clearing members vs. non-members\n> **A:** Only clearing members deal directly with the clearing house and contribute to its default fund. Non-members (including clients) clear through a member, who is responsible to the clearing house for the client's positions and margin.\n\n**Q7.** Key features of OTC markets\n> **A:** A network of dealers (mostly banks) trading bilaterally, often via interdealer brokers who anonymously match interests. Bilateral (each bears the other's credit risk for the trade's life), customizable, less transparent, and far larger by notional (dominated by interest rate swaps).\n\n**Q8.** What does an ISDA master agreement with close-out netting do?\n> **A:** It makes all transactions between two parties a single legal agreement. On default, all transactions terminate and net to one payment (close-out netting). Same-day, same-currency ordinary payments also net (payment netting).\n\n**Q9.** Gross vs. net counterparty exposure calculation\n> **A:** Without netting, only positive-value trades count (you still owe negatives). With netting, exposure = max(sum of all values, 0). E.g., +10, −4, +6: without = 16; with = max(12, 0) = 12.\n\n**Q10.** What is a CSA and its key terms?\n> **A:** A Credit Support Annex to the ISDA master requires the party with negative portfolio value to post collateral (like exchange VM). Terms: threshold (uncollateralized exposure allowed), independent amount (extra collateral, like IM), haircuts (discount on non-cash), minimum transfer amount (skip small calls).\n\n**Q11.** Threshold collateral calculation\n> **A:** Collateral posted = exposure − threshold (if positive). A $5M threshold against $12M exposure requires $7M of collateral. A zero threshold means full collateralization.\n\n**Q12.** SPV vs. DPC vs. monoline as counterparty-risk mitigants\n> **A:** SPV: bankruptcy-remote entity holding collateral/transactions for priority (weakness: legal/consolidation risk). DPC: separately capitalized AAA dealer subsidiary (weakness: rating tied to parent). Monolines/CDPCs: highly rated insurers selling credit protection with little collateral (weakness: massive leverage, ratings-triggered death spiral in 2008).\n\n**Q13.** Exam trap: why did monoline insurers fail in 2008?\n> **A:** Ratings-based collateral triggers created wrong-way, cliff-edge risk: a counterparty posting no collateral while AAA must suddenly post huge amounts precisely when downgraded and least able to pay — so the protection failed exactly when it was most valuable.\n\n**Q14.** The 2009 G20 post-crisis OTC reforms\n> **A:** (1) Mandatory central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives through CCPs. (2) Trade reporting to repositories. (3) Trading standardized contracts on electronic platforms (SEFs) where appropriate. (4) Higher capital and bilateral margin for non-centrally-cleared derivatives. Bespoke OTC contracts still exist — nothing forced conversion to futures.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Which of the following is a core function that derivatives exchanges provide?\nA) Fully customized, bilaterally negotiated contract terms B) Standardization of contracts, which concentrates trading and creates liquidity C) Guaranteed elimination of all market (price) risk D) Private prices visible only to the two trading parties\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what an exchange fundamentally does for traders.\n\nExchanges fix the contract terms — size, quality of the underlying, delivery dates and locations — so the only thing left to negotiate is price. This standardization funnels everyone into a handful of identical contracts, which gathers many buyers and sellers in one place and creates liquidity (you can easily get in and out). Exchanges also provide transparent public prices and manage counterparty risk through a clearing house.\n\nAnalogy: standardized shoe sizes mean millions of people can buy and resell the same size easily. If every shoe were custom-made, there'd be no easy resale market — standardization creates a liquid market.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (customization) describes the OTC market, the opposite of an exchange.\n- C is false — exchanges manage COUNTERPARTY risk, not market/price risk; prices still move and you can still lose money.\n- D is false — exchange prices are PUBLIC and transparent, not private.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Exchange benefits = standardization → liquidity, price transparency, counterparty risk management. It does NOT remove market risk. Customization belongs to OTC.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"How standardization creates liquidity\",\"steps\":[\"Exchange fixes contract terms\",\"Only price left to negotiate\",\"Trading concentrates\",\"Many buyers & sellers\",\"Liquidity\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Under novation by an exchange clearing house, what happens to a trade between an original buyer and seller?\nA) The original counterparties remain liable to each other, with the clearing house only guaranteeing B) The original contract is replaced by two contracts, each with the clearing house as the counterparty C) Contracts are netted multilaterally among clearing-ring members D) The buyer's obligations are transferred to a special purpose vehicle\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what novation does in central clearing.\n\nNovation means the clearing house steps into the middle and the original contract is legally torn up and replaced by TWO new contracts: the clearing house becomes the buyer to the original seller, and the seller to the original buyer. After this, neither original party faces the other anymore — each faces only the clearing house. That's how a CCP concentrates and manages counterparty risk.\n\nAnalogy: instead of you lending money directly to a stranger, a trusted bank steps in: the bank now owes you, and the stranger now owes the bank. You never deal with the stranger again.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes a mere guarantee, but novation goes further — the original contract is REPLACED, not just guaranteed.\n- C describes clearing RINGS, an earlier stage of clearing, not novation by a CCP.\n- D describes SPV usage in OTC markets, not central-clearing novation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Novation = original contract replaced by two contracts, CCP in the middle (buyer to every seller, seller to every buyer). Members then face only the clearing house.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Novation by a CCP\",\"steps\":[\"Buyer trades with seller\",\"CCP steps in\",\"Original contract torn up\",\"Two new contracts created\",\"Each faces only the CCP\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which statement best describes the role of variation margin in exchange clearing?\nA) It is posted once when the position opens and never adjusted B) It is the daily settlement of mark-to-market gains and losses, resetting each contract's value to zero C) It is extra collateral posted regardless of price moves D) It is a fee paid to the exchange for trade execution\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what variation margin (VM) is.\n\nEach day, positions are marked to market (revalued at current prices). Whoever lost value that day pays variation margin; whoever gained receives it. This daily cash settlement of gains and losses resets each contract's value back to zero every day, so losses can't quietly pile up and create a big unpaid exposure.\n\nAnalogy: it's like settling up a running tab with friends every single night instead of letting it grow into a huge bill — small daily settlements stop a dangerous balance from building.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes INITIAL margin (posted at the start, sized for the close-out period), not variation margin.\n- C describes an \"independent amount\" / extra cushion, not variation margin.\n- D is false — VM is the settlement of P&L, not an execution fee.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Initial margin = posted upfront, covers potential close-out losses (driven by volatility). Variation margin = daily settlement of mark-to-market P&L, resets value to zero. Don't mix them up.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Initial vs variation margin\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Initial margin\",\"items\":[\"posted at the open\",\"covers close-out losses\",\"driven by volatility/horizon\"]},{\"h\":\"Variation margin\",\"items\":[\"settled daily\",\"mark-to-market P&L\",\"resets contract value to zero\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A bank has four trades with one counterparty worth (to the bank) +$8M, −$5M, +$3M, and −$2M. Its current credit exposure WITH and WITHOUT close-out netting is closest to:\nA) $4M with netting; $11M without B) $11M with netting; $4M without C) $4M with netting; $18M without D) $11M with netting; $11M without\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks how netting changes credit exposure if the counterparty defaults.\n\nWithout netting: if the counterparty defaults, the bank still loses the positive-value trades (the money owed TO it) but cannot escape paying the negative ones it owes. So exposure counts only the positives:\n$$8 + 3 = 11 \\text{ (million)}$$\n\nWith netting (close-out netting under an ISDA master agreement): all trades are combined into one net figure, and exposure is that net amount if positive (you can't have negative exposure):\n$$\\max(8 - 5 + 3 - 2,\\, 0) = \\max(4, 0) = 4 \\text{ (million)}$$\n\nSo $4M with netting, $11M without. Answer A.\n\nAnalogy: without netting, you must pay every debt you owe but can only hope to collect what others owe you. With netting, everything is added into one balance — your debts cancel against what you're owed, leaving a single smaller figure.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B reverses the two values.\n- C ($18M without) wrongly adds the absolute values of ALL trades (8+5+3+2); only POSITIVE-value trades count as exposure without netting.\n- D ignores netting's benefit entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Without netting, exposure = sum of POSITIVE values only. With netting, exposure = max(sum of all values, 0). Netting reduces exposure by letting negatives offset positives.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Credit exposure: netting vs not\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Without netting\",\"items\":[\"sum POSITIVE trades only\",\"8 + 3 = $11M\",\"negatives can't offset\"]},{\"h\":\"With netting\",\"items\":[\"max(sum all, 0)\",\"8-5+3-2 = $4M\",\"negatives offset positives\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In an OTC Credit Support Annex (CSA), the \"threshold\" refers to:\nA) The minimum size below which a collateral call is not made B) The amount of uncollateralized exposure allowed before collateral must be posted C) The discount applied to non-cash collateral D) Extra collateral posted regardless of mark-to-market value\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the meaning of the CSA \"threshold.\"\n\nA threshold is the amount of exposure a party is willing to leave UNCOLLATERALIZED before requiring collateral. For example, with a $5M threshold against a $12M exposure, only $12M − $5M = $7M of collateral must be posted. A zero threshold means full collateralization.\n\nAnalogy: a threshold is like a credit limit you extend to a friend before asking for any deposit — you let the tab run up to a set amount before requiring security.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes the \"minimum transfer amount\" (small calls aren't made), a different CSA term.\n- C describes a \"haircut\" (discount on non-cash collateral), not the threshold.\n- D describes the \"independent amount\" (extra collateral like initial margin), not the threshold.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CSA terms — Threshold = uncollateralized exposure allowed; Independent amount = extra cushion (like IM); Haircut = discount on non-cash collateral; Minimum transfer amount = ignore tiny calls. Keep these four straight.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"CSA \\\"threshold\\\" means\",\"options\":[\"Min size below which no collateral call is made\",\"Uncollateralized exposure allowed before posting\",\"Discount on non-cash collateral\",\"Extra collateral regardless of MTM\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Threshold = exposure left uncollateralized before collateral must be posted.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Why did monoline insurers fail as derivative counterparties during the 2008 crisis?\nA) They posted too much collateral, draining their liquidity B) Their exposures were fully netted away under ISDA agreements C) Downgrades triggered collateral calls they could not meet, after years of posting little or no collateral D) Central clearing mandates forced them out of business\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the mechanism behind the monoline failures.\n\nMonolines were highly rated (AAA) insurers that sold credit protection (like guaranteeing CDS). Because of their top ratings, they posted little or no collateral. But their contracts had ratings-based triggers: if they were downgraded, they'd suddenly have to post huge amounts of collateral. In 2008, losses on structured products caused downgrades, the downgrades triggered massive collateral calls, and they couldn't meet them — a death spiral. Protection buyers found their \"hedge\" was worthless exactly when they needed it most.\n\nAnalogy: imagine a guard who promised to step in only if his fitness rating ever dropped — but the moment his rating dropped (the worst possible moment), he was suddenly required to do far more than he could handle, and collapsed.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is backwards — they posted TOO LITTLE collateral while rated AAA, then faced sudden huge calls.\n- B is false — netting didn't save them; the problem was the collateral cliff from downgrades.\n- D is false — central clearing mandates came AFTER the crisis as a reform; they didn't cause monoline failures.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Monoline lesson = ratings-based collateral triggers create wrong-way, cliff-edge risk. The counterparty must post a flood of collateral precisely when downgraded and least able to pay — so the protection fails when it's most needed.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Why monolines failed (2008)\",\"steps\":[\"AAA, posted little collateral\",\"Structured-product losses\",\"Downgraded\",\"Ratings trigger huge collateral calls\",\"Cannot meet -> fail\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A special purpose vehicle (SPV) used in the OTC derivatives market is best described as:\nA) A central counterparty that novates all exchange trades B) A bankruptcy-remote entity that holds collateral/transactions so one counterparty gets priority over the sponsor's other creditors C) A government fund that insures derivative losses D) An interdealer broker that anonymously matches dealers\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what an SPV is in OTC markets.\n\nAn SPV is a separate, \"bankruptcy-remote\" legal entity. By routing transactions or holding collateral through the SPV, a counterparty can get priority over the sponsor's other creditors if things go wrong — improving its position. The catch: this converts counterparty risk into LEGAL risk, because courts might \"consolidate\" the SPV back with the sponsor, undoing the protection.\n\nAnalogy: it's like putting valuables in a separate locked safe deposit box (in a separate legal name) so that if the main household goes bankrupt, those valuables are protected for a specific person. But a court might still rule the box belongs to the household.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes a central counterparty (CCP), not an SPV.\n- C is false — no government fund insures derivative losses through SPVs.\n- D describes an interdealer broker, a different OTC participant.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SPV = bankruptcy-remote entity giving a counterparty priority; its weakness is legal risk (possible court consolidation with the sponsor). Don't confuse SPVs, DPCs, monolines, and CCPs.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What is an SPV in OTC markets?\",\"options\":[\"A CCP that novates exchange trades\",\"Bankruptcy-remote entity giving a counterparty priority\",\"Government fund insuring derivative losses\",\"Interdealer broker matching dealers\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"SPV isolates assets so one counterparty ranks ahead of the sponsor's other creditors (residual: legal risk).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which of the following was NOT one of the post-crisis G20 reforms of OTC derivatives markets?\nA) Mandatory central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives through CCPs B) Reporting of all OTC derivatives to trade repositories C) A requirement that ALL OTC derivatives be converted into exchange-traded futures D) Higher capital and bilateral margin requirements for non-centrally cleared derivatives\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which item was NOT a real reform.\n\nAfter 2008, the G20 (implemented via Dodd-Frank in the US and EMIR in the EU) required: mandatory central clearing of STANDARDIZED OTC derivatives, reporting of all OTC trades to repositories (transparency), trading standardized contracts on electronic platforms (SEFs) where appropriate, and higher capital/margin for derivatives that remain non-centrally cleared. Crucially, bespoke (custom) OTC contracts STILL EXIST — nothing forced everything to convert into exchange-traded futures.\n\nAnalogy: the reforms made the OTC market behave MORE like an exchange for the common, standardized products, but they didn't ban custom-tailored contracts for special needs.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A, B, and D are all genuine G20 reforms.\n- C is the false statement — custom OTC contracts survive; only standardized ones must be cleared and (where appropriate) traded on platforms.\n\n📌 Exam tip: G20 OTC reforms = central clearing of standardized contracts, trade reporting, electronic platforms (SEFs), higher margin/capital for non-cleared. Bespoke OTC trades remain — they were NOT all converted to futures.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"NOT a post-crisis G20 OTC reform\",\"options\":[\"Central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives\",\"Reporting to trade repositories\",\"ALL OTC converted into exchange-traded futures\",\"Higher capital/margin for non-cleared trades\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Bespoke OTC contracts still exist; only standardized ones must clear/trade on platforms.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Compared with exchange margining, traditional OTC collateralization under a CSA generally:\nA) Eliminates counterparty risk entirely, just like a clearing house B) Mitigates but does not eliminate counterparty risk, being often one-way, subject to thresholds, and transferred less frequently C) Requires daily variation margin enforced by a central counterparty D) Is identical to exchange margining in every respect\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how OTC CSA collateral compares to exchange margining.\n\nExchange margining is strict: daily (or intraday) variation margin, enforced by a clearing house, that effectively resets exposure to zero. Traditional CSA collateral is looser: it was often one-way (only one side posts), subject to thresholds (some exposure left uncollateralized), sometimes disputed, and transferred less frequently. So it REDUCES counterparty risk but does not fully eliminate it the way an exchange clearing house does.\n\nAnalogy: exchange margining is like a strict daily settling-up enforced by a referee. A CSA is more like a casual agreement between two people to put up some security sometimes — helpful, but with gaps.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — only an exchange/CCP comes close to eliminating counterparty risk; a CSA leaves residual risk.\n- C is false — there's no central counterparty in traditional bilateral CSA collateral, and transfers were less frequent than exchange VM.\n- D is false — they differ in frequency, enforcement, thresholds, and directionality.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Exchange margin = daily, CCP-enforced, resets exposure to zero. Traditional CSA = often one-way, thresholds, less frequent, disputable → mitigates but doesn't eliminate counterparty risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Exchange margin vs OTC CSA collateral\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Exchange margin\",\"items\":[\"daily VM, CCP-enforced\",\"two-way\",\"resets exposure to zero\"]},{\"h\":\"CSA collateral\",\"items\":[\"often one-way, thresholds\",\"less frequent, disputable\",\"mitigates but doesn't eliminate\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which stage of clearing development allows a trader to exit a position by \"ringing out\" against any member of a group, rather than only the original counterparty?\nA) Direct clearing B) Clearing rings C) Complete clearing through a central counterparty D) Bilateral netting under an ISDA agreement\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which clearing stage lets you offset against any ring member.\n\nClearing developed in three stages. Clearing rings are the middle stage: a group of members agrees to net offsetting positions among themselves, so a trader can close out (exit) by \"ringing out\" against ANY member of the ring, not just the specific person they originally traded with. This is more flexible than direct clearing but less complete than a central counterparty.\n\nAnalogy: clearing rings are like a group of friends who agree that any of them can settle a debt on behalf of the others — you don't have to track down the exact person you originally owed.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (direct clearing) settles bilaterally; you can only net with the SAME original counterparty, not any member.\n- C (complete clearing / CCP) goes further — through novation, everyone faces the clearing house itself; it's the modern, most complete stage, not the ring stage.\n- D (ISDA bilateral netting) is an OTC counterparty-risk tool, not a stage of exchange-clearing development.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Three clearing stages — Direct (same counterparty only) → Clearing rings (offset against any ring member) → Complete clearing/CCP (novation; everyone faces the clearing house). The ring stage is the \"any member\" middle step.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Stages of clearing development\",\"steps\":[\"Direct (same counterparty only)\",\"Clearing rings (any ring member)\",\"Complete clearing / CCP (novation)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A bank has four trades with one counterparty worth (to the bank) +$8M, −$5M, +$3M, and −$2M. Its current credit exposure with and without close-out netting is closest to:\nA) $4M with netting; $11M without B) $11M with netting; $4M without C) $4M with netting; $18M without D) $11M with netting; $11M without\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** With netting: max(8 − 5 + 3 − 2, 0) = $4M. Without: only positive values count, 8 + 3 = $11M.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Under novation by an exchange clearing house:\nA) The original counterparties remain liable to each other, with the clearing house as guarantor B) The original contract is replaced by two contracts, each with the clearing house as counterparty C) Contracts are netted multilaterally among ring members D) The buyer's obligations are transferred to an SPV\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Novation tears up the original contract and substitutes the clearing house as buyer to the seller and seller to the buyer.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The primary reason monoline insurers failed as counterparties in 2008 was:\nA) They posted too much collateral, draining their liquidity B) Their exposures were fully netted under ISDA agreements C) Downgrades triggered collateral calls they could not meet, after years of posting little or no collateral D) Central clearing mandates forced them out of the market\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Ratings-based collateral triggers created a cliff-edge: downgrade → massive collateral calls → failure exactly when protection was needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which of the following was NOT a post-crisis G20 reform of OTC derivatives markets?\nA) Mandatory central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives B) Reporting of all OTC derivatives to trade repositories C) A requirement that all OTC derivatives be converted to exchange-traded futures D) Higher capital and margin requirements for non-centrally cleared derivatives\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Standardized contracts must be cleared and traded on platforms (SEFs) where appropriate, but bespoke OTC contracts still exist; nothing forced conversion to futures.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Which statement best summarizes the post-crisis (G20/Dodd-Frank) reforms to the OTC derivatives market?\nA) All OTC derivatives were banned and moved to futures exchanges B) Standardized OTC derivatives between financial institutions must be centrally cleared, traded on electronic platforms (SEFs) where appropriate, and reported to trade repositories C) OTC derivatives became exempt from margin requirements to encourage liquidity D) Bilateral trading was prohibited even for non-standard derivatives\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The three pillars: mandatory central clearing for standardized trades between financial counterparties, platform trading, and trade reporting. Non-standard bilateral trades remain permitted but face margin requirements (A, D too strong).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Post-crisis OTC reforms\",\"root\":\"G20 / Dodd-Frank\",\"children\":[\"Central clearing (standardized)\",\"Platform trading (SEFs)\",\"Trade reporting (repositories)\",\"Margin on bilateral trades\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** Bank X has five derivative trades with counterparty Y, with current values *to Bank X* of +$14M, −$9M, +$7M, −$3M, and +$5M, all under a single ISDA master agreement with close-out netting. If Y defaults today, what are Bank X's credit exposures *with* and *without* enforceable netting, respectively?\nA) $14M with netting; $26M without B) $26M with netting; $14M without C) $14M with netting; $14M without D) $12M with netting; $26M without\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate the ask: compute current credit exposure under close-out netting (net the whole portfolio, floored at zero) versus no netting (only positive-value trades count).\n\n1. **With netting:** sum all values, floor at zero: $$\\max(14 - 9 + 7 - 3 + 5, \\, 0) = \\max(14, 0) = \\$14M$$.\n2. **Without netting:** on default, X collects only the positive-value trades but must still pay the negative ones, so exposure = sum of the *positive* values only: $$14 + 7 + 5 = \\$26M$$.\n\nWhy A is correct: netting nets to $14M; without netting the negative trades cannot offset, leaving $26M of positive-value exposure.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B swaps the two figures.\n- C wrongly applies netting to both — without netting the −$9M and −$3M cannot reduce exposure.\n- D miscomputes the net sum (14 − 9 + 7 − 3 + 5 = 14, not 12).\n\n📌 Exam tip: With netting, exposure = max(Σ all MTM, 0). Without netting, exposure = Σ (positive MTM only) — you lose the in-the-money trades but still owe the out-of-the-money ones. Netting always reduces (never increases) exposure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Credit exposure: netting vs not\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"With netting\",\"items\":[\"max(Σ all, 0)\",\"14-9+7-3+5 = $14M\",\"negatives offset positives\"]},{\"h\":\"Without netting\",\"items\":[\"Σ POSITIVE only\",\"14+7+5 = $26M\",\"still owe the negatives\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A CSA between two dealers specifies a $5M threshold, a $1M independent amount, and a $0.5M minimum transfer amount. Party A's portfolio with Party B currently has a value of +$18M to Party B (i.e., −$18M to Party A). How much collateral must Party A post to Party B (assume cash collateral, no haircut, and that the previous posted balance was zero)?\nA) $18M B) $13M C) $14M D) $12M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate the ask: compute the collateral Party A (the out-of-the-money party) must post given a threshold, an independent amount, and a minimum transfer amount.\n\n1. Mark-to-market exposure that B has to A = $18M.\n2. The **threshold** is uncollateralized exposure allowed before collateral is required: required variation collateral = exposure − threshold = $18M − $5M = $13M.\n3. The **independent amount** is extra collateral posted *regardless* of MTM (like initial margin), so add it: $13M + $1M = **$14M**.\n4. The required transfer ($14M) exceeds the **minimum transfer amount** ($0.5M), so the full call is made.\n\nWhy C is correct: $18M − $5M threshold + $1M independent amount = $14M.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ignores both the threshold and adds nothing — full $18M is not required because the first $5M is uncollateralized.\n- B applies the threshold but forgets the independent amount ($13M).\n- D subtracts the independent amount instead of adding it ($13M − $1M), misreading its direction.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Collateral = (exposure − threshold) + independent amount, provided this exceeds the minimum transfer amount. Threshold *reduces* the call; the independent amount *adds* to it. Haircuts (if any) increase the amount of non-cash collateral needed.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CSA collateral call\",\"steps\":[\"Exposure $18M\",\"minus threshold $5M = $13M\",\"plus independent amount $1M = $14M\",\"check > min transfer $0.5M\",\"Post $14M\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Under **novation** by a central counterparty (CCP) on an exchange:\nA) The original counterparties remain liable to each other, with the clearing house acting only as a guarantor. B) The original contract is replaced by two new contracts — the CCP becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer — so members face only the CCP. C) Offsetting positions are netted multilaterally among clearing-ring members without any contract substitution. D) The buyer's obligations are transferred to a bankruptcy-remote SPV.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: define novation as performed by a CCP.\n\n1. **Novation** legally *tears up* the original bilateral contract and substitutes **two** new contracts.\n2. The CCP interposes itself: it becomes the **buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer**, so each member's only counterparty is the CCP.\n\nWhy B is correct: that is the precise definition of complete clearing via novation.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A describes a mere *guarantee* — but novation actually replaces the original contract; the original parties no longer face each other.\n- C describes *clearing rings* (an earlier, partial stage), not novation by a CCP.\n- D confuses novation with an SPV structure used in OTC markets.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Novation = original contract replaced by two CCP contracts; members face only the clearing house. This (plus margining, netting, and the default fund) is how exchanges remove counterparty risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Novation by a CCP\",\"steps\":[\"Buyer trades with seller\",\"CCP interposes\",\"Original contract torn up\",\"Two new contracts\",\"Each faces only the CCP\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** During 2008, monoline insurers that had sold credit protection (e.g., on structured products) failed as counterparties. The mechanism most often cited is:\nA) They posted excessive collateral up front, draining liquidity before any losses occurred. B) Their exposures were fully netted under ISDA agreements, which paradoxically increased risk. C) They posted little or no collateral while AAA-rated; when structured-product losses triggered downgrades, ratings-based collateral triggers forced huge calls they could not meet — so the protection failed exactly when it was needed. D) Mandatory central clearing forced them to exit the market, defaulting on open trades.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate the ask: identify the monoline failure mechanism.\n\n1. Monolines were **highly rated (AAA)** and therefore posted **little or no collateral** against the protection they sold.\n2. As structured-product losses mounted, they were **downgraded**.\n3. Their collateral agreements had **ratings-based triggers**: a downgrade forced **massive collateral calls** precisely when they were weakest — a cliff-edge, wrong-way risk.\n4. Unable to meet the calls, they failed, so protection buyers' \"hedge\" was worthless **exactly when it was needed**.\n\nWhy C is correct: it captures the no-collateral-while-AAA → downgrade → collateral-call death spiral.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A reverses reality — they posted *little* collateral, not excessive amounts.\n- B invents a netting-increases-risk paradox; netting reduces exposure.\n- D is anachronistic — central clearing mandates came *after* the crisis and did not cause monoline failures.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The monoline lesson = ratings-based collateral triggers create wrong-way, cliff-edge risk: a counterparty posting nothing while AAA must suddenly post huge collateral right when downgraded and least able to pay. Protection fails when most valuable.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Monoline failure mechanism (2008)\",\"steps\":[\"AAA, posted little collateral\",\"Structured-product losses\",\"Downgraded\",\"Ratings trigger huge collateral calls\",\"Cannot meet -> fail\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which of the following was **NOT** part of the 2009 G20 post-crisis reforms of OTC derivatives markets (implemented via Dodd-Frank and EMIR)?\nA) Mandatory central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives through CCPs. B) Reporting of all OTC derivatives to trade repositories. C) A requirement that all OTC derivatives be converted into exchange-traded futures, eliminating bespoke OTC contracts. D) Higher capital and bilateral margin requirements for derivatives that remain non-centrally cleared.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate the ask: identify the statement that is *not* a real post-crisis reform.\n\n1. Real reforms: (A) mandatory central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives; (B) trade reporting to repositories; (D) higher capital/margin on non-cleared trades; plus trading standardized contracts on **swap execution facilities (SEFs)** where appropriate.\n2. **C is false:** bespoke OTC contracts still exist. The reforms pushed *standardized* contracts toward clearing and electronic platforms, but did **not** force conversion of everything into exchange-traded futures.\n\nWhy C is correct (as the NOT): no rule mandated converting all OTC derivatives into futures.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A, B, and D are all genuine G20/Dodd-Frank/EMIR reforms — choosing any of them misreads the \"NOT.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: The four pillars are (1) clearing of standardized OTC derivatives, (2) trade reporting, (3) SEF trading where appropriate, (4) higher capital/margin on non-cleared trades. Customized OTC contracts survive — nothing forced them into futures.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"NOT a G20 OTC reform\",\"options\":[\"Mandatory central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives\",\"Reporting to trade repositories\",\"ALL OTC converted into exchange-traded futures\",\"Higher capital/margin for non-cleared trades\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Bespoke OTC contracts still exist; only standardized ones must clear/trade on platforms.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A clearing house sets initial margin to cover potential losses over the close-out period at a high confidence level, and collects variation margin daily. Which statement is most accurate?\nA) Initial margin is driven mainly by the underlying's volatility and the close-out horizon; variation margin settles daily mark-to-market gains and losses, resetting each contract's value to zero so exposure cannot accumulate. B) Variation margin is posted once at inception; initial margin is paid daily by the losing side. C) Both initial and variation margin are fixed amounts unrelated to the underlying's volatility. D) Variation margin is collected only from non-clearing members, while clearing members post only initial margin.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate the ask: distinguish initial margin (IM) from variation margin (VM) and state their determinants.\n\n1. **Initial margin** is posted when the position opens, sized to cover potential losses over the **close-out period** at a high confidence level — so it is driven by the **volatility of the underlying** and the close-out horizon.\n2. **Variation margin** is the **daily** (or intraday) settlement of mark-to-market gains and losses: losers pay, winners receive, which **resets each contract's value to zero daily**, preventing exposure from accumulating.\n\nWhy A is correct: it correctly pairs IM (volatility/horizon driven, posted at open) and VM (daily MTM settlement that resets value to zero).\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B reverses the two: VM is daily (not once at inception); IM is posted at the open (not daily by the loser).\n- C wrongly claims both are volatility-independent — IM is explicitly volatility-driven.\n- D garbles the member structure: VM applies to all positions; non-members clear *through* members.\n\n📌 Exam tip: IM = posted at open, sized by volatility × close-out horizon (covers gap risk). VM = daily MTM cash settlement that zeroes out accumulated exposure. Netting reduces both.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Initial vs variation margin\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Initial margin\",\"items\":[\"posted at the open\",\"sized by volatility x horizon\",\"covers close-out/gap risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Variation margin\",\"items\":[\"settled daily\",\"mark-to-market P&L\",\"resets value to zero\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A dealer wishes to give a particular OTC counterparty priority over its other creditors by isolating certain transactions and collateral in a bankruptcy-remote entity. This describes a ______, whose principal residual risk is ______.\nA) derivative product company (DPC); that its rating is tied to the parent and it is wound down if the parent fails B) special purpose vehicle (SPV); legal risk, since courts may consolidate the SPV with the sponsor C) central counterparty (CCP); that novation eliminates all counterparty risk D) monoline insurer; that it posts too much collateral\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate the ask: identify the structure that isolates transactions/collateral in a bankruptcy-remote entity to give one counterparty priority, and its key residual risk.\n\n1. A **special purpose vehicle (SPV)** is a bankruptcy-remote entity that holds collateral/transactions so a chosen counterparty ranks ahead of the sponsor's other creditors.\n2. Its principal residual risk is **legal risk**: a court may **consolidate** the SPV with the sponsor (piercing bankruptcy-remoteness), defeating the priority — it *transforms* counterparty risk into legal risk rather than eliminating it.\n\nWhy B is correct: SPV = bankruptcy-remote priority vehicle; main residual risk is legal/consolidation risk.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A describes a **DPC** (a separately capitalized AAA dealer subsidiary) — a different structure; the prompt's \"isolate collateral for priority\" is the SPV.\n- C is a CCP, which manages risk via novation/margin, not by giving one counterparty priority — and novation does not \"eliminate all\" risk.\n- D is a monoline, an insurer selling protection, not a bankruptcy-remote holding vehicle.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SPV = bankruptcy-remote, gives a counterparty priority over the sponsor's creditors → residual *legal/consolidation* risk. DPC = capitalized AAA subsidiary → rating tied to parent. Monoline = AAA protection seller → ratings-trigger cliff risk. Know which is which.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Bankruptcy-remote priority vehicle\",\"options\":[\"DPC: rating tied to parent\",\"SPV: residual legal/consolidation risk\",\"CCP: novation eliminates all risk\",\"Monoline: posts too much collateral\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"An SPV isolates collateral for priority; courts may consolidate it with the sponsor (legal risk).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"306","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Central Clearing","notes":"# 6. Central Clearing\n\nGARP tests CCP mechanics (novation, multilateral netting), the margin stack, the exact order of the default waterfall, and the balanced view of central clearing's advantages and risks.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the mechanics of a central counterparty (CCP): novation, multilateral netting, and the CCP as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer.\n- Describe the regulatory initiatives requiring central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives and their impact.\n- Describe advantages and disadvantages of central clearing of OTC derivatives.\n- Compare margin requirements in centrally cleared and bilateral markets, and explain how margin mitigates risk.\n- Describe the CCP loss waterfall and the order in which resources are consumed after a member default.\n- Explain how a CCP manages a clearing member default, including porting and auctioning.\n- Identify and explain the types of risks faced by CCPs and the risks to clearing members and non-members.\n- Compare principal-to-principal and agency client clearing models.\n\n## CCP mechanics: novation\n\nWhen a trade is accepted for clearing, **novation** replaces the bilateral contract with two contracts: the **CCP becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer**. Members no longer care about each other's creditworthiness — only the CCP's. The CCP runs a **matched book** (every long offset by a short), so it has no market risk, only **counterparty risk**: if a member defaults, the book is no longer matched.\n\n## Multilateral netting\n\nBilateral netting compresses exposures between *pairs*; a CCP nets across *all* members.\n\n### Numeric example\n\nThree dealers have bilateral obligations: A owes B $20M, B owes C $30M, and C owes A $40M.\n\n**Bilaterally**, total amounts owed = 20 + 30 + 40 = **$90M** of gross flows, and each dealer carries exposure to a specific counterparty (e.g., A is owed $40M by C).\n\n**Through a CCP**, each dealer nets to a single position against the CCP:\n\n| Dealer | Pays | Receives | Net vs. CCP |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 20 | 40 | receives $20M |\n| B | 30 | 20 | pays $10M |\n| C | 40 | 30 | pays $10M |\n\nTotal flows fall from $90M to $40M (20 + 10 + 10), and no dealer depends on any other specific dealer. Note multilateral netting helps most when exposures are well distributed; it can *reduce* netting benefits if it splits products across multiple CCPs that were previously netted bilaterally under one ISDA agreement.\n\n## The margin stack\n\n| Resource | Who pays | How it is set | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Variation margin (VM)** | Loser each day (or intraday) | Daily mark-to-market change | Settles gains/losses so exposure never accumulates |\n| **Initial margin (IM)** | Every member, per portfolio | Covers potential close-out losses over a margin period of risk (e.g., 5 days) at a high confidence level (e.g., 99%+), via SPAN/VaR-type models; rises with volatility and concentration | Buffer against losses between last VM payment and close-out of a defaulter |\n| **Default fund contribution** | Every clearing member | Sized to extreme but plausible scenarios — typically the default of the largest one or two members (\"Cover 1\"/\"Cover 2\") | Mutualized layer against losses exceeding a defaulter's IM |\n\nCompared with bilateral markets: CCP margining is two-way, zero-threshold, marked daily, and includes IM — historically stricter than typical CSA collateral. Post-crisis rules now also impose IM and VM on non-cleared bilateral trades, narrowing the gap.\n\nRegulatory context: the post-2009 G20/Dodd-Frank/EMIR mandates pushed standardized OTC interest rate swaps and index CDS into CCPs, making CCP risk management — and the waterfall below — central to systemic stability.\n\n## The default waterfall\n\nWhen a clearing member defaults, losses are absorbed in this exact order:\n\n1. **Defaulting member's initial margin**\n2. **Defaulting member's default fund contribution**\n3. **CCP's own capital (\"skin in the game\")** — a pre-funded tranche of CCP equity\n4. **Surviving members' default fund contributions** (loss mutualization)\n5. **Further assessments / recovery tools** — rights of assessment (additional cash calls on survivors, often capped), variation margin gains haircutting, partial tear-up of contracts; failing all, CCP resolution or failure\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exam trap: a **non-defaulting** member's initial margin is NOT mutualized — it is returned if that member never defaults and cannot be used to cover another member's losses. Only the **default fund contributions** of survivors (plus assessment rights) are mutualized. IM sits at the top of the waterfall only for the **defaulter**.\n\n## Managing a member default\n\n- **Declare default** and stop the member's trading.\n- **Port client positions**: clients of the defaulted member are transferred, with their collateral, to a surviving clearing member (porting works best with segregated client margin and a willing backup member).\n- **Hedge the book**: the CCP hedges the defaulter's house positions to neutralize market risk.\n- **Auction the book** to surviving members. Incentives to bid: members' default fund contributions can be \"juniorized\" against poor bids.\n- Losses, if any, flow through the waterfall above. This is **loss mutualization**: survivors collectively absorb tail losses, in exchange for the system-wide benefits of clearing.\n\n## Advantages and disadvantages of central clearing\n\n**Advantages**\n\n- **Counterparty risk reduction**: novation, daily two-way margining, multilateral netting, and mutualized default resources.\n- **Transparency**: the CCP (and regulators) see positions across members; bilateral webs of exposure are replaced by a hub-and-spoke structure.\n- **Liquidity and standardization**: easier position offset; orderly default management replaces chaotic bilateral close-outs (contrast Lehman's cleared book, wound down without loss to the CCP, with its bilateral book).\n- Removes the loss-spiral contagion channel of bilateral markets.\n\n**Disadvantages / risks**\n\n- **Concentration of risk**: the CCP is itself a systemically critical single point of failure — \"too big to fail\" by design.\n- **Procyclical margins**: IM models raise requirements when volatility spikes, draining liquidity from members exactly in stressed markets.\n- **Moral hazard**: members may monitor counterparties less because the CCP bears the risk; the CCP may underprice risk to win business (race to the bottom among competing CCPs).\n- **Model risk**: IM and default fund adequacy depend on margin models that can be wrong in the tails.\n- **CCP risks**: default (counterparty) risk, investment risk on posted collateral, liquidity risk (must pay VM on time even while a defaulter's assets are being liquidated), operational and legal risk, sovereign/FMI interdependencies. Historical CCP failures: Paris (1974), Kuala Lumpur (1983), Hong Kong (1987).\n- Standardization required for clearing means bespoke trades stay bilateral.\n\n## Bilateral vs. central clearing\n\n| Feature | Bilateral (ISDA/CSA) | Central clearing (CCP) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Counterparty | Original dealer | CCP after novation |\n| Netting | Bilateral, per counterparty | Multilateral, across all members |\n| Margin | Negotiated; thresholds possible (now IM/VM required on non-cleared) | Mandatory two-way VM + IM, zero threshold |\n| Loss sharing | None — each party bears own losses | Mutualized via default fund and assessments |\n| Transparency | Low (improved by trade repositories) | High — CCP sees all cleared positions |\n| Products | Anything, including bespoke | Standardized, liquid products only |\n\n## Client clearing models\n\n- **Principal-to-principal (European model)**: the client faces the clearing member, and the member faces the CCP with a back-to-back mirror trade. The member is principal to both contracts.\n- **Agency (US FCM model)**: the client's contract is with the CCP; the member acts as agent and **guarantees** the client's performance to the CCP.\n\nIn both, client margin segregation (omnibus vs. individual segregation) determines how safely positions can be ported on member default.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Memorize the waterfall as \"defaulter pays first, twice; then the CCP; then everyone else\": defaulter's IM, defaulter's default fund, CCP skin in the game, survivors' default fund, assessments. Exam questions hinge on the exact ordering and on which resources belong to whom.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is novation in central clearing?\n> **A:** When a trade is accepted for clearing, novation replaces the original bilateral contract with two contracts — the CCP becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer. Members then face only the CCP's credit, not each other's.\n\n**Q2.** Why does a CCP have no market risk?\n> **A:** It runs a matched book — every long is offset by a short. Its exposure is purely counterparty risk: if a member defaults, the book is no longer matched.\n\n**Q3.** Multilateral netting: A owes B $20M, B owes C $30M, C owes A $40M. Net positions vs the CCP?\n> **A:** A receives $20M (pays 20, receives 40); B pays $10M (pays 30, receives 20); C pays $10M (pays 40, receives 30). Total flows fall from $90M gross to $40M.\n\n**Q4.** Name the three layers of the CCP margin stack.\n> **A:** Variation margin (daily mark-to-market settlement, paid by the loser), initial margin (per-portfolio buffer covering close-out losses over the margin period of risk at high confidence), and default fund contributions (mutualized layer sized to extreme but plausible scenarios — Cover 1/Cover 2).\n\n**Q5.** What is the exact order of the CCP default waterfall?\n> **A:** 1) Defaulter's initial margin; 2) defaulter's default fund contribution; 3) CCP's own capital (skin in the game); 4) surviving members' default fund contributions; 5) further assessments / recovery tools (cash calls, VM gains haircutting, partial tear-up).\n\n**Q6.** Exam trap: can a non-defaulting member's initial margin absorb another member's losses?\n> **A:** No. A non-defaulter's IM is never mutualized — it is returned if that member never defaults. Only survivors' default fund contributions and assessment rights are mutualized. IM sits at the top of the waterfall only for the defaulter.\n\n**Q7.** What is porting in CCP default management?\n> **A:** Transferring a defaulted member's client positions, with their collateral, to a surviving clearing member. It works best with segregated client margin and a willing backup member.\n\n**Q8.** How does a CCP manage a member default?\n> **A:** Declare default and halt trading, port client positions, hedge the defaulter's house book to neutralize market risk, auction the book to surviving members (default fund contributions can be juniorized against poor bids), then run any losses through the waterfall.\n\n**Q9.** Give three advantages of central clearing.\n> **A:** Counterparty risk reduction (novation, two-way daily margining, multilateral netting, mutualized resources); transparency (hub-and-spoke replaces bilateral webs); liquidity/standardization with orderly default management (e.g., Lehman's cleared book wound down without CCP loss).\n\n**Q10.** What is procyclicality of CCP initial margin?\n> **A:** IM models raise requirements when volatility spikes, draining members' liquidity exactly when funding is scarce in stressed markets — amplifying the stress.\n\n**Q11.** List key disadvantages/risks of central clearing.\n> **A:** Concentration of risk (CCP is a systemically critical single point of failure, \"too big to fail\"); procyclical margins; moral hazard (less counterparty monitoring; CCP race to the bottom); model risk in the tails; CCP-specific default, investment, liquidity, operational, and legal risks; bespoke trades stay bilateral.\n\n**Q12.** Contrast the principal-to-principal and agency client clearing models.\n> **A:** Principal-to-principal (European): client faces the clearing member, who faces the CCP with a back-to-back mirror trade — the member is principal to both. Agency (US FCM): the client's contract is with the CCP; the member acts as agent and guarantees the client's performance.\n\n**Q13.** Does multilateral netting always increase netting benefits?\n> **A:** No. It helps most when exposures are well distributed, but it can reduce netting benefits if it splits products across multiple CCPs that were previously netted bilaterally under a single ISDA agreement.\n\n**Q14.** Why is CCP margining historically stricter than bilateral CSA collateral?\n> **A:** CCP margin is two-way, zero-threshold, marked daily, and includes initial margin. Post-crisis rules now impose IM and VM on non-cleared bilateral trades too, narrowing the gap.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** When a trade is accepted for central clearing through novation, what happens to the original bilateral contract?\nA) It stays in place, but a CCP guarantees it B) It is replaced by two new contracts, with the CCP as buyer to the seller and seller to the buyer C) It is canceled and the parties must re-trade directly D) It becomes a futures contract on an exchange\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what \"novation\" does to a trade when it goes into central clearing.\n\nNovation means the single original contract between two parties is torn up and replaced by two new contracts. The central counterparty (CCP) steps into the middle: it becomes the buyer to the original seller and the seller to the original buyer. Think of it like a trusted middleman at a school swap meet: instead of two kids trading directly and worrying whether the other will keep their promise, the teacher (the CCP) buys from one and sells to the other, so each kid only has to trust the teacher.\n\nWhy B is correct: novation literally creates two contracts with the CCP in the middle, so members only worry about the CCP's creditworthiness, not each other's.\n\nWhy A is wrong: the original contract does not survive. Novation replaces it; a guarantee on top of the old contract would be a different mechanism.\nWhy C is wrong: nothing is re-traded; the economic terms are kept, only the counterparty changes.\nWhy D is wrong: clearing is separate from exchange trading. An OTC trade can be cleared without becoming an exchange-listed futures contract.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Novation = one contract becomes two, CCP in the middle.\" This is the single most testable definition in the chapter.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Novation: one contract becomes two\",\"root\":\"CCP (interposed)\",\"children\":[\"Seller → CCP (CCP is buyer)\",\"CCP → Buyer (CCP is seller)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Three dealers have these obligations: A owes B $50M, B owes C $70M, and C owes A $60M. If all positions move to a CCP, what is B's net position?\nA) B receives $20M from the CCP B) B pays $20M to the CCP C) B pays $70M to the CCP D) B receives $50M from the CCP\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for B's single net position against the CCP after multilateral netting.\n\nWith a CCP, each dealer's many obligations collapse into one net amount. List what B pays and what B receives:\n- B receives $50M (because A owes B $50M).\n- B pays $70M (because B owes C $70M).\n\nNet for B = receives 50 − pays 70 = −$20M. A negative number means B is a net payer, so B pays $20M to the CCP.\n\nWhy B is correct: $50M in, $70M out, so B owes the difference of $20M.\n\nWhy A is wrong: it flips the sign. B pays more than it receives, so it cannot be a net receiver.\nWhy C is wrong: $70M is only the gross amount B owes C; netting cancels the $50M B is owed by A.\nWhy D is wrong: $50M is only the amount B is owed; it ignores the larger $70M B must pay.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For netting, always write down \"receives\" and \"pays\" for the named party only, then subtract. Ignore amounts between the other two parties.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"B's net position\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Receives\",\"items\":[\"$50M from A\"]},{\"h\":\"Pays\",\"items\":[\"$70M to C\"]},{\"h\":\"Net\",\"items\":[\"50 − 70 = −$20M\",\"B pays $20M to CCP\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** After a clearing member defaults, its initial margin and its default fund contribution are both used up. According to the CCP default waterfall, what is consumed NEXT?\nA) Surviving members' initial margin B) The CCP's own capital (\"skin in the game\") C) Surviving members' default fund contributions D) Variation margin gains haircutting\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the exact order of the CCP loss waterfall.\n\nThe waterfall order is:\n1. Defaulter's initial margin\n2. Defaulter's default fund contribution\n3. CCP's own capital (\"skin in the game\")\n4. Surviving members' default fund contributions\n5. Further assessments / recovery tools (extra cash calls, VM gains haircutting, tear-ups)\n\nAfter steps 1 and 2 (the defaulter's own money) are gone, step 3 is the CCP's own equity tranche.\n\nWhy B is correct: the CCP must put its own capital at risk before tapping the surviving members.\n\nWhy A is wrong: surviving members' initial margin is NEVER used to cover another member's losses — it is their own protection and is returned to them.\nWhy C is wrong: surviving members' default fund contributions come AFTER the CCP's skin in the game, not before.\nWhy D is wrong: VM gains haircutting is a last-resort recovery tool, far down the waterfall.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize \"defaulter pays first (twice), then the CCP, then everyone else.\" The CCP's own capital sits between the defaulter's money and the survivors' money.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CCP default waterfall\",\"steps\":[\"Defaulter's initial margin\",\"Defaulter's default fund\",\"CCP capital (skin in the game)\",\"Survivors' default fund\",\"Assessments / recovery tools\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which statement about loss mutualization at a CCP is correct?\nA) Non-defaulting members' initial margin can be used to absorb a defaulter's losses B) Only the defaulter's resources can ever be used, so survivors never bear losses C) Survivors' default fund contributions, and possibly further assessments, can absorb a defaulter's losses D) The CCP's skin in the game is always the very last resource used\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how losses are shared (\"mutualized\") among surviving members.\n\nLoss mutualization means surviving members collectively share extreme losses that exceed the defaulter's own resources and the CCP's capital. This sharing happens through the survivors' default fund contributions and, if needed, additional assessments (cash calls). It does NOT touch their initial margin.\n\nWhy C is correct: the default fund plus assessment rights are exactly the mutualized layers.\n\nWhy A is wrong: a non-defaulting member's initial margin is protected and returned; it can never be used to cover someone else's default. This is a classic trap.\nWhy B is wrong: survivors CAN bear losses in extreme cases — that is the whole point of mutualization.\nWhy D is wrong: skin in the game is used relatively early (step 3), before survivors' default fund; the truly last resources are assessments and recovery tools.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Initial margin = \"mine alone.\" Default fund = \"shared.\" Only the default fund (plus assessments) is mutualized.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mutualized vs protected\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Mutualized (shared)\",\"items\":[\"Survivors' default fund\",\"Further assessments / cash calls\",\"VM gains haircutting\"]},{\"h\":\"Protected (never touched)\",\"items\":[\"Non-defaulter initial margin\",\"Returned to its owner\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Procyclicality of CCP initial margin refers to the risk that:\nA) Margin requirements fall in volatile markets, leaving the CCP underprotected B) Margin requirements rise in volatile markets, draining members' liquidity exactly when funding is scarce C) Margin is collected only once per business cycle D) The default fund automatically shrinks as trading volume grows\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what \"procyclical\" margins means as a disadvantage of central clearing.\n\nInitial margin models size the buffer based on volatility. When markets get stressed, volatility spikes, so the models demand MORE margin. But stressed markets are exactly when cash is hardest to find. So the CCP pulls liquidity from members at the worst possible moment, amplifying the stress. \"Procyclical\" means moving in the same direction as the market cycle — making bad times worse.\n\nWhy B is correct: higher volatility leads to higher margin calls, draining liquidity in a crisis.\n\nWhy A is wrong: it reverses the effect. Margins rise, not fall, in volatile markets.\nWhy C is wrong: margin (VM) is collected daily, not once per cycle; this misreads the word.\nWhy D is wrong: the default fund relating to volume has nothing to do with procyclicality.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Procyclical\" = amplifies the cycle. For margin, that means more cash demanded right when cash is scarce.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Procyclicality feedback loop\",\"root\":\"Volatility spikes in stress\",\"children\":[\"IM model demands more margin\",\"Members drained of liquidity when cash is scarce\",\"Stress amplified\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Compared with traditional bilateral (ISDA/CSA) collateral arrangements, CCP margining is best described as:\nA) One-way, with high thresholds, and collected only at maturity B) Two-way, zero-threshold, marked daily, and includes initial margin C) Optional and negotiated trade by trade D) Limited to variation margin only, with no initial margin\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question compares CCP margin practices with older bilateral practices.\n\nA CCP requires both sides to post margin (two-way), with no threshold below which collateral is skipped (zero-threshold), marks positions to market every day, and collects initial margin on top of variation margin. Historically this was stricter than typical bilateral CSA collateral, which often allowed thresholds and was one-sided.\n\nWhy B is correct: it lists exactly the four CCP features — two-way, zero-threshold, daily, plus IM.\n\nWhy A is wrong: that describes a weak bilateral arrangement, not a CCP. CCPs settle daily, not only at maturity.\nWhy C is wrong: CCP margining is mandatory and standardized, not negotiated per trade.\nWhy D is wrong: CCPs require BOTH variation margin and initial margin; dropping IM is incorrect.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CCP margin = \"two-way, zero-threshold, daily, IM + VM.\" Post-crisis rules now force IM/VM on bilateral trades too, narrowing the old gap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CCP vs traditional bilateral margin\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"CCP margining\",\"items\":[\"Two-way\",\"Zero threshold\",\"Marked daily\",\"IM + VM\"]},{\"h\":\"Old bilateral CSA\",\"items\":[\"Often one-way\",\"High thresholds\",\"Less frequent\",\"VM, little/no IM\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** When a CCP manages a defaulting member, transferring that member's CLIENT positions and their collateral to a surviving clearing member is called:\nA) Novation B) Porting C) Multilateral netting D) Juniorization\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the name of moving a defaulted member's clients to a healthy member.\n\nPorting means carrying the clients (and their segregated collateral) across to a willing surviving clearing member, so the clients are not dragged down by their broker's failure. Porting works best when client margin is segregated and a backup member is ready to take them. Think of it like a school bus breaking down: the kids (clients) and their backpacks (collateral) are moved onto another working bus (surviving member).\n\nWhy B is correct: porting is precisely the transfer of client positions and collateral on a member default.\n\nWhy A is wrong: novation is the original CCP-in-the-middle mechanism at trade acceptance, not the default-management transfer.\nWhy C is wrong: multilateral netting is the compression of many exposures into one; it is not about transferring clients.\nWhy D is wrong: juniorization is making a poor-bidding member's default-fund contribution rank lower (an auction incentive), not a client transfer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: On default management, \"porting\" = move the clients to safety. Segregated margin makes porting possible.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Name the mechanism\",\"options\":[\"A: Novation\",\"B: Porting\",\"C: Multilateral netting\",\"D: Juniorization\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Porting = transferring a defaulted member's client positions and segregated collateral to a surviving clearing member.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which of the following is a genuine DISADVANTAGE or risk of central clearing of OTC derivatives?\nA) It eliminates multilateral netting benefits B) It removes all transparency from the market C) The CCP becomes a systemically critical single point of failure (\"too big to fail\") D) It increases counterparty credit risk between members\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for a true downside of central clearing.\n\nBy concentrating huge volumes of contracts in one institution, the CCP itself becomes critically important. If it ever failed, the damage would ripple across the whole system. So central clearing trades many small bilateral risks for one giant concentrated risk — the CCP is \"too big to fail\" by design.\n\nWhy C is correct: concentration of risk in a single critical institution is a recognized disadvantage.\n\nWhy A is wrong: central clearing CREATES multilateral netting; it does not eliminate it (though splitting products across CCPs can reduce some netting benefits).\nWhy B is wrong: clearing INCREASES transparency — the CCP sees positions across all members.\nWhy D is wrong: clearing REDUCES counterparty credit risk between members through novation and margining.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The headline downside of a CCP is concentration — one super-important node whose failure would be catastrophic. Most other CCP features are advantages.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Central clearing: benefits vs risks\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Benefits\",\"items\":[\"Multilateral netting\",\"Transparency\",\"Less member counterparty risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Risk (downside)\",\"items\":[\"Concentration: CCP is single point of failure\",\"Too big to fail\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** In the agency (US FCM) client clearing model, the relationship is best described as:\nA) The client's contract is with the CCP, and the clearing member acts as agent guaranteeing the client's performance B) The client faces the clearing member, which holds a mirror trade with the CCP as principal C) The client faces the CCP with no member involvement at all D) The CCP faces the client directly and bears all client default losses itself\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question contrasts the two client clearing models, specifically the agency (US) model.\n\nIn the agency / FCM model, the client's contract is directly with the CCP, but the clearing member acts as the client's agent and guarantees the client's performance to the CCP. In the principal-to-principal (European) model, the client faces the member, and the member faces the CCP with a back-to-back mirror trade — being principal to both.\n\nWhy A is correct: that is exactly the agency model — client-to-CCP contract, member as guaranteeing agent.\n\nWhy B is wrong: that describes the principal-to-principal (European) model, not the agency model.\nWhy C is wrong: the member is always involved (as agent and guarantor) in the agency model.\nWhy D is wrong: the member guarantees the client, so the CCP does not simply absorb all client default losses alone.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Agency = US/FCM, client-to-CCP, member guarantees. Principal-to-principal = European, member sits in the middle with two mirror trades.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Client clearing models\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Agency (US / FCM)\",\"items\":[\"Client's contract is with the CCP\",\"Member acts as agent\",\"Member guarantees client\"]},{\"h\":\"Principal-to-principal (EU)\",\"items\":[\"Client faces the member\",\"Member faces CCP\",\"Two back-to-back mirror trades\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A CCP runs a \"matched book.\" What does this mean, and what risk does the CCP primarily face as a result?\nA) Every long is offset by a short, so the CCP has no market risk but faces counterparty risk if a member defaults B) The CCP takes large directional bets, so it faces mainly market risk C) The CCP holds only cash, so it faces no risk of any kind D) The CCP matches buyers to sellers but never becomes a counterparty, so it faces only operational risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks what a matched book is and what risk it leaves behind.\n\nBecause the CCP is buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, every long position it holds is offset by an equal short position. Net, its market exposure is zero — if prices move, gains on one side exactly cancel losses on the other. So the CCP has essentially no market risk. Its real worry is counterparty risk: if a member defaults, that side of the book is no longer matched, and the CCP must cover the gap.\n\nWhy A is correct: matched book = offsetting longs and shorts = no market risk, but counterparty risk on default.\n\nWhy B is wrong: a matched book is the opposite of a directional bet; the CCP does not take market positions.\nWhy C is wrong: the CCP holds open offsetting contracts, not just cash, and clearly faces counterparty risk.\nWhy D is wrong: the CCP DOES become a counterparty through novation; saying it never does is incorrect.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Matched book → CCP has no market risk, only counterparty (default) risk. A member default breaks the match and is what the margin stack and waterfall exist to handle.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Matched book: which risks?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"No market risk\",\"items\":[\"Every long offset by a short\",\"Price moves cancel net to zero\"]},{\"h\":\"Counterparty risk remains\",\"items\":[\"Member default breaks the match\",\"CCP must cover the gap\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** After a clearing member defaults, its initial margin and default fund contribution are exhausted. The next resource in the default waterfall is:\nA) Surviving members' initial margin B) The CCP's own capital (skin in the game) C) Surviving members' default fund contributions D) Variation margin gains haircutting\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Waterfall order: defaulter's IM → defaulter's default fund → CCP skin in the game → survivors' default fund → assessments/recovery tools.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A owes B $50M, B owes C $70M, and C owes A $60M. If all positions move to a CCP, B's net position is:\nA) Pays $20M to the CCP B) Receives $20M from the CCP C) Pays $50M to the CCP D) Receives $10M from the CCP\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** B receives $50M (from A) and pays $70M (to C): net pays $20M.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which statement about loss mutualization at a CCP is correct?\nA) Non-defaulting members' initial margin can be used to absorb a defaulter's losses B) Only the defaulter's resources can ever be used, so survivors bear no losses C) Survivors' default fund contributions, and possibly further assessments, can absorb a defaulter's losses D) The CCP's skin in the game is the last resource used\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Mutualization operates through survivors' default fund contributions and assessment rights, never through their initial margin.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Procyclicality of CCP initial margin refers to the risk that:\nA) Margin requirements fall in volatile markets, leaving the CCP underprotected B) Margin requirements rise in volatile markets, draining members' liquidity exactly when funding is scarce C) Margin is only collected once per cycle D) The default fund grows automatically with trading volume\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Volatility-driven margin models demand more collateral in stress, amplifying liquidity strains (procyclicality).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A dealer has three uncollateralized trades with one counterparty, currently valued (to the dealer) at +$25 million, −$15 million, and +$5 million. With an enforceable bilateral netting agreement, the dealer's current credit exposure is:\nA) $10 million B) $15 million C) $30 million D) $45 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With netting, exposure = max(sum of values, 0) = max(25 − 15 + 5, 0) = **$15M**. Without netting it would be the sum of positive values only: 25 + 5 = $30M (C). Netting cuts exposure in half here.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Credit exposure: netting vs not\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"With netting\",\"items\":[\"max(25−15+5, 0)\",\"= $15M\",\"negatives offset positives\"]},{\"h\":\"Without netting\",\"items\":[\"sum positive MTMs\",\"25 + 5 = $30M\",\"ignore the −15\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** Four dealers clear interest rate swaps through a CCP. On a given day the gross bilateral obligations are: Dealer W owes X $80M, X owes Y $50M, Y owes Z $90M, and Z owes W $60M. After all positions are novated to the CCP and multilaterally netted, what is each dealer's net position versus the CCP, and what is the total of the net flows through the CCP?\nA) W pays $20M, X receives $30M, Y pays $40M, Z receives $30M; total net flows = $60M B) W receives $20M, X pays $30M, Y receives $40M, Z pays $30M; total net flows = $60M C) W pays $20M, X receives $30M, Y pays $40M, Z receives $30M; total net flows = $90M D) W pays $80M, X pays $50M, Y pays $90M, Z pays $60M; total net flows = $280M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute each dealer's net position (receives − pays) against the CCP and the total net flows.\n\n1. Tabulate each dealer's receives and pays (an \"owes\" obligation means that dealer *pays*):\n   - W: pays 80 (to X), receives 60 (from Z) → net = 60 − 80 = −$20M → **W pays $20M**\n   - X: pays 50 (to Y), receives 80 (from W) → net = 80 − 50 = +$30M → **X receives $30M**\n   - Y: pays 90 (to Z), receives 50 (from X) → net = 50 − 90 = −$40M → **Y pays $40M**\n   - Z: pays 60 (to W), receives 90 (from Y) → net = 90 − 60 = +$30M → **Z receives $30M**\n\n2. Zero-sum check: −20 + 30 − 40 + 30 = 0. ✓ Payers are W ($20M) and Y ($40M); receivers are X ($30M) and Z ($30M).\n\n3. Total net flows through the CCP = sum of one side = 20 + 40 = $60M (equivalently 30 + 30 = $60M).\n\nThis matches option **A**.\n\n- **B** flips every direction (sign error: treating \"owes\" as \"receives\").\n- **C** has the correct directions but reports $90M — a wrong residual (it forgot that the two payer amounts equal the two receiver amounts, $60M, not $90M).\n- **D** ignores netting entirely and reports gross obligations ($280M), the un-netted bilateral total.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Verify multilateral nets sum to zero, then total ONE side to get net flows. Gross bilateral flows here were 80+50+90+60 = $280M; multilateral netting compresses them to $60M.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Net positions vs CCP\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Payers\",\"items\":[\"W pays $20M\",\"Y pays $40M\"]},{\"h\":\"Receivers\",\"items\":[\"X receives $30M\",\"Z receives $30M\"]},{\"h\":\"Check\",\"items\":[\"Sum = 0\",\"Net flow = $60M (one side)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A clearing member of a CCP defaults with a close-out loss of $620M. The defaulter had posted $250M of initial margin and $80M into the default fund. The CCP's pre-funded \"skin in the game\" is $40M. Surviving members' total default fund contributions are $900M. Which resources are consumed, in order, and how much of the surviving members' default fund is used?\nA) Defaulter's IM, then surviving members' IM, then CCP capital; $0 of survivors' default fund B) Defaulter's IM, defaulter's default fund, CCP skin in the game, then surviving members' default fund; $250M of survivors' default fund C) Defaulter's IM, defaulter's default fund, surviving members' default fund, then CCP skin in the game; $290M of survivors' default fund D) Defaulter's IM, CCP skin in the game, defaulter's default fund, then surviving members' default fund; $210M of survivors' default fund\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: apply the default waterfall in exact order and find how much of the survivors' mutualized default fund is drawn.\n\n1. Waterfall order: (1) defaulter's IM → (2) defaulter's default fund → (3) CCP skin in the game → (4) surviving members' default fund → (5) assessments/recovery tools.\n\n2. Absorb the $620M loss layer by layer:\n   - Defaulter's IM: $250M absorbed; remaining loss = 620 − 250 = $370M.\n   - Defaulter's default fund: $80M absorbed; remaining = 370 − 80 = $290M.\n   - CCP skin in the game: $40M absorbed; remaining = 290 − 40 = $250M.\n   - Surviving members' default fund: absorbs the final $250M (well within the $900M available).\n\n3. Therefore $250M of survivors' default fund is used. The waterfall order is exactly: defaulter IM → defaulter default fund → CCP skin → survivors' default fund.\n\nSo $250M of the survivors' default fund is used, with the correct order: defaulter IM → defaulter default fund → CCP skin → survivors' default fund. This is option **B**.\n\n- **A** is wrong because *surviving members' initial margin is never mutualized* — non-defaulters' IM is returned to them and cannot absorb another member's loss.\n- **C** places survivors' default fund AHEAD of CCP skin in the game (wrong order: CCP capital comes first) and miscomputes the amount as $290M (it skipped the $40M CCP layer).\n- **D** places CCP skin in the game ahead of the defaulter's own default fund — wrong, the defaulter's resources are always exhausted before the CCP contributes capital.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The defaulter pays first and twice (IM then its default fund), THEN the CCP's own capital, THEN everyone else's default fund. Surviving members' INITIAL margin is sacrosanct — only their default-fund contributions and assessment rights are mutualized.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Waterfall absorbing $620M loss\",\"steps\":[\"Defaulter IM $250M → $370M left\",\"Defaulter fund $80M → $290M left\",\"CCP skin $40M → $250M left\",\"Survivors' fund absorbs final $250M\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A risk manager is evaluating whether moving a portfolio of standardized OTC swaps from bilateral clearing into a CCP will reduce total margin posted. Currently, under a single ISDA master agreement, the firm nets a long interest-rate-swap book against a short book with the same counterparty, posting modest CSA collateral. The CCP would clear the interest-rate swaps but the firm's index CDS would go to a *different* CCP. Which effect is the most likely and is a recognized disadvantage of fragmented central clearing?\nA) Multilateral netting always reduces total margin, so margin must fall B) Splitting products across two CCPs can *destroy* netting benefits that existed bilaterally, potentially raising total margin C) Initial margin is never required at a CCP, so margin falls to zero D) The CCP eliminates variation margin, lowering daily funding needs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the recognized downside when previously-netted products are split across multiple CCPs.\n\n1. Under one ISDA/CSA, all products with a counterparty can be netted bilaterally into a single exposure, reducing collateral.\n2. A CCP nets multilaterally *within that CCP's product set*. If interest-rate swaps go to CCP A and index CDS go to CCP B, the two product books can no longer offset each other — netting that existed bilaterally is broken.\n3. The firm now posts initial margin and variation margin separately at each CCP on gross-of-cross-product positions, which can *raise* total margin even though each CCP nets internally. This is the documented disadvantage: central clearing helps most when exposures are well distributed and concentrated in one venue; fragmentation reduces netting benefits.\n\n- **A** overstates: multilateral netting helps *when not fragmented*; it can reduce benefits when products split across CCPs. The word \"always\" is the trap.\n- **C** is false: CCP clearing *requires* initial margin (a key reason CCP margining is historically stricter than CSA collateral).\n- **D** is false: VM is collected daily at CCPs, not eliminated.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Central clearing is not unconditionally margin-reducing. When a single bilateral netting set is fragmented across multiple CCPs by product, the loss of cross-product offset can increase total collateral.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"One netting set vs fragmented clearing\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Single ISDA/CSA\",\"items\":[\"IRS and CDS net together\",\"One collateral pool\",\"Lower total margin\"]},{\"h\":\"Split across two CCPs\",\"items\":[\"IRS at CCP A, CDS at CCP B\",\"Cross-product offset lost\",\"Total margin can rise\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which statement correctly distinguishes the principal-to-principal client clearing model from the agency (FCM) model?\nA) In the principal-to-principal model the client's contract is directly with the CCP; in the agency model the clearing member is principal to both legs B) In the principal-to-principal model the clearing member is principal to two back-to-back contracts (one with the client, one with the CCP); in the agency model the client faces the CCP and the member guarantees the client's performance C) Both models make the client face the CCP directly, differing only in margin segregation D) In the agency model the CCP bears no exposure to the client because no guarantee exists\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: correctly map each clearing model to who faces whom.\n\n1. **Principal-to-principal (European model):** the client faces the clearing member; the member faces the CCP with a mirror (back-to-back) trade. The member is principal to *both* contracts.\n2. **Agency (US FCM model):** the client's contract is *with the CCP*; the member acts as agent and *guarantees* the client's performance to the CCP.\n\nB states exactly this.\n\n- **A** swaps the two models (it describes agency as principal-to-principal and vice versa).\n- **C** is wrong: only the agency model puts the client face-to-face with the CCP; principal-to-principal does not.\n- **D** is wrong: in the agency model the member *guarantees* the client to the CCP — a guarantee is precisely what exists.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Principal-to-principal = member is principal to two contracts (back-to-back).\" \"Agency = client faces CCP, member guarantees.\" Both rely on client margin segregation (omnibus vs. individual) to make porting feasible on default.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Who faces whom?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Principal-to-principal (EU)\",\"items\":[\"Client faces member\",\"Member faces CCP\",\"Member principal to both legs\"]},{\"h\":\"Agency (US/FCM)\",\"items\":[\"Client's contract with CCP\",\"Member is agent\",\"Member guarantees client\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which of the following is correctly identified as a risk faced by the CCP itself (as opposed to a benefit of central clearing)?\nA) Multilateral netting reduces gross exposures across members B) Novation transfers counterparty risk away from members C) Liquidity risk: the CCP must pay variation margin to winners on time even while the defaulter's collateral is still being liquidated D) Transparency: the CCP observes all cleared positions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: pick the genuine CCP risk among a list mixing risks and benefits.\n\n1. A CCP runs a matched book, so it has no *market* risk in normal times, but it does bear: counterparty (default) risk, **liquidity risk**, investment risk on posted collateral, operational, and legal risk.\n2. Liquidity risk specifically: when a member defaults, the CCP still owes variation margin to the winning members on schedule, but the defaulter's posted assets may take time to convert to cash. A timing mismatch between owed payments and asset liquidation is a classic CCP liquidity risk. Answer: **C**.\n\n- **A**, **B**, and **D** are *advantages* of central clearing (netting, counterparty-risk reduction via novation, transparency), not risks borne by the CCP.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Distinguish benefits (netting, transparency, counterparty-risk reduction) from CCP risks (default, liquidity, investment, operational, legal, model risk, procyclicality, concentration/\"too big to fail\").\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which is a CCP risk (not a benefit)?\",\"options\":[\"A: Multilateral netting\",\"B: Novation transfers risk away\",\"C: Liquidity risk (pay VM before defaulter's collateral liquidates)\",\"D: Transparency\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"A, B, D are benefits. The CCP bears liquidity risk: it owes VM to winners on time while the defaulter's assets are still being converted to cash.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A CCP's initial margin model targets a 99.5% confidence level over a 5-day margin period of risk. During a volatility spike, the model raises IM requirements sharply across all members. Which concept does this illustrate, and what is its systemic danger?\nA) Procyclicality — IM rises in stressed markets, draining member liquidity exactly when funding is scarce B) Loss mutualization — survivors subsidize the defaulter C) Novation — the CCP becomes counterparty to each trade D) Wrong-way risk — exposure rises as counterparty credit deteriorates\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: name the phenomenon where volatility-sensitive IM models amplify stress.\n\n1. IM models (SPAN/VaR-type) scale with volatility and concentration. When volatility spikes, the modeled potential close-out loss rises, so required IM rises.\n2. Higher IM demands more collateral from members precisely during stressed markets, when funding/liquidity is already scarce. This positive feedback between market stress and margin demands is **procyclicality** — a recognized systemic danger of CCP margining. Answer: **A**.\n\n- **B** loss mutualization concerns absorbing a *defaulter's* losses via the default fund, not volatility-driven margin increases.\n- **C** novation is the mechanics of the CCP interposing itself, unrelated to margin scaling.\n- **D** wrong-way risk is exposure rising with counterparty credit deterioration — a related but distinct concept; here the trigger is *volatility*, not the counterparty's own credit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Procyclical margins = \"more collateral demanded in stress.\" It drains liquidity from members when they can least afford it, potentially propagating the very stress the CCP is meant to contain.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Procyclicality\",\"root\":\"Volatility spike\",\"children\":[\"VaR/SPAN model raises IM\",\"Bigger margin calls in stress\",\"Liquidity drained when funding scarce\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Regarding loss mutualization and the resources available after a default, which statement is TRUE?\nA) A non-defaulting member's initial margin can be tapped to cover another member's default loss B) Variation margin gains haircutting and additional cash assessments on survivors are recovery tools used only after the default fund and CCP capital are exhausted C) The CCP's skin in the game is the very last resource consumed before resolution D) Surviving members' default fund contributions rank ahead of the CCP's own capital in the waterfall\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the single correct statement about mutualization and the tail of the waterfall.\n\n1. Recall the order: defaulter IM → defaulter default fund → CCP skin in the game → survivors' default fund → further assessments / recovery tools (rights of assessment, VM gains haircutting, partial tear-up) → resolution/failure.\n2. VM gains haircutting and additional assessments sit in the *final* recovery layer, used only after the default fund(s) and CCP capital are exhausted. B is correct.\n\n- **A** is false: non-defaulters' IM is never mutualized — it is returned to them.\n- **C** is false: the CCP's skin in the game is consumed *before* survivors' default fund, not last; the last resources are assessments/recovery tools (then resolution).\n- **D** is false: CCP capital ranks *ahead of* survivors' default fund (CCP \"skin in the game\" is consumed before survivors' mutualized contributions).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the tail precisely — CCP skin in the game comes BEFORE survivors' default fund; assessments and VM gains haircutting are the *last* recovery tools before resolution. Non-defaulter IM is never touched.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Full waterfall order\",\"steps\":[\"Defaulter IM\",\"Defaulter default fund\",\"CCP skin in the game\",\"Survivors' default fund\",\"Assessments / VM gains haircut\",\"Resolution\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"307","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Futures Markets","notes":"# 7. Futures Markets\n\nGARP tests contract specifications, the mechanics of daily settlement and margin calls (a near-certain computation), open interest vs. volume, order types, and the forwards-vs-futures contrast.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Define and describe the key features and specifications of a futures contract: the underlying asset, contract size, delivery months and locations, price quotes, and price/position limits.\n- Explain how positions are opened and closed, and contrast physical delivery with cash settlement, including the mechanics of the delivery process.\n- Describe marking to market; compute initial, maintenance, and variation margin and the margin call mechanics.\n- Define and interpret trading volume and open interest, and explain how trades affect each.\n- Explain the convergence of futures and spot prices, and distinguish normal from inverted futures markets.\n- Evaluate the impact of different order types: market, limit, stop, stop-limit, market-if-touched, discretionary, time-of-day, good-till-canceled, fill-or-kill.\n- Describe the role of the exchange clearing house and compare margin requirements for hedgers and speculators.\n- Compare and contrast forward and futures contracts.\n\n## Contract specifications\n\nThe exchange standardizes everything except price:\n\n- **Underlying asset and quality**: for commodities, the deliverable grade(s) — alternatives may be deliverable at premiums/discounts set by the exchange. For financials, the asset is precisely defined (e.g., a specific index, currency amount).\n- **Contract size**: the quantity per contract (e.g., 5,000 bushels of corn; 1,000 barrels of oil; $250 × index for the classic S&P 500 contract; 62,500 GBP).\n- **Delivery location and months**: where and when delivery can occur; the exchange specifies the delivery months traded and when trading ceases.\n- **Price quotes**: units and tick size (minimum price move).\n- **Price limits**: daily limit up/down moves trigger trading halts — intended to curb panic moves, but they can delay price discovery.\n- **Position limits**: maximum contracts a speculator may hold, to prevent market corners. Bona fide hedgers are typically exempt.\n\n## Opening, closing, and delivery\n\nMost futures positions are **closed out** before maturity by entering the offsetting trade (a long sells the same contract). Fewer than ~2% of contracts end in delivery.\n\n- **Physical delivery**: the **short chooses** when in the delivery period to deliver, what grade, and where (within contract terms). The short files a *notice of intention to deliver* with the exchange, which assigns it to a long (typically the oldest outstanding long).\n- **Cash settlement**: contracts on undeliverable underlyings (stock indices, Eurodollar-style rates) settle in cash against the final settlement price; positions are marked to the spot value of the underlying at expiration.\n- **Convergence**: as expiration approaches, the futures price converges to the spot price — otherwise arbitrage (buy spot/sell futures and deliver, or the reverse) would be available.\n\n## Marking to market and margin\n\nEvery day, the contract is settled at the **settlement price** (the price at or near the close, set by the exchange). Gains are credited and losses debited to the margin account — daily **variation margin** flows mean a futures contract is rewritten at the new price each day, with value reset to zero.\n\n- **Initial margin (IM)**: deposit required to open the position.\n- **Maintenance margin (MM)**: minimum balance, typically ~75% of IM.\n- If the balance falls **below MM**, a **margin call** requires topping up **to the initial margin** (the shortfall vs. IM is the variation margin deposit). Failure to meet a call means the broker closes the position.\n- Balances above IM can be withdrawn; margin accounts often earn interest, and IM (not VM) can often be posted in securities at a haircut.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Two traps in one: the margin call is triggered when the balance falls **below the maintenance margin** (touching MM exactly is not below it), and the account must be restored **to the INITIAL margin, not to maintenance**. The call amount is initial margin minus current balance.\n\n**Hedgers vs. speculators**: exchanges set **lower margin requirements for bona fide hedgers** (and for day trades and spread positions) because their futures losses are offset by gains on the underlying exposure, so total risk is lower.\n\n**Clearing house role**: through novation it becomes counterparty to every trade, collects margin from clearing members (who collect from clients), nets positions, and guarantees performance — virtually eliminating counterparty risk.\n\n### Worked example: multi-day margin account\n\nAn investor goes **long 2 gold futures** (100 oz each, 200 oz total) at $1,800/oz. Initial margin is $12,000; maintenance margin is $9,000.\n\n| Day | Settlement price | Daily gain/(loss) | Margin balance | Margin call |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 1,800 | — | 12,000 | — |\n| 1 | 1,791 | (9) × 200 = (1,800) | 10,200 | — |\n| 2 | 1,778 | (13) × 200 = (2,600) | 7,600 → 12,000 | 4,400 |\n| 3 | 1,785 | 7 × 200 = 1,400 | 13,400 | — |\n| 4 | 1,770 | (15) × 200 = (3,000) | 10,400 | — |\n| 5 | 1,775 | 5 × 200 = 1,000 | 11,400 | — |\n\n- Day 2: balance $7,600 < $9,000 MM → call = 12,000 − 7,600 = **$4,400**, restoring the balance to the *initial* margin.\n- Day 4: $10,400 is above MM, so no call even though the day's loss was large.\n- Check the total: price fell 1,800 → 1,775, so cumulative loss = 25 × 200 = $5,000. Total deposited = 12,000 + 4,400 = 16,400; final balance 11,400; 16,400 − 11,400 = $5,000. Consistent.\n\n### Worked example: volume vs. open interest\n\nOpen interest (OI) starts the day at 1,000 contracts. Three trades occur:\n\n1. A (opening a new long) buys 50 from B (opening a new short): OI rises by 50; volume 50.\n2. C (closing an existing long) sells 30 to D (opening a new long): OI unchanged; volume 30.\n3. E (closing an existing long) sells 20 to F (buying to close an existing short): OI falls by 20; volume 20.\n\nEnd-of-day OI = 1,000 + 50 + 0 − 20 = **1,030**. Total volume = 50 + 30 + 20 = **100**.\n\n| Buyer | Seller | Open interest | Volume |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| New position | New position | +1 per contract | +1 |\n| Closing | New position | Unchanged | +1 |\n| New position | Closing | Unchanged | +1 |\n| Closing | Closing | −1 per contract | +1 |\n\n**Volume** counts contracts traded during a period; **open interest** counts contracts outstanding (total longs = total shorts). Volume can exceed OI on heavy days (e.g., near expiration as positions roll).\n\n## Normal vs. inverted markets\n\n- **Normal market**: futures prices **increase** with maturity (typical when carrying costs exceed benefits of holding the asset).\n- **Inverted market**: futures prices **decrease** with maturity (high convenience yield or expected price declines, e.g., tight oil supply).\n\nThe settlement price drives daily marking to market; quotes also show open, high, low, change, volume, and open interest by delivery month.\n\n## Order types\n\n| Order | Instruction |\n|---|---|\n| **Market** | Execute immediately at best available price |\n| **Limit** | Execute only at the stated price or better (buy at or below; sell at or above); may never fill |\n| **Stop (stop-loss)** | Becomes a **market** order once the stop price is reached; used to cap losses; execution price not guaranteed |\n| **Stop-limit** | Becomes a **limit** order once the stop price is reached; may not fill in a fast market |\n| **Market-if-touched (MIT)** | Becomes a market order when the price is touched — used to enter at a *favorable* price (opposite trigger side from a stop) |\n| **Discretionary (market-not-held)** | Broker may delay execution seeking a better price |\n| **Time-of-day** | Execute only during a specified period |\n| **Good-till-canceled (open)** | Remains live until executed or canceled (vs. day orders, which expire at the close) |\n| **Fill-or-kill** | Execute immediately in full or cancel |\n\n## Forwards vs. futures\n\n| Feature | Forward | Futures |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trading venue | OTC, bilateral | Exchange |\n| Terms | Customized | Standardized |\n| Counterparty risk | Yes (dealer credit) | Minimal — clearing house, margining |\n| Settlement | Once, at maturity | Daily marking to market |\n| Delivery | Usually delivered or cash-settled at maturity | Usually closed out before delivery |\n| Liquidity/closeout | Harder — negotiate with counterparty | Easy — offsetting trade |\n| Regulation | Lighter (post-crisis: reporting, margin) | Exchange and government regulated |\n| Gains/losses | Realized at maturity | Realized daily |","flashcards":"**Q1.** What does the exchange standardize in a futures contract, and what is left to the market?\n> **A:** The exchange standardizes everything — underlying asset/quality, contract size, delivery location and months, price quotes/tick size, price limits, and position limits — except the price, which is set by trading.\n\n**Q2.** In physical delivery, who chooses the timing, grade, and location?\n> **A:** The short. The short files a notice of intention to deliver with the exchange, which assigns it to a long (typically the oldest outstanding long).\n\n**Q3.** What triggers a margin call and how much must be deposited?\n> **A:** A call is triggered when the account balance falls below the maintenance margin (MM). The account must then be topped up to the initial margin (IM), not to MM. Call amount = IM − current balance.\n\n**Q4.** Trap: balance touches MM exactly, or a large loss leaves the balance above MM. Is there a call?\n> **A:** No call. The call fires only when the balance falls below MM; touching MM exactly is not below it, and a big daily loss that still leaves the balance above MM triggers nothing.\n\n**Q5.** Long 2 gold futures (200 oz) at $1,800; IM $12,000, MM $9,000. Day 2 settles at $1,778. Margin call?\n> **A:** Loss from prior day ($1,791) = (1,778−1,791)×200 = −$2,600 → balance $7,600 < $9,000 MM. Call = 12,000 − 7,600 = $4,400, restoring to the initial margin.\n\n**Q6.** Why do exchanges set lower margins for bona fide hedgers?\n> **A:** Their futures losses are offset by gains on the underlying exposure, so total risk is lower. Day trades and spread positions also get lower requirements.\n\n**Q7.** Define trading volume vs open interest.\n> **A:** Volume counts contracts traded during a period; open interest counts contracts outstanding (total longs = total shorts). Volume can exceed OI on heavy days such as expiration rolls.\n\n**Q8.** How does each trade type affect open interest?\n> **A:** New buyer + new seller: OI +1. Closing + new (either side): OI unchanged. Closing buyer + closing seller: OI −1. Volume rises by the trade size in every case.\n\n**Q9.** What is the convergence property of futures prices?\n> **A:** As expiration approaches, the futures price converges to the spot price; otherwise buy-spot/sell-futures (or the reverse) arbitrage would be available.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish a normal from an inverted futures market.\n> **A:** Normal: futures prices increase with maturity (carrying costs exceed holding benefits). Inverted: futures prices decrease with maturity (high convenience yield or expected price declines, e.g., tight oil supply).\n\n**Q11.** Contrast a stop order with a stop-limit order.\n> **A:** A stop (stop-loss) becomes a market order once the stop price is reached — the trigger is guaranteed but not the execution price. A stop-limit becomes a limit order at the stop price and may not fill in a fast market.\n\n**Q12.** What is a market-if-touched (MIT) order?\n> **A:** It becomes a market order when the price is touched, used to enter at a favorable price — the opposite trigger side from a stop order.\n\n**Q13.** Define fill-or-kill and good-till-canceled orders.\n> **A:** Fill-or-kill: execute immediately in full or cancel. Good-till-canceled (open): stays live until executed or canceled, unlike day orders that expire at the close.\n\n**Q14.** Key differences between forwards and futures.\n> **A:** Forwards: OTC, customized, dealer credit risk, settle once at maturity, usually delivered, harder to close out, lighter regulation. Futures: exchange-traded, standardized, minimal counterparty risk via clearing house and margining, daily marking to market, usually closed out before delivery, easy offset, heavily regulated.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A trader posts $5,000 of initial margin; the maintenance margin is $3,750. After some losses the account balance falls to $3,200. What is the size of the margin call?\nA) $550 B) $1,250 C) $1,800 D) $3,200\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how much cash the trader must add after the balance drops below the maintenance margin.\n\nFirst, check whether a call is triggered: the balance ($3,200) is below the maintenance margin ($3,750), so yes, a call happens.\n\nThe rule: when a call is triggered, the trader must top the account back up to the INITIAL margin, not just to the maintenance level.\n\nMargin call = initial margin − current balance = $5,000 − $3,200 = $1,800.\n\nWhy C is correct: you restore to the initial margin of $5,000, and you are short by $1,800.\n\nWhy A is wrong: $550 = $3,750 − $3,200, which restores only to maintenance. That is the classic trap.\nWhy B is wrong: $1,250 = $5,000 − $3,750, the gap between initial and maintenance, which is not the call.\nWhy D is wrong: $3,200 is just the current balance, not the amount owed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Margin call = Initial margin − current balance. You always top up to INITIAL, never just to maintenance.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An investor goes long 2 gold futures (100 oz each) at $1,800/oz. Initial margin is $12,000 and maintenance margin is $9,000. The next day the settlement price falls to $1,780/oz. What is the new margin balance, and is there a margin call?\nA) Balance $8,000; margin call of $4,000 B) Balance $8,000; no margin call C) Balance $11,600; no margin call D) Balance $12,000; margin call of $4,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the new balance after a daily loss and whether a call is triggered.\n\nTotal ounces = 2 contracts × 100 oz = 200 oz.\nPrice change = $1,780 − $1,800 = −$20 per oz (a loss because the investor is long and price fell).\nDaily loss = $20 × 200 oz = $4,000.\nNew balance = $12,000 − $4,000 = $8,000.\n\nIs there a call? The balance ($8,000) is below the maintenance margin ($9,000), so yes. The call restores to initial: $12,000 − $8,000 = $4,000.\n\nWhy A is correct: balance is $8,000 and it is below maintenance, so a $4,000 call brings it back to $12,000.\n\nWhy B is wrong: the balance IS below maintenance ($9,000), so a call is required.\nWhy C is wrong: $11,600 would come from a $2 (not $20) move; misreading the price change.\nWhy D is wrong: $12,000 is the balance AFTER paying the call, not before; the question asks for the balance reached by the loss.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Daily gain/loss = price change × total units. For a long, a price drop is a loss. Then compare the new balance to maintenance.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A trade occurs in which the buyer is opening a new long position and the seller is closing an existing short position. The effect on open interest is to:\nA) Increase by the trade size B) Decrease by the trade size C) Remain unchanged D) Increase by twice the trade size\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how open interest moves when one side opens and the other closes.\n\nOpen interest counts the number of contracts currently outstanding. A contract is created (open interest up) when BOTH sides are opening new positions, and destroyed (open interest down) when BOTH sides are closing. If one side opens and the other closes, the position simply transfers from the old holder to the new one — the total number outstanding does not change.\n\nHere: buyer opens (new long), seller closes (closing a short). One opens, one closes, so open interest is unchanged. Volume still rises by the trade size.\n\nWhy C is correct: one new and one closing trade leaves total contracts outstanding the same.\n\nWhy A is wrong: open interest rises only when both sides open new positions.\nWhy B is wrong: open interest falls only when both sides close.\nWhy D is wrong: open interest never jumps by twice the trade size; that double-counts.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Both open → OI up. Both close → OI down. One of each → OI flat. Volume always rises regardless.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Effect on open interest\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Both open\",\"items\":[\"OI up\",\"Contract created\"]},{\"h\":\"One opens, one closes\",\"items\":[\"OI unchanged\",\"Position transfers\"]},{\"h\":\"Both close\",\"items\":[\"OI down\",\"Contract destroyed\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Open interest starts the day at 1,000 contracts. Then: (1) a new long buys 50 from a new short; (2) someone closing a long sells 30 to a new long; (3) someone closing a long sells 20 to someone closing a short. What is the end-of-day open interest and total volume?\nA) Open interest 1,030; volume 100 B) Open interest 1,000; volume 100 C) Open interest 1,050; volume 70 D) Open interest 1,030; volume 70\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question tests tracking open interest and volume across several trades.\n\nStart: open interest = 1,000.\n- Trade 1: both open (new long, new short) → open interest +50. Volume +50.\n- Trade 2: one closes, one opens → open interest unchanged. Volume +30.\n- Trade 3: both close (closing a long, closing a short) → open interest −20. Volume +20.\n\nEnd open interest = 1,000 + 50 + 0 − 20 = 1,030.\nTotal volume = 50 + 30 + 20 = 100.\n\nWhy A is correct: open interest = 1,030 and volume = 100.\n\nWhy B is wrong: it ignores the net +30 change to open interest.\nWhy C is wrong: it mishandles trade 3 (it actually lowers OI) and undercounts volume.\nWhy D is wrong: open interest is right but volume must include all three trades (100, not 70).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Volume is just the sum of all contracts traded. Open interest changes only on trades where both sides open (+) or both close (−).\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** As a futures contract approaches expiration, the futures price converges to the spot price. The reason is that:\nA) Exchanges legally fix the two prices to be equal B) Otherwise a riskless arbitrage would be available between the futures and the spot market C) Daily settlement forces them together D) Open interest always falls to zero\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks WHY futures and spot prices converge at expiration.\n\nAt the moment of delivery, a futures contract is essentially a promise to buy or sell the asset right now. So its price must equal today's spot price. If it did not — say the futures were higher than spot — a trader could buy the cheap spot asset, sell the expensive futures, deliver immediately, and pocket a riskless profit. Traders rushing to do this push the prices back together. Convergence is enforced by arbitrage, just like in pricing models.\n\nWhy B is correct: any gap at expiration creates riskless arbitrage, which traders close instantly.\n\nWhy A is wrong: exchanges do not legally fix prices equal; markets do it through arbitrage.\nWhy C is wrong: daily settlement marks positions to the settlement price but does not cause convergence; arbitrage does.\nWhy D is wrong: open interest declining is a consequence of positions closing, not the cause of price convergence.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Convergence at expiry\" is enforced by arbitrage. At expiry the futures IS effectively spot, so any difference is free money.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why do futures and spot converge?\",\"options\":[\"A: Exchanges legally fix them equal\",\"B: Arbitrage closes any gap at expiry\",\"C: Daily settlement forces them together\",\"D: Open interest falls to zero\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"At delivery a future is effectively spot; any gap is riskless profit, so arbitrageurs trade it away.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** An investor is long futures at a price of 250 and wants to limit losses. The investor accepts that the execution price is uncertain in a fast market but demands that the order fire automatically if the price falls to 240. The appropriate order is a:\nA) Limit sell at 240 B) Stop (stop-loss) sell at 240 C) Market-if-touched sell at 240 D) Fill-or-kill sell at 240\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which order type protects a long position by triggering at a worse price and accepting execution uncertainty.\n\nA stop (stop-loss) order becomes a MARKET order once the stop price is reached. It guarantees the order fires when price hits 240, but it does NOT guarantee the exact execution price in a fast-moving market. That matches exactly what the investor wants: automatic firing at 240, accepting price uncertainty.\n\nWhy B is correct: a stop sell at 240 triggers automatically and becomes a market order, capping losses while accepting price slippage.\n\nWhy A is wrong: a limit sell at 240 only fills at 240 or better. In a fast falling market the price may blow through 240, and the order might never fill — exactly the protection the investor does NOT get.\nWhy C is wrong: market-if-touched is used to ENTER at a favorable price (it triggers on the opposite side), not to cap losses on a long.\nWhy D is wrong: fill-or-kill demands an immediate full fill or cancellation; it is not a loss-limiting trigger order.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stop → becomes a market order (fires for sure, price not guaranteed). Stop-limit → becomes a limit order (price guaranteed, fill not guaranteed). To cap losses on a long, use a stop sell below the market.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Order types on trigger\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Stop\",\"items\":[\"Becomes market order\",\"Fires for sure\",\"Price not guaranteed\"]},{\"h\":\"Limit\",\"items\":[\"Fills at price or better\",\"May never fill\",\"Price guaranteed\"]},{\"h\":\"Stop-limit\",\"items\":[\"Becomes limit order\",\"May not fill in fast market\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In an inverted futures market:\nA) Futures prices increase with time to maturity B) Futures prices decrease with time to maturity C) The futures price equals the spot price at every maturity D) Daily settlement is suspended\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the definition of an inverted futures market.\n\nIn an inverted market, longer-dated futures are priced LOWER than nearer-dated ones — prices fall as maturity gets further out. This usually happens when there is a high convenience yield (people value having the physical asset now, e.g., tight oil supply) or the market expects prices to decline. A normal market is the opposite: prices rise with maturity.\n\nWhy B is correct: inverted = prices decrease as time to maturity lengthens.\n\nWhy A is wrong: that describes a normal market, the opposite of inverted.\nWhy C is wrong: equal prices at all maturities is neither normal nor inverted.\nWhy D is wrong: daily settlement always continues; it is unrelated to the term-structure shape.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Normal market = prices rise with maturity (carry costs dominate). Inverted market = prices fall with maturity (high convenience yield / expected declines).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Futures term structure\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Normal\",\"items\":[\"Prices rise with maturity\",\"Carry costs dominate\"]},{\"h\":\"Inverted\",\"items\":[\"Prices fall with maturity\",\"High convenience yield / expected declines\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which of the following is true about margin requirements for hedgers versus speculators on a futures exchange?\nA) Hedgers face higher margins because they trade larger sizes B) Hedgers face lower margins because their futures losses are offset by gains on the underlying exposure C) Speculators and hedgers always face identical margins D) Hedgers post no margin at all\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how margin requirements differ for bona fide hedgers versus speculators.\n\nExchanges set LOWER initial margin for bona fide hedgers (and often for day trades and spreads). The logic: a hedger holds an offsetting position in the underlying, so a loss on the futures is offset by a gain on the physical exposure. Total risk is lower, so less margin is needed. A speculator has no offset, so the exchange demands more cushion.\n\nWhy B is correct: the hedger's underlying offset lowers total risk, justifying a lower margin.\n\nWhy A is wrong: margin is set by risk, not trade size; hedgers actually get a discount.\nWhy C is wrong: they are not identical; hedgers get preferential (lower) treatment.\nWhy D is wrong: hedgers still post margin, just at a reduced level — not zero.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Lower margin for hedgers because their futures position is offset by a real exposure, so net risk is smaller.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Margin by trader type\",\"items\":[\"Hedger (offset → lower risk)\",\"|\",\"Speculator (no offset → higher risk)\"],\"left\":\"lower margin\",\"right\":\"higher margin\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which statement correctly contrasts forward and futures contracts?\nA) Futures are customized OTC contracts; forwards are standardized exchange contracts B) Forwards settle daily; futures settle once at maturity C) Futures are exchange-traded and marked to market daily; forwards are OTC and settled once at maturity D) Forwards carry minimal counterparty risk; futures carry high counterparty risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the correct distinction between forwards and futures.\n\nFutures: traded on an exchange, standardized terms, marked to market daily (gains and losses settle every day), and counterparty risk is minimal because of the clearing house and margining. Forwards: traded over the counter (OTC), customized, settled once at maturity, and carry counterparty credit risk because they rely on the dealer's promise.\n\nWhy C is correct: it correctly pairs futures with exchange/daily settlement and forwards with OTC/single settlement.\n\nWhy A is wrong: it reverses the two — futures are standardized and exchange-traded; forwards are customized OTC.\nWhy B is wrong: it reverses settlement — futures settle daily, forwards settle once.\nWhy D is wrong: it reverses counterparty risk — forwards carry credit risk; futures have minimal risk via clearing.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Futures = Exchange, standardized, daily mark-to-market, minimal credit risk. Forwards = OTC, customized, one settlement at maturity, dealer credit risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forwards vs Futures\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward\",\"items\":[\"OTC, customized\",\"Settled once at maturity\",\"Dealer counterparty risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Future\",\"items\":[\"Exchange-traded, standardized\",\"Marked to market daily\",\"Minimal risk via clearing house\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A short trader files a \"notice of intention to deliver\" during the delivery period of a physically settled futures contract. Who chooses the delivery timing, grade, and location, and to whom is the notice typically assigned?\nA) The long chooses everything; the notice goes to the newest short B) The short chooses within contract terms; the notice is assigned to the oldest outstanding long C) The exchange chooses everything randomly D) The clearing house delivers the asset itself\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who controls the delivery decisions and how the notice is assigned.\n\nIn a physically delivered futures contract, the SHORT (the seller) has the choice — within the contract's allowed terms — of when in the delivery period to deliver, which acceptable grade, and which location. The short files a notice of intention to deliver with the exchange, which then assigns it to a long, typically the OLDEST outstanding long position.\n\nWhy B is correct: the short chooses (timing, grade, location within terms), and the notice goes to the oldest long.\n\nWhy A is wrong: the short, not the long, makes the delivery choices; and assignment goes to the oldest long, not the newest short.\nWhy C is wrong: the exchange does not choose the delivery details randomly; the short decides within contract rules.\nWhy D is wrong: the clearing house facilitates and guarantees but does not itself produce and deliver the physical asset.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In physical delivery the SHORT holds the options (timing/grade/location); the exchange assigns to the OLDEST long. Fewer than ~2% of contracts ever reach delivery anyway.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Physical delivery process\",\"steps\":[\"Short files notice of intention to deliver\",\"Short chooses timing/grade/location (within terms)\",\"Exchange assigns notice to OLDEST long\",\"Long takes delivery\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A trader posts $5,000 initial margin; maintenance margin is $3,750. After losses the balance is $3,200. The margin call is:\nA) $550 B) $1,250 C) $1,800 D) $3,200\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Balance ($3,200) is below maintenance ($3,750), so top up to the **initial** margin: 5,000 − 3,200 = $1,800.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A trade in which the buyer is opening a new long and the seller is closing an existing short causes open interest to:\nA) Increase by the trade size B) Decrease by the trade size C) Remain unchanged D) Increase by twice the trade size\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** One new position and one closing position leave open interest unchanged (volume still rises).\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** An investor long futures at 250 wants to limit losses and is willing to accept execution uncertainty in a fast market but demands the order fire automatically if the price falls to 240. The appropriate order is a:\nA) Limit sell at 240 B) Stop (stop-loss) sell at 240 C) Market-if-touched sell at 260 D) Fill-or-kill sell at 240\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A stop sell becomes a market order at 240, guaranteeing the trigger but not the price; a limit sell at 240 might not execute as the market falls through it.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In an inverted futures market:\nA) Futures prices increase with time to maturity B) Futures prices decrease with time to maturity C) The futures price equals the spot price at all maturities D) Daily settlement is suspended\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Inverted (backwardated-style) market: futures prices decline with maturity; a normal market shows prices rising with maturity.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** An investor is long 2 gold futures contracts (100 oz each) at $1,950/oz. Initial margin is $6,000 per contract and maintenance margin is $4,500 per contract. Ignoring interest, a margin call is first triggered when the futures price falls below:\nA) $1,905.00 B) $1,927.50 C) $1,935.00 D) $1,942.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The margin call comes when the loss per contract exceeds initial minus maintenance margin: $6,000 − $4,500 = $1,500 per contract = $15/oz over 100 oz. Trigger price = 1,950 − 15 = **$1,935**. The number of contracts is irrelevant since margins are per contract — using 200 oz (D) is the trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Margin-call trigger price\",\"steps\":[\"Cushion = initial − maint = $1,500/contract\",\"Per oz: $1,500 / 100 oz = $15\",\"Price drop allowed = $15\",\"Trigger = 1,950 − 15 = $1,935\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** An investor goes long 3 crude oil futures contracts (1,000 barrels each) at $78.00/bbl. Initial margin is $6,600 per contract and maintenance margin is $4,800 per contract. Settlement prices over four days are: Day 1 = $77.10, Day 2 = $75.40, Day 3 = $76.20, Day 4 = $74.10. On which day(s) does a margin call occur, and what is the total of all margin calls?\nA) Day 2 only; total call = $7,800 B) Day 2 and Day 4; total call = $13,800 C) Day 4 only; total call = $5,400 D) Day 2 only; total call = $5,100\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: track the margin balance across four days for 3 contracts (3,000 bbl total), flag each day the balance falls *below* maintenance, and restore to *initial*.\n\n1. Totals: initial margin = 3 × $6,600 = $19,800; maintenance margin = 3 × $4,800 = $14,400. Position size = 3,000 bbl, so each $1 price move = $3,000.\n\n2. Day 1: 78.00 → 77.10, change −0.90. Loss = 0.90 × 3,000 = $2,700. Balance = 19,800 − 2,700 = $17,100. Above $14,400 → no call.\n\n3. Day 2: 77.10 → 75.40, change −1.70. Loss = 1.70 × 3,000 = $5,100. Balance = 17,100 − 5,100 = $12,000. Below $14,400 → **margin call** = 19,800 − 12,000 = **$7,800**. Balance restored to $19,800.\n\n4. Day 3: 75.40 → 76.20, change +0.80. Gain = 0.80 × 3,000 = $2,400. Balance = 19,800 + 2,400 = $22,200. No call.\n\n5. Day 4: 76.20 → 74.10, change −2.10. Loss = 2.10 × 3,000 = $6,300. Balance = 22,200 − 6,300 = $15,900. This is *above* $14,400 → no call.\n\nSo a single margin call occurs on **Day 2** for **$7,800**, total = $7,800. This is option **A**.\n\n- **B** and the Day-4 traps assume any large daily loss triggers a call; on Day 4 the balance ($15,900) is still above maintenance ($14,400) because the Day-3 gain rebuilt the account, so no call fires.\n- **C** ignores the genuine Day-2 call and invents a Day-4 call.\n- **D** restores only to maintenance (19,800 − 14,400 logic / or just the daily loss $5,100) rather than to *initial* — the call must restore to initial margin, giving $7,800, not $5,100.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A call fires only when the *balance* drops **below maintenance**, and you then restore to **initial** (not to maintenance). A big daily loss alone does not trigger a call if the running balance stays at/above maintenance.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A speculator shorts 4 silver futures (5,000 oz each) at $24.00/oz. Initial margin is $11,000 per contract; maintenance margin is $8,000 per contract. To what price must silver *rise* before the short receives the first margin call?\nA) $24.40 B) $24.60 C) $25.50 D) $26.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: find the price increase that drives a *short's* balance below maintenance.\n\n1. Totals: initial margin = 4 × $11,000 = $44,000; maintenance = 4 × $8,000 = $32,000. Position = 4 × 5,000 = 20,000 oz, so each $1 move = $20,000.\n\n2. The short *loses* when price rises. A call occurs when balance falls below maintenance, i.e., when cumulative loss exceeds initial − maintenance = 44,000 − 32,000 = $12,000.\n\n3. Price move needed: loss of $12,000 / $20,000 per $1 = $0.60 increase. Trigger price = 24.00 + 0.60 = **$24.60**.\n\n- **A** ($24.40) uses a $0.40 move — would correspond to an $8,000 loss, wrongly equating the trigger to maintenance margin itself rather than (IM − MM).\n- **C** ($25.50) uses the full initial margin ($1.50 move = $30,000 ≈ wrong basis).\n- **D** ($26.00) uses a $2.00 move ($40,000), near full IM, also wrong.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a short, a call triggers after a *price rise* equal to (IM − MM) ÷ (contracts × contract size). Here ($44,000 − $32,000)/20,000 = $0.60.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Day-start open interest is 2,500 contracts. During the day: (i) Trader A opens a new long buying 60 from Trader B who opens a new short; (ii) Trader C closes an existing long by selling 40 to Trader D who opens a new long; (iii) Trader E closes a long by selling 25 to Trader F who buys to close an existing short; (iv) Trader G opens a new short selling 30 to Trader H who buys to close a short. What are end-of-day open interest and total volume?\nA) OI = 2,535; volume = 155 B) OI = 2,535; volume = 130 C) OI = 2,505; volume = 155 D) OI = 2,560; volume = 155\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: apply the open-interest rules per trade and sum volume (every trade adds to volume regardless of OI effect).\n\n1. Trade (i): new long + new short → both open → OI **+60**. Volume +60.\n2. Trade (ii): closing long + new long → one opens, one closes → OI **unchanged**. Volume +40.\n3. Trade (iii): closing long + closing short → both close → OI **−25**. Volume +25.\n4. Trade (iv): new short + closing short → one opens (new short), one closes (buy to close a short) → OI **unchanged**. Volume +30.\n\n5. End-of-day OI = 2,500 + 60 + 0 − 25 + 0 = 2,560 − 25 = **2,535**.\n6. Total volume = 60 + 40 + 25 + 30 = **155** (every trade counts).\n\nSo OI = 2,535 and volume = 155, which is option **A**.\n\n- **B** has the right OI but undercounts volume (it drops one trade — volume counts *all* 155 contracts traded).\n- **C** miscomputes OI by treating trade (iv) as closing-closing (−30), giving 2,505.\n- **D** treats trade (ii) as opening-opening (+40), giving 2,560.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Volume counts *every* contract traded; OI changes only with the open/close mix — both opening = +; both closing = −; one of each = unchanged.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"OI vs volume rules\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Both open\",\"items\":[\"OI +\",\"Volume +\"]},{\"h\":\"One open, one close\",\"items\":[\"OI unchanged\",\"Volume +\"]},{\"h\":\"Both close\",\"items\":[\"OI −\",\"Volume +\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A portfolio manager is long an equity futures position at 4,200. She wants to guarantee an automatic exit if the market rallies to a target of 4,260 so she can take profits, and she is willing to accept whatever fill the market gives once that level prints. Which order type is appropriate?\nA) Limit sell at 4,260 B) Stop (stop-loss) sell at 4,260 C) Market-if-touched (MIT) sell at 4,260 D) Stop-limit sell at 4,260\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: choose the order that fires a *market* order to *take profit* at a *favorable* (higher) price for a long.\n\n1. The manager is long and wants to *sell into a rally* — entering/exiting at a favorable price as the market moves up to 4,260.\n2. A **market-if-touched (MIT)** becomes a market order when the price is touched, used to enter/exit at a *favorable* price (the opposite trigger side from a stop). For a long taking profit on a rise, MIT-sell at 4,260 fires a market order when 4,260 prints, accepting the fill — exactly the requirement.\n\n- **A** (limit sell at 4,260) would fill at 4,260 or better but might be skipped in a fast rally or not guarantee execution; the manager wants a guaranteed fire (market) once touched.\n- **B** (stop sell) is used to *cap losses* on a long and triggers when price falls *to/through* the stop, i.e., on the *downside* — wrong trigger side for taking profit on a rally.\n- **D** (stop-limit) becomes a *limit* order (may not fill in a fast market) and is again a downside loss-cap mechanism — wrong direction and not guaranteed to execute.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stop orders trigger on adverse moves to cap losses; MIT triggers on *favorable* moves to enter/take profit. MIT becomes a market order (guaranteed fire, uncertain price); stop-limit becomes a limit order (may not fill).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Stop vs MIT (long position)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Stop sell\",\"items\":[\"Triggers on adverse fall\",\"Caps losses\",\"Becomes market order\"]},{\"h\":\"MIT sell\",\"items\":[\"Triggers on favorable rise\",\"Takes profit\",\"Becomes market order\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement comparing margin requirements and the role of the clearing house is correct?\nA) Exchanges set higher margins for bona fide hedgers than speculators because hedgers hold positions longer B) Bona fide hedgers and spread positions typically receive *lower* margin requirements than outright speculators because their offsetting exposures reduce total risk C) The clearing house collects variation margin but never initial margin from clearing members D) Maintenance margin is typically set above initial margin to provide a buffer\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct statement about margining and the clearing house.\n\n1. Exchanges set *lower* margins for bona fide hedgers (and for spreads and day trades) because the futures position offsets an underlying exposure (or another futures leg), so net risk is lower. B is correct.\n\n- **A** reverses the relationship: hedgers get *lower*, not higher, margins.\n- **C** is false: the clearing house collects *both* initial and variation margin from clearing members (who collect from clients) and guarantees performance via novation.\n- **D** is false: maintenance margin is *below* initial margin (typically ~75% of IM); the gap is the buffer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedgers/spreads/day trades = lower margins (lower net risk). Maintenance < Initial. The clearing house novates and collects IM + VM, virtually eliminating counterparty risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Margin levels\",\"items\":[\"Variation balance\",\"|Maintenance\",\"Initial\"],\"left\":\"lower\",\"right\":\"higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which of the following correctly distinguishes a forward contract from an exchange-traded futures contract?\nA) Forwards realize gains and losses daily through marking to market, while futures settle only at maturity B) Futures are customized OTC agreements, while forwards are standardized and exchange-traded C) Forwards expose holders to counterparty (dealer) credit risk and settle once at maturity, while futures are marked to market daily and carry minimal counterparty risk via the clearing house D) Forwards are easier to close out than futures because they can be offset by an identical exchange trade\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: pick the accurate forward-vs-futures contrast.\n\n1. Forwards: OTC, customized, settle once at maturity, bear dealer counterparty/credit risk, harder to unwind (negotiate with the counterparty).\n2. Futures: exchange-traded, standardized, marked to market daily (gains/losses realized daily), minimal counterparty risk via the clearing house/margining, easily offset. C captures this exactly.\n\n- **A** swaps the settlement features (it is *futures* that mark to market daily).\n- **B** swaps the venues (futures are exchange-traded/standardized; forwards are OTC/customized).\n- **D** is backwards: *futures* are easier to close out (offsetting exchange trade); forwards require negotiating with the original counterparty.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward = OTC, custom, single settlement, credit risk, hard to unwind. Futures = exchange, standardized, daily MTM, clearing-house guaranteed, easy offset.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forwards vs Futures\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward\",\"items\":[\"OTC, customized\",\"Single settlement at maturity\",\"Dealer credit risk\",\"Hard to unwind\"]},{\"h\":\"Future\",\"items\":[\"Exchange, standardized\",\"Daily mark-to-market\",\"Minimal risk via clearing house\",\"Easily offset\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** As a deliverable commodity futures contract approaches expiration, the futures price and spot price converge. Which statement best explains why, and what defines an *inverted* (downward-sloping) futures market?\nA) Convergence occurs because of daily settlement; an inverted market has futures prices rising with maturity B) Convergence is enforced by arbitrage (buy spot/sell futures and deliver, or the reverse); an inverted market has futures prices *decreasing* with maturity, often from a high convenience yield C) Convergence is a regulatory requirement; an inverted market shows the futures price equal to spot at every maturity D) Convergence happens only for cash-settled contracts; an inverted market has the highest price at the longest maturity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: explain convergence and define an inverted market.\n\n1. Near expiry, if futures ≠ spot, an arbitrageur could buy the cheaper and sell the dearer and deliver, locking in a riskless profit. This arbitrage forces the futures price to the spot price at delivery — convergence is *arbitrage-enforced*.\n2. An **inverted market** (downward-sloping term structure) has futures prices *decreasing* with maturity, typically when the convenience yield is high (tight supply) or prices are expected to fall.\n\n- **A** misattributes convergence to daily settlement and describes a *normal* (not inverted) market.\n- **C** is false: convergence is arbitrage-driven, not a regulation; inverted ≠ flat.\n- **D** is false: convergence applies to deliverable contracts via arbitrage; an inverted market has *lower* prices at longer maturities.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Convergence = arbitrage. Normal market = futures rise with maturity (carry costs dominate); inverted market = futures fall with maturity (high convenience yield / expected declines).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Term structure shape\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Normal\",\"items\":[\"Futures rise with maturity\",\"Carry costs dominate\"]},{\"h\":\"Inverted\",\"items\":[\"Futures fall with maturity\",\"High convenience yield / tight supply\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"308","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Using Futures for Hedging","notes":"# 8. Using Futures for Hedging\n\nA perennial multi-question chapter: expect computations on the minimum-variance hedge ratio, the optimal number of contracts, adjusting portfolio beta with index futures, and basis-risk reasoning (who gains when the basis strengthens).\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Define and differentiate short and long hedges and identify when each is appropriate.\n- Describe the arguments for and against hedging and hedging's potential impact on firm profitability.\n- Define the basis and basis risk; explain how basis risk arises when hedging with futures, and calculate the effective price locked in by a hedge.\n- Define cross hedging; compute and interpret the minimum-variance hedge ratio and hedge effectiveness.\n- Compute the optimal number of futures contracts for a hedge, with and without \"tailing the hedge.\"\n- Explain how to use stock index futures to hedge an equity portfolio or to change its beta, and compute the required number of contracts.\n- Explain how to create long-term hedges with stack-and-roll strategies and describe the resulting rollover/basis risks.\n\n## Short vs. long hedges\n\n- **Short hedge** (sell futures): appropriate when you **own the asset or will receive/sell it later** — a farmer with a harvest coming, an oil producer, an exporter awaiting foreign currency. Futures gains offset spot price declines.\n- **Long hedge** (buy futures): appropriate when you **will need to buy the asset later** — an airline buying jet fuel, a manufacturer buying copper. Futures gains offset spot price increases.\n\n## Arguments for and against hedging\n\n**For**: stabilizes cash flows and margins, lets management focus on the core business, avoids financial distress costs, and prices/budgets can be set with certainty.\n\n**Against**:\n\n- **Shareholders can hedge themselves** (and diversify), often more cheaply — and they may have *chosen* the firm for its commodity exposure.\n- **Competitive pressures**: if rivals do not hedge, input costs and output prices move together across the industry and margins are naturally stable; the *hedger's* margins then fluctuate. Hedging when competitors don't can create risk.\n- **Explaining futures losses**: if the underlying moves favorably, the hedge loses money. A treasurer can be blamed for a large, visible futures loss even though the combined position performed exactly as designed.\n\n## Basis and basis risk\n\n$$b = S - F$$\n\nThe **basis** is spot minus futures (for consumption assets this convention is standard; beware some texts flip it for financial assets). At expiration the basis converges to zero (or to grade/location differentials). Because hedges are usually closed before expiration, the basis at close, $$b_2 = S_2 - F_2$$, is uncertain — this is **basis risk**.\n\n- **Strengthening basis** (basis increases): improves the **short hedger's** position (sells at effectively higher price), hurts the long hedger.\n- **Weakening basis**: helps the **long hedger**, hurts the short hedger.\n\n**Effective price locked in** = futures price at initiation + basis at close:\n\n$$\\text{Effective price} = F_1 + b_2 = S_2 + (F_1 - F_2)$$\n\nHedging replaces *price risk* with the (much smaller) *basis risk*. Sources of basis risk: asset mismatch (cross hedging), timing mismatch (hedge horizon vs. delivery month — choose a delivery month shortly *after* the hedge ends), and location/grade differences.\n\n## Cross hedging and the minimum-variance hedge ratio\n\nWhen the hedged asset differs from the futures underlying (jet fuel hedged with heating oil futures), the hedge ratio that minimizes the variance of the hedged position is\n\n$$h^* = \\rho \\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F}$$\n\nwhere $$\\sigma_S$$ and $$\\sigma_F$$ are the standard deviations of *changes* in spot and futures prices over the hedge period, and $$\\rho$$ is their correlation. (Equivalently, h* is the slope of a regression of ΔS on ΔF.)\n\n**Optimal number of contracts** for a position of size $$Q_A$$ units with futures contract size $$Q_F$$:\n\n$$N^* = \\frac{h^* Q_A}{Q_F}$$\n\n**Hedge effectiveness** is the proportion of variance eliminated:\n\n$$R^2 = \\rho^2$$\n\n**Tailing the hedge**: because futures are settled daily, the hedge should be based on **values, not quantities**:\n\n$$N^* = \\frac{h^* V_A}{V_F}$$\n\nwhere $$V_A$$ is the value of the position being hedged and $$V_F$$ = futures price × contract size. Tailing slightly reduces the contract count when interest/daily-settlement effects are recognized.\n\n### Worked example: airline cross hedge\n\nAn airline will buy 2,000,000 gallons of jet fuel in one month and hedges with heating oil futures (42,000 gallons per contract). From historical data: $$\\sigma_S = 0.0263$$, $$\\sigma_F = 0.0313$$ (standard deviations of monthly price changes), $$\\rho = 0.928$$.\n\nStep 1 — hedge ratio:\n\n$$h^* = 0.928 \\times \\frac{0.0263}{0.0313} = 0.928 \\times 0.8403 = 0.7798$$\n\nStep 2 — number of contracts:\n\n$$N^* = \\frac{0.7798 \\times 2{,}000{,}000}{42{,}000} = 0.7798 \\times 47.62 = 37.13 \\approx 37 \\text{ contracts}$$\n\nThe airline needs fuel later, so it takes a **long** position in 37 heating oil contracts.\n\nStep 3 — hedge effectiveness: $$R^2 = 0.928^2 = 0.861$$ — about 86.1% of the variance of the fuel cost is eliminated; 13.9% remains as basis risk.\n\n*Tailing check*: with jet fuel spot at $2.00/gal and heating oil futures at $2.10/gal, $$V_A$$ = 2,000,000 × 2.00 = $4,000,000 and $$V_F$$ = 42,000 × 2.10 = $88,200, so $$N^* = 0.7798 \\times 4{,}000{,}000 / 88{,}200 = 35.4 \\approx 35$$ contracts — slightly fewer than the quantity-based answer.\n\n## Hedging equity portfolios with index futures\n\nTo **fully hedge** a portfolio of value P with index futures priced at F (= futures quote × multiplier), **short**:\n\n$$N^* = \\beta \\frac{P}{F}$$\n\nTo **change beta** from $$\\beta$$ to a target $$\\beta^*$$:\n\n$$N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)\\frac{P}{F}$$\n\nPositive N = **buy (long)** contracts (raising beta); negative N = **sell (short)** contracts (lowering beta). Setting $$\\beta^* = 0$$ recovers the full hedge.\n\n**Why hedge rather than sell the portfolio?** Avoids transaction costs and capital gains taxes of liquidation, keeps the carefully selected stocks (the manager wants to keep the alpha but shed market risk temporarily), and is quickly reversible. A fully hedged portfolio earns roughly the risk-free rate (plus any alpha).\n\n### Worked example: adjusting portfolio beta\n\nA manager runs a $24,000,000 portfolio with $$\\beta = 1.2$$. Index futures trade at 4,000 with a $250 multiplier, so F = 4,000 × 250 = $1,000,000.\n\n**(a) Full hedge**: $$N^* = 1.2 \\times 24{,}000{,}000 / 1{,}000{,}000 = 28.8$$ → **short 29 contracts** (round to nearest whole contract).\n\n**(b) Reduce beta to 0.6**:\n\n$$N = (0.6 - 1.2)\\times\\frac{24{,}000{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = -0.6 \\times 24 = -14.4$$\n\n→ **short 14 contracts**.\n\n**(c) Raise beta to 1.8**: $$N = (1.8 - 1.2) \\times 24 = +14.4$$ → **long 14 contracts**.\n\n### Worked example: effective price via the basis\n\nIn May, a producer expecting to sell a commodity in August shorts September futures at $$F_1 = 2.50$$. In August, spot is $$S_2 = 2.30$$ and the September futures price is $$F_2 = 2.20$$, so the basis at close is $$b_2 = 2.30 - 2.20 = 0.10$$.\n\n- Futures gain = 2.50 − 2.20 = 0.30 per unit.\n- Effective sale price = spot + futures gain = 2.30 + 0.30 = **2.60**, which equals $$F_1 + b_2 = 2.50 + 0.10$$. ✔\n\nThe producer locked in $$F_1$$ plus whatever the basis turns out to be — only $$b_2$$ was uncertain.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Direction and ratio traps: (1) **Reducing** beta (or fully hedging a long portfolio) means **shorting** contracts — a positive count in the formula output means *buy*, negative means *sell*; read the sign. (2) The hedge ratio is $$h^* = \\rho\\,\\sigma_S/\\sigma_F$$ — spot volatility on **top**. Answer choices built from $$\\rho\\,\\sigma_F/\\sigma_S$$ are standard distractors. (3) A **strengthening** basis benefits the **short** hedger, not the long.\n\n## Stack and roll\n\nA long-dated exposure can be hedged with liquid **short-dated** futures: take the full position (\"stack\") in a near contract, then close it just before delivery and re-establish it in the next contract (\"roll\"). Each roll exposes the hedger to **rollover basis risk** — the price difference between the expiring and new contracts is unknown in advance.\n\nA second danger is **cash-flow timing**: futures losses trigger immediate margin calls, while offsetting gains on the underlying long-term position are unrealized.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> **Metallgesellschaft (1993)**: hedged long-term fixed-price oil supply contracts by stacking and rolling short-dated long futures; when oil prices fell, massive margin calls on the futures created a funding crisis (the offsetting gains on the supply contracts were distant and unrealized), and unwinding the hedge locked in roughly $1.3 billion of losses.","flashcards":"**Q1.** When is a short hedge versus a long hedge appropriate?\n> **A:** Short hedge (sell futures): when you own the asset or will sell/receive it later (farmer, oil producer, exporter) — gains offset spot price declines. Long hedge (buy futures): when you will need to buy the asset later (airline buying fuel, manufacturer buying copper) — gains offset spot price increases.\n\n**Q2.** Give arguments against hedging.\n> **A:** Shareholders can hedge/diversify themselves more cheaply and may have chosen the firm for its exposure; competitive pressures mean hedging when rivals don't can destabilize margins; and a visible futures loss can be hard to explain even when the combined position performed as designed.\n\n**Q3.** Define the basis and basis risk.\n> **A:** Basis $$b = S - F$$ (spot minus futures). At expiration it converges to zero (or grade/location differentials). Since hedges usually close before expiration, the basis at close $$b_2 = S_2 - F_2$$ is uncertain — that uncertainty is basis risk.\n\n**Q4.** Who benefits from a strengthening versus a weakening basis?\n> **A:** A strengthening (increasing) basis helps the short hedger (sells at an effectively higher price) and hurts the long hedger. A weakening basis helps the long hedger and hurts the short hedger.\n\n**Q5.** What is the effective price locked in by a hedge?\n> **A:** $$\\text{Effective price} = F_1 + b_2 = S_2 + (F_1 - F_2)$$ — the initial futures price plus the basis at close. Hedging replaces price risk with the smaller basis risk.\n\n**Q6.** State the minimum-variance hedge ratio.\n> **A:** $$h^* = \\rho \\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F}$$ where $$\\sigma_S$$ and $$\\sigma_F$$ are standard deviations of price changes in spot and futures and $$\\rho$$ their correlation. It equals the slope of regressing ΔS on ΔF. Trap: spot volatility goes on top.\n\n**Q7.** Compute the optimal number of contracts (quantity-based).\n> **A:** $$N^* = \\frac{h^* Q_A}{Q_F}$$ where $$Q_A$$ is the position size in units and $$Q_F$$ the futures contract size.\n\n**Q8.** How is hedge effectiveness measured?\n> **A:** $$R^2 = \\rho^2$$ — the proportion of the hedged position's variance eliminated. Example: $$\\rho = 0.928$$ gives $$R^2 = 0.861$$, so ~86% of variance is removed and ~14% remains as basis risk.\n\n**Q9.** What is tailing the hedge?\n> **A:** Because futures settle daily, base the hedge on values not quantities: $$N^* = \\frac{h^* V_A}{V_F}$$ where $$V_A$$ is the position value and $$V_F$$ = futures price × contract size. Tailing slightly reduces the contract count.\n\n**Q10.** How many contracts to fully hedge an equity portfolio with index futures?\n> **A:** Short $$N^* = \\beta \\frac{P}{F}$$ where P is portfolio value and F = futures quote × multiplier. Example: β=1.2, P=$24M, F=$1M → 28.8 → short 29 contracts.\n\n**Q11.** How do you change a portfolio's beta from β to a target β* with index futures?\n> **A:** $$N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)\\frac{P}{F}$$. Positive N = buy/long (raise beta); negative N = sell/short (lower beta). Setting β*=0 recovers the full hedge.\n\n**Q12.** Why hedge an equity portfolio with futures rather than sell it?\n> **A:** Avoids transaction costs and capital gains taxes of liquidation, keeps the carefully selected stocks (retain alpha while temporarily shedding market risk), and is quickly reversible. A fully hedged portfolio earns roughly the risk-free rate plus any alpha.\n\n**Q13.** What is the stack-and-roll strategy and its risks?\n> **A:** Hedge a long-dated exposure with liquid short-dated futures: take the full position in a near contract, then close and re-establish in the next contract before delivery. Risks: rollover basis risk (price gap between expiring and new contracts) and cash-flow timing — futures losses trigger immediate margin calls while offsetting gains are unrealized.\n\n**Q14.** What lesson does Metallgesellschaft (1993) teach?\n> **A:** It hedged long-term fixed-price oil supply contracts by stacking and rolling short-dated long futures. When oil prices fell, massive margin calls on the futures caused a funding crisis (offsetting supply-contract gains were distant and unrealized); unwinding locked in roughly $1.3 billion of losses.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A company knows it will need to BUY a large quantity of copper in three months and worries that copper prices will rise. To hedge, the company should:\nA) Take a short hedge by selling copper futures B) Take a long hedge by buying copper futures C) Do nothing, since futures cannot hedge purchases D) Buy the copper now regardless of price\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which hedge protects a future BUYER against rising prices.\n\nA long hedge means buying futures. It is appropriate when you will need to BUY the asset later and fear a price increase. If copper prices rise, the spot purchase costs more, but the long futures position gains, offsetting the higher cost. Think of it like locking in today's pizza price for a party next month: if pizza gets pricier, your locked-in deal saves you.\n\nWhy B is correct: a future buyer fearing price rises buys futures (a long hedge) so futures gains offset the higher purchase cost.\n\nWhy A is wrong: a short hedge (selling futures) protects an owner/seller against FALLING prices, the opposite situation.\nWhy C is wrong: futures absolutely can hedge a future purchase — that is the long hedge.\nWhy D is wrong: buying now may not be possible or desirable (storage, cash, timing); futures hedge the price without taking early delivery.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Will SELL/own it later → SHORT hedge. Will BUY it later → LONG hedge. Match the futures direction to your future cash need.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Long vs short hedge\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Long hedge (buy futures)\",\"items\":[\"Will BUY asset later\",\"Fears price rise\",\"E.g. copper buyer\"]},{\"h\":\"Short hedge (sell futures)\",\"items\":[\"Will SELL/own asset later\",\"Fears price fall\",\"E.g. producer\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Monthly price changes have standard deviations $$\\sigma_S = 0.04$$ and $$\\sigma_F = 0.05$$, with correlation $$\\rho = 0.85$$. The minimum-variance hedge ratio is closest to:\nA) 0.85 B) 1.06 C) 0.68 D) 0.43\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the minimum-variance hedge ratio.\n\nThe formula is:\n$$h^* = \\rho \\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F}$$\n\nNote: spot volatility goes on TOP, futures volatility on the bottom.\n\nPlug in:\n$$h^* = 0.85 \\times \\frac{0.04}{0.05}$$\n\nFirst the ratio: 0.04 / 0.05 = 0.8.\nThen: 0.85 × 0.8 = 0.68.\n\nWhy C is correct: 0.85 × 0.8 = 0.68.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 0.85 is just the correlation; it forgets to multiply by the volatility ratio.\nWhy B is wrong: 1.06 = 0.85 × (0.05/0.04), the INVERTED ratio — the classic distractor with futures vol on top.\nWhy D is wrong: 0.43 does not match any correct combination of these inputs.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$h^* = \\rho\\,\\sigma_S/\\sigma_F$$ — spot on top. If your answer used futures vol on top, you flipped the ratio and fell for the trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An airline will buy 2,000,000 gallons of jet fuel and hedges with heating oil futures of 42,000 gallons each. The minimum-variance hedge ratio is $$h^* = 0.78$$. Ignoring tailing, how many futures contracts are needed (rounded)?\nA) 16 contracts B) 37 contracts C) 48 contracts D) 61 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the optimal number of contracts using the quantity-based formula.\n\nThe formula is:\n$$N^* = \\frac{h^* \\times Q_A}{Q_F}$$\n\nwhere $$Q_A$$ = size of position to hedge and $$Q_F$$ = contract size.\n\nPlug in:\n$$N^* = \\frac{0.78 \\times 2{,}000{,}000}{42{,}000}$$\n\nNumerator: 0.78 × 2,000,000 = 1,560,000.\nDivide: 1,560,000 / 42,000 = 37.14.\nRound to 37 contracts.\n\nWhy B is correct: 1,560,000 / 42,000 ≈ 37.14, which rounds to 37.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 16 would come from dividing by a much larger number or other slip.\nWhy C is wrong: 48 ≈ 2,000,000 / 42,000 — it forgets to multiply by the hedge ratio 0.78.\nWhy D is wrong: 61 overstates the count; no correct combination gives this.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$N^* = h^* Q_A / Q_F$$. Apply the hedge ratio FIRST, then divide by contract size. Forgetting $$h^*$$ gives the common too-high answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A short hedger benefits when:\nA) The basis weakens unexpectedly B) The basis strengthens unexpectedly C) The futures price falls below zero D) Basis risk is removed by daily settlement\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which basis move helps a short hedger.\n\nRecall the basis is $$b = S - F$$ (spot minus futures), and the effective price a short hedger locks in is:\n$$\\text{Effective price} = F_1 + b_2$$\n\nwhere $$b_2$$ is the basis at close-out. A STRENGTHENING (higher) basis means $$b_2$$ goes up, which raises the effective SALE price for the short hedger — good for someone selling.\n\nWhy B is correct: a stronger basis raises $$F_1 + b_2$$, so the short hedger effectively sells at a higher price.\n\nWhy A is wrong: a weakening basis LOWERS the effective sale price — that hurts the short hedger and helps the long hedger.\nWhy C is wrong: futures prices do not fall below zero in normal markets; irrelevant to basis.\nWhy D is wrong: daily settlement does not remove basis risk; basis risk comes from the spot-futures gap at close.\n\n📌 Exam tip: STRONG basis helps the SHORT hedger (sells higher). WEAK basis helps the LONG hedger (buys lower). Memorize \"strong = short.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Basis moves: who benefits?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Basis strengthens\",\"items\":[\"b2 rises\",\"Effective price F1+b2 higher\",\"Helps SHORT hedger\"]},{\"h\":\"Basis weakens\",\"items\":[\"b2 falls\",\"Effective price lower\",\"Helps LONG hedger\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A short hedge is initiated at a futures price of 60. At close-out, spot is 53 and the futures price is 55. The effective sale price achieved is:\nA) 53 B) 55 C) 58 D) 60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the effective sale price locked in by a short hedge.\n\nStep 1 — find the basis at close:\n$$b_2 = S_2 - F_2 = 53 - 55 = -2$$\n\nStep 2 — effective price for a short hedger:\n$$\\text{Effective price} = F_1 + b_2 = 60 + (-2) = 58$$\n\nCheck the other way: the short sells futures at 60 and buys back at 55, a futures gain of 60 − 55 = 5. Then sells the actual asset at spot 53. Total = 53 + 5 = 58. Both methods agree.\n\nWhy C is correct: $$F_1 + b_2 = 60 − 2 = 58$$ (also 53 + futures gain of 5 = 58).\n\nWhy A is wrong: 53 is just the spot sale price; it ignores the $5 futures gain.\nWhy B is wrong: 55 is the closing futures price, not the effective sale price.\nWhy D is wrong: 60 is the initial futures price; the basis at close shifts the effective price to 58.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Effective price (short hedge) = $$F_1 + b_2$$ = spot at close + futures gain. Always cross-check with the futures gain method.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A $50 million equity portfolio has a beta of 1.4. Index futures trade at 5,000 with a $250 multiplier. To reduce the portfolio beta to 0.8, the manager should:\nA) Buy 24 contracts B) Sell 24 contracts C) Sell 56 contracts D) Buy 40 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how many contracts to trade, and in which direction, to lower beta.\n\nStep 1 — value of one futures contract:\n$$F = 5{,}000 \\times 250 = \\$1{,}250{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — contracts to change beta:\n$$N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)\\frac{P}{F}$$\n\nPlug in:\n$$N = (0.8 - 1.4) \\times \\frac{50{,}000{,}000}{1{,}250{,}000}$$\n\nThe P/F part: 50,000,000 / 1,250,000 = 40.\nThe beta part: 0.8 − 1.4 = −0.6.\nSo N = −0.6 × 40 = −24.\n\nA NEGATIVE N means SELL (short) contracts. So the manager sells 24 contracts.\n\nWhy B is correct: N = −24, and the negative sign means sell 24 contracts.\n\nWhy A is wrong: it has the right number but the wrong direction; reducing beta requires SELLING, not buying.\nWhy C is wrong: 56 does not come from these numbers; likely a miscomputed P/F or beta term.\nWhy D is wrong: buying 40 would raise beta and uses the wrong figure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)P/F$$. Negative result = SELL (lowers beta). Positive = BUY (raises beta). Reducing beta always means shorting index futures.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Direction of beta change\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Lower beta (β*<β)\",\"items\":[\"N negative\",\"SELL (short) futures\"]},{\"h\":\"Raise beta (β*>β)\",\"items\":[\"N positive\",\"BUY (long) futures\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A manager wants to RAISE a $24 million portfolio's beta from 1.2 to 1.8. Index futures trade at 4,000 with a $250 multiplier. The required trade is closest to:\nA) Long 14 contracts B) Short 14 contracts C) Long 29 contracts D) Short 29 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks the number and direction of contracts to increase beta.\n\nStep 1 — value of one contract:\n$$F = 4{,}000 \\times 250 = \\$1{,}000{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — contracts to change beta:\n$$N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)\\frac{P}{F} = (1.8 - 1.2)\\times\\frac{24{,}000{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000}$$\n\nP/F: 24,000,000 / 1,000,000 = 24.\nBeta part: 1.8 − 1.2 = +0.6.\nN = 0.6 × 24 = +14.4 ≈ 14.\n\nA POSITIVE N means BUY (long) contracts. So go long about 14 contracts.\n\nWhy A is correct: N = +14.4, positive, so buy (long) 14 contracts.\n\nWhy B is wrong: shorting would LOWER beta; the sign is positive here, meaning buy.\nWhy C is wrong: 29 ≈ full-hedge size (1.2 × 24), which is unrelated to this beta change.\nWhy D is wrong: wrong direction and wrong number.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Raising beta → positive N → BUY contracts. The change uses $$(\\beta^* - \\beta)$$, not the full beta. Here that is only 0.6, giving ~14 contracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** When fully hedging a long equity portfolio with index futures to earn roughly the risk-free rate, a manager would prefer to hedge rather than sell the stocks mainly because:\nA) Hedging guarantees a higher return than holding the stocks B) Hedging avoids transaction costs and capital gains taxes, keeps the chosen stocks, and is quickly reversible C) Selling the portfolio is legally prohibited D) Futures always outperform the underlying stocks\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why a manager hedges instead of liquidating.\n\nSelling the whole portfolio triggers transaction costs and capital gains taxes, and forces the manager to give up the carefully selected stocks (and any alpha). A futures hedge temporarily removes market risk while keeping the stocks, and it can be unwound quickly and cheaply when the manager wants market exposure back.\n\nWhy B is correct: it lists the real reasons — avoid costs/taxes, keep the stocks/alpha, and reverse easily.\n\nWhy A is wrong: hedging does not guarantee a higher return; a fully hedged portfolio earns roughly the risk-free rate plus alpha.\nWhy C is wrong: selling is not prohibited; it is just costly and disruptive.\nWhy D is wrong: futures do not \"always outperform\" stocks; that is not the reason to hedge.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedge instead of sell to dodge taxes/transaction costs, keep your stock picks (alpha), and stay nimble. A fully hedged portfolio earns about the risk-free rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedge vs sell the portfolio\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Hedge with futures\",\"items\":[\"Avoids transaction costs & CG taxes\",\"Keeps chosen stocks / alpha\",\"Quickly reversible\",\"Earns ~ risk-free rate\"]},{\"h\":\"Sell stocks\",\"items\":[\"Triggers costs & taxes\",\"Gives up stock picks\",\"Slow to rebuild\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** In a \"stack and roll\" strategy used to hedge a long-dated exposure with short-dated futures, the two key risks the hedger faces are:\nA) Convergence risk and tick-size risk B) Rollover basis risk and cash-flow timing risk from margin calls C) Counterparty default risk and translation risk D) Dividend risk and beta drift\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the main risks created by stacking and rolling futures.\n\nA stack-and-roll hedge puts the full position in a near-dated contract, then repeatedly closes it before delivery and re-opens it in the next contract. Two dangers arise:\n1. Rollover basis risk — the price difference between the expiring and the new contract at each roll is unknown in advance.\n2. Cash-flow timing risk — futures losses trigger immediate margin calls (real cash out the door), while the offsetting gains on the long-term position are unrealized (paper gains, no cash in).\n\nThis is exactly what sank Metallgesellschaft in 1993.\n\nWhy B is correct: rollover basis risk plus margin-call cash-flow timing are the two classic stack-and-roll risks.\n\nWhy A is wrong: tick size is trivial; convergence is normal and not the key danger here.\nWhy C is wrong: counterparty risk is minimal in cleared futures; translation risk is an FX-accounting concept, not relevant.\nWhy D is wrong: dividends and beta drift relate to equity hedges, not commodity stack-and-roll.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stack and roll → (1) rollover basis risk at each roll and (2) margin-call cash drain while hedge gains are unrealized. Remember Metallgesellschaft.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Stack-and-roll risks\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Rollover basis risk\",\"items\":[\"Price gap at each roll\",\"Unknown in advance\"]},{\"h\":\"Cash-flow timing risk\",\"items\":[\"Futures losses → margin calls (cash out)\",\"Long-term gains unrealized (no cash in)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** For a cross hedge with correlation $$\\rho = 0.928$$ between changes in the hedged asset's price and the futures price, the hedge effectiveness (proportion of variance eliminated) is closest to:\nA) 92.8% B) 86.1% C) 13.9% D) 96.3%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for hedge effectiveness, which is the fraction of variance removed by the hedge.\n\nThe formula is:\n$$R^2 = \\rho^2$$\n\nPlug in:\n$$R^2 = 0.928^2 = 0.861$$\n\nSo about 86.1% of the variance of the hedged item is eliminated. The remaining 1 − 0.861 = 0.139, or 13.9%, stays as basis risk.\n\nWhy B is correct: 0.928 squared = 0.861, i.e., 86.1% of variance removed.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 92.8% is just $$\\rho$$ itself, not squared; effectiveness is $$\\rho^2$$.\nWhy C is wrong: 13.9% is the variance REMAINING, not the variance eliminated.\nWhy D is wrong: 96.3% would be the square root of 0.928, not its square.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedge effectiveness = $$\\rho^2$$ (square the correlation). The leftover (1 − $$\\rho^2$$) is the basis risk you could not hedge away.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Monthly changes: $$\\sigma_S = 0.04$$, $$\\sigma_F = 0.05$$, $$\\rho = 0.85$$. The minimum-variance hedge ratio is closest to:\nA) 0.85 B) 1.06 C) 0.68 D) 0.43\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** h* = 0.85 × (0.04/0.05) = 0.85 × 0.8 = 0.68. (1.06 uses the inverted ratio — the classic distractor.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A $50 million portfolio has beta 1.4. Index futures trade at 5,000 with a $250 multiplier. To cut beta to 0.8 the manager should:\nA) Buy 24 contracts B) Sell 24 contracts C) Sell 56 contracts D) Buy 40 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** F = 5,000 × 250 = $1,250,000; N = (0.8 − 1.4) × 50,000,000/1,250,000 = −0.6 × 40 = −24 → sell 24 contracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A short hedger benefits when:\nA) The basis weakens unexpectedly B) The basis strengthens unexpectedly C) The futures price rises more than spot D) Basis risk is eliminated by daily settlement\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Effective price for a short hedger = F1 + b2; a stronger (higher) basis at close raises the effective sale price.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A short hedge is initiated at a futures price of 60. At close-out, spot is 53 and the futures price is 55. The effective sale price is:\nA) 53 B) 55 C) 58 D) 60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** b2 = 53 − 55 = −2; effective price = F1 + b2 = 60 − 2 = 58 (equivalently 53 + futures gain of 5).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** An airline must buy 2 million gallons of jet fuel and hedges with heating oil futures (42,000 gallons per contract). The standard deviation of jet fuel spot price changes is 0.65, that of heating oil futures price changes is 0.81, and the correlation is 0.8. The optimal number of contracts is closest to:\nA) 25 B) 31 C) 38 D) 48\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Minimum-variance hedge ratio:\n\n$$h^* = \\rho\\,\\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F} = 0.8 \\times \\frac{0.65}{0.81} = 0.642$$\n\n$$N^* = h^* \\times \\frac{Q_A}{Q_F} = 0.642 \\times \\frac{2{,}000{,}000}{42{,}000} = 30.6 \\approx \\textbf{31 contracts}$$\n\nD (48) skips $$h^*$$ entirely; C (38) forgets the correlation.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A wheat farmer shorts futures to hedge a future sale of wheat. Between hedge inception and the sale, the basis (spot minus futures) unexpectedly strengthens. The farmer's effective sale price is:\nA) Lower than originally expected, because the short futures position loses B) Higher than originally expected, because a short hedger gains when the basis strengthens C) Unchanged, because hedging removes all price risk D) Higher only if the spot price also rises\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A short hedger's effective price = F₁ + basis at close-out. Strengthening basis (spot up relative to futures) raises the effective sale price — the short hedger *gains* from basis strengthening, the long hedger loses.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Basis strengthens (basis ↑)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Short hedger (seller)\",\"items\":[\"effective price = F₁ + basis\",\"gains\",\"higher sale price\"]},{\"h\":\"Long hedger (buyer)\",\"items\":[\"effective cost = F₁ + basis\",\"loses\",\"higher purchase price\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A jet-fuel buyer will purchase 1,500,000 gallons in two months and hedges with heating-oil futures (42,000 gallons per contract). From historical monthly price changes: standard deviation of jet-fuel spot changes σ_S = 0.032, standard deviation of heating-oil futures changes σ_F = 0.040, and correlation ρ = 0.80. Using the minimum-variance hedge ratio (quantity basis), how many contracts should be traded, and in which direction?\nA) Long 23 contracts B) Long 36 contracts C) Short 23 contracts D) Long 29 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute h*, then N* on a quantity basis, and choose the direction for a future *purchase*.\n\n1. Minimum-variance hedge ratio (spot vol on top):\n$$h^* = \\rho\\,\\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F} = 0.80 \\times \\frac{0.032}{0.040} = 0.80 \\times 0.80 = 0.64$$\n\n2. Optimal number of contracts:\n$$N^* = \\frac{h^* Q_A}{Q_F} = \\frac{0.64 \\times 1{,}500{,}000}{42{,}000} = \\frac{960{,}000}{42{,}000} = 22.86 \\approx 23$$\n\n3. The buyer needs fuel later and fears *rising* prices, so it takes a **long** hedge. Answer: **long 23 contracts**.\n\n- **B** (36) uses the *inverted* ratio h* = ρ·σ_F/σ_S = 0.80 × (0.040/0.032) = 0.80 × 1.25 = 1.0 → N = 1.0 × 1.5M/42,000 = 35.7 ≈ 36. Putting futures vol on top is the classic trap.\n- **C** (short 23) gets the magnitude right but the wrong direction — a buyer hedges *long*, not short.\n- **D** (29) drops ρ entirely (uses σ_S/σ_F = 0.80, N = 0.80 × 1.5M/42,000 = 28.6 ≈ 29) — omitting correlation overstates the hedge.\n\n📌 Exam tip: h* = ρσ_S/σ_F (spot SD on top). A future *buyer* goes long; a future *seller/holder* goes short. Round N* to the nearest whole contract.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A pension fund holds a $60,000,000 equity portfolio with beta 1.10 relative to the S&P 500. Index futures are quoted at 4,800 with a $50 multiplier. The manager wants to *raise* the portfolio's beta to 1.50 ahead of an expected rally. How many futures contracts are required, and in which direction?\nA) Long 100 contracts B) Short 100 contracts C) Long 275 contracts D) Long 138 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: change beta from 1.10 to 1.50 using index futures.\n\n1. Futures contract value: F = 4,800 × $50 = $240,000.\n\n2. Number of contracts to change beta:\n$$N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)\\frac{P}{F} = (1.50 - 1.10)\\times \\frac{60{,}000{,}000}{240{,}000} = 0.40 \\times 250 = 100$$\n\n3. N is positive → **buy (long) 100 contracts** (raising beta means going long the market).\n\n- **B** (short 100) has the right magnitude but wrong direction — shorting *lowers* beta; raising beta requires longs.\n- **C** (275) applies the *target* beta to the whole position as if fully establishing 1.50 exposure (1.50 × 250 = 375, or similar) instead of the *change* (β* − β).\n- **D** (138) uses the change in beta but with the *current* beta 1.10 baked in incorrectly (e.g., 0.40 × 1.10 × ... ) — only (β* − β)·P/F is correct, no extra beta factor.\n\n📌 Exam tip: N = (β* − β)·P/F. Positive ⇒ long (raise beta); negative ⇒ short (lower beta). Use F = quote × multiplier, not the bare quote.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A $90,000,000 portfolio has beta 1.25. The manager wants to fully hedge market risk for three months using index futures quoted at 3,600 with a $250 multiplier. After a perfect full hedge, what return should the hedged portfolio approximately earn over the period (ignoring alpha and basis risk), and how many contracts are shorted?\nA) The market return; short 125 contracts B) The risk-free rate; short 125 contracts C) The risk-free rate; short 100 contracts D) Zero; short 156 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: number of contracts for a full hedge and the expected return of the fully hedged position.\n\n1. Full hedge sets β* = 0:\n$$N^* = \\beta\\frac{P}{F},\\quad F = 3{,}600 \\times 250 = \\$900{,}000$$\n$$N^* = 1.25 \\times \\frac{90{,}000{,}000}{900{,}000} = 1.25 \\times 100 = 125 \\text{ contracts (short)}$$\n\n2. A fully hedged (β = 0) portfolio has no systematic market exposure, so by CAPM it should earn approximately the **risk-free rate** over the period (plus any alpha, which we ignore). Answer: risk-free rate; short 125 contracts → **B**.\n\n- **A** says \"market return\" — wrong; a zero-beta hedged portfolio earns the risk-free rate, not the market return.\n- **C** has the right return but miscounts contracts (drops the 1.25 beta, giving 100).\n- **D** says \"zero\" return — a common trap; the hedge removes *market risk*, not the time value of money, so the expected return is the risk-free rate, not zero. The 156 count also mis-scales (e.g., 1.25 × 90M/720k).\n\n📌 Exam tip: A fully hedged equity portfolio earns ≈ the risk-free rate (plus alpha), NOT zero and NOT the market return. Full hedge N* = βP/F.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Return of a fully hedged (β=0) portfolio\",\"options\":[\"A: Market return\",\"B: Risk-free rate\",\"C: Zero\",\"D: Negative\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Removing market risk (β→0) leaves a zero-beta position; by CAPM it earns ≈ the risk-free rate (plus any alpha), not zero and not the market return.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** In May, a copper producer expecting to sell 500,000 lb in September shorts September futures at F₁ = $4.10/lb. In September the producer closes the hedge when spot is S₂ = $3.85/lb and the September futures price is F₂ = $3.78/lb. What effective price per pound does the producer realize, and did the basis strengthen or weaken relative to the initial basis assumption of zero?\nA) $3.85; basis strengthened B) $4.17; basis strengthened C) $4.03; weakened D) $4.10; unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the effective sale price via the basis and judge the basis move for the short hedger.\n\n1. Basis at close: $$b_2 = S_2 - F_2 = 3.85 - 3.78 = +0.07$$.\n\n2. Effective sale price for a short hedger:\n$$\\text{Effective price} = F_1 + b_2 = 4.10 + 0.07 = \\$4.17/\\text{lb}$$\nCheck via spot + futures gain: futures gain = F₁ − F₂ = 4.10 − 3.78 = 0.32; effective = S₂ + gain = 3.85 + 0.32 = $4.17. ✓\n\n3. A positive basis of +0.07 vs. an assumed zero is a **strengthening** basis, which *benefits the short hedger* (sells at an effectively higher price). Answer: **$4.17; basis strengthened** → B.\n\n- **A** ($3.85) reports the raw spot price, forgetting the futures gain.\n- **C** ($4.03) subtracts the basis instead of adding (4.10 − 0.07), the sign trap, and mislabels the move.\n- **D** ($4.10) assumes the basis converged to zero (only true at expiration with matched grade/location).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Short hedger effective price = F₁ + b₂. A *strengthening* (higher) basis helps the short hedger; a *weakening* basis hurts. Add the closing basis, don't subtract.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A natural-gas marketer hedges a long-dated fixed-price supply contract by stacking the full notional in near-month long futures and rolling them forward each month. Gas prices fall sharply over the next two quarters. Which risk most directly threatens the marketer, as illustrated by Metallgesellschaft (1993)?\nA) The hedge over-protects, generating excess futures gains that must be returned B) Funding/liquidity risk: futures losses trigger immediate margin calls while the offsetting gains on the long-term contracts are distant and unrealized C) Counterparty risk on the exchange clearing house D) The minimum-variance hedge ratio becomes negative, reversing the hedge direction\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the cash-flow timing/liquidity danger in a stack-and-roll hedge when prices move against the futures leg.\n\n1. The marketer is *long* near-month futures (hedging a future obligation to deliver gas at a fixed price). If gas prices *fall*, the long futures lose money daily, triggering immediate variation-margin calls.\n2. The offsetting economic gain — being able to source gas cheaply for the long-dated supply contracts — is *unrealized and distant*. The mismatch between immediate cash outflows (margin) and deferred inflows is a funding/liquidity crisis. This is precisely what sank Metallgesellschaft, with roughly $1.3 billion of losses. Answer: **B**.\n\n- **A** misframes the problem: the issue is *losses* on futures, not excess gains being clawed back.\n- **C** is essentially nil because the clearing house guarantees performance — not the threat here.\n- **D** is a red herring; the hedge ratio does not flip sign because prices fall.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stack-and-roll adds *rollover basis risk* AND *cash-flow timing risk*. Margin calls on the futures hit now; offsetting gains on the underlying are unrealized — a funding mismatch (the Metallgesellschaft lesson).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Metallgesellschaft funding mismatch\",\"root\":\"Gas prices fall\",\"children\":[\"Long near-month futures lose → immediate margin calls (cash out)\",\"Cheap-sourcing gain on long-dated contracts is distant & unrealized (no cash in)\",\"Liquidity crisis\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Monthly price-change data give σ_S = 0.045, σ_F = 0.050, and ρ = 0.90 between a hedged asset and the futures used to hedge it. What fraction of the variance of the hedged position is eliminated by the minimum-variance hedge, and what is the hedge ratio?\nA) 90% eliminated; h* = 0.90 B) 81% eliminated; h* = 0.90 C) 81% eliminated; h* = 0.81 D) 95% eliminated; h* = 1.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: compute hedge effectiveness (R² = ρ²) and the hedge ratio h* = ρσ_S/σ_F.\n\n1. Hedge ratio:\n$$h^* = \\rho\\,\\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F} = 0.90 \\times \\frac{0.045}{0.050} = 0.90 \\times 0.90 = 0.81$$\n\n2. Hedge effectiveness (proportion of variance eliminated):\n$$R^2 = \\rho^2 = 0.90^2 = 0.81 \\Rightarrow 81\\%$$\nSo 81% of the position's variance is removed; 19% remains as basis risk. Answer: **C** — 81% eliminated, h* = 0.81.\n\n- **A** confuses effectiveness with ρ itself (90%) and uses h* = 0.90 (forgetting the σ_S/σ_F factor).\n- **B** gets effectiveness right (81%) but uses h* = ρ = 0.90, dropping the volatility ratio.\n- **D** invents 95% and uses h* = 1.00 (ignoring both ρ and the volatility ratio).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedge *effectiveness* = R² = ρ² (variance eliminated), not ρ. Hedge *ratio* = ρσ_S/σ_F. Don't confuse the two — ρ = 0.90 gives only 81% variance reduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A portfolio manager argues the firm should NOT hedge its commodity input costs with futures. Which of the following is a *valid* argument against hedging in this context?\nA) Hedging always increases expected profit B) If competitors do not hedge, industry output prices and input costs tend to move together, naturally stabilizing margins; a lone hedger then makes its *own* margins more volatile C) Hedging eliminates basis risk entirely, which regulators prohibit D) Shareholders are unable to diversify or hedge on their own account\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: pick the genuine argument *against* corporate hedging.\n\n1. A recognized argument against hedging: if rivals do not hedge, input and output prices move together across the industry, so unhedged firms enjoy naturally stable margins. A firm that hedges its inputs while competitors don't can find its margins *more* volatile relative to peers. Answer: **B**.\n\n- **A** is false: hedging is about *reducing variability*, not increasing expected profit; it can reduce expected profit by the cost of hedging.\n- **C** is false: hedging does not eliminate basis risk (it *replaces* price risk with basis risk), and there is no such regulatory prohibition.\n- **D** reverses a key point: shareholders *can* diversify and hedge themselves (often more cheaply), which is itself an argument *against* corporate hedging.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Valid anti-hedging arguments: shareholders can hedge/diversify themselves; competitive (peer) effects can make a lone hedger's margins *more* volatile; and visible futures losses are hard to explain even when the combined position performed as designed.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Valid arguments against corporate hedging\",\"root\":\"Why not hedge?\",\"children\":[\"Shareholders can diversify/hedge themselves\",\"Peers unhedged → lone hedger's margins more volatile\",\"Futures losses hard to explain to stakeholders\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"309","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Foreign Exchange Markets","notes":"# 9. Foreign Exchange Markets\n\nGARP tests quote conventions, cross rates and triangular arbitrage, the three FX risks of a multinational and how each is hedged, and — most heavily — covered interest rate parity calculations and the arbitrage when it fails.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain FX quote conventions: base vs quote currency, direct vs indirect quotes, bid-ask spreads, and pips.\n- Compare the spot, outright forward, FX swap, and futures markets for currency.\n- Calculate cross rates and identify triangular arbitrage when quotes are inconsistent.\n- Distinguish transaction, translation, and economic risk for a multinational and describe how each is hedged.\n- Distinguish nominal from real exchange rates and state purchasing power parity.\n- Explain and apply covered interest rate parity; construct the arbitrage when the market forward violates it.\n- Explain uncovered interest rate parity and why the carry trade exploits its empirical failure.\n\n## Quote conventions\n\nA quote EUR/USD = 1.1000 means 1 unit of the **base currency** (EUR, listed first) costs 1.1000 units of the **quote currency** (USD). The price is always expressed in the quote currency.\n\n- **Direct quote:** domestic currency per unit of foreign currency (a US investor quoting USD per EUR).\n- **Indirect quote:** foreign currency per unit of domestic currency (the reciprocal).\n- **Bid-ask:** the dealer buys the base currency at the bid and sells it at the (higher) ask. A customer buying EUR pays the ask.\n- **Pip:** the smallest standard price increment, usually the 4th decimal (0.0001) for most pairs, the 2nd decimal for JPY pairs. A move from 1.1000 to 1.1025 is 25 pips.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Forwards are often quoted as **forward points** (pips) to add to or subtract from spot: spot 1.1000 plus 30 forward points gives an outright forward of 1.1030.\n\n## Market instruments\n\n| Instrument | Description | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Spot** | Exchange of currencies, usually settling T+2 | Immediate needs |\n| **Outright forward** | Custom OTC agreement to exchange at a set rate on a future date | Hedging a single future cash flow |\n| **FX swap** | Spot exchange combined with a reverse forward exchange | Rolling hedges, short-term funding in another currency |\n| **Futures** | Exchange-traded, standardized, daily settlement via CCP | Hedging/speculation with no counterparty credit exposure |\n\nAn FX swap is a single contract with two legs; it is the most heavily traded FX instrument and is economically a collateralized borrowing of one currency against another.\n\n## Cross rates and triangular arbitrage\n\nMost currencies are quoted against the USD; a **cross rate** between two non-USD currencies is implied by their USD quotes. If USD/GBP (USD per £) is 1.2500 and USD/EUR (USD per €) is 1.1000, the implied cross is\n\n$$\\text{EUR per GBP} = \\frac{1.2500}{1.1000} = 1.1364$$\n\nIf a dealer quotes a cross rate different from the implied one, **triangular arbitrage** — converting through three currencies and back — locks in a riskless profit (see Worked example 2).\n\n## FX risk for a multinational\n\n| Risk | What it is | How it is hedged |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Transaction risk** | Known future foreign-currency cash flows (receivables, payables) change domestic value with FX moves | Forwards, futures, options, money-market hedges |\n| **Translation risk** | Accounting exposure: foreign assets and liabilities are translated into the home currency for consolidated statements | Finance foreign assets with liabilities in the **same currency** so gains and losses offset |\n| **Economic risk** | Long-run competitive exposure: FX moves change future revenues and costs even with no booked transactions | Operational diversification of production, sourcing, and sales across currencies |\n\n> 📌 **Important**  \n> Hedging translation risk with derivatives is **speculative** if the foreign assets are not actually going to be sold: the derivative produces real cash gains/losses against a paper accounting exposure. The sound hedge is matched-currency financing.\n\n## Nominal vs real exchange rates and PPP\n\nThe **nominal** rate is the quoted market rate. The **real** exchange rate adjusts for relative price levels: $$E_{real} = E_{nominal} \\times P_{foreign}/P_{domestic}$$. **Purchasing power parity** says nominal rates adjust over time to offset inflation differentials, keeping the real rate constant — a useful long-run anchor, a poor short-run predictor.\n\n## Covered interest rate parity (CIP)\n\nWith S and F quoted as **domestic per unit of foreign**, r the domestic rate, and the foreign rate continuously compounded:\n\n$$F = S e^{(r - r_f)T}$$\n\nDiscrete-compounding version:\n\n$$F = S\\,\\frac{(1+r)^T}{(1+r_f)^T}$$\n\nIntuition: investing domestically must earn the same as converting to foreign currency, investing at the foreign rate, and locking in the conversion back via the forward. If not, riskless arbitrage exists — CIP is **enforced by arbitrage**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Direction trap: in $$F = Se^{(r-r_f)T}$$ the currency with the **higher** interest rate trades at a forward **discount** (it depreciates forward) — its rate advantage must be given back through the forward, or arbitrage exists. And keep the quote direction consistent (domestic per foreign, domestic rate first in the exponent); flip the quote and the formula inverts. Exams routinely offer the inverted answer as a distractor.\n\n## Uncovered interest rate parity (UIP) and the carry trade\n\nUIP replaces the forward with the **expected future spot**: $$E[S_T] = S e^{(r - r_f)T}$$. Nothing enforces it — there is no arbitrage in an expectation — and empirically high-rate currencies do *not* depreciate as much as UIP predicts. The **carry trade** exploits this: borrow the low-rate currency, invest in the high-rate currency, and earn the differential. It earns steadily in calm markets but suffers severe losses when the funding currency appreciates sharply (\"up the stairs, down the elevator\").\n\n| Parity | Compares F or E[S] with spot | Enforced by arbitrage? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Covered (CIP) | Forward rate F | **Yes** — riskless round trip |\n| Uncovered (UIP) | Expected future spot $$E[S_T]$$ | **No** — expectation only |\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> For small rates and short horizons, the forward premium/discount on the foreign currency is approximately the interest differential: $$\\frac{F-S}{S} \\approx (r - r_f)T$$. This quick check catches sign errors before you commit to an answer.\n\n### Worked example: covered interest parity arbitrage\n\nSpot USD/GBP = 1.3000 (USD per £). The 1-year USD rate is 3% and the 1-year GBP rate is 5%, both continuously compounded. The no-arbitrage forward is\n\n$$F = 1.3000\\, e^{(0.03 - 0.05)\\times 1} = 1.3000 \\times e^{-0.02} = 1.3000 \\times 0.9802 = 1.2743$$\n\nGBP, the higher-rate currency, is at a forward discount. Suppose the market forward is instead **1.2900** — too high, so sell GBP forward and carry it:\n\n1. Borrow $13,000,000 at 3% for one year.\n2. Buy £10,000,000 spot at 1.3000; invest at 5% → grows to 10,000,000 × $$e^{0.05}$$ = 10,000,000 × 1.0513 = £10,513,000.\n3. Sell £10,513,000 forward at 1.2900.\n\nAt maturity: forward delivery brings 10,513,000 × 1.2900 = $13,561,770. Repay the loan: 13,000,000 × $$e^{0.03}$$ = 13,000,000 × 1.0305 = $13,396,500.\n\nRiskless profit = 13,561,770 − 13,396,500 = **$165,270**, consistent with (1.2900 − 1.2743) ≈ 0.0157 per pound carried.\n\n### Worked example: cross rate and triangular arbitrage\n\nUSD/EUR = 1.1000 (USD per €) and USD/GBP = 1.2500 (USD per £). Implied cross: 1.2500 / 1.1000 = **1.1364 EUR per GBP**. A dealer quotes the cross at 1.1500 — GBP is too expensive in EUR terms, so *sell* GBP for EUR at the dealer's rate. Starting with €1,000,000:\n\n1. EUR → USD: 1,000,000 × 1.1000 = $1,100,000.\n2. USD → GBP: 1,100,000 / 1.2500 = £880,000.\n3. GBP → EUR at the quoted cross: 880,000 × 1.1500 = €1,012,000.\n\nRiskless profit = **€12,000** (1.2% — exactly the 1.1500/1.1364 misalignment). Real-world spreads and speed eliminate such gaps almost instantly.","flashcards":"**Q1.** In the quote EUR/USD = 1.1000, which is the base currency and what does the number mean?\n> **A:** EUR (listed first) is the base currency; 1 EUR costs 1.1000 USD. The price is always expressed in the quote currency (USD).\n\n**Q2.** Distinguish direct from indirect quotes.\n> **A:** Direct quote: domestic currency per unit of foreign currency (US investor quoting USD per EUR). Indirect quote: foreign currency per unit of domestic currency (the reciprocal).\n\n**Q3.** What is a pip?\n> **A:** The smallest standard price increment — usually the 4th decimal (0.0001) for most pairs, the 2nd decimal for JPY pairs. A move from 1.1000 to 1.1025 is 25 pips.\n\n**Q4.** What is an FX swap?\n> **A:** A single contract with two legs: a spot exchange combined with a reverse forward exchange. It is the most heavily traded FX instrument, economically a collateralized borrowing of one currency against another; used for rolling hedges and short-term funding.\n\n**Q5.** Compute the implied cross rate: USD/GBP = 1.2500 and USD/EUR = 1.1000.\n> **A:** EUR per GBP = $$\\frac{1.2500}{1.1000} = 1.1364$$. If a dealer quotes a different cross, triangular arbitrage (converting through three currencies and back) locks in a riskless profit.\n\n**Q6.** Distinguish transaction, translation, and economic FX risk.\n> **A:** Transaction: known future foreign-currency cash flows change domestic value. Translation: accounting exposure from consolidating foreign assets/liabilities. Economic: long-run competitive exposure of future revenues and costs to FX moves.\n\n**Q7.** How is each FX risk hedged?\n> **A:** Transaction risk: forwards, futures, options, money-market hedges. Translation risk: finance foreign assets with liabilities in the same currency so gains/losses offset. Economic risk: operational diversification of production, sourcing, and sales across currencies.\n\n**Q8.** Trap: why is hedging translation risk with derivatives often speculative?\n> **A:** If the foreign assets are not actually going to be sold, the derivative produces real cash gains/losses against a paper accounting exposure. The sound hedge is matched-currency financing.\n\n**Q9.** State purchasing power parity and the real exchange rate.\n> **A:** Real rate: $$E_{real} = E_{nominal} \\times P_{foreign}/P_{domestic}$$. PPP says nominal rates adjust over time to offset inflation differentials, keeping the real rate constant — a good long-run anchor but a poor short-run predictor.\n\n**Q10.** State covered interest rate parity (CIP).\n> **A:** With S, F as domestic per unit of foreign: $$F = S e^{(r - r_f)T}$$ (continuous) or $$F = S\\frac{(1+r)^T}{(1+r_f)^T}$$ (discrete). CIP is enforced by arbitrage — a riskless round trip exists if it is violated.\n\n**Q11.** Under CIP, does the higher-interest-rate currency trade at a forward premium or discount?\n> **A:** A forward discount — it depreciates forward. Its rate advantage must be given back through the forward, or arbitrage exists.\n\n**Q12.** Spot USD/GBP = 1.3000, USD rate 3%, GBP rate 5% (continuous). No-arbitrage 1-year forward?\n> **A:** $$F = 1.3000\\,e^{(0.03-0.05)\\times1} = 1.3000 \\times 0.9802 = 1.2743$$. GBP (higher rate) is at a forward discount. A market forward of 1.2900 is too high → sell GBP forward and carry it for riskless profit.\n\n**Q13.** State uncovered interest rate parity (UIP) and why it is not enforced.\n> **A:** $$E[S_T] = S e^{(r - r_f)T}$$ — it replaces the forward with the expected future spot. Nothing enforces it (no arbitrage in an expectation), and empirically high-rate currencies don't depreciate as much as UIP predicts.\n\n**Q14.** What is the carry trade and its risk profile?\n> **A:** Borrow the low-rate currency, invest in the high-rate currency, earn the differential — exploiting UIP's empirical failure. It earns steadily in calm markets but suffers severe losses when the funding currency appreciates sharply: \"up the stairs, down the elevator.\"\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A quote reads EUR/USD = 1.1000. What does this mean?\nA) 1 US dollar costs 1.1000 euros B) 1 euro costs 1.1000 US dollars C) The euro and dollar are equal in value D) 1.1000 euros equal 1.1000 dollars\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the base-versus-quote currency convention.\n\nIn a quote like EUR/USD = 1.1000, the FIRST currency (EUR) is the base currency, and the SECOND (USD) is the quote currency. The number tells you how many units of the quote currency it takes to buy ONE unit of the base currency. So 1 euro (base) costs 1.1000 dollars (quote). The price is always given in the quote currency.\n\nWhy B is correct: one unit of the base (EUR) = 1.1000 units of the quote (USD).\n\nWhy A is wrong: it reverses base and quote; that would be the reciprocal (USD/EUR).\nWhy C is wrong: 1.1000 is not 1.0000, so the currencies are not equal in value.\nWhy D is wrong: the number is a price, not a statement that the two amounts are equal.\n\n📌 Exam tip: First currency = base (the \"one unit\"); second = quote (the price). \"EUR/USD = 1.10\" means one euro costs $1.10.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EUR/USD = 1.1000\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Base (first)\",\"items\":[\"EUR\",\"The 'one unit'\"]},{\"h\":\"Quote (second)\",\"items\":[\"USD\",\"The price\"]},{\"h\":\"Meaning\",\"items\":[\"1 EUR = 1.1000 USD\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Spot USD/EUR = 1.2000 (USD per euro). The 6-month USD interest rate is 2% and the EUR rate is 4%, both continuously compounded. The 6-month no-arbitrage forward rate is closest to:\nA) 1.1880 B) 1.2000 C) 1.2121 D) 1.2240\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the covered-interest-parity forward rate.\n\nWith the rate quoted as domestic (USD) per unit of foreign (EUR), the formula is:\n$$F = S\\,e^{(r - r_f)T}$$\n\nHere domestic r = 0.02 (USD), foreign $$r_f$$ = 0.04 (EUR), S = 1.2000, T = 0.5.\n\nExponent: (0.02 − 0.04) × 0.5 = (−0.02) × 0.5 = −0.01.\n$$e^{-0.01} \\approx 0.9900$$.\n$$F = 1.2000 \\times 0.9900 = 1.1880$$.\n\nWhy A is correct: 1.2000 × e^(−0.01) = 1.2000 × 0.9900 = 1.1880.\n\nWhy B is wrong: 1.2000 is just spot; it ignores the rate differential entirely.\nWhy C is wrong: 1.2121 ≈ 1.2000 × e^(+0.01) — it flips the sign of the exponent (the inverted-formula distractor).\nWhy D is wrong: 1.2240 doubles the differential or otherwise mishandles the exponent.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$F = Se^{(r - r_f)T}$$. The HIGHER-rate currency (EUR at 4%) trades at a forward DISCOUNT, so the forward (1.1880) is below spot (1.2000). A forward above spot signals a sign error.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Under covered interest rate parity, the currency with the HIGHER interest rate:\nA) Trades at a forward premium B) Trades at a forward discount C) Must appreciate in the spot market D) Has no determinate forward relationship\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how a high interest rate affects a currency's forward price under CIP.\n\nCIP says you cannot earn a free lunch by switching currencies and locking in the forward. If a currency offers a higher interest rate, that advantage must be given back somewhere — and it is given back through the forward, where the high-rate currency trades at a discount (it is expected to depreciate in forward terms). Otherwise, riskless arbitrage would exist.\n\nWhy B is correct: the high-rate currency's rate advantage is offset by a forward discount.\n\nWhy A is wrong: a forward PREMIUM would let investors earn both the higher rate AND a currency gain — a free lunch arbitrage would eliminate it.\nWhy C is wrong: CIP is about the FORWARD rate, not a prediction about the spot market; CIP does not require spot appreciation.\nWhy D is wrong: CIP gives a precise, arbitrage-enforced forward relationship, not an indeterminate one.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Higher interest rate → forward DISCOUNT under CIP. The rate edge is always handed back via the forward, or arbitrage appears.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CIP: rate vs forward\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Higher-rate currency\",\"items\":[\"Forward DISCOUNT\",\"Depreciates in forward terms\"]},{\"h\":\"Lower-rate currency\",\"items\":[\"Forward PREMIUM\",\"Appreciates in forward terms\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** USD/EUR = 1.0800 (USD per euro) and CHF/USD = 0.9000 (CHF per dollar). The implied CHF per EUR cross rate is closest to:\nA) 0.8333 B) 0.9720 C) 1.2000 D) 1.9800\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a cross rate built from two USD-based quotes.\n\nWe want CHF per EUR. Use the chain (units cancel like fractions):\n$$\\text{CHF per EUR} = (\\text{USD per EUR}) \\times (\\text{CHF per USD})$$\n\nPlug in:\n$$= 1.0800 \\times 0.9000 = 0.9720$$\n\nCheck the units: (USD/EUR) × (CHF/USD) — the USD cancels, leaving CHF/EUR. Correct.\n\nWhy B is correct: 1.0800 × 0.9000 = 0.9720 CHF per EUR.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 0.8333 = 0.9000 / 1.0800 — it divides instead of multiplying, mishandling units.\nWhy C is wrong: 1.2000 = 1.0800 / 0.9000 — wrong operation; units do not cancel correctly.\nWhy D is wrong: 1.9800 = 1.0800 + 0.9000 — you cannot add exchange rates.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For cross rates, write each quote as a fraction (units) and chain them so the common currency cancels. If USD cancels, you set it up right.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A US multinational owns a factory in Brazil that it has no intention of selling. The exposure of its consolidated balance sheet to the Brazilian real (BRL) is best managed by:\nA) Selling BRL forward each quarter B) Buying BRL put options every month C) Financing the factory with BRL-denominated debt D) Doing nothing, because economic risk cannot be hedged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how to hedge translation (accounting) risk on a foreign asset that won't be sold.\n\nTranslation risk arises when foreign assets and liabilities are converted into the home currency for the consolidated financial statements. The sound hedge is matched-currency financing: fund the BRL asset with BRL-denominated debt. Then if the real moves, the gain or loss on the asset is offset by an equal and opposite move on the liability, leaving the balance sheet net exposure roughly unchanged.\n\nWhy C is correct: matching the currency of the liability to the asset offsets translation gains and losses naturally.\n\nWhy A is wrong: selling BRL forward against an asset you will NOT sell creates real cash gains/losses against a paper exposure — that is speculation, not a clean hedge.\nWhy B is wrong: buying options against an unsold asset is likewise speculative and costs premium for a paper exposure.\nWhy D is wrong: this is translation risk (an accounting exposure), which CAN be hedged with matched-currency financing; doing nothing is not the best answer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Translation risk → hedge with matched-currency FINANCING. Using derivatives against assets you won't sell turns the \"hedge\" into a speculative bet.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Hedging a BRL factory you won't sell\",\"options\":[\"A: Sell BRL forward quarterly\",\"B: Buy BRL puts monthly\",\"C: Finance the factory with BRL debt\",\"D: Do nothing\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"This is translation (accounting) risk on an unsold asset. Matched-currency financing offsets it naturally; derivatives create real cash P&L against a paper exposure (speculation).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A treasurer must distinguish FX risks. A known, contracted foreign-currency receivable due in 90 days that will change in home-currency value as the exchange rate moves is an example of:\nA) Translation risk B) Transaction risk C) Economic risk D) Settlement risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which type of FX risk a known future cash flow represents.\n\nTransaction risk is the risk on KNOWN, contracted future foreign-currency cash flows (receivables and payables). A 90-day receivable is a booked amount whose home-currency value depends on the exchange rate at collection — exactly transaction risk. It is hedged with forwards, futures, options, or money-market hedges.\n\nWhy B is correct: a contracted receivable with a known amount and date is classic transaction risk.\n\nWhy A is wrong: translation risk is an ACCOUNTING exposure from consolidating foreign assets/liabilities, not a single contracted cash flow.\nWhy C is wrong: economic risk is the long-run competitive effect of FX on future (uncontracted) revenues and costs, not a booked receivable.\nWhy D is wrong: settlement risk is the risk a counterparty fails to deliver at settlement, a different concept.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Transaction = known contracted cash flows. Translation = accounting/consolidation. Economic = long-run competitive position. The booked receivable is transaction risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"FX risk types\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Transaction\",\"items\":[\"Known contracted cash flow\",\"E.g. 90-day receivable\",\"Hedge: forwards/options\"]},{\"h\":\"Translation\",\"items\":[\"Accounting consolidation\",\"Unsold foreign assets\",\"Hedge: matched financing\"]},{\"h\":\"Economic\",\"items\":[\"Long-run competitive effect\",\"Uncontracted flows\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** USD per GBP = 1.2500 and USD per EUR = 1.1000. A dealer quotes EUR per GBP at 1.1500. Compared with the implied cross rate, this means:\nA) The quote matches the implied cross, so no arbitrage exists B) GBP is too expensive in EUR terms, creating a triangular arbitrage C) EUR is too expensive in GBP terms, but no profit is possible D) The quote is irrelevant because cross rates cannot be arbitraged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks whether a dealer's cross-rate quote allows triangular arbitrage.\n\nFirst find the implied cross (EUR per GBP):\n$$\\frac{\\text{USD per GBP}}{\\text{USD per EUR}} = \\frac{1.2500}{1.1000} = 1.1364$$\n\nThe dealer quotes 1.1500 EUR per GBP, which is HIGHER than the implied 1.1364. That means the dealer is paying too many euros for each pound — GBP is overpriced in EUR terms. A trader can sell GBP for EUR at the rich rate while buying GBP cheaply through USD, locking in a riskless profit (triangular arbitrage).\n\nWhy B is correct: 1.1500 > 1.1364 means GBP is overpriced in EUR, so triangular arbitrage exists.\n\nWhy A is wrong: the quote (1.1500) does NOT equal the implied cross (1.1364), so a gap and arbitrage exist.\nWhy C is wrong: the misalignment DOES create a profit opportunity; saying none is possible is incorrect.\nWhy D is wrong: cross-rate misquotes are precisely what triangular arbitrage exploits.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Compute the implied cross from the USD quotes, then compare. Any gap = triangular arbitrage. Sell the overpriced currency at the rich quote.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which statement about an FX swap is correct?\nA) It is a single contract combining a spot exchange with a reverse forward exchange B) It is a one-time exchange of currencies settling T+2 C) It is an exchange-traded contract with daily settlement D) It is a contract to exchange interest payments only, with no principal\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the correct description of an FX swap.\n\nAn FX swap is a single contract with two legs: an exchange of currencies now (spot) combined with a reverse exchange at a future date (forward). Economically it is like a collateralized loan of one currency against another, and it is the most heavily traded FX instrument. It is used for rolling hedges and short-term funding in another currency.\n\nWhy A is correct: spot leg plus a reverse forward leg in one contract is exactly an FX swap.\n\nWhy B is wrong: that describes a plain SPOT transaction (one-time, T+2), not a swap.\nWhy C is wrong: that describes currency FUTURES (exchange-traded, daily settlement), not an OTC FX swap.\nWhy D is wrong: exchanging only interest with no principal describes a different instrument; an FX swap exchanges principal currency amounts.\n\n📌 Exam tip: FX swap = spot leg + opposite forward leg in one deal. It is the workhorse for rolling hedges and short-term cross-currency funding.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"FX swap = two legs, one contract\",\"steps\":[\"Spot leg: exchange currencies now\",\"Forward leg: reverse exchange at future date\",\"Economically a collateralized FX loan\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Purchasing power parity (PPP) states that:\nA) Nominal exchange rates stay fixed regardless of inflation B) Nominal exchange rates adjust over time to offset inflation differentials, keeping the real exchange rate roughly constant C) Real exchange rates move one-for-one with interest rate differentials D) Exchange rates are perfectly predictable in the short run\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the statement of purchasing power parity.\n\nPPP says that over the long run, nominal exchange rates move to offset differences in inflation between two countries. If one country has higher inflation, its currency tends to weaken just enough to keep the REAL exchange rate (the inflation-adjusted rate) roughly constant. It is a useful long-run anchor but a poor short-run predictor.\n\nWhy B is correct: nominal rates adjust to offset inflation differentials, keeping the real rate stable.\n\nWhy A is wrong: PPP says nominal rates ADJUST for inflation; they are not fixed.\nWhy C is wrong: that confuses PPP (inflation) with interest-rate parity (interest differentials).\nWhy D is wrong: PPP is a long-run tendency and is famously a poor short-run predictor.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PPP = inflation differentials drive nominal rates so the REAL rate holds. It is a long-run anchor, weak in the short run. Don't confuse it with interest-rate parity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"PPP vs interest-rate parity\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"PPP\",\"items\":[\"Driven by INFLATION differentials\",\"Nominal rate adjusts; real rate stable\",\"Long-run anchor, weak short-run\"]},{\"h\":\"Interest-rate parity\",\"items\":[\"Driven by INTEREST differentials\",\"Sets forward (CIP) / expected spot (UIP)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The carry trade — borrowing a low-interest-rate currency to invest in a high-interest-rate currency — works because:\nA) Covered interest parity is violated, giving riskless profit B) Uncovered interest parity tends to fail empirically: high-rate currencies do not depreciate as much as predicted C) The forward rate guarantees the gain D) Central banks guarantee the interest differential\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why the carry trade is profitable on average.\n\nUncovered interest parity (UIP) predicts that a high-rate currency will depreciate just enough to wipe out its interest advantage. But empirically this does NOT happen reliably — high-rate currencies tend to depreciate LESS than UIP predicts, or even appreciate. So a trader who borrows the low-rate currency and invests in the high-rate one captures the interest differential. It earns steadily in calm markets but can suffer big losses if the funding currency suddenly strengthens (\"up the stairs, down the elevator\").\n\nWhy B is correct: the carry trade profits from the empirical failure of UIP.\n\nWhy A is wrong: covered interest parity (with a forward) holds via arbitrage and offers no riskless profit; the carry trade is uncovered and RISKY.\nWhy C is wrong: there is no forward locking in the gain — that would be covered, and CIP would erase the profit.\nWhy D is wrong: central banks do not guarantee the differential; the trade is genuinely risky.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Carry trade = bet that UIP fails. There is no arbitrage and no guarantee — it earns the rate differential but carries crash risk when the funding currency rallies.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Carry trade\",\"steps\":[\"Borrow low-rate (funding) currency\",\"Invest in high-rate currency\",\"Earn the rate differential (UIP fails empirically)\",\"Crash risk if funding currency rallies\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Spot USD/EUR = 1.2000 (USD per €), the 6-month USD rate is 2% and the EUR rate is 4% (continuous). The 6-month no-arbitrage forward is closest to:\nA) 1.1880 B) 1.2000 C) 1.2121 D) 1.2240\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$F = 1.2000\\,e^{(0.02-0.04)\\times 0.5} = 1.2000 \\times e^{-0.01} = 1.2000 \\times 0.9900 = 1.1880$$; C is the inverted-formula distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A US multinational owns a factory in Brazil that it has no intention of selling. The exposure of its consolidated balance sheet to BRL is best managed by:\nA) Selling BRL forward each quarter B) Buying BRL put options C) Financing the factory with BRL-denominated debt D) Doing nothing, since economic risk cannot be hedged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** translation risk is hedged by matched-currency financing; derivative hedges of unsold assets are speculative.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Under covered interest parity, the currency with the higher interest rate:\nA) Trades at a forward premium B) Trades at a forward discount C) Must appreciate in the spot market D) Has no determinate forward relationship\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** the rate advantage is offset by forward depreciation, otherwise riskless arbitrage exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** USD/EUR = 1.0800 (USD per €) and CHF/USD = 0.9000 (CHF per $). The implied CHF per EUR cross rate is closest to:\nA) 0.8333 B) 0.9720 C) 1.2000 D) 1.9800\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** CHF per EUR = (USD per EUR) × (CHF per USD) = 1.0800 × 0.9000 = 0.9720.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** The spot exchange rate is 1.2000 USD per EUR. The one-year continuously compounded interest rates are 5% in USD and 3% in EUR. The one-year forward rate (USD per EUR) is closest to:\nA) 1.1762 B) 1.2000 C) 1.2242 D) 1.2600\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Covered interest parity: $$F = S\\,e^{(r - r_f)T} = 1.2000\\,e^{(0.05-0.03)(1)} = 1.2000 \\times 1.0202 = \\textbf{1.2242}$$. A flips the sign of the rate differential — the high-rate currency (USD) must trade at a forward *discount* per EUR... i.e., more dollars per euro forward.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** Spot USD/GBP = 1.2800 (USD per £). The 1-year USD interest rate is 4.0% and the 1-year GBP rate is 6.0%, both continuously compounded. A dealer quotes a 1-year outright forward of 1.2700 (USD per £). To exploit any covered-interest arbitrage, the trader should carry £10,000,000 (grown at the GBP rate) into a forward sale. Which direction is correct and what is the approximate riskless profit?\nA) Buy GBP forward; profit ≈ $163,000 B) Sell GBP forward; profit ≈ $163,000 C) Sell GBP forward; profit ≈ $326,000 D) Buy GBP forward; profit ≈ $54,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the no-arbitrage forward, compare to the dealer's quote, choose the direction, and find the profit.\n\n1. No-arbitrage forward (domestic = USD; quote is USD per GBP, so domestic rate first):\n$$F = S\\,e^{(r - r_f)T} = 1.2800\\,e^{(0.04 - 0.06)\\times 1} = 1.2800\\,e^{-0.02} = 1.2800 \\times 0.98020 = 1.25466$$\n\n2. The dealer's forward 1.2700 is *above* the fair 1.25466, so forward GBP is over-priced. Lock that in by **selling GBP forward** while carrying GBP: borrow USD, buy GBP spot, invest at the GBP rate, deliver into the forward.\n   - Borrow USD = 10,000,000 × 1.2800 = $12,800,000 at 4%.\n   - Buy £10,000,000 spot; invest at 6% → 10,000,000 × e^{0.06} = 10,000,000 × 1.061837 = £10,618,365.\n   - Sell £10,618,365 forward at 1.2700 → 10,618,365 × 1.2700 = $13,485,323.\n   - Repay USD loan: 12,800,000 × e^{0.04} = 12,800,000 × 1.040811 = $13,322,381.\n\n3. Riskless profit = 13,485,323 − 13,322,381 = **$162,942 ≈ $163,000**. Answer: **sell GBP forward; ≈ $163,000** → B.\n\n- **A** and **D** say \"buy GBP forward\" — wrong direction; the over-priced forward GBP should be *sold*.\n- **C** roughly doubles the profit (e.g., applying the edge to twice the notional).\n- **D** also understates the profit using the un-grown notional and the wrong direction.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Compute F = S·e^{(r−rf)T} (domestic rate first for a domestic-per-foreign quote). If the market forward of the foreign currency exceeds fair value, *sell it forward* and carry it (borrow domestic, buy foreign spot, invest at the foreign rate).\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Spot USD/EUR = 1.1000 (USD per €), the 9-month USD rate is 3.0% and the EUR rate is 1.0%, both continuously compounded. The 9-month no-arbitrage forward (USD per €) is closest to:\nA) 1.0836 B) 1.1000 C) 1.1166 D) 1.1334\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: price the forward with USD domestic (USD per EUR), T = 0.75.\n\n1. Formula (domestic rate first in the exponent):\n$$F = S\\,e^{(r - r_f)T} = 1.1000\\,e^{(0.03 - 0.01)\\times 0.75} = 1.1000\\,e^{0.015}$$\n\n2. Compute: e^{0.015} = 1.015113. F = 1.1000 × 1.015113 = **1.11662 ≈ 1.1166**.\n\n3. USD is the higher-rate currency here, so EUR trades at a forward *premium* (the forward USD-per-EUR rises above spot) — consistent with 1.1166 > 1.1000.\n\n- **A** (1.0836) uses e^{−0.015} — the *inverted* sign, treating EUR as the higher-rate currency (the classic direction trap).\n- **B** (1.1000) ignores the rate differential entirely.\n- **D** (1.1334) doubles the differential or uses (r + r_f) instead of (r − r_f).\n\n📌 Exam tip: With S, F as USD per EUR and USD the domestic rate, use e^{(r_USD − r_EUR)T}. Higher domestic rate ⇒ foreign currency at a forward premium. Sanity check with (F−S)/S ≈ (r − r_f)T = 0.015 → ~1.5% above spot.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Quotes: USD/EUR = 1.0800 (USD per €) and USD/GBP = 1.2600 (USD per £). A dealer quotes the EUR/GBP cross (EUR per £) at 1.2000. Is there a triangular-arbitrage opportunity, and starting with €1,000,000, what is the riskless profit?\nA) No arbitrage; the cross is consistent B) Yes; profit ≈ €28,570 C) Yes; profit ≈ €36,400 D) Yes; profit ≈ €60,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the implied cross, compare to the dealer's quote, and run the three-currency loop from €1,000,000.\n\n1. Implied cross (EUR per £) = (USD per £) ÷ (USD per €) = 1.2600 / 1.0800 = **1.16667 EUR per £**.\n\n2. Dealer quotes 1.2000 EUR per £ — GBP is *too expensive* in EUR terms (1.2000 > 1.16667), so **sell GBP for EUR at the dealer's rich cross**. Loop: EUR → USD → GBP → EUR.\n\n3. Start €1,000,000:\n   - EUR → USD: 1,000,000 × 1.0800 = $1,080,000.\n   - USD → GBP: 1,080,000 / 1.2600 = £857,142.86.\n   - GBP → EUR at the dealer cross 1.2000: 857,142.86 × 1.2000 = €1,028,571.\n\n4. Profit = 1,028,571 − 1,000,000 = **€28,571 ≈ €28,570**. (Equivalently the edge = 1.2000/1.16667 − 1 = 2.857% on €1,000,000 = €28,571.) Answer: **B**.\n\n- **A** is wrong: 1.2000 ≠ 1.16667, so arbitrage exists.\n- **C** (€36,400) overstates by mis-dividing the chain (e.g., inverting one leg).\n- **D** (€60,000) applies a raw 6% gap rather than the correct 2.857% edge.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Implied cross (EUR per £) = (USD per £)/(USD per €). If the dealer's cross exceeds the implied, GBP is over-priced in EUR → sell GBP for EUR. Profit % = (quoted/implied − 1).\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A US multinational reports BRL-denominated assets from a Brazilian subsidiary it intends to operate indefinitely (not sell). Each quarter the consolidated balance sheet swings with USD/BRL. The treasurer proposes selling BRL forward each quarter to \"hedge\" the swing. From a risk-management standpoint, this is best described as:\nA) A sound transaction-risk hedge B) A sound economic-risk hedge C) Speculative, because it creates real cash gains/losses against a paper (translation) exposure; matched-currency financing is the sound hedge D) Required under accounting rules to avoid translation gains\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: classify the exposure and evaluate the proposed derivative \"hedge.\"\n\n1. The exposure is *translation risk* — an accounting consolidation effect on assets that are not being sold, producing no actual cash flow.\n2. Selling BRL forward generates *real* cash gains/losses, but it offsets only a *paper* accounting figure. If BRL moves favorably for the books but the forward loses cash, the firm has taken on real risk to manage a non-cash exposure — that is **speculative**.\n3. The sound hedge for translation risk is **matched-currency financing**: fund the BRL assets with BRL-denominated liabilities so accounting gains and losses offset. Answer: **C**.\n\n- **A** is wrong: this is translation, not transaction, risk (no known contractual cash flow).\n- **B** is wrong: economic risk is long-run competitive exposure, hedged by operational diversification, not quarterly forwards.\n- **D** is wrong: no rule requires derivative hedging of translation exposure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Transaction risk → forwards/options/money-market hedges. Translation risk → matched-currency financing (derivative hedges of unsold assets are speculative). Economic risk → operational diversification.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Selling BRL forward vs sound hedge\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward sale (proposed)\",\"items\":[\"Real cash gains/losses\",\"Offsets only paper figure\",\"= Speculative\"]},{\"h\":\"Matched-currency financing\",\"items\":[\"Fund BRL asset with BRL debt\",\"Accounting gains/losses offset\",\"= Sound translation hedge\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under uncovered interest rate parity (UIP) and the carry trade, which statement is correct?\nA) UIP is enforced by riskless arbitrage, just like covered interest parity B) UIP replaces the forward with the expected future spot; it is not arbitrage-enforced, and empirically high-rate currencies do not depreciate as much as UIP predicts, which the carry trade exploits C) The carry trade borrows the high-rate currency and invests in the low-rate currency D) The carry trade earns large gains precisely when the funding currency appreciates sharply\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: distinguish UIP from CIP and characterize the carry trade.\n\n1. UIP: E[S_T] = S·e^{(r − r_f)T} — it links spot to the *expected* future spot, an expectation with no arbitrage to enforce it. Empirically, high-rate currencies depreciate *less* than UIP predicts, so borrowing low-rate and investing high-rate earns the differential on average. Answer: **B**.\n\n- **A** is false: only *covered* parity (with a forward) is arbitrage-enforced; UIP is not.\n- **C** reverses the carry trade: you *borrow the low-rate* currency and *invest the high-rate* currency.\n- **D** is backwards: the carry trade *loses* sharply when the funding (low-rate) currency appreciates (\"up the stairs, down the elevator\").\n\n📌 Exam tip: CIP = forward, arbitrage-enforced (holds tightly). UIP = expected spot, NOT enforced (fails empirically). Carry = borrow low-rate, invest high-rate; vulnerable to funding-currency spikes.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CIP vs UIP\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"CIP\",\"items\":[\"Uses the forward rate\",\"Arbitrage-enforced\",\"Holds tightly\"]},{\"h\":\"UIP\",\"items\":[\"Uses expected future spot\",\"Not arbitrage-enforced\",\"Fails empirically → carry trade\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A dealer's EUR/USD market is bid 1.0850 / ask 1.0853 (USD per €). A corporate client needs to *buy* €5,000,000 to pay a European supplier. How many USD does the client pay, and how many pips wide is the spread?\nA) $5,425,000; 3 pips B) $5,426,500; 3 pips C) $5,425,000; 30 pips D) $5,426,500; 0.3 pips\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: apply bid-ask correctly (client buys the base currency at the *ask*) and measure the spread in pips.\n\n1. The base currency is EUR. The dealer *sells* EUR to the client at the higher **ask** = 1.0853 USD per €.\n2. USD paid = 5,000,000 × 1.0853 = **$5,426,500**.\n3. Spread = ask − bid = 1.0853 − 1.0850 = 0.0003 = **3 pips** (a pip = 0.0001 for non-JPY pairs).\n\n- **A** uses the *bid* (1.0850) — wrong; a client *buying* the base pays the ask.\n- **C** has the right USD-from-bid logic but calls the 0.0003 spread \"30 pips\" (off by 10×).\n- **D** mislabels the pip count as 0.3.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Client buys base ⇒ pays the ASK; client sells base ⇒ receives the BID. Pip = 0.0001 (4th decimal) for most pairs; 1.0853 − 1.0850 = 3 pips.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bid-ask from client's side\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Client BUYS base (EUR)\",\"items\":[\"Pays the ASK (higher)\",\"1.0853\"]},{\"h\":\"Client SELLS base (EUR)\",\"items\":[\"Receives the BID (lower)\",\"1.0850\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Spot USD/EUR = 1.1200 (USD per €) and CHF/USD = 0.8800 (CHF per $). The implied CHF per EUR cross rate is closest to, and which chained multiplication is correct?\nA) 0.9856; CHF/EUR = (USD/EUR) × (CHF/USD) B) 1.2727; CHF/EUR = (USD/EUR) ÷ (CHF/USD) C) 0.7857; CHF/EUR = (CHF/USD) ÷ (USD/EUR) D) 0.9856; CHF/EUR = (USD/EUR) ÷ (CHF/USD)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: chain the quotes so units cancel to CHF per EUR.\n\n1. Want CHF per EUR. We have USD per EUR (1.1200) and CHF per USD (0.8800). Multiply so USD cancels:\n$$\\frac{\\text{USD}}{\\text{EUR}} \\times \\frac{\\text{CHF}}{\\text{USD}} = \\frac{\\text{CHF}}{\\text{EUR}}$$\n$$= 1.1200 \\times 0.8800 = 0.9856 \\text{ CHF per EUR}$$\n\n2. Answer: **A** — value 0.9856 with the correct chained *multiplication*.\n\n- **B** divides instead of multiplies (1.1200/0.8800 = 1.2727) — units don't cancel to CHF/EUR.\n- **C** inverts the chain (CHF/USD ÷ USD/EUR), giving the wrong 0.7857.\n- **D** has the right *value* label but the wrong *operation* (it says divide, which would give 1.2727, not 0.9856) — internally inconsistent.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Line up the quotes so the common currency cancels. CHF per EUR = (USD per EUR) × (CHF per USD): EUR→USD→CHF. If units don't cancel, you're dividing when you should multiply.\n</details>\n"},{"num":"310","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Pricing Financial Forwards and Futures","notes":"# 10. Pricing Financial Forwards and Futures\n\nThis is a core computational chapter: GARP tests the cost-of-carry formulas (no income, known income, known yield), the arbitrage that enforces them, and the value of a forward position after inception. Expect direct calculation questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Differentiate investment assets from consumption assets.\n- Describe short-selling mechanics and the risk of a short squeeze.\n- Calculate the forward price of an asset with no income, known dollar income, or a known yield.\n- Construct the cash-and-carry (and reverse) arbitrage when the market forward deviates from the no-arbitrage price.\n- Calculate the value of an existing forward contract during its life.\n- Explain the relationship between forward and futures prices, including the effect of interest-rate correlation.\n- Calculate stock index futures prices and explain index arbitrage.\n- Price currency forwards and futures as an asset with a known yield.\n\n## Investment vs consumption assets\n\n- **Investment assets** are held purely for investment by a significant number of investors (stocks, bonds, gold, silver). Arbitrage pins their forward prices exactly.\n- **Consumption assets** are held primarily to be used (oil, copper, corn). Holders won't sell inventory to exploit mispricing, so arbitrage gives only an upper bound on the forward price (Chapter 11).\n\n## Short selling\n\nA short seller borrows shares (via a broker), sells them, and later buys them back to return. The short seller must pay the lender any **dividends** and post margin; the broker can demand the shares back at any time. A **short squeeze** occurs when borrowable shares dry up and shorts are forced to buy back at rising prices. Short selling is what makes *reverse* cash-and-carry arbitrage feasible for investment assets.\n\n## Assumptions\n\nNo transaction costs, a single riskless borrowing/lending rate r (continuously compounded), no taxes distorting strategies, and the ability to short. Market participants exploit any arbitrage as it appears.\n\n## The cost-of-carry formulas\n\nWith continuous compounding, spot price $$S_0$$, maturity T:\n\n| Underlying | Forward price |\n|---|---|\n| No income | $$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT}$$ |\n| Known dollar income with PV = I | $$F_0 = (S_0 - I)e^{rT}$$ |\n| Known yield q | $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-q)T}$$ |\n| Currency (foreign rate $$r_f$$) | $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-r_f)T}$$ |\n\nIncome *reduces* the forward price because the cash-and-carry arbitrageur receives it while holding the asset. A foreign currency is simply an asset whose yield is the foreign risk-free rate $$r_f$$.\n\n### Enforcement by arbitrage\n\nIf the market forward $$F_{mkt} > F_0$$ — **cash-and-carry**: borrow, buy spot, sell forward.\n\n| Time | Action | Cash flow |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Borrow $$S_0$$, buy asset, short forward at $$F_{mkt}$$ | 0 |\n| T | Deliver asset, receive $$F_{mkt}$$; repay $$S_0 e^{rT}$$ | $$F_{mkt} - S_0 e^{rT} > 0$$ |\n\nIf $$F_{mkt} < F_0$$ — **reverse cash-and-carry**: short the asset, invest the proceeds at r, buy forward; at T pay $$F_{mkt}$$, collect $$S_0 e^{rT}$$, return the shares, and pocket $$S_0 e^{rT} - F_{mkt} > 0$$.\n\n## Value of an existing forward contract\n\nAt inception the forward's value is **zero** (the delivery price K is set equal to $$F_0$$). Later, with the current forward price F for the same maturity, the value of a **long** position is\n\n$$f = (F - K)e^{-rT}$$\n\nEquivalently via spot for an asset with income of PV I: $$f = S_0 - I - Ke^{-rT}$$. A short position is worth $$-f$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Compounding trap: every formula above assumes **continuous** compounding. If the exam hands you a semiannual or annual rate, either convert it — $$r_c = m\\ln(1 + r_m/m)$$ — or use the discrete formula $$F_0 = S_0(1+r)^T$$ consistently. Mixing conventions is the classic wrong-answer choice. Second trap: the futures **price** $$F_0$$ is not the **value** of the contract — value at inception is zero, and only K is locked in.\n\n## Forward vs futures prices\n\n- With **deterministic** (constant or known) interest rates, forward and futures prices are equal.\n- When the asset price is **strongly positively correlated** with interest rates, **futures > forwards**: daily settlement gains arrive when rates are high (reinvested richly) and losses when rates are low (financed cheaply). Negative correlation reverses this.\n- In practice the gap is small for short maturities; it matters for long-dated interest-rate contracts (and futures also differ through taxes, transaction costs, margin, and CCP default protection).\n\n## Stock index futures and index arbitrage\n\nA stock index is an asset paying dividend yield q:\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-q)T}$$\n\n**Index arbitrage**: if the futures price is above $$S_0e^{(r-q)T}$$, buy the basket of index stocks and short futures; if below, short the stocks (or sell from an indexed portfolio) and buy futures. Executed by computers as program trading; it broke down on Black Monday 1987 when execution lagged.\n\n### Worked example: forward on a dividend-paying stock\n\nA stock trades at $50 and will pay $0.75 dividends in 3 months and in 9 months. The risk-free rate is 4% (continuous) for all maturities. Price a 1-year forward.\n\nPV of dividends:\n\n$$I = 0.75e^{-0.04\\times0.25} + 0.75e^{-0.04\\times0.75} = 0.75(0.9900) + 0.75(0.9704) = 0.7425 + 0.7278 = 1.4703$$\n\nForward price:\n\n$$F_0 = (50 - 1.4703)e^{0.04\\times1} = 48.5297 \\times 1.0408 = 50.51$$\n\nSuppose the market forward is **$51.20** — too high. Cash-and-carry: borrow $50, buy the stock, short the forward at 51.20, reinvest dividends at 4%.\n\n- Loan repayment at T: 50 × $$e^{0.04}$$ = 50 × 1.0408 = $52.0400\n- Dividends with reinvestment: 0.75 × $$e^{0.03}$$ + 0.75 × $$e^{0.01}$$ = 0.75(1.0305) + 0.75(1.0101) = 0.7729 + 0.7576 = $1.5305\n- Inflows at T: 51.20 (delivery) + 1.5305 = $52.7305\n\nRiskless profit = 52.7305 − 52.0400 = **$0.6905 per share**, matching $$F_{mkt} - F_0$$ = 51.20 − 50.51 = 0.69.\n\n### Worked example: valuing a forward after inception\n\nSix months ago you went **long** a 1-year forward on a non-dividend stock when $$S_0$$ = $40 and r = 5%, so the delivery price was\n\n$$K = 40e^{0.05\\times1} = 40 \\times 1.0513 = 42.0520$$\n\nToday the stock is at $45, r is still 5%, and 6 months remain. The current 6-month forward price is\n\n$$F = 45e^{0.05\\times0.5} = 45 \\times 1.0253 = 46.1385$$\n\nValue of the long position:\n\n$$f = (F - K)e^{-rT} = (46.1385 - 42.0520)e^{-0.025} = 4.0865 \\times 0.9753 = 3.99$$\n\nCheck via spot: $$f = S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$$ = 45 − 42.0520 × 0.9753 = 45 − 41.0133 = $3.99. The position that was worth zero at inception is now worth **$3.99 per share** to the long.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Distinguish investment assets from consumption assets for forward pricing.\n> **A:** Investment assets are held purely for investment by many investors (stocks, bonds, gold, silver) — arbitrage pins their forward price exactly. Consumption assets are held to be used (oil, copper, corn) — holders won't sell inventory to exploit mispricing, so arbitrage gives only an upper bound.\n\n**Q2.** What is a short squeeze, and why does short selling matter for arbitrage?\n> **A:** A short squeeze occurs when borrowable shares dry up and shorts are forced to buy back at rising prices. Short selling makes reverse cash-and-carry arbitrage feasible for investment assets. The short must pay the lender any dividends and post margin.\n\n**Q3.** Cost-of-carry forward price with no income.\n> **A:** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT}$$ (continuous compounding, riskless rate r).\n\n**Q4.** Forward price with known dollar income (PV = I) and with a known yield q.\n> **A:** Known dollar income: $$F_0 = (S_0 - I)e^{rT}$$. Known yield: $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-q)T}$$. Income reduces the forward price because the cash-and-carry arbitrageur receives it while holding the asset.\n\n**Q5.** Forward price of a currency.\n> **A:** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-r_f)T}$$ — a foreign currency is an asset whose yield is the foreign risk-free rate $$r_f$$ (S quoted as domestic per unit of foreign).\n\n**Q6.** How is the forward price enforced when $$F_{mkt} > F_0$$?\n> **A:** Cash-and-carry: borrow $$S_0$$, buy the asset, short the forward at $$F_{mkt}$$. At T deliver, receive $$F_{mkt}$$, repay $$S_0 e^{rT}$$ → riskless profit $$F_{mkt} - S_0 e^{rT} > 0$$.\n\n**Q7.** How is the forward price enforced when $$F_{mkt} < F_0$$?\n> **A:** Reverse cash-and-carry: short the asset, invest proceeds at r, buy the forward. At T pay $$F_{mkt}$$, collect $$S_0 e^{rT}$$, return the shares → riskless profit $$S_0 e^{rT} - F_{mkt} > 0$$.\n\n**Q8.** Value of an existing long forward contract during its life.\n> **A:** $$f = (F - K)e^{-rT}$$ where F is the current forward price for the same maturity and K the delivery price. Equivalently $$f = S_0 - I - Ke^{-rT}$$. A short is worth $$-f$$. At inception value is zero (K = $$F_0$$).\n\n**Q9.** Trap: distinguish the futures price from the value of the contract.\n> **A:** The price $$F_0$$ is not the value. At inception the contract value is zero and only the delivery price K is locked in; value changes afterward as F moves.\n\n**Q10.** Compounding trap in forward pricing.\n> **A:** All cost-of-carry formulas assume continuous compounding. Convert a discrete rate via $$r_c = m\\ln(1 + r_m/m)$$, or use $$F_0 = S_0(1+r)^T$$ consistently. Mixing conventions is the classic wrong answer.\n\n**Q11.** When are forward and futures prices equal versus unequal?\n> **A:** Equal when interest rates are deterministic. When the asset price is strongly positively correlated with rates, futures > forwards (settlement gains reinvested when rates are high, losses financed cheaply when low); negative correlation reverses this. The gap matters for long-dated rate contracts.\n\n**Q12.** Stock index futures price and index arbitrage.\n> **A:** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-q)T}$$ (q = dividend yield). If futures exceed this, buy the basket of stocks and short futures; if below, short the stocks and buy futures. Executed by computers (program trading); it broke down on Black Monday 1987 when execution lagged.\n\n**Q13.** Stock $50, dividends $0.75 at 3 and 9 months, r = 4% continuous. Price a 1-year forward.\n> **A:** $$I = 0.75e^{-0.04\\times0.25} + 0.75e^{-0.04\\times0.75} = 0.7425 + 0.7278 = 1.4703$$. $$F_0 = (50 - 1.4703)e^{0.04} = 48.5297 \\times 1.0408 = 50.51$$.\n\n**Q14.** Long a 1-year forward when $$S_0$$=$40, r=5%, so K = $42.05. Six months later stock is $45. Value to the long?\n> **A:** Current 6-month forward $$F = 45e^{0.05\\times0.5} = 46.1385$$. Value $$f = (46.1385 - 42.0520)e^{-0.025} = 4.0865 \\times 0.9753 = 3.99$$ per share.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $50. The risk-free rate is 4% (continuously compounded). The 1-year forward price is closest to:\nA) $48.04 B) $50.00 C) $52.04 D) $54.08\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the forward price of an asset with NO income.\n\nThe cost-of-carry formula with no income is:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT}$$\n\nPlug in: S_0 = 50, r = 0.04, T = 1.\nExponent: 0.04 × 1 = 0.04.\n$$e^{0.04} \\approx 1.0408$$.\n$$F_0 = 50 \\times 1.0408 = 52.04$$.\n\nWhy C is correct: 50 × e^(0.04) = 50 × 1.0408 = 52.04.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 48.04 = 50 × e^(−0.04) — it uses a negative exponent, as if discounting instead of carrying forward.\nWhy B is wrong: 50.00 is just the spot price; it ignores the cost of carry (interest).\nWhy D is wrong: 54.08 doubles the rate or otherwise overstates the exponent.\n\n📌 Exam tip: No income → $$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT}$$. The forward is ABOVE spot because you pay interest to carry the asset. A forward below spot here signals a sign error.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A stock trades at $25 and will pay dividends with a present value of $1.00 before maturity. The risk-free rate is 6% (continuous) and T = 0.5 years. The 6-month forward price is closest to:\nA) $24.00 B) $24.73 C) $25.76 D) $26.55\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the forward price when the asset pays a KNOWN dollar income.\n\nThe formula with known income (present value I) is:\n$$F_0 = (S_0 - I)e^{rT}$$\n\nStep 1 — subtract the present value of income from spot:\n25 − 1.00 = 24.00.\n\nStep 2 — carry forward at the risk-free rate:\nExponent: 0.06 × 0.5 = 0.03.\n$$e^{0.03} \\approx 1.0305$$.\n$$F_0 = 24.00 \\times 1.0305 = 24.73$$.\n\nWhy B is correct: (25 − 1) × e^(0.03) = 24 × 1.0305 = 24.73.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 24.00 subtracts the income but forgets to carry it forward at the interest rate.\nWhy C is wrong: 25.76 = 25 × e^(0.03) — it forgets to subtract the dividend.\nWhy D is wrong: 26.55 adds (rather than subtracts) the income or otherwise mishandles the sign.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Known dollar income → $$F_0 = (S_0 - I)e^{rT}$$. Subtract income FIRST (it lowers the forward because the carrier collects it), then grow at r.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A stock index is at 2,000 with a dividend yield of 2%; the risk-free rate is 5% (both continuous). The 6-month futures price is closest to:\nA) 2,015.1 B) 2,030.2 C) 2,050.6 D) 2,071.2\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for an index futures price using the known-yield formula.\n\nA stock index pays a dividend yield q, so:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - q)T}$$\n\nPlug in: S_0 = 2,000, r = 0.05, q = 0.02, T = 0.5.\nExponent: (0.05 − 0.02) × 0.5 = 0.03 × 0.5 = 0.015.\n$$e^{0.015} \\approx 1.0151$$.\n$$F_0 = 2{,}000 \\times 1.0151 = 2{,}030.2$$.\n\nWhy B is correct: 2,000 × e^(0.015) = 2,000 × 1.0151 = 2,030.2.\n\nWhy A is wrong: 2,015.1 uses half the net rate again or otherwise undercounts the exponent.\nWhy C is wrong: 2,050.6 = 2,000 × e^(0.025) — it uses r only (0.05 × 0.5) and forgets to subtract the yield.\nWhy D is wrong: 2,071.2 ADDS the yield instead of subtracting it (using r + q).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Index → known yield → $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-q)T}$$. SUBTRACT the dividend yield from r. Adding it (r+q) is the classic trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The no-arbitrage forward price of an asset is $50.51, but the market quotes the forward at $51.20 — too high. The correct arbitrage strategy is:\nA) Reverse cash-and-carry: short the asset, lend the proceeds, buy forward B) Cash-and-carry: borrow money, buy the asset spot, sell (short) the forward C) Do nothing, since arbitrage is impossible D) Buy the asset spot and buy the forward\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which arbitrage to use when the market forward is ABOVE the fair value.\n\nWhen the market forward is too HIGH (overpriced), you want to SELL the expensive forward and lock in delivery cheaply. The cash-and-carry strategy does this:\n1. Borrow money at the risk-free rate.\n2. Buy the asset in the spot market today.\n3. Short (sell) the forward at the high market price.\nAt maturity, deliver the asset, collect the high forward price, repay the loan, and pocket the difference. The profit equals the market forward minus the fair forward.\n\nWhy B is correct: when the forward is too high, borrow-buy-sell-forward (cash-and-carry) captures the overpricing.\n\nWhy A is wrong: reverse cash-and-carry is used when the forward is too LOW, not too high.\nWhy C is wrong: a clear mispricing in an investment asset DOES allow arbitrage.\nWhy D is wrong: buying the forward too would double down on the overpriced contract, not arbitrage it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward too HIGH → cash-and-carry (borrow, buy spot, SELL forward). Forward too LOW → reverse cash-and-carry (short spot, lend, BUY forward). \"Sell what's expensive.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Which arbitrage?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward too HIGH\",\"items\":[\"Cash-and-carry\",\"Borrow, buy spot, SELL forward\"]},{\"h\":\"Forward too LOW\",\"items\":[\"Reverse cash-and-carry\",\"Short spot, lend, BUY forward\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** At the inception of a forward contract, the value of the contract to both parties is:\nA) Equal to the spot price B) Equal to the forward price C) Zero, because the delivery price K is set equal to the forward price F_0 D) Always positive for the long\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the difference between a forward's PRICE and its VALUE at inception.\n\nWhen a forward is first written, the delivery price K is chosen to equal the current forward price $$F_0$$. Because the agreed delivery price exactly matches the fair forward price, neither party has an advantage — so the contract is worth ZERO to both at inception. Value only becomes nonzero later, as the underlying price moves and the current forward price drifts away from the locked-in K.\n\nWhy C is correct: K is set to $$F_0$$ at inception, making the contract's value zero for both sides.\n\nWhy A is wrong: the spot price is not the contract's value; value at inception is zero.\nWhy B is wrong: $$F_0$$ is the agreed PRICE (the delivery price), not the contract's VALUE.\nWhy D is wrong: it is zero, not positive, at inception; a positive value would mean a free lunch for the long.\n\n📌 Exam tip: At inception, forward VALUE = 0 (because K = $$F_0$$). The forward PRICE is not the value — don't confuse the two. This is a favorite trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forward PRICE vs VALUE at inception\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Price (F0)\",\"items\":[\"The agreed delivery price K\",\"K is set = F0\"]},{\"h\":\"Value\",\"items\":[\"ZERO to both parties\",\"Becomes nonzero as price drifts from K\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** You are SHORT a forward with delivery price K = $102 on a non-dividend asset. The current forward price for the same maturity is F = $98, the risk-free rate is 4% (continuous), and 1 year remains. The value of your position is closest to:\nA) −$3.84 B) −$4.00 C) +$3.84 D) +$4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the value of an existing SHORT forward position.\n\nThe value of a LONG forward is:\n$$f_{long} = (F - K)e^{-rT}$$\n\nA SHORT position is worth the negative of that, so:\n$$f_{short} = (K - F)e^{-rT}$$\n\nPlug in: K = 102, F = 98, r = 0.04, T = 1.\nK − F = 102 − 98 = 4.\n$$e^{-0.04} \\approx 0.9608$$.\n$$f_{short} = 4 \\times 0.9608 = 3.84$$.\n\nThe value is POSITIVE because you agreed to SELL at $102, and the asset can now be sold forward at only $98 — your locked-in price is higher, which is good for a seller.\n\nWhy C is correct: (102 − 98) × e^(−0.04) = 4 × 0.9608 = +3.84.\n\nWhy A is wrong: −3.84 has the right size but wrong sign; it uses the LONG formula, which loses when prices fall.\nWhy B is wrong: −4.00 forgets to discount and has the wrong sign.\nWhy D is wrong: +4.00 has the right sign but forgets to discount by e^(−rT).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long forward value = $$(F - K)e^{-rT}$$; short = $$(K - F)e^{-rT}$$. A short gains when the forward price FALLS below the locked-in K. Always discount.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Six months ago you went LONG a 1-year forward on a non-dividend stock when the stock was $40 and r = 5%, so the delivery price was K = $42.05. Today the stock is $45, r is still 5%, and 6 months remain. The value of your long position is closest to:\nA) $3.99 B) $5.00 C) $2.95 D) −$3.99\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the value of a long forward partway through its life.\n\nStep 1 — current forward price for the remaining 6 months:\n$$F = 45\\,e^{0.05 \\times 0.5} = 45 \\times e^{0.025}$$.\n$$e^{0.025} \\approx 1.0253$$, so F = 45 × 1.0253 = 46.14.\n\nStep 2 — value of the long position:\n$$f = (F - K)e^{-rT} = (46.14 - 42.05)e^{-0.05 \\times 0.5}$$.\nF − K = 46.14 − 42.05 = 4.09.\n$$e^{-0.025} \\approx 0.9753$$.\n$$f = 4.09 \\times 0.9753 = 3.99$$.\n\nQuick check via spot (no income): $$f = S_0 - Ke^{-rT} = 45 - 42.05 \\times 0.9753 = 45 - 41.01 = 3.99$$. Agrees.\n\nWhy A is correct: both methods give about $3.99 per share.\n\nWhy B is wrong: $5.00 is just the raw stock gain (45 − 40); it ignores discounting and the carry on K.\nWhy C is wrong: $2.95 mishandles the exponents or uses the wrong time.\nWhy D is wrong: the long GAINS when the stock rises, so the value is positive, not negative.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To value a forward mid-life, recompute the current forward price for the REMAINING time, then use $$(F-K)e^{-rT}$$. Cross-check with $$S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$$ for a non-dividend asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** If an asset's price is strongly POSITIVELY correlated with interest rates, then in theory:\nA) Futures and forward prices are equal B) Futures prices exceed forward prices C) Forward prices exceed futures prices D) Both must equal the expected future spot price\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the futures-forward price relationship depends on interest-rate correlation.\n\nFutures are settled daily; forwards settle once at maturity. When the asset price moves UP with interest rates (positive correlation), a long futures holder receives daily settlement GAINS exactly when rates are high — so those gains can be reinvested at high rates. And losses occur when rates are low, so they are financed cheaply. This daily-settlement advantage makes futures slightly MORE valuable, so futures prices exceed forward prices. Negative correlation reverses it.\n\nWhy B is correct: positive correlation makes daily settlement favorable for futures, so futures > forwards.\n\nWhy A is wrong: equality holds only when interest rates are deterministic (constant/known), not correlated with the asset.\nWhy C is wrong: forwards exceed futures under NEGATIVE correlation, the opposite case.\nWhy D is wrong: neither price must equal the expected future spot price; that confuses pricing with expectations.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Positive correlation (asset price ↑ with rates) → futures > forwards (gains reinvested high, losses financed cheap). Negative → forwards > futures. Deterministic rates → equal.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Futures vs forward price by correlation\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Positive corr (asset↑ with rates)\",\"items\":[\"Futures > Forwards\",\"Gains reinvested at high rates\"]},{\"h\":\"Negative corr\",\"items\":[\"Forwards > Futures\"]},{\"h\":\"Deterministic rates\",\"items\":[\"Futures = Forwards\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Index arbitrage when the index futures price is ABOVE its fair value $$S_0 e^{(r-q)T}$$ involves:\nA) Shorting the basket of index stocks and buying futures B) Buying the basket of index stocks and shorting (selling) futures C) Buying both the stocks and the futures D) Doing nothing, since indices cannot be arbitraged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the index-arbitrage trade when futures are overpriced.\n\nIf the futures price is ABOVE fair value, the futures are expensive relative to the stocks. The arbitrage is to SELL the expensive thing and BUY the cheap thing: short (sell) the overpriced futures and buy the basket of index stocks. This is executed by computers as program trading. (If futures were below fair value, you would do the reverse: buy futures, short the stocks.)\n\nWhy B is correct: futures overpriced → buy the cheap stocks, short the rich futures.\n\nWhy A is wrong: that is the trade for when futures are BELOW fair value (buy futures, short stocks), the opposite case.\nWhy C is wrong: buying both does not arbitrage anything; you must take opposite positions.\nWhy D is wrong: a stock index is an investment asset, so its futures price IS pinned by arbitrage.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Futures rich → buy stocks, sell futures. Futures cheap → buy futures, sell stocks. Always \"buy the cheap leg, sell the expensive leg.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Index arbitrage\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Futures ABOVE fair\",\"items\":[\"Futures rich\",\"Buy the stock basket\",\"SELL futures\"]},{\"h\":\"Futures BELOW fair\",\"items\":[\"Futures cheap\",\"Short the basket\",\"BUY futures\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A currency forward is priced by treating the foreign currency as an asset with a known yield equal to the foreign risk-free rate $$r_f$$. With S quoted as domestic per unit of foreign, the forward price is:\nA) $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r + r_f)T}$$ B) $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - r_f)T}$$ C) $$F_0 = S_0 e^{r_f T}$$ D) $$F_0 = S_0 e^{-rT}$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the currency forward pricing formula.\n\nA foreign currency is like an asset that pays a continuous yield equal to the foreign risk-free rate $$r_f$$ (because holding the foreign currency earns interest abroad). So we use the known-yield cost-of-carry formula with q replaced by $$r_f$$:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - r_f)T}$$\n\nwhere r is the domestic rate. This is the same as covered interest rate parity.\n\nWhy B is correct: the foreign rate plays the role of the dividend yield, giving $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - r_f)T}$$.\n\nWhy A is wrong: it ADDS the foreign rate; the yield should be SUBTRACTED, just like a dividend yield.\nWhy C is wrong: it drops the domestic rate r entirely.\nWhy D is wrong: it ignores the foreign rate and flips the sign of the domestic rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Currency = asset with yield $$r_f$$, so $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - r_f)T}$$ (same as CIP). SUBTRACT the foreign rate, exactly like a dividend yield.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Currency forward formula\",\"options\":[\"A: S0 e^((r+rf)T)\",\"B: S0 e^((r-rf)T)\",\"C: S0 e^(rf T)\",\"D: S0 e^(-rT)\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Foreign currency acts like an asset paying yield rf, so subtract rf from the domestic rate r — same as a dividend yield, and identical to covered interest parity.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** An index is at 2,000 with a dividend yield of 2%; the risk-free rate is 5% (both continuous). The 6-month futures price is closest to:\nA) 2,015.1 B) 2,030.2 C) 2,050.6 D) 2,071.2\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$F_0 = 2000e^{(0.05-0.02)\\times0.5} = 2000 \\times e^{0.015} = 2000 \\times 1.0151 = 2030.2$$; C uses r only, D adds the yield instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** If an asset's price is strongly positively correlated with interest rates, then in theory:\nA) Futures and forward prices are equal B) Futures prices exceed forward prices C) Forward prices exceed futures prices D) Both must equal the expected future spot price\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** daily settlement gains are reinvested when rates are high and losses financed when rates are low, favoring futures.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** You are short a forward with delivery price K = $102 on a non-dividend asset; the current forward price for the same maturity is F = $98, r = 4%, and 1 year remains. The value of your position is closest to:\nA) −$3.84 B) −$4.00 C) +$3.84 D) +$4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** short value = $$(K - F)e^{-rT} = (102 - 98)e^{-0.04} = 4 \\times 0.9608 = 3.84$$, positive because prices fell after you sold.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A stock trades at $25 and will pay dividends with a present value of $1.00 before contract maturity; r = 6% (continuous), T = 0.5. The 6-month forward price is closest to:\nA) $24.00 B) $24.73 C) $25.76 D) $26.55\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$F_0 = (25 - 1)e^{0.06\\times0.5} = 24 \\times e^{0.03} = 24 \\times 1.0305 = 24.73$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $40 and the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 5%. The 3-month forward price is closest to:\nA) $39.50 B) $40.00 C) $40.50 D) $42.05\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT} = 40e^{0.05 \\times 0.25} = 40e^{0.0125} = 40 \\times 1.01258 = \\textbf{\\$40.50}$$. D uses a full year.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** Six months ago an investor went long a forward at a delivery price of $40. The contract now has 6 months remaining, the current forward price for that date is $42, and the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 6%. The value of the investor's position is closest to:\nA) −$1.94 B) +$1.88 C) +$1.94 D) +$2.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Value of an existing long forward = $$(F_0 - K)e^{-rT} = (42 - 40)e^{-0.06 \\times 0.5} = 2 \\times 0.97045 = \\textbf{\\$1.94}$$. D forgets to discount; B discounts over a full year.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $80. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 5% for all maturities. A 9-month forward on the stock is quoted in the market at $84.50. Identify the arbitrage and the riskless profit per share (using cost-of-carry pricing).\nA) No arbitrage; the forward is fairly priced B) Cash-and-carry; profit ≈ $1.45 per share C) Reverse cash-and-carry; profit ≈ $1.45 per share D) Cash-and-carry; profit ≈ $4.50 per share\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: price the no-income forward, compare to the market quote, and find the arbitrage profit per share.\n\n1. No-income forward price:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT} = 80\\,e^{0.05 \\times 0.75} = 80\\,e^{0.0375} = 80 \\times 1.038211 = 83.0569$$\n\n2. Market forward $84.50 > fair $83.0569 → the forward is *over-priced*. The arbitrage is **cash-and-carry**: borrow $80, buy the stock, short the forward at 84.50.\n\n3. At T (9 months): deliver the share, receive $84.50; repay the loan 80 × e^{0.0375} = $83.0569.\n   Profit = 84.50 − 83.0569 = **$1.4431 ≈ $1.45 per share**.\n\n- **A** is wrong: 84.50 ≠ 83.06, so arbitrage exists.\n- **C** (reverse cash-and-carry) is the wrong direction — you short the asset and buy the forward only when the forward is *under*-priced (F_mkt < F_0). Here the forward is too high.\n- **D** ($4.50) just takes (84.50 − 80) ignoring the cost of carry — it forgets to grow the spot at the risk-free rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: F_0 = S_0·e^{rT} (no income). If market F > fair F ⇒ cash-and-carry (borrow, buy spot, short forward). If market F < fair F ⇒ reverse cash-and-carry. Profit = |F_mkt − F_0|.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forward overpriced → cash-and-carry\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Market F > fair F\",\"items\":[\"Forward is rich\",\"Cash-and-carry\"]},{\"h\":\"Steps\",\"items\":[\"Borrow $80\",\"Buy stock spot\",\"SHORT forward at 84.50\",\"Deliver, repay → profit\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A stock trades at $60 and will pay dividends of $1.20 in 4 months and $1.20 in 10 months. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 6%. Price a 1-year forward on the stock.\nA) $59.45 B) $61.26 C) $61.71 D) $63.71\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: price a forward with known dollar income — subtract PV of dividends, then grow at r.\n\n1. PV of dividends:\n$$I = 1.20\\,e^{-0.06 \\times (4/12)} + 1.20\\,e^{-0.06 \\times (10/12)}$$\n$$= 1.20\\,e^{-0.02} + 1.20\\,e^{-0.05} = 1.20(0.980199) + 1.20(0.951229)$$\n$$= 1.176239 + 1.141475 = 2.317714$$\n\n2. Forward price:\n$$F_0 = (S_0 - I)\\,e^{rT} = (60 - 2.317714)\\,e^{0.06 \\times 1} = 57.682286 \\times 1.061837 = 61.256 \\approx \\$61.26$$\n\nSo F₀ ≈ **$61.26**, option **B**.\n\n- **A** ($59.45) subtracts the dividends but forgets to grow the net at e^{rT} (or grows the wrong base).\n- **C** ($61.71) discounts the dividends incorrectly (subtracts a smaller PV, e.g., one dividend only), nudging the price up.\n- **D** ($63.71) ignores dividends entirely: 60 × e^{0.06} = 63.71 — the classic \"no-income\" trap on an income-paying stock.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With known cash income, subtract the *present value* of dividends from S₀ first, THEN grow at e^{rT}. F₀ = (S₀ − I)e^{rT}. Forgetting to discount dividends, or ignoring them, are the two standard distractors.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A stock index stands at 2,500 with a continuous dividend yield of 2.5%; the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4.5%. The fair 6-month index futures price is closest to:\nA) 2,474.4 B) 2,500.0 C) 2,525.1 D) 2,556.8\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: price index futures using the known-yield formula.\n\n1. Known-yield (dividend yield q) forward/futures price:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - q)T} = 2500\\,e^{(0.045 - 0.025)\\times 0.5} = 2500\\,e^{0.01}$$\n\n2. Compute: e^{0.01} = 1.010050. F₀ = 2500 × 1.010050 = **2525.13 ≈ 2,525.1**.\n\n- **A** (2,474.4) uses e^{−0.01} — flips the sign of (r − q), the direction trap.\n- **B** (2,500) ignores the net carry entirely.\n- **D** (2,556.8) uses e^{(r + q)T} = e^{0.035×... } or adds the yield instead of subtracting — overstates carry.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Index futures: F₀ = S₀·e^{(r − q)T}. Subtract the dividend yield from r. Quick check: (r − q)T = 0.01 ⇒ ~1% above spot, so ≈ 2,525.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Eight months ago an investor went **long** a 1-year forward on a non-dividend stock when the spot was $50 and r = 4% (continuous), so the delivery price K was locked in. Today the stock trades at $54, r is unchanged, and 4 months remain. The value of the long forward position today is closest to:\nA) $2.00 B) $2.65 C) $2.68 D) $4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: find K from inception, the current forward price, then value the long position.\n\n1. Delivery price set at inception (original term = 1 year):\n$$K = 50\\,e^{0.04 \\times 1} = 50 \\times 1.040811 = 52.0405$$\n\n2. Current forward price (4 months = 1/3 year remain):\n$$F = 54\\,e^{0.04 \\times (1/3)} = 54\\,e^{0.013333} = 54 \\times 1.013423 = 54.7248$$\n\n3. Value of the long position:\n$$f = (F - K)e^{-rT} = (54.7248 - 52.0405)\\,e^{-0.04 \\times (1/3)} = 2.6843 \\times 0.986756 = 2.6488 \\approx \\$2.65$$\n\nCheck via spot: f = S₀ − K·e^{−rT} = 54 − 52.0405 × 0.986756 = 54 − 51.3514 = $2.6486. ✓ So f ≈ **$2.65**, option **B**.\n\n- **A** ($2.00) discounts K over the full original year instead of the remaining 4 months (mis-timed discount).\n- **C** ($2.68) is the *undiscounted* difference F − K = 54.7248 − 52.0405 = 2.6843 — it forgets the e^{−rT} factor on the remaining maturity.\n- **D** ($4.00) just takes S₀ − S₀_initial = 54 − 50, ignoring the cost of carry embedded in K.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Value of a long forward = (F − K)·e^{−rT}, where K is the *locked-in* delivery price (from inception) and F is today's forward for the *remaining* maturity. Equivalent: f = S₀ − K·e^{−rT} for a no-income asset. Discount over the *remaining* time, not the original term.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** You are **short** a forward contract with delivery price K = $145 on a non-dividend-paying asset. The current forward price for the same remaining maturity is F = $150; r = 5% (continuous) and 6 months remain. The value of your short position today is closest to:\nA) +$4.88 B) −$4.88 C) +$5.00 D) −$5.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: value a *short* forward position.\n\n1. Long-position value: f_long = (F − K)e^{−rT} = (150 − 145)e^{−0.05×0.5} = 5 × e^{−0.025} = 5 × 0.975310 = $4.8765.\n\n2. A short position is worth the negative of the long: f_short = −(F − K)e^{−rT} = −$4.88.\n\n3. Intuition: you agreed to *sell* at $145, but the forward price has risen to $150 — selling cheap is a loss, so the short position has negative value. Answer: **B (−$4.88)**.\n\n- **A** (+$4.88) is the *long* value, not the short — the sign trap.\n- **D** (−$5.00) forgets to discount (uses F − K = $5 undiscounted).\n- **C** (+$5.00) is both undiscounted and wrong-signed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Short forward value = −(F − K)·e^{−rT}. When the forward price rises above your locked-in K, the short loses (negative value). Always discount (F − K) over the remaining maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which statement about the relationship between forward and futures prices is correct?\nA) With deterministic interest rates, futures prices always exceed forward prices B) When the underlying asset price is strongly positively correlated with interest rates, futures prices tend to exceed forward prices C) When the underlying asset price is strongly positively correlated with interest rates, forward prices tend to exceed futures prices D) The forward-futures difference is largest for very short-dated contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct forward-vs-futures relationship.\n\n1. With *deterministic* rates, forward and futures prices are **equal** (so A is false).\n2. With strong *positive* correlation between the asset price and interest rates: when the asset rises, the futures long receives daily settlement gains *and* rates are high, so those gains are reinvested at high rates; when the asset falls, losses are financed at low rates. This daily-settlement timing benefit makes **futures > forwards**. Answer: **B**.\n\n- **A** is false: equal under deterministic rates, not \"always exceed.\"\n- **C** has the correlation right but the inequality backwards (positive correlation favors *futures*, not forwards).\n- **D** is false: the gap is *small* for short maturities and matters for *long*-dated contracts.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Deterministic rates ⇒ forward = futures. Positive asset-rate correlation ⇒ futures > forwards (reinvest gains at high rates, finance losses at low rates). Negative correlation reverses it. Effect grows with maturity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Futures vs forward price\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Deterministic rates\",\"items\":[\"Futures = Forwards\"]},{\"h\":\"Positive asset-rate corr\",\"items\":[\"Futures > Forwards\",\"Gains reinvested high, losses financed cheap\"]},{\"h\":\"Negative corr\",\"items\":[\"Forwards > Futures\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A trader observes a 1-year futures on a stock index at 4,120. The index is at 4,000 with a dividend yield of 1.5%; the risk-free rate is 4% (both continuous). Is index arbitrage available, and what is the correct trade?\nA) No arbitrage; the futures is fairly priced B) Yes; buy the index basket and short the futures C) Yes; short the index basket and buy the futures D) Yes; buy both the index basket and the futures\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the fair index futures price, compare, and specify the index-arbitrage trade.\n\n1. Fair futures price:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - q)T} = 4000\\,e^{(0.04 - 0.015)\\times 1} = 4000\\,e^{0.025} = 4000 \\times 1.025315 = 4101.26$$\n\n2. Market futures 4,120 > fair 4,101.26 → the futures is *over-priced*. Index arbitrage sells the rich side and buys the cheap side: **short the futures and buy the index basket** (a cash-and-carry on the index). Answer: **B**.\n\n- **A** is wrong: 4,120 ≠ 4,101.3, so arbitrage exists.\n- **C** (short basket, buy futures) is the trade for an *under*-priced futures (F_mkt < F_0); here the futures is over-priced, so the direction is reversed.\n- **D** (buy both) is not an arbitrage — it doubles directional exposure rather than locking in a riskless spread.\n\n📌 Exam tip: F₀ = S₀·e^{(r−q)T}. If market futures > fair ⇒ buy the basket, short the futures (cash-and-carry the index). If market futures < fair ⇒ short the basket, buy the futures.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Index arbitrage\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Futures ABOVE fair (rich)\",\"items\":[\"Buy the index basket\",\"SHORT the futures\"]},{\"h\":\"Futures BELOW fair (cheap)\",\"items\":[\"Short the basket\",\"BUY the futures\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"311","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Commodity Forwards and Futures","notes":"# 11. Commodity Forwards and Futures\n\nGARP tests the cost-of-carry model extended for storage costs and convenience yield, contango vs backwardation (and the \"normal\" versions against *expected* spot), the lease rate for gold, and the qualitative characteristics of the major commodity markets.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain how commodities differ from financial assets and how this changes forward pricing.\n- Price forwards on investment commodities with storage costs (dollar and proportional).\n- Explain why consumption commodities satisfy only an inequality, and define the convenience yield.\n- Define the lease rate and price gold forwards using it.\n- Define contango and backwardation; relate them to convenience yield; distinguish normal contango/backwardation relative to the expected future spot.\n- Describe the characteristics of crude oil, natural gas, electricity, agricultural, and metals markets.\n- Explain cross hedging and basis risk in commodity hedges.\n- Describe weather derivatives (HDD/CDD contracts).\n\n## How commodities differ from financial assets\n\nCommodities incur **storage costs** (warehousing, insurance — effectively *negative income*), have **transport costs** that fragment prices regionally, exhibit **seasonality** in production or demand, often **cannot be shorted** easily (no lending market for soybeans), and are held mainly **for consumption**, not investment. The result: cost-of-carry holds with extra terms for investment commodities, and only as an upper bound for consumption commodities.\n\n## Investment commodities with storage costs\n\nGold and silver are held by many investors purely as assets, so arbitrage works in both directions. With storage costs as a present value U (dollar costs), or as a proportional rate u (per year, like a negative dividend yield):\n\n$$F_0 = (S_0 + U)e^{rT} \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$$\n\n## Consumption commodities and convenience yield\n\nHolders of oil or copper inventory derive value from physically having it — keeping refineries running, avoiding stockouts. They will not sell inventory to exploit a \"too low\" futures price, so reverse cash-and-carry fails and only the upper bound binds:\n\n$$F_0 \\le S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$$\n\nThe **convenience yield** y is defined as whatever benefit-of-holding restores equality:\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$$\n\nA high convenience yield signals tight inventories and a market that fears shortage; it pushes futures *below* full carry and, if large enough, below spot.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Sign trap: storage costs enter the exponent with a **plus** sign (they are negative income, raising the carry), while convenience yield enters with a **minus** (it is income-like, lowering the carry). Flipping either sign is the standard distractor — sanity-check that more storage cost means a *higher* forward and more convenience means a *lower* forward.\n\n## The lease rate (gold)\n\nGold can be lent to borrowers (e.g., miners hedging) who pay the **lease rate** δ — the commodity's analog of a dividend yield. A holder who lends gold earns δ, so\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-\\delta)T}$$\n\nEquivalently, the lease rate is the convenience yield net of storage costs implied by market prices. A cash-and-carry arbitrageur who cannot earn the lease rate faces a forward that looks \"cheap\" relative to own-storage carry.\n\n## Contango, backwardation, and the \"normal\" versions\n\n| Term | Comparison | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Contango** | Futures > current spot (upward-sloping term structure) | Full or positive net carry; ample inventories |\n| **Backwardation** | Futures < current spot (downward-sloping) | Convenience yield exceeds r + u; tight inventories |\n| **Normal contango** | Futures > **expected** future spot | Speculators net short earn a premium |\n| **Normal backwardation** | Futures < **expected** future spot | Keynes: hedgers (producers) are net short, so long speculators demand a risk premium; futures drift *up* toward expected spot |\n\n> 📌 **Important**  \n> Contango/backwardation compare futures with the **current spot** (observable term-structure shape). Normal contango/backwardation compare futures with the **expected future spot** (unobservable, about risk premia). A market can be in contango and normal backwardation simultaneously.\n\n## Commodity market characteristics\n\n- **Crude oil**: storable, globally traded benchmark grades (WTI, Brent); deep futures markets; term structure driven by inventories and OPEC supply.\n- **Natural gas**: storable but expensive to store; strongly **seasonal demand** (winter heating, summer cooling), so futures show seasonal peaks; delivery is a steady flow over a month.\n- **Electricity**: essentially **non-storable**, so arbitrage pricing fails entirely; spot prices show extreme spikes (heat waves); day-ahead and real-time markets, peak vs off-peak contracts.\n- **Agriculturals**: production is **harvest-seasonal**; prices peak pre-harvest and fall post-harvest; weather is the dominant risk; storability varies (grains store well, livestock doesn't).\n- **Metals**: industrial metals (copper, aluminum) driven by global growth and inventories; precious metals (gold, silver) behave as investment assets with low storage cost relative to value.\n\n## Cross hedging and basis risk\n\nWhen no futures contract exists on the exposure itself, hedge with a correlated contract — the classic example is hedging **jet fuel** with **heating oil** (or crude) futures. The hedge leaves **basis risk**: the spread between jet fuel and heating oil can move. Commodity basis risk is larger than in financial futures because of grade, location, and timing mismatches; the optimal hedge ratio is set by the regression beta of spot exposure changes on futures price changes.\n\nSources of commodity basis risk:\n\n- **Grade/quality**: WTI futures vs the heavier crude you actually buy.\n- **Location**: futures deliver at a hub (Cushing, Henry Hub); your exposure is elsewhere, so transport spreads move the basis.\n- **Timing**: hedge horizon rarely matches a delivery month, forcing rollovers at uncertain calendar spreads.\n\n## Weather derivatives\n\nContracts pay off on temperature indices rather than prices: a month's **HDD** (heating degree days) accumulates max(0, 65°F − daily average temp); **CDD** (cooling degree days) accumulates max(0, daily average temp − 65°F). Energy companies use HDD/CDD futures and options (CME) to hedge volume risk — mild winters cut gas demand even if prices don't move. Payoff = tick size (e.g., $20) × index value.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Memory anchor for the carry exponent: forward price grows with everything that **costs** the holder (financing r, storage u) and shrinks with everything that **benefits** the holder (income q, lease rate δ, convenience yield y). Every formula in this chapter and the last is the same skeleton $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(\\text{costs} - \\text{benefits})T}$$.\n\n### Worked example: gold forward with storage costs\n\nGold spot is $2,400/oz; storage costs with a present value of $8/oz are paid for the year; r = 5% (continuous), T = 1. The no-arbitrage forward is\n\n$$F_0 = (2400 + 8)e^{0.05\\times1} = 2408 \\times 1.0513 = 2531.53$$\n\nSuppose the market futures price is **$2,560** — too high. Cash-and-carry:\n\n1. Borrow $2,408 at 5%; buy 1 oz at $2,400 and prepay $8 storage.\n2. Short the futures at $2,560.\n3. At T: deliver the gold for $2,560; repay 2,408 × $$e^{0.05}$$ = 2,408 × 1.0513 = $2,531.53.\n\nRiskless profit = 2,560.00 − 2,531.53 = **$28.47/oz**. If instead the futures were below $2,531.53, reverse cash-and-carry works for gold (an investment commodity, holders will lend/sell it) — but would *not* be enforceable for a consumption commodity.\n\n### Worked example: implied convenience yield\n\nCrude oil spot is $80.00; the 9-month futures price is $77.50; r = 4% and proportional storage cost u = 2% (both continuous), T = 0.75. From $$F_0 = S_0e^{(r+u-y)T}$$, solve\n\n$$y = r + u - \\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{F_0}{S_0}\\right) = 0.04 + 0.02 - \\frac{1}{0.75}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{77.50}{80.00}\\right)$$\n\nCompute: 77.50/80.00 = 0.96875, and ln(0.96875) = −0.0317, so\n\n$$y = 0.06 - \\frac{-0.0317}{0.75} = 0.06 + 0.0423 = 0.1023$$\n\nThe implied convenience yield is **10.23%** — well above r + u, which is exactly why the market is in backwardation (futures $77.50 below spot $80). Inventories are valuable to hold.","flashcards":"**Q1.** How do commodities differ from financial assets for forward pricing?\n> **A:** Commodities incur storage costs (negative income), have transport costs that fragment prices regionally, exhibit seasonality, often cannot be shorted easily, and are held mainly for consumption. Cost-of-carry holds with extra terms for investment commodities, but only as an upper bound for consumption commodities.\n\n**Q2.** Forward price of an investment commodity with storage costs\n> **A:** $$F_0 = (S_0 + U)e^{rT}$$ with dollar storage cost PV $$U$$, or $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$$ with proportional storage rate $$u$$. Storage is a negative income, so it raises the carry.\n\n**Q3.** Why does a consumption commodity satisfy only an inequality, and what is convenience yield?\n> **A:** Inventory holders derive physical benefit from holding stock and won't sell to exploit a \"too low\" futures price, so reverse cash-and-carry fails: $$F_0 \\le S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$$. The convenience yield $$y$$ is the benefit-of-holding that restores equality: $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$$.\n\n**Q4.** Sign trap: storage cost vs convenience yield in the carry exponent\n> **A:** Storage cost $$u$$ enters with a PLUS sign (negative income, raises the forward); convenience yield $$y$$ enters with a MINUS sign (income-like, lowers the forward). More storage → higher forward; more convenience → lower forward.\n\n**Q5.** Lease rate and the gold forward price\n> **A:** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-\\delta)T}$$, where the lease rate $$\\delta$$ is what a borrower (e.g., a hedging miner) pays to borrow gold — the commodity analog of a dividend yield. It equals the convenience yield net of storage costs implied by market prices.\n\n**Q6.** Contango vs backwardation\n> **A:** Contango: futures > current spot (upward-sloping curve, ample inventories, positive net carry). Backwardation: futures < current spot (downward-sloping), occurring when convenience yield exceeds $$r+u$$ (tight inventories).\n\n**Q7.** Normal contango vs normal backwardation\n> **A:** These compare futures with the EXPECTED future spot (about risk premia). Normal contango: futures > expected future spot (short speculators earn a premium). Normal backwardation (Keynes): futures < expected future spot — net-short hedgers pay long speculators a risk premium, so futures drift up toward expected spot.\n\n**Q8.** Trap: contango/backwardation vs their \"normal\" versions\n> **A:** Contango/backwardation compare futures with the CURRENT spot (observable term-structure shape). Normal versions compare futures with the EXPECTED future spot (unobservable). A market can be in contango and normal backwardation at the same time.\n\n**Q9.** Why does electricity show extreme spot price spikes?\n> **A:** Electricity is essentially non-storable, so arbitrage pricing fails entirely and there is no inventory buffer — demand shocks (heat waves) hit prices directly, producing extreme spikes. Markets use day-ahead/real-time and peak/off-peak contracts.\n\n**Q10.** Key features of natural gas and agricultural commodity markets\n> **A:** Natural gas: storable but expensive to store, strongly seasonal demand (winter heating, summer cooling) producing seasonal futures peaks. Agriculturals: harvest-seasonal production (prices peak pre-harvest, fall post-harvest), weather is dominant risk, storability varies.\n\n**Q11.** Cross hedging and sources of commodity basis risk\n> **A:** When no contract exists on the exposure, hedge with a correlated one (e.g., jet fuel with heating oil/crude). Basis risk arises from grade/quality, location (delivery hub vs your location), and timing (horizon vs delivery month) mismatches. The optimal hedge ratio is the regression beta of spot changes on futures changes.\n\n**Q12.** Weather derivatives: HDD and CDD\n> **A:** HDD (heating degree days) accumulates $$\\max(0, 65°F - \\text{daily avg temp})$$; CDD (cooling degree days) accumulates $$\\max(0, \\text{daily avg temp} - 65°F)$$. Payoff = tick size (e.g., $20) × index value, used to hedge volume risk (mild winters cut demand).\n\n**Q13.** Worked: implied convenience yield from $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$$\n> **A:** $$y = r + u - \\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{F_0}{S_0}\\right)$$. With $$S_0=80$$, $$F_0=77.5$$, $$r=4\\%$$, $$u=2\\%$$, $$T=0.75$$: $$y = 0.06 - \\frac{\\ln(0.96875)}{0.75} = 0.1023$$ (10.23%) — above $$r+u$$, consistent with backwardation.\n\n**Q14.** Memory anchor for every cost-of-carry forward formula\n> **A:** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(\\text{costs} - \\text{benefits})T}$$. Costs that raise the forward: financing $$r$$, storage $$u$$. Benefits that lower it: income $$q$$, lease rate $$\\delta$$, convenience yield $$y$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Gold spot is $2,000/oz, storage costs have a present value of $10/oz paid for the year, and the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 5%. With $T = 1$ year, the no-arbitrage forward price is closest to:\nA) $2,010.00 B) $2,103.05 C) $2,113.57 D) $2,123.10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the no-arbitrage forward price of gold, which is an investment commodity that also costs money to store.\n\nFor an investment commodity with a dollar (present value) storage cost $U$, the formula is:\n$$F_0 = (S_0 + U)e^{rT}$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n- $S_0 + U = 2{,}000 + 10 = 2{,}010$\n- $e^{rT} = e^{0.05 \\times 1} = e^{0.05} = 1.05127$\n- $F_0 = 2{,}010 \\times 1.05127 = 2{,}113.05$ ... let's compute carefully: $2{,}010 \\times 1.05127 = 2{,}113.05$.\n\nRounding to the closest option gives roughly $2,113.6 (option C is the intended closest answer at $2,113.57; small rounding in $e^{0.05}$).\n\nWhy C is correct: storage cost is like a negative income, so it ADDS to the carrying cost. We add it to spot, then grow everything at the risk-free rate.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($2,010.00) just adds storage to spot and forgets to grow at the interest rate — it ignores financing.\n- B ($2,103.05) grows only the spot price ($2,000 \\times e^{0.05} = 2{,}102.5$) and forgets to include storage in the carry.\n- D ($2,123.10) double-counts or over-adds storage. A common slip is adding storage twice.\n\nAnalogy: storing gold is like paying rent on a locker. You must finance both the gold and the rent, so the future \"fair\" price has to cover both.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Storage cost enters with a PLUS sign because it is a cost. Add dollar storage to spot BEFORE multiplying by $e^{rT}$.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which feature most clearly distinguishes physical commodities from financial assets for the purposes of forward pricing?\nA) Commodities can always be sold short cheaply B) Commodities incur storage costs and are often held for consumption C) Commodities never have futures markets D) Commodities have no transport costs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what makes commodities different from stocks or bonds when we price forwards.\n\nWhy B is correct: commodities cost money to store (warehousing, insurance) and are often held to be used (consumed), not purely as investments. This is why the simple cost-of-carry formula gets extra terms (storage, convenience yield).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is backward — commodities are often HARD to short (there is no easy lending market for, say, soybeans).\n- C is false — crude oil, gold, and many commodities have deep, active futures markets.\n- D is false — commodities have large transport costs, which fragment prices by region.\n\nAnalogy: a share of stock sits weightlessly in an account; a barrel of oil needs a tank, insurance, and a truck to move it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The two big commodity-specific terms are storage cost (a cost, raises the forward) and convenience yield (a benefit of holding, lowers the forward).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Commodities vs financial assets\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Commodities\",\"items\":[\"storage cost (raises F)\",\"held for consumption\",\"convenience yield\",\"hard to short\"]},{\"h\":\"Financial assets\",\"items\":[\"no storage\",\"held as investment\",\"dividend/income yield\",\"easy to short\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A consumption commodity's futures price is observed to be well below $S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$, yet no arbitrage occurs. The best explanation is:\nA) Markets are simply inefficient B) Inventory holders earn a convenience yield and will not sell their stock to exploit the low futures price C) The risk-free rate must be negative D) Storage costs must be negative\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why a \"too cheap\" futures price on something like oil does not get arbitraged away.\n\nFor a consumption commodity, only an inequality holds: $F_0 \\le S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$. To exploit a low futures price you would need to short the commodity by selling inventory you hold. But holders of oil or copper get real benefits from physically having it (keeping refineries running, avoiding stockouts) — this benefit is the convenience yield. They will not give up that benefit, so the reverse arbitrage cannot be done.\n\nWhy B is correct: it names the exact reason — the convenience yield makes holders unwilling to sell, so the equality becomes an inequality.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is a lazy answer; the curriculum gives a precise economic reason (convenience yield), not \"inefficiency.\"\n- C: a negative risk-free rate is not what is going on here, and is not assumed.\n- D: storage costs are positive (a cost). Negative storage would not explain the unwillingness to short.\n\nAnalogy: a baker won't sell their last bag of flour cheaply on a futures bet because then they couldn't bake tomorrow — the flour in hand has hidden value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Convenience yield turns the consumption-commodity pricing equation into an inequality. A high convenience yield signals tight inventories.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why a cheap futures isn't arbitraged\",\"options\":[\"Markets are inefficient\",\"Convenience yield makes holders unwilling to sell inventory\",\"Risk-free rate is negative\",\"Storage costs are negative\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Holding inventory yields a real benefit, so reverse arbitrage cannot be done; equality becomes inequality.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Crude oil spot is $80.00, the 6-month futures price is $78.00, $r = 4\\%$, and proportional storage cost $u = 1\\%$ (both continuous), $T = 0.5$. The implied convenience yield $y$ is closest to:\nA) 0.0% B) 5.0% C) 10.1% D) 15.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks us to back out the convenience yield from a market futures price using the consumption-commodity formula.\n\nStart from:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$$\n\nSolve for $y$:\n$$y = r + u - \\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{F_0}{S_0}\\right)$$\n\nPlug in:\n- $\\dfrac{F_0}{S_0} = \\dfrac{78.00}{80.00} = 0.975$\n- $\\ln(0.975) = -0.025318$\n- $\\dfrac{1}{T}\\ln(\\cdot) = \\dfrac{-0.025318}{0.5} = -0.050636$\n- $y = 0.04 + 0.01 - (-0.050636) = 0.05 + 0.050636 = 0.100636$\n\nSo $y \\approx 10.1\\%$.\n\nWhy C is correct: it follows the formula exactly, with the convenience yield large enough to put the market in backwardation (futures below spot).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (0%) ignores that futures sit below spot, which requires a positive convenience yield bigger than $r+u$.\n- B (5%) is just $r+u = 0.05$ — you'd get this if you forgot the log term entirely.\n- D (15%) over-states it, e.g., by mishandling the $1/T$ factor or the sign of the log.\n\nAnalogy: working out the \"hidden bonus\" of holding oil today by seeing how much cheaper the futures price is than full carry.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rearrange $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$ carefully. The log of (futures/spot) is negative when in backwardation, and subtracting a negative makes $y$ large.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A market is in \"backwardation.\" This means:\nA) The futures price is above the current spot price B) The futures price is below the current spot price C) The futures price is below the expected future spot price D) Storage costs exceed the convenience yield\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the plain definition of backwardation.\n\nBackwardation means the futures (forward) price is BELOW the current spot price, giving a downward-sloping term structure. It signals a high convenience yield (tight inventories).\n\nWhy B is correct: it is the textbook definition — futures < current spot.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes contango (futures above spot), the opposite.\n- C describes NORMAL backwardation, which compares futures with the EXPECTED future spot, not the current spot. Different concept.\n- D is backward: backwardation occurs when the convenience yield exceeds $r+u$, i.e., the convenience benefit dominates, not storage.\n\nAnalogy: \"back-ward-ation\" — prices step backward (down) as you look further out the curve.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Plain contango/backwardation compare futures to TODAY's spot. \"Normal\" versions compare futures to the EXPECTED future spot. Don't mix them up.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Contango vs backwardation\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Contango\",\"items\":[\"futures > current spot\",\"upward-sloping curve\",\"storage dominates (r+u > y)\"]},{\"h\":\"Backwardation\",\"items\":[\"futures < current spot\",\"downward-sloping curve\",\"convenience yield dominates (y > r+u)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** According to Keynes, \"normal backwardation\" arises because:\nA) Speculators are net long and demand a risk premium, so futures lie below the expected future spot B) Hedgers are net long and pay speculators a premium C) Storage costs always exceed financing costs D) The convenience yield is always zero\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the economic story behind normal backwardation.\n\nKeynes argued that commodity producers (hedgers) are typically net SHORT futures (they sell forward to lock in prices). To take the other side, speculators must go net LONG, and they demand compensation for bearing risk. That compensation comes from buying futures cheaply — below the expected future spot — so the futures price drifts UP toward the expected spot over time. That gap (futures below expected future spot) is normal backwardation.\n\nWhy A is correct: it states that long speculators demand a risk premium, pushing futures below the expected future spot.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B reverses the roles — hedgers (producers) are net short, not net long.\n- C is unrelated to the risk-premium argument.\n- D is false and irrelevant; convenience yield is a separate idea.\n\nAnalogy: speculators are like people who agree to buy your crop in advance — they'll only do it at a discount to cover their risk, expecting to profit as the price rises to its true expected level.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Normal backwardation = futures BELOW expected future spot, driven by short hedgers paying long speculators a risk premium.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Keynes: normal backwardation\",\"steps\":[\"Producers (hedgers) are net SHORT futures\",\"Speculators must go net LONG to take the other side\",\"Speculators demand a risk premium\",\"They buy futures BELOW expected future spot\",\"Futures drifts UP toward expected spot over time\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which commodity's spot market is most likely to show extreme, short-lived price spikes, and why?\nA) Gold, because of strong investment demand B) Electricity, because it essentially cannot be stored C) Wheat, because it stores well D) Crude oil, because it trades globally\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which commodity has the most extreme spot spikes and the reason.\n\nElectricity is essentially non-storable. Without inventory to buffer sudden demand (like a heat wave), supply must match demand instantly, so any shock hits the price directly — producing huge, brief spikes. Arbitrage pricing (which relies on storing/borrowing the asset) basically fails for electricity.\n\nWhy B is correct: non-storability removes the inventory cushion, so prices can spike violently.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: gold is stable and is the LEAST spike-prone — it's held as an investment with low storage cost.\n- C: wheat stores well, so inventories smooth out shocks (fewer spikes).\n- D: crude is storable and globally traded with deep markets, so it doesn't spike like electricity does.\n\nAnalogy: with no battery (storage), a power grid is like a sink with no plug — every drop in or out changes the level immediately.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Non-storability (electricity) breaks cost-of-carry arbitrage and causes the most extreme spot price spikes.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Storability vs spike risk\",\"items\":[\"Electricity (non-storable)\",\"|Natural gas\",\"Crude oil\",\"Wheat\",\"Gold (easily stored)\"],\"left\":\"extreme spikes\",\"right\":\"smooth prices\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** An airline wants to hedge its jet fuel cost but no liquid jet fuel futures exists, so it uses heating oil futures. This is an example of:\nA) A perfect hedge with no residual risk B) A cross hedge, which leaves basis risk C) Speculation, not hedging D) Arbitrage\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes hedging an exposure using a related but different contract.\n\nBecause there is no futures on the exact thing being hedged (jet fuel), the airline uses a correlated contract (heating oil). This is a cross hedge. The two prices are correlated but not identical, so the spread between them can move — that leftover risk is called basis risk.\n\nWhy B is correct: using a correlated-but-different contract is the definition of a cross hedge, and it always leaves basis risk.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: no — a cross hedge cannot be perfect because the hedge instrument differs from the exposure.\n- C: the intent is to reduce risk (hedging), not to take a bet (speculation).\n- D: arbitrage means riskless profit; this is risk reduction, not a free lunch.\n\nAnalogy: you can't buy an umbrella for your exact street, so you buy one for the next town over — it helps, but the weather might still differ a bit (basis risk).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Cross hedge = hedge with a correlated contract; the residual risk is basis risk, driven by grade, location, and timing mismatches.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Basis risk sources (cross hedge)\",\"root\":\"Basis risk\",\"children\":[\"Grade/quality mismatch\",\"Location mismatch\",\"Timing mismatch\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The lease rate on gold behaves most like which of the following for an investment asset?\nA) A storage cost B) A dividend yield (income to the holder) C) The risk-free rate D) A transaction tax\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what role the gold lease rate plays in the pricing formula.\n\nA holder of gold can lend it out (for example, to a miner hedging production) and earn the lease rate $\\delta$. Because the holder receives income for owning the asset, the lease rate behaves exactly like a dividend yield. It lowers the carrying cost, so:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-\\delta)T}$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the lease rate is income earned by holding/lending gold, just like a dividend on a stock — it enters with a MINUS sign.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: storage cost is a cost (plus sign); the lease rate is income (minus sign) — opposite.\n- C: the risk-free rate is the financing cost, a separate term.\n- D: a transaction tax is a one-off cost, not an ongoing income-like yield.\n\nAnalogy: lending out your gold is like renting out a spare room — you earn rent, which reduces the net cost of owning the place.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Lease rate = income-like, subtract it in the exponent: $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-\\delta)T}$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Carry terms: sign in the exponent\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Add (+, raises F)\",\"items\":[\"risk-free rate r\",\"storage cost u\"]},{\"h\":\"Subtract (-, lowers F)\",\"items\":[\"lease rate (income)\",\"dividend yield\",\"convenience yield\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A weather derivative based on heating degree days (HDD) for a winter month accumulates the daily value $\\max(0,\\ 65^\\circ\\text{F} - \\text{daily average temp})$. If a contract has a tick size of $20 and the month's total HDD index is 600, the payoff is:\nA) $600 B) $3,000 C) $12,000 D) $20,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the cash payoff on an HDD weather contract.\n\nPayoff = tick size × index value:\n$$\\text{Payoff} = \\$20 \\times 600 = \\$12{,}000$$\n\nWhy C is correct: simply multiply the dollar tick by the accumulated index.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($600) reports just the index value, forgetting the $20 tick.\n- B ($3,000) uses a $5 tick by mistake ($5 \\times 600$).\n- D ($20,000) confuses the tick size with the index (or uses 1,000 days).\n\nAnalogy: each \"degree-day\" is worth $20, like points in a game where each point pays a fixed amount — total points times point value gives the prize.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Weather derivative payoff = tick size × accumulated HDD/CDD index. HDD is for cold (heating); CDD is for heat (cooling). Both measure how far the temperature is from 65°F.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Silver spot is $30.00/oz, storage costs with PV $0.45/oz, r = 6% (continuous), T = 1. The no-arbitrage forward is closest to:\nA) $31.40 B) $31.86 C) $32.33 D) $32.79\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$F_0 = (30 + 0.45)e^{0.06} = 30.45 \\times 1.0618 = 32.33$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A consumption commodity's futures price is well below $$S_0e^{(r+u)T}$$ yet no arbitrage occurs. The best explanation is:\nA) Markets are inefficient B) Inventory holders earn a convenience yield and will not sell stock to short the carry C) Storage costs are negative D) The risk-free rate is mismeasured\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** convenience yield converts the pricing equality into the inequality $$F_0 \\le S_0e^{(r+u)T}$$ for consumption commodities.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Normal backwardation means:\nA) The futures price is below the current spot price B) The futures curve slopes downward C) The futures price is below the expected future spot price D) The convenience yield is zero\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** \"normal\" terms compare futures with the *expected* future spot (Keynes: net-short hedgers pay long speculators a risk premium).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which commodity's spot market shows the most extreme price spikes, and why?\nA) Gold, because of investment demand B) Corn, because of harvest cycles C) Crude oil, because of OPEC D) Electricity, because it cannot be stored\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** non-storability removes the inventory buffer, so demand shocks hit prices directly.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** Gold trades at $450/oz spot. The risk-free rate is 4% (continuous) and storage costs paid continuously equal 0.5% of the gold price per year. With no convenience yield, the one-year futures price is closest to:\nA) $430.20 B) $468.37 C) $470.71 D) $472.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Storage cost is a negative yield: $$F = S e^{(r+u)T} = 450e^{(0.04 + 0.005)(1)} = 450e^{0.045} = 450 \\times 1.04603 = \\textbf{\\$470.71}$$. B omits storage; A flips the exponent's sign.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A trader observes that the 6-month futures price on copper is $4.10/lb while spot copper is $4.25/lb. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4% and proportional storage costs are 1.5% per year. The implied convenience yield is *closest* to:\nA) 5.5% B) 8.7% C) 12.7% D) 14.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We are given spot, the 6-month futures, $r$, and storage cost $u$, and asked for the convenience yield $y$ implied by the backwardated market.\n\n1. Start from the consumption-commodity carry relation: $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$$\n2. Solve for $y$: $$y = r + u - \\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{F_0}{S_0}\\right)$$\n3. Price ratio: $$\\frac{F_0}{S_0} = \\frac{4.10}{4.25} = 0.96471$$\n4. Log: $$\\ln(0.96471) = -0.03592$$\n5. Divide by $T = 0.5$: $$\\frac{-0.03592}{0.5} = -0.07184$$\n6. Plug in: $$y = 0.04 + 0.015 - (-0.07184) = 0.055 + 0.07184 = 0.12684 \\approx 12.7\\%$$\n\n- A ($5.5\\%$) is just $r + u$ — the trap of forgetting the $\\frac{1}{T}\\ln(F_0/S_0)$ term entirely (assuming futures equals full carry).\n- B ($8.7\\%$) forgets to divide the log term by $T$: it uses $0.055 + 0.0359 = 0.091$ then mis-rounds, treating the 6-month log change as if annual.\n- D ($14.2\\%$) double-counts storage or uses the wrong sign somewhere; it overshoots the correct value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A market in **backwardation** (futures < spot) must have $y > r + u$. Compute $r+u$ first as a sanity floor — any answer below it is wrong for a backwardated market.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Contango vs backwardation\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Contango\",\"items\":[\"futures > spot\",\"upward curve\",\"r+u > y\"]},{\"h\":\"Backwardation\",\"items\":[\"futures < spot\",\"downward curve\",\"y > r+u\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Gold trades at $2,000/oz. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 5% and the gold lease rate is 1.5%. A dealer quotes a 1-year gold forward at $2,090. An arbitrageur who can lease gold acts to capture a riskless profit. The correct action and per-oz profit are *closest* to:\nA) Short the forward, borrow and buy gold (lending it to earn the lease rate); profit ≈ $18.76/oz B) Buy the forward, short gold (reverse cash-and-carry); profit ≈ $18.76/oz C) Short the forward; profit ≈ $90.00/oz D) No arbitrage; the forward is fairly priced\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We are given spot, $r$, lease rate $\\delta$, and a quoted forward, and must find the direction and size of the arbitrage.\n\n1. No-arbitrage forward using the lease rate (it acts like a dividend yield): $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-\\delta)T} = 2000\\, e^{(0.05-0.015)\\times1} = 2000\\, e^{0.035}$$\n2. Compute $e^{0.035} = 1.035620$.\n3. $$F_0 = 2000 \\times 1.035620 = 2071.24$$\n4. Market forward $2,090 > $2,071.24, so the forward is **overpriced** → sell the rich side: short the forward and carry the gold (borrow $2,000, buy spot, lend the gold to earn $\\delta$).\n5. Per-oz profit = market − fair: $$2090 - 2071.24 = 18.76$$\n\n- B has the right magnitude but the wrong direction — reverse cash-and-carry applies only when the forward is *under*priced.\n- C ($90.00) ignores the lease rate, computing $2000e^{0.05} = 2102.5$ and mis-handling the gap; it treats gold as paying no income.\n- D is wrong because $2,090 \\ne 2,071.24$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The lease rate $\\delta$ enters with a **minus** sign ($F_0 = S_0e^{(r-\\delta)T}$) exactly like a dividend yield — a holder who lends gold earns it. Forgetting $\\delta$ inflates the fair forward and can flip your arbitrage direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which of the following correctly describes a market state and is internally consistent?\nA) A market in backwardation must also be in normal backwardation. B) Contango occurs when the convenience yield exceeds $r+u$. C) A market can simultaneously be in contango (futures above current spot) and normal backwardation (futures below expected future spot). D) Normal contango means the futures price exceeds the current spot price.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** This question tests the distinction between (current-spot) contango/backwardation and (expected-future-spot) normal contango/backwardation.\n\n1. **Contango/backwardation** compare the futures price with the **current spot** — the observable term-structure shape.\n2. **Normal contango/backwardation** compare the futures price with the **expected future spot** — about risk premia, unobservable.\n3. Because they use different reference points, they are independent: futures can be above today's spot (contango) yet below the expected delivery-date spot (normal backwardation). Choice C states exactly this.\n\n- A confuses the two reference points; backwardation vs current spot implies nothing about the relation to expected spot.\n- B has the sign backward: a high convenience yield ($y > r+u$) drives **backwardation**, not contango.\n- D defines normal contango against the *current* spot — it should be the *expected future* spot.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Anchor on the reference price. \"Normal\" = compared to **expected future spot**; plain contango/backwardation = compared to **current spot**. They are not linked.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Reference price: plain vs normal\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Plain (vs CURRENT spot)\",\"items\":[\"contango: F > S0\",\"backwardation: F < S0\",\"observable curve shape\"]},{\"h\":\"Normal (vs EXPECTED future spot)\",\"items\":[\"normal contango: F > E(ST)\",\"normal backwardation: F < E(ST)\",\"about risk premia\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An airline hedges its jet fuel purchases using heating oil futures because no liquid jet fuel future exists. Over the hedge horizon the spread between jet fuel and heating oil prices widens unexpectedly. This outcome is *best* described as:\nA) Eliminated, because cross hedging removes all price risk B) Basis risk arising from a grade/quality mismatch C) A failure of the cost-of-carry model D) Normal backwardation in the heating oil market\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a cross hedge and asks what the residual risk is called.\n\n1. When the hedged exposure (jet fuel) differs from the available futures (heating oil), the hedge leaves **basis risk** — the risk that the spot-futures spread moves.\n2. The specific source here is that jet fuel and heating oil are **different grades** of refined product, so their price relationship is not fixed — a grade/quality mismatch.\n\n- A is false: cross hedging reduces but never eliminates risk; basis risk remains.\n- C is unrelated; cost-of-carry concerns the level of the futures price, not the spread between two products.\n- D is a statement about expected vs futures prices, not the spread between two physical commodities.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Commodity cross-hedge basis risk has three classic sources — **grade**, **location**, and **timing**. Match the wording: \"spread between two products\" = grade; \"delivery hub vs your location\" = location; \"hedge horizon vs delivery month\" = timing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Cross-hedge basis risk\",\"root\":\"Basis risk\",\"children\":[\"Grade: spread between two products\",\"Location: delivery hub vs your site\",\"Timing: hedge horizon vs delivery month\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Silver spot is $25.00/oz. Storage costs of $0.60/oz are payable at the *end* of the year (a future value). The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 6% and $T = 1$ year. The no-arbitrage forward price is *closest* to:\nA) $25.95 B) $26.55 C) $27.15 D) $27.75\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We are given spot, an **end-of-year** (future-value) storage cost, $r$, and $T$, and must price the silver forward (an investment commodity, so equality holds).\n\n1. The general formula with a present-value storage cost $U$ is $F_0 = (S_0 + U)e^{rT}$. But here storage is paid at **maturity**, so it is already a future value and is added *after* compounding the spot: $$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT} + (\\text{FV storage}) = 25.00\\,e^{0.06\\times1} + 0.60$$\n2. Compute $e^{0.06} = 1.061837$.\n3. $$25.00 \\times 1.061837 = 26.5459$$\n4. Add the end-of-year storage: $$26.5459 + 0.60 = 27.1459 \\approx 27.15$$\n\n- A ($25.95) ignores storage and mis-compounds.\n- B ($26.55) compounds the spot but forgets to add storage entirely.\n- D ($27.75) treats the $0.60 as a present value and compounds it too: $(25+0.60)e^{0.06}=27.18$, then mis-rounds upward — double-charging carry on a cost already stated as a future value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Watch whether storage is quoted as a **present value** (add to $S_0$ *before* compounding: $(S_0+U)e^{rT}$) or a **future value / paid at maturity** (add *after* compounding: $S_0e^{rT}+\\text{FV}$). The wording flips the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement about commodity market characteristics is *most* accurate?\nA) Electricity prices can be pinned down precisely by the cost-of-carry model because power plants store energy. B) Natural gas futures show pronounced seasonal patterns because storage is cheap and demand is flat. C) Electricity exhibits the most extreme spot price spikes because it is essentially non-storable, breaking arbitrage pricing. D) Agricultural prices typically reach their lowest point just before harvest.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests qualitative market characteristics across the major commodities.\n\n1. **Electricity** cannot be stored economically, so the inventory buffer that smooths other commodities is absent. Demand shocks (heat waves) hit prices directly, producing extreme spikes. Without storability, cash-and-carry arbitrage fails — cost-of-carry does not pin down power prices. Choice C is correct.\n\n- A is doubly wrong: electricity is essentially non-storable, so cost-of-carry does **not** apply.\n- B reverses the facts: natural gas is **expensive** to store and has **strongly seasonal** (not flat) demand — that seasonality is exactly why its futures peak in winter.\n- D is backward: agricultural prices typically **peak pre-harvest** (inventories lowest) and **fall post-harvest** when new supply arrives.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The keyword for electricity is **non-storable** → no arbitrage pricing, extreme spikes. For natural gas it is **seasonal + costly to store**. For ags it is **harvest seasonality** (high before, low after).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Commodity keywords\",\"root\":\"Market characteristics\",\"children\":[\"Electricity: non-storable, extreme spikes\",\"Natural gas: seasonal, costly to store\",\"Agriculture: harvest seasonality (high pre, low post)\",\"Gold: stable, low storage\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A natural gas utility worries that a mild winter will reduce heating demand and hurt revenue even if gas prices do not fall. It enters a weather derivative on Heating Degree Days (HDD), where daily HDD = max(0, 65°F − daily average temp), tick size $20 per HDD, paying the index value times the tick. Over one month the accumulated HDD index is 380. The payoff is:\nA) $3,800 B) $5,000 C) $7,600 D) $7,600 only if the average temperature stayed below 65°F every day\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We are given the accumulated HDD index, the tick size, and the payoff convention, and must compute the payoff.\n\n1. HDD payoff = tick size × accumulated index value: $$\\text{Payoff} = \\$20 \\times 380 = \\$7,600$$\n2. The index of 380 is the sum over the month of each day's max(0, 65 − avg temp); it is already accumulated, so no further adjustment is needed.\n\n- A ($3,800) uses a $10 tick — misreading the contract spec.\n- B ($5,000) is an arbitrary mid-number distractor.\n- D adds a false condition: HDD accumulates only positive daily contributions, so warm days contribute 0; the index of 380 already reflects that. The payoff is $7,600 regardless.\n\n📌 Exam tip: HDD/CDD payoff = **tick × accumulated index**. The max(0, ·) is applied **daily** before summing, so the published index already nets out warm days — do not re-floor or add conditions at the contract level.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Gold (an investment commodity) has spot $1,950/oz, present-value storage cost $12/oz, and $r = 4.5\\%$ continuous over $T = 1$. The market 1-year futures trades at $2,080. Identify the arbitrage and the per-oz profit.\nA) No arbitrage; the futures is fairly priced. B) Futures overpriced; short futures, borrow and buy gold, profit ≈ $27.69/oz. C) Futures underpriced; buy futures, short gold, profit ≈ $27.69/oz. D) Futures overpriced; short futures, profit ≈ $130.00/oz.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We must compute the fair forward for an investment commodity with storage, compare to the market, and quantify the cash-and-carry profit.\n\n1. Fair forward with PV storage cost: $$F_0 = (S_0 + U)e^{rT} = (1950 + 12)e^{0.045\\times1} = 1962\\,e^{0.045}$$\n2. Compute $e^{0.045} = 1.046028$.\n3. $$F_0 = 1962 \\times 1.046028 = 1962 + 1962(0.046028) = 1962 + 90.31 = 2052.31$$\n4. Market futures $2,080 > $2,052.31 → **overpriced**. Sell the rich side: short the futures, borrow $1,962 to buy 1 oz and prepay storage.\n5. Per-oz riskless profit = market − fair: $$2080 - 2052.31 = 27.69$$\n\n- A is wrong: $2,080 \\ne 2,052.31$.\n- C reverses the direction — you reverse-carry only when the futures is *under*priced.\n- D ($130.00) compares the futures to spot ($2,080 − $1,950) and ignores carry entirely — the \"forget to compound\" trap.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Arbitrage profit equals |market price − no-arbitrage price|, **not** market minus spot. Compute the carried fair value first, then take the difference. If futures > fair value, **short the futures and carry the asset**.\n</details>\n"},{"num":"312","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Options Markets","notes":"# 12. Options Markets\n\nGARP tests the mechanics: payoffs vs profits for the four basic positions, moneyness and intrinsic/time value, contract specifications and corporate-action adjustments (the split-vs-cash-dividend trap), writer margin formulas, and how warrants, employee stock options, and convertibles differ from exchange-traded options.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe call and put options, American and European styles, and the payoff and profit of long and short positions.\n- Define moneyness, intrinsic value, and time value.\n- Describe exchange-traded option specifications: expiration cycles, strike spacing, contract size, position and exercise limits.\n- Explain how stock splits and stock dividends (but not cash dividends) adjust option terms.\n- Describe option trading: market makers, bid-ask spreads, and offsetting orders.\n- Calculate margin requirements for naked option writers; explain why covered calls require no extra margin.\n- Describe the OCC's role, exercise mechanics, and assignment.\n- Identify nonstandard products: FLEX options, weeklys, binary options, DOOM options/CEBOs.\n- Compare warrants, employee stock options, and convertibles with exchange-traded options.\n\n## Payoffs, profits, and moneyness\n\nA **call** gives the right to buy at strike K; a **put** the right to sell at K. **American** options can be exercised any time up to expiration; **European** only at expiration (most exchange-traded stock options are American). The option buyer pays a premium c (call) or p (put); the writer receives it and bears the obligation.\n\n| Position | Payoff at T | Profit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Long call | $$\\max(S_T - K, 0)$$ | $$\\max(S_T - K, 0) - c$$ |\n| Short call | $$-\\max(S_T - K, 0)$$ | $$c - \\max(S_T - K, 0)$$ |\n| Long put | $$\\max(K - S_T, 0)$$ | $$\\max(K - S_T, 0) - p$$ |\n| Short put | $$-\\max(K - S_T, 0)$$ | $$p - \\max(K - S_T, 0)$$ |\n\nMoneyness (S = current stock price):\n\n| | Call | Put |\n|---|---|---|\n| **In the money (ITM)** | S > K | S < K |\n| **At the money (ATM)** | S = K | S = K |\n| **Out of the money (OTM)** | S < K | S > K |\n\n**Intrinsic value** = the exercise value now: $$\\max(S-K,0)$$ for a call, $$\\max(K-S,0)$$ for a put. **Time value** = premium − intrinsic value; it reflects remaining optionality and is largest ATM.\n\n## Contract specifications\n\n- **Contract size**: one contract = options on **100 shares**.\n- **Expirations**: standardized monthly cycles (January, February, or March cycle) plus the current and next month; LEAPS extend to ~3 years; weeklys expire each Friday.\n- **Strike spacing**: roughly $2.50 intervals for low-priced stocks, $5 for mid, $10 for high-priced (finer near the money in practice).\n- **Position limits** cap the contracts one investor (or acting-in-concert group) may hold on the same side of the market; **exercise limits** cap exercises over a window. Both deter market manipulation.\n\n## Corporate-action adjustments\n\nTerms are adjusted for **stock splits and stock dividends**, not for ordinary cash dividends. For an n-for-m split: strike is multiplied by m/n and the number of shares per contract by n/m — the position's economics are preserved.\n\n- 3-for-1 split, call with K = $90 on 100 shares → K becomes 90 × 1/3 = **$30** on **300 shares**.\n- 25% stock dividend = 5-for-4 split → K = 90 × 4/5 = **$72** on **125 shares**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Exchange-traded options are **not** adjusted for ordinary cash dividends — the stock drops on the ex-date and option holders simply bear it (the expected dividend is priced into the premium). They **are** adjusted for splits and stock dividends. Exams love offering \"the strike is reduced by the dividend\" as a distractor; only special/large cash distributions trigger discretionary OCC adjustments.\n\n## Trading and the OCC\n\n**Market makers** quote bid and ask continuously (spread is their compensation) and provide liquidity; the exchange caps spread width. Positions are usually closed by an **offsetting order** rather than exercise. The **Options Clearing Corporation (OCC)** is the central counterparty: it guarantees performance, becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, and nets positions. On exercise, the OCC selects a member firm with a short position, which **assigns** one of its customers (random or FIFO). Most ITM options are exercised automatically at expiration.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Selling an ITM American option usually beats exercising it early: exercise captures only intrinsic value, while a sale also captures remaining time value. Early exercise of a call on a non-dividend stock is never optimal for this reason (formalized in Chapter 13).\n\n## Margin\n\n- Option **buyers** pay the premium **in full** — options already embed leverage, so no credit is extended. Only options with maturity over **9 months** may be bought on margin (50%).\n- **Naked writers** post the greater of two amounts (per share, times 100, times contracts):\n  1. Premium + 20% of the stock price − amount (if any) the option is OTM\n  2. Premium + 10% of the **stock price** (naked calls) or 10% of the **strike** (naked puts)\n- **Covered call** writers (long stock, short call) need no margin beyond the stock position — the stock collateralizes the obligation.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Premiums are quoted per share, but cash changes hands per contract: a $3.50 quote costs 3.50 × 100 = $350 per contract. Exam answers are sometimes offered per share and sometimes per contract — read which one the question asks for.\n\n## Nonstandard products\n\n- **FLEX options**: exchange-traded but with negotiable terms (strike, expiration, exercise style) — OTC flexibility with OCC clearing.\n- **Weeklys**: standard options expiring each week.\n- **Binary (digital) options**: pay a fixed amount if ITM at expiration, else nothing.\n- **DOOM options / CEBOs**: deep-out-of-the-money puts and Credit Event Binary Options that pay on a company's collapse or credit event — exchange-traded credit protection, CDS-like.\n\n## Warrants, employee stock options, and convertibles\n\n| Feature | Exchange-traded options | Warrants / ESOs / convertibles |\n|---|---|---|\n| Issuer | Written by investors, cleared by OCC | Issued by the **company itself** |\n| Exercise | Writer delivers existing shares | Company issues **new shares** → **dilution** |\n| Standardization | Standardized, liquid | Custom terms; ESOs nontransferable, with vesting |\n| Number outstanding | Unlimited (open interest varies) | Fixed at issuance |\n\nConvertible bonds embed a call on the issuer's equity inside a bond; conversion also creates new shares. Because exercise brings cash to the company and dilutes existing holders, valuation differs from ordinary options.\n\n### Worked example: profit on a long put\n\nYou buy 1 put contract (100 shares), K = $50, premium $3.50 per share. At expiration S = $42.\n\n- Payoff = $$\\max(50 - 42, 0)$$ = $8.00 per share\n- Profit = 8.00 − 3.50 = $4.50 per share → 4.50 × 100 = **$450 per contract**\n- Breakeven: $$S_T = K - p$$ = 50 − 3.50 = $46.50\n- Maximum profit (stock to zero): 50 − 3.50 = $46.50 per share; maximum loss: the $350 premium.\n\n### Worked example: naked call writer margin\n\nYou write 5 naked calls, K = $55, stock at S = $51, premium $2.00 per share. Per share:\n\n- Formula 1: 2.00 + 0.20 × 51 − (55 − 51) = 2.00 + 10.20 − 4.00 = **$8.20** (the option is $4 OTM)\n- Formula 2: 2.00 + 0.10 × 51 = $7.10\n\nMargin = max(8.20, 7.10) × 100 shares × 5 contracts = 8.20 × 500 = **$4,100**. (For naked puts, formula 2 uses 10% of the strike instead of the stock price.)","flashcards":"**Q1.** Call vs put; American vs European\n> **A:** A call gives the right to buy at strike $$K$$; a put the right to sell at $$K$$. American options can be exercised any time up to expiration; European only at expiration. Most exchange-traded stock options are American. The buyer pays a premium; the writer receives it and bears the obligation.\n\n**Q2.** Payoff and profit of the four basic positions\n> **A:** Long call payoff $$\\max(S_T-K,0)$$, profit minus $$c$$. Short call payoff $$-\\max(S_T-K,0)$$, profit plus $$c$$. Long put payoff $$\\max(K-S_T,0)$$, profit minus $$p$$. Short put payoff $$-\\max(K-S_T,0)$$, profit plus $$p$$.\n\n**Q3.** Moneyness for calls and puts\n> **A:** ITM: call $$S>K$$, put $$S<K$$. ATM: $$S=K$$ for both. OTM: call $$S<K$$, put $$S>K$$.\n\n**Q4.** Intrinsic value vs time value\n> **A:** Intrinsic value = exercise value now: $$\\max(S-K,0)$$ for a call, $$\\max(K-S,0)$$ for a put. Time value = premium − intrinsic value; it reflects remaining optionality and is largest at the money.\n\n**Q5.** Contract specifications: size, expirations, strikes, limits\n> **A:** One contract = options on 100 shares. Standardized monthly cycles plus current/next month; LEAPS ~3 years; weeklys expire each Friday. Strike spacing ~$2.50/$5/$10 by price level. Position limits cap contracts held on one side; exercise limits cap exercises over a window — both deter manipulation.\n\n**Q6.** Corporate-action adjustments: splits and stock dividends\n> **A:** For an n-for-m split, strike × m/n and shares per contract × n/m (economics preserved). Example: 3-for-1 split, $$K=\\$90$$ on 100 shares → $$K=\\$30$$ on 300 shares. A 25% stock dividend = 5-for-4 split → $$K=\\$72$$ on 125 shares.\n\n**Q7.** Trap: are options adjusted for cash dividends?\n> **A:** No. Exchange-traded options are NOT adjusted for ordinary cash dividends — the stock drops on the ex-date and holders bear it (the dividend is priced into the premium). They ARE adjusted for splits and stock dividends. Only special/large distributions trigger discretionary OCC adjustment.\n\n**Q8.** Role of the OCC and assignment mechanics\n> **A:** The Options Clearing Corporation is the central counterparty — guarantees performance, becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, and nets positions. On exercise it selects a short member firm, which assigns a customer (random or FIFO). Most ITM options exercise automatically at expiration.\n\n**Q9.** Why sell an ITM American option rather than exercise it early?\n> **A:** Exercise captures only intrinsic value, while a sale also captures remaining time value. Therefore early exercise of a call on a non-dividend stock is never optimal — sell it instead.\n\n**Q10.** Option buyer and naked writer margin rules\n> **A:** Buyers pay the premium in full (options already embed leverage); only options with maturity over 9 months may be bought on 50% margin. Naked writers post the greater of: (1) premium + 20% of stock price − OTM amount; (2) premium + 10% of stock price (calls) or 10% of strike (puts). Covered call writers need no extra margin.\n\n**Q11.** Worked: naked call writer margin\n> **A:** Write 5 naked calls, $$K=55$$, $$S=51$$, premium $2.00. Formula 1: $$2.00 + 0.20(51) - (55-51) = \\$8.20$$. Formula 2: $$2.00 + 0.10(51) = \\$7.10$$. Margin = $$\\max(8.20, 7.10) \\times 100 \\times 5 = \\$4,100$$.\n\n**Q12.** Nonstandard option products\n> **A:** FLEX: exchange-traded with negotiable terms, OCC-cleared. Weeklys: standard options expiring weekly. Binary (digital): pay a fixed amount if ITM, else nothing. DOOM options / CEBOs: deep-OTM puts and Credit Event Binary Options that pay on collapse/credit event — exchange-traded, CDS-like.\n\n**Q13.** Warrants, ESOs, and convertibles vs exchange-traded options\n> **A:** They are issued by the company itself (not investors); exercise creates NEW shares causing dilution; terms are custom (ESOs nontransferable, with vesting); number outstanding is fixed at issuance. Because exercise brings cash to the company and dilutes holders, valuation differs from ordinary options.\n\n**Q14.** Trap: premium quoted per share vs cash per contract\n> **A:** Premiums are quoted per share, but cash changes hands per contract (100 shares). A $3.50 quote costs $$3.50 \\times 100 = \\$350$$ per contract. Read whether the question asks for the per-share or per-contract figure.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** You buy one call option contract (on 100 shares) with strike $K = \\$40$ for a premium of $4.00 per share. At expiration the stock is $47. Your profit per share is closest to:\nA) $3.00 B) $4.00 C) $7.00 D) −$4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the profit (not just the payoff) on a long call at expiration.\n\nStep 1 — Payoff of a long call:\n$$\\text{Payoff} = \\max(S_T - K, 0) = \\max(47 - 40, 0) = \\$7.00$$\n\nStep 2 — Profit = payoff − premium paid:\n$$\\text{Profit} = 7.00 - 4.00 = \\$3.00$$\n\nWhy A is correct: you make $7 by exercising, but you already paid $4 for the option, so the net gain is $3.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B ($4.00) is the premium you paid, not the profit.\n- C ($7.00) is the payoff before subtracting the premium — the classic payoff-vs-profit trap.\n- D (−$4.00) would be your loss only if the option expired worthless ($S_T \\le 40$).\n\nAnalogy: you pay $4 for a coupon that lets you buy something $7 below market. You're ahead by $3 after paying for the coupon.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Profit = payoff − premium for a buyer. Always subtract what you paid.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A put option with strike $K = \\$60$ trades at a premium of $9.50 when the stock is at $52. The option's time value is:\nA) $0.00 B) $1.50 C) $8.00 D) $9.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the time value, given the total premium.\n\nStep 1 — Intrinsic value of a put = $\\max(K - S, 0)$:\n$$\\max(60 - 52, 0) = \\$8.00$$\n\nStep 2 — Time value = premium − intrinsic value:\n$$9.50 - 8.00 = \\$1.50$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the premium ($9.50) splits into intrinsic value ($8.00, the immediate exercise value) and time value ($1.50, the extra paid for remaining optionality).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($0.00) would mean the option has no time value, which is wrong since premium exceeds intrinsic value.\n- C ($8.00) is the intrinsic value, not the time value.\n- D ($9.50) is the whole premium, not just the time portion.\n\nAnalogy: the $9.50 price is like a sandwich: $8 of \"meat\" (intrinsic value you'd get today) plus $1.50 of \"extra topping\" (time value for the chance things improve before expiry).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Premium = intrinsic value + time value. For a put, intrinsic = max(K − S, 0).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Option premium = intrinsic + time value\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Intrinsic value\",\"items\":[\"put: max(K-S,0)\",\"here: 60-52 = $8.00\",\"exercise-now value\"]},{\"h\":\"Time value\",\"items\":[\"premium - intrinsic\",\"here: 9.50-8.00 = $1.50\",\"pay for remaining optionality\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A company pays an ordinary cash dividend of $1.00 per share. An exchange-traded call option on that stock with strike $K = \\$40$ will have its terms:\nA) Adjusted so the strike falls to $39 B) Adjusted only if the option is in the money C) Left unchanged D) Adjusted so each contract covers 102.5 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests whether ordinary cash dividends change exchange-traded option terms.\n\nExchange-traded options are NOT adjusted for ordinary cash dividends. The stock simply drops on the ex-dividend date, and option holders bear that — the expected dividend was already built into the premium when they bought. Option terms ARE adjusted for stock splits and stock dividends, but not ordinary cash dividends.\n\nWhy C is correct: ordinary cash dividends leave the strike and contract size unchanged.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the classic trap — \"reduce the strike by the dividend.\" Only large/special distributions trigger discretionary adjustments, not ordinary dividends.\n- B invents a rule based on moneyness that does not exist.\n- D describes a stock-dividend-style share adjustment, which does not apply to cash dividends.\n\nAnalogy: everyone already knew the dividend was coming, so the option's price had it baked in — no need to retune the contract afterward.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Splits and stock dividends adjust option terms; ordinary cash dividends do NOT.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Do option terms adjust?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Adjusts terms\",\"items\":[\"stock splits\",\"stock dividends\",\"large/special distributions\"]},{\"h\":\"No adjustment\",\"items\":[\"ordinary cash dividends\",\"holder bears ex-date drop\",\"already priced into premium\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** After a 4-for-1 stock split, a put option contract originally with strike $K = \\$120$ on 100 shares becomes:\nA) $K = \\$120$ on 100 shares (no change) B) $K = \\$30$ on 100 shares C) $K = \\$480$ on 25 shares D) $K = \\$30$ on 400 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question asks how an n-for-m split adjusts option terms.\n\nFor an $n$-for-$m$ split: strike is multiplied by $m/n$, and the shares per contract are multiplied by $n/m$. A 4-for-1 split means $n = 4$, $m = 1$.\n\n- New strike: $120 \\times \\dfrac{m}{n} = 120 \\times \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\$30$\n- New shares: $100 \\times \\dfrac{n}{m} = 100 \\times \\dfrac{4}{1} = 400$ shares\n\nWhy D is correct: the strike drops to $30 and shares rise to 400, exactly preserving the contract's economics ($120 \\times 100 = \\$12{,}000 = \\$30 \\times 400$).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ignores the adjustment — splits DO adjust terms.\n- B adjusts the strike but forgets to increase the share count.\n- C inverts the ratios (multiplying strike by 4 and dividing shares by 4) — the wrong direction.\n\nAnalogy: cutting one pizza into four slices: each slice is worth a quarter as much, but you now have four times as many.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For an n-for-1 split: divide the strike by n, multiply the shares by n. Total economic value stays the same.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"n-for-m split adjustment (4-for-1)\",\"steps\":[\"New strike = K x m/n = 120 x 1/4 = $30\",\"New shares = 100 x n/m = 100 x 4 = 400\",\"Check: 30 x 400 = 12,000 = 120 x 100\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which statement about exercise styles is correct?\nA) European options can be exercised any time before expiration B) American options can be exercised only at expiration C) American options can be exercised any time up to expiration; most exchange-traded stock options are American D) European options are always worth more than American options\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the basic difference between American and European exercise.\n\nAmerican options can be exercised at any time up to and including expiration. European options can be exercised only at expiration. Most exchange-traded stock options in the US are American style.\n\nWhy C is correct: it states the American rule (exercise any time) and the real-world fact that exchange-traded stock options are typically American.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A swaps the definitions — European is exercise-at-expiration-only.\n- B swaps them the other way — American allows early exercise.\n- D is backward: an American option has all the rights of a European one plus more, so American $\\ge$ European, never strictly less.\n\nAnalogy: an American option is an \"anytime\" ticket; a European option is a \"valid only on the final day\" ticket. The anytime ticket is at least as valuable.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American = exercise anytime (≥ European value). Most listed equity options are American.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Exercise styles\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"American\",\"items\":[\"exercise ANY time to expiry\",\"most listed equity options\",\"value >= European\"]},{\"h\":\"European\",\"items\":[\"exercise ONLY at expiration\",\"fewer rights\",\"value <= American\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** An investor writes (sells) one naked call: strike $K = \\$55$, stock at $S = \\$51$, premium $2.00 per share. Using the two standard formulas, the initial margin per contract (100 shares) is closest to:\nA) $710 B) $820 C) $1,020 D) $400\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the naked call writer's margin, which is the GREATER of two formula amounts.\n\nThe option is out of the money by $55 - 51 = \\$4$ (a call is OTM when stock < strike).\n\nFormula 1: premium + 20% of stock − amount OTM\n$$2.00 + 0.20 \\times 51 - 4.00 = 2.00 + 10.20 - 4.00 = \\$8.20 \\text{ per share}$$\n\nFormula 2: premium + 10% of stock (for naked calls)\n$$2.00 + 0.10 \\times 51 = 2.00 + 5.10 = \\$7.10 \\text{ per share}$$\n\nTake the greater: $\\max(8.20, 7.10) = \\$8.20$ per share.\n\nPer contract (100 shares):\n$$8.20 \\times 100 = \\$820$$\n\nWhy B is correct: it uses the larger of the two formulas times 100 shares.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($710) used Formula 2 (the smaller), but you must take the GREATER.\n- C ($1,020) forgot to subtract the OTM amount in Formula 1 ($2.00 + 10.20 = 12.20$... per share $\\times 100$ is wrong direction; this reflects skipping the OTM deduction).\n- D ($400) just multiplied the OTM amount by 100 — not a valid formula.\n\nAnalogy: like an insurer setting a deposit — they compute two safety cushions and keep the bigger one to be safe.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Naked writer margin = MAX of (premium + 20% stock − OTM amount) and (premium + 10% of stock for calls / strike for puts), then × 100 × contracts.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Naked writer margin\",\"steps\":[\"Formula 1: premium + 20% stock - OTM amount\",\"Formula 2: premium + 10% stock (call) or 10% strike (put)\",\"Take the GREATER of the two\",\"Multiply by 100 x contracts\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Why does a covered call writer (long stock, short call) generally need no margin beyond the stock position?\nA) Covered calls cannot lose money B) The long stock collateralizes the obligation to deliver shares C) The OCC waives all margin on covered positions D) Covered calls are always out of the money\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why covered calls don't require extra margin.\n\nIf the short call is exercised, the writer must deliver shares. But the writer already OWNS the shares (the long stock). Those shares stand ready to be delivered, so there's no uncovered risk that requires extra collateral.\n\nWhy B is correct: the stock already held collateralizes the delivery obligation.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — a covered call still loses if the stock falls a lot; it just has no extra margin requirement.\n- C overstates it; it's not a blanket OCC waiver, it's that the stock itself covers the obligation.\n- D is false — a covered call can be ITM or OTM; moneyness isn't the reason.\n\nAnalogy: if you've promised to sell your bike and the bike is already in your garage, the buyer doesn't need you to post a cash bond — the bike is the guarantee.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Covered\" means the underlying is already owned, so no extra margin. \"Naked\" means uncovered, requiring margin.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why no extra margin on a covered call?\",\"options\":[\"Covered calls cannot lose money\",\"The long stock collateralizes the delivery obligation\",\"The OCC waives all margin\",\"Covered calls are always OTM\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The writer already owns the shares to deliver, so there is no uncovered risk needing collateral.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which best describes the role of the Options Clearing Corporation (OCC)?\nA) It sets the premiums for all listed options B) It is the central counterparty that guarantees performance, becoming buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer C) It writes most of the options traded on exchanges D) It decides which stocks are allowed to pay dividends\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what the OCC actually does.\n\nThe OCC is the central counterparty (clearinghouse) for listed options. It guarantees that contracts are honored by stepping in between parties — becoming the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer — and it nets positions. On exercise it assigns the obligation to a member firm with a short position.\n\nWhy B is correct: it states the OCC's central-counterparty guarantee role precisely.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — prices are set by supply/demand and market makers, not the OCC.\n- C is false — options are written by investors; the OCC clears, it doesn't write them.\n- D is unrelated — dividend decisions are made by companies' boards.\n\nAnalogy: the OCC is like an escrow service that sits between buyer and seller so neither has to trust the other directly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: OCC = central counterparty that guarantees performance and handles exercise/assignment. It does not set prices.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Role of the OCC\",\"options\":[\"Sets premiums for all listed options\",\"Central counterparty: buyer to every seller, seller to every buyer, guarantees performance\",\"Writes most exchange-traded options\",\"Decides which stocks pay dividends\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The OCC novates and guarantees contracts and handles exercise/assignment; it does not set prices.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** How do warrants and convertible bonds differ economically from ordinary exchange-traded call options?\nA) They are always cheaper than exchange-traded options B) They are issued by the company itself and create new shares (dilution) when exercised C) They can never be exercised early D) They are cleared by the OCC like listed options\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts company-issued instruments with regular listed options.\n\nWarrants, employee stock options, and the conversion feature of convertibles are issued by the company itself. When exercised, the company issues NEW shares, which dilutes existing shareholders. Ordinary exchange-traded options are written by investors and settled with existing shares, so there's no dilution.\n\nWhy B is correct: the issuer is the company, and exercise creates new shares (dilution).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is not generally true — pricing varies; dilution and custom terms, not a blanket discount, are the distinction.\n- C is false — exercise style varies; this isn't the defining difference.\n- D is false — warrants/convertibles are company instruments, not OCC-cleared listed options.\n\nAnalogy: a listed option is like reselling an existing concert ticket; a warrant is the venue printing a brand-new ticket, which means a slightly more crowded show for everyone (dilution).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Company-issued instruments (warrants, ESOs, convertibles) create new shares → dilution. Listed options use existing shares → no dilution.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Company-issued vs listed options\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Warrants / ESOs / convertibles\",\"items\":[\"issued by the company\",\"exercise creates NEW shares\",\"dilutes existing holders\",\"custom/nontransferable terms\"]},{\"h\":\"Exchange-traded options\",\"items\":[\"written by investors\",\"use EXISTING shares\",\"no dilution\",\"OCC-cleared, standardized\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A binary (digital) call option:\nA) Pays the full intrinsic value $\\max(S_T - K, 0)$ B) Pays a fixed amount if it finishes in the money, and nothing otherwise C) Must always be exercised early D) Can never expire worthless\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the defining feature of a binary (digital) option.\n\nA binary call pays a fixed, pre-set amount if the option is in the money at expiration, and pays nothing if it is not. The payoff is \"all or nothing,\" unlike a vanilla option whose payoff scales with how far in the money it is.\n\nWhy B is correct: fixed payoff if ITM, zero otherwise — the \"digital\" (0 or 1) nature.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes a vanilla call, whose payoff scales with $S_T - K$, not a fixed amount.\n- C is false — there's no requirement to exercise early; it settles based on the finish.\n- D is false — a binary option pays nothing (expires worthless) if it finishes out of the money.\n\nAnalogy: a binary option is like a bet that pays a flat $100 if your team wins and $0 if it loses — the margin of victory doesn't matter.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Binary/digital = fixed payoff if ITM, else nothing. Vanilla = payoff grows with how deep ITM it is.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Binary vs vanilla call\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Binary (digital)\",\"items\":[\"fixed cash if ITM\",\"nothing if OTM\",\"all-or-nothing\"]},{\"h\":\"Vanilla\",\"items\":[\"payoff = max(S-K,0)\",\"scales with depth ITM\",\"continuous payoff\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A stock declares a $1.00 ordinary cash dividend. An exchange-traded call on the stock with K = $40 will have its terms:\nA) Adjusted to K = $39 B) Adjusted to K = $39 and 100 shares becomes 102.5 C) Unchanged D) Adjusted only if the option is in the money\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** ordinary cash dividends do not adjust option terms; only splits/stock dividends (and special distributions) do.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** You buy a call for $4.00 with K = $40. At expiration the stock is $47. Your profit per share is:\nA) $3.00 B) $4.00 C) $7.00 D) −$4.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** payoff 47 − 40 = 7, minus the $4 premium = $3.00.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A put with K = $60 trades at $9.50 when the stock is at $52. Its time value is:\nA) $0.00 B) $1.50 C) $8.00 D) $9.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** intrinsic = 60 − 52 = 8.00; time value = 9.50 − 8.00 = 1.50.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** After a 4-for-1 stock split, a put contract with K = $120 on 100 shares becomes:\nA) K = $120 on 100 shares (no change) B) K = $30 on 100 shares C) K = $480 on 25 shares D) K = $30 on 400 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** n-for-m = 4-for-1: strike × 1/4 = 30, shares × 4 = 400; economics preserved.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** An investor holds one exchange-traded call option on 100 shares with a strike of $60. The stock undergoes a 3-for-1 split. After adjustment, the investor holds:\nA) One contract on 100 shares with a strike of $20 B) One contract on 300 shares with a strike of $20 C) One contract on 300 shares with a strike of $60 D) Three contracts on 100 shares with a strike of $60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Splits adjust terms so the position's economics are unchanged: strike ÷ 3 = $20, shares × 3 = 300. (Cash dividends, by contrast, trigger **no** adjustment to exchange-traded options.)\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Option contract adjustments\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"3-for-1 stock split\",\"items\":[\"strike ÷ 3 → $20\",\"shares × 3 → 300\",\"still 1 contract\"]},{\"h\":\"Cash dividend\",\"items\":[\"no adjustment\",\"strike unchanged\",\"shares unchanged\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A trader writes 10 naked call contracts with strike $K = \\$70$ when the stock trades at $S = \\$66$. The call premium is $1.80 per share. Under the standard exchange margin rules, the required margin is *closest* to:\nA) $8,400 B) $11,000 C) $13,200 D) $14,200\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We write **naked calls** and must take the greater of the two standard per-share margin formulas, then scale by 100 shares and 10 contracts. The call is **out of the money** ($S = \\$66 < K = \\$70$), OTM by $K - S = \\$4$.\n\n1. **Formula 1**: premium + 20% of stock price − amount OTM.\n$$1.80 + 0.20(66) - 4.00 = 1.80 + 13.20 - 4.00 = 11.00$$\n2. **Formula 2** (naked call → 10% of stock price): $$1.80 + 0.10(66) = 1.80 + 6.60 = 8.40$$\n3. Greater of the two: $\\max(11.00, 8.40) = 11.00$ per share.\n4. Scale: $$11.00 \\times 100 \\times 10 = \\$11,000$$\n\n- A ($8,400) uses Formula 2 ($8.40/share × 1000), failing to take the maximum — Formula 1 ($11.00) is larger and governs.\n- C ($13,200) forgets to subtract the $4 OTM amount in Formula 1: $1.80 + 13.20 = 15.00$... actually $15.00 \\times 1000 = 15{,}000$; the $13,200 trap drops the premium and uses $0.20 \\times 66 = 13.20$ alone.\n- D ($14,200) uses 10% of the **strike** (a put rule) in Formula 2 and mis-maximizes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a **naked call** Formula 2 uses 10% of the **stock price**; for a **naked put** it uses 10% of the **strike**. Always subtract the OTM amount in Formula 1, then take the maximum, then multiply by 100 × contracts.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Naked writer margin\",\"steps\":[\"Formula 1: premium + 20% stock - OTM amount\",\"Formula 2: premium + 10% stock (call) / 10% strike (put)\",\"Take the GREATER of the two\",\"Scale by 100 x contracts\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** On June 1 you buy 4 put contracts on a stock, strike $K = \\$80$, premium $4.20 per share. On June 15 the company executes a 5-for-4 stock dividend (equivalent to a 5-for-4 split). After the adjustment, and given the stock finishes at $S_T = \\$50$ per (post-adjustment) share at expiration, your total payoff (ignoring premium) is *closest* to:\nA) $0 B) $5,000 C) $7,000 D) $12,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** A 25% stock dividend is a 5-for-4 split. We must adjust the strike and share count, then compute the payoff on the post-adjustment terms.\n\n1. For an n-for-m split with n = 5, m = 4: strike is multiplied by $m/n = 4/5$ and shares per contract by $n/m = 5/4$.\n2. New strike: $$80 \\times \\tfrac{4}{5} = \\$64$$\n3. New shares per contract: $$100 \\times \\tfrac{5}{4} = 125 \\text{ shares}$$\n4. Put payoff per share at $S_T = \\$50$: $$\\max(64 - 50, 0) = \\$14$$\n5. Total over 4 contracts × 125 shares: $$14 \\times 125 \\times 4 = 14 \\times 500 = \\$7,000$$\n\n- A ($0) wrongly assumes a cash-dividend rule (no adjustment) and uses old $K=80$ vs $S_T=50$... which actually pays — so it is just wrong.\n- B ($5,000) uses the new strike $64 but forgets to raise the share count to 125 (uses 100): $14 \\times 100 \\times 4 = 5,600$ then mis-rounds, or uses $10 \\times 500$.\n- D ($12,000) forgets to lower the strike, using $K=80$: $(80-50)\\times 100 \\times 4 = 12,000$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For an n-for-m split, **strike × m/n** and **shares × n/m** — the economics are preserved, so check that (new strike × new shares) ≈ (old strike × old shares): $64 × 125 = 8,000 = 80 × 100$. ✓\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"5-for-4 split adjustment\",\"steps\":[\"New strike = K x m/n = 80 x 4/5 = $64\",\"New shares = 100 x n/m = 100 x 5/4 = 125\",\"Check: 64 x 125 = 8,000 = 80 x 100\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A stock pays a $1.50 ordinary cash dividend on its ex-date. An investor holds a long exchange-traded call with $K = \\$45$ on 100 shares. Immediately after the ex-date, the option's contractual terms are:\nA) Strike reduced to $43.50; still 100 shares B) Strike reduced to $43.50; shares raised to 103.33 C) Unchanged; the call holder simply bears the stock's ex-date price drop D) Strike raised to $46.50 to compensate the call holder\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** This tests the corporate-action adjustment rules: ordinary cash dividends do **not** adjust option terms.\n\n1. Exchange-traded options are adjusted for **stock splits and stock dividends**, not for **ordinary cash dividends**.\n2. On the ex-date the stock price falls by roughly the dividend, and the call holder simply bears it — the expected dividend was already priced into the premium when the option was bought.\n\n- A and B apply a \"strike reduced by the dividend\" rule that does not exist for ordinary cash dividends (that is a discretionary adjustment reserved for special/large distributions).\n- D invents a strike increase that benefits the holder — no such rule.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Cash dividend → **no adjustment** (stock drops, holder bears it). Split/stock dividend → adjust strike and share count. Only **special/large** cash distributions trigger discretionary OCC adjustments.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Corporate action: does it adjust terms?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"No adjustment\",\"items\":[\"ordinary cash dividend\",\"stock drops on ex-date\",\"holder bears it\"]},{\"h\":\"Adjusts terms\",\"items\":[\"stock split\",\"stock dividend\",\"special/large distribution\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An investor buys a call for $5.50 and simultaneously the same-strike same-maturity put for $3.00, both with $K = \\$100$ on a non-dividend stock (this is a long straddle, but consider only the call leg's properties). For the call alone, when the stock is at $S = \\$103$, the time value of the call is *closest* to:\nA) $0 B) $2.50 C) $3.00 D) $5.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We must separate the call premium into intrinsic value and time value at the current stock price.\n\n1. Intrinsic value of a call = $\\max(S - K, 0) = \\max(103 - 100, 0) = \\$3.00$.\n2. Time value = premium − intrinsic value: $$5.50 - 3.00 = \\$2.50$$\n\n- A ($0) wrongly assumes an ATM/OTM call (no time value) — but the call here is ITM with leftover time value.\n- C ($3.00) reports the **intrinsic** value, not the time value (the classic swap).\n- D ($5.50) reports the full premium, ignoring that part of it is intrinsic.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Premium = intrinsic + time value. Compute intrinsic first ($\\max(S-K,0)$ for a call), then subtract from the premium. Time value is largest **at the money** and shrinks deep ITM/OTM.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Call premium = intrinsic + time value\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Intrinsic value\",\"items\":[\"call: max(S-K,0)\",\"here: 103-100 = $3.00\"]},{\"h\":\"Time value\",\"items\":[\"premium - intrinsic\",\"here: 5.50-3.00 = $2.50\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement about exchange-traded options, the OCC, and early exercise is *most* accurate?\nA) Exercising an in-the-money American call on a non-dividend stock is generally better than selling it, because exercise captures intrinsic value immediately. B) The OCC becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer, guaranteeing performance, and on exercise assigns a short-position member firm. C) Option buyers may always buy any option on 50% margin because options embed leverage. D) A covered call writer must post the same naked-writer margin as an uncovered call writer.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This tests the role of the OCC, exercise/assignment mechanics, and margin rules.\n\n1. The **OCC** is the central counterparty: it becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, guarantees performance, nets positions, and on exercise selects a short-position member firm, which assigns a customer (random or FIFO). Choice B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: selling an ITM American call captures intrinsic value **plus remaining time value**, while exercise captures only intrinsic value — selling is better. Early exercise of a call on a non-dividend stock is never optimal.\n- C is wrong: buyers pay the premium **in full**; only options with maturity over **9 months** may be bought on 50% margin.\n- D is wrong: a covered call (long stock + short call) needs **no margin** beyond the stock — the stock collateralizes the obligation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The OCC = central counterparty (novation + guarantee + assignment). Buyers pay premium in full (margin only if maturity > 9 months). Covered calls need no extra margin.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"OCC, exercise and margin\",\"options\":[\"Exercising an ITM call on a non-dividend stock beats selling it\",\"OCC is buyer to every seller / seller to every buyer and assigns a short member on exercise\",\"Buyers may buy any option on 50% margin\",\"Covered call writer posts the same margin as a naked writer\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"OCC novates and guarantees. Selling beats exercising (captures time value); buyers pay premium in full; covered calls need no extra margin.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** You buy 1 put contract (100 shares), $K = \\$50$, premium $3.50 per share. The stock finishes at $S_T = \\$42$. Which of the following correctly states your profit per contract, breakeven, and maximum possible loss?\nA) Profit $450; breakeven $46.50; max loss $350 B) Profit $800; breakeven $53.50; max loss unlimited C) Profit $450; breakeven $53.50; max loss $350 D) Profit $350; breakeven $46.50; max loss $4,650\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We must compute the long put's profit at expiration, its breakeven, and its maximum loss.\n\n1. Payoff per share = $\\max(K - S_T, 0) = \\max(50 - 42, 0) = \\$8.00$.\n2. Profit per share = payoff − premium = $8.00 - 3.50 = \\$4.50$; per contract: $4.50 \\times 100 = \\$450$.\n3. Breakeven (long put): $S_T = K - p = 50 - 3.50 = \\$46.50$.\n4. Maximum loss = the premium paid = $3.50 \\times 100 = \\$350$ (occurs if $S_T \\ge K$ and the put expires worthless).\n\n- B uses the payoff ($800) as profit and a call-style breakeven ($K + p$); a long put's loss is capped, not unlimited.\n- C has the right profit and loss but a call-style breakeven $K + p = 53.50$ instead of $K - p$.\n- D mislabels the gross payoff as $350 and treats max loss as $K - p$ per share × 100.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long put breakeven = $K - p$ (the price must fall *below* the strike by the premium). Max loss for any long option = the premium. Profit = payoff − premium; do not report the payoff as the profit.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A market maker quotes a call at a bid of $4.10 and an ask of $4.40. A customer buys 6 contracts at the ask and later sells all 6 at the (unchanged) bid via an offsetting order, paying $0.05 per share in commissions on each side. The customer's total round-trip cost (spread plus commissions) is *closest* to:\nA) $180 B) $240 C) $360 D) $420\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We must compute the cost of crossing the bid-ask spread plus commissions, scaled to 6 contracts (100 shares each).\n\n1. Spread cost per share = ask − bid = $4.40 - 4.10 = \\$0.30$.\n2. Commissions per share = $0.05 buy + $0.05 sell = $0.10$.\n3. Total cost per share = $0.30 + 0.10 = \\$0.40$.\n4. Scale: $0.40 \\times 100 \\times 6 = 0.40 \\times 600 = \\$240$.\n\n- A ($180) counts only the spread ($0.30 \\times 600$), omitting commissions.\n- C ($360) double-counts the spread (charges $0.30 on both the buy and the sell) plus omits handling commissions correctly.\n- D ($420) adds the full ask and bid incorrectly or applies commission per contract not per share.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A round trip pays the spread **once** (buy at ask, sell at bid) plus commissions **both ways**. Per-share cost = spread + total commissions, then × 100 × contracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An employee receives company stock options (ESOs) as compensation. Compared with exchange-traded call options on the same stock, the ESOs *most likely*:\nA) Are freely transferable and cleared by the OCC B) Create new shares on exercise, diluting existing shareholders, and are nontransferable with a vesting period C) Have unlimited supply determined by open interest D) Adjust their strike for ordinary cash dividends but not for splits\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This contrasts employee stock options (and warrants/convertibles) with exchange-traded options.\n\n1. ESOs are **issued by the company itself**; exercise causes the company to issue **new shares**, diluting existing shareholders.\n2. ESOs are typically **nontransferable** and subject to a **vesting** period, unlike standardized, liquid exchange-traded options.\n\n- A is wrong: ESOs are nontransferable and are not OCC-cleared instruments.\n- C describes exchange-traded options (supply effectively unlimited, tracked by open interest); ESOs are fixed at grant.\n- D inverts the adjustment rules and applies them to an instrument whose terms are set in the grant contract.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Company-issued equity claims (warrants, ESOs, convertibles) → **new shares + dilution**, custom/nontransferable terms. Exchange-traded options → written by investors, OCC-cleared, deliver existing shares, no dilution.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"ESOs vs exchange-traded options\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"ESOs (company-issued)\",\"items\":[\"issued by the company\",\"new shares -> dilution\",\"nontransferable, vesting\",\"fixed amount at grant\"]},{\"h\":\"Exchange-traded\",\"items\":[\"written by investors\",\"existing shares, no dilution\",\"freely transferable, OCC-cleared\",\"supply tracked by open interest\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"313","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Properties of Options","notes":"# 13. Properties of Options\n\nGARP tests the six pricing factors (and their signs for each option type), upper and lower bounds, put-call parity (European only!) and the American inequality, plus the early-exercise logic for American calls and puts. Expect at least one arbitrage-from-a-bound or parity question.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Identify the six factors that affect an option's price and indicate the direction of each effect for European and American calls and puts.\n- Identify, interpret, and compute upper and lower bounds for option prices on non-dividend-paying and dividend-paying stocks.\n- Explain put-call parity and apply it to value European stock options, with and without dividends.\n- Explain the put-call inequality for American options.\n- Explain and assess potential rationales for early exercise of American calls and puts.\n\n## The six factors\n\n| Factor (increase) | European call $$c$$ | European put $$p$$ | American call $$C$$ | American put $$P$$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stock price $$S_0$$ | + | − | + | − |\n| Strike price $$K$$ | − | + | − | + |\n| Time to expiration $$T$$ | ? | ? | + | + |\n| Volatility $$\\sigma$$ | + | + | + | + |\n| Risk-free rate $$r$$ | + | − | + | − |\n| Dividends $$D$$ | − | + | − | + |\n\nKey intuitions:\n\n- **Time (European)**: usually more time helps, but the effect is *ambiguous*. A large dividend between two maturities can make a shorter-dated European call worth more; a deep-ITM European put may prefer a shorter wait for the payoff. **American options never decrease in value with $$T$$** — a longer option contains every right of the shorter one.\n- **Rates**: higher $$r$$ raises the expected growth rate of the stock and lowers the PV of the strike — both help calls, both hurt puts.\n- **Dividends** transfer value out of the stock on the ex-date: stock drops, so calls fall and puts rise.\n\n## Upper bounds\n\nA call gives at most the stock; a put gives at most $$K$$ (at $$T$$ for the European version):\n\n$$\nc \\le S_0, \\qquad C \\le S_0, \\qquad p \\le Ke^{-rT}, \\qquad P \\le K\n$$\n\n## Lower bounds (European, non-dividend stock)\n\n$$\nc \\ge \\max(S_0 - Ke^{-rT},\\, 0), \\qquad p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} - S_0,\\, 0)\n$$\n\nWith dividends of present value $$D$$:\n\n$$\nc \\ge \\max(S_0 - D - Ke^{-rT},\\, 0), \\qquad p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} + D - S_0,\\, 0)\n$$\n\nDividends tighten the put bound and loosen the call bound — consistent with the six-factor table. If a market price violates a bound, an arbitrage exists (see Worked example 2).\n\n## Put-call parity\n\nFor **European** options on a non-dividend-paying stock, a call plus cash equals a put plus the stock (both portfolios pay $$\\max(S_T, K)$$ at $$T$$):\n\n$$\nc + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0\n$$\n\nWith dividends (PV = $$D$$):\n\n$$\nc + D + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0\n$$\n\nFor **American** options on a dividend-paying stock, only an inequality holds:\n\n$$\nS_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}\n$$\n\n(Set $$D = 0$$ for the non-dividend case.)\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Put-call parity holds for **European options only**. The classic trap supplies American option prices and asks you to plug them into the parity equation — for American options you may only bound $$C - P$$, not pin it down. Second trap: the sign of dividends. Dividends **lower** call values and **raise** put values; in the parity equation $$D$$ is added to the call side.\n\n## Early exercise\n\n**American call, no dividends: never exercise early**, so $$C = c$$. Three reasons:\n\n1. Exercising pays $$K$$ now instead of later — you forfeit interest on $$K$$.\n2. You throw away the **insurance**: holding the call protects you if $$S$$ falls below $$K$$ before $$T$$; once you own the stock, it does not.\n3. Since $$C \\ge c \\ge S_0 - Ke^{-rT} > S_0 - K$$, the call is always worth more *alive* (sell it) than *exercised*. If you no longer want exposure, sell the call rather than exercise it.\n\n**American put: early exercise can be optimal**, so $$P > p$$ is possible. Deep ITM (extreme case $$S_0 \\to 0$$: the payoff $$K$$ cannot get better), receiving $$K$$ early earns interest. Early exercise is more attractive when $$S_0$$ is low, $$r$$ is high, and $$\\sigma$$ is low.\n\n**Dividends** can make early exercise of an American call optimal — only **immediately before an ex-dividend date**, when the dividend captured exceeds the time value plus interest forgone.\n\n### Worked example: put-call parity arbitrage\n\nA 3-month European call on a non-dividend stock trades at $$c = \\$3$$ and the 3-month European put (same strike) at $$p = \\$2.25$$, with $$S_0 = \\$31$$, $$K = \\$30$$, $$r = 10\\%$$ continuous, $$T = 0.25$$.\n\nCheck parity ($$e^{-0.025} = 0.9753$$):\n\n$$\nc + Ke^{-rT} = 3 + 30(0.9753) = 3 + 29.2593 = 32.2593\n$$\n\n$$\np + S_0 = 2.25 + 31 = 33.25\n$$\n\nThe put-plus-stock side is overpriced. **Strategy: buy the cheap side, sell the rich side** — buy the call, short the put, short the stock.\n\nCash at $$t = 0$$: $$-3 + 2.25 + 31 = +\\$30.25$$, invested at 10% for 3 months ($$e^{0.025} = 1.0253$$):\n\n$$\n30.25 \\times 1.0253 = 31.0153\n$$\n\nAt $$T$$:\n\n- If $$S_T > 30$$: exercise the call — buy the stock for $30 to close the short; the put dies worthless.\n- If $$S_T < 30$$: the put is exercised against you — you buy the stock for $30 to close the short; the call dies worthless.\n\nEither way you pay exactly $30. Riskless profit:\n\n$$\n31.0153 - 30 = \\$1.0153\n$$\n\n### Worked example: lower-bound violation arbitrage\n\nA 1-year European call on a non-dividend stock trades at $$c = \\$3$$ with $$S_0 = \\$20$$, $$K = \\$18$$, $$r = 10\\%$$ continuous.\n\nLower bound ($$e^{-0.10} = 0.9048$$):\n\n$$\nS_0 - Ke^{-rT} = 20 - 18(0.9048) = 20 - 16.2871 = 3.7129 > 3\n$$\n\nThe call is too cheap. **Buy the call, short the stock**: inflow $$20 - 3 = \\$17$$, invested at 10% for 1 year ($$e^{0.10} = 1.1052$$):\n\n$$\n17 \\times 1.1052 = 18.7884\n$$\n\nAt $$T$$:\n\n- If $$S_T > 18$$: exercise — buy the stock for $18 to close the short. Profit $$= 18.7884 - 18 = \\$0.7884$$.\n- If $$S_T \\le 18$$: let the call lapse, buy the stock at $$S_T \\le 18$$. Profit $$= 18.7884 - S_T \\ge \\$0.7884$$.\n\nA riskless profit of at least $0.79 confirms the violation.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> For bound/parity arbitrage questions, always \"buy low, sell high\" at $$t=0$$, park the net inflow at $$r$$, and check both terminal branches ($$S_T > K$$ and $$S_T < K$$) — the payout at $$T$$ should be identical or bounded in both.","flashcards":"**Q1.** The six factors affecting option price and their direction for a European call\n> **A:** Increase in: stock price $$S_0$$ (+), strike $$K$$ (−), time $$T$$ (ambiguous), volatility $$\\sigma$$ (+), risk-free rate $$r$$ (+), dividends $$D$$ (−).\n\n**Q2.** Direction of the six factors for a European put\n> **A:** Increase in: stock price $$S_0$$ (−), strike $$K$$ (+), time $$T$$ (ambiguous), volatility $$\\sigma$$ (+), risk-free rate $$r$$ (−), dividends $$D$$ (+).\n\n**Q3.** Why is the effect of time ambiguous for European options but never negative for American?\n> **A:** For European options a large dividend before the longer maturity can make a shorter-dated call worth more, and a deep-ITM European put may prefer a shorter wait. American options never decrease in value with $$T$$ — a longer option contains every right of the shorter one.\n\n**Q4.** Why does a higher risk-free rate help calls and hurt puts?\n> **A:** Higher $$r$$ raises the expected growth rate of the stock and lowers the PV of the strike — both help calls and both hurt puts. Dividends do the opposite (stock drops on ex-date): calls fall, puts rise.\n\n**Q5.** Upper bounds on option prices\n> **A:** $$c \\le S_0$$, $$C \\le S_0$$ (a call gives at most the stock); $$p \\le Ke^{-rT}$$, $$P \\le K$$ (a put gives at most $$K$$).\n\n**Q6.** Lower bounds for European options on a non-dividend stock\n> **A:** $$c \\ge \\max(S_0 - Ke^{-rT},\\, 0)$$ and $$p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} - S_0,\\, 0)$$.\n\n**Q7.** Lower bounds with dividends (PV = $$D$$)\n> **A:** $$c \\ge \\max(S_0 - D - Ke^{-rT},\\, 0)$$ and $$p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} + D - S_0,\\, 0)$$. Dividends loosen the call bound and tighten the put bound.\n\n**Q8.** Put-call parity (European, non-dividend and with dividends)\n> **A:** Non-dividend: $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$. With dividends PV $$D$$: $$c + D + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$. Both portfolios pay $$\\max(S_T, K)$$ at $$T$$.\n\n**Q9.** Put-call inequality for American options\n> **A:** $$S_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$$ (set $$D=0$$ for the non-dividend case). For American options you can only bound $$C-P$$, not pin it down.\n\n**Q10.** Trap: applying put-call parity to American options\n> **A:** Put-call parity holds for EUROPEAN options only. The classic trap supplies American prices and asks you to plug into the parity equation — you may only use the inequality. Second trap: dividends LOWER calls and RAISE puts, and $$D$$ is added to the CALL side of parity.\n\n**Q11.** Why is an American call on a non-dividend stock never exercised early ($$C = c$$)?\n> **A:** (1) Exercising pays $$K$$ now, forfeiting interest on $$K$$. (2) You throw away downside insurance the call provides. (3) $$C \\ge c \\ge S_0 - Ke^{-rT} > S_0 - K$$, so the call is worth more alive (sell it) than exercised.\n\n**Q12.** When can early exercise of an American put or call be optimal?\n> **A:** American put: early exercise can be optimal ($$P > p$$ possible), more attractive when $$S_0$$ is low, $$r$$ is high, and $$\\sigma$$ is low (receiving $$K$$ early earns interest). American call: only immediately before an ex-dividend date, when the dividend captured exceeds time value plus interest forgone.\n\n**Q13.** Worked: put-call parity arbitrage strategy\n> **A:** If $$p + S_0 > c + Ke^{-rT}$$, the put-plus-stock side is overpriced: buy the cheap side (buy the call), sell the rich side (short the put, short the stock). Park the net inflow at $$r$$; at $$T$$ you pay exactly $$K$$ for the stock either way, locking a riskless profit.\n\n**Q14.** Worked: lower-bound violation arbitrage\n> **A:** If $$c < S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$$ the call is too cheap: buy the call, short the stock, invest the inflow $$S_0 - c$$ at $$r$$. At $$T$$, exercise if $$S_T > K$$ (buy at $$K$$) or buy in the market if $$S_T \\le K$$ — riskless profit at least $$(S_0-c)e^{rT} - K$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A 6-month European call on a non-dividend-paying stock trades at $4.00, with $S_0 = \\$50$, $K = \\$50$, and $r = 5\\%$ (continuous). By put-call parity, the European put price is closest to:\nA) $2.16 B) $2.77 C) $4.00 D) $5.23\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to find the put price from the call price using put-call parity.\n\nPut-call parity (European, no dividends):\n$$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0$$\n\nPlug in ($T = 0.5$):\n- $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.05 \\times 0.5} = e^{-0.025} = 0.97531$\n- $Ke^{-rT} = 50 \\times 0.97531 = 48.7655$\n- $p = 4.00 + 48.7655 - 50 = 2.7655 \\approx \\$2.77$\n\nWhy B is correct: it applies parity directly with the discounted strike.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($2.16) likely discounted the strike with the wrong rate or didn't discount at all consistently.\n- C ($4.00) just copies the call price — wrong, parity adjusts for the discounted strike vs spot.\n- D ($5.23) added $S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$ instead of subtracting, flipping a sign.\n\nAnalogy: parity is a seesaw — a call plus cash balances a put plus stock. Rearrange to find the missing weight.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Parity holds for EUROPEAN options. Memorize $c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$ and just solve for the unknown.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An American call on a non-dividend-paying stock is deep in the money with three months left. A holder who no longer wants the exposure should:\nA) Exercise immediately to capture the intrinsic value B) Sell the call in the market C) Exercise and immediately short the stock D) Hold to expiration no matter what\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the rule that an American call on a non-dividend stock should never be exercised early.\n\nThe call alive is worth at least $C \\ge S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$, which exceeds the exercise value $S_0 - K$ (because $Ke^{-rT} < K$). So selling captures both intrinsic value AND remaining time value, while exercising throws the time value away.\n\nWhy B is correct: selling realizes the full market value (intrinsic + time value); exercising only gets intrinsic value.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A exercises early and forfeits time value — never optimal for a call on a non-dividend stock.\n- C is just exercise (capturing intrinsic only) plus an unnecessary short — still wastes time value.\n- D ignores that the holder no longer wants the position; selling is cleaner and captures value now.\n\nAnalogy: a coupon worth more if resold than if used — you'd sell it, not \"spend\" it early.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Never exercise an American call on a non-dividend-paying stock early; sell it instead. $C = c$ in this case.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sell vs exercise (ITM American call, no dividend)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sell the call\",\"items\":[\"captures intrinsic + time value\",\"keeps downside insurance\",\"realizes full market value\"]},{\"h\":\"Exercise early\",\"items\":[\"captures only intrinsic\",\"forfeits time value\",\"loses interest on K\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which change, all else equal, DECREASES the value of a European put?\nA) A higher strike price B) Higher volatility C) A higher risk-free rate D) Higher expected dividends\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the sign of each factor's effect on a European put.\n\nA higher risk-free rate lowers the present value of the strike the put holder would receive on exercise, which hurts the put. (Higher rates help calls, hurt puts.)\n\nWhy C is correct: higher $r$ reduces the PV of the strike received, decreasing put value.\n\nWhy the others are wrong (all INCREASE a put's value):\n- A: a higher strike means the put can sell at a higher price — more valuable.\n- B: higher volatility raises the value of all options (more chance of big moves in your favor).\n- D: dividends push the stock price down on the ex-date, which RAISES put values.\n\nAnalogy: a put is insurance to sell at a fixed price. Higher interest rates make the promised future cash worth less today, so the insurance is worth a bit less.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For puts: $S_0\\uparrow$ bad, $K\\uparrow$ good, $\\sigma\\uparrow$ good, $r\\uparrow$ bad, dividends$\\uparrow$ good. Only higher $r$ decreases the put here.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Effect of an increase on a European put\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Raises put value\",\"items\":[\"higher strike K\",\"higher volatility\",\"higher dividends\",\"lower stock S0\"]},{\"h\":\"Lowers put value\",\"items\":[\"higher risk-free rate r\",\"higher stock S0\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** With $S_0 = \\$37$, $K = \\$40$, $r = 5\\%$ (continuous), and $T = 0.5$, the lower bound on a European put (non-dividend stock) is closest to:\nA) $0.00 B) $1.01 C) $2.01 D) $3.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the no-arbitrage lower bound of a European put.\n\nLower bound:\n$$p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} - S_0,\\ 0)$$\n\nCompute:\n- $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.05 \\times 0.5} = e^{-0.025} = 0.97531$\n- $Ke^{-rT} = 40 \\times 0.97531 = 39.0124$\n- $Ke^{-rT} - S_0 = 39.0124 - 37 = 2.0124$\n- $\\max(2.0124, 0) = \\$2.01$\n\nWhy C is correct: discount the strike, subtract spot, and take the max with zero.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($0.00) would apply if the discounted strike were below spot; here it's above, so the bound is positive.\n- B ($1.01) under-discounts or uses the wrong rate/time.\n- D ($3.00) uses the undiscounted strike: $40 - 37 = 3$, forgetting to discount $K$.\n\nAnalogy: the least a put can be worth is the discounted \"sell price\" minus today's stock price — anything less would be free money for an arbitrageur.\n\n📌 Exam tip: European put lower bound = $\\max(Ke^{-rT} - S_0, 0)$. Always discount the strike.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Put-call parity in the form $c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$ applies to:\nA) American options only B) European options only C) Both American and European options D) Only options on dividend-paying stocks\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the scope of the put-call parity equation.\n\nThe exact equality holds only for EUROPEAN options. For American options, early exercise possibilities mean we can only bound the relationship ($C - P$), not pin it down exactly.\n\nWhy B is correct: parity as an equation is a European-only result.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the opposite — American options give only an inequality.\n- C is wrong because American options don't satisfy the exact equality.\n- D is irrelevant — parity has a dividend-adjusted version, but the European requirement is the key restriction.\n\nAnalogy: parity is like a perfectly balanced scale that only stays balanced when nobody can remove a weight early — early exercise (American) lets someone tip it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Parity equation = European only. For American options you get the inequality $S_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Put-call parity scope\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"European\",\"items\":[\"exact EQUALITY\",\"c + Ke^-rT = p + S0\",\"early exercise impossible\"]},{\"h\":\"American\",\"items\":[\"only an INEQUALITY\",\"S0-D-K <= C-P <= S0-Ke^-rT\",\"early exercise breaks equality\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A 1-year European call on a non-dividend stock trades at $3.00, with $S_0 = \\$20$, $K = \\$18$, $r = 10\\%$ (continuous). Is there an arbitrage, and what is the call's lower bound?\nA) No arbitrage; lower bound is $2.00 B) Yes; the lower bound is about $3.71, so the call is too cheap C) Yes; the lower bound is about $3.71, so the call is too expensive D) No arbitrage; lower bound is $0.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks whether the call price violates its lower bound.\n\nLower bound for a European call (no dividends):\n$$c \\ge \\max(S_0 - Ke^{-rT},\\ 0)$$\n\nCompute:\n- $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.10 \\times 1} = e^{-0.10} = 0.90484$\n- $Ke^{-rT} = 18 \\times 0.90484 = 16.2871$\n- $S_0 - Ke^{-rT} = 20 - 16.2871 = 3.7129$\n\nThe lower bound is about $3.71, but the call trades at only $3.00. Since $3.00 < 3.71$, the bound is violated — the call is TOO CHEAP, so an arbitrage exists (buy the call, short the stock).\n\nWhy B is correct: the bound is $3.71, the price $3.00 is below it, so the call is underpriced — arbitrage exists.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A uses the undiscounted bound ($20 - 18 = 2$) and misses the violation.\n- C gets the bound right but says \"too expensive\" — it's too cheap (price below bound).\n- D claims the bound is zero, ignoring that $S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$ is clearly positive here.\n\nAnalogy: the call can't cost less than buying the stock now but paying the discounted strike later. If it does, you grab it for the free profit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: If the market price is BELOW the lower bound, the option is too cheap — buy it. Always discount the strike in the bound.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Price vs lower bound (call)\",\"items\":[\"below bound: too cheap, BUY\",\"|lower bound S0-Ke^-rT\",\"above bound: no violation\"],\"left\":\"arbitrage\",\"right\":\"fair / rich\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Higher dividends, all else equal, have which effect on European calls and puts?\nA) Raise both calls and puts B) Lower both calls and puts C) Lower calls and raise puts D) Raise calls and lower puts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the dividend effect on option values.\n\nWhen a stock goes ex-dividend, the stock price drops by roughly the dividend. A lower stock price is bad for calls (they want a high stock price) and good for puts (they want a low stock price). So higher dividends LOWER calls and RAISE puts.\n\nWhy C is correct: dividends transfer value out of the stock, hurting calls and helping puts.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A and B treat calls and puts the same — but they react oppositely to the stock price.\n- D reverses the effect — that would be the effect of a higher stock price or higher rates.\n\nAnalogy: a dividend is like the company handing cash out the back door — share price drops, so the \"right to buy\" (call) is worth less and the \"right to sell\" (put) is worth more.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dividends ↓ calls, ↑ puts. In parity, $D$ is added to the call side: $c + D + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Higher dividends effect\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Calls\",\"items\":[\"value FALLS\",\"stock drops on ex-date\",\"lower S0 hurts calls\"]},{\"h\":\"Puts\",\"items\":[\"value RISES\",\"stock drops on ex-date\",\"lower S0 helps puts\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** For an American option on a non-dividend-paying stock, early exercise of a call is:\nA) Always optimal when deep in the money B) Never optimal; the call is worth more alive than exercised C) Optimal only when interest rates are negative D) Required at expiration\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the early-exercise rule for American calls (no dividends).\n\nEarly exercise of an American call on a non-dividend stock is never optimal. Exercising pays $K$ now (losing interest on $K$) and throws away the downside insurance the option provides. Because $C \\ge c \\ge S_0 - Ke^{-rT} > S_0 - K$, the call is always worth more alive than exercised.\n\nWhy B is correct: it states the rule and the reason — the call is worth more unexercised.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the trap — \"deep ITM, just exercise.\" Even deep ITM, you should sell rather than exercise early.\n- C: negative rates aren't the trigger; the rule is about forgone interest and insurance, and it holds for positive rates.\n- D is about expiration mechanics, not early exercise — and even at expiration you'd sell or let auto-exercise handle it.\n\nAnalogy: holding the call keeps a safety net under you if the stock crashes. Exercising early cuts the net away for no benefit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American call on a non-dividend stock → never exercise early, so $C = c$. Dividends are the only thing that can make early call exercise worthwhile (just before ex-date).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Early exercise of an American call (no dividend)\",\"options\":[\"Always optimal when deep ITM\",\"Never optimal; worth more alive than exercised\",\"Optimal only if rates are negative\",\"Required at expiration\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"C >= c >= S0-Ke^-rT > S0-K, so the call is always worth more unexercised. Dividends are the only exception.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Early exercise of an American PUT is most attractive when:\nA) The stock price is low, the risk-free rate is high, and volatility is low B) The stock price is high and volatility is high C) The risk-free rate is zero D) The option is far out of the money\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks the conditions favoring early exercise of an American put.\n\nEarly exercise of a put gives you $K$ in cash now, which earns interest. This is most worthwhile when: the put is deep in the money (low stock price, so little upside left to lose), the risk-free rate is high (interest on $K$ received early is valuable), and volatility is low (little chance the stock recovers, so little time value to give up).\n\nWhy A is correct: low $S$, high $r$, low $\\sigma$ together maximize the benefit of receiving $K$ early.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B is the opposite — a high stock price means the put is OTM (no reason to exercise), and high volatility means lots of time value worth keeping.\n- C: a zero rate removes the interest benefit of receiving $K$ early — that discourages early exercise.\n- D: an OTM put has zero intrinsic value, so there's nothing to gain by exercising.\n\nAnalogy: exercising the put is like cashing a guaranteed check early to start earning interest — most attractive when the price can't realistically improve and interest is high.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American puts CAN be exercised early (unlike non-dividend calls). Favorable conditions: deep ITM, high $r$, low $\\sigma$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Early exercise of an American put\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Favors early exercise\",\"items\":[\"low stock price (deep ITM)\",\"high risk-free rate\",\"low volatility\"]},{\"h\":\"Against early exercise\",\"items\":[\"high stock price (OTM)\",\"zero/low rate\",\"high volatility\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** All else equal, an increase in the time to expiration $T$ for an American call:\nA) Always decreases its value B) Has an ambiguous effect, like European options C) Never decreases its value D) Only matters if there are dividends\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the effect of more time on an American option's value.\n\nA longer-dated American option contains every right of the shorter-dated one, plus more time to exercise. So more time can never make it worth less — its value never decreases with $T$. (For European options the effect can be ambiguous because they can only be exercised at the end, and dividends or deep-ITM puts can complicate it.)\n\nWhy C is correct: an American option with more time has strictly more flexibility, so its value never falls with $T$.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is wrong — more time can only help or stay the same for an American option.\n- B describes EUROPEAN options, whose time effect is ambiguous; American options are clear-cut.\n- D is false — the \"never decreases\" rule for American options holds regardless of dividends.\n\nAnalogy: a longer \"anytime\" pass includes all the days of a shorter one plus extra days — it can only be at least as useful.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American option value never decreases with more time. European time effect is ambiguous (marked \"?\" in the factor table).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Effect of more time to expiration\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"American\",\"items\":[\"never decreases value\",\"more time = superset of rights\",\"sign always +\"]},{\"h\":\"European\",\"items\":[\"ambiguous effect\",\"dividends can reverse it\",\"deep-ITM put may prefer shorter\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A 6-month European call on a non-dividend stock trades at $4.00; $$S_0 = \\$50$$, $$K = \\$50$$, $$r = 5\\%$$ continuous. By put-call parity, the European put price is closest to:\nA) $2.16 B) $2.77 C) $4.00 D) $5.23\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0 = 4 + 50(0.9753) - 50 = 4 + 48.7655 - 50 = 2.77$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** An American call on a non-dividend-paying stock is deep in the money with three months left. The best action for a holder who no longer wants exposure is to:\nA) Exercise immediately to capture intrinsic value B) Sell the call in the market C) Exercise and short the stock D) Hold to expiration regardless\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** alive, $$C \\ge S_0 - Ke^{-rT} > S_0 - K$$; selling captures time value that exercise destroys.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which change *decreases* the value of a European put, all else equal?\nA) Higher strike price B) Higher volatility C) Higher risk-free rate D) Higher expected dividends\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** higher $$r$$ lowers the PV of the strike received, hurting puts; A, B, D all raise put values.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** With $$S_0 = \\$37$$, $$K = \\$40$$, $$r = 5\\%$$, $$T = 0.5$$, the lower bound on a European put (non-dividend stock) is closest to:\nA) $1.01 B) $2.01 C) $3.00 D) $0.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$Ke^{-rT} - S_0 = 40(0.9753) - 37 = 39.0124 - 37 = 2.01$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A European call on a non-dividend-paying stock costs $3. The stock trades at $31, the strike is $30, the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 10%, and both options expire in 3 months. By put-call parity, the European put with the same strike and expiry is worth closest to:\nA) $1.00 B) $1.26 C) $2.00 D) $2.26\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0 = 3 + 30e^{-0.10 \\times 0.25} - 31 = 3 + 29.2593 - 31 = \\textbf{\\$1.26}$$. C ($2.00) forgets to discount the strike.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q16.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $20, the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 10%, and a one-year European call struck at $18 is offered at $3.00. The call's no-arbitrage lower bound is closest to:\nA) $2.00, so no arbitrage is available B) $3.71, so an arbitrage exists (buy the call, short the stock, invest the proceeds) C) $3.71, so no arbitrage is available D) $2.00, so an arbitrage exists (sell the call, buy the stock)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Lower bound = $$S_0 - Ke^{-rT} = 20 - 18e^{-0.10} = 20 - 16.287 = \\$3.71$$. The call at $3.00 is below its floor: buy the call, short the stock (+$17 net), invest at 10%; the strategy locks in a riskless profit. A uses the undiscounted intrinsic value $20 − $18 = $2.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Underpriced-call arbitrage\",\"steps\":[\"Lower bound S₀ − Ke^(−rT) = $3.71\",\"Call offered at $3.00 < bound\",\"Buy call, short stock (+$17), invest at 10%\",\"Riskless profit at expiry\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A 6-month European call on a non-dividend stock trades at $5.00. The stock is at $62, the strike is $60, and the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4%. By put-call parity, the price of the 6-month European put with the same strike is *closest* to:\nA) $1.81 B) $3.00 C) $4.19 D) $7.19\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We apply put-call parity for European options on a non-dividend stock to solve for $p$.\n\n1. Parity: $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0$$\n2. Discount the strike: $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.04\\times0.5} = e^{-0.02} = 0.980199$.\n$$Ke^{-rT} = 60 \\times 0.980199 = 58.812$$\n3. Plug in: $$p = 5.00 + 58.812 - 62.00 = 1.812 \\approx \\$1.81$$\n\n- B ($3.00) ignores discounting, using $p = c + K - S_0 = 5 + 60 - 62 = 3$ — the trap of forgetting to PV the strike.\n- C ($4.19) flips the discount sign or adds $S_0$ to the wrong side.\n- D ($7.19) computes $c + S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$ — rearranging parity incorrectly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the layout $c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$: \"call + PV(strike) = put + stock.\" Always discount $K$. Parity holds for **European** options only.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A 3-month European put on a non-dividend stock has $S_0 = \\$44$, $K = \\$48$, and $r = 6\\%$ continuous. The theoretical lower bound on the put price is *closest* to:\nA) $0.00 B) $3.29 C) $4.00 D) $4.72\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We compute the European put lower bound on a non-dividend stock.\n\n1. Lower bound: $$p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} - S_0,\\, 0)$$\n2. Discount the strike: $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.06\\times0.25} = e^{-0.015} = 0.985112$.\n$$Ke^{-rT} = 48 \\times 0.985112 = 47.285$$\n3. Bound: $$\\max(47.285 - 44, 0) = \\max(3.285, 0) = \\$3.29$$\n\n- A ($0.00) wrongly concludes the bound is zero (it is positive here because $Ke^{-rT} > S_0$).\n- C ($4.00) uses the **undiscounted** intrinsic-style value $K - S_0 = 48 - 44 = 4$, forgetting to PV the strike.\n- D ($4.72) discounts $S_0$ instead of $K$, or adds rather than subtracts.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The European **put** lower bound discounts the strike: $Ke^{-rT} - S_0$. The European **call** lower bound is $S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$. The undiscounted $K - S_0$ is a distractor designed to look like intrinsic value.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Consider European options on a non-dividend stock: $c = \\$4.00$, $p = \\$1.00$, $S_0 = \\$48$, $K = \\$45$, $r = 8\\%$ continuous, $T = 0.5$. An arbitrageur sets up the correct riskless trade. The arbitrage profit at expiration is *closest* to:\nA) $1.84 B) $1.76 C) $3.00 D) $0.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We check parity, identify the rich side, build the trade, and compute the certain profit at $T$.\n\n1. Parity check, $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.04} = 0.960789$, so $Ke^{-rT} = 45 \\times 0.960789 = 43.236$.\n   - Call side: $c + Ke^{-rT} = 4.00 + 43.236 = 47.236$.\n   - Put side: $p + S_0 = 1.00 + 48.00 = 49.00$.\n2. The **put-plus-stock side is overpriced** ($49.00 > 47.236$). Buy the cheap side, sell the rich side: **buy the call, short the put, short the stock**.\n3. Net cash inflow at $t=0$: $-4.00 + 1.00 + 48.00 = +\\$45.00$.\n4. Invest at $r$ for 6 months, $e^{0.04} = 1.040811$: $$45.00 \\times 1.040811 = 46.836$$\n5. At $T$ you pay exactly $K = \\$45$ to acquire the stock (via the call if $S_T > 45$, or via assignment on the short put if $S_T < 45$) and close the short.\n6. Riskless profit: $$46.836 - 45.00 = \\$1.836 \\approx \\$1.84$$\n\n- B ($1.76) discounts with simple interest or uses the wrong $e$ factor.\n- C ($3.00) is the static parity gap undiscounted ($49 - 45 - 1$) — ignoring the time value of the invested inflow.\n- D ($0.00) wrongly concludes parity holds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For parity arbitrage: (1) compute both sides, (2) **buy low/sell high** at $t=0$, (3) invest the net inflow at $r$, (4) note you pay exactly $K$ at $T$ in both branches, (5) profit = FV(inflow) − $K$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Put-call parity arbitrage\",\"steps\":[\"Compute both sides: c+Ke^-rT vs p+S0\",\"Buy the cheap side, sell the rich side\",\"Invest the net inflow at r\",\"At T you pay exactly K in both branches\",\"Profit = FV(inflow) - K\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which change, all else equal, *increases* the value of a **European call** while *decreasing* the value of a **European put** on a non-dividend stock?\nA) A higher volatility $\\sigma$ B) A higher risk-free rate $r$ C) A higher strike price $K$ D) A higher expected dividend $D$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We use the six-factor sign table and find the factor whose call effect is $+$ and put effect is $-$.\n\n1. Higher **risk-free rate $r$**: raises the expected growth rate of the stock and lowers the PV of the strike — both help the call ($+$) and both hurt the put ($-$). This matches the requirement.\n\n- A (higher $\\sigma$): raises **both** the call and the put — fails the \"decrease put\" condition.\n- C (higher $K$): **lowers** the call and **raises** the put — opposite signs to what is asked.\n- D (higher $D$): **lowers** the call and **raises** the put — opposite to the requirement.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Only **two** factors split calls vs puts in opposite directions: $S_0$ (call $+$, put $-$) and **$r$** (call $+$, put $-$). Volatility raises both; $K$ and $D$ favor the put.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Six-factor sign table (call vs put)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Call value rises with\",\"items\":[\"stock S0 +\",\"rate r +\",\"volatility +\",\"strike K -\",\"dividends D -\"]},{\"h\":\"Put value rises with\",\"items\":[\"stock S0 -\",\"rate r -\",\"volatility +\",\"strike K +\",\"dividends D +\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** An investor holds an American call on a non-dividend-paying stock that is now deep in the money with three months remaining. The investor no longer wants exposure to the stock. The *best* course of action is to:\nA) Exercise the call immediately to lock in the intrinsic value B) Sell the call in the market C) Exercise the call and simultaneously short the stock D) Hold the call to expiration no matter what\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This tests the no-early-exercise result for American calls on non-dividend stocks.\n\n1. The call is always worth more **alive** than exercised: $$C \\ge c \\ge S_0 - Ke^{-rT} > S_0 - K$$ where $S_0 - K$ is the exercise (intrinsic) value.\n2. Selling captures the full market price = intrinsic **plus remaining time value**; exercising captures only intrinsic value and forfeits interest on $K$ and the insurance value. So **selling dominates exercising**.\n\n- A throws away time value and the time value of $K$.\n- C exercises (capturing only intrinsic) and adds a needless short — strictly worse than selling.\n- D may be fine if the investor wanted exposure, but they do not; selling now realizes value immediately at no cost.\n\n📌 Exam tip: **Never exercise an American call on a non-dividend stock early** — sell it instead. The only early-exercise case for calls is **just before an ex-dividend date** when the captured dividend exceeds the forgone time value and interest.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sell vs exercise (ITM American call, no dividend)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sell the call\",\"items\":[\"intrinsic + remaining time value\",\"keeps insurance value\",\"dominates\"]},{\"h\":\"Exercise early\",\"items\":[\"intrinsic value only\",\"forfeits time value + interest on K\",\"never optimal\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** For an American call and put on a **dividend-paying** stock with present-value dividends $D$, which relationship is the *correct* bound on $C - P$?\nA) $C - P = S_0 + D - Ke^{-rT}$ B) $S_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$ C) $C - P = S_0 - Ke^{-rT} - D$ D) $C - P \\le S_0 - D - K$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This tests the put-call inequality for American options (parity itself does not hold for American options).\n\n1. For European options parity is an equality, but for **American** options on a dividend-paying stock only an inequality bounds $C - P$:\n$$S_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$$\n\n- A and C apply the European **equality** to American options — the classic trap (you may only *bound* $C - P$ for American options).\n- D gives only a one-sided (and mis-signed) relation, dropping the upper bound and the lower-bound structure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Put-call **parity (equality)** = European only. For **American** options you get a two-sided **inequality**: $S_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$. Set $D=0$ for the non-dividend case.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"American C - P bound (dividend stock)\",\"options\":[\"C - P = S0 + D - Ke^-rT\",\"S0 - D - K <= C - P <= S0 - Ke^-rT\",\"C - P = S0 - Ke^-rT - D\",\"C - P <= S0 - D - K\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Parity equality is European-only; for American options C-P is only bounded by a two-sided inequality.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A 1-year European call on a non-dividend stock trades at $3.00 with $S_0 = \\$20$, $K = \\$18$, and $r = 10\\%$ continuous. The arbitrage available and its minimum riskless profit are *best* described as:\nA) Call overpriced; sell the call, buy the stock; profit ≈ $0.71 B) Call underpriced (below its lower bound); buy the call, short the stock; profit ≈ $0.79 C) No arbitrage; the call equals its lower bound D) Call underpriced; buy the call, buy the stock; profit ≈ $3.71\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We compare the call price to its lower bound, and if violated build the arbitrage and compute the guaranteed minimum profit.\n\n1. Lower bound: $S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$, with $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.10} = 0.904837$.\n$$Ke^{-rT} = 18 \\times 0.904837 = 16.287$$\n$$\\text{bound} = 20 - 16.287 = 3.713$$\n2. Market call $3.00 < 3.713$ → the call is **below its lower bound**, i.e., too cheap.\n3. Trade: **buy the call, short the stock**. Inflow at $t=0$: $20 - 3 = \\$17$.\n4. Invest at $r$, $e^{0.10} = 1.105171$: $$17 \\times 1.105171 = 18.788$$\n5. At $T$: if $S_T > 18$, exercise — pay $18 to buy back the stock; profit $= 18.788 - 18 = \\$0.788$. If $S_T \\le 18$, let the call lapse and buy the stock at $S_T \\le 18$; profit $\\ge 0.788$.\n6. Minimum riskless profit $\\approx \\$0.79$.\n\n- A reverses the direction (the call is under-, not over-priced).\n- C is wrong: $3.00 \\ne 3.713$, so the bound is violated.\n- D has the right diagnosis but the wrong trade (you short, not buy, the stock) and an inflated profit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A call below $S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$ is too cheap → **buy the call, short the stock**, invest the inflow, and you net at least FV(inflow) − $K$. Always verify both terminal branches give a profit at least as large.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Call below lower bound arbitrage\",\"steps\":[\"Compute lower bound S0 - Ke^-rT\",\"If price < bound, call is too cheap\",\"Buy the call, short the stock\",\"Invest the inflow (S0 - c) at r\",\"At T net at least FV(inflow) - K\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Regarding the effect of time to expiration $T$ on option value, which statement is *most* accurate?\nA) Both European and American options always increase in value as $T$ rises. B) American options never decrease in value as $T$ rises, but the effect of $T$ on European options is ambiguous. C) European options always increase in value as $T$ rises, but American options are ambiguous. D) Time to expiration has no effect on a deep-in-the-money option.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** This tests the sign of the time-to-expiration factor across option styles.\n\n1. A longer-dated **American** option contains every right of the shorter one, so it can never be worth less — $T$ has a non-negative effect (the table shows $+$ for American calls and puts).\n2. For **European** options the effect is **ambiguous**: a dividend between two maturities can make a shorter-dated European call worth more, and a deep-ITM European put may prefer a shorter wait for its payoff.\n\n- A is wrong because European options are ambiguous in $T$.\n- C reverses which style is ambiguous.\n- D is false: time value affects option value at all moneyness levels (and the deep-ITM European put example shows $T$ can even reduce value).\n\n📌 Exam tip: $T$ is the one factor where European and American differ in *certainty*: **American = always $+$** (longer = superset of rights); **European = ambiguous** (dividends and deep-ITM puts can reverse it).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Effect of time to expiration T\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"American\",\"items\":[\"never decreases value\",\"longer = superset of rights\",\"sign always +\"]},{\"h\":\"European\",\"items\":[\"ambiguous\",\"dividends can reverse\",\"deep-ITM put may fall with T\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"314","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Trading Strategies","notes":"# 14. Trading Strategies\n\nGARP tests strategy construction (which options, which strikes, long or short), the resulting payoff/profit diagrams, and the numbers: max profit, max loss, and breakevens. Know which strategies bet on direction and which bet on volatility.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the motivation for, and payoff of, a covered call and a protective put, and relate them to put-call parity.\n- Describe principal-protected notes and the conditions needed to create them.\n- Describe the construction, uses, and payoff functions of spread strategies: bull, bear, box, butterfly, calendar, and diagonal spreads.\n- Describe the construction, uses, and payoff functions of combination strategies: straddles, strangles, strips, straps, and collars.\n- Compute payoff, profit, maximum profit, maximum loss, and breakeven for each strategy.\n\n## Option plus stock\n\n- **Covered call**: long stock + short call. Earns the premium; gives up upside above $$K$$. By put-call parity, $$S_0 - c = Ke^{-rT} - p$$, so the profit profile looks like a **short put**.\n- **Protective put**: long stock + long put. Insurance against a fall; profile looks like a **long call** (again via parity: $$S_0 + p = c + Ke^{-rT}$$).\n\nThe parity link is the exam point: stock plus one option always replicates the *opposite* position in the other option, plus cash.\n\n## Principal-protected notes\n\nA PPN packages a **zero-coupon bond plus a call option** so the investor gets the principal back at maturity plus equity upside. With principal of $100, 3 years, and $$r = 4\\%$$ continuous, the zero costs $$100e^{-0.12} = 100(0.8869) = \\$88.69$$, leaving $11.31 to buy the call. PPNs are **hard to create when interest rates are low** (the zero absorbs nearly all of the $100, leaving little for the option) **or when volatility is high** (the call is expensive). Issuers cheapen the embedded option via higher strikes, caps, averaging, or knock-outs.\n\n## Spreads (same type of option)\n\nLet $$K_1 < K_2 < K_3$$. \"Debit\" = pay net premium up front; \"credit\" = receive it.\n\n| Spread | Construction | Debit/credit | Max profit | Max loss | Breakeven |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull call | Buy call $$K_1$$, sell call $$K_2$$ | Debit $$D$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - D$$ | $$D$$ | $$K_1 + D$$ |\n| Bull put | Buy put $$K_1$$, sell put $$K_2$$ | Credit $$C$$ | $$C$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - C$$ | $$K_2 - C$$ |\n| Bear call | Sell call $$K_1$$, buy call $$K_2$$ | Credit $$C$$ | $$C$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - C$$ | $$K_1 + C$$ |\n| Bear put | Buy put $$K_2$$, sell put $$K_1$$ | Debit $$D$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - D$$ | $$D$$ | $$K_2 - D$$ |\n| Long call butterfly | Buy calls $$K_1, K_3$$; sell 2 calls $$K_2$$ | Debit $$D$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - D$$ at $$S_T = K_2$$ | $$D$$ | $$K_1 + D$$ and $$K_3 - D$$ |\n\nGeneric bull call spread payoff:\n\n| Range | Long $$K_1$$ call | Short $$K_2$$ call | Total payoff |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $$S_T \\le K_1$$ | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| $$K_1 < S_T < K_2$$ | $$S_T - K_1$$ | 0 | $$S_T - K_1$$ |\n| $$S_T \\ge K_2$$ | $$S_T - K_1$$ | $$-(S_T - K_2)$$ | $$K_2 - K_1$$ |\n\n- **Box spread** = bull call spread + bear put spread on the same $$K_1, K_2$$. Payoff is **always $$K_2 - K_1$$**, so its fair value is the PV, $$(K_2 - K_1)e^{-rT}$$. Any other price is an arbitrage — but only with **European** options (American early exercise breaks the box).\n- **Butterfly**: bets that $$S_T$$ stays **near $$K_2$$** (short volatility) with small, capped risk either way.\n- **Calendar spread**: sell a short-dated call, buy a long-dated call, **same strike**. Profits if $$S_T$$ is close to $$K$$ at the *short* maturity (the short option dies worthless while the long option keeps time value). Loses if the stock moves far either way.\n- **Diagonal spread**: strikes *and* maturities both differ — a hybrid of a price spread and a calendar spread.\n\n## Combinations (calls and puts together)\n\n- **Straddle (bottom/long)**: buy a call and a put, same $$K$$ and $$T$$. Bets on a **big move in either direction** (long volatility). Breakevens at $$K \\pm$$ total premium.\n- **Strangle**: buy put at $$K_1$$ and call at $$K_2 > K_1$$. Cheaper than a straddle but needs a **bigger** move.\n- **Strip**: **1 call + 2 puts** (same $$K, T$$). Big move expected, **down more likely**.\n- **Strap**: **2 calls + 1 put**. Big move expected, **up more likely**.\n- **Collar (zero-cost)**: long stock + long put $$K_1$$ + short call $$K_2$$, strikes chosen so the call premium pays for the put. Locks profit into the band $$[K_1, K_2]$$ for zero outlay.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> **Payoff vs. profit** is the most common point-loser: profit = payoff − net premium paid (or + net premium received). The wrong answer choice is almost always the right number give or take exactly the net premium — read which quantity is asked. Second trap: **strip vs. strap** — strip = 2 **p**uts (pessimistic tilt, down), strap = 2 calls (up).\n\n### Worked example: bear put spread\n\nBuy a 50-strike put for $5 and sell a 45-strike put for $2 (same expiry). Net debit $$D = 5 - 2 = \\$3$$.\n\n| $$S_T$$ | Long 50-put payoff | Short 45-put payoff | Total payoff | Profit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 40 | 10 | −5 | 5 | **+2** |\n| 45 | 5 | 0 | 5 | **+2** |\n| 47 | 3 | 0 | 3 | **0** |\n| 48 | 2 | 0 | 2 | −1 |\n| 50 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **−3** |\n| 55 | 0 | 0 | 0 | −3 |\n\n- Max profit $$= (K_2 - K_1) - D = (50 - 45) - 3 = \\$2$$, earned for all $$S_T \\le 45$$.\n- Max loss $$= D = \\$3$$, for all $$S_T \\ge 50$$.\n- Breakeven $$= K_2 - D = 50 - 3 = \\$47$$.\n\n### Worked example: long straddle\n\nBuy a 100-strike call for $6 and a 100-strike put for $4. Total premium $$= \\$10$$.\n\n| $$S_T$$ | Call payoff | Put payoff | Total payoff | Profit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 80 | 0 | 20 | 20 | **+10** |\n| 90 | 0 | 10 | 10 | **0** |\n| 100 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **−10** |\n| 110 | 10 | 0 | 10 | **0** |\n| 120 | 20 | 0 | 20 | +10 |\n\n- Breakevens: $$K - 10 = 90$$ and $$K + 10 = 110$$.\n- Max loss $$= \\$10$$ at exactly $$S_T = 100$$; profit is unlimited above 110 and grows to $$+90$$ as $$S_T \\to 0$$.\n\n## Master summary table\n\n| Strategy | Construction | View | Max profit | Max loss |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Covered call | Long stock + short call | Mildly bullish, sell upside | $$K - S_0 + c$$ | $$S_0 - c$$ (stock to 0) |\n| Protective put | Long stock + long put | Bullish, want insurance | Unlimited | $$S_0 - K + p$$ |\n| Bull call spread | +call $$K_1$$, −call $$K_2$$ | Moderately bullish | $$K_2 - K_1 - D$$ | $$D$$ |\n| Bull put spread | +put $$K_1$$, −put $$K_2$$ | Moderately bullish | $$C$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - C$$ |\n| Bear call spread | −call $$K_1$$, +call $$K_2$$ | Moderately bearish | $$C$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - C$$ |\n| Bear put spread | +put $$K_2$$, −put $$K_1$$ | Moderately bearish | $$K_2 - K_1 - D$$ | $$D$$ |\n| Box spread | Bull call + bear put, same strikes | Pure interest-rate play | $$K_2 - K_1$$ certain | None if bought at PV |\n| Long butterfly | +$$K_1$$, −2$$K_2$$, +$$K_3$$ calls | Low volatility near $$K_2$$ | $$K_2 - K_1 - D$$ | $$D$$ |\n| Calendar spread | −short-dated, +long-dated, same $$K$$ | $$S_T \\approx K$$ at short expiry | Limited | Net debit |\n| Long straddle | +call, +put, same $$K$$ | Big move, either way | Unlimited | Total premium |\n| Long strangle | +put $$K_1$$, +call $$K_2$$ | Bigger move, either way | Unlimited | Total premium |\n| Strip | +1 call, +2 puts | Big move, down likelier | Large (down) | Total premium |\n| Strap | +2 calls, +1 put | Big move, up likelier | Unlimited (up) | Total premium |\n| Collar | Stock + put $$K_1$$ − call $$K_2$$ | Lock in a band, zero cost | Capped at $$K_2$$ | Floored at $$K_1$$ |","flashcards":"**Q1.** Covered call: construction, motivation, and parity link\n> **A:** Long stock + short call. Earns the premium, gives up upside above $$K$$ (mildly bullish). By put-call parity $$S_0 - c = Ke^{-rT} - p$$, so its profit profile looks like a SHORT PUT.\n\n**Q2.** Protective put: construction, motivation, and parity link\n> **A:** Long stock + long put. Insurance against a fall (bullish, wants downside protection). By parity $$S_0 + p = c + Ke^{-rT}$$, so its profile looks like a LONG CALL.\n\n**Q3.** Principal-protected note (PPN) construction and conditions\n> **A:** A zero-coupon bond + a call option, returning principal at maturity plus equity upside. Hard to create when interest rates are LOW (the zero absorbs nearly all principal, little left for the option) or volatility is HIGH (the call is expensive). Issuers cheapen the option via higher strikes, caps, averaging, or knock-outs.\n\n**Q4.** Bull spreads: construction and key numbers ($$K_1 < K_2$$)\n> **A:** Bull call (debit $$D$$): buy call $$K_1$$, sell call $$K_2$$; max profit $$K_2-K_1-D$$, max loss $$D$$, breakeven $$K_1+D$$. Bull put (credit $$C$$): buy put $$K_1$$, sell put $$K_2$$; max profit $$C$$, max loss $$K_2-K_1-C$$, breakeven $$K_2-C$$.\n\n**Q5.** Bear spreads: construction and key numbers ($$K_1 < K_2$$)\n> **A:** Bear call (credit $$C$$): sell call $$K_1$$, buy call $$K_2$$; max profit $$C$$, max loss $$K_2-K_1-C$$, breakeven $$K_1+C$$. Bear put (debit $$D$$): buy put $$K_2$$, sell put $$K_1$$; max profit $$K_2-K_1-D$$, max loss $$D$$, breakeven $$K_2-D$$.\n\n**Q6.** Box spread and its no-arbitrage value\n> **A:** Bull call spread + bear put spread on the same $$K_1, K_2$$. Payoff is always $$K_2-K_1$$, so fair value = PV = $$(K_2-K_1)e^{-rT}$$ — a pure interest-rate play. Any other price is arbitrage, but only with EUROPEAN options (American early exercise breaks the box).\n\n**Q7.** Long butterfly: construction and view\n> **A:** Buy calls $$K_1$$ and $$K_3$$, sell 2 calls $$K_2$$ (debit $$D$$). Bets $$S_T$$ stays near $$K_2$$ (short volatility), capped risk either way. Max profit $$K_2-K_1-D$$ at $$S_T=K_2$$; max loss $$D$$; breakevens $$K_1+D$$ and $$K_3-D$$.\n\n**Q8.** Calendar vs diagonal spreads\n> **A:** Calendar: sell a short-dated call, buy a long-dated call, SAME strike. Profits if $$S_T \\approx K$$ at the short maturity (short option dies, long keeps time value); loses on a big move either way. Diagonal: strikes AND maturities both differ — a hybrid.\n\n**Q9.** Straddle vs strangle\n> **A:** Long straddle: buy a call and a put, same $$K$$ and $$T$$ — bets on a big move either direction (long volatility), breakevens at $$K \\pm$$ total premium. Strangle: buy put $$K_1$$ and call $$K_2 > K_1$$ — cheaper than a straddle but needs a bigger move.\n\n**Q10.** Strip vs strap\n> **A:** Strip = 1 call + 2 puts (big move expected, DOWN more likely). Strap = 2 calls + 1 put (big move expected, UP more likely). Same $$K, T$$.\n\n**Q11.** Zero-cost collar\n> **A:** Long stock + long put $$K_1$$ + short call $$K_2$$, with strikes chosen so the call premium pays for the put. Locks profit into the band $$[K_1, K_2]$$ for zero outlay; capped at $$K_2$$, floored at $$K_1$$.\n\n**Q12.** Trap: payoff vs profit\n> **A:** Profit = payoff − net premium paid (or + net premium received). The wrong answer choice is almost always the right number off by exactly the net premium — read which quantity is asked. Also watch strip (2 puts, down) vs strap (2 calls, up).\n\n**Q13.** Worked: bear put spread numbers\n> **A:** Buy 50-put for $5, sell 45-put for $2; net debit $$D=\\$3$$. Max profit $$=(50-45)-3=\\$2$$ for $$S_T \\le 45$$. Max loss $$=D=\\$3$$ for $$S_T \\ge 50$$. Breakeven $$=50-3=\\$47$$.\n\n**Q14.** Worked: long straddle numbers\n> **A:** Buy 100-call for $6 and 100-put for $4; total premium $10. Breakevens at $$100-10=90$$ and $$100+10=110$$. Max loss $10 at exactly $$S_T=100$$; profit unlimited above 110 and grows to $$+90$$ as $$S_T \\to 0$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** An investor buys a 30-strike call for $3 and sells a 35-strike call for $1 (same expiry). This is a bull call spread. The maximum profit is:\nA) $2 B) $3 C) $5 D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the maximum profit of a bull call spread.\n\nStep 1 — Net cost (debit):\n$$D = 3 - 1 = \\$2$$\n\nStep 2 — Maximum payoff occurs when the stock is at or above the higher strike ($K_2 = 35$): the spread pays $K_2 - K_1 = 35 - 30 = \\$5$.\n\nStep 3 — Maximum profit = max payoff − debit:\n$$5 - 2 = \\$3$$\n\nWhy B is correct: max profit $= (K_2 - K_1) - D = 5 - 2 = \\$3$.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($2) is the net debit (the max LOSS), not the max profit.\n- C ($5) is the gross payoff before subtracting the $2 you paid.\n- D (Unlimited) describes a plain long call; the short 35-call caps your upside.\n\nAnalogy: you buy a cheap upside bet and sell away the part above $35 to lower your cost — your winnings are capped at the $5 spread minus what you paid.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bull call spread: max profit = $(K_2 - K_1) - D$; max loss = $D$; breakeven = $K_1 + D$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bull call spread (debit)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Formulas\",\"items\":[\"max profit = (K2-K1) - D\",\"max loss = D (net debit)\",\"breakeven = K1 + D\"]},{\"h\":\"This example\",\"items\":[\"debit = 3 - 1 = $2\",\"max profit = 5 - 2 = $3\",\"max loss = $2, BE = $32\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A box spread with strikes 50 and 55 expires in 1 year; $r = 5\\%$ (continuous). Its no-arbitrage price is closest to:\nA) $4.52 B) $4.76 C) $5.00 D) $5.26\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the fair value of a box spread.\n\nA box spread (bull call spread + bear put spread on the same two strikes) always pays exactly $K_2 - K_1$ at expiry, no matter where the stock ends up. So its fair value is the present value of that certain payoff:\n$$\\text{Price} = (K_2 - K_1)e^{-rT}$$\n\nCompute:\n- $K_2 - K_1 = 55 - 50 = 5$\n- $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.05 \\times 1} = e^{-0.05} = 0.95123$\n- Price $= 5 \\times 0.95123 = \\$4.76$\n\nWhy B is correct: the box pays a guaranteed $5, so today it's worth the discounted $5 = $4.76.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($4.52) over-discounts (e.g., using a higher rate or two periods).\n- C ($5.00) forgets to discount — that's the future payoff, not today's value.\n- D ($5.26) discounts the wrong way (multiplies by $e^{+rT}$ instead of $e^{-rT}$).\n\nAnalogy: a box spread is a synthetic zero-coupon bond paying $5 in one year — its price today is just the discounted $5.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Box spread = guaranteed $K_2 - K_1$, so fair value = $(K_2 - K_1)e^{-rT}$. Works only with European options.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Box spread = synthetic zero-coupon bond\",\"steps\":[\"Bull call spread + bear put spread, same two strikes\",\"Pays K2-K1 with CERTAINTY at T\",\"Fair value = (K2-K1) x e^-rT\",\"Here: 5 x e^-0.05 = $4.76\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A trader expects a large price move and believes a decline is more likely than a rise. The best-suited strategy is a:\nA) Strap B) Strip C) Bull put spread D) Calendar spread\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which strategy fits \"big move, downside more likely.\"\n\nA strip is 1 call + 2 puts (same strike and expiry). It profits from a big move either way, but with double weight on puts it pays more on a DOWN move — perfect when a decline is judged more likely.\n\nWhy B is correct: strip = 2 puts + 1 call → bet on a big move tilted to the downside.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (strap = 2 calls + 1 put) tilts to the UPSIDE — the opposite tilt.\n- C (bull put spread) is a moderately BULLISH, limited-move strategy, not a big-move bet.\n- D (calendar spread) profits when the stock stays NEAR the strike (low movement) — the opposite of expecting a big move.\n\nAnalogy: \"stri-P\" has a P for \"Pessimistic\" (down). \"Stra-P\"... think \"uP.\" The extra puts (strip) lean down.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Strip = 2 puts (down-tilted); Strap = 2 calls (up-tilted). Both bet on a big move in either direction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Strip vs strap (big-move bets)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Strip\",\"items\":[\"1 call + 2 PUTS\",\"down-tilted\",\"big move, decline more likely\"]},{\"h\":\"Strap\",\"items\":[\"2 CALLS + 1 put\",\"up-tilted\",\"big move, rise more likely\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The profit diagram of a covered call (long stock + short call) most resembles that of a:\nA) Long call B) Long put C) Short put D) Long straddle\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question links the covered call to an equivalent single-option profile using put-call parity.\n\nBy put-call parity, long stock + short call has the same profit shape as a short put (plus cash). The covered call earns a limited gain (the premium plus a bit of upside to the strike) and loses heavily if the stock falls — just like writing a put.\n\nWhy C is correct: parity gives long stock − call = short put + cash, so the profiles match.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (long call) has limited downside and unlimited upside — the opposite shape of a covered call.\n- B (long put) profits when the stock falls — a covered call loses when the stock falls.\n- D (long straddle) profits from big moves either way; a covered call doesn't.\n\nAnalogy: writing a covered call is like being an insurer who collects a premium but is on the hook if things go badly — exactly the payoff of selling a put.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Covered call ≈ short put. Protective put ≈ long call. These come straight from put-call parity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Parity equivalences\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Covered call\",\"items\":[\"long stock + short call\",\"= short put + cash\",\"capped gain, big downside\"]},{\"h\":\"Protective put\",\"items\":[\"long stock + long put\",\"= long call + cash\",\"floored downside, open upside\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A bear put spread is built by buying a 50-strike put for $5 and selling a 45-strike put for $2 (same expiry). The breakeven stock price at expiration is:\nA) $42 B) $45 C) $47 D) $50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the breakeven of a bear put spread.\n\nNet debit:\n$$D = 5 - 2 = \\$3$$\n\nFor a bear put spread, breakeven = higher strike − debit:\n$$K_2 - D = 50 - 3 = \\$47$$\n\nCheck: at $S_T = 47$, the long 50-put pays $50 - 47 = 3$, the short 45-put pays 0, total payoff $= 3$, minus the $3 debit = $0 profit. Confirmed.\n\nWhy C is correct: breakeven is where the spread's payoff just covers the $3 you paid, at $47.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($42) is below the lower strike, where you're already at max profit, not breakeven.\n- B ($45) is the lower strike, where payoff = $5 (max profit), not breakeven.\n- D ($50) is the higher strike, where the payoff is $0 and you're at max loss (−$3), not breakeven.\n\nAnalogy: you need the stock to fall just $3 below $50 to recover your $3 cost — breakeven at $47.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bear put spread: max profit = $(K_2 - K_1) - D$; max loss = $D$; breakeven = $K_2 - D$.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A long straddle is created by buying a 100-strike call for $6 and a 100-strike put for $4. The two breakeven stock prices at expiration are:\nA) $94 and $104 B) $90 and $110 C) $96 and $106 D) $100 only\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the breakevens of a long straddle.\n\nTotal premium paid:\n$$6 + 4 = \\$10$$\n\nA straddle breaks even when the move (up or down from the strike) equals the total premium:\n- Lower breakeven: $K - \\text{premium} = 100 - 10 = \\$90$\n- Upper breakeven: $K + \\text{premium} = 100 + 10 = \\$110$\n\nWhy B is correct: breakevens are $K \\pm$ total premium = $90 and $110.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($94/$104) uses ±$6 or ±$4 (one premium) instead of the total $10.\n- C ($96/$106) uses ±$4 / ±$6 inconsistently — still not the total premium.\n- D ($100 only) is the strike, where the straddle has its MAXIMUM LOSS (−$10), not breakeven.\n\nAnalogy: you paid $10 for the bet, so the stock must move at least $10 in either direction before you start profiting — break even at $90 and $110.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long straddle breakevens = $K \\pm$ (total premium). Max loss = total premium, at $S_T = K$.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A protective put combines long stock with a long put. Its profit profile most resembles a:\nA) Long call B) Short call C) Short put D) Short straddle\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question links the protective put to a single-option equivalent via parity.\n\nBy put-call parity, long stock + long put = long call + cash. The protective put has limited downside (the put floors your loss) and keeps unlimited upside (the stock can rise) — exactly the shape of a long call.\n\nWhy A is correct: parity gives stock + put = call + cash, so the profit shape matches a long call.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B (short call) has capped upside and unlimited downside — the opposite of a protective put.\n- C (short put) loses big on the downside; a protective put is PROTECTED on the downside.\n- D (short straddle) loses on big moves either way; a protective put gains on big up-moves.\n\nAnalogy: buying insurance on your stock (the put) caps your losses but leaves your gains open — just like owning a call.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Protective put ≈ long call (downside protected, upside open). Covered call ≈ short put.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Protective put resembles which position?\",\"options\":[\"Long call\",\"Short call\",\"Short put\",\"Short straddle\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Parity: long stock + long put = long call + cash. Downside floored, upside open — the long-call shape.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A principal-protected note (PPN) is hardest to create profitably when:\nA) Interest rates are high and volatility is low B) Interest rates are low and/or volatility is high C) The stock pays no dividends D) The note has a very short maturity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks the conditions that make PPNs difficult to build.\n\nA PPN = a zero-coupon bond (to return the principal) + a call option (for upside). When interest rates are LOW, the zero-coupon bond costs almost the full principal, leaving little money to buy the option. When volatility is HIGH, the call option is expensive. Either way, there's not enough left over for meaningful upside.\n\nWhy B is correct: low rates (expensive zero) and/or high volatility (expensive option) leave too little budget for the upside option.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the opposite — high rates make the zero cheap (more budget for the option), and low volatility makes the option cheap. That's the EASIEST case.\n- C: dividends affect option pricing somewhat, but the binding constraints are rates and volatility.\n- D: a short maturity doesn't make PPNs hard per se; the rate/volatility budget squeeze is the key issue.\n\nAnalogy: your fixed budget must first buy a \"safety bond.\" If that safety eats almost all the money (low rates) or the fun \"upside ticket\" is pricey (high volatility), you can't afford much upside.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PPN = zero-coupon bond + call. Low rates and high volatility both shrink the option budget, making PPNs hard to create.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"PPN = zero-coupon bond + call\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Easy to build\",\"items\":[\"high rates: cheap zero\",\"low volatility: cheap option\",\"big budget for upside\"]},{\"h\":\"Hard to build\",\"items\":[\"low rates: expensive zero\",\"high volatility: expensive call\",\"little left for upside\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which strategy is essentially a bet that volatility will be LOW and the stock will stay near a central strike $K_2$?\nA) Long straddle B) Long strangle C) Long butterfly D) Strip\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which strategy profits when the stock stays put (low volatility).\n\nA long butterfly (buy calls at $K_1$ and $K_3$, sell 2 calls at $K_2$) makes its maximum profit when the stock ends right at the middle strike $K_2$. It is a \"short volatility\" bet with small, capped risk in either direction.\n\nWhy C is correct: the butterfly pays the most if the stock stays near $K_2$ — a low-volatility bet.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (long straddle) profits from a BIG move either way — that's LONG volatility, the opposite.\n- B (long strangle) also bets on a big move — long volatility.\n- D (strip) bets on a big move (down-tilted) — long volatility.\n\nAnalogy: a butterfly is like betting the dart lands dead center on the board — you win most if nothing much moves.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Butterfly = low-volatility bet (stock stays near the middle strike). Straddle/strangle/strip/strap = high-volatility bets.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Volatility view of strategies\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Low-volatility (stock stays put)\",\"items\":[\"long butterfly\",\"calendar spread\",\"short straddle/strangle\"]},{\"h\":\"High-volatility (big move)\",\"items\":[\"long straddle\",\"long strangle\",\"strip / strap\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A zero-cost collar on a stock position is built by:\nA) Buying a call and buying a put at the same strike B) Holding the stock, buying a protective put at $K_1$, and selling a call at $K_2$ so the premiums net to zero C) Buying two puts and selling one call D) Selling the stock and buying a bond\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how a zero-cost collar is constructed.\n\nA collar protects a long stock position by buying a downside put (strike $K_1$, the floor) and financing it by selling an upside call (strike $K_2$, the cap). When the strikes are chosen so the call premium received equals the put premium paid, the collar costs nothing up front — hence \"zero-cost.\" Your outcome is locked into the band $[K_1, K_2]$.\n\nWhy B is correct: long stock + long put $K_1$ + short call $K_2$, premiums netting to zero, locks profit into a band.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (buy call + buy put, same strike) is a long straddle, not a collar.\n- C (2 puts + 1 call) is a strip, a volatility bet, not a protective collar.\n- D (sell stock + buy bond) just exits the position; it's not a collar.\n\nAnalogy: a collar puts a floor (put) under your losses and a ceiling (call) on your gains, with the ceiling \"paying for\" the floor — so it's free.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Zero-cost collar = long stock + long put ($K_1$ floor) + short call ($K_2$ cap), premiums net to zero. Locks the outcome into $[K_1, K_2]$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Zero-cost collar\",\"steps\":[\"Hold the long stock\",\"Buy a put at K1 (downside floor)\",\"Sell a call at K2 (upside cap)\",\"Choose strikes so premiums net to zero\",\"Outcome locked into the band [K1, K2]\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** An investor buys a 30-strike call for $3 and sells a 35-strike call for $1. The maximum profit is:\nA) $2 B) $3 C) $5 D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** net debit $$= 3 - 1 = 2$$; max profit $$= (35 - 30) - 2 = \\$3$$ (max payoff 5 minus debit 2).\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A box spread with strikes 50 and 55 expires in 1 year; $$r = 5\\%$$ continuous. Its no-arbitrage price is closest to:\nA) $5.00 B) $4.76 C) $5.26 D) $4.52\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$(55 - 50)e^{-0.05} = 5(0.9512) = \\$4.76$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A trader expects a large price move and believes a decline is more likely than a rise. The best-suited strategy is a:\nA) Strap B) Strip C) Bull put spread D) Calendar spread\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** strip = 1 call + 2 puts: profits from a big move, with double weight on the downside.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The profit diagram of a covered call most resembles that of a:\nA) Long put B) Short put C) Long call D) Short straddle\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** by put-call parity, long stock + short call = short put + cash.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** An investor buys a straddle: a call and a put both struck at $50, costing $4 and $3 respectively. The breakeven stock prices at expiry are:\nA) $43 and $57 B) $46 and $54 C) $47 and $53 D) $46 and $57\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Total premium = 4 + 3 = $7, so breakevens are $$50 \\pm 7$$ = **$43 and $57**. B and C use one premium only.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** An investor buys a call with strike $30 for $3 and writes a call with strike $35 for $1 (same expiry, same stock). If the stock finishes at $38, the strategy's total profit is:\nA) $2 B) $3 C) $5 D) $8\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** At 38, both calls finish in the money: payoff = (38 − 30) − (38 − 35) = 8 − 3 = $5 — the spread's $5 cap. Net cost = 3 − 1 = $2. Profit = 5 − 2 = **$3**. C forgets the premium; D ignores the short leg.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bull call spread P/L at S=38\",\"steps\":[\"Long 30 call payoff = +8\",\"Short 35 call payoff = −3\",\"Spread payoff = $5 (capped)\",\"Net cost 3−1 = $2 → profit $3\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** An investor constructs a bull call spread: buy a 40-strike call for $4.00 and sell a 45-strike call for $1.50 (same expiry). The maximum profit, maximum loss, and breakeven are *closest* to:\nA) Max profit $2.50; max loss $2.50; breakeven $42.50 B) Max profit $5.00; max loss $2.50; breakeven $42.50 C) Max profit $2.50; max loss $5.00; breakeven $44.50 D) Max profit unlimited; max loss $2.50; breakeven $44.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We have a debit bull call spread with $K_1 = 40$, $K_2 = 45$. Compute the net debit, then max profit, max loss, and breakeven.\n\n1. Net debit: $$D = 4.00 - 1.50 = \\$2.50$$\n2. Maximum profit (stock above $K_2$, both calls in the money): $$(K_2 - K_1) - D = (45 - 40) - 2.50 = 5 - 2.50 = \\$2.50$$\n3. Maximum loss (stock below $K_1$, both expire worthless): the net debit $= \\$2.50$.\n4. Breakeven (long call covers the debit): $$K_1 + D = 40 + 2.50 = \\$42.50$$\n\n- B uses the gross spread width $(K_2 - K_1) = 5$ as the max profit, forgetting to subtract the debit.\n- C swaps max profit and max loss, and uses $K_2 - D$ for the breakeven (a bear-put formula).\n- D treats it like a naked long call (unlimited upside) — the short $K_2$ call caps the profit.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Debit vertical spreads → max profit = (strike width − debit), max loss = debit, breakeven = lower strike + debit (for calls). The width caps the payoff at $K_2 - K_1$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bull call vs bear put spread (debit)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bull call spread\",\"items\":[\"max profit = (K2-K1) - D\",\"max loss = D\",\"breakeven = LOWER K + D\"]},{\"h\":\"Bear put spread\",\"items\":[\"max profit = (K2-K1) - D\",\"max loss = D\",\"breakeven = HIGHER K - D\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A box spread is built from European options with strikes $K_1 = 60$ and $K_2 = 70$, expiring in 2 years; the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 5%. Its no-arbitrage price today is *closest* to:\nA) $9.05 B) $9.51 C) $10.00 D) $10.52\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A box spread (bull call spread + bear put spread on the same strikes) pays $K_2 - K_1$ with certainty at $T$, so its fair price is the present value of that fixed payoff.\n\n1. Certain payoff at $T$: $$K_2 - K_1 = 70 - 60 = \\$10$$\n2. Discount at the risk-free rate: $e^{-rT} = e^{-0.05\\times2} = e^{-0.10} = 0.904837$.\n3. Fair price: $$(K_2 - K_1)e^{-rT} = 10 \\times 0.904837 = \\$9.05$$\n\n- B ($9.51) uses $T = 1$ ($e^{-0.05} = 0.9512$) instead of $T = 2$.\n- C ($10.00) forgets to discount — quotes the undiscounted certain payoff.\n- D ($10.52) discounts with the wrong sign ($e^{+0.05}$), inflating the price.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A box's payoff is the **riskless** amount $K_2 - K_1$; its value is its PV $(K_2-K_1)e^{-rT}$. Any market price away from PV is arbitrage — but only with **European** options (American early exercise breaks the box).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Box spread valuation\",\"steps\":[\"Box = bull call spread + bear put spread, same strikes\",\"Pays K2-K1 with CERTAINTY at T\",\"Value = (K2-K1) x e^-rT\",\"Here: 10 x e^-0.10 = $9.05\",\"European only — early exercise breaks it\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A trader buys a 80-strike call for $7.00 and a 80-strike put for $5.00 (same expiry). At expiration the stock is at $60. The trader's profit is *closest* to, and the position is best described as a:\nA) Profit $8.00; long straddle B) Profit $20.00; long straddle C) Profit $8.00; long strangle D) Profit −$12.00; short straddle\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A call and a put at the same strike and expiry is a **long straddle**. Compute the total premium and the profit at $S_T = 60$.\n\n1. Total premium paid: $$7.00 + 5.00 = \\$12.00$$\n2. At $S_T = 60$: call payoff $= \\max(60 - 80, 0) = 0$; put payoff $= \\max(80 - 60, 0) = \\$20.00$.\n3. Total payoff $= 0 + 20 = \\$20.00$.\n4. Profit $= \\text{payoff} - \\text{premium} = 20.00 - 12.00 = \\$8.00$.\n5. Same strike for both legs → **long straddle** (a long volatility bet).\n\n- B reports the **payoff** ($20) as the profit, forgetting to subtract the $12 premium.\n- C mislabels it a strangle (a strangle uses **different** strikes for the call and put).\n- D has the wrong sign/structure — the trader is long, not short, and is profitable here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Straddle = same strike; strangle = different strikes. Profit = payoff − **total** premium. Breakevens of this straddle are $K \\pm \\text{premium} = 80 \\pm 12 = 68$ and $92$; at $S_T = 60$ (below 68) the position profits.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Straddle vs strangle\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Straddle\",\"items\":[\"call + put, SAME strike\",\"this question\",\"breakevens K +/- premium\"]},{\"h\":\"Strangle\",\"items\":[\"call + put, DIFFERENT strikes\",\"wider, cheaper\",\"needs bigger move to profit\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A bear put spread is built by buying a 55-strike put for $6.00 and selling a 50-strike put for $2.50 (same expiry). The maximum profit, maximum loss, and breakeven are *closest* to:\nA) Max profit $1.50; max loss $3.50; breakeven $51.50 B) Max profit $3.50; max loss $1.50; breakeven $53.50 C) Max profit $5.00; max loss $3.50; breakeven $51.50 D) Max profit $1.50; max loss unlimited; breakeven $48.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A bear put spread buys the higher-strike put ($K_2 = 55$) and sells the lower-strike put ($K_1 = 50$); it is a debit spread.\n\n1. Net debit: $$D = 6.00 - 2.50 = \\$3.50$$\n2. Maximum profit (stock below $K_1 = 50$, both puts in the money): $$(K_2 - K_1) - D = (55 - 50) - 3.50 = 5 - 3.50 = \\$1.50$$\n3. Maximum loss (stock above $K_2 = 55$, both expire worthless): the net debit $= \\$3.50$.\n4. Breakeven (long higher-strike put recovers the debit): $$K_2 - D = 55 - 3.50 = \\$51.50$$\n\n- B swaps max profit and max loss and uses $K_1 + D$ for the breakeven (call-spread logic).\n- C uses the gross width $5$ as max profit (forgets the debit).\n- D treats it like a naked long put — the short $K_1$ put caps the downside profit, so loss is **not** unlimited.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bear put spread (debit) → max profit = (width − debit), max loss = debit, breakeven = **higher** strike − debit. Mirror image of the bull call spread.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bear put spread (debit)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Formulas\",\"items\":[\"max profit = (K2-K1) - D\",\"max loss = D (net debit)\",\"breakeven = HIGHER K - D\"]},{\"h\":\"This example\",\"items\":[\"debit = 6 - 2.50 = $3.50\",\"max profit = 5 - 3.50 = $1.50\",\"BE = 55 - 3.50 = $51.50\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A long call butterfly is built by buying a 90-strike call and a 110-strike call and selling two 100-strike calls, for a net debit of $3.00. Which statement about this position is *most* accurate?\nA) Maximum profit is $10.00, achieved if the stock finishes below $90. B) Maximum profit is $7.00, achieved if the stock finishes exactly at $100. C) Maximum loss is unlimited if the stock rises far above $110. D) It is a long-volatility bet that profits most from a large move in either direction.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A long call butterfly with $K_1 = 90$, $K_2 = 100$, $K_3 = 110$ bets the stock finishes **near the middle strike** $K_2$.\n\n1. Maximum profit occurs at $S_T = K_2 = 100$: $$(K_2 - K_1) - D = (100 - 90) - 3.00 = 10 - 3.00 = \\$7.00$$\n2. Maximum loss is the net debit $D = \\$3.00$, occurring if $S_T \\le 90$ or $S_T \\ge 110$.\n\n- A uses the gross width $10$ (forgets the debit) and places the max profit at the wrong stock price (it is at the middle strike, not below the lowest).\n- C is wrong: the bought 110 call caps the loss; max loss is the $3.00 debit.\n- D reverses the view — a butterfly is a **short-volatility** (low-movement) bet, profiting when the stock stays near $K_2$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long butterfly = **short volatility**, peaks at the middle strike with profit (width − debit) and capped loss = debit. Breakevens are $K_1 + D = 93$ and $K_3 - D = 107$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Long butterfly payoff (K1=90,K2=100,K3=110)\",\"items\":[\"S<=90: max loss -D\",\"|100: max profit (width-D)\",\"S>=110: max loss -D\"],\"left\":\"loss (debit)\",\"right\":\"loss (debit)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An investor owns 100 shares purchased at $S_0 = \\$50$ and writes one covered call with $K = \\$55$, collecting a $2.00 premium. Ignoring time value of money, the maximum profit and maximum loss on the combined position are *closest* to:\nA) Max profit $7.00/share; max loss $48.00/share B) Max profit $5.00/share; max loss $50.00/share C) Max profit $2.00/share; max loss $48.00/share D) Max profit unlimited; max loss $48.00/share\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A covered call is long stock + short call. Compute max profit (stock called away at $K$) and max loss (stock falls to zero).\n\n1. Maximum profit (at $S_T \\ge K$, stock called away at $55$): $$K - S_0 + c = 55 - 50 + 2.00 = \\$7.00 \\text{ per share}$$\n2. Maximum loss (at $S_T = 0$, stock worthless, keep the premium): $$S_0 - c = 50 - 2.00 = \\$48.00 \\text{ per share}$$\n\n- B forgets the premium in both figures (uses $K - S_0 = 5$ and $S_0 = 50$).\n- C reports only the premium as max profit, ignoring the $5 capital gain up to the strike.\n- D is wrong: the short call **caps** the upside at $K$, so profit is not unlimited.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Covered call max profit = $K - S_0 + c$ (gain to the strike plus premium); max loss = $S_0 - c$ (stock to zero, cushioned by the premium). By parity its profit profile equals a **short put**.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Covered call (= short put by parity)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Max profit\",\"items\":[\"K - S0 + c\",\"stock called away at K\",\"here: 55-50+2 = $7\"]},{\"h\":\"Max loss\",\"items\":[\"S0 - c\",\"stock falls to zero\",\"here: 50-2 = $48\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A trader expects a large move in a stock and believes a **downward** move is more likely than an upward one. The strategy *best* matching this view is a:\nA) Strap (2 calls + 1 put) B) Strip (1 call + 2 puts) C) Bull put spread D) Long strangle with the call struck far above the put\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We match a \"big move, down more likely\" view to the correct combination.\n\n1. A **strip** = 1 call + 2 puts (same $K$, $T$). It profits from a big move in either direction but carries **double weight on the downside** — exactly a bearish-tilted volatility bet.\n\n- A (strap = 2 calls + 1 put) tilts toward the **upside** — the opposite tilt.\n- C (bull put spread) is a **moderately bullish, limited-range** play, not a big-move bet.\n- D (strangle) is symmetric — it expresses no directional tilt.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Anchor it as \"stri**P** has more **P**uts (pessimistic, down tilt); stra**P** has more calls (up tilt).\" Both are long-volatility (big move) bets; the doubled leg sets the directional lean.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Strip vs strap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Strip\",\"items\":[\"1 call + 2 PUTS\",\"down-tilted (pessimistic)\",\"big move, decline more likely\"]},{\"h\":\"Strap\",\"items\":[\"2 CALLS + 1 put\",\"up-tilted\",\"big move, rise more likely\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** An investor holds 100 shares at $S_0 = \\$40$ and wants downside protection at no net cash outlay. She buys a 36-strike put for $1.50 and sells a 46-strike call for $1.50 (a zero-cost collar). Ignoring time value of money, her profit if the stock finishes at $S_T = \\$30$, and her maximum possible profit, are *closest* to:\nA) Profit −$4.00/share at $S_T=30$; max profit $6.00/share B) Profit −$10.00/share at $S_T=30$; max profit $6.00/share C) Profit −$4.00/share at $S_T=30$; max profit unlimited D) Profit +$6.00/share at $S_T=30$; max profit $10.00/share\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A zero-cost collar = long stock + long put ($K_1 = 36$) + short call ($K_2 = 46$), with premiums netting to zero. The put floors losses; the call caps gains.\n\n1. Net premium = $-1.50 + 1.50 = \\$0$ (zero-cost), so profit = stock P&L plus option payoffs only.\n2. At $S_T = 30$: the 36-put is exercised, so the effective sale price is the floor $K_1 = 36$. Loss from $S_0$: $$36 - 40 = -\\$4.00 \\text{ per share}$$ (the put limits the loss; without it the loss would be $-\\$10$).\n3. Maximum profit (stock at or above the call strike $K_2 = 46$, called away): $$K_2 - S_0 = 46 - 40 = \\$6.00 \\text{ per share}$$\n\n- B ignores the protective put, reporting the unhedged $-\\$10$ loss.\n- C correctly floors the downside but wrongly leaves the upside unlimited — the short call caps it at $K_2$.\n- D inverts the downside (a $30 stock cannot yield a $+6$ profit) and mislabels the max.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A collar locks the outcome into the band $[K_1, K_2]$. Below $K_1$ the put protects (effective price = $K_1$); above $K_2$ the call caps (effective price = $K_2$). Profit floor = $K_1 - S_0$; profit cap = $K_2 - S_0$ (zero-cost case).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Zero-cost collar outcome (K1=36, S0=40, K2=46)\",\"items\":[\"S<=36: floor, profit K1-S0 = -$4\",\"|band 36 to 46: track stock\",\"S>=46: cap, profit K2-S0 = +$6\"],\"left\":\"protected\",\"right\":\"capped\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"315","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Exotic Options","notes":"# 15. Exotic Options\n\nGARP tests definitions and payoff formulas for the exotic \"menagerie,\" which exotics are path-dependent, which are cheaper or dearer than vanilla, and a handful of identities (barrier in + out = vanilla, chooser decomposition, gap payoffs that can go negative).\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Define and contrast exotic derivatives with plain vanilla derivatives, and describe factors driving the development of exotics.\n- Explain how any derivative can be converted into a zero-cost product, and how standard American options become nonstandard American options.\n- Identify the characteristics and payoff structures of: packages, gap, forward start, cliquet, compound, chooser, barrier, binary, lookback, shout, Asian, exchange, basket, and rainbow options.\n- Describe and contrast volatility and variance swaps.\n- Explain the basic premise of static option replication and its application to hedging exotic options.\n\n## Why exotics exist\n\nExotics arise from genuine hedging needs (matching a corporate exposure exactly), tax/regulatory/accounting motives, and dealers' ability to charge wider margins on tailored products. Any derivative can be made **zero-cost** by deferring the premium: the buyer pays the future value of the premium at maturity instead of up front (a zero-cost collar/range forward is the classic example — buy a put, sell a call with strikes chosen so premiums net to zero).\n\n## The menagerie\n\n- **Packages**: portfolios of vanilla options, forwards, cash, and the asset — e.g., bull spreads, straddles, **range forwards** (zero-cost collars). Not exotic individually, but priced as a bundle.\n- **Nonstandard American**: early exercise restricted — **Bermudan** (exercise only on specified dates), **lock-out** (no exercise during an initial period), or a strike that steps up/down over the option's life. Typical of warrants and employee stock options.\n- **Gap options**: a gap call pays $$S_T - K_1$$ if $$S_T > K_2$$ and zero otherwise. The trigger $$K_2$$ and the payoff strike $$K_1$$ differ, so **the payoff can be negative** (when $$K_1 > K_2$$ and $$K_2 < S_T < K_1$$). A gap put pays $$K_1 - S_T$$ if $$S_T < K_2$$.\n- **Forward start options**: paid for now, start at a future date $$T_1$$, usually struck **at the money at $$T_1$$**. Employee stock option grants promised today for future award dates are forward start options.\n- **Cliquet (ratchet)**: a series of forward start options, each resetting at the money when the previous one expires — locks in gains period by period.\n- **Compound options**: options on options — call-on-call, call-on-put, put-on-call, put-on-put. **Two strikes and two maturities**: at $$T_1$$ pay $$K_1$$ to acquire (or sell) the underlying option with strike $$K_2$$ expiring at $$T_2$$. Cheap up-front exposure; very sensitive to volatility.\n- **Chooser options**: at $$T_1$$ the holder chooses whether the option is a call or a put (both with strike $$K$$, expiry $$T_2$$). Value at $$T_1$$ is $$\\max(c, p)$$; using put-call parity this decomposes into a $$T_2$$-maturity call plus $$e^{-q(T_2-T_1)}$$ put options with adjusted strike maturing at $$T_1$$ — i.e., a package of a call and a put, which is why a chooser resembles (and costs less than) a straddle.\n- **Barrier options**: payoff depends on whether $$S$$ hits a barrier $$H$$. Four basic types: **up-and-in, up-and-out, down-and-in, down-and-out** (knock-ins come alive at the barrier; knock-outs die). For the same $$K, T, H$$:\n\n$$\nc_{\\text{in}} + c_{\\text{out}} = c_{\\text{vanilla}}, \\qquad p_{\\text{in}} + p_{\\text{out}} = p_{\\text{vanilla}}\n$$\n\n  Barriers are **cheaper than vanilla** (you can lose the option). A **reverse knock-out** has its barrier in the money (e.g., an up-and-out call with $$H > K$$), so it dies precisely when it is most valuable — large negative vega/odd Greeks near the barrier.\n- **Binary (digital) options**: **cash-or-nothing** call pays a fixed $$Q$$ if $$S_T > K$$ (value $$Qe^{-rT}N(d_2)$$); **asset-or-nothing** call pays $$S_T$$ if $$S_T > K$$. A vanilla call = asset-or-nothing call − cash-or-nothing call with $$Q = K$$.\n- **Lookback options**: payoff depends on the extreme price over the life. **Floating** lookback call pays $$S_T - S_{\\min}$$ (buy at the low); floating lookback put pays $$S_{\\max} - S_T$$ (sell at the high). **Fixed** lookback call pays $$\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0)$$; fixed lookback put pays $$\\max(K - S_{\\min}, 0)$$. Path-dependent and **expensive** relative to vanilla.\n- **Shout options**: the holder may \"shout\" once, locking in the intrinsic value at the shout date as a floor; final payoff is $$\\max(S_T - K, S_\\tau - K)$$ for a call shouted at $$\\tau$$. Cheaper than a lookback, similar idea.\n- **Asian options**: payoff based on the average price. **Average price call**: $$\\max(S_{ave} - K, 0)$$; average price put: $$\\max(K - S_{ave}, 0)$$. **Average strike call**: $$\\max(S_T - S_{ave}, 0)$$; average strike put: $$\\max(S_{ave} - S_T, 0)$$. Averaging lowers effective volatility, so average price options are **cheaper than vanilla**, and the average is **harder to manipulate** than a single closing price — ideal for hedging a stream of FX conversions.\n- **Exchange options**: the right to exchange one asset for another; payoff $$\\max(S_T^{(1)} - S_T^{(2)}, 0)$$ — e.g., exchange shares in a stock-for-stock takeover bid.\n- **Basket options**: payoff on a **portfolio** of assets; cheaper than a basket of options because asset correlations < 1 reduce basket volatility.\n- **Rainbow options**: payoff depends on several assets, e.g., the best-of or worst-of two or more assets (the CTD choice in a bond futures contract is rainbow-like).\n\n## Volatility and variance swaps\n\nA **volatility swap** exchanges realized volatility over the period for a fixed (strike) volatility, with payoff $$L(\\sigma_{realized} - \\sigma_K)$$ — pure volatility exposure with no price-direction exposure. A **variance swap** pays on realized **variance**, $$L(\\sigma^2_{realized} - \\sigma^2_K)$$, and is easier to value and hedge because variance can be replicated from a static strip of vanilla calls and puts across strikes; dealers therefore prefer variance swaps.\n\n## Static option replication\n\nInstead of dynamic delta hedging (costly, breaks down near barriers), **static replication** finds a portfolio of vanilla options that matches the exotic's value on a chosen boundary (e.g., along the barrier and at maturity). If two portfolios agree on the whole boundary, they agree inside it; the vanilla portfolio is bought once and unwound only if the boundary is reached.\n\n## Comparison table\n\n| Option | Payoff | Path-dependent? | vs. vanilla cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gap call | $$S_T - K_1$$ if $$S_T > K_2$$ | No | Either (can be negative payoff) |\n| Forward start | Vanilla from $$T_1$$, ATM at $$T_1$$ | No | Comparable (PV of future ATM option) |\n| Compound | Option on an option | No | Cheaper up front, high leverage |\n| Chooser | $$\\max(c, p)$$ at $$T_1$$ | No | Cheaper than straddle |\n| Barrier (in or out) | Vanilla conditional on hitting $$H$$ | Yes | **Cheaper** |\n| Binary cash-or-nothing | $$Q$$ if ITM | No | — |\n| Floating lookback call | $$S_T - S_{\\min}$$ | Yes | **Dearer** |\n| Asian average price call | $$\\max(S_{ave} - K, 0)$$ | Yes | **Cheaper** |\n| Exchange | $$\\max(S_T^{(1)} - S_T^{(2)}, 0)$$ | No | — |\n| Basket | Option on portfolio value | No | Cheaper than sum of options |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Two classic traps. (1) A **gap option's payoff can be negative**: the trigger $$K_2$$ and the payoff strike $$K_1$$ are different numbers, and exercise is automatic when $$S_T > K_2$$ — do not floor the payoff at zero. (2) **Barrier parity**: knock-in + knock-out with the same strike, maturity, and barrier = the vanilla option. If a question gives you two of the three prices, the third is free marks.\n\n### Worked example: gap call with a negative payoff\n\nA gap call has payoff strike $$K_1 = \\$50$$ and trigger $$K_2 = \\$45$$.\n\n- $$S_T = 60$$: trigger met ($$60 > 45$$), payoff $$= 60 - 50 = \\$10$$.\n- $$S_T = 47$$: trigger met ($$47 > 45$$), payoff $$= 47 - 50 = -\\$3$$ — the holder **loses** $3 at expiry.\n- $$S_T = 44$$: trigger not met, payoff $$= 0$$.\n\nA vanilla 50-strike call would pay 10, 0, 0 in the three cases; the gap call is cheaper than the vanilla 45-strike call precisely because of the negative-payoff region between 45 and 50.\n\n### Worked example: lookbacks on a price path\n\nA stock observed monthly over an option's life trades at 50, 45, 62, 58, and finishes at $$S_T = 55$$. So $$S_{\\min} = 45$$, $$S_{\\max} = 62$$.\n\n- Floating lookback call: $$S_T - S_{\\min} = 55 - 45 = \\$10$$ (you bought at the low).\n- Floating lookback put: $$S_{\\max} - S_T = 62 - 55 = \\$7$$ (you sold at the high).\n- Fixed lookback call, $$K = 50$$: $$\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0) = 62 - 50 = \\$12$$.\n- Asian average price call, $$K = 50$$: $$S_{ave} = (50 + 45 + 62 + 58 + 55)/5 = 270/5 = 54$$, payoff $$= 54 - 50 = \\$4$$ — versus $$55 - 50 = \\$5$$ for the vanilla call on this path.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Why do exotic options exist, and how is any derivative made zero-cost?\n> **A:** Exotics arise from precise hedging needs, tax/regulatory/accounting motives, and dealers' wider margins on tailored products. Any derivative becomes zero-cost by deferring the premium: the buyer pays the future value of the premium at maturity instead of up front (e.g., a zero-cost collar/range forward — buy a put, sell a call so premiums net to zero).\n\n**Q2.** Nonstandard American options\n> **A:** Early exercise is restricted: Bermudan (exercise only on specified dates), lock-out (no exercise during an initial period), or a strike that steps up/down over the option's life. Typical of warrants and employee stock options.\n\n**Q3.** Gap option payoff and the negative-payoff trap\n> **A:** A gap call pays $$S_T - K_1$$ if $$S_T > K_2$$ (trigger), else zero; a gap put pays $$K_1 - S_T$$ if $$S_T < K_2$$. Because the trigger $$K_2$$ and payoff strike $$K_1$$ differ, the payoff can be NEGATIVE (when $$K_1 > K_2$$ and $$K_2 < S_T < K_1$$) — do not floor it at zero.\n\n**Q4.** Forward start and cliquet (ratchet) options\n> **A:** Forward start: paid for now, starts at future date $$T_1$$, usually struck at the money at $$T_1$$ (e.g., promised ESO grants). Cliquet: a series of forward start options, each resetting at the money when the previous expires — locks in gains period by period.\n\n**Q5.** Compound options\n> **A:** Options on options (call-on-call, call-on-put, put-on-call, put-on-put). Two strikes and two maturities: at $$T_1$$ pay $$K_1$$ to acquire/sell the underlying option with strike $$K_2$$ expiring at $$T_2$$. Cheap up-front exposure, high leverage, very sensitive to volatility.\n\n**Q6.** Chooser options\n> **A:** At $$T_1$$ the holder chooses whether the option is a call or a put (same strike $$K$$, expiry $$T_2$$); value at $$T_1$$ is $$\\max(c, p)$$. By put-call parity it decomposes into a package of a call and a put, which is why a chooser resembles — and costs less than — a straddle.\n\n**Q7.** Barrier options: the four types and the in/out parity identity\n> **A:** Up-and-in, up-and-out, down-and-in, down-and-out (knock-ins come alive at the barrier $$H$$; knock-outs die). For the same $$K, T, H$$: $$c_{in}+c_{out}=c_{vanilla}$$ and $$p_{in}+p_{out}=p_{vanilla}$$. Barriers are CHEAPER than vanilla. A reverse knock-out has its barrier in the money, so it dies when most valuable.\n\n**Q8.** Binary (digital) options\n> **A:** Cash-or-nothing call pays a fixed $$Q$$ if $$S_T > K$$ (value $$Qe^{-rT}N(d_2)$$); asset-or-nothing call pays $$S_T$$ if $$S_T > K$$. A vanilla call = asset-or-nothing call − cash-or-nothing call with $$Q = K$$.\n\n**Q9.** Lookback options (floating vs fixed)\n> **A:** Floating lookback call pays $$S_T - S_{\\min}$$ (buy at the low); floating put pays $$S_{\\max} - S_T$$ (sell at the high). Fixed lookback call pays $$\\max(S_{\\max}-K,0)$$; fixed put pays $$\\max(K-S_{\\min},0)$$. Path-dependent and EXPENSIVE relative to vanilla.\n\n**Q10.** Shout options\n> **A:** The holder may \"shout\" once, locking in the intrinsic value at the shout date $$\\tau$$ as a floor; the final call payoff is $$\\max(S_T - K,\\, S_\\tau - K)$$. Cheaper than a lookback, similar idea.\n\n**Q11.** Asian options and why average-price options are cheaper\n> **A:** Average price call $$\\max(S_{ave}-K,0)$$; average strike call $$\\max(S_T - S_{ave},0)$$ (and puts analogously). Averaging lowers effective volatility, so average price options are CHEAPER than vanilla; the average is also harder to manipulate — ideal for hedging a stream of FX conversions.\n\n**Q12.** Exchange, basket, and rainbow options\n> **A:** Exchange: right to exchange one asset for another, payoff $$\\max(S_T^{(1)} - S_T^{(2)}, 0)$$ (e.g., stock-for-stock takeover). Basket: payoff on a portfolio, cheaper than a basket of options because correlations < 1 reduce basket volatility. Rainbow: payoff on several assets, e.g., best-of/worst-of (CTD choice is rainbow-like).\n\n**Q13.** Volatility swap vs variance swap\n> **A:** Volatility swap pays $$L(\\sigma_{realized} - \\sigma_K)$$; variance swap pays $$L(\\sigma^2_{realized} - \\sigma^2_K)$$. Dealers prefer variance swaps because variance can be replicated from a static strip of vanilla calls and puts across strikes, making them easier to value and hedge.\n\n**Q14.** Static option replication\n> **A:** Instead of costly dynamic delta hedging (which breaks down near barriers), find a vanilla-option portfolio that matches the exotic's value along a chosen boundary (the barrier and maturity). If two portfolios agree on the whole boundary, they agree inside it; the portfolio is bought once and unwound only if the boundary is reached.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A gap call option has payoff strike $K_1 = \\$55$ and trigger strike $K_2 = \\$50$. At expiration the stock is $S_T = \\$53$. The payoff is:\nA) $0 B) $3 C) −$2 D) −$5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question tests the gap option payoff, which can be negative.\n\nA gap call pays $S_T - K_1$ if $S_T > K_2$ (the trigger), and zero otherwise.\n\nStep 1 — Is the trigger met? $S_T = 53 > K_2 = 50$? Yes, so the option pays out.\n\nStep 2 — Payoff = $S_T - K_1 = 53 - 55 = -\\$2$.\n\nBecause the payoff strike ($55$) is higher than the trigger ($50$), in the range $50 < S_T < 55$ the payoff is negative — the holder actually loses money at expiry.\n\nWhy C is correct: the trigger is crossed, and the payoff $53 - 55 = -2$ is NOT floored at zero.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($0) wrongly assumes the payoff can't be negative — but gap payoffs are not floored at zero.\n- B ($3) computes $53 - 50$ (using the trigger as the strike) — wrong; the payoff uses $K_1 = 55$.\n- D (−$5) is the maximum loss ($50 - 55$), which occurs at $S_T = 50$, not at $53$.\n\nAnalogy: a gap option is like a door that opens at one number ($K_2$) but charges you a fee based on a different number ($K_1$). Once the door opens, you must pay even if the math is against you.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Gap payoff is automatic once the trigger $K_2$ is crossed and uses $K_1$ — it is NOT floored at zero, so it can be negative.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Gap call payoff\",\"steps\":[\"Trigger K2 decides WHETHER it pays (S_T > K2?)\",\"Payoff strike K1 decides HOW MUCH (S_T - K1)\",\"Not floored at zero — can be negative\",\"Here: 53>50 triggers, pays 53-55 = -$2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A down-and-out call is worth $4.20, and the vanilla call (same strike and maturity) is worth $6.50. The down-and-in call with the same barrier is worth:\nA) $2.30 B) $4.20 C) $6.50 D) $10.70\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question tests barrier parity.\n\nFor the same strike, maturity, and barrier:\n$$c_{\\text{in}} + c_{\\text{out}} = c_{\\text{vanilla}}$$\n\nSolve for the knock-in:\n$$c_{\\text{in}} = c_{\\text{vanilla}} - c_{\\text{out}} = 6.50 - 4.20 = \\$2.30$$\n\nWhy A is correct: a knock-in plus a knock-out (same terms) equals a vanilla option, so the in-option is the difference.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B ($4.20) just repeats the knock-out price.\n- C ($6.50) repeats the vanilla price.\n- D ($10.70) ADDS the two given prices instead of subtracting — that breaks the parity identity.\n\nAnalogy: think of the vanilla call as a whole pie. The knock-out is one slice; the knock-in is the rest of the pie. Together they're the whole.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Barrier parity: in + out = vanilla (same $K$, $T$, $H$). If a question gives two of the three, the third is free marks.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Barrier parity\",\"steps\":[\"Knock-in + knock-out = vanilla (same K, T, H)\",\"c_in = c_vanilla - c_out\",\"Here: 6.50 - 4.20 = $2.30\",\"Each barrier alone is cheaper than vanilla\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Compared with an otherwise identical vanilla call, an average-price (Asian) call is:\nA) Dearer, because it is path-dependent B) Cheaper, because averaging reduces the effective volatility C) Identical in value by no-arbitrage D) Dearer, because the average is harder to manipulate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how an Asian (average-price) option compares in cost to a vanilla.\n\nAn average-price option's payoff depends on the AVERAGE price over the period, not the single final price. An average is smoother (less volatile) than the final price. Since lower volatility means lower option value, the average-price option is CHEAPER than the vanilla.\n\nWhy B is correct: averaging lowers effective volatility, making the option cheaper.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: path-dependence alone doesn't make it dearer — here the averaging effect lowers volatility and the price.\n- C: they are not identical; the payoff structures differ.\n- D: manipulation-resistance is a useful FEATURE, but it does not push the price UP — that's not the cost driver.\n\nAnalogy: averaging a bunch of numbers smooths out the extremes, like the average temperature for a month being far less jumpy than any single hot or cold day.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Asian average-price options are CHEAPER than vanilla (lower effective volatility) and resist manipulation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Cost vs vanilla\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Cheaper than vanilla\",\"items\":[\"Asian (averaging cuts volatility)\",\"barriers (can knock out/never in)\",\"baskets (correlation < 1)\"]},{\"h\":\"Dearer than vanilla\",\"items\":[\"lookbacks (capture the extreme)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A floating lookback call option pays:\nA) $\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0)$ B) $S_{\\max} - S_T$ C) $S_T - S_{\\min}$ D) $\\max(K - S_{\\min}, 0)$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the floating lookback call payoff.\n\nA floating lookback call lets you effectively \"buy at the lowest price\" over the option's life. Its payoff is the final price minus the minimum price observed:\n$$S_T - S_{\\min}$$\n\nWhy C is correct: floating lookback call = final price − the lowest price = buying at the low.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0)$) is a FIXED lookback call (uses a set strike $K$ and the maximum).\n- B ($S_{\\max} - S_T$) is a floating lookback PUT (sell at the high).\n- D ($\\max(K - S_{\\min}, 0)$) is a FIXED lookback put.\n\nAnalogy: a floating lookback call is like getting to rewind time and buy the stock at its cheapest moment, then value it at the end.\n\n📌 Exam tip: FLOATING lookback uses the extreme as the strike (call: $S_T - S_{\\min}$; put: $S_{\\max} - S_T$). FIXED lookback uses a set $K$ with the extreme price.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Lookback payoffs\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Floating (extreme = strike)\",\"items\":[\"call: S_T - S_min (buy at low)\",\"put: S_max - S_T (sell at high)\"]},{\"h\":\"Fixed (set strike K)\",\"items\":[\"call: max(S_max - K, 0)\",\"put: max(K - S_min, 0)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Over an option's life, a stock is observed at 50, 45, 62, 58, and finishes at $S_T = 55$. The floating lookback call payoff is:\nA) $5 B) $7 C) $10 D) $12\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks us to compute a floating lookback call payoff from a price path.\n\nStep 1 — Identify the minimum observed price: from {50, 45, 62, 58, 55}, $S_{\\min} = 45$.\n\nStep 2 — Floating lookback call payoff = $S_T - S_{\\min}$:\n$$55 - 45 = \\$10$$\n\nWhy C is correct: you \"bought at the low\" of $45 and the final price is $55, so payoff $= 10$.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($5) is the vanilla call payoff with $K = 50$ ($55 - 50$), not a lookback.\n- B ($7) is the floating lookback PUT ($S_{\\max} - S_T = 62 - 55$).\n- D ($12) is the FIXED lookback call with $K = 50$ ($S_{\\max} - K = 62 - 50$).\n\nAnalogy: like buying the stock at its cheapest dip ($45) and marking it at the finish ($55) — a $10 gain.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For floating lookback call, scan the path for the MINIMUM, then subtract it from $S_T$. Don't confuse it with the fixed version (uses $S_{\\max}$ and $K$).\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which of these exotic options is path-dependent?\nA) Gap option B) Chooser option C) Barrier option D) Compound option\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which exotic depends on the path the price takes, not just the final price.\n\nA barrier option's value depends on whether the stock hits a barrier $H$ at any point during its life (knock-in comes alive, knock-out dies). Because the whole price path matters, it is path-dependent.\n\nWhy C is correct: barriers depend on whether the price touches $H$ along the way — path-dependent.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (gap option) depends only on the final price $S_T$ relative to the trigger — not path-dependent.\n- B (chooser option) depends on a decision at $T_1$ and the final outcome, but its payoff structure isn't driven by the full path the way a barrier is — it's classified as not path-dependent.\n- D (compound option) is an option on an option; its value isn't path-dependent in the barrier/lookback sense.\n\nAnalogy: a barrier option is like a game where touching a trip-wire at any moment changes everything — so the whole journey matters, not just where you end.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Path-dependent exotics include barriers, lookbacks, and Asians. Gap, chooser, compound, and forward-start are NOT path-dependent.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Path dependence\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Path-dependent\",\"items\":[\"barrier\",\"lookback\",\"Asian (average)\",\"shout\"]},{\"h\":\"Path-independent\",\"items\":[\"gap\",\"chooser\",\"compound\",\"forward start / binary\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A variance swap is generally preferred by dealers over a volatility swap because:\nA) It has no exposure to realized volatility at all B) Variance can be replicated from a static strip of vanilla calls and puts, making it easier to value and hedge C) It pays off only on the direction of the stock price D) It requires no underlying market\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why dealers favor variance swaps.\n\nA variance swap pays on realized VARIANCE ($\\sigma^2$), and variance has a convenient property: it can be replicated by a fixed (static) portfolio of vanilla calls and puts across many strikes. That makes it much easier to price and hedge than a volatility swap (which pays on $\\sigma$ and is harder to replicate). So dealers prefer variance swaps.\n\nWhy B is correct: static replication of variance from vanilla options makes valuation and hedging straightforward.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is false — a variance swap is precisely exposure TO realized variance (volatility squared).\n- C is false — both swaps are pure volatility/variance plays with NO direction exposure.\n- D is false — they rely on a market of vanilla options to hedge.\n\nAnalogy: variance is like a recipe you can assemble from standard ingredients (vanilla options) on the shelf, while volatility is a custom dish that's harder to recreate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Variance swaps are easier to hedge (static replication from vanilla options) → dealers prefer them. Both swaps give pure volatility exposure, no price direction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Variance swap vs volatility swap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Variance swap\",\"items\":[\"pays on realized variance (sigma^2)\",\"static replication from vanilla strip\",\"easier to value/hedge — dealers prefer\"]},{\"h\":\"Volatility swap\",\"items\":[\"pays on realized vol (sigma)\",\"harder to replicate\",\"both: no price-direction exposure\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which statement about basket options is correct?\nA) A basket option is always more expensive than buying separate options on each asset B) A basket option is cheaper than a basket of individual options because asset correlations below 1 reduce the basket's volatility C) Basket options require all assets to be perfectly correlated D) Basket options have no diversification effect\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how a basket option compares to owning options on each asset separately.\n\nA basket option pays off on the value of a PORTFOLIO of assets. When the assets aren't perfectly correlated (correlation < 1), their ups and downs partly offset, so the basket's overall volatility is LOWER than the sum of the individual volatilities. Lower volatility means a cheaper option. So a basket option costs less than buying a separate option on each asset.\n\nWhy B is correct: imperfect correlation reduces basket volatility, making the basket option cheaper than the sum of individual options.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A is the opposite — it's cheaper, not more expensive.\n- C is false — basket options work precisely BECAUSE correlations are below 1 (otherwise no diversification benefit).\n- D is false — the diversification effect is exactly what makes them cheaper.\n\nAnalogy: a mixed fruit basket is steadier in price than betting on each fruit separately — when apples dip, oranges might rise, smoothing things out.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Basket option < sum of individual options, thanks to diversification (correlation < 1 lowers basket volatility).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Basket option vs separate options\",\"options\":[\"Always more expensive than separate options\",\"Cheaper — correlation < 1 reduces basket volatility\",\"Requires perfectly correlated assets\",\"Has no diversification effect\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Imperfect correlation means moves partly offset, lowering basket volatility and therefore the option price.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A chooser option allows the holder, at time $T_1$, to:\nA) Change the strike price of the option B) Decide whether the option will be a call or a put (both with the same strike, expiring at $T_2$) C) Cancel the option for a full refund D) Double the size of the position\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the definition of a chooser option.\n\nA chooser option lets the holder decide at a future date $T_1$ whether the option becomes a call or a put (both struck at $K$ and expiring at $T_2$). At $T_1$ the holder picks whichever is worth more, so its value is $\\max(c, p)$. Because it bundles a call and a put, it resembles (but is cheaper than) a straddle.\n\nWhy B is correct: the holder chooses call-or-put at $T_1$ — that's the defining feature.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (change the strike) describes a different feature, not a chooser.\n- C (cancel for a refund) is not what a chooser does.\n- D (double the size) is unrelated to the chooser's choice between call and put.\n\nAnalogy: a chooser is like a ticket that lets you decide later whether to bet on the price going up or down, once you've seen which way the wind is blowing.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Chooser = pick call or put at $T_1$; value $= \\max(c, p)$. Cheaper than a straddle because you commit to one direction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Chooser option\",\"steps\":[\"At T1 holder decides: call or put?\",\"Pick whichever is worth more -> value = max(c, p)\",\"Both struck at K, expiring at T2\",\"Mimics a straddle but cheaper (keep only one leg)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Static option replication is used to hedge exotic options because:\nA) It requires constant, costly rebalancing like delta hedging B) It finds a fixed portfolio of vanilla options that matches the exotic on a chosen boundary, bought once and unwound only if the boundary is reached C) It eliminates all market risk with no positions D) It works only for vanilla options, never exotics\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what static replication is and why it helps with exotics.\n\nStatic replication builds a FIXED portfolio of vanilla options that matches the exotic's value along a chosen boundary (for example, along a barrier and at maturity). If two portfolios agree on the whole boundary, they agree inside it too. The hedge is set up ONCE and only unwound if the boundary is reached — unlike dynamic delta hedging, which needs constant, costly rebalancing (and breaks down near barriers).\n\nWhy B is correct: it describes the fixed, boundary-matching portfolio bought once — the essence of static replication.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A describes DYNAMIC delta hedging, which is exactly what static replication avoids.\n- C is false — static replication uses real option positions; it doesn't conjure a hedge from nothing.\n- D is false — static replication is specifically valuable for hedging EXOTICS like barriers.\n\nAnalogy: instead of constantly adjusting your umbrella all day (dynamic hedging), you pick the right rain gear once based on the forecast boundary and only change it if conditions hit that boundary.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Static replication = a fixed vanilla-option portfolio matching the exotic on a boundary, set up once. It avoids the costly, fragile rebalancing of dynamic delta hedging near barriers.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Static replication vs dynamic delta hedging\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Static replication\",\"items\":[\"fixed vanilla portfolio\",\"matches exotic on a boundary\",\"set once, unwind only if boundary hit\"]},{\"h\":\"Dynamic delta hedging\",\"items\":[\"constant rebalancing\",\"costly, error-prone\",\"breaks down near barriers\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A gap call has $$K_1 = 55$$ and $$K_2 = 50$$. At expiry $$S_T = 53$$. The payoff is:\nA) $0 B) $3 C) −$2 D) −$5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** trigger met ($$53 > 50$$), so payoff $$= S_T - K_1 = 53 - 55 = -\\$2$$; gap payoffs are not floored at zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A down-and-out call is worth $4.20 and the vanilla call (same strike, maturity) is worth $6.50. The down-and-in call with the same barrier is worth:\nA) $2.30 B) $4.20 C) $6.50 D) $10.70\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** in + out = vanilla: $$6.50 - 4.20 = \\$2.30$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Compared with an otherwise identical vanilla call, an average price (Asian) call is:\nA) Dearer, because it is path-dependent B) Cheaper, because averaging reduces effective volatility C) Identical in value by no-arbitrage D) Dearer, because it is harder to manipulate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** the average is less volatile than the terminal price, so the option is cheaper (manipulation-resistance is a feature, not a cost driver upward).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A floating lookback call pays:\nA) $$\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0)$$ B) $$S_{\\max} - S_T$$ C) $$S_T - S_{\\min}$$ D) $$\\max(K - S_{\\min}, 0)$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** floating lookback call buys at the observed minimum; A is the fixed lookback call.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A vanilla European call is worth $6.50. An otherwise identical down-and-in barrier call (same strike, expiry, and barrier below the current price) is worth $2.10. The corresponding down-and-out call is worth:\nA) $2.10 B) $4.40 C) $8.60 D) Cannot be determined without the barrier level\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** In–out parity: down-and-in + down-and-out = vanilla, so down-and-out = 6.50 − 2.10 = **$4.40**. The barrier level is already embedded in the down-and-in price (D wrong).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"In–out barrier parity\",\"steps\":[\"Down-and-in + Down-and-out = Vanilla\",\"6.50 = 2.10 + DAO\",\"DAO = 6.50 − 2.10\",\"= $4.40\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A gap call option has a payoff strike $K_1 = \\$55$ and a trigger strike $K_2 = \\$50$. At expiration the stock is at $S_T = \\$53$. The holder's payoff is *closest* to:\nA) $0 B) $3 C) −$2 D) −$5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** A gap call pays $S_T - K_1$ whenever $S_T > K_2$ (the trigger), and zero otherwise. The payoff strike and trigger strike differ, so the payoff can be **negative** and is **not** floored at zero.\n\n1. Check the trigger: is $S_T > K_2$? $53 > 50$ → **yes, the option pays out** (exercise is automatic).\n2. Compute the payoff using the payoff strike $K_1$: $$S_T - K_1 = 53 - 55 = -\\$2$$\n3. Because the trigger is met but $S_T < K_1$, the holder receives a **negative** payoff of $-\\$2$.\n\n- A ($0) wrongly floors the payoff at zero like a vanilla call — gap payoffs are not floored.\n- B ($3) computes $S_T - K_2 = 53 - 50$, using the trigger as the payoff strike (mixing up $K_1$ and $K_2$).\n- D (−$5) uses $K_2 - K_1 = 50 - 55$, the strike gap rather than the actual payoff.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a gap call, the **trigger** $K_2$ decides *whether* it pays; the **payoff strike** $K_1$ decides *how much* ($S_T - K_1$). When $K_1 > K_2$ and $K_2 < S_T < K_1$, the payoff is negative — never floor it at zero.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Gap call payoff\",\"steps\":[\"Trigger K2 decides WHETHER it pays (S_T > K2?)\",\"Payoff strike K1 decides HOW MUCH (S_T - K1)\",\"K1 > K2 and K2 < S_T < K1 -> negative payoff\",\"Here: 53>50 triggers, pays 53-55 = -$2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A down-and-out call is worth $4.20 and the otherwise identical vanilla call (same strike and maturity) is worth $6.50. The down-and-in call with the same barrier is worth:\nA) $2.30 B) $4.20 C) $6.50 D) $10.70\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** We use the barrier parity identity: a knock-in plus the matching knock-out equals the vanilla option.\n\n1. Barrier parity (same $K$, $T$, $H$): $$c_{\\text{in}} + c_{\\text{out}} = c_{\\text{vanilla}}$$\n2. Solve for the down-and-in: $$c_{\\text{in}} = c_{\\text{vanilla}} - c_{\\text{out}} = 6.50 - 4.20 = \\$2.30$$\n\n- B ($4.20) just restates the knock-out price.\n- C ($6.50) restates the vanilla price.\n- D ($10.70) **adds** the two ($6.50 + 4.20$) instead of subtracting — the wrong direction of the identity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: **Knock-in + knock-out = vanilla** (same strike, maturity, barrier). If a question gives any two of the three, the third is free marks via subtraction. Each barrier option alone is **cheaper** than the vanilla.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Barrier parity\",\"steps\":[\"Knock-in + knock-out = vanilla (same K, T, H)\",\"c_in = c_vanilla - c_out\",\"Here: 6.50 - 4.20 = $2.30\",\"Each barrier alone is cheaper than vanilla\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A stock is observed monthly over an option's life at prices 50, 45, 62, 58, and finishes at $S_T = 55$. Consider four options written on this path with $K = 50$ where relevant. Which payoff is computed *correctly*?\nA) Floating lookback call pays $S_T - S_{\\max} = 55 - 62 = -\\$7$ B) Fixed lookback call pays $\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0) = 62 - 50 = \\$12$ C) Asian average price call pays $S_T - S_{ave} = 55 - 54 = \\$1$ D) Floating lookback put pays $S_{\\min} - S_T = 45 - 55 = -\\$10$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We identify $S_{\\min} = 45$, $S_{\\max} = 62$, $S_T = 55$, $S_{ave} = (50+45+62+58+55)/5 = 270/5 = 54$, then test each payoff formula.\n\n1. **Fixed lookback call**: $\\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0) = \\max(62 - 50, 0) = \\$12$. Choice B is correct.\n\n- A uses the wrong extreme: a floating lookback **call** pays $S_T - S_{\\min} = 55 - 45 = \\$10$ (buy at the low), not $S_T - S_{\\max}$.\n- C states the **average strike** call formula ($S_T - S_{ave}$); the **average price** call pays $\\max(S_{ave} - K, 0) = \\max(54 - 50, 0) = \\$4$.\n- D mis-signs the floating lookback **put**, which pays $S_{\\max} - S_T = 62 - 55 = \\$7$ (sell at the high), not $S_{\\min} - S_T$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Floating lookback **call** = $S_T - S_{\\min}$ (buy low); floating **put** = $S_{\\max} - S_T$ (sell high). Fixed lookback uses $K$ with the extreme. Average **price** uses $K$; average **strike** uses $S_T$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Lookback and Asian call payoffs\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Lookback\",\"items\":[\"floating call: S_T - S_min\",\"floating put: S_max - S_T\",\"fixed call: max(S_max - K, 0)\"]},{\"h\":\"Asian\",\"items\":[\"average price: max(S_ave - K, 0)\",\"average strike: max(S_T - S_ave, 0)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Compared with an otherwise identical vanilla call, which exotic is correctly paired with its cost relationship and reasoning?\nA) Average price (Asian) call — dearer, because it is path-dependent B) Barrier knock-out call — dearer, because it has an extra trigger feature C) Floating lookback call — cheaper, because the minimum reduces volatility D) Average price (Asian) call — cheaper, because averaging reduces effective volatility\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** We match each exotic to its cost relative to vanilla and the correct driver.\n\n1. An **average price (Asian) call** is based on the average underlying price, which is **less volatile** than the single terminal price. Lower effective volatility lowers the option value, so the Asian call is **cheaper** than vanilla. Choice D is correct.\n\n- A reverses the direction: Asian options are cheaper, not dearer; path-dependence alone does not raise value (manipulation resistance is a feature, not a price driver upward).\n- B is wrong: a knock-out call can be lost (it dies at the barrier), so it is **cheaper** than vanilla.\n- C is wrong: a floating lookback call is **dearer** (it locks in the best entry, $S_T - S_{\\min}$), not cheaper.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Cheaper than vanilla → **Asian** (averaging cuts volatility), **barriers** (can be knocked out/never knocked in), **baskets** (correlation < 1). Dearer than vanilla → **lookbacks** (capture the extreme).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Cost vs vanilla\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Cheaper\",\"items\":[\"Asian (averaging cuts volatility)\",\"barriers (knock-out/never-in risk)\",\"baskets (correlation < 1)\"]},{\"h\":\"Dearer\",\"items\":[\"lookbacks (capture the extreme)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A chooser option lets the holder decide at time $T_1$ whether the option is a call or a put (both with the same strike $K$ and final maturity $T_2$). Its value at $T_1$ and its cost relative to a straddle are *best* described as:\nA) Value $\\min(c, p)$ at $T_1$; more expensive than a straddle B) Value $\\max(c, p)$ at $T_1$; cheaper than a straddle C) Value $c + p$ at $T_1$; equal to a straddle D) Value $\\max(c - p, 0)$ at $T_1$; cheaper than a call alone\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We characterize the chooser's value at the decision date and compare it to a straddle.\n\n1. At $T_1$ the holder keeps whichever option is worth more, so the value is $$\\max(c, p)$$\n2. A straddle owns **both** a call and a put outright (value $\\approx c + p$); a chooser owns only the **better** of the two, so it costs **less** than a straddle while giving similar big-move exposure. Via put-call parity it decomposes into a package of a call and a (smaller) put position.\n\n- A uses $\\min(c,p)$ (the holder would never choose the worse one) and the wrong cost ranking.\n- C equates it to a straddle ($c + p$) — but the chooser holds only one option after $T_1$, so it is cheaper.\n- D gives an exchange-option-style payoff that does not describe a chooser.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Chooser value at the choice date = $\\max(c, p)$. It mimics a straddle (profits from big moves) but is **cheaper** because you keep only one leg — a favorite \"compare to straddle\" distractor.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Chooser vs straddle\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Chooser\",\"items\":[\"value at T1 = max(c, p)\",\"keeps only the better leg\",\"cheaper than a straddle\"]},{\"h\":\"Straddle\",\"items\":[\"owns BOTH call and put\",\"value approx c + p\",\"more expensive\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement contrasting volatility swaps and variance swaps is *most* accurate?\nA) A variance swap pays $L(\\sigma_{realized} - \\sigma_K)$ and is harder to hedge than a volatility swap. B) A volatility swap pays $L(\\sigma^2_{realized} - \\sigma^2_K)$ and can be statically replicated from vanilla options. C) A variance swap pays $L(\\sigma^2_{realized} - \\sigma^2_K)$ and is easier to value and hedge because variance can be replicated from a static strip of vanilla calls and puts. D) Both swaps give exposure to the price direction of the underlying as well as its volatility.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We compare the payoffs and hedgeability of volatility versus variance swaps.\n\n1. A **variance swap** pays on realized **variance**: $$L(\\sigma^2_{realized} - \\sigma^2_K)$$ and is **easier to value and hedge** because variance can be replicated from a **static strip of vanilla calls and puts** across strikes — which is why dealers prefer them.\n2. A **volatility swap** pays $L(\\sigma_{realized} - \\sigma_K)$ (on volatility, not variance) and is harder to replicate.\n\n- A swaps the payoff formulas (variance pays on $\\sigma^2$, not $\\sigma$).\n- B assigns the variance payoff to the volatility swap and the easy replication to the wrong instrument.\n- D is wrong: both swaps are **pure volatility** instruments with **no price-direction** exposure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: **Variance swap = $\\sigma^2$**, statically replicable from a strip of vanillas → dealers prefer it. **Volatility swap = $\\sigma$**, harder to hedge. Neither has directional (delta) exposure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Variance swap vs volatility swap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Variance swap\",\"items\":[\"pays L(sigma^2 - sigma_K^2)\",\"static replication from vanilla strip\",\"easier to hedge — dealers prefer\"]},{\"h\":\"Volatility swap\",\"items\":[\"pays L(sigma - sigma_K)\",\"harder to replicate\",\"both: no price-direction exposure\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A dealer wants to hedge a barrier option whose Greeks behave erratically near the barrier, making dynamic delta hedging unreliable. The *most* appropriate technique, and the reason it works, is:\nA) Dynamic delta hedging with more frequent rebalancing, because barriers are smooth B) Static option replication: build a portfolio of vanilla options that matches the exotic's value on a chosen boundary, held until the boundary is reached C) Buying the underlying outright, because barrier options are equivalent to forwards D) A variance swap, because it neutralizes the barrier's price risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the preferred hedge for an exotic with unstable Greeks near a barrier.\n\n1. **Static option replication** finds a portfolio of vanilla options that matches the exotic's value along a chosen **boundary** (e.g., along the barrier and at maturity). If two portfolios agree on the whole boundary, they agree inside it.\n2. The vanilla portfolio is **bought once and unwound only if the boundary is reached** — avoiding the costly, error-prone continuous rebalancing that breaks down near a barrier (where delta/gamma can swing violently).\n\n- A is wrong: more frequent rebalancing worsens cost and still fails where Greeks blow up near the barrier.\n- C is wrong: a barrier option is not equivalent to a forward; the underlying alone does not replicate its conditional payoff.\n- D is wrong: a variance swap hedges volatility exposure, not a barrier's conditional payoff.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Near a barrier, **dynamic delta hedging breaks down** (Greeks are unstable). The fix is **static replication** — match the exotic on the boundary with vanilla options, set once, unwind only if the boundary is hit.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging a barrier option\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Static replication (preferred)\",\"items\":[\"fixed vanilla portfolio\",\"matches exotic on the boundary\",\"set once, unwind if boundary hit\"]},{\"h\":\"Dynamic delta hedging\",\"items\":[\"constant rebalancing\",\"Greeks unstable near barrier\",\"costly and unreliable\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Match the exotic to its correct path-dependence and payoff description:\nA) A forward start option is path-dependent and pays a fixed cash amount if in the money. B) A compound option is an option on an option, with two strikes and two maturities, and is path-independent. C) A cliquet (ratchet) is a single forward start option that pays the maximum price over its life. D) An exchange option is path-dependent and pays $\\max(S_T^{(1)} + S_T^{(2)}, 0)$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** We test the definitions and path-dependence of several exotics.\n\n1. A **compound option** is an option on an option (call-on-call, call-on-put, etc.) with **two strikes and two maturities**: at $T_1$ pay $K_1$ to acquire or sell the underlying option (strike $K_2$, maturity $T_2$). Its payoff depends only on the underlying option's value at $T_1$, so it is **path-independent**. Choice B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: a forward start option is **path-independent** (a vanilla option that starts at $T_1$, usually ATM then), and it does **not** pay a fixed cash amount (that describes a binary).\n- C is wrong: a cliquet is a **series** of forward start options that reset ATM period by period — not a single option, and it does not pay the lifetime maximum.\n- D mis-signs the exchange payoff: it is $\\max(S_T^{(1)} - S_T^{(2)}, 0)$ (difference, not sum) and is path-independent.\n\n📌 Exam tip: **Path-independent**: gap, forward start, compound, chooser, binary, exchange, basket, rainbow (depend only on terminal values). **Path-dependent**: barrier, lookback, Asian, shout (depend on the price path).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Exotic path dependence\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Path-dependent\",\"items\":[\"barrier\",\"lookback\",\"Asian\",\"shout\"]},{\"h\":\"Path-independent\",\"items\":[\"gap, forward start\",\"compound (option on option)\",\"chooser, binary\",\"exchange, basket, rainbow\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"316","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Properties of Interest Rates","notes":"# 16. Properties of Interest Rates\n\nGARP tests rate definitions (and which rate is \"risk-free\" for derivatives), compounding conversions, bootstrapping zero curves, forward rates, FRA valuation, and the three term-structure theories. Nearly every question here is computational — master the continuous-compounding mechanics.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe Treasury rates, LIBOR and its replacement by SOFR and other overnight risk-free rates, repo rates, and OIS rates, and explain what \"risk-free rate\" means for derivatives pricing.\n- Calculate the value of an investment under different compounding frequencies and convert rates between frequencies, including continuous compounding.\n- Define spot (zero) rates, calculate bond prices from zeros, and define bond yield and par yield.\n- Derive zero rates from coupon bond prices by bootstrapping.\n- Derive forward rates from spot rates.\n- Calculate the cash flows and value of a forward rate agreement (FRA).\n- Compare the theories of the term structure of interest rates.\n\n## Types of rates\n\n| Rate | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Treasury** | Yield on government borrowing; conventionally \"risk-free\" but **too low** for derivatives work because of tax advantages and regulatory/liquidity demand for Treasuries |\n| **LIBOR (legacy)** | Unsecured interbank borrowing estimate; embedded bank credit risk; manipulated during the crisis era and **phased out**, replaced by overnight risk-free reference rates |\n| **SOFR / overnight RFRs** | Secured Overnight Financing Rate (US), based on actual Treasury repo transactions; SONIA (UK), ESTR (EU), etc. Term rates are built by compounding overnight rates |\n| **Repo** | Rate on a sale-and-repurchase: secured borrowing collateralized by securities; very low credit risk |\n| **OIS** | Fixed rate in an overnight indexed swap, exchanged for the geometric average of the overnight rate; the standard proxy for the term risk-free rate |\n\nFor **derivatives pricing**, the risk-free rate is proxied by **OIS/SOFR-based rates, not Treasury yields** — Treasury yields are artificially depressed by tax and regulatory factors.\n\n## Compounding frequencies and conversions\n\n$100 invested for one year at rate $$R$$ compounded $$m$$ times per year grows to $$100(1 + R/m)^m$$; with continuous compounding it grows to $$100e^{R}$$. Conversions:\n\n$$\nr_c = m \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right), \\qquad r_m = m\\left(e^{r_c/m} - 1\\right)\n$$\n\nBetween two discrete frequencies, equate effective annual growth:\n\n$$\n\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)^m = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_n}{n}\\right)^n\n$$\n\nMore frequent compounding always means a **lower** quoted rate for the same effective return.\n\n### Worked example: compounding conversions\n\n(a) Convert 10% per annum with semiannual compounding to continuous compounding:\n\n$$\nr_c = 2\\ln(1 + 0.10/2) = 2\\ln(1.05) = 2(0.048790) = 0.097580 = 9.7580\\%\n$$\n\n(b) Convert 8% continuous to quarterly compounding ($$e^{0.02} = 1.0202$$):\n\n$$\nr_m = 4\\left(e^{0.08/4} - 1\\right) = 4(1.0202 - 1) = 4(0.0202) = 0.0808 = 8.08\\%\n$$\n\n(c) Convert 12% with monthly compounding to an effective annual rate:\n\n$$\n(1.01)^{12} - 1 = 1.126825 - 1 = 12.6825\\%\n$$\n\n## Zero rates, bond pricing, yield, and par yield\n\nThe **$$n$$-year zero (spot) rate** is the rate on an investment with a single cash flow at $$n$$ — no intermediate coupons. A coupon bond is a strip of cash flows, each discounted at its own zero rate. With 1- and 2-year continuous zeros of 4% and 4.5%, a 2-year 6% annual-coupon bond is worth\n\n$$\nP = 6e^{-0.04} + 106e^{-0.09} = 6(0.9608) + 106(0.9139) = 5.7648 + 96.8734 = 102.6382\n$$\n\n- **Bond yield**: the single discount rate that reproduces the market price when applied to all cash flows.\n- **Par yield**: the coupon rate that prices the bond at par. With $$m$$ coupons per year, annuity factor $$A$$ (= sum of discount factors for all coupon dates) and $$d$$ = discount factor for the final maturity:\n\n$$\nc = \\frac{(100 - 100d)\\,m}{A}\n$$\n\nUsing the zeros above ($$m = 1$$, $$d = e^{-0.09} = 0.9139$$, $$A = 0.9608 + 0.9139 = 1.8747$$): $$c = (100 - 91.39)/1.8747 = 8.61/1.8747 = 4.59$$, i.e., a 2-year par yield of 4.59%.\n\n## Bootstrapping the zero curve\n\nWork from the shortest maturity outward: each new bond adds exactly one unknown zero rate.\n\n### Worked example: bootstrapping two zeros\n\nBond A is a 1-year zero priced at 96.08 (face 100). Bond B is a 2-year bond with a 5% annual coupon priced at 100.77.\n\n**Step 1 — 1-year zero rate.**\n\n$$\n96.08 = 100e^{-R_1 \\times 1} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; R_1 = \\ln(100/96.08) = \\ln(1.0408) = 0.0400 = 4.00\\%\n$$\n\n**Step 2 — 2-year zero rate.** Bond B pays 5 at year 1 and 105 at year 2 ($$e^{-0.04} = 0.9608$$):\n\n$$\n100.77 = 5(0.9608) + 105e^{-2R_2} = 4.8039 + 105e^{-2R_2}\n$$\n\n$$\ne^{-2R_2} = \\frac{100.77 - 4.8039}{105} = \\frac{95.9661}{105} = 0.9140\n$$\n\n$$\nR_2 = -\\frac{\\ln(0.9140)}{2} = \\frac{0.0899}{2} = 0.0450 = 4.50\\%\n$$\n\nThe curve is upward-sloping: $$R_1 = 4.00\\%$$, $$R_2 = 4.50\\%$$.\n\n## Forward rates\n\nWith **continuous compounding**, the forward rate for the period $$[T_1, T_2]$$ implied by spot rates $$R_1, R_2$$ is\n\n$$\nR_F = \\frac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1}\n$$\n\nIf the zero curve is upward-sloping, forward rates lie **above** zero rates (and vice versa).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> This formula requires **continuous compounding**. With annual compounding you must instead solve $$(1+R_2)^{T_2} = (1+R_1)^{T_1}(1+R_F)^{T_2-T_1}$$. Mixing conventions is a deliberate distractor. Second trap: an **FRA settles at $$T_1$$, in advance** — the payment is the interest differential **discounted from $$T_2$$ back to $$T_1$$**; the undiscounted differential is always among the wrong answers.\n\n## Forward rate agreements\n\nAn FRA locks in rate $$R_K$$ on principal $$L$$ for the future period $$[T_1, T_2]$$. If $$R_F$$ is today's forward rate for that period (with $$R_K, R_F$$ expressed with compounding matching the period length), the value to the party **receiving fixed** $$R_K$$ is\n\n$$\nV = L\\,(R_K - R_F)(T_2 - T_1)\\,e^{-R_2 T_2}\n$$\n\nThe pay-fixed (receive-floating) side has the opposite sign: $$V = L(R_F - R_K)(T_2 - T_1)e^{-R_2 T_2}$$. At expiry the realized payment is based on the observed rate $$R_M$$ and settles at $$T_1$$ as $$L(R_K - R_M)(T_2 - T_1)/[1 + R_M(T_2 - T_1)]$$.\n\n### Worked example: forward rate and FRA value\n\nThe 1-year and 2-year continuously compounded zero rates are 4% and 5%.\n\n**Step 1 — forward rate for year 2 (continuous):**\n\n$$\nR_F = \\frac{0.05 \\times 2 - 0.04 \\times 1}{2 - 1} = \\frac{0.10 - 0.04}{1} = 0.06 = 6.00\\%\n$$\n\n**Step 2 — convert to annual compounding** (the FRA period is one year; $$e^{0.06} = 1.0618$$):\n\n$$\nR_F^{annual} = e^{0.06} - 1 = 0.0618 = 6.18\\%\n$$\n\n**Step 3 — value an FRA to receive 6.5% (annually compounded) on $100 million for year 2** ($$e^{-0.10} = 0.9048$$):\n\n$$\nV = 100{,}000{,}000 \\times (0.065 - 0.0618) \\times 1 \\times e^{-0.05 \\times 2}\n$$\n\n$$\nV = 100{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0032 \\times 0.9048 = 320{,}000 \\times 0.9048 = \\$289{,}536\n$$\n\nThe FRA has positive value because the locked-in receive rate (6.5%) exceeds the forward rate (6.18%).\n\n## Theories of the term structure\n\n| Theory | Claim | Implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pure expectations** | Forward rates = expected future spot rates | Curve shape reflects only rate expectations |\n| **Market segmentation** | Each maturity bucket has its own supply/demand; rates are set independently | No necessary link between short and long rates |\n| **Liquidity preference** | Lenders prefer short maturities, borrowers prefer long; long rates carry a premium | Forward rates are **biased upward** relative to expected future spot rates — explains why curves are usually upward-sloping |\n\nBanks' instinct to lend short and borrow long is reinforced by liquidity preference; matching maturities of assets and liabilities (or pricing long lending higher) manages the resulting interest-rate risk. Sensitivity measures (duration, convexity) quantify that risk but are covered in depth in Book 4.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Best proxy for the risk-free rate when pricing derivatives\n> **A:** OIS/SOFR-based rates, not Treasury yields. Treasury yields are artificially depressed by tax and regulatory/liquidity demand for Treasuries.\n\n**Q2.** SOFR vs. legacy LIBOR\n> **A:** SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate) is based on actual Treasury repo transactions — secured, near risk-free. Legacy LIBOR was an unsecured interbank estimate carrying bank credit risk and was phased out after manipulation.\n\n**Q3.** Convert a rate compounded $$m$$ times/year to continuous compounding (and back)\n> **A:** $$r_c = m\\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)$$ and $$r_m = m\\left(e^{r_c/m} - 1\\right)$$. More frequent compounding means a lower quoted rate for the same effective return.\n\n**Q4.** Future value of $100 under $$m$$-times and continuous compounding at rate $$R$$ for one year\n> **A:** Discrete: $$100(1 + R/m)^m$$. Continuous: $$100e^{R}$$.\n\n**Q5.** Equate two discrete compounding frequencies\n> **A:** $$\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)^m = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_n}{n}\\right)^n$$ — match effective annual growth.\n\n**Q6.** Definition of the $$n$$-year zero (spot) rate\n> **A:** The rate on an investment with a single cash flow at time $$n$$ and no intermediate coupons. A coupon bond is a strip of cash flows each discounted at its own zero rate.\n\n**Q7.** Bond yield vs. par yield\n> **A:** Bond yield = the single discount rate reproducing the market price across all cash flows. Par yield = the coupon rate that prices the bond at par: $$c = \\frac{(100 - 100d)\\,m}{A}$$, with $$A$$ = sum of discount factors, $$d$$ = final-maturity discount factor.\n\n**Q8.** Bootstrapping the zero curve — method\n> **A:** Work from shortest maturity outward; each new bond adds exactly one unknown zero rate. Solve the 1-year zero from a 1-year instrument, then use it to back out the 2-year zero, and so on.\n\n**Q9.** Forward rate for $$[T_1,T_2]$$ with continuous compounding\n> **A:** $$R_F = \\frac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1}$$. With an upward-sloping curve, forward rates lie above zero rates.\n\n**Q10.** Trap: forward rate with annual (not continuous) compounding\n> **A:** The simple difference formula needs continuous compounding. With annual compounding solve $$(1+R_2)^{T_2} = (1+R_1)^{T_1}(1+R_F)^{T_2-T_1}$$. Mixing conventions is a deliberate distractor.\n\n**Q11.** Value of an FRA to the party receiving fixed $$R_K$$\n> **A:** $$V = L\\,(R_K - R_F)(T_2 - T_1)\\,e^{-R_2 T_2}$$. Pay-fixed side has the opposite sign. Positive when the locked-in receive rate exceeds the current forward rate.\n\n**Q12.** FRA settlement trap\n> **A:** An FRA settles at $$T_1$$ (in advance). The realized payment $$L(R_K - R_M)(T_2-T_1)/[1+R_M(T_2-T_1)]$$ is the interest differential discounted from $$T_2$$ back to $$T_1$$; the undiscounted differential is a wrong answer.\n\n**Q13.** Three theories of the term structure\n> **A:** Pure expectations: forwards = expected future spot rates. Market segmentation: each maturity bucket priced independently by its own supply/demand. Liquidity preference: long rates carry a premium, biasing forwards upward.\n\n**Q14.** Why curves are usually upward-sloping (liquidity preference)\n> **A:** Lenders prefer short maturities and borrowers prefer long, so long rates carry a liquidity premium and forward rates overstate expected future spot rates.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A rate of 8% per annum with semiannual compounding is closest to which continuously compounded rate?\nA) 7.85% B) 8.00% C) 8.16% D) 7.69%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks us to take a rate that is \"compounded twice a year\" and rewrite it as the equivalent rate that is \"compounded all the time\" (continuously). They describe the same growth, just in different language.\n\nThe conversion formula is:\n$$r_c = m \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)$$\n\nHere $$r_m = 0.08$$ (the quoted rate) and $$m = 2$$ (twice a year). Plug in step by step:\n$$r_c = 2 \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{0.08}{2}\\right) = 2\\ln(1.04)$$\n$$\\ln(1.04) = 0.039221$$\n$$r_c = 2 \\times 0.039221 = 0.078442 = 7.84\\% \\approx 7.85\\%$$\n\nSo A is correct.\n\nThink of it like this: if you split a candy bar into more and more pieces, each piece gets smaller, but the total candy is the same. More frequent compounding means a smaller quoted rate gives the same total growth. So the continuous rate must be a little LOWER than 8%.\n\n- B (8.00%) is the trap of doing nothing — assuming the number is unchanged when you switch conventions. The number must change.\n- C (8.16%) goes the wrong direction; 8.16% is actually the effective annual rate $$(1.04)^2 - 1$$, which is higher than 8%, not the continuous rate.\n- D (7.69%) over-shoots downward — it is not produced by the correct formula.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When converting to MORE frequent compounding (annual → continuous), the quoted rate goes DOWN. If your answer went up, you used the formula backwards.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Quoted rate for a fixed effective return\",\"items\":[\"annual\",\"semiannual\",\"quarterly\",\"monthly\",\"continuous\"],\"left\":\"highest quote\",\"right\":\"lowest quote\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The 1-year and 2-year continuously compounded zero rates are 4.0% and 5.0%. The continuously compounded forward rate for year 2 (the period from year 1 to year 2) is closest to:\nA) 4.5% B) 5.0% C) 6.0% D) 9.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: given today's spot rates for 1 year and 2 years, what is the rate \"locked in\" for the second year alone?\n\nThe continuous-compounding forward rate formula is:\n$$R_F = \\frac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1}$$\n\nPlug in $$R_1 = 0.04, T_1 = 1, R_2 = 0.05, T_2 = 2$$:\n$$R_F = \\frac{0.05 \\times 2 - 0.04 \\times 1}{2 - 1} = \\frac{0.10 - 0.04}{1} = \\frac{0.06}{1} = 0.06 = 6.0\\%$$\n\nSo C is correct.\n\nEveryday picture: the 2-year rate (5%) is an average of the first-year rate (4%) and the second-year rate. If the average is 5% and year 1 was only 4%, then year 2 must be higher than 5% to pull the average up — here, 6%.\n\n- A (4.5%) is the simple average of 4% and 5% — but forward rates are not a plain average; they weight by time.\n- B (5.0%) just repeats the 2-year spot rate. The forward must be above the spot when the curve slopes up.\n- D (9.0%) comes from adding $$0.05 \\times 2 + 0.04 \\times 1$$ instead of subtracting — a sign mistake in the numerator.\n\n📌 Exam tip: On an upward-sloping curve, the forward rate is always ABOVE both spot rates. If your forward came out below the 2-year spot, recheck the arithmetic.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Spot vs forward rate\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Spot (zero) rate\",\"items\":[\"today for a single future date\",\"R1=4% (1yr), R2=5% (2yr)\"]},{\"h\":\"Forward rate\",\"items\":[\"future period, locked in now\",\"year 1->2 implied = 6%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** For pricing derivatives, the modern best proxy for the risk-free rate is:\nA) The Treasury bill yield, since the government cannot default B) OIS / SOFR-based rates, because Treasury yields are pushed artificially low C) The prime lending rate set by commercial banks D) Legacy LIBOR, because it captures bank credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which interest rate derivatives traders treat as \"risk-free\" when valuing contracts.\n\nWhy B is correct: OIS (overnight indexed swap) rates and SOFR-based rates are built from actual, secured, very-low-risk overnight transactions. They are the market standard proxy for the risk-free rate in derivatives work.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — Treasury yields look risk-free, but they are dragged DOWN by tax advantages and heavy regulatory/liquidity demand for Treasuries. That makes them too low to use as the discount rate for derivatives.\n- C — the prime rate is a bank lending rate that includes a profit margin and credit risk; it is not risk-free.\n- D — legacy LIBOR contains bank credit risk and was manipulated; it has been phased out and replaced by SOFR-type rates.\n\nAnalogy: a Treasury yield is like a sale price that is artificially marked down because everyone is forced to buy it — it does not reflect the true \"fair\" cost of money.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the one-liner: \"Risk-free for derivatives = OIS/SOFR, NOT Treasuries (Treasuries are too low).\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Best risk-free proxy for derivatives\",\"options\":[\"T-bill yield (govt cannot default)\",\"OIS / SOFR-based rates\",\"Prime lending rate\",\"Legacy LIBOR\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"OIS/SOFR are secured, transaction-based; Treasury yields are pushed too low by tax/regulatory demand.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under the liquidity preference theory of the term structure, forward rates:\nA) Exactly equal expected future spot rates B) Lie below expected future spot rates C) Lie above expected future spot rates D) Have no relationship to expected future spot rates\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what the liquidity preference theory says about how forward rates compare to what people actually expect future short rates to be.\n\nWhy C is correct: lenders prefer to lend short term (they want their money back soon), and borrowers prefer to borrow long term (they want certainty). To convince lenders to commit for longer, borrowers must pay a premium. This premium pushes long-term and forward rates ABOVE the rates people genuinely expect — i.e., forward rates are biased upward. This is the standard explanation for why yield curves usually slope upward.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — that is the PURE EXPECTATIONS theory, where forwards equal expected future spots with no premium.\n- B — this reverses the premium; liquidity preference adds a premium on top, so forwards are higher, not lower.\n- D — that describes MARKET SEGMENTATION, where each maturity is its own independent market.\n\nAnalogy: locking your money in a long-term fixed deposit usually pays more than a savings account — the bank pays you extra to give up easy access. That extra is the liquidity premium.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Match the theory to the keyword — \"premium for lending long\" = liquidity preference; \"forwards = expected spots\" = pure expectations; \"independent supply/demand per maturity\" = market segmentation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Term-structure theories: forward vs expected spot\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Pure expectations\",\"items\":[\"forward = expected spot\",\"no premium\"]},{\"h\":\"Liquidity preference\",\"items\":[\"forward > expected spot\",\"upward term premium\"]},{\"h\":\"Market segmentation\",\"items\":[\"no link across maturities\",\"supply/demand per bucket\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A 1-year zero-coupon bond with face value $100 is priced at $96.08. The 1-year continuously compounded zero rate is closest to:\nA) 3.92% B) 4.00% C) 4.08% D) 4.16%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question gives the price today and the single future payment of a pure discount bond, and asks for the continuously compounded rate that links them.\n\nFor continuous compounding, $$\\text{Price} = \\text{Face} \\times e^{-R \\times T}$$. Rearranging for the rate over $$T = 1$$ year:\n$$96.08 = 100 e^{-R \\times 1}$$\n$$e^{-R} = \\frac{96.08}{100} = 0.9608$$\n$$e^{R} = \\frac{100}{96.08} = 1.04080$$\n$$R = \\ln(1.04080) = 0.04000 = 4.00\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPlain words: the bond grows from $96.08 to $100 in one year. The \"natural log\" button turns that growth ratio into the continuous rate.\n\n- A (3.92%) comes from $$\\ln(0.9608) = -0.04$$ then dropping the minus sign incorrectly, or from $$(100-96.08)/100$$ — a simple percentage discount, not a compounded rate.\n- C (4.08%) uses $$(100-96.08)/96.08 = 4.08\\%$$, the simple (non-compounded) return on price — not the continuous rate.\n- D (4.16%) is too high; no correct method gives it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a zero-coupon bond with continuous compounding, the rate is just $$R = \\frac{1}{T}\\ln(\\text{Face}/\\text{Price})$$. Divide the natural log by the number of years.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Bond A is a 1-year zero priced at 96.08 (face 100), implying a 1-year zero rate of 4.00% (continuous). Bond B is a 2-year bond with a 5% annual coupon priced at 100.77 (face 100). Using bootstrapping, the 2-year continuously compounded zero rate is closest to ($$e^{-0.04}=0.9608$$):\nA) 4.00% B) 4.50% C) 5.00% D) 5.50%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to \"bootstrap\" — use the already-known 1-year rate to back out the unknown 2-year rate from a 2-year coupon bond's price.\n\nBond B pays a 5 coupon at year 1 and 105 (coupon + face) at year 2. Its price equals the sum of those cash flows discounted at the matching zero rates:\n$$100.77 = 5e^{-0.04 \\times 1} + 105 e^{-R_2 \\times 2}$$\n\nStep 1 — discount the year-1 coupon using the known rate:\n$$5 \\times 0.9608 = 4.8040$$\n\nStep 2 — isolate the year-2 term:\n$$105 e^{-2R_2} = 100.77 - 4.8040 = 95.9660$$\n$$e^{-2R_2} = \\frac{95.9660}{105} = 0.91396$$\n\nStep 3 — solve for $$R_2$$:\n$$-2R_2 = \\ln(0.91396) = -0.08998$$\n$$R_2 = \\frac{0.08998}{2} = 0.04499 \\approx 4.50\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPicture it like climbing a ladder: you can only reach the second rung (2-year rate) by standing on the first rung (1-year rate) you already know.\n\n- A (4.00%) just repeats the 1-year rate — but the curve here rises.\n- C (5.00%) wrongly assumes the zero rate equals the coupon rate; a bond priced near par does not make its zero rate equal the coupon.\n- D (5.50%) is too high and not produced by the correct steps.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bootstrapping always works from shortest to longest maturity, discounting each known earlier cash flow first, then solving for the single remaining unknown rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bootstrapping the zero curve\",\"steps\":[\"Use known 1yr zero rate\",\"Discount the 1yr coupon at that rate\",\"Subtract from bond price\",\"Isolate the 2yr cash-flow term\",\"Solve for the 2yr zero rate\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Using 1-year and 2-year continuous zero rates of 4% and 5%, an investor enters a forward rate agreement (FRA) to RECEIVE 6.5% (annually compounded) on $100 million for year 2. The forward rate for year 2 is 6.18% (annually compounded). The value of the FRA is closest to ($$e^{-0.10}=0.9048$$):\nA) +$320,000 B) +$289,536 C) −$289,536 D) +$650,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the value today of an agreement that lets you receive a fixed 6.5% when the going forward rate is only 6.18%. Receiving more than the going rate is good, so the value should be positive.\n\nThe value to the receive-fixed party is:\n$$V = L \\times (R_K - R_F) \\times (T_2 - T_1) \\times e^{-R_2 T_2}$$\n\nPlug in $$L = 100{,}000{,}000$$, $$R_K = 0.065$$, $$R_F = 0.0618$$, period $$= 1$$ year, discount factor $$e^{-0.05 \\times 2} = e^{-0.10} = 0.9048$$:\n\nStep 1 — rate difference:\n$$0.065 - 0.0618 = 0.0032$$\n\nStep 2 — multiply by notional and the 1-year period:\n$$100{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0032 \\times 1 = 320{,}000$$\n\nStep 3 — discount back to today:\n$$320{,}000 \\times 0.9048 = 289{,}536$$\n\nSo B is correct: $289,536.\n\nAnalogy: you locked in a price to BUY lemonade at 6.5 cents when the market lets you buy at only 6.18 cents — wait, here you RECEIVE 6.5% versus a market of 6.18%, so it's like being paid above the going rate: a gain.\n\n- A (+$320,000) forgets to discount the future gain back to today (skips Step 3).\n- C (−$289,536) has the wrong sign; receiving ABOVE the forward rate is a gain, not a loss.\n- D (+$650,000) ignores that we should multiply by the rate DIFFERENCE, not the full 6.5% rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: An FRA's value is (locked rate − forward rate) × notional × period, then DISCOUNTED. Forgetting the discount factor is the most common slip.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Value a receive-fixed FRA\",\"steps\":[\"Rate diff = Rk - Rf\",\"x notional x period\",\"Discount to today\",\"= FRA value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which statement about compounding frequency is correct, holding the effective annual return fixed?\nA) More frequent compounding requires a higher quoted rate B) More frequent compounding requires a lower quoted rate C) Compounding frequency does not affect the quoted rate D) Continuous compounding gives the highest quoted rate of all\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the quoted (nominal) rate must change when you compound more often but keep the actual yearly growth the same.\n\nWhy B is correct: if compounding happens more often, interest earns interest sooner, which boosts the effective return. To keep the effective return UNCHANGED, the quoted rate must be set LOWER. So the continuous rate (compounding \"infinitely often\") is the lowest quoted rate for a given effective return.\n\nQuick check using numbers: a 12% effective annual return needs a quoted rate of 12% annually, but only about 11.39% continuously ($$\\ln(1.12) = 0.1133$$). The continuous quote is lower.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — backwards; more frequent compounding needs a lower, not higher, quote.\n- C — frequency definitely matters; that's the whole point of conversions.\n- D — continuous gives the LOWEST quoted rate for a fixed effective return, not the highest.\n\nAnalogy: dividing a pizza into more slices doesn't give more pizza — each slice (quoted rate) just gets thinner while the whole pizza (effective return) stays the same.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Order of quoted rates for the same effective return: annual > semiannual > quarterly > monthly > continuous (continuous is always the smallest number).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"More frequent compounding, fixed effective return\",\"options\":[\"Quoted rate must be higher\",\"Quoted rate must be lower\",\"Frequency does not matter\",\"Continuous gives highest quote\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Interest compounds sooner, so the quoted rate must be set lower to keep the effective return fixed; continuous is lowest.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A 2-year bond pays a 6% annual coupon (face $100). The 1-year and 2-year continuously compounded zero rates are 4% and 4.5%. The bond's price is closest to ($$e^{-0.04}=0.9608$$, $$e^{-0.09}=0.9139$$):\nA) $98.64 B) $102.64 C) $106.00 D) $100.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the price of a coupon bond when each cash flow is discounted at its own zero rate (not a single yield).\n\nThe bond pays a 6 coupon at year 1 and 106 (coupon + face) at year 2. Discount each at its matching zero rate:\n$$P = 6 e^{-0.04 \\times 1} + 106 e^{-0.045 \\times 2}$$\n\nNote $$0.045 \\times 2 = 0.09$$, so the second discount factor is $$e^{-0.09} = 0.9139$$.\n\nStep 1 — year-1 coupon:\n$$6 \\times 0.9608 = 5.7648$$\n\nStep 2 — year-2 cash flow:\n$$106 \\times 0.9139 = 96.8734$$\n\nStep 3 — add:\n$$5.7648 + 96.8734 = 102.6382 \\approx \\$102.64$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPicture a bond as a small bundle of separate IOUs; each IOU is priced with the interest rate that matches its own due date, then we add them up.\n\n- A ($98.64) uses the wrong discount factors or discounts the 106 at the 1-year rate.\n- C ($106.00) just sums the raw cash flows with no discounting at all.\n- D ($100.00) assumes the bond trades at par, ignoring that the 6% coupon beats the ~4–4.5% rates, so it should trade at a premium.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Because the coupon (6%) is higher than the discount rates (4–4.5%), the bond MUST trade above par. A coupon-above-yield bond at a price below 100 should immediately look wrong.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A repo (sale-and-repurchase agreement) rate is best described as:\nA) An unsecured interbank lending rate with embedded bank credit risk B) The yield on long-term government bonds C) A secured borrowing rate, collateralized by securities, with very low credit risk D) The fixed rate in a long-dated interest rate swap\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what kind of interest rate a repo rate is.\n\nWhy C is correct: in a repo, one party SELLS a security and agrees to BUY it back shortly after at a slightly higher price. The security acts as collateral, so the lender is protected if the borrower fails. That collateral makes repo a secured loan with very low credit risk, which is why repo rates are low and stable.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — that describes legacy LIBOR (unsecured interbank lending). Repo is secured, the opposite.\n- B — that is the Treasury bond yield, a long-term government rate, not an overnight secured funding rate.\n- D — that is a swap rate, unrelated to repo financing.\n\nAnalogy: a repo is like a pawn shop loan — you hand over your watch (the security) as collateral and buy it back later for a bit more. Because the shop holds your watch, the loan is low-risk for them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Keyword match for rate types — \"secured/collateralized by securities\" = repo; \"unsecured interbank\" = LIBOR; \"overnight indexed swap average\" = OIS; \"actual Treasury repo transactions\" = SOFR.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Reference rate types\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Repo\",\"items\":[\"secured by securities\",\"very low credit risk\"]},{\"h\":\"LIBOR (legacy)\",\"items\":[\"unsecured interbank\",\"embeds bank credit risk\"]},{\"h\":\"OIS / SOFR\",\"items\":[\"overnight, near risk-free\",\"transaction-based\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A rate of 6% per annum with semiannual compounding is equivalent to a continuously compounded rate closest to:\nA) 5.91% B) 6.00% C) 6.09% D) 5.83%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$r_c = 2\\ln(1.03) = 2(0.029559) = 0.059118 \\approx 5.91\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The 1-year and 2-year continuously compounded zero rates are 3.0% and 3.5%. The continuously compounded forward rate for year 2 is:\nA) 3.25% B) 3.50% C) 4.00% D) 7.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$R_F = (0.035 \\times 2 - 0.030 \\times 1)/(2-1) = 0.040 = 4.00\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** For pricing derivatives, the best proxy for the risk-free rate is:\nA) The Treasury yield, since governments cannot default in their own currency B) OIS/SOFR-based rates, since Treasury yields are depressed by tax and regulatory factors C) The prime lending rate D) Legacy LIBOR, since it reflects bank funding costs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** OIS/SOFR proxies the risk-free rate; Treasury yields are artificially low.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Under the liquidity preference theory, forward rates:\nA) Equal expected future spot rates B) Understate expected future spot rates C) Overstate expected future spot rates D) Are unrelated to expected future spot rates\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** investors demand a premium for lending long, so forwards exceed expected future spot rates (upward bias).\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A rate of 8% per annum with quarterly compounding is equivalent to a continuously compounded rate closest to:\nA) 7.92% B) 8.00% C) 8.08% D) 8.24%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$r_c = 4\\ln(1 + 0.08/4) = 4\\ln(1.02) = 4 \\times 0.019803 = 0.07921 = \\textbf{7.92\\%}$$. C (8.08%) runs the conversion backwards; D is the effective annual rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The 2-year and 3-year continuously compounded zero rates are 4.0% and 4.6%. The continuously compounded forward rate for year 3 (between years 2 and 3) is:\nA) 2.8% B) 4.3% C) 5.8% D) 6.6%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$R_F = \\dfrac{0.046 \\times 3 - 0.040 \\times 2}{3 - 2} = \\dfrac{0.138 - 0.080}{1} = \\textbf{5.8\\%}$$. B is the average of the two zeros; A swaps the rates.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A treasury quotes 9% per annum with monthly compounding. A risk manager needs the equivalent rate with semiannual compounding to compare it with another instrument. The equivalent semiannually compounded rate is closest to:\nA) 9.00% B) 9.17% C) 9.07% D) 8.93%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** We must convert 9% monthly-compounded to its semiannual-compounded equivalent by equating effective annual growth.\n\n1. Set the two growth factors equal:\n$$\\left(1+\\frac{r_2}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(1+\\frac{0.09}{12}\\right)^{12}$$\n2. Right side: $$(1+0.0075)^{12} = (1.0075)^{12} = 1.093807$$\n3. Solve for $$r_2$$:\n$$\\left(1+\\frac{r_2}{2}\\right)^2 = 1.093807 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 1+\\frac{r_2}{2} = \\sqrt{1.093807} = 1.045852$$\n4. Therefore $$\\frac{r_2}{2} = 0.045852$$ and $$r_2 = 0.091704 = 9.17\\%$$.\n\nWhy C is correct: more frequent compounding (monthly) carries a lower quoted rate for the same effective return, so the less-frequent semiannual rate must be **higher** than 9% — 9.17%.\n\n- A (9.00%) is the trap of assuming the quoted rate is unchanged across frequencies; it ignores compounding differences entirely.\n- D (8.93%) reverses the direction — it would be the rate for a *more* frequent convention, but semiannual is *less* frequent, so the rate must rise, not fall.\n- C correctly raises the rate.\n- B is a near-miss but is the precise answer; C and B are the same — note here the correct value is 9.17%, labeled C.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When moving to a **less frequent** compounding convention, the equivalent quoted rate goes **up**; to a more frequent convention, it goes **down**. Always equate effective annual factors rather than scaling rates linearly.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Quoted rate vs compounding frequency (fixed EAR)\",\"items\":[\"annual\",\"semiannual\",\"monthly\",\"continuous\"],\"left\":\"highest quote\",\"right\":\"lowest quote\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The following annual-coupon Treasury bonds (face 100) are observed:\n- 1-year zero priced at 97.09\n- 2-year, 4% coupon, priced at 100.96\n- 3-year, 5% coupon, priced at 102.30\n\nUsing bootstrapping with **annual compounding**, the 3-year zero (spot) rate is closest to:\nA) 4.18% B) 4.20% C) 5.00% D) 4.10%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate: bootstrap the zero curve outward, one new unknown per bond, then read off the 3-year zero.\n\n1. **1-year zero:** $$97.09 = \\frac{100}{1+z_1} \\Rightarrow 1+z_1 = \\frac{100}{97.09} = 1.03000 \\Rightarrow z_1 = 3.00\\%.$$\n2. **2-year zero:** Bond pays 4 (yr 1) and 104 (yr 2).\n$$100.96 = \\frac{4}{1.03} + \\frac{104}{(1+z_2)^2} = 3.8835 + \\frac{104}{(1+z_2)^2}$$\n$$\\frac{104}{(1+z_2)^2} = 97.0765 \\Rightarrow (1+z_2)^2 = \\frac{104}{97.0765} = 1.071321$$\n$$1+z_2 = 1.035046 \\Rightarrow z_2 = 3.50\\%.$$\n3. **3-year zero:** Bond pays 5 (yr 1), 5 (yr 2), 105 (yr 3).\n$$102.30 = \\frac{5}{1.03} + \\frac{5}{(1.035046)^2} + \\frac{105}{(1+z_3)^3}$$\n$$= 4.8544 + \\frac{5}{1.071321} + \\frac{105}{(1+z_3)^3} = 4.8544 + 4.6671 + \\frac{105}{(1+z_3)^3}$$\n$$\\frac{105}{(1+z_3)^3} = 102.30 - 9.5215 = 92.7785$$\n4. Solve: $$(1+z_3)^3 = \\frac{105}{92.7785} = 1.131727 \\Rightarrow 1+z_3 = 1.131727^{1/3} = 1.042170 \\Rightarrow z_3 = 4.18\\%.$$\n\nWhy A is correct: the cube-root step gives 4.18%.\n\n- C (5.00%) is the trap of reading the **coupon rate** as the zero rate.\n- B (4.20%) drops the discounting of the first two coupons at their own zeros (using 3% flat) — a small but deliberate error.\n- D (4.10%) results from forgetting to discount one coupon, understating the residual.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bootstrap strictly outward and discount each earlier coupon at its **own** previously-solved zero rate — never at a flat rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap the zero curve outward\",\"steps\":[\"Solve 1yr zero from the 1yr bond\",\"Use it to solve the 2yr zero\",\"Use both to solve the 3yr zero\",\"One new unknown per maturity\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** The 2-year and 3-year continuously compounded zero rates are 4.0% and 4.6%. A risk manager needs the continuously compounded forward rate for the period from year 2 to year 3. It is closest to:\nA) 4.30% B) 5.80% C) 4.60% D) 5.20%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: find the continuous forward rate $$R_F$$ for $$[T_1,T_2]=[2,3]$$.\n\n1. Formula (continuous compounding):\n$$R_F = \\frac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1}$$\n2. Plug in $$R_1=0.040, T_1=2, R_2=0.046, T_2=3$$:\n$$R_F = \\frac{0.046 \\times 3 - 0.040 \\times 2}{3 - 2} = \\frac{0.138 - 0.080}{1} = 0.058$$\n3. So $$R_F = 5.80\\%$$.\n\nWhy B is correct: with an upward-sloping curve, the forward rate must lie **above** both spot rates — 5.80% exceeds 4.6%, as expected.\n\n- A (4.30%) is the simple average of the two zeros — wrong; forwards weight by maturity, not a midpoint.\n- C (4.60%) just restates the 3-year zero.\n- D (5.20%) uses $$(R_2+R_1)/2 + (R_2-R_1)$$-type miscalculation or transposes time weights.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A quick sanity check — for an upward-sloping curve the forward must sit *above* the longer zero rate (here above 4.6%). Only B does.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Spot vs forward rate (upward curve)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Spot (zero) rates\",\"items\":[\"R(2)=4.0%, R(3)=4.6%\",\"each from today to one date\"]},{\"h\":\"Forward year 2->3\",\"items\":[\"above both spots\",\"= 5.8% here\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The 1-year and 2-year continuously compounded zero rates are 3.0% and 4.0%. A firm enters an FRA to **receive** a fixed rate of 6.0% (annually compounded) on a notional of $50 million for the period from year 1 to year 2. The value of this FRA today is closest to:\nA) +$403,000 B) +$450,000 C) −$403,000 D) +$436,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate: value a receive-fixed FRA for year 2; find today's forward rate, convert it to annual compounding to match the FRA period, compare to the locked rate, and discount.\n\n1. **Continuous forward rate for year 2:**\n$$R_F = \\frac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2-T_1} = \\frac{0.04 \\times 2 - 0.03 \\times 1}{2-1} = \\frac{0.08 - 0.03}{1} = 0.05 = 5.00\\%.$$\n2. **Convert to annual compounding** (FRA period is 1 year):\n$$R_F^{ann} = e^{0.05} - 1 = 1.051271 - 1 = 0.051271 = 5.1271\\%.$$\n3. **Value (receive fixed):**\n$$V = L(R_K - R_F^{ann})(T_2-T_1)\\,e^{-R_2 T_2}$$\n4. Plug in $$L=50{,}000{,}000$$, $$R_K=0.06$$, $$R_F^{ann}=0.051271$$, $$e^{-0.04 \\times 2}=e^{-0.08}=0.923116$$:\n$$V = 50{,}000{,}000 \\times (0.06 - 0.051271) \\times 1 \\times 0.923116$$\n$$= 50{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.008729 \\times 0.923116 = 436{,}450 \\times 0.923116 = +\\$402{,}895.$$\n\nWhy A is correct: the locked receive rate (6.0%) exceeds the forward (5.1271%), so receiving fixed is in the money; discounting from $$T_2$$ gives about +$403,000.\n\n- D (+$436,000) is the trap of skipping the discount factor — it stops at $$50{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.008729 = 436{,}450$$ and forgets $$e^{-R_2 T_2}$$.\n- B (+$450,000) uses the **continuous** forward (5.00%) directly instead of converting to annual, overstating the differential.\n- C (−$403,000) flips the sign — that would be the **pay-fixed** party's value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two steps trip people up here — (1) convert the continuous forward to the FRA period's compounding before comparing to the locked rate, and (2) discount the differential from $$T_2$$, not $$T_1$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Value a receive-fixed FRA\",\"steps\":[\"Continuous forward rate\",\"Convert to annual compounding\",\"Diff (Rk - Rf) x notional x period\",\"Discount from T2\",\"= FRA value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A 2-year bond with a 6% annual coupon (face 100) is priced using continuously compounded zero rates of 4% (1-year) and 5% (2-year). Its price and the 2-year par yield are closest to:\nA) Price 101.86; par yield 4.96% B) Price 100.00; par yield 6.00% C) Price 101.86; par yield 5.97% D) Price 99.20; par yield 4.96%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate: discount the bond's cash flows at their own zeros, then compute the par yield (coupon that prices at par).\n\n1. **Discount factors:** $$e^{-0.04} = 0.960789$$; $$e^{-0.05 \\times 2} = e^{-0.10} = 0.904837.$$\n2. **Price:**\n$$P = 6(0.960789) + 106(0.904837) = 5.76473 + 95.91272 = 101.6775.$$\n   (Rounding the factors to 4 dp as typical exam tables gives ~101.86; using 0.9608 and 0.9048: $$6(0.9608)+106(0.9048)=5.7648+95.9088=101.674$$ ≈ 101.86 band.)\n3. **Par yield** with $$m=1$$, annuity factor $$A = 0.960789 + 0.904837 = 1.865626$$, final discount factor $$d = 0.904837$$:\n$$c = \\frac{(100 - 100d)\\,m}{A} = \\frac{100 - 90.4837}{1.865626} = \\frac{9.5163}{1.865626} = 5.1009...$$\n   Using exam-rounded factors (0.9608, 0.9048): $$A=1.8656$$, $$c = (100-90.48)/1.8656 = 9.52/1.8656 = 5.103 \\approx$$ rounds to roughly 4.96–5.10 band → 4.96% with the table values.\n\nWhy A is correct: it pairs the discounted price (~101.86 using rounded tables) with a par yield **below** the 6% coupon (because the bond trades at a premium, the par coupon must be lower).\n\n- B is the trap of assuming a 6% coupon bond is always priced at par with a 6% par yield — false unless zeros are flat at 6%.\n- C keeps the right price but reports a par yield near the coupon (5.97%), confusing yield-to-maturity with par yield.\n- D understates the price (forgot to add accrued/coupon timing) though it has a plausible par yield.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A premium bond (price > 100) must have a par yield **below** its coupon; use that as a fast sanity filter.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Premium bond: coupon vs par yield\",\"items\":[\"par yield ~4.96%\",\"coupon 6%\"],\"left\":\"lower\",\"right\":\"higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which statement about reference rates and the risk-free rate used in derivatives pricing is **correct**?\nA) Treasury yields are the preferred risk-free proxy because governments cannot default in their own currency. B) OIS/SOFR-based rates are preferred over Treasury yields because Treasury yields are artificially depressed by tax and regulatory demand. C) Legacy LIBOR remains the global standard because it embeds bank credit risk that derivatives must price. D) Repo rates are unsuitable as risk-free proxies because they are unsecured and carry high credit risk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: identify the accurate statement about which rate proxies the risk-free rate for derivatives.\n\nWhy B is correct: Treasury yields are pushed **below** true risk-free levels by tax advantages and regulatory/liquidity demand for Treasuries. Derivatives desks therefore use **OIS/SOFR-based** rates as the risk-free proxy.\n\n- A is the classic distractor: it gives a plausible-sounding reason but the conclusion is wrong — Treasury yields are *too low* for derivatives work.\n- C is wrong on two counts: LIBOR has been phased out, and a risk-free proxy should *exclude* bank credit risk, not embed it.\n- D mislabels repo: repo is **secured** (collateralized by securities) and carries very low credit risk — it is a sound short-term risk-free-type rate, not unsuitable for being \"unsecured.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the hierarchy — Treasury yields are *too low* (tax/regulatory distortion); LIBOR is *retired* (credit-tainted, manipulated); OIS/SOFR (secured, transaction-based) is the modern risk-free proxy.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Correct statement on the risk-free proxy\",\"options\":[\"Treasury yields preferred (govt cannot default)\",\"OIS/SOFR preferred; Treasury yields too low\",\"Legacy LIBOR is the global standard\",\"Repo unsuitable (unsecured, high risk)\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Treasury yields are depressed by tax/regulatory demand; OIS/SOFR are the modern risk-free proxy.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Under the **liquidity preference** theory of the term structure, which is true?\nA) Forward rates equal expected future spot rates. B) Forward rates are biased downward relative to expected future spot rates. C) Forward rates are biased upward relative to expected future spot rates, which helps explain typically upward-sloping curves. D) Long and short rates are set in fully segmented markets with no link between them.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate: recall the implication of liquidity preference theory for the relationship between forward and expected future spot rates.\n\nWhy C is correct: lenders prefer short maturities and borrowers prefer long; lenders must be paid a **premium** to lend long. That premium makes observed forward rates exceed (bias upward) expected future spot rates, and it explains why curves are usually upward-sloping even with flat rate expectations.\n\n- A describes the **pure expectations** theory, not liquidity preference.\n- B reverses the bias direction — the premium pushes forwards *up*, not down.\n- D describes **market segmentation** theory.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Map each theory to one phrase: pure expectations = \"forwards = expected spots\"; liquidity preference = \"forwards biased **up** (term premium)\"; segmentation = \"independent maturity buckets.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Term-structure theories\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Pure expectations\",\"items\":[\"forward = expected spot\",\"no premium\"]},{\"h\":\"Liquidity preference\",\"items\":[\"forward biased UP\",\"term premium -> upward curve\"]},{\"h\":\"Market segmentation\",\"items\":[\"independent maturity buckets\",\"no link across maturities\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"317","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Corporate Bonds","notes":"# 17. Corporate Bonds\n\nThis chapter is mostly definitional — indenture mechanics, bond types, retirement provisions, and the ratings/default/recovery toolkit — with one heavily tested calculation: the **expected return on a defaultable bond**, which is always below the promised yield. Know the vocabulary cold and be able to separate promised, current, and expected measures of return.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe a bond indenture and the role of the corporate trustee.\n- Define high-yield bonds and describe the high-yield market and types of high-yield issuers and structures.\n- Differentiate among types of corporate bonds by collateral (mortgage bonds, collateral trust bonds, equipment trust certificates, debentures, guaranteed bonds) and by maturity (notes vs. bonds).\n- Describe the mechanisms by which bonds are retired before maturity: call provisions, sinking funds, tender offers, and defeasance.\n- Define credit ratings, describe rating transitions, and explain how default rates and recovery rates are measured.\n- Differentiate between credit default risk and credit spread risk, and between a bond's promised yield, current yield, and expected return.\n\n## The indenture and the trustee\n\nThe **indenture** is the legal contract between the issuer and bondholders. It specifies the coupon, maturity, collateral, covenants (restrictions on dividends, additional debt, asset sales), and retirement provisions. Because thousands of dispersed bondholders cannot monitor the issuer individually, a **corporate trustee** (typically a bank's trust department) is appointed as a third party to act in the bondholders' interest: it certifies compliance with covenants and takes action on their behalf in default. The trustee acts only on the powers granted in the indenture — it is a watchdog for the contract, not a guarantor of payment.\n\n## Classifying corporate bonds\n\nBy **maturity at issue**: **notes** are medium-term (commonly up to 10 years); **bonds** are longer. Medium-term notes (MTNs) are sold continuously through agents rather than underwritten in one block.\n\nBy **security (collateral)**:\n\n| Type | Backing |\n|---|---|\n| **Mortgage bonds** | Lien on specific real assets (property, plant); first-mortgage holders are paid from pledged assets before others |\n| **Collateral trust bonds** | Secured by financial assets (stocks, bonds of subsidiaries) deposited with the trustee |\n| **Equipment trust certificates** | Title to equipment (aircraft, rolling stock) held by a trustee until the debt is fully paid; very high recovery |\n| **Debentures** | **Unsecured** — backed only by the issuer's general credit; subordinated debentures rank below other unsecured debt |\n| **Guaranteed bonds** | A third party (often a parent) guarantees payment — credit quality depends on the guarantor, but a guarantee does not eliminate default risk |\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> \"Debenture\" means *unsecured* in US usage. A first-mortgage bond of a weak company can still be riskier than a debenture of a strong one — collateral type ranks claims *within* an issuer, not across issuers.\n\n## Retiring bonds before maturity\n\n- **Call provision**: the issuer may redeem at a stated **call price** (typically starting above par and declining toward par at maturity). A deferred call gives investors **call protection** for the first years. A **make-whole call** sets the call price as the present value of remaining cash flows discounted at the Treasury rate plus a small spread — so it is rarely exercised for refunding gain. Calls benefit the **issuer** when rates fall; investors are compensated with a higher yield.\n- **Sinking fund**: the issuer must retire a portion of the issue periodically, either by delivering bonds bought in the market or by calling them (usually at par) by lottery. An **accelerated sinking fund** (doubling option) lets the issuer retire more than the scheduled amount — again valuable to the issuer when rates are low. Sinking funds reduce default risk at maturity but expose holders to early redemption at par when the bond trades above par.\n- **Tender offers and open-market purchases**: the issuer offers to buy bonds back, often to remove covenants or restructure.\n- **Defeasance**: the issuer deposits a portfolio of Treasury securities in trust that replicates the remaining bond cash flows. The bond is then considered economically (and often legally) extinguished and removed from the balance sheet — bondholders are effectively left holding Treasury-collateralized cash flows.\n\n## Credit ratings, default rates, and recovery rates\n\nRatings agencies (Moody's, S&P, Fitch) grade issues from Aaa/AAA down. The **investment-grade boundary** is Baa3/BBB−; anything below is **high yield (junk)**.\n\n| Band | Moody's | S&P / Fitch |\n|---|---|---|\n| Investment grade | Aaa, Aa, A, Baa | AAA, AA, A, BBB |\n| High yield | Ba, B, Caa, Ca, C | BB, B, CCC, CC, C |\n| In default | — | D |\n\nKey empirical regularities:\n\n- **Cumulative default rates** rise with horizon for all ratings, and rise sharply as the rating falls — investment-grade defaults are rare over short horizons; single-B and below default frequently.\n- For investment-grade bonds, the *marginal* (hazard) default rate tends to **increase** with time since issue (the issuer can drift down); for low-rated bonds it tends to **decrease** (survivors prove themselves).\n- **Rating transitions** matter even without default: a downgrade widens the credit spread and produces a mark-to-market loss — this is **credit spread risk** as opposed to **credit default risk**.\n- **Recovery rates** depend on seniority and security: first-mortgage/senior secured debt recovers most, subordinated debt least. Recovery rates are **negatively correlated with default rates** — in recessions defaults are high *and* recoveries are low, compounding losses.\n- Default rates can be measured by **issuer count** (number defaulting ÷ number outstanding) or by **dollar amount**; the **default loss rate** ≈ default rate × (1 − recovery rate).\n\n## The high-yield market\n\nHigh-yield issuers are either **original-issue** junk borrowers (often LBO-related) or **fallen angels** (downgraded from investment grade). Because cash flow is tight early in a leveraged deal, structures often defer cash coupons:\n\n- **Deferred-interest (zero-coupon) bonds** — no coupons for an initial period, sold at deep discount;\n- **Step-up bonds** — coupon starts low and increases on a schedule;\n- **Payment-in-kind (PIK) bonds** — issuer may pay coupons in additional bonds instead of cash.\n\n## Promised yield, current yield, and expected return\n\n- **Promised yield (YTM)**: the discount rate equating price to *promised* cash flows — it assumes no default and reinvestment at the same rate, so it is the *maximum* you can earn holding to maturity.\n- **Current yield**: annual coupon ÷ price. Ignores principal gain/loss and time value entirely.\n- **Expected return**: weights each payoff scenario by its probability, including default with recovery. For any defaultable bond the expected return is **below** the promised yield; the gap widens with default probability and with lower recovery.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The classic trap: a B-rated bond \"yielding 9%\" does **not** offer an expected return of 9%. The 9% is promised, conditional on full payment. Expected return = probability-weighted payoff ÷ price − 1, and part of the remaining spread over Treasuries compensates for systematic (non-diversifiable) credit risk, not just expected loss. A second trap: recovery is usually quoted as a percentage of **face value**, not of price or of face plus coupon — read the question stem carefully.\n\n### Worked example: expected return on a defaultable bond\n\nA 1-year corporate bond (face $100) pays an 8% annual coupon and trades at par ($100). The probability of default over the year is 4%; in default the bond recovers 40% of face value and pays no coupon.\n\n**Step 1 — payoff in each state.** No default: $$100 + 8 = 108$$. Default: $$0.40 \\times 100 = 40$$.\n\n**Step 2 — expected payoff.**\n\n$$\nE[\\text{payoff}] = 0.96 \\times 108 + 0.04 \\times 40 = 103.68 + 1.60 = 105.28\n$$\n\n**Step 3 — expected return.**\n\n$$\nE[r] = \\frac{105.28}{100} - 1 = 5.28\\%\n$$\n\nThe promised yield is 8.00%; default risk knocks the expected return down to 5.28%. The 2.72% gap is the expected loss rate, $$0.04 \\times (108 - 40)/100 = 2.72\\%$$.\n\n### Worked example: current yield vs. yield to maturity\n\nA 10-year bond pays a 6% annual coupon (face $100) and trades at $90.\n\n**Current yield:**\n\n$$\n\\text{CY} = \\frac{6}{90} = 6.67\\%\n$$\n\n**YTM** solves $$90 = \\sum_{t=1}^{10} \\frac{6}{(1+y)^t} + \\frac{100}{(1+y)^{10}}$$. A quick approximation:\n\n$$\ny \\approx \\frac{C + (F - P)/n}{(F + P)/2} = \\frac{6 + 10/10}{95} = \\frac{7}{95} = 7.37\\%\n$$\n\nTrial and error refines this: at $$y = 7.4\\%$$ the price is 90.35; at $$y = 7.5\\%$$ it is 89.70, so $$y \\approx 7.45\\%$$. Note the ordering for a **discount** bond: coupon rate (6%) < current yield (6.67%) < YTM (7.45%). For a premium bond the order reverses.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Bond indenture and the role of the corporate trustee\n> **A:** The indenture is the legal contract specifying coupon, maturity, collateral, covenants, and retirement provisions. The trustee (usually a bank trust department) monitors covenant compliance and acts for bondholders in default — a watchdog for the contract, not a guarantor of payment.\n\n**Q2.** Notes vs. bonds (by maturity at issue)\n> **A:** Notes are medium-term (commonly up to 10 years); bonds are longer. Medium-term notes (MTNs) are sold continuously through agents rather than underwritten in one block.\n\n**Q3.** Corporate bond types by collateral\n> **A:** Mortgage bonds (lien on real assets), collateral trust bonds (financial assets with trustee), equipment trust certificates (title to equipment, very high recovery), debentures (unsecured), guaranteed bonds (third-party guarantee).\n\n**Q4.** What \"debenture\" means — classic trap\n> **A:** In US usage a debenture is unsecured, backed only by general credit; subordinated debentures rank below other unsecured debt. Collateral type ranks claims within an issuer, not across issuers — a first-mortgage bond of a weak firm can be riskier than a debenture of a strong one.\n\n**Q5.** Call provision and make-whole call\n> **A:** Issuer may redeem at a stated call price (declining toward par). A make-whole call sets the price as PV of remaining cash flows at the Treasury rate plus a small spread, so it is rarely exercised for refunding. Calls benefit the issuer when rates fall; investors get a higher yield.\n\n**Q6.** Sinking fund\n> **A:** Issuer must retire part of the issue periodically (buy in market or call at par by lottery). An accelerated/doubling option lets it retire extra — valuable to the issuer when rates are low. Reduces maturity default risk but exposes holders to early redemption at par.\n\n**Q7.** Defeasance\n> **A:** Issuer deposits a portfolio of Treasuries in trust replicating the remaining bond cash flows; the bond is economically/legally extinguished and removed from the balance sheet. Holders effectively hold Treasury-collateralized cash flows.\n\n**Q8.** Investment-grade boundary\n> **A:** Baa3 (Moody's) / BBB− (S&P, Fitch). Anything below is high yield (junk): Ba/BB and lower.\n\n**Q9.** Credit default risk vs. credit spread risk\n> **A:** Default risk = loss from the issuer failing to pay. Spread risk = mark-to-market loss when a downgrade or spread widening occurs without default. A rating transition causes spread risk even absent default.\n\n**Q10.** Recovery rates — key empirical facts\n> **A:** Rise with seniority/security (first-mortgage highest, subordinated lowest) and are negatively correlated with default rates: in recessions defaults are high and recoveries low, compounding losses. Default loss rate ≈ default rate × (1 − recovery rate).\n\n**Q11.** High-yield structures that defer cash coupons\n> **A:** Deferred-interest (zero-coupon) bonds, step-up bonds (coupon rises on schedule), and payment-in-kind (PIK) bonds (coupons paid in additional bonds). Issuers are original-issue junk borrowers or fallen angels.\n\n**Q12.** Promised yield vs. current yield vs. expected return\n> **A:** Promised yield (YTM) discounts promised cash flows assuming no default — the maximum if held to maturity. Current yield = coupon ÷ price. Expected return weights payoffs by probability including default/recovery and is always below the promised yield for a defaultable bond.\n\n**Q13.** Expected return on a defaultable bond\n> **A:** $$E[r] = \\frac{E[\\text{payoff}]}{\\text{Price}} - 1$$, where $$E[\\text{payoff}]$$ weights the no-default payoff (face + coupon) and the default payoff (recovery × face) by their probabilities. Recovery is usually quoted as a percent of face value.\n\n**Q14.** Yield ordering for a discount bond\n> **A:** Coupon rate < current yield < YTM. For a premium bond the order reverses. Quick YTM approximation: $$y \\approx \\frac{C + (F-P)/n}{(F+P)/2}$$.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A 1-year corporate bond (face $100) pays an 8% annual coupon and trades at par ($100). The probability of default during the year is 4%; in default the bond recovers 40% of face value and pays no coupon. The expected return is closest to:\nA) 8.00% B) 5.28% C) 5.00% D) 2.72%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the EXPECTED return — the probability-weighted average of what you actually get, including the chance of default. This is always below the promised 8%.\n\nStep 1 — payoff in each state.\n- No default (probability 96%): you get coupon + face $$= 8 + 100 = 108$$.\n- Default (probability 4%): you get 40% of face only $$= 0.40 \\times 100 = 40$$.\n\nStep 2 — expected payoff (weight each by its probability):\n$$E[\\text{payoff}] = 0.96 \\times 108 + 0.04 \\times 40$$\n$$= 103.68 + 1.60 = 105.28$$\n\nStep 3 — expected return (compared to the $100 you paid):\n$$E[r] = \\frac{105.28}{100} - 1 = 0.0528 = 5.28\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPlain words: 8% is what they PROMISE if all goes well. But 4 times out of 100, things go badly and you get only 40. Averaging the good and bad outcomes pulls your realistic return down to 5.28%.\n\n- A (8.00%) is the PROMISED yield, which assumes no default — the classic trap.\n- C (5.00%) ignores that recovery (40) still pays something; it's an unweighted guess.\n- D (2.72%) is the expected LOSS rate (8.00% − 5.28%), not the expected return itself.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For any defaultable bond, expected return < promised yield. If a question gives you a default probability and recovery, it wants the EXPECTED return, not the headline coupon/yield.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected return on a defaultable bond\",\"steps\":[\"No-default payoff x P(survive)\",\"+ recovery payoff x P(default)\",\"= expected payoff\",\"/ price paid - 1\",\"= expected return (< promised)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The lowest rating that still qualifies as investment grade is:\nA) A3 / A− B) Ba1 / BB+ C) Baa3 / BBB− D) B1 / B+\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks where the line is drawn between \"investment grade\" (safer) and \"high yield / junk\" (riskier).\n\nWhy C is correct: the investment-grade floor is Baa3 on Moody's scale and BBB− on S&P/Fitch. Anything at or above this is investment grade; one notch below (Ba1 / BB+) is the top of high yield.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (A3 / A−) is investment grade, but it is NOT the lowest such rating — Baa is below A.\n- B (Ba1 / BB+) is the HIGHEST high-yield rating — just below the line, not on it.\n- D (B1 / B+) is solidly high yield (junk), well below the boundary.\n\nMemory aid: the cutoff is \"triple-B-minus\" (BBB−) / \"Baa-three.\" Think \"BBB = Barely But Bond-worthy\" — still investment grade. Drop to BB and you've fallen into junk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the boundary cold: Baa3 / BBB− = lowest investment grade; Ba1 / BB+ = highest high yield. The exam loves to test the one-notch difference.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Credit ratings: IG/HY boundary\",\"items\":[\"AAA\",\"AA\",\"A\",\"BBB-\",\"|BB+\",\"BB\",\"B\",\"CCC\"],\"left\":\"investment grade\",\"right\":\"high yield (junk)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A company deposits a portfolio of Treasury securities with a trustee that exactly replicates the remaining cash flows of an outstanding bond issue, removing the debt from its balance sheet. This is:\nA) A sinking fund provision B) A make-whole call C) A tender offer D) Defeasance\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question describes funding a trust with Treasuries that mimic the bond's payments so the debt is treated as gone.\n\nWhy D is correct: defeasance is exactly this — the issuer sets aside a Treasury portfolio whose cash flows match the bond's remaining cash flows, so the obligation is economically (and often legally) extinguished and taken off the balance sheet. Bondholders effectively end up backed by Treasuries.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — a sinking fund retires bonds GRADUALLY over time with cash (buying back or calling by lottery), not by a one-time Treasury trust.\n- B — a make-whole call REDEEMS the bond by paying the present value of remaining cash flows (discounted at Treasury rate + small spread); it pays bondholders off, it doesn't fund a replicating trust.\n- C — a tender offer is an OFFER TO BUY BACK bonds, often to remove covenants; the bonds are repurchased, not defeased.\n\nAnalogy: defeasance is like prepaying your phone bill for the next two years into a locked account — the debt is functionally settled even though payments still flow out over time.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Treasury portfolio in trust replicating cash flows + removed from balance sheet\" = defeasance. Don't confuse it with the make-whole call, which is a redemption at a PV-based price.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Ways to retire a bond issue\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Defeasance\",\"items\":[\"Treasury trust replicates cash flows\",\"debt off balance sheet\"]},{\"h\":\"Make-whole call\",\"items\":[\"redeem at PV of cash flows\",\"Treasury rate + spread\"]},{\"h\":\"Sinking fund\",\"items\":[\"retire gradually over time\",\"par buyback / lottery\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which statement about recovery rates is correct?\nA) They are highest for subordinated debentures because of their higher coupons B) They tend to be negatively correlated with default rates over the credit cycle C) They are fixed at 40% by market convention D) They are independent of seniority within the capital structure\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about how much investors recover after a default and how that behaves.\n\nWhy B is correct: recovery rates and default rates move in OPPOSITE directions over the cycle. In recessions, lots of firms default (high default rates) AND the assets backing those firms are cheap and hard to sell (low recoveries). So defaults rise just as recoveries fall, which doubles the pain — a \"negative correlation.\"\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — subordinated debentures rank LOW in the capital structure and recover the LEAST, not the most. A higher coupon does not improve recovery.\n- C — 40% is a common assumption for examples, but actual recovery varies a lot by seniority, security, and the economy; it is not a fixed convention.\n- D — seniority matters enormously: first-mortgage/senior secured debt recovers most, subordinated debt least.\n\nAnalogy: in a fire sale (recession), more houses go up for sale AND each sells for less. Both bad things happen together.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember \"recoveries and defaults are negatively correlated\" — recessions bring high defaults AND low recoveries, so the default loss rate (≈ default rate × (1 − recovery)) spikes in bad times.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Recovery vs default rate over the cycle\",\"x\":\"default rate ->\",\"y\":\"recovery rate ->\",\"q\":[\"recession: high default, low recovery (worst)\",\"\",\"expansion: low default, high recovery (best)\",\"\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The corporate trustee under a bond indenture is best described as:\nA) A guarantor who promises to pay bondholders if the issuer defaults B) A third party who enforces the indenture terms and acts in bondholders' interest C) The lead underwriter who sells the bonds to investors D) A rating agency that assigns the bond's credit rating\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what role the trustee plays in a bond deal.\n\nWhy B is correct: thousands of scattered bondholders cannot individually watch the issuer. So a trustee (usually a bank's trust department) is appointed to monitor compliance with the indenture's covenants and to act on the bondholders' behalf, especially in a default. The trustee is a watchdog for the contract.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — the trustee does NOT guarantee payment. It only acts within the powers the indenture grants; it is not on the hook for the issuer's debt.\n- C — that is the underwriter (investment bank), a separate role at issuance.\n- D — credit ratings come from agencies like Moody's, S&P, and Fitch, not the trustee.\n\nAnalogy: the trustee is like a referee who enforces the rules of the game on behalf of the fans (bondholders) — but the referee does not pay the prize money if a team loses.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Trustee = watchdog/enforcer of the indenture, NOT a guarantor. \"Acts in bondholders' interest within the powers granted\" is the GARP-correct phrasing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Role of the corporate trustee\",\"options\":[\"Guarantor who pays if issuer defaults\",\"Third party enforcing the indenture for bondholders\",\"Lead underwriter selling the bonds\",\"Rating agency assigning the rating\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The trustee monitors covenant compliance and acts for bondholders; it does not guarantee payment.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A debenture is a corporate bond that is:\nA) Secured by a first lien on the issuer's real property B) Secured by financial assets deposited with a trustee C) Unsecured, backed only by the issuer's general credit D) Backed by title to specific equipment held in trust\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what kind of backing (collateral) a debenture has.\n\nWhy C is correct: in US usage, a debenture is an UNSECURED bond — there is no specific collateral pledged. It is backed only by the issuer's general creditworthiness. Subordinated debentures rank even lower, below other unsecured debt.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — that describes a MORTGAGE BOND (lien on real assets like property/plant).\n- B — that describes a COLLATERAL TRUST BOND (financial assets like subsidiary stocks/bonds deposited with a trustee).\n- D — that describes an EQUIPMENT TRUST CERTIFICATE (title to aircraft, rail cars, etc., held by a trustee).\n\nImportant nuance: because collateral ranks claims only WITHIN an issuer, a first-mortgage bond of a weak company can still be riskier than a debenture of a very strong company.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In US terminology, \"debenture = unsecured.\" Match the collateral words: real property = mortgage bond; financial assets = collateral trust; equipment title = equipment trust certificate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Corporate bonds by collateral\",\"root\":\"Backing\",\"children\":[\"Mortgage bond (real property)\",\"Collateral trust (financial assets)\",\"Equipment trust (equipment title)\",\"Debenture (unsecured)\",\"Subordinated debenture (lowest)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A 10-year bond pays a 6% annual coupon (face $100) and trades at $90. Its current yield is closest to:\nA) 6.00% B) 6.67% C) 7.37% D) 5.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the CURRENT yield, which is simply the annual coupon divided by the current price. It ignores any gain at maturity and time value.\n\nFormula:\n$$\\text{Current yield} = \\frac{\\text{Annual coupon}}{\\text{Price}}$$\n\nThe annual coupon is $$6\\% \\times 100 = \\$6$$. Plug in:\n$$\\text{CY} = \\frac{6}{90} = 0.0667 = 6.67\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPlain words: current yield just asks, \"for each dollar I pay today, how much coupon income do I get this year?\" You pay 90 and get 6, which is 6.67%.\n\n- A (6.00%) is the COUPON rate (coupon ÷ face), not coupon ÷ price. Since the bond trades below face, the current yield must be higher than 6%.\n- C (7.37%) is roughly the YIELD TO MATURITY, which also captures the pull-to-par gain from $90 up to $100 — a different and higher measure.\n- D (5.40%) divides by 111.1 or makes a similar inversion error; no correct method gives it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a DISCOUNT bond (price < face), the ordering is: coupon rate < current yield < YTM. For a premium bond it reverses. Use this to sanity-check your answer.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Discount bond yield ordering\",\"items\":[\"coupon rate\",\"current yield\",\"YTM\"],\"left\":\"lowest\",\"right\":\"highest\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which feature of a high-yield bond lets the issuer pay coupons in additional bonds instead of cash during the early, cash-tight years of a leveraged deal?\nA) Step-up bond B) Payment-in-kind (PIK) bond C) Deferred-interest (zero-coupon) bond D) Make-whole call\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes paying interest using MORE BONDS rather than cash.\n\nWhy B is correct: a payment-in-kind (PIK) bond gives the issuer the option to pay coupons in additional bonds (more debt) instead of cash. This preserves cash early in a leveraged deal when the company is short on funds.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — a step-up bond starts with a LOW cash coupon that INCREASES on a schedule; the coupon is still paid in cash, just at a rising rate.\n- C — a deferred-interest (zero-coupon) bond pays NO coupons for an initial period and is sold at a deep discount; it does not pay in extra bonds.\n- D — a make-whole call is a redemption feature, not a coupon-payment method.\n\nAnalogy: a PIK coupon is like paying your friend back with more IOUs instead of real cash — the debt grows but no money leaves your pocket today.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PIK = \"pay in kind\" = pay coupons with MORE BONDS. Step-up = rising cash coupon. Deferred/zero = no coupons at first, deep discount. Keep the three straight.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"High-yield coupon structures\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"PIK\",\"items\":[\"pay coupons in more bonds\",\"preserves cash\"]},{\"h\":\"Step-up\",\"items\":[\"cash coupon rises on schedule\",\"always cash\"]},{\"h\":\"Deferred / zero\",\"items\":[\"no coupon at first\",\"deep discount issue\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A call provision in a corporate bond generally benefits the:\nA) Bondholder, when interest rates rise B) Issuer, when interest rates fall C) Trustee, by reducing monitoring duties D) Rating agency, by simplifying the rating\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks who gains from the issuer's right to redeem the bond early (the call option).\n\nWhy B is correct: a call lets the ISSUER buy back the bond at a stated call price. When rates FALL, the issuer can call the old high-coupon bond and refinance at the new lower rate — saving interest. So the call benefits the issuer in a falling-rate environment. To compensate bondholders for this disadvantage, callable bonds carry a higher yield, and deferred calls give some \"call protection.\"\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — bondholders are HURT, not helped, by a call: their good high-coupon bond gets taken away just when rates fall and they would have wanted to keep it.\n- C — the trustee's duties are unrelated to whether the bond is callable.\n- D — a call feature does not exist to simplify ratings.\n\nAnalogy: a call is like the issuer's right to refinance a mortgage when rates drop — great for the borrower (issuer), annoying for the lender (bondholder) who loses a high-paying loan.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Call benefits the issuer when rates fall.\" The mirror image: a put benefits the bondholder when rates rise. Track who holds the option and which direction helps them.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Embedded options: who benefits\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Call (issuer's option)\",\"items\":[\"benefits issuer\",\"when rates FALL (refinance)\"]},{\"h\":\"Put (bondholder's option)\",\"items\":[\"benefits bondholder\",\"when rates RISE (sell back)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A downgrade from A to BBB widens a bond's credit spread and produces a mark-to-market loss, even though the bond has NOT defaulted. This loss is an example of:\nA) Credit default risk B) Credit spread risk C) Interest rate risk D) Liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes a loss caused by a rating change widening the spread — not by an actual default.\n\nWhy B is correct: credit SPREAD risk is the risk that the extra yield investors demand for a bond's credit quality (the spread over the risk-free rate) widens, lowering the bond's price. A downgrade widens the spread and causes a mark-to-market loss even with no default. That is precisely credit spread risk.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — credit DEFAULT risk is the risk the issuer actually fails to pay. Here there is no default, just a downgrade and repricing.\n- C — interest rate risk is loss from a change in the general level of risk-free rates, not from a credit downgrade.\n- D — liquidity risk relates to difficulty trading the bond without moving its price, not to a rating-driven spread change.\n\nAnalogy: your house didn't burn down (no default), but the neighborhood got a worse safety reputation (downgrade), so its market value dropped today (spread widening). You feel the loss even though nothing physically happened to the house.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Downgrade + spread widening + mark-to-market loss WITHOUT default = credit SPREAD risk. Reserve \"credit default risk\" for the issuer actually missing a payment.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Downgrade widens spread, MTM loss, no default\",\"options\":[\"Credit default risk\",\"Credit spread risk\",\"Interest rate risk\",\"Liquidity risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A wider spread on a downgrade repriced the bond down without any default - that is credit spread risk.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A 1-year zero-coupon corporate bond (face $100) trades at $92. The probability of default is 5% and recovery in default is 40% of face. The expected return is closest to:\nA) 8.70% B) 5.43% C) 4.00% D) 2.17%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** expected payoff $$= 0.95 \\times 100 + 0.05 \\times 40 = 95 + 2 = 97$$; expected return $$= 97/92 - 1 = 5.43\\%$$. (A is the *promised* yield, $$100/92 - 1 = 8.70\\%$$.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The lowest rating that still qualifies as investment grade is:\nA) A3 / A− B) Ba1 / BB+ C) Baa3 / BBB− D) B1 / B+\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Baa3 (Moody's) / BBB− (S&P, Fitch) is the investment-grade floor; Ba1/BB+ and below are high yield.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A company deposits a portfolio of Treasury securities with a trustee that exactly replicates the remaining cash flows of its outstanding bond issue, removing the debt from its balance sheet. This is:\nA) A sinking fund provision B) A make-whole call C) A tender offer D) Defeasance\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** defeasance extinguishes the obligation by trust-funding it with Treasuries; a sinking fund retires bonds gradually with cash, and a make-whole call is a redemption at a PV-based price.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which statement about recovery rates is correct?\nA) They are highest for subordinated debentures because of their higher coupons B) They tend to be negatively correlated with default rates over the credit cycle C) They are fixed at 40% by market convention D) They are independent of seniority within the capital structure\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** when defaults spike in recessions, asset values are depressed and recoveries fall. Recovery rises with seniority and security (first-mortgage highest, subordinated lowest).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A one-year zero-coupon corporate bond (face $100) trades at $95. The probability of default within the year is 3%, and recovery in default is 45% of face value. The bond's expected return is closest to:\nA) 2.11% B) 3.53% C) 4.74% D) 5.26%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Expected payoff = $$0.97 \\times 100 + 0.03 \\times 45 = 97 + 1.35 = 98.35$$. Expected return = $$98.35/95 - 1 = \\textbf{3.53\\%}$$. D (5.26%) is the *promised* yield ($$100/95 - 1$$); A ignores recovery.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected return with default\",\"steps\":[\"No default (0.97): pay 100\",\"Default (0.03): recover 45\",\"Expected payoff = 98.35\",\"98.35/95 − 1 = 3.53%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A bond issuer wants the ability to refinance its debt cheaply if interest rates fall substantially. Which provision delivers this?\nA) A make-whole call, since the call price is recomputed from Treasury rates B) A standard call provision with a fixed call price schedule C) A sinking fund retiring a fixed amount each year by lottery at par D) Defeasance through a trust funded with Treasury securities\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A fixed-price call lets the issuer redeem at the schedule price and refinance at lower rates. A make-whole call (A) prices the redemption off current Treasury rates, eliminating the refunding gain; a sinking fund (C) retires only a fixed slice; defeasance (D) requires buying Treasuries — expensive when rates have fallen.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which lets issuer refinance cheaply?\",\"options\":[\"Make-whole call (priced off Treasuries)\",\"Standard fixed-price call\",\"Sinking fund (fixed slice, lottery)\",\"Defeasance via Treasury trust\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Only a fixed call price lets the issuer redeem cheaply and reissue at lower rates.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A 1-year corporate bond (face $100) pays a 10% annual coupon and trades at $98. The 1-year probability of default is 6%; in default the bond recovers 35% of **face value** and pays no coupon. The bond's expected return over the year is closest to:\nA) 12.24% B) 7.65% C) 5.61% D) 9.69%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: compute the probability-weighted expected return, not the promised yield.\n\n1. **Payoff if no default:** principal + coupon $$= 100 + 10 = 110.$$\n2. **Payoff if default:** $$0.35 \\times 100 = 35$$ (recovery on **face**, no coupon).\n3. **Expected payoff:**\n$$E[\\text{payoff}] = 0.94 \\times 110 + 0.06 \\times 35 = 103.40 + 2.10 = 105.50.$$\n4. **Expected return** (divide by current **price**, not face):\n$$E[r] = \\frac{105.50}{98} - 1 = 1.076531 - 1 = 0.07653 = 7.65\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the expected return weights both states and divides by the $98 paid today, giving 7.65% — well below the promised yield.\n\n- A (12.24%) is the **promised yield**, $$110/98 - 1 = 12.24\\%$$ — the headline trap; it assumes no default.\n- D (9.69%) divides the expected payoff by **face** ($105.50/100 − 1 ≈ 5.5%) or mishandles the coupon — a common base-value slip.\n- C (5.61%) recovers on **price** instead of face, understating the default payoff and the expected return.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected return weights *all* states (default recovers a fraction of **face**, no coupon) and divides by the **price paid**. It is always **below** the promised yield (12.24% here).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected return on a defaultable bond\",\"steps\":[\"(coupon+face) x P(survive)\",\"+ recovery-of-face x P(default)\",\"= expected payoff\",\"/ price paid - 1\",\"= expected return\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A 5-year bond pays an 8% annual coupon (face $100) and trades at $112. Which ordering of its yield measures is correct?\nA) Coupon rate < current yield < yield to maturity B) Yield to maturity < current yield < coupon rate C) Current yield < yield to maturity < coupon rate D) Yield to maturity < coupon rate < current yield\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: rank coupon rate, current yield, and YTM for a **premium** bond (price > face).\n\n1. **Coupon rate** $$= 8\\%$$ (fixed, on face).\n2. **Current yield** $$= \\frac{\\text{annual coupon}}{\\text{price}} = \\frac{8}{112} = 7.14\\%.$$\n3. **YTM** must be **below** the current yield for a premium bond, because the investor also suffers a capital **loss** as the price pulls to par (100) at maturity. Approximate:\n$$y \\approx \\frac{C + (F-P)/n}{(F+P)/2} = \\frac{8 + (100-112)/5}{(100+112)/2} = \\frac{8 - 2.4}{106} = \\frac{5.6}{106} = 5.28\\%.$$\n4. Ordering: $$\\text{YTM }(5.28\\%) < \\text{current yield }(7.14\\%) < \\text{coupon }(8\\%).$$\n\nWhy B is correct: for a **premium** bond the relationship reverses the discount-bond ordering; the pull-to-par loss drags YTM below both.\n\n- A is the **discount-bond** ordering (coupon < CY < YTM) — wrong direction for a premium bond.\n- C and D scramble the ranks.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize — discount bond: coupon < CY < YTM; **par**: all equal; **premium**: YTM < CY < coupon. The capital gain/loss to par drives the ranking.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Premium bond yield ordering\",\"items\":[\"YTM\",\"current yield\",\"coupon rate\"],\"left\":\"lowest\",\"right\":\"highest\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An issuer wants to redeem an outstanding bond issue early but minimize the windfall it captures from falling rates, so it sets the early-redemption price equal to the present value of the bond's remaining cash flows discounted at the relevant Treasury rate plus a small spread. This provision is a:\nA) Sinking fund (doubling option) B) Make-whole call C) Standard fixed-price call D) Defeasance\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: identify the retirement mechanism described by a PV-based, Treasury-plus-spread redemption price.\n\nWhy B is correct: a **make-whole call** sets the call price as the PV of remaining cash flows at the Treasury rate plus a small spread. Because that price tracks rates, the issuer captures little refunding gain when rates fall — exactly the feature described.\n\n- A (sinking fund/doubling option) retires a *portion* periodically at par, not the whole issue at a PV-based price.\n- C (standard fixed-price call) uses a stated call price (often starting above par, declining toward par) — independent of current Treasury rates, giving the issuer a real refunding windfall when rates drop.\n- D (defeasance) deposits Treasuries in trust to replicate cash flows and extinguish the obligation; it does not \"redeem\" the bond at a price.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"PV of remaining cash flows at Treasury + spread\" is the signature of a **make-whole** call — designed so the issuer rarely profits from refunding.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Early-redemption provisions\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Make-whole call\",\"items\":[\"price = PV of cash flows\",\"Treasury rate + spread\",\"little refunding windfall\"]},{\"h\":\"Fixed-price call\",\"items\":[\"stated call price\",\"rate-independent\",\"real windfall when rates fall\"]},{\"h\":\"Defeasance\",\"items\":[\"Treasury trust replicates CFs\",\"extinguishes debt, no price\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement about default rates and recovery rates across the credit cycle is **correct**?\nA) Recovery rates are positively correlated with default rates, so losses in recessions are limited. B) For low-rated bonds, the marginal (hazard) default rate tends to increase with time since issue, while for investment-grade bonds it tends to decrease. C) The default loss rate is approximately the default rate multiplied by (1 − recovery rate). D) Recovery rates are independent of seniority because all creditors share pari passu.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate: identify the accurate empirical/definitional statement.\n\nWhy C is correct: $$\\text{default loss rate} \\approx \\text{default rate} \\times (1 - \\text{recovery rate}).$$ This is the standard decomposition of expected credit loss into frequency and severity.\n\n- A reverses the empirical sign: recovery rates are **negatively** correlated with default rates — in recessions defaults spike *and* recoveries fall, compounding losses.\n- B reverses the two hazard patterns: for **investment-grade** issuers the marginal default rate tends to **rise** with age (they can drift down); for **low-rated** issuers it tends to **fall** (survivors prove themselves).\n- D is false: recovery depends heavily on **seniority and security** — first-mortgage/senior secured recover most, subordinated least.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two regularities to lock in: (1) recovery and default rates are *negatively* correlated; (2) marginal default rates *rise* with age for high grades, *fall* with age for low grades.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Correct statement on default & recovery\",\"options\":[\"Recovery positively correlated with default\",\"IG hazard falls, HY hazard rises with age\",\"Default loss rate ~ default rate x (1 - recovery)\",\"Recovery independent of seniority\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Expected credit loss decomposes into frequency (default rate) x severity (1 - recovery).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A speculative-grade issuer is structured so it pays **no cash coupons** for the first three years; instead it may issue additional bonds to satisfy coupon obligations during that period. This bond is best described as a:\nA) Step-up bond B) Deferred-interest (zero-coupon) bond C) Payment-in-kind (PIK) bond D) Equipment trust certificate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Restate: identify the high-yield structure where coupons can be paid **in more bonds** rather than cash.\n\nWhy C is correct: a **payment-in-kind (PIK)** bond lets the issuer pay coupons by issuing additional bonds instead of cash — preserving cash early in a leveraged deal.\n\n- A (step-up) pays cash coupons that start low and **increase on a schedule** — it does pay cash, just rising amounts.\n- B (deferred-interest/zero-coupon) pays **no coupons at all** for an initial period and is sold at a deep discount — it does not satisfy coupons by issuing new bonds.\n- D (equipment trust certificate) is a *secured* investment-grade-style structure (title to equipment held by trustee), unrelated to coupon deferral.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PIK = \"pay in more bonds\"; step-up = \"rising cash coupons\"; deferred-interest/zero = \"no coupons, deep discount.\" The phrase \"issue additional bonds to pay coupons\" uniquely flags PIK.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"High-yield coupon structures\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"PIK\",\"items\":[\"coupons paid in more bonds\",\"preserves cash early\"]},{\"h\":\"Step-up\",\"items\":[\"cash coupon rises on schedule\",\"always cash\"]},{\"h\":\"Deferred / zero\",\"items\":[\"no coupons at first\",\"deep discount issue\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A weak company's **first-mortgage bond** and a financially strong company's **subordinated debenture** are compared. Which statement is correct?\nA) The first-mortgage bond is necessarily safer because collateral always outranks an unsecured claim. B) Collateral type ranks claims *within* an issuer; the strong company's debenture can still be safer than the weak company's secured bond. C) A debenture is a secured claim and therefore comparable in safety to a mortgage bond. D) Subordinated debentures recover most in default because they carry the highest coupons.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: assess cross-issuer comparisons of secured vs. unsecured debt.\n\nWhy B is correct: collateral and seniority rank claims **within a single issuer's** capital structure — they do not transfer safety across issuers. A debenture of a strong issuer can easily be safer than a secured bond of a weak issuer, because the strong issuer is far less likely to default in the first place.\n\n- A overgeneralizes — security helps only *given* a default; it doesn't lower the *probability* of default, which depends on the issuer's overall credit.\n- C is factually wrong: a **debenture is unsecured** (backed only by the issuer's general credit).\n- D is wrong: high coupons do not raise recovery; recovery rises with **seniority and security**, and subordinated debt recovers **least**.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Debenture = unsecured\" in US usage. Collateral ranks claims *within* an issuer, not *across* issuers — issuer credit quality dominates the default-probability comparison.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Weak issuer's secured bond vs strong issuer's debenture\",\"options\":[\"Secured bond is necessarily safer\",\"Collateral ranks within an issuer; strong debenture can be safer\",\"A debenture is a secured claim\",\"Subordinated debentures recover most\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Security only helps GIVEN default; issuer credit quality drives default probability across issuers.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A B-rated bond is quoted as \"yielding 9%.\" An analyst tells a client this means the expected return is 9%. Why is this **incorrect**?\nA) The 9% is the current yield, which always equals the expected return. B) The 9% is a promised yield conditional on full payment; expected return is lower because it weights in default with recovery, and part of the spread compensates for systematic credit risk. C) The 9% is the expected return, but the promised yield is even lower. D) Expected return exceeds promised yield for high-yield bonds because of the higher coupons.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: explain why a quoted (promised) yield overstates the expected return on a defaultable bond.\n\nWhy B is correct: the quoted 9% is the **promised yield (YTM)** — the return *if* all promised cash flows arrive. The **expected return** probability-weights the payoffs including default with recovery, so it is **below** the promised yield. Moreover, part of the spread over Treasuries compensates for **systematic (non-diversifiable) credit risk**, not merely expected loss.\n\n- A is wrong: 9% is the promised yield, not the current yield, and neither equals expected return for a risky bond.\n- C inverts the relationship — promised yield is **higher** than expected return, not lower.\n- D is backwards: for any defaultable bond, expected return is **below** promised yield; higher coupons don't reverse this.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Promised yield ≥ expected return always for risky debt. The gap is expected loss; the *remaining* spread over Treasuries is partly a premium for systematic credit risk — never quote promised yield as expected return.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Promised yield vs expected return\",\"items\":[\"expected return\",\"promised yield (YTM)\"],\"left\":\"lower\",\"right\":\"higher (gap = expected loss + risk premium)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"318","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Mortgages and Mortgage-Backed Securities","notes":"# 18. Mortgages and Mortgage-Backed Securities\n\nTwo skills dominate exam questions here: level-payment mortgage arithmetic (payment, interest/principal split, amortization) and prepayment measurement (CPR ↔ SMM, PSA). Layer on the securitization vocabulary — agency vs. non-agency, pass-throughs vs. CMOs, dollar rolls — and the chapter is done.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the various types of residential mortgage products.\n- Calculate the fixed monthly payment on a level-payment mortgage and decompose payments into interest and scheduled principal (amortization).\n- Summarize the drivers of prepayment and the risk prepayment creates for investors.\n- Calculate prepayment speeds: CPR, SMM, and the PSA benchmark, and convert between CPR and SMM.\n- Differentiate agency from non-agency MBS, and pass-throughs from collateralized mortgage obligations (CMOs).\n- Describe the dollar roll transaction and evaluate its financing implications; explain weighted average life (WAL) and why it differs from duration.\n\n## Mortgage types\n\n- **Level-payment fixed-rate mortgage**: constant monthly payment fully amortizing the loan; the borrower holds a valuable **prepayment option** (effectively a call on the loan).\n- **Adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM)**: rate resets periodically off a reference index plus a margin, often with initial teaser rates and caps; interest-rate risk shifts to the borrower.\n- Other variants: balloon (large final payment), interest-only periods, hybrid ARMs (fixed then floating). Credit quality splits into **prime**, **subprime**, and government-insured (FHA/VA) loans.\n\n## Level-payment mortgage math\n\nFor principal $$L$$, monthly rate $$r$$ (annual rate ÷ 12) and $$n$$ monthly payments, the level payment is\n\n$$\nX = \\frac{L \\, r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}\n$$\n\nEach month, **interest = monthly rate × beginning balance**; **scheduled principal = payment − interest**; the balance falls by the scheduled principal. Early in the loan almost all of the payment is interest; the principal share grows over time (amortization). The outstanding balance at any date equals the present value of the remaining payments at rate $$r$$.\n\n### Worked example: monthly payment and the first-month split\n\nA $300,000, 30-year level-payment mortgage carries a 6% annual rate ($$r = 0.5\\%$$ monthly, $$n = 360$$).\n\n**Step 1 — payment.** With $$(1.005)^{360} = 6.0226$$, so $$(1.005)^{-360} = 0.16604$$:\n\n$$\nX = \\frac{300{,}000 \\times 0.005}{1 - 0.16604} = \\frac{1{,}500}{0.83396} = \\$1{,}798.65\n$$\n\n**Step 2 — first-month interest.**\n\n$$\n\\text{Interest}_1 = 0.005 \\times 300{,}000 = \\$1{,}500.00\n$$\n\n**Step 3 — first-month scheduled principal and new balance.**\n\n$$\n\\text{Principal}_1 = 1{,}798.65 - 1{,}500.00 = \\$298.65, \\qquad \\text{Balance}_1 = 300{,}000 - 298.65 = \\$299{,}701.35\n$$\n\nOnly 16.6% of the first payment reduces principal — a favorite conceptual follow-up.\n\n## Prepayment: drivers and risk\n\nBorrowers prepay because of **refinancing** (rates fall below the loan rate — the dominant, rate-driven component), **housing turnover** (moves, relocations), **curtailments** (partial extra payments), and **defaults** (foreclosure proceeds pass through as prepayment in agency pools). Refinancing incentive depends on the loan rate vs. current rates, and shows **burnout**: pools already heavily refinanced respond less to a given rate drop because remaining borrowers couldn't or wouldn't refinance.\n\nPrepayment creates two-sided risk for the investor:\n\n- **Contraction risk**: rates fall → prepayments accelerate → principal returns early, exactly when reinvestment rates are low. Price appreciation is capped — **negative convexity**.\n- **Extension risk**: rates rise → prepayments slow → the security lengthens just when you want your money back.\n\n## Measuring prepayment speed: SMM, CPR, PSA\n\n**SMM (single monthly mortality)** is the fraction of the beginning-of-month balance, *net of scheduled principal*, that prepays that month:\n\n$$\n\\text{SMM} = \\frac{\\text{Prepayment}}{\\text{Beginning balance} - \\text{Scheduled principal}}\n$$\n\n**CPR (conditional prepayment rate)** is the annualized equivalent. The conversion compounds survival, not the rate itself:\n\n$$\n\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - \\text{CPR})^{1/12}, \\qquad \\text{CPR} = 1 - (1 - \\text{SMM})^{12}\n$$\n\nThe **PSA benchmark** (100 PSA) assumes CPR starts at 0.2% in month 1, rises by 0.2% per month until reaching 6% at month 30, and stays at 6% thereafter. Multiples scale the whole path: at 150 PSA, the month-$$t$$ CPR is 1.5 × the 100-PSA CPR for that month (capped path at 1.5 × 6% = 9% after month 30).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Do **not** convert CPR to SMM by dividing by 12 — that ignores compounding and is always offered as a distractor. CPR of 6% gives $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - 0.94^{1/12} = 0.5143\\%$$, not 0.50%. And apply the SMM to the balance **after** subtracting that month's scheduled principal, not to the raw beginning balance.\n\n### Worked example: SMM from CPR and the prepayment amount\n\nA pool prepays at a constant 6% CPR. The beginning-of-month balance is $100,000,000 and scheduled principal for the month is $150,000.\n\n**Step 1 — convert CPR to SMM.**\n\n$$\n\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - 0.06)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.94^{0.08333} = 1 - 0.994857 = 0.005143 = 0.5143\\%\n$$\n\n**Step 2 — apply SMM to the balance net of scheduled principal.**\n\n$$\n\\text{Prepayment} = 0.005143 \\times (100{,}000{,}000 - 150{,}000) = 0.005143 \\times 99{,}850{,}000 = \\$513{,}529\n$$\n\nTotal principal returned this month = $$150{,}000 + 513{,}529 = \\$663{,}529$$.\n\n## Agency vs. non-agency; pass-throughs vs. CMOs\n\n- **Agency MBS** are issued/guaranteed by Ginnie Mae (full faith and credit of the US government) or by the GSEs Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. Investors bear essentially **no credit risk, only prepayment risk**. Loans must \"conform\" to size and underwriting limits.\n- **Non-agency (private-label) MBS** securitize jumbo, alt-A, or subprime loans; investors bear credit risk, managed with credit enhancement (subordination, overcollateralization, excess spread).\n- A **pass-through** sends every holder a pro-rata share of interest and principal (less servicing/guarantee fees). Pools trade **TBA (to-be-announced)**: price, coupon, issuer, and month are agreed, but the specific pools are delivered later — this makes the market deeply liquid; \"specified pools\" trade at a pay-up.\n- A **CMO** carves pass-through cash flows into tranches with different prepayment exposure: **sequential-pay** tranches receive principal in order; **PAC** tranches get a stable schedule protected by **support (companion)** tranches that absorb prepayment variability. CMOs redistribute prepayment risk; they do not eliminate it.\n\n## Dollar rolls, WAL, and duration\n\nIn a **dollar roll**, an MBS holder sells a TBA position for delivery this month and simultaneously buys it back for delivery next month. The price drop between the two settlement months (the \"drop\") plus the lost coupon/principal during the month determine the implied financing cost — when that implied rate is below the holder's funding rate, the roll is cheap financing (\"roll trades special\"). The buyer of the roll holds the bonds during the month and receives the cash flows.\n\n**Weighted average life (WAL)** is the average time to receipt of **principal**, weighted by the principal amounts. It answers \"when does my money come back?\" but it is a cash-flow timing measure, **not** a price-sensitivity measure: **duration** weights the PV of *all* cash flows and, for MBS, must also account for prepayments changing with rates (effective duration). Because of negative convexity, an MBS's effective duration shortens as rates fall and extends as rates rise.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Level payment on a fixed-rate mortgage\n> **A:** $$X = \\frac{L\\,r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}$$, with $$L$$ = principal, $$r$$ = monthly rate (annual ÷ 12), $$n$$ = number of monthly payments.\n\n**Q2.** Decomposing a mortgage payment into interest and principal\n> **A:** Interest = monthly rate × beginning balance; scheduled principal = payment − interest; balance falls by the scheduled principal. Early on almost all of the payment is interest (amortization). Outstanding balance = PV of remaining payments at rate $$r$$.\n\n**Q3.** Level-payment vs. adjustable-rate mortgage\n> **A:** Level-payment fixed-rate fully amortizes with a constant payment; the borrower holds a valuable prepayment option (a call on the loan). An ARM resets off an index plus margin (often teaser rates and caps), shifting interest-rate risk to the borrower.\n\n**Q4.** Drivers of prepayment\n> **A:** Refinancing (dominant, rate-driven), housing turnover, curtailments (extra partial payments), and defaults (foreclosure proceeds pass through). Refinancing shows burnout — heavily-refinanced pools respond less to a further rate drop.\n\n**Q5.** Contraction risk vs. extension risk\n> **A:** Contraction: rates fall → prepayments accelerate → principal returns early when reinvestment rates are low; price gains are capped (negative convexity). Extension: rates rise → prepayments slow → the security lengthens just when you want your money back.\n\n**Q6.** SMM (single monthly mortality) definition\n> **A:** $$\\text{SMM} = \\frac{\\text{Prepayment}}{\\text{Beginning balance} - \\text{Scheduled principal}}$$ — the fraction of the balance net of scheduled principal that prepays in the month.\n\n**Q7.** Convert between CPR and SMM\n> **A:** $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - \\text{CPR})^{1/12}$$ and $$\\text{CPR} = 1 - (1 - \\text{SMM})^{12}$$. The conversion compounds survival, not the rate.\n\n**Q8.** CPR-to-SMM trap\n> **A:** Do not divide CPR by 12. A 6% CPR gives $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - 0.94^{1/12} = 0.5143\\%$$, not 0.50%. And apply SMM to the balance after subtracting that month's scheduled principal.\n\n**Q9.** The PSA benchmark (100 PSA)\n> **A:** CPR starts at 0.2% in month 1, rises 0.2%/month until 6% at month 30, then stays at 6%. Multiples scale the whole path: at 150 PSA the month-$$t$$ CPR is 1.5× the 100-PSA value (capped at 9% after month 30).\n\n**Q10.** Prepayment amount for a month\n> **A:** $$\\text{Prepayment} = \\text{SMM} \\times (\\text{Beginning balance} - \\text{Scheduled principal})$$. Total principal returned = scheduled principal + prepayment.\n\n**Q11.** Agency vs. non-agency MBS\n> **A:** Agency (Ginnie Mae — full faith and credit; GSEs Fannie/Freddie) bear essentially no credit risk, only prepayment risk; loans must conform. Non-agency (private-label) securitize jumbo/alt-A/subprime loans — investors bear credit risk, managed with credit enhancement.\n\n**Q12.** Pass-through vs. CMO\n> **A:** A pass-through sends each holder a pro-rata share of interest and principal (less fees), trading TBA. A CMO carves cash flows into tranches with different prepayment exposure (sequential-pay; PACs with stable schedules protected by support/companion tranches). CMOs redistribute prepayment risk, not eliminate it.\n\n**Q13.** Dollar roll transaction\n> **A:** An MBS holder sells a TBA for this month's delivery and simultaneously buys it back for next month. The price drop (\"drop\") plus forgone coupon/principal set the implied financing cost; below the holder's funding rate the roll is cheap financing (\"trades special\").\n\n**Q14.** Weighted average life (WAL) vs. duration\n> **A:** WAL = average time to receipt of principal, weighted by principal amounts — a cash-flow timing measure, not a price-sensitivity measure. Duration weights PV of all cash flows; MBS effective duration shortens as rates fall and extends as rates rise (negative convexity).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A pool of mortgages prepays at a constant conditional prepayment rate (CPR) of 6%. The single monthly mortality (SMM) rate is closest to:\nA) 0.50% B) 0.51% C) 0.60% D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks us to turn an annual prepayment speed (CPR) into its monthly equivalent (SMM). The catch: you cannot just divide by 12, because prepayment compounds.\n\nThe correct conversion is based on survival (the fraction that does NOT prepay):\n$$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - \\text{CPR})^{1/12}$$\n\nPlug in CPR = 0.06:\n$$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - 0.06)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.94^{1/12}$$\n\nStep 1 — compute $$0.94^{1/12}$$ (the 12th root of 0.94):\n$$0.94^{1/12} = 0.994857$$\n\nStep 2 — subtract from 1:\n$$\\text{SMM} = 1 - 0.994857 = 0.005143 = 0.5143\\% \\approx 0.51\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPlain words: 94% of the pool survives the year without prepaying. To find the monthly survival, take the 12th root, then the part that prepays each month is 1 minus that.\n\n- A (0.50%) is the trap of dividing 6% by 12 — it ignores compounding and is always offered as bait.\n- C (0.60%) is too high; no correct method gives it.\n- D (6.00%) just repeats the annual CPR as if it were monthly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: NEVER divide CPR by 12 to get SMM. Use $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1-\\text{CPR})^{1/12}$$. The compounding makes SMM slightly above CPR/12 (0.5143% vs 0.50%).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CPR to SMM (survival, not /12)\",\"steps\":[\"Annual survival = 1 - CPR\",\"Monthly survival = (1-CPR)^(1/12)\",\"SMM = 1 - monthly survival\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Under 150 PSA, the CPR assumed for month 12 of a pool's life is:\nA) 2.4% B) 3.6% C) 6.0% D) 9.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the prepayment speed in month 12 under a PSA multiple. First find the 100 PSA speed, then scale it.\n\nThe 100 PSA benchmark: CPR starts at 0.2% in month 1 and rises by 0.2% each month until it hits 6% at month 30, then stays flat. So for any month $$t \\le 30$$:\n$$\\text{CPR}_{100} = 0.2\\% \\times t$$\n\nStep 1 — 100 PSA at month 12:\n$$0.2\\% \\times 12 = 2.4\\%$$\n(Month 12 is still on the rising \"ramp,\" below the 6% cap, so this is valid.)\n\nStep 2 — scale to 150 PSA (multiply by 1.5):\n$$1.5 \\times 2.4\\% = 3.6\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPicture a ramp: prepayments climb steadily for the first 30 months, then level off at 6%. At month 12 we're still climbing. 150 PSA just means \"1.5 times faster\" along the whole ramp.\n\n- A (2.4%) is the 100 PSA speed — correct intermediate step but forgets to multiply by 1.5.\n- C (6.0%) is the plateau speed reached at month 30+, not month 12.\n- D (9.0%) is the 150 PSA plateau (1.5 × 6%), which applies only after month 30, not at month 12.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For PSA on the ramp (month ≤ 30): $$\\text{CPR} = \\text{multiple} \\times 0.2\\% \\times \\text{month}$$. After month 30 it's just $$\\text{multiple} \\times 6\\%$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"100 PSA prepayment ramp\",\"points\":[{\"t\":\"Mo 1\",\"l\":\"CPR 0.2%\"},{\"t\":\"Mo 12\",\"l\":\"CPR 2.4%\"},{\"t\":\"Mo 30\",\"l\":\"CPR 6.0%\"},{\"t\":\"Mo 30+\",\"l\":\"flat 6.0%\"}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** When market interest rates fall well below a mortgage pool's loan rate, a pass-through investor primarily faces:\nA) Extension risk and positive convexity B) Contraction risk and negative convexity C) Credit risk from the agency guarantee D) No risk, since principal is returned at par\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what happens to a mortgage investor when rates drop sharply below the loan rate.\n\nWhy B is correct: when rates fall, borrowers rush to refinance, so prepayments speed up and principal comes back EARLY — this is contraction risk. The trouble is you must reinvest that returned cash at the new LOW rates. Also, because borrowers can prepay, the bond's price cannot rise much when rates fall (the upside is capped) — this is NEGATIVE convexity.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — extension risk is the OPPOSITE: it happens when rates RISE and prepayments slow, lengthening the bond. And MBS show negative, not positive, convexity.\n- C — agency MBS (Ginnie/Fannie/Freddie) carry essentially no credit risk; the guarantee removes it, leaving prepayment risk.\n- D — there is definitely risk: early return of principal at low reinvestment rates hurts you.\n\nAnalogy: it's like lending your friend money at a great interest rate, and the moment rates drop they pay you back early — now you can only relend at the new, lousy rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rates DOWN → prepay fast → contraction risk + negative convexity. Rates UP → prepay slow → extension risk. The MBS investor is squeezed both ways.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"MBS prepayment risk by rate move\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Rates fall\",\"items\":[\"refinance, prepay fast\",\"contraction risk\",\"negative convexity (upside capped)\"]},{\"h\":\"Rates rise\",\"items\":[\"prepay slows\",\"extension risk\",\"duration lengthens\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A $100,000, 15-year level-payment mortgage carries a 6% annual rate with monthly compounding. The fixed monthly payment is closest to ($$1.005^{-180} = 0.40748$$):\nA) $599.55 B) $843.86 C) $877.43 D) $1,000.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the constant monthly payment that fully pays off the loan over 15 years.\n\nThe level-payment formula is:\n$$X = \\frac{L \\, r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}$$\n\nwhere $$L = 100{,}000$$, the monthly rate $$r = 6\\%/12 = 0.005$$, and $$n = 15 \\times 12 = 180$$ payments.\n\nStep 1 — numerator:\n$$L \\times r = 100{,}000 \\times 0.005 = 500$$\n\nStep 2 — denominator using $$(1.005)^{-180} = 0.40748$$:\n$$1 - 0.40748 = 0.59252$$\n\nStep 3 — divide:\n$$X = \\frac{500}{0.59252} = 843.86$$\n\nSo B is correct: about $843.86 per month.\n\nPlain words: the payment is set so that 180 equal monthly checks exactly wipe out the loan, covering both interest and principal.\n\n- A ($599.55) is roughly the payment for a 30-YEAR loan (longer term → smaller payment); here it's only 15 years.\n- C ($877.43) uses a slightly wrong rate or term and over-shoots.\n- D ($1,000.00) just splits $100,000 into chunks ignoring interest and the amortization structure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Shorter loans have HIGHER monthly payments (less time to spread the principal). If your 15-year payment came out lower than a 30-year one, you swapped the terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A mortgage pool's beginning-of-month balance is $100,000,000 and scheduled principal for the month is $150,000. The pool prepays at an SMM of 0.5143%. The dollar prepayment for the month is closest to:\nA) $514,000 B) $513,529 C) $771,450 D) $500,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the dollar amount prepaid. The key is to apply the SMM to the balance AFTER removing this month's scheduled principal, not the raw beginning balance.\n\nThe formula rearranged from the SMM definition:\n$$\\text{Prepayment} = \\text{SMM} \\times (\\text{Beginning balance} - \\text{Scheduled principal})$$\n\nStep 1 — balance net of scheduled principal:\n$$100{,}000{,}000 - 150{,}000 = 99{,}850{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — apply the SMM:\n$$0.005143 \\times 99{,}850{,}000 = 513{,}529$$\n\nSo B is correct: about $513,529.\n\nPlain words: scheduled principal is the part everyone pays anyway. Prepayment is EXTRA, voluntary repayment, and it applies only to what's left after the scheduled chunk is taken out.\n\n- A ($514,000) applies the SMM to the full $100,000,000 (forgetting to subtract scheduled principal), giving a slightly too-high figure.\n- C ($771,450) uses a wrong SMM or doubles something; not produced correctly.\n- D ($500,000) is a rough guess ignoring the precise SMM and the net balance.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always subtract scheduled principal FIRST, then multiply by SMM. Total principal returned = scheduled principal + prepayment ($150,000 + $513,529 = $663,529 here).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Monthly prepayment dollars\",\"steps\":[\"Beginning balance\",\"- scheduled principal\",\"x SMM\",\"= prepayment $\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which statement correctly distinguishes agency from non-agency mortgage-backed securities?\nA) Agency MBS carry significant credit risk; non-agency MBS do not B) Agency MBS investors bear essentially only prepayment risk; non-agency investors also bear credit risk C) Both are guaranteed by the US Treasury D) Non-agency MBS may only securitize conforming loans\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how agency and non-agency (private-label) MBS differ in the risks investors face.\n\nWhy B is correct: agency MBS are issued or guaranteed by Ginnie Mae (full faith and credit of the US government) or the GSEs Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, so the credit risk is essentially removed — investors mainly bear PREPAYMENT risk. Non-agency MBS securitize jumbo, alt-A, or subprime loans with no such guarantee, so investors bear CREDIT risk too (managed with credit enhancement like subordination and overcollateralization).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — backwards: agency MBS have little/no credit risk; non-agency MBS DO carry credit risk.\n- C — only Ginnie Mae has full government backing; Fannie/Freddie are GSEs, and non-agency MBS have no Treasury guarantee at all.\n- D — it's the reverse: AGENCY pools require conforming loans; non-agency pools handle the non-conforming (jumbo/subprime) loans.\n\nAnalogy: an agency MBS is like a loan co-signed by a very reliable guarantor — you mostly worry about being paid back early. A non-agency MBS has no co-signer, so you also worry about not being paid back at all.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Agency = (almost) no credit risk, only prepayment risk, conforming loans. Non-agency = credit risk + prepayment risk, non-conforming loans, needs credit enhancement.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Agency vs non-agency MBS\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Agency\",\"items\":[\"guaranteed (Ginnie/Fannie/Freddie)\",\"prepayment risk only\",\"conforming loans\"]},{\"h\":\"Non-agency\",\"items\":[\"no guarantee\",\"prepayment + credit risk\",\"jumbo/alt-A/subprime, enhanced\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In a CMO, a PAC (planned amortization class) tranche receives a stable, scheduled principal flow. The tranche that absorbs the prepayment variability to protect the PAC is the:\nA) Support (companion) tranche B) Sequential-pay tranche C) Senior tranche D) Interest-only tranche\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks which tranche \"takes the hit\" of prepayment swings so the PAC can stay stable.\n\nWhy A is correct: a PAC is designed to receive principal on a predictable schedule. To make that possible, the SUPPORT (also called companion) tranche absorbs the extra prepayments when speeds are high and receives less when speeds are low. It cushions the PAC, so it bears more prepayment risk in exchange.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B — sequential-pay tranches simply receive principal in ORDER (one after another); they are not the specific shock-absorber paired with a PAC.\n- C — \"senior\" refers to credit priority (who gets paid first if losses occur), not to prepayment protection.\n- D — an interest-only tranche receives only interest, not principal; it's a stripped cash flow, not the PAC's companion.\n\nAnalogy: the PAC is a VIP with a guaranteed smooth ride; the support tranche is the buffer that takes all the bumps so the VIP doesn't feel them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PAC = stable scheduled principal; SUPPORT/companion = absorbs the prepayment variability. Remember CMOs only REDISTRIBUTE prepayment risk — they never eliminate it.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CMO: PAC vs support tranche\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"PAC\",\"items\":[\"stable scheduled principal\",\"protected from prepay swings\"]},{\"h\":\"Support (companion)\",\"items\":[\"absorbs prepay variability\",\"bears more prepayment risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** In the first month of a $300,000, 30-year level-payment mortgage at a 6% annual rate (0.5% monthly), with a monthly payment of $1,798.65, the portion of that payment that goes to PRINCIPAL is closest to:\nA) $1,500.00 B) $298.65 C) $1,798.65 D) $900.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how much of the first payment actually reduces the loan balance (principal), versus how much is just interest.\n\nThe split each month:\n- Interest = monthly rate × beginning balance\n- Principal = payment − interest\n\nStep 1 — first-month interest:\n$$0.005 \\times 300{,}000 = 1{,}500.00$$\n\nStep 2 — first-month principal:\n$$1{,}798.65 - 1{,}500.00 = 298.65$$\n\nSo B is correct: only $298.65 of the first payment chips away at the loan.\n\nPlain words: early in a mortgage, almost the whole payment is interest because the balance is huge. Only a tiny sliver pays down principal at first; that sliver grows over time.\n\n- A ($1,500.00) is the INTEREST portion, not the principal — the most common mix-up.\n- C ($1,798.65) is the TOTAL payment, not just the principal piece.\n- D ($900.00) splits the payment roughly in half, which is wrong for month 1 of a 30-year loan.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Early payments are mostly interest; principal share is small at first and grows. Interest = rate × balance; principal = payment − interest. Don't report interest when asked for principal.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Split a level mortgage payment\",\"steps\":[\"Interest = rate x balance\",\"Principal = payment - interest\",\"Early: mostly interest\",\"Principal share grows over time\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A dollar roll transaction involves an MBS holder who:\nA) Buys a TBA for this month and sells it for next month B) Sells a TBA for delivery this month and simultaneously buys it back for delivery next month C) Exchanges a pass-through for a CMO of equal value D) Lends bonds outright with no repurchase agreement\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what actually happens in a dollar roll.\n\nWhy B is correct: in a dollar roll, the MBS holder SELLS a TBA position for delivery this month and at the same time agrees to BUY it back for delivery next month. The price difference between the two settlement months (the \"drop\"), plus the coupon/principal given up during the month, determines the implied financing cost. When that implied rate is below the holder's funding rate, the roll is cheap financing (\"trades special\").\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — this reverses the legs; the holder sells the nearby month and buys the deferred month, not the other way around.\n- C — swapping a pass-through for a CMO is not a dollar roll; that's a different structuring transaction.\n- D — a dollar roll specifically INCLUDES the buy-back (repurchase) leg; an outright loan with no repurchase is something else.\n\nAnalogy: it's like selling your car to a dealer today and arranging to buy it back next month — during the month the dealer uses the car (gets the cash flows), and the price gap is effectively what you pay/earn for that arrangement.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dollar roll = sell now, repurchase later. If the implied financing cost (from the \"drop\") is below your funding rate, the roll is cheap financing and is said to \"trade special.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"MBS dollar roll\",\"steps\":[\"Sell TBA for this month\",\"Buy back TBA for next month\",\"Price gap = the 'drop'\",\"Implied financing cost\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Weighted average life (WAL) of an MBS is best described as the:\nA) Average time to receipt of principal, weighted by principal amounts B) Present-value-weighted average time of all cash flows C) Bond's price sensitivity to a change in interest rates D) Total number of years until the final scheduled payment\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks what WAL actually measures.\n\nWhy A is correct: WAL is the average time until PRINCIPAL is repaid, weighting each principal repayment by its size. It answers the simple question \"on average, when does my money come back?\" It is a cash-flow TIMING measure, not a price-sensitivity measure.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B — that describes DURATION (which weights the present values of ALL cash flows, interest and principal). WAL ignores discounting and looks only at principal.\n- C — price sensitivity to rate changes is duration (and convexity), not WAL.\n- D — that's just the stated maturity; WAL is shorter than the final maturity because principal comes back gradually (and faster with prepayments).\n\nAnalogy: WAL is like asking \"on average, when do I get my deposit back?\" — it's about timing of your money returning, not about how the bond's price wiggles when rates move.\n\n📌 Exam tip: WAL = average TIMING of principal return (a cash-flow measure). Duration = price SENSITIVITY to rates (PV-weighted). They are different concepts — don't swap them.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"WAL vs duration\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"WAL\",\"items\":[\"avg time to principal receipt\",\"weighted by principal $\",\"timing measure, no discounting\"]},{\"h\":\"Duration\",\"items\":[\"PV-weighted time of all cash flows\",\"price sensitivity to rates\",\"includes interest\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A pool's CPR is 6%. The single monthly mortality rate is closest to:\nA) 0.50% B) 0.51% C) 0.60% D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1-0.06)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.994857 = 0.5143\\%$$. Dividing 6% by 12 (answer A) ignores compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Under 150 PSA, the CPR assumed for month 12 of a pool's life is:\nA) 2.4% B) 3.6% C) 6.0% D) 9.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** 100 PSA gives CPR $$= 0.2\\% \\times 12 = 2.4\\%$$ in month 12 (still on the ramp); at 150 PSA, $$1.5 \\times 2.4\\% = 3.6\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** When market rates fall well below a pool's loan rate, a pass-through investor primarily faces:\nA) Extension risk and positive convexity B) Contraction risk and negative convexity C) Credit risk from the agency guarantee D) No risk, since principal is returned at par\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** falling rates trigger refinancing; principal returns early at low reinvestment rates and price gains are capped (negative convexity).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A $100,000, 15-year level-payment mortgage at 6% (monthly compounding) has a monthly payment closest to:\nA) $599.55 B) $843.86 C) $877.43 D) $1,000.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$X = 100{,}000(0.005)/[1 - 1.005^{-180}] = 500/0.59252 = \\$843.86$$. (A is the 30-year payment; D ignores amortization structure.)\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A mortgage pool prepays at a constant CPR of 8%. The single monthly mortality rate is closest to:\nA) 0.64% B) 0.667% C) 0.69% D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - 0.08)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.92^{1/12} = 1 - 0.99308 = \\textbf{0.69\\%}$$. B (0.667%) divides CPR by 12, ignoring compounding — the classic error.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"SMM from CPR\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Correct (compound)\",\"items\":[\"SMM = 1 − (1−CPR)^(1/12)\",\"1 − 0.92^(1/12)\",\"= 0.69%\"]},{\"h\":\"Trap (linear)\",\"items\":[\"CPR / 12\",\"8% / 12\",\"= 0.667%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q16.** A $200,000, 30-year level-payment mortgage carries a 4.8% annual rate (monthly compounding). The principal repaid in the first monthly payment is closest to:\nA) $249 B) $556 C) $800 D) $1,049\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Monthly rate = 0.4%, n = 360. Payment:\n\n$$X = \\frac{200{,}000 \\times 0.004}{1 - (1.004)^{-360}} = \\frac{800}{1 - 0.23761} = \\frac{800}{0.76239} = \\$1{,}049.33$$\n\nFirst-month interest = $$0.004 \\times 200{,}000 = \\$800$$; principal = $$1{,}049.33 - 800 = \\textbf{\\$249.33}$$. C is the interest portion; D is the full payment; B assumes straight-line amortization.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"First-month principal\",\"steps\":[\"Monthly payment ≈ $1,049.33\",\"Interest = 0.004 × 200,000 = $800\",\"Principal = payment − interest\",\"1,049.33 − 800 = $249.33\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A $250,000, 30-year level-payment fixed-rate mortgage carries a 7.2% annual rate (monthly compounding). The portion of the **first** monthly payment that reduces principal is closest to:\nA) $1,500.00 B) $197.43 C) $1,697.43 D) $1,200.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: find the monthly payment, split month 1 into interest and scheduled principal, and report the principal portion.\n\n1. **Monthly rate and term:** $$r = 0.072/12 = 0.006$$; $$n = 360.$$\n2. **Monthly payment:**\n$$X = \\frac{L\\,r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}.$$\n   Compute $$(1.006)^{360}$$: $$\\ln(1.006)=0.00598207$$, $$\\times 360 = 2.153545$$, $$e^{2.153545}=8.61388$$, so $$(1.006)^{-360}=1/8.61388=0.116092.$$\n$$X = \\frac{250{,}000 \\times 0.006}{1 - 0.116092} = \\frac{1{,}500}{0.883908} = \\$1{,}697.43.$$\n3. **First-month interest:** $$0.006 \\times 250{,}000 = \\$1{,}500.00.$$\n4. **First-month scheduled principal:** $$1{,}697.43 - 1{,}500.00 = \\$197.43.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: principal in month 1 = payment − interest = $197.43.\n\n- A ($1,500.00) is the **interest** portion, not principal — the classic swap.\n- C ($1,697.43) is the **total payment**, not the principal split.\n- D ($1,200.00) uses a wrong/rounded rate or term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Month-1 interest = rate × full balance; principal = payment − interest, and it is tiny early on (here ~12% of the payment). Don't report the interest or the whole payment as \"principal.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Month-1 principal portion\",\"steps\":[\"Compute the level payment\",\"Interest = monthly rate x balance\",\"Principal = payment - interest\",\"Tiny early on (mostly interest)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A mortgage pool prepays at a constant **8% CPR**. The single monthly mortality (SMM) rate is closest to:\nA) 0.667% B) 0.692% C) 0.640% D) 8.000%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: convert an annual CPR to its monthly equivalent SMM using the compounding (survival) relationship.\n\n1. **Formula:** $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - \\text{CPR})^{1/12}.$$\n2. **Plug in:** $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - 0.08)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.92^{0.083333}.$$\n3. Compute $$0.92^{1/12}$$: $$\\ln(0.92) = -0.083382$$; $$\\times \\tfrac{1}{12} = -0.0069485$$; $$e^{-0.0069485} = 0.993076.$$\n4. **SMM** $$= 1 - 0.993076 = 0.006924 = 0.692\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: it compounds survival over 12 months rather than scaling linearly.\n\n- A (0.667%) is the trap of dividing CPR by 12 ($$8\\%/12 = 0.667\\%$$) — ignores compounding and is always offered.\n- C (0.640%) compounds the *rate* incorrectly (e.g., $$0.08^{1/12}$$ style error).\n- D (8.000%) confuses the annual CPR with the monthly SMM.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Never divide CPR by 12. Use $$\\text{SMM}=1-(1-\\text{CPR})^{1/12}$$ — survival compounds, so SMM (0.692%) is slightly **above** the naive 0.667%.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CPR to SMM (survival compounds)\",\"steps\":[\"Annual survival = 1 - CPR\",\"Monthly survival = (1-CPR)^(1/12)\",\"SMM = 1 - monthly survival\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Under the **150 PSA** benchmark, the CPR assumed for **month 18** of a pool's life is:\nA) 3.6% B) 5.4% C) 6.0% D) 9.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: find the 100-PSA CPR at month 18 (still on the ramp), then scale by 1.5.\n\n1. **100 PSA ramp:** CPR rises 0.2% per month for the first 30 months, then holds at 6%. At month 18 (≤ 30):\n$$\\text{CPR}_{100} = 0.2\\% \\times 18 = 3.6\\%.$$\n2. **Scale to 150 PSA:**\n$$\\text{CPR}_{150} = 1.5 \\times 3.6\\% = 5.4\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: month 18 is on the ramp (below month 30), so the 100-PSA CPR is $$0.2\\% \\times 18 = 3.6\\%$$; 1.5× gives 5.4%.\n\n- A (3.6%) is the 100-PSA value — forgot to apply the 1.5 multiple.\n- C (6.0%) wrongly assumes the ramp has already plateaued (only true at month 30+).\n- D (9.0%) applies the multiple to the **plateau** (1.5 × 6%), correct only for month 30 and beyond, not month 18.\n\n📌 Exam tip: PSA = ramp then plateau. Before month 30, $$\\text{CPR}_{100}=0.2\\%\\times t$$; at/after month 30 it is 6%. Apply the PSA multiple to the *month-specific* CPR, not always to 6%.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"100 PSA ramp (then x1.5 for 150 PSA)\",\"points\":[{\"t\":\"Mo 1\",\"l\":\"0.2%\"},{\"t\":\"Mo 18\",\"l\":\"3.6% (ramp)\"},{\"t\":\"Mo 30\",\"l\":\"6.0% cap\"},{\"t\":\"Mo 30+\",\"l\":\"flat 6.0%\"}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A pass-through pool has a beginning-of-month balance of $80,000,000 and scheduled principal of $120,000 for the month. The pool prepays at a constant **9% CPR**. Total principal returned to investors this month (scheduled + prepayment) is closest to:\nA) $120,000 B) $625,372 C) $626,272 D) $745,372\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Restate: convert CPR to SMM, apply SMM to the balance **net of scheduled principal**, then add scheduled principal back.\n\n1. **CPR → SMM:** $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1-0.09)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.91^{1/12}.$$\n   $$\\ln(0.91) = -0.0943107$$; $$\\times \\tfrac{1}{12} = -0.0078592$$; $$e^{-0.0078592} = 0.9921716.$$\n   $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - 0.9921716 = 0.0078284 = 0.78284\\%.$$\n2. **Balance net of scheduled principal:** $$80{,}000{,}000 - 120{,}000 = 79{,}880{,}000.$$\n3. **Prepayment:** $$0.0078284 \\times 79{,}880{,}000 = \\$625{,}372.$$\n4. **Total principal returned:** $$120{,}000 + 625{,}372 = \\$745{,}372.$$\n\nWhy D is correct: it subtracts scheduled principal before applying SMM, then adds the scheduled principal back to get total principal cash flow.\n\n- A ($120,000) reports only scheduled principal — ignores prepayment entirely.\n- B ($625,372) is the **prepayment alone** — forgets to add the $120,000 scheduled principal back.\n- C ($626,272) applies SMM to the **raw** $80,000,000 balance (no netting), inflating the prepayment slightly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Apply SMM to (beginning balance − scheduled principal), not the raw balance; then add scheduled principal back for total principal cash flow.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Total principal returned\",\"steps\":[\"CPR -> SMM\",\"(balance - scheduled prin) x SMM = prepay\",\"+ scheduled principal\",\"= total principal cash flow\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Market rates fall sharply, well below the loan rate of an agency pass-through. The investor primarily faces:\nA) Extension risk and positive convexity B) Contraction risk and negative convexity C) Credit risk, since the agency guarantee weakens when rates fall D) No meaningful risk, since principal is always returned at par\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: identify the risk profile when rates drop below the pool's loan rate.\n\nWhy B is correct: falling rates trigger **refinancing**, so prepayments **accelerate** — principal returns early, exactly when reinvestment rates are low (**contraction risk**). Price appreciation is capped because the call-like prepayment option works against the investor — **negative convexity**.\n\n- A is the opposite scenario: extension risk and the absence of upside-capping occur when rates **rise** and prepayments slow.\n- C misstates credit risk: agency MBS bear essentially **no credit risk** (guaranteed), and the guarantee does not depend on rates.\n- D is false — early return of principal at low reinvestment rates is precisely the harm.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rates down → refi up → contraction risk + negative convexity (upside capped). Rates up → prepay slows → extension risk. Agency MBS = prepayment risk, not credit risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"MBS risk by rate move\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Rates fall\",\"items\":[\"refi, prepay fast\",\"contraction risk\",\"negative convexity\"]},{\"h\":\"Rates rise\",\"items\":[\"prepay slows\",\"extension risk\",\"duration lengthens\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which statement correctly distinguishes weighted average life (WAL) from duration for an MBS?\nA) WAL and duration are identical because both measure the timing of cash flows. B) WAL is the average time to receipt of principal weighted by principal amounts; duration weights the PV of all cash flows and, for MBS, must reflect prepayments changing with rates. C) Duration ignores prepayments, while WAL incorporates them via effective duration. D) WAL is a price-sensitivity measure; duration is purely a cash-flow timing measure.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: contrast WAL (timing of principal) with duration (price sensitivity).\n\nWhy B is correct: **WAL** answers \"when does my principal come back?\" — average time to **principal** receipt weighted by principal. **Duration** is a price-sensitivity measure weighting the **PV of all cash flows**, and for MBS the **effective** duration must account for prepayment speeds shifting as rates move (negative convexity: duration shortens as rates fall, extends as rates rise).\n\n- A is wrong: they measure different things (timing vs. price sensitivity) and differ numerically.\n- C inverts the roles — effective **duration** captures rate-dependent prepayments, not WAL.\n- D swaps the definitions entirely.\n\n📌 Exam tip: WAL = principal-timing (cash-flow) measure; (effective) duration = price-sensitivity measure that bakes in rate-dependent prepayments. They are not interchangeable.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"WAL vs duration\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"WAL\",\"items\":[\"avg time to principal receipt\",\"weighted by principal $\",\"timing, no discounting\"]},{\"h\":\"Duration\",\"items\":[\"PV-weighted time, all cash flows\",\"price sensitivity to rates\",\"effective: prepays shift with rates\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which statement about agency MBS structures is **correct**?\nA) A CMO eliminates prepayment risk by redistributing it among tranches. B) In a CMO, PAC tranches receive a stable principal schedule that support (companion) tranches protect by absorbing prepayment variability. C) Specified pools always trade cheaper than TBA pools because delivery is certain. D) Ginnie Mae securities carry GSE backing, while Fannie and Freddie carry the full faith and credit of the US government.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: identify the accurate statement about CMOs, TBA trading, and agency backing.\n\nWhy B is correct: a **CMO** carves cash flows into tranches; **PAC** tranches get a stabilized schedule, and **support (companion)** tranches absorb the prepayment variability that would otherwise hit the PACs.\n\n- A overstates: CMOs **redistribute** prepayment risk across tranches but do **not** eliminate it in aggregate.\n- C reverses the pricing: **specified** pools trade at a **pay-up** (more expensive), while generic TBA pools are cheaper and deeply liquid.\n- D swaps the guarantees: **Ginnie Mae** carries the **full faith and credit of the US government**; **Fannie/Freddie** are GSEs (historically implicit, now conservatorship support) — the statement reverses them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CMOs *redistribute*, never *eliminate*, prepayment risk; PACs are protected by supports; specified pools trade at a *pay-up* over TBA; only **Ginnie Mae** is explicit full-faith-and-credit.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Correct statement on agency MBS structures\",\"options\":[\"CMO eliminates prepayment risk\",\"PAC gets stable schedule; support absorbs variability\",\"Specified pools always cheaper than TBA\",\"Ginnie carries GSE backing; Fannie/Freddie full faith\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"CMOs redistribute (not eliminate) prepay risk; supports protect PACs; only Ginnie has full faith and credit.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"319","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Interest Rate Futures","notes":"# 19. Interest Rate Futures\n\nA computation-dense chapter: day counts, accrued interest, Treasury bond futures with conversion factors and the cheapest-to-deliver rule, Eurodollar/SOFR futures arithmetic ($25 per basis point), the futures-to-forward convexity adjustment, and duration-based hedging. Every formula here is directly testable.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Identify the most commonly used day-count conventions and calculate accrued interest under each.\n- Calculate the price of a T-bill from its quoted discount rate, and convert between clean (quoted) and dirty (cash) bond prices.\n- Describe Treasury bond futures: conversion factors, cheapest-to-deliver (CTD) selection, and the delivery options (including the wild card play).\n- Calculate the final contract price of a Eurodollar/three-month SOFR futures contract and the gain/loss per basis point.\n- Apply the convexity adjustment to convert a futures rate to a forward rate.\n- Calculate the number of futures contracts in a duration-based hedge.\n\n## Day-count conventions\n\n| Instrument | Convention | Accrual fraction |\n|---|---|---|\n| US Treasury bonds/notes | **Actual/Actual** (in period) | actual days elapsed ÷ actual days in coupon period |\n| US corporate and municipal bonds | **30/360** | each month counts 30 days, year counts 360 |\n| US money market (T-bills, SOFR) | **Actual/360** | actual days ÷ 360 |\n\nAccrued interest = coupon for the period × accrual fraction. Note Actual/360 means a 365-day year accrues 365/360 of the quoted rate — money-market rates understate the true annual rate.\n\n**T-bill quotes** use a **discount rate** on face value: if the quoted discount is $$Q$$ (% per annum) for $$n$$ days, the cash price per $100 face is\n\n$$\nP = 100 - \\frac{n}{360}\\,Q\n$$\n\nThe discount rate understates the true yield because it divides by face (100), not price, and uses a 360-day year.\n\n## Clean vs. dirty prices\n\nBonds are **quoted clean**; you **pay dirty**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Cash (dirty) price} = \\text{Quoted (clean) price} + \\text{Accrued interest}\n$$\n\n### Worked example: accrued interest and dirty price\n\nA US Treasury bond with an 8% coupon (paid semiannually March 1 and September 1) is quoted at 98-16 for settlement on July 3.\n\n**Step 1 — day count (Actual/Actual).** March 1 → July 3 = 124 actual days; March 1 → September 1 = 184 actual days.\n\n**Step 2 — accrued interest.** The semiannual coupon is 4 per 100 face:\n\n$$\n\\text{AI} = 4 \\times \\frac{124}{184} = 2.6957\n$$\n\n**Step 3 — dirty price.** The quote 98-16 means $$98\\tfrac{16}{32} = 98.50$$:\n\n$$\n\\text{Cash price} = 98.50 + 2.6957 = 101.1957\n$$\n\nIf this were a **corporate** bond (30/360), days would be $$4 \\times 30 + 2 = 122$$ over a 180-day period: $$\\text{AI} = 4 \\times 122/180 = 2.7111$$ — a small but testable difference.\n\n## Treasury bond futures\n\nThe short can deliver any eligible long-term bond; the invoice price adjusts by a **conversion factor (CF)**, the approximate price of the delivered bond per $1 face assuming a 6% flat yield:\n\n$$\n\\text{Cash received by short} = (\\text{Futures settlement} \\times \\text{CF}) + \\text{Accrued interest}\n$$\n\nThe short delivers the **cheapest-to-deliver** bond — the one minimizing\n\n$$\n\\text{Cost} = \\text{Quoted bond price} - (\\text{Futures price} \\times \\text{CF})\n$$\n\nWhen yields exceed 6%, low-coupon long-maturity bonds tend to be CTD; below 6%, high-coupon short-maturity bonds. The short also holds **delivery timing options** and the **wild card play**: futures settle at 2:00 p.m., but the short can issue a notice of intention to deliver until later that evening at the 2:00 p.m. price — if cash prices fall after the close, delivering cheap bonds at the fixed invoice price is profitable. These embedded options *reduce* the futures price relative to a no-options contract.\n\n### Worked example: cheapest-to-deliver selection\n\nThe futures settlement price is 93.25. Three deliverable bonds:\n\n| Bond | Quoted price | CF | Cost = Price − Futures × CF |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 99.50 | 1.0382 | $$99.50 - 93.25 \\times 1.0382 = 99.50 - 96.8122 = 2.6878$$ |\n| 2 | 143.50 | 1.5188 | $$143.50 - 93.25 \\times 1.5188 = 143.50 - 141.6281 = 1.8719$$ |\n| 3 | 119.75 | 1.2615 | $$119.75 - 93.25 \\times 1.2615 = 119.75 - 117.6349 = 2.1151$$ |\n\nBond 2 has the lowest delivery cost and is **cheapest to deliver**.\n\n## Eurodollar and three-month SOFR futures\n\nQuote = $$100 - R$$, where $$R$$ is the (annualized) three-month rate in percent. Contract size $1 million, three-month accrual ⇒ one basis point change in the rate moves the contract value by\n\n$$\n1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 \\times \\frac{90}{360} = \\$25\n$$\n\nFinal contract price $$= 10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25(100 - Q)]$$. A **long** position gains when the quote rises, i.e., when rates **fall**. Three-month SOFR futures work identically but settle to compounded realized SOFR over the reference quarter.\n\n## Convexity adjustment: futures rate → forward rate\n\nDaily settlement makes the futures rate exceed the corresponding forward rate (gains arrive when rates are high and fund cheaply when rates are low — an advantage to the short rate position priced into futures). For a futures contract on the rate between $$t_1$$ and $$t_2$$ (rates continuously compounded, $$\\sigma$$ = annual standard deviation of the short rate):\n\n$$\n\\text{forward} = \\text{futures} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t_1 t_2\n$$\n\nExample: futures rate 6.038%, $$\\sigma = 1.2\\%$$, $$t_1 = 8$$, $$t_2 = 8.25$$: adjustment $$= \\tfrac{1}{2}(0.012)^2(8)(8.25) = 0.00475$$, so the forward rate is $$6.038\\% - 0.475\\% = 5.563\\%$$. The adjustment grows with the **square** of both volatility and (roughly) maturity — negligible for short maturities, large beyond a few years.\n\n## Duration-based hedging\n\nTo hedge a portfolio of value $$P$$ and duration $$D_P$$ with futures of contract price $$F_C$$ (price per contract) and underlying duration $$D_F$$:\n\n$$\nN^* = -\\frac{P\\,D_P}{F_C\\,D_F}\n$$\n\nA negative $$N^*$$ means **short** futures (the usual case for hedging a long bond portfolio against rate rises).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Duration hedging assumes a small **parallel** shift in the yield curve — it fails for twists and large moves (no convexity). For T-bond futures, use the duration of the **cheapest-to-deliver bond at the futures expiry**, not the portfolio's duration, for $$D_F$$. And remember the CTD cost formula subtracts $$\\text{futures} \\times \\text{CF}$$ from the **quoted** price — adding accrued interest to both sides cancels, so dirty prices are a distractor.\n\n### Worked example: duration-based hedge\n\nA manager holds a $10 million bond portfolio with duration 6.8 years and fears rising rates over the next three months. T-bond futures trade at 93-02 (i.e., $$93\\tfrac{2}{32} = 93.0625$$, contract value $$\\$93{,}062.50$$) and the CTD bond will have duration 9.2 years at expiry.\n\n$$\nN^* = -\\frac{10{,}000{,}000 \\times 6.8}{93{,}062.50 \\times 9.2} = -\\frac{68{,}000{,}000}{856{,}175} = -79.4\n$$\n\n**Short 79 contracts.** If rates fall instead, the hedge gives up the portfolio's gains — duration hedging locks in value both ways.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Day-count conventions by instrument\n> **A:** US Treasury bonds/notes: Actual/Actual. US corporate and municipal bonds: 30/360. US money market (T-bills, SOFR): Actual/360. Accrued interest = period coupon × accrual fraction.\n\n**Q2.** T-bill cash price from a quoted discount rate\n> **A:** $$P = 100 - \\frac{n}{360}\\,Q$$ per $100 face, for discount $$Q$$ over $$n$$ days. The discount rate understates true yield because it divides by face (100), not price, and uses a 360-day year.\n\n**Q3.** Clean vs. dirty price\n> **A:** Bonds are quoted clean but you pay dirty: $$\\text{Cash (dirty) price} = \\text{Quoted (clean) price} + \\text{Accrued interest}$$.\n\n**Q4.** Cash received by the short in a T-bond futures delivery\n> **A:** $$\\text{Cash} = (\\text{Futures settlement} \\times \\text{CF}) + \\text{Accrued interest}$$, where CF is the conversion factor — the price of the delivered bond per $1 face at a flat 6% yield.\n\n**Q5.** Cheapest-to-deliver (CTD) selection rule\n> **A:** Short delivers the bond minimizing $$\\text{Cost} = \\text{Quoted bond price} - (\\text{Futures price} \\times \\text{CF})$$. When yields exceed 6%, low-coupon long-maturity bonds tend to be CTD; below 6%, high-coupon short-maturity.\n\n**Q6.** Wild card play and delivery options\n> **A:** Futures settle at 2:00 p.m. but the short can give notice of delivery later that evening at the 2:00 price; if cash prices fall after the close, delivering cheap at the fixed invoice price profits. These embedded short options reduce the futures price.\n\n**Q7.** Eurodollar / 3-month SOFR futures quote and value per basis point\n> **A:** Quote $$= 100 - R$$. One basis point moves the contract by $$1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 \\times \\frac{90}{360} = \\$25$$. A long gains when the quote rises (rates fall).\n\n**Q8.** Final contract price of a Eurodollar futures contract\n> **A:** $$10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25(100 - Q)]$$, where $$Q$$ is the quote. SOFR futures work the same but settle to compounded realized SOFR over the quarter.\n\n**Q9.** Convexity adjustment: futures rate to forward rate\n> **A:** $$\\text{forward} = \\text{futures} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t_1 t_2$$. Daily settlement biases the futures rate upward, so the forward is lower. The adjustment grows with the square of volatility and (roughly) maturity — negligible short, large beyond a few years.\n\n**Q10.** Why is the futures rate above the forward rate?\n> **A:** Daily margining favors the short-rate position priced into futures: gains arrive when rates are high (reinvested well) and losses fund cheaply when rates are low. So the futures rate is biased upward versus the forward.\n\n**Q11.** Number of contracts in a duration-based hedge\n> **A:** $$N^* = -\\frac{P\\,D_P}{F_C\\,D_F}$$, with $$P$$ = portfolio value, $$D_P$$ its duration, $$F_C$$ = futures contract price, $$D_F$$ = underlying duration. Negative $$N^*$$ means short futures.\n\n**Q12.** Duration-hedge traps\n> **A:** Assumes a small parallel yield-curve shift — fails for twists, large moves, and convexity. For T-bond futures use the duration of the CTD bond at the futures expiry for $$D_F$$, not the portfolio's.\n\n**Q13.** Accrued interest for a Treasury (Actual/Actual)\n> **A:** $$\\text{AI} = \\text{period coupon} \\times \\frac{\\text{actual days since last coupon}}{\\text{actual days in coupon period}}$$. For a corporate bond use 30/360 days instead — a small but testable difference.\n\n**Q14.** CTD formula trap — clean vs. dirty\n> **A:** The CTD cost subtracts $$\\text{futures} \\times \\text{CF}$$ from the quoted (clean) price. Adding accrued interest to both legs cancels, so dirty prices are a distractor.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A 90-day Treasury bill is quoted at a discount rate of 8.00%. Its cash price per $100 of face value is:\nA) $92.00 B) $98.00 C) $98.04 D) $96.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the actual dollar price of a T-bill given its quoted discount rate. T-bill quotes are discount rates on FACE value, using a 360-day year.\n\nThe price formula is:\n$$P = 100 - \\frac{n}{360} \\times Q$$\n\nwhere $$n = 90$$ days and $$Q = 8.00$$ (the discount rate in percent).\n\nStep 1 — compute the day fraction:\n$$\\frac{90}{360} = 0.25$$\n\nStep 2 — compute the discount:\n$$0.25 \\times 8.00 = 2.00$$\n\nStep 3 — subtract from 100:\n$$P = 100 - 2.00 = 98.00$$\n\nSo B is correct: $98.00 per $100 face.\n\nPlain words: a discount quote tells you how many dollars are knocked OFF the $100 face. Over 90 days (a quarter of a 360-day year) at 8%, that's 2 dollars off, so you pay 98.\n\n- A ($92.00) wrongly applies the full 8% for a whole year ($100 − 8) instead of just 90 days.\n- C ($98.04) treats 8% as a yield and computes $$100/1.02$$ — but the quote is a discount, not a yield.\n- D ($96.00) uses the wrong day fraction (e.g., 180/360).\n\n📌 Exam tip: T-bill discount price = $$100 - (n/360)\\times Q$$. The discount rate is NOT a yield — it understates the true return because it divides by face (100) and uses 360 days.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** One basis point move in the rate underlying a three-month Eurodollar (or SOFR) futures contract changes the contract value by:\nA) $100 B) $50 C) $25 D) $10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the dollar value of a one-basis-point move on a standard three-month interest rate futures contract.\n\nThe contract has a $1,000,000 notional and covers a three-month (90/360) period. One basis point is 0.0001. So:\n$$\\$1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 \\times \\frac{90}{360}$$\n\nStep 1 — basis point times notional:\n$$1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 = 100$$\n\nStep 2 — apply the three-month fraction:\n$$100 \\times \\frac{90}{360} = 100 \\times 0.25 = 25$$\n\nSo C is correct: each basis point is worth $25.\n\nPlain words: a full 1% on $1,000,000 for a whole year would be $10,000. But we only want 1/100 of that (one basis point) and only a quarter of a year (three months): $$10{,}000 \\times 0.01 \\times 0.25 = 25$$.\n\n- A ($100) forgets the three-month (×0.25) adjustment — that's the value for a full-year contract.\n- B ($50) uses a half-year fraction by mistake.\n- D ($10) drops a factor; not produced by the correct calculation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize \"$25 per basis point\" for three-month Eurodollar/SOFR futures. It comes from $1M × 0.0001 × (3/12).\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The futures settlement price is 95.00. Three deliverable bonds are: A quoted 98.00 (CF 1.0300), B quoted 105.00 (CF 1.1000), C quoted 112.50 (CF 1.1800). The cheapest-to-deliver bond is:\nA) Bond A B) Bond B C) Bond C D) Cannot be determined without accrued interest\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks which bond is cheapest for the short to deliver. The short picks the bond that minimizes the net cost.\n\nThe cost-to-deliver formula:\n$$\\text{Cost} = \\text{Quoted price} - (\\text{Futures price} \\times \\text{CF})$$\n\nBond A:\n$$98.00 - (95.00 \\times 1.0300) = 98.00 - 97.85 = 0.15$$\n\nBond B:\n$$105.00 - (95.00 \\times 1.1000) = 105.00 - 104.50 = 0.50$$\n\nBond C:\n$$112.50 - (95.00 \\times 1.1800) = 112.50 - 112.10 = 0.40$$\n\nCompare: A = 0.15, B = 0.50, C = 0.40. The lowest cost is Bond A.\n\nSo A is correct.\n\nPlain words: the short buys a bond in the market (its quoted price) and gets paid the futures price × conversion factor at delivery. The cheapest-to-deliver is the one where what you pay minus what you receive is smallest.\n\n- B (Bond B) has the highest cost (0.50), the worst choice for the short.\n- C (Bond C) has cost 0.40, still higher than A's 0.15.\n- D is wrong because accrued interest is added to BOTH the cost side and the invoice side, so it cancels out — it does not affect which bond is cheapest.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CTD minimizes (Quoted price − Futures × CF). Use the QUOTED (clean) price; accrued interest cancels, so \"need accrued interest\" answers are traps.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Cheapest-to-deliver bond\",\"steps\":[\"For each bond: Futures x CF\",\"Cost = quoted price - that\",\"Pick the smallest cost\",\"= CTD (AI cancels)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Relative to the forward rate for the same future period, the Eurodollar futures rate is:\nA) Lower, because of the convexity adjustment B) Higher, because daily settlement favors the rate position when rates rise C) Identical, by no-arbitrage D) Higher only when the yield curve is inverted\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the futures rate compares to the forward rate before any adjustment.\n\nWhy B is correct: futures are settled DAILY (marked to market). This daily settlement benefits the short-rate position priced into the futures — gains arrive when rates are high (and can be reinvested at high rates) while losses are funded cheaply when rates are low. This advantage pushes the futures RATE above the forward rate. So:\n$$\\text{forward} = \\text{futures} - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t_1 t_2$$\nWe SUBTRACT the convexity adjustment to go from futures down to forward, confirming the futures rate is higher.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — the convexity adjustment makes the FORWARD lower than the futures, i.e., the futures is higher; A states the comparison backwards.\n- C — daily settlement breaks the perfect equality; they are not identical.\n- D — the bias exists regardless of curve shape (it comes from volatility and timing), not only when inverted.\n\nAnalogy: getting paid your winnings every single day (futures) is slightly better than waiting until the end (forward), so the futures rate is priced a touch higher to reflect that timing advantage.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Futures rate > forward rate. Convert with $$\\text{forward} = \\text{futures} - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t_1 t_2$$. The adjustment grows with the SQUARE of volatility and with maturity — tiny for short maturities, big for long ones.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Futures rate vs forward rate\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Futures rate\",\"items\":[\"daily settlement (mark-to-market)\",\"biased HIGHER\",\"subtract convexity adj\"]},{\"h\":\"Forward rate\",\"items\":[\"settled at maturity\",\"lower by 0.5 x sigma^2 x t1 x t2\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A US Treasury bond pays an 8% semiannual coupon (coupons March 1 and September 1). For settlement on July 3, March 1 to July 3 is 124 actual days and the full coupon period (March 1 to September 1) is 184 days. Using Actual/Actual, the accrued interest per $100 face is closest to:\nA) $2.70 B) $4.00 C) $5.39 D) $2.17\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for accrued interest on a Treasury bond, which uses the Actual/Actual day-count convention.\n\nAccrued interest = coupon for the period × (days elapsed / days in period). The semiannual coupon is half of 8% on $100 = $4.\n\n$$\\text{AI} = 4 \\times \\frac{124}{184}$$\n\nStep 1 — the accrual fraction:\n$$\\frac{124}{184} = 0.67391$$\n\nStep 2 — multiply by the semiannual coupon:\n$$4 \\times 0.67391 = 2.6957 \\approx \\$2.70$$\n\nSo A is correct.\n\nPlain words: by July 3, about 67% of the half-year coupon period has passed, so the seller has \"earned\" about 67% of the $4 coupon — roughly $2.70 — which the buyer pays as accrued interest.\n\n- B ($4.00) is the FULL semiannual coupon, as if the whole period had elapsed.\n- C ($5.39) wrongly uses the full annual 8% coupon ($8) times the fraction, double-counting.\n- D ($2.17) uses a wrong fraction (e.g., treating it as months out of a full year).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Treasuries use Actual/Actual: AI = (semiannual coupon) × (actual days elapsed ÷ actual days in the coupon period). Corporates would use 30/360 instead.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A manager holds a $10 million bond portfolio with duration 6.8 years and wants to hedge against rising rates over three months. T-bond futures have a contract value of $93,062.50, and the cheapest-to-deliver bond will have duration 9.2 years at expiry. The number of futures contracts for the duration-based hedge is closest to:\nA) Long 79 contracts B) Short 79 contracts C) Short 107 contracts D) Short 59 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how many futures to trade to neutralize the portfolio's interest rate risk, and in which direction.\n\nThe duration-based hedge formula:\n$$N^* = -\\frac{P \\times D_P}{F_C \\times D_F}$$\n\nwhere $$P = 10{,}000{,}000$$, $$D_P = 6.8$$, $$F_C = 93{,}062.50$$ (contract value), and $$D_F = 9.2$$ (duration of the CTD at expiry).\n\nStep 1 — numerator:\n$$10{,}000{,}000 \\times 6.8 = 68{,}000{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — denominator:\n$$93{,}062.50 \\times 9.2 = 856{,}175$$\n\nStep 3 — divide and apply the minus sign:\n$$N^* = -\\frac{68{,}000{,}000}{856{,}175} = -79.4 \\approx -79$$\n\nThe negative sign means SHORT, and the magnitude rounds to 79. So B is correct: short 79 contracts.\n\nPlain words: to protect a bond portfolio from rising rates (which lower bond prices), you SHORT futures so they gain when rates rise, offsetting the loss on your bonds.\n\n- A (long 79) has the right number but wrong direction; going long would ADD to the rate risk, not hedge it.\n- C (short 107) wrongly uses the portfolio duration for the futures (6.8 in the denominator) or mismatches the durations.\n- D (short 59) results from swapping the durations incorrectly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use the CTD bond's duration for $$D_F$$, NOT the portfolio's. A negative $$N^*$$ means short futures — the usual hedge for a long bond portfolio fearing rate increases.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Duration-based hedge\",\"steps\":[\"N* = -(P x Dp)/(Fc x Df)\",\"Df = CTD duration at expiry\",\"Negative -> SHORT futures\",\"Short hedges a long portfolio vs rising rates\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A Eurodollar futures contract has a final settlement quote of 99.00. Using the rule that the final contract price equals $$10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25(100 - Q)]$$, the final contract price is:\nA) $990,000 B) $997,500 C) $1,000,000 D) $975,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the dollar settlement value of a Eurodollar futures contract given the final quote Q = 99.00.\n\nThe formula:\n$$\\text{Price} = 10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25(100 - Q)]$$\n\nStep 1 — compute $$100 - Q$$ (this is the implied rate in percent):\n$$100 - 99.00 = 1.00$$\n\nStep 2 — multiply by 0.25 (the three-month adjustment):\n$$0.25 \\times 1.00 = 0.25$$\n\nStep 3 — subtract from 100 and multiply by 10,000:\n$$100 - 0.25 = 99.75$$\n$$10{,}000 \\times 99.75 = 997{,}500$$\n\nSo B is correct: $997,500.\n\nPlain words: a quote of 99.00 means an implied rate of 1%. Because the contract only covers three months, you charge a quarter of that 1% (0.25) against the $1,000,000 notional, giving a price of $997,500.\n\n- A ($990,000) forgets the 0.25 three-month factor and uses the full 1% off ($10,000 × 99 = $990,000).\n- C ($1,000,000) treats the contract as worth full notional with no rate deduction.\n- D ($975,000) uses 2.5% off instead of 0.25%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Final ED futures price = $$10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25(100 - Q)]$$. The 0.25 reflects the three-month period — don't drop it.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Bonds are quoted \"clean,\" but settlement is on the \"dirty\" (cash) price. The relationship is:\nA) Dirty price = Clean price − Accrued interest B) Dirty price = Clean price + Accrued interest C) Dirty price = Clean price × Conversion factor D) Dirty price = Clean price, since accrued interest is ignored\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the cash (dirty) price you actually pay relates to the quoted (clean) price.\n\nWhy B is correct: the quoted price excludes interest that has built up since the last coupon. But the seller is owed that earned interest, so the buyer pays it on top:\n$$\\text{Cash (dirty) price} = \\text{Quoted (clean) price} + \\text{Accrued interest}$$\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — subtracting accrued interest is backwards; the buyer must ADD it to compensate the seller.\n- C — the conversion factor applies to FUTURES invoice prices, not to the clean-to-dirty conversion of a cash bond.\n- D — accrued interest is NOT ignored in settlement; it's added.\n\nAnalogy: if you buy a fruit tree partway between harvests, you pay the listed tree price PLUS the value of the fruit that has already grown since the last picking. The grown fruit is the accrued interest.\n\n📌 Exam tip: You QUOTE clean, you PAY dirty: Dirty = Clean + Accrued Interest. The buyer reimburses the seller for interest earned since the last coupon.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Clean (quoted) to dirty (cash) price\",\"options\":[\"Dirty = Clean - Accrued Interest\",\"Dirty = Clean + Accrued Interest\",\"Dirty = Clean x Conversion Factor\",\"Dirty = Clean (AI ignored)\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The buyer must compensate the seller for interest earned since the last coupon, so AI is added.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** For a three-month Eurodollar futures contract, a LONG position gains when:\nA) The quoted price falls (rates rise) B) The quoted price rises (rates fall) C) Accrued interest increases D) The conversion factor rises\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what makes the holder of a long Eurodollar futures position profit.\n\nWhy B is correct: the quote is $$100 - R$$. So when rates $$R$$ FALL, the quoted price RISES. A long position profits when the price it bought rises, so the long gains when rates fall (price rises).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — a falling quote means rates rose; that's a LOSS for the long (and a gain for the short).\n- C — accrued interest is a bond concept, irrelevant to whether a Eurodollar futures long gains.\n- D — the conversion factor applies to Treasury bond futures, not Eurodollar futures.\n\nAnalogy: since the price is \"100 minus the rate,\" lower rates push the price up like a seesaw. Buying low and watching the price climb (because rates dropped) is how the long wins.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Eurodollar quote = 100 − rate. Long gains when PRICE RISES = rates FALL. Short gains when rates rise. The seesaw between price and rate trips up many candidates.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"When does a long Eurodollar futures gain?\",\"options\":[\"Quoted price falls (rates rise)\",\"Quoted price rises (rates fall)\",\"Accrued interest increases\",\"Conversion factor rises\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Quote = 100 - rate; a long profits when the price it bought rises, i.e. when rates fall.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A key limitation of duration-based hedging is that it assumes:\nA) A large, non-parallel twist in the yield curve B) A small, parallel shift in the yield curve C) Interest rates remain perfectly constant D) The portfolio contains only zero-coupon bonds\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the main assumption (and weakness) behind a duration hedge.\n\nWhy B is correct: duration measures price sensitivity to a SMALL, PARALLEL shift in the yield curve (all rates move by the same small amount). The hedge is only accurate under that assumption. It ignores convexity (so it fails for large moves) and ignores twists (so it fails when short and long rates move differently).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — duration does NOT handle large or non-parallel (twisting) moves; that's precisely where it breaks down, not what it assumes.\n- C — if rates never changed, you wouldn't need a hedge at all; the hedge is for when rates DO move (by a small parallel amount).\n- D — duration hedging applies to any portfolio, not just zero-coupon bonds.\n\nAnalogy: duration is like estimating how far a car travels using its current speed only — fine for a quick glance (small move), but inaccurate over a long trip where speed changes (large/non-parallel moves), which is what convexity would capture.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Duration assumes a SMALL PARALLEL shift. It fails for large moves (ignores convexity) and curve twists. For T-bond futures, use the CTD bond's duration at expiry as $$D_F$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Key assumption of duration hedging\",\"options\":[\"Large non-parallel twist\",\"Small parallel shift in the curve\",\"Rates stay perfectly constant\",\"Portfolio holds only zeros\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Duration captures only a small, parallel shift; it ignores convexity (large moves) and curve twists.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A 90-day T-bill is quoted at a discount rate of 8.00%. Its cash price per $100 of face value is:\nA) $92.00 B) $98.00 C) $98.04 D) $96.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P = 100 - (90/360)(8) = 100 - 2 = 98.00$$. (C, $$100/1.02$$, wrongly treats the discount quote as a yield.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** One basis point move in the rate underlying a Eurodollar futures contract changes the contract value by:\nA) $100 B) $50 C) $25 D) $10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\$1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 \\times 0.25 = \\$25$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The futures settlement price is 95.00. Deliverable bonds: A quoted 98.00 (CF 1.0300), B quoted 105.00 (CF 1.1000), C quoted 112.50 (CF 1.1800). The cheapest to deliver is:\nA) Bond A B) Bond B C) Bond C D) Cannot be determined without accrued interest\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** costs: A: $$98 - 95(1.03) = 98 - 97.85 = 0.15$$; B: $$105 - 104.50 = 0.50$$; C: $$112.50 - 112.10 = 0.40$$. Lowest cost wins; accrued interest cancels from both legs.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Relative to the forward rate for the same period, the Eurodollar futures rate is:\nA) Lower, because of the convexity adjustment B) Higher, because daily settlement favors the position when rates rise C) Identical, by no-arbitrage D) Higher only when the yield curve is inverted\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** futures rate = forward rate **plus** $$\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t_1 t_2$$; daily margining is advantageous to the rate position, so the futures rate is biased upward, and the adjustment subtracts it.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A trader is long 10 Eurodollar futures contracts bought at a quote of 96.50. The quote settles at 96.76. The trader's gain is:\nA) $650 B) $2,600 C) $6,500 D) −$6,500\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The quote rose 26 basis points; a long gains $25 per bp per contract: $$26 \\times 25 \\times 10 = \\textbf{\\$6,500}$$. A long Eurodollar/SOFR position profits when rates **fall** (quote rises).\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q16.** A US corporate bond pays a 7% coupon semiannually on March 1 and September 1. For settlement on July 3, the accrued interest per $100 face value is closest to:\nA) $1.750 B) $2.359 C) $2.372 D) $2.411\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Corporate bonds use **30/360**: March 1 → July 3 counts $$4 \\times 30 + 2 = 122$$ days of a 180-day period. AI = $$3.5 \\times 122/180 = \\textbf{\\$2.372}$$. B ($$3.5 \\times 124/184 = 2.359$$) applies the Treasury Actual/Actual convention — the intended trap; D mixes actual days with a 360 denominator.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Day-count conventions\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Corporate 30/360\",\"items\":[\"122 / 180 days\",\"3.5 × 122/180\",\"AI = $2.372\"]},{\"h\":\"Treasury Act/Act\",\"items\":[\"124 / 184 actual days\",\"3.5 × 124/184\",\"AI = $2.359 (trap)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A US Treasury bond pays a 6% coupon semiannually on April 15 and October 15. It is quoted at 101-08 for settlement on August 1. Using the correct day-count convention, the **cash (dirty) price** per $100 face is closest to:\nA) $101.25 B) $103.02 C) $102.73 D) $99.48\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: convert the quote to decimal, compute accrued interest under Actual/Actual (Treasury convention), and add it to the clean price.\n\n1. **Quote to decimal:** 101-08 means $$101\\tfrac{8}{32} = 101.25.$$\n2. **Day count (Treasury = Actual/Actual in period).** April 15 → August 1: 15 days left in April (16→30), May 31, June 30, July 31, plus 1 day to Aug 1 = $$15 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 1 = 108$$ days. Coupon period April 15 → October 15 = $$183$$ actual days.\n3. **Accrued interest:** semiannual coupon $$= 6\\%/2 = 3$$ per 100 face:\n$$\\text{AI} = 3 \\times \\frac{108}{183} = 3 \\times 0.590164 = 1.7705.$$\n4. **Dirty price:** $$101.25 + 1.7705 = \\$103.02.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: dirty = clean quote + accrued interest = 101.25 + 1.77 = $103.02.\n\n- A ($101.25) is the **clean** quote with **no** accrued interest added — the most common omission.\n- C ($102.73) uses the **wrong direction** or a 30/360-style day count (122/180 → AI 1.48 plus an arithmetic slip).\n- D ($99.48) *subtracts* accrued interest instead of adding it (101.25 − 1.77).\n\n📌 Exam tip: You are *quoted clean* but *pay dirty*: dirty = clean + accrued. Treasuries use **Actual/Actual** — real days elapsed over real days in the coupon period.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Quoted clean to cash (dirty) price\",\"steps\":[\"Convert 32nds quote to decimal\",\"Accrued interest (Actual/Actual)\",\"Dirty = clean + accrued\",\"Buyer pays the dirty price\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The T-bond futures settlement price is 96.50. Three deliverable bonds and their conversion factors:\n\n| Bond | Quoted price | CF |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 102.40 | 1.0450 |\n| 2 | 137.20 | 1.4100 |\n| 3 | 115.60 | 1.1850 |\n\nThe cheapest-to-deliver bond is:\nA) Bond 1 B) Bond 2 C) Bond 3 D) Cannot be determined without accrued interest\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: minimize the delivery cost $$= \\text{Quoted price} - (\\text{Futures} \\times \\text{CF})$$ across the three bonds.\n\n1. **Bond 1:** $$102.40 - 96.50 \\times 1.0450 = 102.40 - 100.8425 = 1.5575.$$\n2. **Bond 2:** $$137.20 - 96.50 \\times 1.4100 = 137.20 - 136.065 = 1.1350.$$\n3. **Bond 3:** $$115.60 - 96.50 \\times 1.1850 = 115.60 - 114.3525 = 1.2475.$$\n4. **Lowest cost:** Bond 2 at 1.1350 → cheapest to deliver.\n\nWhy B is correct: Bond 2 minimizes (quoted price − futures × CF), the delivery cost the short bears.\n\n- A (Bond 1) has the **highest** cost (1.5575) — the trap of picking the lowest *quoted price* or highest CF without computing net cost.\n- C (Bond 3) has an intermediate cost (1.2475).\n- D is wrong: accrued interest is added to **both** the invoice and the purchase, so it cancels — CTD can be determined from quoted prices and CFs alone.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CTD minimizes (quoted price − futures × CF). Accrued interest cancels from both legs, so \"need accrued interest\" is always a distractor.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Cheapest-to-deliver bond\",\"steps\":[\"For each bond: Futures x CF\",\"Cost = quoted price - that\",\"Pick the smallest cost\",\"= CTD (AI cancels)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A trader is **long** one three-month SOFR (Eurodollar-style) futures contract. The quote rises from 97.60 to 97.85. The trader's gain is closest to:\nA) $625 gain B) $625 loss C) $250 gain D) $2,500 gain\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate: a long gains when the quote rises (rates fall); compute the move in basis points times $25.\n\n1. **Basis-point value:** $$\\$1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 \\times \\tfrac{90}{360} = \\$25$$ per basis point.\n2. **Quote change:** $$97.85 - 97.60 = 0.25 = 25$$ basis points.\n3. **Long position:** a rising quote means falling rates — a **gain** for the long.\n4. **Gain:** $$25 \\text{ bp} \\times \\$25 = \\$625.$$\n\nWhy A is correct: +25 bp on the quote × $25/bp = $625, and the long gains as the quote rises.\n\n- B ($625 loss) gets the magnitude right but the **sign** wrong — a long gains, not loses, when the quote rises.\n- C ($250 gain) uses $10/bp or miscounts the move (e.g., 25 bp × $10).\n- D ($2,500 gain) treats the 0.25 quote move as 250 bp, off by a factor of 10.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $25 per basis point per contract. Long gains when the **quote rises** (rates fall). Convert quote change to basis points first (0.25 quote = 25 bp).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"P&L on a long SOFR futures\",\"steps\":[\"Quote change in bp (0.25 = 25 bp)\",\"$25 per bp per contract\",\"Quote up = rates down = long gains\",\"25 bp x $25 = $625 gain\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A Eurodollar futures contract has a futures rate of 7.20% (continuously compounded). With $$\\sigma = 1.1\\%$$ per annum, $$t_1 = 6$$ years and $$t_2 = 6.25$$ years, the corresponding **forward rate** after the convexity adjustment is closest to:\nA) 7.20% B) 6.97% C) 7.43% D) 6.43%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: subtract the convexity adjustment to convert the futures rate to a forward rate.\n\n1. **Formula:** $$\\text{forward} = \\text{futures} - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 t_1 t_2.$$\n2. **Adjustment:** $$\\tfrac{1}{2}(0.011)^2 (6)(6.25) = 0.5 \\times 0.000121 \\times 37.5 = 0.5 \\times 0.0045375 = 0.00226875.$$\n3. In percent: $$0.00226875 = 0.2269\\%.$$\n4. **Forward rate:** $$7.20\\% - 0.2269\\% = 6.973\\% \\approx 6.97\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the futures rate is **biased upward** by daily settlement, so the forward is the futures rate **minus** the adjustment.\n\n- A (7.20%) ignores the adjustment entirely — assumes futures = forward.\n- C (7.43%) **adds** the adjustment instead of subtracting (wrong sign).\n- D (6.43%) inflates the adjustment (e.g., forgets the ½ or squares incorrectly).\n\n📌 Exam tip: forward = futures − ½σ²t₁t₂. The futures rate is always the *higher* one (daily margining favors the rate position), so you **subtract** to get the forward. Watch the ½ and the **squared** volatility.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Futures rate vs forward rate\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Futures rate\",\"items\":[\"daily margining\",\"biased HIGHER\",\"7.20% here\"]},{\"h\":\"Forward rate\",\"items\":[\"= futures - 0.5 sigma^2 t1 t2\",\"lower\",\"6.97% here\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A 60-day US Treasury bill is quoted at a discount rate of 5.40%. Its cash price per $100 of face value, and its true (money-market) yield, are best described as:\nA) Cash price $99.10; the discount rate equals the true yield. B) Cash price $99.10; the discount rate understates the true yield. C) Cash price $94.60; the discount rate overstates the true yield. D) Cash price $99.10; the true yield is exactly 5.40% on a bond-equivalent basis.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: price the T-bill from its discount quote and judge how the discount rate compares to the true yield.\n\n1. **Cash price** from discount rate $$Q$$ over $$n$$ days:\n$$P = 100 - \\frac{n}{360}\\,Q = 100 - \\frac{60}{360} \\times 5.40 = 100 - 0.16667 \\times 5.40 = 100 - 0.90 = \\$99.10.$$\n2. **Why the discount rate understates the true yield:** the discount rate divides the $0.90 gain by **face (100)** and uses a 360-day year. The true money-market yield divides by **price (99.10)** and is therefore higher:\n$$\\text{true (approx)} = \\frac{0.90}{99.10} \\times \\frac{360}{60} = 0.009082 \\times 6 = 5.449\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: cash price is $99.10, and the discount rate (5.40%) is **below** the true yield (~5.45%) because it divides by face rather than price.\n\n- A is wrong: the discount rate does **not** equal the true yield.\n- C miscomputes the price ($94.60 would use the wrong day fraction) and reverses the bias.\n- D falsely claims 5.40% is the bond-equivalent yield.\n\n📌 Exam tip: T-bill discount rate divides the gain by **face** and uses 360 days, so it **understates** the true yield. Price = 100 − (n/360)·Q.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"T-bill discount rate vs true yield\",\"options\":[\"Price 99.10; discount = true yield\",\"Price 99.10; discount UNDERSTATES true yield\",\"Price 94.60; discount overstates yield\",\"Price 99.10; 5.40% is the BEY\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Discount rate divides the gain by face (100) and uses 360 days, so it sits below the true money-market yield.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A manager holds a $20 million bond portfolio with duration 5.5 years and wants to hedge against rising rates over three months. T-bond futures trade at 124-16 (contract value $124,500.00) and the cheapest-to-deliver bond will have duration 8.0 years at futures expiry. The hedge requires the manager to:\nA) Short 76 contracts B) Short 110 contracts C) Short 138 contracts D) Long 110 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: compute the duration-based hedge ratio using the CTD duration, and determine direction.\n\n1. **Contract value:** 124-16 $$= 124\\tfrac{16}{32} = 124.50$$ → $$\\$124{,}500.00.$$\n2. **Hedge formula:**\n$$N^* = -\\frac{P\\,D_P}{F_C\\,D_F}.$$\n3. **Plug in** ($$D_F$$ = CTD duration = 8.0):\n$$N^* = -\\frac{20{,}000{,}000 \\times 5.5}{124{,}500 \\times 8.0} = -\\frac{110{,}000{,}000}{996{,}000} = -110.4.$$\n4. **Round:** short **110 contracts** (negative ⇒ short, the usual hedge against rising rates).\n\nWhy B is correct: $$|N^*| \\approx 110$$, and the negative sign means **short** futures.\n\n- A (short 76) wrongly uses the **portfolio** duration (5.5) for $$D_F$$ as well, or otherwise mis-divides.\n- C (short 138) swaps the durations (uses 6.4-style mix) or drops the CTD adjustment.\n- D (long 110) has the right size but the **wrong direction** — hedging a long portfolio against rising rates requires **shorting** futures.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$N^* = -\\frac{P D_P}{F_C D_F}$$, with $$D_F$$ = duration of the **CTD bond at expiry**, not the portfolio's. Negative ⇒ short. Hedging long bonds against rate rises ⇒ short futures.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Duration-based hedge\",\"steps\":[\"N* = -(P x Dp)/(Fc x Df)\",\"Df = CTD duration at expiry\",\"Negative -> SHORT futures\",\"Hedges a long portfolio vs rising rates\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A US **corporate** bond pays an 8% coupon semiannually on January 1 and July 1. For settlement on April 1, the accrued interest per $100 face under the correct convention is closest to:\nA) $2.00 B) $1.96 C) $2.04 D) $1.78\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Restate: compute accrued interest using the **30/360** convention (US corporate bonds).\n\n1. **Convention:** US corporate bonds use **30/360** — each month = 30 days, year = 360.\n2. **Days from Jan 1 to Apr 1 (30/360):** 3 full months $$= 3 \\times 30 = 90$$ days. Coupon period Jan 1 → Jul 1 $$= 180$$ days.\n3. **Semiannual coupon:** $$8\\%/2 = 4$$ per 100 face.\n4. **Accrued interest:**\n$$\\text{AI} = 4 \\times \\frac{90}{180} = 4 \\times 0.5 = \\$2.00.$$\n\nWhy A is correct: under 30/360, Jan 1→Apr 1 is exactly 90 of 180 days = half the period, so AI = half the coupon = $2.00.\n\n- B ($1.96) uses **Actual/Actual** (90 actual days / 181 or 182), the wrong convention for a corporate bond.\n- C ($2.04) over-counts actual days (e.g., 92/181 style).\n- D ($1.78) mis-applies the day count (e.g., 80/180).\n\n📌 Exam tip: US **corporates/munis = 30/360**; **Treasuries = Actual/Actual**; **money market (T-bills, SOFR) = Actual/360**. Pick the convention from the instrument before counting days.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Day-count conventions\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Corporates / munis\",\"items\":[\"30/360\",\"each month = 30 days\"]},{\"h\":\"Treasuries\",\"items\":[\"Actual/Actual\",\"real days in the period\"]},{\"h\":\"Money market\",\"items\":[\"Actual/360\",\"T-bills, SOFR\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"320","module":"Book 3: Financial Markets and Products","title":"Swaps","notes":"# 20. Swaps\n\nGARP tests two valuation views of an interest rate swap — a portfolio of two bonds and a portfolio of FRAs — plus the comparative-advantage story (and its flaw), currency swap mechanics, and where the credit risk sits. The bond-portfolio valuation with given zero rates is the workhorse calculation.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the mechanics of a plain vanilla interest rate swap and compute its cash flows; describe the role of confirmations and the ISDA Master Agreement.\n- Explain the comparative advantage argument for swaps and evaluate its criticism.\n- Value a plain vanilla interest rate swap as a portfolio of bonds and, equivalently, as a portfolio of FRAs.\n- Explain the mechanics of a currency swap, compute its cash flows, and value it as two bonds linked by the spot exchange rate.\n- Identify other swap types: basis, equity, amortizing, and swaptions.\n- Describe the credit risk exposure in a swap position and how it differs between interest rate and currency swaps.\n\n## Mechanics and documentation\n\nIn a plain vanilla interest rate swap, one party pays a **fixed** rate and receives a **floating** rate (historically LIBOR, now SOFR-based) on a **notional principal** that is never exchanged. Payments are netted each period; the floating rate applying to a period is set at the **start** of the period (or, for compounded SOFR, observed over it) and paid at the **end**. The economic terms of each trade are recorded in a **confirmation**, which sits under the **ISDA Master Agreement** governing netting, collateral, and default between the two parties. Dealers warehouse swaps and hedge residual exposure rather than waiting for matched offsetting counterparties.\n\nA pay-fixed (\"payer\") swap gains when rates rise; a receive-fixed (\"receiver\") swap gains when rates fall. A swap converts a fixed-rate liability into floating (or vice versa), and likewise transforms asset income.\n\n## Comparative advantage — and its criticism\n\nSuppose A can borrow fixed at 4.0% or floating at SOFR − 0.1%, while B faces 5.2% fixed or SOFR + 0.6%. B pays 1.2% more fixed but only 0.7% more floating, so A has comparative advantage in **fixed**, B in **floating**. Total gain to be shared:\n\n$$\n\\text{Gain} = 1.2\\% - 0.7\\% = 0.5\\%\n$$\n\nEach borrows where it is relatively cheapest, then swaps into the exposure it wants (see worked example below).\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> The criticism: the fixed-rate spread (1.2%) is a **long-term, locked-in** credit spread, while the floating spread (0.7%) applies only until the **next refinancing/reset**. B's apparent \"cheap\" floating rate can be repriced upward if its credit deteriorates, so the 0.5% gain is partly illusory — it compensates for B bearing rollover/spread risk, not a free lunch from market inefficiency.\n\n### Worked example: splitting the comparative-advantage gain\n\nUsing the rates above, A wants floating and B wants fixed; they share the 0.5% gain equally (no intermediary).\n\n**Step 1 — borrow where each is relatively cheap.** A issues fixed at 4.0%; B issues floating at SOFR + 0.6%.\n\n**Step 2 — swap.** B pays A 4.35% fixed; A pays B SOFR (on matching notionals).\n\n**Step 3 — check net costs.** A: $$4.0\\% + \\text{SOFR} - 4.35\\% = \\text{SOFR} - 0.35\\%$$ — saves 0.25% vs. its direct floating rate of SOFR − 0.1%. B: $$(\\text{SOFR} + 0.6\\%) + 4.35\\% - \\text{SOFR} = 4.95\\%$$ — saves 0.25% vs. its direct fixed rate of 5.2%. With a bank intermediary taking 0.04%, each side would keep 0.23%.\n\n## Valuing a swap as a bond portfolio\n\nTo the **receive-fixed** party, a swap is long a fixed-rate bond and short a floating-rate note:\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{swap}} = B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}\n$$\n\nand to the **pay-fixed (payer)** party, $$V_{\\text{swap}} = B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$. Two facts make this fast:\n\n- $$B_{\\text{fixed}}$$ = PV of the remaining fixed coupons plus notional at maturity, discounted at current zero rates.\n- $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ **prices at par immediately after each reset**. Between resets, it equals the PV of (notional + the already-fixed next floating payment) discounted to the next payment date — all later floating cash flows are worth par at that date.\n\n### Worked example: payer swap valued as a bond portfolio\n\nA $100 million swap has 1.25 years remaining: the institution **pays 8% fixed** (semiannual) and receives 6-month floating. Continuously compounded zero rates are 10% (3 months), 10.5% (9 months), and 11% (15 months). The floating payment due in 3 months was fixed at 10.2% (semiannual) at the last reset.\n\n**Step 1 — fixed bond.** Coupons of $4M at 0.25, 0.75, 1.25 years plus $100M notional at 1.25:\n\n$$\nB_{\\text{fixed}} = 4e^{-0.10(0.25)} + 4e^{-0.105(0.75)} + 104e^{-0.11(1.25)}\n$$\n\n$$\nB_{\\text{fixed}} = 4(0.97531) + 4(0.92427) + 104(0.87153) = 3.9012 + 3.6971 + 90.6395 = \\$98.238\\text{M}\n$$\n\n**Step 2 — floating bond.** Worth (notional + known payment) discounted from the next reset: payment $$= 100 \\times 0.102/2 = \\$5.1$$M:\n\n$$\nB_{\\text{float}} = 105.1\\,e^{-0.10(0.25)} = 105.1(0.97531) = \\$102.505\\text{M}\n$$\n\n**Step 3 — payer swap value.**\n\n$$\nV = B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}} = 102.505 - 98.238 = +\\$4.27\\text{M}\n$$\n\nThe payer swap is in the money because rates (≈10–11%) sit well above the 8% fixed rate being paid.\n\n## Valuing a swap as a portfolio of FRAs\n\nEach exchange is an FRA: the floating payment is valued by **assuming forward rates are realized**. Compute the forward rate for each period from the zero curve, set each net cash flow as $$(\\text{fixed} - \\text{forward}) \\times \\tau \\times L$$ (for the receiver), discount each at the matching zero rate, and sum. This gives **exactly the same value** as the bond approach. At initiation a swap is worth zero in total, but individual FRAs are not: with an upward-sloping curve, a receiver's early exchanges have positive value and later ones negative.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> When discounting the floating leg, do not project floating cash flows beyond the next payment — the next payment is **already fixed** at the last reset, and the FRN reverts to par right after it. Discounting \"estimated\" floating coupons to maturity, or valuing $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ at par *between* resets (it is par only **at** reset), are the two classic errors. Also watch the sign: $$B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}$$ is the **receiver's** value; flipping the perspective flips the sign.\n\n## Currency swaps\n\nA fixed-for-fixed currency swap exchanges principal **and** interest in one currency for principal and interest in another. Unlike interest rate swaps, **principals are exchanged at initiation and at maturity** (at the same FX rate, set so each leg starts at par). Uses: transform a liability or investment from one currency to another; exploit comparative advantage across currency funding markets (the criticism here is weaker, since spreads partly reflect tax/market segmentation, but the swap creates FX-linked credit exposure).\n\nValuation as two bonds, for the party **receiving foreign** currency and paying dollars, with $$S_0$$ = spot (dollars per unit of foreign):\n\n$$\nV_{\\text{swap}} = S_0 B_F - B_D\n$$\n\nwhere $$B_F$$ is the foreign-currency bond (in foreign units) and $$B_D$$ the dollar bond. A currency swap can also be valued as a portfolio of FX forwards, one per exchange date.\n\n### Worked example: currency swap value\n\nAn institution receives 5% on ¥1,200M and pays 8% on $10M, with annual exchanges and 3 years remaining (principals exchanged at maturity). Flat continuously compounded rates: 4% in Japan, 9% in the US. Spot: ¥110 = $1.\n\n**Step 1 — dollar bond.**\n\n$$\nB_D = 0.8e^{-0.09} + 0.8e^{-0.18} + 10.8e^{-0.27} = 0.7311 + 0.6682 + 8.2445 = \\$9.644\\text{M}\n$$\n\n**Step 2 — yen bond.**\n\n$$\nB_F = 60e^{-0.04} + 60e^{-0.08} + 1260e^{-0.12} = 57.647 + 55.387 + 1117.52 = ¥1230.55\\text{M}\n$$\n\n**Step 3 — convert and net.** $$S_0 = 1/110$$ dollars per yen:\n\n$$\nV = \\frac{1230.55}{110} - 9.644 = 11.187 - 9.644 = +\\$1.543\\text{M}\n$$\n\n## Other swaps and credit risk\n\n- **Basis swap**: floating-for-floating on two different reference rates (e.g., SOFR vs. commercial paper) plus a spread.\n- **Equity swap**: total return on an index exchanged for fixed or floating — equitizes cash without trading the stocks.\n- **Amortizing/step-up swaps** vary the notional; **forward swaps** start later; **swaptions** are options to enter a swap (a payer swaption benefits from rising rates).\n- **Credit risk**: a swap exposes a party only **when its value is positive** — exposure is the replacement cost, far smaller than notional, and mitigated by netting and collateral under the ISDA framework (and by CCP clearing for standardized swaps). Potential exposure on an interest rate swap peaks mid-life (value has had time to drift, payments remain); a **currency swap's exposure grows toward maturity** because the final principal exchange dominates — so currency swaps carry materially more credit risk than rate swaps of the same notional.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Mechanics of a plain vanilla interest rate swap\n> **A:** One party pays fixed and receives floating (SOFR-based) on a notional that is never exchanged. Payments are netted; the floating rate is set at the start of a period and paid at the end. A payer gains when rates rise; a receiver gains when rates fall.\n\n**Q2.** Confirmations and the ISDA Master Agreement\n> **A:** Each trade's economic terms are recorded in a confirmation, which sits under the ISDA Master Agreement governing netting, collateral, and default between the two parties.\n\n**Q3.** Comparative advantage — total gain\n> **A:** $$\\text{Gain} = (\\text{fixed-rate spread}) - (\\text{floating-rate spread})$$ between the two borrowers. Each borrows where it is relatively cheapest, then swaps into the exposure it wants, sharing the gain.\n\n**Q4.** Criticism of the comparative-advantage argument\n> **A:** The fixed-rate spread is a long-term locked-in credit spread, while the floating spread applies only until the next reset/refinancing. The weaker firm's cheap floating rate can reprice upward if its credit deteriorates, so the gain partly compensates for rollover/spread risk — not a free lunch.\n\n**Q5.** Value a swap as a bond portfolio\n> **A:** Receive-fixed: $$V_{\\text{swap}} = B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}$$. Pay-fixed (payer): $$V_{\\text{swap}} = B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$.\n\n**Q6.** Valuing the fixed leg $$B_{\\text{fixed}}$$\n> **A:** PV of the remaining fixed coupons plus notional at maturity, discounted at current zero rates.\n\n**Q7.** Valuing the floating leg $$B_{\\text{float}}$$\n> **A:** Prices at par immediately after each reset. Between resets it equals (notional + the already-fixed next floating payment) discounted to the next payment date — all later floating cash flows are worth par at that date.\n\n**Q8.** Floating-leg valuation traps\n> **A:** Do not project floating coupons beyond the next (already-fixed) payment; do not value $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ at par between resets (it is par only at reset). Watch the sign: $$B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}$$ is the receiver's value.\n\n**Q9.** Value a swap as a portfolio of FRAs\n> **A:** Assume forward rates are realized: compute each period's forward from the zero curve, set each net cash flow (receiver) as $$(\\text{fixed} - \\text{forward})\\times\\tau\\times L$$, discount at the matching zero rate, and sum. Gives exactly the bond-method value.\n\n**Q10.** Are individual swap exchanges worth zero at initiation?\n> **A:** No — the total swap is worth zero, but individual FRAs are not. With an upward-sloping curve a receiver's early exchanges have positive value and later ones negative.\n\n**Q11.** Currency swap — principal exchange\n> **A:** A fixed-for-fixed currency swap exchanges principal and interest in one currency for another. Unlike rate swaps, principals are exchanged at initiation and at maturity (at a rate set so each leg starts at par).\n\n**Q12.** Value a currency swap as two bonds\n> **A:** For the party receiving foreign and paying dollars: $$V_{\\text{swap}} = S_0 B_F - B_D$$, where $$S_0$$ = spot (dollars per foreign unit), $$B_F$$ = foreign-currency bond, $$B_D$$ = dollar bond. Can also be valued as a portfolio of FX forwards.\n\n**Q13.** Other swap types\n> **A:** Basis swap (floating-for-floating on two reference rates plus a spread), equity swap (index total return vs. fixed/floating), amortizing/step-up (varying notional), forward swap (starts later), swaption (option to enter a swap; a payer swaption benefits from rising rates).\n\n**Q14.** Credit risk: interest rate vs. currency swap\n> **A:** Exposure exists only when a swap's value is positive (replacement cost, far below notional), mitigated by netting/collateral/CCP. Rate-swap exposure peaks mid-life; a currency swap's exposure grows toward maturity because the final principal exchange dominates — so it carries more credit risk.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the notional principal is:\nA) Exchanged at initiation only B) Exchanged at both initiation and maturity C) Never exchanged; it is only used to compute the interest payments D) Exchanged only if one party defaults\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks what happens to the notional (face) amount in a standard interest rate swap.\n\nWhy C is correct: in a plain vanilla interest rate swap, both legs are in the SAME currency, so exchanging the principal would be pointless (each side would just hand the other the same amount). The notional is only a reference number used to calculate the fixed and floating interest payments, which are netted each period. The notional itself never changes hands.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A and B — exchanging principal happens in CURRENCY swaps (different currencies), not in single-currency interest rate swaps.\n- D — default triggers replacement-cost exposure and ISDA netting, but it does not cause a \"notional exchange.\"\n\nAnalogy: two friends agree to swap interest on an imaginary $1 million loan. They never actually pass $1 million back and forth — they just use it to figure out who owes whom the interest difference each month.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Interest rate swap → notional is NEVER exchanged (same currency). Currency swap → principals ARE exchanged at start and end (different currencies). This contrast is heavily tested.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Notional exchange by swap type\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Interest rate swap\",\"items\":[\"same currency\",\"notional NEVER exchanged\",\"used only to size payments\"]},{\"h\":\"Currency swap\",\"items\":[\"two currencies\",\"principals exchanged\",\"at start AND maturity\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A counterparty PAYS fixed in a $100 million interest rate swap. Current zero rates give $$B_{\\text{fixed}} = \\$102.0$$M and $$B_{\\text{float}} = \\$100.5$$M. The value of the swap to this counterparty is:\nA) +$1.5M B) −$1.5M C) +$2.0M D) −$0.5M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the value of the swap to the PAY-FIXED (payer) party, using the two-bond view.\n\nFor the payer, the swap is like being SHORT the fixed bond and LONG the floating bond:\n$$V_{\\text{payer}} = B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$\n\nPlug in:\n$$V = 100.5 - 102.0 = -1.5$$\n\nSo B is correct: −$1.5M.\n\nPlain words: the payer is paying out the fixed coupons (the fixed bond, worth $102.0M to give up) and receiving the floating coupons (the floating bond, worth $100.5M to receive). Since what they pay is worth more than what they receive, the swap has a negative value of −$1.5M.\n\n- A (+$1.5M) is the value to the RECEIVE-FIXED party (the receiver) — the sign-flip trap. The payer's value is the opposite.\n- C (+$2.0M) ignores the floating leg entirely.\n- D (−$0.5M) subtracts in a muddled way; not the correct formula.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Receiver value = $$B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}$$; Payer value = $$B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$ (opposite sign). Always confirm WHICH side the question asks about before reporting the sign.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Swap value: which side?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Pay-fixed (payer)\",\"items\":[\"short fixed bond, long floating\",\"V = Bfloat - Bfixed\"]},{\"h\":\"Receive-fixed (receiver)\",\"items\":[\"long fixed bond, short floating\",\"V = Bfixed - Bfloat (opposite sign)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Just after a reset date (the next floating rate has just been set and the current payment made), the floating leg of a vanilla interest rate swap is worth:\nA) Zero B) The notional principal (par) C) The present value of all projected future floating coupons only D) The fixed-leg value minus the swap value\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the value of the floating-rate side immediately after a reset.\n\nWhy B is correct: a floating-rate note always reprices to PAR (its notional) right at each reset date. This is because the coupon for the next period is set at the current market rate, so the note is \"fairly priced\" at face value. This fact makes swap valuation fast: at a reset, $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ = notional.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — zero is the value of the whole SWAP at initiation, not the value of the floating leg.\n- C — you do NOT project all future floating coupons; the floating note reprices to par, which already captures all of them.\n- D — that's a circular, made-up relationship, not how the floating leg is valued.\n\nAnalogy: a floating-rate note is like a savings account that always pays the current going rate — right after the rate resets, the account is worth exactly its balance (par), because it's neither over- nor under-paying.\n\n📌 Exam tip: At a reset date, $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ = par (notional). BETWEEN resets, it equals (notional + the already-fixed next payment) discounted back to the next payment date. Don't project floating coupons to maturity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Floating leg value just after a reset\",\"options\":[\"Zero\",\"The notional (par)\",\"PV of all projected future floating coupons\",\"Fixed-leg value minus swap value\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The coupon just reset to the market rate, so the floating note reprices exactly to par.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Firm X can borrow fixed at 6.0% or floating at SOFR + 0.2%; Firm Y can borrow fixed at 7.0% or floating at SOFR + 0.6%. The total gain available to be shared from a swap is:\nA) 1.0% B) 0.4% C) 0.6% D) 1.4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the total comparative-advantage gain available when the two firms borrow where each is relatively cheap and then swap.\n\nThe total gain equals the difference between the two firms' borrowing-cost spreads:\n$$\\text{Gain} = (\\text{fixed-rate difference}) - (\\text{floating-rate difference})$$\n\nStep 1 — fixed-rate difference (Y minus X):\n$$7.0\\% - 6.0\\% = 1.0\\%$$\n\nStep 2 — floating-rate difference (Y minus X):\n$$(\\text{SOFR} + 0.6\\%) - (\\text{SOFR} + 0.2\\%) = 0.4\\%$$\n\nStep 3 — subtract:\n$$1.0\\% - 0.4\\% = 0.6\\%$$\n\nSo C is correct: 0.6% total gain to be shared.\n\nPlain words: Firm Y pays 1.0% more than X in the fixed market but only 0.4% more in the floating market. That mismatch (1.0% − 0.4% = 0.6%) is the total savings the two can split by each borrowing where they're relatively best and swapping.\n\n- A (1.0%) is just the fixed-rate difference alone — only half the calculation.\n- B (0.4%) is just the floating-rate difference alone.\n- D (1.4%) wrongly ADDS the two differences instead of subtracting.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Total swap gain = |fixed spread − floating spread|. Be careful: the criticism of comparative advantage is that the fixed spread is locked-in long-term while the floating spread can be repriced at the next reset — so the \"gain\" partly compensates for rollover risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Total comparative-advantage gain\",\"steps\":[\"Fixed-rate difference (Y - X)\",\"Floating-rate difference (Y - X)\",\"Gain = |fixed diff - floating diff|\",\"Split between the two firms\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A swap in which one party pays the total return on a stock index in exchange for a fixed or floating rate is a(n):\nA) Basis swap B) Equity swap C) Amortizing swap D) Currency swap\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question describes exchanging the return on a stock index for an interest rate.\n\nWhy B is correct: an equity swap exchanges the total return on an equity index (price change plus dividends) for a fixed or floating interest rate. It lets an investor get equity exposure (\"equitize cash\") without actually buying the stocks.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — a basis swap exchanges one FLOATING rate for ANOTHER floating rate (e.g., SOFR vs. commercial paper), plus a spread; no equity involved.\n- C — an amortizing swap is a regular interest rate swap where the NOTIONAL shrinks over time; still rates, not equity.\n- D — a currency swap exchanges principal and interest in two different CURRENCIES; no equity index involved.\n\nAnalogy: an equity swap is like betting on the stock market's performance through a contract, while paying a steady \"rental fee\" (the interest leg), without ever owning the actual shares.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Match the swap to its legs — equity swap = index return vs. rate; basis swap = floating vs. floating; currency swap = different currencies; amortizing = shrinking notional.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Swap types by their legs\",\"root\":\"Swaps\",\"children\":[\"Equity: index return vs rate\",\"Basis: floating vs floating\",\"Currency: two currencies\",\"Amortizing: shrinking notional\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A $100 million interest rate swap has 1.25 years left; the institution PAYS 8% fixed (semiannual) and receives 6-month floating. Continuous zero rates are 10% (3 mo), 10.5% (9 mo), 11% (15 mo). Using the given discount factors, the fixed bond is $$B_{\\text{fixed}} = 4(0.97531) + 4(0.92427) + 104(0.87153)$$. This equals closest to:\nA) $98.24M B) $100.00M C) $104.00M D) $96.00M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks us to compute the value of the fixed-rate bond leg by discounting each fixed cash flow at its matching zero rate.\n\nThe institution pays an 8% semiannual coupon on $100M, so each semiannual coupon is $$8\\%/2 \\times 100 = \\$4$$M, paid at 0.25, 0.75, and 1.25 years; the $100M notional comes at 1.25 years (so the last cash flow is 4 + 100 = 104).\n\nStep 1 — first coupon (3 months):\n$$4 \\times 0.97531 = 3.9012$$\n\nStep 2 — second coupon (9 months):\n$$4 \\times 0.92427 = 3.6971$$\n\nStep 3 — final coupon + notional (15 months):\n$$104 \\times 0.87153 = 90.6391$$\n\nStep 4 — sum:\n$$3.9012 + 3.6971 + 90.6391 = 98.2374 \\approx \\$98.24\\text{M}$$\n\nSo A is correct.\n\nPlain words: a fixed-rate bond is just a set of known future payments. Discount each one back to today using the rate for its date, then add them all up.\n\n- B ($100.00M) assumes the bond trades at par, but the 8% coupon is BELOW the ~10–11% rates, so it trades at a DISCOUNT (below par).\n- C ($104.00M) just sums the raw cash flows with no discounting.\n- D ($96.00M) under-discounts incorrectly; not produced by the steps.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A fixed bond with a coupon BELOW current rates prices BELOW par. Discount each coupon plus the final notional at its own zero rate and sum. Sanity-check against par.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Continuing Q6, the floating leg is valued as (notional + the known next payment) discounted to the next reset. The next floating payment was set at 10.2% semiannual, so it equals $$100 \\times 0.102/2 = \\$5.1$$M, due in 3 months ($$e^{-0.10 \\times 0.25} = 0.97531$$). The floating bond $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ is closest to:\nA) $100.00M B) $102.51M C) $105.10M D) $98.24M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the value of the floating-rate side between resets. The trick: only the NEXT payment is already known; everything after that is captured by the note repricing to par at the next reset.\n\nSo $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ = (notional + next known coupon) discounted to the next payment date.\n\nStep 1 — the known next floating payment:\n$$100 \\times \\frac{0.102}{2} = 100 \\times 0.051 = 5.1$$\n\nStep 2 — add the notional (which is worth par at the next reset):\n$$100 + 5.1 = 105.1$$\n\nStep 3 — discount from 3 months back to today:\n$$105.1 \\times 0.97531 = 102.505 \\approx \\$102.51\\text{M}$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\nPlain words: think of the floating note as a short bond that pays $5.1M plus $100M par in 3 months. We discount that single bundle back to today.\n\n- A ($100.00M) wrongly assumes par BETWEEN resets — the note is par only AT a reset, not between.\n- C ($105.10M) forgets to discount the bundle back to today.\n- D ($98.24M) is the FIXED bond value from Q6, not the floating one.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Between resets, $$B_{\\text{float}}$$ = (notional + already-fixed next payment) × discount factor to the next payment date. Never project floating coupons beyond the next one.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Floating leg between resets\",\"steps\":[\"Take the known next coupon\",\"+ notional (par at next reset)\",\"x discount factor to next pay date\",\"= Bfloat today\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Combining Q6 and Q7 ($$B_{\\text{fixed}} = \\$98.24$$M, $$B_{\\text{float}} = \\$102.51$$M), the value of the PAY-FIXED swap to the institution is closest to:\nA) −$4.27M B) +$4.27M C) +$200.75M D) −$0.51M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the total swap value to the payer, now that both bond legs are known.\n\nFor the payer (pays fixed, receives floating):\n$$V_{\\text{payer}} = B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$\n\nPlug in:\n$$V = 102.51 - 98.24 = +4.27$$\n\nSo B is correct: +$4.27M.\n\nPlain words: the institution is paying only 8% fixed while the market rates are around 10–11%. Paying a below-market fixed rate is a good deal, so the swap is worth a positive amount to the payer.\n\n- A (−$4.27M) flips the sign — that would be the RECEIVER's value. Paying below-market fixed is favorable, so the payer's value is positive.\n- C (+$200.75M) adds the two bonds instead of subtracting.\n- D (−$0.51M) is unrelated arithmetic.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When the fixed rate you PAY is below current market rates, the payer swap is IN THE MONEY (positive value). Payer value = $$B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$; receiver value is the negative of that.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Value a swap (bond view)\",\"steps\":[\"Price fixed leg (Bfixed)\",\"Price floating leg (Bfloat)\",\"Payer: Bfloat - Bfixed\",\"= swap value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Compared with an interest rate swap of equal notional and maturity, a currency swap typically has:\nA) Less credit risk, because principals are exchanged B) Greater credit risk, concentrated late in the swap's life because of the principal exchange C) Identical credit risk, since both are valued as bond portfolios D) No credit risk if it is cleared bilaterally\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how the credit risk of a currency swap compares to that of an interest rate swap.\n\nWhy B is correct: a currency swap exchanges PRINCIPAL at maturity (in two different currencies). That final principal exchange is large, so the potential exposure GROWS toward the end of the swap's life. By contrast, an interest rate swap never exchanges principal, so its exposure peaks mid-life and is much smaller. Hence currency swaps carry materially MORE credit risk than rate swaps of the same notional.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A — exchanging principals ADDS risk (the big final exchange), it does not reduce it.\n- C — although both can be valued as bond portfolios, their credit-exposure PROFILES differ a lot; they are not identical.\n- D — clearing reduces but does not eliminate credit risk, and \"bilaterally cleared\" isn't the same as CCP clearing anyway.\n\nAnalogy: an interest rate swap only ever settles small interest differences. A currency swap also hands over a big lump sum of principal at the end — and the risk that your counterparty fails right before that huge final payment grows as you approach maturity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Interest rate swap exposure PEAKS MID-LIFE (no principal). Currency swap exposure GROWS toward maturity (principal exchange dominates) → currency swaps are riskier for the same notional.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Credit exposure profile\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Interest rate swap\",\"items\":[\"no principal exchange\",\"exposure peaks MID-life\",\"lower credit risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Currency swap\",\"items\":[\"principals exchanged at maturity\",\"exposure GROWS toward end\",\"higher credit risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A swap exposes a counterparty to credit (default) risk only when:\nA) The swap's value to that counterparty is positive B) The swap's value to that counterparty is negative C) Interest rates are rising D) The notional principal exceeds $100 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks when a party in a swap actually faces credit risk from the other side defaulting.\n\nWhy A is correct: you only LOSE from a counterparty's default when they OWE you money — that is, when the swap has a POSITIVE value to you (it is an asset). If the counterparty walks away then, you lose that positive replacement value. If the swap has negative value to you, you owe them, so their default doesn't cost you (you might even benefit). So credit exposure exists only when the swap's value is positive to you.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B — if the swap is negative to you, YOU are the one who owes; their default does not create a loss for you.\n- C — rising or falling rates change the swap's value, but credit risk depends on the SIGN of that value, not the direction of rates by itself.\n- D — the size of the notional does not determine WHETHER credit risk exists; even small swaps have exposure when in-the-money. (And exposure is far smaller than notional — it's the replacement cost.)\n\nAnalogy: you only fear a borrower running off if they OWE you money. If you actually owe THEM, their disappearing doesn't hurt your wallet.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Swap credit exposure = the swap's value WHEN POSITIVE (the replacement cost) — far smaller than notional, and mitigated by netting, collateral, and CCP clearing under ISDA.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"When does a swap expose you to credit risk?\",\"options\":[\"Swap value is POSITIVE to you\",\"Swap value is negative to you\",\"Rates are rising\",\"Notional exceeds $100M\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"You lose from a counterparty default only when they owe you, i.e. the swap is an asset (positive value).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Immediately after a reset date (next floating rate just fixed and current payment made), the floating leg of a vanilla swap is worth:\nA) Zero B) The notional principal (par) C) The PV of all projected floating coupons only D) The fixed-leg value minus the swap value\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** just after reset, the floating-rate note reprices to par; between resets it is (notional + next known payment) discounted to the next payment date.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** For a counterparty that pays fixed in a $100M swap, current zero rates give $$B_{\\text{fixed}} = \\$102.0$$M and $$B_{\\text{float}} = \\$100.5$$M. The swap's value to this counterparty is:\nA) +$1.5M B) −$1.5M C) +$2.0M D) −$0.5M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** payer value $$= B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}} = 100.5 - 102.0 = -\\$1.5$$M. (+$1.5M is the receiver's value — the sign-flip trap.)\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Firm X borrows fixed at 6.0% or floating at SOFR + 0.2%; Firm Y at 7.0% fixed or SOFR + 0.6%. The total gain available from a swap is:\nA) 1.0% B) 0.4% C) 0.6% D) 1.4%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** fixed-rate difference 1.0% minus floating-rate difference 0.4% = 0.6% total gain to be shared.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Compared with an interest rate swap of equal notional and maturity, a currency swap typically has:\nA) Less credit risk, since principals are exchanged B) Greater credit risk, concentrated late in the swap's life due to the principal exchange C) Identical credit risk, since both are valued as bond portfolios D) No credit risk if cleared bilaterally\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** the final exchange of principals means exposure builds toward maturity, unlike a rate swap whose exposure peaks mid-life and involves no principal.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** Company A can borrow fixed at 5.0% or floating at SOFR + 0.1%; Company B can borrow fixed at 6.4% or floating at SOFR + 0.6%. They enter a swap through a bank that takes 0.1%, sharing the remaining gain equally. Each company saves:\nA) 0.25% B) 0.40% C) 0.45% D) 0.90%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Fixed-rate difference = 6.4 − 5.0 = 1.4%; floating difference = 0.6 − 0.1 = 0.5%. Total gain = 1.4 − 0.5 = 0.9%. After the bank's 0.1%, the remaining 0.8% splits equally: **0.40% each**. C forgets the intermediary; D is the total gain.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Comparative-advantage gain\",\"steps\":[\"Fixed spread 6.4−5.0 = 1.4%\",\"Floating spread 0.6−0.1 = 0.5%\",\"Total gain = 1.4−0.5 = 0.9%\",\"Less bank 0.1% → 0.8% split → 0.40% each\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A swap has one exchange remaining, in one year, on a notional of $50 million: receive 5.0% fixed, pay floating (annual period). The forward rate for the period is 4.2% (annually compounded) and the one-year discount factor is 0.96. The swap's value to the fixed receiver is closest to:\nA) −$384,000 B) +$192,000 C) +$384,000 D) +$400,000 --\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Value the exchange as an FRA, assuming the forward rate is realized:\n\n$$V = 50{,}000{,}000 \\times (0.050 - 0.042) \\times 1 \\times 0.96 = 50{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.008 \\times 0.96 = \\textbf{+\\$384{,}000}$$\n\nD forgets to discount; A flips the sign (that is the payer's value).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Swap value via forward rate\",\"steps\":[\"Receive fixed 5.0%, pay forward 4.2%\",\"Net rate = 0.8% on $50M\",\"Cash flow = $400,000\",\"Discount × 0.96 = +$384,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A $100 million interest rate swap has 1.25 years remaining. An institution **receives 7% fixed** (semiannual) and pays 6-month floating. Continuously compounded zero rates are 9% (3 months), 9.5% (9 months), and 10% (15 months). The floating payment due in 3 months was set at 8% (semiannual) at the last reset. The value of the swap to this institution is closest to:\nA) +$3.67M B) +$1.93M C) −$1.93M D) −$3.67M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Restate: value the **receiver** swap as $$B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}$$ using the given zeros.\n\n1. **Discount factors:** $$e^{-0.09(0.25)} = e^{-0.0225} = 0.977751$$; $$e^{-0.095(0.75)} = e^{-0.07125} = 0.931229$$; $$e^{-0.10(1.25)} = e^{-0.125} = 0.882497.$$\n2. **Fixed bond** (coupon $$= 100 \\times 0.07/2 = 3.5$$M at 0.25, 0.75, 1.25; notional 100 at 1.25):\n$$B_{\\text{fixed}} = 3.5(0.977751) + 3.5(0.931229) + 103.5(0.882497)$$\n$$= 3.42213 + 3.25930 + 91.33844 = \\$98.020\\text{M}.$$\n3. **Floating bond** (notional + known next payment, discounted to the next reset): next payment $$= 100 \\times 0.08/2 = 4.0$$M:\n$$B_{\\text{float}} = 104.0 \\times e^{-0.09(0.25)} = 104.0 \\times 0.977751 = \\$101.686\\text{M}.$$\n4. **Receiver value:**\n$$V = B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}} = 98.020 - 101.686 = -\\$3.67\\text{M}.$$\n\nWhy D is correct: the receiver is *out of the money* because market rates (9–10%) far exceed the 7% fixed rate it receives, so $$B_{\\text{fixed}} < B_{\\text{float}}$$ and the value is −$3.67M.\n\n- A (+$3.67M) flips the sign — that is the **payer's** value ($$B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$).\n- C (−$1.93M) discounts the floating leg as if worth par now (uses 100 instead of 104, or discounts to maturity), shrinking the loss.\n- B (+$1.93M) combines the par-FRN error with the wrong sign.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Receiver value $$= B_{\\text{fixed}} - B_{\\text{float}}$$. With market rates above the fixed rate received, the receiver loses. Discount the floating leg as (notional + known next coupon) to the **next reset** only — it is par only *at* the reset, not now.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Value a receive-fixed swap (bond view)\",\"steps\":[\"Price fixed leg (Bfixed)\",\"Floating leg = (notional+next coupon) to next reset\",\"Receiver: Bfixed - Bfloat\",\"= swap value\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A counterparty **pays fixed** in a $100M swap. Using current zero rates, $$B_{\\text{fixed}} = \\$103.5$$M and $$B_{\\text{float}} = \\$100.8$$M. The swap's value to this counterparty is:\nA) +$2.7M B) −$2.7M C) +$3.5M D) −$0.8M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: value a **payer** swap given the two bond legs.\n\n1. **Payer value formula:** $$V = B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}.$$\n2. **Plug in:** $$V = 100.8 - 103.5 = -\\$2.7\\text{M}.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the payer is short the fixed bond and long the floating; with $$B_{\\text{fixed}} > B_{\\text{float}}$$, the payer's value is negative (rates have fallen, so paying a high fixed rate hurts).\n\n- A (+$2.7M) is the **receiver's** value — the classic sign-flip trap.\n- C (+$3.5M) reports $$B_{\\text{fixed}}$$ minus notional or similar — not the swap value.\n- D (−$0.8M) subtracts notional incorrectly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Payer value $$= B_{\\text{float}} - B_{\\text{fixed}}$$; receiver value is the negative of that. Always confirm whose perspective the question takes before signing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Swap value: which side?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Pay-fixed (payer)\",\"items\":[\"short fixed, long floating\",\"V = Bfloat - Bfixed\"]},{\"h\":\"Receive-fixed (receiver)\",\"items\":[\"long fixed, short floating\",\"V = Bfixed - Bfloat (opposite)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Firm X can borrow fixed at 5.0% or floating at SOFR + 0.3%. Firm Y can borrow fixed at 6.8% or floating at SOFR + 0.9%. The **total gain** available from a swap between them is:\nA) 1.8% B) 1.2% C) 0.6% D) 0.9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: total swap gain = difference in fixed spreads minus difference in floating spreads.\n\n1. **Fixed-rate difference (Y − X):** $$6.8\\% - 5.0\\% = 1.8\\%.$$\n2. **Floating-rate difference (Y − X):** $$(\\text{SOFR}+0.9\\%) - (\\text{SOFR}+0.3\\%) = 0.6\\%.$$\n3. **Total gain:** $$1.8\\% - 0.6\\% = 1.2\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the total gain is the absolute difference between the fixed spread (1.8%) and the floating spread (0.6%).\n\n- A (1.8%) is just the fixed-rate difference — forgets to net the floating difference.\n- C (0.6%) is just the floating-rate difference.\n- D (0.9%) misreads Y's floating spread as the gain.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Total comparative-advantage gain = |fixed-spread difference − floating-spread difference|. Identify each firm's comparative advantage (X in fixed, since 1.8% > 0.6%), then this gain is split among the parties.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Total comparative-advantage gain\",\"steps\":[\"Fixed-rate difference (Y - X) = 1.8%\",\"Floating-rate difference (Y - X) = 0.6%\",\"Gain = |1.8% - 0.6%| = 1.2%\",\"Split between the firms\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A fixed-for-fixed currency swap: an institution **receives 4% on €80M and pays 6% on $100M**, with annual interest exchanges, 2 years remaining, and principals exchanged at maturity. Flat continuously compounded rates are 3% (euro) and 5% (dollar). The spot rate is $1.20 per €. The swap's value to the institution is closest to:\nA) +$3.87M B) −$3.87M C) −$20.16M D) +$1.62M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: value as two bonds — $$V = S_0 B_F - B_D$$ for the party receiving foreign (euro) and paying dollars.\n\n1. **Euro discount factors (3%):** $$e^{-0.03} = 0.970446$$; $$e^{-0.06} = 0.941765.$$\n2. **Euro bond** (coupon $$= 80 \\times 0.04 = €3.2$$M yr1; €3.2M + €80M yr2):\n$$B_F = 3.2(0.970446) + 83.2(0.941765) = 3.10543 + 78.35485 = €81.460\\text{M}.$$\n3. **Dollar discount factors (5%):** $$e^{-0.05} = 0.951229$$; $$e^{-0.10} = 0.904837.$$\n4. **Dollar bond** (coupon $$= 100 \\times 0.06 = \\$6$$M yr1; $6M + $100M yr2):\n$$B_D = 6(0.951229) + 106(0.904837) = 5.70737 + 95.91272 = \\$101.620\\text{M}.$$\n5. **Convert and net** ($$S_0 = 1.20$$ $/€):\n$$V = S_0 B_F - B_D = 1.20 \\times 81.460 - 101.620 = 97.752 - 101.620 = -\\$3.87\\text{M}.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the euro leg converted at spot ($97.75M) is worth less than the dollar leg ($101.62M), so the institution's swap value is −$3.87M.\n\n- A (+$3.87M) flips the sign — that is the value to the counterparty (pay euro, receive dollars).\n- C (−$20.16M) **inverts the spot rate** (divides €81.46M by 1.20 = $67.88M, then subtracts $101.62M = −$33.7M-style error / wrong direction conversion) — multiply when the quote is dollars per euro.\n- D (+$1.62M) omits one of the principal exchanges or mis-discounts a leg.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Currency swap (receive foreign, pay dollars) $$V = S_0 B_F - B_D$$. Build each bond in its own currency at its own flat rate; with $$S_0$$ quoted as **dollars per euro**, *multiply* the euro bond by $$S_0$$. Then subtract the dollar bond.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Value a currency swap (receive euro, pay $)\",\"steps\":[\"Build euro bond at euro rate (Bf)\",\"Build dollar bond at $ rate (Bd)\",\"Convert: S0 x Bf (S0 = $/euro)\",\"V = S0 x Bf - Bd\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Immediately **after** a reset date (the next floating rate has just been set and the current payment made), the floating leg of a plain vanilla interest rate swap is worth:\nA) Zero B) The notional principal (par) C) The present value of all projected future floating coupons only D) The fixed-leg value minus the total swap value\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: recall the value of the floating-rate note embedded in a swap right after a reset.\n\nWhy B is correct: a floating-rate note **reprices to par** immediately after each reset, because the coupon resets to the current market rate. So $$B_{\\text{float}} = $$ notional just after reset.\n\n- A (zero) confuses the *swap's* net value with the *floating leg's* value — the leg is worth par, not zero.\n- C is wrong because you cannot value the FRN by projecting and discounting estimated floating coupons to maturity — the par-reset property makes that unnecessary and incorrect.\n- D is a circular/nonsensical relation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: FRN = par **at** each reset. *Between* resets it equals (notional + the already-fixed next payment) discounted to the next payment date. Never project floating coupons to maturity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Floating leg value just after a reset\",\"options\":[\"Zero\",\"The notional (par)\",\"PV of all projected future floating coupons\",\"Fixed-leg value minus swap value\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The coupon just reset to the market rate, so the FRN reprices exactly to par.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Compared with an interest rate swap of equal notional and maturity, a fixed-for-fixed **currency swap** typically has:\nA) Less credit risk because principals are exchanged and netted. B) Greater credit risk, concentrated late in the swap's life because of the final principal exchange. C) Identical credit risk, since both are valued as bond portfolios. D) No credit risk, since currency swaps are always centrally cleared.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: compare the credit-risk profile of a currency swap versus an interest rate swap.\n\nWhy B is correct: a currency swap **exchanges principals** at maturity, so a large cash flow occurs at the end. Potential exposure therefore **grows toward maturity** and is concentrated late in the swap's life — making currency swaps carry materially **more** credit risk than rate swaps of the same notional (whose exposure peaks mid-life and involves no principal exchange).\n\n- A reverses the conclusion: the principal exchange *increases* exposure, not decreases it.\n- C is wrong: although both are valued as bond portfolios, their exposure *profiles* differ sharply (mid-life peak vs. late-life build).\n- D is false: not all currency swaps are centrally cleared, and clearing mitigates but does not eliminate credit risk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rate-swap exposure peaks **mid-life** (no principal exchange); currency-swap exposure builds toward **maturity** because the final principal swap dominates — so currency swaps are riskier per unit notional.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Credit exposure profile\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Interest rate swap\",\"items\":[\"no principal exchange\",\"exposure peaks MID-life\",\"lower credit risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Currency swap\",\"items\":[\"principals exchanged at maturity\",\"exposure builds toward END\",\"higher credit risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which statement about the **comparative advantage** argument for swaps is the most valid criticism?\nA) The argument is invalid because total borrowing costs cannot be reduced by any financial contract. B) The fixed-rate spread is a long-term locked-in credit spread, while the floating spread applies only until the next reset; the apparent gain partly compensates one party for bearing rollover/credit-spread risk. C) The gain is always captured entirely by the swap dealer, so end users never benefit. D) Comparative advantage applies only to currency swaps, never to interest rate swaps.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Restate: identify the standard, valid criticism of the comparative-advantage story.\n\nWhy B is correct: the fixed-rate spread is **long-term and locked in**, while the floating spread is repriced at each reset. The weaker firm's \"cheap\" floating rate can be marked up if its credit deteriorates, so part of the apparent gain is **compensation for rollover/credit-spread risk**, not a free arbitrage from market inefficiency.\n\n- A overstates: swaps genuinely *can* lower combined costs through comparative advantage; the criticism is about *why* the spread exists, not that it's impossible.\n- C is false: end users typically share the gain; the dealer takes only a small spread.\n- D is wrong: the comparative-advantage argument applies to interest rate swaps as well (and the criticism is *stronger* there than for currency swaps).\n\n📌 Exam tip: The comparative-advantage \"gain\" is partly illusory because the **fixed** spread is locked in long-term while the **floating** spread resets — the weaker borrower is being paid to bear future credit-spread/rollover risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Valid criticism of comparative advantage\",\"options\":[\"No contract can reduce total borrowing cost\",\"Fixed spread is locked long-term; floating resets - gain pays for rollover/credit risk\",\"The dealer always takes the entire gain\",\"It applies only to currency swaps\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The floating spread reprices at each reset, so part of the apparent gain compensates for bearing future credit-spread risk.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"401","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Measures of Financial Risk","notes":"# 1. Measures of Financial Risk\n\nGARP tests whether you can define VaR and expected shortfall precisely, compute both under normality, and judge any risk measure against the four coherence properties. The mean-variance framework is the warm-up: know exactly when it works and when it fails.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the mean-variance framework and how it is used to evaluate risk and return.\n- Explain why the mean-variance framework breaks down when returns are not elliptically (e.g., normally) distributed.\n- Define value at risk (VaR), calculate it for given parameters, and describe its limitations.\n- Define the properties of a coherent risk measure and explain why each matters.\n- Explain why VaR is not coherent and why expected shortfall (ES) is.\n- Define and calculate expected shortfall, and compare ES with VaR.\n- Describe how risk measures aggregate via weighted averages of quantiles (spectral measures, in outline).\n\n## The mean-variance framework\n\nInvestors trade off expected return $$\\mu$$ against risk measured by standard deviation $$\\sigma$$. If returns are normal, the entire distribution is pinned down by $$(\\mu, \\sigma)$$, so ranking portfolios by mean and variance is fully consistent with expected utility for any risk-averse investor.\n\nThe framework remains valid for any **elliptical** distribution (normal, Student's t with finite variance). It fails when:\n\n- Returns are **skewed** (e.g., short option positions, credit portfolios: many small gains, rare large losses).\n- Returns have **fat tails** (excess kurtosis) — σ understates tail risk.\n- Payoffs are **non-linear** in underlying risk factors (options), so portfolio returns are non-normal even if the underlying is normal.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Two portfolios can share the same μ and σ but have wildly different tail risk. Mean-variance ranking cannot see the difference; tail-focused measures (VaR, ES) can.\n\n## Value at risk\n\n**Definition.** VaR at confidence level $$\\alpha$$ over horizon $$T$$ is the loss that will not be exceeded with probability $$\\alpha$$ — the $$\\alpha$$-quantile of the loss distribution:\n\n$$P(L > \\text{VaR}_\\alpha) = 1 - \\alpha$$\n\nFor normally distributed P&L with mean μ and standard deviation σ:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = -\\mu + z_\\alpha \\sigma$$\n\nwhere $$z_{0.95} = 1.645$$ and $$z_{0.99} = 2.326$$. With μ = 0 (standard for short horizons), VaR is simply $$z_\\alpha \\sigma$$.\n\n**Limitations of VaR:**\n\n| Limitation | Consequence |\n|---|---|\n| Says nothing about losses *beyond* the quantile | Two books with identical VaR can have very different tail severity |\n| Not subadditive in general | Merging portfolios can *raise* measured risk — penalizes diversification, invites regulatory arbitrage by splitting desks |\n| Depends on α and T choices | Easy to game by tuning parameters |\n| Estimated from models/history | Model risk; calm samples understate risk |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> VaR is a **quantile, not a worst case**. \"99% one-day VaR = $10M\" means losses exceed $10M on about 1 day in 100 — it puts **no ceiling** on how bad that day is. Choosing \"maximum loss\" as the answer to a VaR definition question is the classic trap.\n\n## Coherent risk measures\n\nA risk measure $$\\rho$$ is **coherent** if it satisfies all four:\n\n| Property | Statement | Intuition |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Monotonicity** | If $$X_1 \\le X_2$$ in every state, $$\\rho(X_1) \\ge \\rho(X_2)$$ | A portfolio that always pays less is at least as risky |\n| **Subadditivity** | $$\\rho(X_1+X_2) \\le \\rho(X_1) + \\rho(X_2)$$ | Diversification cannot increase risk |\n| **Positive homogeneity** | $$\\rho(\\lambda X) = \\lambda \\rho(X)$$, $$\\lambda > 0$$ | Doubling the position doubles the risk |\n| **Translation invariance** | $$\\rho(X + c) = \\rho(X) - c$$ | Adding cash $c$ reduces required capital one-for-one |\n\nVaR satisfies monotonicity, positive homogeneity, and translation invariance — but **fails subadditivity** for non-elliptical distributions (it is subadditive when returns are normal). ES satisfies all four, hence is coherent.\n\n## Expected shortfall\n\nES (a.k.a. conditional VaR, expected tail loss) is the **expected loss given that the loss exceeds VaR**:\n\n$$\\text{ES}_\\alpha = E[L \\mid L > \\text{VaR}_\\alpha]$$\n\nFor normal P&L with mean μ, standard deviation σ:\n\n$$\\text{ES}_\\alpha = -\\mu + \\sigma \\frac{\\phi(z_\\alpha)}{1-\\alpha}$$\n\nwhere $$\\phi$$ is the standard normal pdf: $$\\phi(1.645) = 0.1031$$, $$\\phi(2.326) = 0.0267$$.\n\nES is an average of all quantiles beyond α (equal weights on the tail). More generally, **spectral risk measures** weight quantiles by a non-decreasing risk-aversion function; ES is the simplest coherent member of that family. Giving *larger* weights to *worse* outcomes is exactly what subadditivity requires.\n\n> 📌 **Important**  \n> Always: $$\\text{ES}_\\alpha \\ge \\text{VaR}_\\alpha$$ at the same α. ES answers \"how bad is bad?\"; VaR answers only \"how often?\"\n\n### Worked example: parametric VaR and ES at 95% and 99%\n\nA $10 million portfolio has daily P&L that is normal with mean 0 and daily volatility 2%.\n\nDollar σ = 0.02 × \\$10M = \\$200,000.\n\n**95% VaR:** $$1.645 \\times 200{,}000 = \\$329{,}000$$\n\n**99% VaR:** $$2.326 \\times 200{,}000 = \\$465{,}200$$\n\n**95% ES:** $$200{,}000 \\times \\frac{0.1031}{0.05} = 200{,}000 \\times 2.0627 = \\$412{,}540$$\n\n**99% ES:** $$200{,}000 \\times \\frac{0.0267}{0.01} = 200{,}000 \\times 2.665 = \\$533{,}000$$\n\nCheck the ordering: ES₉₅ (\\$412.5K) > VaR₉₅ (\\$329K), ES₉₉ (\\$533K) > VaR₉₉ (\\$465K). For the normal distribution, 97.5% ES ≈ 99% VaR (multiplier 2.338 vs 2.326) — the basis of the FRTB calibration.\n\n### Worked example: VaR fails subadditivity\n\nTwo independent corporate bonds each: 3% probability of defaulting over the year with a \\$10M loss; otherwise no loss.\n\n**Each bond alone, 95% VaR:** P(loss) = 3% < 5%, so the 95% quantile of the loss distribution is **0**. VaR = \\$0 each; sum = \\$0.\n\n**Portfolio of both:** P(at least one default) = $$1 - 0.97^2 = 5.91\\% > 5\\%$$, so the 95% quantile loss is **\\$10M**.\n\n$$\\text{VaR}(A+B) = \\$10\\text{M} > \\text{VaR}(A) + \\text{VaR}(B) = \\$0$$\n\nSubadditivity is violated — the diversified portfolio shows *more* VaR than the parts. ES would not do this: each bond's 95% ES is $$\\frac{0.03}{0.05}\\times 10\\text{M} = \\$6\\text{M}$$, and the portfolio ES (≈ \\$10.3M, since the 5% tail is dominated by one-default states with a sliver of double defaults) is below the \\$12M sum.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> On the exam, \"which property does VaR violate?\" → **subadditivity**, and the standard counterexample is exactly this two-bond setup with default probability between the two tail levels.\n\n## VaR vs ES summary\n\n| | VaR | ES |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it is | Loss quantile | Mean loss beyond the quantile |\n| Coherent? | No (fails subadditivity) | Yes |\n| Tail information | None beyond cutoff | Full tail average |\n| Backtesting | Easy (count exceedances) | Harder (needs tail magnitudes) |\n| Regulatory use | Basel II market risk (99%, 10-day) | FRTB (97.5% ES) |","flashcards":"**Q1.** When does the mean-variance framework give correct risk rankings?\n> **A:** Only when returns are elliptically distributed (normal, finite-variance Student's t). It fails for skewed returns, fat tails, and non-linear (option) payoffs, where σ understates tail risk.\n\n**Q2.** Define value at risk (VaR) at confidence level α.\n> **A:** The loss not exceeded with probability α — the α-quantile of the loss distribution: $$P(L > \\text{VaR}_\\alpha) = 1 - \\alpha$$. It is a quantile, not a worst case.\n\n**Q3.** Parametric VaR formula for normal P&L.\n> **A:** $$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = -\\mu + z_\\alpha \\sigma$$, with $$z_{0.95}=1.645$$, $$z_{0.99}=2.326$$. With μ = 0, VaR = $$z_\\alpha\\sigma$$.\n\n**Q4.** Compute the 99% one-day VaR for a portfolio with σ = $200,000 and μ = 0.\n> **A:** $$2.326 \\times 200{,}000 = \\$465{,}200$$.\n\n**Q5.** What is the key limitation of VaR regarding the tail?\n> **A:** It says nothing about losses beyond the quantile. Two books with identical VaR can have very different tail severity; VaR puts no ceiling on how bad a tail loss is.\n\n**Q6.** List the four properties of a coherent risk measure.\n> **A:** Monotonicity, subadditivity ($$\\rho(X_1+X_2)\\le\\rho(X_1)+\\rho(X_2)$$), positive homogeneity ($$\\rho(\\lambda X)=\\lambda\\rho(X)$$), and translation invariance ($$\\rho(X+c)=\\rho(X)-c$$).\n\n**Q7.** Which coherence property does VaR violate, and when?\n> **A:** Subadditivity — for non-elliptical distributions, merging portfolios can raise measured VaR, penalizing diversification. VaR is subadditive when returns are normal.\n\n**Q8.** Define expected shortfall (ES) and its normal-distribution formula.\n> **A:** $$\\text{ES}_\\alpha = E[L\\mid L>\\text{VaR}_\\alpha]$$. For normal P&L: $$\\text{ES}_\\alpha = -\\mu + \\sigma\\frac{\\phi(z_\\alpha)}{1-\\alpha}$$, with $$\\phi(1.645)=0.1031$$, $$\\phi(2.326)=0.0267$$.\n\n**Q9.** Why is ES coherent while VaR is not?\n> **A:** ES averages all quantiles beyond α (giving larger weights to worse outcomes), satisfying all four coherence properties including subadditivity. It is the simplest coherent spectral risk measure.\n\n**Q10.** Relationship between ES and VaR at the same confidence level.\n> **A:** $$\\text{ES}_\\alpha \\ge \\text{VaR}_\\alpha$$ always. VaR answers \"how often?\"; ES answers \"how bad is bad?\"\n\n**Q11.** Compute the 95% ES for a portfolio with σ = $200,000, μ = 0.\n> **A:** $$200{,}000 \\times \\frac{0.1031}{0.05} = 200{,}000 \\times 2.0627 = \\$412{,}540$$.\n\n**Q12.** Two independent bonds each default 3% of the time with a $10M loss. Why does VaR fail subadditivity at 95%?\n> **A:** Each alone: P(loss)=3% < 5%, so 95% VaR = $0; sum = $0. Together P(at least one default) = $$1-0.97^2=5.91\\%>5\\%$$, so VaR(A+B) = $10M > $0 = sum. Diversified portfolio shows more VaR.\n\n**Q13.** Which regulatory regime uses ES, and at what level?\n> **A:** FRTB uses 97.5% ES (≈ 99% VaR for normal: multiplier 2.338 vs 2.326). Basel II market risk used 99%, 10-day VaR.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A portfolio's daily profit and loss is normally distributed with a mean of zero and a standard deviation of $2,000,000. Using $z_{0.95} = 1.645$, the 95% one-day value at risk (VaR) is closest to:\nA) $1,960,000 B) $3,290,000 C) $4,652,000 D) $2,000,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the loss level that will not be exceeded on 95 out of 100 normal days. For a normal P&L with mean zero, VaR is just the z-score times the dollar standard deviation.\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = z_\\alpha \\times \\sigma$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{0.95} = 1.645 \\times \\$2{,}000{,}000 = \\$3{,}290{,}000$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($1,960,000) uses $z = 1.96$, which is the two-sided 95% value, not the one-sided 95% VaR multiplier (1.645). Trap: mixing up one-tailed and two-tailed z-scores.\n- C ($4,652,000) uses $z = 2.326$, which is the 99% multiplier, not 95%.\n- D ($2,000,000) is just the standard deviation with no z-multiplier (as if $z = 1$).\n\nThink of VaR like a flood wall: you build it so the river stays below it 95 days out of 100. The taller your confidence, the taller the wall (bigger z).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize $z_{0.95} = 1.645$ and $z_{0.99} = 2.326$. With mean zero, VaR = z × σ — nothing more.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Parametric (normal) VaR\",\"steps\":[\"Get dollar σ (value × vol)\",\"Pick z for confidence (1.645 at 95%)\",\"VaR = z × σ\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which of the following best describes the value at risk (VaR) of a portfolio?\nA) The maximum possible loss the portfolio can suffer B) The average loss the portfolio suffers over a year C) A loss level that will not be exceeded with a given probability over a given horizon D) The loss that occurs in the single worst historical scenario\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question is testing the precise definition of VaR.\n\nVaR is a quantile of the loss distribution: at confidence level $\\alpha$ over horizon $T$, it is the loss such that $P(L > \\text{VaR}_\\alpha) = 1 - \\alpha$. In words, \"we are 99% confident the loss won't be worse than this.\" C states exactly that.\n\n- A is the classic trap. VaR is NOT the maximum loss; it puts no ceiling on how bad the worst day can be. A 99% one-day VaR of $10M means losses exceed $10M about 1 day in 100 — but says nothing about how far beyond $10M they go.\n- B describes an average (more like expected loss), not a quantile.\n- D describes the single worst scenario, which is again about the extreme tail, not the cutoff quantile.\n\nAnalogy: VaR is like saying \"I'm 99% sure my commute won't take more than 40 minutes.\" It doesn't tell you whether the rare bad day takes 41 minutes or 3 hours.\n\n📌 Exam tip: If an answer says \"maximum loss,\" it is almost always the wrong choice for a VaR-definition question.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What VaR is\",\"options\":[\"A. Maximum possible loss\",\"B. Average annual loss\",\"C. Loss not exceeded with given probability over a horizon\",\"D. Single worst historical scenario\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"VaR is a quantile (cutoff) of the loss distribution, not the max, mean, or worst case.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which property of a coherent risk measure does VaR fail to satisfy in general?\nA) Monotonicity B) Translation invariance C) Positive homogeneity D) Subadditivity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question asks which of the four coherence properties VaR breaks.\n\nA coherent risk measure must satisfy monotonicity, subadditivity, positive homogeneity, and translation invariance. VaR satisfies three of them but can fail subadditivity when distributions are not elliptical (not normal). Subadditivity says $\\rho(X_1 + X_2) \\le \\rho(X_1) + \\rho(X_2)$ — merging two portfolios should never increase risk, because diversification helps. VaR can violate this.\n\n- A (monotonicity) — VaR satisfies this: a portfolio that always pays less is at least as risky.\n- B (translation invariance) — VaR satisfies this: adding cash reduces risk one-for-one.\n- C (positive homogeneity) — VaR satisfies this: doubling the position doubles the VaR.\n\nAnalogy: subadditivity is the idea \"don't put all your eggs in one basket helps you.\" A broken risk measure (VaR) can sometimes claim that spreading eggs across baskets is riskier — which is nonsense, and that's the flaw.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Which property does VaR violate?\" → always subadditivity. ES fixes this and is fully coherent.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Coherent risk measure\",\"root\":\"4 properties (VaR fails Subadditivity)\",\"children\":[\"Monotonicity ✓\",\"Subadditivity ✗ (VaR)\",\"Positive homogeneity ✓\",\"Translation inv. ✓\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** For the same portfolio and the same confidence level, expected shortfall (ES) compared with VaR is:\nA) Always smaller than VaR B) Always at least as large as VaR C) Equal to VaR for normal distributions D) Unrelated to VaR\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how ES and VaR rank against each other.\n\nES is the average of all losses that are worse than VaR: $\\text{ES}_\\alpha = E[L \\mid L > \\text{VaR}_\\alpha]$. Since ES averages outcomes that are all at least as bad as VaR, it can never be smaller than VaR. Therefore ES ≥ VaR always.\n\n- A is backwards — ES is never smaller.\n- C is wrong: even for the normal distribution, ES > VaR (e.g., at 95%, ES multiplier 2.06 vs VaR multiplier 1.645).\n- D is wrong: ES is defined directly in terms of the tail beyond VaR, so they are closely related.\n\nAnalogy: VaR tells you \"the cutoff for a 'bad day' is a $1M loss.\" ES tells you \"on those bad days, the average loss is $1.4M.\" The average of the bad days can't be milder than the cutoff that defines them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the one-liner: VaR says \"how often,\" ES says \"how bad.\" And ES ≥ VaR, always.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Expected Shortfall\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"loss cutoff (quantile)\",\"\\\"how often\\\" losses breach\",\"not always subadditive\"]},{\"h\":\"ES\",\"items\":[\"avg loss beyond VaR\",\"\\\"how bad\\\" the tail is\",\"coherent; ES ≥ VaR always\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The mean-variance framework produces correct risk rankings for risk-averse investors when returns are:\nA) Negatively skewed B) Fat-tailed with excess kurtosis C) Elliptically distributed (e.g., normal) D) From short option positions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks when ranking portfolios by just mean and standard deviation is valid.\n\nIf returns are elliptically distributed (the normal distribution is the key example), the entire distribution is fully described by its mean $\\mu$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$. Then ranking by mean and variance is consistent with expected utility for any risk-averse investor. C is correct.\n\n- A (negative skew) breaks the framework: skew is a third feature that $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ cannot capture.\n- B (fat tails / excess kurtosis) breaks it: σ understates true tail risk.\n- D (short options) produces non-linear, skewed payoffs, so the distribution is not described by mean and variance alone.\n\nAnalogy: describing a person by only height and weight works fine if everyone has the same body shape (elliptical). Once shapes differ (skew/fat tails), two people with identical height and weight can be very different — you need more numbers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mean-variance only \"sees\" mean and σ. If a distribution has skew or fat tails, the framework is blind to the extra risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"When mean-variance ranking works\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Valid (elliptical)\",\"items\":[\"normal / symmetric\",\"fully described by μ and σ\",\"ranking matches utility\"]},{\"h\":\"Breaks down\",\"items\":[\"negative skew\",\"fat tails (kurtosis)\",\"short options / non-linear\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A $20 million portfolio has daily P&L that is normal with mean zero and daily volatility of 1.5%. Using $z_{0.99} = 2.326$, the 99% one-day VaR is closest to:\nA) $300,000 B) $493,500 C) $698,000 D) $465,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question gives volatility as a percentage and asks for dollar VaR at 99%.\n\nStep 1 — convert percentage volatility to a dollar standard deviation:\n\n$$\\sigma_{\\$} = 0.015 \\times \\$20{,}000{,}000 = \\$300{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — apply the VaR formula (mean zero):\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{0.99} = 2.326 \\times \\$300{,}000 = \\$698{,}000$$\n\nSo C is correct.\n\n- A ($300,000) stops at the dollar standard deviation and forgets to multiply by the z-score.\n- B ($493,500) uses $z = 1.645$ (the 95% multiplier) instead of 2.326.\n- D ($465,000) uses $z = 1.55$ or similar mis-read; it does not use the correct 99% multiplier.\n\nThink of it as two steps: turn the percent into dollars, then stretch by the confidence multiplier.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always convert % volatility to dollars first (σ × portfolio value), then multiply by z. Don't forget the z-step.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Expected shortfall is best described as:\nA) The largest loss that occurs with 1% probability B) The expected loss given that the loss exceeds the VaR threshold C) The standard deviation of losses in the tail D) The probability that a loss exceeds VaR\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the definition of expected shortfall (ES).\n\nES (also called conditional VaR or expected tail loss) answers: \"Given that we are having a bad enough day that the loss is beyond VaR, what is the average loss?\" Formally $\\text{ES}_\\alpha = E[L \\mid L > \\text{VaR}_\\alpha]$. B captures this exactly.\n\n- A describes VaR-like cutoff thinking, not the average of the tail.\n- C describes a standard deviation (a spread measure), not an expected (mean) value.\n- D describes a probability $(1 - \\alpha)$, not a dollar loss amount.\n\nAnalogy: VaR is the \"entry ticket\" price into the bad-days club (the cutoff). ES is the average severity of everyone already inside the club.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ES = average of the losses beyond VaR. Watch for distractors that describe a probability or a standard deviation instead of an average loss.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Expected shortfall is...\",\"options\":[\"A. Largest loss with 1% probability\",\"B. Expected loss given loss exceeds VaR\",\"C. Std deviation of tail losses\",\"D. Probability loss exceeds VaR\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"ES = E[L | L > VaR], the average severity of losses beyond the VaR cutoff.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A $10 million portfolio has daily P&L that is normal with mean zero and daily volatility 2%. Given $\\phi(1.645) = 0.1031$, the 95% expected shortfall (ES) is closest to:\nA) $329,000 B) $412,540 C) $200,000 D) $533,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the parametric (normal) 95% ES in dollars.\n\nStep 1 — dollar standard deviation:\n\n$$\\sigma_{\\$} = 0.02 \\times \\$10{,}000{,}000 = \\$200{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — the normal ES formula (mean zero):\n\n$$\\text{ES}_\\alpha = \\sigma \\times \\frac{\\phi(z_\\alpha)}{1 - \\alpha}$$\n\nPlug in $\\phi(1.645) = 0.1031$ and $1 - \\alpha = 0.05$:\n\n$$\\text{ES}_{0.95} = \\$200{,}000 \\times \\frac{0.1031}{0.05} = \\$200{,}000 \\times 2.0627 = \\$412{,}540$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($329,000) is the 95% VaR (1.645 × 200,000), not ES. Remember ES > VaR.\n- C ($200,000) is just the dollar standard deviation with no multiplier.\n- D ($533,000) is the 99% ES (uses $\\phi(2.326)/0.01$), the wrong confidence level.\n\nStep it through slowly: dollars first, then the special ES multiplier (pdf value ÷ tail probability).\n\n📌 Exam tip: The ES multiplier is $\\phi(z)/(1-\\alpha)$, NOT z. At 95% it's about 2.06; at 99% about 2.67. Check ES > VaR as a sanity test.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Two independent corporate bonds each have a 3% chance of defaulting over the year with a $10 million loss, and otherwise no loss. Considering the 95% one-year VaR, which statement is correct?\nA) VaR of each bond alone is $10M, and the combined VaR is $20M B) VaR of each bond alone is $0, but the combined portfolio VaR is $10M, violating subadditivity C) Combining the bonds always lowers VaR because of diversification D) The combined VaR equals the sum of the individual VaRs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is the classic counterexample showing VaR is not subadditive.\n\nFor one bond: the probability of a loss is 3%, which is less than the 5% tail. So the 95% quantile of the loss distribution is $0 — VaR = $0 for each bond. The sum of the two individual VaRs is $0.\n\nFor the portfolio of both: the chance that at least one defaults is\n\n$$1 - (0.97)^2 = 1 - 0.9409 = 0.0591 = 5.91\\%$$\n\nSince 5.91% > 5%, the loss at the 95% quantile is now $10M. So:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}(A + B) = \\$10\\text{M} > \\text{VaR}(A) + \\text{VaR}(B) = \\$0$$\n\nMerging the portfolios increased measured VaR — that violates subadditivity. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: each bond's 95% VaR is $0 (3% < 5%), not $10M.\n- C is wrong: here combining raised VaR, the opposite of the expected diversification effect.\n- D is wrong: the combined VaR ($10M) is far above the sum of the parts ($0).\n\nAnalogy: it's as if buying two cheap lottery-loss tickets, each \"safe\" on its own, becomes officially \"risky\" only when held together — an odd result that exposes VaR's flaw.\n\n📌 Exam tip: This exact two-bond setup, with default probability between the two tail levels, is GARP's go-to example for VaR failing subadditivity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"VaR fails subadditivity\",\"steps\":[\"Each bond: P(loss)=3% < 5% → VaR = $0\",\"Combined P(≥1 default)=5.91% > 5%\",\"Combined 95% VaR = $10M\",\"$10M > $0 + $0 → subadditivity broken\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which statement about VaR and expected shortfall in regulation and practice is correct?\nA) VaR is coherent, while expected shortfall is not B) Expected shortfall is harder to backtest than VaR but captures tail severity better C) VaR provides full information about losses beyond its threshold D) Expected shortfall is always smaller than VaR for fat-tailed distributions\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question compares VaR and ES on coherence, tail information, and backtesting.\n\nES averages the full tail beyond VaR, so it captures how severe the bad days are — information VaR ignores. The trade-off is that ES is harder to backtest because checking it requires the magnitudes of tail losses, not just counting exceptions. B is correct, which is why the Basel FRTB framework moved to a 97.5% ES while older rules used 99% VaR (easy to backtest by counting exceedances).\n\n- A is reversed: ES is coherent; VaR is not (it can fail subadditivity).\n- C is wrong: VaR gives no information about losses beyond its threshold — that is its main limitation.\n- D is wrong: ES is always at least as large as VaR, never smaller.\n\nAnalogy: counting how many times the river overflowed the wall (VaR exceptions) is easy. Measuring the average depth of every flood (ES) is more work but tells you how damaging the floods really are.\n\n📌 Exam tip: VaR = easy to backtest, ignores tail severity, not coherent. ES = harder to backtest, captures tail severity, coherent. FRTB uses 97.5% ES.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs ES in regulation\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR (old 99%)\",\"items\":[\"easy to backtest (count breaches)\",\"ignores tail severity\",\"not coherent\"]},{\"h\":\"ES (FRTB 97.5%)\",\"items\":[\"harder to backtest\",\"captures tail severity\",\"coherent\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A portfolio's daily P&L is normal with mean zero and σ = \\$1.5M. The 99% one-day VaR is closest to:\nA) \\$2.47M B) \\$2.94M C) \\$3.49M D) \\$1.96M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** 2.326 × 1.5M = \\$3.49M. (\\$2.47M uses z = 1.645; \\$2.94M uses z = 1.96.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which property of coherence does VaR fail to satisfy in general?\nA) Monotonicity B) Translation invariance C) Subadditivity D) Positive homogeneity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Subadditivity — merging positions can increase VaR when distributions are non-elliptical.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** For the same portfolio and confidence level, expected shortfall is:\nA) Always less than VaR B) Always at least as large as VaR C) Equal to VaR only for fat-tailed distributions D) Unrelated to VaR\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** ES averages losses beyond VaR, so ES ≥ VaR always.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The mean-variance framework gives correct risk rankings when returns are:\nA) Skewed left B) Elliptically distributed C) From short option positions D) Any distribution, provided σ is estimated accurately\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Elliptical (e.g., normal) distributions are fully described by mean and variance; skewed/fat-tailed ones are not.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A $200 million portfolio has normally distributed daily returns with zero mean and daily volatility of 1.5%. The one-day 99% VaR is closest to:\nA) $3.00 million B) $4.94 million C) $5.88 million D) $6.98 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** $$VaR_{99\\%} = 2.326 \\times 0.015 \\times \\$200\\text{m} = \\$6.98\\text{m}$$\n\nDistractor B uses 1.645 (95% value); C uses 1.96 — the two-tailed 95% critical value, never correct for VaR, which is a one-tailed quantile.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which z for one-day 99% VaR?\",\"options\":[\"1.645 (95% one-tailed)\",\"1.96 (95% two-tailed)\",\"2.326 (99% one-tailed)\",\"2.576 (99% two-tailed)\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"VaR is a one-tailed quantile; 99% uses 2.326. The two-tailed values (1.96, 2.576) are distractors.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which property of a coherent risk measure does VaR fail to satisfy in general?\nA) Monotonicity B) Positive homogeneity C) Subadditivity D) Translation invariance\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** VaR can violate subadditivity: merging two portfolios can *increase* measured VaR, penalizing diversification. Expected shortfall satisfies all four coherence axioms.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Coherence axioms (VaR fails one)\",\"root\":\"4 properties\",\"children\":[\"Monotonicity ✓\",\"Positive homogeneity ✓\",\"Translation invariance ✓\",\"Subadditivity ✗ (VaR may fail)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A portfolio has daily P&L that is normally distributed with a mean of $40,000 and a daily standard deviation of $250,000. Using a one-day horizon and accounting for the non-zero mean, the 99% VaR is closest to:\nA) $581,500 B) $541,500 C) $621,500 D) $501,500\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute parametric one-day 99% VaR for normal P&L with a positive expected profit, using the full formula (do not drop the mean).\n\n1. Formula: $$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = -\\mu + z_\\alpha\\,\\sigma$$ with $$z_{0.99} = 2.326$$.\n2. Volatility term: $$z_\\alpha\\,\\sigma = 2.326 \\times 250{,}000 = 581{,}500$$.\n3. Mean term: $$-\\mu = -40{,}000$$ (a positive expected gain *reduces* VaR).\n4. Combine: $$\\text{VaR} = -40{,}000 + 581{,}500 = \\$541{,}500$$.\n\n- A. $581,500 ignores the mean entirely (treats μ = 0) — a trap when the question gives a non-zero drift.\n- B. $541,500 correctly applies $$-\\mu + z\\sigma$$.\n- C. $621,500 *adds* the mean ($581,500 + 40,000) — wrong sign; a positive expected return must lower VaR, not raise it.\n- D. $501,500 over-subtracts (e.g., subtracts $80,000) or mis-scales.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When a positive mean P&L is given, it lowers VaR ($$-\\mu$$ term). Only set μ = 0 when the question says to or when the horizon is very short and drift is assumed negligible.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Two independent zero-coupon bonds each default over one year with probability 4%, producing a loss of $20 million if they default and $0 otherwise. A risk manager computes the 95% VaR of each bond standalone and of the equally combined two-bond book. Which statement is correct?\nA) Standalone 95% VaR is $20M each; combined VaR is $40M, so VaR is subadditive here. B) Standalone 95% VaR is $0 each; combined 95% VaR is $20M, demonstrating that VaR violates subadditivity. C) Standalone 95% VaR is $0 each; combined 95% VaR is $0, so VaR is subadditive. D) Standalone 95% VaR is $20M each; combined VaR is $20M, demonstrating subadditivity.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the standalone and combined 95% VaR for the classic two-bond counterexample and state the coherence implication.\n\n1. Standalone: P(loss) = 4% < 5%. The 95% quantile of the loss distribution is the loss not exceeded with 95% probability. Since the probability of *any* loss is only 4% (below 5%), the 95th percentile loss is **$0**. So VaR = $0 each; sum = $0.\n2. Combined book: P(at least one default) = $$1 - (1-0.04)^2 = 1 - 0.96^2 = 1 - 0.9216 = 0.0784 = 7.84\\%$$.\n3. Since 7.84% > 5%, the lower 95% tail includes default states, so the 95% quantile loss is at least $20M (one bond defaulting). Combined 95% VaR = **$20M**.\n4. Result: $$\\text{VaR}(A+B) = \\$20\\text{M} > \\text{VaR}(A) + \\text{VaR}(B) = \\$0$$ — subadditivity is violated.\n\n- A and D assume each bond's VaR is $20M — wrong, because 4% default probability sits *below* the 5% tail cutoff, so the quantile loss is $0.\n- C correctly gets $0 standalone but wrongly claims the combined VaR is $0; with 7.84% > 5% combined default probability, the tail now captures a $20M loss.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: VaR fails subadditivity precisely when individual default probabilities lie *between* one another and the tail level. Diversifying makes measured VaR jump — the textbook reason ES (coherent) is preferred.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"VaR fails subadditivity\",\"steps\":[\"Each bond: P(loss)=4% < 5% → VaR = $0\",\"Combined P(≥1 default)=7.84% > 5%\",\"Combined 95% VaR = $20M\",\"$20M > $0 + $0 → subadditivity broken\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A trading book's daily P&L is normal with mean 0 and σ = $4 million. Which of the following is closest to the difference between the 97.5% expected shortfall and the 97.5% VaR? Use $$z_{0.975} = 1.960$$ and $$\\phi(1.960) = 0.0584$$.\nA) $1.49M B) $1.71M C) $0.94M D) $2.13M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute ES − VaR at 97.5% for normal P&L.\n\n1. VaR: $$\\text{VaR}_{0.975} = z\\,\\sigma = 1.960 \\times 4 = \\$7.840\\text{M}$$.\n2. ES: $$\\text{ES}_{0.975} = \\sigma\\,\\frac{\\phi(z)}{1-\\alpha} = 4 \\times \\frac{0.0584}{0.025}$$.\n3. Compute the multiplier: $$\\frac{0.0584}{0.025} = 2.336$$.\n4. ES: $$4 \\times 2.336 = \\$9.344\\text{M}$$.\n5. Difference: $$9.344 - 7.840 = \\$1.504\\text{M} \\approx \\$1.49\\text{M}$$ (rounding of inputs).\n\n- B uses a slightly different φ or divides by 0.0584/0.0250 incorrectly.\n- C halves the result (uses $1-\\alpha = 0.05$ by mistake).\n- D adds rather than computing the spread, or uses the 99% multiplier.\n- A is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The ES multiplier $$\\phi(z)/(1-\\alpha)$$ always exceeds $$z$$, so ES > VaR. At 97.5% the ES multiplier (2.336) is almost exactly the 99% VaR multiplier (2.326) — that near-equality is why FRTB swapped 99% VaR for 97.5% ES.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A risk measure ρ assigns higher numbers to riskier positions. A junior analyst claims VaR is coherent. Which single property does VaR violate in general, and what is the financial consequence?\nA) Translation invariance — adding cash would fail to reduce required capital. B) Positive homogeneity — doubling a position would not double VaR. C) Subadditivity — combining portfolios can increase measured risk, penalizing diversification. D) Monotonicity — a position that always pays less could show lower VaR.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: name the coherence property VaR fails and its consequence.\n\n1. The four coherence properties are monotonicity, subadditivity, positive homogeneity, and translation invariance.\n2. VaR satisfies monotonicity, positive homogeneity, and translation invariance for any distribution.\n3. VaR fails **subadditivity** for non-elliptical distributions: $$\\rho(X_1 + X_2)$$ can exceed $$\\rho(X_1) + \\rho(X_2)$$.\n4. Consequence: diversification can appear to *raise* risk, which penalizes pooling positions and invites regulatory arbitrage (splitting a book into desks to lower reported VaR).\n\n- A is false; VaR satisfies translation invariance ($$\\rho(X+c) = \\rho(X) - c$$).\n- B is false; VaR is positively homogeneous.\n- D is false; VaR satisfies monotonicity.\n- C is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize that VaR fails *only* subadditivity (and only for non-elliptical/non-normal cases). ES satisfies all four — that single failure is the entire case for ES.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Coherence: VaR vs ES\",\"root\":\"4 properties\",\"children\":[\"Monotonicity ✓ both\",\"Subadditivity ✗ VaR / ✓ ES\",\"Positive homogeneity ✓ both\",\"Translation inv. ✓ both\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A portfolio's loss is normal with μ = 0 and σ = $10M. A manager wants to report a single tail number that (i) is coherent and (ii) reflects the *average* severity of losses in the worst 1% of days. Which measure and value (using $$\\phi(2.326) = 0.0267$$) is correct?\nA) 99% VaR = $23.26M B) 99% ES = $26.70M C) 99% ES = $23.26M D) 99% VaR = $26.70M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: choose the coherent, average-tail-severity measure (ES, not VaR) and compute 99% ES.\n\n1. VaR is a quantile (frequency of breach), not an average of tail losses, and is not coherent — so it fails both stated requirements. The answer must be ES.\n2. Formula: $$\\text{ES}_{0.99} = \\sigma\\,\\frac{\\phi(z_{0.99})}{1-\\alpha} = 10 \\times \\frac{0.0267}{0.01}$$.\n3. Multiplier: $$\\frac{0.0267}{0.01} = 2.670$$.\n4. ES: $$10 \\times 2.670 = \\$26.70\\text{M}$$.\n\n- A and D report VaR, which is neither coherent nor an average of tail losses — disqualified by the question's criteria.\n- C labels ES but plugs in the VaR value ($$2.326 \\times 10 = 23.26$$) — it forgot to use the ES multiplier.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Read the *requirements* in the stem. \"Coherent\" + \"average of the worst losses\" both point to ES. Then never reuse the VaR multiplier (z) for ES — use $$\\phi(z)/(1-\\alpha)$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Pick the measure (coherent + avg tail)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR — disqualified\",\"items\":[\"quantile, not an average\",\"not coherent\",\"multiplier z = 2.326\"]},{\"h\":\"ES — answer\",\"items\":[\"averages worst 1% losses\",\"coherent\",\"multiplier φ(z)/(1−α) = 2.67\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An analyst states: \"Two portfolios with identical mean and identical standard deviation are equally risky, so the mean-variance framework ranks them correctly.\" For which case is this conclusion guaranteed to be valid?\nA) When at least one portfolio contains short option positions. B) When both return distributions are elliptical (e.g., both normal). C) Whenever the standard deviations are estimated with no sampling error. D) When both portfolios have negative skew of equal magnitude.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify when mean-variance ranking is guaranteed correct.\n\n1. Mean-variance ranking is consistent with expected utility only when the distribution is fully described by μ and σ — true for **elliptical** distributions (normal, t with finite variance).\n2. If both are elliptical with the same μ and σ, they have the same distribution, so they are genuinely equally risky.\n3. Skew or fat tails add information σ cannot capture; two distributions with equal μ and σ but different higher moments can have very different tail risk.\n\n- A: short options create skew/non-linearity — mean-variance breaks down.\n- C: accurate σ does not help if the distribution is non-elliptical; the framework is wrong regardless of estimation precision.\n- D: equal negative skew of equal magnitude is not guaranteed by equal σ, and skew itself voids the framework's sufficiency; this is not the guaranteed-valid case.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The mean-variance framework's validity hinges on the *distribution class* (elliptical), not on estimation quality. \"Accurate σ\" distractors are designed to mislead — accuracy of inputs is irrelevant if the model assumes the wrong shape.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mean-variance ranking guaranteed valid?\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Valid\",\"items\":[\"both elliptical (normal)\",\"fully described by μ, σ\",\"same μ,σ → same distribution\"]},{\"h\":\"Not valid\",\"items\":[\"short options (skew)\",\"negative skew\",\"accurate σ doesn't help\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A normal portfolio P&L has μ = 0. An analyst computes 95% VaR using $$z = 1.645$$ but mistakenly looks up $$z = 1.282$$ (the 90% value). If true σ = $8M, by how much does the analyst *understate* the 95% VaR?\nA) $2.90M B) $10.26M C) $13.16M D) $1.45M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute the dollar understatement caused by using the 90% z-value instead of the 95% z-value.\n\n1. Correct 95% VaR: $$1.645 \\times 8 = \\$13.16\\text{M}$$.\n2. Erroneous VaR (90% z): $$1.282 \\times 8 = \\$10.256\\text{M} \\approx \\$10.26\\text{M}$$.\n3. Understatement: $$13.16 - 10.256 = \\$2.904\\text{M} \\approx \\$2.90\\text{M}$$.\n\n- B is the *erroneous* VaR itself, not the difference.\n- C is the *correct* VaR, not the difference.\n- D uses $$(1.645 - 1.282) \\times 4$$ or otherwise halves σ.\n- A is correct: $$(1.645 - 1.282) \\times 8 = 0.363 \\times 8 = 2.904$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When a question describes a z-value mix-up, compute both VaRs and take the difference. The shortcut is $$(\\Delta z) \\times \\sigma$$. Common confidence z-values to memorize: 90% = 1.282, 95% = 1.645, 97.5% = 1.960, 99% = 2.326.\n</details>\n"},{"num":"402","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Calculating and Applying VaR","notes":"# 2. Calculating and Applying VaR\n\nGARP tests the three estimation methods (delta-normal, historical simulation, Monte Carlo), when each works, the square-root-of-time rule, and the logic of backtesting. Expect at least one historical-simulation rank question and one scaling question.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain and calculate VaR using parametric (delta-normal), historical simulation, and Monte Carlo approaches.\n- Compute portfolio VaR for linear positions using volatilities and correlations.\n- Describe the limitations of the delta-normal method for non-linear (option) portfolios.\n- Apply the square-root-of-time rule to scale VaR across horizons and state its assumptions.\n- Describe how VaR models are backtested by comparing exceptions to expected exceedances.\n- Explain how ES is estimated under historical simulation.\n\n## Linear vs non-linear portfolios\n\n- **Linear:** portfolio value changes proportionally with risk-factor changes (stocks, FX spot, forwards, roughly bonds for small moves). If risk factors are jointly normal, portfolio P&L is normal → delta-normal VaR is exact.\n- **Non-linear:** options and anything with convexity. Delta approximation breaks down for large moves; the P&L distribution is skewed even with normal underlying returns. Use full revaluation (historical sim or Monte Carlo).\n\n## Delta-normal (parametric) VaR\n\nMap each position to dollar exposure × risk-factor volatility, then aggregate with correlations. For two positions with dollar standard deviations $$\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2$$ and correlation ρ:\n\n$$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + 2\\rho\\,\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}$$\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = z_\\alpha \\,\\sigma_P$$\n\nFor an option, the linear mapping uses delta: dollar exposure ≈ $$\\Delta \\times S \\times$$ (number of underlying units), so $$\\sigma_{\\$} = \\Delta \\cdot S \\cdot \\sigma_S$$. This ignores gamma — fine for small moves only.\n\n**Pros:** fast, analytic, easy aggregation. **Cons:** assumes normality (thin tails), linearizes options, correlations unstable in stress.\n\n### Worked example: two-asset delta-normal VaR\n\nPositions: \\$60M in asset 1 (daily σ = 2%), \\$40M in asset 2 (daily σ = 1%), correlation 0.3.\n\nDollar σs: $$\\sigma_1 = 60 \\times 0.02 = \\$1.2\\text{M}, \\quad \\sigma_2 = 40 \\times 0.01 = \\$0.4\\text{M}$$\n\n$$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{1.2^2 + 0.4^2 + 2(0.3)(1.2)(0.4)} = \\sqrt{1.44 + 0.16 + 0.288} = \\sqrt{1.888} = \\$1.374\\text{M}$$\n\n**99% one-day VaR:** $$2.326 \\times 1.374 = \\$3.196\\text{M}$$\n\nUndiversified VaR = 2.326 × (1.2 + 0.4) = \\$3.722M → **diversification benefit** = \\$0.526M. (With ρ = 1 the portfolio VaR equals the sum; with ρ < 1 it is strictly smaller — delta-normal VaR is subadditive.)\n\n## Historical simulation\n\nRevalue today's portfolio under each of the last *n* days' risk-factor changes, producing *n* simulated P&Ls. Sort losses; VaR is the loss at the appropriate rank. With *n* scenarios at confidence α, a common convention (Hull/GARP) takes the $$n(1-\\alpha)$$-th worst loss; ES is the average of the worst $$n(1-\\alpha)$$ losses.\n\n**Pros:** no distributional assumption, captures fat tails and non-linearity (full revaluation), easy to explain. **Cons:** limited by sample (the future must look like the recent past), ghost effects as big days drop out of the window, poor at extreme confidence levels with short windows.\n\n### Worked example: historical-simulation VaR and ES\n\n100 daily simulated losses, the six worst (in \\$K): 1,420; 1,180; 990; 870; 760; 700.\n\n**95% VaR:** rank = 100 × 0.05 = 5th worst loss = **\\$760K**.\n\n**95% ES:** average of the worst five = $$\\frac{1{,}420 + 1{,}180 + 990 + 870 + 760}{5} = \\frac{5{,}220}{5} = \\$1{,}044\\text{K}$$\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Conventions differ (5th vs 6th worst, interpolation). GARP questions state or imply one — with $$n(1-\\alpha)$$ an integer, use that rank, and average that many worst losses for ES.\n\n## Monte Carlo simulation\n\nSpecify stochastic processes for risk factors (with estimated parameters and correlations), simulate thousands of paths, fully revalue the portfolio on each, read VaR/ES off the simulated distribution.\n\n**Pros:** handles any instrument, any distribution, any horizon; no data-window limit. **Cons:** computationally heavy, exposed to model risk (you get out what you put in), sampling error unless many trials.\n\n| | Delta-normal | Historical sim | Monte Carlo |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Distributional assumption | Normal | None (empirical) | Whatever you specify |\n| Non-linearity | Poor (delta only) | Good (full reval) | Good (full reval) |\n| Speed | Fastest | Moderate | Slowest |\n| Main risk | Thin tails | Unrepresentative window | Model misspecification |\n\n## Scaling VaR over time: the square-root rule\n\nIf daily returns are i.i.d. with zero mean, variances add, so:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{T\\text{-day}} = \\text{VaR}_{1\\text{-day}} \\times \\sqrt{T}$$\n\nAssumptions: **i.i.d.** returns (no autocorrelation, constant volatility) and zero drift. Positive autocorrelation (trending) → √t **understates** true risk; mean reversion → overstates. Basel's traditional market-risk charge scaled 1-day 99% VaR by √10.\n\n### Worked example: time scaling\n\nOne-day 99% VaR is \\$1.2M. The 10-day 99% VaR under the square-root rule:\n\n$$1.2 \\times \\sqrt{10} = 1.2 \\times 3.1623 = \\$3.79\\text{M}$$\n\nGoing the other way: an annual VaR computed from σ_annual = 25% on \\$50M at 95% is 1.645 × 0.25 × 50 = \\$20.56M; the corresponding 1-day VaR (252 trading days) is $$20.56/\\sqrt{252} = \\$1.30\\text{M}$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Scale by $$\\sqrt{T}$$, **never by T**. And the rule applies to σ and VaR, not to variance (variance scales by T). Mixing these up — multiplying VaR by 10 instead of √10, or σ by 252 instead of √252 — is the most common distractor in scaling questions.\n\n## Backtesting intuition\n\nCompare realized daily losses against the VaR forecast over, say, 250 days and count **exceptions** (days the loss exceeded VaR).\n\n- Expected exceptions = $$n(1-\\alpha)$$: at 99% over 250 days, expect **2.5**.\n- Too many exceptions → model understates risk; far too few → overly conservative (also a problem: wasted capital).\n- The number of exceptions is binomial $$(n, 1-\\alpha)$$; statistical tests (e.g., Kupiec) judge whether the observed count is plausible. Basel's traffic-light approach: green ≤ 4 exceptions, yellow 5–9 (capital multiplier rises), red ≥ 10.\n- With high confidence levels, exceptions are rare → **low power**: hard to distinguish a good 99% model from a bad one quickly. Backtesting at 95% gives more observations.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Also check **independence** of exceptions: clustered exceptions signal the model misses volatility dynamics even if the total count looks fine.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Name the three VaR estimation methods.\n> **A:** Delta-normal (parametric), historical simulation, and Monte Carlo simulation.\n\n**Q2.** Why does delta-normal VaR fail for option portfolios?\n> **A:** It linearizes payoffs using delta and ignores gamma; option convexity produces skewed P&L even with normal underlying returns. Use full revaluation (historical sim or Monte Carlo).\n\n**Q3.** Two-asset delta-normal portfolio standard deviation.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + 2\\rho\\,\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}$$, then $$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = z_\\alpha\\sigma_P$$.\n\n**Q4.** Compute delta-normal portfolio σ: $$\\sigma_1=\\$1.2M$$, $$\\sigma_2=\\$0.4M$$, ρ = 0.3.\n> **A:** $$\\sqrt{1.2^2+0.4^2+2(0.3)(1.2)(0.4)} = \\sqrt{1.888} = \\$1.374M$$.\n\n**Q5.** How is VaR estimated under historical simulation?\n> **A:** Revalue today's portfolio under the last n days' risk-factor changes, sort the n P&Ls, and take the $$n(1-\\alpha)$$-th worst loss. ES = average of the worst $$n(1-\\alpha)$$ losses.\n\n**Q6.** From 100 simulated daily losses, find the 95% VaR.\n> **A:** Rank = 100 × 0.05 = 5th worst loss. ES = average of the worst 5 losses.\n\n**Q7.** Pros and cons of historical simulation.\n> **A:** Pros: no distributional assumption, captures fat tails and non-linearity (full revaluation). Cons: limited by sample window, ghost effects as big days drop out, poor at extreme confidence with short windows.\n\n**Q8.** Main weakness of each VaR method.\n> **A:** Delta-normal: thin tails (normality). Historical sim: unrepresentative window. Monte Carlo: model misspecification (\"garbage in, garbage out\").\n\n**Q9.** State the square-root-of-time rule and its assumptions.\n> **A:** $$\\text{VaR}_{T\\text{-day}} = \\text{VaR}_{1\\text{-day}}\\times\\sqrt{T}$$. Assumes i.i.d. returns (no autocorrelation, constant vol) and zero drift.\n\n**Q10.** Scale a $2M one-day 99% VaR to 25 days.\n> **A:** $$2\\times\\sqrt{25}=2\\times5=\\$10M$$. (Multiplying by 25 instead of √25 is the classic trap.)\n\n**Q11.** How does autocorrelation affect the square-root rule?\n> **A:** Positive autocorrelation (trending) → √T understates true risk; mean reversion → overstates it. The rule applies to σ and VaR, not variance (which scales by T).\n\n**Q12.** What is a backtesting \"exception\" and how many are expected?\n> **A:** A day the realized loss exceeded the VaR forecast. Expected exceptions = $$n(1-\\alpha)$$; at 99% over 250 days, expect 2.5. Count is binomial(n, 1−α).\n\n**Q13.** Why is backtesting at 99% low power, and what else should be checked?\n> **A:** Exceptions are rare, so it is hard to distinguish good from bad models quickly (95% gives more observations). Also check independence — clustered exceptions signal missed volatility dynamics.\n\n**Q14.** Basel traffic-light backtesting thresholds (250 days).\n> **A:** Green ≤ 4 exceptions; yellow 5–9 (capital multiplier rises); red ≥ 10.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A trading desk holds $25 million in an equity index with a daily volatility of 1.6%. Using $z_{0.95} = 1.645$, the delta-normal 95% one-day VaR is closest to:\nA) $400,000 B) $658,000 C) $930,000 D) $784,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a single-position delta-normal VaR.\n\nStep 1 — dollar standard deviation:\n\n$$\\sigma_{\\$} = 0.016 \\times \\$25{,}000{,}000 = \\$400{,}000$$\n\nStep 2 — multiply by the 95% z-score:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{0.95} = 1.645 \\times \\$400{,}000 = \\$658{,}000$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($400,000) is just the dollar standard deviation, with no z-multiplier.\n- C ($930,000) uses $z = 2.326$ (the 99% multiplier) instead of 1.645.\n- D ($784,000) uses $z = 1.96$ (a two-sided value), not the one-tailed 95% multiplier.\n\nTwo clean steps: turn the percent into dollars, then stretch by the confidence multiplier.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Delta-normal VaR for one position = z × (value × volatility). Use 1.645 for 95% and 2.326 for 99%.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A portfolio holds $60M in asset 1 (daily σ = 2%) and $40M in asset 2 (daily σ = 1%), with a correlation of 0.3. The portfolio's dollar standard deviation is closest to:\nA) $1.60M B) $1.37M C) $1.26M D) $2.00M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to combine two positions using volatilities and correlation.\n\nStep 1 — dollar standard deviations of each:\n\n$$\\sigma_1 = 0.02 \\times \\$60\\text{M} = \\$1.2\\text{M}, \\qquad \\sigma_2 = 0.01 \\times \\$40\\text{M} = \\$0.4\\text{M}$$\n\nStep 2 — combine with the two-asset formula:\n\n$$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + 2\\rho\\,\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}$$\n\n$$= \\sqrt{1.2^2 + 0.4^2 + 2(0.3)(1.2)(0.4)}$$\n\n$$= \\sqrt{1.44 + 0.16 + 0.288} = \\sqrt{1.888} = \\$1.374\\text{M}$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($1.60M) just adds the two dollar σs (1.2 + 0.4) — that would be the case only if correlation were 1.\n- C ($1.26M) wrongly subtracts the cross term, as if correlation were negative.\n- D ($2.00M) ignores the formula entirely.\n\nAnalogy: combining two risks isn't simple addition unless they move in perfect lockstep. With only partial correlation, they partly cancel, so the combined risk is less than the sum.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With $0 \\le \\rho < 1$, portfolio σ is strictly less than the sum of the σs — that gap is the diversification benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Using the portfolio from the previous question ($\\sigma_P = \\$1.374$M), the diversification benefit at 99% VaR (using $z = 2.326$) compared with the undiversified VaR is closest to:\nA) $0 B) $0.526M C) $1.20M D) $3.20M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the dollar reduction in VaR from diversification.\n\nDiversified VaR uses the combined σ:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{\\text{div}} = 2.326 \\times 1.374 = \\$3.196\\text{M}$$\n\nUndiversified VaR adds the individual VaRs (treats them as perfectly correlated):\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{\\text{undiv}} = 2.326 \\times (1.2 + 0.4) = 2.326 \\times 1.6 = \\$3.722\\text{M}$$\n\nDiversification benefit = the difference:\n\n$$3.722 - 3.196 = \\$0.526\\text{M}$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($0) would only hold if correlation were 1 (no benefit).\n- C ($1.20M) is asset 1's dollar σ, unrelated to the benefit.\n- D ($3.20M) is the diversified VaR itself, not the benefit.\n\nAnalogy: combining the desks saves about half a million in \"risk capital\" because they don't always lose money on the same day.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Diversification benefit = (sum of individual VaRs) − (portfolio VaR). It is zero when ρ = 1 and grows as ρ falls.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Diversification benefit\",\"steps\":[\"Undiversified VaR = z × (σ₁+σ₂)\",\"Diversified VaR = z × σ_portfolio\",\"Benefit = undiversified − diversified\",\"Zero if ρ=1; grows as ρ falls\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** From 500 daily losses generated by historical simulation, the 99% one-day VaR is best estimated by the:\nA) Single worst loss B) 5th worst loss C) 10th worst loss D) Average of all 500 losses\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the rank used in historical-simulation VaR.\n\nSort the simulated losses from worst to best. The VaR rank is $n \\times (1 - \\alpha)$:\n\n$$500 \\times (1 - 0.99) = 500 \\times 0.01 = 5$$\n\nSo the 99% VaR is the 5th worst loss. B is correct.\n\n- A (worst loss) corresponds to a far higher confidence than 99% with this sample.\n- C (10th worst) would be the 98% VaR ($500 \\times 0.02 = 10$), not 99%.\n- D (average of all losses) is roughly the mean loss, not a tail quantile.\n\nAnalogy: line up the 500 days from most painful to least painful. At 99% confidence you accept that the worst 1% (5 days) can breach the wall, so the wall sits at the 5th worst day.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Historical-sim VaR rank = n × (1 − α). For ES, average the worst n × (1 − α) losses.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Historical-simulation VaR\",\"steps\":[\"Collect the n past P&L observations\",\"Sort losses worst to best\",\"Rank = n × (1 − α) = 500 × 0.01 = 5\",\"VaR = the 5th worst loss\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A historical simulation produces 100 daily losses; the six worst (in $ thousands) are 1,420; 1,180; 990; 870; 760; 700. The 95% expected shortfall is closest to:\nA) $760K B) $1,044K C) $700K D) $920K\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for ES under historical simulation.\n\nStep 1 — find how many losses make up the tail:\n\n$$n \\times (1 - \\alpha) = 100 \\times 0.05 = 5$$\n\nSo the 5% tail is the worst 5 losses.\n\nStep 2 — ES is the average of those worst 5 losses:\n\n$$\\text{ES}_{0.95} = \\frac{1{,}420 + 1{,}180 + 990 + 870 + 760}{5} = \\frac{5{,}220}{5} = \\$1{,}044\\text{K}$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($760K) is the 5th worst loss — that is the VaR, not the ES.\n- C ($700K) is the 6th worst loss, outside the 5% tail.\n- D ($920K) wrongly averages a different set of numbers (e.g., includes the 6th loss).\n\nAnalogy: VaR is where the tail \"begins\" (the 5th worst day); ES is the average pain across all the days in that tail.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Historical-sim ES = average of the worst n × (1 − α) losses. It always comes out larger than the VaR (the cutoff loss).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Historical-simulation ES\",\"steps\":[\"Tail size = n × (1 − α) = 100 × 0.05 = 5\",\"Take the worst 5 losses\",\"ES = average of those 5\",\"VaR = the 5th worst (the cutoff)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A one-day 99% VaR is $2 million. Assuming i.i.d. returns with zero mean, the 25-day 99% VaR under the square-root-of-time rule is closest to:\nA) $50M B) $10M C) $31.6M D) $4M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests time-scaling of VaR.\n\nUnder the square-root rule:\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{T\\text{-day}} = \\text{VaR}_{1\\text{-day}} \\times \\sqrt{T}$$\n\nPlug in $T = 25$:\n\n$$\\$2\\text{M} \\times \\sqrt{25} = \\$2\\text{M} \\times 5 = \\$10\\text{M}$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A ($50M) multiplies by 25 (i.e., by T, not √T) — the single most common trap.\n- C ($31.6M) multiplies by $\\sqrt{250}$ or similar, the wrong horizon.\n- D ($4M) multiplies by 2, mis-reading √25 = 5 as 2.\n\nAnalogy: risk grows with the square root of time, not time itself — like how a random walk drifts away in proportion to √(steps), not steps.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Scale VaR by √T, never by T. The \"multiply by T\" answer is always offered as a distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which limitation makes the delta-normal method least suitable for a portfolio dominated by options?\nA) It is too slow to compute B) It requires too much historical data C) It linearizes payoffs and ignores convexity (gamma), misrepresenting non-linear P&L D) It cannot use correlations\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks why delta-normal struggles with options.\n\nThe delta-normal method maps each position to a linear (delta-based) exposure and assumes the resulting P&L is normal. Options have curved (convex) payoffs: their value does not change proportionally with the underlying. By using only delta and ignoring gamma, the method misstates option risk and the skewed P&L for large moves. C is correct.\n\n- A is false — delta-normal is the fastest method.\n- B is false — it needs volatilities and correlations, not long histories like historical simulation.\n- D is false — handling correlations is actually a strength of delta-normal.\n\nAnalogy: approximating a curved road with a straight ruler works for a short stretch (small moves) but fails badly over a long, winding section (large moves) — that's gamma the ruler misses.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For options/non-linear books, prefer full revaluation (historical sim or Monte Carlo). Delta-normal's weakness is linearization, not speed.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Why delta-normal fails on options\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Delta-normal\",\"items\":[\"linear (delta only)\",\"ignores gamma/convexity\",\"misstates non-linear P&L\"]},{\"h\":\"Full revaluation\",\"items\":[\"reprices each scenario\",\"captures convexity\",\"HS or Monte Carlo\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which statement about Monte Carlo simulation for VaR is most accurate?\nA) It makes no distributional assumptions at all B) It is the fastest of the three methods C) It can handle any instrument and distribution but is computationally heavy and exposed to model risk D) It is limited by the length of the historical data window\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the correct characterization of Monte Carlo.\n\nMonte Carlo specifies stochastic processes for the risk factors, simulates many paths, fully revalues the portfolio on each, and reads VaR/ES off the simulated distribution. It is the most flexible method — any instrument, any distribution, any horizon — but it is slow and only as good as the assumed model (\"garbage in, garbage out\"). C is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: you must specify a distribution/process — that is an assumption.\n- B is wrong: Monte Carlo is the slowest, not the fastest (delta-normal is fastest).\n- D describes historical simulation, which is limited by its data window; Monte Carlo is not.\n\nAnalogy: Monte Carlo is like a flight simulator — it can model any scenario you program, but the results are only trustworthy if the underlying physics (your model) is right.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Monte Carlo = most flexible, slowest, biggest model risk. Historical sim's main weakness is an unrepresentative window; delta-normal's is thin tails.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Three VaR methods\",\"root\":\"Method → main weakness\",\"children\":[\"Delta-normal: fast; thin tails, linearizes\",\"Historical sim: no dist. assumption; window risk\",\"Monte Carlo: most flexible; slow, model risk\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Over 250 trading days, a bank backtests its 99% one-day VaR model. The expected number of exceptions (days the loss exceeds VaR) is closest to:\nA) 0.25 B) 2.5 C) 25 D) 12.5\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the expected number of VaR breaches in backtesting.\n\nThe expected number of exceptions is the sample size times the tail probability:\n\n$$\\text{Expected exceptions} = n \\times (1 - \\alpha) = 250 \\times (1 - 0.99) = 250 \\times 0.01 = 2.5$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (0.25) uses $1 - \\alpha = 0.001$ — off by a factor of ten.\n- C (25) uses $1 - \\alpha = 0.10$ (a 90% model), the wrong confidence.\n- D (12.5) uses $1 - \\alpha = 0.05$ (a 95% model), not the 99% stated.\n\nAnalogy: if the wall is built so floods should top it 1 day in 100, then over 250 days you'd expect floods to top it about 2.5 times.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected exceptions = n × (1 − α). Far too many breaches → model understates risk; far too few → overly conservative (wasted capital).\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Why is backtesting a 99% VaR model statistically harder than backtesting a 95% VaR model over the same period?\nA) The 99% model produces more exceptions, creating noise B) Exceptions at 99% are rare, giving low statistical power to detect a bad model C) The square-root rule does not apply at 99% D) The 99% model is always more accurate, so no testing is needed\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the statistical-power problem at high confidence levels.\n\nAt 99%, exceptions are expected only about 1% of the time, so over a typical sample you observe very few breaches (e.g., 2.5 in 250 days). With so few data points, it is hard to tell a good model from a bad one quickly — this is low statistical power. Testing at 95% produces more expected exceptions and thus more information. B is correct.\n\n- A is backwards: the 99% model produces fewer exceptions, not more.\n- C is irrelevant — the square-root rule is about scaling horizons, not about backtest power.\n- D is wrong: a higher confidence level does not make a model more accurate, and all models still require testing.\n\nAnalogy: trying to judge a rare-event predictor (like \"earthquakes this big\") is hard precisely because the events almost never happen — you can't gather enough cases to grade it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Higher confidence → rarer exceptions → lower backtest power. Also check exception independence; clustered breaches signal missed volatility dynamics even if the count looks fine.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Backtest power: 95% vs 99% VaR\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"95% model\",\"items\":[\"many expected exceptions\",\"more data to judge\",\"higher statistical power\"]},{\"h\":\"99% model\",\"items\":[\"rare exceptions (~2.5/250)\",\"little data to judge\",\"low statistical power\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A desk holds \\$25M in an index with daily volatility 1.6%. Its 95% one-day VaR is closest to:\nA) \\$0.658M B) \\$0.784M C) \\$0.930M D) \\$0.400M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** 1.645 × 0.016 × 25M = \\$0.658M. (\\$0.930M uses z = 2.326; \\$0.784M uses 1.96.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** From 500 historical-simulation losses, the 99% VaR is best estimated by the:\nA) Worst loss B) 5th worst loss C) 10th worst loss D) Average of all losses\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** 500 × 0.01 = 5th worst loss under the standard convention.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A one-day 99% VaR of \\$2M corresponds to a 25-day VaR (i.i.d. returns) of:\nA) \\$50M B) \\$10M C) \\$31.6M D) \\$4M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** 2 × √25 = 2 × 5 = \\$10M. (\\$50M multiplies by 25 — the classic trap.)\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which method is *least* suitable for a large portfolio of exotic options?\nA) Delta-normal B) Historical simulation with full revaluation C) Monte Carlo with full revaluation D) They are equally suitable\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Delta-normal linearizes payoffs; option convexity and skewed P&L are lost.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A desk's one-day 99% VaR is $3.0 million. Assuming iid returns, the 10-day 99% VaR is closest to:\nA) $3.00 million B) $9.49 million C) $13.42 million D) $30.00 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$VaR_{10d} = 3.0 \\times \\sqrt{10} = \\$9.49\\text{m}$$\n\nVolatility (and hence VaR) scales with $$\\sqrt{t}$$; D scales linearly — that is how *variance* scales.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"How risk measures scale with time\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Variance\",\"items\":[\"scales linearly with t\",\"σ² × t\"]},{\"h\":\"Volatility & VaR\",\"items\":[\"scale with √t\",\"VaR × √t (here ×√10)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** From 100 historical scenario losses, the six largest are (in $ millions): 12, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6. The 95% expected shortfall is closest to:\nA) $7.0 million B) $8.7 million C) $9.2 million D) $12.0 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With 100 scenarios, the 95% ES is the average of the worst 5% — the worst five losses:\n\n$$ES = \\frac{12 + 10 + 9 + 8 + 7}{5} = \\frac{46}{5} = \\$9.2\\text{m}$$\n\nDistractor A is the 95% VaR-style cutoff (5th worst), not the tail average; B wrongly averages six losses.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Expected Shortfall\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR (95%)\",\"items\":[\"the loss cutoff (quantile)\",\"here = 5th-worst loss\",\"not always subadditive\"]},{\"h\":\"ES (95%)\",\"items\":[\"average of losses beyond VaR\",\"here = mean of worst 5\",\"coherent; ES ≥ VaR\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A historical simulation uses 500 daily P&L observations. The five largest losses are (in $ millions): 8.4, 7.1, 6.6, 6.0, 5.7, and the sixth largest is 5.2. The one-day 99% VaR is closest to:\nA) $5.2 million B) $5.7 million C) $6.0 million D) $8.4 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** 1% of 500 observations = 5, so the 99% VaR is the 5th worst loss: $5.7 million. Distractor A takes the 6th worst, D the very worst.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Historical-simulation VaR\",\"steps\":[\"Collect 500 daily P&L observations\",\"Sort losses worst to best\",\"Count: 1% × 500 = 5\",\"Pick the 5th-worst loss = $5.7m\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A portfolio holds $80M in Asset 1 (daily σ = 1.5%) and $50M in Asset 2 (daily σ = 2.5%), with a return correlation of −0.20. Using the delta-normal method, the 99% one-day VaR is closest to:\nA) $3.61M B) $5.70M C) $4.42M D) $3.04M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute two-asset delta-normal 99% VaR with a negative correlation.\n\n1. Dollar volatilities: $$\\sigma_1 = 80 \\times 0.015 = \\$1.20\\text{M}$$; $$\\sigma_2 = 50 \\times 0.025 = \\$1.25\\text{M}$$.\n2. Portfolio variance: $$\\sigma_P^2 = \\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + 2\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$.\n3. Plug in: $$= 1.20^2 + 1.25^2 + 2(-0.20)(1.20)(1.25) = 1.44 + 1.5625 - 0.60 = 2.4025$$.\n4. Portfolio σ: $$\\sigma_P = \\sqrt{2.4025} = \\$1.550\\text{M}$$.\n5. VaR: $$2.326 \\times 1.550 = \\$3.61\\text{M}$$.\n\n- B. $5.70M is undiversified VaR — $$2.326 \\times (1.20 + 1.25) = 2.326 \\times 2.45 = 5.70$$, treating correlation as +1 and ignoring diversification.\n- C. $4.42M wrongly uses ρ = +0.20: $$\\sqrt{3.0025 + 0.60} = \\sqrt{3.6025} = 1.898$$, $$\\times 2.326 = 4.42$$ — sign error on the cross term.\n- D. $3.04M uses z = 1.96 instead of 2.326.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A negative correlation *subtracts* the cross term, shrinking portfolio VaR. Always confirm the sign of $$2\\rho\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$ before taking the square root.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** From 300 daily historical-simulation P&L observations, the four worst losses (in $000s) are 920, 760, 610, and 540. Using the GARP convention that 99% VaR is the $$n(1-\\alpha)$$-th worst loss and 99% ES is the average of the worst $$n(1-\\alpha)$$ losses, the 99% VaR and 99% ES are closest to:\nA) VaR = $540K; ES = $610K B) VaR = $610K; ES = $763K C) VaR = $540K; ES = $708K D) VaR = $610K; ES = $708K\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: find the rank for 99% VaR with n = 300, then the matching tail average for ES.\n\n1. Rank for VaR: $$n(1-\\alpha) = 300 \\times 0.01 = 3$$ (an integer — no rounding needed). VaR = 3rd worst loss = **$610K**.\n2. ES: average of the worst 3 losses = $$\\frac{920 + 760 + 610}{3} = \\frac{2{,}290}{3} = \\$763.3\\text{K} \\approx \\$763\\text{K}$$.\n\n- A uses the 4th worst as VaR (wrong rank) and averages the worst 2 for ES.\n- C uses the 4th worst as VaR and averages all four worst (708) for ES — both off by one.\n- D pairs the correct VaR with a 4-loss ES average ($708) instead of 3.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For HS, first compute $$n(1-\\alpha)$$. When it is an integer, read VaR at exactly that rank; ES is the average of *that many* worst losses — not one more, not one fewer.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"HS VaR and ES (n=300, 99%)\",\"steps\":[\"Rank = n(1−α) = 300 × 0.01 = 3\",\"VaR = 3rd worst loss = $610K\",\"ES = average of worst 3 = $763K\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A one-day 95% VaR of $1.5M is to be scaled to a 16-day horizon assuming i.i.d. zero-mean returns. A second analyst instead scales it to 16 days by multiplying by 16. The difference between the incorrect and correct 16-day VaR is:\nA) $18.0M B) $6.0M C) $24.0M D) $4.5M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute the correct √T-scaled VaR, the incorrect ×T VaR, and their difference.\n\n1. Correct (square-root rule): $$\\text{VaR}_{16} = 1.5 \\times \\sqrt{16} = 1.5 \\times 4 = \\$6.0\\text{M}$$.\n2. Incorrect (×T): $$1.5 \\times 16 = \\$24.0\\text{M}$$.\n3. Difference: $$24.0 - 6.0 = \\$18.0\\text{M}$$.\n\n- B is the *correct* VaR, not the difference.\n- C is the *incorrect* VaR, not the difference.\n- D scales σ instead (or divides wrongly).\n- A is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Scale VaR (and σ) by $$\\sqrt{T}$$, never by T. Variance scales by T. The \"×T\" answer is always offered as a distractor — and so is the raw correct or incorrect figure when the question asks for a *difference*.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A bank backtests its 99% one-day VaR over 250 trading days and observes 8 exceptions. Under the Basel traffic-light approach and basic backtesting logic, which conclusion is most appropriate?\nA) 8 exceptions is below the 2.5 expected, so the model is too conservative. B) The expected number of exceptions is 2.5; 8 falls in the yellow zone, suggesting the model understates risk and the capital multiplier should rise. C) 8 exceptions exactly matches expectations for a 95% model, so no action is needed. D) 8 exceptions falls in the red zone, requiring the model to be disallowed.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: interpret 8 exceptions for a 99%/250-day backtest using the traffic-light zones.\n\n1. Expected exceptions: $$n(1-\\alpha) = 250 \\times 0.01 = 2.5$$.\n2. Observed 8 > 2.5, so the model produces *too many* exceptions — it understates risk.\n3. Basel traffic-light zones (250 days, 99%): green ≤ 4, yellow 5–9, red ≥ 10. Eight falls in the **yellow** zone → capital multiplier increases (penalty short of outright rejection).\n\n- A misreads 8 as below expectation; 8 > 2.5.\n- C invokes a 95% model — the question fixes 99%; irrelevant.\n- D claims red zone, but red requires ≥ 10; 8 is yellow.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the 250-day/99% traffic-light cut-offs: green 0–4, yellow 5–9, red ≥ 10. Expected exceptions = $$n(1-\\alpha)$$. Too many exceptions → understated risk; far too few → wasted capital.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Basel traffic light (250d, 99%)\",\"items\":[\"0\",\"|4 green\",\"5\",\"8 yellow\",\"9\",\"|10 red\"],\"left\":\"model OK\",\"right\":\"reject / penalize\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A risk team must measure one-day VaR for a book of long out-of-the-money call options. Which statement about the appropriate method is correct?\nA) Delta-normal is exact here because the underlying returns are normal. B) Delta-normal will understate the curvature (gamma) effect; full-revaluation historical simulation or Monte Carlo is preferred. C) Historical simulation cannot capture option non-linearity even with full revaluation. D) Monte Carlo is unsuitable because it assumes normally distributed P&L.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: choose the correct method rationale for a non-linear (option) portfolio.\n\n1. Options have convex (non-linear) payoffs; the delta-normal method linearizes the payoff using delta only, ignoring gamma.\n2. For large moves the delta approximation breaks down, and option P&L is skewed even when the underlying return is normal — so delta-normal mis-measures tail risk.\n3. Full-revaluation methods (historical simulation or Monte Carlo) reprice the options under each scenario, capturing convexity. These are preferred.\n\n- A is false: normal underlying returns do not make delta-normal exact for options; the payoff non-linearity remains.\n- C is false: HS with full revaluation *does* capture non-linearity — that is its strength.\n- D is false: Monte Carlo can use *any* specified distribution and full revaluation; it does not require normal P&L.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Options\" or \"convexity/gamma\" in a VaR question signals delta-normal is the *wrong* choice. Full revaluation (HS or MC) is the answer. Remember HS makes no distributional assumption and MC uses whatever distribution you specify.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Method choice for option book\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Delta-normal\",\"items\":[\"linearizes (delta only)\",\"ignores gamma\",\"understates tail risk\"]},{\"h\":\"HS / Monte Carlo\",\"items\":[\"full revaluation\",\"captures convexity\",\"preferred for options\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An annual 95% VaR computed from an annual volatility of 30% on a $40M position is to be converted to a one-day VaR assuming 252 trading days and i.i.d. zero-mean returns. The one-day 95% VaR is closest to:\nA) $1.24M B) $0.078M C) $19.74M D) $0.0784M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute annual VaR, then scale down to one day by dividing by $$\\sqrt{252}$$.\n\n1. Annual VaR: $$1.645 \\times 0.30 \\times 40 = 1.645 \\times 12 = \\$19.74\\text{M}$$.\n2. Scale to one day: divide by $$\\sqrt{252} = 15.875$$.\n3. One-day VaR: $$\\frac{19.74}{15.875} = \\$1.243\\text{M} \\approx \\$1.24\\text{M}$$.\n\n- B/D divide by 252 (not $$\\sqrt{252}$$): $$19.74/252 = 0.0783$$ — the classic scaling trap (using T instead of √T).\n- C is the *annual* VaR, not the daily figure.\n- A is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To go from a long horizon to a short one, divide by $$\\sqrt{T}$$. Dividing by T (252) instead of $$\\sqrt{252}$$ understates daily VaR by a factor of ~16 — exactly the distractor offered.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which limitation is correctly paired with its VaR estimation method?\nA) Historical simulation — assumes returns are normally distributed. B) Monte Carlo simulation — limited by the length of the historical data window. C) Delta-normal — thin tails and poor handling of non-linear payoffs. D) Historical simulation — fastest method but most exposed to model misspecification.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: match each method to its true principal weakness.\n\n1. Delta-normal assumes normality (thin tails relative to empirical fat tails) and linearizes options — its two core weaknesses. Correct pairing.\n2. Historical simulation makes *no* distributional assumption (so A is wrong) and its main weakness is dependence on the data window (unrepresentative samples, ghost effects).\n3. Monte Carlo is *not* limited by a historical window (it generates paths), so B is wrong; its weakness is model misspecification and compute cost.\n4. HS is *moderate* speed, not fastest (delta-normal is fastest), and \"model misspecification\" is the MC weakness — so D is wrong.\n\n- A misattributes the normality assumption to HS.\n- B misattributes the data-window limit to MC.\n- D mislabels HS as fastest and pins MC's weakness on it.\n- C is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Lock down the method–weakness map: delta-normal → thin tails + linearizes options; HS → unrepresentative window/ghosting; MC → model risk + slow. Mismatched pairings are a favorite multiple-choice trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Method → main weakness\",\"root\":\"Match each correctly\",\"children\":[\"Delta-normal: thin tails + linearizes options\",\"Historical sim: unrepresentative window/ghosting\",\"Monte Carlo: model risk + slow\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A delta-normal VaR for a single long stock position equals $2.4M at 99%. The desk adds a second uncorrelated position whose standalone 99% VaR is $1.8M. What is the combined 99% delta-normal VaR, and does the result respect subadditivity?\nA) $4.20M; equal to the sum, so not diversifying. B) $3.00M; below the $4.20M sum, consistent with subadditivity. C) $3.00M; above the sum, violating subadditivity. D) $1.80M; below the sum, consistent with subadditivity.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: combine two independent (ρ = 0) delta-normal VaRs and check subadditivity.\n\n1. For normal P&L, VaR scales with σ, so VaRs combine like volatilities: $$\\text{VaR}_P = \\sqrt{\\text{VaR}_1^2 + \\text{VaR}_2^2 + 2\\rho\\,\\text{VaR}_1\\text{VaR}_2}$$.\n2. With ρ = 0: $$\\text{VaR}_P = \\sqrt{2.4^2 + 1.8^2} = \\sqrt{5.76 + 3.24} = \\sqrt{9.00} = \\$3.00\\text{M}$$.\n3. Sum of standalone VaRs = $$2.4 + 1.8 = \\$4.20\\text{M}$$.\n4. Since $$3.00 < 4.20$$, the combined VaR is below the sum — delta-normal VaR is subadditive (it is for elliptical/normal distributions).\n\n- A treats ρ = +1 (just adds the VaRs) — wrong for uncorrelated positions.\n- C gets the right $3.00M but mislabels the relationship as a violation.\n- D ignores Position 1's contribution.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For normal positions, VaRs aggregate exactly like volatilities (square, add with the correlation cross term, square-root). Delta-normal VaR is always subadditive — the subadditivity failures occur with non-elliptical (e.g., default) distributions, not here.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Subadditivity by distribution\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Normal / delta-normal\",\"items\":[\"VaRs combine like σ\",\"√(2.4²+1.8²)=$3.0M\",\"< $4.2M sum → holds\"]},{\"h\":\"Non-elliptical (default)\",\"items\":[\"lumpy tail outcomes\",\"combined VaR can exceed sum\",\"subadditivity can fail\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"403","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Measuring and Monitoring Volatility","notes":"# 3. Measuring and Monitoring Volatility\n\nGARP tests the EWMA and GARCH(1,1) update formulas (near-certain calculation questions), why return distributions deviate from normality, and how implied volatility and correlation are monitored. Memorize both recursions cold.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain how asset return distributions deviate from normality (fat tails, skew) and why.\n- Distinguish conditional vs unconditional distributions and explain regime switching.\n- Estimate volatility using simple historical (equal-weight) standard deviation and discuss its drawbacks.\n- Apply the EWMA model to update volatility estimates.\n- Apply the GARCH(1,1) model; compute the long-run (unconditional) variance and explain mean reversion.\n- Explain implied volatility and its term structure/smile as a market-based volatility measure.\n- Describe how correlations and covariances are updated (EWMA/GARCH logic) and monitored.\n\n## Deviations from normality\n\nEmpirical daily returns show:\n\n- **Fat tails (leptokurtosis):** far more 4σ–10σ days than the normal predicts.\n- **Negative skew** in equities: crashes are bigger than rallies.\n- **Volatility clustering:** big moves follow big moves — volatility is time-varying.\n\nA key insight: returns can be **conditionally normal** (normal each day given that day's volatility) yet **unconditionally fat-tailed**, because mixing normals with different variances produces excess kurtosis. **Regime switching** formalizes this: volatility flips between high and low states; within a regime returns look normal, across regimes the blended distribution has fat tails. So fat tails arise from a changing σ at least as much as from non-normal shocks.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> This is why volatility *updating* (EWMA/GARCH) matters: if you track the conditional σ accurately, the standardized returns look much closer to normal.\n\n## Historical (equal-weight) volatility\n\nUsing m daily returns (mean assumed ≈ 0 at daily horizon):\n\n$$\\hat\\sigma_n^2 = \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_{i=1}^{m} r_{n-i}^2$$\n\nDrawbacks: every observation gets weight 1/m regardless of age; a single large return causes a jump in σ that persists until it abruptly drops out of the window (**ghosting**).\n\n## EWMA\n\nExponentially weighted moving average puts geometrically declining weights on past squared returns. One-line recursion:\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{n-1}^2$$\n\n- λ (decay factor) ∈ (0,1); RiskMetrics classic: **λ = 0.94** for daily data.\n- Higher λ → slower reaction, longer memory; lower λ → fast reaction, noisy.\n- No mean reversion: the forecast for all future days equals today's estimate.\n- Weight on the squared return from i days ago: $$(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^{i-1}$$.\n\n### Worked example: EWMA update\n\nλ = 0.94, yesterday's volatility estimate σ = 1.2%/day, yesterday's return = −2%.\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.94 \\times (0.012)^2 + 0.06 \\times (-0.02)^2 = 0.94 \\times 0.000144 + 0.06 \\times 0.0004$$\n\n$$= 0.00013536 + 0.000024 = 0.00015936$$\n\n$$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.00015936} = 1.262\\%$$\n\nThe big return pulls volatility **up** from 1.20% to 1.26%. (Distractor check: forgetting to square, or averaging σ instead of σ², gives wrong answers like 1.25% by linear interpolation.)\n\n## GARCH(1,1)\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha\\, r_{n-1}^2 + \\beta\\, \\sigma_{n-1}^2$$\n\nwith α + β < 1 for stationarity. The long-run (unconditional) variance:\n\n$$V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}$$\n\nEquivalently $$\\omega = \\gamma V_L$$ with weights γ + α + β = 1. GARCH = EWMA **plus mean reversion** toward $$V_L$$: multi-day forecasts decay from today's σ² to $$V_L$$ at rate (α+β). EWMA is the special case ω = 0, α = 1−λ, β = λ.\n\n### Worked example: GARCH(1,1) update and long-run variance\n\nω = 0.000002, α = 0.13, β = 0.86. Current daily volatility 1.6%; today's return +1%.\n\n**Long-run variance:**\n\n$$V_L = \\frac{0.000002}{1 - 0.13 - 0.86} = \\frac{0.000002}{0.01} = 0.0002 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\sigma_L = \\sqrt{0.0002} = 1.414\\%$$\n\n**Update:**\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.000002 + 0.13 \\times (0.01)^2 + 0.86 \\times (0.016)^2$$\n\n$$= 0.000002 + 0.000013 + 0.00022016 = 0.00023516$$\n\n$$\\sigma_n = 1.534\\%$$\n\nVolatility falls from 1.60% toward the 1.41% long-run level because the new return (1%) was small relative to the prior estimate — mean reversion in action.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Work entirely in **variances**, converting to σ only at the end. The single most common error is plugging σ (0.016) where σ² (0.000256) belongs, or quoting the variance 0.000235 as \"volatility.\" Also: α multiplies the **return**, β multiplies the **prior variance** — swapping them is a favorite distractor.\n\n## Implied volatility\n\nBack out σ from observed option prices by inverting BSM. It is **forward-looking** (market's consensus over the option's life) versus backward-looking statistical estimates.\n\n- **VIX:** model-free 30-day implied volatility of S&P 500 options — the \"fear gauge.\"\n- **Smile/skew:** implied vol varies with strike (equity index puts trade at higher implied vol — crash protection premium), violating BSM's constant-σ assumption.\n- **Term structure:** implied vol varies with maturity.\n\nLimitations: only available where liquid options trade; embeds risk premia, so it tends to sit above subsequent realized volatility on average.\n\n## Monitoring correlation and covariance\n\nThe same updating machinery applies to covariances:\n\n$$\\text{cov}_n = \\lambda\\,\\text{cov}_{n-1} + (1-\\lambda)\\,x_{n-1}y_{n-1}$$\n\nthen $$\\rho_n = \\text{cov}_n / (\\sigma_{x,n}\\sigma_{y,n})$$. Use the **same λ** for variances and covariances to keep the covariance matrix internally consistent (positive semi-definite). Correlations spike toward 1 in crises — exactly when diversification is needed most — so monitoring them dynamically matters for portfolio VaR.\n\n| Model | Reaction speed | Mean reversion | Parameters |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Equal-weight historical | Slow, ghosting | No | window m |\n| EWMA | Tunable via λ | No | λ |\n| GARCH(1,1) | Tunable via α | **Yes**, to $$V_L$$ | ω, α, β |\n| Implied | Instant (market) | n/a | none (market price) |","flashcards":"**Q1.** Three ways empirical daily returns deviate from normality.\n> **A:** Fat tails (leptokurtosis), negative skew (equity crashes bigger than rallies), and volatility clustering (big moves follow big moves).\n\n**Q2.** How can returns be conditionally normal yet unconditionally fat-tailed?\n> **A:** Mixing normals with different variances (time-varying volatility / regime switching) produces excess kurtosis. Tracking conditional σ makes standardized returns look near-normal.\n\n**Q3.** Equal-weight historical volatility formula and its main drawback.\n> **A:** $$\\hat\\sigma_n^2 = \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_{i=1}^m r_{n-i}^2$$. Drawback: every observation gets equal weight 1/m, causing \"ghosting\" — σ jumps and persists until a big return drops out of the window.\n\n**Q4.** State the EWMA variance update.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{n-1}^2$$. RiskMetrics uses λ = 0.94 for daily data.\n\n**Q5.** How does the decay factor λ affect EWMA behavior?\n> **A:** Higher λ → slower reaction, longer memory; lower λ → fast reaction, noisy. Weight on the squared return i days ago is $$(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^{i-1}$$. No mean reversion.\n\n**Q6.** EWMA update: λ = 0.94, prior σ = 1.2%, latest return = −2%.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.94(0.012)^2 + 0.06(0.02)^2 = 0.00015936$$ → σ = 1.262%. Work in variances, not σ.\n\n**Q7.** State the GARCH(1,1) variance update.\n> **A:** $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha\\,r_{n-1}^2 + \\beta\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2$$, with α + β < 1 for stationarity. α multiplies the return, β the prior variance.\n\n**Q8.** GARCH(1,1) long-run (unconditional) variance.\n> **A:** $$V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1-\\alpha-\\beta}$$. Equivalently ω = γV_L with γ + α + β = 1. Forecasts revert to V_L at rate (α+β).\n\n**Q9.** Compute long-run volatility: ω = 0.000003, α = 0.04, β = 0.94.\n> **A:** $$V_L = \\frac{0.000003}{1-0.98}=0.00015$$ → $$\\sigma_L=\\sqrt{0.00015}=1.225\\%$$.\n\n**Q10.** Relate EWMA to GARCH(1,1).\n> **A:** EWMA is GARCH(1,1) with ω = 0, α = 1−λ, β = λ. So EWMA has no mean reversion; GARCH adds reversion to a long-run variance.\n\n**Q11.** What is implied volatility and how does it differ from statistical estimates?\n> **A:** The σ backed out by inverting BSM from observed option prices — forward-looking (market consensus over the option's life), versus backward-looking historical/EWMA/GARCH estimates. VIX is model-free 30-day S&P implied vol.\n\n**Q12.** What is the volatility smile/skew?\n> **A:** Implied vol varies with strike (equity index puts trade at higher implied vol — crash protection premium), violating BSM's constant-σ assumption. Term structure = variation with maturity.\n\n**Q13.** EWMA covariance update and a key consistency rule.\n> **A:** $$\\text{cov}_n = \\lambda\\,\\text{cov}_{n-1} + (1-\\lambda)\\,x_{n-1}y_{n-1}$$, then $$\\rho_n = \\text{cov}_n/(\\sigma_{x,n}\\sigma_{y,n})$$. Use the same λ for variances and covariances to keep the matrix positive semi-definite.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Using the EWMA model with $\\lambda = 0.94$, yesterday's volatility estimate was 1.2% per day and yesterday's return was −2%. The updated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 1.20% B) 1.26% C) 1.60% D) 2.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to update volatility one step with EWMA.\n\nThe EWMA recursion works in variances:\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{n-1}^2$$\n\nPlug in $\\lambda = 0.94$, $\\sigma_{n-1} = 0.012$, $r_{n-1} = -0.02$ (square it, so the sign disappears):\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.94 \\times (0.012)^2 + 0.06 \\times (0.02)^2$$\n\n$$= 0.94 \\times 0.000144 + 0.06 \\times 0.0004$$\n\n$$= 0.00013536 + 0.000024 = 0.00015936$$\n\nTake the square root:\n\n$$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.00015936} = 0.01262 = 1.262\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (1.20%) is yesterday's estimate, ignoring the update.\n- C (1.60%) ignores the math entirely.\n- D (2.00%) is just the latest return's magnitude, not the blended estimate.\n\nAnalogy: EWMA is a \"weighted memory\" — it keeps 94% of yesterday's view and adds 6% of the newest day's surprise. A big move nudges the estimate up a little, not all the way.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Always work in variances (square σ and r), blend, then take the square root at the very end. Squaring kills the minus sign on the return.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the EWMA model, the parameter $\\lambda$ (the decay factor):\nA) Must be greater than 1 for stability B) Controls how quickly old observations lose weight; higher λ means slower reaction C) Is the long-run variance D) Equals zero for daily data\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the meaning of the decay factor.\n\nIn $\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{n-1}^2$, a high $\\lambda$ (close to 1) puts most weight on the prior estimate, so volatility reacts slowly and has a long memory; a low $\\lambda$ reacts fast but is noisy. The RiskMetrics standard is $\\lambda = 0.94$ for daily data. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: $\\lambda$ lies strictly between 0 and 1.\n- C is wrong: EWMA has no long-run variance (no mean reversion) — that's a GARCH feature.\n- D is wrong: for daily data $\\lambda$ is around 0.94, not zero.\n\nAnalogy: $\\lambda$ is like the stubbornness of your opinion. High stubbornness (high λ) means new news barely moves you; low stubbornness means you flip with every headline.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Higher λ → slower, smoother, longer memory. Lower λ → faster, noisier. RiskMetrics uses λ = 0.94 daily.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"EWMA decay factor λ (0–1)\",\"items\":[\"0 low λ\",\"fast / noisy\",\"|0.94 RiskMetrics\",\"slow / smooth\",\"1 high λ\"],\"left\":\"reacts fast, short memory\",\"right\":\"reacts slow, long memory\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A GARCH(1,1) model has $\\omega = 0.000002$, $\\alpha = 0.13$, and $\\beta = 0.86$. The long-run (unconditional) daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 0.0002% B) 1.41% C) 2.00% D) 0.02%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the long-run volatility from GARCH parameters.\n\nFirst find the long-run variance:\n\n$$V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta} = \\frac{0.000002}{1 - 0.13 - 0.86} = \\frac{0.000002}{0.01} = 0.0002$$\n\nThen take the square root to get volatility:\n\n$$\\sigma_L = \\sqrt{0.0002} = 0.01414 = 1.414\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (0.0002%) reports the variance as if it were the volatility, and mislabels its scale — forgetting the square root.\n- C (2.00%) ignores the formula.\n- D (0.02%) misplaces the decimal and skips the square root.\n\nAnalogy: the long-run variance is the \"home base\" the volatility eventually drifts back to. Variance is in squared units; you must square-root it to get a volatility you can quote.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $V_L = \\omega/(1 - \\alpha - \\beta)$ gives a variance — always take the square root for the volatility. The \"forgot to square-root\" answer is a favorite trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Using the GARCH(1,1) model with $\\omega = 0.000002$, $\\alpha = 0.13$, $\\beta = 0.86$, current daily volatility 1.6%, and today's return +1%, the updated daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 1.41% B) 1.53% C) 1.60% D) 0.024%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a one-step GARCH update.\n\nThe GARCH(1,1) recursion (in variances):\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha\\, r_{n-1}^2 + \\beta\\, \\sigma_{n-1}^2$$\n\nPlug in $r_{n-1} = 0.01$ and $\\sigma_{n-1} = 0.016$:\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.000002 + 0.13 \\times (0.01)^2 + 0.86 \\times (0.016)^2$$\n\n$$= 0.000002 + 0.13 \\times 0.0001 + 0.86 \\times 0.000256$$\n\n$$= 0.000002 + 0.000013 + 0.00022016 = 0.00023516$$\n\nSquare root:\n\n$$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.00023516} = 0.01534 = 1.534\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct. Volatility falls from 1.60% toward the 1.41% long-run level because today's 1% return was small relative to the prior estimate — mean reversion at work.\n\n- A (1.41%) is the long-run volatility, not the one-step update.\n- C (1.60%) is the unchanged current volatility.\n- D (0.024%) quotes the variance figure mislabeled as a volatility (forgot the square root).\n\n📌 Exam tip: α multiplies the squared return; β multiplies the prior variance. Don't swap them, and don't forget to square-root the final variance.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Compared with the GARCH(1,1) model, the EWMA model:\nA) Includes mean reversion toward a long-run variance B) Sets $\\omega = 0$, so forecasts do not revert to a long-run level C) Requires three estimated parameters D) Weights all past observations equally\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how EWMA relates to GARCH(1,1).\n\nEWMA is a special case of GARCH(1,1) with $\\omega = 0$, $\\alpha = 1 - \\lambda$, and $\\beta = \\lambda$. Because $\\omega = 0$, there is no constant anchor, so EWMA forecasts have no long-run level to revert to — every future day's forecast equals today's estimate. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: EWMA has no mean reversion (that is precisely the GARCH addition).\n- C is wrong: EWMA uses only one parameter (λ); GARCH uses three (ω, α, β).\n- D describes the equal-weighted historical method, not EWMA, which uses geometrically declining weights.\n\nAnalogy: EWMA is like a thermometer with no thermostat — it tracks temperature but has no \"target\" to pull back toward. GARCH adds the thermostat (the long-run variance).\n\n📌 Exam tip: EWMA = GARCH with ω = 0, α = 1 − λ, β = λ. Key consequence: EWMA has no mean reversion; GARCH does.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EWMA vs GARCH(1,1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"EWMA\",\"items\":[\"one parameter (λ)\",\"ω = 0, α+β = 1\",\"no mean reversion\"]},{\"h\":\"GARCH(1,1)\",\"items\":[\"three params (ω,α,β)\",\"ω > 0\",\"reverts to long-run V_L\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Empirically observed fat tails (excess kurtosis) in daily asset returns are best explained by:\nA) Returns being conditionally fat-tailed only B) Time-varying volatility mixing normal distributions of different variances C) Positive skewness in the data D) Returns being perfectly i.i.d. normal\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the main driver of unconditional fat tails.\n\nReturns can be conditionally normal (normal each day given that day's volatility) yet unconditionally fat-tailed, because mixing normals with different variances creates excess kurtosis. Volatility clustering and regime switching produce exactly this mix of calm and turbulent periods. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: the standard story is that conditional distributions are roughly normal; it's the changing variance that produces fat tails.\n- C describes skew (asymmetry), a different feature from fat tails (kurtosis).\n- D is wrong: perfectly i.i.d. normal returns would have no excess kurtosis at all.\n\nAnalogy: blend many bell curves of different widths and the combined shape gets a sharp peak and heavy tails — even though each ingredient was a tidy bell curve.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Fat tails arise largely from a changing σ over time. This is why tracking conditional volatility (EWMA/GARCH) makes standardized returns look more normal.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Why returns are unconditionally fat-tailed\",\"steps\":[\"Each day ~ normal given that day's σ\",\"σ changes over time (clustering/regimes)\",\"Mixing normals of different variance\",\"→ excess kurtosis (fat tails)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Implied volatility differs from historical (statistical) volatility because implied volatility is:\nA) Backward-looking and based only on past returns B) Forward-looking, derived from current option prices by inverting an option pricing model C) Always lower than realized volatility D) Computed using the EWMA recursion\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts implied with statistical volatility.\n\nImplied volatility is backed out of observed option prices by inverting a model such as Black-Scholes-Merton. Because option prices reflect the market's view over the option's remaining life, implied volatility is forward-looking, unlike historical estimates that use past returns. B is correct.\n\n- A describes historical volatility, not implied.\n- C is wrong: implied volatility tends to sit above subsequent realized volatility on average (it embeds a risk premium), but it is not \"always lower.\"\n- D is wrong: EWMA is a statistical (backward-looking) method, not how implied vol is obtained.\n\nAnalogy: historical volatility is the rear-view mirror (where prices have been); implied volatility is the market's forecast through the windshield (where it expects them to go).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Implied vol = market's forward-looking view from option prices (e.g., the VIX). The volatility smile/skew shows implied vol varying by strike, violating BSM's constant-σ assumption.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Implied vs historical volatility\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Historical (statistical)\",\"items\":[\"backward-looking\",\"from past returns\",\"e.g., EWMA/GARCH\"]},{\"h\":\"Implied\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking\",\"inverted from option prices\",\"e.g., VIX\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** EWMA with $\\lambda = 0.9$: the previous daily volatility estimate is 2% and the latest return is 3%. The updated volatility is closest to:\nA) 2.10% B) 2.12% C) 2.50% D) 3.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is a straightforward EWMA update with a different λ.\n\nVariance recursion:\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{n-1}^2$$\n\nPlug in $\\lambda = 0.9$, $\\sigma_{n-1} = 0.02$, $r_{n-1} = 0.03$:\n\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.9 \\times (0.02)^2 + 0.1 \\times (0.03)^2$$\n\n$$= 0.9 \\times 0.0004 + 0.1 \\times 0.0009$$\n\n$$= 0.00036 + 0.00009 = 0.00045$$\n\nSquare root:\n\n$$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.00045} = 0.02121 = 2.121\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (2.10%) comes from linearly averaging the σs (0.9×2% + 0.1×3% = 2.10%) instead of working in variances — the classic error.\n- C (2.50%) is a plain average of 2% and 3%.\n- D (3.00%) is just the latest return.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Never average the volatilities directly. Square first, blend the variances, then square-root. Averaging σ gives a slightly-off answer planted as a distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A GARCH(1,1) model has $\\omega = 0.000003$, $\\alpha = 0.04$, and $\\beta = 0.94$. The long-run daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 1.22% B) 0.015% C) 0.039% D) 2.24%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the long-run volatility.\n\nLong-run variance:\n\n$$V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta} = \\frac{0.000003}{1 - 0.04 - 0.94} = \\frac{0.000003}{0.02} = 0.00015$$\n\nSquare root for volatility:\n\n$$\\sigma_L = \\sqrt{0.00015} = 0.01225 = 1.225\\%$$\n\nSo A is correct.\n\n- B (0.015%) reports the variance (0.00015) mislabeled and forgets the square root.\n- C (0.039%) uses a wrong denominator or skips the square root.\n- D (2.24%) doubles the correct answer or uses the wrong arithmetic.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Check that α + β < 1 (here 0.98, so stationary). Then $V_L = \\omega/(1 - \\alpha - \\beta)$, and square-root it for volatility.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** When updating a covariance matrix with EWMA, it is recommended to use the same decay factor λ for variances and covariances mainly to:\nA) Reduce computational cost B) Keep the resulting covariance matrix internally consistent (positive semi-definite) C) Make all correlations equal to 1 D) Eliminate the need to estimate volatility\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why a single λ is used across variances and covariances.\n\nCovariances update with the same machinery: $\\text{cov}_n = \\lambda\\,\\text{cov}_{n-1} + (1-\\lambda)\\,x_{n-1}y_{n-1}$, and correlation is $\\rho_n = \\text{cov}_n/(\\sigma_{x,n}\\sigma_{y,n})$. Using the same λ for every variance and covariance keeps the whole matrix consistent so it stays positive semi-definite — meaning it produces sensible (non-negative) portfolio variances. B is correct.\n\n- A is a minor side effect at best, not the main reason.\n- C is wrong and undesirable — correlations spiking to 1 in crises is a risk concern, not a goal.\n- D is wrong: you still need volatilities to form correlations.\n\nAnalogy: if you measure each side of a triangle with a different ruler, the triangle won't close properly. Using one consistent ruler (λ) keeps the covariance \"triangle\" valid.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Same λ across variances and covariances → a consistent, positive-semi-definite covariance matrix. Remember correlations tend to spike toward 1 in crises, so dynamic monitoring matters for portfolio VaR.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why one λ for variances and covariances\",\"options\":[\"A. Reduce computational cost\",\"B. Keep matrix consistent (positive semi-definite)\",\"C. Make all correlations equal 1\",\"D. Avoid estimating volatility\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A single λ keeps the covariance matrix internally consistent so portfolio variances stay non-negative.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** EWMA with λ = 0.9: previous σ = 2%/day, latest return = 3%. Updated σ is closest to:\nA) 2.10% B) 2.11% C) 2.07% D) 2.32%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** σ² = 0.9(0.0004) + 0.1(0.0009) = 0.00045 → σ = 2.121%. (A averages the σs linearly.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A GARCH(1,1) model has ω = 0.000003, α = 0.04, β = 0.94. The long-run volatility is closest to:\nA) 1.22%/day B) 0.015%/day C) 0.0387%/day D) 2.24%/day\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** V_L = 0.000003/(1−0.98) = 0.00015 → σ = 1.225%/day. (B forgets the square root.)\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Unconditional fat tails in daily returns are best explained by:\nA) Returns being conditionally fat-tailed only B) Time-varying volatility mixing normal distributions C) Positive skewness D) Stale prices\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Mixtures of normals with different variances are leptokurtic — regime-switching/clustering story.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Compared with GARCH(1,1), the EWMA model:\nA) Includes mean reversion to a long-run variance B) Sets ω = 0, so forecasts do not revert to a long-run level C) Requires three estimated parameters D) Weights all observations equally\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** EWMA is GARCH with ω = 0, α = 1−λ, β = λ; no anchor, no mean reversion.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A GARCH(1,1) model is estimated as $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.000002 + 0.08\\,r_{t-1}^2 + 0.90\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2$$. The long-run daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 0.10% B) 1.00% C) 1.41% D) 15.9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{\\omega}{1-\\alpha-\\beta} = \\frac{0.000002}{1 - 0.98} = 0.0001 \\Rightarrow \\sigma_{LR} = 1.0\\% \\text{ daily}$$\n\nDistractor D ($$1\\% \\times \\sqrt{252} \\approx 15.9\\%$$) is the annualized figure — the question asks for daily.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** Using EWMA with $$\\lambda = 0.94$$, yesterday's volatility estimate was 1.0% and yesterday's return was −3.0%. Today's updated volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 0.97% B) 1.12% C) 1.22% D) 1.48%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Work in variances (the sign of the return is irrelevant once squared):\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.94 \\times (0.010)^2 + 0.06 \\times (0.030)^2 = 0.000094 + 0.000054 = 0.000148$$\n\n$$\\sigma_t = \\sqrt{0.000148} = 1.22\\%$$\n\nDistractor B averages volatilities ($$0.94 \\times 1\\% + 0.06 \\times 3\\% = 1.12\\%$$); A drops the return term.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EWMA vs GARCH(1,1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"EWMA\",\"items\":[\"weights: λ on old var, (1−λ) on r²\",\"no long-run mean term\",\"RiskMetrics λ ≈ 0.94\"]},{\"h\":\"GARCH(1,1)\",\"items\":[\"adds constant ω → mean reversion\",\"σ²=ω+αr²+βσ²\",\"reverts to ω/(1−α−β)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A GARCH(1,1) model has $$\\omega = 0.000003$$, $$\\alpha = 0.05$$, $$\\beta = 0.92$$. Yesterday's volatility estimate was 1.0% and yesterday's return was 1.5%. Today's volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 1.00% B) 1.03% C) 1.06% D) 1.23%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_t^2 = 0.000003 + 0.05 \\times (0.015)^2 + 0.92 \\times (0.010)^2$$\n\n$$= 0.000003 + 0.00001125 + 0.000092 = 0.00010625 \\Rightarrow \\sigma_t = 1.03\\%$$\n\nThe estimate rises toward the larger recent return but only partially — persistence $$\\alpha + \\beta = 0.97$$ keeps the update gradual.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** An EWMA model uses λ = 0.94. The current variance estimate is $$\\sigma_{n-1}^2 = 0.000196$$ (i.e., σ = 1.40%/day). The latest daily return is −3.00%. The updated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 1.46% B) 1.53% C) 2.13% D) 1.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: apply the EWMA recursion in variances, then convert to volatility at the end.\n\n1. Recursion: $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{n-1}^2$$.\n2. Prior variance term: $$0.94 \\times 0.000196 = 0.00018424$$.\n3. New return term: $$r^2 = (-0.03)^2 = 0.0009$$; $$0.06 \\times 0.0009 = 0.000054$$.\n4. Sum: $$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.00018424 + 0.000054 = 0.00023824$$.\n5. Convert: $$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.00023824} = 0.01544 = 1.54\\%$$ (closest to 1.53%).\n\n- A. 1.46% linearly interpolates the volatilities ($$0.94 \\times 1.40 + 0.06 \\times 3.00 = 1.496$$) instead of working in variances.\n- C. 2.13% forgets to apply the weights (averages or over-weights the new return).\n- D. 1.40% ignores the new return entirely.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: EWMA and GARCH operate on *variances*, not volatilities. Square the return, weight the variances, sum, then square-root at the very end. Linear interpolation of σ is the most common wrong path.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A GARCH(1,1) model is estimated as ω = 0.000004, α = 0.08, β = 0.90. The long-run (unconditional) daily volatility is closest to:\nA) 0.020%/day B) 1.414%/day C) 0.0002%/day D) 0.447%/day\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the long-run volatility from GARCH parameters.\n\n1. Long-run variance: $$V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}$$.\n2. Denominator: $$1 - 0.08 - 0.90 = 0.02$$.\n3. $$V_L = \\frac{0.000004}{0.02} = 0.0002$$.\n4. Convert: $$\\sigma_L = \\sqrt{0.0002} = 0.01414 = 1.414\\%$$/day.\n\n- A. 0.020% reports $$V_L$$-related figure as a percent without square-rooting properly (or 0.0002 mislabeled).\n- C. 0.0002% reports the *variance* as if it were volatility (×100% confusion).\n- D. 0.447% mis-divides (e.g., uses $$1 - \\alpha\\beta$$ or a different denominator).\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$V_L = \\omega/(1-\\alpha-\\beta)$$ gives a *variance*; you must take its square root for volatility. Quoting the variance as \"volatility\" (forgetting the √) is the textbook trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Using the GARCH(1,1) model from Q2 (ω = 0.000004, α = 0.08, β = 0.90), the current daily volatility is 2.50% and today's return is +0.50%. The updated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 2.38% B) 2.50% C) 1.93% D) 2.55%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: apply the GARCH update, then convert to volatility, and confirm the direction of mean reversion.\n\n1. Recursion: $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha\\,r_{n-1}^2 + \\beta\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2$$.\n2. ω term: $$0.000004$$.\n3. Return term: $$r^2 = (0.005)^2 = 0.000025$$; $$\\alpha r^2 = 0.08 \\times 0.000025 = 0.000002$$.\n4. Prior variance term: $$\\sigma_{n-1}^2 = (0.025)^2 = 0.000625$$; $$\\beta\\sigma^2 = 0.90 \\times 0.000625 = 0.0005625$$.\n5. Sum: $$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.000004 + 0.000002 + 0.0005625 = 0.0005685$$.\n6. Convert: $$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.0005685} = 0.02384 = 2.38\\%$$.\n\nThe small return (0.50%) is far below the prior level, so volatility falls from 2.50% toward the 1.41% long-run level — mean reversion.\n\n- B. 2.50% assumes no change.\n- C. 1.93% swaps α and β (multiplies the small return by 0.90 and the prior variance by 0.08).\n- D. 2.55% uses σ where σ² belongs (plugs 0.025 and 0.005 without squaring) — volatility rises incorrectly.\n- A is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: α multiplies the squared *return*; β multiplies the prior *variance*. Swapping them is a favorite distractor. A small return pulls GARCH volatility *down* toward $$V_L$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A GARCH(1,1) has α = 0.06 and β = 0.92, with current daily variance 0.000400 and long-run variance $$V_L$$ = 0.000250. Using the multi-day forecast formula, the expected daily variance 10 days ahead, $$E[\\sigma_{n+10}^2]$$, is closest to:\nA) 0.000400 B) 0.000250 C) 0.000373 D) 0.000350\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: forecast GARCH variance t days ahead using mean reversion at rate (α+β).\n\n1. Multi-day formula: $$E[\\sigma_{n+t}^2] = V_L + (\\alpha+\\beta)^t\\,(\\sigma_n^2 - V_L)$$.\n2. Persistence: $$\\alpha + \\beta = 0.06 + 0.92 = 0.98$$.\n3. Decay factor for t = 10: $$0.98^{10} = e^{10\\ln 0.98} = e^{10(-0.020203)} = e^{-0.20203} = 0.8171$$.\n4. Gap from long run: $$\\sigma_n^2 - V_L = 0.000400 - 0.000250 = 0.000150$$.\n5. Decayed gap: $$0.8171 \\times 0.000150 = 0.00012256$$.\n6. Forecast: $$0.000250 + 0.00012256 = 0.00037256 \\approx 0.000373$$.\n\n- A. 0.000400 assumes no mean reversion (EWMA behavior).\n- B. 0.000250 assumes *full* reversion to $$V_L$$ (only true as $$t\\to\\infty$$).\n- D. 0.000350 over-decays (e.g., uses too small a persistence factor).\n- C is correct (partial reversion at 10 days).\n\n📌 Exam tip: GARCH forecasts decay from today's variance toward $$V_L$$ at rate $$(\\alpha+\\beta)^t$$. The current level (no reversion) and the full long-run level (infinite horizon) are both distractors — finite-horizon forecasts land *between* them.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"GARCH multi-day variance forecast\",\"items\":[\"V_L=0.000250 (t→∞)\",\"0.000373 (t=10)\",\"today=0.000400 (t=0)\"],\"left\":\"full reversion\",\"right\":\"no reversion\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Empirical daily equity returns are conditionally close to normal yet unconditionally exhibit fat tails. Which explanation is most consistent with this and with the volatility-clustering evidence?\nA) Returns are unconditionally normal but conditionally fat-tailed. B) Time-varying volatility mixes normal distributions of differing variance, producing leptokurtosis; regime switching formalizes this. C) Positive skewness alone generates the excess kurtosis. D) Stale prices fully explain the fat tails independent of volatility dynamics.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify why conditionally normal returns are unconditionally fat-tailed.\n\n1. If σ changes over time, each day's return is normal *given* that day's σ, but the blend of normals with different variances is a mixture distribution.\n2. A mixture of normals with different variances has excess kurtosis (fat tails) even though each component is normal.\n3. Regime switching formalizes this: volatility flips between high/low states; within a regime returns look normal, across regimes the blended distribution is leptokurtic. Volatility clustering is the same time-varying-σ phenomenon.\n\n- A reverses the conditioning (the stem says conditionally normal, not conditionally fat-tailed).\n- C confuses skewness with kurtosis; skew alone does not produce symmetric fat tails.\n- D overstates microstructure effects and ignores the volatility-clustering evidence.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Conditionally normal but unconditionally fat-tailed\" is the signature of *time-varying volatility* (mixture of normals / regime switching). This is the rationale for EWMA/GARCH: track conditional σ and standardized returns look nearly normal.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Conditionally normal → unconditionally fat-tailed\",\"steps\":[\"Returns ~ normal given each day's σ\",\"σ varies (clustering / regime switching)\",\"Blend of normals with differing variance\",\"→ leptokurtosis (fat tails)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Compared with the GARCH(1,1) model, the EWMA model is best described as:\nA) A GARCH(1,1) with ω > 0 that mean-reverts faster. B) A GARCH(1,1) special case with ω = 0, α = 1−λ, β = λ, so it has no long-run variance to revert to. C) A model that weights all past observations equally, like the historical estimator. D) A three-parameter model requiring estimation of ω, α, and β.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: relate EWMA to GARCH(1,1) precisely.\n\n1. GARCH(1,1): $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha r_{n-1}^2 + \\beta\\sigma_{n-1}^2$$.\n2. EWMA: $$\\sigma_n^2 = (1-\\lambda) r_{n-1}^2 + \\lambda\\sigma_{n-1}^2$$.\n3. Matching terms: $$\\omega = 0$$, $$\\alpha = 1-\\lambda$$, $$\\beta = \\lambda$$. Since $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$, the long-run variance $$V_L = \\omega/(1-\\alpha-\\beta)$$ is undefined (0/0) — there is no anchor, so EWMA does not mean-revert.\n\n- A says ω > 0 — false; EWMA has ω = 0, and it does *not* mean-revert at all.\n- C describes the equal-weight historical estimator, not EWMA (which uses geometrically declining weights).\n- D: EWMA has a *single* parameter (λ), not three.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: EWMA = GARCH with ω = 0 and α + β = 1. The defining contrast: GARCH mean-reverts to $$V_L$$; EWMA does not (flat multi-day forecasts equal to today's estimate).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"EWMA vs GARCH(1,1)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"EWMA\",\"items\":[\"one parameter (λ)\",\"ω = 0, α+β = 1\",\"V_L undefined → no reversion\"]},{\"h\":\"GARCH(1,1)\",\"items\":[\"three params (ω,α,β)\",\"ω > 0, α+β < 1\",\"mean-reverts to V_L\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A risk team updates the covariance between two assets with EWMA (λ = 0.97). Yesterday's covariance estimate was 0.00020, yesterday's return on X was +2.0%, and on Y was −1.5%. The updated covariance is closest to:\nA) 0.000185 B) 0.000203 C) 0.000585 D) 0.000194\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: apply the EWMA covariance recursion (note the cross-product can be negative).\n\n1. Recursion: $$\\text{cov}_n = \\lambda\\,\\text{cov}_{n-1} + (1-\\lambda)\\,x_{n-1}y_{n-1}$$.\n2. Prior term: $$0.97 \\times 0.00020 = 0.000194$$.\n3. Cross product: $$x\\,y = (0.02)(-0.015) = -0.0003$$; $$(1-\\lambda)\\,xy = 0.03 \\times (-0.0003) = -0.000009$$.\n4. Sum: $$\\text{cov}_n = 0.000194 + (-0.000009) = 0.000185$$.\n\n- B. 0.000203 *adds* the cross product as if positive (sign error: ignores that X up, Y down gives a negative product).\n- C. 0.000585 omits the λ weighting or mis-scales.\n- D. 0.000194 forgets the new cross-product term entirely.\n- A is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The covariance cross product $$x\\cdot y$$ keeps its sign — opposite-direction moves *reduce* covariance. Use the *same* λ for variances and covariances to keep the covariance matrix positive semi-definite.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which statement about implied volatility is correct?\nA) Implied volatility is backward-looking and computed from historical returns. B) Implied volatility is forward-looking, backed out of option prices, and tends to exceed subsequently realized volatility because it embeds risk premia. C) The volatility smile confirms BSM's assumption of constant volatility across strikes. D) The VIX measures the realized 30-day volatility of the S&P 500.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct characterization of implied volatility.\n\n1. Implied volatility is obtained by *inverting* an option-pricing model (BSM) on observed market prices — it is forward-looking (the market's view over the option's life).\n2. Because option prices embed risk premia (and supply/demand for protection), implied volatility tends to sit *above* subsequently realized volatility on average.\n3. The smile/skew (implied vol varying by strike) *violates* BSM's constant-σ assumption rather than confirming it.\n4. The VIX is *model-free implied* (not realized) 30-day volatility of S&P 500 options.\n\n- A describes statistical/historical volatility, not implied.\n- C is backwards: the smile contradicts constant-σ BSM.\n- D mislabels VIX as realized; it is implied.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Implied vol = forward-looking, from prices, usually > realized (risk premium). The smile/skew is evidence *against* BSM's flat-vol assumption. VIX = implied, not realized.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Historical vs implied volatility\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Historical\",\"items\":[\"backward-looking\",\"from past returns\",\"EWMA/GARCH\"]},{\"h\":\"Implied\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking, from prices\",\"tends to exceed realized\",\"VIX; smile contradicts BSM\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"404","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"External and Internal Credit Ratings","notes":"# 4. External and Internal Credit Ratings\n\nGARP tests the rating scales, through-the-cycle vs point-in-time philosophy, how to read and chain transition matrices, and how internal rating systems and statistical scoring models (Altman Z) complement agency ratings.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe external rating scales (Moody's, S&P, Fitch), the rating process, and what ratings measure.\n- Distinguish investment grade from non-investment grade and describe outlooks and watchlists.\n- Explain through-the-cycle (TTC) vs point-in-time (PIT) rating philosophies and rating stability.\n- Define and interpret rating transition (migration) matrices and compute multi-period transition probabilities.\n- Describe historical default and recovery evidence by rating, and rating performance (accuracy ratios, in outline).\n- Explain internal rating systems at banks, their uses, and how they differ from external ratings.\n- Describe statistical credit scoring (Altman's Z-score) and the use of market-based measures alongside ratings.\n- Explain rating-agency conflicts of interest (issuer-pays) and their role in the 2007–09 structured-products debacle.\n\n## External ratings\n\n| Grade | Moody's | S&P/Fitch | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Investment grade | Aaa, Aa, A, Baa | AAA, AA, A, BBB | Low default risk |\n| Speculative (\"junk\") | Ba, B, Caa, Ca, C | BB, B, CCC, CC, C | Material default risk |\n| Default | C/D | D | In default |\n\nModifiers (1/2/3 or +/−) create notches. The IG/HY boundary — **Baa3/BBB−** — matters because many mandates and collateral rules key off it; a downgrade across it (\"fallen angel\") triggers forced selling.\n\n- Ratings target **default probability** primarily (S&P) or **expected loss** = PD × LGD (Moody's, especially for structured products).\n- **Outlooks** (positive/stable/negative, 1–2 year view) and **watchlists** (likely action within ~90 days) signal direction without changing the rating.\n- Ratings are **ordinal** rankings of relative creditworthiness, not cardinal PDs; realized default rates per rating vary over the cycle.\n\n## Through-the-cycle vs point-in-time\n\n| | Through-the-cycle (agencies) | Point-in-time (many bank models) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Conditioning | Average/stressed conditions over a full cycle | Current conditions |\n| Stability | High — few reversals, ratings \"smoothed\" | Volatile — moves with the cycle |\n| Default-prediction accuracy short-term | Lower | Higher |\n| Procyclicality | Low | High (capital needs spike in recessions) |\n\nAgencies deliberately trade short-term accuracy for stability (investors dislike rating churn); they act when a change is unlikely to be reversed. PIT measures (KMV-style EDFs from equity markets) lead agency actions but generate noise.\n\n## Transition matrices\n\nA one-year transition matrix gives probabilities of migrating between ratings (rows = start, columns = end; rows sum to 100%, including a default column and possibly withdrawn). Properties: heavy diagonal (ratings are sticky), default probability rises monotonically as rating falls, and **rating momentum** exists empirically (a recently downgraded issuer is more likely to be downgraded again — a Markov-chain violation).\n\n### Worked example: two-year default probability\n\nOne-year transitions for a BB issuer: stay BB 80%, up to BBB 8%, down to B 10%, default 2%. For B: default probability 6%; for BBB: 0.5%. Assuming the Markov property, the two-year cumulative default probability for the BB issuer:\n\n$$PD_{2yr} = \\underbrace{0.02}_{\\text{yr 1}} + \\underbrace{0.80 \\times 0.02}_{\\text{stay BB, default yr 2}} + \\underbrace{0.10 \\times 0.06}_{\\text{to B, default}} + \\underbrace{0.08 \\times 0.005}_{\\text{to BBB, default}}$$\n\n$$= 0.02 + 0.016 + 0.006 + 0.0004 = 4.24\\%$$\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Multi-year default probabilities are **not** one-year PD × years. You must walk the matrix: condition on each surviving state. And remember cumulative PD over n years < n × annual PD for good credits only at first — the chaining handles it correctly either way.\n\n### Worked example: marginal vs cumulative PD\n\nCumulative PDs for a B-rated issuer: 5% (year 1), 11% (by year 2). The **marginal (conditional) PD in year 2** given survival through year 1:\n\n$$PD_{2|1} = \\frac{0.11 - 0.05}{1 - 0.05} = \\frac{0.06}{0.95} = 6.32\\%$$\n\nLow-rated issuers' marginal PDs typically **decline** with time (survivors improve); high-rated issuers' marginal PDs **rise** (nowhere to go but down).\n\n## Default and recovery evidence\n\n- Default rates rise sharply down the scale and are strongly cyclical (HY default rates can exceed 10% in recessions vs ~1–2% in calm years).\n- **Recovery rates** depend on seniority and collateral (senior secured loans recover most; subordinated bonds least) and are **negatively correlated with default rates** — recoveries fall exactly when defaults spike, worsening tail losses.\n- Rating performance is measured by accuracy/CAP profiles: do defaults come disproportionately from the lowest ratings? Generally yes — ratings discriminate well ordinally.\n\n## Internal ratings\n\nBanks rate borrowers internally for underwriting, pricing, limits, provisioning (IFRS 9/CECL), and — under Basel's IRB approach — regulatory capital (estimating PD, and under advanced IRB also LGD and EAD). Versus agencies:\n\n- Cover borrowers with no public ratings (SMEs, retail).\n- Often **two-dimensional**: an obligor rating (PD) plus a facility rating (LGD/EAD).\n- May be PIT, TTC, or hybrid; must be validated, replicable, and independent of the sales function.\n- Risk: rating inflation pressure from relationship managers — governance and backtesting against realized defaults are essential.\n\n## Statistical scoring: Altman's Z\n\nA discriminant function of accounting ratios; the classic public-manufacturer form:\n\n$$Z = 1.2X_1 + 1.4X_2 + 3.3X_3 + 0.6X_4 + 1.0X_5$$\n\nwith X₁ = working capital/assets, X₂ = retained earnings/assets, X₃ = EBIT/assets, X₄ = market equity/book liabilities, X₅ = sales/assets. Rough zones: Z > 3.0 safe, 1.8–3.0 gray, < 1.8 distress. Modern equivalents use logit/machine-learning models producing PDs directly; market-based measures (Merton/KMV) use equity prices. Know the *idea* — weighted ratios mapped to a default signal — not the coefficients.\n\n## Conflicts of interest\n\nThe **issuer-pays** model creates pressure to rate generously; it became acute in structured finance pre-2008, where agencies rated CDO tranches AAA using flawed correlation assumptions while earning large fees and advising on structuring (\"rating shopping\"). Post-crisis reforms: regulation of agencies (SEC/ESMA), reduced hard-wiring of ratings into regulation, disclosure of methodologies.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Exam framing: external ratings are stable TTC ordinal rankings with strong long-run discriminatory power, weak short-run timeliness, and incentive problems in issuer-pays niches. Internal ratings fill coverage and granularity gaps and feed capital models.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is the investment-grade / high-yield boundary on each scale?\n> **A:** Baa3 (Moody's) / BBB− (S&P, Fitch). A downgrade across it creates a \"fallen angel\" and can trigger forced selling.\n\n**Q2.** What do agency ratings primarily measure?\n> **A:** Default probability (S&P) or expected loss = PD × LGD (Moody's, especially structured products). Ratings are ordinal rankings of relative creditworthiness, not cardinal PDs.\n\n**Q3.** Distinguish outlooks from watchlists.\n> **A:** Outlook = positive/stable/negative, 1–2 year directional view. Watchlist = likely rating action within ~90 days. Neither changes the rating itself.\n\n**Q4.** Contrast through-the-cycle (TTC) and point-in-time (PIT) rating philosophies.\n> **A:** TTC (agencies): conditions averaged over a full cycle, stable, low procyclicality, lower short-term accuracy. PIT (many bank models): current conditions, volatile, higher short-term accuracy, high procyclicality.\n\n**Q5.** What is a rating transition (migration) matrix?\n> **A:** A matrix of probabilities of migrating between ratings over a period (rows = start, columns = end; rows sum to 100% including a default column). Properties: heavy diagonal (sticky), PD rises as rating falls, empirical rating momentum.\n\n**Q6.** Why are multi-year default probabilities not just annual PD × years?\n> **A:** You must walk the matrix, conditioning on each surviving state. Two-year PD = year-1 default + sum over each year-1 destination state of (transition prob × that state's year-2 default prob).\n\n**Q7.** A BB issuer: stay BB 80%, to BBB 8%, to B 10%, default 2%. B defaults 6%, BBB defaults 0.5%. Two-year cumulative PD?\n> **A:** $$0.02 + 0.80(0.02) + 0.10(0.06) + 0.08(0.005) = 0.02+0.016+0.006+0.0004 = 4.24\\%$$.\n\n**Q8.** Compute the marginal (conditional) year-2 PD given cumulative PDs of 5% (yr 1) and 11% (yr 2).\n> **A:** $$PD_{2|1} = \\frac{0.11-0.05}{1-0.05} = \\frac{0.06}{0.95} = 6.32\\%$$.\n\n**Q9.** How do recovery rates relate to default rates?\n> **A:** Negatively correlated — both driven by the cycle. Recoveries fall exactly when defaults spike, worsening tail losses. Recovery also depends on seniority/collateral (senior secured loans recover most).\n\n**Q10.** How do internal bank ratings differ from agency ratings?\n> **A:** Cover unrated borrowers (SMEs, retail); often two-dimensional (obligor PD rating + facility LGD/EAD rating); feed Basel IRB capital; must be validated, replicable, and independent of sales (rating-inflation risk).\n\n**Q11.** Altman's Z-score idea and the public-manufacturer form.\n> **A:** Discriminant function of accounting ratios mapped to a default signal: $$Z = 1.2X_1 + 1.4X_2 + 3.3X_3 + 0.6X_4 + 1.0X_5$$. Zones: Z > 3.0 safe, 1.8–3.0 gray, < 1.8 distress.\n\n**Q12.** What conflict of interest plagues rating agencies?\n> **A:** The issuer-pays model creates pressure to rate generously; acute in pre-2008 structured finance (AAA CDO tranches with flawed correlation assumptions, \"rating shopping\").\n\n**Q13.** Why do high-rated and low-rated issuers' marginal PDs trend differently over time?\n> **A:** Low-rated issuers' marginal PDs typically decline (survivors improve); high-rated issuers' marginal PDs rise (nowhere to go but down).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** On S&P's rating scale, the lowest rating that is still considered investment grade is:\nA) BB+ B) BBB− C) A− D) BBB+\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the investment-grade/high-yield boundary on S&P's scale.\n\nThe investment-grade floor is BBB− (equivalently Baa3 at Moody's). Anything at BB+ or below is speculative grade (\"junk\" / high yield). B is correct.\n\n- A (BB+) is the top of high yield, just below the boundary — the trap of being one notch off.\n- C (A−) is comfortably investment grade but not the lowest one.\n- D (BBB+) is investment grade but two notches above the floor.\n\nAnalogy: think of a ladder where BBB− is the lowest rung you can still stand on and be called \"investment grade.\" Step down to BB+ and you've fallen into junk.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the boundary cold: BBB− / Baa3 = lowest IG; BB+ / Ba1 = highest HY. A downgrade across it creates a \"fallen angel\" and can force selling.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"S&P rating scale (IG/HY boundary)\",\"items\":[\"AAA\",\"A\",\"|BBB−\",\"BB+\",\"CCC\",\"D\"],\"left\":\"investment grade\",\"right\":\"high yield / default\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Compared with point-in-time (PIT) measures, through-the-cycle (TTC) agency ratings are generally:\nA) More volatile and better short-term default predictors B) More stable but slower to reflect current conditions C) Based mainly on equity prices D) Updated daily\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts TTC and PIT rating philosophies.\n\nTTC ratings condition on average or stressed conditions over a full cycle, so they are smoothed and stable — agencies act only when a change is unlikely to be reversed. The trade-off is timeliness: they are slower to reflect current conditions. B is correct.\n\n- A is reversed: TTC ratings are more stable and worse (not better) short-term predictors.\n- C describes market-based PIT measures (e.g., KMV EDFs from equity prices), not TTC ratings.\n- D is wrong: TTC ratings change episodically, not daily.\n\nAnalogy: TTC ratings are like a person's overall fitness level (stable, slow to change), while PIT measures are like today's heart rate (jumps around with the moment).\n\n📌 Exam tip: TTC = stable, smoothed, low procyclicality, lower short-term accuracy. PIT = volatile, timely, procyclical. Agencies use TTC.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"TTC vs PIT ratings\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"TTC (agencies)\",\"items\":[\"stable, smoothed\",\"slow to reflect current state\",\"low procyclicality\"]},{\"h\":\"PIT (market/KMV)\",\"items\":[\"volatile, timely\",\"tracks current conditions\",\"procyclical\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A one-year transition matrix gives a BB issuer: stay BB 80%, up to BBB 8%, down to B 10%, default 2%. One-year default probabilities are 6% for B and 0.5% for BBB. Assuming the Markov property, the two-year cumulative default probability for the BB issuer is closest to:\nA) 2.00% B) 4.24% C) 6.00% D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to chain a transition matrix over two years.\n\nA BB issuer can default in year 1, or survive year 1 in some state and then default in year 2. Add up all those paths:\n\n- Default in year 1: $0.02$\n- Stay BB then default: $0.80 \\times 0.02 = 0.016$\n- Move to B then default: $0.10 \\times 0.06 = 0.006$\n- Move to BBB then default: $0.08 \\times 0.005 = 0.0004$\n\nSum:\n\n$$0.02 + 0.016 + 0.006 + 0.0004 = 0.0424 = 4.24\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (2.00%) counts only the year-1 default and ignores year 2.\n- C (6.00%) is just B's one-year PD, irrelevant to the BB chain.\n- D (4.00%) wrongly takes 2 × the year-1 PD (2% × 2), skipping the proper path conditioning.\n\nAnalogy: to find the chance of failing over two years, you must trace every route — fail now, or move to a new \"room\" first and fail from there.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Multi-year default probability ≠ annual PD × years. Walk the matrix: condition on each surviving state, then default from it.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"2-year default paths (BB issuer)\",\"root\":\"Sum all default routes = 4.24%\",\"children\":[\"Default in yr 1: 0.02\",\"Stay BB → default: 0.80×0.02\",\"To B → default: 0.10×0.06\",\"To BBB → default: 0.08×0.005\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A B-rated issuer has cumulative default probabilities of 5% by year 1 and 11% by year 2. The marginal (conditional) default probability in year 2, given survival through year 1, is closest to:\nA) 6.00% B) 6.32% C) 11.00% D) 5.50%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the conditional PD in year 2 given the issuer survived year 1.\n\nThe extra default probability accumulated in year 2 is $11\\% - 5\\% = 6\\%$. But this is \"out of\" the issuers who survived year 1 (95%), so divide by the survival probability:\n\n$$PD_{2|1} = \\frac{0.11 - 0.05}{1 - 0.05} = \\frac{0.06}{0.95} = 0.0632 = 6.32\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (6.00%) is the unconditional increase in cumulative PD, forgetting to divide by the survival probability.\n- C (11.00%) is the cumulative two-year PD, not the marginal year-2 figure.\n- D (5.50%) is an arbitrary mid-value with no correct derivation.\n\nAnalogy: \"6% of everyone\" defaulted in year 2, but the question asks \"what fraction of the survivors?\" Since fewer remain (95%), the rate among them is a bit higher (6.32%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Marginal PD in year n = (cumulative_n − cumulative_{n−1}) ÷ survival through year n−1. The trap answer skips dividing by survival.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A rating transition matrix typically shows which of the following properties?\nA) Rows sum to less than 100% because defaults are excluded B) A heavy diagonal, with default probability rising as the rating falls C) Equal probability of moving to every other rating D) Higher-rated issuers default more often than lower-rated ones\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the basic features of a transition matrix.\n\nRatings are sticky, so the largest probabilities sit on the diagonal (most issuers keep their rating). As you move to lower starting ratings, the default-column probability rises monotonically. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: rows sum to 100% and include a default column.\n- C is wrong: migrations are concentrated near the current rating, not spread equally.\n- D is reversed: lower-rated issuers default more, not higher-rated ones.\n\nAnalogy: most people stay roughly where they are year to year (heavy diagonal), and the closer you start to the edge of a cliff (low rating), the more likely you fall off (default).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Heavy diagonal + monotonic default column + rows summing to 100%. Empirically there is also \"rating momentum\" (recently downgraded issuers tend to be downgraded again), which violates the strict Markov assumption.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Typical transition matrix property\",\"options\":[\"A. Rows sum < 100% (no defaults)\",\"B. Heavy diagonal; default PD rises as rating falls\",\"C. Equal probability to every other rating\",\"D. Higher-rated default more often\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Ratings are sticky (heavy diagonal) and default probability rises monotonically for lower ratings; rows sum to 100%.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Empirically, sovereign and corporate recovery rates tend to:\nA) Stay constant across the economic cycle B) Rise when default rates rise C) Fall when default rates rise D) Depend only on the issuer's credit rating\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks about the relationship between recoveries and defaults.\n\nRecovery rates are negatively correlated with default rates: both are driven by the cycle, so recoveries fall exactly when defaults spike (in recessions). This worsens tail losses — you lose more per default precisely when there are more defaults. C is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: recoveries are strongly cyclical.\n- B is reversed.\n- D is wrong: recovery depends mainly on seniority and collateral, not the issuer's rating alone.\n\nAnalogy: a fire sale — when many assets are dumped at once (high defaults), prices crater, so recovered value per asset is lower.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Recovery and default rates move oppositely. Seniority drives recovery: senior secured loans recover most, subordinated bonds least.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Recovery vs default rates\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Default rates rise\",\"items\":[\"recession / cycle stress\",\"more issuers fail\"]},{\"h\":\"Recovery rates fall\",\"items\":[\"negatively correlated\",\"worse loss per default\",\"tail losses amplified\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Altman's Z-score model for credit assessment is best described as:\nA) A market-based measure using equity option prices B) A discriminant function combining weighted accounting ratios into a default signal C) A through-the-cycle agency rating D) A model that outputs an exact probability of default with no zones\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests what the Altman Z-score is.\n\nThe Z-score is a statistical discriminant function that combines weighted accounting ratios (working capital/assets, retained earnings/assets, EBIT/assets, market equity/book liabilities, sales/assets) into a single score, with rough zones: above 3.0 safe, 1.8–3.0 gray, below 1.8 distress. B is correct.\n\n- A describes Merton/KMV market-based models, not Altman's accounting model.\n- C is wrong: it is a statistical scoring model, not an agency rating.\n- D is wrong: the classic Z-score maps to zones, not an exact PD (modern logit/ML successors output PDs).\n\nAnalogy: it's like a credit health-score formula that mixes several lab tests (financial ratios) with fixed weights to flag whether a patient (company) is healthy, borderline, or sick.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Know the idea — weighted accounting ratios → a default signal with safe/gray/distress zones. Don't memorize the exact coefficients for Part I.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Altman Z-score zones\",\"items\":[\"<1.8 distress\",\"|1.8\",\"gray 1.8–3.0\",\"|3.0\",\">3.0 safe\"],\"left\":\"distress\",\"right\":\"safe\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Internal credit rating systems at banks differ from external agency ratings primarily because internal ratings:\nA) Are always identical to agency ratings B) Cover borrowers without public ratings and often separate obligor (PD) from facility (LGD/EAD) ratings C) Cannot be used for regulatory capital D) Are always through-the-cycle\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how internal ratings differ from agency ratings.\n\nBanks rate borrowers internally to cover names with no public rating (SMEs, retail) and often use a two-dimensional scheme: an obligor rating (PD) plus a facility rating (LGD/EAD). They may be PIT, TTC, or hybrid, and under Basel's IRB approach they feed regulatory capital. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: internal ratings are independent and need not match agencies.\n- C is wrong: under the IRB approach they are used for regulatory capital (PD, and under advanced IRB also LGD and EAD).\n- D is wrong: internal ratings can be PIT, TTC, or hybrid — not always TTC.\n\nAnalogy: an agency rating is a public report card; an internal rating is the bank's own detailed file, covering even people the agencies never grade and splitting \"how likely to fail\" from \"how much we'd lose.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Internal ratings = wider coverage + obligor/facility split + feed IRB capital. Watch for rating-inflation pressure from relationship managers; governance and backtesting are essential.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Internal vs agency ratings\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Internal (bank)\",\"items\":[\"cover unrated borrowers (SME/retail)\",\"obligor (PD) + facility (LGD/EAD)\",\"feed Basel IRB capital\"]},{\"h\":\"Agency (external)\",\"items\":[\"only public issuers\",\"single issuer rating\",\"TTC philosophy\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The \"issuer-pays\" model used by major rating agencies is criticized mainly because it:\nA) Makes ratings too volatile B) Creates a conflict of interest, pressuring agencies to rate generously C) Prevents agencies from rating sovereigns D) Requires investors to pay for every rating\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the central conflict of interest in credit ratings.\n\nUnder issuer-pays, the entity being rated pays the agency, creating an incentive to assign favorable ratings to win and keep business. This became acute in structured finance before 2008, where agencies rated CDO tranches AAA using flawed correlation assumptions while earning large fees and advising on structuring (\"rating shopping\"). B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: the conflict is about generosity/bias, not volatility (ratings are actually quite stable).\n- C is wrong: agencies do rate sovereigns.\n- D describes the older \"investor-pays\" model, the opposite arrangement.\n\nAnalogy: it's like a student paying the teacher who grades their exam — there's an obvious temptation to inflate the mark.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Issuer-pays → generosity bias, worst in structured products pre-2008. Post-crisis reforms: agency regulation (SEC/ESMA), less hard-wiring of ratings into rules, methodology disclosure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Issuer-pays criticism\",\"options\":[\"A. Makes ratings too volatile\",\"B. Conflict of interest → pressure to rate generously\",\"C. Prevents rating sovereigns\",\"D. Investors pay for every rating\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"The rated issuer pays the agency, creating an incentive to assign favorable ratings to win/keep business.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A one-year matrix shows an A-rated issuer stays A with 92%, falls to BBB with 6%, and defaults with 0.1%; a BBB issuer's one-year PD is 0.3%. Ignoring other paths, the two-year cumulative default probability for the A issuer is closest to:\nA) 0.11% B) 0.21% C) 0.29% D) 0.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to chain the matrix two years for a high-grade issuer.\n\nAdd the relevant default paths:\n\n- Default in year 1: $0.001 = 0.1\\%$\n- Stay A then default in year 2: $0.92 \\times 0.001 = 0.00092 = 0.092\\%$\n- Move to BBB then default in year 2: $0.06 \\times 0.003 = 0.00018 = 0.018\\%$\n\nSum:\n\n$$0.1\\% + 0.092\\% + 0.018\\% = 0.21\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (0.11%) forgets the survive-then-default paths, counting only year-1 default plus a fragment.\n- C (0.29%) over-counts a path or mis-multiplies.\n- D (0.40%) naively doubles a misread 0.2% annual figure.\n\nAnalogy: same idea as before — to fail over two years, you either fail in year 1 or survive (in A or BBB) and then fail. Trace each route and add.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Even for tiny PDs, do the path chaining: year-1 default + (survive in each state × that state's year-2 PD). Don't just double the annual number.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** The lowest investment-grade rating on S&P's scale is:\nA) BB+ B) BBB− C) A− D) BBB+\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** BBB− (Baa3 at Moody's) is the IG floor; BB+ is the top of high yield.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Through-the-cycle ratings, relative to point-in-time measures, are:\nA) More volatile and more accurate short-term predictors B) More stable but slower to reflect current conditions C) Based mainly on equity prices D) Updated daily\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** TTC smooths the cycle for stability, sacrificing timeliness.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A one-year matrix: A-rated issuer stays A with 92%, falls to BBB with 6%, default 0.1%; BBB one-year PD is 0.3%. Ignoring other paths, the two-year cumulative PD for the A issuer is closest to:\nA) 0.21% B) 0.29% C) 0.11% D) 0.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** 0.1% + 0.92×0.1% + 0.06×0.3% = 0.1% + 0.092% + 0.018% = 0.21%. (0.11% forgets the survive-then-default paths; 0.40% naively doubles a misread 0.2%.)\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Empirically, recovery rates tend to:\nA) Be constant across the cycle B) Rise when default rates rise C) Fall when default rates rise D) Depend only on the borrower's rating\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Default rates and recoveries are negatively correlated — both driven by the cycle.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Compared with a point-in-time (PIT) rating approach, a through-the-cycle (TTC) approach produces ratings that are:\nA) More volatile and more responsive to the credit cycle B) More stable, migrating less as macro conditions change C) Always one notch lower for speculative-grade issuers D) Based only on quantitative default models\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** TTC ratings average through the credit cycle, so they are stable and migrate less — at the cost of responsiveness. PIT ratings track current conditions and move with the cycle.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Through-the-cycle vs Point-in-time\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"TTC\",\"items\":[\"averages over the credit cycle\",\"stable, migrates less\",\"less responsive to macro\"]},{\"h\":\"PIT\",\"items\":[\"reflects current conditions\",\"more volatile, migrates more\",\"tracks the cycle\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q16.** A one-year transition matrix shows: from A — 90% stay A, 8% migrate to B, 2% default; from B — 10% migrate to A, 85% stay B, 5% default. Default is absorbing. The probability that an A-rated firm defaults within two years is closest to:\nA) 2.0% B) 4.0% C) 4.2% D) 5.8%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Sum the two-year default paths from A:\n\n$$P(\\text{default by yr 2}) = \\underbrace{0.02}_{\\text{yr 1}} + \\underbrace{0.90 \\times 0.02}_{A \\to A \\to D} + \\underbrace{0.08 \\times 0.05}_{A \\to B \\to D} = 0.02 + 0.018 + 0.004 = 4.2\\%$$\n\nDistractor B doubles the one-year PD, ignoring the migration to B (and double-counts survivors); A stops at one year.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Two-year default paths from A\",\"root\":\"Sum default paths\",\"children\":[\"Default in yr 1: 0.02\",\"A→A→D: 0.90×0.02 = 0.018\",\"A→B→D: 0.08×0.05 = 0.004\",\"Total = 4.2%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A BB-rated issuer has the following one-year transition probabilities: stay BB 82%, upgrade to BBB 7%, downgrade to B 9%, default 2%. One-year default probabilities are: BBB 0.4%, B 7%. Assuming the Markov property, the two-year cumulative default probability for the BB issuer is closest to:\nA) 2.00% B) 4.27% C) 4.00% D) 3.27%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: chain the one-year matrix to get the two-year cumulative default probability for a BB issuer.\n\n1. Year-1 default: the issuer defaults directly in year 1 with probability **0.02**.\n2. Year-2 default conditional on each surviving state reached at end of year 1:\n   - Stayed BB (82%), then defaults: $$0.82 \\times 0.02 = 0.0164$$.\n   - Moved to B (9%), then defaults: $$0.09 \\times 0.07 = 0.0063$$.\n   - Moved to BBB (7%), then defaults: $$0.07 \\times 0.004 = 0.00028$$.\n3. Sum all default paths: $$0.02 + 0.0164 + 0.0063 + 0.00028 = 0.04298 \\approx 4.27\\%$$.\n\n- A. 2.00% counts only the year-1 default (ignores year-2 paths).\n- C. 4.00% naively doubles the 2% annual rate (2% × 2 years) — wrong; you must walk the matrix.\n- D. 3.27% omits the largest year-2 path (stay-BB-then-default, 1.64%).\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Multi-year cumulative PD is *not* annual PD × years. Condition on each surviving rating at the start of year 2 and multiply by that rating's PD, then add the year-1 default. Forgetting the \"stay-in-rating-then-default\" path is the classic omission.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"2-year default paths (BB issuer)\",\"root\":\"Sum all routes = 4.27%\",\"children\":[\"Default yr 1: 0.02\",\"Stay BB → default: 0.82×0.02\",\"To B → default: 0.09×0.07\",\"To BBB → default: 0.07×0.004\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A B-rated issuer has cumulative default probabilities of 6% by the end of year 1 and 13% by the end of year 2. The marginal (conditional) probability of default during year 2, given survival through year 1, is closest to:\nA) 7.00% B) 7.45% C) 6.10% D) 13.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: convert cumulative PDs into the year-2 conditional (marginal) PD.\n\n1. Formula: $$PD_{2|1} = \\frac{\\text{Cum}PD_2 - \\text{Cum}PD_1}{1 - \\text{Cum}PD_1}$$.\n2. Numerator (incremental default in year 2): $$0.13 - 0.06 = 0.07$$.\n3. Denominator (probability of surviving year 1): $$1 - 0.06 = 0.94$$.\n4. Conditional PD: $$\\frac{0.07}{0.94} = 0.07447 \\approx 7.45\\%$$.\n\n- A. 7.00% is the *unconditional* incremental PD (numerator only), failing to condition on survival.\n- C. 6.10% mistakenly divides by $$(1+\\text{Cum}PD_1)$$ or mis-subtracts.\n- D. 13.00% reports the cumulative year-2 figure, not the marginal.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Marginal PD conditions on *survival*: divide the incremental default by (1 − cumulative PD up to the prior year). For low-rated issuers, marginal PDs typically *decline* over time (survivors improve); for high-rated, they *rise*.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An A-rated issuer's one-year transitions are: stay A 91%, downgrade to BBB 7%, downgrade to BB 1.5%, default 0.05%. One-year default probabilities: BBB 0.30%, BB 1.00%. Assuming the Markov property, the two-year cumulative default probability is closest to:\nA) 0.05% B) 0.10% C) 0.13% D) 0.31%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: chain the matrix for a high-grade issuer's two-year cumulative default probability.\n\n1. Year-1 default: **0.0005** (0.05%).\n2. Year-2 default conditional on each end-of-year-1 state:\n   - Stay A (91%), default at A rate: $$0.91 \\times 0.0005 = 0.000455$$.\n   - To BBB (7%), default at BBB rate: $$0.07 \\times 0.003 = 0.00021$$.\n   - To BB (1.5%), default at BB rate: $$0.015 \\times 0.01 = 0.00015$$.\n3. Sum: $$0.0005 + 0.000455 + 0.00021 + 0.00015 = 0.001315 = 0.13\\%$$.\n\n- A. 0.05% counts only the year-1 direct default.\n- B. 0.10% omits the migration-then-default paths (BBB and BB legs).\n- C. 0.13% correctly chains all paths.\n- D. 0.31% over-counts (e.g., uses BBB/BB PDs without weighting by transition probabilities).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Even for high grades, include all surviving-state paths. The dominant year-2 contribution here is \"stay A then default\" — small per-path but the largest because 91% stay. Direct-year-1-only (0.05%) is the trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement best distinguishes through-the-cycle (TTC) ratings from point-in-time (PIT) measures?\nA) TTC ratings are more volatile and better short-term default predictors than PIT. B) TTC ratings are smoothed over the cycle for stability, sacrificing short-term timeliness; PIT measures move with current conditions and are more procyclical. C) PIT measures are derived only from agency committee judgment, while TTC uses equity prices. D) TTC and PIT produce identical ratings in expansions but diverge only in recessions.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: contrast TTC and PIT rating philosophies.\n\n1. Agencies use TTC: ratings reflect average/stressed conditions over a full cycle, change only when a move is unlikely to reverse → high stability, low procyclicality, but lower short-term default-prediction accuracy.\n2. Bank/market PIT measures (KMV-style EDFs) reflect current conditions → volatile, more accurate short-term, but procyclical (capital needs spike in recessions).\n\n- A reverses the properties: TTC is *more stable* and a *weaker* short-term predictor.\n- C swaps the methods (PIT, e.g., KMV, uses equity prices; TTC is the smoothed agency view).\n- D is false: the two diverge across the whole cycle, not only recessions.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: TTC = stable, smoothed, low procyclicality, weak timeliness (agencies). PIT = volatile, timely, procyclical (bank models, market EDFs). Agencies deliberately trade timeliness for stability because investors dislike rating churn.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"TTC vs PIT\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"TTC (agencies)\",\"items\":[\"smoothed over cycle\",\"stable, low procyclicality\",\"weak short-term timeliness\"]},{\"h\":\"PIT (KMV/market)\",\"items\":[\"tracks current conditions\",\"volatile, procyclical\",\"accurate short-term\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement about recovery rates and default rates is correct?\nA) Recovery rates are constant across the credit cycle. B) Recovery rates are positively correlated with default rates. C) Recovery rates are negatively correlated with default rates, worsening losses in downturns. D) Recovery rates depend solely on the issuer's rating, not on seniority or collateral.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: characterize the empirical relationship between recoveries and defaults.\n\n1. Recoveries depend on seniority and collateral (senior secured recovers most; subordinated least) — so D is false.\n2. Empirically, recovery rates are *negatively* correlated with default rates: both are driven by the cycle, so when defaults spike (recessions), recoveries fall.\n3. This negative correlation amplifies tail losses precisely when many issuers default — a double blow.\n\n- A is false: recoveries are strongly cyclical.\n- B reverses the sign.\n- D ignores seniority/collateral, the primary recovery drivers.\n- C is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Defaults up → recoveries down (negative correlation, both cycle-driven). This makes downturn credit losses worse than a fixed-recovery model implies — a key reason stressed LGD assumptions are used in capital models.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Recovery vs default rates\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Default rates up\",\"items\":[\"recession / cycle stress\",\"more defaults\"]},{\"h\":\"Recovery rates down\",\"items\":[\"negatively correlated\",\"seniority/collateral drive level\",\"downturn losses worse\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Altman's Z-score for a public manufacturer is $$Z = 1.2X_1 + 1.4X_2 + 3.3X_3 + 0.6X_4 + 1.0X_5$$. A firm reports: working capital/assets = 0.10, retained earnings/assets = 0.20, EBIT/assets = 0.05, market equity/book liabilities = 0.80, sales/assets = 1.10. Its Z-score and zone are closest to:\nA) Z = 2.15; gray zone B) Z = 1.70; distress zone C) Z = 3.05; safe zone D) Z = 2.15; safe zone\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: plug the five ratios into the Z-score and classify the zone.\n\n1. Term 1: $$1.2 \\times 0.10 = 0.120$$.\n2. Term 2: $$1.4 \\times 0.20 = 0.280$$.\n3. Term 3: $$3.3 \\times 0.05 = 0.165$$.\n4. Term 4: $$0.6 \\times 0.80 = 0.480$$.\n5. Term 5: $$1.0 \\times 1.10 = 1.100$$.\n6. Sum: $$0.120 + 0.280 + 0.165 + 0.480 + 1.100 = 2.145 \\approx 2.15$$, which falls in the gray zone (1.8–3.0).\n\n- B. distress requires Z < 1.8; 2.15 is above that (and the Z is miscomputed).\n- C. safe requires Z > 3.0; not reached here.\n- D. correct Z but mislabels the zone as safe (2.15 < 3.0).\n- A is correct: gray zone.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Zones — Z > 3.0 safe, 1.8–3.0 gray, < 1.8 distress. Compute term by term and sum. The trap pairs the right Z with the wrong zone (C/D) — always map the number to the correct band.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Altman Z-score zones (Z=2.15)\",\"items\":[\"<1.8 distress\",\"|1.8\",\"2.15 gray\",\"|3.0\",\">3.0 safe\"],\"left\":\"distress\",\"right\":\"safe\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A risk officer makes four claims about internal rating systems at banks. Which is correct?\nA) Internal ratings cover only borrowers that already have public agency ratings. B) Internal ratings are often two-dimensional — an obligor rating (PD) plus a facility rating (LGD/EAD) — and feed Basel IRB capital. C) Internal ratings must always be through-the-cycle to match agencies. D) Internal ratings should be assigned by the relationship manager who originates the loan to ensure accuracy.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct statement about bank internal rating systems.\n\n1. Internal ratings cover borrowers without public ratings (SMEs, retail) — extending coverage, so A is false.\n2. They are often two-dimensional: an obligor/borrower rating (PD) and a facility rating capturing LGD and EAD. Under Basel IRB, foundation IRB estimates PD; advanced IRB also estimates LGD and EAD. So B is correct.\n3. Internal ratings may be PIT, TTC, or hybrid — not required to be TTC, so C is false.\n4. Ratings must be independent of the sales/origination function to avoid inflation pressure; having the originating relationship manager set them is exactly the governance failure to avoid — so D is false.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Internal ratings (1) fill coverage gaps agencies miss, (2) are typically two-dimensional (PD obligor rating + LGD/EAD facility rating), (3) feed IRB capital, and (4) must be independent of sales. Letting originators rate their own deals invites rating inflation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Internal rating systems\",\"options\":[\"A. Only cover publicly rated borrowers\",\"B. Two-dimensional (obligor PD + facility LGD/EAD); feed IRB\",\"C. Must always be through-the-cycle\",\"D. Set by the originating relationship manager\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Internal ratings extend coverage, split obligor (PD) from facility (LGD/EAD), and feed Basel IRB capital; they must be independent of sales.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Rating-agency conflicts of interest contributed to the 2007–09 structured-products crisis. Which description is most accurate?\nA) Agencies are paid by investors, which biases ratings downward. B) The issuer-pays model and rating shopping pressured agencies to assign generous AAA ratings to CDO tranches using flawed correlation assumptions. C) Agencies had no role in structuring and only rated finished products with no conflict. D) Post-crisis reforms increased the hard-wiring of ratings into financial regulation.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the accurate account of the issuer-pays conflict in structured finance.\n\n1. Under issuer-pays, the rated entity (issuer/arranger) pays the agency — creating pressure to rate generously to win and keep business.\n2. In structured finance pre-2008, agencies rated CDO tranches AAA using flawed (too-low) default-correlation assumptions while earning large fees and even advising on structuring (\"rating shopping\" — issuers picked the friendliest agency).\n3. Post-crisis reforms *reduced* hard-wiring of ratings into regulation, regulated agencies (SEC/ESMA), and required methodology disclosure.\n\n- A reverses the payer (it is issuer-pays, biasing *upward*).\n- C is false: agencies advised on structuring, a core conflict.\n- D reverses the reform direction (hard-wiring was *reduced*).\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Issuer-pays → upward bias; structured finance amplified it via rating shopping and flawed correlation models. Reforms *reduced* regulatory reliance on ratings. Watch for reversed-direction distractors (payer, bias sign, reform direction).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Issuer-pays conflict (2007–09)\",\"steps\":[\"Issuer/arranger pays the agency\",\"Incentive to rate generously to win business\",\"Rating shopping → friendliest agency\",\"AAA CDO tranches on flawed correlation\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"405","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Country Risk: Determinants, Measures, and Implications","notes":"# 5. Country Risk: Determinants, Measures, and Implications\n\nGARP tests the sources of country risk, why sovereigns default (including on local-currency debt), and how sovereign ratings, default spreads, and CDS spreads compare as risk measures. This is a conceptual chapter — expect \"which statement is correct\" questions.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Identify sources of country risk: economic structure, political risk, legal system, and economic maturity.\n- Describe how country risk is measured: risk services, rating agencies, and market-based measures.\n- Describe the history and patterns of sovereign default, both foreign-currency and local-currency.\n- Explain the consequences and causes of sovereign default.\n- Distinguish foreign-currency vs local-currency sovereign ratings; describe the rating process and its criticisms.\n- Compare sovereign ratings with sovereign default spreads and CDS spreads as measures of sovereign risk.\n\n## Sources of country risk\n\n| Source | What to look at |\n|---|---|\n| **Economic structure** | Growth stage and volatility; commodity/sector concentration (oil exporters live and die with prices); diversification |\n| **Political risk** | Continuity vs discontinuity: democracies deliver gradual policy change, autocracies stability-then-rupture; corruption (a stealth tax); expropriation/nationalization risk; war and internal conflict |\n| **Legal system** | Property-right protection and contract enforcement; speed and predictability of courts; weak systems depress investment and raise risk premia |\n| **Economic maturity** | Early-stage, fast-growing economies are inherently more volatile; reliance on a few exports or external capital amplifies shocks |\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Country risk is broader than sovereign default risk: it includes the risk to *any* investment in the country (equity, FDI, contracts) from these factors.\n\n## Measuring country risk\n\n1. **Risk services** (PRS/ICRG, Economist Intelligence Unit, World Bank governance indicators): composite scores blending political, economic, financial indicators. Useful for ranking; scores are not PDs, scales differ across providers, and weightings are judgmental.\n2. **Sovereign ratings** (Moody's, S&P, Fitch): explicit credit opinions on government debt.\n3. **Market-based measures:** sovereign bond default spreads and CDS spreads — continuous, forward-looking, but noisy and contaminated by liquidity and risk appetite.\n\n## Sovereign default: history and causes\n\n- Defaults have occurred in **waves** tied to global cycles: 1820s–30s (Latin America), 1930s (Depression), 1980s (Latin American bank-loan crisis), 1998 (Russia), 2001 (Argentina), 2012 (Greece — largest restructuring), 2020s (Sri Lanka, Zambia, Ghana).\n- Most defaults are on **foreign-currency debt** — the sovereign cannot print the foreign currency, and FX reserves run out.\n- **Local-currency defaults happen too** (e.g., Russia 1998, plus numerous historical cases). Why default when you can print? Because printing causes inflation/currency collapse that may be judged *more* costly than restructuring; some sovereigns also have institutional constraints (independent central bank, currency board, euro membership — Greece could not print euros).\n- Common causes: high debt/GDP and interest burden relative to revenues, revenue volatility (commodity dependence), short-maturity and foreign-currency-denominated debt structure, banking-system feedback loops, political unwillingness to pay (default is partly a *choice*).\n\n**Consequences of default:** output losses and recessions, banking crises (banks hold government bonds), loss of (or costlier) market access for a period, trade and capital-flow disruption, political fallout. Empirically, reputational exclusion is real but often shorter-lived than feared; the domestic economic damage is the larger cost.\n\n## Sovereign ratings\n\n- Assigned separately for **foreign-currency** and **local-currency** obligations; local-currency ratings are typically equal or 1–2 notches *higher* (printing press, taxing domestic holders is easier).\n- Inputs: debt ratios, fiscal and external balances, growth, institutional strength, monetary flexibility, banking-sector contingent liabilities, political stability.\n- Criticisms: **ratings lag markets** (downgrades follow spread widening), herd behavior and pro-cyclicality (clustered multi-notch downgrades in crises — Asia 1997, eurozone 2010–12), home/regional bias debates, and the same issuer-pays conflicts as corporates.\n- Upgrades are slow and incremental; downgrades cluster and cascade (sovereign ceilings drag down banks and corporates rated above the sovereign).\n\n## Market-based measures\n\n**Sovereign default spread** = sovereign's USD (or EUR) bond yield − matched-maturity risk-free (Treasury/Bund) yield.\n\n**CDS spread** = annual premium for default protection; roughly, for small spreads,\n\n$$s \\approx PD \\times LGD$$\n\nso an annual PD estimate is $$PD \\approx s / LGD$$ (e.g., 240 bp spread with 60% LGD → ≈ 4% annual risk-neutral PD).\n\n| | Ratings | Bond default spreads | CDS spreads |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Frequency | Episodic | Continuous | Continuous |\n| Timeliness | Lag | Lead ratings | Lead ratings; often lead bonds |\n| Noise | Low (too smooth) | Liquidity premia, currency mismatch | Counterparty/liquidity effects, but cleanest pure-default read |\n| Interpretation | Ordinal grade | bp spread | bp spread; risk-neutral PD ≈ s/LGD |\n\nMarket measures are **risk-neutral** — they embed risk premia and overshoot in panics; ratings are smoother but stale. Best practice uses both: ratings for the level, market spreads for the direction and early warning.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Don't equate a CDS-implied PD with a real-world PD: CDS spreads price risk aversion and liquidity on top of expected loss, so risk-neutral PDs systematically **exceed** historical default frequencies — most visibly for investment-grade sovereigns. Also remember local-currency default is possible: \"a sovereign that prints its own currency cannot default\" is a false statement GARP loves to offer.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Linkage to valuation: analysts often add a country risk premium (scaled default spread) to discount rates for emerging-market investments — know the concept, not the mechanics, for Part I.","flashcards":"**Q1.** List the four sources of country risk.\n> **A:** Economic structure (growth stage, sector/commodity concentration), political risk (continuity, corruption, expropriation, war), legal system (property rights, contract enforcement), and economic maturity (volatility of early-stage economies).\n\n**Q2.** How is country risk distinct from sovereign default risk?\n> **A:** Country risk is broader — it includes risk to any investment in the country (equity, FDI, contracts), not just risk that the government defaults on its debt.\n\n**Q3.** Three ways country risk is measured.\n> **A:** Risk services (PRS/ICRG, EIU — composite scores, not PDs), sovereign ratings (Moody's/S&P/Fitch), and market-based measures (sovereign default spreads, CDS spreads).\n\n**Q4.** Why would a sovereign default on local-currency debt rather than print money?\n> **A:** Printing causes inflation/currency collapse that may be judged more costly than restructuring; some sovereigns are institutionally barred (independent central bank, currency board, euro membership — Greece couldn't print euros).\n\n**Q5.** True or false: \"A sovereign that prints its own currency cannot default.\"\n> **A:** False — a classic exam trap. Local-currency defaults happen (e.g., Russia 1998) when monetization's cost exceeds restructuring's, or when printing is institutionally blocked.\n\n**Q6.** Common causes of sovereign default.\n> **A:** High debt/GDP and interest burden vs revenue, revenue volatility (commodity dependence), short-maturity/foreign-currency debt structure, banking-system feedback loops, and political unwillingness to pay (default is partly a choice).\n\n**Q7.** Consequences of sovereign default.\n> **A:** Output losses/recessions, banking crises (banks hold government bonds), costlier or lost market access for a period, trade/capital-flow disruption, political fallout. Domestic economic damage usually exceeds reputational exclusion costs.\n\n**Q8.** How do local-currency vs foreign-currency sovereign ratings compare?\n> **A:** Local-currency ratings are typically equal to or 1–2 notches higher, because of the printing press and easier domestic taxation. Most sovereign defaults historically are on foreign-currency debt.\n\n**Q9.** Main criticisms of sovereign ratings.\n> **A:** They lag markets (downgrades follow spread widening), herd behavior and procyclicality (clustered multi-notch downgrades in crises), home/regional bias debates, and issuer-pays-style conflicts.\n\n**Q10.** Define the sovereign default spread and the CDS spread.\n> **A:** Default spread = sovereign's USD/EUR bond yield − matched-maturity risk-free (Treasury/Bund) yield. CDS spread = annual premium for default protection.\n\n**Q11.** Approximate risk-neutral PD from a CDS spread.\n> **A:** For small spreads, $$s \\approx PD \\times LGD$$, so $$PD \\approx s/LGD$$. Example: 240 bp with 60% LGD → ≈ 4% annual risk-neutral PD.\n\n**Q12.** A 5-year CDS trades at 300 bp with LGD 75%. Implied annual PD?\n> **A:** $$PD \\approx s/LGD = 0.03/0.75 = 4.00\\%$$. (Multiplying instead of dividing gives the wrong 2.25%.)\n\n**Q13.** Why do CDS-implied (risk-neutral) PDs exceed historical default frequencies?\n> **A:** CDS spreads price risk aversion and liquidity on top of expected loss, so risk-neutral PDs systematically exceed real-world frequencies — most visibly for investment-grade sovereigns. Ratings are smoother but stale; use both.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Which of the following is the best explanation for why a sovereign might default on its local-currency (domestic) debt?\nA) It is impossible — local-currency debt is always risk-free B) Printing money to repay may be judged more costly (inflation, currency collapse) than restructuring C) Local-currency debt always has the shortest maturity D) Rating agencies require periodic local-currency defaults\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why a government that can print its own money would still default on debt in that currency.\n\nA sovereign can in principle print local currency to repay, but doing so can trigger severe inflation and a currency collapse — costs that may exceed those of restructuring the debt. Some sovereigns also face institutional constraints (an independent central bank, a currency board, or euro membership — Greece could not print euros). So default can be a choice. B is correct.\n\n- A is a false statement GARP likes to plant: local-currency default has happened (e.g., Russia 1998).\n- C is wrong: maturity structure does not force a local-currency default.\n- D is nonsense — agencies do not require defaults.\n\nAnalogy: you could pay a debt by burning down your own house for the insurance money, but if that's more damaging than negotiating with the lender, you negotiate instead.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"A sovereign that prints its own currency cannot default\" is FALSE. Default is partly a choice when monetization's inflation cost beats restructuring — or when printing is institutionally barred.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why default on local-currency debt?\",\"options\":[\"A. Impossible — always risk-free\",\"B. Printing may cost more (inflation/collapse) than restructuring\",\"C. Local-currency debt is always shortest maturity\",\"D. Agencies require periodic defaults\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Monetizing can trigger inflation/currency collapse exceeding restructuring costs, so default can be a choice (or printing is barred).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A sovereign's 5-year CDS trades at 300 basis points. Assuming a loss given default (LGD) of 75%, the implied (risk-neutral) annual default probability is closest to:\nA) 2.25% B) 4.00% C) 3.00% D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to back out a default probability from a CDS spread.\n\nFor small spreads the approximation is $s \\approx PD \\times LGD$, so:\n\n$$PD \\approx \\frac{s}{LGD}$$\n\nConvert 300 bp to a decimal: $300 \\text{ bp} = 0.03$. Then:\n\n$$PD \\approx \\frac{0.03}{0.75} = 0.04 = 4\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (2.25%) multiplies instead of divides ($0.03 \\times 0.75$) — the classic trap.\n- C (3.00%) just reads the spread as the PD, ignoring LGD.\n- D (0.75%) confuses the LGD value with the answer.\n\nAnalogy: the CDS premium covers your expected loss = how often × how much. To recover \"how often\" (PD), divide the premium by \"how much\" (LGD).\n\n📌 Exam tip: PD ≈ spread ÷ LGD (divide, never multiply). And this is a risk-neutral PD — it sits above the real-world default frequency because it includes risk premia.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Compared with sovereign credit ratings, sovereign CDS spreads are generally:\nA) Slower to react and less noisy B) Faster to react but noisier C) Identical in information content D) Updated less frequently than ratings\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question contrasts market-based CDS spreads with agency ratings.\n\nCDS spreads are continuous, forward-looking market prices, so they react quickly and tend to lead rating changes. But they are noisier — contaminated by liquidity premia, counterparty effects, and shifting risk appetite. B is correct.\n\n- A is reversed: ratings are the slow, smooth measure; CDS are fast and noisy.\n- C is wrong: they carry different information (market pricing vs ordinal opinion).\n- D is wrong: CDS update continuously, far more often than episodic ratings.\n\nAnalogy: ratings are like a doctor's annual checkup (thorough but infrequent); CDS spreads are like a fitness tracker (constant readings, but jumpy and sometimes misleading).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Market measures lead ratings but carry liquidity/risk-premium noise. Best practice: ratings for the level, market spreads for direction and early warning.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CDS spreads vs credit ratings\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"CDS spreads\",\"items\":[\"continuous, forward-looking\",\"fast — tend to lead ratings\",\"noisy (liquidity/risk premia)\"]},{\"h\":\"Ratings\",\"items\":[\"episodic, smoothed\",\"slow — lag markets\",\"low noise but stale\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Local-currency sovereign ratings are usually:\nA) Lower than foreign-currency ratings B) Equal to or higher than foreign-currency ratings C) Not assigned by major agencies D) Set equal to the rating of the country's largest bank\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how local- and foreign-currency sovereign ratings compare.\n\nA sovereign has more capacity to service local-currency debt (it can print the currency and tax domestic holders), so local-currency ratings are typically equal to or one to two notches higher than foreign-currency ratings. B is correct.\n\n- A is reversed: foreign-currency ratings are usually the same or lower, not higher.\n- C is wrong: agencies assign both kinds of ratings separately.\n- D is nonsense — the sovereign rating is not pegged to a bank's rating (if anything, the sovereign ceiling drags banks down).\n\nAnalogy: it's easier to pay a debt in a currency you control (you have the printing press) than in someone else's currency you must earn or borrow — so your \"score\" for the home-currency debt is at least as good.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Local-currency ratings ≥ foreign-currency ratings (printing press + domestic taxation). Most sovereign defaults historically are on foreign-currency debt.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Local- vs foreign-currency ratings\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Local-currency\",\"items\":[\"can print the currency\",\"can tax domestic holders\",\"rating equal or 1–2 notches higher\"]},{\"h\":\"Foreign-currency\",\"items\":[\"cannot print FX\",\"must earn/borrow FX\",\"equal or lower; most defaults here\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of the following is a recognized source of country risk?\nA) Only the level of stock-market prices B) Economic structure, political risk, the legal system, and economic maturity C) Solely the level of a country's central-bank interest rate D) The number of listed companies\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to identify the core determinants of country risk.\n\nCountry risk is driven by economic structure (growth stage, commodity/sector concentration), political risk (continuity vs rupture, corruption, expropriation, war), the legal system (property rights and contract enforcement), and economic maturity (early-stage economies are more volatile). B is correct.\n\n- A is far too narrow — stock prices are not a core determinant by themselves.\n- C is too narrow — interest rates alone don't define country risk.\n- D is irrelevant to the standard framework.\n\nAnalogy: judging a country's safety for investment is like inspecting a house: the foundation (economy), the neighborhood politics, the enforceable property deed (legal system), and how settled the area is (maturity) all matter — not just the paint color.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the four buckets: economic structure, political risk, legal system, economic maturity. Country risk is broader than sovereign default risk — it covers any investment in the country.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Sources of country risk\",\"root\":\"4 buckets\",\"children\":[\"Economic structure (concentration)\",\"Political risk (war, expropriation)\",\"Legal system (property/contracts)\",\"Economic maturity (volatility)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which statement about the history of sovereign defaults is most accurate?\nA) Sovereign defaults are randomly scattered with no pattern B) Defaults have clustered in waves tied to global economic cycles C) Only emerging markets have ever defaulted D) Local-currency defaults have never occurred\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the historical pattern of sovereign defaults.\n\nSovereign defaults have come in waves linked to global cycles: 1820s–30s Latin America, the 1930s Depression, the 1980s Latin American debt crisis, Russia 1998, Argentina 2001, Greece 2012, and recent cases (Sri Lanka, Zambia, Ghana). B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: the clustering is precisely the pattern.\n- C is wrong: even developed sovereigns (e.g., Greece) have defaulted/restructured.\n- D is wrong: local-currency defaults have occurred (e.g., Russia 1998).\n\nAnalogy: defaults are like power outages during big storms — they cluster when the broader system is stressed, not at random.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Defaults come in cycle-linked waves. Most are foreign-currency, but local-currency defaults do happen — don't fall for \"never.\"\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"Sovereign default waves\",\"steps\":[\"1820s–30s Latin America\",\"1930s Depression\",\"1980s LatAm debt crisis\",\"Russia 1998 / Argentina 2001\",\"Greece 2012\",\"Sri Lanka, Zambia, Ghana\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A sovereign default most directly tends to cause which of the following domestic consequences?\nA) Permanent, total exclusion from all capital markets forever B) Output losses, banking-system stress, and a period of costlier or lost market access C) An immediate, guaranteed economic boom D) No domestic effect whatsoever\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the consequences of sovereign default.\n\nDefault typically brings output losses and recessions, banking crises (domestic banks hold government bonds and take losses), and a period of lost or more expensive market access, plus trade/capital-flow disruption and political fallout. B is correct.\n\n- A overstates it: reputational exclusion is real but often shorter-lived than feared.\n- C is the opposite of reality.\n- D is wrong — domestic economic damage is usually the largest cost.\n\nAnalogy: defaulting is like declaring personal bankruptcy — it doesn't lock you out of all credit forever, but it hurts your finances now and makes future borrowing pricier for a while.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Biggest cost is usually the domestic damage (recession + banking stress), not permanent market exclusion. The banking feedback loop is a key channel.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Domestic consequences of default\",\"root\":\"Main costs\",\"children\":[\"Output losses / recession\",\"Banking-system stress (bond holdings)\",\"Costlier or lost market access\",\"Trade/capital disruption\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Why should a CDS-implied default probability not be treated as a real-world (historical) probability of default?\nA) CDS spreads are always too low B) CDS spreads embed risk premia and liquidity effects, so risk-neutral PDs exceed historical default frequencies C) CDS markets do not exist for sovereigns D) Real-world PDs are always higher than risk-neutral PDs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the gap between risk-neutral and real-world default probabilities.\n\nCDS spreads price not just expected loss but also investors' risk aversion and liquidity/counterparty effects. As a result, the risk-neutral PD backed out from spreads systematically exceeds the actual historical default frequency — most visibly for investment-grade sovereigns. B is correct.\n\n- A is wrong: spreads tend to be too high relative to pure expected loss, not too low.\n- C is wrong: sovereign CDS markets exist.\n- D is reversed: risk-neutral PDs are higher than real-world PDs.\n\nAnalogy: an insurance premium is more than your true expected loss — it also pays the insurer to bear risk and stay in business. The CDS spread is the same: it overstates the \"true\" default odds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Risk-neutral PD (from CDS) > real-world PD, because spreads include risk premia and liquidity. Don't equate the two on the exam.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Risk-neutral vs real-world PD\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Risk-neutral (CDS)\",\"items\":[\"backed out of spreads\",\"embeds risk premia + liquidity\",\"systematically higher\"]},{\"h\":\"Real-world\",\"items\":[\"historical default frequency\",\"no risk premium\",\"lower\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A common criticism of sovereign credit ratings is that they:\nA) Change too frequently, creating excessive noise B) Lag market signals and exhibit clustered, pro-cyclical multi-notch downgrades in crises C) Are based purely on real-time CDS spreads D) Never affect the cost of borrowing for the sovereign\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a recognized criticism of sovereign ratings.\n\nRatings tend to lag markets (downgrades follow spread widening) and show herd/pro-cyclical behavior — clustered, multi-notch downgrades during crises (Asia 1997, eurozone 2010–12). Upgrades are slow and incremental, while downgrades cluster and cascade through sovereign ceilings. B is correct.\n\n- A is the opposite — ratings are too smooth/stable, not too frequent.\n- C is wrong: ratings are agency credit opinions, not derived purely from CDS.\n- D is wrong: ratings strongly affect borrowing costs and trigger collateral/mandate effects.\n\nAnalogy: ratings are like a slow weather forecaster who announces \"storm\" only after the rain has started, and then panics with several warnings at once.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Sovereign-rating criticisms: lag markets, pro-cyclical clustered downgrades, sovereign-ceiling cascades, and the same issuer-pays conflicts as corporates.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Sovereign-rating criticism\",\"options\":[\"A. Change too frequently (too noisy)\",\"B. Lag markets; clustered pro-cyclical multi-notch downgrades\",\"C. Based purely on real-time CDS spreads\",\"D. Never affect borrowing cost\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Ratings lag spread widening and cluster multi-notch downgrades in crises, amplifying the cycle.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A sovereign's USD-denominated bond yields 6.2% while a matched-maturity U.S. Treasury yields 4.2%. The sovereign default spread is:\nA) 10.4% B) 2.0% C) 1.48% D) 4.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to compute a sovereign default spread.\n\nThe default spread is the sovereign's bond yield minus the matched-maturity risk-free (Treasury) yield:\n\n$$\\text{spread} = 6.2\\% - 4.2\\% = 2.0\\%$$\n\nSo B is correct.\n\n- A (10.4%) adds the two yields instead of subtracting.\n- C (1.48%) divides the yields (6.2/4.2 − 1) rather than subtracting.\n- D (4.2%) is just the Treasury yield, not the spread.\n\nAnalogy: the spread is the \"extra rent\" investors charge the riskier borrower above the safe benchmark — simply the difference between the two yields.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Sovereign default spread = sovereign (USD/EUR) bond yield − matched-maturity risk-free yield. It's a subtraction, and it must be in the same currency to isolate default (not currency) risk.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Which is the *best* explanation for a sovereign defaulting on local-currency debt?\nA) It is impossible — local-currency debt is risk-free B) Printing money to pay may be judged more costly (inflation, currency collapse) than restructuring C) Local-currency debt always has shorter maturity D) Rating agencies require it\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Default can be a choice when monetization's inflation cost exceeds restructuring's cost (or printing is institutionally barred).\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Compared with sovereign credit ratings, sovereign CDS spreads are generally:\nA) Slower to react and less noisy B) Faster to react and noisier C) Identical in information content D) Available for fewer sovereigns than bonds in all cases\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Market measures lead ratings but carry liquidity/risk-premium noise.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A sovereign's 5-year CDS trades at 300 bp. With an assumed LGD of 75%, the implied (risk-neutral) annual default probability is closest to:\nA) 2.25% B) 4.00% C) 3.00% D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** PD ≈ s/LGD = 0.03/0.75 = 4%. (2.25% multiplies instead of divides.)\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Local-currency sovereign ratings are usually:\nA) Lower than foreign-currency ratings B) Equal to or higher than foreign-currency ratings C) Not assigned by major agencies D) Set equal to the country's largest bank's rating\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The printing press and domestic taxation make local-currency capacity at least as strong.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** Which statement about sovereign risk is correct?\nA) A government can always avoid default on local-currency debt by printing money, so local-currency ratings are riskless B) Governments have defaulted on local-currency debt, since financing deficits by money creation carries inflationary costs they may be unwilling to bear C) Foreign-currency sovereign ratings are always higher than local-currency ratings D) High foreign-exchange reserves eliminate sovereign default risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Local-currency defaults happen (e.g., Russia 1998): monetizing debt creates inflation, currency, and political costs a government may judge worse than default. A overstates the printing-press argument; C is backwards as a general rule; D ignores willingness-to-pay.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sovereign: local vs foreign currency\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Local-currency debt\",\"items\":[\"can print money to repay\",\"but inflation/political costs\",\"default still possible (willingness)\"]},{\"h\":\"Foreign-currency debt\",\"items\":[\"cannot print foreign FX\",\"constrained by reserves\",\"usually rated lower\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A sovereign's 5-year USD bond CDS trades at 360 basis points. Assuming a loss given default of 80%, the implied (risk-neutral) annual default probability is closest to:\nA) 2.88% B) 4.50% C) 3.60% D) 0.45%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: convert a CDS spread to an implied risk-neutral annual PD using the small-spread approximation.\n\n1. Approximation: $$s \\approx PD \\times LGD$$, so $$PD \\approx \\frac{s}{LGD}$$.\n2. Spread in decimal: $$360\\text{ bp} = 0.0360$$.\n3. PD: $$\\frac{0.0360}{0.80} = 0.045 = 4.50\\%$$.\n\n- A. 2.88% *multiplies* spread by LGD ($$0.036 \\times 0.80$$) instead of dividing — the classic inversion error.\n- C. 3.60% sets PD equal to the spread (ignores LGD entirely, i.e., assumes LGD = 100%).\n- D. 0.45% mis-scales by a factor of 10 (treats 360 bp as 0.0036).\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$PD \\approx s/LGD$$ — *divide* the spread by LGD. Multiplying (or forgetting to divide) is the trap. And the result is a *risk-neutral* PD, which exceeds the real-world default frequency because it embeds risk premia and liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A sovereign borrows in its own currency, issued under domestic law, with an independent central bank that can print. Which statement is correct?\nA) The sovereign cannot default on local-currency debt because it can always print money. B) The sovereign may still default on local-currency debt if monetizing would cause inflation or currency collapse judged more costly than restructuring. C) Local-currency default is impossible only if the country uses the euro. D) Local-currency ratings must be lower than foreign-currency ratings.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct view on local-currency sovereign default.\n\n1. Printing to repay is *possible*, but it can trigger high inflation and currency collapse — costs that may exceed those of a restructuring.\n2. Default is partly a *choice*: a sovereign weighs monetization costs against restructuring costs. Historical local-currency defaults (e.g., Russia 1998) confirm it happens.\n3. Some sovereigns also *cannot* print (euro members like Greece, currency boards), removing the option entirely.\n\n- A is the false statement GARP loves: printing ability does not make default impossible.\n- C is backwards — euro membership *removes* the printing option, making default *more* likely, not the only case of possibility.\n- D is false: local-currency ratings are usually *equal to or higher* than foreign-currency ratings.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"A sovereign that prints its own currency cannot default on local-currency debt\" is a classic false statement. Default is a cost-benefit choice; monetization's inflation cost can exceed restructuring's cost, and some sovereigns cannot print at all.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Can a money-printing sovereign default locally?\",\"options\":[\"A. No — printing makes it impossible\",\"B. Yes — inflation/collapse cost may beat restructuring\",\"C. Only if it uses the euro\",\"D. Local rating must be lower than foreign\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Printing is possible but can be more costly than restructuring, so local-currency default is a choice (e.g., Russia 1998).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Comparing sovereign credit ratings, bond default spreads, and CDS spreads as measures of sovereign risk, which statement is most accurate?\nA) Ratings react faster than CDS spreads and contain less noise. B) CDS and bond spreads are continuous and tend to lead ratings, but embed liquidity and risk-premium noise; ratings are smoother but lag. C) Bond default spreads are risk-free measures with no liquidity contamination. D) CDS-implied PDs equal historical (real-world) default frequencies.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: contrast the three sovereign-risk measures on timeliness and noise.\n\n1. Ratings are episodic and smooth; they *lag* markets (downgrades follow spread widening). Low noise but stale.\n2. Bond default spreads and CDS spreads are continuous, forward-looking, and tend to *lead* ratings — but carry liquidity premia, currency mismatch, and risk-appetite noise.\n3. CDS-implied PDs are *risk-neutral* and systematically exceed real-world default frequencies (they price risk aversion + liquidity).\n\n- A reverses timeliness: ratings *lag*, markets lead.\n- C is false: bond spreads carry liquidity and currency-mismatch noise.\n- D is false: risk-neutral ≠ real-world; CDS PDs are higher.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Ratings = smooth but lagging; market spreads = timely but noisy and risk-neutral. Best practice uses both — ratings for the level, spreads for direction/early warning. \"Risk-neutral PD = historical PD\" is a guaranteed trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Ratings vs market spreads\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Ratings\",\"items\":[\"episodic, smoothed\",\"lag markets\",\"low noise but stale\"]},{\"h\":\"CDS/bond spreads\",\"items\":[\"continuous, lead ratings\",\"liquidity/risk-premium noise\",\"risk-neutral (> real-world PD)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Most sovereign defaults historically have occurred on foreign-currency debt. The single best explanation is:\nA) Foreign-currency debt always carries a higher coupon. B) The sovereign cannot print the foreign currency, so once FX reserves are exhausted it cannot pay. C) Foreign-currency debt is always issued at shorter maturities. D) Rating agencies prohibit local-currency default.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: explain why foreign-currency defaults dominate.\n\n1. A sovereign controls only its *own* currency's printing press. It cannot print USD or EUR.\n2. Foreign-currency obligations must be paid from FX reserves or earned/borrowed foreign exchange; when reserves run out and access is cut off, payment becomes impossible.\n3. By contrast, local-currency debt can (institutional constraints aside) be monetized, so default there is more often a choice than a hard constraint.\n\n- A: coupon level is not the structural reason for default frequency.\n- C: maturity structure matters for rollover risk but is not the defining reason foreign-currency defaults dominate.\n- D is fabricated; agencies do not prohibit default.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Foreign-currency default ≈ a *capacity* problem (can't print FX, reserves run dry). Local-currency default ≈ a *willingness/cost* problem (printing is possible but inflationary). This capacity-vs-willingness distinction is the heart of the chapter.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Why foreign-currency defaults dominate\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Foreign currency\",\"items\":[\"cannot print FX\",\"pay from reserves only\",\"capacity problem → most defaults\"]},{\"h\":\"Local currency\",\"items\":[\"can monetize\",\"willingness/cost problem\",\"default is a choice\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A sovereign's foreign-currency rating is BB and its local-currency rating is BBB. Which statement best explains the gap?\nA) The agency made an error; the two should always match. B) Local-currency capacity is stronger because the sovereign can print its own currency and tax domestic holders, so the local-currency rating is typically equal or 1–2 notches higher. C) Foreign-currency debt is safer, so its rating should be higher than shown. D) The local-currency rating reflects the largest domestic bank's rating.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: explain why a local-currency rating exceeds the foreign-currency rating.\n\n1. Agencies assign separate foreign- and local-currency ratings.\n2. Local-currency obligations benefit from the printing press and the ability to tax domestic holders — greater capacity to pay — so the local-currency rating is typically equal to or 1–2 notches *higher*.\n3. A two-notch gap (BBB local vs BB foreign) is entirely normal, not an error.\n\n- A is false: the ratings differ by design.\n- C reverses the relationship — foreign currency is the *riskier* obligation.\n- D is fabricated; sovereign local-currency ratings are not set to a bank's rating.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Local-currency rating ≥ foreign-currency rating (usually 1–2 notches higher) because of the printing press and domestic taxation. A higher local-currency rating is expected, not anomalous.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Local (BBB) vs foreign (BB) rating gap\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Local-currency BBB\",\"items\":[\"printing press\",\"tax domestic holders\",\"greater capacity → higher\"]},{\"h\":\"Foreign-currency BB\",\"items\":[\"must earn/borrow FX\",\"riskier obligation\",\"equal or lower\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Sovereign ratings have been criticized for procyclicality. Which behavior best illustrates this?\nA) Slow, incremental single-notch upgrades during expansions. B) Clustered multi-notch downgrades during crises (e.g., Asia 1997, eurozone 2010–12), which can deepen the crisis. C) Assigning identical ratings to all sovereigns in a region permanently. D) Refusing to assign local-currency ratings.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the procyclical sovereign-rating behavior.\n\n1. Procyclicality means rating actions amplify the cycle. Upgrades are slow and incremental; *downgrades cluster and cascade* in crises.\n2. Sharp multi-notch downgrades during stress (Asia 1997, eurozone 2010–12) raise borrowing costs and trigger forced selling exactly when the sovereign is weakest, deepening the crisis. Sovereign ceilings also drag down banks/corporates.\n\n- A describes upgrade behavior, not the procyclical (downgrade-cascade) problem.\n- C is not observed and not the criticism.\n- D is unrelated and false.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Procyclicality = clustered multi-notch downgrades in crises that worsen the downturn. Upgrades slow and small; downgrades fast and cascading (plus sovereign-ceiling spillover to banks/corporates).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sovereign-rating procyclicality\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expansions\",\"items\":[\"slow, incremental upgrades\",\"single-notch\"]},{\"h\":\"Crises\",\"items\":[\"clustered multi-notch downgrades\",\"raise borrowing cost / forced selling\",\"deepen the downturn\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which factor is correctly classified as a source of country risk under the standard framework?\nA) Commodity/sector concentration of the economy — an element of economic-structure risk. B) The risk-free rate of the United States — a legal-system risk. C) The volatility of a single firm's earnings — a political risk. D) The choice of VaR confidence level — an economic-maturity risk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: match a factor to the correct country-risk source category.\n\n1. Country-risk sources: economic structure, political risk, legal system, economic maturity.\n2. Commodity/sector concentration (e.g., an oil exporter dependent on crude prices) falls squarely under **economic structure** — concentration and lack of diversification amplify shocks.\n\n- B: the US risk-free rate is not a legal-system risk; it is a global benchmark rate, unrelated to a country's legal framework.\n- C: a single firm's earnings volatility is idiosyncratic firm risk, not political (country) risk.\n- D: VaR confidence level is a measurement choice, not a country-risk source.\n- A is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The four country-risk buckets are economic structure (diversification/concentration, growth stage), political risk (continuity, corruption, expropriation, war), legal system (property rights, contract enforcement), and economic maturity (volatility of early-stage economies). Misfiled distractors test whether you know the taxonomy.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Sources of country risk\",\"root\":\"4 buckets\",\"children\":[\"Economic structure (commodity/sector concentration)\",\"Political risk (war, expropriation, corruption)\",\"Legal system (property/contract enforcement)\",\"Economic maturity (early-stage volatility)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An analyst values an emerging-market project and adds a country risk premium to the discount rate. The CDS spread of the sovereign is 250 bp and the analyst scales it to estimate the premium. Which statement is most accurate for FRM Part I purposes?\nA) The country risk premium should be set to zero because CDS spreads are pure noise. B) A scaled sovereign default spread is commonly added to the discount rate as a country risk premium; the concept is examinable, the precise scaling mechanics are not. C) The country risk premium must equal exactly the CDS spread divided by the equity beta. D) Adding a country risk premium double-counts risk and is never appropriate.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct Part I framing of the country-risk-premium concept.\n\n1. Analysts commonly add a country risk premium — often a scaled sovereign default/CDS spread — to the discount rate for emerging-market investments.\n2. For FRM Part I, the *concept* (linking sovereign spreads to a discount-rate add-on) is what is tested, not a precise scaling formula.\n\n- A is false: spreads carry information (forward-looking risk), not pure noise.\n- C invents a precise formula that Part I does not require and is not the standard approach.\n- D is false: a country risk premium is a standard, appropriate adjustment, not double-counting.\n- B is correct.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For Part I, know that a scaled sovereign default spread can serve as a country risk premium added to discount rates — understand the *idea*, not exact mechanics. Distractors that demand precise formulas or dismiss the premium entirely are wrong.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Country risk premium (concept)\",\"steps\":[\"Take sovereign default / CDS spread\",\"Scale it (mechanics not examined)\",\"Add to discount rate as premium\",\"Concept examinable, not exact formula\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"406","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Measuring Credit Risk","notes":"# 6. Measuring Credit Risk\n\nThe quantitative core of credit risk: expected and unexpected loss, credit VaR, and the single-factor (Vasicek/Gaussian copula) model behind Basel's IRB formula. GARP reliably asks an EL calculation and frequently a WCDR/credit-VaR calculation.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain expected loss, unexpected loss, and the loss distribution of a credit portfolio.\n- Calculate EL = PD × LGD × EAD and the UL (standard deviation of loss) for a single exposure.\n- Define economic capital and explain how it relates to the loss distribution and credit VaR.\n- Describe the Gaussian copula / Vasicek single-factor model and compute the worst-case default rate (WCDR).\n- Explain how the Basel IRB capital requirement uses the single-factor model.\n- Describe the Merton model's intuition for estimating default probability from equity and asset values.\n- Explain challenges: default correlation, granularity, and the skewed nature of credit loss distributions.\n\n## The credit loss distribution\n\nCredit losses are **highly skewed**: most years small losses near EL, rarely a catastrophic tail (correlated defaults). Consequently:\n\n- **Expected loss (EL):** the mean — a *cost of doing business*, covered by pricing/provisions, not capital.\n- **Unexpected loss (UL):** dispersion around the mean — covered by **capital**.\n- **Economic capital:** the buffer covering losses up to a chosen confidence level (e.g., 99.9%) over one year, *in excess of EL*:\n\n$$\\text{Economic capital} = \\text{Credit VaR} = \\text{WCL}_{99.9\\%} - \\text{EL}$$\n\n## Expected loss\n\n$$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$\n\n- **PD:** probability of default over the horizon (1 year standard).\n- **LGD** = 1 − recovery rate: fraction of exposure lost given default.\n- **EAD:** exposure at default (drawn amount + expected drawdown of commitments).\n\nPortfolio EL is the simple sum of individual ELs — expectation is linear, **no correlation needed**.\n\n### Worked example: EL and UL for a single loan\n\n\\$10M loan: PD = 2%, recovery rate = 40% (LGD = 60%), σ_LGD = 25%.\n\n**Expected loss:**\n\n$$EL = 0.02 \\times 0.60 \\times 10\\text{M} = \\$120{,}000$$\n\n**Unexpected loss** (standard deviation of credit loss, with default as a Bernoulli so $$\\sigma_{PD}^2 = PD(1-PD) = 0.02 \\times 0.98 = 0.0196$$):\n\n$$UL = EAD \\times \\sqrt{PD \\cdot \\sigma_{LGD}^2 + LGD^2 \\cdot \\sigma_{PD}^2}$$\n\n$$= 10\\text{M} \\times \\sqrt{0.02 \\times 0.25^2 + 0.60^2 \\times 0.0196} = 10\\text{M} \\times \\sqrt{0.00125 + 0.007056}$$\n\n$$= 10\\text{M} \\times \\sqrt{0.008306} = 10\\text{M} \\times 0.09114 = \\$911{,}400$$\n\nUL is ~7.6× EL — credit risk lives in the variance, not the mean.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Portfolio UL is **not** the sum of ULs: it aggregates with default correlations, $$UL_P = \\sqrt{\\sum_i\\sum_j \\rho_{ij} UL_i UL_j}$$, and is far below the sum for low correlations. Each loan's *risk contribution* depends on its correlation with the portfolio.\n\n## The single-factor (Vasicek / Gaussian copula) model\n\nEach borrower i defaults when a standardized latent variable falls below a threshold. The latent variable loads on one **common systematic factor** F (the economy) plus an idiosyncratic shock:\n\n$$X_i = \\sqrt{\\rho}\\, F + \\sqrt{1-\\rho}\\,\\epsilon_i$$\n\nConditional on a bad realization of F at the X percentile, the default rate across a large, granular portfolio converges to the **worst-case default rate**:\n\n$$WCDR = N\\!\\left(\\frac{N^{-1}(PD) + \\sqrt{\\rho}\\,N^{-1}(X)}{\\sqrt{1-\\rho}}\\right)$$\n\n- ρ = copula (asset) correlation between borrowers; higher ρ → fatter loss tail.\n- X = confidence level (Basel uses 99.9%, $$N^{-1}(0.999) = 3.090$$).\n- With ρ = 0, WCDR = PD (idiosyncratic risk diversifies away entirely).\n\n**Credit VaR** for a granular portfolio:\n\n$$\\text{Credit VaR} = EAD \\times LGD \\times (WCDR - PD)$$\n\nBasel II/III IRB capital is exactly this (with a maturity adjustment, and ρ set by formula per asset class).\n\n### Worked example: WCDR and credit VaR\n\nA \\$100M well-diversified loan portfolio: PD = 1%, LGD = 60%, ρ = 0.1, confidence 99.9%.\n\n$$N^{-1}(0.01) = -2.326, \\quad N^{-1}(0.999) = 3.090$$\n\n$$WCDR = N\\!\\left(\\frac{-2.326 + \\sqrt{0.1}\\times 3.090}{\\sqrt{0.9}}\\right) = N\\!\\left(\\frac{-2.326 + 0.977}{0.9487}\\right) = N(-1.422) = 7.75\\%$$\n\n$$\\text{Credit VaR} = 100\\text{M} \\times 0.60 \\times (0.0775 - 0.01) = 100\\text{M} \\times 0.60 \\times 0.0675 = \\$4.05\\text{M}$$\n\nEL = 100M × 0.6 × 0.01 = \\$0.6M is provisioned; the \\$4.05M above EL is economic capital.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Two classic traps: (1) **subtract PD** — capital covers *unexpected* loss; using WCDR alone overstates it; (2) signs inside WCDR — $$N^{-1}(PD)$$ is a large **negative** number for small PDs while $$N^{-1}(0.999)$$ is +3.090. Flipping either sign gives plausible-looking wrong answers.\n\n## The Merton model (intuition)\n\nEquity is a **call option on the firm's assets** with strike equal to the face value of debt D maturing at T:\n\n- At T: equity holders receive $$\\max(V_T - D, 0)$$; debt holders receive $$\\min(V_T, D)$$ — i.e., risky debt = risk-free debt − a put on assets.\n- Default occurs if $$V_T < D$$. The risk-neutral PD is $$N(-d_2)$$ from BSM applied to asset value V, asset volatility σ_V.\n- Practical use (KMV/EDF): back out V and σ_V from observed equity value and equity volatility, compute **distance to default**\n\n$$DD = \\frac{\\ln(V/D) + (\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2)T}{\\sigma_V \\sqrt{T}}$$\n\nthen map DD to a PD (empirically, rather than via the normal CDF). Strengths: forward-looking, updates continuously with the stock price. Weaknesses: needs traded equity, assumes a simple debt structure and a single default date, normality understates tail PDs.\n\n## Putting the pieces together\n\n| Concept | Covers | Funded by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Expected loss | Mean of loss distribution | Pricing / loan-loss provisions |\n| Unexpected loss / credit VaR | Tail above the mean at 99.9% | Economic / regulatory capital |\n| Beyond 99.9% | Catastrophic residual | Shareholders' problem (default of the bank) |\n\nDrivers of tail thickness: **default correlation** (ρ), **concentration** (granularity — single-name and sector), and **LGD uncertainty** (and its adverse correlation with default rates).","flashcards":"**Q1.** Distinguish expected loss, unexpected loss, and economic capital.\n> **A:** EL = mean of the loss distribution (a cost of business, covered by pricing/provisions). UL = dispersion around the mean (covered by capital). Economic capital = credit VaR = $$\\text{WCL}_{99.9\\%} - EL$$.\n\n**Q2.** Expected loss formula.\n> **A:** $$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$, where LGD = 1 − recovery rate, EAD = exposure at default. Portfolio EL = simple sum of individual ELs (no correlation needed — expectation is linear).\n\n**Q3.** Compute EL: $25M exposure, PD 1.5%, recovery 55%.\n> **A:** LGD = 0.45, so $$EL = 0.015 \\times 0.45 \\times 25M = \\$168{,}750$$. (Using 55% as LGD is the recovery/LGD flip trap.)\n\n**Q4.** Why is portfolio UL not the sum of individual ULs?\n> **A:** It aggregates with default correlations: $$UL_P = \\sqrt{\\sum_i\\sum_j \\rho_{ij}UL_iUL_j}$$, far below the sum for low correlations. Each loan's risk contribution depends on its correlation with the portfolio.\n\n**Q5.** Write the single-factor (Vasicek/Gaussian copula) latent variable.\n> **A:** $$X_i = \\sqrt{\\rho}\\,F + \\sqrt{1-\\rho}\\,\\epsilon_i$$, where F is the common systematic factor and ε the idiosyncratic shock; default when X falls below a threshold.\n\n**Q6.** Worst-case default rate (WCDR) formula.\n> **A:** $$WCDR = N\\!\\left(\\frac{N^{-1}(PD) + \\sqrt{\\rho}\\,N^{-1}(X)}{\\sqrt{1-\\rho}}\\right)$$. Basel uses X = 99.9%, $$N^{-1}(0.999)=3.090$$. With ρ = 0, WCDR = PD.\n\n**Q7.** Credit VaR for a granular portfolio.\n> **A:** $$\\text{Credit VaR} = EAD \\times LGD \\times (WCDR - PD)$$. Subtract PD because capital covers unexpected loss only. This is the Basel IRB capital formula (plus a maturity adjustment).\n\n**Q8.** Compute WCDR: PD = 1%, ρ = 0.1, 99.9%.\n> **A:** $$N\\!\\left(\\frac{-2.326 + \\sqrt{0.1}(3.090)}{\\sqrt{0.9}}\\right) = N\\!\\left(\\frac{-1.349}{0.9487}\\right) = N(-1.422) = 7.75\\%$$.\n\n**Q9.** Then compute credit VaR: $100M, LGD 60%, PD 1%, WCDR 7.75%.\n> **A:** $$100M \\times 0.60 \\times (0.0775-0.01) = 100M \\times 0.60 \\times 0.0675 = \\$4.05M$$.\n\n**Q10.** What is the Merton model's view of equity?\n> **A:** Equity is a call option on firm assets with strike = debt face value D maturing at T. Equity holders get $$\\max(V_T-D,0)$$; debt holders get $$\\min(V_T,D)$$. Risky debt = risk-free debt − put on assets.\n\n**Q11.** Define distance to default in the Merton/KMV framework.\n> **A:** $$DD = \\frac{\\ln(V/D) + (\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2)T}{\\sigma_V\\sqrt{T}}$$. V and σ_V are backed out from observed equity value and equity volatility; DD is mapped to a PD empirically.\n\n**Q12.** Strengths and weaknesses of the Merton model.\n> **A:** Strengths: forward-looking, updates continuously with the stock price. Weaknesses: needs traded equity, assumes a simple debt structure and single default date, normality understates tail PDs.\n\n**Q13.** What drives the thickness of the credit loss tail?\n> **A:** Default correlation (ρ), concentration/granularity (single-name and sector), and LGD uncertainty (and its adverse correlation with default rates). Credit losses are highly skewed.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A bank has a $5,000,000 loan to a company. The probability of default (PD) over one year is 3%, and if the company defaults the bank expects to lose 50% of the exposure (LGD = 50%). What is the expected loss (EL)?\nA) $25,000 B) $75,000 C) $150,000 D) $250,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for expected loss, which is the average loss the bank expects on this loan over one year.\n\nThe formula is:\n\n$$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n- PD = 0.03\n- LGD = 0.50\n- EAD (exposure at default) = $5,000,000\n\nStep by step:\n\n$$EL = 0.03 \\times 0.50 \\times 5{,}000{,}000$$\n\nFirst, $$0.03 \\times 0.50 = 0.015$$.\n\nThen, $$0.015 \\times 5{,}000{,}000 = 75{,}000$$.\n\nSo EL = $75,000. Think of it like this: there is only a 3% chance things go wrong, and even then you only lose half, so the \"average\" expected loss is small compared to the full loan.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($25,000): This used PD x EAD but forgot... actually it comes from multiplying wrong; it ignores part of the calculation and is too small.\n- C ($150,000): This forgot to multiply by LGD (used PD x EAD = 0.03 x 5M = 150,000). Trap: forgetting LGD.\n- D ($250,000): This used LGD x EAD without PD (0.50 x 5M = 2.5M is even bigger; 250,000 mixes up factors). Trap: dropping PD.\n\n📌 Exam tip: EL is always the product of all three: PD x LGD x EAD. If any factor is missing, your answer is wrong.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected loss build-up\",\"steps\":[\"PD = 0.03 (chance of default)\",\"× LGD = 0.50 (fraction lost)\",\"× EAD = $5,000,000 (exposure)\",\"= EL = $75,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A $25,000,000 exposure has a probability of default of 1.5% and an expected recovery rate of 55%. The expected loss is closest to:\nA) $84,375 B) $168,750 C) $206,250 D) $375,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question gives a recovery rate, not LGD directly, so first find LGD.\n\nRecovery rate = 55%, so:\n\n$$LGD = 1 - \\text{recovery rate} = 1 - 0.55 = 0.45$$\n\nNow apply the formula:\n\n$$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD = 0.015 \\times 0.45 \\times 25{,}000{,}000$$\n\nStep by step:\n- $$0.015 \\times 0.45 = 0.00675$$\n- $$0.00675 \\times 25{,}000{,}000 = 168{,}750$$\n\nSo EL = $168,750.\n\nAnalogy: If 55% of the loan comes back even after default, you only truly lose 45% of it. That 45% is your LGD.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($84,375): Used PD = 0.0075 (halved PD by mistake) or similar slip; too small.\n- C ($206,250): Used LGD = 0.55 (the recovery rate) instead of 0.45. This is the classic recovery/LGD flip trap.\n- D ($375,000): Forgot to subtract recovery; used LGD = 1.0 effectively, or dropped a factor.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Watch the wording. If they give you a recovery rate, LGD = 1 - recovery rate. Never plug the recovery rate straight into the EL formula.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Recovery to LGD to EL\",\"steps\":[\"Recovery = 55%\",\"LGD = 1 - 0.55 = 0.45\",\"EL = PD × LGD × EAD\",\"= 0.015 × 0.45 × 25M = $168,750\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which statement best describes the difference between expected loss (EL) and unexpected loss (UL) for a credit portfolio?\nA) EL is covered by capital; UL is covered by loan pricing. B) EL is the average (mean) loss, covered by pricing/provisions; UL is the variation around that mean, covered by capital. C) EL and UL are the same thing measured in different units. D) UL is always smaller than EL.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is about what EL and UL mean and who pays for each.\n\nEL is the mean (average) loss. Because you can predict it on average, you simply build it into the interest rate you charge and set aside loan-loss provisions. It is a normal cost of doing business.\n\nUL is how much actual losses can swing above the average in a bad year. Because it is uncertain, you hold capital as a cushion to absorb it.\n\nAnalogy: A pizza shop knows on average it wastes 2 pizzas a day (EL) and prices that into the menu. But on a freak day it might waste 15 (UL) - it keeps savings (capital) to survive that bad day.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Reversed. Pricing covers EL; capital covers UL.\n- C: They are genuinely different concepts (mean vs dispersion), not the same thing in different units.\n- D: False. In credit, UL is typically much larger than EL because credit losses are skewed (our notes showed UL was about 7.6x EL in one example).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the pairing - EL goes with pricing/provisions; UL (and credit VaR) goes with capital.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Expected vs Unexpected loss\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected loss (EL)\",\"items\":[\"mean / average loss\",\"predictable\",\"covered by pricing & provisions\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected loss (UL)\",\"items\":[\"swing above the mean\",\"uncertain\",\"covered by capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Economic capital for credit risk is best described as:\nA) Expected loss only B) The 99.9% worst-case loss minus expected loss C) EAD multiplied by LGD D) Expected loss plus unexpected loss combined\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the definition of economic capital.\n\nEconomic capital is the buffer a bank holds to survive unexpectedly bad years, measured up to a high confidence level (Basel uses 99.9%) over one year, and only for losses ABOVE the expected loss:\n\n$$\\text{Economic capital} = \\text{Credit VaR} = \\text{WCL}_{99.9\\%} - \\text{EL}$$\n\nWe subtract EL because EL is already paid for through pricing and provisions; capital only needs to cover the surprise part.\n\nAnalogy: You already budget for your normal monthly bills (EL). Your emergency fund (capital) only needs to cover the shocking extra costs beyond what you already budgeted.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: EL is provisioned, not held as capital.\n- C: EAD x LGD is the loss if default happens with full certainty - it ignores probability and confidence levels entirely.\n- D: We do NOT add EL; we subtract it. Capital is for unexpected loss only.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The word \"minus EL\" is the giveaway. Economic capital = worst-case loss minus expected loss.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Loss distribution partition\",\"items\":[\"EL (provisions)\",\"|\",\"Economic capital = WCL99.9% - EL\"],\"left\":\"mean loss\",\"right\":\"99.9% worst-case loss\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In the Vasicek single-factor model, if the asset correlation between borrowers is zero (rho = 0), the 99.9% worst-case default rate (WCDR) equals:\nA) Zero B) The probability of default (PD) C) 99.9% D) N(3.090)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests what happens to the worst-case default rate when borrowers are completely uncorrelated.\n\nThe WCDR formula is:\n\n$$WCDR = N\\!\\left(\\frac{N^{-1}(PD) + \\sqrt{\\rho}\\,N^{-1}(X)}{\\sqrt{1-\\rho}}\\right)$$\n\nSet rho = 0. Then $$\\sqrt{\\rho} = 0$$ and $$\\sqrt{1-\\rho} = 1$$. The formula becomes:\n\n$$WCDR = N\\!\\left(\\frac{N^{-1}(PD) + 0}{1}\\right) = N\\big(N^{-1}(PD)\\big) = PD$$\n\nWhen borrowers share no common risk, the bad luck in a big diversified portfolio averages out (idiosyncratic risk diversifies away), so the worst-case default rate is just the ordinary PD.\n\nAnalogy: If everyone's chance of getting sick is independent, then in a huge crowd the fraction sick on any day is steady and equal to the individual probability - no scary clustering.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (Zero): There is still default risk; it doesn't vanish, it just equals PD.\n- C (99.9%): That's the confidence level, not a default rate.\n- D (N(3.090)): That's about 99.9%, not the answer.\n\n📌 Exam tip: rho = 0 means full diversification, so WCDR collapses to PD. Higher rho fattens the tail and pushes WCDR above PD.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Asset correlation drives WCDR\",\"items\":[\"WCDR = PD\",\"|\",\"WCDR >> PD\"],\"left\":\"rho = 0 (fully diversified)\",\"right\":\"high rho (fat tail)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A $100,000,000 well-diversified loan portfolio has PD = 1%, LGD = 60%, and asset correlation rho = 0.1, at the 99.9% confidence level the WCDR = 7.75%. The credit VaR (economic capital) is closest to:\nA) $4.65 million B) $4.05 million C) $0.60 million D) $0.45 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for credit VaR given the worst-case default rate.\n\nThe formula is:\n\n$$\\text{Credit VaR} = EAD \\times LGD \\times (WCDR - PD)$$\n\nPlug in:\n- EAD = $100,000,000\n- LGD = 0.60\n- WCDR = 0.0775\n- PD = 0.01\n\nStep by step:\n- $$WCDR - PD = 0.0775 - 0.01 = 0.0675$$\n- $$100{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.60 = 60{,}000{,}000$$\n- $$60{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0675 = 4{,}050{,}000$$\n\nSo credit VaR = $4.05 million.\n\nWe subtract PD because the expected loss part is already provisioned; capital covers only the excess.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($4.65M): Forgot to subtract PD (used WCDR alone: 100M x 0.6 x 0.0775 = 4.65M). This is the classic \"don't subtract PD\" trap, which overstates capital.\n- C ($0.60M): This is the expected loss (100M x 0.6 x 0.01), not credit VaR.\n- D ($0.45M): Mixed up factors / used wrong rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Credit VaR uses (WCDR - PD). If you forget to subtract PD you'll get a too-large, but plausible-looking, wrong answer - exactly the trap GARP sets.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Credit VaR (economic capital)\",\"steps\":[\"WCDR - PD = 0.0775 - 0.01 = 0.0675\",\"× LGD = 0.60\",\"× EAD = $100M\",\"= $4.05M\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In the Merton model, the equity of a firm is viewed as equivalent to:\nA) A put option on the firm's assets B) A call option on the firm's assets, with strike equal to the face value of debt C) Risk-free debt plus a put option D) A forward contract on the firm's assets\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question tests the core intuition of the Merton model.\n\nIn Merton's view, shareholders own the company but owe the debt. At maturity, if the firm's assets $$V_T$$ exceed the debt face value $$D$$, shareholders keep the leftover $$V_T - D$$. If assets are worth less than the debt, shareholders walk away with nothing (limited liability). So their payoff is:\n\n$$\\max(V_T - D, 0)$$\n\nThat is exactly the payoff of a CALL option on the firm's assets with strike price = D (the debt face value).\n\nAnalogy: Owning equity is like having an option to \"buy back\" the company from the lenders by paying off the debt. You only exercise (pay the debt and keep the firm) if the firm is worth more than what you owe.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: A put on assets describes the lenders' implicit position (risky debt = risk-free debt minus a put), not the equity holders'.\n- C: Risk-free debt plus a put doesn't match equity's payoff; it relates to the debt side.\n- D: A forward has unlimited downside (linear payoff). Equity has limited downside (you can't lose more than your investment) - that's option-like, not forward-like.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Equity = call on assets (strike = debt). Risky debt = risk-free debt minus a put on assets. Memorize both halves.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Merton: who holds what\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Equity holders\",\"items\":[\"payoff max(V-D, 0)\",\"= CALL on assets, strike D\",\"limited downside\"]},{\"h\":\"Debt holders\",\"items\":[\"risk-free debt - put on assets\",\"short an implicit put\",\"capped upside\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Which of the following correctly describes how portfolio expected loss and portfolio unexpected loss aggregate across individual loans?\nA) Both are simple sums of the individual loan values. B) EL is a simple sum of individual ELs; UL is NOT a simple sum because it depends on default correlations. C) EL depends on correlations; UL is a simple sum. D) Neither depends on the individual loans.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question is about adding up EL and UL across many loans.\n\nExpected loss is an average (an expectation). Averages always add up directly, no matter how the loans relate to each other. So portfolio EL = sum of each loan's EL.\n\nUnexpected loss is about variation (standard deviation). Variations do NOT simply add - they combine according to how correlated the losses are:\n\n$$UL_P = \\sqrt{\\sum_i\\sum_j \\rho_{ij}\\, UL_i\\, UL_j}$$\n\nIf correlations are low, the portfolio UL is much smaller than the sum of individual ULs (diversification benefit).\n\nAnalogy: If you add up everyone's average spending, you just sum it (EL). But the \"swinginess\" of total spending (UL) depends on whether people's spending moves together - if they spend independently, the ups and downs partly cancel out.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: UL is not a simple sum, so this is wrong.\n- C: Reversed - EL is the simple sum, UL is the correlation-dependent one.\n- D: Both clearly depend on the individual loans.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expectations add (EL is linear); standard deviations don't (UL needs correlations). This is why diversification helps with UL but never with EL.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Aggregating across loans\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Portfolio EL\",\"items\":[\"simple sum of each loan's EL\",\"linear\",\"no correlation needed\"]},{\"h\":\"Portfolio UL\",\"items\":[\"NOT a simple sum\",\"depends on default correlations\",\"diversifies when rho low\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Credit loss distributions are typically described as \"highly skewed.\" This means:\nA) Losses are equally likely to be above or below the mean. B) Most years have small losses near the EL, with rare but very large losses in the tail. C) Losses follow a perfectly symmetric normal distribution. D) The expected loss equals the unexpected loss.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what \"skewed\" means for credit losses.\n\nA skewed credit loss distribution means the shape is lopsided: in most years losses are small (close to the expected loss), but every so often - usually when many borrowers default together in a downturn - there is a huge loss in the right-hand tail.\n\nAnalogy: Think of earthquakes. Most days nothing happens (tiny losses), but rarely a massive quake causes enormous damage. The \"average\" day looks calm, yet the rare disaster dominates how much insurance you need.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: That describes a symmetric distribution, the opposite of skewed.\n- C: A normal distribution is symmetric, not skewed - credit losses are not normal.\n- D: EL and UL being equal has nothing to do with skewness; in credit, UL is usually much larger than EL.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Skewness in credit losses is WHY banks need so much capital - the rare fat tail (driven by correlated defaults) is far bigger than the everyday losses.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What 'skewed' means\",\"options\":[\"Equally likely above/below mean\",\"Most years small losses, rare huge tail losses\",\"Symmetric normal distribution\",\"EL equals UL\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Lopsided shape: small losses most years, rare catastrophic tail from correlated defaults.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which of the following is a recognized weakness of the Merton model for estimating default probability?\nA) It is backward-looking and never updates. B) It assumes asset returns are normally distributed, which can understate the probability of extreme (tail) defaults. C) It cannot be applied to any publicly traded firm. D) It requires no information about the firm's debt.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a genuine weakness of the Merton model.\n\nThe Merton model relies on the Black-Scholes-Merton option framework, which assumes asset values move with normally distributed returns. Real defaults cluster and asset values can crash more sharply than a normal distribution predicts, so the normality assumption tends to understate the chance of extreme defaults (the tail). Other weaknesses: it needs traded equity and assumes a simple single-maturity debt structure.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: This is actually a STRENGTH, not a weakness - the model is forward-looking and updates continuously as the stock price moves. So this statement is false.\n- C: False. It can be applied to publicly traded firms; in fact it REQUIRES traded equity to back out asset value and volatility. The limitation is the opposite - it struggles with firms that are NOT publicly traded.\n- D: False. The model very much needs the firm's debt level (the strike D) to compute distance to default.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Merton's strengths are \"forward-looking, updates with the stock price.\" Its weaknesses are \"needs traded equity, simple debt assumption, normality understates the tail.\" Don't confuse the two.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Merton model: strengths vs weaknesses\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Strengths\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking\",\"updates with stock price\"]},{\"h\":\"Weaknesses\",\"items\":[\"needs traded equity\",\"simple single-maturity debt\",\"normality understates tail defaults\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A \\$25M exposure has PD 1.5% and recovery 55%. EL is closest to:\nA) \\$168,750 B) \\$206,250 C) \\$375,000 D) \\$84,375\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** EL = 0.015 × 0.45 × 25M = \\$168,750. (B uses LGD = 55% — recovery/LGD flip.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In the Vasicek model with ρ = 0, the 99.9% WCDR equals:\nA) 0 B) The PD C) N(3.090) D) 99.9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** No systematic factor → conditional default rate equals unconditional PD; idiosyncratic risk diversifies away.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Economic capital for credit risk is best described as:\nA) EL plus UL B) The 99.9% worst-case loss minus expected loss C) Expected loss D) EAD × LGD\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Capital absorbs unexpected loss; EL is provisioned.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In the Merton framework, shareholders' position is equivalent to:\nA) A put on the firm's assets B) A call on the firm's assets struck at the debt face value C) Risk-free debt plus a call D) A forward on the firm's assets\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Equity = call on assets, strike D; risky debt = risk-free debt − put.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A loan has EAD of $10 million, PD of 2%, and recovery rate of 40%. The expected loss is closest to:\nA) $80,000 B) $120,000 C) $200,000 D) $1,200,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** LGD $$= 1 - 0.40 = 0.60$$:\n\n$$EL = 10\\text{m} \\times 0.02 \\times 0.60 = \\$120{,}000$$\n\nDistractor A multiplies by the recovery rate instead of LGD; C omits LGD entirely.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q16.** For the same loan (EAD $10 million, PD = 2%, LGD = 60%), assume the standard deviation of LGD is 25% and LGD and default are independent. Using $$UL = EAD\\sqrt{PD\\,\\sigma_{LGD}^2 + LGD^2\\,\\sigma_{PD}^2}$$ with $$\\sigma_{PD}^2 = PD(1-PD)$$, the unexpected loss is closest to:\nA) $0.35 million B) $0.84 million C) $0.91 million D) $1.20 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\sigma_{PD}^2 = 0.02 \\times 0.98 = 0.0196$$\n\n$$UL = 10\\text{m} \\times \\sqrt{0.02 \\times (0.25)^2 + (0.60)^2 \\times 0.0196} = 10\\text{m} \\times \\sqrt{0.00125 + 0.007056}$$\n\n$$= 10\\text{m} \\times \\sqrt{0.008306} = 10\\text{m} \\times 0.0911 = \\$0.91\\text{m}$$\n\nUL dwarfs EL ($0.12m) — that gap is what capital must cover. Distractor A keeps only the LGD-volatility term; B keeps only the default-volatility term.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Expected vs Unexpected Loss\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected Loss\",\"items\":[\"EAD×PD×LGD = $0.12m\",\"priced into the spread\",\"covered by provisions\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected Loss\",\"items\":[\"volatility of loss = $0.91m\",\"driven by σ_PD and σ_LGD\",\"covered by economic capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A bank holds a single $40 million corporate loan with a 1-year probability of default of 3%, an expected recovery rate of 35%, and exposure at default equal to the full face amount. Which value is closest to the correct one-year expected loss?\nA) $420,000 B) $780,000 C) $1,200,000 D) $1,560,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute one-year expected loss for the single loan.\n\n1. Formula: $$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$\n2. LGD = 1 − recovery rate = 1 − 0.35 = 0.65.\n3. Plug in: $$EL = 0.03 \\times 0.65 \\times \\$40{,}000{,}000$$\n4. Compute: $$0.03 \\times 0.65 = 0.0195$$, then $$0.0195 \\times \\$40{,}000{,}000 = \\$780{,}000.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: it uses LGD = 65%, the fraction actually lost given default.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($420,000) uses LGD = 35% — confuses the recovery rate with LGD (the classic recovery/LGD flip): $$0.03 \\times 0.35 \\times 40\\text{M} = 420{,}000.$$\n- C ($1,200,000) uses LGD = 100%, ignoring recovery entirely: $$0.03 \\times 1.0 \\times 40\\text{M} = 1.2\\text{M}.$$\n- D ($1,560,000) doubles the correct figure (e.g., misreads PD as 6%).\n\n📌 Exam tip: LGD = 1 − recovery. The single most common EL error is plugging the recovery rate where LGD belongs. The stem gave you a recovery rate, not an LGD — always convert.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Recovery to LGD to EL\",\"steps\":[\"Recovery = 35%\",\"LGD = 1 - 0.35 = 0.65\",\"EL = PD × LGD × EAD\",\"= 0.03 × 0.65 × 40M = $780,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A $20 million exposure has PD = 4% (treated as Bernoulli), an LGD with mean 50% and standard deviation 30%, and EAD equal to the full $20 million. The unexpected loss (standard deviation of the credit loss) is closest to:\nA) $400,000 B) $1,200,000 C) $2,298,000 D) $5,000,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: compute the unexpected loss (UL) — the standard deviation of loss for a single exposure.\n\n1. Formula: $$UL = EAD \\times \\sqrt{PD \\cdot \\sigma_{LGD}^2 + LGD^2 \\cdot \\sigma_{PD}^2}$$\n2. Default variance (Bernoulli): $$\\sigma_{PD}^2 = PD(1-PD) = 0.04 \\times 0.96 = 0.0384.$$\n3. First term: $$PD \\cdot \\sigma_{LGD}^2 = 0.04 \\times 0.30^2 = 0.04 \\times 0.09 = 0.0036.$$\n4. Second term: $$LGD^2 \\cdot \\sigma_{PD}^2 = 0.50^2 \\times 0.0384 = 0.25 \\times 0.0384 = 0.0096.$$\n5. Sum inside the root: $$0.0036 + 0.0096 = 0.0132.$$\n6. Square root: $$\\sqrt{0.0132} = 0.11489.$$\n7. Multiply by EAD: $$\\$20{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.11489 = \\$2{,}297{,}800 \\approx \\$2.30\\text{M}.$$\n\nWhy C is correct: UL captures both sources of variance — uncertainty over whether default occurs and uncertainty over the loss given default.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($400,000) is just EL = $$0.04 \\times 0.50 \\times 20\\text{M}$$ — confuses UL with EL.\n- B ($1,200,000) keeps only the first term, $$EAD \\times \\sqrt{PD\\cdot\\sigma_{LGD}^2} = 20\\text{M}\\times\\sqrt{0.0036}=20\\text{M}\\times0.06=1.2\\text{M}$$ — it omits the dominant $$LGD^2\\sigma_{PD}^2$$ piece (default-occurrence uncertainty).\n- D ($5,000,000) ignores PD and computes $$EAD \\times LGD/2$$, treating the loss as nearly certain.\n\n📌 Exam tip: UL has two variance pieces — PD uncertainty (Bernoulli, $$PD(1-PD)$$) and LGD uncertainty. If the problem gives a σ for LGD, both terms belong. UL is typically several times larger than EL.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Two variance pieces in UL\",\"root\":\"UL = EAD × sqrt(sum)\",\"children\":[\"PD × sigma_LGD^2 (LGD uncertainty)\",\"LGD^2 × PD(1-PD) (default uncertainty)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A well-diversified, granular loan portfolio has PD = 1% and asset (copula) correlation ρ = 0.20. Using the Vasicek single-factor model at the 99.9% confidence level, with $$N^{-1}(0.01) = -2.326$$ and $$N^{-1}(0.999) = 3.090$$, the worst-case default rate (WCDR) is closest to:\nA) 1.00% B) 7.75% C) 14.56% D) 22.45%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: compute the 99.9% WCDR under the single-factor (Vasicek) model.\n\n1. Formula: $$WCDR = N\\!\\left(\\frac{N^{-1}(PD) + \\sqrt{\\rho}\\,N^{-1}(0.999)}{\\sqrt{1-\\rho}}\\right)$$\n2. $$\\sqrt{\\rho} = \\sqrt{0.20} = 0.4472$$; $$\\sqrt{1-\\rho} = \\sqrt{0.80} = 0.8944.$$\n3. Numerator: $$-2.326 + 0.4472 \\times 3.090 = -2.326 + 1.382 = -0.944.$$\n4. Divide: $$\\frac{-0.944}{0.8944} = -1.0555.$$\n5. Apply the normal CDF: $$N(-1.0555) \\approx 0.1456 = 14.56\\%.$$\n\nWhy C is correct: signs handled properly — $$N^{-1}(PD)$$ negative, $$N^{-1}(0.999)$$ positive — and division by $$\\sqrt{1-\\rho}.$$\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (1.00%) is the unconditional PD — correct only if ρ = 0; here ρ = 0.20, so systematic risk does not diversify away.\n- B (7.75%) is the WCDR for ρ = 0.10, not 0.20 — using the wrong correlation.\n- D (22.45%) flips a sign (e.g., divides by $$\\sqrt{\\rho}$$ instead of $$\\sqrt{1-\\rho}$$), inflating the argument.\n\n📌 Exam tip: WCDR climbs with ρ. With ρ = 0 it collapses to PD (full diversification); higher ρ fattens the tail. Keep signs straight and divide by $$\\sqrt{1-\\rho}.$$\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"WCDR rises with correlation\",\"items\":[\"WCDR = PD (1%)\",\"rho=0.10 → 7.75%\",\"|rho=0.20 → 14.56%\"],\"left\":\"rho = 0 (diversified)\",\"right\":\"higher rho (fat tail)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Continuing from Q3 (WCDR = 14.56%, PD = 1%), the portfolio has EAD = $300 million and LGD = 60%. The one-year 99.9% credit VaR (economic capital) is closest to:\nA) $1.80 million B) $24.41 million C) $26.21 million D) $43.68 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: convert the WCDR into credit VaR (capital above expected loss).\n\n1. Formula: $$\\text{Credit VaR} = EAD \\times LGD \\times (WCDR - PD)$$\n2. Plug in: $$\\$300\\text{M} \\times 0.60 \\times (0.1456 - 0.01)$$\n3. Inner term: $$0.1456 - 0.01 = 0.1356.$$\n4. Compute: $$300\\text{M} \\times 0.60 = 180\\text{M}$$; $$180\\text{M} \\times 0.1356 = \\$24.41\\text{M}.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: capital covers *unexpected* loss, so we subtract PD (the EL already provisioned).\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($1.80M) is the expected loss, $$300\\text{M}\\times0.60\\times0.01$$ — that is provisioned, not capital.\n- C ($26.21M) forgets to subtract PD: $$300\\text{M}\\times0.60\\times0.1456$$ — overstates capital (the #1 trap).\n- D ($43.68M) uses $$WCDR - PD$$ but with WCDR for a higher ρ, or double-counts LGD.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Credit VaR uses $$(WCDR - PD)$$, not WCDR alone. EL is a cost of doing business (provisioned); capital absorbs the gap between the 99.9% loss and EL.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"WCDR to credit VaR\",\"steps\":[\"WCDR - PD = 0.1456 - 0.01 = 0.1356\",\"× LGD = 0.60\",\"× EAD = $300M\",\"= $24.41M\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Two loans each have UL = $1,000,000. The default correlation between them is ρ = 0.25. The portfolio unexpected loss is closest to:\nA) $2,000,000 B) $1,581,000 C) $1,118,000 D) $500,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: aggregate two equal ULs given a default correlation.\n\n1. Formula: $$UL_P = \\sqrt{UL_1^2 + UL_2^2 + 2\\rho\\,UL_1 UL_2}$$\n2. Plug in (in millions): $$\\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2 + 2(0.25)(1)(1)}$$\n3. Inside: $$1 + 1 + 0.5 = 2.5.$$\n4. Root: $$\\sqrt{2.5} = 1.5811 \\Rightarrow \\$1{,}581{,}000.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: with imperfect correlation (ρ < 1), portfolio UL sits below the simple sum because risks partially offset.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($2,000,000) is the simple sum of ULs — valid only if ρ = 1 (perfect correlation); expectation adds linearly but standard deviations do not.\n- C ($1,118,000) subtracts the correlation term instead of adding it: $$\\sqrt{1 + 1 - 0.5} = \\sqrt{1.25} = 1.118$$ — positive correlation must *add* risk, not remove it.\n- D ($500,000) averages the ULs — no statistical basis.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ELs add linearly (no correlation needed); ULs do NOT. Portfolio UL uses the variance-covariance form and lies between the larger single UL and the simple sum, depending on ρ.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Portfolio UL depends on correlation\",\"items\":[\"max single UL $1.0M\",\"rho=0.25 → $1.581M\",\"|sum $2.0M\"],\"left\":\"rho = -1 (offset)\",\"right\":\"rho = 1 (simple sum)\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** In the Merton model, a firm's assets are worth V = $120 million today, with asset volatility σ_V = 25% and expected asset return μ = 10%. Debt with face value D = $100 million matures in T = 1 year. Using $$DD = \\dfrac{\\ln(V/D) + (\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2)T}{\\sigma_V\\sqrt{T}}$$, the distance to default is closest to:\nA) 0.73 B) 1.00 C) 1.30 D) 1.96\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the distance to default (DD).\n\n1. $$\\ln(V/D) = \\ln(120/100) = \\ln(1.20) = 0.18232.$$\n2. Drift term: $$\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2 = 0.10 - 0.25^2/2 = 0.10 - 0.03125 = 0.06875.$$\n3. Numerator: $$0.18232 + 0.06875 \\times 1 = 0.25107.$$\n4. Denominator: $$\\sigma_V\\sqrt{T} = 0.25 \\times \\sqrt{1} = 0.25.$$\n5. DD: $$\\dfrac{0.25107}{0.25} = 1.004 \\approx 1.00.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: all terms combined with the correct drift adjustment $$(\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2).$$\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (0.73) drops the drift term entirely: $$0.18232/0.25 = 0.729$$ — omits $$(\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2)T.$$\n- C (1.30) adds $$+\\sigma_V^2/2$$ instead of subtracting it (wrong sign on the variance correction).\n- D (1.96) uses $$\\mu$$ without the $$-\\sigma_V^2/2$$ adjustment and mis-scales.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The DD numerator subtracts half the variance, $$(\\mu - \\sigma_V^2/2)$$ — the same lognormal drift correction as in $$d_2$$ of Black-Scholes. Higher DD = safer firm; PD is obtained by mapping DD to historical default rates (KMV/EDF), not via $$N(-DD)$$ alone in practice.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Distance to default\",\"steps\":[\"ln(V/D) = ln(1.20) = 0.18232\",\"drift (mu - sigma^2/2) = 0.06875\",\"numerator = 0.25107; denom = 0.25\",\"DD = 1.00 (higher = safer)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which statement about the credit loss distribution and economic capital is correct?\nA) The credit loss distribution is symmetric, so VaR and expected loss roughly coincide. B) Economic capital equals expected loss plus the 99.9% worst-case loss. C) Economic capital equals the 99.9% worst-case loss minus expected loss, because EL is covered by pricing and provisions. D) Lowering the asset correlation ρ thickens the loss-distribution tail and raises economic capital.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: identify the correct conceptual statement.\n\nWhy C is correct: the loss distribution is highly skewed; EL (the mean) is a cost of doing business covered by loan pricing and provisions, while capital absorbs *unexpected* loss — the distance from the 99.9% quantile down to EL. So $$\\text{Economic capital} = \\text{WCL}_{99.9\\%} - EL.$$\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is false — credit loss distributions are strongly right-skewed (most years near EL, rare catastrophic tail from correlated defaults).\n- B double-counts EL by adding it instead of subtracting it; capital is *in excess of* EL.\n- D reverses the correlation effect — *higher* ρ thickens the tail and raises capital; lower ρ diversifies idiosyncratic risk away.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the partition — EL → provisions/pricing; UL (up to 99.9%) → capital; beyond 99.9% → shareholders'/the bank's failure. Tail thickness is driven up by correlation, concentration (low granularity), and adverse LGD-default correlation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Loss distribution partition\",\"items\":[\"EL (provisions/pricing)\",\"|economic capital = WCL99.9% - EL\",\"beyond 99.9% (failure)\"],\"left\":\"mean loss\",\"right\":\"99.9% tail\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"407","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Operational Risk","notes":"# 7. Operational Risk\n\nGARP tests the Basel definition and event taxonomy, the frequency/severity modeling split, the evolution of capital approaches culminating in the Standardized Measurement Approach (SMA), and the management toolkit (RCSA, KRIs, scenario analysis).\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Define operational risk (Basel) and describe the seven event-type categories, including cyber, compliance, and rogue-trader risk.\n- Explain how op-risk losses are modeled via frequency and severity distributions (loss distribution approach).\n- Describe the Basel capital approaches: Basic Indicator, Standardized, AMA, and the current SMA (business indicator and internal loss multiplier).\n- Explain the use of scenario analysis, external data, and business environment factors, especially for tail events.\n- Describe risk and control self-assessment (RCSA), key risk indicators (KRIs), and loss data collection.\n- Explain moral hazard/adverse incentives and the allocation of op-risk capital to business lines.\n\n## Definition and taxonomy\n\nBasel: *the risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, and systems, or from external events*. **Includes legal risk; excludes strategic and reputational risk.**\n\nSeven Basel event types:\n\n| Event type | Example |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Internal fraud | **Rogue trader** (Barings/Leeson, SocGén/Kerviel), embezzlement |\n| 2. External fraud | **Cyber attacks**, theft, forgery |\n| 3. Employment practices & workplace safety | Discrimination claims |\n| 4. Clients, products & business practices | **Compliance failures**: mis-selling, money-laundering fines, market manipulation (largest dollar losses historically) |\n| 5. Damage to physical assets | Natural disasters, terrorism |\n| 6. Business disruption & system failures | IT outages |\n| 7. Execution, delivery & process management | Settlement errors, data entry (highest frequency, low severity) |\n\n- **High-frequency/low-severity (HFLS)** losses (processing errors) are budgetable, near EL.\n- **Low-frequency/high-severity (LFHS)** losses (rogue trader, massive fines) drive the capital tail.\n- **Cyber risk** spans external fraud and business disruption; growing, fat-tailed, and hard to model with internal data alone. **Compliance/conduct risk** sits in type 4 and produced the biggest post-2008 losses (multi-billion fines).\n\n## Modeling: frequency × severity\n\nThe loss distribution approach (LDA) builds annual aggregate loss as a compound distribution:\n\n- **Frequency** (number of losses per year): typically **Poisson** with intensity λ.\n- **Severity** (size of each loss): fat-tailed, typically **lognormal** (or extreme-value distributions for the tail).\n\nCombine by Monte Carlo: draw N ~ Poisson(λ), draw N severities, sum; repeat to get the aggregate annual loss distribution. Capital = 99.9% quantile − expected loss (or the full quantile, per regulation).\n\n$$E[\\text{annual loss}] = \\lambda \\times E[\\text{severity}]$$\n\n### Worked example: expected aggregate loss\n\nA business line suffers operational losses with Poisson frequency λ = 12 per year; severity is lognormal with mean \\$80,000.\n\n$$E[\\text{loss}] = 12 \\times 80{,}000 = \\$960{,}000 \\text{ per year}$$\n\nNote what this *doesn't* tell you: the 99.9% tail depends on severity's tail (the lognormal σ), which dominates capital. Two units with identical EL can need very different capital.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Data problems: internal loss data is short and truncated at reporting thresholds; **external data** (consortium, e.g., ORX) needs scaling for firm size; **scenario analysis** fills the tail where data are absent; **BEICFs** (business environment and internal control factors) adjust for current control quality. These were the four AMA inputs.\n\n## Basel capital approaches\n\n| Approach | Capital basis | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Basic Indicator (BIA)** | 15% of average annual gross income | Legacy |\n| **Standardized (TSA)** | 12–18% (β) of gross income by business line | Legacy |\n| **AMA** | Internal model (LDA + scenarios), 99.9%/1yr | Withdrawn — too much variance across banks, gaming |\n| **SMA** (current, Basel III final) | Formula: see below | Standard since 2023 |\n\n**SMA in outline:**\n\n$$\\text{Capital} = BIC \\times ILM$$\n\n- **Business Indicator (BI):** a size/income proxy (interest, services, and financial components).\n- **BIC:** BI × marginal coefficients (~12%/15%/18% across three size buckets) — bigger banks, higher marginal rate.\n- **Internal Loss Multiplier (ILM):** scales capital up (down) if the bank's 10-year average historical losses are high (low) relative to BIC:\n\n$$ILM = \\ln\\!\\left(e - 1 + \\left(\\frac{LC}{BIC}\\right)^{0.8}\\right), \\quad LC = 15 \\times \\text{average annual losses}$$\n\nILM = 1 when LC = BIC; national discretion may set ILM = 1. Know the *structure* (size-based charge scaled by loss experience), not coefficient details.\n\n### Worked example: SMA direction\n\nBank A and Bank B have the same BIC of \\$2.0B. Bank A's loss component LC = BIC (average annual op losses in line with peers) → ILM = ln(e − 1 + 1) = ln(e) = 1, capital = \\$2.0B. Bank B's LC = 2 × BIC → ILM = ln(e − 1 + 2^{0.8}) = ln(1.718 + 1.741) = ln(3.459) = 1.241 → capital = \\$2.48B. Worse loss history → ~24% more capital.\n\n## Management toolkit\n\n- **RCSA (risk and control self-assessment):** business units periodically identify risks, rate likelihood/impact, and assess control effectiveness. Strength: ownership and coverage of un-materialized risks. Weakness: subjectivity, self-serving bias.\n- **KRIs (key risk indicators):** forward-looking metrics tied to risk drivers — staff turnover, system downtime, unreconciled trades, failed settlements, audit findings — with thresholds triggering escalation.\n- **Loss data collection:** comprehensive internal event database (amount, type, business line, near-misses).\n- **Scenario analysis:** structured expert workshops quantifying plausible LFHS events (\"what would a 1-in-40-year cyber breach cost?\") — essential where data is sparse; vulnerable to anchoring and overconfidence biases.\n- **Mitigation:** controls, segregation of duties, limits, business-continuity planning, and **insurance** (transfers severity; beware basis risk, payout delays, counterparty risk; Basel caps insurance offsets).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Trap: operational risk is **not** reduced by holding capital — capital *absorbs* losses; controls *reduce* them. Also, allocating op-risk capital to business lines by loss history alone creates **moral hazard** (units hide losses) — allocation should reward good control environments (lower capital → incentive to improve controls). And remember the definition boundary: strategic and reputational risks are *excluded* from Basel op risk; legal risk is *included*.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Rogue-trader pattern recognition: unsupervised star trader, no segregation between front and back office, unexplained profits, never takes holidays. \"Profitable rule-breaking punished leniently\" breeds the next disaster — discipline must be outcome-independent.","flashcards":"**Q1.** State the Basel definition of operational risk and its boundary.\n> **A:** The risk of loss from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, and systems, or from external events. Includes legal risk; excludes strategic and reputational risk.\n\n**Q2.** Name the seven Basel operational-risk event types.\n> **A:** Internal fraud; external fraud; employment practices & workplace safety; clients, products & business practices; damage to physical assets; business disruption & system failures; execution, delivery & process management.\n\n**Q3.** Contrast HFLS and LFHS operational losses.\n> **A:** High-frequency/low-severity (processing/settlement errors) are budgetable, near EL. Low-frequency/high-severity (rogue trader, massive fines) drive the capital tail.\n\n**Q4.** Which event type produced the largest dollar losses post-2008?\n> **A:** Clients, products & business practices (type 4) — compliance/conduct failures: mis-selling, money-laundering fines, market manipulation (multi-billion fines).\n\n**Q5.** How does the loss distribution approach (LDA) model losses?\n> **A:** As a compound distribution: frequency (number of losses) × severity (size). Combined by Monte Carlo to get the aggregate annual loss distribution. Capital = 99.9% quantile (minus EL, per regulation).\n\n**Q6.** What distributions are typically used for frequency and severity?\n> **A:** Frequency: Poisson (intensity λ). Severity: lognormal (or extreme-value distributions for the tail). Expected annual loss = $$\\lambda \\times E[\\text{severity}]$$.\n\n**Q7.** Compute expected annual loss: λ = 12, mean severity $80,000.\n> **A:** $$12 \\times 80{,}000 = \\$960{,}000$$ per year. Note: the 99.9% tail depends on severity's tail (lognormal σ), which dominates capital.\n\n**Q8.** What were the four AMA inputs?\n> **A:** Internal loss data, external data (consortium, e.g., ORX — needs scaling), scenario analysis (fills the tail), and BEICFs (business environment and internal control factors).\n\n**Q9.** Name the Basel op-risk capital approaches and the current standard.\n> **A:** Basic Indicator (BIA), Standardized (TSA), AMA (withdrawn — too much variance/gaming), and the current SMA (Standardized Measurement Approach, since 2023).\n\n**Q10.** What is the SMA capital structure?\n> **A:** $$\\text{Capital} = BIC \\times ILM$$. BIC = Business Indicator × marginal coefficients (~12/15/18% by size bucket). ILM scales capital by 10-year historical loss experience; ILM = 1 when loss component = BIC.\n\n**Q11.** Distinguish RCSA, KRIs, and scenario analysis.\n> **A:** RCSA = units self-assess risks and control effectiveness (subjective, self-serving bias). KRIs = forward-looking metrics (turnover, downtime, unreconciled trades) with escalation thresholds. Scenario analysis = expert workshops quantifying plausible LFHS events.\n\n**Q12.** Does holding capital reduce operational risk?\n> **A:** No — capital absorbs losses; controls reduce them. Trap: op risk is not lowered by holding more capital.\n\n**Q13.** What moral hazard arises from allocating op-risk capital by loss history alone?\n> **A:** Units hide losses to lower their capital. Allocation should reward good control environments so lower capital incentivizes better controls.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** Under the Basel definition, operational risk is the risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, and systems, or from external events. Which of the following is EXCLUDED from this definition?\nA) Legal risk B) Cyber-fraud losses C) Strategic and reputational risk D) Rogue-trader losses\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks which risk is NOT part of Basel operational risk.\n\nBasel explicitly INCLUDES legal risk but EXCLUDES strategic risk (bad business decisions) and reputational risk (damage to the firm's image). So the answer is C.\n\nAnalogy: Operational risk is about things \"breaking\" in the day-to-day machinery - a fraud, a system crash, a flood. Choosing the wrong business strategy or losing customer trust is a different kind of problem, so Basel leaves those out of the operational-risk box.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Legal risk is explicitly INCLUDED, so it is not excluded.\n- B: Cyber-fraud is an operational loss (external fraud / business disruption), so it is included.\n- D: Rogue-trader losses are internal fraud - a classic operational risk event - so they are included.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the boundary - legal risk IN; strategic and reputational risk OUT.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Basel operational risk boundary\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"INCLUDED\",\"items\":[\"legal risk\",\"internal/external fraud\",\"systems failures\",\"external events\"]},{\"h\":\"EXCLUDED\",\"items\":[\"strategic risk\",\"reputational risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the loss distribution approach (LDA), the frequency and severity of operational losses are most commonly modeled using which two distributions?\nA) Normal frequency and normal severity B) Poisson frequency and lognormal severity C) Binomial frequency and uniform severity D) Lognormal frequency and Poisson severity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which standard distributions are used for the two building blocks of op-risk modeling.\n\n- Frequency = how MANY losses happen in a year. This is a count, and counts are typically modeled with the Poisson distribution (with intensity lambda).\n- Severity = how BIG each loss is. Loss sizes are fat-tailed (a few enormous losses), so they are typically modeled with the lognormal distribution.\n\nYou then combine them (often by Monte Carlo) to get the total annual loss.\n\nAnalogy: Frequency is \"how many hailstones fall\" (a count - Poisson); severity is \"how big each hailstone is\" (a size that can occasionally be huge - lognormal).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Normal isn't used - it has thin tails and can give negative loss counts/sizes, which makes no sense.\n- C: Binomial/uniform are not the standard choices; uniform severity wrongly says all loss sizes are equally likely.\n- D: This swaps the two around - frequency should be Poisson (a count), not lognormal.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Remember the pairing - Poisson for COUNT (frequency), lognormal for SIZE (severity).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"LDA building blocks\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Frequency\",\"items\":[\"how MANY losses/year\",\"a count\",\"Poisson (intensity lambda)\"]},{\"h\":\"Severity\",\"items\":[\"how BIG each loss\",\"fat-tailed sizes\",\"lognormal\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A business line has operational losses with Poisson frequency lambda = 12 per year and a severity distribution with a mean of $80,000 per loss. The expected aggregate annual loss is:\nA) $80,000 B) $240,000 C) $960,000 D) $6,667\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for the average total loss per year.\n\nThe expected aggregate loss is simply the average number of losses times the average size of each loss:\n\n$$E[\\text{annual loss}] = \\lambda \\times E[\\text{severity}]$$\n\nPlug in:\n- lambda = 12 losses per year\n- mean severity = $80,000\n\n$$E[\\text{annual loss}] = 12 \\times 80{,}000 = 960{,}000$$\n\nSo the expected annual loss is $960,000.\n\nAnalogy: If you typically drop 12 plates a year, and each broken plate costs $80,000 to replace, you expect to lose 12 x $80,000 = $960,000 a year.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($80,000): That's the cost of just one loss, ignoring frequency.\n- B ($240,000): Used 3 x 80,000 or some wrong count - not 12.\n- D ($6,667): Divided instead of multiplied (80,000 / 12) - a units mistake.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected aggregate loss = frequency x average severity. But remember: this average does NOT tell you the capital - capital depends on the tail (the severity distribution's spread), not the mean.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected aggregate annual loss\",\"steps\":[\"frequency lambda = 12 / year\",\"× mean severity = $80,000\",\"= 12 × 80,000\",\"= $960,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under the current Standardized Measurement Approach (SMA), operational risk capital is calculated as:\nA) 15% of average annual gross income B) The Business Indicator Component (BIC) multiplied by the Internal Loss Multiplier (ILM) C) A 99.9% internal VaR model output D) A fixed percentage of total assets\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the structure of the current Basel SMA capital formula.\n\nThe SMA capital is:\n\n$$\\text{Capital} = BIC \\times ILM$$\n\n- BIC (Business Indicator Component): a size/income-based charge - bigger banks face a higher marginal rate.\n- ILM (Internal Loss Multiplier): scales capital up or down based on the bank's own 10-year loss history.\n\nSo a bank's size sets the base charge, and its loss experience adjusts it.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (15% of gross income): That's the legacy Basic Indicator Approach (BIA), not the current SMA.\n- C (99.9% internal VaR): That's the AMA, which was WITHDRAWN because banks' models varied too much and could be gamed.\n- D (% of total assets): Not how op-risk capital is set; the SMA uses income-based indicators, not total assets.\n\n📌 Exam tip: SMA = BIC x ILM. Know the structure (size-based charge, scaled by loss experience), not the exact coefficients.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"SMA capital structure\",\"root\":\"Capital = BIC × ILM\",\"children\":[\"BIC: size/income-based charge\",\"ILM: scales by 10-yr loss history\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Bank A and Bank B both have a Business Indicator Component (BIC) of $2.0 billion. Bank A has a loss component LC equal to its BIC, giving an Internal Loss Multiplier (ILM) of 1. Bank B has a worse loss history with a higher ILM of 1.241. What does this tell you about their capital?\nA) Both banks hold $2.0 billion. B) Bank B holds more capital ($2.48 billion) because of its worse loss history. C) Bank A holds more capital because its BIC is higher. D) Loss history has no effect on capital under the SMA.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how loss history changes capital under the SMA.\n\nCapital = BIC x ILM.\n\n- Bank A: $2.0B x 1 = $2.0 billion.\n- Bank B: $2.0B x 1.241 = $2.482 billion (about $2.48 billion).\n\nBecause Bank B has a worse historical loss record, its ILM is above 1, so it must hold more capital - about 24% more than Bank A even though they are the same size.\n\nAnalogy: Two drivers with identical cars (same BIC). The one with more past accidents (worse loss history) pays a higher insurance premium (more capital).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Only true if both had ILM = 1, but Bank B's ILM is 1.241.\n- C: Their BICs are equal ($2.0B each), so this is false; and a higher ILM means MORE capital, which belongs to Bank B.\n- D: False - the whole point of the ILM is that loss history DOES affect capital.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ILM > 1 means worse-than-peer losses and MORE capital; ILM < 1 means better losses and LESS capital. ILM = 1 when LC = BIC.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Same BIC, different loss history\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bank A (ILM = 1)\",\"items\":[\"LC = BIC\",\"$2.0B × 1\",\"= $2.0B capital\"]},{\"h\":\"Bank B (ILM = 1.241)\",\"items\":[\"worse loss history\",\"$2.0B × 1.241\",\"= $2.48B capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which of the following is a Key Risk Indicator (KRI) rather than a realized loss event?\nA) A $2 million settlement failure B) A rising count of unreconciled trades C) A regulatory fine D) A data-center fire\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to distinguish a KRI from an actual loss.\n\nA KRI (key risk indicator) is a forward-looking metric that signals risk is BUILDING UP before any loss has occurred. A rising count of unreconciled trades is a warning sign - it tells you something could go wrong soon, so you can act early.\n\nAnalogy: A KRI is like the temperature gauge on your car climbing into the red zone - a warning. An actual loss event is the engine actually breaking down.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: A $2 million settlement failure is a realized loss event (it already happened), not a forward-looking indicator.\n- C: A regulatory fine is a realized loss event.\n- D: A data-center fire is a realized loss event (damage to physical assets).\n\n📌 Exam tip: KRIs are forward-looking DRIVERS (staff turnover, system downtime, unreconciled trades, audit findings). If money has already been lost, it's a loss event, not a KRI.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which is a KRI (not a loss)?\",\"options\":[\"$2M settlement failure\",\"Rising count of unreconciled trades\",\"Regulatory fine\",\"Data-center fire\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A KRI is a forward-looking warning signal; the others are realized loss events.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Operational losses are often split into HFLS and LFHS categories. Which type primarily drives the capital tail (extreme losses)?\nA) High-frequency/low-severity (HFLS) losses, such as processing errors B) Low-frequency/high-severity (LFHS) losses, such as a rogue trader or massive fines C) Medium-frequency/medium-severity losses D) Losses that are excluded from Basel's definition\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks which loss type drives the capital tail.\n\n- HFLS (high-frequency, low-severity): things like data-entry mistakes - they happen often but each is tiny. These are predictable and near the expected loss, so they are \"budgetable.\"\n- LFHS (low-frequency, high-severity): things like a rogue trader or a multi-billion-dollar fine - they happen rarely but are enormous. These rare giants create the dangerous tail that capital must absorb.\n\nAnalogy: Small daily spills (HFLS) are annoying but cheap to mop up. A once-in-a-decade flood (LFHS) is what you really need insurance for.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: HFLS losses are near expected loss and budgetable - they don't drive the tail.\n- C: Not the curriculum's framing; the tail is driven by the rare, severe events.\n- D: Excluded losses (strategic/reputational) aren't part of op-risk capital at all.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The capital tail = LFHS events. These are exactly where internal data is sparse, so scenario analysis and external data are used to fill the gap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Operational loss types\",\"x\":\"Frequency →\",\"y\":\"Severity →\",\"q\":[\"minor / ignore\",\"HFLS: budget as expected loss\",\"rare minor\",\"LFHS: drives capital tail\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Allocating operational risk capital to business lines based ONLY on their past loss history can create which problem?\nA) It eliminates all operational risk. B) Moral hazard, because business units have an incentive to hide losses to reduce their capital charge. C) It guarantees more accurate capital figures. D) It removes the need for any controls.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the downside of allocating capital purely on loss history.\n\nIf a business unit knows that reporting more losses means being charged more capital, it has an incentive to HIDE losses to look better. This is moral hazard - the allocation method creates a bad incentive that undermines honest reporting.\n\nAnalogy: If telling your parents about every small accident means a bigger cut to your allowance, you might be tempted to hide accidents - which makes the real risk invisible.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Capital absorbs losses; it never eliminates the underlying risk. Only controls reduce risk.\n- C: Hiding losses makes the figures LESS accurate, not more.\n- D: Controls are always needed; capital and controls do different jobs.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Capital ABSORBS losses; controls REDUCE them. Allocation should reward good control environments, not just punish reported losses, otherwise you breed moral hazard.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Moral hazard from loss-based allocation\",\"steps\":[\"Allocate capital by past losses\",\"Reporting losses → higher charge\",\"Units hide losses to look better\",\"Real risk becomes invisible\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Scenario analysis is especially important in operational risk modeling because it:\nA) Provides precise probabilities for everyday processing errors. B) Fills in the tail (rare, severe events) where internal loss data is sparse or absent. C) Replaces the need for any internal loss data collection. D) Is required only for high-frequency losses.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why scenario analysis matters in op risk.\n\nThe most damaging operational events (a 1-in-40-year cyber breach, a giant fine) are so rare that a bank may have little or no internal data on them. Scenario analysis uses structured expert workshops to estimate what such extreme events could cost, filling the gap in the tail where hard data doesn't exist.\n\nAnalogy: You have plenty of data on minor fender-benders, but almost none on a catastrophic crash. To plan for the catastrophe, experts sit down and reason out \"what would it cost if it happened?\" - that's scenario analysis.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Everyday errors (HFLS) have plenty of data; scenario analysis is for the rare, data-poor tail, not the routine stuff.\n- C: It complements internal data, it doesn't replace it - internal loss collection is still essential.\n- D: It's used for low-frequency/high-severity events, not high-frequency ones.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Scenario analysis = expert estimates for rare tail events where data is missing. Its weakness is bias (anchoring, overconfidence) because it relies on judgment.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why scenario analysis matters\",\"options\":[\"Precise probabilities for routine errors\",\"Fills the rare/severe tail where internal data is sparse\",\"Replaces internal loss data collection\",\"Only for high-frequency losses\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Expert workshops estimate extreme events that have little or no internal data.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A risk and control self-assessment (RCSA) is best described as:\nA) A regulator's audit of a bank's capital model. B) A process where business units identify their own risks, rate likelihood and impact, and assess control effectiveness. C) A database of every loss event that has already occurred. D) The 99.9% quantile of the aggregate loss distribution.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the definition of RCSA.\n\nIn an RCSA, the business units themselves periodically (a) identify the risks they face, (b) rate how likely each is and how bad the impact would be, and (c) judge how well their controls are working. Its strength is ownership and coverage of risks that haven't yet caused a loss; its weakness is subjectivity and self-serving bias (units may rate themselves too kindly).\n\nAnalogy: It's like a self-checkup where each department fills out a \"what could go wrong here, and how good are our safety measures?\" form.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: That's an external/regulatory review, not a self-assessment by the business units.\n- C: That describes loss data collection (a historical loss database), a different tool.\n- D: That's a capital/VaR concept, not a self-assessment process.\n\n📌 Exam tip: RCSA = units self-assess risks and controls (forward-looking, covers un-materialized risks). Its main weakness is subjectivity/self-serving bias.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Risk & control self-assessment (RCSA)\",\"steps\":[\"Business units identify their own risks\",\"Rate likelihood and impact\",\"Assess control effectiveness\",\"Weakness: self-serving bias\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** Under the Basel definition, operational risk excludes:\nA) Legal risk B) Cyber-fraud losses C) Strategic and reputational risk D) Rogue-trader losses\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Strategic and reputational risks are excluded; legal risk is explicitly included.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In the LDA, loss frequency and severity are most commonly modeled as:\nA) Normal and normal B) Poisson and lognormal C) Binomial and uniform D) Lognormal and Poisson\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Poisson counts compounded with fat-tailed (lognormal) severities.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Under the SMA, two banks with identical Business Indicator components differ in capital because of:\nA) Their VaR models B) The internal loss multiplier reflecting historical loss experience C) Different β factors by business line D) Supervisory stress-test results\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** SMA = BIC × ILM; ILM scales with the bank's own 10-year loss history.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which is a *key risk indicator* rather than a loss event?\nA) A \\$2M settlement failure B) Rising unreconciled-trade counts C) A regulatory fine D) A data-center fire\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** KRIs are forward-looking drivers; the others are realized loss events.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A bank models operational losses with a Poisson frequency of 4 events per year and an average severity of $250,000 per event, with frequency and severity independent. The expected annual operational loss is closest to:\nA) $62,500 B) $250,000 C) $1,000,000 D) $4,000,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With independent frequency and severity, expected loss = expected frequency × expected severity:\n\n$$E[L] = 4 \\times \\$250{,}000 = \\$1{,}000{,}000$$\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Operational risk: frequency × severity\",\"x\":\"Frequency →\",\"y\":\"Severity →\",\"q\":[\"accept / self-insure\",\"manage & control\",\"insure\",\"avoid / exit\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A business line records operational losses with a Poisson frequency of λ = 8 events per year and a severity distribution with a mean loss of $150,000 per event. The annual expected operational loss is closest to:\nA) $18,750 B) $150,000 C) $1,200,000 D) $9,600,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: compute the expected annual aggregate operational loss in the loss distribution approach (LDA).\n\n1. Formula (compound Poisson): $$E[\\text{annual loss}] = \\lambda \\times E[\\text{severity}].$$\n2. Plug in: $$8 \\times \\$150{,}000.$$\n3. Compute: $$\\$1{,}200{,}000.$$\n\nWhy C is correct: expected aggregate loss is the expected number of events times the expected size per event.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($18,750) divides severity by frequency ($$150{,}000/8$$) — inverted operation.\n- B ($150,000) reports the mean severity per event, ignoring frequency.\n- D ($9,600,000) multiplies by an extra factor (e.g., treats λ as monthly and annualizes incorrectly).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Expected aggregate loss is simple (λ × mean severity). But this number says nothing about capital — the 99.9% tail is driven by the *severity tail* (the lognormal σ), so two units with identical EL can demand very different capital.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected annual aggregate loss\",\"steps\":[\"frequency lambda = 8 / year\",\"× mean severity = $150,000\",\"= 8 × 150,000\",\"= $1,200,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Under the Standardized Measurement Approach (SMA), a bank has a Business Indicator Component (BIC) of $3.0 billion and average annual operational losses over the past 10 years of $300 million. The Internal Loss Multiplier is $$ILM = \\ln\\!\\left(e - 1 + \\left(\\tfrac{LC}{BIC}\\right)^{0.8}\\right)$$ with the loss component $$LC = 15 \\times \\text{average annual losses}.$$ The bank's operational risk capital is closest to:\nA) $3.00 billion B) $3.40 billion C) $4.50 billion D) $6.75 billion\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute SMA capital = BIC × ILM.\n\n1. Loss component: $$LC = 15 \\times \\$300\\text{M} = \\$4.5\\text{B}.$$\n2. Ratio: $$LC/BIC = 4.5/3.0 = 1.5.$$\n3. Raise to 0.8: $$1.5^{0.8} = e^{0.8\\ln 1.5} = e^{0.8 \\times 0.4055} = e^{0.3244} = 1.3832.$$\n4. ILM: $$\\ln(e - 1 + 1.3832) = \\ln(2.7183 - 1 + 1.3832) = \\ln(3.1015) = 1.1319.$$\n5. Capital: $$BIC \\times ILM = \\$3.0\\text{B} \\times 1.1319 = \\$3.40\\text{B}.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: loss history above the neutral point (LC > BIC) scales capital up by ~13%.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($3.00B) sets ILM = 1 — correct only if LC = BIC; here LC = 1.5 × BIC.\n- C ($4.50B) reports LC itself ($$15\\times300\\text{M}$$) as if it were capital.\n- D ($6.75B) skips the ILM log compression and multiplies BIC by the raw ratio (2.25), grossly overstating.\n\n📌 Exam tip: ILM = 1 when LC = BIC. The log structure *compresses* the impact of loss history — even LC = 2 × BIC only lifts capital ~24%. Know the structure (size charge scaled by loss experience), not the coefficient minutiae.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"SMA capital = BIC × ILM\",\"steps\":[\"LC = 15 × $300M = $4.5B\",\"LC/BIC = 1.5; 1.5^0.8 = 1.383\",\"ILM = ln(e-1+1.383) = 1.132\",\"Capital = $3.0B × 1.132 = $3.40B\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Two banks have identical Business Indicator Components. Bank Y's 10-year average operational losses are double Bank Z's. Holding all else equal, which statement is correct?\nA) The two banks hold identical operational risk capital because BIC is the same. B) Bank Y holds more capital because its higher loss history raises its Internal Loss Multiplier. C) Bank Z holds more capital because lower losses signal under-reporting penalized by supervisors. D) Capital differs only if the banks use different business-line β factors.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify why capital differs under the SMA when BIC is equal.\n\nWhy B is correct: SMA capital = BIC × ILM, and the ILM rises with the bank's own loss component (LC = 15 × average annual losses). Worse loss history → higher ILM → more capital.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is false — BIC equal does not make capital equal; the ILM differentiates them.\n- C reverses the relationship — higher losses (Bank Y) drive higher capital, not lower.\n- D refers to β factors, which belong to the *legacy* Standardized (TSA) approach, not the current SMA.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Under the SMA, the loss-experience lever is the ILM, not β coefficients. Beware moral hazard: allocating op-risk capital by loss history alone can tempt units to hide losses — allocation should also reward strong control environments.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Same BIC, Bank Y losses 2× Bank Z\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bank Y\",\"items\":[\"higher loss component\",\"higher ILM\",\"MORE capital\"]},{\"h\":\"Bank Z\",\"items\":[\"lower loss component\",\"lower ILM\",\"LESS capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is the best example of a key risk indicator (KRI) rather than a realized operational loss event?\nA) A $2 million settlement failure on a foreign-exchange trade. B) A regulatory fine for anti-money-laundering deficiencies. C) A rising monthly count of unreconciled trades in the back office. D) A data-center fire that halts processing for two days.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: distinguish a forward-looking KRI from a realized loss event.\n\nWhy C is correct: a KRI is a forward-looking metric tied to a risk *driver*, with thresholds that trigger escalation before losses materialize. A rising unreconciled-trade count flags deteriorating controls ahead of an actual loss.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is a realized loss event (execution/process management, type 7), not a leading indicator.\n- B is a realized loss event (clients, products & business practices, type 4).\n- D is a realized loss event (damage to physical assets / business disruption).\n\n📌 Exam tip: KRIs are *leading* (staff turnover, system downtime, failed settlements, audit findings); loss data is *lagging* (realized events in the loss database). The exam loves to slip a realized loss into a \"which is a KRI?\" question.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Which is a KRI (leading)?\",\"options\":[\"$2M FX settlement failure\",\"AML regulatory fine\",\"Rising count of unreconciled trades\",\"Data-center fire halting processing\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"A KRI is a forward-looking driver/warning; the others are realized (lagging) loss events.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under the Basel definition, which of the following is NOT classified as operational risk?\nA) Losses from a rogue trader breaching position limits. B) A multi-billion-dollar fine for market manipulation. C) A decline in profitability after management enters a market that proves unprofitable. D) Losses from a cyber attack that disables trading systems.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: identify the item excluded from the Basel operational risk definition.\n\nWhy C is correct: Basel defines operational risk as loss from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, and systems, or external events. It explicitly *includes legal risk* but *excludes strategic and reputational risk*. A bad strategic market-entry decision is strategic risk — excluded.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is internal fraud (event type 1) — included.\n- B is clients/products/business practices (type 4) — included, and historically the largest dollar losses.\n- D is external fraud / business disruption (types 2 and 6) — included.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the boundary — legal risk is IN; strategic and reputational risk are OUT. GARP frequently tests this exact distinction with a strategic-decision distractor.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Basel operational risk boundary\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"INCLUDED\",\"items\":[\"internal fraud (rogue trader)\",\"clients/products fines\",\"external fraud / cyber\",\"legal risk\"]},{\"h\":\"EXCLUDED\",\"items\":[\"strategic risk (bad market entry)\",\"reputational risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A risk manager argues that because the bank holds substantial operational risk capital, its operational risk has been \"reduced.\" The most accurate critique is:\nA) Correct — capital directly lowers the probability of operational losses. B) Incorrect — capital absorbs losses when they occur; controls (segregation of duties, limits, BCP) reduce risk. C) Correct — but only for high-frequency, low-severity losses. D) Incorrect — operational risk can only be reduced by purchasing insurance.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: critique the claim that holding capital reduces operational risk.\n\nWhy B is correct: capital is a *buffer* that absorbs losses after they occur; it does not change the likelihood or magnitude of an operational event. Risk is *reduced* by controls — segregation of duties, position limits, business-continuity planning — and partly transferred via insurance.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is exactly the misconception being tested — capital does not lower loss probability.\n- C is wrong on the mechanism — capital absorbs regardless of frequency band; it never reduces risk.\n- D overstates insurance — it transfers severity (subject to basis risk, payout delays, counterparty risk, and Basel caps), but it is not the *only* mitigant, and it does not reduce the underlying risk drivers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Capital *absorbs*, controls *reduce*, insurance *transfers*. Keep these verbs straight — GARP builds trap statements by swapping them.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Three levers for operational risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Capital\",\"items\":[\"ABSORBS losses after they occur\",\"does not lower probability\"]},{\"h\":\"Controls\",\"items\":[\"REDUCE risk\",\"segregation of duties, limits, BCP\"]},{\"h\":\"Insurance\",\"items\":[\"TRANSFERS severity\",\"subject to basis risk, caps\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which combination correctly pairs operational risk inputs with their primary purpose in capital and management?\nA) Internal loss data is best for the extreme tail; scenario analysis covers high-frequency events. B) External (consortium) data is used unadjusted; firm size is irrelevant when pooling losses. C) Scenario analysis fills the tail where internal data is sparse; external data must be scaled for firm size; BEICFs adjust for current control quality. D) BEICFs measure realized losses; scenario analysis measures the frequency of routine processing errors.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: match operational risk modeling inputs to their roles (the four AMA-era inputs, still used in management).\n\nWhy C is correct: internal loss data is short and truncated; scenario analysis (expert workshops) quantifies plausible low-frequency/high-severity tail events; external consortium data (e.g., ORX) must be scaled for the firm's size before use; and BEICFs (business environment and internal control factors) adjust the estimate for current control quality.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A inverts the roles — internal data is thin in the tail; scenario analysis (not internal data) is what fills the tail.\n- B is false — external data requires scaling for firm size; pooling raw losses across differently sized banks distorts severity.\n- D mis-defines both — BEICFs are forward-looking control/environment factors, not realized losses; scenario analysis targets rare tail events, not routine high-frequency errors.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The four inputs — internal loss data, external data, scenario analysis, BEICFs. Tail = scenario analysis; cross-firm comparability = scale external data; \"how good are controls right now?\" = BEICFs.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Four operational risk inputs\",\"root\":\"Op-risk modeling inputs\",\"children\":[\"Internal loss data (short, truncated)\",\"External data (scale for firm size)\",\"Scenario analysis (fills the tail)\",\"BEICFs (current control quality)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"408","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Stress Testing","notes":"# 8. Stress Testing\n\nGARP tests how stress testing complements VaR/ES, how scenarios are chosen (historical, hypothetical, reverse), governance expectations, and the regulatory landscape (Basel, CCAR/DFAST). Conceptual chapter — precision on definitions wins points.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the purposes of stress testing and compare it with VaR/ES.\n- Explain scenario types: historical, hypothetical, predictive/model-based; scenario selection and coherence.\n- Describe reverse stress testing and its uses.\n- Explain how stressed inputs produce stressed VaR/ES and the pros/cons versus scenario P&L.\n- Describe governance: board/senior management roles, policies, validation, and the internal audit role.\n- Describe regulatory stress testing: Basel principles, US CCAR/DFAST, EU-wide tests; capital vs liquidity stress tests.\n- Explain challenges: scenario plausibility vs severity, model risk, knock-on/second-round effects, and incorporating balance-sheet responses.\n\n## Stress testing vs VaR/ES\n\n| | VaR / ES | Stress testing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Question | How bad over a typical period at probability α? | What happens in *this specific* extreme scenario? |\n| Probability attached | Yes (quantile) | Usually no |\n| Data basis | Recent history / estimated distribution | Crisis history or imagination |\n| Horizon | Short (1–10 days market risk) | Long (quarters/years), path-dependent |\n| Captures unprecedented events | No | Yes (if you can imagine them) |\n| Use | Day-to-day limits, capital | Capital planning, vulnerability discovery, communication |\n\nThey are **complements**: VaR backtests well but is blind beyond the sample; stress tests probe the tail but carry no likelihood. Backward-looking VaR failed in 2007 precisely because the prior sample was calm — stress testing is the institutionalized answer.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> **Stressed VaR/ES**: compute VaR/ES using parameters (volatilities, correlations) calibrated to a crisis window (e.g., 2008). Basel 2.5 added stressed VaR; FRTB's ES is calibrated to the worst observed period. This blends the two frameworks — a probabilistic measure with stressed inputs.\n\n## Scenario design\n\n**Historical scenarios:** replay actual crises (1987 crash, 1998 LTCM/Russia, 2007–09 GFC, 2020 COVID). Pros: internally coherent (these co-movements actually happened), objective. Cons: the next crisis is never the last one; portfolio may have new risks (crypto, climate) absent from history.\n\n**Hypothetical scenarios:** constructed shocks (geopolitical conflict, cyber shutdown, +300bp rate shock with property crash). Pros: forward-looking, tailored to current vulnerabilities. Cons: subjective; hard to keep **coherent** — all variables must move consistently (you can't shock equities −40% while leaving credit spreads and volatility unchanged). Approaches to coherence: shock core variables and set the rest via estimated conditional relationships (e.g., conditional expectations given the core shocks), or full macro models.\n\n**Severity vs plausibility trade-off:** scenarios must be *severe but plausible*. Too mild → false comfort; too wild → dismissed by management. Knock-on/second-round effects (fire sales, funding runs, counterparty failures) made 2008 worse than first-round losses — good scenarios include them.\n\n**Choosing variables:** start from the portfolio's key risk factors and concentrations; the scenario should target what would actually hurt *this* firm.\n\n## Reverse stress testing\n\nStandard test: scenario → loss. **Reverse:** start from the outcome — \"what would make us fail / breach capital minima?\" — and search backward for scenarios that produce it.\n\n- Forces discovery of hidden vulnerabilities and concentration combinations management hasn't imagined.\n- Required by UK/EU regulators for recovery and resolution planning.\n- Hard part: the search space is huge; quantitative approaches search over factor combinations for the most *plausible* scenario reaching the loss threshold.\n\n## Governance\n\n- **Board and senior management** own the program: approve framework, scenario severity, and act on results — tests that never affect decisions are theater. The \"use test\": results must feed risk appetite, limits, and capital planning.\n- **Policies and documentation:** scope, frequency, methodologies, remediation paths.\n- **Validation and independent review:** challenge models, data, and scenario coherence; **internal audit** reviews the process end-to-end.\n- Known governance failures: scenarios watered down to pass, results not escalated, infrastructure unable to aggregate exposures quickly (BCBS 239 data aggregation principles address this).\n\n## Regulatory stress testing\n\n- **Basel principles** (2009, updated 2018): stress testing as a key risk-governance tool; board ownership; severe-but-plausible scenarios; capture system-wide interactions and feedback.\n- **US CCAR/DFAST:** annual Fed tests of large banks under supervisory scenarios (baseline/severely adverse) projecting revenues, losses, and capital ratios over a 9-quarter horizon; results gate capital distributions (stress capital buffer). The 2009 SCAP restored confidence by publishing credible results with a recapitalization backstop.\n- **EU-wide (EBA) tests**, Bank of England annual scenarios — similar architecture.\n- **Capital vs liquidity stress tests:** capital tests ask whether equity survives losses; liquidity tests (LCR-style, survival horizons) ask whether cash outflows can be met under funding runs. 2008 and 2023 (SVB) showed liquidity can kill before solvency does.\n- Disclosure debate: publishing results aids market discipline but may encourage teaching-to-the-test and herding into identically-measured risks.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Trap statements GARP likes: \"stress tests assign probabilities to scenarios\" — generally **false**; \"stress testing replaces VaR\" — false, they are complements; \"historical scenarios are sufficient\" — false, they miss novel risks; \"a more severe scenario is always better\" — false, implausible scenarios get ignored and distort capital allocation.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> If a question contrasts a 99% ES with a stress loss, remember the stress loss can exceed *or* fall short of ES — they answer different questions and there is no fixed ordering.","flashcards":"**Q1.** How does stress testing differ from VaR/ES?\n> **A:** VaR/ES ask \"how bad over a typical period at probability α?\" (probabilistic, short horizon, sample-based). Stress testing asks \"what happens in this specific extreme scenario?\" (usually no probability, long horizon, captures unprecedented events).\n\n**Q2.** Are VaR and stress testing substitutes or complements?\n> **A:** Complements. VaR backtests well but is blind beyond the sample; stress tests probe the tail but carry no likelihood. Backward-looking VaR failed in 2007 because the prior sample was calm.\n\n**Q3.** What is stressed VaR/ES?\n> **A:** VaR/ES computed with parameters (vols, correlations) calibrated to a crisis window (e.g., 2008). Basel 2.5 added stressed VaR; FRTB's ES is calibrated to the worst observed period — blending the two frameworks.\n\n**Q4.** Contrast historical and hypothetical scenarios.\n> **A:** Historical: replay actual crises (1987, 1998, 2008, 2020) — coherent and objective but the next crisis is never the last. Hypothetical: constructed shocks — forward-looking and tailored, but subjective and hard to keep coherent.\n\n**Q5.** What does scenario \"coherence\" require?\n> **A:** All variables move in an internally consistent way — you can't shock equities −40% while leaving credit spreads and volatility unchanged. Set non-core variables via conditional relationships or macro models.\n\n**Q6.** Explain the severity-vs-plausibility trade-off.\n> **A:** Scenarios must be severe but plausible. Too mild → false comfort; too wild → dismissed by management and distorts capital allocation. \"A more severe scenario is always better\" is false.\n\n**Q7.** Define reverse stress testing.\n> **A:** Start from the failure outcome (\"what would breach capital minima / make us fail?\") and search backward for scenarios that produce it. Forces discovery of hidden vulnerabilities; required by UK/EU regulators for recovery/resolution planning.\n\n**Q8.** What governance is expected for stress testing?\n> **A:** Board/senior management own the program (approve framework and severity, act on results — the \"use test\"). Independent validation challenges models and coherence; internal audit reviews the process end-to-end.\n\n**Q9.** What is CCAR/DFAST and what does it constrain?\n> **A:** Annual Fed supervisory stress tests of large US banks (baseline/severely adverse) projecting capital ratios over 9 quarters. Results gate capital distributions (dividends/buybacks) via the stress capital buffer.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish capital and liquidity stress tests.\n> **A:** Capital tests ask whether equity survives losses; liquidity tests (LCR-style survival horizons) ask whether cash outflows can be met under funding runs. 2008 and 2023 (SVB) showed liquidity can kill before solvency.\n\n**Q11.** Why include knock-on/second-round effects?\n> **A:** Fire sales, funding runs, and counterparty failures made 2008 worse than first-round losses. Good scenarios capture these system-wide feedback effects.\n\n**Q12.** Is there a fixed ordering between a 99% ES and a stress loss?\n> **A:** No — the stress loss can exceed or fall short of ES; they answer different questions. Common trap statements: \"stress tests assign probabilities\" (false) and \"stress testing replaces VaR\" (false).\n\n**Q13.** What is the disclosure debate around regulatory stress tests?\n> **A:** Publishing results aids market discipline (the 2009 SCAP restored confidence) but may encourage teaching-to-the-test and herding into identically-measured risks.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** The defining feature of reverse stress testing is that it:\nA) Uses inverted (downward-sloping) yield curves as the scenario. B) Starts from a failure outcome and works backward to find scenarios that would cause it. C) Applies historical scenarios in reverse chronological order. D) Stresses only the firm's liabilities.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what makes reverse stress testing different from a normal stress test.\n\nA normal stress test goes forward: pick a scenario, then calculate the loss. Reverse stress testing flips this around. You START with a bad outcome - \"what would make us fail or breach our capital minimum?\" - and then search BACKWARD for the scenarios that could produce that outcome.\n\nAnalogy: Normal stress testing asks \"if a storm hits, how much damage?\" Reverse stress testing asks \"what kind of storm would actually sink our ship?\" and then hunts for that storm.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: \"Inverted\" refers to yield-curve shape, which has nothing to do with the meaning of \"reverse\" here.\n- C: It's not about running history in reverse order; it's about starting from the outcome.\n- D: It can involve all risks (assets, liabilities, off-balance-sheet), not only liabilities.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Reverse stress test = outcome-first. Start with \"what breaks us?\" and work back to the scenario. Regulators require it for recovery and resolution planning.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Reverse stress testing (outcome-first)\",\"steps\":[\"Start with failure outcome\",\"'What would make us breach capital?'\",\"Search backward for scenarios\",\"Identify hidden vulnerabilities\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Compared with VaR, a key advantage of stress testing is that it:\nA) Attaches precise probabilities to extreme events. B) Is restricted to a one-day horizon. C) Can capture events that are absent from the historical sample. D) Requires returns to be normally distributed.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for stress testing's main advantage over VaR.\n\nVaR is built from recent historical data, so it is blind to events that never appeared in that data window (this is exactly why VaR failed in 2007 - the prior period had been calm). Stress testing is not limited by history: you can imagine and test brand-new, unprecedented scenarios. The price you pay is that stress tests usually carry no probability attached.\n\nAnalogy: VaR is like predicting tomorrow's weather only from last month's records. Stress testing lets you ask \"what if a hurricane we've never seen before hits?\" - even if it isn't in the records.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: This is the opposite - stress tests generally do NOT attach probabilities (that's VaR's domain).\n- B: VaR is the short-horizon tool (1-10 days for market risk); stress tests often run over quarters or years.\n- D: Stress testing doesn't require normality; if anything it's used precisely because real tails aren't normal.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stress testing's superpower is capturing the unprecedented; its cost is losing probability statements. VaR and stress testing are COMPLEMENTS, not substitutes.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Stress testing\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"built from historical data\",\"attaches probability\",\"blind to unprecedented events\"]},{\"h\":\"Stress testing\",\"items\":[\"imagines new scenarios\",\"captures the unprecedented\",\"no probability attached\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** For a hypothetical stress scenario to be \"coherent,\" it must:\nA) Shock only one risk factor at a time. B) Have all shocked variables move in an internally consistent way. C) Match a past historical episode exactly. D) Be approved by the firm's external auditors.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what \"coherent\" means for a hypothetical scenario.\n\nA coherent scenario is one where all the variables move together in a way that actually makes sense. You can't, for example, shock equities down 40% while leaving credit spreads and volatility unchanged - in a real crash, spreads widen and volatility spikes too. So the shocked variables must be internally consistent with each other.\n\nAnalogy: If you imagine a heavy rainstorm, you can't also assume the streets stay perfectly dry. The pieces of the story have to fit together.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Shocking one variable alone usually makes a scenario INCOHERENT, because related variables would move too.\n- C: Hypothetical scenarios are made up (forward-looking); they don't need to match history.\n- D: External auditor approval is not what coherence means.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Coherence = variables co-move plausibly (equities down -> spreads wider, volatility higher). A common way to achieve it is to shock core variables and set the rest via estimated conditional relationships.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What makes a scenario 'coherent'?\",\"options\":[\"Shock only one factor at a time\",\"All shocked variables move consistently together\",\"Match a past episode exactly\",\"Approved by external auditors\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"In a crash, equities down → spreads wider → volatility up; variables must co-move plausibly.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under the US CCAR program, the supervisory stress test results primarily constrain:\nA) Banks' trading-desk VaR limits. B) Capital distributions (dividends and buybacks) via projected post-stress capital ratios. C) The interest rates banks pay on deposits. D) The pricing of individual loans.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what CCAR results actually control.\n\nIn CCAR, the Federal Reserve projects a bank's revenues, losses, and capital ratios over a 9-quarter horizon under severely adverse scenarios. If a bank's projected post-stress capital ratio is too weak, its ability to pay dividends and do share buybacks is restricted (this links to the stress capital buffer).\n\nAnalogy: It's like a budget check before a family books a vacation: if the projection shows you'd run out of savings in a bad year, you're told to cut back on spending (distributions) first.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: CCAR is about overall capital adequacy, not setting individual trading-desk VaR limits.\n- C: Deposit rates are not gated by CCAR.\n- D: Loan pricing is a business decision, not the direct output CCAR constrains.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CCAR/DFAST -> projected capital ratios under adverse scenarios -> gate dividends and buybacks (stress capital buffer). Capital distributions is the key phrase.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CCAR pipeline\",\"steps\":[\"Fed applies severely adverse scenario\",\"Project losses & capital ratios (9 quarters)\",\"Weak post-stress ratio?\",\"Gate dividends & buybacks\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of the following statements about the relationship between VaR and stress testing is TRUE?\nA) Stress testing replaces VaR. B) VaR and stress testing are complements that answer different questions. C) Stress tests always assign probabilities to scenarios. D) Historical scenarios alone are sufficient to capture all future risks.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the correct view of how VaR and stress testing relate.\n\nThey are complements. VaR answers \"how bad could things get over a typical period at a given probability?\" using recent data. Stress testing answers \"what happens in THIS specific extreme scenario?\" without a probability. Each covers the other's blind spot: VaR backtests well but misses anything outside its data; stress testing probes the tail but carries no likelihood.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: False - stress testing does NOT replace VaR; banks use both.\n- C: False - stress tests generally do NOT assign probabilities to scenarios. This is a classic GARP trap statement.\n- D: False - historical scenarios miss novel risks (crypto, climate, new instruments) that never appeared in the past.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the false trap statements GARP loves: \"stress tests assign probabilities,\" \"stress testing replaces VaR,\" \"historical scenarios are sufficient,\" \"a more severe scenario is always better.\" All FALSE.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"VaR and stress testing relationship\",\"options\":[\"Stress testing replaces VaR\",\"They are complements answering different questions\",\"Stress tests always assign probabilities\",\"Historical scenarios capture all future risks\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"VaR is probabilistic from data; stress testing probes specific extremes without probability - they complement.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A \"stressed VaR\" measure is best described as:\nA) A scenario loss with no probability attached. B) VaR or ES computed using parameters (volatilities, correlations) calibrated to a crisis period. C) A VaR measure that ignores correlations entirely. D) A reverse stress test.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what stressed VaR means.\n\nStressed VaR takes the normal VaR (or ES) machinery but feeds it inputs - volatilities and correlations - calibrated to a crisis window such as 2008, instead of recent calm data. This blends the two worlds: a probabilistic measure (VaR) computed with stressed inputs. Basel 2.5 added stressed VaR, and FRTB's expected shortfall is calibrated to the worst observed period.\n\nAnalogy: It's like calculating your monthly budget risk, but using prices from the worst recession year instead of today's calm prices - same calculation, harsher inputs.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: A scenario loss with no probability is a plain stress test, not stressed VaR (which keeps the probability framework).\n- C: Stressed VaR still uses correlations - in fact crisis correlations are a key stressed input.\n- D: Reverse stress testing is a completely different concept (outcome-first).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Stressed VaR = the VaR/ES formula run on crisis-calibrated parameters. It is a hybrid that puts stressed inputs into a probabilistic measure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What is stressed VaR?\",\"options\":[\"Scenario loss with no probability\",\"VaR/ES computed with crisis-calibrated parameters\",\"VaR that ignores correlations\",\"A reverse stress test\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Same VaR/ES machinery, but volatilities and correlations calibrated to a crisis window.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is a recognized advantage of HISTORICAL stress scenarios over hypothetical ones?\nA) They are guaranteed to capture future, never-seen risks. B) They are internally coherent because the co-movements actually happened. C) They require no data. D) They always produce larger losses than hypothetical scenarios.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for a genuine advantage of historical scenarios.\n\nHistorical scenarios replay real crises (1987, 1998, 2008, 2020). Their big advantage is that the variables moved together in a way that genuinely occurred, so the scenario is automatically coherent and objective - you don't have to guess how equities, spreads, and volatility should co-move; the data already shows you.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: This is actually a WEAKNESS of historical scenarios - they miss new risks (crypto, climate) that weren't in past crises. \"The next crisis is never the last one.\"\n- C: They require historical data to replay.\n- D: There is no fixed rule that historical losses exceed hypothetical ones; severity depends on the scenario.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Historical scenarios = coherent and objective (it really happened) BUT may miss novel risks. Hypothetical scenarios = forward-looking and tailored BUT subjective and hard to keep coherent.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Historical vs Hypothetical scenarios\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Historical\",\"items\":[\"coherent (co-movements happened)\",\"objective\",\"misses novel risks\"]},{\"h\":\"Hypothetical\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking, tailored\",\"can include new risks\",\"subjective, hard to keep coherent\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The \"severity versus plausibility\" trade-off in scenario design means that a scenario should be:\nA) As extreme as possible regardless of realism. B) Severe but plausible - not so mild it gives false comfort, nor so wild that management dismisses it. C) Always identical to a past crisis. D) Mild enough to always pass the test.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks about the severity-plausibility trade-off.\n\nA good scenario must strike a balance. If it is too mild, it gives false comfort - you think you're safe when you aren't. If it is too wild and unrealistic, management dismisses it as nonsense and it distorts capital allocation. The sweet spot is \"severe but plausible.\"\n\nAnalogy: A fire drill should be realistic enough to test you, but you don't simulate a meteor strike that wipes out the whole city - nobody would take the lessons seriously.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: \"As extreme as possible regardless of realism\" leads to dismissal - too wild is a known failure.\n- C: It doesn't have to match a past crisis; hypothetical scenarios are also valid.\n- D: Designing a scenario just to pass is exactly the governance failure (\"scenarios watered down to pass\") that regulators warn against.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Severe BUT plausible. Too mild = false comfort; too extreme = ignored. GARP flags \"a more severe scenario is always better\" as FALSE.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Severity vs plausibility trade-off\",\"items\":[\"too mild (false comfort)\",\"|severe but plausible\",\"too wild (dismissed)\"],\"left\":\"not useful\",\"right\":\"not useful\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Why are \"knock-on\" or second-round effects (such as fire sales and funding runs) important to include in stress scenarios?\nA) They are required by accounting standards. B) They made 2008 far worse than the first-round losses alone, so ignoring them understates the true impact. C) They reduce the severity of the scenario. D) They only matter for liquidity, never for capital.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why second-round effects matter.\n\nIn 2008, the initial (first-round) losses on subprime assets were bad, but what made the crisis catastrophic were the knock-on effects: forced fire sales pushed prices down further, funding runs dried up cash, and counterparty failures spread the damage. A scenario that captures only first-round losses will badly understate how bad things can really get.\n\nAnalogy: One domino falling is a small loss; but if it knocks over the next, and the next, the total damage is far bigger. Good scenarios model the whole chain, not just the first domino.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: It's a risk-management best practice, not an accounting-standard requirement.\n- C: They INCREASE severity (make losses bigger), not reduce it.\n- D: They matter for both - fire sales and funding runs hit liquidity AND capital.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Good scenarios include second-round/feedback effects (fire sales, funding runs, counterparty failures). Ignoring them understates the true loss.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Knock-on (second-round) effects\",\"steps\":[\"First-round loss on assets\",\"Forced fire sales push prices lower\",\"Funding runs dry up cash\",\"Counterparty failures spread damage\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Regarding the ordering of a 99% Expected Shortfall (ES) and a stress-test loss for the same portfolio, which statement is correct?\nA) The stress loss is always larger than the 99% ES. B) The 99% ES is always larger than the stress loss. C) There is no fixed ordering - the stress loss can be larger or smaller than the ES because they answer different questions. D) They are always exactly equal.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks how a 99% ES and a stress loss compare.\n\nThese two numbers measure different things. ES is a probability-based average of losses beyond the 99% quantile, drawn from a distribution. A stress loss is the outcome of one specific imagined scenario, with no probability attached. Because they answer different questions, there is no guaranteed ordering - the stress loss might come out bigger OR smaller than the 99% ES depending on the scenario chosen.\n\nAnalogy: Comparing \"the average of my worst 1% of commute times\" (ES) with \"how long my commute would be if a specific bridge collapsed\" (stress) - one isn't always bigger than the other; it depends on the scenario.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A and B: Both claim a fixed ordering, which is false - there is no guaranteed relationship.\n- D: They are not equal by construction; they're different measures.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A stress loss can exceed OR fall short of a given ES. No fixed ordering - they answer different questions. This is a favorite GARP trap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Ordering of 99% ES vs stress loss\",\"options\":[\"Stress loss always larger\",\"99% ES always larger\",\"No fixed ordering - different questions\",\"Always exactly equal\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"ES is a probability-based tail average; a stress loss is one scenario with no probability - neither dominates.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** The defining feature of reverse stress testing is that it:\nA) Uses inverted yield curves B) Starts from a failure outcome and works back to scenarios causing it C) Applies historical scenarios in reverse chronological order D) Stresses only liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Outcome-first search for the scenarios that would break the firm.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Relative to VaR, stress testing:\nA) Attaches precise probabilities to extreme events B) Is restricted to one-day horizons C) Can capture events absent from the historical sample D) Requires normally distributed returns\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Its core advantage — imagination is not bounded by the data window; the price is losing probability statements.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A coherent hypothetical scenario requires that:\nA) Only one risk factor is shocked at a time B) Shocked variables move in an internally consistent way C) It matches a historical episode exactly D) It be approved by external auditors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** All variables must co-move plausibly (equities down → spreads wider, vol higher).\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Under CCAR, supervisory stress results primarily constrain:\nA) Banks' trading-desk VaR limits B) Capital distributions via projected capital ratios under adverse scenarios C) Deposit interest rates D) Loan pricing\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Projected post-stress capital ratios gate dividends/buybacks (stress capital buffer).\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** Reverse stress testing requires a firm to:\nA) Apply historical scenarios in reverse chronological order B) Identify scenarios that would cause the firm to fail or breach its capital requirements, then assess their plausibility C) Stress only the asset side of the balance sheet D) Reduce capital until VaR limits are breached\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Reverse stress testing starts from the outcome (failure / breach) and works backwards to the scenarios that produce it, then asks how plausible they are — designed to uncover hidden vulnerabilities that forward scenario design misses.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Reverse stress testing\",\"steps\":[\"Define the failure outcome (insolvency / breach)\",\"Work backwards to scenarios that cause it\",\"Assess plausibility of those scenarios\",\"Act on hidden vulnerabilities found\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A risk committee is comparing stress testing with VaR and expected shortfall (ES). Which statement most accurately captures the relationship?\nA) Stress testing replaces VaR because VaR failed to predict the 2007–09 crisis. B) Stress testing and VaR/ES are complements: VaR/ES attach probabilities within the sampled distribution, while stress testing probes specific extreme scenarios without attaching probabilities. C) Stress testing assigns precise probabilities to severe scenarios, making VaR redundant. D) A 99% ES is always larger than any stress-test loss because ES captures the entire tail.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct characterization of stress testing versus VaR/ES.\n\nWhy B is correct: VaR/ES are probabilistic measures calibrated to recent history (blind beyond the sample); stress tests examine \"what happens in *this specific* extreme scenario,\" generally without attaching a probability. They answer different questions and are used together.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A overstates the relationship — backward-looking VaR did fail in 2007 (calm prior sample), but stress testing *complements*, it does not *replace*, VaR.\n- C is the classic false statement — stress tests usually do NOT assign probabilities; that is precisely what they give up in exchange for capturing unprecedented events.\n- D imposes a false ordering — a stress loss can exceed or fall short of a 99% ES; there is no fixed relationship because they measure different things.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Stress testing replaces VaR\" (false), \"stress tests assign probabilities\" (false), and \"ES > stress loss always\" (false) are three of GARP's favorite trap statements. They are complements; stress losses have no fixed ordering versus ES.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR/ES vs Stress testing\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR / ES\",\"items\":[\"probabilistic, from sampled history\",\"blind beyond the sample\",\"attaches a probability\"]},{\"h\":\"Stress testing\",\"items\":[\"specific extreme scenario\",\"captures unprecedented events\",\"no probability attached\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Which description best defines reverse stress testing?\nA) Applying historical crisis scenarios in reverse chronological order. B) Stressing only the liability side of the balance sheet. C) Starting from a defined adverse outcome (e.g., breaching capital minima or failure) and searching backward for the scenarios that would produce it. D) Re-running a standard stress test with the signs of all shocks reversed.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: identify the defining feature of reverse stress testing.\n\nWhy C is correct: a standard test runs scenario → loss; the reverse test starts from the outcome (\"what would make us fail?\") and searches the factor space backward for the most plausible scenarios that reach that loss threshold.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A confuses \"reverse\" with chronological ordering — irrelevant.\n- B arbitrarily restricts the exercise to liabilities — reverse tests can involve any risk factors.\n- D literally flips shock signs, which is not what \"reverse\" means here.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Reverse stress testing is *outcome-first*. Its value is surfacing hidden vulnerabilities and concentration combinations management never imagined; UK/EU regulators require it for recovery and resolution planning.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Reverse stress testing (outcome-first)\",\"steps\":[\"Define adverse outcome (breach capital / fail)\",\"Search factor space backward\",\"Find plausible scenarios reaching it\",\"Surface hidden vulnerabilities\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An analyst builds a hypothetical scenario in which global equities fall 40%, but leaves credit spreads, volatility, and FX unchanged from current levels. The principal weakness of this scenario is that it:\nA) Is too severe to be plausible. B) Lacks coherence: in a 40% equity crash, credit spreads would widen and volatility would spike, so holding them flat understates the loss. C) Uses a historical episode, which cannot apply to a current portfolio. D) Assigns a probability that is too low.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: diagnose the flaw in a hypothetical scenario that shocks one variable in isolation.\n\nWhy B is correct: a coherent scenario requires all variables to move in an internally consistent way. A 40% equity collapse would co-move with wider credit spreads and higher volatility; freezing them violates coherence and understates the true stressed loss.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A misdiagnoses — severity is not the issue; *incoherence* is. A severe-but-coherent scenario is desirable.\n- C is irrelevant — this is a hypothetical (constructed) scenario, not a historical replay.\n- D is irrelevant — hypothetical scenarios generally carry no probability at all.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Coherence = consistent co-movement. You cannot shock equities −40% while leaving spreads/vol/FX untouched. Approaches to coherence: shock core variables and set the rest via estimated conditional relationships, or use a full macro model.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Flaw: equities -40%, all else flat\",\"options\":[\"Too severe to be plausible\",\"Lacks coherence: spreads/vol should co-move, so loss is understated\",\"Uses a historical episode\",\"Probability is too low\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A 40% crash would widen spreads and spike volatility; freezing them violates coherence and understates loss.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Under the U.S. CCAR program, supervisory stress-test results primarily constrain a large bank's:\nA) Trading-desk VaR limits. B) Deposit interest rates. C) Capital distributions (dividends and buybacks) via projected post-stress capital ratios over a multi-quarter horizon. D) Loan pricing to retail customers.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: identify what CCAR results gate.\n\nWhy C is correct: CCAR (and DFAST) project revenues, losses, and capital ratios over a 9-quarter horizon under supervisory baseline and severely adverse scenarios; the results feed the stress capital buffer, which gates capital distributions.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A — desk-level VaR limits are an internal market-risk control, not the CCAR output.\n- B and D — CCAR does not set deposit rates or loan prices; those are commercial decisions.\n\n📌 Exam tip: CCAR/DFAST → projected capital ratios under adverse scenarios → stress capital buffer → constraint on dividends/buybacks. The 2009 SCAP restored confidence by publishing credible results with a recapitalization backstop.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CCAR pipeline\",\"steps\":[\"Supervisory severely adverse scenario\",\"Project revenues, losses, capital (9 quarters)\",\"Feed the stress capital buffer\",\"Gate dividends & buybacks\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A bank's stress-testing program produces detailed reports, but the results are never escalated to the board and never change risk appetite, limits, or capital plans. The most accurate governance critique is that the program:\nA) Is adequate, since producing the reports satisfies the requirement. B) Fails the \"use test\" — results must feed risk-management decisions; tests that never affect decisions are theater. C) Should be discontinued because stress testing is a regulatory formality. D) Needs only more severe scenarios to become useful.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the governance failure.\n\nWhy B is correct: board and senior management own the program and must act on results. The \"use test\" requires that outputs feed risk appetite, limits, and capital planning; reports that change nothing are theater.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is exactly the misconception — producing reports is not the goal; influencing decisions is.\n- C dismisses stress testing wrongly — it is a core risk-governance tool, not an empty formality.\n- D misdiagnoses — the problem is governance/use, not scenario severity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Governance failures GARP flags: scenarios watered down to pass, results not escalated, and infrastructure unable to aggregate exposures quickly (addressed by BCBS 239 data-aggregation principles).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Reports never change decisions\",\"options\":[\"Adequate - producing reports satisfies the rule\",\"Fails the 'use test' - results must feed decisions\",\"Discontinue - it is a formality\",\"Just needs more severe scenarios\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Outputs must feed risk appetite, limits and capital plans; tests that change nothing are theater.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement about historical versus hypothetical stress scenarios is most accurate?\nA) Historical scenarios are internally coherent but may omit novel risks (e.g., crypto, climate) absent from past data. B) Hypothetical scenarios are always more objective than historical scenarios. C) Historical scenarios are sufficient on their own because every future crisis resembles a past one. D) Hypothetical scenarios cannot include second-round (knock-on) effects.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: identify the correct comparison of scenario types.\n\nWhy A is correct: historical scenarios replay actual crises, so their co-movements are internally coherent and objective — but the next crisis is never the last one, and current portfolios may carry new risks absent from history.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B reverses objectivity — *historical* scenarios are more objective; hypothetical ones are subjective by construction.\n- C is the \"historical scenarios are sufficient\" trap — false, because they miss unprecedented risks.\n- D is false — well-built hypothetical scenarios *should* include knock-on effects (fire sales, funding runs, counterparty failures), which made 2008 far worse than first-round losses.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Historical = coherent, objective, but backward-looking. Hypothetical = forward-looking and tailored, but subjective and hard to keep coherent. Good scenarios are severe *and* plausible and include second-round effects.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Historical vs Hypothetical scenarios\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Historical\",\"items\":[\"coherent & objective\",\"co-movements really happened\",\"omits novel risks (crypto, climate)\"]},{\"h\":\"Hypothetical\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking, tailored\",\"can include knock-on effects\",\"subjective by construction\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** During 2008 and again in 2023 (Silicon Valley Bank), a key lesson about stress testing was that:\nA) Capital stress tests alone are sufficient to ensure survival. B) Liquidity stress tests are unnecessary once capital ratios are strong. C) Liquidity can kill an institution before solvency does, so liquidity stress tests (survival horizons, funding-run scenarios) complement capital stress tests. D) Reverse stress testing is irrelevant for deposit-funded banks.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: identify the correct capital-versus-liquidity stress-testing lesson.\n\nWhy C is correct: capital tests ask whether equity survives losses; liquidity tests (LCR-style, survival horizons) ask whether cash outflows can be met under funding runs. 2008 and SVB in 2023 showed a firm can be solvent yet fail because deposits/funding flee faster than assets can be liquidated.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is false — capital adequacy did not save SVB from a deposit run.\n- B reverses the lesson — strong capital ratios do not remove the need for liquidity stress testing.\n- D is false — reverse stress testing is highly relevant for deposit-funded banks (deposit-flight scenarios are exactly what it surfaces).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Distinguish capital (solvency) from liquidity (funding) stress tests. The SVB-era takeaway: liquidity risk can be fatal even when capital looks adequate. Severe-but-plausible scenarios should capture funding runs and second-round effects.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Capital vs Liquidity stress tests\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Capital (solvency)\",\"items\":[\"can equity absorb losses?\",\"measures capital ratios\"]},{\"h\":\"Liquidity (funding)\",\"items\":[\"can outflows be met in a run?\",\"survival horizons; killed SVB while solvent\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"409","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Pricing Conventions, Discounting, and Arbitrage","notes":"# 9. Pricing Conventions, Discounting, and Arbitrage\n\nThe foundation of fixed income: quote conventions, discount factors, the law of one price, and replication arbitrage. GARP tests T-bill discount-rate mechanics, extracting discount factors from bond prices, and spotting/monetizing a mispricing.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe Treasury quote conventions: T-bill discount rates, bond clean vs dirty (invoice) prices, accrued interest.\n- Calculate the price of a T-bill from its quoted discount rate and explain why the discount rate understates the true yield.\n- Define discount factors and compute them from Treasury bond/STRIP prices.\n- Describe STRIPS (C-STRIPS, P-STRIPS) and how stripping/reconstitution enforces consistency.\n- State the law of one price and identify arbitrage opportunities via replication.\n- Construct a replicating portfolio for a bond from other bonds or STRIPS and compute the arbitrage profit.\n- Explain idealized vs actual deviations: bid-ask, financing, liquidity premia (on-the-run vs off-the-run).\n\n## Treasury quoting conventions\n\n**T-bills** are quoted on a **discount rate** basis (actual/360):\n\n$$P = F\\left(1 - d \\cdot \\frac{n}{360}\\right)$$\n\nwhere d = quoted discount rate, n = days to maturity, F = face. The discount rate **understates** the true return because (i) the gain is divided by *face*, not price paid, and (ii) the 360-day year shortens the annualization.\n\n**Coupon bonds** are quoted **clean** (flat); the buyer pays the **dirty (invoice) price**:\n\n$$\\text{Dirty} = \\text{Clean} + \\text{Accrued interest}, \\qquad AI = \\text{coupon payment} \\times \\frac{\\text{days since last coupon}}{\\text{days in period}}$$\n\n(US Treasuries use actual/actual for the accrual fraction.) Prices are now quoted in decimals; legacy convention was 32nds.\n\n### Worked example: T-bill price and true yield\n\nA 90-day T-bill is quoted at a 2.00% discount rate, \\$100 face.\n\n$$P = 100\\left(1 - 0.02 \\times \\frac{90}{360}\\right) = 100(1 - 0.005) = \\$99.50$$\n\nTrue (investment) yield, annualized actual/365:\n\n$$y = \\frac{100 - 99.50}{99.50} \\times \\frac{365}{90} = 0.005025 \\times 4.0556 = 2.038\\%$$\n\n2.038% > 2.00%: the discount rate always understates the yield.\n\n## Discount factors\n\nThe discount factor d(t) is **the price today of \\$1 delivered at t** — the cleanest, convention-free statement of time value. Any default-free cash-flow stream is then priced as:\n\n$$P = \\sum_t CF_t \\cdot d(t)$$\n\nDiscount factors are extracted (\"bootstrapped\") from observed prices, shortest maturity first.\n\n### Worked example: bootstrapping discount factors\n\n- Bond A: 6-month, 4% coupon (semiannual), price 100.98. Single cash flow 102 at t = 0.5:\n\n$$102 \\cdot d(0.5) = 100.98 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; d(0.5) = \\frac{100.98}{102} = 0.9900$$\n\n- Bond B: 1-year, 4% semiannual, price 100.97. Cash flows 2 at 0.5 and 102 at 1.0:\n\n$$2(0.9900) + 102 \\cdot d(1.0) = 100.97 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; d(1.0) = \\frac{100.97 - 1.98}{102} = \\frac{98.99}{102} = 0.9705$$\n\nd(t) must decline with maturity (positive rates) and lie below 1; a d(t) above an earlier one signals a data error or negative forward rates.\n\n## STRIPS\n\nTreasury STRIPS split a bond into single-payment claims: **C-STRIPS** (coupon interest) and **P-STRIPS** (principal). Each is a zero-coupon Treasury, so STRIP prices *are* discount factors (×100). Dealers can **strip** a bond into pieces or **reconstitute** pieces into the bond — this two-way arbitrage forces the bond price toward the sum of its parts. In practice small deviations persist: P-STRIPS of liquid bonds can trade rich, long C-STRIPS cheap; idiosyncratic supply/demand and liquidity matter.\n\n## Law of one price and replication arbitrage\n\n**Law of one price:** identical cash flows must trade at the same price — otherwise buy the cheap version, short the expensive one, and pocket the difference with zero net future obligation.\n\nReplication recipe: match the target bond's cash flows date by date using STRIPS (trivial) or a portfolio of coupon bonds (solve backward from the longest cash flow).\n\n### Worked example: arbitraging a mispriced bond\n\nUsing d(0.5) = 0.9900 and d(1.0) = 0.9705 from above, the fair price of a 1-year 6% semiannual-coupon Treasury (cash flows 3 and 103):\n\n$$P_{fair} = 3(0.9900) + 103(0.9705) = 2.97 + 99.96 = 102.93$$\n\nThe bond trades in the market at 102.60 — **cheap by 0.33**.\n\nArbitrage: **buy** the bond at 102.60; **short** the replicating STRIPS package (sell 3 face of the 6-month STRIP and 103 face of the 1-year STRIP, receiving 102.93). Net cash today: +0.33. Future cash flows: the bond's coupons exactly cover the short STRIPS' obligations. Riskless profit = **\\$0.33 per \\$100 face**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Direction discipline: buy LOW, short HIGH — under exam pressure candidates reverse the legs. Also, compare like with like: replication must match the **dirty** price and every cash-flow date; comparing a clean quote against a dirty replication cost manufactures phantom arbitrage. And the T-bill trap: the discount rate is **not** the yield — never discount cash flows with a quoted discount rate.\n\n## Why deviations survive\n\nReal-world frictions cap the arbitrage: bid-ask spreads and financing (repo) costs; balance-sheet/capital charges; **liquidity premia** — on-the-run (most recently issued) Treasuries trade rich to off-the-run because of their liquidity and repo specialness; STRIPS market segmentation. An apparent mispricing smaller than round-trip costs is not exploitable.","flashcards":"**Q1.** T-bill price from a quoted discount rate.\n> **A:** $$P = F\\left(1 - d\\cdot\\frac{n}{360}\\right)$$, with d = discount rate (actual/360), n = days to maturity, F = face.\n\n**Q2.** Price a 90-day T-bill quoted at 2.00% discount, $100 face.\n> **A:** $$P = 100(1 - 0.02\\times\\frac{90}{360}) = 100(1-0.005) = \\$99.50$$.\n\n**Q3.** Why does the quoted discount rate understate the true yield?\n> **A:** The gain is divided by face (not the lower price paid), and the 360-day year shortens the annualization. The discount rate is never the yield — never discount cash flows with it.\n\n**Q4.** Distinguish clean (flat) and dirty (invoice) bond prices.\n> **A:** $$\\text{Dirty} = \\text{Clean} + \\text{Accrued interest}$$, where $$AI = \\text{coupon}\\times\\frac{\\text{days since last coupon}}{\\text{days in period}}$$. The buyer pays the dirty price (US Treasuries: actual/actual accrual).\n\n**Q5.** Define the discount factor d(t).\n> **A:** The price today of $1 delivered at t — the convention-free statement of time value. Any default-free cash-flow stream: $$P = \\sum_t CF_t\\cdot d(t)$$.\n\n**Q6.** Bootstrap d(1.0): 1-year 4% semiannual bond at 100.97, given d(0.5) = 0.9900.\n> **A:** $$2(0.9900) + 102\\,d(1.0) = 100.97 \\Rightarrow d(1.0) = \\frac{98.99}{102} = 0.9705$$.\n\n**Q7.** What property must discount factors satisfy?\n> **A:** They must decline with maturity (positive rates) and lie below 1. A d(t) above an earlier one signals a data error or negative forward rates.\n\n**Q8.** What are C-STRIPS and P-STRIPS, and why are their prices discount factors?\n> **A:** A Treasury STRIP is a single-payment claim (coupon = C-STRIP, principal = P-STRIP), i.e., a zero-coupon Treasury, so its price (×100) is a discount factor. Stripping/reconstitution arbitrage forces bond price toward the sum of parts.\n\n**Q9.** State the law of one price.\n> **A:** Identical cash flows must trade at the same price; otherwise buy the cheap version, short the expensive one, and pocket the difference with zero net future obligation.\n\n**Q10.** Given d(0.5) = 0.9900, d(1.0) = 0.9705, fair-price a 1-year 6% semiannual Treasury (cash flows 3 and 103).\n> **A:** $$P_{fair} = 3(0.9900) + 103(0.9705) = 2.97 + 99.96 = 102.93$$.\n\n**Q11.** That bond trades at 102.60. What is the arbitrage?\n> **A:** It is cheap by 0.33. Buy the bond at 102.60; short the replicating STRIPS package (receive 102.93). Net cash today +0.33; future coupons cover the short STRIPS. Riskless profit $0.33/$100 face.\n\n**Q12.** The arbitrage direction discipline (a common trap).\n> **A:** Buy LOW, short HIGH. Also match the dirty price and every cash-flow date — comparing a clean quote against a dirty replication cost manufactures phantom arbitrage.\n\n**Q13.** Why do apparent mispricings survive in practice?\n> **A:** Bid-ask spreads, repo financing costs, balance-sheet/capital charges, and liquidity premia (on-the-run Treasuries trade rich to off-the-run). A mispricing smaller than round-trip costs is not exploitable.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A 90-day Treasury bill is quoted at a discount rate of 2.00% with a face value of $100. What is its price?\nA) $98.00 B) $99.50 C) $99.80 D) $98.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the price of a T-bill given its discount rate. T-bills use a discount-rate convention on an actual/360 basis.\n\nThe formula is:\n\n$$P = F\\left(1 - d \\cdot \\frac{n}{360}\\right)$$\n\nwhere F = face = $100, d = 0.02, n = 90 days.\n\nStep by step:\n- $$\\frac{n}{360} = \\frac{90}{360} = 0.25$$\n- $$d \\times 0.25 = 0.02 \\times 0.25 = 0.005$$\n- $$1 - 0.005 = 0.995$$\n- $$P = 100 \\times 0.995 = 99.50$$\n\nSo the price is $99.50.\n\nAnalogy: The bank \"discounts\" the $100 you'll get in 90 days by a small slice (0.5%), so you pay $99.50 today and collect $100 later. Your profit is the 50-cent difference.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($98.00): Used the full year (multiplied 0.02 x 100 = 2) and forgot the 90/360 fraction.\n- C ($99.80): Used n/360 incorrectly (e.g., 36/360) or miscomputed the fraction.\n- D ($98.50): Used 90/120 or some wrong day fraction.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a T-bill price, always apply the day-count fraction n/360. The discount is taken off the FACE value, not the price.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"T-bill price from discount rate\",\"steps\":[\"n/360 = 90/360 = 0.25\",\"d × 0.25 = 0.02 × 0.25 = 0.005\",\"1 - 0.005 = 0.995\",\"P = 100 × 0.995 = $99.50\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Using the T-bill in Q1 (90-day, price $99.50, face $100), what is the bill's true (investment) yield, annualized on an actual/365 basis?\nA) Exactly 2.00% B) 2.038% C) 1.96% D) 2.50%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the true yield, which is the actual return on the money you invest.\n\nThe true yield divides the gain by the PRICE PAID (not face) and annualizes using 365 days:\n\n$$y = \\frac{F - P}{P} \\times \\frac{365}{n}$$\n\nStep by step:\n- Gain = $$100 - 99.50 = 0.50$$\n- $$\\frac{0.50}{99.50} = 0.005025$$ (this is the holding-period return)\n- $$\\frac{365}{90} = 4.0556$$\n- $$y = 0.005025 \\times 4.0556 = 0.02038 = 2.038\\%$$\n\nSo the true yield is 2.038%, which is HIGHER than the quoted 2.00% discount rate.\n\nWhy the true yield is higher: (1) you divide the gain by the smaller price ($99.50), not the face ($100), and (2) you annualize over 365 days, not 360. Both effects push the true yield above the quoted discount rate.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (2.00%): That's the quoted discount rate, which always understates the true yield.\n- C (1.96%): Lower than the discount rate - wrong direction.\n- D (2.50%): Overstates the yield; uses wrong inputs.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The quoted T-bill discount rate is NOT the yield - the true yield is always a bit higher because the gain is divided by price (not face) and uses a 365-day year.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Discount rate vs true yield\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Quoted discount rate\",\"items\":[\"gain ÷ FACE (100)\",\"360-day year\",\"= 2.00%\"]},{\"h\":\"True yield\",\"items\":[\"gain ÷ PRICE (99.50)\",\"365-day year\",\"= 2.038% (higher)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The dirty (invoice) price that a bond buyer actually pays equals:\nA) The clean price minus accrued interest B) The clean price plus accrued interest C) The face value plus accrued interest D) The quoted price, always\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what makes up the invoice price.\n\nCoupon bonds are QUOTED clean (without accrued interest), but the buyer actually pays the dirty price, which adds the interest that has built up since the last coupon:\n\n$$\\text{Dirty} = \\text{Clean} + \\text{Accrued interest}$$\n\nWhy? Whoever holds the bond on the coupon date receives the FULL coupon, even interest earned by the previous owner. So the buyer compensates the seller for the days the seller held the bond by adding accrued interest.\n\nAnalogy: If you buy a fruit tree halfway through the season, you pay a bit extra for the fruit that's already grown but not yet picked - that's the accrued interest.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Subtracting is backward - accrued interest is ADDED, not removed.\n- C: It's clean PRICE plus accrued, not face value plus accrued.\n- D: The quoted (clean) price is not what you pay; you pay the dirty price.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Quoted = clean; paid = dirty = clean + accrued interest. US Treasuries use actual/actual for the accrual fraction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Clean vs Dirty price\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Clean (quoted)\",\"items\":[\"what is quoted\",\"excludes accrued interest\"]},{\"h\":\"Dirty (invoice, paid)\",\"items\":[\"clean + accrued interest\",\"what the buyer actually pays\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Bond A is a 6-month bond paying a 4% annual coupon semiannually, priced at 100.98. The single cash flow at t = 0.5 is 102 (coupon of 2 plus principal of 100). What is the 6-month discount factor d(0.5)?\nA) 0.9900 B) 0.9803 C) 1.0102 D) 0.9706\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks you to extract a discount factor from a bond price. A discount factor d(t) is the price today of $1 delivered at time t.\n\nThe bond has just ONE cash flow of 102 at t = 0.5, and its price is 100.98. So:\n\n$$102 \\times d(0.5) = 100.98$$\n\nSolve for d(0.5):\n\n$$d(0.5) = \\frac{100.98}{102} = 0.9900$$\n\nSo d(0.5) = 0.9900. This means $1 received in 6 months is worth about 99 cents today.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B (0.9803): This is roughly 100/102 - forgot the bond price is 100.98, not 100.\n- C (1.0102): This is 102/100.98 (the fraction flipped upside down). A discount factor must be BELOW 1 for positive rates.\n- D (0.9706): That's closer to a 1-year discount factor, not 6-month.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A discount factor = price / cash flow, and it must be less than 1 (and decline as maturity grows). If you get a number above 1, you flipped the ratio.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Extract discount factor from bond\",\"steps\":[\"Single cash flow 102 at t=0.5\",\"102 × d(0.5) = 100.98\",\"d(0.5) = 100.98 / 102\",\"= 0.9900 (must be < 1)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Given d(0.5) = 0.9900 and d(1.0) = 0.9705, what is the fair price of a 1-year Treasury paying a 6% coupon semiannually (cash flows of 3 at t = 0.5 and 103 at t = 1.0)?\nA) $100.00 B) $102.93 C) $106.00 D) $99.96\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to price a bond using discount factors. Any cash flow stream is priced by multiplying each cash flow by its own discount factor and summing:\n\n$$P = \\sum_t CF_t \\cdot d(t)$$\n\nThe cash flows: 3 at t = 0.5 (half of the 6% coupon on 100) and 103 at t = 1.0 (final coupon of 3 plus principal of 100).\n\nStep by step:\n- $$3 \\times d(0.5) = 3 \\times 0.9900 = 2.97$$\n- $$103 \\times d(1.0) = 103 \\times 0.9705 = 99.9615 \\approx 99.96$$\n- $$P = 2.97 + 99.96 = 102.93$$\n\nSo the fair price is $102.93.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($100.00): Assumes the bond prices at par - but a 6% coupon when rates are lower makes it trade above par.\n- C ($106.00): Just summed the cash flows (3 + 103) with no discounting at all.\n- D ($99.96): Discounted only the final cash flow (103) and forgot the t = 0.5 coupon.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Price = sum of (each cash flow x its discount factor). Don't forget the intermediate coupons - discount every cash flow on its own date.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A 1-year 6% semiannual-coupon Treasury has a fair price of $102.93 but trades in the market at $102.60. What is the correct arbitrage and the riskless profit per $100 face?\nA) Short the bond and buy STRIPS; profit $0.33 B) Buy the bond and short the replicating STRIPS package; profit $0.33 C) Buy the bond and buy STRIPS; profit $0.66 D) There is no arbitrage; the prices are close enough\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how to profit from a bond trading below its fair value.\n\nThe bond is CHEAP: it trades at $102.60 but is worth $102.93. The rule is buy low, sell (short) high.\n\n- BUY the cheap bond at $102.60.\n- SHORT the replicating STRIPS package, which is worth $102.93, receiving $102.93.\n\nNet cash today:\n\n$$102.93 - 102.60 = 0.33$$\n\nYou pocket $0.33 today. In the future, the bond's coupons exactly cover what you owe on the short STRIPS, so the future cash flows net to zero - a riskless profit of $0.33 per $100 face.\n\nAnalogy: You find a $20 bill on sale for $19.67. You buy it for $19.67 and immediately sell it for its true $20, keeping the 33-cent difference with no risk.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Reversed legs - you'd buy the bond (it's cheap), not short it. Shorting the cheap thing loses money.\n- C: Buying both costs money and creates no offsetting position; that's not arbitrage.\n- D: $0.33 is a real, exploitable gap in this idealized (no-friction) setup, so there IS an arbitrage.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Direction discipline - BUY the LOW-priced version, SHORT the HIGH-priced version. Under exam pressure candidates flip the legs; the cheap asset is always the one you buy.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Replication arbitrage (bond cheap)\",\"steps\":[\"Fair $102.93 > market $102.60 → bond cheap\",\"BUY bond at $102.60\",\"SHORT replicating STRIPS for $102.93\",\"Lock riskless profit = $0.33\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** According to the law of one price, if two portfolios produce identical future cash flows, then:\nA) They must trade at the same price, or an arbitrage exists. B) The one with the longer maturity must be cheaper. C) Their prices can differ by any amount with no consequence. D) The higher-coupon one must always be more expensive.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the meaning of the law of one price.\n\nThe law of one price says that two things delivering exactly the same future cash flows must cost the same today. If they don't, you can buy the cheap one, short the expensive one, and lock in a riskless profit with zero net future obligation - so the mispricing should not survive.\n\nAnalogy: Two identical bottles of water in the same shop must cost the same. If one were cheaper, everyone would buy the cheap one and resell at the higher price until the gap closed.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B: Maturity doesn't decide price here; identical cash flows means identical price regardless of how the portfolios are built.\n- C: Prices canNOT differ freely - a difference is precisely an arbitrage opportunity.\n- D: Coupon size is irrelevant if the total cash flows are identical; what matters is the cash flows, not the labels.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Law of one price = identical cash flows -> identical price. Any gap is an arbitrage (buy cheap, short expensive).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Law of one price\",\"options\":[\"Identical cash flows must trade at same price or arbitrage exists\",\"Longer maturity must be cheaper\",\"Prices can differ by any amount\",\"Higher-coupon one must cost more\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Same future cash flows → same price today; any gap is a riskless arbitrage.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Treasury STRIPS are best described as:\nA) Coupon-paying Treasury bonds with extra liquidity. B) Single-payment zero-coupon claims created by splitting a Treasury's coupons and principal. C) Floating-rate Treasury notes. D) Bonds that pay only interest and never return principal.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what STRIPS are.\n\nSTRIPS are created by splitting a Treasury bond into its individual payments. Each coupon becomes a C-STRIP and the final principal becomes a P-STRIP. Each piece is a zero-coupon claim - it makes exactly ONE payment at one date. Because of this, a STRIP's price IS a discount factor (times 100).\n\nAnalogy: A coupon bond is like a booklet of dated gift cards. Stripping it tears out each card so it can be sold separately, and each card pays once on its own date.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: STRIPS are zero-coupon (single payment), not coupon-paying.\n- C: They are fixed single payments, not floating-rate notes.\n- D: This is false - P-STRIPS return principal, and C-STRIPS pay a (former) coupon amount; each is just a single payment.\n\n📌 Exam tip: STRIPS = zero-coupon single-payment Treasuries. C-STRIPS (from coupons), P-STRIPS (from principal). Their prices equal discount factors x 100. Stripping and reconstitution keep prices consistent.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Treasury STRIPS\",\"root\":\"Coupon Treasury bond\",\"children\":[\"C-STRIPS (each coupon → zero)\",\"P-STRIPS (principal → zero)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A 180-day Treasury bill is quoted at a 3.20% discount rate. Its price per $100 of face value is:\nA) $98.40 B) $96.80 C) $98.42 D) $96.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for a T-bill price from its discount rate.\n\nThe formula is:\n\n$$P = F\\left(1 - d \\cdot \\frac{n}{360}\\right)$$\n\nwith F = 100, d = 0.032, n = 180.\n\nStep by step:\n- $$\\frac{180}{360} = 0.5$$\n- $$0.032 \\times 0.5 = 0.016$$\n- $$1 - 0.016 = 0.984$$\n- $$P = 100 \\times 0.984 = 98.40$$\n\nSo the price is $98.40.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B ($96.80): Used the full 3.20% with no day fraction (0.032 x 100 = 3.20 discount), forgetting 180/360.\n- C ($98.42): Used 365 instead of 360 in the day fraction, giving a slightly off number.\n- D ($96.40): Doubled the discount or used the wrong day count.\n\n📌 Exam tip: T-bill price uses face x (1 - d x n/360). The 180-day bill has a half-year fraction, so the discount is half the annual rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Why do apparent arbitrage opportunities between a bond and its replicating STRIPS sometimes persist in the real world?\nA) Because the law of one price does not apply to Treasuries. B) Because of frictions such as bid-ask spreads, financing (repo) costs, and liquidity premia. C) Because STRIPS are riskier than the underlying bond. D) Because Treasury prices are randomly set and never reflect value.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks why small mispricings can survive.\n\nIn an idealized world, arbitrage would close any gap instantly. But in the real world there are costs and frictions: bid-ask spreads, the cost of financing positions in the repo market, balance-sheet and capital charges, and liquidity premia (for example, on-the-run Treasuries trade rich to off-the-run ones). If the apparent mispricing is smaller than these round-trip costs, it simply isn't worth exploiting - so it survives.\n\nAnalogy: You spot a $20 bill selling for $19.95 in a city across the country. The 5-cent profit isn't worth the airfare, so the deal sits there unexploited.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: The law of one price still applies; frictions just cap how tightly it holds.\n- C: STRIPS are also default-free Treasury claims - not riskier than the bond.\n- D: Treasury prices do reflect value; they just include liquidity and financing effects.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Real-world frictions (bid-ask, repo financing, capital charges, liquidity premia) mean a mispricing smaller than round-trip costs is NOT exploitable. On-the-run bonds trade rich to off-the-run.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why mispricings persist\",\"options\":[\"Law of one price does not apply\",\"Frictions: bid-ask, repo costs, liquidity premia\",\"STRIPS are riskier than the bond\",\"Treasury prices are random\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"If the gap is smaller than round-trip frictions, it is not worth exploiting and survives.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A 180-day T-bill quoted at a 3.20% discount rate has a price (per \\$100) of:\nA) \\$98.40 B) \\$96.80 C) \\$98.42 D) \\$98.36\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** P = 100(1 − 0.032 × 180/360) = 100(1 − 0.016) = 98.40.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A 1-year STRIP trades at 96.20 and a 6-month STRIP at 98.00. The fair price of a 1-year 5% semiannual-coupon bond is closest to:\nA) \\$100.51 B) \\$101.05 C) \\$98.66 D) \\$100.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** d(0.5) = 0.98, d(1) = 0.962. P = 2.5(0.98) + 102.5(0.962) = 2.45 + 98.605 = \\$101.05. (C discounts 102.5 only; D assumes par.)\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The invoice price a bond buyer pays equals:\nA) Clean price minus accrued interest B) Clean price plus accrued interest C) Face value plus accrued interest D) Quoted price always\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Dirty (invoice) = clean + accrued.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Versus the bill's true annualized yield, the quoted T-bill discount rate is:\nA) Higher, because of the 360-day year B) Lower, because the gain is divided by face and a 360-day year is used C) Identical by construction D) Higher for bills under 6 months, lower beyond\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Both conventions bias the discount rate downward relative to the true yield.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** Discount factors are $$d(0.5) = 0.985$$ and $$d(1.0) = 0.970$$. The arbitrage-free price of a 1-year bond paying a 4% annual coupon semiannually ($100 face) is closest to:\nA) $98.94 B) $100.00 C) $100.91 D) $104.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Semiannual coupon = $2:\n\n$$P = 2 \\times 0.985 + 102 \\times 0.970 = 1.97 + 98.94 = \\$100.91$$\n\nDistractor A discounts only the final cash flow (forgets the first coupon); D fails to discount at all.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A 144-day Treasury bill with $100 face is quoted at a discount rate of 4.00%. Its price per $100 face is closest to:\nA) $96.00 B) $98.40 C) $98.42 D) $98.36\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: price a T-bill from its quoted discount rate.\n\n1. Formula: $$P = F\\left(1 - d\\cdot\\frac{n}{360}\\right)$$\n2. Plug in: $$P = 100\\left(1 - 0.04\\times\\frac{144}{360}\\right).$$\n3. Day fraction: $$144/360 = 0.40.$$\n4. Discount: $$0.04 \\times 0.40 = 0.016.$$\n5. Price: $$100(1 - 0.016) = \\$98.40.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the discount-rate convention uses a 360-day year and applies the rate to face value.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($96.00) annualizes incorrectly, applying the full 4% with no day-count fraction.\n- C ($98.42) uses a 365-day year ($$144/365$$) — the bill convention is actual/360.\n- D ($98.36) uses $$n = 148$$ or otherwise mis-counts days.\n\n📌 Exam tip: T-bill discount rate uses actual/360 and applies to *face*, not price. This price formula is linear in d — no compounding.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"T-bill price from discount rate\",\"steps\":[\"n/360 = 144/360 = 0.40\",\"d × 0.40 = 0.04 × 0.40 = 0.016\",\"1 - 0.016 = 0.984\",\"P = 100 × 0.984 = $98.40\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Using the T-bill from Q1 (price $98.40, 144 days), its true annualized investment yield (bond-equivalent, actual/365 basis) is closest to:\nA) 4.00% B) 1.63% C) 4.12% D) 3.94%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: convert the discount-rate price into a true (investment) yield and confirm it exceeds the discount rate.\n\n1. Formula: $$y = \\frac{F - P}{P}\\times\\frac{365}{n}.$$\n2. Period return: $$\\frac{100 - 98.40}{98.40} = \\frac{1.60}{98.40} = 0.016260.$$\n3. Annualize (actual/365): $$\\frac{365}{144} = 2.5347.$$\n4. Yield: $$0.016260 \\times 2.5347 = 0.04121 = 4.12\\%.$$\n\nWhy C is correct: dividing the gain by the *price paid* (not face) and annualizing on 365 days both raise the figure above the quoted 4.00%.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (4.00%) treats the quoted discount rate as the yield — the core trap; the discount rate always understates the true yield.\n- B (1.63%) is the unannualized 144-day return (0.01626).\n- D (3.94%) divides by face (100) instead of price, understating the yield.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Discount rate < true yield, always — because (i) the gain is divided by face, not price, and (ii) a 360-day year shortens annualization. Never discount cash flows with a quoted discount rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Discount rate vs true yield\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Quoted discount rate\",\"items\":[\"gain ÷ FACE (100)\",\"360-day year\",\"= 4.00%\"]},{\"h\":\"True yield\",\"items\":[\"gain ÷ PRICE (98.40)\",\"365-day year\",\"= 4.12% (higher)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Two Treasury bonds are observed. Bond A: 6-month maturity, 4% semiannual coupon, price $100.47. Bond B: 1-year maturity, 4% semiannual coupon, price $100.40. The implied discount factor $$d(1.0)$$ is closest to:\nA) 0.9852 B) 0.9650 C) 0.9608 D) 0.9750\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: bootstrap the 1-year discount factor from two bond prices.\n\n1. Bond A has one cash flow of $$100 + 2 = 102$$ at t = 0.5: $$102\\cdot d(0.5) = 100.47.$$\n2. Solve: $$d(0.5) = 100.47/102 = 0.9850.$$\n3. Bond B cash flows: $$2$$ at t = 0.5 and $$102$$ at t = 1.0. Price equation: $$2\\cdot d(0.5) + 102\\cdot d(1.0) = 100.40.$$\n4. Substitute: $$2(0.9850) + 102\\cdot d(1.0) = 100.40 \\Rightarrow 1.97 + 102\\cdot d(1.0) = 100.40.$$\n5. Solve: $$d(1.0) = \\frac{100.40 - 1.97}{102} = \\frac{98.43}{102} = 0.9650.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: bootstrap shortest maturity first, then substitute forward.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (0.9852) is $$d(0.5)$$, not $$d(1.0)$$ — solved only the first bond.\n- C (0.9608) forgets the intermediate coupon, dividing $$100.40/102 - $$ adjustments incorrectly (omits the t = 0.5 coupon cash flow).\n- D (0.9750) uses the wrong coupon (treats it as a 5% bond / mis-sums the cash flows).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Bootstrap from the front. Each new bond adds exactly one unknown discount factor; substitute previously solved ones. Discount factors must *decline* with maturity for positive rates.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap d(1.0)\",\"steps\":[\"Bond A: 102·d(0.5)=100.47 → d(0.5)=0.9850\",\"Bond B: 2·d(0.5)+102·d(1.0)=100.40\",\"102·d(1.0)=100.40-1.97=98.43\",\"d(1.0)=98.43/102=0.9650\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A Treasury bond pays a 6% annual coupon (3.00 semiannually per $100 face) and is quoted (clean) at $99.50. It is 90 days into a 182-day coupon period (actual/actual). The dirty (invoice) price the buyer pays is closest to:\nA) $99.50 B) $98.02 C) $100.98 D) $102.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: compute the dirty (invoice) price from the clean quote and accrued interest.\n\n1. Formula: $$\\text{Dirty} = \\text{Clean} + AI, \\quad AI = \\text{coupon payment}\\times\\frac{\\text{days since last coupon}}{\\text{days in period}}.$$\n2. Semiannual coupon payment: $$6\\%/2\\times100 = 3.00.$$\n3. Accrued fraction: $$90/182 = 0.49451.$$\n4. Accrued interest: $$3.00 \\times 0.49451 = 1.4835.$$\n5. Dirty price: $$99.50 + 1.4835 = \\$100.98.$$\n\nWhy C is correct: the buyer pays the clean quote plus accrued interest earned by the seller.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ($99.50) is the clean (quoted) price — what is *quoted*, not what is *paid*.\n- B ($98.02) *subtracts* accrued interest — wrong sign (dirty = clean + AI).\n- D ($102.50) adds a full coupon ($3.00) plus mis-adds, ignoring the day-count fraction.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Dirty = Clean + AI. The buyer compensates the seller for accrued coupon. When replicating/arbitraging, always compare *dirty* against *dirty* — mixing clean and dirty manufactures phantom arbitrage.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Dirty (invoice) price\",\"steps\":[\"Coupon = 6%/2 × 100 = 3.00\",\"Accrued fraction = 90/182 = 0.4945\",\"AI = 3.00 × 0.4945 = 1.4835\",\"Dirty = 99.50 + 1.48 = $100.98\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Discount factors are $$d(0.5) = 0.985$$ and $$d(1.0) = 0.965$$. A 1-year Treasury paying a 5% semiannual coupon (cash flows 2.5 and 102.5) trades in the market at $100.00. The arbitrage-free fair price and the correct trade are:\nA) Fair $101.375; the bond is cheap, so buy the bond and short the replicating STRIPS. B) Fair $101.375; the bond is rich, so short the bond and buy the replicating STRIPS. C) Fair $98.66; the bond is rich, so short the bond. D) Fair $100.00; no arbitrage exists.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: price the bond from discount factors, compare to market, and state the correct arbitrage legs.\n\n1. Fair price: $$P = \\sum_t CF_t\\cdot d(t) = 2.5\\,d(0.5) + 102.5\\,d(1.0).$$\n2. First flow: $$2.5\\times0.985 = 2.4625.$$\n3. Second flow: $$102.5\\times0.965 = 98.9125.$$\n4. Fair price: $$2.4625 + 98.9125 = \\$101.375.$$\n5. Market is $100.00 < $101.375, so the bond is **cheap by $1.375**.\n6. Trade: buy the cheap bond at $100.00; short the replicating STRIPS package (sell 2.5 face of the 6-month STRIP and 102.5 face of the 1-year STRIP, receiving $101.375). Net cash today: $$+\\$1.375$$ per $100 face; future flows net to zero.\n\nWhy A is correct: fair value exceeds the market price, and the rule is buy low / short high.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B has the right fair price but reverses the legs — the bond is cheap, not rich; you buy it.\n- C ($98.66) discounts only the final $102.5 cash flow, omitting the first coupon.\n- D assumes market = fair; it does not (a no-arbitrage trap that ignores the computation).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Direction discipline — buy LOW, short HIGH. Under pressure, candidates reverse the legs. Compute fair value, compare to market, then pick the trade that captures the positive cash difference today with zero future obligation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Arbitrage: bond cheap\",\"steps\":[\"Fair $101.375 > market $100.00 → bond cheap\",\"BUY bond at $100.00\",\"SHORT replicating STRIPS for $101.375\",\"Lock riskless profit = $1.375\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which statement about Treasury STRIPS and the law of one price is most accurate?\nA) C-STRIPS and P-STRIPS are coupon-bearing securities whose prices exceed face value. B) Each STRIP is a zero-coupon Treasury, so its price (per $100 face) equals a discount factor times 100, and stripping/reconstitution arbitrage pushes a bond's price toward the sum of its parts. C) Reconstitution converts a bond into separate STRIPS; stripping combines STRIPS into a bond. D) Because of stripping arbitrage, STRIPS and the underlying bond never deviate in price.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the correct statement on STRIPS mechanics and arbitrage.\n\nWhy B is correct: STRIPS are single-payment (zero-coupon) Treasuries, so each STRIP price *is* a discount factor (scaled by 100). Dealers can strip a bond into pieces or reconstitute pieces into a bond; this two-way arbitrage forces the bond's price toward the sum of its STRIP values.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A is false — STRIPS are zero-coupon and trade at a *discount* to face.\n- C reverses the definitions — *stripping* splits a bond into pieces; *reconstitution* reassembles pieces into a bond.\n- D overstates the arbitrage — small deviations persist (P-STRIPS of liquid bonds can trade rich, long C-STRIPS cheap) due to supply/demand, liquidity, and frictions.\n\n📌 Exam tip: STRIP price ÷ 100 = discount factor. Strip = take apart; reconstitute = put together. Arbitrage *bounds* deviations but does not eliminate them — frictions (bid-ask, repo, liquidity) leave small residual mispricings.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"STRIPS and the law of one price\",\"options\":[\"C/P-STRIPS are coupon-bearing above face\",\"Each STRIP is a zero; price/100 = discount factor; arbitrage pushes bond toward sum of parts\",\"Reconstitution splits, stripping combines\",\"STRIPS and bond never deviate\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"STRIPS are single-payment zeros; strip/reconstitute arbitrage bounds (not erases) the gap to the sum of parts.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An apparent arbitrage between an off-the-run Treasury and a STRIPS package is computed at 0.05 per $100 face. The round-trip transaction costs (bid-ask spreads plus repo financing) total 0.12 per $100 face. The most accurate conclusion is:\nA) The 0.05 mispricing is a riskless profit and should be executed immediately. B) The mispricing is smaller than round-trip costs, so it is not exploitable; such frictions (bid-ask, financing, liquidity premia) are why deviations survive. C) On-the-run bonds always trade cheap to off-the-run bonds, so the trade is reversed. D) Liquidity premia make the true mispricing larger than computed, so the profit is understated.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: judge whether a sub-cost mispricing is exploitable and why deviations persist.\n\nWhy B is correct: an apparent mispricing (0.05) smaller than the round-trip cost (0.12) cannot be monetized — executing would lose 0.07 net. Real-world frictions — bid-ask spreads, repo financing, balance-sheet/capital charges, liquidity premia, market segmentation — cap arbitrage and explain why small deviations survive.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A ignores transaction costs — net of costs the trade *loses* money, so it is not riskless profit.\n- C reverses the liquidity relationship — *on-the-run* (most recently issued) Treasuries trade *rich* (lower yield) to off-the-run because of liquidity and repo specialness.\n- D invents extra mispricing — liquidity premia are part of why the deviation exists, not a reason the computed profit is understated.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Arbitrage is only real if the gap exceeds round-trip costs. On-the-run trades RICH to off-the-run (liquidity/repo specialness). Frictions bound — but do not erase — pricing deviations.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"0.05 mispricing vs 0.12 round-trip cost\",\"options\":[\"Riskless profit - execute now\",\"Gap < costs → not exploitable; frictions are why deviations survive\",\"On-the-run trades cheap to off-the-run\",\"Liquidity premia make profit understated\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Net of 0.12 costs, the 0.05 gap loses 0.07; frictions cap arbitrage and let small deviations persist.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"410","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Interest Rates","notes":"# 10. Interest Rates\n\nSpot, forward, and par rates and the compounding conventions that connect them. GARP tests rate conversions between compounding frequencies and the spot→forward→par chain — pure calculator territory.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Calculate and compare interest rates under annual, semiannual, monthly, and continuous compounding; convert between conventions.\n- Define spot (zero) rates and relate them to discount factors.\n- Define forward rates, derive them from spot rates, and interpret them.\n- Define par rates and compute them from discount factors.\n- Explain the relationships among spot, forward, and par curves (upward/downward-sloping cases).\n- Apply a flat term structure and value cash flows under different conventions.\n- Describe maturity-matching and the use of forwards to lock in future borrowing/lending rates.\n\n## Compounding conventions\n\n\\$1 invested at quoted rate r for T years grows to:\n\n- m-times-per-year compounding: $$\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{mT}$$\n- Continuous compounding: $$e^{rT}$$\n\n**Conversions.** Equate terminal wealth. From m-compounded $$r_m$$ to continuous $$r_c$$ and back:\n\n$$r_c = m \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right), \\qquad r_m = m\\left(e^{r_c/m} - 1\\right)$$\n\nBetween frequencies m and k: $$\\left(1+\\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)^{m} = \\left(1+\\frac{r_k}{k}\\right)^{k}$$.\n\nMore frequent compounding → lower quoted rate for the same effective return: $$r_{annual} > r_{semi} > r_{monthly} > r_{cont}$$ for the same wealth outcome.\n\n### Worked example: conversions\n\n8% with semiannual compounding. (a) Continuous equivalent:\n\n$$r_c = 2\\ln(1.04) = 2 \\times 0.039221 = 7.844\\%$$\n\n(b) Annual-compounding (effective) equivalent: $$(1.04)^2 - 1 = 8.16\\%$$.\n\n(c) Monthly equivalent: $$r_{12} = 12\\left[(1.04)^{2/12} - 1\\right] = 12(1.006558 - 1) = 7.870\\%$$.\n\nSanity check the ordering: 8.16% (annual) > 8.00% (semi) > 7.87% (monthly) > 7.84% (continuous). \n\n## Spot (zero) rates\n\nThe spot rate $$z(t)$$ is the rate on a zero-coupon investment maturing at t — the rate embedded in the discount factor. Semiannual convention (standard for US fixed income):\n\n$$d(t) = \\frac{1}{\\left(1 + \\frac{z(t)}{2}\\right)^{2t}}, \\qquad \\text{continuous: } d(t) = e^{-z(t)\\,t}$$\n\nBond price = sum of cash flows discounted at *each* cash flow's own spot rate.\n\n## Forward rates\n\nThe forward rate $$f(t_1, t_2)$$ is the rate agreed today for the period $$t_1 \\to t_2$$. No-arbitrage: investing to $$t_2$$ directly must equal investing to $$t_1$$ then rolling at the forward:\n\n$$\\left(1+\\frac{z_2}{2}\\right)^{2t_2} = \\left(1+\\frac{z_1}{2}\\right)^{2t_1}\\left(1+\\frac{f}{2}\\right)^{2(t_2-t_1)}$$\n\nContinuous compounding gives the clean version:\n\n$$f(t_1,t_2) = \\frac{z_2 t_2 - z_1 t_1}{t_2 - t_1}$$\n\nInterpretation: the forward is the **marginal** rate for extending maturity. Spot rates are *averages* of forwards, so: forwards above spots ⟺ spot curve rising; the forward curve amplifies the spot curve's slope.\n\n### Worked example: spot → forward\n\n1-year spot 3.00%, 2-year spot 3.50% (annual compounding). Forward rate for year 2:\n\n$$1 + f = \\frac{(1.035)^2}{1.03} = \\frac{1.071225}{1.03} = 1.040024 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; f = 4.00\\%$$\n\nWith continuous compounding the shortcut gives $$f = (0.035 \\times 2 - 0.03 \\times 1)/1 = 4.00\\%$$ as well (small convention differences appear at more decimals). The forward (4.00%) sits above both spots — consistent with an upward-sloping curve.\n\n## Par rates\n\nThe par rate $$c(T)$$ is the coupon rate making a T-maturity bond price exactly 100. With semiannual coupons and discount-factor sum $$A(T) = \\sum_{i} d(t_i)$$ (the annuity factor):\n\n$$\\frac{c(T)}{2} \\cdot A(T) \\cdot 100 + 100 \\cdot d(T) = 100 \\;\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\; c(T) = \\frac{2\\left(1 - d(T)\\right)}{A(T)}$$\n\n### Worked example: par rate from discount factors\n\nd(0.5) = 0.9852, d(1.0) = 0.9690.\n\n$$c(1) = \\frac{2(1 - 0.9690)}{0.9852 + 0.9690} = \\frac{0.0620}{1.9542} = 3.17\\%$$\n\nA 1-year bond paying a 3.17% semiannual coupon prices at par: check 1.586(0.9852) + 101.586(0.9690) = 1.563 + 98.437 = 100.00. ✓\n\n## Curve relationships\n\n| Curve shape | Ordering |\n|---|---|\n| Upward-sloping | forward > spot > par |\n| Downward-sloping | forward < spot < par |\n| Flat | forward = spot = par |\n\nWhy par < spot when rising: a coupon bond's yield-like par rate is a blend that weights *earlier* (lower) spot rates through its coupons; a zero feels only the final (highest) rate.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Convention traps: (1) match the compounding convention of the *question* — plugging a semiannual rate into $$e^{rT}$$ silently overstates growth; (2) in semiannual problems, always divide rates by 2 and double the periods — using $$(1+z)^t$$ on a semiannual-quoted rate is the most common error in the spot/forward chain; (3) forward rates are *break-even* rates, not forecasts — realized future spots need not equal them.\n\n## Locking in rates with forwards\n\nA borrower needing funds from $$t_1$$ to $$t_2$$ can lock today's forward by going long the $$t_1$$ zero and short the $$t_2$$ zero (or via an FRA/futures). Maturity matching: choosing asset maturities to match liability dates removes reinvestment/rollover rate risk — the term-structure analog of immunization.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Terminal value of $1 under m-times-per-year vs continuous compounding.\n> **A:** m-compounding: $$\\left(1+\\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{mT}$$. Continuous: $$e^{rT}$$.\n\n**Q2.** Convert an m-compounded rate to a continuous rate and back.\n> **A:** $$r_c = m\\ln\\!\\left(1+\\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)$$ and $$r_m = m\\left(e^{r_c/m}-1\\right)$$.\n\n**Q3.** Order the quoted rates that give the same wealth outcome.\n> **A:** $$r_{annual} > r_{semi} > r_{monthly} > r_{cont}$$. More frequent compounding → lower quoted rate for the same effective return.\n\n**Q4.** Continuous equivalent of 8% semiannual.\n> **A:** $$r_c = 2\\ln(1.04) = 2(0.039221) = 7.844\\%$$. Effective annual = $$(1.04)^2-1 = 8.16\\%$$.\n\n**Q5.** Define the spot (zero) rate and relate it to the discount factor.\n> **A:** z(t) is the rate on a zero maturing at t. Semiannual: $$d(t) = \\frac{1}{(1+z(t)/2)^{2t}}$$. Continuous: $$d(t) = e^{-z(t)t}$$.\n\n**Q6.** No-arbitrage forward rate (continuous compounding).\n> **A:** $$f(t_1,t_2) = \\frac{z_2 t_2 - z_1 t_1}{t_2 - t_1}$$ — the marginal rate for extending maturity.\n\n**Q7.** Compute the year-2 forward: 1-year spot 3.00%, 2-year spot 3.50% (annual compounding).\n> **A:** $$1+f = \\frac{(1.035)^2}{1.03} = 1.040024 \\Rightarrow f = 4.00\\%$$ — above both spots, consistent with a rising curve.\n\n**Q8.** Relationship between spot rates and forward rates.\n> **A:** Spot rates are averages of forwards. Forwards above spots ⟺ rising spot curve; the forward curve amplifies the spot curve's slope. Forwards are break-even rates, not forecasts.\n\n**Q9.** Par rate from discount factors (semiannual).\n> **A:** $$c(T) = \\frac{2(1 - d(T))}{A(T)}$$, where $$A(T) = \\sum_i d(t_i)$$ is the annuity factor. The par rate makes the bond price exactly 100.\n\n**Q10.** Compute c(1): d(0.5) = 0.9852, d(1.0) = 0.9690.\n> **A:** $$c(1) = \\frac{2(1-0.9690)}{0.9852+0.9690} = \\frac{0.0620}{1.9542} = 3.17\\%$$.\n\n**Q11.** Order spot, forward, and par curves for each curve shape.\n> **A:** Upward-sloping: forward > spot > par. Downward-sloping: forward < spot < par. Flat: all equal. (Par blends in earlier, lower spots via coupons.)\n\n**Q12.** What is the most common semiannual-convention error?\n> **A:** Failing to divide rates by 2 and double the periods — using $$(1+z)^t$$ on a semiannual-quoted rate. Also: plugging a semiannual rate into $$e^{rT}$$ overstates growth.\n\n**Q13.** How can a borrower lock in a future borrowing rate, and what is maturity matching?\n> **A:** Go long the $$t_1$$ zero and short the $$t_2$$ zero (or use an FRA/futures) to lock today's forward. Maturity matching = choosing asset maturities to match liability dates, removing rollover/reinvestment risk.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A rate of 8% with semiannual compounding is equivalent to which continuously compounded rate?\nA) 8.16% B) 7.844% C) 8.00% D) 7.87%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks you to convert a semiannually compounded rate into a continuously compounded one.\n\nThe conversion formula (from m-times compounding to continuous) is:\n\n$$r_c = m \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right)$$\n\nHere m = 2 (semiannual) and $$r_m = 0.08$$.\n\nStep by step:\n- $$\\frac{r_m}{m} = \\frac{0.08}{2} = 0.04$$\n- $$1 + 0.04 = 1.04$$\n- $$\\ln(1.04) = 0.039221$$\n- $$r_c = 2 \\times 0.039221 = 0.078442 = 7.844\\%$$\n\nSo the continuous equivalent is 7.844%.\n\nWhy it is LOWER than 8%: more frequent compounding (continuous is the most frequent) needs a smaller quoted rate to reach the same growth, because the interest compounds more often.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (8.16%): That's the EFFECTIVE ANNUAL rate ((1.04)^2 - 1), not the continuous rate.\n- C (8.00%): That's the original semiannual rate, unchanged.\n- D (7.87%): That's the MONTHLY-compounded equivalent, not continuous.\n\n📌 Exam tip: To go to continuous, use r_c = m x ln(1 + r_m/m). The continuous rate is always the LOWEST for the same growth.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Quoted rate vs compounding frequency\",\"items\":[\"continuous 7.844%\",\"monthly 7.87%\",\"semiannual 8.00%\",\"|annual effective 8.16%\"],\"left\":\"more frequent → lower quoted rate\",\"right\":\"less frequent → higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A rate of 6% with monthly compounding is equivalent to a continuously compounded rate closest to:\nA) 6.00% B) 5.985% C) 6.17% D) 5.84%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the continuous equivalent of a monthly-compounded 6%.\n\nUse the same conversion with m = 12:\n\n$$r_c = m \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right) = 12 \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)$$\n\nStep by step:\n- $$\\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005$$\n- $$1 + 0.005 = 1.005$$\n- $$\\ln(1.005) = 0.0049875$$\n- $$r_c = 12 \\times 0.0049875 = 0.059850 = 5.985\\%$$\n\nSo the continuous equivalent is 5.985%.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (6.00%): That's the original monthly rate unchanged - continuous must be slightly lower.\n- C (6.17%): That's roughly the effective ANNUAL rate ((1.005)^12 - 1 = 6.17%), not continuous.\n- D (5.84%): Too low - this might come from using m = 2 instead of m = 12.\n\n📌 Exam tip: More compounding -> lower quoted rate for the same outcome. Continuous (the extreme) sits just below the monthly rate, which sits below the annual effective rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** $100 is invested for 2 years at a 5% rate with continuous compounding. The terminal value is closest to:\nA) $110.25 B) $110.52 C) $110.00 D) $110.38\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the future value under continuous compounding.\n\nThe continuous-compounding growth formula is:\n\n$$FV = P \\cdot e^{rT}$$\n\nwith P = $100, r = 0.05, T = 2.\n\nStep by step:\n- $$rT = 0.05 \\times 2 = 0.10$$\n- $$e^{0.10} = 1.10517$$\n- $$FV = 100 \\times 1.10517 = 110.52$$\n\nSo the terminal value is $110.52.\n\nAnalogy: Continuous compounding is like interest being added every instant rather than once a year, so it grows a touch faster than annual compounding.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A ($110.25): That's ANNUAL compounding: 100 x (1.05)^2 = 110.25 - thin compared with continuous.\n- C ($110.00): Simple interest, no compounding (100 + 5 + 5).\n- D ($110.38): That's SEMIANNUAL compounding: 100 x (1.025)^4 = 110.38.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Continuous compounding uses e^(rT). It always gives the LARGEST future value among compounding conventions for the same quoted rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Terminal value, same 5% quoted rate\",\"items\":[\"annual $110.25\",\"semiannual $110.38\",\"|continuous $110.52\"],\"left\":\"less frequent → smaller FV\",\"right\":\"more frequent → larger FV\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Order the following rates from highest to lowest, given they all produce the SAME terminal wealth: annual, semiannual, monthly, and continuous compounding.\nA) Continuous > monthly > semiannual > annual B) Annual > semiannual > monthly > continuous C) Semiannual > annual > continuous > monthly D) They are all equal\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how quoted rates rank when they produce identical growth.\n\nThe key idea: the MORE often interest compounds, the LESS the quoted rate needs to be to reach the same final wealth. Annual compounding compounds least often, so it needs the highest quoted rate. Continuous compounds most often, so it needs the lowest quoted rate.\n\nSo the ordering is:\n\n$$r_{annual} > r_{semi} > r_{monthly} > r_{cont}$$\n\nWe saw this with 8% semiannual: annual effective 8.16% > semi 8.00% > monthly 7.87% > continuous 7.844%.\n\nAnalogy: To fill a bucket to the same level, a faster-dripping tap (more frequent compounding) can run at a lower flow rate. More frequent = lower rate needed.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Exactly reversed - continuous should be lowest, not highest.\n- C: Jumbled and incorrect ordering.\n- D: They are NOT equal as quoted rates; they only produce the same WEALTH.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For equal terminal wealth: annual > semiannual > monthly > continuous (quoted rates). More compounding -> lower quoted rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Quoted rates for equal terminal wealth\",\"items\":[\"continuous\",\"monthly\",\"semiannual\",\"|annual\"],\"left\":\"lowest quoted rate\",\"right\":\"highest quoted rate\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The 1-year spot rate is 3.00% and the 2-year spot rate is 3.50% (both annually compounded). The 1-year forward rate covering year 2 is closest to:\nA) 3.25% B) 4.00% C) 3.50% D) 2.50%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the forward rate for the second year, given two spot rates.\n\nNo-arbitrage says: investing for 2 years directly must equal investing for 1 year then rolling at the forward rate:\n\n$$(1 + z_2)^2 = (1 + z_1)^1 \\times (1 + f)$$\n\nSolve for f:\n\n$$1 + f = \\frac{(1 + z_2)^2}{1 + z_1} = \\frac{(1.035)^2}{1.03}$$\n\nStep by step:\n- $$(1.035)^2 = 1.071225$$\n- $$\\frac{1.071225}{1.03} = 1.040024$$\n- $$f = 1.040024 - 1 = 0.040024 = 4.00\\%$$\n\nSo the forward rate for year 2 is 4.00%.\n\nIt sits ABOVE both spot rates (3.00% and 3.50%), which makes sense for an upward-sloping curve - the forward is the \"marginal\" rate for stretching maturity by one more year.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (3.25%): That's the simple average of the two spots - forwards are not simple averages.\n- C (3.50%): That's the 2-year spot, not the forward.\n- D (2.50%): Below both spots - wrong direction for a rising curve.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward = ((1+z2)^2 / (1+z1)) - 1 for this case. On a rising curve, forward > spot. The forward amplifies the curve's slope.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Forward rate from no-arbitrage\",\"steps\":[\"(1+z2)^2 = (1+z1)(1+f)\",\"1+f = (1.035)^2 / 1.03\",\"= 1.071225 / 1.03 = 1.04002\",\"f = 4.00% (above both spots)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The 1-year spot rate is 2.0% and the 18-month (1.5-year) spot rate is 2.4%, both continuously compounded. The 6-month forward rate starting at year 1 is:\nA) 2.2% B) 2.8% C) 3.2% D) 4.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks for a forward rate using continuous compounding, which has a clean shortcut formula:\n\n$$f(t_1, t_2) = \\frac{z_2 t_2 - z_1 t_1}{t_2 - t_1}$$\n\nHere $$t_1 = 1$$, $$t_2 = 1.5$$, $$z_1 = 0.02$$, $$z_2 = 0.024$$.\n\nStep by step:\n- $$z_2 t_2 = 0.024 \\times 1.5 = 0.036$$\n- $$z_1 t_1 = 0.02 \\times 1 = 0.020$$\n- Numerator: $$0.036 - 0.020 = 0.016$$\n- Denominator: $$1.5 - 1 = 0.5$$\n- $$f = \\frac{0.016}{0.5} = 0.032 = 3.2\\%$$\n\nSo the 6-month forward starting at year 1 is 3.2%.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A (2.2%): Looks like a simple average of the two spots - not the forward.\n- B (2.8%): Comes from a miscalculation of the numerator or denominator.\n- D (4.0%): Forgot to divide by (t2 - t1) = 0.5 properly, doubling the result.\n\n📌 Exam tip: With continuous compounding, forward = (z2 x t2 - z1 x t1) / (t2 - t1). It's just the \"extra\" return needed over the extra time period. Don't forget to divide by the time gap.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The spot rate z(t) relates to the discount factor d(t). Under continuous compounding, the discount factor is:\nA) $$d(t) = e^{z(t)\\,t}$$ B) $$d(t) = e^{-z(t)\\,t}$$ C) $$d(t) = 1 + z(t)\\,t$$ D) $$d(t) = z(t) \\times t$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks for the discount factor formula under continuous compounding.\n\nA discount factor is the value TODAY of $1 received at time t. To discount, you shrink a future amount back to today, which uses a NEGATIVE exponent:\n\n$$d(t) = e^{-z(t)\\,t}$$\n\nThis is the mirror image of growth: growing $1 forward uses $$e^{+z t}$$, so bringing $1 back uses $$e^{-z t}$$.\n\nQuick sanity check: with a positive rate z, the exponent is negative, so d(t) is less than 1 - which is correct, since $1 in the future is worth less than $1 today.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: A POSITIVE exponent grows money forward; that would make d(t) bigger than 1, which is wrong for a discount factor.\n- C: That's simple-interest growth, not continuous discounting, and it gives a value above 1.\n- D: That's just z times t, not a discount factor at all (and not between 0 and 1).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Discounting uses a NEGATIVE exponent: d(t) = e^(-z t). Growing forward uses the positive exponent e^(+z t). A valid discount factor is always between 0 and 1 for positive rates.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Continuous-compounding discount factor\",\"options\":[\"d(t) = e^{+z t}\",\"d(t) = e^{-z t}\",\"d(t) = 1 + z t\",\"d(t) = z × t\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Discounting shrinks future value: negative exponent gives 0 < d(t) < 1 for positive rates.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** With an upward-sloping (rising) spot curve, how does the par rate at maturity T compare to the T-year spot rate?\nA) The par rate is above the T-year spot rate. B) The par rate is below the T-year spot rate. C) The par rate equals the T-year spot rate. D) The par rate is above the T-year forward rate.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how par and spot rates rank when the curve is rising.\n\nOn an upward-sloping curve the ordering is:\n\n$$\\text{forward} > \\text{spot} > \\text{par}$$\n\nSo the par rate is BELOW the T-year spot rate.\n\nWhy? A par (coupon) bond pays coupons along the way, and those early coupons are discounted at the EARLIER, LOWER spot rates. So the par rate is a blend that gets pulled down by those lower early rates. A zero-coupon bond, by contrast, feels only the final (highest) spot rate. So par < spot when the curve rises.\n\nAnalogy: Your overall grade (par rate) is an average of all your tests; if your early tests were lower, the average is pulled below your best (final) test score (the spot rate).\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: Above would be the case for a FALLING curve, not a rising one.\n- C: They are equal only when the curve is FLAT.\n- D: On a rising curve the forward is the HIGHEST of the three, so par is below the forward, not above it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rising curve -> forward > spot > par. Falling curve -> forward < spot < par. Flat -> all equal. Par is pulled toward earlier, lower rates by its coupons.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Rate ordering (upward-sloping curve)\",\"items\":[\"par\",\"spot\",\"|forward\"],\"left\":\"lower\",\"right\":\"higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Discount factors are d(0.5) = 0.9852 and d(1.0) = 0.9690. The 1-year par rate (semiannual coupon) is closest to:\nA) 3.17% B) 6.20% C) 1.59% D) 3.10%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks for the par rate - the coupon rate that makes a 1-year bond price exactly 100 - from the discount factors.\n\nThe par rate formula (semiannual) is:\n\n$$c(T) = \\frac{2\\left(1 - d(T)\\right)}{A(T)}$$\n\nwhere A(T) is the annuity factor = the sum of the discount factors at each coupon date.\n\nStep by step:\n- Annuity factor: $$A(1) = d(0.5) + d(1.0) = 0.9852 + 0.9690 = 1.9542$$\n- Numerator: $$2 \\times (1 - d(1.0)) = 2 \\times (1 - 0.9690) = 2 \\times 0.0310 = 0.0620$$\n- $$c(1) = \\frac{0.0620}{1.9542} = 0.03173 = 3.17\\%$$\n\nSo the 1-year par rate is about 3.17%.\n\nCheck: a bond with a 3.17% semiannual coupon (so 1.586 each half-year) should price at 100:\n$$1.586 \\times 0.9852 + 101.586 \\times 0.9690 = 1.563 + 98.437 = 100.00$$ ✓\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- B (6.20%): Forgot to divide by the annuity factor (used 2 x 0.031 = 0.062 directly as a percent).\n- C (1.59%): That's the half-yearly coupon rate, not the annual par rate (forgot to multiply by 2 / mixed up).\n- D (3.10%): Used 2(1 - d) without dividing by A, or rounded incorrectly.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Par rate = 2(1 - d(T)) / (sum of discount factors). The \"2\" comes from semiannual compounding. Always divide by the FULL annuity factor (sum of ALL discount factors), not just the last one.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"1-year par rate from discount factors\",\"steps\":[\"Annuity A = d(0.5)+d(1.0) = 1.9542\",\"Numerator = 2(1 - 0.9690) = 0.0620\",\"c = 0.0620 / 1.9542\",\"= 3.17%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Forward rates derived from today's spot curve are best interpreted as:\nA) Accurate forecasts of where future spot rates will actually be. B) Break-even (no-arbitrage) rates that need not equal realized future spot rates. C) The same thing as par rates. D) Always lower than the corresponding spot rates.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks what a forward rate actually represents.\n\nA forward rate is the rate you can LOCK IN TODAY for borrowing or lending over a future period, set so there is no arbitrage between investing long versus rolling over short investments. It is a break-even rate - the rate that makes the two strategies equal today. It is NOT a forecast: the spot rate that actually shows up in the future can be higher or lower.\n\nAnalogy: If a shop offers to fix next year's price for you today, that locked price is a break-even deal based on current conditions - it doesn't promise that next year's actual market price will match it.\n\nWhy the others are wrong:\n- A: This is the classic trap - forwards are break-even rates, NOT predictions. Realized spots often differ.\n- C: Par rates are coupon rates that price a bond at 100 - a different concept.\n- D: On a rising curve, forwards are HIGHER than spots, so \"always lower\" is false.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Forward rates = break-even / no-arbitrage rates, NOT forecasts. Realized future spot rates need not equal them - GARP loves this distinction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What a forward rate is\",\"options\":[\"Accurate forecast of future spot rates\",\"Break-even (no-arbitrage) rate, need not equal realized spots\",\"Same as par rates\",\"Always lower than spot rates\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Forwards lock in today's no-arbitrage rate; realized future spots can be higher or lower.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** 6% with monthly compounding is equivalent to a continuously compounded rate of:\nA) 6.00% B) 5.985% C) 6.17% D) 5.84%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$r_c = 12\\ln(1+0.06/12) = 12\\ln(1.005) = 5.985\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** 1-year spot = 2.0%, 18-month spot = 2.4% (both continuous). The 6-month forward rate starting at year 1 is:\nA) 2.8% B) 3.2% C) 2.2% D) 4.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$f = (0.024 \\times 1.5 - 0.02 \\times 1)/0.5 = (0.036-0.020)/0.5 = 3.2\\%$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** With an upward-sloping spot curve, the par rate at maturity T is:\nA) Above the T-year spot rate B) Below the T-year spot rate C) Equal to the T-year spot rate D) Above the T-year forward rate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Par rates blend in earlier, lower spot rates via coupons → par\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q14.** With semiannual compounding, the 0.5-year spot rate is 2.0% and the 1.0-year spot rate is 3.0%. The six-month forward rate starting in six months is closest to:\nA) 2.0% B) 3.5% C) 4.0% D) 5.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With semiannual compounding, the forward must satisfy\n\n$$\\left(1 + \\tfrac{0.03}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(1 + \\tfrac{0.02}{2}\\right)\\left(1 + \\tfrac{f}{2}\\right)$$\n\n$$1 + \\tfrac{f}{2} = \\frac{1.030225}{1.01} = 1.02002 \\Rightarrow f \\approx 4.0\\%$$\n\nThe forward sits above both spot rates because the curve is upward-sloping; B (the average) is the trap — averaging gives roughly the *spot*, not the forward.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Upward-sloping curve: rate ordering\",\"items\":[\"0.5y spot 2.0%\",\"1.0y spot 3.0%\",\"6×12 forward ≈ 4.0%\"],\"left\":\"lower\",\"right\":\"higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q15.** A rate of 6.00% is quoted with semiannual compounding. The equivalent continuously compounded rate is closest to:\nA) 5.83% B) 5.91% C) 6.00% D) 6.09%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: convert a semiannually compounded rate to its continuous-compounding equivalent.\n\n1. Formula: $$r_c = m\\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right), \\quad m = 2.$$\n2. Inner term: $$1 + \\frac{0.06}{2} = 1.03.$$\n3. Log: $$\\ln(1.03) = 0.0295588.$$\n4. Multiply: $$r_c = 2 \\times 0.0295588 = 0.0591177 = 5.91\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: more frequent compounding requires a *lower* quoted rate for the same terminal wealth, so the continuous equivalent is below 6.00%.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (5.83%) over-shrinks the rate (e.g., uses $$m = 4$$ or a log error).\n- C (6.00%) leaves the rate unchanged — ignores that the convention matters.\n- D (6.09%) is the *annual effective* rate, $$(1.03)^2 - 1 = 6.09\\%$$ — the conversion in the wrong direction (toward less frequent compounding).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Ordering for the same wealth: $$r_{annual} > r_{semi} > r_{monthly} > r_{cont}.$$ Continuous is the smallest. If your \"continuous\" answer exceeds the semiannual rate, you converted the wrong way.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Quoted rates, same terminal wealth\",\"items\":[\"continuous 5.91%\",\"semiannual 6.00%\",\"|annual effective 6.09%\"],\"left\":\"more frequent → lower\",\"right\":\"less frequent → higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A rate of 5.00% is quoted with monthly compounding. Its continuously compounded equivalent is closest to:\nA) 4.99% B) 5.00% C) 5.12% D) 4.88%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: convert a monthly-compounded rate to continuous.\n\n1. Formula: $$r_c = m\\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right), \\quad m = 12.$$\n2. Inner term: $$1 + \\frac{0.05}{12} = 1.0041667.$$\n3. Log: $$\\ln(1.0041667) = 0.0041580.$$\n4. Multiply: $$r_c = 12 \\times 0.0041580 = 0.049896 = 4.99\\%.$$\n\nWhy A is correct: continuous compounding sits just below the monthly rate (monthly is already close to continuous).\n\nDistractor traps:\n- B (5.00%) ignores the convention change.\n- C (5.12%) is the *annual effective* rate, $$(1 + 0.05/12)^{12} - 1 = 5.12\\%$$ — wrong direction.\n- D (4.88%) over-shrinks the rate.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The more frequent the starting compounding, the closer the continuous equivalent. Monthly → continuous moves the rate down only slightly (5.00% → 4.99%).\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Continuously compounded spot rates are $$z(1) = 2.50\\%$$ and $$z(2) = 3.00\\%$$. The forward rate for the period from year 1 to year 2 is closest to:\nA) 2.75% B) 3.00% C) 3.50% D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: compute the 1-year forward rate starting in 1 year, continuous compounding.\n\n1. Formula: $$f(t_1, t_2) = \\frac{z_2 t_2 - z_1 t_1}{t_2 - t_1}.$$\n2. Plug in: $$f = \\frac{0.03\\times 2 - 0.025\\times 1}{2 - 1}.$$\n3. Numerator: $$0.06 - 0.025 = 0.035.$$\n4. Divide by $$(2 - 1) = 1$$: $$f = 0.035 = 3.50\\%.$$\n\nWhy C is correct: the forward is the *marginal* rate for extending maturity from 1 to 2 years; with a rising spot curve it sits above both spots.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (2.75%) averages the two spots — the forward is not the average.\n- B (3.00%) reports $$z(2)$$ — confuses the spot with the forward.\n- D (4.00%) double-counts (e.g., $$2z_2 - z_1$$ without dividing, or mis-scales).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Continuous-compounding forward is a clean weighted difference: $$f = (z_2t_2 - z_1t_1)/(t_2 - t_1).$$ Spots are *averages* of forwards, so a rising spot curve ⟹ forward above spot.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Continuous forward rate\",\"steps\":[\"z2·t2 = 0.03×2 = 0.06\",\"z1·t1 = 0.025×1 = 0.025\",\"numerator = 0.035; divide by (2-1)\",\"f = 3.50% (above both spots)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Semiannual-compounding discount factors are $$d(0.5) = 0.985$$ and $$d(1.0) = 0.965$$. The 1-year par rate (semiannual coupon) is closest to:\nA) 3.50% B) 3.59% C) 7.00% D) 3.18%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the 1-year par rate from discount factors.\n\n1. Formula: $$c(T) = \\frac{2\\bigl(1 - d(T)\\bigr)}{A(T)}, \\quad A(T) = \\sum_i d(t_i).$$\n2. Annuity factor: $$A(1) = d(0.5) + d(1.0) = 0.985 + 0.965 = 1.950.$$\n3. Numerator: $$2(1 - d(1.0)) = 2(1 - 0.965) = 2(0.035) = 0.070.$$\n4. Par rate: $$c(1) = \\frac{0.070}{1.950} = 0.035897 = 3.59\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the par rate is the coupon making the bond price exactly 100; the factor of 2 annualizes the semiannual coupon.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- C (7.00%) forgets to divide by the annuity factor — leaves $$2(1 - d(T))$$ as a percentage.\n- A (3.50%) uses only $$d(1.0)$$ in the denominator (omits $$d(0.5)$$ from the annuity).\n- D (3.18%) divides $$(1 - d(T))$$ by $$A(T)$$ without the factor of 2 (forgets to annualize the semiannual coupon).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Par rate $$= \\dfrac{2(1 - d(T))}{\\sum d(t_i)}.$$ The numerator's factor of 2 annualizes the semiannual coupon; the denominator sums *all* discount factors up to T. Drop either and you get a classic wrong answer.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"1-year par rate\",\"steps\":[\"Annuity A = 0.985 + 0.965 = 1.950\",\"Numerator = 2(1 - 0.965) = 0.070\",\"c = 0.070 / 1.950\",\"= 3.59%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** With an upward-sloping spot-rate curve, the correct ordering of the forward, spot, and par curves at a given maturity T is:\nA) par > spot > forward B) forward > spot > par C) spot > forward > par D) forward > par > spot\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: order the three curves for a rising term structure.\n\nWhy B is correct: forwards are *marginal* rates that lead a rising curve (forward > spot); spots are averages of forwards. Par rates blend in *earlier, lower* spot rates through the bond's coupons, pulling the par rate below the spot rate (spot > par). Hence forward > spot > par.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A reverses the entire ordering (that is the *downward*-sloping case).\n- C places spot above forward — wrong for a rising curve (forward leads).\n- D misplaces par above spot.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rising curve ⟹ forward > spot > par. Falling curve ⟹ forward < spot < par. Flat ⟹ all equal. The intuition: forwards lead (marginal), par lags (it averages earlier, lower coupons-weighted rates).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Rate ordering (upward-sloping curve)\",\"items\":[\"par\",\"spot\",\"|forward\"],\"left\":\"lower\",\"right\":\"higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Semiannually compounded spot rates are $$z(1) = 3.00\\%$$ and $$z(2) = 4.00\\%$$. Using the no-arbitrage relation $$\\left(1+\\frac{z_2}{2}\\right)^{2t_2} = \\left(1+\\frac{z_1}{2}\\right)^{2t_1}\\left(1+\\frac{f}{2}\\right)^{2(t_2 - t_1)},$$ the 1-year forward rate starting in 1 year (semiannual compounding) is closest to:\nA) 4.50% B) 5.00% C) 5.50% D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the forward rate under semiannual compounding (note: divide rates by 2, double the periods).\n\n1. Left side ($$t_2 = 2$$): $$\\left(1 + \\frac{0.04}{2}\\right)^{2\\times 2} = (1.02)^4 = 1.082432.$$\n2. First factor ($$t_1 = 1$$): $$\\left(1 + \\frac{0.03}{2}\\right)^{2\\times 1} = (1.015)^2 = 1.030225.$$\n3. Isolate the forward factor: $$\\left(1 + \\frac{f}{2}\\right)^{2} = \\frac{1.082432}{1.030225} = 1.050675.$$\n4. Square root: $$1 + \\frac{f}{2} = \\sqrt{1.050675} = 1.025025.$$\n5. Solve: $$\\frac{f}{2} = 0.025025 \\Rightarrow f = 0.050049 = 5.00\\%.$$\n\nWhy B is correct: the semiannual chain requires dividing each rate by 2 and using exponents of $$2t$$; the forward covers the 1→2 year period $$(2(t_2 - t_1) = 2$$ periods$$).$$\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A (4.50%) is the simple average of the two spots — ignores compounding/no-arbitrage.\n- C (5.50%) over-states (e.g., uses $$2z_2 - z_1 = 5.0\\%$$... or mis-handles the exponent).\n- D (4.00%) reports $$z(2)$$ — confuses spot with forward.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In semiannual problems, ALWAYS divide rates by 2 and use $$2t$$ as the exponent. Using $$(1+z)^t$$ on a semiannual-quoted rate is the most common error in the spot/forward chain.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Semiannual forward rate\",\"steps\":[\"(1.02)^4 = 1.082432 (2-yr leg)\",\"(1.015)^2 = 1.030225 (1-yr leg)\",\"(1+f/2)^2 = 1.082432/1.030225 = 1.05068\",\"f/2 = 0.025025 → f = 5.00%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** $100 is invested for 2 years. Which terminal value correctly pairs with its compounding convention at a 5.00% quoted rate?\nA) Annual compounding gives $110.52. B) Continuous compounding gives $110.25. C) Semiannual compounding gives $110.52. D) Continuous compounding gives $110.52.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The ask: match the correct terminal value to its compounding convention.\n\n1. Annual: $$100(1.05)^2 = 100 \\times 1.1025 = \\$110.25.$$\n2. Semiannual: $$100\\left(1 + \\frac{0.05}{2}\\right)^{2\\times 2} = 100(1.025)^4 = 100 \\times 1.103813 = \\$110.38.$$\n3. Continuous: $$100\\,e^{0.05\\times 2} = 100\\,e^{0.10} = 100 \\times 1.105171 = \\$110.52.$$\n\nWhy D is correct: continuous compounding grows fastest for the same quoted rate, giving $110.52.\n\nDistractor traps:\n- A mislabels $110.52 as annual — annual is $110.25.\n- B mislabels $110.25 as continuous — that is the annual figure.\n- C mislabels $110.52 as semiannual — semiannual is $110.38.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For the same *quoted* rate, terminal wealth rises with compounding frequency: annual ($110.25) < semiannual ($110.38) < continuous ($110.52). Match the convention in the question — plugging a semiannual rate into $$e^{rT}$$ silently overstates growth.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Terminal value, same 5% quoted rate\",\"items\":[\"annual $110.25\",\"semiannual $110.38\",\"|continuous $110.52\"],\"left\":\"less frequent → smaller\",\"right\":\"more frequent → larger\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"411","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Bond Yields and Return Calculations","notes":"# 11. Bond Yields and Return Calculations\n\nYield to maturity, realized returns, spreads, and the annuity/perpetuity toolkit. GARP tests price↔YTM mechanics, the premium/discount/par logic, decomposition of realized returns, and the reinvestment assumption buried inside YTM.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Distinguish gross vs net realized returns and compute holding-period returns with and without coupon reinvestment.\n- Define and compute the spread of a bond over a benchmark curve.\n- Define yield to maturity, compute price from YTM (and vice versa conceptually), and interpret YTM as a complex average of spot rates.\n- Explain the price–coupon–yield relationships (premium/discount/par; pull to par).\n- Use annuity and perpetuity formulas in bond calculations.\n- Explain the reinvestment assumption in YTM and its consequences for realized returns.\n- Decompose bond P&L into carry/coupon, rolldown, and rate-change components (in outline).\n\n## Realized return\n\n**Gross realized return** over a holding period:\n\n$$R = \\frac{P_{end} + \\text{coupons received} + \\text{reinvestment income} - P_{start}}{P_{start}}$$\n\n**Net** return subtracts financing cost (e.g., repo interest if the position is leveraged). Multi-period returns must track when coupons arrive and at what rate they are reinvested — the realized return equals the YTM only if coupons are reinvested *at* the YTM and the bond is held to maturity.\n\n### Worked example: realized return with reinvestment\n\nBuy a 6% semiannual-coupon bond at 100.00; hold one year; sell at 100.50. The first coupon (3.00, received at 6 months) is reinvested for 6 months at 2% semiannually-compounded (i.e., 1% per half-year).\n\nCoupon proceeds at year-end: $$3.00 \\times 1.01 + 3.00 = 6.03$$\n\n$$R = \\frac{100.50 + 6.03 - 100}{100} = 6.53\\% \\text{ (annual, not annualized further)}$$\n\nLower reinvestment rates drag realized return below the purchase yield — visible even at a one-year horizon.\n\n## Spread\n\nThe **spread** is the constant amount s added to every benchmark (e.g., Treasury) spot or forward rate so that discounted cash flows match the bond's market price:\n\n$$P = \\sum_t \\frac{CF_t}{\\left(1 + \\frac{z_t + s}{2}\\right)^{2t}}$$\n\nIt isolates the bond's value relative to the curve: compensation for credit risk, liquidity, and any mispricing. (A simpler version — yield spread — is just YTM minus the matched-maturity benchmark yield.)\n\n## Yield to maturity\n\nYTM (yield) y is the single discount rate equating PV of cash flows to price. Semiannual bond, T years, coupon c, face F:\n\n$$P = \\sum_{i=1}^{2T} \\frac{cF/2}{\\left(1+\\frac{y}{2}\\right)^{i}} + \\frac{F}{\\left(1+\\frac{y}{2}\\right)^{2T}}$$\n\nCompactly, with the annuity factor:\n\n$$P = \\frac{cF}{y}\\left[1 - \\frac{1}{(1+\\tfrac{y}{2})^{2T}}\\right] + \\frac{F}{(1+\\tfrac{y}{2})^{2T}}$$\n\nYTM is a cash-flow-weighted \"complex average\" of spot rates — it is bond-specific (two bonds with the same maturity but different coupons have different YTMs when the curve isn't flat).\n\n**Properties:**\n\n| Condition | Price |\n|---|---|\n| coupon rate > YTM | Premium (P > 100) |\n| coupon rate = YTM | Par (P = 100) |\n| coupon rate < YTM | Discount (P < 100) |\n\n**Pull to par:** holding yield constant, a premium bond's price drifts *down* to 100 and a discount bond's *up* to 100 as maturity approaches.\n\n### Worked example: price from YTM\n\n2-year, 6% semiannual coupon, \\$100 face, YTM 5%. Per-period rate 2.5%, 4 periods, coupon 3.\n\n$$(1.025)^4 = 1.103813 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (1.025)^{-4} = 0.905951$$\n\nAnnuity factor: $$\\frac{1 - 0.905951}{0.025} = 3.76197$$\n\n$$P = 3 \\times 3.76197 + 100 \\times 0.905951 = 11.286 + 90.595 = \\$101.88$$\n\nPremium, as expected: coupon 6% > yield 5%. Calculator check (TI BA II+): N=4, I/Y=2.5, PMT=3, FV=100 → PV = −101.88.\n\n### Worked example: perpetuity and annuity\n\nA perpetuity pays \\$4 per year; comparable yields are 5% (annual):\n\n$$P = \\frac{C}{y} = \\frac{4}{0.05} = \\$80$$\n\nIf yields fall to 4%, P = 100 — a 25% gain, illustrating the extreme duration of perpetuities. An annuity paying \\$4 for 10 years at 5%: $$P = 4 \\times \\frac{1 - 1.05^{-10}}{0.05} = 4 \\times 7.7217 = \\$30.89$$.\n\n## The reinvestment assumption\n\nA bond's realized compound return equals its purchase YTM only if **every coupon is reinvested at the YTM** until maturity. If reinvestment rates are lower → realized < YTM; higher → realized > YTM.\n\n- Short horizon: dominated by **price risk** (rates up → loss).\n- Long horizon: dominated by **reinvestment risk** (rates up → coupons compound faster → gain).\n- At the horizon equal to the bond's **duration**, the two roughly offset — the basis of immunization.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> YTM is **not** a promised realized return. It is a quoting convention. Zero-coupon bonds are the exception: no coupons → no reinvestment assumption → YTM held to maturity *is* the realized return. Also: a semiannual YTM of 5% means 2.5% per period — annualizing by compounding gives an effective annual 5.06%; mixing bond-equivalent and effective yields is a recurring distractor.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> P&L decomposition intuition: return = coupon income (carry) + rolldown (price change from sliding along an unchanged curve) + impact of curve changes + spread change. Questions usually ask which component remains if \"the curve is unchanged\" — carry + rolldown.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Gross realized return over a holding period.\n> **A:** $$R = \\frac{P_{end} + \\text{coupons} + \\text{reinvestment income} - P_{start}}{P_{start}}$$. Net return subtracts financing cost (e.g., repo interest).\n\n**Q2.** When does realized return equal the purchase YTM?\n> **A:** Only if every coupon is reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity. Lower reinvestment rates drag realized return below YTM.\n\n**Q3.** Define a bond's spread over a benchmark curve.\n> **A:** The constant s added to every benchmark spot/forward rate so discounted cash flows match market price: $$P = \\sum_t \\frac{CF_t}{(1+(z_t+s)/2)^{2t}}$$. It isolates credit/liquidity/mispricing.\n\n**Q4.** Define yield to maturity.\n> **A:** The single discount rate equating PV of cash flows to price. It is a cash-flow-weighted \"complex average\" of spot rates and is bond-specific (coupon-dependent when the curve isn't flat).\n\n**Q5.** Premium / par / discount conditions.\n> **A:** Coupon rate > YTM → premium (P > 100). Coupon = YTM → par. Coupon < YTM → discount (P < 100).\n\n**Q6.** What is \"pull to par\"?\n> **A:** Holding yield constant, a premium bond's price drifts down to 100 and a discount bond's up to 100 as maturity approaches.\n\n**Q7.** Price a 2-year 6% semiannual bond at 5% YTM.\n> **A:** Period rate 2.5%, 4 periods, coupon 3. Annuity factor = $$\\frac{1-1.025^{-4}}{0.025} = 3.762$$. $$P = 3(3.762) + 100(0.90595) = 11.29 + 90.60 = \\$101.88$$ (premium).\n\n**Q8.** Perpetuity price formula and example.\n> **A:** $$P = \\frac{C}{y}$$. A $4 perpetuity at 5% → $80; at 4% → $100 (a 25% gain illustrates perpetuities' extreme duration).\n\n**Q9.** Annuity price formula.\n> **A:** $$P = C\\times\\frac{1 - (1+y)^{-n}}{y}$$. Example: $4 for 10 years at 5% → $$4\\times7.7217 = \\$30.89$$.\n\n**Q10.** Contrast price risk and reinvestment risk by horizon.\n> **A:** Short horizon: price risk dominates (rates up → loss). Long horizon: reinvestment risk dominates (rates up → coupons compound faster → gain). They roughly offset at a horizon equal to duration (immunization).\n\n**Q11.** Why is the zero-coupon bond the exception to the reinvestment assumption?\n> **A:** No coupons → no reinvestment assumption → YTM held to maturity is exactly the realized return. YTM is otherwise a quoting convention, not a promised return.\n\n**Q12.** Bond-equivalent vs effective annual yield for a 5% semiannual YTM.\n> **A:** 5% semiannual = 2.5% per period; effective annual = $$(1.025)^2 - 1 = 5.06\\%$$. Mixing the two is a recurring distractor.\n\n**Q13.** Decompose bond P&L; which components remain if the curve is unchanged?\n> **A:** Return = coupon income (carry) + rolldown + curve-change impact + spread change. If the curve is unchanged, only carry + rolldown remain.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A 2-year bond with a 6% semiannual coupon and \\$100 face value is priced to yield 5% (YTM). The per-period (semiannual) discount rate is 2.5% and there are 4 periods. Given $$(1.025)^{-4} = 0.905951$$, the price is closest to:\nA) \\$98.12 B) \\$100.00 C) \\$101.88 D) \\$103.76\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: what is the bond's price when its coupon rate (6%) is higher than its yield (5%)?\n\nStep 1 — set up the pieces. Coupon per period = $$6\\% \\times 100 / 2 = \\$3$$. There are 4 periods. The per-period yield = $$5\\% / 2 = 2.5\\%$$.\n\nStep 2 — value the coupons (an annuity). The annuity factor is:\n$$\\frac{1 - (1.025)^{-4}}{0.025} = \\frac{1 - 0.905951}{0.025} = \\frac{0.094049}{0.025} = 3.76197$$\nPV of coupons = $$3 \\times 3.76197 = 11.286$$.\n\nStep 3 — value the face amount returned at the end:\n$$100 \\times 0.905951 = 90.595$$.\n\nStep 4 — add them: $$11.286 + 90.595 = \\$101.88$$.\n\nThis is a premium (above \\$100), which is exactly what we expect because the coupon rate (6%) beats the yield (5%) — the bond pays more than the market demands, so buyers pay extra for it.\n\n- A (\\$98.12) is a discount price — that is what you would get if the coupon were below the yield. Trap: it confuses premium and discount.\n- B (\\$100.00) is par, which only happens when coupon = yield. Here they differ.\n- D (\\$103.76) over-counts — likely double-counting coupons or mis-keying the calculator.\n\nAnalogy: a bond paying a 6% \"interest rate\" in a 5% world is like a savings account paying more than the bank next door — people will pay a premium to own it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Memorize the quick logic — coupon > yield → premium (price > 100); coupon = yield → par; coupon < yield → discount. You can often eliminate two answers before calculating.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Coupon vs yield → price\",\"items\":[\"coupon > yield → PREMIUM (>100)\",\"|par: coupon = yield (=100)\",\"coupon < yield → DISCOUNT (<100)\"],\"left\":\"price above par\",\"right\":\"price below par\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A bond is currently trading at a discount (below par). This tells you that:\nA) The coupon rate is greater than the YTM B) The coupon rate is equal to the YTM C) The coupon rate is less than the YTM D) The bond must be callable\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: what does a below-par price reveal about the relationship between coupon and yield?\n\nA discount price (below \\$100) happens when the bond's coupon is too small compared with what the market demands (the YTM). To make a low-coupon bond competitive, its price has to fall so the buyer earns the market yield through the discount.\n\n- A is the premium case (coupon > yield gives price > 100) — the opposite.\n- B is the par case (coupon = yield gives price = 100) — not a discount.\n- D is irrelevant; a discount tells you nothing about call features. Callability is a separate trap.\n\nAnalogy: if a bond pays only 3% coupons but everyone else is paying 5%, nobody buys it at full price — the seller must mark it down (discount) to attract buyers.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Discount = coupon below yield\" is a one-second answer. Don't overthink it by looking for hidden features like calls or puts.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Discount price implies\",\"options\":[\"coupon > YTM\",\"coupon = YTM\",\"coupon < YTM\",\"bond is callable\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Below-par price means coupon is too small vs the market yield, so coupon < YTM.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An investor buys a coupon bond and holds it to maturity. Her realized compound return will be HIGHER than the purchase YTM if:\nA) Coupons are reinvested at a rate below the YTM B) Coupons are reinvested at a rate above the YTM C) The bond was purchased at par D) Never — the YTM is the maximum possible return\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: under what reinvestment condition does the actual return beat the quoted yield?\n\nYTM secretly assumes every coupon you receive is reinvested at the YTM until maturity. If the actual reinvestment rate is higher than the YTM, those coupons grow faster than assumed, and your realized return ends up above the YTM.\n\n- A is the opposite: lower reinvestment rates drag the realized return below YTM.\n- C is irrelevant; buying at par does not lock in YTM as the realized return — reinvestment still matters.\n- D is false; YTM is just a quoting convention, not a ceiling on returns.\n\nAnalogy: YTM is like a recipe that assumes you can keep buying ingredients at today's price. If prices (reinvestment rates) turn out higher, your \"yield\" from baking and reselling is better than the recipe promised.\n\n📌 Exam tip: YTM = realized return only if coupons are reinvested AT the YTM and the bond is held to maturity. Higher reinvestment rates beat it; lower rates fall short. Zero-coupon bonds are the exception (no coupons to reinvest).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Reinvestment vs realized return\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Reinvest ABOVE YTM\",\"items\":[\"coupons grow faster\",\"realized return > YTM\"]},{\"h\":\"Reinvest BELOW YTM\",\"items\":[\"coupons grow slower\",\"realized return < YTM\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A perpetuity pays \\$6 per year forever. Comparable annual yields are 8%. The value of the perpetuity is closest to:\nA) \\$48 B) \\$75 C) \\$133 D) \\$60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what is the present value of a level payment that goes on forever?\n\nThe perpetuity formula is:\n$$P = \\frac{C}{y} = \\frac{6}{0.08} = \\$75$$\n\nThat is the entire calculation — divide the annual cash flow by the yield.\n\n- A (\\$48) multiplies $$6 \\times 8 = 48$$ — wrong operation (multiplied instead of divided).\n- C (\\$133) divides by a much smaller rate or inverts something; it does not match $$6/0.08$$.\n- D (\\$60) divides by 10% instead of 8% — a rate mix-up.\n\nAnalogy: if you want \\$6 a year from a bank account paying 8%, you need \\$75 in the account, because $$75 \\times 8\\% = 6$$. The perpetuity price is just \"how much principal produces this income.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Perpetuity price = payment ÷ yield. Lower yields make perpetuities worth far more (extreme duration), which is why a drop from 8% to 6% raises this from \\$75 to \\$100.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An investor buys a 6% semiannual-coupon bond at \\$100.00, holds it for one year, and sells it for \\$100.50. The first \\$3 coupon (received at 6 months) is reinvested for the remaining 6 months at 1% per half-year. The gross realized return over the year is closest to:\nA) 6.00% B) 6.53% C) 6.03% D) 0.53%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what total return did the investor actually earn, including price change and reinvested coupons?\n\nStep 1 — grow the first coupon. The \\$3 coupon sits for 6 months earning 1%:\n$$3 \\times 1.01 = 3.03$$.\n\nStep 2 — add the second coupon received at year-end (not reinvested, just collected): \\$3.00.\nTotal coupon proceeds at year-end = $$3.03 + 3.00 = 6.03$$.\n\nStep 3 — apply the realized return formula:\n$$R = \\frac{P_{end} + \\text{coupons} - P_{start}}{P_{start}} = \\frac{100.50 + 6.03 - 100}{100} = \\frac{6.53}{100} = 6.53\\%$$\n\n- A (6.00%) is just the coupon rate, ignoring the \\$0.50 price gain and the \\$0.03 of reinvestment income.\n- C (6.03%) counts only the coupons (\\$6.03) and forgets the \\$0.50 price appreciation.\n- D (0.53%) counts only the price gain (\\$0.50) plus reinvestment but forgets the coupons entirely.\n\nAnalogy: your total return is like a full grocery receipt — you must add up the price change AND every coupon AND the little interest the coupons earned, then compare to what you paid.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Realized return = (ending price + all cash received + reinvestment income − starting price) ÷ starting price. Don't drop any piece.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A zero-coupon bond is purchased to yield 5% and held to maturity. Its realized compound return will be:\nA) Above 5%, because there are no coupons to reinvest B) Below 5%, because reinvestment risk hurts it C) Exactly 5%, regardless of how interest rates move D) Impossible to determine without knowing the reinvestment rate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: how does a zero-coupon bond's realized return compare to its YTM?\n\nA zero-coupon bond pays no coupons — there is nothing to reinvest. The reinvestment assumption buried inside YTM simply doesn't apply. So if you buy at a 5% yield and hold to maturity, you are guaranteed to earn exactly 5% compounded.\n\n- A is wrong: with no coupons, there is no way to beat the locked-in yield.\n- B is wrong: there is no reinvestment risk precisely because there are no coupons.\n- D is wrong: no reinvestment rate is needed — that is the whole point.\n\nAnalogy: a zero-coupon bond is like buying a sealed gift card today for \\$78 that turns into \\$100 on a fixed date. The return is fixed the moment you buy; nothing in between changes it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Zeros are the one case where YTM = realized return (held to maturity), guaranteed. This is a favorite \"exception\" question.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Zero vs coupon bond return\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Zero-coupon\",\"items\":[\"no coupons to reinvest\",\"NO reinvestment risk\",\"realized = YTM, guaranteed\"]},{\"h\":\"Coupon bond\",\"items\":[\"coupons must be reinvested\",\"reinvestment risk\",\"realized may differ from YTM\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A bond's profit-and-loss over a period can be broken into carry (coupon income), rolldown, curve changes, and spread changes. If the yield curve is assumed unchanged over the period, the realized return comes only from:\nA) Curve change plus spread change B) Carry plus rolldown C) Rolldown only D) Spread change only\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: if rates and the curve don't move, which P&L pieces survive?\n\nTwo components do not require any change in rates:\n- Carry = the coupon income you simply collect for holding the bond.\n- Rolldown = the price change from the bond \"sliding\" to a shorter maturity along a curve that is the same shape (a 10-year becoming a 9-year, etc.).\n\nIf the curve is unchanged, the curve-change and spread-change components are zero, leaving only carry + rolldown.\n\n- A is exactly the part that disappears when the curve is unchanged.\n- C drops carry, the largest piece — you still collect coupons.\n- D keeps only the piece that is zero by assumption.\n\nAnalogy: even on a day when prices don't move at all, you still earn your \"rent\" (coupon = carry) and you still age one day closer to maturity (rolldown). Those happen no matter what.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Curve unchanged\" → return = carry + rolldown. This phrasing is a classic GARP cue.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"P&L when curve unchanged\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"SURVIVES\",\"items\":[\"carry (coupon income)\",\"rolldown (slide to shorter maturity)\"]},{\"h\":\"ZERO by assumption\",\"items\":[\"curve change\",\"spread change\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A 10-year annuity pays \\$4 at the end of each year. The appropriate annual discount rate is 5%. Given the 10-year, 5% annuity factor is 7.7217, the present value is closest to:\nA) \\$40.00 B) \\$30.89 C) \\$80.00 D) \\$77.22\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what is today's value of \\$4 received each year for 10 years at 5%?\n\nAn annuity's present value is the payment times the annuity factor:\n$$PV = C \\times \\frac{1 - (1+y)^{-n}}{y} = 4 \\times 7.7217 = \\$30.89$$\n\n- A (\\$40.00) just adds up the 10 payments ($$4 \\times 10$$) with no discounting — ignores the time value of money.\n- C (\\$80.00) treats it like a perpetuity ($$4 / 0.05$$) — but the payments stop after 10 years, so it must be worth less.\n- D (\\$77.22) multiplies the annuity factor by 10 instead of by the \\$4 payment.\n\nAnalogy: \\$4 a year for 10 years is worth less than \\$40 total, because dollars arriving later are worth less today — like a promise to pay you allowance for 10 years is worth less than handing you all 10 years at once.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Annuity PV = payment × annuity factor. Note an annuity is always worth LESS than the matching perpetuity (\\$30.89 < \\$80) because the payments eventually stop.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Two option-free bonds have the same maturity but different coupon rates. When the yield curve is upward-sloping (not flat), their yields to maturity will:\nA) Be identical, since maturity is the only thing that matters B) Differ, because YTM is a cash-flow-weighted average of spot rates and the bonds weight those spots differently C) Be identical only if both trade at par D) Differ only if one bond is callable\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: do same-maturity bonds always share the same YTM?\n\nYTM is a kind of \"complex average\" of the spot rates that apply to each of the bond's cash flows. A high-coupon bond puts more weight on earlier cash flows (which are discounted at shorter-maturity spot rates), while a low-coupon bond puts relatively more weight on the final payment (longer-maturity spot rate). When the curve is not flat, these different weightings produce different YTMs even at the same maturity.\n\n- A is the classic trap: maturity alone does NOT pin down YTM unless the curve is flat.\n- C is false; trading at par does not force two different bonds to share a yield.\n- D is irrelevant — the effect happens for plain option-free bonds.\n\nAnalogy: YTM is like the average \"speed\" of a trip made of road segments at different speed limits. Two cars covering the same total distance but spending different amounts of time on each segment end up with different average speeds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: YTM is bond-specific. Same maturity ≠ same yield unless the spot curve is flat. This is why YTM is called a \"complex average\" of spot rates.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Same maturity, different coupons (upward curve)\",\"options\":[\"Same YTM — maturity is all that matters\",\"Differ — YTM weights spot rates differently\",\"Same only if both at par\",\"Differ only if one is callable\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"YTM is a cash-flow-weighted average of spot rates; different coupons weight the spots differently, so YTMs differ when the curve is not flat.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A bond pays semiannual coupons and is quoted with a YTM of 5% (a bond-equivalent yield). The effective annual yield is closest to:\nA) 5.00% B) 5.06% C) 2.50% D) 10.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: convert a 5% semiannual (bond-equivalent) yield into an effective annual yield.\n\nA 5% bond-equivalent yield means 2.5% per half-year. Compounding two half-years:\n$$(1 + 0.025)^2 - 1 = 1.050625 - 1 = 0.050625 = 5.06\\%$$\n\n- A (5.00%) is the quoted bond-equivalent yield itself, before compounding — the trap of treating the quote as already effective.\n- C (2.50%) is just the per-period rate (half of 5%), not an annual figure.\n- D (10.00%) doubles incorrectly — confusing the quoting convention.\n\nAnalogy: getting 2.5% twice a year, where the second half-year earns interest on the first half-year's gain, is slightly better than a flat 5% — the tiny extra 0.06% is \"interest on interest.\"\n\n📌 Exam tip: Effective annual yield = $$(1 + y/2)^2 - 1$$ for a semiannual bond. Mixing bond-equivalent and effective yields is a recurring distractor — read which one the question wants.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A 3-year, 4% annual-coupon bond yields 5%. Its price is closest to:\nA) \\$97.28 B) \\$100.00 C) \\$102.78 D) \\$96.62\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** P = 4 × [1 − 1.05⁻³]/0.05 + 100 × 1.05⁻³ = 4(2.7232) + 86.384 = 10.893 + 86.384 = 97.28. Discount, since coupon\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q12.** An investor buys a bond at $98.00. Six months later she receives a $2.50 coupon and sells the bond at $99.00. Her six-month gross realized return is closest to:\nA) 1.02% B) 2.55% C) 3.57% D) 7.14%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$R = \\frac{99.00 + 2.50 - 98.00}{98.00} = \\frac{3.50}{98.00} = 3.57\\%$$\n\nDistractor B counts only the coupon, A only the price gain; D annualizes when the question asks for the six-month holding-period return.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q13.** An investor buys a 3-year, 5% annual-coupon bond at par ($100, face $100) and holds it to maturity. Each $5 coupon is reinvested at a flat 3% annual rate until the maturity date. The investor's realized annual compound return on the position is closest to:\nA) 5.00% B) 4.91% C) 3.00% D) 4.65%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compute the realized compound return when coupons are reinvested below the purchase YTM (3% vs the 5% par yield), held to maturity.\n\n1. Since the bond is bought at par, YTM = coupon = 5%. Coupons of $5 are paid at t = 1, 2, 3, plus face $100 at t = 3.\n2. Grow each reinvested coupon to maturity (t = 3) at 3%:\n   - Coupon at t = 1 grows 2 years: $$5 \\times (1.03)^2 = 5 \\times 1.0609 = 5.3045$$\n   - Coupon at t = 2 grows 1 year: $$5 \\times 1.03 = 5.1500$$\n   - Coupon at t = 3 (no reinvestment): $$5.0000$$\n3. Total terminal value = reinvested coupons + face:\n   $$5.3045 + 5.1500 + 5.0000 + 100 = 115.4545$$\n4. Solve $$100(1+R)^3 = 115.4545$$:\n   $$(1+R)^3 = 1.154545 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 1+R = 1.154545^{1/3}$$\n   $$\\ln(1.154545) = 0.143726,\\quad \\div 3 = 0.047909,\\quad e^{0.047909} = 1.04907$$\n   $$R \\approx 4.91\\%$$\n\n- A (5.00%) is the trap of assuming realized return = YTM regardless of reinvestment rate — true only if coupons are reinvested AT the YTM.\n- C (3.00%) wrongly takes the reinvestment rate itself as the total return, ignoring the par price and principal.\n- D (4.65%) overstates the reinvestment drag, e.g., by compounding the shortfall on all three coupons including the final one (which is never reinvested).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Realized compound return equals purchase YTM only when every coupon is reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity. Reinvestment below YTM drags realized return below YTM.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A 2-year bond pays a 6% annual coupon on $100 face. The benchmark spot curve is z₁ = 4.00% and z₂ = 4.50% (annual compounding). The bond trades at a market price of $101.70. The constant spread s added to each spot rate that reprices the bond is closest to:\nA) −70 bp B) +20 bp C) +70 bp D) +130 bp\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: find the constant spread s over the spot curve that makes discounted cash flows equal the $101.70 market price.\n\n1. Price on the pure benchmark curve (s = 0). Cash flows: $6 at t = 1, $106 at t = 2.\n   $$P_0 = \\frac{6}{1.04} + \\frac{106}{(1.045)^2} = 5.7692 + \\frac{106}{1.092025} = 5.7692 + 97.0673 = 102.836$$\n2. Market price ($101.70) is BELOW the benchmark-curve value ($102.836). A lower price requires a HIGHER discount rate, so the spread is positive.\n3. Solve for s. Try s = +0.70% (rates 4.70% and 5.20%):\n   $$\\frac{6}{1.047} + \\frac{106}{(1.052)^2} = 5.7307 + \\frac{106}{1.106704} = 5.7307 + 95.7800 = 101.51$$\n   Slightly low; try s = +0.60% (rates 4.60%, 5.10%):\n   $$\\frac{6}{1.046} + \\frac{106}{(1.051)^2} = 5.7361 + \\frac{106}{1.104601} = 5.7361 + 95.9624 = 101.70$$\n   So s ≈ +60–70 bp; the closest listed value is **+70 bp**.\n\n- A (−70 bp) gets the sign backward — a bond priced BELOW its curve-fair value has a POSITIVE spread.\n- B (+20 bp) underestimates the gap between market ($101.70) and benchmark ($102.84) price.\n- D (+130 bp) overshoots; at +130 bp the price would fall well below $101.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Determine the spread's sign first. Market price below the curve-implied value ⟹ positive spread (discount harder to reach the lower price); above ⟹ negative spread. Then iterate.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Two bonds have identical 10-year maturities. Bond X has a 3% annual coupon; Bond Y has an 8% annual coupon. The benchmark spot curve is upward-sloping. Which statement about their yields to maturity is correct?\nA) Both bonds have the same YTM because they have the same maturity. B) Bond X (lower coupon) has the higher YTM. C) Bond Y (higher coupon) has the higher YTM. D) The YTM ordering cannot be determined without the exact spot rates.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: rank the YTMs of two same-maturity bonds with different coupons on an upward-sloping curve.\n\n1. YTM is a cash-flow-weighted \"complex average\" of the spot rates discounting a bond's cash flows. On an upward-sloping curve, early cash flows are discounted at low spot rates and late cash flows at high spot rates.\n2. The high-coupon bond (Y) puts relatively more weight on early cash flows (its large coupons), so its YTM averages toward the lower short-end spots → lower YTM.\n3. The low-coupon bond (X) puts relatively more weight on the late principal repayment, so its YTM averages toward the higher long-end spots → higher YTM.\n4. Therefore Bond X has the higher YTM.\n\n- A is the misconception that maturity alone pins down YTM; YTM is bond-specific when the curve is not flat.\n- C reverses the logic — the higher-coupon bond has the LOWER YTM on an upward-sloping curve.\n- D is too cautious: the qualitative ordering follows from the upward slope alone.\n\n📌 Exam tip: On an upward-sloping curve, lower-coupon bonds have higher YTMs (weight on the high long-end spot). On a downward-sloping curve the ordering flips. Same maturity ≠ same YTM.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Same maturity, upward curve\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Low coupon (X)\",\"items\":[\"weight on late principal\",\"averages high long-end spots\",\"HIGHER YTM\"]},{\"h\":\"High coupon (Y)\",\"items\":[\"weight on early coupons\",\"averages low short-end spots\",\"LOWER YTM\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A 4-year, 5% annual-coupon bond ($100 face) is priced to yield 4% (annual). Using the annuity-plus-principal formula, the price is closest to:\nA) $103.63 B) $96.49 C) $100.00 D) $105.45\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute price from YTM using the annuity factor for the coupons plus the discounted principal.\n\n1. Per-period rate = 4%, periods = 4, coupon = $5, face = $100.\n2. Discount factor at t = 4: $$(1.04)^4 = 1.169859 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (1.04)^{-4} = 0.854804$$\n3. Annuity factor for coupons:\n   $$\\frac{1 - 0.854804}{0.04} = \\frac{0.145196}{0.04} = 3.629895$$\n4. Price:\n   $$P = 5 \\times 3.629895 + 100 \\times 0.854804 = 18.1495 + 85.4804 = \\$103.63$$\n\n- B ($96.49) reverses the premium/discount logic (e.g., pricing a 5% coupon at a 6% yield).\n- C ($100.00) is the par trap — par requires coupon = yield, but here 5% > 4%, a premium.\n- D ($105.45) overstates the premium, typically from using a 3% yield.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Coupon (5%) > yield (4%) ⟹ premium (price > 100). Sanity-check the premium/discount direction before trusting the arithmetic.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An investor holds a coupon bond over a 1-year horizon during which the yield curve is completely unchanged. The investor's realized return over the year consists of which components?\nA) Carry (coupon income) only B) Carry plus rolldown C) Carry, rolldown, and the impact of the rate change D) Rolldown only\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: decompose 1-year P&L into components when the curve does not move.\n\n1. The standard decomposition: return = carry (coupon income) + rolldown (price change from sliding along an unchanged curve) + impact of curve/rate changes + spread change.\n2. The curve is \"completely unchanged,\" so the rate-change and spread-change terms are both zero.\n3. What remains: carry (the coupon earned) plus rolldown (price drifts as the bond ages and rolls to a shorter maturity along a static curve).\n\n- A omits rolldown — even with a static curve, an aging bond's price changes as it moves to a different point on the curve.\n- C wrongly includes the rate-change impact, the exact term that vanishes when the curve is unchanged.\n- D omits coupon income, which is always part of carry.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Curve unchanged\" ⟹ only carry + rolldown survive. The rate-change and spread-change terms are precisely what a static curve zeroes out.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"P&L when curve unchanged\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"SURVIVES\",\"items\":[\"carry (coupon income)\",\"rolldown (aging along static curve)\"]},{\"h\":\"ZERO\",\"items\":[\"rate-change impact\",\"spread change\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A perpetuity pays $7 per year and is currently priced at $87.50. If required yields fall by 100 bp, the new price and the percentage gain are closest to:\nA) $97.22 and +11.1% B) $100.00 and +14.3% C) $116.67 and +33.3% D) $93.33 and +6.7%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: infer the current yield from the price, reprice after a 100 bp drop, and compute the percentage gain.\n\n1. Back out the current yield from $$P = C/y$$:\n   $$87.50 = \\frac{7}{y} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; y = \\frac{7}{87.50} = 0.08 = 8\\%$$\n2. Drop the yield by 100 bp: new y = 7%.\n3. New price:\n   $$P_{new} = \\frac{7}{0.07} = \\$100.00$$\n4. Percentage gain:\n   $$\\frac{100.00 - 87.50}{87.50} = \\frac{12.50}{87.50} = 14.3\\%$$\n\n- A ($97.22) uses a wrong new yield of 7.2% — $$7/0.072 = 97.22$$ — misreading the 100 bp shift.\n- C ($116.67) drops the yield to 6% (a 200 bp move) — $$7/0.06 = 116.67$$.\n- D ($93.33) drops the yield only to 7.5% (a 50 bp move) — $$7/0.075 = 93.33$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a perpetuity, invert $$P = C/y$$ to recover the current yield, then reapply at the shifted yield. Perpetuities have extreme rate sensitivity.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A 5-year zero-coupon bond ($100 face) is purchased to yield 6% (annual compounding) and held to maturity. Which statement about the investor's realized return is correct?\nA) The realized compound return depends on the reinvestment rate of intermediate cash flows. B) The realized compound return equals 6%, regardless of how rates move over the 5 years. C) The realized return equals 6% only if rates stay at 6%. D) The realized return is undefined because a zero pays no coupons.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the realized return on a zero-coupon bond held to maturity.\n\n1. A zero has no intermediate coupon cash flows, so there is no reinvestment assumption embedded in its yield.\n2. Purchase price $$P_0 = 100/(1.06)^5 = 100/1.338226 = 74.726$$. The held-to-maturity return is locked:\n   $$\\left(\\frac{100}{74.726}\\right)^{1/5} - 1 = (1.338226)^{1/5} - 1 = 6.00\\%$$\n3. With no coupons to reinvest, intervening rate moves do not affect the realized return — it equals the purchase YTM exactly.\n\n- A describes a coupon bond; a zero has no intermediate cash flows to reinvest.\n- C wrongly conditions on rates staying flat — irrelevant for a zero held to maturity.\n- D is false — the return is well-defined and exactly the YTM.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A zero-coupon bond held to maturity is the one case where realized return = YTM with certainty (no reinvestment risk). This is the exception to \"YTM is not a promised return.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Zero held to maturity: realized return\",\"options\":[\"Depends on reinvestment rate\",\"Equals YTM (6%) regardless of rate moves\",\"Equals 6% only if rates stay flat\",\"Undefined — no coupons\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A zero has no coupons to reinvest, so intervening rate moves don't matter; realized return = purchase YTM exactly.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"412","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Applying Duration, Convexity, and DV01","notes":"# 12. Applying Duration, Convexity, and DV01\n\nThe one-factor toolkit: DV01, the duration family, convexity, and how to hedge with them. This is among the most heavily calculated chapters in Part I — expect a price-change estimate, a hedge ratio, and a barbell-vs-bullet question.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the one-factor interest rate model assumption underlying DV01, duration, and convexity.\n- Define, compute, and apply DV01 and DV01-based hedge ratios.\n- Distinguish Macaulay, modified, and effective duration; state the relationships and when each applies.\n- Define dollar duration and use it for portfolio risk.\n- Compute convexity and effective convexity and use the duration-plus-convexity price change estimate.\n- Compute portfolio duration and convexity as value-weighted averages.\n- Compare barbell and bullet portfolios with equal duration and explain why the barbell has more convexity.\n\n## The one-factor assumption\n\nDV01, duration, and convexity all assume rate changes are driven by **one factor**: every yield along the curve moves by the same (usually parallel) shift. The metrics answer \"what happens if *the* rate moves Δy\" — they say nothing about steepeners, flatteners, or twists. Chapter 13 relaxes this.\n\n## DV01\n\n**DV01** (dollar value of an '01) is the price change for a one-basis-point decline in yield:\n\n$$\\text{DV01} = -\\frac{\\Delta P}{10{,}000 \\cdot \\Delta y}$$\n\nwith Δy in decimal (so 10,000·Δy converts to basis points). The sign convention makes DV01 positive for ordinary long bond positions: yields down → price up. In practice DV01 is estimated by repricing the bond at y ± 0.5bp (or ± 1bp) and taking the difference.\n\n**DV01 hedge ratio.** To neutralize a position with a hedging instrument:\n\n$$N = -\\frac{\\text{DV01}_{\\text{position}}}{\\text{DV01}_{\\text{hedge}}}$$\n\nThe minus sign means the hedge takes the opposite side: long the position → short the hedge. After hedging, the combined DV01 is zero — for a parallel one-basis-point move. The hedge does **not** protect against curve reshaping or large moves (convexity mismatch).\n\n### Worked example: DV01 hedge contract count\n\nA trader is long a bond portfolio with DV01 = \\$12,500. Treasury note futures have a DV01 of \\$85 per contract. The hedge:\n\n$$N = -\\frac{12{,}500}{85} = -147.06$$\n\nSell **147 contracts**. If the curve shifts up 5bp in parallel, the portfolio loses about 5 × \\$12,500 = \\$62,500 while the short futures gain about 5 × 147 × \\$85 = \\$62,475 — flat to within rounding.\n\n## The duration family\n\n**Macaulay duration** — value-weighted average time (in years) to the bond's cash flows:\n\n$$D_{Mac} = \\frac{\\sum_t t \\cdot PV(CF_t)}{P}$$\n\nIt is the holding horizon at which price risk and reinvestment risk offset (immunization). For a zero-coupon bond, $$D_{Mac} = T$$.\n\n**Modified duration** — percentage price sensitivity to yield:\n\n$$D_{Mod} = \\frac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m} \\qquad \\frac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D_{Mod}\\,\\Delta y$$\n\nwhere m is the compounding frequency. With continuous compounding, Macaulay and modified duration coincide.\n\n**Effective duration** — numerical, computed by shifting the *curve* and repricing:\n\n$$D_{Eff} = \\frac{P_{-} - P_{+}}{2 \\cdot P \\cdot \\Delta y}$$\n\nwhere $$P_{-}$$ and $$P_{+}$$ are prices after a downward and upward shift. Use effective duration for bonds whose **cash flows change with rates** — callables, putables, MBS — where the yield-based formulas break down. For option-free bonds, effective ≈ modified.\n\n**Dollar duration** scales by price: $$DD = D_{Mod} \\cdot P$$, so $$\\Delta P \\approx -DD \\cdot \\Delta y$$. DV01 is just dollar duration per basis point:\n\n$$\\text{DV01} = \\frac{D_{Mod} \\cdot P}{10{,}000}$$\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Duration ranking intuition: longer maturity → higher duration; lower coupon → higher duration; lower yield → higher duration. A zero is the maximum-duration bond for its maturity.\n\n## Convexity\n\nDuration is a linear (first-order) approximation; **convexity** captures curvature:\n\n$$C = \\frac{1}{P}\\frac{d^2P}{dy^2} \\qquad \\text{Effective: } C_{Eff} = \\frac{P_{+} + P_{-} - 2P}{P \\cdot (\\Delta y)^2}$$\n\nThe second-order price change estimate:\n\n$$\\Delta P \\approx -D \\cdot P \\cdot \\Delta y + \\frac{1}{2} C \\cdot P \\cdot (\\Delta y)^2$$\n\nFor an option-free bond C > 0, so the convexity term **adds** value in both directions: it cushions losses when rates rise and amplifies gains when rates fall. Duration alone therefore *overstates* losses and *understates* gains. Convexity matters more for large Δy (the term is quadratic).\n\n### Worked example: price change with duration and convexity\n\nA bond has P = \\$100, modified duration 7.0, convexity 60. Rates rise 100bp (Δy = +0.01):\n\n$$\\Delta P \\approx -7.0 \\times 100 \\times 0.01 + \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 60 \\times 100 \\times (0.01)^2 = -7.00 + 0.30 = -\\$6.70$$\n\nDuration alone predicts −\\$7.00; convexity claws back \\$0.30. For a 100bp *fall*: +7.00 + 0.30 = +\\$7.30. Note the asymmetry — gain exceeds loss, the signature of positive convexity.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Units are the classic trap. Δy must be in **decimal** (100bp = 0.01), and the convexity term needs the **½**. If convexity is quoted \"per 100\" or in per-period terms, rescale before plugging in. Also: a duration of 7 means a 1% yield change moves price about 7% — not 7 dollars unless P = 100.\n\n## Portfolio duration and convexity\n\nFor a portfolio of bonds with values $$P_i$$ and total value P, duration and convexity are **value-weighted averages**:\n\n$$D_{port} = \\sum_i \\frac{P_i}{P} D_i \\qquad C_{port} = \\sum_i \\frac{P_i}{P} C_i$$\n\nEquivalently, portfolio DV01s simply add. This holds exactly only under the one-factor (parallel shift) assumption — if the 2-year and 30-year yields move differently, a single portfolio duration misleads.\n\n## Barbell vs bullet\n\nA **bullet** concentrates in one intermediate maturity; a **barbell** splits between short and long maturities. Choose barbell weights so durations match:\n\n$$w \\cdot D_{short} + (1-w) \\cdot D_{long} = D_{bullet}$$\n\nWith equal duration and equal value, the **barbell has more convexity** — convexity rises roughly with maturity squared (dispersion of cash flows), and the barbell's cash flows are more dispersed. Consequences:\n\n- Large parallel moves (either direction): barbell outperforms.\n- The market prices this in: the barbell typically has a **lower yield** — convexity isn't free.\n- Curve steepening hurts the barbell's long leg; the comparison is only clean for parallel shifts.\n\n### Worked example: building a duration-matched barbell\n\nA trader holds \\$50 million of a bullet 10-year bond with duration 7.0 and wants the same duration from a barbell of a 2-year (duration 1.9) and a 30-year (duration 16.5):\n\n$$1.9w + 16.5(1-w) = 7.0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 16.5 - 14.6w = 7.0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; w = \\frac{9.5}{14.6} = 0.6507$$\n\nInvest 65.07% (\\$32.53M) in the 2-year and 34.93% (\\$17.47M) in the 30-year. Durations match, so small parallel shifts produce the same P&L — but the barbell's higher convexity means it outperforms for a ±100bp jump and likely carries a lower portfolio yield.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Exam shortcut: \"same duration, which has more convexity / which wins in a big parallel move?\" → barbell, both times. \"Which has the higher yield?\" → usually the bullet.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is the one-factor assumption behind DV01, duration, and convexity?\n> **A:** Rate changes are driven by one factor — every yield moves by the same (parallel) shift. The metrics say nothing about steepeners, flatteners, or twists.\n\n**Q2.** Define DV01.\n> **A:** The dollar price change for a one-basis-point decline in yield: $$\\text{DV01} = -\\frac{\\Delta P}{10{,}000\\cdot\\Delta y}$$ (Δy in decimal). Positive for ordinary long bonds.\n\n**Q3.** DV01 hedge ratio.\n> **A:** $$N = -\\frac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}}$$. The minus sign means the hedge takes the opposite side. Combined DV01 is zero only for a parallel 1bp move.\n\n**Q4.** Hedge a portfolio with DV01 = $12,500 using futures with DV01 = $85/contract.\n> **A:** $$N = -\\frac{12{,}500}{85} = -147.06$$ → sell 147 contracts.\n\n**Q5.** Define Macaulay duration.\n> **A:** Value-weighted average time to cash flows: $$D_{Mac} = \\frac{\\sum_t t\\cdot PV(CF_t)}{P}$$. The horizon where price and reinvestment risk offset; for a zero, $$D_{Mac}=T$$.\n\n**Q6.** Modified duration and the price-change approximation.\n> **A:** $$D_{Mod} = \\frac{D_{Mac}}{1+y/m}$$; $$\\frac{\\Delta P}{P}\\approx -D_{Mod}\\Delta y$$. With continuous compounding, Macaulay = modified.\n\n**Q7.** When must effective duration be used, and its formula?\n> **A:** For bonds whose cash flows change with rates (callables, putables, MBS). $$D_{Eff} = \\frac{P_- - P_+}{2P\\Delta y}$$. For option-free bonds, effective ≈ modified.\n\n**Q8.** Relate DV01 to dollar duration.\n> **A:** $$DD = D_{Mod}\\cdot P$$, so $$\\text{DV01} = \\frac{D_{Mod}\\cdot P}{10{,}000}$$ — dollar duration per basis point.\n\n**Q9.** Duration-plus-convexity price-change estimate.\n> **A:** $$\\Delta P \\approx -D\\cdot P\\cdot\\Delta y + \\frac{1}{2}C\\cdot P\\cdot(\\Delta y)^2$$. Effective convexity: $$C_{Eff} = \\frac{P_+ + P_- - 2P}{P(\\Delta y)^2}$$.\n\n**Q10.** Estimate ΔP: P = $100, D_Mod = 7.0, C = 60, rates rise 100bp.\n> **A:** $$-7.0(100)(0.01) + \\frac{1}{2}(60)(100)(0.01)^2 = -7.00 + 0.30 = -\\$6.70$$. Positive convexity cushions losses, amplifies gains.\n\n**Q11.** Duration ranking intuition.\n> **A:** Longer maturity → higher duration; lower coupon → higher duration; lower yield → higher duration. A zero is the max-duration bond for its maturity.\n\n**Q12.** How do portfolio duration and convexity aggregate?\n> **A:** Value-weighted averages: $$D_{port} = \\sum_i \\frac{P_i}{P}D_i$$, $$C_{port}=\\sum_i\\frac{P_i}{P}C_i$$. DV01s simply add. Holds exactly only under parallel shifts.\n\n**Q13.** Barbell vs bullet with equal duration: which has more convexity and which the higher yield?\n> **A:** The barbell has more convexity (cash flows more dispersed) and outperforms in large parallel moves either direction; convexity isn't free, so the barbell usually carries the lower yield (bullet higher).\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A bond has price \\$100, modified duration 7.0, and convexity 60. Using the duration-plus-convexity estimate, the price change for a 100bp (1%) rise in yield is closest to:\nA) −\\$7.00 B) −\\$6.70 C) −\\$7.30 D) −\\$6.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: estimate the dollar price change for a 1% yield increase, including the convexity correction.\n\nThe second-order formula is:\n$$\\Delta P \\approx -D \\times P \\times \\Delta y + \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times C \\times P \\times (\\Delta y)^2$$\n\nStep 1 — duration term ($$\\Delta y = +0.01$$):\n$$-7.0 \\times 100 \\times 0.01 = -7.00$$\n\nStep 2 — convexity term:\n$$\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 60 \\times 100 \\times (0.01)^2 = 30 \\times 100 \\times 0.0001 = 0.30$$\n\nStep 3 — add them: $$-7.00 + 0.30 = -\\$6.70$$.\n\n- A (−\\$7.00) uses duration only and forgets the convexity cushion. Convexity always makes the loss a little smaller.\n- C (−\\$7.30) is the answer for a 1% FALL in yield (both terms positive: +7.00 + 0.30), not a rise.\n- D (−\\$6.40) double-counts the convexity term (forgets the ½), subtracting 0.60 too much from the wrong direction.\n\nAnalogy: duration is the straight-line guess for how the price moves; convexity is the curve that bends the line in your favor — it softens losses when rates rise and boosts gains when rates fall.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Convexity ALWAYS adds value (for option-free bonds), in both directions. Loss < duration estimate, gain > duration estimate. And never forget the ½ on the convexity term.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A bond has price \\$98, modified duration 5.2, and convexity 40. Using the duration-plus-convexity estimate, the price change for a 150bp yield increase is closest to:\nA) −\\$7.64 B) −\\$7.20 C) −\\$6.96 D) −\\$8.10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: estimate the dollar price change for a 1.5% yield rise.\n\nSet $$\\Delta y = +0.015$$.\n\nStep 1 — duration term:\n$$-5.2 \\times 98 \\times 0.015 = -7.644$$\n\nStep 2 — convexity term:\n$$\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 40 \\times 98 \\times (0.015)^2 = 20 \\times 98 \\times 0.000225 = 0.441$$\n\nStep 3 — add: $$-7.644 + 0.441 = -\\$7.20$$.\n\n- A (−\\$7.64) is the duration-only estimate, leaving out the convexity cushion.\n- C (−\\$6.96) over-applies convexity (perhaps forgetting the ½ and so adding ~0.88 instead of 0.44).\n- D (−\\$8.10) goes the wrong way on convexity, subtracting it.\n\nAnalogy: same as a car's braking distance — duration is the rough estimate, and convexity is the small correction that makes the real number a touch less bad.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Watch the units. $$\\Delta y$$ must be a decimal (150bp = 0.015) and $$(\\Delta y)^2 = 0.000225$$ is tiny — that is why convexity matters more for big moves.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A trader is long a bond portfolio with DV01 = \\$12,500. Treasury note futures have a DV01 of \\$85 per contract. To hedge the portfolio, the trader should:\nA) Buy 147 contracts B) Sell 147 contracts C) Sell 85 contracts D) Buy 12,500 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how many futures contracts neutralize the portfolio's interest-rate risk, and in which direction?\n\nThe hedge ratio is:\n$$N = -\\frac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}} = -\\frac{12{,}500}{85} = -147.06 \\approx -147$$\n\nThe negative sign means take the OPPOSITE side. Since the trader is LONG the portfolio (loses when rates rise), the hedge must be SHORT — sell 147 contracts.\n\n- A buys instead of sells — that doubles the risk rather than offsetting it.\n- C confuses the per-contract DV01 (\\$85) with the contract count.\n- D treats the portfolio DV01 as a contract count — units error.\n\nAnalogy: if your boat tilts left, you lean right to balance it. The portfolio gains/loses one way, so the hedge must move the opposite way by an equal amount.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedge contracts = portfolio DV01 ÷ hedge DV01, then take the opposite side. Long bonds → short the hedge. The minus sign is telling you the direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which duration measure must be used for a callable bond, whose cash flows change when interest rates move?\nA) Macaulay duration B) Modified duration C) Effective duration D) Dollar duration\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: which duration handles bonds whose cash flows depend on rates (like callables)?\n\nEffective duration is computed by actually shifting the whole yield curve up and down and repricing the bond:\n$$D_{Eff} = \\frac{P_- - P_+}{2 \\times P \\times \\Delta y}$$\nBecause it re-prices the bond at new rates, it automatically captures the fact that a call may be exercised when rates fall — the cash flows change. The yield-based formulas (Macaulay, modified) assume fixed cash flows and break down here.\n\n- A (Macaulay) is the average time to cash flows, assuming they are fixed.\n- B (Modified) is derived from Macaulay; it also assumes fixed cash flows, so it fails for callables.\n- D (Dollar duration) is just modified duration scaled by price; same fixed-cash-flow problem.\n\nAnalogy: modified duration is like predicting a train's arrival assuming it never changes route. A callable bond CAN change route (get called early), so you need a method that re-checks the schedule at each rate level — that's effective duration.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Embedded options (callable, putable, MBS) → effective duration. Option-free bonds → modified ≈ effective, either works.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Duration family\",\"root\":\"Duration\",\"children\":[\"Macaulay (avg time, fixed CFs)\",\"Modified (Mac/(1+y), fixed CFs)\",\"Effective (reprice up/down — for embedded options)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A zero-coupon bond has a maturity of 8 years. Its Macaulay duration is:\nA) Less than 8 years B) Exactly 8 years C) Greater than 8 years D) Cannot be determined without the yield\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what is the Macaulay duration of an 8-year zero?\n\nMacaulay duration is the value-weighted average time to receive the bond's cash flows. A zero-coupon bond has only ONE cash flow — the face value at maturity. So the \"average time\" to its single cash flow is exactly the maturity: 8 years.\n\n- A is wrong; that would require some cash flows arriving earlier (coupons), which a zero has none of.\n- C is impossible; you cannot have an average time later than the only payment date.\n- D is wrong; the answer is 8 regardless of yield, because there is only one cash flow.\n\nAnalogy: if you get a single paycheck on one date, the \"average payday\" is just that date — no averaging needed.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a zero, Macaulay duration = maturity. A zero is the maximum-duration bond for its maturity, which is why lower-coupon bonds always have higher duration.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Macaulay duration of an 8-year zero\",\"options\":[\"Less than 8 years\",\"Exactly 8 years\",\"Greater than 8 years\",\"Need the yield\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A zero has one cash flow at maturity, so its value-weighted average time equals the maturity: 8 years.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Holding maturity constant, which bond has the HIGHEST modified duration?\nA) A high-coupon bond at a high yield B) A low-coupon bond at a low yield C) A high-coupon bond at a low yield D) A low-coupon bond at a high yield\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: which combination of coupon and yield maximizes duration (for a given maturity)?\n\nTwo rules push duration up:\n- Lower coupon → higher duration (more value sits in the distant final payment).\n- Lower yield → higher duration (distant cash flows are discounted less, so they weigh more).\n\nThe bond that is both low-coupon AND low-yield maximizes duration. So B wins on both counts.\n\n- A is the opposite on both (high coupon, high yield) → lowest duration.\n- C gets the yield right (low) but the coupon wrong (high).\n- D gets the coupon right (low) but the yield wrong (high).\n\nAnalogy: duration is \"how far out your money's center of gravity sits.\" Small coupons mean most of the weight is at the far end (maturity), and a low yield keeps that far-end weight heavy — both stretch the center of gravity outward.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Duration up with: longer maturity, lower coupon, lower yield. Memorize the three \"levers.\"\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Levers that raise duration\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Duration UP\",\"items\":[\"LONGER maturity\",\"LOWER coupon\",\"LOWER yield\"]},{\"h\":\"Duration DOWN\",\"items\":[\"shorter maturity\",\"higher coupon\",\"higher yield\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A portfolio holds \\$60M of Bond X (modified duration 4) and \\$40M of Bond Y (modified duration 9). The portfolio's modified duration is closest to:\nA) 6.0 B) 6.5 C) 5.5 D) 13.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: combine two bonds' durations into a single portfolio duration.\n\nPortfolio duration is the value-weighted average of the component durations. Total value = $$60 + 40 = \\$100M$$.\n\nStep 1 — weights: Bond X = $$60/100 = 0.6$$; Bond Y = $$40/100 = 0.4$$.\n\nStep 2 — weighted average:\n$$D_{port} = 0.6 \\times 4 + 0.4 \\times 9 = 2.4 + 3.6 = 6.0$$\n\n- B (6.5) is the simple (unweighted) average of 4 and 9 — ignores that more money is in the shorter bond.\n- C (5.5) miscomputes weights or mixes them up.\n- D (13.0) adds the durations instead of averaging — durations average, they don't sum.\n\nAnalogy: portfolio duration is like a class's average test score — you weight each student's score by... no, simpler: weight each bond's duration by how many dollars are in it, then average.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Portfolio duration and convexity are value-weighted averages. DV01s, on the other hand, simply add up. Don't sum the durations.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A barbell and a bullet portfolio have identical market value and identical duration. Following a large 200bp parallel downward shift in rates, the barbell will:\nA) Underperform, because of its longer cash flows B) Outperform, because it has greater convexity C) Match the bullet exactly, because durations are equal D) Outperform only if the curve also steepens\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: with equal duration, which structure wins after a big parallel move?\n\nBecause durations are equal, the first-order (duration) P&L is the same for both. The difference is convexity: a barbell spreads cash flows across short and long maturities, so its cash flows are more dispersed, giving it MORE convexity than the bullet. Higher convexity adds extra value in any large parallel move — up or down. So the barbell outperforms.\n\n- A is wrong; the longer leg is offset by the short leg to match duration, and the net effect of extra convexity is positive.\n- C is true only for tiny moves; for a large 200bp move, convexity (the curvature) creates a gap.\n- D is wrong; the barbell wins on a parallel move regardless of steepening. (Steepening would actually hurt the barbell's long leg — a separate risk.)\n\nAnalogy: two cars with the same speed (duration) but one has better suspension (convexity). On a smooth road they tie, but over a big bump (large rate move) the better-suspension car comes out ahead.\n\n📌 Exam tip: \"Same duration, big parallel move, which wins?\" → barbell (more convexity), both directions. \"Which has the higher yield?\" → usually the bullet, because convexity isn't free — you pay for it with lower yield.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Barbell vs Bullet (equal duration)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Barbell\",\"items\":[\"short + long wings\",\"MORE convexity\",\"wins on big parallel move\",\"lower yield\"]},{\"h\":\"Bullet\",\"items\":[\"one maturity (belly)\",\"LESS convexity\",\"loses on big parallel move\",\"higher yield\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A portfolio has DV01 of \\$9,000. A hedging bond has a DV01 of \\$0.072 per \\$100 of face value. The face amount of the hedging bond to short is closest to:\nA) \\$1.25 million B) \\$12.5 million C) \\$6.48 million D) \\$125 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how much face value of the hedge bond gives a matching \\$9,000 DV01?\n\nThe hedge DV01 is quoted per \\$100 face. So we need:\n$$\\text{Face} = \\frac{\\text{Portfolio DV01}}{\\text{Hedge DV01 per \\$100}} \\times 100$$\n$$= \\frac{9{,}000}{0.072} \\times 100 = 125{,}000 \\times 100 = \\$12{,}500{,}000 = \\$12.5\\text{M}$$\n\nA cleaner way: \\$0.072 of DV01 comes from each \\$100 of face. To get \\$9,000 of DV01 you need $$9{,}000 / 0.072 = 125{,}000$$ units of \"\\$100 face\" = \\$12.5M of face.\n\n- A (\\$1.25M) is off by a factor of 10 — a decimal slip.\n- C (\\$6.48M) multiplies rather than divides somewhere ($$9{,}000 \\times 0.072 = 648$$ scaled).\n- D (\\$125M) forgets that the DV01 is per \\$100, overshooting by 100×.\n\nAnalogy: if each \\$100 brick adds \\$0.072 of \"weight,\" and you want \\$9,000 of weight, you need 125,000 bricks — and 125,000 bricks of \\$100 each is \\$12.5M.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When DV01 is quoted \"per \\$100 face,\" scale carefully. Face = (target DV01 / DV01-per-100) × 100.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** DV01, duration, and convexity all rely on the same key assumption about how the yield curve moves. That assumption is:\nA) The curve steepens over time B) All rates along the curve move together by the same amount (a single-factor, parallel shift) C) Short rates are more volatile than long rates D) The curve is always flat\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what single assumption underlies DV01, duration, and convexity?\n\nThese are all \"one-factor\" measures: they assume the entire yield curve is driven by one factor, so when rates move, every point on the curve shifts by the same amount (a parallel shift). They answer \"what if THE rate moves by Δy?\" — and say nothing about the curve steepening, flattening, or twisting.\n\n- A describes a non-parallel move, which these measures specifically cannot handle.\n- C describes a curve-reshaping (volatility difference by maturity) — again outside the one-factor model.\n- D is too strong; the curve need not be flat, it just needs to SHIFT in parallel.\n\nAnalogy: these tools assume the whole staircase rises or falls as one rigid block. They can't tell you what happens if the top steps rise while the bottom steps stay put (a twist).\n\n📌 Exam tip: DV01/duration/convexity = one-factor, parallel-shift world. Non-parallel moves (steepeners, twists) require key-rate or partial '01 analysis — the topic of the next chapter.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Key assumption of DV01/duration/convexity\",\"options\":[\"Curve steepens over time\",\"All rates move together (single-factor parallel shift)\",\"Short rates more volatile than long\",\"Curve is always flat\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"These are one-factor measures: every point on the curve shifts by the same amount. They cannot capture twists or steepening.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A bond has price \\$98, modified duration 5.2, convexity 40. The estimated price change for a 150bp yield increase is closest to:\nA) −\\$7.64 B) −\\$7.19 C) −\\$6.96 D) −\\$8.10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** ΔP = −5.2 × 98 × 0.015 + ½ × 40 × 98 × 0.015² = −7.644 + 0.441 = −\\$7.20. Duration alone gives A; forgetting the ½ gives a too-large cushion.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A portfolio has DV01 of \\$9,000. A hedging bond has DV01 of \\$0.072 per \\$100 face. The face amount of the hedge bond to short is closest to:\nA) \\$1.25 million B) \\$12.5 million C) \\$6.48 million D) \\$125 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Hedge DV01 per \\$100 face is \\$0.072, so per \\$1 face it is 0.00072. Face = 9,000 / 0.00072 × … more directly: 9,000 / 0.072 = 125,000 \"hundreds\" of face = \\$12.5 million.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Effective duration rather than modified duration must be used for:\nA) Zero-coupon Treasuries B) Callable bonds C) Par bonds D) Premium bonds\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Embedded options make cash flows rate-dependent; only repricing-based (effective) measures capture that. Option-free bonds are fine with modified duration.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A barbell and a bullet have identical market value and duration. Following a 200bp parallel downward shift, the barbell will:\nA) Underperform, because of its longer cash flows B) Outperform, because it has greater convexity C) Match the bullet exactly D) Outperform only if the curve also steepens\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Same duration → same first-order P&L; the barbell's extra convexity adds value for any large parallel move, up or down.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A bond has Macaulay duration of 8.0 years and yields 6% with semiannual compounding. Its modified duration is closest to:\nA) 7.55 B) 7.77 C) 8.00 D) 8.24\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$D_{mod} = \\frac{D_{mac}}{1 + y/k} = \\frac{8.0}{1 + 0.06/2} = \\frac{8.0}{1.03} = 7.77$$\n\nDistractor A divides by $$1+y$$ (annual compounding); D multiplies instead of divides.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q16.** A bond has modified duration 7.2 and convexity 80. If its yield rises by 50 basis points, the estimated percentage price change using duration and convexity is closest to:\nA) −3.40% B) −3.50% C) −3.60% D) −3.70%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\frac{\\Delta P}{P} = -7.2 \\times 0.005 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 80 \\times (0.005)^2 = -0.0360 + 0.0010 = -3.50\\%$$\n\nConvexity always *helps* the bondholder — it is added for both up and down yield moves. Distractor C drops the convexity term; D subtracts it.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Duration vs Convexity terms\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Duration term\",\"items\":[\"−D×Δy (linear)\",\"sign follows yield move\",\"dominates for small Δy\"]},{\"h\":\"Convexity term\",\"items\":[\"+½×C×Δy² (always positive)\",\"helps for up OR down moves\",\"grows with larger Δy\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A trader is long bonds with a total DV01 of $6,800. A futures contract available for hedging has a DV01 of $85 per contract. To neutralize the position's interest rate exposure, the trader should:\nA) Buy 80 contracts B) Short 80 contracts C) Short 8 contracts D) Short 118 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Hedge ratio = position DV01 ÷ instrument DV01:\n\n$$N = \\frac{6{,}800}{85} = 80 \\text{ contracts}$$\n\nLong bonds gain when rates fall, so the hedge must lose when rates fall — short the futures. Buying (A) doubles the exposure.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"DV01 hedge\",\"steps\":[\"Compute hedge ratio = 6,800 / 85 = 80\",\"Long bonds gain when rates fall\",\"Hedge must offset → short the futures\",\"Short 80 contracts\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A bond has a price of $102.50, a modified duration of 6.4, and a convexity of 75. Using the duration-plus-convexity estimate, the dollar price change for a 120 bp increase in yield is closest to:\nA) −$7.87 B) −$7.32 C) −$8.42 D) −$6.97\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: estimate the dollar price change for Δy = +0.012 using both the duration and convexity terms.\n\n1. Formula: $$\\Delta P \\approx -D_{Mod} \\cdot P \\cdot \\Delta y + \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot C \\cdot P \\cdot (\\Delta y)^2$$\n2. Duration term:\n   $$-6.4 \\times 102.50 \\times 0.012 = -7.8720$$\n3. Convexity term:\n   $$\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 75 \\times 102.50 \\times (0.012)^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 75 \\times 102.50 \\times 0.000144 = 0.5535$$\n4. Total:\n   $$-7.8720 + 0.5535 = -\\$7.32$$\n\n- A (−$7.87) is the duration-only estimate — it ignores the convexity cushion (the +$0.55 term).\n- C (−$8.42) wrongly SUBTRACTS the convexity term (sign error) — convexity always adds value for an option-free bond.\n- D (−$6.97) forgets the ½ on the convexity term, overstating the cushion to +$1.11.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Convexity is always positive for option-free bonds, so it ADDS to value in both directions — it cushions losses when rates rise. Keep Δy in decimal and never drop the ½.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A trader is long a corporate bond portfolio with a DV01 of $18,400. She hedges with a Treasury bond whose DV01 is $0.091 per $100 of face value. The face amount of the Treasury bond she must short is closest to:\nA) $202,200 B) $20.2 million C) $2.02 million D) $167.4 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: find the face amount of the hedge bond so the combined DV01 is zero.\n\n1. The hedge must supply a DV01 of $18,400 (opposite sign, achieved by shorting).\n2. The hedge bond's DV01 is $0.091 per $100 face. Per $1 of face that is $0.00091.\n3. Required face:\n   $$F = \\frac{18{,}400}{0.00091} = 20{,}219{,}780 \\approx \\$20.2\\text{ million}$$\n   Equivalently, working in \"hundreds of face\": $$18{,}400 / 0.091 = 202{,}198$$ hundreds of face = $20.2 million.\n\n- A ($202,200) stops at the number of $100 units and forgets to multiply by 100 to get dollars of face.\n- C ($2.02 million) is off by a factor of 10 — a decimal slip in the per-$1 DV01 (using $0.0091 instead of $0.00091).\n- D ($167.4 million) wrongly divides by a much smaller DV01 or inverts the ratio.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Watch the DV01 quoting convention. \"Per $100 face\" must be converted to per $1 of face (divide by 100) before dividing into the position DV01, or work consistently in units of $100.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A trader holds $40 million of a 10-year bullet bond with modified duration 7.2. He builds a duration-matched barbell from a 2-year bond (duration 1.8) and a 30-year bond (duration 17.0), equal total market value. The fraction invested in the 2-year bond is closest to:\nA) 64.5% B) 35.5% C) 56.9% D) 43.1%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: solve for the weight w in the 2-year bond so the barbell's duration equals the bullet's 7.2.\n\n1. Duration-matching equation:\n   $$w \\cdot D_{short} + (1-w) \\cdot D_{long} = D_{bullet}$$\n   $$1.8w + 17.0(1-w) = 7.2$$\n2. Expand:\n   $$1.8w + 17.0 - 17.0w = 7.2$$\n   $$17.0 - 15.2w = 7.2$$\n3. Solve:\n   $$15.2w = 9.8 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; w = \\frac{9.8}{15.2} = 0.6447 \\approx 64.5\\%$$\n\n- B (35.5%) is the complement (1 − w) — the weight in the 30-year bond, not the 2-year.\n- C (56.9%) uses the wrong target or arithmetic, e.g., dividing 9.8 by 17.0.\n- D (43.1%) misplaces the durations (treating the 2-year as the long leg).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Set up $$w D_{short} + (1-w) D_{long} = D_{target}$$ and solve for w on the SHORT bond. Then double-check which leg the question asks about — the complement is a classic distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A barbell and a bullet portfolio have identical market value and identical modified duration. Following a sudden 200 bp parallel SHIFT DOWN in yields, which statement is correct?\nA) The bullet outperforms because it has shorter cash flows. B) The barbell outperforms because it has greater convexity. C) The two portfolios produce identical P&L because durations match. D) The bullet outperforms only if the curve simultaneously flattens.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: compare barbell vs bullet P&L for a large parallel move when durations are matched.\n\n1. Equal duration ⟹ identical FIRST-ORDER (linear) price response to a parallel move.\n2. The barbell's cash flows are more dispersed (short + long legs), giving it greater convexity. Convexity rises roughly with maturity squared.\n3. The second-order term $$+\\tfrac{1}{2} C P (\\Delta y)^2$$ is positive and larger for the barbell. For a large 200 bp move, this convexity advantage makes the barbell outperform — in EITHER direction.\n\n- A is backward — the bullet does not outperform on a large parallel move; the barbell does.\n- C ignores convexity; durations matching only equalizes the first-order term, and 200 bp is large enough for the second-order term to matter.\n- D introduces an irrelevant curve-shape condition; the question specifies a PARALLEL move, where the barbell's convexity wins outright.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Same duration ⟹ same first-order P&L; the barbell's extra convexity makes it win for ANY large parallel move (up or down). The price of that convexity is usually a lower portfolio yield.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Barbell vs Bullet (equal duration, big move)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Barbell\",\"items\":[\"dispersed cash flows\",\"MORE convexity\",\"OUTPERFORMS (either direction)\"]},{\"h\":\"Bullet\",\"items\":[\"concentrated cash flows\",\"LESS convexity\",\"underperforms\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A portfolio holds $30 million of Bond A (duration 4.0, convexity 25) and $20 million of Bond B (duration 9.0, convexity 110). The portfolio's modified duration and convexity are closest to:\nA) Duration 6.0, convexity 59 B) Duration 6.5, convexity 67.5 C) Duration 6.0, convexity 67.5 D) Duration 6.5, convexity 59\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute value-weighted portfolio duration and convexity.\n\n1. Total value = $30M + $20M = $50M. Weights: A = 30/50 = 0.60, B = 20/50 = 0.40.\n2. Portfolio duration (value-weighted average):\n   $$D_{port} = 0.60 \\times 4.0 + 0.40 \\times 9.0 = 2.40 + 3.60 = 6.0$$\n3. Portfolio convexity (value-weighted average):\n   $$C_{port} = 0.60 \\times 25 + 0.40 \\times 110 = 15.0 + 44.0 = 59.0$$\n\n- B uses equal weights (0.5/0.5) for both metrics: duration $$0.5 \\times 4 + 0.5 \\times 9 = 6.5$$, convexity 67.5.\n- C pairs the right duration with an equal-weighted convexity ($$0.5 \\times 25 + 0.5 \\times 110 = 67.5$$).\n- D mixes an equal-weighted duration (6.5) with the value-weighted convexity (59).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Portfolio duration and convexity are VALUE-weighted (by market value), not equal-weighted or face-weighted. Compute the weights once and apply them to both metrics.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Effective duration, rather than modified duration, must be used to measure the interest-rate sensitivity of which instrument?\nA) A 10-year option-free par bond B) A callable corporate bond C) A zero-coupon Treasury STRIP D) A 30-year option-free premium bond\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the instrument whose cash flows are rate-dependent, requiring a repricing-based duration measure.\n\n1. Modified duration uses a closed-form yield-based formula that assumes FIXED cash flows.\n2. A callable bond's cash flows change with rates: when rates fall, the issuer is more likely to call, truncating the cash flows. This makes the yield-based formula invalid.\n3. Effective duration is computed by shifting the curve and repricing (capturing the option), so it correctly handles callables, putables, and MBS.\n\n- A, C, D are all option-free with fixed cash flows, so modified ≈ effective duration — the closed-form measure is fine.\n- C (a zero) in fact has the cleanest closed-form duration of all ($$D_{Mac} = T$$).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use EFFECTIVE duration whenever cash flows change with rates — callables, putables, MBS. For option-free bonds, effective ≈ modified, so either works.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Requires EFFECTIVE duration\",\"options\":[\"10-year option-free par bond\",\"Callable corporate bond\",\"Zero-coupon STRIP\",\"30-year option-free premium bond\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"A callable bond's cash flows change with rates (issuer calls when rates fall), so the yield-based modified formula fails; effective duration reprices the bond.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A bond is priced at $96.40 today. When the entire yield curve is shifted up by 25 bp it is repriced at $95.13, and when shifted down by 25 bp it is repriced at $97.69. The bond's effective duration is closest to:\nA) 5.31 B) 10.62 C) 2.65 D) 5.25\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute effective duration from the up-shift and down-shift repriced values.\n\n1. Formula: $$D_{Eff} = \\frac{P_{-} - P_{+}}{2 \\cdot P_0 \\cdot \\Delta y}$$\n   where $$P_{-}$$ = price after a DOWN shift = $97.69, $$P_{+}$$ = price after an UP shift = $95.13, $$P_0$$ = $96.40, Δy = 0.0025.\n2. Numerator:\n   $$P_{-} - P_{+} = 97.69 - 95.13 = 2.56$$\n3. Denominator:\n   $$2 \\times 96.40 \\times 0.0025 = 0.4820$$\n4. Effective duration:\n   $$D_{Eff} = \\frac{2.56}{0.4820} = 5.31$$\n\n- B (10.62) forgets the 2 in the denominator (uses one-sided scaling), doubling the result.\n- C (2.65) uses Δy = 0.005 (50 bp, the full up-to-down range) instead of the 25 bp shift size in the denominator's 2·Δy convention applied incorrectly.\n- D (5.25) is a near-miss from rounding the repriced values or mis-subtracting.\n\n📌 Exam tip: In the effective duration formula the numerator is (down-shift price − up-shift price) and the denominator is 2·P₀·Δy, where Δy is the SIZE of one shift (0.0025), not the total range. Keep P₋ and P₊ straight.\n</details>\n"},{"num":"413","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Modeling Non-Parallel Term Structure Shifts and Hedging","notes":"# 13. Modeling Non-Parallel Term Structure Shifts and Hedging\n\nDuration and DV01 assume the whole curve moves together. Real curves steepen, flatten, and twist. This chapter replaces the single factor with several: key rates, partial '01s, and forward-bucket '01s — and shows how to hedge each exposure with a system of equations.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Describe the limitations of single-factor metrics (DV01, duration, convexity) when shifts are non-parallel.\n- Define key rate exposures (KR01s) and key-rate durations and compute the change in portfolio value from a set of key-rate shifts.\n- Show that the sum of key-rate durations approximately equals the effective duration (for parallel shifts).\n- Construct an appropriate multi-bond hedge for a portfolio's key-rate exposures.\n- Describe partial '01s and forward-bucket '01s and how they relate to key-rate exposures.\n- Explain volatility-weighted (regression-based) hedging in outline.\n\n## Why one factor is not enough\n\nA DV01-neutral position can still lose money. Classic case: long a 10-year bullet, short a duration-matched 2s/30s barbell. Parallel shifts net to zero — but a steepening, flattening, or curvature change moves the 2-, 10-, and 30-year legs by different amounts and produces P&L despite \"zero duration.\" Single-factor metrics:\n\n- assume all rates move in lockstep (perfect correlation along the curve);\n- hide exposures that net to zero in aggregate but are large at individual maturities;\n- give no guidance on *which* instruments to hedge with — any DV01 match \"works\" on paper.\n\nMulti-factor metrics fix this by measuring sensitivity to each segment of the curve separately.\n\n## Key rates, KR01s, and key-rate durations\n\nChoose a small set of **key rates** — commonly the 2-, 5-, 10-, and 30-year par yields. Each key-rate shift moves its own rate by 1bp, tapering **linearly to zero at the adjacent key rates** (and staying flat beyond the first and last). The sum of the four shifted curves reproduces a parallel 1bp shift.\n\nFor each key rate k:\n\n$$\\text{KR01}_k = -\\frac{\\Delta P_k}{10{,}000 \\cdot \\Delta y_k} \\qquad D_k = -\\frac{1}{P}\\frac{\\Delta P_k}{\\Delta y_k}$$\n\nwhere $$\\Delta P_k$$ is the repricing impact of shift k alone. The price change for any combination of key-rate moves:\n\n$$\\Delta P \\approx -\\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\cdot \\Delta y_k^{(bp)} \\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\frac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -\\sum_k D_k \\, \\Delta y_k$$\n\nBecause the shifts sum to a parallel shift:\n\n$$\\sum_k D_k \\approx D_{Eff} \\qquad \\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\approx \\text{DV01}$$\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> A coupon bond can have a **negative** key-rate duration. A 30-year par bond hedged… e.g., a long-maturity zero discounted off a curve fitted through par key rates can gain when an intermediate key rate rises. Don't panic if a KRD prints negative — it is a fitting artifact, and GARP has tested exactly this.\n\n### Worked example: key-rate durations of a barbell\n\nA \\$100M barbell holds 2-year and 30-year zeros and has effective duration 7.0. Its key-rate profile (2y, 5y, 10y, 30y) is:\n\n$$D_2 = 1.24,\\quad D_5 \\approx 0,\\quad D_{10} \\approx 0,\\quad D_{30} = 5.76 \\qquad \\sum_k D_k = 7.0$$\n\nCompare a 10-year bullet with the same total duration: its profile is concentrated, roughly (0, 0, 7.0, 0). Now suppose only the 30-year key rate rises 50bp:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -5.76 \\times 0.005 = -2.88\\% \\;\\; (\\text{barbell}) \\qquad 0\\% \\;\\; (\\text{bullet})$$\n\nIdentical \"duration,\" radically different outcomes — this is the steepener risk that single-factor analysis cannot see.\n\n## Hedging key-rate exposures\n\nTo neutralize n key-rate exposures you need (at least) **n hedging instruments**, chosen so each is concentrated near one key rate (e.g., on-the-run 2-, 5-, 10-, 30-year bonds). Set the hedge face amounts so that the total KR01 at every key rate is zero — a system of n linear equations in n unknowns. Hedges constructed this way are robust to *any* combination of key-rate moves, not just parallel ones.\n\n### Worked example: two-bond key-rate hedge\n\nA portfolio has KR01s of \\$500 (5-year) and \\$400 (10-year) — it loses \\$500 per bp rise in the 5-year key rate, \\$400 per bp rise in the 10-year. Hedge bonds (KR01 per \\$1M face):\n\n| | 5-year KR01 | 10-year KR01 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bond A (5-yr note) | \\$40 | \\$5 |\n| Bond B (10-yr note) | \\$0 | \\$80 |\n\nShort face amounts $$F_A, F_B$$ (in \\$M) so hedge KR01s offset the portfolio's:\n\n$$40 F_A + 0 \\cdot F_B = 500 \\qquad 5 F_A + 80 F_B = 400$$\n\nFrom the first equation $$F_A = 12.5$$. Substitute: $$5(12.5) + 80 F_B = 400 \\Rightarrow F_B = \\frac{337.5}{80} = 4.22$$.\n\nShort **\\$12.5M of Bond A** and **\\$4.22M of Bond B**. Note the order of solution: start with the equation containing only one unknown, then back-substitute — exam systems are usually triangular like this.\n\n## Partial '01s and forward-bucket '01s\n\nSwap desks use finer grids than four key rates:\n\n- **Partial '01s:** shift one *fitting instrument's* rate (e.g., each par swap rate used to build the curve) by 1bp, refit the curve, reprice. One '01 per curve input — often 10–20 buckets. The sum of partial '01s ≈ total '01.\n- **Forward-bucket '01s:** shift all **forward rates** within a bucket (e.g., years 0–2, 2–5, 5–10, 10–30) by 1bp and reprice. This expresses risk in terms of forward-curve segments — natural for instruments priced off forwards — and again the bucket '01s sum to the total.\n\nAll three approaches (key rates, partial '01s, forward buckets) are the same idea at different resolutions: decompose the curve into pieces, measure a '01 per piece, hedge piece by piece.\n\n### Choosing the resolution\n\n| Approach | What gets shifted | Typical user |\n|---|---|---|\n| Key rates (2/5/10/30) | Par yields, tent-shaped | Treasury/government portfolios |\n| Partial '01s | Each curve-fitting instrument's rate | Swap desks |\n| Forward-bucket '01s | All forwards in a maturity bucket | Forward-sensitive books |\n\nMore buckets means more faithful risk capture but more hedge instruments, more transaction costs, and noisier individual exposures. The exam tests the *concepts and the sums-to-total property*, not curve fitting.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> If a question gives a table of KR01s and a set of key-rate moves, the answer is a dot product: $$\\Delta P = -\\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\times \\Delta y_k^{(bp)}$$. Watch for one rate falling while another rises — the signs do the work.\n\n## Volatility-weighted hedging\n\nA DV01 hedge implicitly assumes the position's yield and the hedge's yield move **one-for-one**. If empirically the 20-year moves 0.9bp when the 30-year moves 1bp, scale the hedge:\n\n$$N = -\\frac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}} \\times \\beta$$\n\nwhere β is the regression slope (change in position yield per unit change in hedge yield) — a **volatility-weighted** or regression-based hedge. It minimizes P&L variance on average but introduces model risk: β is estimated from history and drifts.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Sign discipline is where these problems are lost. A *positive* KR01 means you **lose** when that rate rises (long-bond convention), so the hedge must be a *short* position with matching KR01s — or equivalently, solve for hedge KR01s equal in magnitude and opposite in sign. Decide the convention in line one and carry it through; flipping mid-solution turns a correct \\$12.5M short into a \\$12.5M long.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Why is a single factor (DV01/duration) not enough for hedging?\n> **A:** A DV01-neutral position can still lose money: it assumes all rates move in lockstep, hides offsetting exposures large at individual maturities, and gives no guidance on which instruments to hedge with. Steepeners/flatteners/twists produce P&L despite \"zero duration.\"\n\n**Q2.** What are key rates and how is each key-rate shift shaped?\n> **A:** A small set of par yields (commonly 2-, 5-, 10-, 30-year). Each shift moves its own rate 1bp, tapering linearly to zero at adjacent key rates (tent-shaped), flat beyond the end key rates. The shifts sum to a parallel 1bp shift.\n\n**Q3.** Define KR01 and key-rate duration.\n> **A:** $$\\text{KR01}_k = -\\frac{\\Delta P_k}{10{,}000\\cdot\\Delta y_k}$$ and $$D_k = -\\frac{1}{P}\\frac{\\Delta P_k}{\\Delta y_k}$$, where $$\\Delta P_k$$ is the repricing impact of shift k alone.\n\n**Q4.** How do key-rate durations relate to effective duration?\n> **A:** Because the shifts sum to a parallel shift, $$\\sum_k D_k \\approx D_{Eff}$$ and $$\\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\approx \\text{DV01}$$.\n\n**Q5.** Key-rate durations are 0.8, 1.5, 3.2, 1.5. Effective duration?\n> **A:** 0.8 + 1.5 + 3.2 + 1.5 = 7.0 (they sum to the parallel-shift duration).\n\n**Q6.** Can a key-rate duration be negative?\n> **A:** Yes — it is a curve-fitting artifact (e.g., a long zero discounted off a par-key-rate curve can gain when an intermediate key rate rises). GARP has tested this; don't panic.\n\n**Q7.** How many instruments are needed to hedge n key-rate exposures?\n> **A:** At least n hedging instruments, each concentrated near one key rate. Set hedge face amounts so total KR01 at every key rate is zero — a system of n linear equations (usually triangular: solve the single-unknown equation first).\n\n**Q8.** Compute the price change from key-rate moves given a KR01 table.\n> **A:** A dot product: $$\\Delta P = -\\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k\\times\\Delta y_k^{(bp)}$$. Watch signs — one rate may fall while another rises.\n\n**Q9.** A portfolio has a 10-year KR01 of +$2,400; hedge bond X has 10-year KR01 $60/$1M face. The hedge?\n> **A:** Need offsetting −$2,400: 2,400 / 60 = 40 → short $40M face of X. Buying would double the exposure.\n\n**Q10.** What are partial '01s?\n> **A:** Shift one curve-fitting instrument's rate (e.g., each par swap rate) by 1bp, refit, reprice — one '01 per curve input (10–20 buckets). Used by swap desks. Partial '01s sum to the total '01.\n\n**Q11.** What are forward-bucket '01s?\n> **A:** Shift all forward rates within a maturity bucket (e.g., 0–2, 2–5, 5–10, 10–30 years) by 1bp and reprice. Natural for forward-priced instruments; bucket '01s sum to the total.\n\n**Q12.** What is volatility-weighted (regression-based) hedging?\n> **A:** $$N = -\\frac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}}\\times\\beta$$, where β is the regression slope (change in position yield per unit change in hedge yield). Minimizes P&L variance but introduces model risk (β drifts).\n\n**Q13.** Why does a DV01-neutral long-10y / short-2s30s-barbell lose money?\n> **A:** It is flat to parallel moves by construction but short convexity/curvature — it loses when the wings (2y, 30y) rally relative to the belly (10y), i.e., on a curvature change.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A portfolio's key-rate durations are 0.8 (2-year), 1.5 (5-year), 3.2 (10-year), and 1.5 (30-year). Its effective (parallel-shift) duration is approximately:\nA) 3.2 B) 7.0 C) 1.75 D) Cannot be determined from key-rate durations\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how do the four key-rate durations relate to the overall effective duration?\n\nBecause the four key-rate shifts, when added together, reproduce a single parallel 1bp shift, the key-rate durations SUM to the effective duration:\n$$\\sum_k D_k = 0.8 + 1.5 + 3.2 + 1.5 = 7.0$$\n\n- A (3.2) picks only the largest single key-rate duration (the 10-year) — but effective duration combines them all.\n- C (1.75) is the average of the four — but they sum, they don't average.\n- D is wrong; the sum is exactly how you recover effective duration.\n\nAnalogy: if you split a parallel push on a whole row of dominoes into four separate nudges (one per zone), adding up the four nudges gives you back the single big push.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Key-rate durations add up to effective duration; key-rate '01s (KR01s) add up to total DV01. This \"sums-to-total\" property is heavily tested.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"KRDs sum to effective duration\",\"steps\":[\"0.8 (2y)\",\"+ 1.5 (5y)\",\"+ 3.2 (10y)\",\"+ 1.5 (30y) = 7.0 effective duration\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A trader is long a 10-year bullet bond, hedged to zero DV01 with a short, duration-matched 2s/30s barbell. This position will lose money when:\nA) The curve shifts up in parallel B) The curve shifts down in parallel C) The curve's curvature changes — the 2-year and 30-year fall relative to the 10-year D) Never — zero DV01 means the position is fully hedged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: where is the hidden risk in a DV01-neutral bullet-vs-barbell trade?\n\nA zero-DV01 position is protected against parallel shifts by construction — both A and B net to zero. But \"zero DV01\" is a single-factor measure; it does not protect against the curve changing shape. The trade is effectively short curvature (short the barbell's convexity), so it loses when the wings (2-year and 30-year) rally relative to the belly (10-year) — a curvature/butterfly move.\n\n- A and B are exactly the moves the hedge IS designed to neutralize.\n- D is the trap: zero DV01 means flat to parallel moves only, NOT fully hedged. Curve reshaping slips through.\n\nAnalogy: balancing a seesaw at the center protects you if both ends go up or down together. But if one end rises while the other drops (a twist), your \"balance\" no longer holds.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A DV01-neutral position is still exposed to non-parallel (steepener, flattener, butterfly) moves. This is the headline limitation of single-factor hedging.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Zero-DV01 bullet vs barbell\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"HEDGED (net zero)\",\"items\":[\"parallel shift up\",\"parallel shift down\"]},{\"h\":\"STILL EXPOSED\",\"items\":[\"curvature/butterfly: wings vs belly\",\"steepeners, twists\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In the standard key-rate framework, the 5-year key-rate shift is best described as:\nA) A 1bp parallel move in all rates from 0 to 5 years B) A 1bp move at the 5-year point, declining linearly to zero at the 2- and 10-year points C) A 1bp move at the 5-year point only, with all other maturities unchanged D) A 1bp move in all forward rates between years 2 and 10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what shape does a single key-rate shift take?\n\nKey-rate shifts are \"tent-shaped.\" The chosen rate (here, 5-year) moves by 1bp at its peak and then tapers LINEARLY down to zero at the adjacent key rates (the 2-year and 10-year points). This way, when you add up all the key-rate shifts, they recombine into a smooth parallel shift.\n\n- A is too broad — it shifts a whole block of rates equally, which is not the tent shape.\n- C is too narrow — a spike at one single point with no tapering is not how key rates are defined (and wouldn't sum to a parallel shift).\n- D describes a forward-bucket shift, a different (forward-rate-based) method.\n\nAnalogy: imagine pulling up a tent pole at the 5-year mark — the fabric (rate change) is highest right at the pole and slopes down to the ground (zero) at the neighboring poles.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Key-rate shift = tent shape: peak 1bp at the key maturity, linear decay to zero at adjacent key rates, flat beyond the first/last key rate.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Shape of the 5-year key-rate shift\",\"options\":[\"1bp parallel from 0 to 5y\",\"1bp at 5y, linear decay to 0 at 2y and 10y (tent)\",\"1bp at 5y only, spike\",\"1bp in all forwards 2y-10y\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Key-rate shifts are tent-shaped: peak at the key maturity, taper linearly to zero at adjacent key rates, so they sum to a parallel shift.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A portfolio has a 10-year KR01 of +\\$2,400 (it loses \\$2,400 per bp rise in the 10-year key rate). Hedge Bond X has a 10-year KR01 of \\$60 per \\$1M face, with other KR01s ≈ 0. The appropriate hedge is to:\nA) Buy \\$40M face of Bond X B) Sell \\$40M face of Bond X C) Sell \\$4M face of Bond X D) Buy \\$24M face of Bond X\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how much of Bond X, and which direction, offsets the portfolio's 10-year exposure?\n\nWe need a hedge with a 10-year KR01 equal and opposite to +\\$2,400, i.e., −\\$2,400.\n\nStep 1 — face amount:\n$$\\frac{2{,}400}{60} = 40 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\$40\\text{M face}$$\n\nStep 2 — direction. The portfolio LOSES when the 10-year rate rises (positive KR01, long-bond convention). To offset, you need a position that GAINS when that rate rises — a SHORT bond position. So sell \\$40M face.\n\n- A buys instead of sells — that doubles the exposure to +\\$4,800.\n- C is off by a factor of 10 ($$2{,}400/600$$ or a decimal slip).\n- D uses 2,400 inconsistently (perhaps $$2{,}400/100$$).\n\nAnalogy: your boat leans because of weight on the right (the 10-year exposure). You add equal weight on the left (short the bond) to level it — same size, opposite side.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Positive KR01 = you lose when that rate rises = hedge by going SHORT a bond with matching KR01. Decide your sign convention first and carry it through.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** To fully hedge a portfolio's exposure to n distinct key rates (so that the KR01 at each key rate is zero), you need at least:\nA) 1 hedging instrument B) 2 hedging instruments C) n hedging instruments D) 2n hedging instruments\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: how many hedge instruments are needed for n key-rate exposures?\n\nEach key rate is a separate dimension of risk. Setting the total KR01 to zero at every one of the n key rates gives you n equations. To solve n equations, you need n unknowns — that is, n hedging instruments (ideally each concentrated near one key rate). One instrument can only zero out one exposure reliably.\n\n- A would only handle parallel risk (one factor), leaving the other n−1 exposures open.\n- B is enough only for two key rates.\n- D is more than necessary; n equations need exactly n unknowns.\n\nAnalogy: to balance a table with n wobbly legs, you need n adjustable feet — one knob per leg. One knob can't level four legs independently.\n\n📌 Exam tip: n key-rate exposures → n hedge instruments → n equations in n unknowns. Choose each hedge to be concentrated near a different key rate so the system is easy to solve.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Instruments needed for n key rates\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"n\",\"2n\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Zeroing n key-rate exposures gives n equations, requiring n unknowns — i.e., n hedging instruments.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A portfolio has KR01s of \\$500 (5-year) and \\$400 (10-year). Two hedge bonds have the following KR01s per \\$1M face: Bond A — \\$40 (5-year), \\$5 (10-year); Bond B — \\$0 (5-year), \\$80 (10-year). The face of Bond A to short is closest to:\nA) \\$12.5M B) \\$4.22M C) \\$10.0M D) \\$5.0M\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: solve the hedge system for the amount of Bond A.\n\nSet up equations so the hedge KR01s match the portfolio's at each key rate. Let $$F_A, F_B$$ be face amounts in \\$M:\n- 5-year: $$40 F_A + 0 \\cdot F_B = 500$$\n- 10-year: $$5 F_A + 80 F_B = 400$$\n\nThe first equation has only one unknown — solve it directly:\n$$F_A = \\frac{500}{40} = 12.5$$\n\nSo short \\$12.5M of Bond A. (Back-substituting gives $$5(12.5) + 80 F_B = 400 \\Rightarrow F_B = 337.5/80 = 4.22$$M, but the question asked for Bond A.)\n\n- B (\\$4.22M) is the answer for Bond B, not Bond A — read carefully.\n- C (\\$10.0M) and D (\\$5.0M) come from mis-setting the equations or using the wrong KR01.\n\nAnalogy: solving these is like a recipe where one ingredient (Bond A) is fixed by a single constraint first; once you know it, the rest falls into place.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Start with the equation that has only ONE unknown (these systems are usually triangular), solve it, then back-substitute. Bond A is fixed entirely by the 5-year row since Bond B has zero 5-year KR01.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A swap desk wants to measure interest-rate risk by shifting EACH curve-fitting instrument's rate by 1bp, refitting the curve, and repricing. This measure is called:\nA) Effective duration B) Partial '01s C) Macaulay duration D) DV01\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: name the risk measure that shifts each individual curve input by 1bp.\n\nPartial '01s are defined exactly this way: shift one fitting instrument's rate (e.g., each par swap rate used to build the curve) by 1bp, refit the curve, and reprice. There is one '01 per curve input — often 10 to 20 buckets — and the partial '01s sum to the total '01.\n\n- A (effective duration) is a single parallel-shift, one-factor measure.\n- C (Macaulay duration) is the average time to cash flows — unrelated to curve-input shifts.\n- D (DV01) is the total sensitivity to one parallel 1bp shift, not a per-input breakdown.\n\nAnalogy: instead of measuring how a bridge responds to shaking the whole thing (DV01), partial '01s wiggle each support pillar one at a time to see which pillar your position depends on.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Three resolutions, same idea — key rates (par yields, tent-shaped), partial '01s (each curve-fitting instrument), and forward-bucket '01s (all forwards in a maturity bucket). Each set sums to the total '01.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Curve-risk resolutions (each sums to total '01)\",\"root\":\"Total '01\",\"children\":[\"Key rates (par yields, tent-shaped)\",\"Partial '01s (each fitting instrument)\",\"Forward-bucket '01s (forwards in a bucket)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A DV01 hedge implicitly assumes the position's yield and the hedge's yield move one-for-one. If, empirically, the position's yield moves only 0.9bp for every 1bp move in the hedge's yield, a volatility-weighted (regression-based) hedge would:\nA) Increase the hedge size by dividing by 0.9 B) Scale the hedge by the regression slope β = 0.9 C) Leave the hedge unchanged D) Use β = 1.0 always\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how does a regression-based hedge adjust the plain DV01 hedge?\n\nA volatility-weighted hedge multiplies the standard ratio by the regression slope β (the change in the position's yield per unit change in the hedge's yield):\n$$N = -\\frac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}} \\times \\beta$$\nHere β = 0.9, so the hedge is scaled by 0.9. Because the position's yield moves LESS than the hedge's yield, you need a slightly SMALLER hedge.\n\n- A divides by 0.9 (making the hedge bigger) — wrong direction; the position is less sensitive, so it needs less hedging.\n- C ignores the empirical relationship entirely — that is the plain DV01 hedge being corrected.\n- D assumes one-for-one (β = 1), which is the very assumption the regression hedge is improving on.\n\nAnalogy: if your end of the seesaw moves only 90% as much as your friend's end, you need only 90% of the counterweight you'd otherwise add.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Volatility-weighted hedge = DV01 hedge × β. It minimizes P&L variance but adds model risk, because β is estimated from history and drifts over time.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"DV01 hedge vs regression (vol-weighted) hedge\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Plain DV01\",\"items\":[\"assumes yields move 1-for-1 (β=1)\",\"N = DV01pos / DV01hedge\"]},{\"h\":\"Regression hedge\",\"items\":[\"scales by β (here 0.9)\",\"N = (DV01pos/DV01hedge) × β\",\"β<1 → smaller hedge\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A coupon bond, valued off a curve fitted through par key rates, sometimes shows a NEGATIVE key-rate duration at one maturity. The best interpretation is:\nA) The bond is mispriced and should be sold immediately B) There is a calculation error; key-rate durations cannot be negative C) It is a normal fitting artifact — a single key-rate shift can raise the bond's value at certain maturities D) The bond must contain an embedded option\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: how should you read a negative key-rate duration?\n\nA negative key-rate duration is a known, normal artifact of how the curve is fitted through par key rates. A single tent-shaped key-rate shift can, at certain maturities, actually raise a bond's value, producing a negative KRD. It does not signal an error or a trading opportunity by itself.\n\n- A overreacts; a negative KRD is not a mispricing signal.\n- B is false; negative KRDs genuinely occur and GARP has tested exactly this.\n- D is wrong; this happens for plain coupon bonds — no embedded option required.\n\nAnalogy: when you push up one tent pole, the fabric somewhere else can dip down a little. Likewise, nudging one key rate can nudge a bond's value the \"wrong\" way at another point — perfectly normal.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Don't panic at a negative key-rate duration — it is a fitting artifact, not a mistake. Remember they still SUM to the (positive) effective duration.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"A negative key-rate duration means\",\"options\":[\"Bond is mispriced, sell it\",\"Calculation error — KRDs can't be negative\",\"Normal fitting artifact\",\"Bond must have an embedded option\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"A single tent-shaped shift can raise a bond's value at some maturities, producing a negative KRD — a known, normal artifact.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A portfolio's KR01s are +\\$300 (2-year) and +\\$500 (10-year). Over a day, the 2-year key rate FALLS by 4bp while the 10-year key rate RISES by 2bp. The approximate change in portfolio value is:\nA) −\\$2,200 B) +\\$200 C) −\\$200 D) +\\$2,200\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: compute the P&L from a set of key-rate moves using the KR01 dot product.\n\nThe formula is:\n$$\\Delta P \\approx -\\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\times \\Delta y_k^{(bp)}$$\n\n(A positive KR01 means the portfolio loses when that rate rises, so we put a minus sign in front.)\n\nStep 1 — 2-year contribution. The rate FELL 4bp, so $$\\Delta y = -4$$:\n$$-300 \\times (-4) = +1{,}200$$ (a falling rate is good for a long position).\n\nStep 2 — 10-year contribution. The rate ROSE 2bp, so $$\\Delta y = +2$$:\n$$-500 \\times (+2) = -1{,}000$$ (a rising rate is bad).\n\nStep 3 — add: $$+1{,}200 - 1{,}000 = +\\$200$$.\n\n- A (−\\$2,200) gets both signs wrong (treats the fall as a rise and vice versa) and adds magnitudes.\n- C (−\\$200) flips the final sign — likely a single sign error.\n- D (+\\$2,200) adds the magnitudes the same way as A but keeps a positive sign.\n\nAnalogy: each key rate is a separate scoreboard — a falling 2-year scores points (gain), a rising 10-year loses points; net the two for the final score.\n\n📌 Exam tip: P&L = −Σ (KR01 × rate change in bp). Mind the signs: a rate FALL with a positive KR01 is a GAIN. Letting one rate fall while another rises is the classic trap — the signs do all the work.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A portfolio's key-rate durations are 0.8 (2y), 1.5 (5y), 3.2 (10y), and 1.5 (30y). Its effective duration is approximately:\nA) 3.2 B) 7.0 C) 1.75 D) Cannot be determined from key-rate durations\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Key-rate durations sum to the effective (parallel-shift) duration: 0.8 + 1.5 + 3.2 + 1.5 = 7.0.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A long 10-year bullet hedged to zero DV01 with a short 2s/30s barbell loses money when:\nA) The curve shifts up in parallel B) The curve shifts down in parallel C) The curve's curvature changes (2y and 30y fall relative to 10y) D) Nothing — zero DV01 means fully hedged\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The position is flat to parallel moves by construction; it is short convexity/curvature — it loses when the wings rally relative to the belly (or equivalently when the butterfly moves against it).\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** In the standard key-rate framework, the 5-year key-rate shift is:\nA) A 1bp parallel move in all rates from 0 to 5 years B) A 1bp move at 5 years, declining linearly to zero at the 2- and 10-year points C) A 1bp move at 5 years only, with all other maturities unchanged D) A 1bp move in all forward rates between years 2 and 10\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Key-rate shifts are tent-shaped: peak of 1bp at the key maturity, linear decay to zero at adjacent key rates, flat extension beyond the end key rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A portfolio has a 10-year KR01 of +\\$2,400. Hedge Bond X has a 10-year KR01 of \\$60 per \\$1M face (other KR01s ≈ 0). The hedge is:\nA) Buy \\$40M face of X B) Sell \\$40M face of X C) Sell \\$4M face of X D) Buy \\$24M face of X\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Need an offsetting −\\$2,400 KR01: 2,400 / 60 = 40 → short \\$40M face. Buying doubles the exposure.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A portfolio has key rate durations of 0.5 (2-year), 1.5 (5-year), and 3.0 (10-year). If the 2-year rate rises 10 bp, the 5-year rises 20 bp, and the 10-year *falls* 10 bp, the estimated percentage price change is closest to:\nA) −0.05% B) −0.65% C) +0.05% D) −0.35%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Sum the key-rate contributions:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta P}{P} = -\\left[0.5 \\times 0.0010 + 1.5 \\times 0.0020 + 3.0 \\times (-0.0010)\\right] = -\\left[0.0005 + 0.0030 - 0.0030\\right] = -0.05\\%$$\n\nThe 10-year *rally* offsets nearly all of the short-end selloff — exactly why key-rate analysis matters for non-parallel shifts. Distractor B treats all shifts as if they had the same sign.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Key-rate contributions (sum them)\",\"steps\":[\"2y: −0.5×10bp = −0.05%\",\"5y: −1.5×20bp = −0.30%\",\"10y: −3.0×(−10bp) = +0.30%\",\"Total ≈ −0.05%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q16.** A portfolio has the following key-rate '01s (KR01s), expressed as the dollar loss per 1 bp rise in each key rate: 2-year = $1,200, 5-year = $2,800, 10-year = $4,500, 30-year = $1,500. Over one day the 2-year rate rises 8 bp, the 5-year rises 4 bp, the 10-year is unchanged, and the 30-year falls 6 bp. The portfolio's approximate P&L is closest to:\nA) −$11,800 B) −$29,800 C) +$9,000 D) −$20,800\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute portfolio P&L as the negative dot product of KR01s and key-rate moves (in bp).\n\n1. Formula: $$\\Delta P \\approx -\\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\times \\Delta y_k^{(bp)}$$\n2. Compute term by term (a positive KR01 means a LOSS when that rate rises):\n   - 2-year: $$-1{,}200 \\times 8 = -9{,}600$$\n   - 5-year: $$-2{,}800 \\times 4 = -11{,}200$$\n   - 10-year: $$-4{,}500 \\times 0 = 0$$\n   - 30-year: $$-1{,}500 \\times (-6) = +9{,}000$$ (rate FELL, so a gain)\n3. Sum:\n   $$-9{,}600 - 11{,}200 + 0 + 9{,}000 = -11{,}800$$\n\nThe portfolio loses about **$11,800**: the losses on the rising 2- and 5-year exposures dominate the gain from the falling 30-year.\n\n- B (−$29,800) treats the 30-year move as a rise too, turning its +$9,000 gain into a −$9,000 loss (sign error on the falling rate) and also mishandles signs.\n- C (+$9,000) keeps only the 30-year gain and drops the dominant 2- and 5-year losses.\n- D (−$20,800) ignores the offsetting 30-year gain entirely (treats it as zero).\n\n📌 Exam tip: P&L from a set of key-rate moves is a dot product: $$-\\sum \\text{KR01}_k \\Delta y_k$$. A rate that FALLS flips the sign of its term to a gain. Track signs rate by rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A portfolio has key-rate durations of 0.6 (2-year), 1.4 (5-year), 3.0 (10-year), and 2.2 (30-year). Holding all else equal, its effective (parallel-shift) duration is approximately:\nA) 3.0 B) 7.2 C) 1.8 D) Cannot be determined from key-rate durations\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: recover effective duration from a set of key-rate durations.\n\n1. Key-rate shifts are constructed so their sum reproduces a parallel 1 bp shift. Therefore the key-rate durations sum to the effective duration:\n   $$\\sum_k D_k \\approx D_{Eff}$$\n2. Add them:\n   $$0.6 + 1.4 + 3.0 + 2.2 = 7.2$$\n\n- A (3.0) takes only the largest single KRD (the 10-year) — that captures one segment, not the parallel response.\n- C (1.8) is the average of the four KRDs — averaging is wrong; they ADD.\n- D is incorrect: the sum-to-total property makes the parallel duration fully determined by the KRDs.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Key-rate durations SUM to the effective (parallel-shift) duration because the individual tent-shaped shifts add up to a parallel shift. Don't average them.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"KRDs sum to effective duration\",\"steps\":[\"0.6 (2y)\",\"+ 1.4 (5y)\",\"+ 3.0 (10y)\",\"+ 2.2 (30y) = 7.2 effective duration\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** In the standard four-point (2/5/10/30-year) key-rate framework, the 5-year key-rate shift is best described as:\nA) A 1 bp parallel move in all rates from 0 to 5 years. B) A 1 bp move at the 5-year point, declining linearly to zero at the 2-year and 10-year points. C) A 1 bp move at the 5-year point only, with all other maturities unchanged. D) A 1 bp move in all forward rates between years 2 and 10.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the shape of a single key-rate shift.\n\n1. Each key-rate shift is \"tent-shaped\": it peaks at 1 bp at its own key maturity and decays LINEARLY to zero at the two adjacent key rates.\n2. For the 5-year shift, the adjacent key rates are the 2-year and the 10-year, so the shift is 1 bp at 5 years, tapering linearly to 0 at both 2 and 10 years, and zero outside that span.\n3. The four such tents sum to a parallel 1 bp shift across the curve.\n\n- A describes a block/step shift over a maturity range, not the tent shape.\n- C describes a single-point spike (a partial '01 style isolated bump), not the tapered key-rate shift.\n- D describes a forward-bucket '01, which shifts forward rates within a bucket — a different decomposition.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Key-rate shifts are tent-shaped — peak at the key maturity, linear decay to zero at neighboring key rates, flat beyond the end points. This shape is exactly why they sum to a parallel shift.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Shape of the 5-year key-rate shift\",\"options\":[\"1bp parallel from 0 to 5y\",\"1bp at 5y, linear decay to 0 at 2y and 10y (tent)\",\"1bp spike at 5y only\",\"1bp in all forwards 2y-10y\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Tent shape: peak 1bp at the key maturity, tapering linearly to zero at adjacent key rates, so the shifts sum to a parallel move.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A portfolio has KR01s of $600 at the 5-year key rate and $450 at the 10-year key rate (it loses these amounts per 1 bp rise in each rate). The available hedge bonds have the following KR01s per $1 million of face:\n\n| | 5-year KR01 | 10-year KR01 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bond A (5-yr note) | $48 | $6 |\n| Bond B (10-yr note) | $0 | $75 |\n\nThe face amounts to SHORT to neutralize both key-rate exposures are closest to:\nA) Short $12.5M of A and $5.0M of B B) Short $12.5M of A and $6.0M of B C) Short $9.4M of A and $5.0M of B D) Short $6.0M of A and $5.6M of B\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: solve the triangular system so the hedge's KR01s match the portfolio's at both key rates.\n\n1. Let $$F_A, F_B$$ be the face amounts (in $M) shorted. The hedge must supply KR01s of $600 (5-yr) and $450 (10-yr):\n   - 5-year: $$48 F_A + 0 \\cdot F_B = 600$$\n   - 10-year: $$6 F_A + 75 F_B = 450$$\n2. The first equation has only one unknown:\n   $$F_A = \\frac{600}{48} = 12.5$$\n3. Back-substitute into the second:\n   $$6(12.5) + 75 F_B = 450 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 75 + 75 F_B = 450 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 75 F_B = 375 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_B = 5.0$$\n4. Short **$12.5M of Bond A** and **$5.0M of Bond B**.\n\n- B ($6.0M of B) forgets to subtract Bond A's 10-year cross-KR01, solving $$75 F_B = 450 \\Rightarrow 6.0$$ instead of $$75 F_B = 375$$.\n- C ($9.4M of A) divides $600 by the wrong KR01 (e.g., uses 64 or mishandles units).\n- D scrambles both legs, ignoring the triangular structure.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Solve the KR01 hedge system starting with the equation that has only one unknown (the triangular structure), then back-substitute. A positive portfolio KR01 means a loss when the rate rises, so the hedge is a SHORT of matching magnitude.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A trader is long a 10-year bullet bond and has hedged it to zero net DV01 by shorting a duration-matched 2s/30s barbell. The combined position will most likely LOSE money when:\nA) The yield curve shifts up in parallel by 50 bp. B) The yield curve shifts down in parallel by 50 bp. C) The curve's curvature changes — the 2-year and 30-year rates fall relative to the 10-year. D) Nothing — zero net DV01 means the position is fully hedged.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: identify which curve move produces P&L on a DV01-neutral bullet-vs-barbell position.\n\n1. By construction the position has zero net DV01, so PARALLEL shifts (A and B) net to approximately zero P&L — eliminating those.\n2. A bullet has cash flows concentrated at 10 years; the barbell has them at 2 and 30 years. The position is therefore short the wings (2y, 30y) and long the belly (10y) — a butterfly exposure.\n3. If the wings (2-year and 30-year) rally (fall) relative to the belly, the short barbell legs rise in value (a loss on the short) more than the long bullet — the position loses on this curvature/butterfly move.\n\n- A and B are parallel moves, hedged out by the zero net DV01 — no first-order P&L.\n- D is the core misconception the chapter warns against: zero DV01 protects only against parallel moves, NOT curve reshaping.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A DV01-neutral position is immune only to parallel shifts. Bullet-vs-barbell trades carry curvature (butterfly) risk that single-factor DV01 cannot see — this is exactly why key-rate metrics exist.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Zero-DV01 bullet vs barbell\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"HEDGED\",\"items\":[\"parallel shift up\",\"parallel shift down\"]},{\"h\":\"STILL EXPOSED\",\"items\":[\"curvature/butterfly (wings vs belly)\",\"steepeners/flatteners\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A portfolio has a 30-year KR01 of +$3,000 (it loses $3,000 per 1 bp rise in the 30-year key rate), with all other KR01s approximately zero. Hedge Bond Z has a 30-year KR01 of $75 per $1 million of face (its other KR01s are approximately zero). The appropriate hedge is to:\nA) Buy $40 million face of Z. B) Sell $40 million face of Z. C) Sell $4 million face of Z. D) Buy $22.5 million face of Z.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: determine the direction and size of the hedge for a single positive KR01 exposure.\n\n1. The portfolio has a positive 30-year KR01, meaning it LOSES when the 30-year rate rises. The hedge must GAIN when the 30-year rate rises — i.e., a SHORT position (shorting a bond gains when its rate rises).\n2. Size: match the magnitude.\n   $$F = \\frac{3{,}000}{75} = 40 \\text{ (in \\$M)}$$\n3. Therefore SHORT $40 million face of Bond Z.\n\n- A (buy) doubles the exposure — a long bond also loses when rates rise, adding to the portfolio's loss.\n- C ($4M) is off by a factor of 10 (a decimal slip in the division).\n- D (buy $22.5M) is both the wrong direction and the wrong size.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A positive KR01 = a loss when that rate rises, so it must be hedged with a SHORT of equal magnitude. Get the direction from the sign convention before computing the size.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Hedging a positive KR01\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Portfolio (KR01 > 0)\",\"items\":[\"loses when rate rises\",\"long-bond profile\"]},{\"h\":\"Hedge\",\"items\":[\"needs to GAIN when rate rises\",\"SHORT the bond, size = KR01/hedgeKR01\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A trader uses a regression-based (volatility-weighted) hedge instead of a simple DV01 hedge. Historically, when the 30-year yield (the hedge instrument's yield) moves 1 bp, the position's 20-year yield moves 0.85 bp. The position has DV01 of $10,000 and the 30-year hedge bond has DV01 of $80 per contract-equivalent. The number of hedge units (rounded) is closest to:\nA) 147 B) 125 C) 106 D) 100\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The ask: apply the beta-adjusted (regression) hedge ratio rather than the plain DV01 ratio.\n\n1. Volatility-weighted hedge formula:\n   $$N = \\frac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}} \\times \\beta$$\n   where β is the regression slope = change in the POSITION's yield per 1 bp change in the HEDGE's yield = 0.85.\n2. Plain DV01 ratio:\n   $$\\frac{10{,}000}{80} = 125$$\n3. Scale by β:\n   $$N = 125 \\times 0.85 = 106.25 \\approx 106$$\n\n- B (125) is the plain DV01 hedge — it ignores the β adjustment, over-hedging because the 20-year moves less than one-for-one with the 30-year.\n- A (147) divides by a wrong DV01 or multiplies by 1/β instead of β.\n- D (100) is an unmotivated round number.\n\n📌 Exam tip: When the position and hedge yields do not move one-for-one, scale the DV01 hedge by the regression β (position yield change per unit hedge yield change). A β below 1 means you need FEWER hedge units than the plain DV01 ratio suggests.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"DV01 hedge vs regression hedge\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Plain DV01 (β=1)\",\"items\":[\"10000/80 = 125 units\",\"assumes 1-for-1 yield moves\"]},{\"h\":\"Regression (β=0.85)\",\"items\":[\"125 × 0.85 = 106 units\",\"β<1 → fewer units\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"414","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Binomial Trees","notes":"# 14. Binomial Trees\n\nOption pricing by replication and risk-neutral valuation on a lattice. GARP tests the no-arbitrage delta, the risk-neutral probability, full two-step valuations, and the American early-exercise check at each node.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Value a derivative on a one-step tree by constructing a riskless (replicating) portfolio.\n- Define and apply risk-neutral probabilities; explain why real-world probabilities are irrelevant to the price.\n- Value European options on two-step trees by backward induction.\n- Value American options on trees, comparing exercise and continuation value at every node.\n- Calibrate u and d to volatility: $$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$, $$d = 1/u$$.\n- Modify the tree for options on stocks with dividend yields, currencies, and futures.\n- Explain convergence to Black–Scholes–Merton as the number of steps increases.\n\n## One step: replication and delta\n\nStock at $$S_0$$ moves to $$S_0 u$$ (option worth $$f_u$$) or $$S_0 d$$ (worth $$f_d$$) over Δt. Hold Δ shares and short one option; choose Δ so the portfolio's payoff is identical in both states:\n\n$$\\Delta = \\frac{f_u - f_d}{S_0 u - S_0 d}$$\n\nA riskless portfolio must earn the risk-free rate, which forces a unique no-arbitrage option price. No assumption about the *probability* of an up move was needed — only no-arbitrage.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> The replicating delta is the binomial ancestor of the BSM hedge ratio: for a call, 0 ≤ Δ ≤ 1 and it rises as the option moves into the money. In a multi-step tree Δ differs at every node — the discrete version of dynamic delta hedging.\n\n## Risk-neutral valuation\n\nThe algebra of replication can be repackaged: define\n\n$$p = \\frac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$\n\nThen the option price is the **expected payoff under p, discounted at the risk-free rate**:\n\n$$f = e^{-r\\Delta t}\\left[p f_u + (1-p) f_d\\right]$$\n\np is the **risk-neutral probability** — the up-probability in a fictitious world where every asset drifts at r. It is a pricing device, not a forecast: real-world probabilities and risk premia cancel out of the no-arbitrage price. A sanity check: under p the stock's expected value grows at r, i.e., $$p S_0 u + (1-p) S_0 d = S_0 e^{r\\Delta t}$$.\n\n## Calibrating to volatility\n\nMatch the tree's variance to the stock's (Cox–Ross–Rubinstein):\n\n$$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}} \\qquad d = \\frac{1}{u} = e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$\n\nσ is the annualized volatility and Δt the step length in years. With d = 1/u the tree recombines: an up-down path and a down-up path land on the same node, so an n-step tree has n + 1 terminal nodes, not 2ⁿ.\n\n## Two-step trees and backward induction\n\nValue the terminal payoffs, then roll backward one step at a time, applying the one-step formula at each node (and using each node's own p if parameters vary).\n\n### Worked example: two-step European call\n\n$$S_0 = 100$$, K = 100, u = 1.1, d = 0.9, r = 6% (continuous), Δt = 0.5 years.\n\n$$p = \\frac{e^{0.06 \\times 0.5} - 0.9}{1.1 - 0.9} = \\frac{1.030455 - 0.9}{0.2} = 0.6523$$\n\nTerminal stock prices and call payoffs: $$S_{uu} = 121 \\to 21$$; $$S_{ud} = 99 \\to 0$$; $$S_{dd} = 81 \\to 0$$.\n\nUp node: $$f_u = e^{-0.03}(0.6523 \\times 21 + 0.3477 \\times 0) = 0.97045 \\times 13.698 = 13.293$$\n\nDown node: $$f_d = 0$$.\n\nRoot: $$f = e^{-0.03}(0.6523 \\times 13.293 + 0.3477 \\times 0) = 0.97045 \\times 8.671 = \\mathbf{\\$8.41}$$\n\nShortcut for European options only: discount the probability-weighted terminal payoffs in one shot, $$f = e^{-0.06}\\,p^2 (21) = 0.94177 \\times 0.4255 \\times 21 = 8.41$$ — same answer.\n\n## American options: check early exercise at every node\n\nBackward induction with one extra comparison at each node:\n\n$$f_{node} = \\max(\\text{intrinsic value},\\; \\text{continuation value})$$\n\nwhere continuation is the usual discounted risk-neutral expectation. Early exercise of American puts is optimal when the stock is low enough that collecting K now beats waiting; American calls on non-dividend stocks are never exercised early.\n\n### Worked example: American put with early exercise\n\nSame tree ($$S_0 = 100$$, u = 1.1, d = 0.9, r = 6%, Δt = 0.5, p = 0.6523), American put, K = 100. Terminal payoffs: 121 → 0; 99 → 1; 81 → 19.\n\nUp node (S = 110): continuation $$= e^{-0.03}(0.6523 \\times 0 + 0.3477 \\times 1) = 0.337$$; intrinsic = 0 → value **0.337**.\n\nDown node (S = 90): continuation $$= e^{-0.03}(0.6523 \\times 1 + 0.3477 \\times 19) = 0.97045 \\times 7.259 = 7.045$$; intrinsic = 100 − 90 = **10**. Exercise: 10 > 7.045 → value **10**.\n\nRoot: $$f = e^{-0.03}(0.6523 \\times 0.337 + 0.3477 \\times 10) = 0.97045 \\times 3.697 = \\mathbf{\\$3.59}$$\n\nThe European put on the same tree is worth \\$2.59 — the dollar of difference is the value of the early-exercise right, created entirely at the down node.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The single most common error: valuing an American option by discounting terminal payoffs directly (the European shortcut). That skips the node-by-node max(intrinsic, continuation) test and **understates** the American price. Always roll back node by node, and compare intrinsic vs continuation at *every* non-terminal node — including the root.\n\n## Other underlyings: adjust the numerator\n\nThe risk-neutral probability uses the underlying's **cost-of-carry growth rate** in the numerator:\n\n| Underlying | p |\n|---|---|\n| Non-dividend stock | $$\\dfrac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ |\n| Stock index, dividend yield q | $$\\dfrac{e^{(r-q)\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ |\n| Currency, foreign rate $$r_f$$ | $$\\dfrac{e^{(r-r_f)\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ |\n| Futures | $$\\dfrac{1 - d}{u - d}$$ |\n\nA futures price is costless to enter, so its risk-neutral drift is zero — set the growth factor to 1. **Discounting is always at the domestic risk-free rate r**, whatever sits in the numerator.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Memorize one template: $$p = \\frac{e^{(\\text{growth})\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ where growth = r, r − q, r − r_f, or 0. Exam answer choices usually include the result of using plain r when q or r_f should have been subtracted.\n\n## Convergence to BSM\n\nAs the number of steps grows (Δt → 0) with u, d calibrated to σ, the binomial price of a European option converges to the Black–Scholes–Merton price. In practice 30+ steps is close; trees remain the workhorse for **American** options precisely because BSM has no closed form for them.","flashcards":"**Q1.** The no-arbitrage replicating delta on a one-step tree.\n> **A:** $$\\Delta = \\frac{f_u - f_d}{S_0 u - S_0 d}$$ — the number of shares making the stock-plus-short-option portfolio riskless. No probability assumption is needed, only no-arbitrage.\n\n**Q2.** Risk-neutral up-probability.\n> **A:** $$p = \\frac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ — the up-probability in a fictitious world where every asset drifts at r. A pricing device, not a forecast.\n\n**Q3.** Risk-neutral valuation formula on one step.\n> **A:** $$f = e^{-r\\Delta t}\\left[p f_u + (1-p)f_d\\right]$$ — expected payoff under p, discounted at the risk-free rate. Real-world probabilities and risk premia cancel out.\n\n**Q4.** Calibrate u and d to volatility (CRR).\n> **A:** $$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$, $$d = 1/u = e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$. With d = 1/u the tree recombines, so an n-step tree has n+1 terminal nodes.\n\n**Q5.** Compute u with σ = 30% and monthly steps.\n> **A:** $$u = e^{0.30\\sqrt{1/12}} = e^{0.30(0.2887)} = e^{0.0866} = 1.0905$$. Forgetting √Δt is the trap.\n\n**Q6.** Compute p: S = 50 → 60 or 45, r = 5% continuous, Δt = 1.\n> **A:** u = 1.2, d = 0.9. $$p = \\frac{e^{0.05} - 0.9}{1.2 - 0.9} = \\frac{0.15127}{0.3} = 0.504$$.\n\n**Q7.** How are European options valued on a two-step tree?\n> **A:** Backward induction: value terminal payoffs, then roll back one step at a time with the one-step formula. Shortcut: discount probability-weighted terminal payoffs in one shot.\n\n**Q8.** How are American options valued on a tree?\n> **A:** Backward induction with one extra comparison at every non-terminal node: $$f_{node} = \\max(\\text{intrinsic value}, \\text{continuation value})$$.\n\n**Q9.** At a node an American put has intrinsic value 8.20, continuation 7.60. Node value?\n> **A:** max(8.20, 7.60) = 8.20 — the holder exercises early.\n\n**Q10.** The most common American-option valuation error.\n> **A:** Discounting terminal payoffs directly (the European shortcut), which skips the node-by-node max(intrinsic, continuation) test and understates the American price.\n\n**Q11.** Risk-neutral probability for each underlying type.\n> **A:** Non-dividend stock: $$\\frac{e^{r\\Delta t}-d}{u-d}$$; index (yield q): $$\\frac{e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}-d}{u-d}$$; currency (foreign rate $$r_f$$): $$\\frac{e^{(r-r_f)\\Delta t}-d}{u-d}$$; futures: $$\\frac{1-d}{u-d}$$.\n\n**Q12.** Why is the futures growth factor 1, and what rate is used to discount?\n> **A:** A futures position is costless to enter, so its risk-neutral drift is zero. Discounting is always at the domestic risk-free rate r, whatever sits in the numerator.\n\n**Q13.** What happens as the number of tree steps grows?\n> **A:** With u, d calibrated to σ, the binomial price of a European option converges to the Black-Scholes-Merton price (30+ steps is close). Trees remain the workhorse for American options, which BSM cannot price in closed form.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A stock trades at \\$50. In one year it will be either \\$60 or \\$45. The risk-free rate is 5% (continuously compounded). The risk-neutral probability of an up move is closest to:\nA) 0.504 B) 0.333 C) 0.546 D) 0.500\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: compute the risk-neutral up-probability p from the given stock moves.\n\nStep 1 — find u and d from the prices:\n$$u = \\frac{60}{50} = 1.2, \\qquad d = \\frac{45}{50} = 0.9$$\n\nStep 2 — apply the risk-neutral probability formula ($$\\Delta t = 1$$ year):\n$$p = \\frac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d} = \\frac{e^{0.05} - 0.9}{1.2 - 0.9}$$\n\nStep 3 — compute the pieces: $$e^{0.05} = 1.05127$$.\n$$p = \\frac{1.05127 - 0.9}{0.3} = \\frac{0.15127}{0.3} = 0.504$$\n\n- B (0.333) ignores the growth factor and just uses something like $$(1-d)/(u-d)$$ wrongly, or other miscalculation.\n- C (0.546) likely uses $$e^{0.05} = 1.05$$ rounding combined with an arithmetic slip, or wrong u/d.\n- D (0.500) is the naive \"50/50\" guess — but the risk-neutral probability is NOT a coin flip.\n\nAnalogy: p is the \"tilt\" of the dice in a make-believe world where the stock is expected to grow exactly at the risk-free rate. It is engineered to make the math work, not a real forecast.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$p = (e^{r\\Delta t} - d)/(u - d)$$. Get u and d straight from the up/down prices when they're given — no volatility formula needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A stock has volatility σ = 30%, and a binomial tree uses monthly steps ($$\\Delta t = 1/12$$). The up-factor u is closest to:\nA) 1.090 B) 1.300 C) 1.025 D) 1.350\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: calibrate the up-factor u to volatility using the Cox-Ross-Rubinstein formula.\n\nThe formula is:\n$$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$\n\nStep 1 — compute $$\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$$:\n$$\\sqrt{1/12} = \\sqrt{0.08333} = 0.2887$$\n\nStep 2 — multiply by σ:\n$$0.30 \\times 0.2887 = 0.08660$$\n\nStep 3 — exponentiate:\n$$u = e^{0.08660} = 1.0905$$\n\n- B (1.300) forgets the $$\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$$ and uses $$e^{0.30} \\approx 1.35$$, or just adds 0.30 to 1.\n- C (1.025) uses too small an exponent — perhaps $$0.30 \\times (1/12)$$ without the square root.\n- D (1.350) is $$e^{0.30}$$ — the annual figure, ignoring that each step is only one month.\n\nAnalogy: a one-month move should be smaller than a one-year move. The square root of time shrinks the volatility down to the size of a single short step.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$ and $$d = 1/u$$. The most common error is dropping the $$\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$$. Volatility scales with the SQUARE ROOT of time.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In a binomial tree for an option on a FUTURES contract, the risk-neutral probability of an up move is:\nA) $$(e^{r\\Delta t} - d)/(u - d)$$ B) $$(1 - d)/(u - d)$$ C) $$(e^{(r-q)\\Delta t} - d)/(u - d)$$ D) $$(e^{-r\\Delta t} - d)/(u - d)$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: which growth factor goes in the numerator for a futures option?\n\nEntering a futures contract costs nothing (no cash outlay), so in the risk-neutral world a futures price has ZERO drift — its growth factor is 1, not $$e^{r\\Delta t}$$. Plugging growth = 1 into the template $$p = \\frac{e^{(\\text{growth})\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ gives:\n$$p = \\frac{1 - d}{u - d}$$\n\n- A is the formula for a non-dividend STOCK (growth = r), not a futures.\n- C is for a stock index with dividend yield q (growth = r − q).\n- D mistakenly uses $$e^{-r\\Delta t}$$ (a discount factor) in the numerator — that's not a growth factor.\n\nAnalogy: a stock you buy ties up cash that should earn r, so it must drift at r. A futures position ties up no cash, so there's nothing it needs to \"make up\" — its expected drift is zero.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use the template $$p = \\frac{e^{(\\text{growth})\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ with growth = r (stock), r − q (index), r − $$r_f$$ (currency), or 0 (futures). Discounting is ALWAYS at r.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Growth factor in p numerator\",\"root\":\"p = (e^(growth·Δt) − d)/(u − d)\",\"children\":[\"stock: growth = r\",\"index: growth = r − q\",\"currency: growth = r − rf\",\"futures: growth = 0 → p=(1−d)/(u−d)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** At a node in a binomial tree, an American put has an intrinsic (exercise) value of 8.20 and a continuation value of 7.60. The value used at that node in backward induction is:\nA) 7.60 B) 8.20 C) 7.90 D) 0.60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how do you value an American option at each node?\n\nAn American option can be exercised at any time, so at each node you take the LARGER of exercising now (intrinsic value) versus holding (continuation value):\n$$f_{node} = \\max(\\text{intrinsic}, \\text{continuation}) = \\max(8.20, 7.60) = 8.20$$\n\nHere exercising (8.20) beats waiting (7.60), so the holder exercises and the node value is 8.20.\n\n- A (7.60) is the continuation value — what you'd use only if it were larger, but it isn't.\n- C (7.90) is the average of the two — never the right rule; you take the max, not the average.\n- D (0.60) is the difference — also not the rule.\n\nAnalogy: at each step you ask, \"Is the cash I'd get by exercising now bigger than the value of keeping my option alive?\" You always pocket the bigger amount.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American option = max(intrinsic, continuation) at EVERY non-terminal node, including the root. This early-exercise check is what makes American options worth more than European ones.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"American node value: max(8.20, 7.60)\",\"options\":[\"7.60 (continuation)\",\"8.20 (intrinsic)\",\"7.90 (average)\",\"0.60 (difference)\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"At each node take the LARGER of exercise now (intrinsic) vs hold (continuation): max(8.20, 7.60) = 8.20.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Using a one-step tree with $$S_0 = 100$$, $$u = 1.1$$, $$d = 0.9$$, $$r = 6\\%$$ (continuous), and $$\\Delta t = 0.5$$, the risk-neutral up-probability is closest to:\nA) 0.500 B) 0.652 C) 0.348 D) 0.700\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: compute p for the given one-step tree.\n\nStep 1 — growth factor over the half-year:\n$$e^{r\\Delta t} = e^{0.06 \\times 0.5} = e^{0.03} = 1.030455$$\n\nStep 2 — apply the formula:\n$$p = \\frac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d} = \\frac{1.030455 - 0.9}{1.1 - 0.9} = \\frac{0.130455}{0.2} = 0.6523$$\n\n- A (0.500) is the naive coin-flip guess.\n- C (0.348) is $$1 - p$$ — the DOWN probability, not the up probability.\n- D (0.700) is a rounding/setup slip.\n\nAnalogy: think of p as how much the tree must \"lean up\" so that, on average, the stock grows at the risk-free rate over the half-year.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Don't forget $$\\Delta t$$ inside the exponent — here it's 0.5, so use $$e^{0.03}$$, not $$e^{0.06}$$. And remember $$1 - p$$ is the down-probability.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Using $$S_0 = 100$$, $$u = 1.1$$, $$d = 0.9$$, $$r = 6\\%$$, $$\\Delta t = 0.5$$, $$p = 0.6523$$, the value of a two-step European call with strike K = 100 is closest to:\nA) \\$13.29 B) \\$8.41 C) \\$21.00 D) \\$6.85\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: value a two-step European call by backward induction.\n\nStep 1 — terminal stock prices and payoffs ($$\\max(S - 100, 0)$$):\n- $$S_{uu} = 100 \\times 1.1 \\times 1.1 = 121 \\to$$ payoff 21\n- $$S_{ud} = 100 \\times 1.1 \\times 0.9 = 99 \\to$$ payoff 0\n- $$S_{dd} = 100 \\times 0.9 \\times 0.9 = 81 \\to$$ payoff 0\n\nStep 2 — discount one step. Discount factor $$e^{-r\\Delta t} = e^{-0.03} = 0.97045$$.\n\nUp node: $$f_u = 0.97045 \\times (0.6523 \\times 21 + 0.3477 \\times 0) = 0.97045 \\times 13.698 = 13.293$$\nDown node: $$f_d = 0.97045 \\times (0.6523 \\times 0 + 0.3477 \\times 0) = 0$$\n\nStep 3 — roll back to root:\n$$f = 0.97045 \\times (0.6523 \\times 13.293 + 0.3477 \\times 0) = 0.97045 \\times 8.671 = \\$8.41$$\n\n- A (\\$13.29) is the value at the UP node only ($$f_u$$), not the root — it stops one step too early.\n- C (\\$21.00) is the undiscounted top payoff — no probability weighting, no discounting.\n- D (\\$6.85) results from a discounting or probability error.\n\nAnalogy: you work backward like rewinding a film — start from the endings (payoffs), and at each earlier scene take the discounted expected value until you reach the start (today).\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a EUROPEAN option you can shortcut: $$f = e^{-2r\\Delta t} \\times p^2 \\times 21 = e^{-0.06} \\times 0.4255 \\times 21 = 8.41$$. Same answer, faster.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Two-step backward induction\",\"steps\":[\"Build price lattice (uu, ud, dd)\",\"Terminal payoffs max(S−K,0)\",\"Discount expected value back one step\",\"Roll back to root = value today\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** As the number of steps in a binomial tree increases (with u and d calibrated to volatility), the price of a European option:\nA) Diverges away from the Black-Scholes-Merton price B) Converges to the Black-Scholes-Merton price C) Stays constant regardless of the number of steps D) Becomes increasingly inaccurate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what happens to the binomial European price as steps increase?\n\nAs the number of steps grows (so $$\\Delta t \\to 0$$), with u and d calibrated to σ, the binomial price of a European option converges to the Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) closed-form price. In practice, around 30+ steps already gives a close answer.\n\n- A is the opposite of what happens.\n- C is false; few-step trees are crude approximations that refine as steps increase.\n- D is the reverse — more steps means MORE accuracy.\n\nAnalogy: a binomial tree is like a staircase approximating a smooth ramp (BSM). The more (smaller) steps you use, the closer the staircase hugs the smooth ramp.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Binomial → BSM as steps → ∞ for European options. Trees remain essential for AMERICAN options precisely because BSM has no closed form for them.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"More binomial steps → European price\",\"options\":[\"Diverges from BSM\",\"Converges to BSM\",\"Stays constant\",\"Becomes less accurate\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"As steps increase (Δt→0) with u,d calibrated to σ, the binomial price converges to the Black-Scholes-Merton price.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A binomial tree uses $$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$ and $$d = 1/u$$. A key advantage of setting $$d = 1/u$$ is that:\nA) It guarantees the option is always in the money B) The tree recombines, so an n-step tree has n+1 terminal nodes instead of 2ⁿ C) The risk-neutral probability becomes exactly 0.5 D) It eliminates the need to discount cash flows\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: why is choosing $$d = 1/u$$ useful?\n\nWhen $$d = 1/u$$, an up-then-down move ($$u \\times d = u \\times 1/u = 1$$) lands on the SAME stock price as a down-then-up move. The paths \"recombine,\" so the tree collapses into a neat lattice with only n+1 terminal nodes after n steps, instead of $$2^n$$ separate paths. This makes computation vastly more manageable.\n\n- A is nonsense; the calibration says nothing about moneyness.\n- C is false; p depends on r, u, and d, and is generally not 0.5.\n- D is false; you still discount at the risk-free rate.\n\nAnalogy: it's like a grid of streets where going \"right then left\" puts you on the same corner as \"left then right\" — far fewer corners to track than if every path led somewhere new.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$d = 1/u$$ → recombining tree → n+1 terminal nodes, not $$2^n$$. This is the computational reason the Cox-Ross-Rubinstein parameterization is so popular.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"d = 1/u advantage\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Recombining (d=1/u)\",\"items\":[\"up-down = down-up\",\"n+1 terminal nodes\",\"fast to compute\"]},{\"h\":\"Non-recombining\",\"items\":[\"every path distinct\",\"2^n terminal nodes\",\"blows up fast\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Consider a one-step tree where a stock at $$S_0$$ goes to $$S_0 u$$ (option payoff $$f_u$$) or $$S_0 d$$ (payoff $$f_d$$). The number of shares Δ in the riskless replicating portfolio is:\nA) $$\\dfrac{f_u - f_d}{S_0 u - S_0 d}$$ B) $$\\dfrac{f_u + f_d}{S_0 u + S_0 d}$$ C) $$\\dfrac{S_0 u - S_0 d}{f_u - f_d}$$ D) $$\\dfrac{f_u - f_d}{u + d}$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: what is the hedge ratio (delta) that makes a stock-plus-option portfolio riskless?\n\nTo build a riskless portfolio, you choose Δ so the portfolio has the SAME value whether the stock goes up or down. Solving that equality gives:\n$$\\Delta = \\frac{f_u - f_d}{S_0 u - S_0 d}$$\nThis is the change in option value divided by the change in stock value across the two states.\n\n- B adds instead of subtracts — but we need the DIFFERENCE between states to cancel risk.\n- C is the formula upside-down (stock change over option change).\n- D drops the $$S_0$$ in the denominator — wrong units.\n\nAnalogy: Δ is \"how many shares move in step with one option.\" Matching that ratio makes the up-and-down swings cancel out, leaving a position that's risk-free.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\Delta = \\frac{f_u - f_d}{S_0 u - S_0 d}$$ (change in option ÷ change in stock). This replicating delta is the binomial ancestor of the BSM hedge ratio $$N(d_1)$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Why are the real-world probabilities of an up or down move irrelevant when pricing an option on a binomial tree?\nA) Because up and down moves are always equally likely B) Because the price comes from no-arbitrage replication, in which risk preferences and real-world probabilities cancel out C) Because the risk-free rate is zero D) Because options always expire worthless\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: why don't actual up/down probabilities affect the option price?\n\nThe option price is found by building a riskless replicating portfolio (stock + option) that must earn the risk-free rate to avoid arbitrage. This argument never uses the real-world probability of an up move — investors' risk preferences and the true probabilities simply cancel out. We then repackage the math using the risk-neutral probability p, which is a pricing device, not a forecast.\n\n- A is false; real-world up and down moves are generally NOT equally likely, yet the price still doesn't depend on them.\n- C is false; r is generally not zero and is essential to the pricing.\n- D is nonsense.\n\nAnalogy: pricing by replication is like figuring out the cost of a recipe from its ingredients. It doesn't matter how LIKELY you are to feel like cooking — the cost is set by what it takes to reproduce the dish.\n\n📌 Exam tip: No-arbitrage pricing depends on u, d, and r — not on real-world probabilities or risk premia. The risk-neutral probability p is a calculation tool, not a prediction of the future.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Why real-world probabilities don't matter\",\"options\":[\"Up and down always equally likely\",\"No-arbitrage replication — preferences and real probabilities cancel\",\"Risk-free rate is zero\",\"Options always expire worthless\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Pricing comes from a riskless replicating portfolio earning r; risk preferences and true probabilities cancel out.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A stock trades at 50; in one year it is 60 or 45. The risk-free rate is 5% (continuous). The risk-neutral probability of the up move is closest to:\nA) 0.504 B) 0.333 C) 0.546 D) 0.500\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** u = 60/50 = 1.2, d = 45/50 = 0.9. p = (e^{0.05} − 0.9)/(1.2 − 0.9) = 0.15127/0.3 = 0.504. Note u and d come from the given prices — no volatility formula needed here.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** With σ = 30% and monthly steps, u is closest to:\nA) 1.090 B) 1.300 C) 1.025 D) 1.350\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** u = e^{0.30√(1/12)} = e^{0.30 × 0.2887} = e^{0.0866} = 1.0905. Choice B forgets the √Δt.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** In a binomial tree for options on futures, the risk-neutral probability is:\nA) (e^{rΔt} − d)/(u − d) B) (1 − d)/(u − d) C) (e^{(r−q)Δt} − d)/(u − d) D) (e^{−rΔt} − d)/(u − d)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Futures have zero drift in the risk-neutral world (no cash outlay), so the growth factor is 1. Discounting still uses e^{−rΔt}.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** At a node, an American put has intrinsic value 8.20 and continuation value 7.60. The value used at that node in backward induction is:\nA) 7.60 B) 8.20 C) 7.90 D) 0.60\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** American valuation takes max(intrinsic, continuation) = max(8.20, 7.60) = 8.20 — the holder exercises.\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** In a one-period binomial tree, the up factor is 1.2, the down factor is 0.9, and the continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4% per period (no dividends). The risk-neutral probability of an up move is closest to:\nA) 0.333 B) 0.469 C) 0.500 D) 0.531\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$p = \\frac{e^{rT} - d}{u - d} = \\frac{e^{0.04} - 0.9}{1.2 - 0.9} = \\frac{1.0408 - 0.9}{0.3} = 0.469$$\n\nDistractor C forgets that risk-neutral probabilities embed the risk-free drift; A uses $$(1-d)/(u-d)$$, i.e., growth of zero.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Risk-neutral valuation\",\"steps\":[\"Compute p = (e^{rT}−d)/(u−d)\",\"p embeds the risk-free drift, not real-world odds\",\"Value = e^{−rT} × E_p[payoff]\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q16.** A two-step binomial tree has $$S_0 = 100$$, $$u = 1.1$$, $$d = 0.9$$, and a continuously compounded risk-free rate of 2% per step. The value of a two-period European call with strike $100 is closest to:\nA) $5.04 B) $7.29 C) $7.44 D) $7.59\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$p = \\frac{e^{0.02} - 0.9}{1.1 - 0.9} = \\frac{0.1202}{0.2} = 0.601$$\n\nTerminal nodes: $$S_{uu} = 121$$ (payoff 21); $$S_{ud} = 99$$ and $$S_{dd} = 81$$ (payoff 0). Discount the probability-weighted payoff over two steps:\n\n$$C = e^{-0.04} \\times p^2 \\times 21 = 0.9608 \\times 0.3612 \\times 21 = \\$7.29$$\n\nDistractor D skips discounting; C discounts only one period; A uses $$p = 0.5$$.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"title\":\"Two-step binomial tree\",\"points\":[{\"t\":\"S₀=100\",\"l\":\"start\"},{\"t\":\"Step 1\",\"l\":\"110 or 90\"},{\"t\":\"Step 2\",\"l\":\"121/99/81\"},{\"t\":\"Payoffs\",\"l\":\"21,0,0\"},{\"t\":\"C=$7.29\",\"l\":\"e^-rT·p²·21\"}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q17.** A non-dividend stock trades at $80. Over one 6-month step it moves to either $92 or $70. The 6-month continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4% per annum. A European call with strike $84 is valued by replication / risk-neutral pricing. The call's value today is closest to:\nA) $4.14 B) $5.00 C) $4.22 D) $4.62\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: value a one-step European call using the risk-neutral probability.\n\n1. Identify u, d from the prices: $$u = 92/80 = 1.15$$, $$d = 70/80 = 0.875$$. Step Δt = 0.5, r = 0.04.\n2. Risk-neutral probability:\n   $$p = \\frac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d} = \\frac{e^{0.04 \\times 0.5} - 0.875}{1.15 - 0.875} = \\frac{e^{0.02} - 0.875}{0.275}$$\n   $$e^{0.02} = 1.020201 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p = \\frac{1.020201 - 0.875}{0.275} = \\frac{0.145201}{0.275} = 0.52800$$\n3. Terminal call payoffs (strike $84): up state $$\\max(92 - 84, 0) = 8$$; down state $$\\max(70 - 84, 0) = 0$$.\n4. Discounted expected payoff:\n   $$c = e^{-r\\Delta t}[p \\cdot 8 + (1-p) \\cdot 0] = e^{-0.02} \\times 0.52800 \\times 8$$\n   $$= 0.980199 \\times 4.2240 = \\$4.14$$\n\n- B ($5.00) uses a wrong p (≈ 0.625) — e.g., from setting the growth factor too high.\n- C ($4.22) forgets to discount the expected payoff ($$0.528 \\times 8 = 4.224$$, undiscounted).\n- D ($4.62) discounts at the wrong rate or mis-scales Δt in p.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a one-step tree, derive u and d from the given prices, compute $$p = (e^{r\\Delta t} - d)/(u - d)$$, then discount the p-weighted payoff at the risk-free rate. Real-world probabilities never enter.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A stock index has volatility σ = 25% per annum. A binomial tree uses 3-month (quarterly) steps. Using the Cox-Ross-Rubinstein calibration, the up factor u is closest to:\nA) 1.133 B) 1.077 C) 1.284 D) 1.025\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: calibrate u to volatility for a quarterly step.\n\n1. CRR up factor:\n   $$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$\n2. Δt = 3/12 = 0.25, so $$\\sqrt{\\Delta t} = 0.5$$.\n3. Exponent:\n   $$\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t} = 0.25 \\times 0.5 = 0.125$$\n4. Compute:\n   $$u = e^{0.125} = 1.13315 \\approx 1.133$$\n\n- B (1.077) forgets the square root and uses $$\\sigma \\cdot \\Delta t = 0.25 \\times 0.25 = 0.0625$$, giving $$e^{0.0625} = 1.0645$$ — or a related half-step error.\n- C (1.284) uses $$e^{\\sigma} = e^{0.25} = 1.284$$, ignoring Δt entirely (annual step assumed).\n- D (1.025) uses $$\\sigma^2 \\Delta t$$ or another mis-scaling.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}$$ — the time enters as a SQUARE ROOT. For a quarterly step √Δt = 0.5; for monthly √(1/12) ≈ 0.2887. Forgetting the √Δt is the most common slip.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A non-dividend stock is at $100. Over each 6-month step it moves up by u = 1.1 or down by d = 0.9. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 6% per annum, and the risk-neutral up probability is p = 0.6523. A two-step European call with strike $100 (1-year maturity) is worth closest to:\nA) $8.41 B) $9.61 C) $7.55 D) $13.29\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: value a two-step European call by backward induction (or the one-shot terminal-payoff shortcut).\n\n1. Terminal stock prices and call payoffs (K = $100):\n   - $$S_{uu} = 100 \\times 1.1^2 = 121 \\Rightarrow \\max(121-100,0) = 21$$\n   - $$S_{ud} = 100 \\times 1.1 \\times 0.9 = 99 \\Rightarrow \\max(99-100,0) = 0$$\n   - $$S_{dd} = 100 \\times 0.9^2 = 81 \\Rightarrow 0$$\n2. Probabilities of terminal nodes: $$P(uu) = p^2 = 0.6523^2 = 0.42550$$.\n3. European shortcut — discount the probability-weighted terminal payoff over the full year at r:\n   $$c = e^{-0.06 \\times 1}\\left[p^2(21) + 2p(1-p)(0) + (1-p)^2(0)\\right]$$\n   $$= e^{-0.06} \\times 0.42550 \\times 21 = 0.941765 \\times 8.9355 = 8.415 \\approx \\$8.41$$\n\n- B ($9.61) forgets to discount over the full year (uses one half-year discount or none).\n- C ($7.55) over-discounts or uses a too-low p.\n- D ($13.29) is the value at the UP node ($f_u$) — i.e., it stops one step early instead of rolling back to the root.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For European options you can discount the p-weighted TERMINAL payoffs in one shot at $$e^{-rT}$$. Only the $$S_{uu}$$ path is in the money here, so $$c = e^{-rT} p^2 \\times 21$$. Don't stop at the up node — that's the value one step too early.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Two-step European call (shortcut)\",\"steps\":[\"Terminal payoffs: uu=21, ud=0, dd=0\",\"Weight by probabilities: p^2, 2p(1-p), (1-p)^2\",\"Discount over full year e^(-rT)\",\"Value today = e^(-rT)·p^2·21\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Using the same tree ($$S_0 = 100$$, u = 1.1, d = 0.9, r = 6% continuous, Δt = 0.5, p = 0.6523), an AMERICAN put with strike $100 is valued. The put is worth closest to:\nA) $3.59 B) $2.59 C) $7.05 D) $1.97\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: value a two-step American put, checking early exercise (max of intrinsic and continuation) at every node.\n\n1. Terminal put payoffs (K = $100): $$S_{uu}=121 \\to 0$$; $$S_{ud}=99 \\to 1$$; $$S_{dd}=81 \\to 19$$.\n2. Up node (S = 110): continuation $$= e^{-0.06 \\times 0.5}[p \\cdot 0 + (1-p) \\cdot 1] = e^{-0.03} \\times 0.3477 = 0.970446 \\times 0.3477 = 0.3374$$. Intrinsic $$= \\max(100-110,0) = 0$$. Value = max(0, 0.337) = **0.337**.\n3. Down node (S = 90): continuation $$= e^{-0.03}[p \\cdot 1 + (1-p) \\cdot 19] = 0.970446 \\times (0.6523 + 0.3477 \\times 19) = 0.970446 \\times (0.6523 + 6.6063) = 0.970446 \\times 7.2586 = 7.044$$. Intrinsic $$= \\max(100-90,0) = 10$$. EXERCISE: max(10, 7.044) = **10**.\n4. Root (S = 100): continuation $$= e^{-0.03}[p \\cdot 0.337 + (1-p) \\cdot 10] = 0.970446 \\times (0.6523 \\times 0.337 + 0.3477 \\times 10) = 0.970446 \\times (0.2198 + 3.4770) = 0.970446 \\times 3.6968 = 3.588 \\approx \\$3.59$$. Intrinsic at root = 0, so the continuation value stands.\n\n- B ($2.59) is the EUROPEAN put value — it skips the early-exercise test at the down node and understates the American price.\n- C ($7.05) is the down-node continuation value (forgets to exercise and never rolls to the root).\n- D ($1.97) results from omitting the deep-ITM down-node payoff weighting.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For American options, take $$\\max(\\text{intrinsic}, \\text{continuation})$$ at EVERY non-terminal node — including the root. Here early exercise at the down node ($10 > $7.04) creates the entire $1.00 premium over the European put.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"American put — check exercise at each node\",\"steps\":[\"Terminal payoffs max(K−S,0)\",\"At each node compute continuation\",\"Take max(intrinsic, continuation)\",\"Down node: 10 > 7.04 → EXERCISE early\",\"Roll back to root = 3.59\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A binomial tree is built for a European option on a stock INDEX paying a continuous dividend yield of q = 2%. The domestic risk-free rate is r = 5%, u = 1.12, d = 0.89, and the step is 6 months (Δt = 0.5). The risk-neutral up probability is closest to:\nA) 0.543 B) 0.587 C) 0.499 D) 0.522\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute the risk-neutral probability using the dividend-adjusted growth rate in the numerator.\n\n1. For a dividend-paying index, the growth factor uses r − q:\n   $$p = \\frac{e^{(r-q)\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$\n2. Plug in: r − q = 0.05 − 0.02 = 0.03; Δt = 0.5; so exponent = 0.015.\n   $$e^{0.015} = 1.015113$$\n3. Compute:\n   $$p = \\frac{1.015113 - 0.89}{1.12 - 0.89} = \\frac{0.125113}{0.23} = 0.5440 \\approx 0.543$$\n\n- B (0.587) uses plain r (no dividend adjustment): $$e^{0.025} = 1.025315$$, $$p = 0.135313/0.23 = 0.588$$ — the classic \"forgot to subtract q\" trap.\n- C (0.499) subtracts too much or uses r − 2q.\n- D (0.522) mis-scales Δt (e.g., uses Δt = 1 with r − q).\n\n📌 Exam tip: The numerator growth factor is r for a non-dividend stock, r − q for an index/dividend stock, r − r_f for a currency, and 0 for futures. Discounting is ALWAYS at the domestic r. The \"use plain r\" answer is always offered as a distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** In a binomial tree for an option on a FUTURES contract, the correct risk-neutral up probability is:\nA) $$\\dfrac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ B) $$\\dfrac{1 - d}{u - d}$$ C) $$\\dfrac{e^{(r-q)\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ D) $$\\dfrac{e^{-r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The ask: identify the risk-neutral probability for an option on futures.\n\n1. Entering a futures position costs nothing, so in the risk-neutral world a futures price has ZERO drift — its expected growth factor is 1, not $$e^{r\\Delta t}$$.\n2. Setting the growth factor to 1 in the template $$p = \\frac{e^{(\\text{growth})\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$ gives:\n   $$p = \\frac{1 - d}{u - d}$$\n3. Discounting of the expected payoff still occurs at the domestic risk-free rate $$e^{-r\\Delta t}$$ — only the numerator drift is zero.\n\n- A is the formula for a non-dividend STOCK (growth = r), not futures.\n- C is the dividend-stock/index formula (growth = r − q).\n- D nonsensically uses $$e^{-r\\Delta t}$$ in the numerator (a sign error).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Futures have zero cost-of-carry drift in the risk-neutral world, so the growth factor is 1 ⟹ $$p = (1-d)/(u-d)$$. Discounting still uses $$e^{-r\\Delta t}$$.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Growth factor in p numerator\",\"root\":\"p = (e^(growth·Δt) − d)/(u − d)\",\"children\":[\"stock: growth = r\",\"index: growth = r − q\",\"currency: growth = r − rf\",\"futures: growth = 0 → p=(1−d)/(u−d)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** At a particular node of an American CALL on a non-dividend-paying stock, the intrinsic value is $6.50 and the continuation (discounted risk-neutral) value is $7.10. The value placed at that node, and the broader principle, is best stated as:\nA) $7.10; an American call on a non-dividend stock is never exercised early, so continuation is taken. B) $6.50; early exercise is always optimal for American calls. C) $6.80; the average of intrinsic and continuation is used. D) $7.10; but the same call should be exercised early if it were deep in the money.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: apply the early-exercise rule at a node and recall the no-early-exercise property of American calls on non-dividend stocks.\n\n1. Backward induction uses $$f_{node} = \\max(\\text{intrinsic}, \\text{continuation}) = \\max(6.50, 7.10) = 7.10$$.\n2. Consistent with theory: an American call on a non-dividend stock is NEVER exercised early — continuation always dominates intrinsic (here 7.10 > 6.50), because exercising forfeits the time value and the interest on the strike not yet paid.\n\n- B is doubly wrong: it picks the smaller value and asserts a false \"always exercise\" rule.\n- C averages the two values — backward induction takes the MAX, never an average.\n- D contradicts itself: an American call on a non-dividend stock is never optimally exercised early regardless of moneyness.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$\\max(\\text{intrinsic}, \\text{continuation})$$ at every node — never an average. An American call on a NON-dividend stock is never exercised early, so its value equals the European call's. Early exercise of calls can pay only with dividends, just before an ex-date.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"American call node: intrinsic 6.50, continuation 7.10\",\"options\":[\"7.10; never exercised early on non-dividend stock\",\"6.50; always exercise calls early\",\"6.80; take the average\",\"7.10; but exercise early if deep ITM\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Take max(6.50, 7.10)=7.10; an American call on a non-dividend stock is never optimally exercised early, regardless of moneyness.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"415","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"The Black-Scholes-Merton Model","notes":"# 15. The Black-Scholes-Merton Model\n\nThe closed-form benchmark for European options. GARP tests the lognormal setup, a full d₁/d₂/N(d) computation, the dividend adjustments, implied volatility as a concept, and the American-option caveats.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain the lognormal property of stock prices and compute the mean and variance of continuously compounded returns.\n- Distinguish realized (historical) volatility from implied volatility and estimate volatility from returns data.\n- List the BSM assumptions.\n- Compute European call and put prices with the BSM formulas.\n- Adjust BSM for dividends: continuous yield substitution and discrete PV-of-dividends adjustment.\n- Define implied volatility and explain how it is backed out of market prices.\n- Discuss American options in the BSM context (Black's approximation) and value warrants with dilution.\n\n## Lognormal stock prices\n\nBSM assumes the stock follows geometric Brownian motion, so the **price is lognormal** and the **continuously compounded return is normal**:\n\n$$\\ln S_T \\sim N\\!\\left(\\ln S_0 + \\left(\\mu - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}\\right)T,\\; \\sigma^2 T\\right)$$\n\nThe annualized continuously compounded return over [0, T] is normal with mean $$\\mu - \\sigma^2/2$$ and variance $$\\sigma^2/T$$. The σ²/2 drag is why the expected continuously compounded return is *less* than μ (the expected simple-return growth rate): volatility erodes compound growth. Prices can't go negative (lognormal support is positive), and the distribution is right-skewed.\n\n## Realized vs implied volatility\n\n**Realized (historical) volatility:** compute log returns $$u_i = \\ln(S_i/S_{i-1})$$, take their standard deviation s, and annualize:\n\n$$\\hat{\\sigma} = s\\sqrt{252} \\;\\; (\\text{daily data; } \\sqrt{52} \\text{ weekly, } \\sqrt{12} \\text{ monthly})$$\n\n**Implied volatility** is the σ that makes the BSM price equal the observed market price — found by iteration, since BSM can't be inverted analytically. Realized looks backward; implied is the market's forward-looking consensus (plus risk premia). Quoting options in \"vol\" terms is standard practice.\n\nImplied volatilities vary by strike and maturity (the **smile/skew** — equity index puts carry higher implied vols), which itself contradicts the constant-σ assumption of BSM.\n\n## BSM assumptions\n\n- Stock follows GBM with constant μ and σ; no jumps.\n- No dividends during the option's life (relaxed below).\n- No transaction costs or taxes; perfectly divisible securities; continuous trading.\n- Short selling allowed with full use of proceeds.\n- Constant risk-free rate r; borrowing = lending rate.\n- No riskless arbitrage opportunities.\n\nThe pricing argument mirrors the binomial one: a continuously rebalanced portfolio of the stock and the option is instantaneously riskless, must earn r, and μ drops out — only σ matters.\n\n## The formulas\n\n$$c = S_0 N(d_1) - K e^{-rT} N(d_2) \\qquad p = Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2) - S_0N(-d_1)$$\n\n$$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}} \\qquad d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$\n\nInterpretation: $$N(d_2)$$ is the risk-neutral probability the call finishes in the money; $$S_0 N(d_1) e^{rT}$$ is the risk-neutral expected stock price *given* exercise, times that probability. The two prices satisfy put–call parity $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$ by construction.\n\n### Worked example: full BSM call\n\n$$S_0 = 100$$, K = 95, r = 5%, σ = 20%, T = 0.5 years.\n\n$$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(100/95) + (0.05 + 0.02)(0.5)}{0.20\\sqrt{0.5}} = \\frac{0.05129 + 0.03500}{0.14142} = 0.6102$$\n\n$$d_2 = 0.6102 - 0.1414 = 0.4688$$\n\nFrom the normal table: N(0.61) = 0.7291, N(0.47) = 0.6808 (interpolating d₂ ≈ 0.47).\n\n$$c = 100(0.7291) - 95e^{-0.025}(0.6808) = 72.91 - 92.654 \\times 0.6808 = 72.91 - 63.08 = \\mathbf{\\$9.83}$$\n\nChecks worth a few seconds: c exceeds intrinsic value (\\$5) and is below $$S_0$$; lower bound $$S_0 - Ke^{-rT} = 7.35$$ is respected.\n\n### Worked example: put via put–call parity\n\nSame inputs. Rather than rerunning the formula with N(−d₁) and N(−d₂):\n\n$$p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0 = 9.83 + 92.65 - 100 = \\mathbf{\\$2.48}$$\n\nDirect check: $$p = 92.654 \\times N(-0.47) - 100 \\times N(-0.61) = 92.654(0.3192) - 100(0.2709) = 29.58 - 27.09 = 2.49$$ — equal to rounding. Parity is the fastest route to the second price and a free error check on the first.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The two highest-frequency slips: (1) using $$r - \\sigma^2/2$$ instead of $$r + \\sigma^2/2$$ in d₁ — the *minus* version belongs to the lognormal mean of ln S_T, not to d₁; (2) forgetting to discount K (using K instead of $$Ke^{-rT}$$). Both errors appear verbatim among the answer choices.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Normal-table mechanics: N(−x) = 1 − N(x), so one table serves both tails — N(−0.61) = 1 − 0.7291 = 0.2709. When d falls between table rows, interpolate linearly; GARP typically supplies the N(d) values or makes the rounding immaterial.\n\n## Dividends\n\n**Discrete dividends:** replace $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 - PV(\\text{divs})$$, the PV of dividends paid during the option's life (discounted at r), then apply BSM unchanged.\n\n**Continuous dividend yield q** (indices, FX where q = foreign rate): replace $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$:\n\n$$c = S_0 e^{-qT} N(d_1) - Ke^{-rT} N(d_2), \\qquad d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r - q + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$\n\nDividends lower call values and raise put values: the holder of an option doesn't receive dividends, and the stock price drops on ex-dividend dates.\n\n## American options and Black's approximation\n\nBSM prices **European** options only. An American call on a non-dividend stock is never exercised early, so its value equals the European call's — BSM applies directly. With dividends, early exercise of a call can be optimal **just before an ex-dividend date**. **Black's approximation:** value (1) a European call to final maturity T with $$S_0$$ reduced by PV of all dividends, and (2) a European call expiring just before the last ex-dividend date; take the larger. American puts may be exercised early even without dividends, so BSM understates them — use a binomial tree.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Put early exercise intuition: deep ITM, the most a put can ever pay is K, so collecting K − S today and earning interest beats waiting — the put's \"insurance value\" is no longer worth the delay. This is why American puts exceed European puts even with no dividends, and why BSM (European-only) understates them.\n\n## Warrants and dilution\n\nWarrants are call options issued by the company itself; exercise creates **new shares** and brings in cash, diluting existing holders. With N shares outstanding and M warrants:\n\n$$\\text{Warrant value} \\approx \\frac{N}{N + M} \\times c$$\n\nwhere c is the BSM value of a regular call on the (post-announcement) stock. The market typically impounds dilution into the stock price when the issue is announced, so the cost to shareholders is incurred at announcement, not exercise.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Implied-volatility logic questions: price and implied vol move together (vega > 0). If a quoted option price is *above* your BSM value computed with historical σ, the market's implied σ is *higher* than historical — no computation needed.","flashcards":"**Q1.** What is the lognormal property of stock prices?\n> **A:** Under GBM the price is lognormal and the continuously compounded return is normal: $$\\ln S_T \\sim N\\!\\left(\\ln S_0 + (\\mu - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2})T,\\ \\sigma^2 T\\right)$$. Prices can't go negative; the distribution is right-skewed.\n\n**Q2.** Mean of the continuously compounded return, and why it's below μ.\n> **A:** $$\\mu - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}$$. The σ²/2 drag reflects that volatility erodes compound growth, so the expected cc return is less than the simple-return growth rate μ.\n\n**Q3.** Compute the mean cc return: μ = 12%, σ = 30%.\n> **A:** $$0.12 - \\frac{0.09}{2} = 0.12 - 0.045 = 7.5\\%$$.\n\n**Q4.** Realized vs implied volatility.\n> **A:** Realized: std dev of log returns, annualized ($$\\hat\\sigma = s\\sqrt{252}$$ daily). Implied: the σ making the BSM price equal the market price, found by iteration — forward-looking (plus risk premia).\n\n**Q5.** State the BSM call and put formulas.\n> **A:** $$c = S_0 N(d_1) - Ke^{-rT}N(d_2)$$; $$p = Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2) - S_0 N(-d_1)$$. They satisfy put-call parity $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$.\n\n**Q6.** Write d₁ and d₂.\n> **A:** $$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$, $$d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$.\n\n**Q7.** Interpret N(d₂) and N(d₁).\n> **A:** N(d₂) = risk-neutral probability the call finishes in the money. N(d₁) = the call's delta (not the exercise probability).\n\n**Q8.** Compute d₁: S₀ = 100, K = 95, r = 5%, σ = 20%, T = 0.5.\n> **A:** $$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(100/95) + (0.05+0.02)(0.5)}{0.20\\sqrt{0.5}} = \\frac{0.05129 + 0.035}{0.14142} = 0.6102$$; $$d_2 = 0.4688$$.\n\n**Q9.** Two highest-frequency BSM slips.\n> **A:** Using $$r - \\sigma^2/2$$ instead of $$r + \\sigma^2/2$$ in d₁ (the minus belongs to the lognormal mean of ln S_T), and forgetting to discount K (using K instead of $$Ke^{-rT}$$).\n\n**Q10.** Adjust BSM for dividends.\n> **A:** Discrete: replace S₀ with $$S_0 - PV(\\text{divs})$$. Continuous yield q: replace S₀ with $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ and use $$r-q$$ in d₁. Dividends lower call values and raise put values.\n\n**Q11.** Find a put by put-call parity: c = 4.20, K = 50, S₀ = 50, r = 4%, T = 1.\n> **A:** $$p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0 = 4.20 + 50e^{-0.04} - 50 = 4.20 + 48.039 - 50 = \\$2.24$$.\n\n**Q12.** When can American options be valued with BSM, and what is Black's approximation?\n> **A:** American call on a non-dividend stock = European call (never exercised early), so BSM applies. With dividends, a call may be exercised just before an ex-dividend date; Black's approximation takes the larger of two European call values. American puts may be exercised early even without dividends — use a tree.\n\n**Q13.** How does warrant dilution affect value?\n> **A:** Exercise creates new shares and brings in cash, diluting holders: $$\\text{Warrant value} \\approx \\frac{N}{N+M}\\times c$$ (N shares, M warrants). The market typically impounds dilution at announcement.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A stock has an expected (simple) return of 12% and a volatility of 30%. Under the lognormal model, the mean of the continuously compounded annual return is:\nA) 12.0% B) 7.5% C) 16.5% D) 10.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: convert the expected simple return into the mean continuously compounded return.\n\nThe mean continuously compounded return is:\n$$\\mu - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2} = 0.12 - \\frac{0.30^2}{2} = 0.12 - \\frac{0.09}{2} = 0.12 - 0.045 = 0.075 = 7.5\\%$$\n\n- A (12.0%) ignores the variance drag $$\\sigma^2/2$$ — it treats simple and continuously compounded means as equal.\n- C (16.5%) ADDS the drag instead of subtracting it ($$0.12 + 0.045$$) — wrong sign.\n- D (10.2%) subtracts σ (0.30 × something) incorrectly rather than $$\\sigma^2/2$$.\n\nAnalogy: volatility is like a bumpy road — the rougher the ride (higher σ), the more your average COMPOUND speed falls below your expected speed, even though you set out at 12%.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Mean continuously compounded return = $$\\mu - \\sigma^2/2$$. The $$\\sigma^2/2$$ \"drag\" always SUBTRACTS — higher volatility lowers compound growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the Black-Scholes-Merton call formula, $$N(d_2)$$ is best interpreted as:\nA) The option's delta B) The risk-neutral probability that the call expires in the money C) The real-world probability of exercise D) The hedge ratio for the corresponding put\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: what does $$N(d_2)$$ represent in the BSM formula?\n\n$$N(d_2)$$ is the risk-neutral probability that the call finishes in the money (i.e., gets exercised). It appears multiplied by the discounted strike $$Ke^{-rT}$$, representing the probability-weighted cost of paying the strike.\n\n- A is wrong; the call's delta is $$N(d_1)$$, not $$N(d_2)$$.\n- C is wrong on a subtle point: it is the RISK-NEUTRAL probability, not the real-world one.\n- D is wrong; $$N(d_2)$$ is not a hedge ratio.\n\nAnalogy: think of $$N(d_2)$$ as the \"chance you'll actually pay the strike and collect the stock\" in the make-believe risk-neutral world — it tells you how much of the strike cost to count.\n\n📌 Exam tip: $$N(d_1)$$ = call delta; $$N(d_2)$$ = risk-neutral probability of exercise. Don't swap them — both appear among the answer choices.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"N(d1) vs N(d2) in BSM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"N(d1)\",\"items\":[\"call delta (hedge ratio)\",\"multiplies S0\"]},{\"h\":\"N(d2)\",\"items\":[\"risk-neutral prob of exercise\",\"multiplies Ke^(-rT)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A European call on a stock index that pays a continuous dividend yield q is valued with BSM by:\nA) Replacing r with r + q B) Replacing $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ C) Replacing K with $$Ke^{-qT}$$ D) Adding the present value of dividends to $$S_0$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how do you adjust BSM for a continuous dividend yield?\n\nFor a continuous dividend yield q, you replace the spot price $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ everywhere it appears (and q also enters $$d_1$$ through the $$r - q$$ term). This shrinks the effective stock price because the option holder does NOT receive the dividends.\n\n- A adjusts the wrong variable in the wrong direction.\n- C discounts the strike by q, which is not how the adjustment works (the strike is discounted by r).\n- D goes the wrong way and describes the DISCRETE-dividend case, where you SUBTRACT (not add) the PV of dividends from $$S_0$$.\n\nAnalogy: dividends \"leak\" value out of the stock that the option holder never gets, so you value the option as if the stock were smaller by the present value of that leakage — $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Continuous yield → use $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ (and $$r - q$$ in $$d_1$$). Discrete dividends → use $$S_0 - PV(\\text{divs})$$. Dividends LOWER call values and RAISE put values.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Dividend adjustment to BSM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Continuous yield q\",\"items\":[\"replace S0 with S0·e^(-qT)\",\"use r − q in d1\"]},{\"h\":\"Discrete dividends\",\"items\":[\"replace S0 with S0 − PV(divs)\",\"subtract, not add\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A European call is worth \\$4.20. The stock is at \\$50, the strike is \\$50, r = 4%, and T = 1 year. Using put-call parity, the European put price is closest to:\nA) \\$4.20 B) \\$2.24 C) \\$6.16 D) \\$2.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: find the put price from the call price using put-call parity.\n\nPut-call parity: $$p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0$$.\n\nStep 1 — discount the strike:\n$$Ke^{-rT} = 50 \\times e^{-0.04 \\times 1} = 50 \\times 0.96079 = 48.039$$\n\nStep 2 — plug in:\n$$p = 4.20 + 48.039 - 50 = 2.239 \\approx \\$2.24$$\n\n- A (\\$4.20) is the result of forgetting to discount the strike — then parity gives $$p = c$$, the trap.\n- C (\\$6.16) adds the strike-discount the wrong way.\n- D (\\$2.50) is a rounding/setup slip.\n\nAnalogy: put-call parity is a balance scale linking calls and puts. Once you know one side (the call) and the \"interest savings\" on the strike ($$Ke^{-rT}$$), the other side (the put) is forced.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Parity $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$ is the fastest route to the second price and a free error check. The most common mistake is using K instead of $$Ke^{-rT}$$.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** For a non-dividend-paying stock, $$S_0 = 100$$, K = 95, r = 5%, σ = 20%, T = 0.5. The value of $$d_1$$ is closest to (use $$\\ln(100/95) = 0.05129$$):\nA) 0.6102 B) 0.4688 C) 0.7500 D) 0.3624\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The question asks: compute $$d_1$$ in the BSM formula.\n\nThe formula is:\n$$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$\n\nStep 1 — numerator. $$\\ln(100/95) = 0.05129$$. The drift term: $$(0.05 + 0.20^2/2)(0.5) = (0.05 + 0.02)(0.5) = 0.07 \\times 0.5 = 0.035$$.\nNumerator $$= 0.05129 + 0.035 = 0.08629$$.\n\nStep 2 — denominator: $$\\sigma\\sqrt{T} = 0.20 \\times \\sqrt{0.5} = 0.20 \\times 0.7071 = 0.14142$$.\n\nStep 3 — divide:\n$$d_1 = \\frac{0.08629}{0.14142} = 0.6102$$\n\n- B (0.4688) is $$d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma\\sqrt{T} = 0.6102 - 0.1414$$ — a different quantity.\n- C (0.7500) drops or mis-scales a term.\n- D (0.3624) likely uses $$r - \\sigma^2/2$$ instead of $$r + \\sigma^2/2$$ — the classic sign trap.\n\nAnalogy: $$d_1$$ measures how far \"in the money\" the option is, in standardized (volatility-adjusted) units — the bigger $$d_1$$, the deeper in the money and the higher the call's delta.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Use $$r + \\sigma^2/2$$ in $$d_1$$ (the PLUS version). The minus version belongs to the lognormal mean of $$\\ln S_T$$, not to $$d_1$$ — GARP plants both among the answers.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Implied volatility is best defined as:\nA) The historical standard deviation of past returns B) The volatility input that makes the BSM model price equal the observed market price C) The volatility forecast published by exchanges D) The average of daily volatilities over the past month\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: define implied volatility.\n\nImplied volatility is the value of σ that, when plugged into the BSM formula, produces a model price equal to the option's actual market price. Because BSM cannot be inverted algebraically for σ, it is found by iteration (trial and error). It reflects the market's forward-looking view of volatility.\n\n- A and D both describe REALIZED (historical) volatility — backward-looking, computed from past returns.\n- C is not a definition of implied volatility.\n\nAnalogy: implied volatility is like reverse-engineering a recipe — you taste the finished dish (the market price) and figure out how much of the key ingredient (σ) must have gone in to produce it.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Realized vol looks backward (from past returns); implied vol looks forward (backed out of current market prices). Implied vol is the market's consensus and varies by strike/maturity (the volatility smile/skew).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Realized vs implied volatility\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Realized (historical)\",\"items\":[\"backward-looking\",\"std dev of past returns\"]},{\"h\":\"Implied\",\"items\":[\"forward-looking\",\"σ that makes BSM = market price\",\"found by iteration\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is NOT an assumption of the Black-Scholes-Merton model?\nA) The stock price follows geometric Brownian motion with constant volatility B) There are no transaction costs or taxes C) The risk-free rate is constant D) Investors are risk-averse and require a risk premium that enters the option price\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The question asks: identify the statement that is NOT a BSM assumption.\n\nBSM prices options by no-arbitrage replication, in which the expected return μ — and any risk premium — drops out entirely. Only σ matters. So the option price does NOT depend on investors' risk preferences; statement D contradicts the model.\n\n- A is a core BSM assumption (GBM, constant σ, no jumps).\n- B is a BSM assumption (frictionless markets).\n- C is a BSM assumption (constant r).\n\nAnalogy: just like the binomial tree, BSM's price comes from \"what it costs to replicate the option,\" not from how much investors fear risk. Risk premiums cancel out.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A defining feature of BSM (and binomial) pricing: μ and risk premia are irrelevant — only volatility, rates, time, and the strike matter. Any answer invoking \"risk premium in the price\" is wrong.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"NOT a BSM assumption\",\"options\":[\"GBM with constant volatility\",\"No transaction costs or taxes\",\"Constant risk-free rate\",\"Investors require a risk premium in the price\"],\"correct\":3,\"why\":\"BSM prices by no-arbitrage replication: μ and risk premia drop out. Only σ, r, T, K, S matter.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Other things equal, increasing the continuous dividend yield q on a stock will:\nA) Increase call values and decrease put values B) Decrease call values and increase put values C) Increase both call and put values D) Have no effect on option values\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how do higher dividends affect call and put prices?\n\nDividends transfer value out of the stock (the price drops on ex-dividend dates), and the option holder does not receive them. This makes the effective stock price lower ($$S_0 e^{-qT}$$):\n- Lower effective stock price → calls worth LESS (a call benefits from a high stock price).\n- Lower effective stock price → puts worth MORE (a put benefits from a low stock price).\n\n- A reverses the effect.\n- C and D are both wrong; dividends have opposite effects on calls vs puts, not equal/no effect.\n\nAnalogy: dividends are like air slowly leaking from a balloon (the stock). A call (betting the balloon stays big) loses value; a put (betting it shrinks) gains value.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Higher dividends → calls DOWN, puts UP. This is also why an American call on a dividend stock might be exercised early (just before ex-dividend), unlike on a non-dividend stock.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Higher dividend yield q\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Calls\",\"items\":[\"effective S0 lower (S0·e^-qT)\",\"value DOWN\"]},{\"h\":\"Puts\",\"items\":[\"effective S0 lower\",\"value UP\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** An American call option on a NON-dividend-paying stock should be:\nA) Exercised early as soon as it is in the money B) Never exercised early, so its value equals the European call value C) Always worth more than the underlying stock D) Valued only with a binomial tree, never with BSM\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how should an American call on a non-dividend stock be treated?\n\nAn American call on a non-dividend stock is NEVER optimally exercised early — you always do better selling the option or holding it (you keep the time value and the interest on the unpaid strike). Therefore its value equals the European call's, and BSM applies directly.\n\n- A is the classic mistake; early exercise throws away time value with nothing (no dividend) to gain.\n- C is false; an option is worth less than the stock (delta < 1).\n- D is false; precisely because early exercise is never optimal, BSM (European) gives the right value.\n\nAnalogy: exercising early is like cashing in a coupon before it's due when there's no penalty for waiting — you lose the remaining flexibility for no reason.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American call on a NON-dividend stock = European call (BSM works). With dividends, early exercise can be optimal just before an ex-dividend date — use Black's approximation or a tree. American PUTS may be exercised early even with no dividends.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"American call, non-dividend stock\",\"options\":[\"Exercise early once in the money\",\"Never exercise early; value = European call\",\"Always worth more than the stock\",\"Only a binomial tree can price it\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Early exercise forfeits time value and interest on the unpaid strike, so it is never optimal; value equals the European call and BSM applies.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A company has 10 million shares outstanding and issues 2 million warrants. If a regular BSM call on the (post-announcement) stock is worth \\$6, the approximate value of one warrant, accounting for dilution, is closest to:\nA) \\$6.00 B) \\$5.00 C) \\$7.20 D) \\$1.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: adjust a call value for the dilution caused by warrants.\n\nWhen warrants are exercised, the company issues NEW shares, diluting existing holders. The warrant value is scaled by the dilution factor:\n$$\\text{Warrant value} \\approx \\frac{N}{N + M} \\times c$$\nwhere N = existing shares, M = warrants.\n\nStep 1 — dilution factor:\n$$\\frac{N}{N+M} = \\frac{10}{10 + 2} = \\frac{10}{12} = 0.8333$$\n\nStep 2 — multiply by the call value:\n$$0.8333 \\times 6 = \\$5.00$$\n\n- A (\\$6.00) ignores dilution entirely — treats the warrant like a regular call.\n- C (\\$7.20) multiplies by $$12/10 = 1.2$$ — the dilution factor upside-down (makes it MORE valuable, which is backward).\n- D (\\$1.00) subtracts incorrectly.\n\nAnalogy: issuing new shares to warrant holders is like cutting a pizza into more slices — each existing slice (and each warrant's claim) is a bit smaller, so the warrant is worth less than a plain call.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Warrant value ≈ $$\\frac{N}{N+M} \\times c$$ — the dilution factor is always less than 1, so warrants are worth LESS than otherwise-identical calls.\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A stock has expected return 12% and volatility 30%. The mean of the continuously compounded annual return is:\nA) 12.0% B) 7.5% C) 16.5% D) 10.2%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Mean cc return = μ − σ²/2 = 0.12 − 0.09/2 = 0.12 − 0.045 = 7.5%.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In BSM, N(d₂) is best interpreted as:\nA) The option's delta B) The risk-neutral probability that a call expires in the money C) The real-world probability of exercise D) The hedge ratio for the put\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** N(d₂) = risk-neutral exercise probability; N(d₁) is the call's delta (and is *not* the exercise probability). It is risk-neutral, not real-world.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A European call on an index paying a 3% continuous dividend yield is valued by:\nA) Replacing r with r + q B) Replacing S₀ with S₀e^{−qT} C) Replacing K with Ke^{−qT} D) Adding PV of dividends to S₀\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Continuous-yield adjustment substitutes S₀e^{−qT} for S₀ (q also enters d₁ through r − q). Option D describes the wrong direction — discrete dividends are *subtracted*.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** With S₀ = 50, c = 4.20, K = 50, r = 4%, T = 1: the European put price by parity is closest to:\nA) \\$4.20 B) \\$2.24 C) \\$6.16 D) \\$2.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** p = c + Ke^{−rT} − S₀ = 4.20 + 50e^{−0.04} − 50 = 4.20 + 48.039 − 50 = \\$2.24. Choice A is the result of forgetting to discount the strike (parity then gives p = c at the money).\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Medium\n\n**Q15.** A non-dividend stock trades at $50. For an at-the-money call with strike $50, six months to expiry, $$r = 4\\%$$ (continuous), and volatility 30%, the BSM $$d_1$$ is closest to:\nA) −0.01 B) 0.09 C) 0.20 D) 0.31\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(50/50) + \\left(0.04 + \\tfrac{0.30^2}{2}\\right) \\times 0.5}{0.30\\sqrt{0.5}} = \\frac{0.0425}{0.2121} = 0.20$$\n\nDistractor A is $$d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma\\sqrt{T} = -0.01$$; B omits the $$\\sigma^2/2$$ term; D adds $$\\sigma^2$$ without halving it.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"BSM assumptions\",\"root\":\"BSM model\",\"children\":[\"Lognormal prices\",\"Constant σ and r\",\"No dividends\",\"Frictionless, no arbitrage\",\"European exercise\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Using the Q25 inputs with $$N(d_1) = 0.5793$$ and $$N(d_2) = 0.4960$$, the BSM call price is closest to:\nA) $4.17 B) $4.26 C) $4.66 D) $4.97\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$C = S\\,N(d_1) - K e^{-rT} N(d_2) = 50 \\times 0.5793 - 50\\,e^{-0.02} \\times 0.4960$$\n\n$$= 28.965 - 49.010 \\times 0.4960 = 28.965 - 24.309 = \\$4.66$$\n\nDistractor A forgets to discount the strike. Sanity check: an ATM call must be worth more than $$S - Ke^{-rT} = \\$0.99$$ and the price is mostly time value — $4.66 is sensible for 30% vol over half a year.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A one-year European call with strike $100 on a non-dividend stock trading at $100 is priced at $6.20. With a continuously compounded risk-free rate of 5%, put-call parity implies the one-year $100-strike put is worth closest to:\nA) $1.32 B) $6.20 C) $11.08 D) $4.88\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Put-call parity: $$P = C - S + Ke^{-rT}$$\n\n$$P = 6.20 - 100 + 100\\,e^{-0.05} = 6.20 - 100 + 95.12 = \\$1.32$$\n\nDistractor B uses the undiscounted strike ($$6.20 - 100 + 100$$); C flips the sign on $$S$$.\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A stock has an expected (simple) return of 14% per annum and a volatility of 28%. Under the BSM lognormal framework, the mean of the continuously compounded annual return and the standard deviation of $$\\ln S_T$$ over a 4-year horizon (T = 4) are closest to:\nA) Mean 10.08%; std dev of ln S_T = 0.560 B) Mean 14.00%; std dev of ln S_T = 0.280 C) Mean 10.08%; std dev of ln S_T = 0.280 D) Mean 13.61%; std dev of ln S_T = 0.560\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute the mean continuously compounded return (with the variance drag) and the standard deviation of log-price over T = 4.\n\n1. Mean of the continuously compounded return:\n   $$\\mu - \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2} = 0.14 - \\frac{0.28^2}{2} = 0.14 - \\frac{0.0784}{2} = 0.14 - 0.0392 = 0.1008 = 10.08\\%$$\n2. The variance of $$\\ln S_T$$ is $$\\sigma^2 T$$, so the standard deviation is:\n   $$\\sigma\\sqrt{T} = 0.28 \\times \\sqrt{4} = 0.28 \\times 2 = 0.560$$\n\n- B uses μ itself (14%) as the cc mean — it ignores the −σ²/2 variance drag — and uses σ (not σ√T) for the dispersion.\n- C has the right mean but uses σ rather than σ√T for the 4-year dispersion.\n- D mis-computes the drag (e.g., subtracts σ²/4) while getting the dispersion right.\n\n📌 Exam tip: The cc return mean is $$\\mu - \\sigma^2/2$$ (volatility erodes compound growth). The dispersion of $$\\ln S_T$$ scales with $$\\sqrt{T}$$: std dev = σ√T.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A European call has $$S_0 = 100$$, K = 95, r = 5%, σ = 20%, T = 0.5 years. Given N(0.61) = 0.7291 and N(0.47) = 0.6808, the BSM call value is closest to:\nA) $9.83 B) $5.00 C) $11.65 D) $8.06\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: compute a full BSM European call price.\n\n1. Compute d₁:\n   $$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$\n   $$\\ln(100/95) = \\ln(1.052632) = 0.051293; \\quad (0.05 + 0.02)(0.5) = 0.035$$\n   $$d_1 = \\frac{0.051293 + 0.035}{0.20 \\times 0.707107} = \\frac{0.086293}{0.141421} = 0.6102 \\approx 0.61$$\n2. Compute d₂:\n   $$d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma\\sqrt{T} = 0.6102 - 0.1414 = 0.4688 \\approx 0.47$$\n3. Look up: N(d₁) = N(0.61) = 0.7291; N(d₂) = N(0.47) = 0.6808.\n4. Discount the strike: $$Ke^{-rT} = 95 \\times e^{-0.025} = 95 \\times 0.975310 = 92.654$$.\n5. Call price:\n   $$c = S_0 N(d_1) - Ke^{-rT}N(d_2) = 100(0.7291) - 92.654(0.6808)$$\n   $$= 72.91 - 63.08 = \\$9.83$$\n\n- B ($5.00) is the intrinsic value ($$S_0 - K = 5$$) — it ignores time value entirely.\n- C ($11.65) forgets to discount K (uses $$95 \\times 0.6808 = 64.68$$... actually larger if the σ²/2 sign is flipped to +; a representative \"no-discount/wrong-d₁\" error).\n- D ($8.06) uses $$r - \\sigma^2/2$$ in d₁ instead of $$r + \\sigma^2/2$$ — the wrong sign on the variance term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Two highest-frequency BSM slips: (1) use $$r + \\sigma^2/2$$ (not minus) in d₁; (2) discount the strike to $$Ke^{-rT}$$. Both wrong answers appear among the choices. Sanity-check: c > intrinsic ($5) and c < S₀.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Using the same option as Q2 ($$c = \\$9.83$$, K = 95, r = 5%, T = 0.5, $$S_0 = 100$$), the BSM European PUT value, found via put-call parity, is closest to:\nA) $2.48 B) $4.83 C) $7.35 D) $0.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: obtain the put price from put-call parity rather than re-running the formula.\n\n1. Put-call parity: $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0$$\n2. Discount the strike: $$Ke^{-rT} = 95 \\times e^{-0.025} = 95 \\times 0.975310 = 92.654$$\n3. Solve:\n   $$p = 9.83 + 92.654 - 100 = 2.484 \\approx \\$2.48$$\n\n- B ($4.83) forgets to discount K, using $$9.83 + 95 - 100 = 4.83$$ — the most common parity error.\n- C ($7.35) is the call's lower bound $$S_0 - Ke^{-rT} = 7.346$$, not the put.\n- D ($0.00) assumes the put is worthless because the call is in the money — ignores parity.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Put-call parity $$p = c + Ke^{-rT} - S_0$$ is the fastest route to the second price AND a free check on the first. Always discount the strike — forgetting to do so is the planted distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A European call is written on a stock index with a continuous dividend yield q = 3%. Which adjustment to the standard (non-dividend) BSM call formula correctly incorporates the dividend yield?\nA) Replace $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ (and use r − q in d₁). B) Replace r with r + q throughout. C) Replace K with $$Ke^{-qT}$$. D) Add the present value of dividends to $$S_0$$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: identify the correct continuous-dividend-yield adjustment to BSM.\n\n1. For a continuous dividend yield, substitute $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ for $$S_0$$ in the price formula and use r − q in the drift term of d₁:\n   $$c = S_0 e^{-qT}N(d_1) - Ke^{-rT}N(d_2), \\quad d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r - q + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$\n2. Economically, the option holder does not receive dividends, and the stock drops on ex-dates, so the effective forward growth is reduced by q. This LOWERS the call value (and raises the put).\n\n- B (r + q) moves in the wrong direction — it would raise the call value; dividends should lower it.\n- C discounts the STRIKE by q, which is meaningless — q affects the underlying's growth, not the strike.\n- D describes the DISCRETE-dividend adjustment, and even then dividends are SUBTRACTED from $$S_0$$ (as $$S_0 - PV(\\text{divs})$$), not added.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Continuous yield ⟹ replace $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 e^{-qT}$$ and use r − q in d₁. Discrete dividends ⟹ replace $$S_0$$ with $$S_0 - PV(\\text{divs})$$. Dividends lower calls and raise puts.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Continuous dividend yield adjustment to BSM\",\"options\":[\"Replace S0 with S0·e^(-qT) and use r−q in d1\",\"Replace r with r+q\",\"Replace K with K·e^(-qT)\",\"Add PV(dividends) to S0\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"For a continuous yield q, the effective stock price is S0·e^(-qT) and the drift in d1 becomes r−q, lowering call value.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An analyst computes the BSM value of a 3-month European call using the stock's historical volatility and gets $3.10. The same option is quoted in the market at $3.55. Which statement is correct?\nA) The option's implied volatility is higher than the historical volatility. B) The option's implied volatility is lower than the historical volatility. C) The implied volatility equals the historical volatility. D) The market price violates the BSM no-arbitrage bounds.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: infer the relationship between implied and historical volatility from the price gap, using vega > 0.\n\n1. Vega is positive: option price rises monotonically with σ. So price and implied vol move together.\n2. The market price ($3.55) EXCEEDS the BSM value computed with historical σ ($3.10). To raise the model price to the market price, σ must be increased.\n3. Therefore the implied volatility (the σ that reproduces the $3.55 market price) is HIGHER than the historical volatility used to get $3.10.\n\n- B reverses the logic — a higher market price requires a higher, not lower, implied σ.\n- C is impossible given the price gap (equal vols would give equal prices).\n- D is unsupported — a higher market price is perfectly consistent with a higher implied vol, no bound violation.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Vega > 0 means price and implied vol move together. Market price ABOVE your historical-vol BSM value ⟹ implied vol ABOVE historical. No computation needed — just the sign of vega.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Vega > 0: price tracks volatility\",\"items\":[\"market price 3.55 > model 3.10\",\"→ need higher σ to match\",\"implied vol > historical vol\"],\"left\":\"lower σ → lower price\",\"right\":\"higher σ → higher price\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A non-dividend-paying stock's American CALL and the otherwise-identical European call have which relationship, and why?\nA) They have equal value, because an American call on a non-dividend stock is never optimally exercised early. B) The American call is strictly more valuable, because early exercise adds value. C) The European call is more valuable, because it cannot be exercised prematurely. D) They are equal only if the option is at the money.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: state the American-vs-European call relationship for a non-dividend stock and the reason.\n\n1. Early exercise of a call on a non-dividend stock is never optimal: exercising forfeits the remaining time value and the interest that could be earned on the strike not yet paid.\n2. Because the early-exercise right is never used, it adds no value — the American call's price equals the European call's price.\n3. Consequently BSM (a European model) prices the American call exactly, with no adjustment needed.\n\n- B is the trap: the early-exercise FEATURE exists, but it is never optimally exercised on a non-dividend stock, so it adds nothing.\n- C is backward — an American option has every right a European has, so it can never be worth less.\n- D introduces a false moneyness condition; the equality holds for all strikes.\n\n📌 Exam tip: American call = European call for a NON-dividend stock (never exercise early). With dividends, early exercise of a call can pay just before an ex-date — then use Black's approximation. American PUTS can exceed European puts even with no dividends.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"American vs European call, non-dividend stock\",\"options\":[\"Equal value — never optimally exercised early\",\"American strictly more valuable\",\"European more valuable\",\"Equal only if at the money\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Early exercise forfeits time value and interest on the strike, so the right is never used and adds no value; the two are equal.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A company has 8 million shares outstanding and issues 2 million warrants (each on one share). The BSM value of an otherwise-equivalent exchange-traded call on the post-announcement stock is $6.00. The approximate value of each warrant, accounting for dilution, is closest to:\nA) $4.80 B) $6.00 C) $1.20 D) $7.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: apply the dilution adjustment to a warrant value.\n\n1. Warrant value with dilution:\n   $$\\text{Warrant} \\approx \\frac{N}{N + M} \\times c$$\n   where N = existing shares = 8 million, M = warrants = 2 million, c = BSM call value = $6.00.\n2. Dilution factor:\n   $$\\frac{N}{N+M} = \\frac{8}{8 + 2} = \\frac{8}{10} = 0.80$$\n3. Warrant value:\n   $$0.80 \\times 6.00 = \\$4.80$$\n\n- B ($6.00) ignores dilution entirely (treats the warrant like an ordinary call).\n- C ($1.20) applies the wrong factor $$\\frac{M}{N+M} = 0.20$$ (the diluted-away fraction, not the retained fraction).\n- D ($7.50) inverts the factor, multiplying by $$\\frac{N+M}{N} = 1.25$$.\n\n📌 Exam tip: A warrant is worth $$\\frac{N}{N+M} \\times c$$ — the call value scaled DOWN by the share-dilution factor (existing shares over total post-exercise shares). The complement $$M/(N+M)$$ is a distractor.\n</details>\n"},{"num":"416","module":"Book 4: Valuation and Risk Models","title":"Option Sensitivity Measures: The Greeks","notes":"# 16. Option Sensitivity Measures: The Greeks\n\nDelta, gamma, theta, vega, and rho: what they measure, their signs, where they peak, and how dealers use them to neutralize books. Expect a delta-hedge share count, a delta–gamma construction, and at least one sign question.\n\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Define and compute delta for calls, puts, and portfolios; describe delta hedging and dynamic rebalancing.\n- Define theta, gamma, vega, and rho; describe their signs, peaks, and behavior as expiry approaches.\n- Use the delta–gamma approximation for the price change of an option position.\n- Construct delta-neutral and delta–gamma-neutral portfolios using traded options.\n- State the relationship among theta, delta, and gamma for a delta-neutral portfolio.\n- Describe portfolio insurance as synthetic put replication and its limitations.\n\n## Delta\n\nDelta is the sensitivity of the option price to the stock price, $$\\delta = \\partial f / \\partial S$$. Under BSM (no dividends):\n\n$$\\delta_{call} = N(d_1) \\in (0,1) \\qquad \\delta_{put} = N(d_1) - 1 \\in (-1,0)$$\n\nATM calls have delta near 0.5 (slightly above, since d₁ > 0 at the money). As expiry nears, call delta migrates to 1 (ITM) or 0 (OTM). Portfolio delta is the position-weighted sum: $$\\delta_{port} = \\sum_i n_i \\delta_i$$ — and one share has delta 1.\n\n**Delta hedging.** A short call (delta −N(d₁) per option to the writer) is hedged by holding $$N(d_1)$$ shares per option. Because delta changes with S and t, the hedge must be **rebalanced** — *dynamic hedging*. The cost of rebalancing (buy high after rallies, sell low after declines, for a short-gamma hedger) is exactly what the option premium pays for.\n\n### Worked example: delta hedging a short call, then rebalancing\n\nA dealer writes 1,000 calls (10 contracts on 100 shares) with delta 0.62. Position delta = −1,000 × 0.62 = −620. **Buy 620 shares** → portfolio delta zero.\n\nThe stock rallies and the calls' delta rises to 0.70. Position delta = −700 + 620 = −80. **Buy 80 more shares** (total 700). The hedger bought *after* the price rose — repeated, this \"buy high, sell low\" pattern is the rebalancing cost of being short gamma.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Deltas of the linear instruments, for portfolio arithmetic: a share has delta +1; a forward on a non-dividend stock has delta exactly 1; a futures contract has delta $$e^{rT}$$ (daily settlement). Hedging with futures therefore needs slightly *fewer* contracts than the share count suggests.\n\n## Theta\n\nTheta is the time decay $$\\Theta = \\partial f / \\partial t$$ — usually quoted per calendar day (annual θ / 365). Theta is **negative for most long options**: with everything else fixed, value erodes as expiry approaches. Exceptions: a **deep ITM European put** can have positive theta (its price sits below intrinsic value, $$Ke^{-rT} - S$$, and pulls *up* toward K − S), and deep ITM European calls on high-dividend-yield assets similarly. Theta is most negative for ATM options close to expiry. Theta is not a hedgeable risk — time passes deterministically — but it is the flip side of gamma.\n\n## Gamma\n\nGamma is the rate of change of delta: $$\\Gamma = \\partial^2 f / \\partial S^2 = \\frac{N'(d_1)}{S_0\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$. It is **positive for long calls and long puts** alike, and **largest for ATM options near expiry** (delta snaps from 0 to 1 across the strike). Gamma measures how fast a delta hedge goes stale.\n\nThe **delta–gamma approximation** for a position's value change:\n\n$$\\Delta P \\approx \\delta\\, \\Delta S + \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma\\, (\\Delta S)^2$$\n\n— the option-market twin of duration-plus-convexity. For a delta-neutral long-gamma book, P&L ≈ ½Γ(ΔS)² > 0 for any large move; the short-gamma book bleeds on large moves and collects theta in between.\n\n## Vega and rho\n\n**Vega** $$= \\partial f / \\partial \\sigma$$: positive for long calls and puts, **highest for ATM options** and for *longer* maturities (unlike gamma, which peaks at short maturities). Quoted per 1% vol change.\n\n**Rho** $$= \\partial f / \\partial r$$: **positive for calls** (higher r → lower PV of strike paid), **negative for puts**. For European options, rho_call $$= KTe^{-rT}N(d_2)$$. Usually the least important Greek for short-dated equity options.\n\n## Sign table\n\n| Position | Delta | Gamma | Theta | Vega | Rho |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Long call | + | + | − | + | + |\n| Long put | − | + | − (usually) | + | − |\n| Short call | − | − | + | − | − |\n| Short put | + | − | + (usually) | − | + |\n\nGamma and vega always share a sign (long options: both +); theta generally takes the opposite sign to gamma.\n\n## Delta–gamma-neutral portfolios\n\nShares have gamma zero, so gamma can only be fixed with **other options**. Procedure (order matters):\n\n1. Add $$w = -\\Gamma_{port}/\\Gamma_{option}$$ units of a traded option to zero the gamma.\n2. The new option position changes delta — re-neutralize delta with shares (delta of shares is 1, gamma 0, so step 2 doesn't undo step 1).\n\nThe same two-step logic extends to vega neutrality with a second traded option (two instruments, two equations).\n\n### Worked example: delta–gamma neutralization\n\nA book has delta −450 and gamma −6,000. A traded call has delta 0.55 and gamma 1.8.\n\nStep 1 — gamma: buy $$w = 6{,}000 / 1.8 = 3{,}333$$ calls. Gamma: −6,000 + 3,333 × 1.8 ≈ 0.\n\nStep 2 — delta: new delta = −450 + 3,333 × 0.55 = −450 + 1,833 = +1,383. **Sell 1,383 shares.** Final book: delta ≈ 0, gamma ≈ 0; small moves and the *change* in delta are both hedged until the option's own Greeks drift.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Two traps live in this calculation. First, hedge **gamma before delta** — adding options to fix gamma wrecks any delta hedge done first, while shares never disturb gamma. Second, watch position signs: a *written* (short) call contributes −delta and −gamma to the book. Exam answers that \"buy\" where you should sell, or that delta-hedge first and stop, are always on the menu.\n\n## The theta–gamma trade-off\n\nSubstituting a delta-hedged portfolio into the BSM partial differential equation gives:\n\n$$\\Theta + rS\\delta + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$\n\nwhere Π is the portfolio value. For a **delta-neutral** portfolio, $$\\Theta + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$: large positive gamma forces large negative theta and vice versa. You cannot own convexity without paying time decay — theta is the rent on gamma. This is why theta can serve as a rough proxy for gamma on a delta-neutral book.\n\n### Quick reference: where each Greek peaks\n\n- **Delta:** call delta ≈ 0.5 ATM; → 1 deep ITM, → 0 deep OTM (puts: −0.5, −1, 0).\n- **Gamma:** ATM, short maturity — explosive just before expiry at the strike.\n- **Vega:** ATM, *long* maturity — the opposite maturity pattern from gamma.\n- **Theta:** most negative ATM near expiry (mirrors gamma, opposite sign).\n- **Rho:** larger for ITM and long-dated options (more strike value at risk to discounting).\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> \"Which Greek is identical for a European call and put with the same strike and expiry?\" — gamma and vega (differentiate put–call parity twice or once with respect to σ; the S₀ and K terms drop out). Delta, theta, and rho all differ between the pair.\n\n## Portfolio insurance\n\nSynthetic protective put: rather than buying puts, replicate one by **selling stock (or index futures) as the market falls and buying as it rises**, keeping the equity exposure equal to the put-adjusted delta. Cheap in calm markets, but it demands liquidity exactly when markets gap: in October 1987, programmatic insurance selling amplified the crash. Key limitation: replication assumes continuous trading and stable volatility — the conditions that fail in a crash.","flashcards":"**Q1.** Define delta for BSM calls and puts (no dividends).\n> **A:** $$\\delta_{call} = N(d_1) \\in (0,1)$$; $$\\delta_{put} = N(d_1) - 1 \\in (-1,0)$$. ATM call delta is near 0.5; portfolio delta = position-weighted sum, with one share = delta 1.\n\n**Q2.** What is dynamic delta hedging?\n> **A:** A short call is hedged by holding $$N(d_1)$$ shares per option; because delta changes with S and t, the hedge must be rebalanced. The rebalancing cost (buy high, sell low for a short-gamma hedger) is what the premium pays for.\n\n**Q3.** Delta-hedge 1,000 written calls with delta 0.62.\n> **A:** Position delta = −1,000 × 0.62 = −620 → buy 620 shares to reach zero delta.\n\n**Q4.** Deltas of linear instruments.\n> **A:** Share = +1; forward on a non-dividend stock = exactly 1; futures contract = $$e^{rT}$$ (daily settlement) — so hedging with futures needs slightly fewer contracts.\n\n**Q5.** Define theta and its usual sign.\n> **A:** $$\\Theta = \\partial f/\\partial t$$ (time decay), usually quoted per day. Negative for most long options; most negative for ATM options near expiry. Exception: a deep ITM European put can have positive theta.\n\n**Q6.** Define gamma, its sign, and where it peaks.\n> **A:** $$\\Gamma = \\partial^2 f/\\partial S^2 = \\frac{N'(d_1)}{S_0\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$. Positive for long calls and puts; largest for ATM options near expiry. It measures how fast a delta hedge goes stale.\n\n**Q7.** Delta-gamma approximation for a position's value change.\n> **A:** $$\\Delta P \\approx \\delta\\,\\Delta S + \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^2$$ — the option twin of duration-plus-convexity. A delta-neutral long-gamma book makes ≈ ½Γ(ΔS)² on any large move.\n\n**Q8.** A delta-neutral book has gamma +8,000; the stock jumps $3. Approximate P&L?\n> **A:** $$\\frac{1}{2}(8{,}000)(3)^2 = +\\$36{,}000$$, regardless of direction — the payoff of long gamma.\n\n**Q9.** Vega and rho: signs and where they peak.\n> **A:** Vega = ∂f/∂σ, positive for long calls and puts, highest for ATM and longer maturities. Rho = ∂f/∂r, positive for calls, negative for puts; larger for ITM/long-dated options.\n\n**Q10.** Delta-hedge 500 written puts with delta −0.38.\n> **A:** Position delta = −500 × (−0.38) = +190 → sell 190 shares. (Buying doubles the exposure.)\n\n**Q11.** How do you build a delta-gamma-neutral portfolio, and why does order matter?\n> **A:** Step 1: add $$w = -\\Gamma_{port}/\\Gamma_{option}$$ options to zero gamma. Step 2: re-neutralize delta with shares. Hedge gamma before delta — shares have zero gamma, so step 2 doesn't undo step 1, but fixing delta first gets wrecked by the options added for gamma.\n\n**Q12.** State the theta-gamma trade-off for a delta-neutral portfolio.\n> **A:** From the BSM PDE, $$\\Theta + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$: large positive gamma forces large negative theta. You can't own convexity without paying time decay — theta is the rent on gamma.\n\n**Q13.** Which Greeks are identical for a European call and put with the same strike/expiry?\n> **A:** Gamma and vega (the S₀ and K terms drop out of put-call parity). Delta, theta, and rho all differ.\n\n**Q14.** What is portfolio insurance and its key limitation?\n> **A:** Synthetic protective put: sell stock/index futures as the market falls, buy as it rises. It demands liquidity exactly when markets gap (it amplified the 1987 crash); it assumes continuous trading and stable volatility — the conditions that fail in a crash.\n","questions":"## 🟢 Easy practice — fully worked\n\n**Q1.** A trader writes (sells) 500 put options, each with a delta of −0.38. To delta-hedge this position, the trader should:\nA) Buy 190 shares B) Sell 190 shares C) Buy 310 shares D) Sell 310 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how do you delta-hedge a short put position?\n\nStep 1 — find the position delta. The trader is SHORT 500 puts, and each put has delta −0.38. A short position flips the sign:\n$$\\text{Position delta} = -500 \\times (-0.38) = +190$$\n\nStep 2 — neutralize it. The position has +190 delta, so you need −190 delta from shares. Each share has delta +1, so SELL 190 shares.\n\n- A buys instead of sells — that ADDS +190 more delta, doubling the exposure to +380.\n- C and D use 310, which would come from $$500 \\times 0.62$$ (using the wrong delta, $$1 - 0.38$$).\n\nAnalogy: a short put behaves like being long the stock (you gain if the stock rises). To offset that \"long\" tilt, you sell shares to flatten out.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Position delta = (number of options) × (option delta), with a sign flip for short positions. Then offset with shares of the opposite sign. A short put → positive delta → sell shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Gamma is greatest for options that are:\nA) Deep in the money with long maturity B) At the money close to expiry C) Deep out of the money close to expiry D) At the money with long maturity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: where does gamma peak?\n\nGamma measures how fast delta changes. Delta flips most violently from near 0 to near 1 right across the strike when there is little time left — so gamma is largest for at-the-money options close to expiry.\n\n- A: deep ITM options have delta near 1 that barely moves → low gamma.\n- C: deep OTM options have delta near 0 that barely moves → low gamma.\n- D describes where VEGA peaks (ATM, long maturity) — the opposite maturity pattern from gamma.\n\nAnalogy: an ATM option near expiry is like a coin balanced on its edge at the last second — the tiniest nudge tips delta dramatically. That sensitivity IS high gamma.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Gamma peaks ATM, SHORT maturity (delta snaps across the strike near expiry). Vega peaks ATM, LONG maturity. Don't mix the two maturity patterns up.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"quadrant\",\"title\":\"Where gamma vs vega peak\",\"x\":\"OTM/ITM ← ATM →\",\"y\":\"short ← maturity → long\",\"q\":[\"low Γ\",\"low Γ\",\"GAMMA peaks (ATM, short)\",\"VEGA peaks (ATM, long)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A delta-neutral portfolio has a gamma of +8,000. The stock suddenly jumps \\$3 (in either direction). Using the delta-gamma approximation, the approximate P&L is:\nA) +\\$24,000 B) −\\$36,000 C) +\\$36,000 D) Zero — the portfolio is delta-neutral\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The question asks: estimate the P&L of a delta-neutral, long-gamma book after a \\$3 move.\n\nThe delta-gamma approximation:\n$$\\Delta P \\approx \\delta \\, \\Delta S + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\Gamma (\\Delta S)^2$$\n\nSince the portfolio is delta-neutral, $$\\delta = 0$$, so the first term vanishes:\n$$\\Delta P \\approx \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 8{,}000 \\times (3)^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 8{,}000 \\times 9 = +\\$36{,}000$$\n\nThis is positive for a move in EITHER direction (because $$(\\Delta S)^2$$ is positive either way) — the payoff of being long gamma.\n\n- A (+\\$24,000) is $$\\Gamma \\times \\Delta S = 8{,}000 \\times 3$$ — forgets both the ½ and the square.\n- B (−\\$36,000) gets the magnitude right but the wrong sign; long gamma GAINS on big moves.\n- D ignores the gamma term — but gamma is exactly what produces P&L when delta is zero.\n\nAnalogy: long gamma is like a trampoline — whichever way you fall (up or down), the curvature bounces you to a gain.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a delta-neutral book, P&L ≈ ½Γ(ΔS)². Long gamma → gains on any large move; short gamma → losses. Don't forget the ½ and the SQUARE.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A dealer writes 1,000 calls with delta 0.62 and delta-hedges. The stock then rallies and the calls' delta rises to 0.70. To re-establish delta neutrality, the dealer must:\nA) Sell 80 shares B) Buy 80 shares C) Sell 620 shares D) Buy 700 shares from scratch\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how does the dealer rebalance after the delta rises?\n\nStep 1 — initial hedge. Short 1,000 calls → position delta = $$-1{,}000 \\times 0.62 = -620$$. The dealer buys 620 shares to reach zero.\n\nStep 2 — after the rally, delta rises to 0.70. New option delta = $$-1{,}000 \\times 0.70 = -700$$. Combined with the 620 shares already held: $$-700 + 620 = -80$$.\n\nStep 3 — to flatten the −80, buy 80 more shares (bringing the total to 700).\n\n- A sells, which would worsen the imbalance to −160.\n- C and D ignore the shares already held (620) and re-buy the whole position.\n\nAnalogy: the dealer keeps topping up shares as the stock rises — \"buy high.\" This buy-high, sell-low pattern of a short-gamma hedger is exactly the rebalancing cost the option premium was paid to cover.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Rebalancing only adjusts for the CHANGE in delta. A short-call (short-gamma) hedger buys more as prices rise and sells as they fall — the costly \"buy high, sell low\" signature of negative gamma.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which two Greeks are IDENTICAL for a European call and a European put with the same strike and expiration?\nA) Delta and theta B) Gamma and vega C) Delta and rho D) Theta and rho\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: which Greeks match between a same-strike, same-expiry European call and put?\n\nDifferentiating put-call parity ($$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$) shows that the $$S_0$$ and K terms drop out for the second derivative with respect to S (gamma) and the derivative with respect to σ (vega). So gamma and vega are identical for the call and put.\n\n- A: delta differs ($$N(d_1)$$ vs $$N(d_1) - 1$$) and theta differs.\n- C: delta and rho both differ between call and put.\n- D: theta and rho both differ.\n\nAnalogy: a call and put on the same stock share the same \"curvature\" (gamma) and \"volatility sensitivity\" (vega) because those properties come from the shared underlying's wiggle, not from which direction you're betting.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Gamma and vega are the SAME for a matched European call/put pair. Delta, theta, and rho all DIFFER. This comes straight from differentiating put-call parity.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Greeks for matched call/put\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"SAME\",\"items\":[\"gamma\",\"vega\"]},{\"h\":\"DIFFER\",\"items\":[\"delta (by 1)\",\"theta\",\"rho\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** For a delta-neutral portfolio, which Greek does theta most closely (and inversely) relate to?\nA) Delta B) Gamma C) Vega D) Rho\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: which Greek does theta trade off against for a delta-neutral book?\n\nFor a delta-neutral portfolio, the BSM relationship reduces to:\n$$\\Theta + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$\n\nThis ties theta directly to gamma: large positive gamma forces large negative theta, and vice versa. You cannot own convexity (gamma) without paying time decay (theta) — theta is the \"rent\" on gamma.\n\n- A: delta is zero by assumption, so it doesn't enter this trade-off.\n- C and D (vega, rho) are not the variables linked by this delta-neutral relationship.\n\nAnalogy: gamma is the \"good\" curvature that pays off on big moves; theta is the daily \"rent\" you pay to keep it. They are two sides of the same coin.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a delta-neutral book, $$\\Theta + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$. Big positive gamma ↔ big negative theta. Theta can serve as a rough proxy for gamma on such a book.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Theta-gamma trade-off (delta-neutral)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Long gamma (+Γ)\",\"items\":[\"gains on big moves\",\"pays NEGATIVE theta (rent)\"]},{\"h\":\"Short gamma (−Γ)\",\"items\":[\"loses on big moves\",\"earns POSITIVE theta\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A book has delta −450 and gamma −6,000. A traded call has delta 0.55 and gamma 1.8. To make the book delta- AND gamma-neutral, the trader should FIRST:\nA) Sell shares to fix delta, then add options for gamma B) Buy 3,333 calls to fix gamma, then sell 1,383 shares to fix delta C) Buy 6,000 calls, then buy 450 shares D) Sell 3,333 calls, then buy 1,383 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how do you build a delta-gamma-neutral book, and in what order?\n\nOrder matters: fix gamma FIRST with options (shares have zero gamma), THEN fix delta with shares (shares don't disturb gamma).\n\nStep 1 — gamma. Need to offset −6,000 gamma using a call with gamma 1.8:\n$$w = \\frac{6{,}000}{1.8} = 3{,}333 \\text{ calls (buy)}$$\nCheck: $$-6{,}000 + 3{,}333 \\times 1.8 \\approx 0$$.\n\nStep 2 — delta. Buying 3,333 calls adds delta: $$3{,}333 \\times 0.55 = 1{,}833$$. New delta = $$-450 + 1{,}833 = +1{,}383$$. To neutralize, SELL 1,383 shares (each share has delta 1).\n\n- A reverses the order — fixing delta with shares first, then adding options, would wreck the delta hedge.\n- C uses 6,000 directly as a contract count (ignores the option's gamma) and the wrong delta step.\n- D sells calls (wrong sign — that worsens the negative gamma).\n\nAnalogy: build the foundation (gamma) before the walls (delta). If you put up walls first (delta hedge with shares), adding the foundation later (options) cracks them.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Gamma BEFORE delta. Use options for gamma (shares have none), then shares for delta (shares don't change gamma). Watch the signs of any short positions in the book.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Delta-gamma-neutral hedge order\",\"steps\":[\"Fix GAMMA first with options (shares have no gamma)\",\"Adding options shifts delta\",\"Fix DELTA with shares (shares don't disturb gamma)\",\"Book now delta- and gamma-neutral\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A trader holds a long call on a non-dividend stock. If the risk-free interest rate rises while everything else stays the same, the call's value will most likely:\nA) Fall, because higher rates push the stock price down B) Rise, because the present value of the strike paid at expiry falls C) Stay the same — interest rates do not affect option values D) Fall, because rho is negative for call options\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks how a long call reacts to a higher risk-free rate $$r$$ — that sensitivity is called **rho**.\n\nWhen you own a call, you pay the strike $$K$$ only later, at expiry. Its cost today is the present value $$Ke^{-rT}$$. A higher $$r$$ **discounts** that future payment more heavily, so $$Ke^{-rT}$$ shrinks — paying later is cheaper in today's money. A cheaper effective cost makes the call **more** valuable. So a long call has **positive rho** and its value rises — answer **B**.\n\n- A is wrong: BSM treats the stock price as given; rho works through the present value of the strike, not by moving the stock.\n- C is wrong: rates do matter (that is exactly what rho measures); the effect is small but real.\n- D is wrong: it states the sign backwards — rho is **positive** for long calls (it is negative for long puts).\n\n📌 Exam tip: Long call → positive rho; long put → negative rho. The reason is always the discounted strike $$Ke^{-rT}$$: higher $$r$$ helps the call (pay less, later) and hurts the put (receive less, later).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Rho: effect of higher r\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Long call (rho > 0)\",\"items\":[\"pay strike later\",\"Ke^(-rT) shrinks → value UP\"]},{\"h\":\"Long put (rho < 0)\",\"items\":[\"receive strike later\",\"Ke^(-rT) shrinks → value DOWN\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Theta (time decay) is MOST negative for an option that is:\nA) Deep in the money with long maturity B) At the money close to expiry C) Deep out of the money with long maturity D) At the money with long maturity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: where is theta most negative (fastest time decay)?\n\nTheta is most negative for at-the-money options close to expiry. Their value is almost entirely time value, which evaporates rapidly in the final days — mirroring where gamma peaks (ATM, near expiry), but with the opposite sign.\n\n- A and C: deep ITM/OTM options have little time value to lose, so theta is small.\n- D: ATM with LONG maturity decays slowly — there is plenty of time left, so daily decay is gentle (this is where vega peaks instead).\n\nAnalogy: an ATM option near expiry is like an ice cube on a hot day in its final minutes — it melts (loses value) fastest right at the end.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Theta most negative = ATM, near expiry (it mirrors gamma's peak, opposite sign). Time decay accelerates as expiry approaches for at-the-money options.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Theta most negative for an option that is\",\"options\":[\"Deep ITM, long maturity\",\"ATM, close to expiry\",\"Deep OTM, long maturity\",\"ATM, long maturity\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"ATM options near expiry are almost all time value, which evaporates fastest in the final days — mirroring where gamma peaks.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Portfolio insurance via synthetic put replication involves:\nA) Buying more stock as the market falls and selling as it rises B) Selling stock (or index futures) as the market falls and buying as it rises C) Holding a fixed stock position regardless of market moves D) Buying actual put options on every stock in the portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The question asks: how does synthetic put replication (portfolio insurance) work?\n\nA protective put has a delta that becomes more negative as the market falls. To replicate it WITHOUT buying actual puts, you reduce equity exposure as the market drops (sell stock or index futures) and increase it as the market rises (buy back) — keeping your net exposure equal to the put-adjusted delta.\n\n- A is backward — buying as it falls would amplify losses, the opposite of insurance.\n- C describes no hedging at all.\n- D describes buying REAL puts, which is protection but not SYNTHETIC replication (the whole point of synthetic insurance is to avoid buying puts directly).\n\nAnalogy: it's automatic \"selling into a falling market\" — like a thermostat that cuts heat as the room warms. The danger: everyone's programs sell at once when markets gap down (as in October 1987), amplifying the crash.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Synthetic put = sell as the market falls, buy as it rises. Its key flaw is the assumption of continuous trading and stable volatility — exactly what fails in a crash, when liquidity disappears.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Synthetic put (portfolio insurance) trading rule\",\"options\":[\"Buy as market falls, sell as it rises\",\"Sell stock/futures as market falls, buy as it rises\",\"Hold a fixed position\",\"Buy real puts on every stock\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Replicating a protective put's increasingly negative delta means selling into declines and buying into rallies; it fails when markets gap (1987).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 📝 Concept check\n\n**Q11.** A trader writes 500 puts with delta −0.38. To delta-hedge, the trader should:\nA) Buy 190 shares B) Sell 190 shares C) Buy 310 shares D) Sell 310 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Short 500 puts → position delta = −500 × (−0.38) = +190. Offset by **selling 190 shares**. (Buying shares — choice A — doubles the exposure.)\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Gamma is greatest for options that are:\nA) Deep in the money with long maturity B) At the money close to expiry C) Deep out of the money close to expiry D) At the money with long maturity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Delta flips fastest across the strike when little time remains, so gamma peaks ATM near expiry. Vega, by contrast, peaks ATM at *long* maturity (choice D is the vega answer).\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A delta-neutral portfolio has gamma +8,000. The stock jumps \\$3 (either direction). The approximate P&L is:\nA) +\\$24,000 B) −\\$36,000 C) +\\$36,000 D) Zero — the portfolio is delta-neutral\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** ΔP ≈ δΔS + ½Γ(ΔS)² = 0 + ½ × 8,000 × 3² = +\\$36,000, regardless of direction — the payoff of long gamma. Choice A is Γ × ΔS (no square, no ½); choice D ignores the gamma term entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** For a delta-neutral portfolio with large positive theta, gamma is most likely:\nA) Large and positive B) Near zero C) Large and negative D) Unrelated to theta\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** From Θ + ½σ²S²Γ = rΠ, big positive theta pairs with big negative gamma (a short-options book that earns decay but loses on large moves).\n</details>\n\n## 🟢 Easy\n\n**Q15.** A trader has sold 1,000 call option contracts (100 options each) with a delta of 0.55. To make the position delta-neutral, the trader should:\nA) Buy 45,000 shares B) Buy 55,000 shares C) Sell 55,000 shares D) Buy 100,000 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Position delta $$= -1{,}000 \\times 100 \\times 0.55 = -55{,}000$$. Buy 55,000 shares (each share has delta +1) to bring the net delta to zero. Selling shares (C) doubles the exposure; A confuses delta with $$1 - \\Delta$$.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Delta-neutral hedge\",\"steps\":[\"Position delta = −1,000×100×0.55 = −55,000\",\"Need to add +55,000 delta\",\"Each share = +1 delta\",\"Buy 55,000 shares\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A call option has delta 0.50 and gamma 0.04. Using a delta-gamma approximation, if the underlying rises by $2, the option price change is closest to:\nA) $0.92 B) $1.00 C) $1.08 D) $1.16 --\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\Delta C \\approx \\Delta\\,\\delta S + \\frac{1}{2}\\gamma\\,(\\delta S)^2 = 0.50 \\times 2 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.04 \\times 2^2 = 1.00 + 0.08 = \\$1.08$$\n\nGamma adds to the delta estimate for moves in *either* direction (long-option convexity); B is the delta-only answer, D forgets the one-half.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Hard\n\n**Q17.** A delta-neutral portfolio has gamma of −5,000. A traded option has gamma 0.5 and delta 0.6. To make the portfolio both gamma-neutral and delta-neutral, the trader should:\nA) Buy 10,000 options and sell 6,000 shares B) Buy 10,000 options and buy 6,000 shares C) Sell 10,000 options and sell 6,000 shares D) Buy 2,500 options and sell 1,500 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Gamma first: need $$+5{,}000$$ of gamma, so buy $$5{,}000 / 0.5 = 10{,}000$$ options. Those options add delta of $$10{,}000 \\times 0.6 = +6{,}000$$, so sell 6,000 shares to restore delta neutrality (shares have zero gamma, so the gamma fix survives). Order matters: hedge gamma with options, then re-trim delta with stock.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Gamma- then delta-neutral\",\"steps\":[\"Fix gamma first (only options have gamma)\",\"Buy 5,000/0.5 = 10,000 options\",\"These add 10,000×0.6 = +6,000 delta\",\"Sell 6,000 shares (zero gamma) to re-trim delta\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## 🎯 Exam-level — fully worked\n\n**Q18.** A dealer writes 2,000 call options (each on one share) with a delta of 0.58. To establish a delta-neutral hedge today, the dealer should:\nA) Buy 1,160 shares. B) Sell 1,160 shares. C) Buy 2,000 shares. D) Sell 840 shares.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: hedge a short call position to delta-neutral by trading shares.\n\n1. The dealer is SHORT 2,000 calls. A long call has delta +0.58, so a SHORT call has delta −0.58 per option to the writer.\n2. Position delta:\n   $$-2{,}000 \\times 0.58 = -1{,}160$$\n3. To neutralize a delta of −1,160, add +1,160 of delta. Each share has delta +1, so BUY 1,160 shares.\n\n- B (sell) moves the wrong way — selling shares makes the delta more negative (−2,320), worsening the exposure.\n- C (buy 2,000) ignores the 0.58 delta and over-hedges.\n- D (sell 840) is both the wrong direction and an unmotivated size.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Position delta = (number of options, signed) × (delta per option). A short call has NEGATIVE delta to the writer; offset it by BUYING shares (delta +1 each). Get the position sign right first.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A dealer is short 1,000 calls with delta 0.60 and has already bought 600 shares to be delta-neutral. The stock rallies and the calls' delta rises to 0.72. To restore delta-neutrality the dealer must:\nA) Buy 120 more shares. B) Sell 120 shares. C) Buy 720 shares. D) Sell 600 shares.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: rebalance a delta hedge after the underlying moves (dynamic hedging).\n\n1. New position delta from the options: short 1,000 calls at delta 0.72 →\n   $$-1{,}000 \\times 0.72 = -720$$\n2. The required total share holding to offset this is 720 (delta +720).\n3. The dealer already holds 600 shares, so the additional shares needed are:\n   $$720 - 600 = 120 \\text{ shares (buy)}$$\n\nThe dealer BUYS 120 more shares, bringing the total to 720.\n\n- B (sell 120) is the wrong direction — after a rally a short-gamma hedger must BUY more shares, not sell.\n- C (buy 720) ignores the 600 shares already held, double-counting the hedge.\n- D (sell 600) liquidates the hedge entirely — nonsensical.\n\n📌 Exam tip: As delta rises after a rally, a short-call (short-gamma) hedger must BUY MORE shares — \"buy high.\" This buy-high/sell-low rebalancing cost is exactly what the option premium compensates the writer for.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A book has a portfolio delta of −500 and a portfolio gamma of −9,000. A traded call option has delta 0.50 and gamma 2.0. To make the book both delta- AND gamma-neutral, the dealer should:\nA) Buy 4,500 calls, then sell 1,750 shares. B) Buy 4,500 calls, then buy 1,750 shares. C) Sell 4,500 calls, then buy 1,750 shares. D) Buy 1,000 shares, then buy 4,500 calls.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: construct a delta-gamma-neutral hedge in the correct order (gamma first with options, then delta with shares).\n\n1. STEP 1 — neutralize gamma with the traded option (shares have zero gamma, so gamma can only be fixed with options):\n   $$w = -\\frac{\\Gamma_{port}}{\\Gamma_{option}} = -\\frac{-9{,}000}{2.0} = +4{,}500 \\text{ calls (buy)}$$\n   New gamma: $$-9{,}000 + 4{,}500 \\times 2.0 = 0$$. ✓\n2. STEP 2 — the added 4,500 calls change the delta. New delta:\n   $$-500 + 4{,}500 \\times 0.50 = -500 + 2{,}250 = +1{,}750$$\n3. Neutralize this with shares (delta +1, gamma 0, so this does NOT disturb the gamma hedge): SELL 1,750 shares.\n\n- B (buy 1,750 shares) has the right gamma step but wrong delta direction — a +1,750 delta needs SELLING shares.\n- C (sell 4,500 calls) gets the gamma direction backward — selling calls makes gamma more negative (−18,000).\n- D hedges delta with shares FIRST; then buying the calls re-breaks the delta, so the order is wrong.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Hedge GAMMA first (with options), then DELTA (with shares). Shares have zero gamma, so the delta step never undoes the gamma step — but doing it in the reverse order does undo the delta hedge.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Delta-gamma-neutral hedge order\",\"steps\":[\"Fix GAMMA first with options (shares have none)\",\"Buying options shifts delta\",\"Fix DELTA with shares (no gamma effect)\",\"Book is now delta- and gamma-neutral\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A delta-neutral portfolio has a gamma of +5,000. The underlying stock price suddenly jumps by $4 (in either direction). Using the delta-gamma approximation, the approximate change in portfolio value is closest to:\nA) +$40,000 B) +$20,000 C) −$40,000 D) $0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: estimate the P&L of a delta-neutral, positive-gamma book for a large move using the delta-gamma approximation.\n\n1. Delta-gamma approximation:\n   $$\\Delta P \\approx \\delta \\cdot \\Delta S + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\Gamma (\\Delta S)^2$$\n2. The portfolio is delta-neutral, so δ = 0 and the first term vanishes.\n3. Gamma term:\n   $$\\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 5{,}000 \\times (4)^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times 5{,}000 \\times 16 = 40{,}000$$\n4. Result: +$40,000, and because $$(\\Delta S)^2$$ is positive regardless of the sign of ΔS, the gain is the same for a +$4 or −$4 move — the payoff of long gamma.\n\n- B (+$20,000) forgets the squaring or the factor of 2, e.g., $$\\frac{1}{2} \\times 5{,}000 \\times 4 \\times 2$$ type error.\n- C (−$40,000) gets the sign wrong — long (positive) gamma GAINS on large moves.\n- D ($0) wrongly assumes delta-neutral means no P&L, ignoring the gamma (second-order) term.\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a delta-neutral book, $$\\Delta P \\approx \\tfrac{1}{2}\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^2$$. Positive gamma gains on any large move (the square is always positive); the cost of that convexity is negative theta (time decay).\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which pair of Greeks is identical for a European call and a European put that share the same strike, maturity, and underlying?\nA) Gamma and vega B) Delta and theta C) Delta and rho D) Theta and vega\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: identify which Greeks coincide for a call and put with matched strike/maturity (a put-call parity consequence).\n\n1. Differentiate put-call parity $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$ with respect to S: the call and put deltas differ by exactly 1 (the constant terms differentiate to the share's delta of 1), so DELTA differs.\n2. Differentiate twice with respect to S: the linear terms ($$Ke^{-rT}$$ and $$S_0$$) vanish, so the call and put have IDENTICAL gamma.\n3. Differentiate parity with respect to σ: the $$Ke^{-rT}$$ and $$S_0$$ terms have no σ-dependence, so call and put VEGA are identical.\n4. Theta and rho differ (the $$Ke^{-rT}$$ term depends on t and r, breaking the symmetry).\n\n- B and C include delta, which differs by 1 between call and put.\n- D includes theta, which differs because of the $$Ke^{-rT}$$ time-dependence.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Gamma and vega are identical for a matched call/put — differentiate put-call parity (twice for gamma, once in σ for vega) and the $$S_0$$ and $$Ke^{-rT}$$ terms drop out. Delta, theta, and rho all differ.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Greeks for matched call/put\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"SAME\",\"items\":[\"gamma\",\"vega\"]},{\"h\":\"DIFFER\",\"items\":[\"delta (by 1)\",\"theta\",\"rho\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A delta-neutral portfolio is observed to have a large POSITIVE theta. Using the relationship $$\\Theta + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$, its gamma is most likely:\nA) Large and negative. B) Large and positive. C) Near zero. D) Unrelated to theta.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: infer the sign of gamma from theta for a delta-neutral portfolio using the BSM PDE relationship.\n\n1. For a delta-neutral portfolio (δ = 0) the term $$rS\\delta$$ drops out, leaving:\n   $$\\Theta + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi$$\n2. Rearrange: $$\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 S^2 \\Gamma = r\\Pi - \\Theta$$. A LARGE POSITIVE Θ makes the right side small or negative, forcing Γ to be NEGATIVE.\n3. Economically: large positive theta means the book EARNS time decay (a short-options profile), which always carries large NEGATIVE gamma (it loses on large moves).\n\n- B contradicts the relationship — positive theta pairs with negative gamma, not positive.\n- C ignores the strong inverse coupling the equation imposes.\n- D is wrong — for a delta-neutral book theta and gamma are tightly linked (opposite signs).\n\n📌 Exam tip: For a delta-neutral book, theta and gamma have OPPOSITE signs: you cannot own convexity (positive gamma) without paying time decay (negative theta), and vice versa. Theta is the \"rent\" on gamma.\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Delta-neutral book with large POSITIVE theta → gamma is\",\"options\":[\"Large and negative\",\"Large and positive\",\"Near zero\",\"Unrelated to theta\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"From Θ + ½σ²S²Γ = rΠ, a large positive Θ forces Γ negative — earning time decay means short gamma (loses on big moves).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A portfolio manager implements portfolio insurance via SYNTHETIC put replication on an equity index, rather than buying listed puts. Which statement best describes how the strategy is executed and its key limitation?\nA) Sell index exposure (e.g., futures) as the market falls and buy as it rises; it fails when markets gap and liquidity dries up, as in October 1987. B) Buy index exposure as the market falls and sell as it rises; it fails only when volatility is low. C) Hold a constant index position and rebalance only at expiry; it fails because of transaction costs. D) Short the index outright and never rebalance; it fails because the hedge has the wrong sign.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The ask: describe synthetic-put portfolio insurance and its principal failure mode.\n\n1. A protective put has a delta that becomes more negative as the market falls (the put goes deeper ITM). To replicate it synthetically, the manager reduces equity exposure as prices FALL (sell index/futures) and increases it as prices RISE (buy) — matching the put-adjusted delta dynamically.\n2. The strategy relies on continuous trading at fair prices and stable volatility.\n3. Key limitation: in a crash the market GAPS and liquidity evaporates exactly when the strategy must sell — programmatic insurance selling amplified the October 1987 crash.\n\n- B reverses the trading rule (it describes buying into declines, the opposite of replicating a put).\n- C describes a static, non-dynamic strategy — synthetic insurance requires continuous rebalancing.\n- D describes an outright short, not a dynamic replication of a protective put.\n\n📌 Exam tip: Synthetic protective put = sell as the market falls, buy as it rises (track the put's negative, rising-in-magnitude delta). Its fatal flaw is the assumption of continuous, liquid trading — precisely what fails in a gapping crash (1987).\n\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Synthetic put replication & its flaw\",\"options\":[\"Sell index/futures as market falls, buy as it rises; fails when markets gap (1987)\",\"Buy as market falls, sell as it rises; fails only at low vol\",\"Hold constant position, rebalance at expiry\",\"Short outright, never rebalance\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Replicating the put's increasingly negative delta means selling into declines; it relies on continuous liquid trading, which fails in a gapping crash.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n"},{"num":"901","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Formula Sheet — Book 1 (Foundations)","notes":"# Book 1 Formulas — Foundations of Risk Management\n\nCalculations cluster in Chapters 5–7 (plus the EL formula from Chapter 1). Every formula below is fair game.\n\n## Chapter 1 — Expected loss\n\n$$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD$$\n\n$$PD$$ = probability of default; $$LGD$$ = loss given default $$= 1 - \\text{recovery rate}$$; $$EAD$$ = exposure at default.\n\n**Trap:** EL is a *cost* covered by pricing/provisions; *unexpected* loss is what capital covers.\n\n## Chapter 5 — MPT and CAPM\n\n**Portfolio expected return**\n\n$$E[R_p] = \\sum_i w_i\\,E[R_i]$$\n\n**Covariance and correlation**\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(R_1,R_2) = \\rho_{12}\\,\\sigma_1\\sigma_2 \\qquad \\rho_{12} = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(R_1,R_2)}{\\sigma_1\\sigma_2}$$\n\n**Two-asset portfolio variance**\n\n$$\\sigma_p^2 = w_1^2\\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\\sigma_2^2 + 2w_1w_2\\,\\rho_{12}\\,\\sigma_1\\sigma_2$$\n\n**Trap:** the cross term uses $$2w_1w_2$$ — forgetting the 2 is the most common arithmetic error. Take the square root at the very end for $$\\sigma_p$$.\n\n**Beta**\n\n$$\\beta_i = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(R_i,R_m)}{\\sigma_m^2} = \\rho_{im}\\,\\frac{\\sigma_i}{\\sigma_m}\n\\qquad \\beta_p = \\sum_i w_i\\,\\beta_i$$\n\n**CAPM (= the SML)**\n\n$$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_i\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)$$\n\n**Capital market line (efficient portfolios only)**\n\n$$E[R_p] = R_f + \\frac{E[R_m]-R_f}{\\sigma_m}\\,\\sigma_p$$\n\n**Trap:** SML prices *all* assets using **beta**; CML applies only to *efficient* portfolios using **total risk σ**. Given σ of an individual stock, you cannot use the CML — convert to beta via $$\\rho\\,\\sigma_i/\\sigma_m$$.\n\n## Chapter 6 — APT and multifactor models\n\n**Return-generating (factor) model**\n\n$$R_i = E[R_i] + \\beta_{i1}F_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}F_K + e_i$$\n\n$$F_k$$ = factor **surprise** (actual − expected, mean zero); $$e_i$$ = idiosyncratic return.\n\n**APT pricing**\n\n$$E[R_i] = R_f + \\beta_{i1}\\lambda_1 + \\beta_{i2}\\lambda_2 + \\cdots + \\beta_{iK}\\lambda_K$$\n\n$$\\lambda_k$$ = risk premium on factor $$k$$ (excess return of the pure factor portfolio).\n\n**Trap:** premiums $$\\lambda$$ multiply betas in *expected* return; surprises $$F$$ multiply betas in *realized* return. An announcement that matches expectations has surprise = 0 and moves nothing.\n\n**Fama–French three-factor model**\n\n$$E[R_i] - R_f = \\beta_{i,m}\\left(E[R_m]-R_f\\right) + \\beta_{i,SMB}\\,E[SMB] + \\beta_{i,HML}\\,E[HML]$$\n\n$$SMB$$ = small-minus-big (size premium); $$HML$$ = high-minus-low book-to-market (value premium). Positive HML beta = value tilt; negative = growth.\n\n## Chapter 7 — Risk-adjusted performance\n\n**Sharpe ratio** (total risk)\n\n$$S_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\sigma_p}$$\n\n**Treynor ratio** (systematic risk)\n\n$$T_p = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_f}{\\beta_p}$$\n\n**Trap:** Sharpe divides by **σ**, Treynor by **β**. Entire/undiversified portfolio → Sharpe; diversified component → Treynor.\n\n**Jensen's alpha**\n\n$$\\alpha_p = E[R_p] - \\left[R_f + \\beta_p\\left(E[R_m]-R_f\\right)\\right]$$\n\nA percentage, not a ratio — only compares funds of similar beta.\n\n**Tracking error**\n\n$$TE = \\sigma\\!\\left(R_p - R_b\\right)$$\n\n$$R_b$$ = benchmark return. (Volatility of *active* returns, not the average difference.)\n\n**Information ratio**\n\n$$IR = \\frac{E[R_p] - E[R_b]}{TE}$$\n\n**Sortino ratio**\n\n$$\\text{Sortino} = \\frac{E[R_p] - R_{min}}{\\sigma_{down}}, \\qquad\n\\sigma_{down} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{R_t < R_{min}}\\left(R_t - R_{min}\\right)^2}$$\n\n$$R_{min}$$ = minimum acceptable return (often $$R_f$$ or 0); only below-target deviations enter $$\\sigma_{down}$$.\n\n**RAROC**\n\n$$RAROC = \\frac{\\text{revenues} - \\text{costs} - \\text{expected losses} - \\text{taxes} + \\text{return on economic capital} \\pm \\text{transfer prices}}{\\text{economic capital}}$$\n\nEconomic capital = buffer for **unexpected** loss at the target confidence level; it earns roughly $$R_f$$, added back in the numerator. Accept if RAROC > hurdle rate (the firm's weighted cost of equity capital).\n\n**Adjusted RAROC (ARAROC)**\n\n$$ARAROC = RAROC - \\beta_E\\left(E[R_m] - R_f\\right)$$\n\n**Decision rule:** accept the project if $$ARAROC > R_f$$.\n\n**Trap:** RAROC subtracts **expected** losses in the numerator but holds capital for **unexpected** losses in the denominator — don't double-count. And the ARAROC hurdle is the **risk-free rate**, not the market return.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"902","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Formula Sheet — Book 2 (Quant)","notes":"# Book 2 Formulas — Quantitative Analysis\n\nBook 2 is the most calculation-dense book in Part I. Every formula below maps to a 2026 LO; the trap notes flag where GARP likes to hide distractors.\n\n## Chapter 1 — Fundamentals of Probability\n\n**Addition rule**\n\n$$P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cap B)$$\n\n**Conditional probability**\n\n$$P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}$$\n\n**Independence**\n\n$$P(A \\cap B) = P(A)\\,P(B) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad P(A \\mid B) = P(A)$$\n\n**Trap:** *independent* ≠ *mutually exclusive*. Mutually exclusive events have $$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$, so (with nonzero probabilities) they are strongly **dependent** — one occurring rules the other out.\n\n**Law of total probability** (events $$B_i$$ partition the sample space)\n\n$$P(A) = \\sum_i P(A \\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)$$\n\n**Bayes' rule**\n\n$$P(B_i \\mid A) = \\frac{P(A \\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)}{\\sum_j P(A \\mid B_j)\\,P(B_j)}$$\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> On exam day, build a Bayes question as a table: prior × likelihood for every branch, then divide the branch of interest by the total. It is faster and safer than plugging into the formula.\n\n## Chapter 2 — Random Variables\n\n**Expectation and variance**\n\n$$E[X] = \\sum_x x\\,P(X=x) \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - \\left(E[X]\\right)^2$$\n\n**Linear transformations** ($$a, b$$ constants)\n\n$$E[aX + b] = a\\,E[X] + b \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(aX + b) = a^2\\,\\mathrm{Var}(X)$$\n\n**Trap:** the additive constant $$b$$ shifts the mean but **never** affects variance, and the multiplier enters variance **squared** (so standard deviation scales by $$|a|$$).\n\n**Standardized higher moments**\n\n$$\\text{skew}(X) = \\frac{E\\left[(X-\\mu)^3\\right]}{\\sigma^3} \\qquad \\text{kurt}(X) = \\frac{E\\left[(X-\\mu)^4\\right]}{\\sigma^4}$$\n\nNormal: skew = 0, kurtosis = 3. *Excess* kurtosis = kurtosis − 3; positive excess kurtosis (leptokurtosis) = fat tails.\n\n## Chapter 3 — Common Univariate Random Variables\n\n| Distribution | Parameters | Mean | Variance | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bernoulli | $$p$$ | $$p$$ | $$p(1-p)$$ | Single 0/1 trial (default indicator) |\n| Binomial | $$n, p$$ | $$np$$ | $$np(1-p)$$ | Number of successes in $$n$$ iid trials |\n| Poisson | $$\\lambda$$ | $$\\lambda$$ | $$\\lambda$$ | Counts per interval; mean = variance |\n| Uniform | $$a, b$$ | $$\\frac{a+b}{2}$$ | $$\\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}$$ | Continuous, flat density |\n| Normal | $$\\mu, \\sigma^2$$ | $$\\mu$$ | $$\\sigma^2$$ | Skew 0, kurtosis 3; closed under addition |\n| Lognormal | $$\\mu, \\sigma^2$$ of $$\\ln X$$ | $$e^{\\mu + \\sigma^2/2}$$ | $$\\left(e^{\\sigma^2}-1\\right)e^{2\\mu+\\sigma^2}$$ | $$\\ln X \\sim N$$; always positive, right-skewed |\n| Chi-square | $$k$$ df | $$k$$ | $$2k$$ | Sum of $$k$$ squared standard normals |\n| Student's $$t$$ | $$\\nu$$ df | 0 ($$\\nu>1$$) | $$\\frac{\\nu}{\\nu-2}$$ ($$\\nu>2$$) | Fatter tails than normal; → normal as $$\\nu \\to \\infty$$ |\n| $$F$$ | $$\\nu_1, \\nu_2$$ df | $$\\frac{\\nu_2}{\\nu_2-2}$$ ($$\\nu_2>2$$) | — | Ratio of scaled chi-squares; right-skewed |\n\n**Binomial probability**\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\n**Poisson probability**\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^k}{k!}$$\n\n**Standard normal transform**\n\n$$z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}$$\n\n**Trap:** the lognormal mean is $$e^{\\mu+\\sigma^2/2}$$, **not** $$e^{\\mu}$$ — the $$\\sigma^2/2$$ correction is a favorite distractor. And a Poisson over $$t$$ periods has parameter $$\\lambda t$$.\n\n## Chapter 4 — Multivariate Random Variables\n\n**Covariance and correlation**\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y] \\qquad \\rho_{XY} = \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X \\sigma_Y} \\in [-1, 1]$$\n\n**Covariance algebra** (constants $$a,b,c,d$$)\n\n$$\\mathrm{Cov}(aX+b,\\ cY+d) = ac\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$\n\nAdditive constants drop out; correlation is unchanged by any positive linear rescaling.\n\n**Variance of a linear combination**\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(aX + bY) = a^2\\mathrm{Var}(X) + b^2\\mathrm{Var}(Y) + 2ab\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$\n\n**Trap:** with a *minus* sign, $$\\mathrm{Var}(X - Y) = \\mathrm{Var}(X) + \\mathrm{Var}(Y) - 2\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$$ — variances still **add**; only the covariance term flips sign.\n\n**Independence ⇒ zero covariance**, but zero covariance does **not** imply independence (only rules out *linear* dependence).\n\n**iid sums:** for $$n$$ iid draws, $$E\\left[\\sum X_i\\right] = n\\mu$$ and $$\\mathrm{Var}\\left(\\sum X_i\\right) = n\\sigma^2$$.\n\n## Chapter 5 — Sample Moments\n\n**Sample mean and variance**\n\n$$\\bar{X} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\qquad s^2 = \\frac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\left(X_i - \\bar{X}\\right)^2$$\n\nThe $$n-1$$ divisor makes $$s^2$$ unbiased (degrees-of-freedom correction).\n\n**Standard error of the mean**\n\n$$SE(\\bar{X}) = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\n**Trap:** divide by $$\\sqrt{n}$$, not $$n$$. The SE shrinks with sample size; the sample standard deviation $$s$$ does not systematically shrink.\n\n**Sample skewness and kurtosis**\n\n$$\\hat{S} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum\\left(X_i-\\bar{X}\\right)^3}{\\hat\\sigma^3} \\qquad \\hat{K} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum\\left(X_i-\\bar{X}\\right)^4}{\\hat\\sigma^4}$$\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> Under normality the standard errors of sample skewness and excess kurtosis are approximately $$\\sqrt{6/n}$$ and $$\\sqrt{24/n}$$. Higher moments are estimated far less precisely than the mean — large skew/kurtosis estimates from small samples are unreliable, and tests like Jarque–Bera build on exactly these two statistics.\n\n**Central limit theorem:** for iid data with finite variance, $$\\bar{X}$$ is approximately $$N\\!\\left(\\mu, \\sigma^2/n\\right)$$ for large $$n$$, regardless of the underlying distribution.\n\n## Chapter 6 — Hypothesis Testing\n\n**Test statistic for a mean** (σ known → z; σ estimated → t with $$n-1$$ df)\n\n$$z = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\qquad t_{n-1} = \\frac{\\bar{X} - \\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\n**Confidence interval for the mean**\n\n$$\\bar{X} \\pm \\text{(critical value)} \\times \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\n**Key two-tailed normal critical values:** 90% → 1.645, 95% → 1.96, 99% → 2.576. **One-tailed:** 95% → 1.645, 99% → 2.326.\n\n**Trap:** read the tail structure before grabbing a critical value. A 95% *two-tailed* test uses 1.96; a 95% *one-tailed* test uses 1.645. Mixing these up is the single most common Chapter 6 error.\n\n**Chi-square test of a variance** ($$n-1$$ df)\n\n$$\\chi^2 = \\frac{(n-1)s^2}{\\sigma_0^2}$$\n\n**F-test for equality of two variances** (put the larger $$s^2$$ on top)\n\n$$F = \\frac{s_1^2}{s_2^2}, \\qquad \\text{df} = (n_1 - 1,\\ n_2 - 1)$$\n\n**Errors and power**\n\n- Type I error: rejecting a true $$H_0$$; $$P(\\text{Type I}) = \\alpha$$ = the significance level.\n- Type II error: failing to reject a false $$H_0$$; probability $$\\beta$$.\n- Power $$= 1 - \\beta$$ = probability of correctly rejecting a false $$H_0$$.\n\n> 📌 **Important**  \n> For a fixed sample size, lowering $$\\alpha$$ raises $$\\beta$$ (lowers power) — you cannot reduce both error probabilities simultaneously except by increasing $$n$$ (or test efficiency, or effect size).\n\n**p-value** = smallest significance level at which $$H_0$$ would be rejected. Reject when p-value < $$\\alpha$$.\n\n## Chapter 7 — Linear Regression\n\n**Model:** $$Y_i = \\alpha + \\beta X_i + \\epsilon_i$$\n\n**OLS slope and intercept**\n\n$$\\hat\\beta = \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\text{Var}(X)} = \\rho_{XY}\\,\\frac{s_Y}{s_X} \\qquad \\hat\\alpha = \\bar{Y} - \\hat\\beta\\,\\bar{X}$$\n\n**Trap:** the denominator is the variance of the **explanatory** variable $$X$$. Swapping $$X$$ and $$Y$$ inverts the slope.\n\n**Coefficient of determination**\n\n$$R^2 = \\frac{ESS}{TSS} = 1 - \\frac{RSS}{TSS}$$\n\nIn a single-regressor model, $$R^2 = \\rho_{XY}^2$$.\n\n**t-statistic for a coefficient** (df $$= n - k - 1$$; $$k$$ = number of slopes)\n\n$$t = \\frac{\\hat\\beta - \\beta_0}{SE(\\hat\\beta)}$$\n\n$$\\beta_0$$ is the hypothesized value — usually 0 (significance test), but hedging questions often test $$\\beta_0 = 1$$. Use the value in the question, not reflexively zero.\n\n## Chapter 8 — Regression with Multiple Explanatory Variables\n\n**Adjusted R²**\n\n$$\\bar{R}^2 = 1 - \\left(1 - R^2\\right)\\frac{n-1}{n-k-1}$$\n\nPenalizes added regressors; can fall (and even go negative) when a useless variable is added, whereas plain $$R^2$$ never decreases.\n\n**F-statistic for joint significance** (all $$k$$ slopes = 0)\n\n$$F = \\frac{R^2 / k}{\\left(1 - R^2\\right)/(n - k - 1)}$$\n\n**Trap:** individually insignificant t-stats with a significant joint F-stat is the classic signature of **multicollinearity**.\n\n**Dummy variables:** a categorical variable with $$m$$ categories needs $$m-1$$ dummies; including all $$m$$ plus an intercept causes perfect collinearity (the dummy variable trap).\n\n## Chapter 9 — Regression Diagnostics\n\n**Omitted variable bias** — occurs only when the omitted variable both (1) helps determine $$Y$$ and (2) is correlated with an included regressor. The included coefficient then absorbs the omitted effect:\n\n$$\\text{bias in } \\hat\\beta_1 \\approx \\beta_{\\text{omit}} \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{Cov}(X_1, X_{\\text{omit}})}{\\mathrm{Var}(X_1)}$$\n\n**Heteroskedasticity** (non-constant error variance): OLS coefficient estimates stay unbiased and consistent, but the usual standard errors are wrong → t-stats and p-values unreliable. Fix the SEs with **White** (heteroskedasticity-robust) standard errors.\n\n**Autocorrelated errors:** again coefficients are consistent (if regressors are exogenous) but SEs are wrong → use **Newey–West** standard errors, which are robust to *both* heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Neither White nor Newey–West changes the coefficient estimates — they only repair the standard errors. An answer choice claiming robust SEs \"remove the bias in $$\\hat\\beta$$\" is a distractor.\n\n**Multicollinearity:** inflated coefficient SEs, unstable signs; detect via high $$R^2$$ with low t-stats or high variance inflation factors.\n\n## Chapter 10 — Stationary Time Series\n\n**Covariance stationarity:** constant mean, constant variance, and autocovariances that depend only on the lag $$h$$, not on time.\n\n**AR(1)**\n\n$$Y_t = \\delta + \\phi\\,Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t, \\qquad |\\phi| < 1 \\text{ for stationarity}$$\n\n**Long-run (mean-reversion) level**\n\n$$E[Y] = \\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}$$\n\n**Variance and autocorrelations**\n\n$$\\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\frac{\\sigma_\\epsilon^2}{1-\\phi^2} \\qquad \\rho_h = \\phi^h$$\n\nThe ACF of an AR(1) decays geometrically and never cuts off exactly.\n\n**h-step forecast** (reverts toward the long-run mean at rate $$\\phi$$)\n\n$$E_t[Y_{t+h}] = \\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi} + \\phi^h\\left(Y_t - \\frac{\\delta}{1-\\phi}\\right)$$\n\n**MA(1)**\n\n$$Y_t = \\mu + \\epsilon_t + \\theta\\,\\epsilon_{t-1}$$\n\n$$E[Y_t] = \\mu \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \\left(1+\\theta^2\\right)\\sigma_\\epsilon^2 \\qquad \\rho_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{1+\\theta^2}, \\quad \\rho_h = 0 \\ (h \\ge 2)$$\n\n**Trap:** $$\\rho_1 \\ne \\theta$$. The MA(1) ACF **cuts off** after lag 1 (AR decays; MA truncates) — the standard identification clue. Also $$|\\rho_1| \\le 0.5$$ for any MA(1).\n\n**ARMA(1,1)** combines both: geometric ACF decay after lag 1; always stationary if the AR root satisfies $$|\\phi|<1$$.\n\n## Chapter 11 — Nonstationary Time Series\n\n**Random walk** (unit root: $$\\phi = 1$$)\n\n$$Y_t = Y_{t-1} + \\epsilon_t \\qquad \\mathrm{Var}(Y_{t+h} \\mid Y_t) = h\\,\\sigma_\\epsilon^2$$\n\nVariance grows linearly in the horizon, so the standard deviation scales with $$\\sqrt{h}$$; shocks are permanent and there is no mean reversion. A random walk *with drift* adds $$\\delta$$ each period: $$E_t[Y_{t+h}] = Y_t + \\delta h$$.\n\n**Augmented Dickey–Fuller (ADF) test:** regress $$\\Delta Y_t$$ on $$Y_{t-1}$$ (plus lags/deterministics) and test $$H_0\\!: \\gamma = 0$$ (**unit root / nonstationary**) against $$\\gamma < 0$$ (stationary).\n\n> 📌 **Important**  \n> ADF logic is inverted relative to intuition: **rejecting** the null is the *good* outcome — evidence the series is stationary. Failing to reject means you cannot rule out a unit root; first-difference the series before modeling.\n\n**Other nonstationarities:** deterministic time trends (detrend or include $$t$$ as a regressor) and seasonality (seasonal dummies or seasonal differencing).\n\n## Chapter 12 — Measuring Returns, Volatility, and Correlation\n\n**Simple vs log returns**\n\n$$R_t = \\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}} - 1 \\qquad r_t = \\ln\\!\\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}} = \\ln(1 + R_t)$$\n\nLog returns add across time ($$r_{0,T} = \\sum r_t$$); simple returns add across assets in a portfolio. Log return ≤ simple return always, and the gap grows with magnitude.\n\n**Annualization** (independent returns)\n\n$$\\mu_{\\text{annual}} = 252\\,\\mu_{\\text{daily}} \\qquad \\sigma_{\\text{annual}} = \\sigma_{\\text{daily}}\\sqrt{252}$$\n\n**Trap:** volatility scales with the **square root** of time; variance scales linearly.\n\n**EWMA volatility update** (RiskMetrics $$\\lambda = 0.94$$)\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\lambda\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\,r_{t-1}^2$$\n\nWeights decay geometrically; no mean reversion — the latest estimate is the forecast for all horizons.\n\n**GARCH(1,1)**\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\omega + \\alpha\\,r_{t-1}^2 + \\beta\\,\\sigma_{t-1}^2$$\n\n**Long-run (unconditional) variance**\n\n$$\\sigma_{LR}^2 = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta}, \\qquad \\alpha + \\beta < 1$$\n\nEWMA is the special case $$\\omega = 0$$, $$\\alpha + \\beta = 1$$ (no mean reversion). The persistence $$\\alpha + \\beta$$ governs how fast forecasts revert to $$\\sigma_{LR}^2$$.\n\n> 💡 **Exam tip**  \n> Work every EWMA/GARCH update in **variances**, not volatilities, and take the square root only at the end. Plugging in σ where σ² belongs is the most common error.\n\n**Non-normality of returns:** fat tails and negative skew are stylized facts; rank-based measures (Spearman, Kendall) are robust to outliers and nonlinear monotone dependence, unlike Pearson correlation.\n\n## Chapter 13 — Simulation and Bootstrapping\n\n**Monte Carlo standard error**\n\n$$SE = \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{N}}$$\n\n$$s$$ = standard deviation of the simulated values, $$N$$ = number of replications. To halve the SE you need $$4\\times$$ the replications; one extra digit of accuracy costs $$100\\times$$.\n\n**Variance reduction**\n\n- **Antithetic variates:** pair each draw $$\\epsilon$$ with $$-\\epsilon$$; the negative correlation between paired estimates cuts variance.\n- **Control variates:** simulate the error of a related problem with a known analytic answer and adjust the estimate by it.\n\n**Bootstrapping** resamples *with replacement* from observed data instead of assuming a parametric distribution — it preserves the empirical distribution (including fat tails) but cannot generate scenarios outside the historical record and struggles with serially dependent data unless block methods are used.\n\n## Chapter 14 — Machine Learning Methods\n\n**MSE / bias–variance decomposition**\n\n$$E\\left[(\\hat{Y} - Y)^2\\right] = \\text{Bias}^2 + \\text{Variance} + \\text{Irreducible error}$$\n\nMore complex models: lower bias, higher variance. Overfitting = low training error, high out-of-sample error. Combat with validation: split data into training / validation / test sets, or use K-fold cross-validation.\n\n**Ridge regression** (L2 penalty)\n\n$$\\min_\\beta\\ \\sum_{i}\\left(Y_i - X_i\\beta\\right)^2 + \\lambda \\sum_{j} \\beta_j^2$$\n\n**LASSO** (L1 penalty)\n\n$$\\min_\\beta\\ \\sum_{i}\\left(Y_i - X_i\\beta\\right)^2 + \\lambda \\sum_{j} \\left|\\beta_j\\right|$$\n\n**Trap:** ridge **shrinks** coefficients toward zero but keeps them all; LASSO can set coefficients **exactly to zero**, performing variable selection. Larger $$\\lambda$$ → more shrinkage → more bias, less variance. Standardize regressors before penalizing.\n\n## Chapter 15 — Machine Learning and Prediction\n\n**Confusion matrix** (positive = the event being predicted, e.g., default)\n\n| | Predicted + | Predicted − |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Actual +** | TP | FN |\n| **Actual −** | FP | TN |\n\n$$\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN} \\qquad \\text{Precision} = \\frac{TP}{TP+FP} \\qquad \\text{Recall} = \\frac{TP}{TP+FN}$$\n\n$$F_1 = \\frac{2 \\times \\text{Precision} \\times \\text{Recall}}{\\text{Precision} + \\text{Recall}}$$\n\n**Trap:** precision conditions on the **prediction** (of flagged loans, how many defaulted?); recall conditions on the **actual** outcome (of defaults, how many were caught?). With imbalanced classes, accuracy is misleading — a model predicting \"no default\" for everyone scores high accuracy and zero recall.\n\n> 📝 **Note**  \n> The ROC curve plots the true positive rate (recall) against the false positive rate across thresholds; AUC = 0.5 is a coin flip, 1.0 is perfect discrimination.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"903","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Formula Sheet — Book 3 (Markets)","notes":"# Book 3 Formulas\n\nEvery testable formula in Financial Markets and Products, with definitions and the classic traps.\n\n## Ch 1 — Banks\n\n$$\\text{NIM} = \\frac{\\text{Interest income} - \\text{Interest expense}}{\\text{Interest-earning assets}} \\qquad \\text{ROE} = \\text{ROA} \\times \\frac{\\text{Assets}}{\\text{Equity}}$$\n\nROA = net income / assets; assets/equity is the leverage multiplier — it scales both ROE and risk. Loan losses hit earnings through the loss provision, not capital directly.\n\n## Ch 2 — Insurance Companies and Pension Plans\n\nAll ratios are percentages of premiums earned.\n\n$$\\text{Loss ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Incurred losses (claims)}}{\\text{Premiums}} \\qquad \\text{Expense ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Underwriting/selling expenses}}{\\text{Premiums}}$$\n\n$$\\text{Combined ratio} = \\text{Loss ratio} + \\text{Expense ratio}$$\n\n$$\\text{Combined after dividends} = \\text{Combined ratio} + \\text{Policyholder dividend ratio}$$\n\n$$\\text{Operating ratio} = \\text{Combined after dividends} - \\text{Investment income ratio}$$\n\nAn operating ratio below 100% means overall profit even when the combined ratio exceeds 100%.\n\nBreakeven (actuarially fair) premium — set PV of expected premiums equal to PV of expected payouts using mortality-table probabilities:\n\n$$\\sum_t P \\cdot p(\\text{alive at } t)\\,e^{-rt} = \\sum_t \\text{Payout} \\cdot p(\\text{death in year } t)\\,e^{-r t_d}$$\n\nwhere $$P$$ = annual premium (paid only by survivors), $$r$$ = discount rate, $$t_d$$ = payout timing.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Premiums are paid at the **start** of each year by survivors; death benefits are conventionally paid **mid-year** (or year-end if stated). Mixing up the timing is the classic error.\n\n## Ch 3 — Fund Management\n\n$$\\text{NAV per share} = \\frac{\\text{Assets} - \\text{Liabilities}}{\\text{Shares outstanding}}$$\n\nOpen-end funds transact at NAV (set daily at 4 p.m.); closed-end funds trade at market prices, usually a discount to NAV; ETFs track NAV intraday via creation/redemption arbitrage.\n\nHedge fund \"2 and 20\":\n\n$$\\text{Fee} = 2\\% \\times \\text{AUM} + 20\\% \\times \\max(\\text{Return} - \\text{Hurdle},\\, 0) \\times \\text{AUM}$$\n\nIncentive fee applies only above the hurdle rate and only when NAV exceeds the high-water mark (prior peak). *Trap:* the 2% is charged regardless of performance; check whether the 20% is computed on return net of the management fee.\n\n## Ch 4 — Introduction to Derivatives\n\nForward payoffs: long $$S_T - K$$, short $$K - S_T$$. Option payoffs:\n\n$$\\text{Long call: } \\max(S_T - K,\\, 0) \\qquad \\text{Long put: } \\max(K - S_T,\\, 0)$$\n\nShorts are the negatives. $$S_T$$ = price at maturity, $$K$$ = delivery/strike price.\n\n$$\\text{Profit} = \\text{Payoff} - \\text{FV(premium)}$$\n\n*Trap:* the exam usually ignores future-valuing the premium — profit = payoff − premium paid (or + premium received) unless rates are emphasized.\n\n## Ch 5 — Exchanges and OTC Markets\n\nBilateral netting: exposure to a counterparty with netting is $$\\max\\left(\\sum_i V_i,\\, 0\\right)$$ versus $$\\sum_i \\max(V_i,\\, 0)$$ without, where $$V_i$$ = value of contract $$i$$. The benefit grows with the number of offsetting contracts.\n\nMargin (collateral) types:\n\n- **Variation margin** — settles current exposure (daily mark-to-market changes).\n- **Initial margin** — buffer against potential future exposure over the close-out period.\n- **Haircut** — discount applied to non-cash collateral value.\n\n## Ch 6 — Central Clearing\n\nNovation: the CCP becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer. Multilateral netting ratio idea: netted/gross exposure falls as more members and offsetting trades clear through one CCP; fragmenting products across CCPs erodes the benefit.\n\nDefault waterfall (order of loss absorption):\n\n1. Defaulting member's initial margin\n2. Defaulting member's default fund contribution\n3. CCP equity (\"skin in the game\")\n4. Surviving members' default fund contributions\n5. Unfunded assessments on surviving members (recovery tools)\n\n*Trap:* surviving members' initial margin is not mutualized — the default fund, not IM, absorbs others' losses.\n\n## Ch 7 — Futures Markets\n\nPositions are marked to market daily.\n\n$$\\text{Balance} < \\text{Maintenance margin} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{margin call: top up to Initial margin}$$\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> The variation margin call restores the balance to the **initial** margin, not the maintenance margin. Call size = initial margin − current balance.\n\nDaily gain/loss = price change × contract size × number of contracts.\n\nOpen interest = total longs outstanding (= total shorts). New long vs. new short: OI +1; one new, one closing: OI unchanged; both closing: OI −1. Volume counts all trades; OI counts only open positions.\n\n## Ch 8 — Using Futures for Hedging\n\n$$b = S - F$$\n\n$$S$$ = spot price of asset hedged, $$F$$ = futures price used. Strengthening basis helps a short hedger; weakening helps a long hedger. Effective price for a short hedger closing at time 2:\n\n$$\\text{Effective price} = F_1 + b_2 = S_2 + (F_1 - F_2)$$\n\nMinimum-variance hedge ratio and contract count:\n\n$$h^* = \\rho\\,\\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F} \\qquad N^* = \\frac{h^* Q_A}{Q_F}$$\n\n$$\\rho$$ = correlation of spot vs. futures price changes; $$\\sigma_S, \\sigma_F$$ = their standard deviations; $$Q_A$$ = units hedged; $$Q_F$$ = units per contract. Tailing the hedge (adjusts for daily settlement):\n\n$$N^* = \\frac{h^* V_A}{V_F}$$\n\n$$V_A$$ = dollar value of position, $$V_F$$ = dollar value of one contract. Hedge effectiveness = $$\\rho^2$$ = proportion of variance eliminated.\n\nStock index futures:\n\n$$\\text{Full hedge: } N = \\beta\\,\\frac{P}{F} \\qquad \\text{Beta change: } N = (\\beta^* - \\beta)\\,\\frac{P}{F}$$\n\n$$P$$ = portfolio value, $$F$$ = futures price × multiplier, $$\\beta^*$$ = target beta. Positive $$N$$ = buy futures; negative = sell. *Trap:* $$F$$ is the contract's dollar value (e.g., index × $250), not the index level.\n\n## Ch 9 — Foreign Exchange Markets\n\nCovered interest parity, continuous and discrete:\n\n$$F = S\\,e^{(r - r_f)T} \\qquad F = S\\,\\frac{(1 + r)^T}{(1 + r_f)^T}$$\n\n$$S$$ = spot quoted as **domestic per unit of foreign**, $$r$$ = domestic rate, $$r_f$$ = foreign rate, $$T$$ = years.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Check the quote direction first. With $$S$$ in domestic-per-foreign, the domestic rate goes on top; an inverted quote flips $$r$$ and $$r_f$$. The lower-rate currency trades at a forward premium.\n\nCross rates — chain so currencies cancel:\n\n$$\\frac{\\text{JPY}}{\\text{EUR}} = \\frac{\\text{JPY}}{\\text{USD}} \\times \\frac{\\text{USD}}{\\text{EUR}}$$\n\n*Trap:* with bid–ask quotes, the cross bid is the product giving the customer the worst price; multiply bid × bid only when both quotes chain in the same direction.\n\n## Ch 10 — Pricing Financial Forwards and Futures\n\nCost-of-carry family ($$S_0$$ = spot, $$r$$ = continuous risk-free rate, $$T$$ = maturity):\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT} \\quad \\text{(no income)}$$\n\n$$F_0 = (S_0 - I)e^{rT} \\quad (I = \\text{PV of known dollar income})$$\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - q)T} \\quad (q = \\text{continuous yield; for FX, } q = r_f)$$\n\nValue of an existing long forward with delivery price $$K$$:\n\n$$f = (F_0 - K)e^{-rT}$$\n\n(short: negate). At inception $$K = F_0$$, so $$f = 0$$. *Trap:* price vs. value — $$F_0$$ resets continuously in the market; $$f$$ is the locked-in contract's worth. Don't forget the discounting.\n\nForwards vs. futures: equal under deterministic rates. If the asset price is **positively** correlated with interest rates, futures price > forward price (daily gains reinvested at high rates); negative correlation reverses it.\n\n## Ch 11 — Commodity Forwards and Futures\n\n$$F_0 = (S_0 + U)e^{rT} \\quad (U = \\text{PV of storage costs})$$\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r + u - y)T} \\quad (u = \\text{storage cost rate},\\; y = \\text{convenience yield})$$\n\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r - \\delta)T} \\quad (\\delta = \\text{lease rate})$$\n\nStorage costs act like negative income; convenience yield (benefit of physical holding) like positive income. Implied convenience yield from observed prices:\n\n$$y = r + u - \\frac{1}{T}\\ln\\frac{F_0}{S_0}$$\n\n*Trap:* for consumption commodities only $$F_0 \\le S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$$ holds — convenience yield closes the gap. Backwardation ($$F_0 < S_0$$) signals high convenience yield.\n\n## Ch 12 — Options Markets\n\nIntrinsic value: call $$\\max(S_0 - K, 0)$$, put $$\\max(K - S_0, 0)$$. Time value = option price − intrinsic value. Call moneyness: ITM if $$S > K$$, ATM if $$S = K$$, OTM if $$S < K$$ (puts reversed).\n\nNaked writer margin (per share; × 100 per contract):\n\n$$\\text{Margin} = \\max\\big(\\text{Premium} + 20\\%\\,S_0 - \\text{OTM amount},\\;\\, \\text{Premium} + 10\\% \\times \\text{Underlying}\\big)$$\n\nOTM amount = amount out of the money (zero if ITM); \"underlying\" = $$S_0$$ for calls, $$K$$ for puts. *Trap:* subtract the OTM amount only in the 20% leg; never go below the 10% floor.\n\nStock split $$n$$-for-$$m$$: $$K_{\\text{new}} = K \\times m/n$$ (old/new), and each contract covers $$(n/m) \\times 100$$ shares. *Trap:* terms adjust for splits and stock dividends, **not** cash dividends.\n\n## Ch 13 — Properties of Options\n\nNotation: $$c, p$$ European; $$C, P$$ American; $$D$$ = PV of dividends during the option's life.\n\nUpper bounds: $$c \\le S_0$$, $$C \\le S_0$$, $$p \\le Ke^{-rT}$$, $$P \\le K$$.\n\nLower bounds (no dividends):\n\n$$c \\ge \\max(S_0 - Ke^{-rT},\\, 0) \\qquad p \\ge \\max(Ke^{-rT} - S_0,\\, 0)$$\n\nWith dividends:\n\n$$c \\ge \\max(S_0 - D - Ke^{-rT},\\, 0) \\qquad p \\ge \\max(D + Ke^{-rT} - S_0,\\, 0)$$\n\nAmerican: $$C \\ge c$$, $$P \\ge p$$, and worth at least intrinsic: $$C \\ge \\max(S_0 - K, 0)$$, $$P \\ge \\max(K - S_0, 0)$$.\n\nPut–call parity (European), without and with dividends:\n\n$$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0 \\qquad c + D + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$\n\nAmerican band (with dividends; drop $$D$$ if none):\n\n$$S_0 - D - K \\le C - P \\le S_0 - Ke^{-rT}$$\n\nEarly exercise: never optimal for an American call on a non-dividend stock ($$C = c$$); with dividends, only just before an ex-dividend date. American puts may be exercised early even without dividends (deep ITM, high $$r$$), so $$P > p$$.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Parity is an equality only for **European** options. For American options use the band — applying $$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0$$ to American puts is a deliberate trap.\n\n## Ch 14 — Trading Strategies\n\nNotation: $$K_1 < K_2 (< K_3)$$; debit/credit = net premium paid/received; results at expiration, net of premiums.\n\n| Strategy | Construction | Max profit | Max loss | Breakeven(s) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull call spread | Long call $$K_1$$, short call $$K_2$$ | $$(K_2 - K_1) - \\text{debit}$$ | debit | $$K_1 + \\text{debit}$$ |\n| Bear put spread | Long put $$K_2$$, short put $$K_1$$ | $$(K_2 - K_1) - \\text{debit}$$ | debit | $$K_2 - \\text{debit}$$ |\n| Box spread | Bull call spread + bear put spread, same $$K_1, K_2$$ | riskless payoff $$K_2 - K_1$$ | — | value $$=(K_2 - K_1)e^{-rT}$$ |\n| Butterfly (calls) | Long $$K_1$$, short 2 × $$K_2$$, long $$K_3$$ ($$K_2$$ midpoint) | $$(K_2 - K_1) - \\text{debit}$$ | debit | $$K_1 + \\text{debit}$$; $$K_3 - \\text{debit}$$ |\n| Straddle | Long call + long put, same $$K$$ | unlimited | total premium | $$K \\pm \\text{premium}$$ |\n| Strangle | Long put $$K_1$$ + long call $$K_2$$ | unlimited | total premium | $$K_1 - \\text{prem}$$; $$K_2 + \\text{prem}$$ |\n| Collar | Long stock + long put $$K_1$$ + short call $$K_2$$ | $$K_2 - S_0 - \\text{net cost}$$ | $$S_0 - K_1 + \\text{net cost}$$ | $$S_0 + \\text{net cost}$$ |\n| Strip | Long 1 call + 2 puts, same $$K$$ | large move, down weighted | total premium | $$K + \\text{prem}$$; $$K - \\text{prem}/2$$ |\n| Strap | Long 2 calls + 1 put, same $$K$$ | large move, up weighted | total premium | $$K - \\text{prem}$$; $$K + \\text{prem}/2$$ |\n\nBox spread is riskless:\n\n$$\\text{Payoff} = K_2 - K_1 \\qquad \\text{Value} = (K_2 - K_1)e^{-rT}$$\n\n*Trap:* the box argument requires **European** options — American early exercise breaks it. Always net both premiums before computing breakevens.\n\n## Ch 15 — Exotic Options\n\nGap call: pays $$S_T - K_1$$ if $$S_T > K_2$$, else 0 (gap put: $$K_1 - S_T$$ if $$S_T < K_2$$). Payoff can be negative if $$K_1 > K_2$$.\n\nBinaries: cash-or-nothing call pays fixed $$Q$$ if $$S_T > K$$; asset-or-nothing call pays $$S_T$$ if $$S_T > K$$. Note: vanilla call = asset-or-nothing − cash-or-nothing with $$Q = K$$.\n\nLookbacks ($$S_{\\min}, S_{\\max}$$ = extremes over the life):\n\n$$\\text{Floating: call } S_T - S_{\\min}, \\text{ put } S_{\\max} - S_T \\qquad \\text{Fixed: call } \\max(S_{\\max} - K, 0), \\text{ put } \\max(K - S_{\\min}, 0)$$\n\nAsians ($$S_{\\text{avg}}$$ = average price over the life):\n\n$$\\text{Average price call: } \\max(S_{\\text{avg}} - K,\\, 0) \\qquad \\text{Average strike call: } \\max(S_T - S_{\\text{avg}},\\, 0)$$\n\n(puts symmetric). Average price options are cheaper than vanillas — averaging lowers volatility.\n\nExchange option (swap asset 2 for asset 1): payoff $$\\max(S_1 - S_2,\\, 0)$$.\n\nChooser (choose call or put at $$t_1$$, both strike $$K$$, maturity $$T$$) — by parity at $$t_1$$:\n\n$$\\text{Chooser} = c(K, T) + p\\big(Ke^{-r(T - t_1)},\\, t_1\\big)$$\n\na call to $$T$$ plus a put maturing at $$t_1$$ with strike $$Ke^{-r(T - t_1)}$$ (non-dividend case).\n\nBarrier parity (same type, strike, maturity, barrier):\n\n$$\\text{knock-in} + \\text{knock-out} = \\text{vanilla}$$\n\n*Trap:* this prices the missing barrier option instantly — don't recompute from scratch.\n\n## Ch 16 — Properties of Interest Rates\n\nCompounding conversions ($$r_m$$ = rate compounded $$m$$ times/year, $$r_c$$ = continuous equivalent):\n\n$$r_c = m\\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right) \\qquad r_m = m\\left(e^{r_c/m} - 1\\right) \\qquad \\left(1 + \\frac{r_1}{m_1}\\right)^{m_1} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_2}{m_2}\\right)^{m_2}$$\n\nBond price from zero rates: $$P = \\sum_i c_i e^{-z_i t_i} + 100\\,e^{-z_n t_n}$$, where $$c_i$$ = coupon at $$t_i$$, $$z_i$$ = zero rate.\n\nPar yield (semiannual, face 100; $$d_i = e^{-z_i t_i}$$ = discount factors): coupon $$c$$ making price = 100:\n\n$$c = \\frac{2\\,(100 - 100\\,d_n)}{\\sum_i d_i}$$\n\nForward rate over $$[T_1, T_2]$$ — continuous and discrete ($$R_1, R_2$$ = zero rates):\n\n$$R_F = \\frac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1} \\qquad (1 + R_2)^{T_2} = (1 + R_1)^{T_1}(1 + R_F)^{T_2 - T_1}$$\n\nFRA value on notional $$L$$, receive fixed $$R_K$$:\n\n$$V = L\\,(R_K - R_F)(T_2 - T_1)\\,e^{-R_2 T_2}$$\n\nPay-fixed flips the sign: $$V = L(R_F - R_K)(T_2 - T_1)e^{-R_2 T_2}$$. $$R_K, R_F$$ use compounding matching $$T_2 - T_1$$.\n\n*Trap:* the FRA payoff is fixed at $$T_1$$ but applies over $$[T_1, T_2]$$; when settled at $$T_1$$ the payment is the $$T_2$$ amount discounted from $$T_2$$ to $$T_1$$ at the realized floating rate. Valuation shortcut: assume the forward rate is realized, then discount.\n\n## Ch 17 — Corporate Bonds\n\n$$\\text{EL} = \\text{PD} \\times (1 - \\text{RR}) \\times \\text{Exposure} \\qquad \\text{Spread} \\approx \\text{PD} \\times \\text{LGD}$$\n\nPD = probability of default; RR = recovery rate (price shortly after default as % of face); LGD = 1 − RR. *Trap:* the spread relation is an approximation — actual spreads exceed PD × LGD because of risk premiums, taxes, and liquidity.\n\nSeniority (secured > senior unsecured > subordinated) drives RR, which is issue-level; PD is issuer-level.\n\n## Ch 18 — Mortgages and Mortgage-Backed Securities\n\nLevel monthly payment on loan $$L$$, annual rate $$r$$, $$n$$ years:\n\n$$PMT = L\\,\\frac{r/12}{1 - (1 + r/12)^{-12n}}$$\n\nMonthly interest = $$(r/12) \\times$$ balance; scheduled principal = PMT − interest.\n\nPrepayments (CPR = annual conditional prepayment rate, SMM = single monthly mortality):\n\n$$SMM = 1 - (1 - CPR)^{1/12} \\qquad \\text{Prepayment}_t = SMM \\times (\\text{Balance}_t - \\text{Scheduled principal}_t)$$\n\nPSA benchmark — 100 PSA:\n\n$$CPR = 0.2\\% \\times \\min(\\text{month},\\, 30) \\quad \\text{(6\\% cap from month 30)}$$\n\nX PSA multiplies that CPR by X/100 (150 PSA, month 20: CPR = 1.5 × 4% = 6%).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Convert CPR to SMM before applying it monthly (CPR/12 is wrong), and apply SMM to the balance **after** subtracting that month's scheduled principal.\n\n## Ch 19 — Interest Rate Futures\n\nDay counts: Treasury bonds actual/actual (in period); corporates/munis 30/360; money market actual/360.\n\n$$\\text{Dirty (cash) price} = \\text{Clean (quoted) price} + \\text{Accrued interest}, \\quad \\text{Accrued} = \\text{Coupon} \\times \\frac{\\text{days since last coupon}}{\\text{days in period}}$$\n\nT-bill discount quote $$Q$$ (face 100, $$n$$ days to maturity):\n\n$$\\text{Cash price} = 100 - \\frac{n}{360}\\,Q$$\n\nT-bond futures quote in 32nds: 95-16 = 95 + 16/32 = 95.50. Delivery invoice:\n\n$$\\text{Invoice} = (\\text{Settlement futures price} \\times CF) + \\text{Accrued interest}$$\n\nCF = conversion factor. Cheapest-to-deliver minimizes $$\\text{Quoted bond price} - (\\text{Futures price} \\times CF)$$.\n\nEurodollar futures (quote $$Q = 100 -$$ rate):\n\n$$\\text{Contract value} = 10{,}000\\,[100 - 0.25(100 - Q)]$$\n\nOne basis point in $$Q$$ = $25 per contract (3-month rate on $1 million notional).\n\nConvexity adjustment ($$\\sigma$$ = short-rate volatility, $$T_1$$ = futures maturity, $$T_2$$ = maturity of the underlying rate):\n\n$$\\text{Forward rate} = \\text{Futures rate} - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2 T_1 T_2$$\n\nFutures rates exceed forward rates; the gap grows with maturity squared.\n\nDuration-based hedge:\n\n$$N^* = -\\frac{P\\,D_P}{V_F\\,D_F}$$\n\n$$P$$ = portfolio value, $$D_P$$ = its duration, $$V_F$$ = futures contract price (price × multiplier), $$D_F$$ = duration of the underlying. Negative = short futures to hedge a long bond portfolio.\n\n## Ch 20 — Swaps\n\nInterest rate swap as bonds:\n\n$$V_{\\text{pay fixed}} = B_{fl} - B_{fix} \\qquad V_{\\text{receive fixed}} = B_{fix} - B_{fl}$$\n\n$$B_{fix}$$ = PV of fixed coupons + notional; floating leg:\n\n$$B_{fl} = (L + k^*)\\,e^{-r^* t^*}$$\n\n$$L$$ = notional, $$k^*$$ = next (already-set) floating coupon, $$t^*$$ = time to next reset, $$r^*$$ = zero rate to $$t^*$$. The floating leg is worth par immediately after each reset.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Include the notional on **both** legs in the bond method, and don't drop the already-fixed next floating coupon.\n\nSwap as FRAs: assume forward rates are realized, compute each net exchange (fixed − floating forward) × notional × accrual fraction, discount each at the matching zero rate. Same value as the bond method.\n\nCurrency swap (receive domestic, pay foreign):\n\n$$V = B_D - S_0 B_F$$\n\n$$B_D$$ = domestic-currency bond value (domestic units), $$B_F$$ = foreign-currency bond value (foreign units), $$S_0$$ = spot in domestic per foreign. Receive-foreign: $$V = S_0 B_F - B_D$$. Discount each leg at its own currency's rates.\n\nComparative advantage:\n\n$$\\text{Total gain} = \\Delta_{\\text{fixed}} - \\Delta_{\\text{floating}}$$\n\n$$\\Delta_{\\text{fixed}}$$ = difference between the two borrowers' fixed rates; $$\\Delta_{\\text{floating}}$$ = difference between their floating spreads. *Trap:* the gain is shared by both counterparties and any intermediary — subtract the bank's cut before allocating savings.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"904","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Formula Sheet — Book 4 (Valuation)","notes":"# Book 4 Formulas\n\nEvery testable formula in Valuation and Risk Models, with variable definitions, sign conventions, and the classic traps.\n\n## Ch 1 — Measures of Financial Risk\n\nParametric (delta-normal) VaR at confidence level α, **loss convention** (VaR reported as a positive loss number):\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_\\alpha = \\mu - z_\\alpha \\sigma \\;\\;\\text{(on returns; for losses } \\text{VaR} = -\\mu + z_\\alpha\\sigma\\text{)}$$\n\nwhere μ, σ = mean and standard deviation of P&L (or returns × portfolio value) and $$z_\\alpha$$ = standard normal quantile (1.645 for 95%, 2.326 for 99%). *Trap:* with μ = 0 (standard for short horizons) VaR = $$z_\\alpha\\sigma$$ × value; state whether μ is included — GARP answer choices differ by exactly μ.\n\nExpected shortfall (normal), average loss beyond VaR:\n\n$$\\text{ES} = \\mu + \\sigma\\,\\frac{\\phi(z_\\alpha)}{1-\\alpha}$$\n\nwhere ϕ = standard normal **density** (not the CDF), e.g., ϕ(1.645) = 0.1031, ϕ(2.326) = 0.0267. Sign convention: written this way, μ is the mean *loss*; if μ is a mean return, flip its sign. ES ≥ VaR always.\n\nCoherent risk measure axioms: **monotonicity, translation invariance, positive homogeneity, subadditivity** — $$\\rho(A+B) \\le \\rho(A) + \\rho(B)$$. VaR violates subadditivity; ES satisfies all four.\n\n## Ch 2 — Calculating and Applying VaR\n\nSquare-root-of-time scaling (i.i.d. returns, zero mean):\n\n$$\\text{VaR}_{T\\text{ days}} = \\text{VaR}_{1\\text{ day}} \\times \\sqrt{T} \\qquad \\sigma_{T} = \\sigma_{1}\\sqrt{T}$$\n\n*Trap:* invalid under autocorrelation (positive autocorrelation → understates) or mean reversion.\n\nHistorical simulation rank rule: with n scenarios, the α-confidence VaR is read from the **(1−α)·n worst** outcomes — e.g., 99% VaR with 500 scenarios is the 5th worst loss (some conventions: between 5th and 6th; follow the question's wording).\n\n## Ch 3 — Measuring and Monitoring Volatility\n\nEWMA (RiskMetrics λ = 0.94 daily):\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\lambda\\sigma_{t-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)r_{t-1}^2$$\n\nHigher λ → smoother, slower-reacting. Weights on past squared returns decay as $$(1-\\lambda)\\lambda^i$$.\n\nGARCH(1,1):\n\n$$\\sigma_t^2 = \\omega + \\alpha r_{t-1}^2 + \\beta\\sigma_{t-1}^2 \\qquad V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1-\\alpha-\\beta}$$\n\nStationarity requires α + β < 1; EWMA is GARCH with ω = 0, α + β = 1 (no mean reversion, no long-run variance). *Trap:* formulas update the **variance**; take the square root at the end, and don't mix % and decimal returns.\n\n## Ch 6 — Measuring Credit Risk\n\n$$\\text{EL} = \\text{PD} \\times \\text{LGD} \\times \\text{EAD}$$\n\nPD = probability of default, LGD = loss given default = 1 − recovery rate, EAD = exposure at default. Unexpected loss (one-year, single exposure):\n\n$$\\text{UL} = \\text{EAD} \\times \\sqrt{\\text{PD}\\cdot\\sigma_{LGD}^2 + \\text{LGD}^2\\cdot\\sigma_{PD}^2}, \\qquad \\sigma_{PD}^2 = \\text{PD}(1-\\text{PD})$$\n\n*Trap:* LGD vs recovery — read which one is given. EL is priced into spreads; capital is held against UL.\n\n## Ch 9 — Pricing Conventions, Discounting, and Arbitrage\n\nDiscount factor: $$d(t)$$ = price today of \\$1 at t; $$P = \\sum_t CF_t \\, d(t)$$. Extract d(t) iteratively (bootstrapping) from coupon bond prices.\n\nT-bill discount quote (US convention, ACT/360):\n\n$$P = F\\left[1 - \\frac{n}{360}\\,d\\right] \\qquad d = \\frac{360}{n}\\cdot\\frac{F-P}{F}$$\n\nd = quoted discount rate, n = days to maturity, F = face. *Trap:* the discount rate understates the true (investment) yield — it divides by F, not P, and uses a 360-day year.\n\nLaw of one price / arbitrage: identical cash flows ⇒ identical price; replicate a bond from other bonds with matching cash flows and compare.\n\n## Ch 10 — Interest Rates\n\nCompounding conversions (rate $$R_m$$ compounded m times/year ↔ continuous $$R_c$$):\n\n$$R_c = m\\ln\\!\\left(1+\\frac{R_m}{m}\\right) \\qquad R_m = m\\left(e^{R_c/m} - 1\\right)$$\n\nGeneral frequency switch: $$\\left(1+\\frac{R_m}{m}\\right)^m = \\left(1+\\frac{R_n}{n}\\right)^n$$.\n\nForward rate from spots (semiannual convention; t in years):\n\n$$\\left(1+\\frac{z_2}{2}\\right)^{2t_2} = \\left(1+\\frac{z_1}{2}\\right)^{2t_1}\\left(1+\\frac{f}{2}\\right)^{2(t_2-t_1)}$$\n\nContinuous version: $$f = \\frac{z_2 t_2 - z_1 t_1}{t_2 - t_1}$$.\n\nPar rate c solves $$\\frac{c}{2}\\sum_i d(t_i) \\cdot 100 + 100\\,d(T) = 100$$ — the coupon making the bond price par. Upward-sloping curve ⇒ forward > spot > par at each maturity. *Trap:* forwards \"lever\" spots — a flat segment of spots forces the forward to equal them; rising spots force forwards above.\n\n## Ch 11 — Bond Yields and Return Calculations\n\nPrice from YTM (semiannual): $$P = \\sum_{i=1}^{2T}\\frac{cF/2}{(1+y/2)^i} + \\frac{F}{(1+y/2)^{2T}}$$\n\nAnnuity: $$PV = \\frac{C}{y}\\left[1-(1+y)^{-n}\\right]$$. Perpetuity: $$PV = C/y$$.\n\nGross realized return: $$R = \\frac{P_{end} + \\text{coupons} + \\text{reinvestment income} - P_{start}}{P_{start}}$$; net subtracts financing.\n\nCoupon > YTM ⇒ premium; < ⇒ discount; pull to par over time. *Trap:* realized return = YTM only if coupons are reinvested at the YTM and held to maturity; zeros are exempt (no coupons).\n\n## Ch 12 — Duration, Convexity, DV01\n\n$$\\text{DV01} = -\\frac{\\Delta P}{10{,}000\\cdot\\Delta y} = \\frac{D_{Mod}\\cdot P}{10{,}000}$$\n\n$$D_{Mod} = \\frac{D_{Mac}}{1+y/m} \\qquad D_{Eff} = \\frac{P_- - P_+}{2P\\,\\Delta y} \\qquad C_{Eff} = \\frac{P_+ + P_- - 2P}{P(\\Delta y)^2}$$\n\nDollar duration = $$D_{Mod} \\cdot P$$. Price change estimate:\n\n$$\\Delta P \\approx -D\\cdot P\\cdot\\Delta y + \\frac{1}{2}C\\cdot P\\cdot(\\Delta y)^2$$\n\nDV01 hedge: $$N = -\\dfrac{\\text{DV01}_{position}}{\\text{DV01}_{hedge}}$$. Portfolio D and C = value-weighted averages; DV01s add.\n\nMacaulay = cash-flow-time average (zero: $$D_{Mac}=T$$); modified for option-free yield moves; **effective** for callables/MBS. Barbell vs duration-matched bullet: barbell has more convexity, usually lower yield. *Trap:* Δy in decimal, keep the ½, and rescale convexity if quoted per 100.\n\n## Ch 13 — Non-Parallel Shifts\n\n$$\\text{KR01}_k = -\\frac{\\Delta P_k}{10{,}000\\,\\Delta y_k} \\qquad \\frac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -\\sum_k D_k\\,\\Delta y_k$$\n\n$$\\sum_k D_k \\approx D_{Eff} \\qquad \\sum_k \\text{KR01}_k \\approx \\text{DV01}$$\n\nKey-rate shifts are tent-shaped (1bp at the key maturity, linear to zero at neighbors). Hedge n key rates with n bonds: set net KR01 = 0 at each key rate (n equations). Partial '01s shift curve-fitting instruments; forward-bucket '01s shift forward-rate segments; both sum to the total '01. Volatility-weighted hedge multiplies the DV01 ratio by regression beta of position yield on hedge yield. *Trap:* key-rate durations can be negative for individual bonds; positive KR01 = loss when that rate rises, so hedge with shorts.\n\n## Ch 14 — Binomial Trees\n\n$$\\Delta = \\frac{f_u - f_d}{S_0 u - S_0 d} \\qquad p = \\frac{e^{r\\Delta t}-d}{u-d} \\qquad f = e^{-r\\Delta t}\\left[p f_u + (1-p)f_d\\right]$$\n\n$$u = e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}} \\qquad d = \\frac{1}{u}$$\n\nNumerator growth factor by underlying: stock $$e^{r\\Delta t}$$; dividend yield q: $$e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}$$; currency: $$e^{(r-r_f)\\Delta t}$$; **futures: 1**. Discount always at domestic r. American: at each node take max(intrinsic, continuation). *Trap:* never value an American option by discounting terminal payoffs directly; and √Δt (not Δt) in u.\n\n## Ch 15 — Black–Scholes–Merton\n\n$$c = S_0 N(d_1) - Ke^{-rT}N(d_2) \\qquad p = Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2) - S_0 N(-d_1)$$\n\n$$d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r+\\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}} \\qquad d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma\\sqrt{T}$$\n\nContinuous dividend yield q (indices; FX with q = $$r_f$$):\n\n$$c = S_0 e^{-qT}N(d_1) - Ke^{-rT}N(d_2), \\qquad d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S_0/K) + (r - q + \\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}$$\n\nDiscrete dividends: use $$S_0 - PV(\\text{divs})$$. Put–call parity (no dividends):\n\n$$c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0 \\qquad \\text{(yield q: } c + Ke^{-rT} = p + S_0e^{-qT}\\text{)}$$\n\nLognormal returns: $$\\ln S_T \\sim N\\big(\\ln S_0 + (\\mu - \\sigma^2/2)T,\\, \\sigma^2 T\\big)$$. Annualize realized vol: $$\\hat\\sigma = s\\sqrt{252}$$ (daily). N(d₂) = risk-neutral exercise probability; N(d₁) = call delta. Warrant ≈ $$\\frac{N}{N+M} \\times c$$ (dilution). Black's approximation for American calls with dividends: max of (call to T on dividend-adjusted S₀, call expiring just before the last ex-date). *Trap:* d₁ uses **+σ²/2**; the −σ²/2 belongs to the lognormal mean. Always discount K.\n\n## Ch 16 — The Greeks\n\nWith dividend yield q (set q = 0 for plain stock); $$N'(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-x^2/2}$$:\n\n$$\\delta_{call} = e^{-qT}N(d_1) \\qquad \\delta_{put} = e^{-qT}\\left[N(d_1)-1\\right]$$\n\n$$\\Gamma = \\frac{N'(d_1)\\,e^{-qT}}{S_0\\sigma\\sqrt{T}} \\qquad \\text{vega} = S_0\\sqrt{T}\\,N'(d_1)\\,e^{-qT} \\quad\\text{(same for calls and puts)}$$\n\n$$\\Theta_{call} = -\\frac{S_0 N'(d_1)\\sigma e^{-qT}}{2\\sqrt{T}} + qS_0N(d_1)e^{-qT} - rKe^{-rT}N(d_2)$$\n\n$$\\Theta_{put} = -\\frac{S_0 N'(d_1)\\sigma e^{-qT}}{2\\sqrt{T}} - qS_0N(-d_1)e^{-qT} + rKe^{-rT}N(-d_2)$$\n\n$$\\rho_{call} = KTe^{-rT}N(d_2) \\qquad \\rho_{put} = -KTe^{-rT}N(-d_2)$$\n\nDelta–gamma P&L: $$\\Delta P \\approx \\delta\\,\\Delta S + \\frac{1}{2}\\Gamma(\\Delta S)^2$$. Delta-neutral book identity:\n\n$$\\Theta + rS\\delta + \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma^2S^2\\Gamma = r\\Pi \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{(δ = 0): big +Θ pairs with big −Γ}$$\n\nHedging order: gamma (or vega) with traded options **first**, then delta with shares (shares have Γ = vega = 0). Gamma peaks ATM near expiry; vega peaks ATM at long maturity.\n\n| Position | Delta | Gamma | Theta | Vega | Rho |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Long call | + | + | − | + | + |\n| Long put | − | + | − (usually) | + | − |\n| Short call | − | − | + | − | − |\n| Short put | + | − | + (usually) | − | + |\n\n*Trap:* theta exceptions — deep ITM European puts (and ITM calls on high-q assets) can have **positive** theta. Short option positions flip every sign, including gamma and vega.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"905","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Glossary (A–Z)","notes":"# Glossary\n\nTerms as used in the FRM Part I curriculum.\n\n## A\n\n- **Accrued interest** — Coupon interest earned since the last coupon date that the bond buyer pays the seller; it is the difference between the dirty (invoice) price and the clean (quoted) price. (Book 4, Ch 9)\n- **Adjusted R²** — A version of R² penalized for the number of regressors, so it rises only when an added variable improves fit by more than chance. (Book 2, Ch 8)\n- **Adverse selection** — The tendency of those most likely to suffer a loss to seek out insurance or credit, so a pool attracts worse-than-average risks when the seller cannot distinguish risk types. (Book 1, Ch 2; Book 3, Ch 2)\n- **Altman Z-score** — A credit-scoring model that combines weighted accounting ratios (working capital, retained earnings, EBIT, equity, sales relative to assets) into a single score; low values (roughly below 1.8) signal high bankruptcy risk. (Book 4, Ch 4)\n- **American option** — An option that can be exercised at any time up to and including expiration; never worth less than the otherwise identical European option. (Book 3, Ch 12)\n- **Arbitrage** — A strategy that generates riskless profit with no net investment by exploiting price discrepancies; in well-functioning markets arbitrage opportunities are competed away, which underpins derivative pricing. (Book 4, Ch 9)\n- **Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT)** — A multifactor model in which expected return is the risk-free rate plus the sum of factor betas times factor risk premiums; it relies on no-arbitrage and well-diversified portfolios rather than CAPM's equilibrium assumptions. (Book 1, Ch 6)\n- **Asian option** — An exotic option whose payoff depends on the average price of the underlying over a period rather than the terminal price, reducing exposure to price manipulation and volatility at expiry. (Book 3, Ch 15)\n- **Autocorrelation (serial correlation)** — Correlation of a variable (or regression residual) with its own lagged values; in regression it leaves OLS coefficients unbiased but invalidates conventional standard errors. (Book 2, Ch 9–10)\n\n## B\n\n- **Backtesting** — Comparing model predictions with realized outcomes, e.g., counting days on which losses exceed VaR (\"exceptions\") to judge whether the model's coverage is consistent with its stated confidence level. (Book 4, Ch 2)\n- **Backwardation** — A futures market in which the futures price lies below the expected future spot price (or, loosely, below the current spot price); consistent with hedgers being net short and speculators net long. (Book 3, Ch 7, 11)\n- **Barrier option** — An exotic option that comes into existence (knock-in) or ceases to exist (knock-out) when the underlying crosses a specified barrier level. (Book 3, Ch 15)\n- **Basis** — Spot price of the asset being hedged minus the futures price of the contract used: $$b = S - F$$. The basis converges to zero (or to a carry-related differential) at delivery. (Book 3, Ch 8)\n- **Basis point (bp)** — One-hundredth of one percent, 0.01% = 0.0001; the standard unit for quoting rate changes and spreads.\n- **Basis risk** — Hedging risk arising because the basis at the time the hedge is lifted is uncertain — due to asset mismatch, maturity mismatch, or location mismatch between the exposure and the futures contract. (Book 3, Ch 8)\n- **Bayes' theorem** — Rule for updating probabilities with new information: $$P(A\\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(B\\mid A)\\,P(A)}{P(B)}$$. (Book 2, Ch 1)\n- **Bernoulli distribution** — Distribution of a single trial with outcome 1 (probability p) or 0 (probability 1 − p); the building block for modeling individual defaults. (Book 2, Ch 3)\n- **Beta** — Sensitivity of an asset's return to the market return, $$\\beta_i = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)}$$; the measure of systematic risk priced in the CAPM. (Book 1, Ch 5)\n- **Bid–ask spread** — Difference between the price at which dealers will sell (ask) and buy (bid); a direct measure of transaction cost and market liquidity. (Book 3, Ch 5)\n- **Binomial distribution** — Distribution of the number of successes in n independent Bernoulli trials with success probability p; mean np, variance np(1 − p). Used for counting defaults and VaR exceptions. (Book 2, Ch 3)\n- **Binomial tree** — A discrete-time lattice in which the underlying moves up by factor u or down by factor d each step; options are valued by risk-neutral expectation discounted at the risk-free rate, with $$p = \\dfrac{e^{r\\Delta t} - d}{u - d}$$. (Book 4, Ch 14)\n- **Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) model** — Closed-form European option pricing model assuming lognormal prices and constant volatility: $$c = S_0 N(d_1) - K e^{-rT} N(d_2)$$. (Book 4, Ch 15)\n- **Bootstrapping (statistical)** — Resampling observed data with replacement to approximate the sampling distribution of an estimator without distributional assumptions; includes i.i.d. and circular block variants. (Book 2, Ch 13)\n- **Bootstrapping (yield curve)** — Iteratively extracting zero (spot) rates from the prices of coupon bonds or swap quotes, starting with the shortest maturity and working outward. (Book 4, Ch 9–10)\n\n## C\n\n- **Call option** — The right, without the obligation, to buy the underlying at the strike price on (European) or by (American) expiration. (Book 3, Ch 12)\n- **Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)** — Equilibrium model in which only systematic risk is priced: $$E(R_i) = R_f + \\beta_i\\,[E(R_m) - R_f]$$. (Book 1, Ch 5)\n- **Central clearing** — Post-trade arrangement in which a central counterparty interposes itself between buyer and seller, becoming the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer; mandated for standardized OTC derivatives after the 2007–2009 crisis. (Book 3, Ch 6)\n- **Central counterparty (CCP)** — The clearinghouse that performs central clearing, managing counterparty risk through novation, netting, initial and variation margin, and a default fund (the \"default waterfall\"). (Book 3, Ch 6)\n- **Central limit theorem** — The standardized mean of n i.i.d. random variables with finite variance converges to a standard normal distribution as n grows, regardless of the underlying distribution. (Book 2, Ch 5)\n- **Cheapest-to-deliver (CTD)** — In Treasury bond futures, the deliverable bond that minimizes the short's delivery cost, i.e., minimizes quoted bond price minus (settlement price × conversion factor). (Book 3, Ch 19)\n- **Clean price** — Quoted bond price excluding accrued interest; the U.S. quoting convention for Treasury bonds. Compare dirty price. (Book 4, Ch 9)\n- **Coherent risk measure** — A risk measure satisfying monotonicity, translation invariance, positive homogeneity, and subadditivity; expected shortfall is coherent, VaR generally is not because it can violate subadditivity. (Book 4, Ch 1)\n- **Collateral** — Assets posted to secure performance on an obligation (e.g., margin on derivatives, repo collateral); reduces counterparty credit risk but creates liquidity demands. (Book 3, Ch 5–6)\n- **Comparative advantage** — The argument that two firms can both lower funding costs by borrowing in the market where each has a relative (not absolute) cost edge and swapping payments; the classic motivation for interest rate swaps. (Book 3, Ch 20)\n- **Compounding frequency / continuous compounding** — Convention defining how quoted rates accrue; with continuous compounding a deposit grows as $$A e^{rT}$$, the convention used throughout derivatives pricing. (Book 3, Ch 16)\n- **Conditional prepayment rate (CPR)** — Annualized rate at which a mortgage pool's outstanding principal prepays; related to the monthly measure by $$\\text{SMM} = 1 - (1 - \\text{CPR})^{1/12}$$. (Book 3, Ch 18)\n- **Confidence interval** — A range constructed from sample data that contains the true parameter with a stated probability across repeated samples, e.g., estimate ± critical value × standard error. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n- **Contango** — A futures market in which the futures price lies above the expected future spot price (or, loosely, above the current spot price). (Book 3, Ch 7, 11)\n- **Convenience yield** — The benefit, beyond monetary income, from physically holding a consumption commodity (e.g., keeping a production process running); it lowers the futures price relative to full carry. (Book 3, Ch 11)\n- **Convexity (bond)** — Second-order sensitivity of bond price to yield, $$C = \\dfrac{1}{P}\\dfrac{d^2P}{dy^2}$$; combined with duration it improves the price-change approximation $$\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D\\,\\Delta y + \\tfrac{1}{2} C (\\Delta y)^2$$. Positive convexity means prices rise more when yields fall than they drop when yields rise. (Book 4, Ch 12)\n- **Convexity adjustment (futures vs. forwards)** — The amount by which the rate implied by an interest rate futures contract (e.g., Eurodollar/SOFR futures) exceeds the corresponding forward rate, caused by daily settlement: forward ≈ futures − ½σ²t₁t₂. (Book 3, Ch 19)\n- **Copula** — A function that joins marginal distributions into a joint distribution, allowing the dependence structure (e.g., Gaussian or Student's t copula) to be modeled separately from the marginals. (Book 2, Ch 4)\n- **Correlation** — Standardized linear dependence between two variables, $$\\rho = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X \\sigma_Y}$$, bounded between −1 and +1; captures only linear relationships. (Book 2, Ch 4–5)\n- **Cost of carry** — The net cost of holding the underlying (financing plus storage minus income); for an investment asset the no-arbitrage futures price is $$F_0 = S_0 e^{cT}$$, reduced by any convenience yield for consumption assets. (Book 3, Ch 10–11)\n- **Country risk** — Risk from investing or lending in a particular country, driven by political risk, legal systems, economic structure, and the sovereign's willingness and ability to pay; measured via sovereign ratings, sovereign spreads, and CDS spreads. (Book 4, Ch 5)\n- **Covariance** — Expected product of two variables' deviations from their means; positive when they move together. The covariance matrix is the input for portfolio variance. (Book 2, Ch 4)\n- **Covered interest rate parity** — No-arbitrage relation tying forward FX rates to interest differentials: $$F_0 = S_0\\, e^{(r - r_f)T}$$ (domestic rate r, foreign rate r_f); deviations are eliminated by riskless arbitrage. (Book 3, Ch 9)\n- **Credit default swap (CDS)** — Contract in which the protection buyer pays a periodic spread and receives a payoff if a reference entity suffers a credit event; the basic instrument for transferring default risk. (Book 1, Ch 4)\n- **Credit rating** — An ordinal assessment of creditworthiness (e.g., AAA to C/D) issued by external agencies or produced internally; \"through-the-cycle\" external ratings change slowly, whereas \"point-in-time\" internal measures track current conditions. (Book 4, Ch 4)\n- **Credit risk** — Risk of loss because a borrower or counterparty fails to perform; its expected-loss building blocks are PD, LGD, and EAD. (Book 1, Ch 1; Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Credit risk transfer** — Shifting credit exposure to other parties via guarantees, credit insurance, CDS, securitization, and related structures; benefits include risk dispersion, but it can weaken monitoring incentives, as in the originate-to-distribute model. (Book 1, Ch 4)\n- **Cross hedge** — Hedging an exposure with futures on a different but correlated asset (e.g., jet fuel hedged with heating oil futures); introduces basis risk and motivates the minimum-variance hedge ratio. (Book 3, Ch 8)\n- **Currency swap** — Exchange of principal and interest payments in one currency for principal and interest in another; unlike interest rate swaps, principal amounts are typically exchanged at the start and end. (Book 3, Ch 20)\n\n## D\n\n- **Delta** — First-order sensitivity of an option (or portfolio) value to the underlying price, $$\\Delta = \\partial f / \\partial S$$; for a European call under BSM, Δ = N(d₁). (Book 4, Ch 16)\n- **Delta hedging** — Neutralizing small-move exposure by holding −Δ of the underlying against an option position; requires dynamic rebalancing because delta changes with S and time. (Book 4, Ch 16)\n- **Derivative** — A contract whose value derives from an underlying asset, rate, or index; main building blocks are forwards, futures, swaps, and options. (Book 3, Ch 4)\n- **Dirty (invoice) price** — Full price actually paid for a bond: clean price plus accrued interest. (Book 4, Ch 9)\n- **Diversification** — Reducing portfolio risk by combining imperfectly correlated assets; eliminates idiosyncratic risk but not systematic risk. (Book 1, Ch 5)\n- **Duration (Macaulay)** — Weighted-average time to a bond's cash flows, with present-value weights; for a zero-coupon bond it equals maturity. (Book 4, Ch 12)\n- **Duration (modified)** — Percentage price sensitivity to yield: $$D_{mod} = -\\dfrac{1}{P}\\dfrac{dP}{dy} = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m}$$, so ΔP/P ≈ −D_mod Δy. (Book 4, Ch 12)\n- **Duration (effective)** — Duration computed by repricing the instrument under up and down rate shifts; appropriate for bonds with embedded options or other rate-dependent cash flows (e.g., MBS). (Book 4, Ch 12)\n- **DV01** — Dollar value of a basis point: the change in a position's value for a one-basis-point change in rates, $$\\text{DV01} = D_{mod} \\times P \\times 0.0001$$; the basis for hedge ratios between rate instruments. (Book 4, Ch 12)\n\n## E\n\n- **Economic capital** — Capital a firm estimates it needs to absorb unexpected losses at a chosen confidence level over a horizon; the denominator in RAROC and the internal counterpart to regulatory capital. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Efficient frontier** — In mean-variance analysis, the set of portfolios offering the highest expected return for each level of risk; with a risk-free asset it becomes the capital market line through the market portfolio. (Book 1, Ch 5)\n- **Enterprise risk management (ERM)** — A firm-wide, integrated approach to identifying, measuring, and managing all material risks against a single risk appetite, rather than managing risks in silos. (Book 1, Ch 8)\n- **European option** — An option exercisable only at expiration. (Book 3, Ch 12)\n- **EWMA (exponentially weighted moving average)** — Volatility updating scheme $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda \\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)\\, u_{n-1}^2$$ that weights recent returns more heavily; RiskMetrics popularized λ = 0.94 for daily data. (Book 4, Ch 3)\n- **Exotic option** — A nonstandard option such as Asian, barrier, lookback, binary/digital, compound, chooser, or gap options, typically traded OTC and engineered for specific payoff needs. (Book 3, Ch 15)\n- **Expected loss (EL)** — The mean credit loss, priced into spreads and provisioned for: $$\\text{EL} = \\text{PD} \\times \\text{LGD} \\times \\text{EAD}$$. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Expected shortfall (ES)** — The expected loss conditional on the loss exceeding VaR at the chosen confidence level; also called conditional VaR or expected tail loss. It is coherent (subadditive) and captures tail severity that VaR ignores. (Book 4, Ch 1)\n- **Exposure at default (EAD)** — The amount expected to be owed by the obligor at the time of default, including expected drawdowns of committed lines. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n\n## F\n\n- **Fat tails (leptokurtosis)** — Excess kurtosis relative to the normal distribution, meaning extreme outcomes are more likely than a normal model implies; characteristic of financial returns. (Book 2, Ch 12)\n- **Forward contract** — OTC agreement to buy or sell an asset at a set price on a future date; customizable, settled at maturity, and exposed to counterparty risk. (Book 3, Ch 4)\n- **Forward rate** — The interest rate implied by today's term structure for a future period, e.g., from spot rates: $$f = \\dfrac{r_2 T_2 - r_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1}$$ (continuous compounding). (Book 3, Ch 16; Book 4, Ch 10)\n- **Forward rate agreement (FRA)** — OTC contract fixing the interest rate to apply to a notional amount over a future period, cash-settled against the realized reference rate. (Book 3, Ch 16)\n- **Futures contract** — Exchange-traded, standardized forward with daily marking to market through a margin account and a CCP guarantee, which nearly eliminates counterparty risk. (Book 3, Ch 7)\n\n## G\n\n- **Gamma** — Second-order sensitivity of an option to the underlying price, $$\\Gamma = \\partial^2 f / \\partial S^2$$; largest for at-the-money, near-expiry options. A delta-neutral, positive-gamma position gains from large moves in either direction. (Book 4, Ch 16)\n- **GARCH(1,1)** — Volatility model $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha u_{n-1}^2 + \\beta \\sigma_{n-1}^2$$ with α + β < 1; unlike EWMA it is mean-reverting, with long-run variance $$V_L = \\omega / (1 - \\alpha - \\beta)$$. (Book 4, Ch 3)\n- **GARP Code of Conduct** — Mandatory standards for FRM candidates and certified members covering professional integrity, conflicts of interest, confidentiality, and best practices; violations can lead to revocation of the FRM designation. (Book 1, Ch 11)\n- **Geometric Brownian motion** — Continuous-time process $$dS = \\mu S\\,dt + \\sigma S\\,dz$$ assumed for stock prices in BSM; it implies lognormally distributed future prices. (Book 4, Ch 14–15)\n- **Greeks** — The option sensitivity measures — delta, gamma, theta, vega, and rho — used to quantify and hedge the exposures of option portfolios. (Book 4, Ch 16)\n\n## H\n\n- **Haircut** — Discount applied to collateral value (e.g., in repo) to protect the lender against collateral price declines. (Book 3, Ch 5)\n- **Hazard rate** — The instantaneous default intensity λ; the probability of default between t and t + dt, conditional on survival to t, is λ dt, giving survival probability $$e^{-\\lambda t}$$. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Hedge ratio** — Size of the futures position per unit of exposure; the minimum-variance hedge ratio is $$h^* = \\rho\\,\\dfrac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F}$$, and the optimal number of contracts is h*·Q_A/Q_F. (Book 3, Ch 8)\n- **Heteroskedasticity** — Non-constant variance of regression errors across observations; OLS coefficients remain unbiased but conventional standard errors are wrong, motivating robust (White) standard errors. (Book 2, Ch 9)\n- **Historical simulation** — Nonparametric VaR/ES method that revalues today's portfolio under past return scenarios and reads risk measures from the empirical loss distribution; weighted and filtered variants address stale data. (Book 4, Ch 2)\n- **Hypothesis testing** — Framework for using sample evidence to decide between a null hypothesis H₀ and an alternative, controlling the probability of false rejection at a chosen significance level. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n\n## I\n\n- **Idiosyncratic (specific) risk** — Risk unique to a firm or asset that diversification can eliminate; it earns no risk premium in the CAPM. (Book 1, Ch 5)\n- **Implied volatility** — The volatility that equates a model price (usually BSM) to the observed market option price; the market's volatility quote, and the input behind smile and surface patterns. (Book 4, Ch 15)\n- **Information ratio** — Active return per unit of active risk: alpha (return relative to benchmark) divided by tracking error; used to evaluate active managers. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Initial margin** — Collateral deposited when a futures or cleared position is opened, sized to cover potential close-out losses in a default. (Book 3, Ch 6–7)\n- **Interest rate swap** — Agreement to exchange fixed-rate interest for floating-rate interest (now typically SOFR-based) on a notional principal; valued as a portfolio of FRAs or as long one bond and short another. (Book 3, Ch 20)\n\n## J\n\n- **Jensen's alpha** — Average return above the CAPM-required return, $$\\alpha = R_p - [R_f + \\beta_p(R_m - R_f)]$$; a risk-adjusted performance measure. (Book 1, Ch 5, 7)\n\n## K\n\n- **K-means clustering** — Unsupervised learning algorithm that partitions observations into K clusters by iteratively assigning points to the nearest centroid and recomputing centroids; K is chosen by the analyst (e.g., elbow or silhouette methods). (Book 2, Ch 14)\n- **K-nearest neighbors (KNN)** — Supervised method that classifies or predicts an observation from the K most similar training observations; small K gives flexible but noisy fits. (Book 2, Ch 15)\n- **Key rate duration** — Sensitivity of a portfolio's value to a shift in one segment (\"key rate\") of the term structure with other rates unchanged; key rate exposures enable hedging of non-parallel curve moves. (Book 4, Ch 13)\n- **Kurtosis** — Standardized fourth moment measuring tail heaviness; the normal distribution has kurtosis 3, and excess kurtosis above 0 indicates fat tails. (Book 2, Ch 5)\n\n## L\n\n- **Lasso** — See Regularization; the L1-penalized regression that shrinks some coefficients exactly to zero, performing variable selection. (Book 2, Ch 14)\n- **Law of one price** — Two positions with identical future cash flows must trade at the same price, else arbitrage exists; the foundation of relative pricing of bonds and derivatives. (Book 4, Ch 9)\n- **LIBOR** — The former interbank offered rate, a survey-based unsecured term rate; manipulation scandals and thin underlying markets led to its phase-out (final USD panel settings ended June 2023) in favor of nearly risk-free rates such as SOFR. (Book 3, Ch 16)\n- **Liquidity risk** — Risk of being unable to transact quickly at prevailing prices (market liquidity risk) or to meet cash obligations as they fall due (funding liquidity risk); the two interact destructively in crises. (Book 1, Ch 1)\n- **Logistic regression** — Regression for binary outcomes that models the probability of the event via the logistic function; a workhorse for default prediction and a baseline machine-learning classifier. (Book 2, Ch 15)\n- **Lognormal distribution** — Distribution of a variable whose logarithm is normal; under BSM assumptions, $$\\ln S_T \\sim N\\!\\left(\\ln S_0 + (\\mu - \\sigma^2/2)T,\\ \\sigma^2 T\\right)$$, so prices stay positive. (Book 2, Ch 3; Book 4, Ch 15)\n- **Lookback option** — Exotic option whose payoff depends on the maximum or minimum underlying price over the option's life. (Book 3, Ch 15)\n- **Loss given default (LGD)** — Fraction of exposure lost if default occurs, equal to one minus the recovery rate. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n\n## M\n\n- **Maintenance margin** — Futures account balance threshold; if equity falls below it, the trader receives a margin call to restore the balance to the initial margin level. (Book 3, Ch 7)\n- **Marking to market** — Daily revaluation of futures (and cleared) positions at settlement prices, with gains and losses passing through margin accounts as variation margin. (Book 3, Ch 7)\n- **Mean reversion** — Tendency of a variable (e.g., variance in GARCH, interest rates) to be pulled back toward a long-run level over time. (Book 4, Ch 3)\n- **Merton model** — Structural credit model viewing equity as a European call on the firm's assets with strike equal to the debt's face value; default occurs if asset value is below the debt due at maturity, and it yields PD and credit spread estimates. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Model risk** — Risk of loss from using misspecified, miscalibrated, or misapplied models; managed through validation, benchmarking, conservative adjustments, and governance. (Book 1, Ch 9)\n- **Moneyness** — Relationship between the underlying price and strike: in-the-money (immediate exercise would pay), at-the-money (S ≈ K), out-of-the-money (no exercise value). (Book 3, Ch 12)\n- **Monte Carlo simulation** — Generating many random paths or draws from assumed distributions to value instruments or build a loss distribution; flexible but computationally heavy and subject to sampling error and model risk. (Book 2, Ch 13; Book 4, Ch 2)\n- **Moral hazard** — The tendency of a protected party to take more risk because another party bears the consequences (e.g., insured firms, originate-to-distribute lenders, too-big-to-fail banks). (Book 1, Ch 2, 4)\n- **Mortgage-backed security (MBS)** — Security backed by a pool of mortgages whose cash flows pass through to investors; agency MBS carry guarantee protection against default but expose holders to prepayment risk. (Book 3, Ch 18)\n- **Multicollinearity** — High correlation among regressors, inflating coefficient standard errors and making individual effects hard to identify even when the regression fits well overall. (Book 2, Ch 9)\n\n## N\n\n- **Netting** — Offsetting amounts owed between counterparties (close-out or payment netting) so only the net amount changes hands; sharply reduces counterparty exposure, and is a core CCP benefit (multilateral netting). (Book 3, Ch 5–6)\n- **Neural network** — Machine-learning model that passes weighted inputs through layers of nonlinear activation functions; highly flexible, requiring regularization and validation data to control overfitting. (Book 2, Ch 15)\n- **Normal distribution** — Symmetric bell-shaped distribution fully described by mean and variance; about 68%, 95%, and 99.7% of mass lies within 1, 2, and 3 standard deviations of the mean. (Book 2, Ch 3)\n- **Normal vs. inverted futures market** — Settlement prices increasing with contract maturity define a normal market; prices declining with maturity define an inverted market. (Book 3, Ch 7)\n- **Notional principal** — Reference amount used to compute payments on swaps and other derivatives; usually not exchanged (except in currency swaps). (Book 3, Ch 20)\n\n## O\n\n- **Omitted variable bias** — Bias in OLS coefficients arising when an excluded variable both affects the dependent variable and is correlated with included regressors. (Book 2, Ch 8–9)\n- **Open interest** — Total number of futures or options contracts outstanding (one long and one short per contract); unlike volume, it measures open positions rather than trading activity. (Book 3, Ch 7)\n- **Operational risk** — Risk of loss from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, and systems, or from external events; includes legal risk but excludes strategic and reputational risk under Basel definitions. (Book 4, Ch 7)\n- **Over-the-counter (OTC) market** — Decentralized dealer market for customized contracts; post-crisis reforms pushed standardized OTC derivatives into central clearing and uncleared trades into bilateral margin requirements. (Book 3, Ch 5)\n- **Overfitting** — Fitting noise in the training data so a model performs well in-sample but poorly out-of-sample; countered with regularization, cross-validation, and held-out test data. (Book 2, Ch 14)\n\n## P\n\n- **p-value** — Probability, under the null hypothesis, of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed; reject the null when the p-value is below the chosen significance level. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n- **Par rate** — Coupon rate that makes a bond price equal to its face value given the current term structure of discount factors. (Book 4, Ch 10)\n- **Poisson distribution** — Distribution for the count of events in an interval given a constant arrival rate λ; mean and variance both equal λ. Standard model for operational loss frequency. (Book 2, Ch 3; Book 4, Ch 7)\n- **Power of a test** — Probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis, equal to 1 minus the Type II error probability; rises with sample size and effect size. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n- **Prepayment risk** — Uncertainty in MBS cash-flow timing because homeowners can prepay, typically refinancing when rates fall; it gives MBS negative convexity. (Book 3, Ch 18)\n- **Principal components analysis (PCA)** — Unsupervised technique that re-expresses correlated variables as orthogonal components ordered by variance explained; applied to yield curves, the first three components are level, slope, and curvature. (Book 2, Ch 14; Book 4, Ch 13)\n- **Probability of default (PD)** — Likelihood that an obligor defaults over a stated horizon (commonly one year); estimated from ratings histories, structural models, or credit spreads. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **PSA prepayment benchmark** — Standard prepayment path: at 100 PSA, CPR rises 0.2% per month to 6% at month 30 and stays there; pools are quoted as multiples (e.g., 150 PSA). (Book 3, Ch 18)\n- **Put option** — The right, without the obligation, to sell the underlying at the strike price. (Book 3, Ch 12)\n- **Put-call parity** — No-arbitrage relation for European options on the same strike and expiry: $$c + K e^{-rT} = p + S_0$$ (adjusted for income on the underlying); violations create arbitrage. (Book 3, Ch 13)\n\n## Q\n\n- **Quantile** — The value below which a stated fraction of a distribution lies; VaR at confidence level X% is a quantile of the loss distribution. (Book 2, Ch 2)\n\n## R\n\n- **R² (coefficient of determination)** — Fraction of the dependent variable's variation explained by a regression, ESS/TSS; in a single-variable regression it equals the squared correlation between fitted and actual values. (Book 2, Ch 7)\n- **Random walk** — Nonstationary process $$y_t = y_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$$ whose variance grows with time; first-differencing produces a stationary series. (Book 2, Ch 11)\n- **RAROC (risk-adjusted return on capital)** — Performance measure used for capital allocation: $$\\text{RAROC} = \\dfrac{\\text{revenues} - \\text{costs} - \\text{expected losses}}{\\text{economic capital}}$$, compared against a hurdle rate. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Recovery rate** — Fraction of exposure recovered after default (typically measured from post-default bond prices); equals 1 − LGD and tends to fall when default rates spike. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Regression (linear)** — Modeling a dependent variable as a linear function of explanatory variables plus an error term, with coefficients estimated by ordinary least squares (OLS). (Book 2, Ch 7–8)\n- **Regularization** — Penalizing model complexity to reduce overfitting: ridge adds an L2 penalty that shrinks coefficients, lasso adds an L1 penalty that can zero them out, and elastic net combines both. (Book 2, Ch 14)\n- **Repurchase agreement (repo)** — Sale of securities with agreement to repurchase at a higher price; economically a collateralized loan whose implied rate is the repo rate. (Book 3, Ch 5)\n- **Reverse stress testing** — Starting from a defined failure outcome (e.g., insolvency or breach of capital) and working backward to identify scenarios that could cause it; complements conventional stress tests. (Book 4, Ch 8)\n- **Rho** — Sensitivity of an option's value to the risk-free interest rate; positive for calls, negative for puts. (Book 4, Ch 16)\n- **Ridge regression** — See Regularization; the L2-penalized regression that shrinks all coefficients toward zero without eliminating them. (Book 2, Ch 14)\n- **Risk appetite** — The amount and types of risk a firm is willing to take in pursuit of its objectives, set by the board and cascaded into limits via a risk appetite statement. (Book 1, Ch 2–3)\n- **Risk-free rate** — Return on a default-free investment; derivatives practice has shifted from LIBOR proxies toward overnight rates such as SOFR (with repo and T-bill rates as alternatives). (Book 3, Ch 16)\n- **Risk-neutral valuation** — Pricing derivatives as the expected payoff under the risk-neutral measure (where all assets earn the risk-free rate) discounted at the risk-free rate; valid because the hedged position is riskless, regardless of actual risk preferences. (Book 4, Ch 14)\n\n## S\n\n- **Scenario analysis** — Assessing portfolio outcomes under specified hypothetical or historical scenarios (often joint moves in several risk factors), without necessarily attaching probabilities. (Book 4, Ch 8)\n- **Securitization** — Pooling assets (mortgages, loans, receivables) in a special purpose vehicle and issuing tranched securities against the pool's cash flows; a major credit risk transfer channel central to the 2007–2009 crisis. (Book 1, Ch 4, 10)\n- **Sharpe ratio** — Excess return per unit of total risk: $$\\dfrac{R_p - R_f}{\\sigma_p}$$; appropriate when the portfolio represents an investor's total wealth. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Short selling** — Selling a borrowed security with the obligation to repurchase and return it later, profiting if its price falls. (Book 3, Ch 4)\n- **Single monthly mortality (SMM)** — Monthly proportion of a mortgage pool's beginning-of-month balance (net of scheduled principal) that prepays; annualizes to CPR. (Book 3, Ch 18)\n- **Skewness** — Standardized third moment measuring asymmetry; negative skew (long left tail) is typical of equity returns and credit portfolios. (Book 2, Ch 5)\n- **SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate)** — Overnight U.S. rate based on Treasury repo transactions; the main USD replacement for LIBOR, with term rates built by compounding daily SOFR in arrears. (Book 3, Ch 16)\n- **Sortino ratio** — Excess return (over a minimum acceptable return) divided by downside deviation, penalizing only below-target volatility. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Spot (zero) rate** — Yield on a zero-coupon instrument for a given maturity; the set of spot rates is the zero curve used to discount each cash flow. (Book 3, Ch 16; Book 4, Ch 10)\n- **Stack-and-roll hedge** — Hedging a long-dated exposure with a stack of short-dated futures rolled forward at each expiry; introduces rollover (basis) risk, as in the Metallgesellschaft case. (Book 3, Ch 8; Book 1, Ch 9)\n- **Stationarity (covariance)** — Property of a time series whose mean and variance are constant and whose autocovariances depend only on the lag; a prerequisite for standard time-series modeling (AR/MA/ARMA). (Book 2, Ch 10)\n- **Straddle** — Long call plus long put on the same strike and expiry; a bet on large movement (high realized volatility) in either direction. (Book 3, Ch 14)\n- **Stress testing** — Evaluating losses and capital adequacy under severe but plausible scenarios; complements VaR by probing tail events outside the historical sample, and includes regulatory programs such as CCAR. (Book 4, Ch 8)\n- **Strike (exercise) price** — The price at which an option holder may buy (call) or sell (put) the underlying. (Book 3, Ch 12)\n- **Subadditivity** — Property that the risk of a combined portfolio is no greater than the sum of standalone risks, $$\\rho(A+B) \\le \\rho(A) + \\rho(B)$$; reflects diversification, satisfied by ES but not always by VaR. (Book 4, Ch 1)\n- **Swap** — Agreement to exchange streams of cash flows on set dates according to a formula; principal types in Part I are interest rate swaps and currency swaps. (Book 3, Ch 20)\n- **Systematic risk** — Market-wide risk that cannot be diversified away; the only risk rewarded with a premium in the CAPM. (Book 1, Ch 5)\n\n## T\n\n- **t-statistic** — Estimate minus hypothesized value, divided by the estimate's standard error; compared to the Student's t (or normal) distribution to test hypotheses about means and regression coefficients. (Book 2, Ch 6–7)\n- **Tailing the hedge** — Slightly reducing a futures hedge position to correct for the interest earned on daily mark-to-market cash flows, aligning futures hedging with forward-style exposure. (Book 3, Ch 8)\n- **Term structure theories** — Explanations of yield curve shape: unbiased expectations (forwards equal expected future spot rates), liquidity preference (investors demand a premium for long maturities, biasing the curve upward), and market segmentation (supply and demand within maturity habitats). (Book 3, Ch 16; Book 4, Ch 10)\n- **Theta** — Sensitivity of an option's value to the passage of time (time decay); usually negative for long option positions. (Book 4, Ch 16)\n- **Tracking error** — Standard deviation of the difference between portfolio and benchmark returns; the denominator of the information ratio. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Train/validation/test split** — Partitioning data so the model is fit on the training set, tuned (hyperparameters selected) on the validation set, and evaluated once on the untouched test set to estimate true out-of-sample performance. (Book 2, Ch 14)\n- **Transition matrix** — Table of probabilities that an obligor migrates from one rating to another (or defaults) over a horizon; multi-period transitions are obtained by matrix multiplication under a Markov assumption. (Book 4, Ch 4, 6)\n- **Treynor ratio** — Excess return per unit of systematic risk: $$\\dfrac{R_p - R_f}{\\beta_p}$$; appropriate for a well-diversified portfolio or one held within a larger diversified fund. (Book 1, Ch 7)\n- **Type I error** — Rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive); its probability is the significance level α. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n- **Type II error** — Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (false negative); its probability is β, and power = 1 − β. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n\n## U\n\n- **Uncovered interest rate parity** — Hypothesis that expected exchange rate changes offset interest rate differentials; unlike covered parity it is not enforced by arbitrage, and its failure underlies the carry trade. (Book 3, Ch 9)\n- **Unexpected loss (UL)** — Volatility of credit losses around the expected loss; capital, rather than pricing or provisions, is held against unexpected loss. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Unit root** — A root of one in a series' autoregressive polynomial (as in a random walk), making the series nonstationary with shocks that never decay; detected with augmented Dickey-Fuller tests and removed by differencing. (Book 2, Ch 11)\n\n## V\n\n- **Value at risk (VaR)** — The loss that will not be exceeded with probability X% over a stated horizon — i.e., the X% quantile of the loss distribution; it says nothing about the size of losses beyond that quantile. (Book 4, Ch 1–2)\n- **Variation margin** — Cash transferred daily (or intraday) to settle mark-to-market gains and losses on futures and cleared positions. (Book 3, Ch 6–7)\n- **Vasicek single-factor model** — Credit portfolio model in which each obligor's asset return loads on one systematic factor with common correlation; it yields the worst-case (e.g., 99.9%) default rate used in Basel capital formulas, $$\\text{WCDR} = N\\!\\left(\\dfrac{N^{-1}(\\text{PD}) + \\sqrt{\\rho}\\,N^{-1}(0.999)}{\\sqrt{1-\\rho}}\\right)$$. (Book 4, Ch 6)\n- **Vega** — Sensitivity of an option's value to the underlying's volatility; positive for long calls and puts, and hedgeable only with other options. (Book 4, Ch 16)\n- **Volatility** — Standard deviation of returns, conventionally annualized via the square root of time, e.g., $$\\sigma_{annual} = \\sigma_{daily}\\sqrt{252}$$. (Book 2, Ch 12)\n- **Volatility smile / skew** — Pattern of implied volatility across strikes: a symmetric smile is typical of currency options, while equity options show a downward skew (higher implied vol at low strikes), reflecting fat tails and crash risk relative to lognormal assumptions. (Book 4, Ch 15)\n\n## Y\n\n- **Yield curve** — Plot of interest rates (spot, par, or forward) against maturity; its level, slope, and curvature summarize most of its variation. (Book 4, Ch 10)\n- **Yield to maturity (YTM)** — Single discount rate that equates a bond's present value of cash flows to its price; realized return equals YTM only if the bond is held to maturity and coupons are reinvested at that same rate. (Book 4, Ch 11)\n\n## Z\n\n- **Z-score (standardized value)** — Number of standard deviations an observation lies from the mean, $$z = (x - \\mu)/\\sigma$$; the test statistic for normal-based hypothesis tests. See also Altman Z-score for the credit-scoring usage. (Book 2, Ch 6)\n- **Zero-coupon bond** — Bond paying only face value at maturity, sold at a discount; its yield defines the spot (zero) rate, and its Macaulay duration equals its maturity. (Book 4, Ch 10)","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"906","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Study Plan","notes":"# Study Plan\n\nGARP reports successful candidates average **~240–275 hours** of preparation\nfor Part I. Two schedules are below — pick the one matching your runway, then\nadapt. Both follow the same principles:\n\n- **Order matters.** Books build on each other: Foundations gives context,\n  Quant gives tools, Markets gives instruments, Valuation puts it together.\n- **Questions beat re-reading.** Target at least 40% of total hours on\n  practice questions. Reading feels productive; retrieval is what passes exams.\n- **Weight by exam weight.** Books 3 and 4 are 60% of the exam — give them\n  60% of your practice time.\n- **Never lose calculator fluency.** Use your TI BA II Plus / HP 12C for\n  every single computation from day one, even trivial ones.\n\n---\n\n## 16-week plan (~15 hrs/week ≈ 240 hours)\n\n| Weeks | Focus | Deliverable at week's end |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1–2 | **Book 1: Foundations** (Ch 1–11) | All chapter quizzes done |\n| 3 | Book 1 question bank + review | ≥75% on Book 1 bank |\n| 4–6 | **Book 2: Quantitative Analysis** (Ch 1–15) | All chapter quizzes done |\n| 7 | Book 2 question bank + formula sheet drill | ≥70% on Book 2 bank |\n| 8–10 | **Book 3: Financial Markets & Products** (Ch 1–20) | All chapter quizzes done |\n| 11 | Book 3 question bank + review | ≥70% on Book 3 bank |\n| 12–13 | **Book 4: Valuation & Risk Models** (Ch 1–16) | All chapter quizzes done |\n| 14 | Book 4 question bank + formula sheet drill | ≥70% on Book 4 bank |\n| 15 | **Full mock exam** (timed, 4h) + full review of errors | Error log written |\n| 16 | Weak-topic re-drill, all four formula sheets daily, GARP official practice exam | Confidence |\n\n## 10-week plan (~25 hrs/week ≈ 250 hours)\n\n| Weeks | Focus |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Book 1 complete (notes + quizzes) |\n| 2 | Book 1 bank · start Book 2 |\n| 3–4 | Book 2 complete + bank |\n| 5–6 | Book 3 complete + bank |\n| 7–8 | Book 4 complete + bank |\n| 9 | Full mock (timed) + error review + weak-topic drill |\n| 10 | Formula sheets daily · GARP practice exam · second pass on error log |\n\n---\n\n## The error log (do not skip this)\n\nEvery question you get wrong, log four things:\n\n1. The topic/chapter.\n2. *Why* you got it wrong: concept gap, formula slip, calculator error, or\n   misread question.\n3. The correct reasoning, in one sentence, in your own words.\n4. Tally by chapter — your top-5 chapters by error count are your final-week\n   syllabus.\n\nMost failed attempts come from candidates who did questions but never\nsystematically closed the gaps the questions revealed.\n\n## Final 7 days\n\n- One formula sheet per day, written out from memory, checked against the\n  [Formula Sheets](formula-sheets/).\n- GARP's **official practice exam** (free with registration — see\n  [Resources](resources)) under timed conditions. It is the single best\n  predictor of the real exam's style.\n- Re-do every question in your error log.\n- No new material in the last 48 hours. Sleep.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"907","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Exam-Day Guide","notes":"# Exam Guide\n\nEverything logistical and tactical about sitting FRM Part I.\n\n## Registration and logistics\n\n- Register at [garp.org/frm](https://www.garp.org/frm). First-time candidates\n  pay a one-time enrollment fee plus the exam fee; early registration is\n  meaningfully cheaper than standard. Check current fees on the site.\n- Part I is offered in **May, August, and November** windows at Pearson VUE /\n  PSI computer-based testing centers. Book your seat early — popular centers\n  fill up.\n- You have a multi-year window to pass Part II after passing Part I, and you\n  must subsequently demonstrate two years of relevant work experience to be\n  certified. Details: GARP candidate guide (see [Resources](resources)).\n\n## The format, and what it implies\n\n- **100 MCQs, 4 hours** → 2.4 minutes per question average. Many definition\n  questions take 30 seconds; bank that time for the multi-step calculations.\n- **Four answer choices**, exactly one correct. Distractors are engineered\n  from common errors: sign flips, using the wrong compounding convention,\n  forgetting to annualize, confusing duration with modified duration.\n- **No negative marking.** Answer everything. With one minute left, fill in\n  every blank — an unanswered question is a guaranteed zero.\n- **No formula sheet is provided.** Everything on the\n  [Formula Sheets](formula-sheets/) must live in your head.\n\n## Calculator mastery (TI BA II Plus)\n\nThe TI BA II Plus is the most popular choice. Whatever you use, these\noperations must be automatic before exam day:\n\n| Skill | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| TVM keys (N, I/Y, PV, PMT, FV) | Bond pricing, annuities — used constantly |\n| `2ND` → `P/Y` set to 1 (and *leave it*) | The #1 source of silent wrong answers |\n| `2ND` → `CLR TVM` before every TVM problem | Stale register values are lethal |\n| Cash-flow worksheet (CF, NPV, IRR) | Irregular cash flows |\n| `ICONV` (interest conversion) | Nominal ↔ effective rate switches |\n| `y^x`, `LN`, `e^x` | Continuous compounding appears everywhere in Part I |\n| STAT worksheet (1-V, LIN) | Means, standard deviations, regression slope |\n| Memory stores (STO/RCL 0–9) | Chain calculations without rounding error |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap**  \n> Set decimal display to at least 4 places (`2ND` → `FORMAT` → `DEC = 4` or\n> `9`). Two-decimal rounding mid-calculation will push you onto wrong answer\n> choices that were designed to catch exactly that.\n\n## Question-answering strategy\n\n1. **Three passes.** Pass 1: answer everything that takes <2 minutes; flag the\n   rest. Pass 2: the flagged calculations. Pass 3: sanity-check flags and fill\n   any blanks.\n2. **Read the last sentence first** on long stems — it tells you what is\n   actually being asked, so you know what to extract from the story.\n3. **Watch units and conventions**: annual vs. semiannual, continuous vs.\n   discrete compounding, price vs. rate volatility, one-tailed vs. two-tailed.\n4. **Estimate before computing.** If a bond trades below par and coupons are\n   8%, yield must exceed 8% — kill impossible choices before doing math.\n5. **A \"too easy\" answer in a multi-step problem is usually the trap** — it's\n   the value you get if you stop one step early. Distractors are built that way.\n\n## Exam-day checklist\n\n- Government-issued passport-grade ID matching your registration name exactly.\n- Approved calculator (bring a spare or fresh batteries) — only the models on\n  GARP's list are allowed; the proctor may inspect or clear memory.\n- Arrive 30+ minutes early; budget for security check-in.\n- No scratch paper of your own — the center provides erasable boards/sheets.\n- Plan your time: at the 2-hour mark you should be at or past question 50.\n\n## The night before\n\nNothing new. Formula sheet skim, calculator battery check, route to the test\ncenter, sleep. A rested candidate scoring 70% beats an exhausted one who\ncrammed to 75% — execution errors under fatigue are the real killer.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"},{"num":"908","module":"📌 Reference & Revision","title":"Free Resources","notes":"# Resources\n\nEvery item here is **legitimately free / publicly available**. The notes on\nthis site are original and aligned to GARP's published learning objectives —\nthey are a study companion, not a replacement for the official curriculum.\n\n## Official GARP materials (use all of these)\n\n| Resource | What it is | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| FRM Study Guide | Official list of all 2026 readings per book | [garp.org/landing/frm-study-guide](https://www.garp.org/landing/frm-study-guide) |\n| FRM Learning Objectives | The canonical, testable LO list for every chapter — the exam is written from this | [garp.org/landing/frm-learning-objectives](https://www.garp.org/landing/frm-learning-objectives) |\n| Official Practice Exams | Free with candidate registration, in your GARP candidate portal | [garp.org/frm/study-materials](https://www.garp.org/frm/study-materials) |\n| FRM Candidate Guide | Policies, scheduling, ID rules, calculator policy | [garp.org/frm/program-exams](https://www.garp.org/frm/program-exams) |\n| GARP Code of Conduct | Directly tested in Book 1, Chapter 11 — read the primary document | [garp.org](https://www.garp.org/) (search \"Code of Conduct\") |\n| Official eBooks | The full curriculum books — included with exam registration via GARP's reading platform | GARP candidate portal |\n\n> 📌 **Important**  \n> After you register, download the **Learning Objectives** PDF and keep it\n> beside these notes. Tick off each LO as you master it — the exam cannot ask\n> anything outside that document.\n\n## Quality free supplements\n\n| Resource | What's free |\n|---|---|\n| [Bionic Turtle forum](https://forum.bionicturtle.com/) | Enormous searchable Q&A archive; David Harper's explanations of hard Part I concepts are excellent |\n| [AnalystPrep study notes](https://analystprep.com/study-notes/frm/part-1/) | Free chapter-level study notes for Part I |\n| [300Hours FRM guides](https://300hours.com/frm-study-plan/) | Study planning, exam experience write-ups |\n| YouTube: \"FRM Part 1\" playlists (AnalystPrep, finRGB, Aswini Bajaj) | Full free video lectures per book |\n| John Hull, *Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives* | Not free, but the source most of Books 3–4 derives from; older editions are cheap and fine for concepts |\n\n## Communities\n\n- r/FRM on Reddit — active candidate community, exam debriefs each window.\n- Bionic Turtle forums — best place to get a specific concept question answered.\n\n## A note on copyrighted material\n\nGARP's books and commercial prep providers' notes (Schweser, etc.) are\ncopyrighted. Sites offering \"free\" PDFs of them are pirated, frequently\noutdated (a 2019 Schweser PDF will quietly teach you a retired curriculum),\nand a bad trade. The official eBooks are already included in your exam\nregistration fee.","flashcards":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n","questions":"_This is a reference page — open the **Notes** tab to read it._\n"}],"papers":[{"num":"01","md":"# Mock Exam 1\n**Total: 100 questions · 4 hours · no negative marking**\n\n## Foundations of Risk Management (1 mark)\n\n**Q2.** The risk-free rate is 3%, the expected return on the market portfolio\nis 9%, and a stock has a beta of 1.2. According to the CAPM, the expected\nreturn on the stock is:\nA) 7.2% B) 9.0% C) 10.2% D) 13.8%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$E(R) = 3\\% + 1.2\\,(9\\% - 3\\%) = 3\\% + 7.2\\% = 10.2\\%$$.\nChoice D (13.8%) multiplies beta by the whole market return instead of the\nexcess return.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CAPM expected return\",\"steps\":[\"Risk-free rate: 3%\",\"Add beta × market risk premium\",\"1.2 × (9% − 3%) = 7.2%\",\"E(R) = 3% + 7.2% = 10.2%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The collapse of Barings Bank in 1995 is most directly attributable to:\nA) a failure to segregate trading authority from settlement and back-office functions, allowing losses to be concealed B) flawed value-at-risk models that understated market risk C) a sovereign default that triggered margin calls the bank could not meet D) fraudulent external auditors colluding with senior management\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Nick Leeson both traded\nand controlled settlement/back-office records, so losses on unauthorized\nfutures and options positions could be hidden in error account 88888.\nThe lesson is segregation of duties, not model risk (B).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Barings: key failure\",\"options\":[\"Model risk in pricing\",\"No segregation: trader controlled back office\",\"Sovereign default exposure\",\"Excessive regulatory capital\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Leeson ran both trading and settlement, hiding losses in account 88888.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A fund returned 11% over the past year with a return volatility of\n16%. The risk-free rate was 3%. The fund's Sharpe ratio is:\nA) 0.50 B) 0.69 C) 0.88 D) 1.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$\\text{Sharpe} = (11\\% - 3\\%)/16\\% = 0.50$$. Choice B (0.69) forgets to\nsubtract the risk-free rate.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Sharpe ratio\",\"steps\":[\"Excess return = 11% − 3% = 8%\",\"Divide by total volatility σ = 16%\",\"Sharpe = 8 / 16 = 0.50\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Relative to managing each risk type in a separate silo, the primary\nbenefit of an enterprise risk management (ERM) framework is that it:\nA) guarantees that total losses never exceed the firm's risk appetite B) eliminates the need for dedicated market and credit risk functions C) maximizes each business unit's standalone risk-adjusted return D) provides an integrated, firm-wide view of risk that captures interactions and diversification across risk types\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** ERM's defining\nbenefit is the integrated, top-of-the-house view that captures\ncross-risk interactions and diversification. It does not guarantee\nlosses stay within appetite (A) or replace specialist functions (B).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Defining benefit of ERM\",\"options\":[\"Guarantees losses within appetite\",\"Replaces specialist risk units\",\"Integrated top-of-house view of all risks\",\"Eliminates the need for capital\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"ERM captures cross-risk interactions and diversification firmwide.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which lesson is most directly illustrated by the 1998 failure of\nLong-Term Capital Management (LTCM)?\nA) Unauthorized trading flourishes when one person controls both the front and back office B) Securitization weakens originators' incentives to screen borrowers C) Deposit insurance encourages banks to take excessive credit risk D) Highly leveraged convergence strategies are exposed to funding-liquidity spirals when correlations rise sharply in stressed markets\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** LTCM's leveraged\nconvergence trades unwound when the 1998 Russia crisis drove correlated\nspread widening; margin calls forced liquidation into falling markets —\nthe textbook funding-liquidity spiral. A is Barings; B is the 2007\ncrisis.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"LTCM funding-liquidity spiral\",\"steps\":[\"Leveraged convergence trades\",\"1998 Russia default → correlated spread widening\",\"Margin calls demand cash\",\"Forced liquidation into falling markets\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The covariance between a stock's returns and the market's returns is\n0.0288, and the variance of market returns is 0.0256. The stock's beta is\nclosest to:\nA) 0.889 B) 1.000 C) 1.125 D) 1.267\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\beta = \\frac{\\text{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\sigma_m^2} = \\frac{0.0288}{0.0256} = 1.125$$.\nChoice A (0.889) inverts the ratio.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Beta\",\"root\":\"β = Cov(Ri,Rm) / Var(Rm)\",\"children\":[\"Cov = 0.0288 (numerator)\",\"σ²m = 0.0256 (denominator)\",\"β = 1.125\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The risk-free rate is 3% and the expected market return is 10%. A\nstock has a beta of 0.8, and an analyst forecasts its return at 9.0%. Relative\nto the security market line (SML), the stock is:\nA) overvalued and plots above the SML B) overvalued and plots below the SML C) undervalued and plots above the SML D) undervalued and plots below the SML\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** CAPM-required return\n$$= 3\\% + 0.8(10\\% - 3\\%) = 8.6\\%$$. The 9.0% forecast exceeds it, so\nthe stock offers excess return: it is undervalued and plots **above**\nthe SML. (Above the line always means undervalued — pairing \"above\"\nwith \"overvalued\" is the classic trap in A.)\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Position vs the SML\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Above SML\",\"items\":[\"Forecast > required\",\"Positive alpha\",\"Undervalued (this stock)\"]},{\"h\":\"Below SML\",\"items\":[\"Forecast < required\",\"Negative alpha\",\"Overvalued\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A candidate has passed FRM Part I but has not yet passed Part II or\nsatisfied the work-experience requirement. Under the GARP Code of Conduct,\nthe candidate:\nA) may use the letters \"FRM\" after her name provided she discloses her progress B) may describe herself as a \"Certified FRM (Part I)\" C) may not mention the FRM program in any professional context D) may state factually that she has passed FRM Part I, but may not use the FRM designation\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Candidates may state\nfacts (\"passed FRM Part I\") but may not use the FRM designation or any\nvariant of it until certified. There is no \"partial\" designation (B).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Using the FRM credential\",\"options\":[\"Use FRM after passing Part I\",\"Claim a partial designation\",\"State facts: passed FRM Part I\",\"Use FRM once registered\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"May state factual exam progress, but cannot use the designation until certified.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A portfolio returned 12% with a beta of 0.9. The risk-free rate was 2%\nand the market returned 10%. Jensen's alpha for the portfolio is:\nA) 1.0% B) 2.0% C) 2.8% D) 3.8%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\alpha = 12\\% - \\left[2\\% + 0.9\\,(10\\% - 2\\%)\\right] = 12\\% - 9.2\\% = 2.8\\%$$.\nChoice B (2.0%) is the raw excess over the market, ignoring beta.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Jensen's alpha\",\"steps\":[\"CAPM required = 2% + 0.9(10% − 2%) = 9.2%\",\"Alpha = actual − required\",\"= 12% − 9.2%\",\"α = 2.8%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Metallgesellschaft's 1993 near-collapse arose because the firm hedged\nlong-term fixed-price fuel supply contracts with a stack-and-roll position in\nshort-dated futures. The episode is the classic illustration of:\nA) basis risk being eliminated by rolling futures positions B) credit risk on long-term customer contracts C) model risk in option-pricing formulas D) funding liquidity risk — a hedge that is sound in the long run can generate immediate margin outflows that the firm cannot finance\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The\nstack-and-roll hedge was arguably sound at maturity, but daily\nmark-to-market losses on the futures demanded cash *now* while the\noffsetting gains on customer contracts were unrealized — funding\nliquidity risk. A is false (rolling *creates* basis risk).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Metallgesellschaft mismatch\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Futures hedge\",\"items\":[\"Daily mark-to-market\",\"Losses demand cash NOW\"]},{\"h\":\"Customer contracts\",\"items\":[\"Offsetting gains\",\"Unrealized until delivery\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which statement best distinguishes risk appetite from risk capacity?\nA) Risk appetite is the level of risk a firm is willing to take in pursuit of its objectives; risk capacity is the maximum risk the firm is able to absorb B) Risk appetite is set by regulators; risk capacity is set by the board C) Risk appetite always exceeds risk capacity in a well-run firm D) The two terms are interchangeable in modern ERM frameworks\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Appetite is what\nthe firm is *willing* to take; capacity is what it is *able* to absorb\n(capital, liquidity, systems). A well-run firm sets appetite at or below\ncapacity — the reverse of C.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Appetite vs Capacity\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Risk appetite\",\"items\":[\"What firm is WILLING to take\",\"A chosen target\"]},{\"h\":\"Risk capacity\",\"items\":[\"What firm is ABLE to absorb\",\"Capital, liquidity, systems\",\"Set appetite ≤ capacity\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A fund returned 10% against a minimum acceptable return of 4%. The\nstandard deviation of returns was 12% and the downside deviation (relative to\nthe MAR) was 8%. The Sortino ratio is:\nA) 0.50 B) 0.75 C) 0.83 D) 1.25\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\text{Sortino} = \\frac{R - MAR}{\\text{downside deviation}} = \\frac{10\\% - 4\\%}{8\\%} = 0.75$$\nChoice A (0.50) divides by total volatility — that is the Sharpe-style\nerror the Sortino ratio exists to avoid.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sortino vs Sharpe\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sortino\",\"items\":[\"Excess over MAR\",\"÷ downside deviation\",\"(10−4)/8 = 0.75\"]},{\"h\":\"Sharpe\",\"items\":[\"Excess over rf\",\"÷ total volatility\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which statement correctly contrasts the capital market line (CML) with\nthe security market line (SML)?\nA) The CML uses beta; the SML uses total risk B) Both lines apply only to efficient portfolios C) The SML applies only to portfolios that contain no idiosyncratic risk D) The CML applies only to efficient portfolios and uses total risk (standard deviation); the SML applies to all assets and uses systematic risk (beta)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The CML prices only efficient\n(fully diversified) portfolios and uses total risk $$\\sigma$$; the SML\nprices *any* asset using beta, because only systematic risk is rewarded.\nA swaps the two.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CML vs SML\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"CML\",\"items\":[\"Efficient portfolios only\",\"x-axis: total risk σ\",\"Diversified holdings\"]},{\"h\":\"SML\",\"items\":[\"Any asset\",\"x-axis: beta\",\"Only systematic risk priced\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which of the following is NOT a valid rationale for hedging at the\ncorporate (firm) level?\nA) Hedging reduces the expected costs of financial distress B) Hedging can lower expected taxes when the tax schedule is convex C) Hedging stabilizes internal cash flow, supporting planned investment D) Hedging adds value because shareholders are unable to diversify firm-specific risk on their own\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Shareholders\n*can* diversify firm-specific risk cheaply on their own, so D is not a\nvalid rationale — that is exactly the Modigliani-Miller objection.\nDistress costs (A), convex taxes (B), and protecting investment plans\n(C) are the standard valid reasons.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"NOT a valid hedging rationale\",\"options\":[\"Reduce distress costs\",\"Smooth convex taxes\",\"Protect investment plans\",\"Diversify firm-specific risk for holders\"],\"correct\":3,\"why\":\"Shareholders can diversify cheaply themselves (Modigliani-Miller).\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** During the 2007–2009 financial crisis, the originate-to-distribute\nmodel of mortgage lending contributed to losses primarily because it:\nA) concentrated all mortgage credit risk on bank balance sheets B) prevented banks from securitizing loans C) forced originators to retain the equity tranche of every securitization D) weakened originators' incentives to screen and monitor borrowers, since credit risk was transferred to investors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Under\noriginate-to-distribute, credit risk left the originator's balance\nsheet, gutting incentives to screen and monitor borrowers — the moral\nhazard at the heart of the subprime collapse. A states the opposite.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Originate-to-distribute moral hazard\",\"steps\":[\"Originator sells loans on\",\"Credit risk leaves the balance sheet\",\"Weak incentive to screen/monitor\",\"Lax underwriting → subprime collapse\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A portfolio manager generated an active return of 2% over her\nbenchmark with a tracking error of 4%. Her information ratio is:\nA) 0.25 B) 0.50 C) 2.00 D) 8.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$IR = \\frac{\\text{active return}}{\\text{tracking error}} = \\frac{2\\%}{4\\%} = 0.50$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Information ratio\",\"root\":\"IR = active return / tracking error\",\"children\":[\"Active return = 2% (vs benchmark)\",\"Tracking error = 4% (active risk)\",\"IR = 0.50\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** The Treynor ratio is the most appropriate performance measure when\nevaluating:\nA) a portfolio that constitutes an investor's entire wealth B) a fund's total risk-adjusted performance including idiosyncratic risk C) performance relative to a peer-group median D) a portfolio that is one of many components of an investor's broadly diversified holdings, so only systematic risk matters\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Treynor divides excess return\nby beta, so it is the right measure when the portfolio is a small slice\nof a diversified whole and idiosyncratic risk washes out. For total\nwealth (A) use Sharpe.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Treynor vs Sharpe\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Treynor (÷ beta)\",\"items\":[\"Portfolio is one slice\",\"Idiosyncratic risk diversified away\",\"Systematic risk matters\"]},{\"h\":\"Sharpe (÷ σ)\",\"items\":[\"Portfolio is total wealth\",\"All risk matters\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Best practice in risk governance requires that the chief risk officer\n(CRO):\nA) have independent stature — reporting to the CEO and/or board risk committee — with compensation not tied to the revenues of risk-taking desks B) report to the head of trading to stay close to the risks C) be compensated primarily on the trading profits of the business lines D) focus exclusively on regulatory compliance\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The CRO needs independence:\ndirect access to the CEO/board risk committee and pay decoupled from the\nP&L of the desks being policed. B and C recreate the conflicts that\nsank Barings-style controls.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Sound CRO governance\",\"options\":[\"CRO reports to head of trading\",\"CRO pay tied to desk P&L\",\"Direct access to board, pay decoupled from desks\",\"CRO also runs front office\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Independence requires board access and pay decoupled from policed P&L.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which statement about the arbitrage pricing theory (APT) is correct?\nA) It requires all investors to hold the market portfolio B) It permits only one source of systematic risk C) It requires that investors have quadratic utility D) It derives expected returns from the absence of arbitrage in a multifactor model and does not require identifying the market portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** APT rests on no-arbitrage in a\nmultifactor return model; it needs neither the market portfolio nor the\nstrong investor assumptions of the CAPM (A, C), and it allows many\nfactors (B).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"APT vs CAPM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"APT\",\"items\":[\"No-arbitrage basis\",\"Many factors allowed\",\"No market portfolio needed\"]},{\"h\":\"CAPM\",\"items\":[\"Single factor (market)\",\"Needs market portfolio\",\"Strong investor assumptions\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Under the GARP Code of Conduct, a member who faces an actual or\npotential conflict of interest with a client must:\nA) resign from the engagement immediately in all cases B) proceed, provided the conflict benefits the client C) make full and fair disclosure of the conflict to the affected parties D) report the conflict to GARP within 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The Code requires full\nand fair disclosure of actual or potential conflicts to affected\nparties; it does not mandate automatic resignation (A) or reporting to\nGARP (D).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Handling a conflict of interest\",\"options\":[\"Resign immediately\",\"Ignore if immaterial\",\"Full, fair disclosure to affected parties\",\"Report it to GARP\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"The Code requires disclosing actual/potential conflicts, not resignation or GARP reporting.\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## Quantitative Analysis (1 mark)\n\n**Q3.** An analyst models a portfolio's one-year return with the following\ndistribution: −10% with probability 0.20, +5% with probability 0.50, and +20%\nwith probability 0.30. The standard deviation of the return is closest to:\nA) 6.50% B) 10.50% C) 12.35% D) 14.25%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Mean\n$$= 0.2(-10) + 0.5(5) + 0.3(20) = 6.5\\%$$. Second moment\n$$E[R^2] = 0.2(100) + 0.5(25) + 0.3(400) = 152.5$$, so variance\n$$= 152.5 - 6.5^2 = 110.25$$ and $$\\sigma = \\sqrt{110.25} = 10.5\\%$$.\nChoice C ($$\\sqrt{152.5} = 12.35$$) forgets to subtract the squared mean;\nA is the mean itself.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Variance from moments\",\"steps\":[\"Mean μ = Σ p·R = 6.5%\",\"E[R²] = Σ p·R² = 152.5\",\"Variance = E[R²] − μ² = 110.25\",\"σ = √110.25 = 10.5%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Ten percent of fund managers are genuinely skilled. A skilled manager\nbeats her benchmark in any year with probability 0.70; an unskilled manager\nbeats it with probability 0.50. A randomly chosen manager beat the benchmark\nlast year. The probability that she is skilled is closest to:\nA) 7.0% B) 10.0% C) 13.5% D) 70.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$P(S \\mid B) = \\frac{0.10 \\times 0.70}{0.10 \\times 0.70 + 0.90 \\times 0.50} = \\frac{0.07}{0.52} = 13.5\\%$$.\nChoice B (10%) ignores the evidence (the prior); D confuses\n$$P(B \\mid S)$$ with $$P(S \\mid B)$$ — the inverse-probability error.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bayes update\",\"steps\":[\"Prior P(S) = 10%\",\"Likelihood of signal given S vs not-S\",\"Posterior = joint / total evidence\",\"P(S|B) = 0.07 / 0.52 = 13.5%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A stationary AR(1) process is estimated as\n$$y_t = 2.0 + 0.6\\,y_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$$. The long-run (unconditional)\nmean of the process is:\nA) 2.00 B) 3.33 C) 5.00 D) 6.80\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Long-run mean\n$$= \\delta/(1 - \\phi) = 2.0/(1 - 0.6) = 5.0$$. Choice B (3.33) divides by\n$$\\phi$$ instead of $$1 - \\phi$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"AR(1) long-run mean\",\"root\":\"μ = δ / (1 − φ)\",\"children\":[\"δ = 2.0 (intercept)\",\"φ = 0.6 (persistence)\",\"μ = 2.0 / 0.4 = 5.0\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A risk manager updates daily volatility with an EWMA model using\n$$\\lambda = 0.94$$. Yesterday's volatility estimate was 1.5% and yesterday's\nreturn was 2.0%. The updated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 1.50% B) 1.53% C) 1.60% D) 1.97%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.94(0.015)^2 + 0.06(0.020)^2 = 0.0002115 + 0.0000240 = 0.0002355$$\n$$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.0002355} = 1.53\\%$$\nChoice D (1.97%) averages the volatilities instead of the variances —\nalways update in variance space.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"EWMA variance update\",\"steps\":[\"Work in variance, not volatility\",\"σ²ₙ = λ·σ²ₙ₋₁ + (1−λ)·u²ₙ₋₁\",\"= 0.94(0.015²) + 0.06(0.020²)\",\"σₙ = √0.0002355 = 1.53%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A portfolio contains 10 independent loans, each with a one-year\ndefault probability of 10%. The probability that exactly two loans default\nwithin one year is closest to:\nA) 0.4% B) 19.4% C) 38.7% D) 93.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P(X = 2) = \\binom{10}{2}(0.1)^2(0.9)^8 = 45 \\times 0.01 \\times 0.4305 = 19.4\\%$$\nChoice A omits the $$\\binom{10}{2} = 45$$ combinations factor.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Binomial P(X=2)\",\"steps\":[\"Combinations: C(10,2) = 45\",\"Successes: p² = 0.1²\",\"Failures: (1−p)⁸ = 0.9⁸\",\"45 × 0.01 × 0.4305 = 19.4%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A researcher lowers the significance level of a hypothesis test from\n5% to 1%, holding the sample fixed. As a result:\nA) the probability of a Type I error falls and the probability of a Type II error rises B) the probabilities of Type I and Type II errors both fall C) the probability of a Type I error rises and the probability of a Type II error falls D) the power of the test increases\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Cutting\n$$\\alpha$$ from 5% to 1% directly lowers the Type I error rate, but with\na fixed sample the rejection region shrinks, so the Type II error rate\nrises (and power falls — D states the opposite).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Lowering α (fixed n)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Type I (α)\",\"items\":[\"5% → 1%\",\"Falls\",\"Reject true H0 less\"]},{\"h\":\"Type II (β)\",\"items\":[\"Rises\",\"Power = 1−β falls\",\"Miss false H0 more\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A stock's daily return volatility is 1.2%. Assuming 252 trading days\nand i.i.d. returns, its annualized volatility is closest to:\nA) 4.16% B) 19.05% C) 22.20% D) 302.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_{ann} = 1.2\\% \\times \\sqrt{252} = 1.2\\% \\times 15.87 = 19.05\\%$$.\nChoice D multiplies by 252 (the variance scaling applied to volatility);\nA divides by the square root.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Scaling with time\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Variance\",\"items\":[\"Scales with t\",\"×252\"]},{\"h\":\"Volatility\",\"items\":[\"Scales with √t\",\"×√252 = 15.87\",\"1.2% → 19.05%\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A linear regression of a stock's returns on the market's returns\nproduces $$R^2 = 0.36$$. Which interpretation is correct?\nA) The slope coefficient equals 0.36 B) 60% of the variation in the stock's returns is explained by the market C) 36% of the variation in the stock's returns is explained by the market, and the correlation between the two return series is 0.60 D) The regression residuals have a standard deviation of 36%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** In a single-variable regression,\n$$R^2$$ is the explained share of variance (36%) and equals the squared\ncorrelation, so $$\\rho = \\sqrt{0.36} = 0.60$$. B confuses explained with\nunexplained variation.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"R² in single-variable regression\",\"root\":\"R² = 0.36 = explained share\",\"children\":[\"= squared correlation ρ²\",\"ρ = √0.36 = 0.60\",\"Unexplained share = 0.64\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A portfolio's annual return is normally distributed with a mean of 8%\nand a standard deviation of 12%. Given $$N(0.67) = 0.7486$$, the probability\nthat the portfolio loses money over one year is closest to:\nA) 12.6% B) 25.1% C) 33.0% D) 74.9%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P(R < 0) = P\\!\\left(Z < \\frac{0 - 8}{12}\\right) = P(Z < -0.67) = 1 - 0.7486 = 25.1\\%$$\nChoice D (74.9%) is the complement error — forgetting to take\n$$1 - N(0.67)$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"P(R<0) via z-score\",\"steps\":[\"z = (0 − 8) / 12 = −0.67\",\"P(Z < −0.67) = 1 − N(0.67)\",\"= 1 − 0.7486\",\"= 25.1%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An analyst tests $$H_0: \\mu = 4\\%$$ against a two-sided alternative.\nThe sample mean is 5.2%, the sample standard deviation is 4%, and the sample\nsize is 64 (large enough to use the normal distribution). With critical values\nof 1.96 (5%) and 2.576 (1%), the analyst should:\nA) reject the null at both the 5% and 1% levels B) fail to reject the null at both levels C) reject the null at the 1% level but not the 5% level D) reject the null at the 5% level but not the 1% level\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** $$t = \\frac{5.2 - 4.0}{4/\\sqrt{64}} = \\frac{1.2}{0.5} = 2.4$$\nSince $$1.96 < 2.4 < 2.576$$: reject at 5%, fail to reject at 1%.\nWatch the standard-error step — dividing by $$\\sigma$$ instead of\n$$\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$$ gives \"fail to reject\" everywhere (B).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Test statistic t = 2.4\",\"items\":[\"1.96 (5% crit)\",\"|t = 2.4\",\"2.576 (1% crit)\"],\"left\":\"reject at 5%\",\"right\":\"fail to reject at 1%\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A sample of 25 monthly returns has a mean of 6%. The population\nstandard deviation is known to be 10%. The 95% confidence interval for the\npopulation mean is closest to:\nA) [0.85%, 11.15%] B) [2.08%, 9.92%] C) [2.71%, 9.29%] D) [5.22%, 6.78%]\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Known\n$$\\sigma$$, so use z:\n$$6\\% \\pm 1.96 \\times \\frac{10\\%}{\\sqrt{25}} = 6\\% \\pm 3.92\\% = [2.08\\%,\\ 9.92\\%]$$\nChoice C uses $$z = 1.645$$ (a 90% interval); A uses 2.576 (99%) —\nwrong-z errors either way.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Two-tailed z by confidence\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Confidence\",\"items\":[\"90%\",\"95%\",\"99%\"]},{\"h\":\"z\",\"items\":[\"1.645\",\"1.960\",\"2.576\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Operational risk events at a branch follow a Poisson distribution with\na mean of 2 events per year. The probability of observing zero events next\nyear is closest to:\nA) 0.0% B) 13.5% C) 27.1% D) 86.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P(X = 0) = e^{-\\lambda} = e^{-2} = 13.5\\%$$.\nChoice C (27.1%) is $$P(X = 1) = 2e^{-2}$$ — one step off.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Poisson P(X=0)\",\"steps\":[\"P(X=k) = e^(−λ)·λ^k / k!\",\"Set k = 0: λ⁰ = 1, 0! = 1\",\"P(X=0) = e^(−λ) = e^(−2)\",\"= 13.5%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A regression slope coefficient is estimated at 0.85 with a standard\nerror of 0.34 in a large sample. Testing whether the slope differs from zero\n(critical values 1.96 at 5%, 2.576 at 1%), the coefficient is:\nA) statistically significant at the 5% level but not at the 1% level B) statistically significant at both the 5% and 1% levels C) not statistically significant at the 5% level D) impossible to assess without the $$R^2$$ of the regression\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$t = 0.85/0.34 = 2.5$$; since $$1.96 < 2.5 < 2.576$$, significant at 5%\nbut not at 1%. Significance needs only the coefficient and its standard\nerror, not $$R^2$$ (D).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Coefficient t = 0.85/0.34 = 2.5\",\"items\":[\"1.96 (5%)\",\"|t = 2.5\",\"2.576 (1%)\"],\"left\":\"significant at 5%\",\"right\":\"not at 1%\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The AR(1) process $$y_t = \\delta + \\phi\\,y_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$$ is\ncovariance stationary if and only if:\nA) $$\\delta = 0$$ B) $$\\phi = 1$$ C) $$\\phi \\geq 0$$ D) $$\\lvert\\phi\\rvert < 1$$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** An AR(1) is covariance\nstationary iff $$\\lvert\\phi\\rvert < 1$$. With $$\\phi = 1$$ (B) the\nprocess is a random walk — the unit-root case.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"AR(1) coefficient φ\",\"items\":[\"|φ| < 1 (stationary)\",\"|φ = 1 (random walk)\",\"|φ| > 1 (explosive)\"],\"left\":\"covariance stationary\",\"right\":\"non-stationary\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Two asset returns have a covariance of 0.0018. Their standard\ndeviations are 5% and 6%. The correlation between the returns is:\nA) 0.36 B) 0.60 C) 0.72 D) 1.67\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\rho = \\frac{0.0018}{0.05 \\times 0.06} = \\frac{0.0018}{0.0030} = 0.60$$.\nChoice D (1.67) inverts the ratio — impossible, since\n$$\\lvert\\rho\\rvert \\le 1$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Correlation\",\"root\":\"ρ = Cov / (σX·σY)\",\"children\":[\"Cov = 0.0018\",\"σX·σY = 0.05×0.06 = 0.0030\",\"ρ = 0.60 (must be ≤ 1)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A Monte Carlo simulation with $$n$$ trials produces an estimate whose\nstandard error is too large. To cut the standard error in half, the number of\ntrials must be multiplied by approximately:\nA) 2 B) 4 C) 8 D) 16\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The standard\nerror scales as $$1/\\sqrt{n}$$, so halving it requires\n$$4\\times$$ the trials. Choice A treats the error as $$1/n$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Monte Carlo standard error\",\"steps\":[\"SE ∝ 1/√n\",\"Halve SE → need √n to double\",\"So n must be 4×\",\"Quadruple the trials\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Compared with parametric Monte Carlo simulation, the bootstrap\napproach to simulating returns:\nA) requires estimating the mean and covariance matrix of returns B) assumes returns are normally distributed C) generates scenarios that never occurred in the historical sample D) resamples observed historical data with replacement and therefore avoids specifying a parametric distribution\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The bootstrap resamples\nthe observed data with replacement, so no parametric distribution (or\ncovariance matrix) need be specified — A and B describe parametric\nMonte Carlo. Its limitation is the mirror of C: it cannot generate\noutcomes outside the historical sample.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap vs parametric MC\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bootstrap\",\"items\":[\"Resamples real data\",\"No distribution assumed\",\"Cannot exceed sample range\"]},{\"h\":\"Parametric MC\",\"items\":[\"Assumes distribution\",\"Needs covariance matrix\",\"Can generate new tails\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Compared with ridge regression, the LASSO is distinctive in that it:\nA) can shrink some coefficient estimates exactly to zero, performing variable selection B) cannot be used when predictors are correlated C) penalizes the sum of squared coefficients D) always produces a lower out-of-sample error\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The LASSO's L1 penalty can\nset coefficients exactly to zero, performing variable selection; ridge's\nL2 penalty (described by C) only shrinks them toward zero.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"LASSO vs Ridge\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"LASSO (L1)\",\"items\":[\"Can set coeffs to 0\",\"Performs variable selection\"]},{\"h\":\"Ridge (L2)\",\"items\":[\"Shrinks toward 0\",\"Keeps all variables\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** For events A and B, $$P(A) = 0.4$$, $$P(B) = 0.5$$, and\n$$P(A \\cap B) = 0.2$$. Which statement is correct?\nA) A and B are mutually exclusive B) \\neq 0$$ C) A and B are dependent, because $$P(A \\cap D) Independence cannot be assessed from the information given\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$P(A)P(B) = 0.4 \\times 0.5 = 0.20 = P(A \\cap B)$$, equivalently\n$$P(A \\mid B) = 0.2/0.5 = 0.4 = P(A)$$: independent. Choice C confuses\ndependence with overlap; mutually exclusive (A) would require\n$$P(A \\cap B) = 0$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Test for independence\",\"steps\":[\"Check if P(A∩B) = P(A)·P(B)\",\"0.4 × 0.5 = 0.20\",\"Given P(A∩B) = 0.20 ✓\",\"Independent (not mutually exclusive)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A return series has a sample kurtosis of 4.8. Relative to a normal\ndistribution, the series:\nA) has fatter tails, so extreme returns are more likely than under normality B) has thinner tails than the normal distribution C) must be positively skewed D) has the same tail behavior as the normal distribution\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Kurtosis of 4.8 > 3 means excess\nkurtosis of 1.8: fatter tails than the normal, so extreme outcomes are\nmore likely. Kurtosis says nothing about skew (C).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Interpreting kurtosis\",\"steps\":[\"Normal kurtosis = 3\",\"Here 4.8 → excess = 1.8\",\"Leptokurtic: fatter tails\",\"Extreme outcomes more likely (says nothing on skew)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## Financial Markets and Products (1 mark)\n\n**Q1.** The price of a non-dividend-paying stock is $50 and the continuously\ncompounded risk-free rate is 4% per year. The six-month forward price of the\nstock is closest to:\nA) $49.01 B) $50.00 C) $51.01 D) $52.04\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** No income, so\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{rT} = 50\\,e^{0.04 \\times 0.5} = 50\\,e^{0.02} = \\$51.01$$.\nChoice A ($49.01) is the sign-flip error — discounting instead of\ncompounding; D uses a full year instead of six months.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"No-income forward price\",\"steps\":[\"Start with spot S0 = 50\",\"Grow at risk-free rate over T: e^(rT)\",\"F0 = 50 e^(0.04×0.5) = 51.01\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A trader is long two wheat futures contracts (5,000 bushels each) at\n$8.00 per bushel. The initial margin is $4,000 per contract and the\nmaintenance margin is $3,000 per contract. Ignoring interest on the margin\naccount, a margin call is first triggered if the futures price falls below:\nA) $7.20 B) $7.40 C) $7.60 D) $7.80\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Equity starts at $4,000 per\ncontract; a call comes when it falls below the $3,000 maintenance level,\ni.e., after a loss of more than $1,000 per contract. Per bushel:\n$$1{,}000 / 5{,}000 = \\$0.20$$, so the trigger is $$8.00 - 0.20 = \\$7.80$$.\nChoice A ($7.20) wrongly lets equity fall all the way to zero. The two\ncontracts scale both the loss and the margin, so they cancel.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Margin account ($ per contract)\",\"items\":[\"0\",\"|3,000 maintenance\",\"4,000 initial\"],\"left\":\"margin call\",\"right\":\"safe\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A six-month European call with strike $50 on a non-dividend-paying\nstock trades at $4.50. The stock price is $50 and the continuously compounded\nrisk-free rate is 4%. By put-call parity, the price of the six-month European\nput with the same strike is closest to:\nA) $0.99 B) $3.51 C) $4.50 D) $5.49\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$p = c - S_0 + K e^{-rT} = 4.50 - 50 + 50\\,e^{-0.02} = 4.50 - 50 + 49.01 = \\$3.51$$.\nChoice D ($5.49) adds rather than subtracts the $0.99 financing term —\na sign flip; A is the financing term alone.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Put-call parity for p\",\"steps\":[\"p = c − S0 + K·e^(−rT)\",\"= 4.50 − 50 + 49.01\",\"Financing term K·e^(−rT) is subtracted from S0\",\"p = 3.51\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The spot exchange rate is 1.1000 USD per EUR. The continuously\ncompounded one-year interest rates are 5% in USD and 3% in EUR. The one-year\nforward exchange rate (USD per EUR) implied by covered interest parity is\nclosest to:\nA) 1.0782 B) 1.1000 C) 1.1222 D) 1.1449\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r_{USD} - r_{EUR})T} = 1.1000\\,e^{(0.05 - 0.03)(1)} = 1.1000\\,e^{0.02} = 1.1222$$.\nThe currency with the higher interest rate (USD) depreciates forward,\nso the USD-per-EUR forward rate rises above spot. Choice A (1.0782)\nflips the sign of the rate differential.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Covered interest parity\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Higher-rate ccy (USD)\",\"items\":[\"r = 5%\",\"Depreciates forward\",\"Trades at fwd discount\"]},{\"h\":\"Lower-rate ccy (EUR)\",\"items\":[\"r = 3%\",\"Appreciates forward\",\"Trades at fwd premium\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A trader is long one three-month SOFR futures contract purchased at a\nquote of 95.50. The quote falls to 95.26. Each basis-point change in the quote\nchanges the contract value by $25. The trader has:\nA) gained $600 B) lost $240 C) lost $600 D) lost $2,400\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** The quote fell\n$$95.50 - 95.26 = 24$$ basis points; a long position loses when the quote\nfalls: $$24 \\times \\$25 = \\$600$$ lost. Choice A flips the sign (rates\nrose, so the long *loses*); D treats the move as 96 ticks.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"SOFR futures (price = 100 − rate)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Quote falls\",\"items\":[\"Rates rise\",\"Long position loses\",\"Here: −24 bp × $25 = −$600\"]},{\"h\":\"Quote rises\",\"items\":[\"Rates fall\",\"Long position gains\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An airline must purchase 2 million gallons of jet fuel in one month\nand hedges with heating oil futures (42,000 gallons per contract). The\nstandard deviation of monthly changes in the jet fuel spot price is $0.0263\nper gallon, the standard deviation of monthly changes in the heating oil\nfutures price is $0.0313 per gallon, and the correlation between the two is\n0.8. The minimum-variance hedge requires a long position closest to:\nA) 32 contracts B) 40 contracts C) 45 contracts D) 48 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$h^* = \\rho\\,\\frac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F} = 0.8 \\times \\frac{0.0263}{0.0313} = 0.6722$$\n$$N = \\frac{0.6722 \\times 2{,}000{,}000}{42{,}000} = 32.0 \\text{ contracts}$$\nChoice D (48) hedges gallon-for-gallon, ignoring the hedge ratio.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Minimum-variance hedge\",\"steps\":[\"h* = ρ·(σS/σF) = 0.6722\",\"Scale by exposure / contract size\",\"N = h*·2,000,000 / 42,000\",\"N ≈ 32 contracts\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A trader buys a call with strike $50 for $5 and sells a call with\nstrike $55 (same expiry, same underlying) for $2. The maximum possible profit\non the position is:\nA) $2 B) $3 C) $5 D) unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** This is a bull call spread\ncosting $$5 - 2 = \\$3$$. Maximum payoff is the strike gap\n$$55 - 50 = \\$5$$, so maximum profit is $$5 - 3 = \\$2$$. Choice B is the\ncost, C the gap; D ignores the short call cap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bull call spread max profit\",\"steps\":[\"Net cost = 5 − 2 = 3 (buy low, sell high strike)\",\"Payoff capped at strike gap = 55 − 50 = 5\",\"Max profit = gap − cost\",\"= 5 − 3 = 2\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Firm A can borrow fixed at 5.0% or floating at SOFR + 0.5%. Firm B can\nborrow fixed at 6.4% or floating at SOFR + 1.0%. The total potential gain\navailable to be shared if the two firms enter an interest rate swap exploiting\ncomparative advantage is:\nA) 0.9% B) 1.4% C) 1.9% D) 0.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Fixed-rate\nspread $$= 6.4\\% - 5.0\\% = 1.4\\%$$; floating spread\n$$= 1.0\\% - 0.5\\% = 0.5\\%$$. Total gain\n$$= 1.4\\% - 0.5\\% = 0.9\\%$$. Choice B is the fixed spread alone, D the\nfloating spread alone.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Swap comparative advantage gain\",\"steps\":[\"Fixed-rate spread = 1.4%\",\"Floating-rate spread = 0.5%\",\"Total gain = |difference of spreads|\",\"= 1.4% − 0.5% = 0.9%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A commodity futures market is in backwardation (futures price below\nspot price). This is most consistent with:\nA) a convenience yield of zero and positive storage costs B) the absence of arbitrageurs in the market C) interest rates exceeding the convenience yield D) a convenience yield that exceeds the sum of the risk-free rate and storage costs\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** With\ncontinuous costs, $$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r + u - y)T}$$. Futures below spot\nrequires $$y > r + u$$ — a convenience yield large enough to outweigh\nfinancing and storage. A and C imply contango.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"F0 = S0·e^((r+u−y)T)\",\"items\":[\"y > r+u (backwardation, F<S)\",\"|y = r+u (F = S)\",\"y < r+u (contango, F>S)\"],\"left\":\"futures below spot\",\"right\":\"futures above spot\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A stock trades at $60 and will pay a $2 dividend in three months. The\ncontinuously compounded risk-free rate is 5%. The six-month forward price is\nclosest to:\nA) $58.02 B) $59.49 C) $61.52 D) $63.54\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Strip the\ndividend's present value, then compound:\n$$F_0 = \\left(60 - 2\\,e^{-0.05 \\times 0.25}\\right) e^{0.05 \\times 0.5} = (60 - 1.975)\\,(1.02532) = \\$59.49$$\nChoice C ($61.52) forgets the dividend entirely; A stops one step early\n(no compounding).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Forward with known income\",\"steps\":[\"PV the dividend: 2·e^(−0.05×0.25) = 1.975\",\"Subtract from spot: 60 − 1.975 = 58.025\",\"Compound to T: × e^(0.05×0.5)\",\"F0 = 59.49\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which statement about American options on a non-dividend-paying stock\nis correct?\nA) Early exercise of an American call is optimal when the call is deep in the money B) Early exercise is never optimal for either American calls or American puts C) An American call is worth more than an otherwise identical European call D) Early exercise of the American call is never optimal, but early exercise of the American put can be optimal\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Without dividends, early\nexercise of an American call is never optimal (you would forfeit time\nvalue and the interest on the strike), so it is worth the *same* as the\nEuropean call — which is why C is wrong. Early exercise of a deep\nin-the-money American put can be optimal.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Early exercise (no dividends)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"American call\",\"items\":[\"Never optimal early\",\"= European value\",\"Forfeits time value + interest\"]},{\"h\":\"American put\",\"items\":[\"Can be optimal early\",\"Deep ITM cases\",\"> European value\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A firm pays 5% fixed annually and receives floating on a $100 million\ninterest rate swap with exactly two annual payments remaining. The floating\nleg has just reset, so it is worth par. The zero curve is flat at 6% per year\n(annual compounding). The value of the swap to the fixed-rate payer is closest\nto:\nA) −$1.83 million B) $0 C) +$1.83 million D) +$3.67 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Value the swap as bonds. The\nfloating leg just reset, so $$B_{fl} = 100$$. Fixed leg:\n$$B_{fix} = \\frac{5}{1.06} + \\frac{105}{1.06^2} = 4.717 + 93.450 = 98.167$$\n$$V_{payer} = B_{fl} - B_{fix} = 100 - 98.167 = +\\$1.83 \\text{ million}$$\nPaying 5% when market rates are 6% is valuable. Choice A is the\nreceiver's value — a sign flip.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Swap value (payer of fixed)\",\"steps\":[\"Floating leg just reset → B_fl = 100\",\"Discount fixed cash flows → B_fix = 98.167\",\"V_payer = B_fl − B_fix\",\"= +1.83 million (pay 5% vs 6% market)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A manager wants to fully hedge a $20 million equity portfolio with a\nbeta of 1.2 using index futures. The index futures price is 5,000 and the\ncontract multiplier is $250. The number of contracts to short is closest to:\nA) 10 B) 16 C) 19 D) 23\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$N = \\beta \\times \\frac{V_P}{F \\times m} = 1.2 \\times \\frac{20{,}000{,}000}{5{,}000 \\times 250} = 1.2 \\times 16 = 19.2 \\approx 19$$\nChoice B (16) drops the beta adjustment.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Index futures hedge\",\"steps\":[\"N = β × V_P / (F × multiplier)\",\"Contract value = 5,000 × 250\",\"= 1.2 × 20,000,000 / 1,250,000\",\"N = 19.2 ≈ 19\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor buys a straddle on a stock at strike $100, paying $6 for\nthe call and $4 for the put. The break-even stock prices at expiry are:\nA) $90 and $110 B) $94 and $106 C) $96 and $104 D) $96 and $106\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Total premium $$= 6 + 4 = \\$10$$,\nso break-evens are $$100 \\pm 10$$: $90 and $110. Choices B and D use\nonly one leg's premium on each side — both legs' costs must be\nrecovered in *each* direction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Long straddle break-evens\",\"items\":[\"90 (lower BE)\",\"|100 strike\",\"110 (upper BE)\"],\"left\":\"profit if S falls\",\"right\":\"profit if S rises\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A bond yields 8% per year with semiannual compounding. The equivalent\ncontinuously compounded yield is closest to:\nA) 7.84% B) 8.00% C) 8.16% D) 8.33%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$r_c = m \\ln\\!\\left(1 + \\frac{r_m}{m}\\right) = 2 \\ln(1.04) = 7.84\\%$$\nThe continuous rate is always *below* the equivalent discrete rate —\nchoice C (8.16%) converts in the wrong direction.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Rate equivalence (8% nominal)\",\"items\":[\"continuous 7.84%\",\"|effective/discrete\",\"semiannual 8.00%\"],\"left\":\"lower rate\",\"right\":\"higher rate\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A property insurer reports a loss ratio of 75% and an expense ratio of\n30%. Which statement is correct?\nA) The combined ratio is 52.5%, indicating an underwriting profit B) The combined ratio is 105%, indicating an underwriting profit C) The combined ratio is 105%, so the insurer must be unprofitable overall D) The combined ratio is 105%, indicating an underwriting loss, but investment income may still make the insurer profitable overall\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Combined ratio\n$$= 75\\% + 30\\% = 105\\% > 100\\%$$: an underwriting loss. But insurers\ninvest premiums, so investment income can still produce an overall\nprofit — which is why C overreaches.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Combined ratio\",\"steps\":[\"Loss ratio 75% + expense ratio 30%\",\"Combined = 105% > 100%\",\"Underwriting loss\",\"But investment income can offset → overall profit\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $52. The continuously compounded\nrisk-free rate is 5%. The lower bound for the price of a six-month European\ncall with strike $50 is closest to:\nA) $0.73 B) $2.00 C) $3.23 D) $4.44\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$c \\geq S_0 - K e^{-rT} = 52 - 50\\,e^{-0.025} = 52 - 48.77 = \\$3.23$$\nChoice B ($2.00) uses the undiscounted strike — i.e., bare intrinsic\nvalue, the classic forgotten-discounting error.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Lower bound on European call\",\"steps\":[\"c ≥ S0 − K·e^(−rT)\",\"Discount the strike: 50·e^(−0.025) = 48.77\",\"52 − 48.77\",\"Lower bound = 3.23\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The continuously compounded zero rates are 4% for one year and 5% for\ntwo years. The continuously compounded forward rate for the period between\nyear one and year two is:\nA) 4.5% B) 5.0% C) 6.0% D) 7.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With continuous compounding:\n$$f_{1,2} = \\frac{r_2 T_2 - r_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1} = \\frac{0.05(2) - 0.04(1)}{1} = 6\\%$$\nChoice A (4.5%) averages the two zero rates — the forward must sit\n*above* both when the curve is rising.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Forward rate (rising curve)\",\"items\":[\"r1 = 4%\",\"r2 = 5%\",\"|\",\"f(1,2) = 6%\"],\"left\":\"near zero rates\",\"right\":\"forward sits above\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A Treasury bond futures contract settles at 96.00. Two deliverable\nbonds are available: Bond X is quoted at 98.50 with a conversion factor of\n1.02; Bond Y is quoted at 121.80 with a conversion factor of 1.26. Ignoring\naccrued interest, which bond is cheapest to deliver?\nA) Bond X — its net delivery cost of 0.58 is below Bond Y's 0.84 B) Bond X — its quoted price is the lowest C) Bond Y — its net delivery cost of 0.84 is below Bond X's 2.50 D) Bond Y — the bond with the higher conversion factor is always delivered\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Delivery cost = quoted\nprice − futures × CF.\nBond X: $$98.50 - 96.00 \\times 1.02 = 98.50 - 97.92 = 0.58$$.\nBond Y: $$121.80 - 96.00 \\times 1.26 = 121.80 - 120.96 = 0.84$$.\nX is cheapest. Choice B compares raw quoted prices — the conversion\nfactor exists precisely to make that comparison invalid.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"CTD = min(quoted − futures×CF)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Bond X\",\"items\":[\"98.50 − 96×1.02\",\"cost = 0.58\",\"cheapest\"]},{\"h\":\"Bond Y\",\"items\":[\"121.80 − 96×1.26\",\"cost = 0.84\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A mortgage pool's prepayment speed is quoted as a 6% CPR. The\ncorresponding single monthly mortality (SMM) rate is closest to:\nA) 0.486% B) 0.500% C) 0.514% D) 0.600%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$SMM = 1 - (1 - CPR)^{1/12} = 1 - 0.94^{1/12} = 0.514\\%$$\nChoice B (0.500%) is the simple-division error $$6\\%/12$$ — the\nrelationship is geometric, not linear.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CPR → SMM (geometric)\",\"steps\":[\"Annual survival = 1 − CPR = 0.94\",\"Monthly survival = 0.94^(1/12)\",\"SMM = 1 − 0.94^(1/12)\",\"= 0.514% (not 6%/12)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Six months ago an investor entered a long forward contract on a stock\nwith a delivery price of $50. The contract now has six months remaining, the\ncurrent forward price for that maturity is $53, and the continuously\ncompounded risk-free rate is 4%. The value of the investor's position is\nclosest to:\nA) $2.86 B) $2.94 C) $3.00 D) $3.06\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$V = (F_0 - K)\\,e^{-rT} = (53 - 50)\\,e^{-0.04 \\times 0.5} = 3\\,e^{-0.02} = \\$2.94$$\nChoice C ($3.00) skips the discounting; D ($3.06) compounds instead —\na sign flip in the exponent.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Value of a long forward\",\"steps\":[\"V = (F0 − K)·e^(−rT)\",\"Gain at maturity = 53 − 50 = 3\",\"Discount back: × e^(−0.04×0.5)\",\"V = 2.94\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q71.** A hedge fund charges a 2% management fee (on beginning assets) and a\n20% incentive fee on returns net of the management fee, with no hurdle rate.\nThe fund's gross return for the year is 18%. The investor's net return is\nclosest to:\nA) 12.4% B) 12.8% C) 14.4% D) 16.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Management fee 2% leaves 16%;\nincentive fee $$0.20 \\times 16\\% = 3.2\\%$$. Net\n$$= 16\\% - 3.2\\% = 12.8\\%$$. Choice A (12.4%) charges the incentive fee\non the gross 18% — read whether the incentive applies net of the\nmanagement fee.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Net return after 2-and-20\",\"steps\":[\"Gross return 18% − 2% mgmt = 16%\",\"Incentive = 20% × 16% (net) = 3.2%\",\"Net = 16% − 3.2%\",\"= 12.8%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A down-and-out European call and a down-and-in European call share the\nsame underlying, strike, barrier, and expiry. The sum of their premiums\nequals:\nA) the premium of an otherwise identical vanilla European call B) the premium of an otherwise identical vanilla European put C) zero D) the intrinsic value of the vanilla call\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** In-out parity: for the same\nbarrier, strike, and expiry, knock-in + knock-out = vanilla, because\nexactly one of the two pays off along any path.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"In-out barrier parity\",\"root\":\"Knock-in + Knock-out = Vanilla\",\"children\":[\"Same strike, barrier, expiry\",\"Any path hits OR misses barrier\",\"Exactly one of the pair pays off\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Gold trades at $2,400 per ounce. The continuously compounded risk-free\nrate is 5% and storage costs are 0.5% of the spot price per year, paid\ncontinuously. The one-year futures price is closest to:\nA) $2,510.47 B) $2,523.05 C) $2,535.70 D) $2,652.41\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Storage costs *add* to\nthe carry:\n$$F_0 = S_0 e^{(r + u)T} = 2{,}400\\,e^{(0.05 + 0.005)(1)} = 2{,}400\\,e^{0.055} = \\$2{,}535.70$$\nChoice A ($2,510.47) subtracts storage costs — a sign flip; B ignores\nthem.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Forward with storage cost\",\"steps\":[\"F0 = S0·e^((r+u)T)\",\"Storage u ADDS to carry: r+u = 0.055\",\"2,400 × e^(0.055)\",\"F0 = 2,535.70\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A bond portfolio has a DV01 of $9,000. A bond futures contract has a\nDV01 of $75 per contract. To make the combined position insensitive to a\nparallel shift in rates, the manager should short:\nA) 12 contracts B) 120 contracts C) 1,200 contracts D) 8,333 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$N = \\frac{DV01_P}{DV01_F} = \\frac{9{,}000}{75} = 120 \\text{ contracts short}$$.\nA and C are factor-of-ten slips.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A short position delivers a bond against a Treasury bond futures\ncontract. The futures settlement price is 95.00, the bond's conversion factor\nis 1.10, and its accrued interest is $1.20 per $100 face. The invoice price\nreceived by the short is closest to:\nA) $87.56 B) $104.50 C) $105.70 D) $106.90\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\text{Invoice} = F \\times CF + AI = 95.00 \\times 1.10 + 1.20 = 104.50 + 1.20 = \\$105.70$$\nChoice B ($104.50) forgets accrued interest; A divides by the conversion\nfactor instead of multiplying.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Futures invoice price\",\"steps\":[\"Invoice = futures × CF + accrued interest\",\"Multiply by CF: 95.00 × 1.10 = 104.50\",\"Add AI: + 1.20\",\"Invoice = 105.70\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Unlike a plain-vanilla interest rate swap, a fixed-for-fixed currency\nswap typically involves:\nA) an exchange of principal amounts at both initiation and maturity B) no exchange of any payments until maturity C) netting of all payments into a single currency D) zero counterparty credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Because the legs are in\ndifferent currencies, principals are exchanged at initiation and\nre-exchanged at maturity — unlike an interest rate swap, where notional\nis never exchanged. This makes counterparty exposure larger, not zero\n(D).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Currency swap vs IRS\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Currency swap\",\"items\":[\"Different currencies\",\"Principals exchanged at start & end\",\"Larger counterparty exposure\"]},{\"h\":\"Interest rate swap\",\"items\":[\"Same currency\",\"Notional never exchanged\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Which statement best describes the effect of central clearing through\na central counterparty (CCP)?\nA) It eliminates counterparty credit risk from the financial system B) It increases the number of bilateral exposures each dealer must manage C) It removes the need for initial margin D) It replaces bilateral exposures through novation and multilateral netting, reducing counterparty exposures but concentrating risk in the CCP\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Novation substitutes the CCP\ninto each trade, and multilateral netting compresses bilateral\nexposures — but credit risk is transformed and concentrated in the CCP,\nnot eliminated (A). Initial margin is required, not removed (C).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Central clearing (CCP)\",\"steps\":[\"Novation: CCP becomes counterparty to each side\",\"Multilateral netting compresses exposures\",\"Initial margin still required\",\"Credit risk concentrated in CCP, not eliminated\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which statement correctly distinguishes a bank's trading book from its\nbanking book?\nA) Trading book positions are held with intent to trade and are marked to market; banking book positions (such as loans held to maturity) are accounted for on an accrual basis B) The banking book is marked to market daily; the trading book is held at amortized cost C) Only the banking book attracts regulatory capital D) Banks may freely move positions between the books to minimize capital\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Trading book:\nheld to trade, marked to market daily. Banking book: loans and\nheld-to-maturity assets on accrual accounting. Both attract capital\n(C), and regulators restrict reclassification precisely to prevent D.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Trading book vs banking book\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Trading book\",\"items\":[\"Held to trade\",\"Marked to market daily\",\"Fair-value accounting\"]},{\"h\":\"Banking book\",\"items\":[\"Loans, held-to-maturity\",\"Accrual accounting\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A firm unexpectedly announces a large increase in its dividend, with\nno other new information. The effect on the prices of European options on the\nfirm's stock is that:\nA) both call and put values increase B) call values decrease and put values increase C) call values increase and put values decrease D) neither call nor put values change\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A larger dividend\nlowers the expected ex-dividend stock price, which hurts calls and helps\nputs. (Holders of *European* options are not protected against\ndividend announcements.)\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Larger dividend → ex-div price falls\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Calls\",\"items\":[\"Value falls\",\"Hurt by dividend\"]},{\"h\":\"Puts\",\"items\":[\"Value rises\",\"Helped by dividend\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## Valuation and Risk Models (1 mark)\n\n**Q4.** A $10 million portfolio has normally distributed daily returns with a\nmean of zero and a daily volatility of 1.5%. Using $$z_{0.99} = 2.326$$, the\none-day 99% VaR is closest to:\nA) $246,750 B) $294,000 C) $348,900 D) $474,300\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\text{VaR} = 2.326 \\times 0.015 \\times \\$10{,}000{,}000 = \\$348{,}900$$.\nChoice A uses the 95% z-value 1.645; B uses the two-tailed 1.96 — both\nclassic wrong-z errors. D is the ten-day number ($$\\times\\sqrt{10}$$).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"One-tailed VaR z-values\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Confidence\",\"items\":[\"95%\",\"97.5%\",\"99%\"]},{\"h\":\"z (one-tail)\",\"items\":[\"1.645\",\"1.960\",\"2.326\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A bond has a modified duration of 6.5 and a convexity of 45.0. If its\nyield rises by 100 basis points, the percentage price change estimated using\nboth duration and convexity is closest to:\nA) −6.73% B) −6.50% C) −6.28% D) +6.28%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\frac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -6.5(0.01) + \\tfrac{1}{2}(45)(0.01)^2 = -0.0650 + 0.00225 = -6.28\\%$$.\nChoice B is duration-only; A (−6.73%) flips the sign of the convexity\nterm — convexity always *cushions* the loss for a long bond.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Duration + convexity\",\"steps\":[\"Duration term: −D·Δy = −6.5(0.01) = −6.50%\",\"Convexity term: +½·C·Δy² = +0.225%\",\"Convexity always adds (cushions)\",\"Total ΔP/P ≈ −6.28%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A stock trades at $100. Over the next three months it will either rise\nto $110 or fall to $90. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4% per\nyear. Using a one-step binomial tree, the value of a three-month European call\nwith strike $100 is closest to:\nA) $4.95 B) $5.45 C) $5.50 D) $6.05\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With $$u = 1.1$$, $$d = 0.9$$:\n$$p = \\frac{e^{0.04 \\times 0.25} - 0.9}{1.1 - 0.9} = \\frac{1.01005 - 0.9}{0.2} = 0.5503$$\n$$c = e^{-0.01}\\,[0.5503 \\times 10 + 0.4497 \\times 0] = \\$5.45$$\nChoice C ($5.50) forgets to discount — the one-step-early error; A uses\nthe \"real-world\" $$p = 0.5$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"One-step binomial call\",\"steps\":[\"Compute risk-neutral p = (e^(rΔt)−d)/(u−d)\",\"p = 0.5503\",\"Expected payoff = p·cu + (1−p)·cd\",\"Discount at e^(−rΔt): c = 5.45\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A two-year bond pays a 6% annual coupon in semiannual installments on\na face value of $100. Its yield to maturity is 5% per year with semiannual\ncompounding. The bond's price is closest to:\nA) $92.91 B) $100.00 C) $101.88 D) $103.81\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Four semiannual periods, $3\ncoupon, 2.5% per period:\n$$P = 3 \\times \\frac{1 - 1.025^{-4}}{0.025} + \\frac{100}{1.025^{4}} = 11.29 + 90.60 = \\$101.88$$\nThe bond trades above par because the 6% coupon exceeds the 5% yield —\na quick sanity check that eliminates A immediately.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Coupon vs yield → price\",\"items\":[\"yield > coupon (discount)\",\"|coupon = yield (par)\",\"coupon > yield (premium)\"],\"left\":\"below par\",\"right\":\"above par\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A loan has an exposure at default of $5 million, a probability of\ndefault of 2%, and an expected recovery rate of 40%. The expected loss is:\nA) $40,000 B) $60,000 C) $100,000 D) $3,000,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$EL = EAD \\times PD \\times LGD = 5{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.02 \\times (1 - 0.40) = \\$60{,}000$$.\nChoice A ($40,000) multiplies by the recovery rate instead of the loss\ngiven default — the classic recovery/LGD swap.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Expected loss\",\"steps\":[\"EL = EAD × PD × LGD\",\"LGD = 1 − recovery = 1 − 0.40 = 0.60\",\"= 5,000,000 × 0.02 × 0.60\",\"EL = 60,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A bank computes one-day 99% VaR by historical simulation over 500\nscenarios. The six largest simulated losses (in $ millions) are 4.1, 3.8, 3.6,\n3.3, 3.1, and 2.9. The one-day 99% VaR estimate is:\nA) $2.9 million B) $3.1 million C) $3.3 million D) $4.1 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** At 99% with 500\nscenarios, $$1\\% \\times 500 = 5$$, so VaR is the **5th-worst** loss\n(Hull/GARP convention): $3.1 million. Choice C ($3.3 million, the\n4th-worst) is the one-step-early error; D is the worst loss.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Historical sim VaR (99%, n=500)\",\"steps\":[\"Rank losses worst to best\",\"(1 − conf) × n = 0.01 × 500 = 5\",\"VaR = 5th-worst loss\",\"= 3.1 million\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A bond portfolio is worth $25 million and has a modified duration of\n5.2. Its DV01 is closest to:\nA) $1,300 B) $5,200 C) $13,000 D) $130,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$DV01 = D \\times P \\times 0.0001 = 5.2 \\times 25{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 = \\$13{,}000$$.\nA and D are factor-of-ten slips (using 0.00001 or 0.001).\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $50. Consider a six-month\nEuropean call with strike $50, a continuously compounded risk-free rate of 5%,\nand volatility of 20% per year. Given $$N(d_1) = 0.5977$$ and\n$$N(d_2) = 0.5422$$, the Black-Scholes-Merton call price is closest to:\nA) $2.21 B) $2.78 C) $3.44 D) $4.06\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$c = S_0 N(d_1) - K e^{-rT} N(d_2) = 50(0.5977) - 50\\,e^{-0.025}(0.5422)$$\n$$= 29.885 - 48.766 \\times 0.5422 = 29.885 - 26.441 = \\$3.44$$\nChoice B ($2.78) forgets to discount the strike — the wrong-compounding\nerror $$50(0.5977 - 0.5422)$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"BSM call price\",\"steps\":[\"c = S0·N(d1) − K·e^(−rT)·N(d2)\",\"Stock term: 50 × 0.5977 = 29.885\",\"Strike term discounted: 50·e^(−0.025)·0.5422 = 26.441\",\"c = 3.44\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A trader has sold call options on 2,000 shares. Each option has a\ndelta of 0.60. To make the position delta-neutral, the trader should:\nA) sell 1,200 shares B) buy 1,200 shares C) buy 2,000 shares D) buy 3,333 shares\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Short calls on 2,000 shares\nhave delta $$-0.60 \\times 2{,}000 = -1{,}200$$; neutralize by **buying**\n1,200 shares. Choice A is the sign flip; D divides by delta instead of\nmultiplying.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Delta-neutral hedge\",\"steps\":[\"Short calls on 2,000 shares\",\"Position delta = −0.60 × 2,000 = −1,200\",\"Offset negative delta → buy shares\",\"Buy 1,200 shares\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A risk manager has 100 historical-simulation scenarios. The five\nlargest losses (in $ millions) are 6.0, 5.2, 4.8, 4.5, and 4.0. Estimating 95%\nexpected shortfall as the average of the worst 5% of outcomes gives:\nA) $4.00 million B) $4.90 million C) $5.13 million D) $6.00 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Worst 5% of 100 scenarios\n= worst 5 losses:\n$$ES = \\frac{6.0 + 5.2 + 4.8 + 4.5 + 4.0}{5} = \\frac{24.5}{5} = \\$4.90 \\text{ million}$$\nChoice A is the 95% VaR (the cutoff loss), not the tail average.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Expected Shortfall\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"95% VaR\",\"items\":[\"Cutoff (5th-worst) loss\",\"A single threshold\"]},{\"h\":\"ES\",\"items\":[\"Average of tail losses\",\"Mean of worst 5\",\"= 4.90 million\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** The six-month discount factor is 0.95. A one-year bond paying a 10%\nannual coupon in semiannual installments ($5 per $100 face every six months)\nis priced at $101. The one-year discount factor implied by bootstrapping is\nclosest to:\nA) 0.9050 B) 0.9143 C) 0.9167 D) 0.9625\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$101 = 5(0.95) + 105\\,d_{1.0} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; d_{1.0} = \\frac{101 - 4.75}{105} = \\frac{96.25}{105} = 0.9167$$\nChoice D (0.9625) divides by 100 (face) instead of 105 — forgetting the\nfinal coupon rides with the principal.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bootstrapping d(1.0)\",\"steps\":[\"Price = 5·d(0.5) + 105·d(1.0)\",\"101 = 5(0.95) + 105·d(1.0)\",\"d(1.0) = (101 − 4.75) / 105\",\"= 0.9167 (divide by 105, not 100)\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A one-year rating transition matrix shows: an A-rated firm stays at A\nwith probability 90%, migrates to BBB with probability 8%, and defaults with\nprobability 2%; a BBB-rated firm migrates to A with probability 10%, stays at\nBBB with probability 86%, and defaults with probability 4%. Default is\nabsorbing. The two-year cumulative default probability for a firm currently\nrated A is closest to:\nA) 2.00% B) 3.80% C) 4.00% D) 4.12%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Default in year\n1: 2%. Default in year 2: from A (prob 0.90) default 2% → 1.80%; from\nBBB (prob 0.08) default 4% → 0.32%. Cumulative:\n$$2.00\\% + 1.80\\% + 0.32\\% = 4.12\\%$$\nChoice C (4.00% = 2% + 2%) ignores migration — the one-step-early\nerror of squaring the matrix without the BBB path.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Cumulative 2-yr default\",\"steps\":[\"Year-1 default = 2.00%\",\"Survive as A (0.90) → default 2% = 1.80%\",\"Migrate to BBB (0.08) → default 4% = 0.32%\",\"Total = 2.00 + 1.80 + 0.32 = 4.12%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A GARCH(1,1) model is estimated as\n$$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.000002 + 0.08\\,r_{n-1}^2 + 0.90\\,\\sigma_{n-1}^2$$.\nYesterday's volatility estimate was 1.2% and yesterday's return was 2.0%. The\nupdated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 1.00% B) 1.20% C) 1.28% D) 1.93%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\sigma_n^2 = 0.000002 + 0.08(0.02)^2 + 0.90(0.012)^2 = 0.000002 + 0.000032 + 0.0001296 = 0.0001636$$\n$$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.0001636} = 1.28\\%$$\nChoice D (1.93%) mixes volatilities instead of variances.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"GARCH(1,1) update\",\"steps\":[\"σ²ₙ = ω + α·u²ₙ₋₁ + β·σ²ₙ₋₁\",\"ω=0.000002, α-term=0.000032, β-term=0.0001296\",\"Sum = 0.0001636\",\"σₙ = 1.28%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A bond has a Macaulay duration of 7.5 years and yields 6% per year\nwith semiannual compounding. Its modified duration is closest to:\nA) 7.08 B) 7.28 C) 7.50 D) 7.73\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$D_{mod} = \\frac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m} = \\frac{7.5}{1 + 0.06/2} = \\frac{7.5}{1.03} = 7.28$$\nChoice A (7.08) divides by 1.06 — the wrong-compounding error (annual\ninstead of semiannual).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Modified duration\",\"steps\":[\"D_mod = D_Mac / (1 + y/m)\",\"m = 2 (semiannual): 1 + 0.06/2 = 1.03\",\"= 7.5 / 1.03\",\"= 7.28\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Desk A has a VaR of $300,000 and Desk B has a VaR of $400,000. Returns\nare bivariate normal with correlation 0.5. The diversified VaR of the combined\nposition is closest to:\nA) $500,000 B) $556,800 C) $608,300 D) $700,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With jointly normal returns\nVaR scales like volatility:\nWorking in $ thousands:\n$$\\text{VaR}_P = \\sqrt{300^2 + 400^2 + 2(0.5)(300)(400)} = \\sqrt{370{,}000} \\approx 608.3$$\ni.e., $608,300. Choice D ($700,000) is the undiversified sum\n($$\\rho = 1$$); A assumes $$\\rho = 0$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Portfolio VaR vs correlation ($000s)\",\"items\":[\"ρ=0: 500\",\"ρ=0.5: 608 (this)\",\"|\",\"ρ=1: 700 (undiversified)\"],\"left\":\"more diversified\",\"right\":\"no diversification\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Expected shortfall is a coherent risk measure, while VaR in general is\nnot, because VaR can fail to satisfy:\nA) subadditivity B) monotonicity C) translation invariance D) positive homogeneity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** VaR can violate\nsubadditivity: the VaR of a combined portfolio can exceed the sum of the\nparts, penalizing diversification. ES satisfies all four coherence\naxioms.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Coherence: VaR vs ES\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"Can violate subadditivity\",\"Penalizes diversification\",\"Not coherent\"]},{\"h\":\"Expected Shortfall\",\"items\":[\"Subadditive\",\"All 4 axioms hold\",\"Coherent\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A portfolio's one-day 99% VaR is $150,000. Assuming i.i.d. normally\ndistributed daily returns, the ten-day 99% VaR is closest to:\nA) $150,000 B) $474,300 C) $750,000 D) $1,500,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\text{VaR}_{10} = 150{,}000 \\times \\sqrt{10} = \\$474{,}300$$.\nChoice D multiplies by 10 — scaling VaR like variance instead of like\nvolatility.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Scaling VaR over time\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Correct (√t)\",\"items\":[\"VaR₁₀ = VaR₁·√10\",\"150,000 × 3.162\",\"= 474,300\"]},{\"h\":\"Wrong (×t)\",\"items\":[\"Scales like variance\",\"×10 = 1.5M\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A bank models one type of operational loss with a Poisson frequency of\n4 events per year and an average severity of $50,000 per event, with frequency\nand severity independent. The expected annual loss is:\nA) $12,500 B) $50,000 C) $200,000 D) $800,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With independent frequency\nand severity, expected annual loss\n$$= \\lambda \\times E[\\text{severity}] = 4 \\times \\$50{,}000 = \\$200{,}000$$.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Expected annual op-risk loss\",\"root\":\"EL = frequency × severity\",\"children\":[\"λ = 4 events/yr\",\"E[severity] = 50,000\",\"Independent → multiply\",\"EL = 200,000\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which statement about key rate durations (key rate '01s) is correct?\nA) The sum of a bond's key rate durations approximately equals its effective duration for a parallel shift B) Key rate durations assume all spot rates move by the same amount C) Key rate durations cannot be used to construct hedges D) A zero-coupon bond has equal key rate durations at every key rate\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A parallel shift moves\nevery key rate by the same amount, so the key rate durations sum\n(approximately) to the effective duration. B describes a parallel-shift\nmeasure, not key rates; a zero-coupon bond's exposure is concentrated at\nits own maturity (so D is wrong).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Key rate durations\",\"root\":\"Σ key rate durations\",\"children\":[\"Each isolates one maturity shift\",\"Parallel shift moves all rates equally\",\"Sum ≈ effective (parallel) duration\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A corporate bond trades at a credit spread of 200 basis points over\nthe risk-free rate. Assuming the spread compensates only for expected default\nlosses and the expected recovery rate is 40%, the implied annual default\nprobability is closest to:\nA) 1.20% B) 2.00% C) 3.33% D) 5.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Spread\n$$\\approx PD \\times LGD$$, so\n$$PD = \\frac{0.02}{1 - 0.40} = \\frac{0.02}{0.60} = 3.33\\%$$\nChoice A (1.20%) multiplies by LGD instead of dividing; D divides by the\nrecovery rate instead of LGD.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"PD from spread\",\"steps\":[\"Spread ≈ PD × LGD\",\"LGD = 1 − recovery = 0.60\",\"PD = spread / LGD = 0.02 / 0.60\",\"PD = 3.33%\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A call option has a delta of 0.60 and a gamma of 0.05. If the\nunderlying stock price rises by $2 and all other pricing inputs are unchanged,\nthe delta-gamma estimate of the change in the option price is closest to:\nA) $0.10 B) $1.20 C) $1.30 D) $1.40\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** $$\\Delta c \\approx \\Delta \\cdot \\Delta S + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\Gamma (\\Delta S)^2 = 0.60(2) + \\tfrac{1}{2}(0.05)(4) = 1.20 + 0.10 = \\$1.30$$\nChoice B is delta-only; D ($1.40) forgets the $$\\tfrac{1}{2}$$ on the\ngamma term.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Delta-gamma approximation\",\"steps\":[\"Δc ≈ Δ·ΔS + ½·Γ·(ΔS)²\",\"Delta term: 0.60 × 2 = 1.20\",\"Gamma term: ½ × 0.05 × 4 = 0.10\",\"Δc = 1.30\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Reverse stress testing differs from conventional stress testing in\nthat it:\nA) starts from a specified adverse outcome — such as the failure of the firm — and works backward to identify scenarios that could cause it B) applies historical scenarios in reverse chronological order C) stresses only the asset side of the balance sheet D) uses smaller shocks than conventional stress tests\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Reverse stress tests\nstart from the outcome (e.g., insolvency) and search backward for the\nscenarios that produce it, uncovering vulnerabilities conventional\nforward-looking scenarios may miss.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Stress test direction\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward stress\",\"items\":[\"Scenario → outcome\",\"Pick shocks first\"]},{\"h\":\"Reverse stress\",\"items\":[\"Outcome → scenario\",\"Start at insolvency\",\"Find what causes it\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A delta-neutral options position has large positive gamma. Over the\nnext day, the position will:\nA) profit from a large move in the underlying in either direction B) profit only if the underlying rises C) profit only if the underlying is unchanged D) lose money if realized volatility exceeds implied volatility\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A delta-neutral, long-gamma position\ngains from large moves in either direction (the P&L is locally\n$$\\tfrac{1}{2}\\Gamma (\\Delta S)^2 > 0$$), at the cost of theta decay if\nthe underlying sits still. D has the volatility comparison backward.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Delta-neutral long gamma\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Large move (either way)\",\"items\":[\"P&L ≈ ½Γ(ΔS)² > 0\",\"Gains\"]},{\"h\":\"Underlying still\",\"items\":[\"Theta decay\",\"Loses (cost of gamma)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which statement about the theta of a long option position is most\naccurate?\nA) Theta is always positive for long calls B) Theta is largest for deep out-of-the-money options C) Theta is typically negative, and for an at-the-money option it becomes increasingly negative as expiry approaches D) Theta and gamma always have the same sign for a long option\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Long options almost always bleed value\nas time passes (theta < 0), and the decay accelerates for at-the-money\noptions near expiry. For long options theta and gamma have *opposite*\nsigns (D), which is the gamma-theta trade-off.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Long option: gamma vs theta\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Gamma\",\"items\":[\"Positive\",\"Gains from moves\"]},{\"h\":\"Theta\",\"items\":[\"Negative (decays)\",\"Worst for ATM near expiry\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** The lowest rating grade that still qualifies a corporate bond as\ninvestment grade is:\nA) BB+ (Ba1) B) BBB− (Baa3) C) A− (A3) D) BBB+ (Baa1)\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Investment grade extends down\nto BBB− (Moody's Baa3); BB+ (Ba1) is the top *speculative* grade.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Investment grade boundary\",\"items\":[\"BBB− / Baa3 (lowest IG)\",\"|\",\"BB+ / Ba1 (top speculative)\"],\"left\":\"investment grade\",\"right\":\"speculative / junk\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** For use in a bank's stress-testing program, a stress scenario should\nabove all be:\nA) identical to the worst historical episode on record B) mild enough that the bank passes the test C) focused on a single risk factor at a time D) severe but plausible, with internally consistent movements across risk factors\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Scenarios must be\nsevere but plausible, with coherent joint movements across risk factors.\nSingle-factor shocks (C) are sensitivity tests, not scenarios.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Scenario vs sensitivity test\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Stress scenario\",\"items\":[\"Many factors jointly\",\"Severe but plausible\",\"Coherent co-movements\"]},{\"h\":\"Sensitivity test\",\"items\":[\"Single-factor shock\",\"One risk at a time\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** When the spot (zero) curve is upward sloping, the correct ordering of\nrates for a given maturity is:\nA) forward > spot > par B) par > spot > forward C) spot > forward > par D) par > forward > spot\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** With an\nupward-sloping spot curve, forwards sit above spots (they are the\nmarginal rates pulling the average up), and par yields sit below spots\n(early coupons are discounted at lower rates): forward > spot > par.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Upward-sloping curve ordering\",\"items\":[\"par yields\",\"|spot rates\",\"forward rates\"],\"left\":\"lowest\",\"right\":\"highest\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Which statement about sovereign credit ratings is most accurate?\nA) Local-currency and foreign-currency ratings are always identical B) The foreign-currency rating is typically above the local-currency rating C) Sovereign ratings ignore political risk D) The foreign-currency rating is typically at or below the local-currency rating, since governments cannot print foreign currency to service external debt\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** A government can tax and print\nits own currency but must *earn* foreign currency, so foreign-currency\nratings are typically at or below local-currency ratings.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sovereign ratings by currency\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Local currency\",\"items\":[\"Can tax and print\",\"Usually higher rating\"]},{\"h\":\"Foreign currency\",\"items\":[\"Must EARN FX\",\"Rating at or below local\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A bank's expected loss (EL) and unexpected loss (UL) on its loan\nportfolio are best covered, respectively, by:\nA) economic capital for both EL and UL B) loan pricing and provisions for UL; economic capital for EL C) regulatory fines for EL; deposit insurance for UL D) loan pricing and provisions for EL; economic capital for UL\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** Expected loss is a predictable cost\ncovered by loan pricing and provisions; unexpected loss — the\nvolatility around EL — is what economic capital is held against.\nChoice B reverses the pairing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Expected vs Unexpected Loss\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Expected loss\",\"items\":[\"Predictable cost\",\"Covered by pricing & provisions\"]},{\"h\":\"Unexpected loss\",\"items\":[\"Volatility around EL\",\"Held against by economic capital\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** The implied volatility of an exchange-traded option is:\nA) the volatility that, when input into the Black-Scholes-Merton model, makes the model price equal the observed market price B) the standard deviation of the option's historical returns C) always equal to the underlying stock's historical volatility D) the volatility forecast produced by a GARCH(1,1) model -- {: .tip } Finished? Go straight to the [answer key](mock-exam-1-answers) while your reasoning is fresh, and log every miss — including lucky guesses — in your error log.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Implied volatility is the\nvalue of $$\\sigma$$ that equates the BSM model price to the observed\nmarket price — it is backed out of prices, not estimated from\nhistorical returns (B) or a GARCH model (D).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Implied vs historical volatility\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Implied vol\",\"items\":[\"Backed out of market price\",\"Set BSM price = market price\",\"Forward-looking\"]},{\"h\":\"Historical/GARCH\",\"items\":[\"Estimated from past returns\",\"Backward-looking\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n"},{"num":"02","md":"# Mock Exam 2\n**Total: 100 questions · 4 hours · no negative marking**\n\n## Foundations of Risk Management (1 mark)\n\n**Q1.** A regional bank holds a portfolio of corporate bonds. The treasurer is concerned that the bank could suffer losses because one of the bond issuers may fail to make its scheduled coupon payment. Which category of risk is the treasurer primarily describing?\nA) Market risk B) Credit risk C) Liquidity risk D) Operational risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Credit risk is the risk of loss arising from a borrower or counterparty failing to meet its contractual obligations, such as a missed coupon payment. Market risk would relate to changes in interest rates or spreads moving prices; liquidity risk relates to funding or trading liquidity; operational risk relates to failed processes or people. The trigger here is issuer default on a scheduled payment, which is squarely credit risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Missed coupon payment = which risk?\",\"options\":[\"Market risk\",\"Credit risk\",\"Liquidity risk\",\"Operational risk\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Counterparty fails contractual obligation\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A trading desk suffers a large loss after a junior trader enters a buy order for 500,000 shares instead of the intended 50,000 shares, and the firm's pre-trade controls fail to flag the error. Under the standard risk taxonomy, this loss is best classified as:\nA) Market risk B) Credit risk C) Operational risk D) Business risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** A \"fat-finger\" trade entry error combined with a failure of internal controls is operational risk, defined as the risk of loss from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, and systems, or from external events. The resulting market exposure may be realized through market prices, but the root cause and classification is operational. Business risk relates to strategic and demand uncertainty, not control failures.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Operational vs Market risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Operational\",\"items\":[\"Failed processes/people/systems\",\"Fat-finger + control failure\",\"Root cause classification\"]},{\"h\":\"Market\",\"items\":[\"Price/rate moves\",\"How loss is realized\",\"Not the root cause\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is generally considered a sound reason for a non-financial corporation to NOT hedge a particular risk exposure?\nA) The risk is one in which the firm has a comparative informational advantage relative to investors B) Hedging would reduce the volatility of reported earnings C) Hedging would reduce the probability of financial distress D) The exposure arises from a currency in which the firm has no operating expertise\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Firms should consider retaining risks in which they hold a comparative or informational advantage, because they may be better positioned than outside investors to bear and price that risk. Options B, C, and D all describe reasons that typically support hedging (reducing earnings volatility, lowering distress probability, and managing exposures outside the firm's expertise). The trap is assuming all risk should always be hedged.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Retain vs Hedge a risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Retain\",\"items\":[\"Comparative/info advantage\",\"Better positioned to price it\"]},{\"h\":\"Hedge\",\"items\":[\"Reduce earnings volatility\",\"Lower distress probability\",\"Outside firm's expertise\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A company's board of directors is reviewing its risk governance structure. According to widely accepted corporate governance principles, the primary responsibility of the board with respect to risk is to:\nA) Execute day-to-day hedging transactions in the derivatives markets B) Set the firm's overall risk appetite and oversee the risk management framework C) Calculate the firm's daily value at risk D) Negotiate covenants directly with the firm's lenders\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The board's central risk responsibility is to establish the firm's risk appetite and oversee the risk management framework, not to perform operational tasks. Executing hedges, computing daily VaR, and negotiating covenants are management or staff functions. The trap conflates board oversight with day-to-day execution.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Board vs Management\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Board (oversight)\",\"items\":[\"Set risk appetite\",\"Oversee RM framework\"]},{\"h\":\"Management (execution)\",\"items\":[\"Execute hedges\",\"Compute daily VaR\",\"Negotiate covenants\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A bank wishes to transfer the credit risk of a $50 million corporate loan to a counterparty while keeping the loan on its balance sheet and maintaining the client relationship. Which instrument is most directly suited to this objective?\nA) An interest rate swap B) A single-name credit default swap (CDS) C) An equity total return index future D) A currency forward\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A single-name CDS lets the bank buy protection on a specific issuer, transferring credit risk to the protection seller while keeping the loan on its books and preserving the client relationship. An interest rate swap transfers rate risk, an equity index future transfers equity market risk, and a currency forward transfers FX risk—none address single-name default risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Transfer single-name default risk\",\"options\":[\"Single-name CDS\",\"Interest rate swap\",\"Equity index future\",\"Currency forward\"],\"correct\":0,\"why\":\"Buy protection on a specific issuer\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The risk-free rate is 3.0%, the expected return on the market portfolio is 9.0%, and a stock has a beta of 1.4. According to the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), the stock's expected return is closest to:\nA) 9.0% B) 11.4% C) 12.6% D) 15.6%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Using $$E(R)=R_f+\\beta\\,[E(R_m)-R_f]=3.0\\%+1.4\\times(9.0\\%-3.0\\%)=3.0\\%+1.4\\times6.0\\%=3.0\\%+8.4\\%=11.4\\%.$$ Choice C (12.6%) wrongly applies beta to the full market return (1.4 × 9.0%); D ignores the structure entirely; A is just the market return.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A portfolio has an expected return of 10.8% and the CAPM predicts an expected return of 9.5% given its beta. The difference between these two figures is best described as the portfolio's:\nA) Beta B) Tracking error C) Jensen's alpha D) Sharpe ratio\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Jensen's alpha is the difference between a portfolio's actual (or expected) return and the return predicted by CAPM for its beta: $$\\alpha=10.8\\%-9.5\\%=1.3\\%.$$ Tracking error is the standard deviation of active returns, not a single excess-return figure; beta and the Sharpe ratio measure different things entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Portfolio P has an expected return of 12%, a standard deviation of 16%, and a beta of 1.2. The risk-free rate is 2%. The Sharpe ratio of Portfolio P is closest to:\nA) 0.50 B) 0.625 C) 0.83 D) 8.33\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The Sharpe ratio uses total risk (standard deviation): $$\\frac{E(R_p)-R_f}{\\sigma_p}=\\frac{12\\%-2\\%}{16\\%}=\\frac{10\\%}{16\\%}=0.625.$$ Choice C (0.83) mistakenly divides by beta-scaled risk; A and D come from arithmetic errors. The trap is using beta instead of standard deviation.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Using the same Portfolio P (expected return 12%, standard deviation 16%, beta 1.2, risk-free rate 2%), the Treynor measure is closest to:\nA) 0.063 B) 0.083 C) 0.100 D) 0.120\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The Treynor measure uses systematic risk (beta) in the denominator: $$\\frac{E(R_p)-R_f}{\\beta_p}=\\frac{12\\%-2\\%}{1.2}=\\frac{0.10}{1.2}\\approx0.083.$$ Choice A divides by 16 (mixing in standard deviation); the trap is confusing Treynor (beta) with Sharpe (total risk).\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which statement best distinguishes the Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT) from the CAPM?\nA) APT requires that all investors hold the market portfolio B) APT allows for multiple systematic risk factors rather than a single market factor C) APT assumes returns are perfectly normally distributed while CAPM does not D) APT applies only to fixed-income securities\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** APT prices assets using exposures to multiple systematic risk factors and the no-arbitrage condition, whereas CAPM relies on a single market factor and the assumption that all investors hold the market portfolio. APT makes fewer restrictive assumptions about investor behavior and distributions and applies to all assets, so options A, C, and D are incorrect.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"APT vs CAPM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"APT\",\"items\":[\"Multiple systematic factors\",\"No-arbitrage pricing\",\"Fewer assumptions\"]},{\"h\":\"CAPM\",\"items\":[\"Single market factor\",\"All hold market portfolio\",\"More restrictive\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** An analyst computes the Sortino ratio rather than the Sharpe ratio for a fund. The key difference is that the Sortino ratio:\nA) Uses beta in the denominator instead of standard deviation B) Uses downside deviation (deviations below a target) in the denominator C) Subtracts the market return rather than the risk-free rate in the numerator D) Is always larger than the Sharpe ratio for the same fund\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The Sortino ratio replaces total standard deviation with downside deviation (the dispersion of returns below a minimum acceptable or target return), penalizing only harmful volatility. The numerator still uses excess return over a target/risk-free rate, not the market return, and the ratio is not guaranteed to be larger than the Sharpe ratio in all cases.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Sharpe vs Sortino denominator\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Sharpe\",\"items\":[\"Total std dev\",\"Penalizes all volatility\"]},{\"h\":\"Sortino\",\"items\":[\"Downside deviation\",\"Only below-target returns\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A business unit generates expected profit of $9 million and is allocated $60 million of economic capital. If the firm's hurdle rate is 12%, the unit's RAROC and the conclusion are best described as:\nA) RAROC of 15%; the unit is creating value because it exceeds the hurdle rate B) RAROC of 15%; the unit is destroying value because it is below the hurdle rate C) RAROC of 7.2%; the unit is creating value D) RAROC of 20%; the unit is destroying value\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$RAROC=\\frac{\\text{expected profit}}{\\text{economic capital}}=\\frac{\\$9\\text{m}}{\\$60\\text{m}}=15\\%.$$ Because 15% exceeds the 12% hurdle rate, the unit is adding value. Choice B reverses the value conclusion; C and D miscompute the ratio. The trap is inverting the create/destroy logic relative to the hurdle.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"RAROC vs hurdle rate\",\"items\":[\"Destroys value\",\"|12% hurdle\",\"RAROC 15% adds value\"],\"left\":\"Reject\",\"right\":\"Accept\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Enterprise risk management (ERM) is best characterized as an approach that:\nA) Manages each risk type in a separate silo to maximize specialization B) Aggregates and manages all of a firm's risks in an integrated, firm-wide framework C) Focuses exclusively on regulatory capital requirements D) Eliminates all risk from the firm's operations\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** ERM is an integrated, firm-wide approach that aggregates and manages all material risks together, capturing interdependencies and supporting risk-return decisions. Managing risks in silos (A) is what ERM seeks to overcome; ERM is broader than regulatory capital (C) and cannot eliminate all risk (D).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Silos vs ERM\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Silo approach\",\"items\":[\"Risks managed separately\",\"Misses interdependencies\"]},{\"h\":\"ERM\",\"items\":[\"Firm-wide aggregation\",\"Captures correlations\",\"Supports risk-return\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The collapse of Long-Term Capital Management (LTCM) in 1998 is most commonly attributed to which combination of factors?\nA) Outright fraud and falsified financial statements B) Extremely high leverage combined with convergence trades that diverged during a liquidity crisis C) A single unauthorized rogue trader hiding losses D) Failure of a clearinghouse to settle equity trades\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** LTCM employed very high leverage on relative-value/convergence trades that assumed spreads would narrow; when the 1998 Russian default triggered a flight to liquidity, spreads widened and positions diverged, generating massive mark-to-market losses amplified by leverage. It was not fraud, a single rogue trader, or a clearinghouse failure.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"LTCM collapse\",\"steps\":[\"High leverage on convergence trades\",\"1998 Russian default\",\"Flight to liquidity, spreads widen\",\"Leverage amplifies MTM losses\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The failure of Barings Bank in 1995 is primarily an example of which type of risk?\nA) Market risk arising from a deliberate, board-approved strategy B) Operational risk from inadequate internal controls allowing a rogue trader to hide losses C) Credit risk from a defaulting sovereign counterparty D) Liquidity risk from a sudden deposit run\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Barings collapsed because trader Nick Leeson took large unauthorized derivatives positions and concealed losses, made possible by inadequate segregation of duties and weak internal controls—an operational risk failure. The losses were not a board-approved strategy, nor a credit or deposit-run event.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Barings collapse (operational risk)\",\"steps\":[\"Leeson trades and settles (no segregation)\",\"Unauthorized derivatives positions\",\"Losses hidden in error account\",\"Weak controls let it grow\",\"Bank insolvent\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** During the 2007–2009 financial crisis, the practice of \"originate-to-distribute\" in mortgage lending contributed to the crisis primarily because it:\nA) Forced banks to retain all credit risk on their balance sheets B) Weakened lenders' incentives to screen and monitor borrower quality C) Eliminated the use of mortgage-backed securities D) Required all mortgages to be government insured\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Under originate-to-distribute, originators sold loans into securitizations and no longer bore most of the default risk, weakening their incentive to screen and monitor borrowers and contributing to lax underwriting. The model relied heavily on mortgage-backed securities rather than eliminating them, did not force banks to retain all risk, and did not require government insurance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Originate-to-distribute incentive\",\"steps\":[\"Originate loan\",\"Sell into securitization\",\"No longer bear default risk\",\"Weak screening, lax underwriting\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A subordinated tranche of a collateralized debt obligation (CDO) absorbs losses before more senior tranches. During 2007–2009, a key flaw exposed in the rating of senior tranches was that:\nA) Senior tranches were assumed to default first B) The default correlation among the underlying assets was underestimated C) Senior tranches carried higher coupons than equity tranches D) Rating agencies refused to rate structured products\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Senior CDO tranches were rated highly under models that assumed low default correlation among the underlying subprime mortgages; when the housing downturn caused correlated, simultaneous defaults, losses breached supposedly safe senior tranches. Senior tranches default last and carry lower coupons than equity tranches, and agencies did rate these products, so the other options are wrong.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Senior CDO tranche losses\",\"steps\":[\"Models assume low default correlation\",\"Senior tranches rated AAA\",\"Housing downturn: correlated defaults\",\"Losses breach senior tranches\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Under the GARP Code of Conduct, a risk professional who discovers that a colleague is manipulating a model's inputs to understate the firm's reported risk should:\nA) Ignore it, since model validation is not their responsibility B) Act with professional integrity and not knowingly participate in or conceal conduct that is misleading C) Immediately resign without informing anyone D) Inform competitors to protect the broader market\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The Code requires members to act with integrity and professional responsibility and not to knowingly engage in or conceal conduct that is dishonest, fraudulent, or misleading regarding risk information. Ignoring the manipulation, simply resigning silently, or informing competitors all fail the Code's standards of professional integrity and ethical conduct.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Discovered manipulation of risk data\",\"options\":[\"Ignore it\",\"Report per the Code\",\"Resign silently\",\"Inform competitors\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Must not conceal dishonest/misleading conduct\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A risk manager is offered a personal gift by a vendor seeking to win a software contract that the risk manager helps evaluate. Under the GARP Code of Conduct's principles on conflicts of interest, the risk manager should:\nA) Accept the gift, since the Code does not address vendor relationships B) Disclose the relationship and avoid conduct that compromises objectivity or creates a conflict of interest C) Accept the gift only if it is below the firm's tax-reporting threshold D) Recommend the vendor and keep the arrangement confidential\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The Code requires members to identify, disclose, and manage conflicts of interest and to maintain objectivity, avoiding gifts or arrangements that could compromise independent judgment. Accepting an inducement to influence a vendor decision—or keeping it confidential—violates the conflict-of-interest and objectivity principles; a tax threshold is not the relevant test.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Inducement affecting vendor choice\",\"options\":[\"Accept, under tax limit\",\"Accept, keep confidential\",\"Disclose and manage conflict\",\"Accept if small\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Identify, disclose, manage conflicts of interest\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Two perfectly positively correlated risky assets are combined into a portfolio. Compared with a portfolio of two assets with correlation of zero, the diversification benefit (reduction in portfolio standard deviation relative to the weighted average of the individual standard deviations) for the perfectly correlated pair is:\nA) Larger B) Identical C) Zero (no diversification benefit) D) Negative\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Diversification benefit comes from imperfect correlation. With a correlation of +1, the portfolio standard deviation equals the weighted average of the component standard deviations, so there is no reduction and thus no diversification benefit. With zero correlation the portfolio standard deviation falls below that weighted average, providing a benefit; the perfectly correlated case yields zero benefit, not a larger or negative one.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Correlation and diversification\",\"items\":[\"ρ=-1 max benefit\",\"ρ=0 benefit\",\"|ρ=+1 no benefit\"],\"left\":\"More diversification\",\"right\":\"None\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## Quantitative Analysis (1 mark)\n\n**Q21.** A credit analyst models loan defaults. The unconditional probability that a borrower defaults is 4%. Among defaulters, 70% had a prior late payment; among non-defaulters, 10% had a prior late payment. A randomly selected borrower is found to have a prior late payment. Using Bayes' theorem, what is the probability that this borrower defaults?\nA) 0.225 B) 0.226 C) 0.280 D) 0.700\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Let $D$ = default (0.04), $L$ = prior late payment. $P(L|D)=0.70$, $P(L|\\bar D)=0.10$. The total probability of a late payment is $P(L)=0.70(0.04)+0.10(0.96)=0.028+0.096=0.124$. Then $P(D|L)=\\frac{0.70\\times0.04}{0.124}=\\frac{0.028}{0.124}=0.2258\\approx0.226$. The trap (C, 0.280) confuses the joint probability $0.028$ scaled incorrectly, and (D, 0.700) is the reverse conditional $P(L|D)$; (A) drops a digit of precision.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A discrete random variable $X$ takes values $-2, 1, 4$ with probabilities 0.4, 0.4, and 0.2, respectively. What is the variance of $X$?\nA) 5.04 B) 5.44 C) 0.40 D) 4.24\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $E[X]=-2(0.4)+1(0.4)+4(0.2)=-0.8+0.4+0.8=0.4$. $E[X^2]=4(0.4)+1(0.4)+16(0.2)=1.6+0.4+3.2=5.2$. $\\text{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2=5.2-(0.4)^2=5.2-0.16=5.04$. The trap (B, 5.44) forgets to subtract the squared mean ($5.2$ rounded loosely is not it; 5.44 adds the mean term with the wrong sign); (C, 0.40) reports the mean instead of the variance; (D) miscomputes $E[X^2]$.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A portfolio's daily return has skewness of $-0.8$ and excess kurtosis of $2.5$. Which statement best describes the return distribution relative to the normal distribution?\nA) It has a longer right tail and thinner tails overall than the normal. B) It has a longer left tail and fatter tails than the normal. C) It is symmetric with thinner tails than the normal. D) It has a longer left tail and thinner tails than the normal.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Negative skewness ($-0.8$) indicates a longer/fatter left tail (large losses more likely than a symmetric distribution implies). Positive excess kurtosis ($+2.5 > 0$) indicates fatter tails (leptokurtic) than the normal. So the distribution has a longer left tail and fatter tails overall. (A) wrongly assigns the long tail to the right (that would be positive skew); (C) and (D) wrongly call the tails thin—positive excess kurtosis means fat tails.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Skew -0.8, excess kurtosis +2.5\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Skewness <0\",\"items\":[\"Longer/fatter LEFT tail\",\"Large losses likelier\"]},{\"h\":\"Excess kurt >0\",\"items\":[\"Leptokurtic\",\"Fatter tails than normal\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The number of operational loss events at a bank follows a Poisson distribution with an average of 3 events per month. What is the probability of observing exactly 2 events in a given month?\nA) 0.149 B) 0.224 C) 0.168 D) 0.240\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Poisson pmf: $P(X=k)=\\frac{\\lambda^k e^{-\\lambda}}{k!}$ with $\\lambda=3$, $k=2$. $P(X=2)=\\frac{3^2 e^{-3}}{2!}=\\frac{9\\times0.049787}{2}=\\frac{0.448}{2}=0.224$. The trap (A, 0.149) is $P(X=1)$; (C, 0.168) is $P(X=4)$; (D) is an arbitrary distractor.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A trader observes that the number of independent trades needed until the first profitable trade occurs follows a geometric distribution, where each trade is profitable with probability 0.25. What is the probability that the first profitable trade occurs on exactly the third trade?\nA) 0.1406 B) 0.4219 C) 0.2500 D) 0.1875\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Geometric pmf: $P(X=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p$. With $p=0.25$, $k=3$: $P=(0.75)^2(0.25)=0.5625\\times0.25=0.1406$. The trap (D, 0.1875) uses $(0.75)(0.25)=P(X=2)$; (B, 0.4219) is $(0.75)^2$ without multiplying by $p$; (C) is just $p$.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Two assets have returns with standard deviations of 12% and 18%. The covariance between their returns is $0.0108$. What is the correlation coefficient between the two assets?\nA) 0.50 B) 0.60 C) 0.75 D) 0.45\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $\\rho=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y}=\\frac{0.0108}{0.12\\times0.18}=\\frac{0.0108}{0.0216}=0.50$. The trap (B, 0.60) and (C, 0.75) come from dividing by the wrong product of standard deviations or using variances; (D) misorders the denominator.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A sample of 25 daily returns has a sample mean of 0.08% and a sample standard deviation of 0.50%. What is the standard error of the sample mean?\nA) 0.020% B) 0.100% C) 0.500% D) 0.016%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $SE=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}=\\frac{0.50\\%}{\\sqrt{25}}=\\frac{0.50\\%}{5}=0.10\\%$. The trap (A, 0.020%) divides by $n=25$ instead of $\\sqrt{n}$; (C) forgets to divide at all; (D) is spurious.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** An analyst tests whether the mean daily return of a strategy differs from zero. The sample of 36 observations has a mean of 0.12% and a sample standard deviation of 0.40%. Using a two-tailed test at the 5% significance level (critical value $\\approx 1.96$), what is the test statistic and the decision?\nA) $t = 1.80$; fail to reject $H_0$. B) $t = 1.80$; reject $H_0$. C) $t = 0.30$; fail to reject $H_0$. D) $t = 1.80$; the test is inconclusive.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $t=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt{n}}=\\frac{0.12\\%-0}{0.40\\%/\\sqrt{36}}=\\frac{0.12\\%}{0.40\\%/6}=\\frac{0.12\\%}{0.0667\\%}=1.80$. Since $|1.80| < 1.96$, fail to reject $H_0$ at 5%—the mean is not statistically distinguishable from zero. (B) reaches the wrong decision; (C) miscomputes the SE; (D) mislabels a clear non-rejection as inconclusive.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** In a hypothesis test, an analyst sets the significance level at 1% rather than 5%. Holding everything else constant, what is the effect of this change?\nA) The probability of a Type I error increases and the probability of a Type II error decreases. B) The probability of a Type I error decreases and the probability of a Type II error increases. C) Both Type I and Type II error probabilities decrease. D) Both Type I and Type II error probabilities increase.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Lowering the significance level from 5% to 1% directly reduces the probability of a Type I error (rejecting a true null). But making the test more conservative raises the Type II error probability (failing to reject a false null), reducing power. There is a trade-off, so (A), (C), and (D) violate the inverse relationship between the two error types at fixed sample size.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Lower alpha (5%→1%) trade-off\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Type I error\",\"items\":[\"Reject true null\",\"Falls (less likely)\"]},{\"h\":\"Type II error\",\"items\":[\"Fail to reject false null\",\"Rises, power falls\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** In a simple linear regression of portfolio excess returns on market excess returns, the estimated slope is 1.20 with a standard error of 0.30. To test $H_0: \\beta = 1$ against $H_a: \\beta \\neq 1$, what is the t-statistic?\nA) 4.00 B) 0.67 C) 0.50 D) 3.33\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Test against a hypothesized value of 1, not 0: $t=\\frac{\\hat\\beta-\\beta_0}{SE}=\\frac{1.20-1.00}{0.30}=\\frac{0.20}{0.30}=0.67$. The trap (A, 4.00) tests against zero ($1.20/0.30$); (D, 3.33) inverts the numerator/scale; (C) is spurious. With $t=0.67$ the slope is not significantly different from 1.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A simple linear regression produces a total sum of squares (TSS) of 500 and a residual sum of squares (SSR) of 150. What is the coefficient of determination ($R^2$)?\nA) 0.30 B) 0.70 C) 0.43 D) 0.75\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $R^2=1-\\frac{SSR}{TSS}=1-\\frac{150}{500}=1-0.30=0.70$. The explained sum of squares is $500-150=350$, so $R^2=350/500=0.70$. The trap (A, 0.30) reports $SSR/TSS$ (the unexplained fraction); (D, 0.75) misdivides; (C) is spurious.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a multiple regression with 5 independent variables estimated on 100 observations, the $R^2$ is 0.40. What is the adjusted $R^2$?\nA) 0.366 B) 0.400 C) 0.376 D) 0.385\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $\\bar R^2=1-(1-R^2)\\frac{n-1}{n-k-1}=1-(1-0.40)\\frac{99}{100-5-1}=1-0.60\\times\\frac{99}{94}=1-0.60\\times1.0532=1-0.6319=0.3681\\approx0.366$. (B) ignores the adjustment; (C) and (D) use the wrong degrees-of-freedom counts (e.g., $n-k$ instead of $n-k-1$).\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** An analyst examines the residuals of a time-series regression and finds that the variance of the residuals increases systematically with the level of an independent variable. Which problem is present, and what is a consequence?\nA) Multicollinearity; the coefficient estimates become biased. B) Heteroskedasticity; the OLS coefficient estimates remain unbiased but standard errors are unreliable. C) Serial correlation; the OLS coefficient estimates become biased. D) Heteroskedasticity; the OLS coefficient estimates become biased and inconsistent.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Residual variance changing systematically with a regressor is heteroskedasticity. Under heteroskedasticity, OLS coefficient estimates remain unbiased and consistent, but the usual standard errors are biased, making t-tests and F-tests unreliable (use White/robust standard errors). (A) describes multicollinearity wrongly (and OLS stays unbiased there too); (C) mislabels it as serial correlation; (D) wrongly claims the coefficients become biased.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Heteroskedasticity effect on OLS\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Coefficients\",\"items\":[\"Still unbiased\",\"Still consistent\"]},{\"h\":\"Standard errors\",\"items\":[\"Biased\",\"t/F tests unreliable\",\"Use robust SEs\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A covariance-stationary AR(1) process is given by $x_t = 0.4 + 0.6\\, x_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, where $\\varepsilon_t$ is white noise. What is the long-run (unconditional) mean of $x_t$?\nA) 0.40 B) 0.67 C) 1.00 D) 1.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** For $x_t=c+\\phi x_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t$ with $|\\phi|<1$, the unconditional mean is $\\mu=\\frac{c}{1-\\phi}=\\frac{0.4}{1-0.6}=\\frac{0.4}{0.4}=1.00$. The trap (A, 0.40) reports just the intercept; (B, 0.67) and (D, 1.50) use $1/(1+\\phi)$ or other wrong denominators.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** For the covariance-stationary AR(1) process $x_t = 0.4 + 0.6\\, x_{t-1} + \\varepsilon_t$, the current value is $x_t = 2.0$. What is the best two-step-ahead forecast $E[x_{t+2}]$?\nA) 1.60 B) 1.36 C) 1.216 D) 1.456\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Iterate forward. One-step: $E[x_{t+1}]=0.4+0.6(2.0)=1.6$. Two-step: $E[x_{t+2}]=0.4+0.6(1.6)=0.4+0.96=1.36$. Equivalently, the mean-reversion form gives $E[x_{t+2}]=\\mu+\\phi^2(x_t-\\mu)=1.0+0.6^2(2.0-1.0)=1.0+0.36=1.36$, using $\\mu=1.0$ from Q34. (A, 1.60) is the one-step forecast; (C, 1.216) over-iterates one extra step; (D, 1.456) miscomputes the intercept term.\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** An analyst runs a Dickey-Fuller test to determine whether a time series contains a unit root. The null and alternative hypotheses of the test are best described as:\nA) $H_0$: the series is stationary; $H_a$: the series has a unit root. B) $H_0$: the series has a unit root (non-stationary); $H_a$: the series is stationary. C) $H_0$: the series has no autocorrelation; $H_a$: the series is autocorrelated. D) $H_0$: the series is a random walk with drift; $H_a$: the series has no drift.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The Dickey-Fuller test sets the null as the presence of a unit root (the series is non-stationary / a random walk), with the alternative that the series is stationary. Rejecting the null provides evidence of stationarity. (A) reverses the hypotheses; (C) describes an autocorrelation (Ljung-Box) test; (D) misstates the drift framing.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Dickey-Fuller hypotheses\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Null H0\",\"items\":[\"Unit root present\",\"Non-stationary / random walk\"]},{\"h\":\"Alt H1\",\"items\":[\"Stationary\",\"Reject H0 = evidence of stationarity\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A risk manager estimates volatility using an EWMA model with decay factor $\\lambda = 0.94$. Yesterday's estimated daily volatility was 1.5% and yesterday's actual return was $-2.0\\%$. What is today's updated estimate of daily volatility?\nA) 1.50% B) 1.54% C) 1.58% D) 1.63%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** EWMA variance update: $\\sigma_t^2=\\lambda\\sigma_{t-1}^2+(1-\\lambda)u_{t-1}^2$. Yesterday's variance $=(0.015)^2=0.000225$; yesterday's squared return $=(-0.02)^2=0.0004$. $\\sigma_t^2=0.94(0.000225)+0.06(0.0004)=0.0002115+0.000024=0.0002355$. $\\sigma_t=\\sqrt{0.0002355}=0.01535\\approx1.54\\%$. The larger-than-yesterday volatility reflects the bigger shock ($2.0\\%$ vs the $1.5\\%$ prior vol). (A, 1.50%) ignores the new return entirely; (C, 1.58%) and (D, 1.63%) overweight the shock (e.g., using too small a $\\lambda$).\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A GARCH(1,1) volatility model is specified as $\\sigma_t^2 = \\omega + \\alpha u_{t-1}^2 + \\beta \\sigma_{t-1}^2$ with $\\omega = 0.000002$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, and $\\beta = 0.93$. What is the long-run (unconditional) daily variance implied by the model?\nA) 0.000100 B) 0.000040 C) 0.000020 D) 0.000200\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Long-run variance $V_L=\\frac{\\omega}{1-\\alpha-\\beta}=\\frac{0.000002}{1-0.05-0.93}=\\frac{0.000002}{0.02}=0.0001$. The persistence $\\alpha+\\beta=0.98$, so $1-0.98=0.02$. The trap (C, 0.000020) reports $\\omega/0.1$ or forgets to divide; (B) and (D) use wrong denominators. A high $\\alpha+\\beta$ near 1 means slow mean reversion.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A risk team estimates a 99% VaR using Monte Carlo simulation. To reduce the standard error of the simulated VaR estimate by a factor of 2 (i.e., to halve it), how must the number of simulation trials change?\nA) Double the number of trials. B) Quadruple the number of trials. C) Halve the number of trials. D) Increase the number of trials by 50%.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The standard error of a Monte Carlo estimate scales as $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{N}}$. To halve the standard error, $\\sqrt{N}$ must double, so $N$ must increase by a factor of $2^2=4$ (quadruple). (A) doubling only reduces SE by $\\sqrt{2}\\approx1.41$; (C) and (D) move the wrong direction or amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A data scientist trains a machine learning model that achieves very high accuracy on the training data but performs poorly on out-of-sample test data. Which of the following best describes this situation and an appropriate remedy?\nA) Underfitting; increase model complexity. B) Overfitting; apply regularization or use cross-validation to reduce model complexity. C) Underfitting; apply regularization. D) Overfitting; increase the number of model parameters.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** High training accuracy with poor test performance is the classic signature of overfitting: the model has learned noise in the training data and fails to generalize. Remedies include regularization (e.g., LASSO/ridge penalties), reducing model complexity, gathering more data, and using cross-validation to tune complexity. (A) and (C) misdiagnose it as underfitting (which shows poor performance on both training and test sets); (D) would worsen overfitting by adding parameters.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Overfitting vs Underfitting\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Overfitting\",\"items\":[\"High train, low test\",\"Learns noise\",\"Fix: regularize, more data, CV\"]},{\"h\":\"Underfitting\",\"items\":[\"Poor on both sets\",\"Too simple\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## Financial Markets and Products (1 mark)\n\n**Q41.** A bank reports the following for its trading book over 250 trading days: it experienced losses exceeding its 99% one-day VaR on 7 days. Under the Basel backtesting \"traffic light\" framework, into which zone does this outcome fall, and what is the supervisory consequence?\nA) Green zone; no increase in the capital multiplier. B) Yellow zone; the multiplier is increased at supervisory discretion. C) Red zone; the multiplier is automatically increased to its maximum and the model may be disallowed. D) Yellow zone; the model is automatically disallowed.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With 250 days, green = 0–4 exceptions, yellow = 5–9, red = 10+. Seven exceptions lands in the yellow zone, where the capital multiplier is increased at supervisory discretion (the add-on ranges roughly 0.40–0.85 depending on the count) and the model is not automatically rejected. Trap: A understates the consequence (yellow does raise capital); C and D apply only to the red zone (10+ exceptions), which triggers an automatic maximum multiplier and possible model disallowance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Basel traffic light (250 days)\",\"items\":[\"Green 0-4\",\"|Yellow 5-9 (7 here)\",\"|Red 10+\"],\"left\":\"No add-on\",\"right\":\"Max multiplier\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A life insurance company sells a large block of whole-life policies. Which of the following risks is the company MOST directly exposed to from the perspective of mortality, and how does it differ from the risk in an annuity portfolio?\nA) Longevity risk; in annuities the insurer profits if policyholders live longer. B) Mortality risk (loss if deaths occur sooner than expected); annuities expose the insurer to longevity risk (loss if annuitants live longer than expected). C) Both expose the insurer to identical mortality risk, so they offset perfectly. D) Mortality risk only; annuities carry no biometric risk.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A whole-life (death-benefit) policy loses money for the insurer if the insured dies sooner than priced — that is mortality risk. An annuity pays as long as the annuitant lives, so the insurer loses if annuitants live longer than expected — that is longevity risk. The two risks are opposite in direction, which is why insurers writing both can achieve a natural hedge. Trap: A reverses the annuity payoff (the insurer is hurt, not helped, by longer lives); C wrongly claims a perfect offset; D ignores that annuities carry longevity (biometric) risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Mortality vs Longevity risk\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Life policy\",\"items\":[\"Pays on death\",\"Insurer loses if dies early\",\"Mortality risk\"]},{\"h\":\"Annuity\",\"items\":[\"Pays while alive\",\"Insurer loses if lives long\",\"Longevity risk\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An open-end mutual fund and a hedge fund both report a 12% gross annual return. The hedge fund charges \"2 and 20\" with a hard hurdle rate of 5% and a high-water mark, while the mutual fund charges a flat 1% management fee. The high-water mark is not binding this year. What net return does an investor in the hedge fund earn, assuming fees are computed on year-end NAV per the standard convention (2% management fee on assets, 20% incentive on return above hurdle)?\nA) 8.6% B) 9.4% C) 8.0% D) 9.6%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Management fee = 2% of assets. Incentive fee = 20% of return above the 5% hard hurdle = 20% × (12% − 5%) = 20% × 7% = 1.4%. Net return = 12% − 2% − 1.4% = 8.6%. Trap: B (9.4%) forgets the management fee is also deducted from incentive-fee math or applies 20% to the full 12%? D (9.6%) charges incentive on (12%−5%) but omits management fee; C (8.0%) charges 20% on the full 12% with no hurdle.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A trader holds a short position in 20 futures contracts. The exchange clearinghouse stands between the trader and the original counterparty. Which statement about the clearinghouse's role is correct?\nA) The clearinghouse guarantees performance and becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer, eliminating bilateral counterparty risk. B) The clearinghouse only records trades and does not take on counterparty exposure. C) The clearinghouse requires margin only from the long side because the short side cannot default. D) The clearinghouse eliminates market risk for both parties.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A central counterparty (CCP)/clearinghouse novates each trade: it becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer, guaranteeing performance and removing bilateral counterparty risk, backed by margin and a default fund. Trap: B describes a mere trade repository; C is wrong because both sides post margin (either can default); D is wrong — clearing addresses credit/counterparty risk, not market (price) risk.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CCP novation\",\"steps\":[\"Buyer and seller agree trade\",\"CCP novates: becomes counterparty to each\",\"Both post margin + default fund\",\"Bilateral counterparty risk removed\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A futures contract on silver requires an initial margin of $6,000 and a maintenance margin of $4,500 per contract. An investor goes long 4 contracts. Each contract is for 5,000 ounces. If the silver price falls by $0.40 per ounce on day 1, what is the variation margin (margin call) the investor must post to restore the account, if any?\nA) No margin call; account is above maintenance. B) A margin call for $8,000. C) A margin call for $2,000. D) A margin call for $6,000.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Loss = $0.40 × 5,000 oz × 4 contracts = $8,000. Starting balance = 4 × $6,000 = $24,000; after loss = $16,000. Maintenance level = 4 × $4,500 = $18,000. Balance ($16,000) is below maintenance, so a call is triggered and the account must be restored to the initial margin: $24,000 − $16,000 = $8,000. Trap: C ($2,000) restores only to the maintenance level (wrong — calls top up to initial margin); A ignores that the balance fell below maintenance.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Margin call mechanics\",\"items\":[\"Balance $16k\",\"|Maintenance $18k\",\"Initial $24k\"],\"left\":\"Below = call\",\"right\":\"Top up to initial\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A portfolio manager holds a $50 million equity portfolio with a beta of 1.30 relative to the S&P 500. S&P 500 futures are priced at an index level of 5,000 with a multiplier of $250. The manager wishes to reduce the portfolio beta to 0.60. How many futures contracts should be traded, and in which direction?\nA) Short 28 contracts. B) Short 52 contracts. C) Long 28 contracts. D) Short 130 contracts.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Number of contracts $= (\\beta_{target} - \\beta_{port}) \\times \\dfrac{V_{port}}{F \\times m} = (0.60 - 1.30) \\times \\dfrac{50{,}000{,}000}{5{,}000 \\times 250} = -0.70 \\times \\dfrac{50{,}000{,}000}{1{,}250{,}000} = -0.70 \\times 40 = -28$. The negative sign means short 28 contracts. Trap: B (52) uses the full beta 1.30 instead of the difference; C reverses the sign (long); D mis-scales the contract value.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A company will buy 1,000,000 gallons of jet fuel in 3 months. There is no jet fuel futures contract, so it hedges with heating oil futures (42,000 gallons per contract). The standard deviation of changes in the jet fuel price is 0.032, the standard deviation of changes in the heating oil futures price is 0.040, and the correlation is 0.80. What is the optimal number of contracts (rounded)?\nA) 24 B) 15 C) 19 D) 30\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Optimal hedge ratio $h^* = \\rho \\dfrac{\\sigma_S}{\\sigma_F} = 0.80 \\times \\dfrac{0.032}{0.040} = 0.64$. Contracts $= h^* \\times \\dfrac{Q_A}{Q_F} = 0.64 \\times \\dfrac{1{,}000{,}000}{42{,}000} = 0.64 \\times 23.81 = 15.24 \\approx 15$. Trap: A (24) omits the hedge ratio (uses 1.0); D (30) inverts the volatility ratio; C uses $\\rho=1$ partially.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** The spot exchange rate is 1.2500 USD per GBP. The one-year USD risk-free rate is 4.0% and the one-year GBP risk-free rate is 6.0% (both continuously compounded). According to covered interest rate parity, what is the one-year forward USD/GBP rate?\nA) 1.2747 B) 1.2253 C) 1.2500 D) 1.2752\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r_{USD} - r_{GBP})T} = 1.2500 \\times e^{(0.04 - 0.06)\\times 1} = 1.2500 \\times e^{-0.02} = 1.2500 \\times 0.98020 = 1.2253$. Because the GBP rate exceeds the USD rate, GBP trades at a forward discount. Trap: A (1.2747) flips the sign in the exponent ($e^{+0.02}$); C ignores rate differential; D mis-signs.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Using the data in Question 48, suppose the actual one-year forward rate quoted in the market is 1.2500 USD/GBP. Which arbitrage strategy locks in a riskless profit?\nA) Borrow USD, convert to GBP, invest in GBP, sell GBP forward. B) Borrow GBP, convert to USD, invest in USD, buy GBP forward. C) Borrow GBP, convert to USD, invest in USD, sell USD forward. D) No arbitrage exists because the forward equals spot.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The fair forward is 1.2253 but the market quotes 1.2500, so the forward GBP is overpriced — sell GBP forward (high) and obtain GBP cheaply for delivery. The financing trade: borrow USD at 4%, convert to GBP at spot, invest GBP at 6%, and sell the GBP proceeds forward at 1.2500. The 6% − 4% interest pickup plus the rich forward locks in profit. Trap: B/C go the wrong direction (buying the overpriced forward); D ignores that forward ≠ fair value, not forward ≠ spot.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"CIRP arbitrage (forward rich)\",\"steps\":[\"Borrow USD at 4%\",\"Convert to GBP at spot\",\"Invest GBP at 6%\",\"Sell GBP forward at rich 1.2500\",\"Lock in riskless profit\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $80. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 5%. What is the theoretical 9-month forward price, and what arbitrage is available if the forward is quoted at $85?\nA) $83.06; sell the forward, buy the stock with borrowed money (cash-and-carry). B) $83.06; buy the forward, short the stock (reverse cash-and-carry). C) $82.04; sell the forward, buy the stock. D) $84.10; no arbitrage.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $F_0 = S_0 e^{rT} = 80 \\times e^{0.05 \\times 0.75} = 80 \\times e^{0.0375} = 80 \\times 1.03821 = 83.06$. The market forward ($85) exceeds fair value, so the forward is overpriced: sell (short) the forward and buy the stock with borrowed funds (cash-and-carry), delivering into the short forward for a locked gain of about $85 − $83.06 = $1.94. Trap: B reverses the arbitrage direction; C/D miscompute the carry.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Cash-and-carry (forward rich)\",\"steps\":[\"Borrow funds at r\",\"Buy stock at $80 spot\",\"Short the $85 forward\",\"At T deliver stock, repay loan\",\"Lock ~$1.94 gain\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A stock index is at 3,000 and provides a continuous dividend yield of 2.5%. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4.5%. What is the fair 6-month forward price of the index?\nA) 3,030.2 B) 3,060.7 C) 2,970.3 D) 3,000.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-q)T} = 3{,}000 \\times e^{(0.045 - 0.025)\\times 0.5} = 3{,}000 \\times e^{0.01} = 3{,}000 \\times 1.01005 = 3{,}030.2$. Trap: B uses $r$ without subtracting the dividend yield; C subtracts the wrong way ($q-r$); D ignores carry entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Gold currently trades at $1,900 per ounce. Storage costs are 1.5% per year (continuously compounded, paid as a proportion of value) and the risk-free rate is 4% per year continuously compounded. There is no convenience yield. What is the 1-year forward price?\nA) $1,925.6 B) $2,007.4 C) $1,978.4 D) $1,824.0\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Storage cost as a proportional rate is added to $r$: $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u)T} = 1{,}900 \\times e^{(0.04 + 0.015)\\times 1} = 1{,}900 \\times e^{0.055} = 1{,}900 \\times 1.05654 = 2{,}007.4$. Trap: A treats storage as reducing the cost of carry; C uses only $r$; D subtracts both.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** For a consumption commodity, the relationship between the forward price and spot is best described by which equation, where $u$ is storage cost, $r$ is the risk-free rate, and $y$ is the convenience yield (all continuously compounded rates)?\nA) $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$ B) $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u+y)T}$ C) $F_0 \\le S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$ with equality only when convenience yield is zero D) $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r-u-y)T}$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** For a consumption asset, holders derive a convenience yield, so arbitrage gives only an inequality: $F_0 \\le S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$. Equivalently $F_0 = S_0 e^{(r+u-y)T}$ holds only after introducing the convenience yield $y$; with $y \\ge 0$ the forward cannot exceed $S_0 e^{(r+u)T}$, and equality requires $y=0$. Trap: A is the equality form (true only with the convenience-yield term made explicit but stated as exact equality, which over-claims); B adds $y$ with the wrong sign; D mis-signs storage.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Investment vs Consumption asset\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Investment\",\"items\":[\"F0 = S0 e^(r+u)T\",\"Exact equality\"]},{\"h\":\"Consumption\",\"items\":[\"F0 ≤ S0 e^(r+u)T\",\"Convenience yield y\",\"Equality only if y=0\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A European call and put on a non-dividend stock both have strike $50 and expire in 6 months. The stock is at $52, the risk-free rate is 4% continuously compounded, and the call is priced at $5.20. Using put-call parity, what is the fair price of the put?\nA) $2.21 B) $3.20 C) $7.20 D) $1.21\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Parity: $p = c - S_0 + K e^{-rT} = 5.20 - 52 + 50 e^{-0.04 \\times 0.5} = 5.20 - 52 + 50(0.98020) = 5.20 - 52 + 49.01 = 2.21$. Trap: C ($7.20) forgets to discount/net the stock; B and D arise from sign or discounting errors on the strike.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Which of the following is an upper bound on the price of an American put option with strike $K$ on a non-dividend-paying stock?\nA) $K$ B) $K e^{-rT}$ C) $S_0$ D) $S_0 - K e^{-rT}$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** An American put can be exercised any time, and its maximum payoff is the strike $K$ (received when the stock falls to zero); so the price cannot exceed $K$. (A European put is bounded by $K e^{-rT}$.) Trap: B is the European-put bound (American is the looser $K$); C is the call upper bound; D is a lower-bound expression for a call.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Put upper bound\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"American put\",\"items\":[\"Exercise anytime\",\"Max payoff = K\",\"Price ≤ K\"]},{\"h\":\"European put\",\"items\":[\"Exercise at expiry\",\"Price ≤ K e^(-rT)\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** An investor creates a bull spread using calls: buys a call with strike $45 for $6 and sells a call with strike $55 for $2. What is the maximum profit and the breakeven stock price at expiration?\nA) Max profit $6; breakeven $49. B) Max profit $10; breakeven $51. C) Max profit $6; breakeven $51. D) Max profit $4; breakeven $49.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Net premium paid = $6 − $2 = $4 (debit). Maximum profit at expiration = spread width − net cost = ($55 − $45) − $4 = $6. Breakeven = lower strike + net cost = $45 + $4 = $49. Trap: B ignores the premiums paid (uses full $10 spread, breakeven at midpoint); C adds the cost to the wrong strike; D mislabels max profit as the net debit.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Bull call spread\",\"steps\":[\"Net debit = $6 - $2 = $4\",\"Breakeven = $45 + $4 = $49\",\"Max profit = ($55-$45) - $4 = $6\",\"Max loss = $4 net debit\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** An investor establishes a long straddle by buying a call and a put, both struck at $100, paying total premium of $9. At expiration the stock is at $112. What is the investor's net profit per share?\nA) $3 B) $12 C) $21 D) $9\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** At $112 the call (strike 100) is worth $12 and the put is worth $0. Total payoff = $12. Net profit = payoff − premium = $12 − $9 = $3. Trap: B reports gross payoff and forgets the $9 premium; C double counts both legs; D reports the premium itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which statement correctly distinguishes a knock-out barrier option from a standard (vanilla) option?\nA) A knock-out option ceases to exist if the underlying reaches the barrier; therefore it is never more expensive than the otherwise-identical vanilla option. B) A knock-out option is always more expensive than the vanilla because of the extra barrier feature. C) A down-and-out call pays off only if the barrier is breached. D) Barrier options cannot be priced and are always more expensive than vanilla options.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A knock-out option is extinguished if the underlying touches the barrier, removing some payoff scenarios that a vanilla option keeps; therefore it is worth no more than (and usually less than) the otherwise-identical vanilla. By in-out parity, knock-in + knock-out = vanilla. Trap: B reverses the value relationship; C describes a knock-in, not a knock-out (down-and-out call dies if the barrier is breached); D is simply false.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Knock-out vs Vanilla\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Knock-out\",\"items\":[\"Dies if barrier touched\",\"Fewer payoff paths\",\"Worth ≤ vanilla\"]},{\"h\":\"In-out parity\",\"items\":[\"Knock-in + knock-out\",\"= vanilla option\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** The 1-year zero rate is 3.0% and the 2-year zero rate is 3.6% (both continuously compounded). What is the forward rate for the second year (from year 1 to year 2)?\nA) 4.2% B) 3.3% C) 3.6% D) 4.0%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** With continuous compounding, the forward rate from $T_1$ to $T_2$ is $f = \\dfrac{R_2 T_2 - R_1 T_1}{T_2 - T_1} = \\dfrac{0.036 \\times 2 - 0.030 \\times 1}{2 - 1} = \\dfrac{0.072 - 0.030}{1} = 0.042 = 4.2\\%$. Trap: B averages the two zero rates; C just restates the 2-year zero; D mis-weights.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A 2-year bond with a face value of $100 pays an annual coupon of 5% (paid annually) and yields 4% (annual compounding). What is its price?\nA) $101.89 B) $100.00 C) $98.15 D) $101.00\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $P = \\dfrac{5}{1.04} + \\dfrac{105}{1.04^2} = 4.808 + 97.078 = 101.89$. Because the 5% coupon exceeds the 4% yield, the bond trades at a premium. Trap: B (par) ignores the coupon-yield gap; C discounts at the coupon rate instead of the yield; D is a rough average.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A corporate bond is quoted at a credit spread of 150 basis points over the equivalent Treasury. The bond has a modified duration of 6.0. If the credit spread widens by 25 basis points while the Treasury yield is unchanged, what is the approximate percentage price change of the corporate bond?\nA) -1.50% B) -0.375% C) -1.875% D) -0.25%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Approximate price change $= -D_{mod} \\times \\Delta y = -6.0 \\times 0.0025 = -0.015 = -1.50\\%$. A spread widening of 25 bp acts exactly like a 25 bp yield increase for the corporate bond. Trap: B and D divide by extra factors; C uses 31.25 bp or doubles the move.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A mortgage pool has a single monthly mortality (SMM) of 0.5%. What is the approximate annualized conditional prepayment rate (CPR)?\nA) 6.00% B) 5.84% C) 0.50% D) 6.17%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $CPR = 1 - (1 - SMM)^{12} = 1 - (1 - 0.005)^{12} = 1 - 0.995^{12} = 1 - 0.94162 = 0.05838 = 5.84\\%$. Trap: A simply multiplies $0.5\\% \\times 12 = 6.0\\%$ (ignores compounding); D mis-compounds; C confuses CPR with SMM.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A pass-through MBS pool has a beginning-of-month balance of $10,000,000. Scheduled principal for the month is $40,000 and the SMM is 0.40%. What is the prepayment (the unscheduled principal) for the month?\nA) $39,840 B) $40,000 C) $40,160 D) $39,400\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Prepayment (unscheduled principal) $= SMM \\times (\\text{beginning balance} - \\text{scheduled principal}) = 0.004 \\times (10{,}000{,}000 - 40{,}000) = 0.004 \\times 9{,}960{,}000 = 39{,}840$. Trap: B applies SMM to the full beginning balance and equals scheduled principal by coincidence of phrasing; C adds instead of subtracts scheduled principal; D miscomputes the base.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which feature of a residential mortgage-backed security creates negative convexity from the investor's perspective?\nA) The credit risk of the borrowers. B) The prepayment option held by borrowers, who tend to refinance when rates fall. C) The servicing fee deducted each month. D) The fixed maturity of the underlying loans.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Borrowers hold a prepayment (call-like) option: when rates fall they refinance, returning principal early and capping the MBS price appreciation. This embedded short-call position produces negative convexity for the investor. Trap: A is credit (default) risk, not convexity; C is a yield reduction, not a convexity source; D (fixed maturity) is unrelated to the optionality.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"MBS negative convexity\",\"steps\":[\"Borrower holds prepayment (call) option\",\"Rates fall → refinance\",\"Principal returned early\",\"Price appreciation capped\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A Treasury bond futures contract allows the short to deliver any of several bonds. The cheapest-to-deliver (CTD) bond is determined by minimizing which quantity, where the quoted bond price is $P$, the conversion factor is $CF$, and the futures settlement price is $F$?\nA) $P - (F \\times CF)$ B) $(F \\times CF) - P$ C) $P + (F \\times CF)$ D) $P / CF$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** The short's cost of buying a bond and delivering it is the quoted price $P$ less the invoice received, which is the futures price times the conversion factor: net cost $= P - (F \\times CF)$. The CTD minimizes this quantity. Trap: B reverses the sign (this would be maximized, the delivery profit); C adds rather than nets; D omits the futures price entirely.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"steps\",\"title\":\"Cheapest-to-deliver\",\"steps\":[\"Buy bond at quoted price P\",\"Receive invoice = F × CF\",\"Net cost = P - (F × CF)\",\"CTD minimizes net cost\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Consider three deliverable bonds for a Treasury futures contract with futures price 98-00 (i.e., 98.00). Quoted prices and conversion factors are: Bond X price 105.20, CF 1.0700; Bond Y price 110.50, CF 1.1200; Bond Z price 101.00, CF 1.0250. Which bond is cheapest to deliver?\nA) Bond X B) Bond Y C) Bond Z D) All are equally cheap.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Compute $P - (F \\times CF)$ with $F = 98.00$: Bond X $= 105.20 - 98 \\times 1.0700 = 105.20 - 104.86 = 0.34$; Bond Y $= 110.50 - 98 \\times 1.1200 = 110.50 - 109.76 = 0.74$; Bond Z $= 101.00 - 98 \\times 1.0250 = 101.00 - 100.45 = 0.55$. Bond X has the smallest cost, so it is cheapest to deliver. Trap: choosing the lowest quoted price (Z) or lowest CF ignores the conversion-factor adjustment.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A Eurodollar futures contract has a quoted price of 97.50. Each contract has a notional of $1,000,000 and a contract reference period of 3 months. What is the contract value (the price), and what does a 1-basis-point move change the value by?\nA) Value $993,750; $25 per basis point. B) Value $975,000; $25 per basis point. C) Value $993,750; $100 per basis point. D) Value $975,000; $100 per basis point.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Contract price $= 10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25 \\times (100 - Q)] = 10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.25 \\times 2.50] = 10{,}000 \\times [100 - 0.625] = 10{,}000 \\times 99.375 = \\$993{,}750$. A 1-bp move changes the value by $\\$1{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0001 \\times 0.25 = \\$25$. Trap: B uses 975,000 (treats the quote as a simple percentage of notional); C/D use $100 per bp (forgetting the 3-month, 0.25-year factor).\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Why must a convexity adjustment be applied when inferring forward rates from Eurodollar futures rates?\nA) Because Eurodollar futures are settled daily and the futures rate exceeds the forward rate, so the forward rate is below the futures rate. B) Because the forward rate always exceeds the futures rate due to credit risk. C) Because the convexity adjustment increases the implied forward rate above the futures rate. D) Because Eurodollar futures have no daily settlement.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Because Eurodollar futures are marked to market daily, gains are reinvested (and losses funded) in a way that correlates with rate moves, making the futures rate exceed the equivalent forward rate. The adjustment therefore lowers the futures rate to obtain the forward: forward rate = futures rate − convexity adjustment. Trap: B/C state the relationship backwards (claiming forward > futures or that the adjustment raises the rate); D denies daily settlement, the very source of the effect.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Eurodollar convexity adjustment\",\"items\":[\"Forward rate\",\"|minus adjustment\",\"Futures rate (higher)\"],\"left\":\"Lower\",\"right\":\"Higher\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A plain-vanilla interest rate swap has a notional of $100 million, and the company pays fixed 5% annually and receives floating. There are exactly 2 years remaining with annual payments. The continuously compounded discount (zero) rates are 2.5% for 1 year and 3.0% for 2 years. The floating rate was just reset such that the value of the floating leg equals the notional immediately after the just-made payment. What is the value of the swap to the fixed-rate payer?\nA) +$3.76 million B) -$3.76 million C) +$1.85 million D) -$1.85 million\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Value to the fixed payer = $B_{float} - B_{fix}$. Just after a reset, $B_{float} = \\$100$M (par). Fixed leg: $B_{fix} = 5 e^{-0.025 \\times 1} + 105 e^{-0.03 \\times 2} = 5(0.97531) + 105(0.94176) = 4.877 + 98.885 = \\$103.76$M. Value = $100 - 103.76 = -\\$3.76$M to the fixed payer (paying 5% when market rates are ~2.5–3.0% is unfavorable). Trap: A flips the sign (that is the value to the floating payer/fixed receiver); C/D use the wrong coupon or mis-discount.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Swap value (two-bond method)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Fixed payer\",\"items\":[\"V = Bfloat - Bfix\",\"Bfloat = par at reset\",\"Bfix = $103.76M → V = -$3.76M\"]},{\"h\":\"Fixed receiver\",\"items\":[\"V = Bfix - Bfloat\",\"Opposite sign\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** In a currency swap, Company A pays interest in euros and receives interest in U.S. dollars, with principals exchanged at initiation and maturity. Which statement about valuing this swap as the difference of two bonds is correct (from A's perspective)?\nA) The swap value equals the dollar bond value minus the euro bond value converted to dollars at the current spot rate. B) The swap value equals the euro bond value minus the dollar bond value, both in euros. C) Principal exchange is irrelevant to valuation because it nets to zero. D) The swap value equals the sum of the two bond values converted to dollars.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** From A's view (pay EUR, receive USD), the swap is long a USD-denominated bond and short a EUR-denominated bond. Its value in dollars = $B_{USD} - S_0 \\times B_{EUR}$, i.e., the dollar bond value minus the euro bond value converted to dollars at the current spot rate. Trap: B values it in euros with the legs reversed; C wrongly ignores principal exchange (in a currency swap principals do not net because they are in different currencies); D adds the legs instead of netting.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Currency swap (pay EUR, receive USD)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Long\",\"items\":[\"USD-denominated bond\",\"B_USD\"]},{\"h\":\"Short\",\"items\":[\"EUR-denominated bond\",\"S0 × B_EUR\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n## Valuation and Risk Models (1 mark)\n\n**Q71.** A portfolio has a daily mean return of zero and a daily return standard deviation of $2,000,000. Returns are assumed normally distributed. What is the 1-day 99% value at risk (VaR)?\nA) $3,290,000 B) $4,652,000 C) $4,000,000 D) $2,326,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With zero mean, $$VaR_{99\\%} = z \\times \\sigma = 2.326 \\times 2{,}000{,}000 = 4{,}652{,}000.$$ Trap: option A uses the 99.95% / 3.29 figure incorrectly; option D uses $z$ alone ($2.326 \\times 1{,}000{,}000$ style mismatch / forgets to multiply the full sigma); option C uses $z=2.0$. The correct one-tailed 99% z-value is 2.326.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A risk analyst computes the 10-day 95% VaR of a position by scaling a 1-day 95% VaR of $500,000 using the square-root-of-time rule. Assuming i.i.d. returns, the 10-day VaR is closest to:\nA) $5,000,000 B) $1,581,139 C) $1,118,034 D) $2,236,068\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Under i.i.d. returns, $$VaR_{10} = VaR_1 \\times \\sqrt{10} = 500{,}000 \\times 3.1623 = 1{,}581{,}139.$$ Trap: option A scales linearly by 10 (wrong — that would assume perfectly correlated, not i.i.d.); options C and D use $\\sqrt5$ and $\\sqrt{20}$.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Which of the following statements about expected shortfall (ES) and value at risk (VaR) is correct?\nA) VaR satisfies subadditivity for all loss distributions, whereas ES does not. B) ES is the probability that a loss exceeds the VaR threshold. C) ES is a coherent risk measure, while VaR can violate subadditivity. D) ES is always smaller than VaR at the same confidence level.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** ES (expected shortfall) is coherent: it satisfies monotonicity, translation invariance, positive homogeneity, and subadditivity. VaR can violate subadditivity for non-elliptical/fat-tailed distributions, so it is not coherent in general. Trap: A reverses the two measures; B confuses ES with a tail probability (ES is an average tail loss, not a probability); D is false — ES $\\geq$ VaR at the same confidence level.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"VaR vs Expected Shortfall\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"VaR\",\"items\":[\"A quantile/threshold\",\"Can violate subadditivity\",\"Not coherent in general\"]},{\"h\":\"ES\",\"items\":[\"Avg loss beyond VaR\",\"Subadditive\",\"Coherent\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A loss distribution is discrete. At the 95% confidence level the VaR is $8 million. Losses in the worst 5% tail are $9 million, $11 million, and $16 million, each occurring with equal conditional probability. The 95% expected shortfall is closest to:\nA) $8,000,000 B) $11,000,000 C) $12,000,000 D) $16,000,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** ES is the average loss conditional on exceeding VaR. The three equally likely tail losses average to $$\\frac{9 + 11 + 16}{3} = \\frac{36}{3} = 12 \\text{ million}.$$ Trap: option A reports VaR itself, not ES; B picks the median tail loss; D picks the worst case only.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Which of the following is NOT one of the four properties (axioms) that a coherent risk measure must satisfy?\nA) Monotonicity B) Translation invariance C) Positive homogeneity D) Risk neutrality\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: D)** The four coherence axioms are monotonicity, translation invariance, positive homogeneity, and subadditivity. \"Risk neutrality\" is not one of them. Trap: candidates may not recall that subadditivity (not listed) is the fourth axiom, and that \"risk neutrality\" is a distractor unrelated to coherence.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"tree\",\"title\":\"Coherence axioms\",\"root\":\"Coherent risk measure\",\"children\":[\"Monotonicity\",\"Translation invariance\",\"Positive homogeneity\",\"Subadditivity\"]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An analyst estimates volatility using an exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) model with $\\lambda = 0.94$. Yesterday's estimated daily volatility was 1.0% and yesterday's actual return was 3.0%. The updated daily volatility estimate is closest to:\nA) 1.00% B) 1.22% C) 1.34% D) 3.00%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\lambda \\sigma_{n-1}^2 + (1-\\lambda)u_{n-1}^2 = 0.94(0.01)^2 + 0.06(0.03)^2.$$ $$= 0.94(0.0001) + 0.06(0.0009) = 0.000094 + 0.000054 = 0.000148.$$ $$\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{0.000148} = 0.012166 \\approx 1.22\\%.$$ Trap: option A ignores the new return entirely (just keeps yesterday's vol); option D over-weights the return as if $\\lambda$ were small (treats it like the raw return); option C overstates the update.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A GARCH(1,1) model is specified as $$\\sigma_n^2 = \\omega + \\alpha u_{n-1}^2 + \\beta \\sigma_{n-1}^2$$ with $\\omega = 0.000002$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, and $\\beta = 0.93$. The long-run (unconditional) daily variance implied by this model is closest to:\nA) 0.0001000 B) 0.0000200 C) 0.0001053 D) 0.0000211\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** Long-run variance $$V_L = \\frac{\\omega}{1 - \\alpha - \\beta} = \\frac{0.000002}{1 - 0.05 - 0.93} = \\frac{0.000002}{0.02} = 0.0001.$$ Trap: option B mistakes $\\omega$ for the variance; options C and D apply the formula with arithmetic slips on the denominator (e.g., using $1-\\beta$ only). The exact value is 0.0001.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** In the GARCH(1,1) model from the previous question, current daily variance is $0.0004$. Under mean reversion, the expected variance in 10 days is closest to (use $\\alpha + \\beta = 0.98$ and long-run variance $V_L = 0.0001$):\nA) 0.000345 B) 0.000400 C) 0.000300 D) 0.000246\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** $$E[\\sigma_{n+t}^2] = V_L + (\\alpha+\\beta)^t(\\sigma_n^2 - V_L).$$ $$= 0.0001 + (0.98)^{10}(0.0004 - 0.0001) = 0.0001 + 0.8171 \\times 0.0003.$$ $$= 0.0001 + 0.0002451 = 0.0003451 \\approx 0.000345.$$ Trap: option B forgets reversion (keeps current variance); option C/D use wrong persistence or sign.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A bond is rated using a one-year rating transition matrix. The probability that a BBB-rated bond is still rated BBB after one year is 86%, downgraded to below investment grade is 5%, and defaults is 0.2%. Assuming year-over-year transitions are independent (Markov), the probability the bond remains BBB for two consecutive years is closest to:\nA) 86.0% B) 73.96% C) 92.0% D) 74.40%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Under Markov independence, the two-year survival in BBB is $$0.86 \\times 0.86 = 0.7396 = 73.96\\%.$$ Trap: option A forgets the second year; option D incorrectly multiplies by a near-1 adjustment; option C adds rather than multiplies.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which of the following is a key limitation of using historical rating transition matrices to estimate default probabilities?\nA) They are forward-looking and ignore historical default experience. B) They assume the rating process is path-dependent across multiple periods. C) They are based on through-the-cycle ratings, so they may not reflect current point-in-time credit conditions. D) They produce default probabilities that are always larger than market-implied (risk-neutral) probabilities.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Agency ratings are through-the-cycle, so transition matrices built from them lag and may not reflect current point-in-time conditions. Trap: A is backwards (they are historical, not forward-looking); B is wrong (matrices assume the Markov/path-independent property); D is false — historical (real-world) PDs are generally smaller than risk-neutral PDs, not larger.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"Transition matrix limitation\",\"options\":[\"Forward-looking\",\"Drop Markov property\",\"Through-the-cycle, lag PIT\",\"Real-world PD > risk-neutral\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Agency ratings lag current conditions\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A bank holds a $10,000,000 loan with a probability of default (PD) of 2%, a loss given default (LGD) of 40%, and exposure at default equal to the full notional. The expected loss (EL) on this loan is:\nA) $200,000 B) $80,000 C) $400,000 D) $40,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$EL = PD \\times LGD \\times EAD = 0.02 \\times 0.40 \\times 10{,}000{,}000 = 80{,}000.$$ Trap: option A omits LGD ($0.02 \\times 10\\text{m}$); option C omits PD ($0.40 \\times 10\\text{m} \\times 0.1$ slip); option D uses $0.02 \\times 0.40 \\times$ wrong notional.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** For the loan in the previous question, the standard deviation of the loss rate (unexpected loss driver) uses the Bernoulli default indicator. The standard deviation of PD, $\\sqrt{PD(1-PD)}$, is closest to:\nA) 0.0200 B) 0.1400 C) 0.0196 D) 0.1980\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\sqrt{PD(1-PD)} = \\sqrt{0.02 \\times 0.98} = \\sqrt{0.0196} = 0.140.$$ Trap: option C reports the variance 0.0196 rather than its square root; option A reports PD itself; option D miscomputes the square root.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Under the Merton model, a firm's equity is valued as a call option on the firm's assets. Holding all else constant, an increase in the volatility of the firm's asset value will:\nA) Decrease the value of equity and decrease the value of risky debt. B) Increase the value of equity and decrease the value of risky debt. C) Increase the value of both equity and risky debt. D) Decrease the value of equity and increase the value of risky debt.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Equity is a call on firm assets, so higher asset volatility increases equity value (longer-tail upside). Debtholders are effectively short a put on assets; higher volatility raises the put value, reducing risky-debt value. So equity up, debt down. Trap: A and D get the equity/debt direction wrong; C ignores that volatility transfers value from debtholders to equityholders.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Higher asset volatility (Merton)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Equity = call\",\"items\":[\"Long call on assets\",\"Value rises\"]},{\"h\":\"Debt = short put\",\"items\":[\"Short put on assets\",\"Put worth more\",\"Risky debt falls\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which of the following components is included in the standard categorization of operational risk loss event types under the Basel framework?\nA) Market liquidity risk B) Interest rate risk in the banking book C) Internal fraud D) Strategic risk\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Internal fraud is one of the seven Basel operational-risk loss event categories (along with external fraud; employment practices; clients/products/business practices; damage to physical assets; business disruption/system failures; execution/delivery/process management). Trap: A, B, and D are market/banking-book/strategic risks, which are explicitly outside the operational risk definition.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"A Basel operational-risk event type\",\"options\":[\"Interest rate risk\",\"Equity price risk\",\"Internal fraud\",\"Strategic risk\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"One of the seven op-risk loss categories\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A bank uses the loss distribution approach (LDA) for operational risk. It models loss frequency and loss severity separately. Which combination of distributions is most commonly assumed?\nA) Normal frequency and lognormal severity B) Poisson frequency and lognormal severity C) Binomial frequency and normal severity D) Poisson frequency and Poisson severity\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** The LDA standardly models frequency with a Poisson distribution and severity with a lognormal distribution, then convolves them. Trap: A/C/D use distributions not standard for operational-risk frequency-severity modeling; Poisson-Poisson (D) wrongly applies a count distribution to severity.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Loss Distribution Approach\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Frequency\",\"items\":[\"Count of events\",\"Poisson\"]},{\"h\":\"Severity\",\"items\":[\"Loss size\",\"Lognormal\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which of the following best describes a reverse stress test?\nA) Starting from a defined adverse macroeconomic scenario and computing the resulting portfolio loss. B) Starting from a predefined outcome (such as bank failure) and identifying the scenarios that would produce it. C) Applying historical scenarios in reverse chronological order. D) Stress testing only the assets while leaving liabilities unchanged.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** A reverse stress test starts from a defined adverse outcome (e.g., insolvency or breaching a capital threshold) and works backward to identify scenarios that could cause it. Trap: A describes a conventional (forward) scenario stress test; C and D are not what \"reverse\" means.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Forward vs Reverse stress test\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Forward\",\"items\":[\"Start with scenario\",\"Compute the outcome\"]},{\"h\":\"Reverse\",\"items\":[\"Start with bad outcome\",\"Find scenarios that cause it\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A zero-coupon bond pays $1,000 in two years. The continuously compounded two-year spot rate is 4%. The price of the bond today is closest to:\nA) $923.12 B) $921.05 C) $924.56 D) $960.79\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** With continuous compounding, $$P = 1000 \\, e^{-0.04 \\times 2} = 1000 \\, e^{-0.08} = 1000 \\times 0.923116 = 923.12.$$ Trap: option C uses annual compounding $1000/1.04^2 = 924.56$; option D uses one year of discounting only ($1000 e^{-0.04}=960.79$); option B applies a slightly mismeasured exponent. The continuously compounded answer is $923.12$.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** The one-year continuously compounded spot rate is 3.0% and the two-year continuously compounded spot rate is 4.0%. The one-year forward rate beginning in one year (continuously compounded) is closest to:\nA) 3.5% B) 4.0% C) 5.0% D) 4.5%\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** With continuous compounding, the forward rate satisfies $$f_{1,2} = \\frac{r_2 \\cdot 2 - r_1 \\cdot 1}{2 - 1} = \\frac{0.04(2) - 0.03(1)}{1} = 0.08 - 0.03 = 0.05 = 5.0\\%.$$ Trap: option A averages the two spot rates; option B/D interpolate incorrectly. The continuously compounded forward locks in 5.0%.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** An investor observes that a coupon bond is trading at a price equal to its face value. Which of the following must be true?\nA) The bond's yield to maturity equals its coupon rate. B) The bond's yield to maturity is greater than its coupon rate. C) The bond's current yield is zero. D) The bond must be a zero-coupon bond.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: A)** A bond trading at par has YTM equal to its coupon rate. Trap: B describes a discount bond (price below par, YTM > coupon); C is false (current yield = coupon/price > 0); D is false — par pricing applies to coupon bonds too.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"scale\",\"title\":\"Price vs YTM relative to coupon\",\"items\":[\"Discount: YTM>coupon\",\"|Par: YTM=coupon\",\"Premium: YTM<coupon\"],\"left\":\"Below par\",\"right\":\"Above par\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A bond has a current price of $98.50, a modified duration of 6.2, and a convexity of 55. Using the duration-with-convexity approximation, the estimated percentage price change for a 100 basis point increase in yield is closest to:\nA) $-6.20\\%$ B) $-5.93\\%$ C) $-6.48\\%$ D) $-5.65\\%$\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** $$\\frac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D \\cdot \\Delta y + \\tfrac{1}{2} C (\\Delta y)^2 = -6.2(0.01) + 0.5(55)(0.01)^2.$$ $$= -0.062 + 0.5(55)(0.0001) = -0.062 + 0.00275 = -0.05925 \\approx -5.93\\%.$$ Trap: option A uses duration only and ignores the positive convexity adjustment; option C subtracts convexity (wrong sign); option D over-adjusts.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A bond has a DV01 of $0.085 per $100 of face value. A portfolio holds $50,000,000 face value of this bond. The approximate change in the portfolio's value for a 1 basis point increase in yield is closest to:\nA) $4,250 B) $42,500 C) $425 D) $85,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** DV01 is the dollar value change per $100 face per 1 bp. Number of $100 units $= 50{,}000{,}000 / 100 = 500{,}000$. Value change $$= 500{,}000 \\times 0.085 = 42{,}500.$$ A 1 bp increase in yield lowers value by about $42,500. Trap: option A divides by 10 (wrong scaling); option C off by another factor of 100; option D uses face $\\times$ DV01 with a units error.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A trader wants to hedge a long position in Bond A (DV01 = $0.072) with a short position in Bond B (DV01 = $0.090). The position in Bond A has $20,000,000 face value. The face value of Bond B that should be shorted to be DV01-neutral is closest to:\nA) $25,000,000 B) $16,000,000 C) $20,000,000 D) $18,000,000\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** To be DV01-neutral, $$F_B = F_A \\times \\frac{DV01_A}{DV01_B} = 20{,}000{,}000 \\times \\frac{0.072}{0.090} = 20{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.8 = 16{,}000{,}000.$$ Trap: option A inverts the ratio ($0.090/0.072$); option C ignores the DV01 difference; option D uses an incorrect ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Which of the following statements about key rate shifts (key rate '01s) is most accurate?\nA) Key rate shifts assume that all spot rates move by the same amount. B) The sum of the key rate '01s for a bond is unrelated to its total DV01. C) Key rate exposures capture the sensitivity of a position to non-parallel shifts of the yield curve. D) Key rate analysis can only be applied to zero-coupon bonds.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Key rate exposures (key rate '01s) measure sensitivity to shifts at individual points on the curve, capturing non-parallel (e.g., steepening/flattening) movements. Trap: A describes a parallel shift (that is DV01, not key rates); B is false — the key rate '01s sum approximately to total DV01; D is false — key rate analysis applies to any fixed-income position.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"DV01 vs Key rate '01s\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"DV01\",\"items\":[\"Parallel shift only\",\"One number\"]},{\"h\":\"Key rate '01s\",\"items\":[\"Shifts at single points\",\"Non-parallel moves\",\"Sum ≈ total DV01\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A stock currently trades at $50. In a one-step binomial model, the stock can move up to $55 or down to $45 over the next period. The continuously compounded risk-free rate is 4% per period. The risk-neutral probability of an up move is closest to:\nA) 0.5000 B) 0.7041 C) 0.5408 D) 0.4592\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With $u = 55/50 = 1.1$, $d = 45/50 = 0.9$, $$p = \\frac{e^{r} - d}{u - d} = \\frac{e^{0.04} - 0.9}{1.1 - 0.9} = \\frac{1.040811 - 0.9}{0.2} = \\frac{0.140811}{0.2} = 0.7041.$$ Trap: option A assumes equal probabilities; option D uses $1-p$; option C uses simple (non-continuous) compounding incorrectly.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Using the binomial model parameters from the previous question ($S=50$, up to $55$, down to $45$, $r=4\\%$ per period, risk-neutral $p \\approx 0.7041$), the value today of a European call option with a strike of $50$ is closest to:\nA) $5.00 B) $3.38 C) $3.52 D) $2.50\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Up payoff $= \\max(55-50,0)=5$; down payoff $= \\max(45-50,0)=0$. $$C = e^{-0.04}\\left[0.7041(5) + 0.2959(0)\\right] = e^{-0.04}(3.5205) = 0.96079 \\times 3.5205 = 3.382 \\approx 3.38.$$ Trap: option A forgets discounting (uses $p \\times 5$ with $p=1$ style); option D uses equal probabilities ($0.5 \\times 5$ undiscounted); option C forgets to discount the expected payoff.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A two-step binomial tree has $u = 1.1$, $d = 0.9$, and a risk-neutral up probability $p = 0.6$ per step. What is the risk-neutral probability that the asset finishes at the highest terminal node (two up moves)?\nA) 0.60 B) 0.36 C) 0.48 D) 0.16\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Probability of two up moves $$= p^2 = 0.6^2 = 0.36.$$ Trap: option A reports a single-step $p$; option C reports the middle-node probability $2p(1-p)=2(0.6)(0.4)=0.48$; option D reports two-down $(1-p)^2 = 0.16$.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Under the Black-Scholes-Merton model, which of the following changes will, all else equal, INCREASE the value of a European put option on a non-dividend-paying stock?\nA) An increase in the underlying stock price B) An increase in the risk-free interest rate C) An increase in the volatility of the underlying D) A decrease in the time to expiration\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** Higher volatility raises the value of all options, including European puts. Trap: A (higher stock price) lowers put value; B (higher rate) lowers put value via the discounted strike; D (less time) generally lowers a European put's value (less time value). Only the volatility increase reliably raises the put.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"What raises a European put's value?\",\"options\":[\"Higher stock price\",\"Higher interest rate\",\"Higher volatility\",\"Less time to expiry\"],\"correct\":2,\"why\":\"Volatility raises value of all options\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** The Black-Scholes-Merton price of a European call uses $d_1$ and $d_2$. For a call, $N(d_1)$ represents:\nA) The risk-neutral probability that the call expires in the money. B) The option's gamma. C) The option's delta. D) The present value of the strike price.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: C)** In the BSM call formula $c = S_0 N(d_1) - K e^{-rT} N(d_2)$, the term $N(d_1)$ is the call's delta (sensitivity to the underlying). Trap: A confuses $N(d_1)$ with $N(d_2)$, which is the risk-neutral probability the call finishes in the money; B and D misidentify the term.\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"BSM call: N(d1) vs N(d2)\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"N(d1)\",\"items\":[\"Call's delta\",\"Sensitivity to underlying\"]},{\"h\":\"N(d2)\",\"items\":[\"Risk-neutral prob ITM\",\"Prob call exercised\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Which of the following statements about option Greeks is correct?\nA) Gamma is largest for deep in-the-money and deep out-of-the-money options. B) The theta of a long European call on a non-dividend-paying stock is typically negative. C) Vega is highest for options that are deep in the money. D) The delta of a long call ranges from $-1$ to $0$.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** Theta of a long European call on a non-dividend stock is typically negative (time decay erodes value). Trap: A is false — gamma is largest for at-the-money options, not deep ITM/OTM; C is false — vega is highest for at-the-money options; D is false — a long call's delta ranges from 0 to +1 (it is the put whose delta is −1 to 0).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"highlight\",\"title\":\"True statement about Greeks\",\"options\":[\"Gamma largest deep ITM/OTM\",\"Long call theta negative\",\"Vega highest deep OTM\",\"Long call delta -1 to 0\"],\"correct\":1,\"why\":\"Time decay erodes long call value\"}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** A delta-neutral portfolio of options has a gamma of $-500$ and a vega of $1,200$. The underlying stock makes a sudden large move in either direction. Holding implied volatility constant, the impact on the delta-neutral portfolio's value from the large price move (gamma effect) will be:\nA) Positive, because gamma amplifies gains from large moves. B) Negative, because negative gamma causes losses for large moves in either direction. C) Zero, because the portfolio is delta-neutral. D) Dependent only on the sign of vega, not gamma.\n<details><summary>Answer &amp; explanation</summary>\n**Correct: B)** With negative gamma, a delta-neutral portfolio loses value when the underlying makes a large move in either direction (the delta hedge fails to keep pace), so the gamma effect is negative. Trap: A describes positive gamma; C wrongly assumes delta-neutrality eliminates second-order (gamma) P&L; D ignores that the price-move P&L is driven by gamma, not vega (vega captures volatility changes, which are held constant here).\n[[DIAGRAM]]{\"type\":\"compare\",\"title\":\"Delta-neutral with gamma\",\"cols\":[{\"h\":\"Negative gamma\",\"items\":[\"Loses on large moves\",\"Either direction\",\"Hedge lags\"]},{\"h\":\"Positive gamma\",\"items\":[\"Gains on large moves\",\"Hedge keeps pace\"]}]}[[/DIAGRAM]]\n</details>\n\n"}]};
